the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"SOURCE STATUS NOTE — NOT A TRANSCRIPTION\nRecord: Section 21 response referred to in the 21 September 2026 request\nDate: 14 June 2026\nSender: Paul Egan, Clerk (as described in later correspondence)\nTimestamp: original header not recovered\n\nThe later request states that Paul Egan’s 14 June response said that the minute books from 1982 onwards had been deposited with Glamorgan Archives in 2002 and invoked FOIA section 21 (information accessible by other means). The 14 June source email has not been separately recovered in the public correspondence catalogue; this note records the later description, not the original wording.\n\nCross-reference: https://greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com/evidence-library/ghf-e170-llandough-council-foi-eir-request-section-12-refusal-2026/\n"},{"id":"wb-b7a3d2df","subject":"Llandough Community Council reply quoted source status","name":"2026-09-20 - Llandough Community Council reply quoted - source status - EN - V1.0.0.00.txt","date":"2026-09-20","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Council and local authority correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-09-20%20-%20Llandough%20Community%20Council%20reply%20quoted%20-%20source%20status%20-%20EN%20-%20V1.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"llandough-community-council-reply-quoted-source-status","scopeTopics":["email","ghf","llandough","council","paul-egan"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email","ghf","llandough","council","paul-egan"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-09-20 - Llandough Community Council reply quoted - source status - EN - V1.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"20 September 2026 — wording quoted inside the Council’s 28 September email; original 20 September message not separately recovered.\n\n“I note that our Clerk, Paul Egan, has already directed you to all the information we have on the matter as indicated in paragraph 6 of your email. We have nothing more to add.”\n\nSource: published Wiki transcription GHF-E106-R53, quoting the 28 September correspondence. This is a quotation, not a recovered original message.\n"},{"id":"wb-eb857135","subject":"Llandough Community Council FOI EIR request public transcription","name":"2026-09-21 - Llandough Community Council FOI EIR request - public transcription - EN - V1.0.0.00.txt","date":"2026-09-21","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Council and local authority correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-09-21%20-%20Llandough%20Community%20Council%20FOI%20EIR%20request%20-%20public%20transcription%20-%20EN%20-%20V1.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"llandough-community-council-foi-eir-request-public-transcription","scopeTopics":["email","ghf","llandough","council"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email","ghf","llandough","council"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-09-21 - Llandough Community Council FOI EIR request - public transcription - EN - V1.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"PUBLIC TRANSCRIPTION — NOT THE ORIGINAL SENT-ITEM FILE\n\nPUBLIC TRANSCRIPTION FROM A LATER QUOTATION — NOT THE ORIGINAL SENT-ITEM FILE\nRecord: GHF-E170 / 21 September 2026 request\nDate: 21 September 2026 (the page also records a reference in the text to a 20 September request date)\nSender: Requester\nTimestamp: not independently verified from original headers\n\nDr Misra,\n\nThank you for replying, and for doing so promptly and at a weekend. Good\ndrills Sir. What an honour. I know you and the Council serve as volunteers,\nand I am grateful for the time.\n\nLet me set out plainly what I am asking of the Council, which is narrow.\nIf you'll allow me?\n\nI am not asking your Council to revisit Mr Egan's answer of 14 June 2026.\nHe explained that the minute books from 1982 onwards were deposited with\nGlamorgan Archives in 2002, and I am pursuing those there.\n\nWhat I am asking about is what that answer did not cover. Llandough\nCommunity Council is a public authority under Schedule 1, Part II,\nparagraph 7(b) of the Freedom of Information Act 2000. The Act applies to\nthe recorded information set out below generally; the Environmental\nInformation Regulations 2004 apply in addition to any part of it that is\nenvironmental information.\n\nPART ONE — REQUEST FOR RECORDED INFORMATION\n\n1. Whether the Council holds any recorded information created after the\n2002 deposit — minutes, correspondence, reports, emails, photographs or\nfiles of any kind — concerning Great House Farm (Ty Mawr) / Church View\nClose, the village green in front of St Dochdwy's including registered\ngreen VG 41, or the roadside verge by the churchyard wall.\n\n2. Whether the Council holds any photographs, postcards or images of the\nfarm, the barn, the green or the church surroundings, of any date.\n\n3. Whether the Council holds any recorded information showing what, if\nanything, transferred to it from Penarth Town Council at the 1982\nreorganisation, and any recorded information identifying where the pre-1982\nLlandough community records were deposited.\n\nIf the answer to any of these is that no such information is held, I would\nbe content with a short statement to that effect — but I do need it stated.\nSection 1(1)(a) of the Act requires the Council to confirm or deny whether\nthe information is held.\n\nSection 10 (and regulation 5 of the EIR) allows twenty working days from\nreceipt, which on my request of 20 September 2026 I calculate as 16 October\n2026.\n\nIf the Council is refusing, section 17 (and regulation 14) requires a\nwritten refusal notice identifying the exemption or exception relied on;\nsection 17(7) requires the Council to give particulars of any complaints\nprocedure it operates, or to state that it has none, and to notify me of my\nright to apply to the Information Commissioner; and regulation 11 gives a\nright to an internal review of any refusal under the EIR.\n\n\"We have nothing more to add\" does not meet those requirements. If the\nperiod passes without a compliant response I will have no option but to\ncomplain to the Information Commissioner.\n\nPART TWO — MATTERS FOR THE COUNCIL'S DISCRETION\n\nThese are not part of the request above and carry no deadline. They are\nsimply things I would be grateful for.\n\nA. I am not asking for the name or contact details of any individual. I am\nasking whether the Council would be willing to pass my details to any\nformer member, former clerk or long-standing resident who might remember\nthe village between the 1950s and the 1990s, leaving it entirely to them\nwhether to contact me. I appreciate the Council may have data protection or\nadministrative reasons for declining, and I would not press it.\n\nB. Whether the Council would be willing to table this correspondence at a\nmeeting and minute it. I am content for it to be tabled in full.\n\nC. Whether the Council would consider, in due course, whether an\ninterpretation board or a plaque near the green would be appropriate. The\nexcavation on the land beneath Church View Close recorded 1,026 burials,\ndescribed in the published report as by far the largest early medieval\nburial population so far recovered from Wales, above a Roman villa and an\nIron Age roundhouse. That history belongs to Llandough as much as to my\nfamily, and there is nothing on the ground that records it. I would be glad\nto give the Council or the community a short talk on the documentary\nrecord, without charge, if that would be useful.\n\nI should be grateful for a response to items 1 to 3 by 16 October 2026.\n\nRegards,\n\nSion Buckler (Ret'd Sergeant)\nRoyal Signals Cyber/ Electronic Warfare Professional and Life Member\n\nProvenance: this text is reproduced in the public GHF-E170 page as transcribed from the request quoted in the 28 September correspondence. The original sent-item email was not separately recovered in the reviewed public record.\n"},{"id":"wb-b0aca775","subject":"Llandough Community Council reply public transcription","name":"2026-09-28 - Llandough Community Council reply - public transcription - EN - V1.0.0.00.txt","date":"2026-09-28","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Council and local authority correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-09-28%20-%20Llandough%20Community%20Council%20reply%20-%20public%20transcription%20-%20EN%20-%20V1.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"llandough-community-council-reply-public-transcription","scopeTopics":["email","ghf","llandough","council"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email","ghf","llandough","council"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-09-28 - Llandough Community Council reply - public transcription - EN - V1.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"PUBLIC TRANSCRIPTION — NOT THE ORIGINAL EMAIL FILE\nRecord: GHF-E106-R54\nDate: 28 September 2026\nSender: Dr Mo Misra, Chair, Llandough Community Council\nTimestamp: not stated in the published transcription\n\nDear Mr Buckler,\n\nYour email of the 21st September has been passed over to our clerk (in copy) for consideration.\n\nBest regards\nDr Mo Misra\nChair, Llandough Community Council\n\nProvenance: transcription from the published Great House Farm Wiki page GHF-E106-R54. Original .eml/header timestamps were not included in the public source.\n"},{"id":"wb-cca30757","subject":"Llandough Community Council section 12 refusal public transcription","name":"2026-10-11 - Llandough Community Council Section 12 refusal - public transcription - EN - V1.0.0.00.txt","date":"2026-10-11","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Council and local authority correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-10-11%20-%20Llandough%20Community%20Council%20Section%2012%20refusal%20-%20public%20transcription%20-%20EN%20-%20V1.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"llandough-community-council-section-12-refusal-public-transcription","scopeTopics":["email","ghf","llandough","council","paul-egan"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email","ghf","llandough","council","paul-egan"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-10-11 - Llandough Community Council Section 12 refusal - public transcription - EN - V1.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"PUBLIC TRANSCRIPTION — NOT THE ORIGINAL EMAIL FILE\nRecord: GHF-E106-R55\nDate: 11 October 2026\nSender: Paul R. Egan, signed as Clerk to Llandough Community Council\nTimestamp: not stated in the published transcription\n\nDear Mr Buckler,\r\n\r\nThank you for your email of  21 September 2026 making a request for \r\ninformation.\r\n\r\nWe are treating your correspondence as a request for information under \r\nthe Freedom of Information Act 2000 (the act).\r\n\r\nUnder the provisions of the act and as defined by \"The Freedom of \r\nInformation and Data Protection (Appropriate Limit and Fees) Regulations \r\n2004\" (the regulations) , an authority is not obliged to comply with a \r\nrequest for information if the authority estimates that the cost of \r\ncomplying with the request would exceed the \"appropriate limit\".  This \r\nappropriate limit is set by the regulations at £450 for our type of \r\nauthority.\r\n\r\nIn the regulations our staff time is valued at £25 per hour.  This has \r\nthe effect of imposing a time limit of 18 hours of staff time for \r\nactivities specified in the regulations.\r\n\r\n  The regulations make the following activities by an authority countable \r\ntowards the cost limit.\r\n\r\n  \t* determining whether it holds the information,\r\n\r\n  \t* locating the information, or a document which may contain the \r\ninformation,\r\n\r\n  \t* retrieving the information, or a document which may contain the \r\ninformation, and\r\n\r\n  \t* extracting the information from a document containing it.\r\n\r\n  We have estimated the time and therefore costs as provided by the \r\nregulations, and in doing so have taken into account the above \r\nactivities.\r\n\r\n  We estimate that this work will take in excess of 18 hours of effort by \r\nour staff. As we have a significant amount of documents on file from the \r\nperiod 2002-2016 where minutes are in documents rather than published on \r\nthe website or in archives.\r\n\r\n  We estimate this will exceed the limit provided for by the regulations \r\nand therefore your request is refused on the grounds provided for under \r\nsection 12 of the act.\r\n\r\nOur estimate of time is based on the requirement to search all minutes \r\nof the council and its committees for a period of 14 years. It is \r\nestimated that it will take about 20 minutes to read each set of minutes \r\nof which there are estimated to 224 sets of minutes. It is considered \r\nthat this is likely to take about 10 minutes to assess each set of \r\nminutes a total of over 2240 minutes which equates to 37 hours.\r\n\r\n  We may may be able to provide some information if you are able to \r\nreduce or refine your request to bring the cost of compliance under the \r\nregulations limit.  We suggest that you could limit the time frame of \r\nminutes that we would need to look through. For example 2002-2006.\r\n\r\n  Please contact us if you would like to refine your request.\r\n\r\n  If you are not satisfied with this response or you wish to complain \r\nabout any aspect of the handling of your request, then you should \r\ncontact us in the first instance and we will undertake an internal \r\nreview.\r\n\r\n  If after the internal review you wish to complain further then you can \r\ncomplain to the Information Commissioners Office http://www.ico.gov.uk \r\n[1] and request a decision notice.\r\n\r\nThe other matters you include in your email will be considered at the \r\nOctober meeting of the council and I will let you have the views of the \r\ncouncil after the meeting.\n\nKind regards,\n\nPaul R. Egan BA, Chartered MCIPD, CiLCA, F. Inst. LM, FIPSM\nClerk to Llandough Community Council\n[Postal address, telephone details, standard privacy footer and quoted earlier message omitted.]\n\nProvenance: transcription from the published Great House Farm Wiki page GHF-E106-R55. Postal address, telephone details, standard footer and quoted earlier message were omitted in the source page.\n"},{"id":"wb-9f8d3063","subject":"Llandough Community Council family email superseded 0107 utc public","name":"2026-10-13 - Llandough Community Council outgoing family email - superseded 0107 UTC - public copy - EN - V1.0.0.00.txt","date":"2026-10-13","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Council and local authority correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-10-13%20-%20Llandough%20Community%20Council%20outgoing%20family%20email%20-%20superseded%200107%20UTC%20-%20public%20copy%20-%20EN%20-%20V1.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"llandough-community-council-family-email-superseded-0107-utc-public","scopeTopics":["email","ghf","llandough","council"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email","ghf","llandough","council"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-10-13 - Llandough Community Council outgoing family email - superseded 0107 UTC - public copy - EN - V1.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"PUBLIC ARCHIVE RELEASE COPY — REDACTED FOR PRIVACY\nEvidence key: outgoing family email; chronology links this version to 13 October 2026, 01:07 UTC.\nStatus: superseded by the revised message at 03:25 UTC; retained to preserve the record of the thread.\nSource lineage: Wayback version V0.0.0.00. The source text itself has no original message header. A home postal address and personal email address are withheld from this public copy.\n\nDear Mr Egan,\n\nThank you for your email of 11 October 2026. It contains several separate matters, so I have answered each under its own number. Please respond to each separately. Nothing below withdraws or narrows my original request of 21 September, and attendance at a meeting is not a condition of my information rights.\n\nI am not asking the Council to decide the family's title dispute or to make findings about any individual, and I am not alleging wrongdoing by you. I ask that records be identified, searched for and disclosed, and that the matters in section 4 be considered.\n\n1. INTERNAL REVIEW OF THE 11 OCTOBER REFUSAL (FOIA s.12; EIR reg. 11)\n\nPlease treat this as a formal request for the internal review you offered, of the section 12 refusal of my request of 21 September. To the extent any information is environmental, please also treat it as representations under regulation 11 of the EIR 2004.\n\nMy request had three categories: (a) records created after the 2002 deposit concerning Great House Farm / Tý Mawr, Church View Close, the village green (including VG 41) or the roadside verge by the churchyard wall; (b) photographs, postcards or images of the farm, barn, green or church surroundings, of any date; (c) records showing what, if anything, transferred from Penarth Town Council at the 1982 reorganisation, and where pre-1982 records were deposited. I ask the reviewer to address:\n\n(i) Scope. The estimate covers only the reading of 224 sets of minutes for 2002-2016. Please explain why that is necessary and sufficient for each category, particularly (b) and (c), and what other searches were considered: correspondence and email files, photograph collections, transfer or deposit records, any index or electronic copy of the minutes, and sampling.\n\n(ii) Arithmetic. The letter gives 20 minutes to read plus 10 minutes to assess each set, which is 30 minutes x 224 = 6,720 minutes (112 hours), but states 2,240 minutes (37 hours), which is 10 minutes x 224. Please confirm which calculation was used and which activities under regulation 4 of the Fees Regulations it includes. I accept the limit is exceeded on either figure.\n\n(iii) Section 12(2). Please say whether the cost of determining whether the information is held would itself exceed the limit, or whether the estimate concerns locating and extracting information once its existence is established.\n\n(iv) EIR. The green, verge, trees, woodland and land boundaries are likely to be environmental information. Please say which parts were assessed under the EIR. The FOIA cost limit is not an EIR refusal ground; if regulation 12(4)(b) is relied on, please give the reasons, the public interest assessment and the presumption in favour of disclosure.\n\n(v) Advice and assistance (s.16 FOIA; reg. 9 EIR). Limiting the minutes to 2002-2006 does not address (b) or (c). Please say whether any part can be answered within the limit and suggest a practical refinement.\n\n(vi) Without prejudice to the review: if the refusal is maintained, please treat parts (b) and (c) alone as a refined request, assessed under the correct regime, so that no time is lost.\n\nPlease have the review carried out by someone who took no part in the original decision, with the outcome and reasons in writing. I would be grateful for the outcome within 20 working days.\n\n2. NEW REQUEST FOR RECORDED INFORMATION (FOIA and, where environmental, EIR)\n\nThis is a separate request, not a refinement of the one under review. Please search for Council-held or controlled minutes, agendas, reports, correspondence, complaints, notes or referrals:\n\n(i) from approximately 1986 to 1990 concerning Great House Farm / Tý Mawr, its occupation, title or tenure, development or demolition, including any concerns raised by my late father, William \"Billy\" Buckler, or other family members. I have family information that my father raised concerns at Council meetings around 1988, described within the family as fraud-related. I cannot verify whether he did, what was said or what followed, and I do not present it as established fact. I ask whether the Council's records can establish what, if anything, was reported, how it was handled, and whether it was referred to any other body;\n\n(ii) of any date concerning the village green, roadside verge, trees and adjoining woodland, and any reported extension of Church View Close gardens beyond their boundaries into the woodland, including correspondence with the charity that holds the woodland, residents or other authorities;\n\n(iii) concerning the 2002 deposit: please confirm whether the Council retains or controls any copies of the minutes deposited with Glamorgan Archives, and identify any record of that transfer, its date, and any archival reference or destination known to the Council. I am separately pursuing the deposited volumes with Glamorgan Archives.\n\nI assert a proprietary interest in Parcel A and the adjoining woodland, but I do not ask the Council to adjudicate title. If nothing is held after appropriate searches, please say so. If anything is withheld, please identify the statutory basis and reasons.\n\n3. SUBJECT ACCESS REQUEST (Article 15 UK GDPR; Data Protection Act 2018)\n\nPlease provide a copy of my personal data held by the Council, including data in minutes, correspondence, reports, photographs or other records (including retained historical records) concerning my presence at Great House Farm as a child, the events surrounding the family's removal and the 1988 demolition, and any later record that identifies or refers to me. An electronic copy, ordinarily without charge, is fine. If you reasonably need identity evidence, please tell me promptly what is required. I understand the one-month period under Article 12(3) runs from receipt of this request, or of any identity evidence reasonably requested. This section concerns only my personal data; my father's reported statements and the wider records are dealt with in section 2.\n\n4. THE OCTOBER MEETING\n\nYour email says my \"other matters\" will be considered at the October meeting. Please confirm:\n- the purpose of that statement, and whether I am invited to attend;\n- the date, time and venue, public attendance arrangements and any remote option;\n- the agenda, item numbers and papers, as soon as available;\n- whether my correspondence will be circulated to councillors, how decisions and any actions will be minuted, and how I can obtain the minutes or formal outcome;\n- how I may make representations as a member of the public, including any procedure, deadline and speaking limit.\nI have not yet decided whether to attend.\n\nI ask that the following be placed on the agenda, as issues for clarification and not as findings:\n\n(a) From my 21 September email: whether the Council will pass my contact details to any former member, former clerk or long-standing resident who may wish to contact me (entirely at their choice); table and minute my correspondence in full; and accept my offer of a short talk, without charge, on the documentary record.\n\n(b) The green south of the former farmhouse: what the Council understands its present role to be (owner, manager, custodian or otherwise), and the recorded instrument, plan or Council decision on which that understanding rests. The family's research raises a question whether this land was distinct from the agricultural parcel addressed in the 1955 possession proceedings. I ask the Council to check its own records, not to decide that question.\n\n(c) The woodland behind the houses: I understand the charity responsible for it has raised concerns that some adjoining gardens extend beyond their boundaries. I cannot verify that. Please establish the charity's precise account, whether the Council holds relevant plans or correspondence, and refer any substantiated concern to the appropriate landowner or authority. I do not ask for findings about individual residents without evidence.\n\n(d) The 1989 planning record: the copy available to me refers to annexes containing representations from historic societies concerning Great House Farm, which appear to be absent. Please consider whether the Council holds copies and whether it will ask the planning authority or relevant archive to locate the complete papers. This matters to understanding what was put before the decision-makers following the demolition.\n\n(e) The historic site today: a site visit and, in due course, the merits of an interpretation board or plaque addressing the farm, the archaeological discoveries (the excavation beneath Church View Close recorded 1,026 burials, above a Roman villa and an Iron Age roundhouse) and the history of the green.\n\nThe same distinction applies to my father's reported concerns and to my own claim: they are matters requiring clarification, not established findings. If any proposed action is outside the Community Council's powers, please identify the appropriate body.\n\n5. CHARGES AND THE CLERK'S POSITION\n\nI do not authorise paid searches or accept any charge, and ask that no cost chargeable to me be incurred without my prior written agreement. If the Council offers to comply for a fee (FOIA s.13) or proposes a charge for environmental information, please send an itemised quotation for consideration only (not acceptance): the legal basis, the work proposed, hours and hourly rate, the total, whether the work can be staged (for example, parts 1(b) and 1(c) first), any published EIR charging schedule, payment methods, payee, payment reference and deadline.\n\nYour 11 October email is signed as Clerk, and I understood from public information that you retired from that post in June 2026. Please confirm: (a) whether you are currently Clerk, or acting or locum, and since when; (b) who is the Council's Proper Officer and who has custody of its records; (c) whether the 11 October decision was yours personally or the Council's; (d) whether any handover or inventory of records was made on a change of clerk; and (e) who will conduct the internal review. I ask only to establish who is accountable for the review and the records.\n\nPlease reply by email and by post to [… personal postal address withheld in public copy].\n\nThank you again for your time and courtesy.\n\nRegards,\n\nSion Buckler (Ret'd Sergeant)\n\nRoyal Signals Cyber / Electronic Warfare Professional and Life Member\n\n[… personal postal address withheld in public copy]\n"},{"id":"wb-f508e542","subject":"Llandough Community Council family email revised 0325 utc public","name":"2026-10-13 - Llandough Community Council outgoing family email - revised 0325 UTC - public copy - EN - V1.0.0.01.txt","date":"2026-10-13","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Council and local authority correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-10-13%20-%20Llandough%20Community%20Council%20outgoing%20family%20email%20-%20revised%200325%20UTC%20-%20public%20copy%20-%20EN%20-%20V1.0.0.01.txt","threadSlug":"llandough-community-council-family-email-revised-0325-utc-public","scopeTopics":["email","ghf","llandough","council"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email","ghf","llandough","council"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-10-13 - Llandough Community Council outgoing family email - revised 0325 UTC - public copy - EN - V1.0.0.01.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"PUBLIC ARCHIVE RELEASE COPY — REDACTED FOR PRIVACY\nEvidence key: outgoing family email; chronology links this version to 13 October 2026, 03:25 UTC.\nStatus: revised/canonical follow-up; it supersedes the 01:07 UTC message.\nSource lineage: Wayback version V0.0.0.01. The source text itself has no original message header. A home postal address and personal email address are withheld from this public copy.\n\nDear Mr Egan,\n\nThank you for your email of 11 October 2026. I write about matters of serious public interest to the family and to Llandough's history, and I ask the Council to treat this correspondence with priority and to give it its full cooperation. The email contains six separate matters. Please respond to each under its own number. Nothing below withdraws, narrows or replaces my original request of 21 September, and attendance at a meeting is not a condition of my information rights.\n\nI am not asking the Council to adjudicate the family's title dispute or to make findings about any individual, and I am not alleging wrongdoing by you. I am asking the Council to account accurately for its own records, knowledge, decisions and referrals, and to distinguish clearly between what its primary records establish, what is merely recorded as said, and what remains uncertain. If a search shows that nothing was recorded or referred, I ask that the Council say so plainly, because that is itself important.\n\n1. PRESERVATION OF RECORDS\n\nBecause these requests concern historic records that may be time-sensitive for other proceedings and bodies, please:\n(a) confirm in writing, within 5 working days, that all potentially relevant records have been preserved and that any scheduled disposal, destruction or retention-schedule review affecting them is suspended. This covers paper and electronic minutes, agendas, correspondence, emails, clerk's notes, plans, photographs, files and archive-transfer or deposit paperwork, and any such material held on the Council's behalf by a current or former councillor, clerk or officer, including at home;\n(b) ask each current councillor, and, where practicable, the Council's former clerks and members, whether they hold any such material, and include whatever is found in the searches below.\nI raise this as a precaution. For completeness, section 77 FOIA and regulation 19 of the EIR make it an offence to alter, conceal or destroy records with intent to prevent their disclosure once a request has been made.\n\n2. INTERNAL REVIEW OF THE 11 OCTOBER REFUSAL (FOIA s.12; EIR reg. 11)\n\nPlease treat this as a formal request for the internal review you offered, of the section 12 refusal of my request of 21 September. To the extent any information is environmental, please also treat it as representations under regulation 11 of the EIR 2004.\n\nMy request had three categories: (a) records created after the 2002 deposit concerning Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr, Church View Close, the village green (including VG 41) or the roadside verge by the churchyard wall; (b) photographs, postcards or images of the farm, barn, green or church surroundings, of any date; (c) records showing what, if anything, transferred from Penarth Town Council at the 1982 reorganisation, and where pre-1982 records were deposited. I ask the reviewer to address:\n\n(i) Scope. The estimate covers only the reading of 224 sets of minutes for 2002-2016. Please explain why that is necessary and sufficient for each category, particularly (b) and (c), and what other searches were considered: correspondence and email files, photograph collections, transfer or deposit records, any index, catalogue, minute heading or electronic copy of the minutes, targeted searches on the place names, former names and dates in my request, and sampling.\n\n(ii) Arithmetic. The letter gives 20 minutes to read plus 10 minutes to assess each set, which is 30 minutes x 224 = 6,720 minutes (112 hours), but states 2,240 minutes (37 hours), which is 10 minutes x 224. The figures do not reconcile. Please confirm which calculation was used and which activities under regulation 4 of the Fees Regulations it includes. I accept the limit is exceeded on either figure, so I do not rely on this point alone.\n\n(iii) Section 12(2). Please say whether the cost of determining whether the information is held would itself exceed the limit, or whether the estimate concerns locating and extracting information once its existence is established. Section 1(1)(a) duty to confirm or deny is preserved unless that cost alone exceeds the limit.\n\n(iv) EIR. The green, verge, trees, woodland and land boundaries are likely to be environmental information. Please say which parts were assessed under the EIR. The FOIA cost limit is not an EIR refusal ground; if regulation 12(4)(b) is relied on, please state it expressly with reasons and carry out the public interest assessment, including the presumption in favour of disclosure.\n\n(v) Advice and assistance (s.16 FOIA; reg. 9 EIR). Limiting the minutes to 2002-2006 does not address categories (b) or (c). Please say whether any part can be answered within the limit and advise a practical refinement.\n\n(vi) Without prejudice to the review: if the refusal is maintained, please treat categories (b) and (c) alone as a refined request, assessed under the correct regime, so that no time is lost.\n\nPlease acknowledge this request, identify who will conduct the review (someone who took no part in the original decision), and give the anticipated date. I would be grateful for the outcome and reasons in writing within 20 working days.\n\n3. NEW REQUEST FOR RECORDED INFORMATION (FOIA and, where environmental, EIR)\n\nThis is a separate request, not a refinement of the one under review. Please search for Council-held or controlled minutes, agendas, reports, correspondence, complaints, notes, representations or referrals:\n\n(i) from 1 January 1986 to 31 December 1990 concerning Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr, its occupation, title or tenure, development, removal of the occupants or demolition, including communications with the Vale of Glamorgan Council, planning authorities, developers, solicitors, landowners, police, heritage bodies or other public authorities. This includes any concerns raised by my late father, William \"Billy\" Buckler, or other family members. I have family information that my father raised concerns at Council meetings around 1988, described within the family as fraud-related. I cannot verify whether he did, what was said or what followed, and I do not present it as established fact. I ask whether the Council's records establish what, if anything, was reported, how it was handled, whether it was referred to any other body, and what response followed. References to fraud identify a subject for the search; they are not a claim that a record of fraud exists;\n\n(ii) planning records, plans, annexes, correspondence and supporting documents relating to application 89/01396/OUT (as I understand the reference) and any related consideration of the redevelopment. The copy available to me refers to annexes containing representations from historic societies concerning Great House Farm, which appear to be absent. Please confirm whether the Council holds them and, if not, whether its records identify another custodian or archive;\n\n(iii) of any date concerning the village green, roadside verge, trees and adjoining woodland: history, status, boundaries, maintenance, planting, public use, and any reported extension of Church View Close gardens beyond their boundaries into the woodland, including correspondence with the charity that holds the woodland, residents, the Vale of Glamorgan Council or other authorities. I am aware the official VG 41 registration file is understood to be held by the Vale of Glamorgan Council; I ask only for the Council's own copies, correspondence, representations and referrals;\n\n(iv) concerning the 2002 deposit: please confirm whether the Council retains or controls any copies of the minutes deposited with Glamorgan Archives, and identify any record of that transfer, its date, and any archival reference or destination known to the Council. I am separately pursuing the deposited volumes with Glamorgan Archives.\n\nI assert a proprietary interest in Parcel A and the adjoining woodland, but I do not ask the Council to adjudicate title. If any part is thought too broad, please identify that part and say what narrower search is practicable, rather than refusing the whole. If nothing is held after appropriate searches, please say so and identify any known transfer or deposit location. If anything is withheld, please identify the statutory basis and reasons. I understand the 20-working-day period under section 10 FOIA and regulation 5 EIR runs from receipt of this email.\n\n4. SUBJECT ACCESS REQUEST (Article 15 UK GDPR; Data Protection Act 2018)\n\nPlease provide a copy of my personal data held by the Council, including data in minutes, correspondence, reports, photographs or other records (including retained historical records) concerning my presence at Great House Farm as a child, the events surrounding the family's removal and the 1988 demolition, and any later record that identifies or refers to me. An electronic copy, ordinarily without charge, is fine. Please acknowledge this as a subject access request. If you reasonably need identity evidence, please tell me promptly what is required. I understand the one-month period under Article 12(3) runs from receipt of this request, or of any identity evidence reasonably requested. This section concerns only my personal data; my father's reported statements and the wider records are dealt with in section 3.\n\n5. THE OCTOBER MEETING\n\nYour email says my \"other matters\" will be considered at the October meeting. You gave no date, time, venue or agenda. Please confirm within 5 working days:\n- whether the meeting has already taken place and, if so, its date, the agenda and papers, the minute or other record of consideration, and any decision or action;\n- if it has not, the date, time, venue, public attendance arrangements and any remote option, and the draft or published agenda showing which of my matters are scheduled and under which item numbers;\n- whether I am invited to attend (I have not yet decided whether to) and how a member of the public may make representations on relevant business, including any procedure, deadline and speaking limit;\n- that this entire email, and my earlier correspondence, will be circulated to all councillors, how decisions and actions will be minuted, and how I can obtain the minutes or formal outcome.\n\nI ask that the following be placed on the agenda, as issues for clarification and not as findings:\n\n(a) From my 21 September email: whether the Council will pass my contact details to any former member, former clerk or long-standing resident who may wish to contact me (entirely at their choice, and with no disclosure of their details to me without consent); table and minute my correspondence in full; and accept my offer of a short talk, without charge, on the documentary record.\n\n(b) The green south of the former farmhouse: what the Council understands its present role to be (owner, manager, custodian or otherwise), and the recorded instrument, plan or Council decision on which that understanding rests. The family's research raises a question whether this land was distinct from the agricultural parcel addressed in the 1955 possession proceedings. I ask the Council to check its own records, not to decide that question.\n\n(c) The woodland behind the houses: I understand the charity responsible for it has raised concerns that some adjoining gardens extend beyond their boundaries. I cannot verify that. Please establish the charity's precise account, whether the Council holds relevant plans or correspondence, and refer any substantiated concern to the appropriate landowner or authority. I do not ask for findings about individual residents without evidence.\n\n(d) The 1989 planning record and the missing annexes (see 3(ii)): whether the Council holds copies and whether it will ask the planning authority or relevant archive to locate the complete papers. This matters to understanding what was put before the decision-makers following the demolition.\n\n(e) My father's reported 1980s concerns (see 3(i)): that the Council consider what its own records show and, if they show nothing, say so.\n\n(f) The historic site today: a site visit and, in due course, the merits of an interpretation board or plaque addressing the farm, the archaeological discoveries (the excavation beneath Church View Close recorded 1,026 burials, above a Roman villa and an Iron Age roundhouse) and the history of the green.\n\nIf any proposed action is outside the Community Council's powers, please identify the appropriate body.\n\n6. CHARGES AND THE CLERK'S POSITION\n\nI do not authorise paid searches or accept any charge, and ask that no cost chargeable to me be incurred without my prior written agreement. If the Council offers to comply for a fee (FOIA s.13) or proposes a charge for environmental information, please send an itemised quotation for consideration only (not acceptance): the legal basis, the work proposed, hours and hourly rate, the total, whether the work can be staged (for example, categories 2(b) and 2(c) first), any published EIR charging schedule, payment methods, payee, payment reference and deadline.\n\nYour 11 October email is signed as Clerk, and I understood from public information that you retired from that post in June 2026. I treat that as unverified. Please confirm: (a) whether you are currently Clerk, or acting or locum, and since when; (b) who is the Council's Proper Officer and who has custody of its records; (c) whether the 11 October decision was yours personally or the Council's; (d) whether any handover or inventory of records was made on a change of clerk; and (e) who will conduct the internal review.\n\nI would prefer to resolve all of this cooperatively and with the Council's full help. If the matters above are not dealt with properly, I will have to consider a complaint to the Information Commissioner (including on advice and assistance, the duty to confirm or deny and the EIR handling) and to the Public Services Ombudsman for Wales. I would rather not need to. Please acknowledge receipt of this email and each numbered part within 5 working days, and reply by email and by post to [… personal postal address withheld in public copy].\n\nThank you for your time and courtesy.\n\nRegards,\nSion Buckler (Ret'd Sergeant)\nRoyal Signals Cyber / Electronic Warfare Professional and Life Member\n[… personal postal address withheld in public copy]\n"},{"id":"wb-a18018fd","subject":"Llandough Community Council family reply source status 0626 utc","name":"2026-10-14 - Llandough Community Council family reply - source status - 0626 UTC - EN - V1.0.0.00.txt","date":"2026-10-14","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Council and local authority correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-10-14%20-%20Llandough%20Community%20Council%20family%20reply%20-%20source%20status%20-%200626%20UTC%20-%20EN%20-%20V1.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"llandough-community-council-family-reply-source-status-0626-utc","scopeTopics":["email","ghf","llandough","council"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email","ghf","llandough","council"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-10-14 - Llandough Community Council family reply - source status - 0626 UTC - EN - V1.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"SOURCE STATUS NOTE — NOT A TRANSCRIPTION\nDate/time: 14 October 2026, 06:26 UTC\nSender: Requester\n\nThe published correspondence chronology records a family reply thanking the Clerk and pointing to the meeting toolkit prepared for Item 10. The original sent message body is not transcribed in the current public source.\n\nCross-reference: https://greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com/llandough-community-council-cllr-carroll-correspondence-chronology/\n"},{"id":"wb-3914524a","subject":"Llandough Community Council agenda forward source status 0710 utc","name":"2026-10-14 - Llandough Community Council agenda forward - source status - 0710 UTC - EN - V1.0.0.00.txt","date":"2026-10-14","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Council and local authority correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-10-14%20-%20Llandough%20Community%20Council%20agenda%20forward%20-%20source%20status%20-%200710%20UTC%20-%20EN%20-%20V1.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"llandough-community-council-agenda-forward-source-status-0710-utc","scopeTopics":["email","ghf","llandough","council"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email","ghf","llandough","council"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-10-14 - Llandough Community Council agenda forward - source status - 0710 UTC - EN - V1.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"SOURCE STATUS NOTE — NOT A TRANSCRIPTION\nDate/time: 14 October 2026, 07:10 UTC\nSender: Requester\n\nThe published correspondence chronology records that the Clerk’s agenda email was forwarded to a Vale of Glamorgan councillor for information. The original forward body is not transcribed in the current public source.\n\nCross-reference: https://greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com/llandough-community-council-cllr-carroll-correspondence-chronology/\n"},{"id":"wb-b12a0f27","subject":"Glamorgan archives 2026 4097b","name":"2026-09-30 - Glamorgan Archives 2026-4097b - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","date":"2026-09-30","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Heritage and archives correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-09-30%20-%20Glamorgan%20Archives%202026-4097b%20-%20EN%20-%20V0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"glamorgan-archives-2026-4097b","scopeTopics":["email","ghf","foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email","ghf","foi"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-09-30 - Glamorgan Archives 2026-4097b - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"2026-09-30 Glamorgan Archives ref 2026/4097b\nFrom: Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg <Glamro@cardiff.gov.uk>\nTo: Requester <[redacted]>\nSubject: RE: Ty Mawr, Llandough, Cardiff\nGmail message 1a0f2f0f54a36630 thread 1a02ad4c2f0fd6e3\nMatching pdf/images path: not in wayback/pdf or wayback/images at filing.\n\nDear Requester,\n\nThank you for your additional email that was received on 8th September. Please accept our apologies for the delay in responding; we have recently been receiving a large number of enquiries.\n\n1. Daniel Thomas 1877 deed: we do not appear to hold any deeds relating to this transaction. A search of our catalogue has been carried out and we cannot suggest any documents that would be worth searching to find record of the transaction you are seeking information on that are within our collections. There are some records in the Cardiff Library Bute collection that may be relevant, see point 3.\n2. D696 Western Ground Rents: catalogue descriptions of D696/1, D696/2, D696/3. A member of the team could search these records at a charge, or they can be accessed free in the public searchroom. Only once searched could they confirm whether any information is held relating to land in Llandough and Ty Mawr / Great House Farm.\n3. Cardiff Library Bute Estate records: Bute X.6(12) contains abstracts of payments and receipts by the trustees of the Marquis of Bute's properties, 1877 (copy). Other similar records in Bute X might also be of interest. These records are currently still with Cardiff Library.\n4. Land Valuation 1910 maps at National Archives: research guide on the 1910 land valuation survey maps. Relevant Ordnance Survey sheet Glamorgan XLVII.6.\n\nThey will answer remaining emails in due course.\n\nYours sincerely,\nLouise Hunt, Archivist, Glamorgan Archives.\n"},{"id":"wb-9e686d72","subject":"Science museum FOI 2026 0038","name":"2026-10-01 - Science Museum FOI-2026-0038 - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","date":"2026-10-01","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"FOI / EIR correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-10-01%20-%20Science%20Museum%20FOI-2026-0038%20-%20EN%20-%20V0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"science-museum-foi-2026-0038","scopeTopics":["email","ghf","foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email","ghf","foi"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-10-01 - Science Museum FOI-2026-0038 - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"2026-10-01 Science Museum Group FOI/2026/0038 internal review outcome\nFrom: SCM Freedom of Information <FreedomofInformation@sciencemuseum.ac.uk>\nTo: Requester\nGmail message 1a0f816a33d92723 thread 19fd79ca6cd9a2af\nMatching pdf/images path: not filed; email body only.\n\nReview request received 3 September 2026.\n\nFiles searched: 80/502 MO Marconi Wareform Monitor Prom I.B.A.; 81/185 Ms Offer Mk I Camera GEC-Marconi Electronics Ltd; 81/261 Ms Marconi MK III camera channel Rowensbourne College of Art and Design; 82/120 MISCELLANEOUS OFFER - MARCONI TRANSMITTER; 84/75 MC Marconi Ltd; 84/380 DA Marconi; 1119 Nominal File Marconi-Osram Valves Co. Ltd. 1922-1994; 1120/55/1 Nominal File The Marconi Co. Ltd. list of items on loan 1971-1976; 1120 Pt1 Marconi's Wireless Telegraph Co. Ltd. 1906-1931; 1120 Pt2 1928-1972; 1120 Pt3 1931-1998; 6033 SCM Publications Marconi 1877-1937; 9643 Marconi Instruments Ltd.; T/2004-171 Gavey's ring transformer 1892 Lavernock and Flatholm; T/1948-87 fifteen unbound pages of scrap book press cuttings and photographs.\n\nT/1948-87 file contained an object label only. Object 1948-87 is the scrap book 1905-1910. One clipping timeline entry: 1897 Mr Marconi sent signals from Lavernock to Flatholm. No Williams. Archive searched and curators consulted. Williams not run as a bare name search; checked inside the listed files, none found. Object 2004-171 donated 2004 by BT Heritage; file T/2004-90 checked, no reference to the 1897 experiment.\n"},{"id":"wb-42447432","subject":"Moj 260904007","name":"2026-10-02 - MoJ 260904007 - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","date":"2026-10-02","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"FOI / EIR correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-10-02%20-%20MoJ%20260904007%20-%20EN%20-%20V0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"moj-260904007","scopeTopics":["email","ghf","foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email","ghf","foi"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-10-02 - MoJ 260904007 - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"2026-10-02 Ministry of Justice / HMCTS SAR internal review extension\nRef: 260904007 SAR IR (underlying SAR 260803049)\nFrom: NSUKilo@justice.gov.uk\nGmail message 1a0fd363100cc68b\nAttachment: 260904007 – SAR IR extension letter.pdf (not copied into wayback/pdf this run)\n\nRequest received 4 September 2026. Art 12(3) two-month extension. They say the request is complex, multiple systems, searches still underway. Response by 4 December 2026.\nUser reply same day narrowed scope and renounced the 133-house parcel.\n"},{"id":"wb-33e20441","subject":"Heneb ggat02038s","name":"2026-10-06 - Heneb GGAT02038s - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","date":"2026-10-06","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Heritage and archives correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-10-06%20-%20Heneb%20GGAT02038s%20-%20EN%20-%20V0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"heneb-ggat02038s","scopeTopics":["email","ghf","foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email","ghf","foi"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-10-06 - Heneb GGAT02038s - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"2026-10-06 Heneb HER reply\nFrom: HER <her@heneb.org.uk>\nTo: Requester\nDate: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 08:03:48 +0000\nSubject: RE: Great House Farm, Llandough, Cardiff\nGmail thread: 1a07efa0b6b10900 message: 1a1103d9d380a3b6\nMatching pdf/ images/: none.\n\nTranscript of the operative reply:\nI write in response to your email, Great House Farm, Llandough, Cardiff, dated 8 September 2026.\n\nI note that you have framed your request under the Environmental Information Regulations 2004. The question of whether Heneb falls within the scope of those Regulations is a matter of legal determination, and nothing in this response should be taken as expressing a view on that matter. Irrespective of that question, access to the information concerned is not restricted and the information is available on request directly from the HER team. Following its digitisation, it is now freely available online via the Archwilio website via record 02038s.\n\nThe additional information referred to in your email dated 19 September 2026 will require further time to review, insofar as it is held within the HER. We will respond separately in relation to that information.\n\nWithheld / not supplied in this letter: the four source-file items requested on 8 September (1880 ironwork entry and sources; RCAHMW/Suggett 1988 copy; museum accession slip; distinction between 1880, 1979 villa, and 1994 Burial 631 iron). No series, box, shelf, or physical-check statement.\n"},{"id":"wb-66c881e5","subject":"Rcahmw rc26 0375","name":"2026-10-06 - RCAHMW RC26-0375 - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","date":"2026-10-06","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Heritage and archives correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-10-06%20-%20RCAHMW%20RC26-0375%20-%20EN%20-%20V0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"rcahmw-rc26-0375","scopeTopics":["email","ghf","foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email","ghf","foi"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-10-06 - RCAHMW RC26-0375 - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"2026-10-06 RCAHMW RC26-0375 (includes RC26-0353, RC26-0368, RC26-0371)\nFrom: NMR Enquiries <nmr.wales@rcahmw.gov.uk> (Adam Coward)\nTo: Requester\nDate: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 13:47:33 +0000\nSubject: RC26-0375 - Ty Mawr/ Great House, Llandough, Cardiff - Further response\nGmail message: 1a11178b91ab138c\nMatching pdf/ images/: order form and price list referred to as attached; not stored in this transcript.\n\nGiven: extensive searches of registered files, administrative records, NMRW archive (term and spatial), manual search for uncatalogued material. No records of demolition circumstances. Archive 6048945 is the only allusion to lost C.N. Johns / Mrs Buckler correspondence; one page front and back; NMR Site Files, collection 6202405; no date or purpose of Suggett visit beyond catalogue; no site note, field sheet, photograph, sketch or correspondence. RCS2/1/1777 and RCS2/1/1776 physically reviewed and said to relate to the sites in the catalogue, not Great House Farm Llandough. Aerial search would be charged. Archive 6048943 is 1974 investigator notes; 6048941 is the ink plan with 12/12/1988 additions, copy in walesher1974 report 301361628. No transfer/disposal record. No provenance for 6048942 beyond what is public. Relies on FOIA s.21 (publicly accessible by other means). Physical searches of Registered Files and Site Files; staff consulted; nothing excluded.\nPublic catalogue: https://coflein.gov.uk/en/sites/20182/archives\n"},{"id":"wb-0a99e104","subject":"Museum wales FOI 2026 036","name":"2026-10-07 - Museum Wales FOI-2026-036 - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","date":"2026-10-07","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"FOI / EIR correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-10-07%20-%20Museum%20Wales%20FOI-2026-036%20-%20EN%20-%20V0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"museum-wales-foi-2026-036","scopeTopics":["email","ghf","foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email","ghf","foi"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-10-07 - Museum Wales FOI-2026-036 - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"2026-10-07 Museum Wales FOI 2026-036\nFrom: FOI Requests <FOI.Requests@museumwales.ac.uk>\nTo: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 07:11:32 +0000\nSubject: RE: FOI 2026-036 – Great House Farm, Llandough – filtered schedule still not supplied; unanswered points from FOI 2026-032 correction\nGmail thread: 1a1149cc3b8ce394 message: 1a115341cd46142d\nMatching pdf/ images/: none attached to this reply.\n\nTranscript:\nDear Mr Buckler\n\nThank you for your email.\n\nIn relation to the filtered schedule, Amgueddfa Cymru does not hold a separate recorded document identifying, item by item, which search results were excluded following review. Search results were reviewed by staff to determine whether they related to the location specified in your request, and the outcome of that review was reflected in our response of 5 October 2026. We do not hold a separate audit schedule of those decisions.\n\nIn relation to MS 3360/3, the relevant page was previously supplied in response to FOI 2026-032. That page records that \"Great House\" formed part of the Llandough Estate sale particulars of 1924, was occupied by W.G. Thomas & Sons, comprised approximately 202 acres, was situated in the Parish of Llanfair, and describes the homestead as being situated in the Village of Llandough. No additional recorded information identifying the holding has been identified within the material reviewed.\n\nIn relation to Ref 13.2, the collections record describes the item as a \"Waterwheel from Llandough Farm (opposite hospital)\". No further location information has been identified within the record.\n\nWe trust this clarifies the position.\n\nCofion cynnes,\nFOI Team\n"},{"id":"wb-66402953","subject":"Museum wales FOI 2026 036","name":"2026-10-07 - Museum Wales FOI-2026-036 - EN - V0.0.0.01.txt","date":"2026-10-07","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"FOI / EIR correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-10-07%20-%20Museum%20Wales%20FOI-2026-036%20-%20EN%20-%20V0.0.0.01.txt","threadSlug":"museum-wales-foi-2026-036","scopeTopics":["email","ghf","foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email","ghf","foi"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-10-07 - Museum Wales FOI-2026-036 - EN - V0.0.0.01.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"2026-10-07 Museum Wales FOI 2026-036 sent follow-up\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]>\nTo: FOI Requests <FOI.Requests@museumwales.ac.uk>\nDate: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 18:10:12 +0100\nSubject: Re: FOI 2026-036 – Great House Farm, Llandough – filtered schedule still not supplied; unanswered points from FOI 2026-032 correction\nLabels: SENT. Attachments: none.\nIn reply to Museum Wales 7 Oct 2026 07:11 UTC clarification, already stored as V0.0.0.00.\n\nTranscript:\nFOI Team,\n\nThank you for the clarification.\n\nYour email of 7 October materially clarifies the position, and I have now corrected my records to distinguish the different Llandough farmsteads.\n\nRef 13.2 — Llandough Farm opposite the hospital\nI understand and accept the distinction in the catalogue description “Waterwheel from Llandough Farm (opposite hospital)”. This refers to the separate Llandough Farm in the Llandough near Penarth/Cardiff locality, not to Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr. Not seeking to attribute the waterwheel to Great House Farm. Treat Ref 13.2 as a separate Llandough Farm record unless a primary Museum record expressly establishes otherwise.\n\nMS 3360/3 — Great House / Llandough Estate\nAccepts the stated page content: Great House formed part of the Llandough Estate sale particulars of 1924; occupied by W.G. Thomas & Sons; approximately 202 acres; Parish of Llanfair; homestead in the Village of Llandough.\nAsks one narrow point from the record already examined: whether the MS 3360/3 page or plan itself contains any further recorded identifier (place-name form, neighbouring farms, estate owner, parish/benefice wording, acreage breakdown, plan number, tenant information, road, church or other geographical feature). Not a request for a new identification or an opinion.\nStates two farmsteads in the Llandough near Penarth/Cardiff locality must not be treated as alternative names: (1) Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr beside St Dochdwy's Church; (2) Llandough Farm described as opposite hospital. Accession 63.24 wording “Llandough Church Farm” (1858) left unresolved unless the underlying record expressly identifies it.\n\nFiltered schedule\nAccepts that the Museum does not hold a separate recorded audit schedule of excluded search results, and does not ask the Museum to create one. Distinguishes positively identified Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr; Llandough Farm opposite the hospital; other Llandoughs including Llandough-juxta-Cowbridge; and unresolved source-specific records.\n\nCofion,\nSion Buckler\n"},{"id":"wb-d451b237","subject":"Museum wales FOI 2026 036","name":"2026-10-08 - Museum Wales FOI-2026-036 - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","date":"2026-10-08","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"FOI / EIR correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-10-08%20-%20Museum%20Wales%20FOI-2026-036%20-%20EN%20-%20V0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"museum-wales-foi-2026-036","scopeTopics":["email","ghf","foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email","ghf","foi"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-10-08 - Museum Wales FOI-2026-036 - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"2026-10-08 Museum Wales FOI 2026-036 not-held close\nFrom: FOI Requests <FOI.Requests@museumwales.ac.uk>\nTo: Requester <[redacted]>\nCc: FOI Requests <FOI.Requests@museumwales.ac.uk>\nDate: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 09:22:51 +0000\nSubject: RE: FOI 2026-036 – Great House Farm, Llandough – filtered schedule still not supplied; unanswered points from FOI 2026-032 correction\nAttachments: inline signature images only (image002.png). No schedule or page attached.\nIn reply to requester 7 Oct 2026 18:10 BST, already stored as V0.0.0.01.\n\nTranscript:\nDear Mr Buckler\n\nThank you for your email.\n\nThe Museum has reviewed the material held under reference MS 3360/3 and has already provided the relevant page identified during that review.\n\nThere is no catalogue record identifying Great House Farm within this document. Following additional research by Collections staff, one page within the larger document was identified as relating to Great House Farm and was supplied to you.\n\nThe information contained on that page has already been disclosed and summarised in our previous correspondence. No further recorded information relating to Great House Farm has been identified within the material held under reference MS 3360/3.\n\nWe therefore have nothing further to add on this matter.\n\nCofion cynnes,\nFOI Team\nfoi.requests@museumwales.ac.uk\namgueddfa.cymru | museum.wales\nCharity number 525774\n"},{"id":"wb-e23cf9e8","subject":"Museum wales FOI 2026 036","name":"2026-10-10 - Museum Wales FOI-2026-036 - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","date":"2026-10-10","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"FOI / EIR correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-10-10%20-%20Museum%20Wales%20FOI-2026-036%20-%20EN%20-%20V0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"museum-wales-foi-2026-036","scopeTopics":["email","ghf","foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email","ghf","foi"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-10-10 - Museum Wales FOI-2026-036 - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"2026-10-10 Museum Wales FOI 2026-036 requester follow-up\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]>\nTo: FOI.Requests@museumwales.ac.uk\nDate: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 19:14:29 +0000\nSubject: RE: FOI 2026-036 – Great House Farm, Llandough – filtered schedule still not supplied; unanswered points from FOI 2026-032 correction\nAttachments: none\n\nTranscript:\nDear FOI Team,\n\nFOI 2026-036 — your letter of 7 October 2026\n\nThank you. I note three points from that letter.\n\n1. You do not hold a separate recorded document listing, item by item, which search results were excluded. Please state which systems were searched for that schedule (collections database, search-export, review log, email, paper file) and whether a physical file was retrieved, and by whom and on what date. A not-held answer is incomplete without that (FOIA s.16).\n\n2. MS 3360/3: the page already supplied records Great House as part of the 1924 Llandough Estate sale particulars, occupied by W.G. Thomas & Sons, about 202 acres, Parish of Llanfair, homestead in the Village of Llandough. Please confirm whether the catalogue or the page itself names which Llandough (Llandough-juxta-Penarth or Llandough-juxta-Cowbridge). If that is not on the page or in the catalogue entry, say so.\n\n3. Ref 13.2 is recorded as a waterwheel from Llandough Farm (opposite hospital). I treat that as the Penarth hospital site, not the farmhouse demolished on 6 December 1988, unless a further recorded field says otherwise.\n\nI am not asking for a new search or for a document you say you do not hold. I am asking for the systems searched and the physical-file check on the not-held point, and the parish identification if it is already on the page or catalogue entry.\n\nCofion,\n\nSion Buckler (Ret'd Sergeant)\nRoyal Signals Cyber/Electronic Warfare Professional and Life Member\n"},{"id":"wb-ce22924f","subject":"Llandough community council internal review request","name":"2026-10-12 - Llandough Community Council internal-review-request - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","date":"2026-10-12","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Council and local authority correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-10-12%20-%20Llandough%20Community%20Council%20internal-review-request%20-%20EN%20-%20V0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"llandough-community-council-internal-review-request","scopeTopics":["email","ghf","foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email","ghf","foi"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-10-12 - Llandough Community Council internal-review-request - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Mr Egan,\n\nThank you for your email of 11 October 2026. It contains several separate matters, so I have answered each under its own number. Please respond to each separately. Nothing below withdraws or narrows my original request of 21 September, and attendance at a meeting is not a condition of my information rights.\n\nI am not asking the Council to decide the family's title dispute or to make findings about any individual, and I am not alleging wrongdoing by you. I ask that records be identified, searched for and disclosed, and that the matters in section 4 be considered.\n\n1. INTERNAL REVIEW OF THE 11 OCTOBER REFUSAL (FOIA s.12; EIR reg. 11)\n\nPlease treat this as a formal request for the internal review you offered, of the section 12 refusal of my request of 21 September. To the extent any information is environmental, please also treat it as representations under regulation 11 of the EIR 2004.\n\nMy request had three categories: (a) records created after the 2002 deposit concerning Great House Farm / Tý Mawr, Church View Close, the village green (including VG 41) or the roadside verge by the churchyard wall; (b) photographs, postcards or images of the farm, barn, green or church surroundings, of any date; (c) records showing what, if anything, transferred from Penarth Town Council at the 1982 reorganisation, and where pre-1982 records were deposited. I ask the reviewer to address:\n\n(i) Scope. The estimate covers only the reading of 224 sets of minutes for 2002-2016. Please explain why that is necessary and sufficient for each category, particularly (b) and (c), and what other searches were considered: correspondence and email files, photograph collections, transfer or deposit records, any index or electronic copy of the minutes, and sampling.\n\n(ii) Arithmetic. The letter gives 20 minutes to read plus 10 minutes to assess each set, which is 30 minutes x 224 = 6,720 minutes (112 hours), but states 2,240 minutes (37 hours), which is 10 minutes x 224. Please confirm which calculation was used and which activities under regulation 4 of the Fees Regulations it includes. I accept the limit is exceeded on either figure.\n\n(iii) Section 12(2). Please say whether the cost of determining whether the information is held would itself exceed the limit, or whether the estimate concerns locating and extracting information once its existence is established.\n\n(iv) EIR. The green, verge, trees, woodland and land boundaries are likely to be environmental information. Please say which parts were assessed under the EIR. The FOIA cost limit is not an EIR refusal ground; if regulation 12(4)(b) is relied on, please give the reasons, the public interest assessment and the presumption in favour of disclosure.\n\n(v) Advice and assistance (s.16 FOIA; reg. 9 EIR). Limiting the minutes to 2002-2006 does not address (b) or (c). Please say whether any part can be answered within the limit and suggest a practical refinement.\n\n(vi) Without prejudice to the review: if the refusal is maintained, please treat parts (b) and (c) alone as a refined request, assessed under the correct regime, so that no time is lost.\n\nPlease have the review carried out by someone who took no part in the original decision, with the outcome and reasons in writing. I would be grateful for the outcome within 20 working days.\n\n2. NEW REQUEST FOR RECORDED INFORMATION (FOIA and, where environmental, EIR)\n\nThis is a separate request, not a refinement of the one under review. Please search for Council-held or controlled minutes, agendas, reports, correspondence, complaints, notes or referrals:\n\n(i) from approximately 1986 to 1990 concerning Great House Farm / Tý Mawr, its occupation, title or tenure, development or demolition, including any concerns raised by my late father, William \"Billy\" Buckler, or other family members. I have family information that my father raised concerns at Council meetings around 1988, described within the family as fraud-related. I cannot verify whether he did, what was said or what followed, and I do not present it as established fact. I ask whether the Council's records can establish what, if anything, was reported, how it was handled, and whether it was referred to any other body;\n\n(ii) of any date concerning the village green, roadside verge, trees and adjoining woodland, and any reported extension of Church View Close gardens beyond their boundaries into the woodland, including correspondence with the charity that holds the woodland, residents or other authorities;\n\n(iii) concerning the 2002 deposit: please confirm whether the Council retains or controls any copies of the minutes deposited with Glamorgan Archives, and identify any record of that transfer, its date, and any archival reference or destination known to the Council. I am separately pursuing the deposited volumes with Glamorgan Archives.\n\nI assert a proprietary interest in Parcel A and the adjoining woodland, but I do not ask the Council to adjudicate title. If nothing is held after appropriate searches, please say so. If anything is withheld, please identify the statutory basis and reasons.\n\n3. SUBJECT ACCESS REQUEST (Article 15 UK GDPR; Data Protection Act 2018)\n\nPlease provide a copy of my personal data held by the Council, including data in minutes, correspondence, reports, photographs or other records (including retained historical records) concerning my presence at Great House Farm as a child, the events surrounding the family's removal and the 1988 demolition, and any later record that identifies or refers to me. An electronic copy, ordinarily without charge, is fine. If you reasonably need identity evidence, please tell me promptly what is required. I understand the one-month period under Article 12(3) runs from receipt of this request, or of any identity evidence reasonably requested. This section concerns only my personal data; my father's reported statements and the wider records are dealt with in section 2.\n\n4. THE OCTOBER MEETING\n\nYour email says my \"other matters\" will be considered at the October meeting. Please confirm:\n- the purpose of that statement, and whether I am invited to attend;\n- the date, time and venue, public attendance arrangements and any remote option;\n- the agenda, item numbers and papers, as soon as available;\n- whether my correspondence will be circulated to councillors, how decisions and any actions will be minuted, and how I can obtain the minutes or formal outcome;\n- how I may make representations as a member of the public, including any procedure, deadline and speaking limit.\nI have not yet decided whether to attend.\n\nI ask that the following be placed on the agenda, as issues for clarification and not as findings:\n\n(a) From my 21 September email: whether the Council will pass my contact details to any former member, former clerk or long-standing resident who may wish to contact me (entirely at their choice); table and minute my correspondence in full; and accept my offer of a short talk, without charge, on the documentary record.\n\n(b) The green south of the former farmhouse: what the Council understands its present role to be (owner, manager, custodian or otherwise), and the recorded instrument, plan or Council decision on which that understanding rests. The family's research raises a question whether this land was distinct from the agricultural parcel addressed in the 1955 possession proceedings. I ask the Council to check its own records, not to decide that question.\n\n(c) The woodland behind the houses: I understand the charity responsible for it has raised concerns that some adjoining gardens extend beyond their boundaries. I cannot verify that. Please establish the charity's precise account, whether the Council holds relevant plans or correspondence, and refer any substantiated concern to the appropriate landowner or authority. I do not ask for findings about individual residents without evidence.\n\n(d) The 1989 planning record: the copy available to me refers to annexes containing representations from historic societies concerning Great House Farm, which appear to be absent. Please consider whether the Council holds copies and whether it will ask the planning authority or relevant archive to locate the complete papers. This matters to understanding what was put before the decision-makers following the demolition.\n\n(e) The historic site today: a site visit and, in due course, the merits of an interpretation board or plaque addressing the farm, the archaeological discoveries (the excavation beneath Church View Close recorded 1,026 burials, above a Roman villa and an Iron Age roundhouse) and the history of the green.\n\nThe same distinction applies to my father's reported concerns and to my own claim: they are matters requiring clarification, not established findings. If any proposed action is outside the Community Council's powers, please identify the appropriate body.\n\n5. CHARGES AND THE CLERK'S POSITION\n\nI do not authorise paid searches or accept any charge, and ask that no cost chargeable to me be incurred without my prior written agreement. If the Council offers to comply for a fee (FOIA s.13) or proposes a charge for environmental information, please send an itemised quotation for consideration only (not acceptance): the legal basis, the work proposed, hours and hourly rate, the total, whether the work can be staged (for example, parts 1(b) and 1(c) first), any published EIR charging schedule, payment methods, payee, payment reference and deadline.\n\nYour 11 October email is signed as Clerk, and I understood from public information that you retired from that post in June 2026. Please confirm: (a) whether you are currently Clerk, or acting or locum, and since when; (b) who is the Council's Proper Officer and who has custody of its records; (c) whether the 11 October decision was yours personally or the Council's; (d) whether any handover or inventory of records was made on a change of clerk; and (e) who will conduct the internal review. I ask only to establish who is accountable for the review and the records.\n\nPlease reply by email and by post to [redacted].\n\nThank you again for your time and courtesy.\n\nRegards,\n\nSion Buckler (Ret'd Sergeant)\n\nRoyal Signals Cyber / Electronic Warfare Professional and Life Member\n\n[redacted]"},{"id":"wb-30795830","subject":"Llandough community council internal review request","name":"2026-10-12 - Llandough Community Council internal-review-request - EN - V0.0.0.01.txt","date":"2026-10-12","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Council and local authority correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-10-12%20-%20Llandough%20Community%20Council%20internal-review-request%20-%20EN%20-%20V0.0.0.01.txt","threadSlug":"llandough-community-council-internal-review-request","scopeTopics":["email","ghf","foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email","ghf","foi"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-10-12 - Llandough Community Council internal-review-request - EN - V0.0.0.01.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Mr Egan,\n\nThank you for your email of 11 October 2026. I write about matters of serious public interest to the family and to Llandough's history, and I ask the Council to treat this correspondence with priority and to give it its full cooperation. The email contains six separate matters. Please respond to each under its own number. Nothing below withdraws, narrows or replaces my original request of 21 September, and attendance at a meeting is not a condition of my information rights.\n\nI am not asking the Council to adjudicate the family's title dispute or to make findings about any individual, and I am not alleging wrongdoing by you. I am asking the Council to account accurately for its own records, knowledge, decisions and referrals, and to distinguish clearly between what its primary records establish, what is merely recorded as said, and what remains uncertain. If a search shows that nothing was recorded or referred, I ask that the Council say so plainly, because that is itself important.\n\n1. PRESERVATION OF RECORDS\n\nBecause these requests concern historic records that may be time-sensitive for other proceedings and bodies, please:\n(a) confirm in writing, within 5 working days, that all potentially relevant records have been preserved and that any scheduled disposal, destruction or retention-schedule review affecting them is suspended. This covers paper and electronic minutes, agendas, correspondence, emails, clerk's notes, plans, photographs, files and archive-transfer or deposit paperwork, and any such material held on the Council's behalf by a current or former councillor, clerk or officer, including at home;\n(b) ask each current councillor, and, where practicable, the Council's former clerks and members, whether they hold any such material, and include whatever is found in the searches below.\nI raise this as a precaution. For completeness, section 77 FOIA and regulation 19 of the EIR make it an offence to alter, conceal or destroy records with intent to prevent their disclosure once a request has been made.\n\n2. INTERNAL REVIEW OF THE 11 OCTOBER REFUSAL (FOIA s.12; EIR reg. 11)\n\nPlease treat this as a formal request for the internal review you offered, of the section 12 refusal of my request of 21 September. To the extent any information is environmental, please also treat it as representations under regulation 11 of the EIR 2004.\n\nMy request had three categories: (a) records created after the 2002 deposit concerning Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr, Church View Close, the village green (including VG 41) or the roadside verge by the churchyard wall; (b) photographs, postcards or images of the farm, barn, green or church surroundings, of any date; (c) records showing what, if anything, transferred from Penarth Town Council at the 1982 reorganisation, and where pre-1982 records were deposited. I ask the reviewer to address:\n\n(i) Scope. The estimate covers only the reading of 224 sets of minutes for 2002-2016. Please explain why that is necessary and sufficient for each category, particularly (b) and (c), and what other searches were considered: correspondence and email files, photograph collections, transfer or deposit records, any index, catalogue, minute heading or electronic copy of the minutes, targeted searches on the place names, former names and dates in my request, and sampling.\n\n(ii) Arithmetic. The letter gives 20 minutes to read plus 10 minutes to assess each set, which is 30 minutes x 224 = 6,720 minutes (112 hours), but states 2,240 minutes (37 hours), which is 10 minutes x 224. The figures do not reconcile. Please confirm which calculation was used and which activities under regulation 4 of the Fees Regulations it includes. I accept the limit is exceeded on either figure, so I do not rely on this point alone.\n\n(iii) Section 12(2). Please say whether the cost of determining whether the information is held would itself exceed the limit, or whether the estimate concerns locating and extracting information once its existence is established. Section 1(1)(a) duty to confirm or deny is preserved unless that cost alone exceeds the limit.\n\n(iv) EIR. The green, verge, trees, woodland and land boundaries are likely to be environmental information. Please say which parts were assessed under the EIR. The FOIA cost limit is not an EIR refusal ground; if regulation 12(4)(b) is relied on, please state it expressly with reasons and carry out the public interest assessment, including the presumption in favour of disclosure.\n\n(v) Advice and assistance (s.16 FOIA; reg. 9 EIR). Limiting the minutes to 2002-2006 does not address categories (b) or (c). Please say whether any part can be answered within the limit and advise a practical refinement.\n\n(vi) Without prejudice to the review: if the refusal is maintained, please treat categories (b) and (c) alone as a refined request, assessed under the correct regime, so that no time is lost.\n\nPlease acknowledge this request, identify who will conduct the review (someone who took no part in the original decision), and give the anticipated date. I would be grateful for the outcome and reasons in writing within 20 working days.\n\n3. NEW REQUEST FOR RECORDED INFORMATION (FOIA and, where environmental, EIR)\n\nThis is a separate request, not a refinement of the one under review. Please search for Council-held or controlled minutes, agendas, reports, correspondence, complaints, notes, representations or referrals:\n\n(i) from 1 January 1986 to 31 December 1990 concerning Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr, its occupation, title or tenure, development, removal of the occupants or demolition, including communications with the Vale of Glamorgan Council, planning authorities, developers, solicitors, landowners, police, heritage bodies or other public authorities. This includes any concerns raised by my late father, William \"Billy\" Buckler, or other family members. I have family information that my father raised concerns at Council meetings around 1988, described within the family as fraud-related. I cannot verify whether he did, what was said or what followed, and I do not present it as established fact. I ask whether the Council's records establish what, if anything, was reported, how it was handled, whether it was referred to any other body, and what response followed. References to fraud identify a subject for the search; they are not a claim that a record of fraud exists;\n\n(ii) planning records, plans, annexes, correspondence and supporting documents relating to application 89/01396/OUT (as I understand the reference) and any related consideration of the redevelopment. The copy available to me refers to annexes containing representations from historic societies concerning Great House Farm, which appear to be absent. Please confirm whether the Council holds them and, if not, whether its records identify another custodian or archive;\n\n(iii) of any date concerning the village green, roadside verge, trees and adjoining woodland: history, status, boundaries, maintenance, planting, public use, and any reported extension of Church View Close gardens beyond their boundaries into the woodland, including correspondence with the charity that holds the woodland, residents, the Vale of Glamorgan Council or other authorities. I am aware the official VG 41 registration file is understood to be held by the Vale of Glamorgan Council; I ask only for the Council's own copies, correspondence, representations and referrals;\n\n(iv) concerning the 2002 deposit: please confirm whether the Council retains or controls any copies of the minutes deposited with Glamorgan Archives, and identify any record of that transfer, its date, and any archival reference or destination known to the Council. I am separately pursuing the deposited volumes with Glamorgan Archives.\n\nI assert a proprietary interest in Parcel A and the adjoining woodland, but I do not ask the Council to adjudicate title. If any part is thought too broad, please identify that part and say what narrower search is practicable, rather than refusing the whole. If nothing is held after appropriate searches, please say so and identify any known transfer or deposit location. If anything is withheld, please identify the statutory basis and reasons. I understand the 20-working-day period under section 10 FOIA and regulation 5 EIR runs from receipt of this email.\n\n4. SUBJECT ACCESS REQUEST (Article 15 UK GDPR; Data Protection Act 2018)\n\nPlease provide a copy of my personal data held by the Council, including data in minutes, correspondence, reports, photographs or other records (including retained historical records) concerning my presence at Great House Farm as a child, the events surrounding the family's removal and the 1988 demolition, and any later record that identifies or refers to me. An electronic copy, ordinarily without charge, is fine. Please acknowledge this as a subject access request. If you reasonably need identity evidence, please tell me promptly what is required. I understand the one-month period under Article 12(3) runs from receipt of this request, or of any identity evidence reasonably requested. This section concerns only my personal data; my father's reported statements and the wider records are dealt with in section 3.\n\n5. THE OCTOBER MEETING\n\nYour email says my \"other matters\" will be considered at the October meeting. You gave no date, time, venue or agenda. Please confirm within 5 working days:\n- whether the meeting has already taken place and, if so, its date, the agenda and papers, the minute or other record of consideration, and any decision or action;\n- if it has not, the date, time, venue, public attendance arrangements and any remote option, and the draft or published agenda showing which of my matters are scheduled and under which item numbers;\n- whether I am invited to attend (I have not yet decided whether to) and how a member of the public may make representations on relevant business, including any procedure, deadline and speaking limit;\n- that this entire email, and my earlier correspondence, will be circulated to all councillors, how decisions and actions will be minuted, and how I can obtain the minutes or formal outcome.\n\nI ask that the following be placed on the agenda, as issues for clarification and not as findings:\n\n(a) From my 21 September email: whether the Council will pass my contact details to any former member, former clerk or long-standing resident who may wish to contact me (entirely at their choice, and with no disclosure of their details to me without consent); table and minute my correspondence in full; and accept my offer of a short talk, without charge, on the documentary record.\n\n(b) The green south of the former farmhouse: what the Council understands its present role to be (owner, manager, custodian or otherwise), and the recorded instrument, plan or Council decision on which that understanding rests. The family's research raises a question whether this land was distinct from the agricultural parcel addressed in the 1955 possession proceedings. I ask the Council to check its own records, not to decide that question.\n\n(c) The woodland behind the houses: I understand the charity responsible for it has raised concerns that some adjoining gardens extend beyond their boundaries. I cannot verify that. Please establish the charity's precise account, whether the Council holds relevant plans or correspondence, and refer any substantiated concern to the appropriate landowner or authority. I do not ask for findings about individual residents without evidence.\n\n(d) The 1989 planning record and the missing annexes (see 3(ii)): whether the Council holds copies and whether it will ask the planning authority or relevant archive to locate the complete papers. This matters to understanding what was put before the decision-makers following the demolition.\n\n(e) My father's reported 1980s concerns (see 3(i)): that the Council consider what its own records show and, if they show nothing, say so.\n\n(f) The historic site today: a site visit and, in due course, the merits of an interpretation board or plaque addressing the farm, the archaeological discoveries (the excavation beneath Church View Close recorded 1,026 burials, above a Roman villa and an Iron Age roundhouse) and the history of the green.\n\nIf any proposed action is outside the Community Council's powers, please identify the appropriate body.\n\n6. CHARGES AND THE CLERK'S POSITION\n\nI do not authorise paid searches or accept any charge, and ask that no cost chargeable to me be incurred without my prior written agreement. If the Council offers to comply for a fee (FOIA s.13) or proposes a charge for environmental information, please send an itemised quotation for consideration only (not acceptance): the legal basis, the work proposed, hours and hourly rate, the total, whether the work can be staged (for example, categories 2(b) and 2(c) first), any published EIR charging schedule, payment methods, payee, payment reference and deadline.\n\nYour 11 October email is signed as Clerk, and I understood from public information that you retired from that post in June 2026. I treat that as unverified. Please confirm: (a) whether you are currently Clerk, or acting or locum, and since when; (b) who is the Council's Proper Officer and who has custody of its records; (c) whether the 11 October decision was yours personally or the Council's; (d) whether any handover or inventory of records was made on a change of clerk; and (e) who will conduct the internal review.\n\nI would prefer to resolve all of this cooperatively and with the Council's full help. If the matters above are not dealt with properly, I will have to consider a complaint to the Information Commissioner (including on advice and assistance, the duty to confirm or deny and the EIR handling) and to the Public Services Ombudsman for Wales. I would rather not need to. Please acknowledge receipt of this email and each numbered part within 5 working days, and reply by email and by post to [redacted].\n\nThank you for your time and courtesy.\n\nRegards,\nSion Buckler (Ret'd Sergeant)\nRoyal Signals Cyber / Electronic Warfare Professional and Life Member\n[redacted]"},{"id":"wb-497522e1","subject":"Museum wales FOI 2026 024 036","name":"2026-10-12 - Museum Wales FOI 2026-024 036 - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","date":"2026-10-12","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"FOI / EIR correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-10-12%20-%20Museum%20Wales%20FOI%202026-024%20036%20-%20EN%20-%20V0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"museum-wales-foi-2026-024-036","scopeTopics":["email","ghf","foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email","ghf","foi"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-10-12 - Museum Wales FOI 2026-024 036 - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: FOI Requests <FOI.Requests@museumwales.ac.uk>\nTo: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 14:56:43 +0000\nSubject: RE: ALLANOL/EXTERNAL - FOI 2026-024 / 032 / 036 – outstanding item 7: recorded basis for treating already-found records as “unrelated / not in scope”\n\nMr Buckler\n\nThank you for your email.\n\nIn relation to your request for the recorded basis on which records were treated as outside the scope of FOI 2026-024, Amgueddfa Cymru does not hold a separate search log, file note, internal note or other recorded document setting out the rationale for that assessment.\n\nThe original response was based on searches which identified no records relating to the reported discovery described in your request, namely the alleged discovery of a soldier, horse remains and associated armour at Great House Farm, Llandough. While searches identified records relating to other archaeological finds from Church Farm and archaeological investigations at Great House Farm, staff concluded that those records did not relate to the specific discovery described in your request and therefore fell outside its scope. No separate record of that assessment was created. We have since provided you with the records referred to as being from Church Farm.\n\nNo records remain withheld.\n\nCofion cynnes,\n\nFOI Team\n\nfoi.requests @museumwales.ac.uk\namgueddfa.cymru | museum.wales\nCharity number 525774\n"},{"id":"wb-8746883f","subject":"Rcahmw rc26 0375","name":"2026-10-13 - RCAHMW RC26-0375 - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","date":"2026-10-13","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Heritage and archives correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-10-13%20-%20RCAHMW%20RC26-0375%20-%20EN%20-%20V0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"rcahmw-rc26-0375","scopeTopics":["email","ghf","foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email","ghf","foi"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-10-13 - RCAHMW RC26-0375 - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"2026-10-13 RCAHMW RC26-0375 further reply from Adam Coward, NMR Enquiries.\n\nDear Requester,\n\nWith regard to the three remaining points noted below:\n\n1) If you would like to order scans of these items, please complete and return the attached order form. While I cannot confirm a quote for this order before receiving a completed order form, an order of these three items would likely require four research-quality scans. As per the attached price list, we waive fees for orders requiring fewer than five research-quality scans for personal, non-commercial use.\n\n2) As has been noted, our searches have not been limited to the registered files. No further information related to this is held beyond what is noted in the archive catalogue.\n\n3) As noted in my emails of 29 September and 6 October, the item Archive Number 6364275 from the Emergency Recording Collection has been physically examined and does not relate to Great House Farm, Llandough. As noted (correctly) in the archive catalogue, it relates to Great House, Bonvilston.\n\nAll the best,\n\nAdam Coward\n\n(Source: Gmail message_id 1a135bdc3ab000b2, thread 1a135bdc3ab000b2, 13 Oct 2026 14:49 UTC. Order form and price list referenced as attached but not present in retrieved message metadata beyond signature images.)\n\nWorking theory preserved: two parcels; Mary Williams owned Parcel A (house/garden); synthetic title by false permissive occupancy.\n"},{"id":"wb-a85411b6","subject":"Dwr cymru welsh water EIR 3106 2026","name":"2026-10-14 - Dwr Cymru Welsh Water EIR-3106-2026 - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","date":"2026-10-14","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"FOI / EIR correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-10-14%20-%20Dwr%20Cymru%20Welsh%20Water%20EIR-3106-2026%20-%20EN%20-%20V0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"dwr-cymru-welsh-water-eir-3106-2026","scopeTopics":["email","ghf","foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email","ghf","foi"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-10-14 - Dwr Cymru Welsh Water EIR-3106-2026 - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"2026-10-14 EIR/3106/2026 Acknowledgement from Environmental Information Requests <EnvironmentalInformationRequests@dwrcymru.com>\n\nDear Requester\n\nRequest for information\nWe refer to your request for information, dated 12th October 2026. We are dealing with your request as one made under the Environmental Information Regulations 2004 (“the Regulations”).\n\nIn accordance with the Regulations, we will respond to your request within 20 working days of the date of receipt. Please note: Under Regulation 7 of the EIR, if a request is exceptionally complex or voluminous, this period may be extended to 40 working days. We will notify you if an extension is required.\n\nFor completeness, we advise that the Information Commissioner’s Office states that the time period for responding should be calculated from the day after the request is received.\n\nIn the meantime, if you have any queries, please contact us on email EnvironmentalInformationRequests@dwrcymru.com\n\nWe have assigned reference EIR/3106/2026 to your request. Please kindly note this in all correspondence with us regarding this matter.\n\nYours faithfully\n\nCeryn\nDŵr Cymru Welsh Water\n\n[Request summary: rDNA / plasmid DNA / engineered nucleic-acid constructs in water supply, catchment risk assessments under Reg 27/28, methodology. Dated 12 Oct 2026.]"},{"id":"wb-51603942","subject":"Heneb ggat02038s digitisation","name":"2026-10-14 - Heneb GGAT02038s digitisation - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","date":"2026-10-14","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Heritage and archives correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-10-14%20-%20Heneb%20GGAT02038s%20digitisation%20-%20EN%20-%20V0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"heneb-ggat02038s-digitisation","scopeTopics":["email","ghf","foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email","ghf","foi"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-10-14 - Heneb GGAT02038s digitisation - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: HER <her@heneb.org.uk>\nTo: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 15:19:37 +0000\nSubject: RE: Great House Farm, Llandough, Cardiff\n\nDear sir,\n\nThe information held in the HER source file has now been digitised and uploaded to Archwilio and is accessible via record 02038s as PDF files shown in the Sources section of the record. This completes the provision of the source material previously held only in the physical file.\n\n[Full message content to be confirmed against Gmail thread 1a07efa0b6b10900 message 1a13aff7c0c5d8b4]\n\nPhysical check of original source file: not stated. Viewing not requested.\n"},{"id":"wb-b6644f98","subject":"National archives cas 359317 q8q4r9","name":"2026-10-14 - National Archives CAS-359317-Q8Q4R9 - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","date":"2026-10-14","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Heritage and archives correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-10-14%20-%20National%20Archives%20CAS-359317-Q8Q4R9%20-%20EN%20-%20V0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"national-archives-cas-359317-q8q4r9","scopeTopics":["email","ghf","foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email","ghf","foi"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-10-14 - National Archives CAS-359317-Q8Q4R9 - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"2026-10-14 Response to National Archives enquiry. Case number CAS-359317-Q8Q4R9 CRM:022001165 from RESO Records Enquiries <RESORecordsEnquiries@nationalarchives.gov.uk>\n\nDear Requester,\n\nThank you for contacting The National Archives of the United Kingdom.\n\nWe may hold records related to your enquiry. The map you mention is listed in our catalogue.\n\nI have searched our catalogue for records using Glamorgan XLVII as search terms. You can view the results of the search here and try some similar searches yourself.\n\nSearch results: (Glamorgan AND XLVII) AND 6 | The National Archives\nhttps://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/results/r?_aq=Glamorgan%20XLVII&_or1=6&_d=IR&_dss=range&_ro=any&_hb=tna&_st=adv\n\nPenarth field books ….\nSearch results: penarth | The National Archives\nhttps://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/results/r?_srt=1&_aq=penarth&_cr=ir58&_d=IR&_dss=range&_ro=any&_hb=tna&_st=adv\n\nFurther advice about using Valuation Office records can be found here….\nLand use, value and ownership: Valuation Office Survey 1910-1915 - The National Archives\nhttps://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/help-with-your-research/research-guides/valuation-office-survey-land-value-ownership-1910-1915/\n\nRecords are represented in our online catalogue by a title, a reference and a brief description. When you search our catalogue using keywords you are searching across these titles and descriptions, rather than the contents of the records themselves.\n\nBy clicking on any search result, you can see whether the record is available online or if you need to visit us to view the original. You can also request copies of records from their catalogue description page.\n\nIf the record is held by another archive, you will need to contact them directly to find out how you can access it.\n\n[General visit and paid search advice]\n\nYours sincerely,\nMichael McGrady\nRemote Enquiries Duty Officer\n\n[Original request was location-only FOIA for series/piece/item, accession/box/shelf, Discovery id, physical check yes/no/who/date/store, open/closed status, systems searched and whether physical item retrieved, for Valuation Office field book/plan/index covering OS Glamorgan XLVII.6 for Llandough-juxta-Penarth. No viewing requested.]"},{"id":"wb-bfaeb5d6","subject":"Welsh government atisn 26 0010","name":"2026-10-14 - Welsh Government ATISN 26-0010 - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","date":"2026-10-14","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Heritage and archives correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-10-14%20-%20Welsh%20Government%20ATISN%2026-0010%20-%20EN%20-%20V0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"welsh-government-atisn-26-0010","scopeTopics":["email","ghf","foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email","ghf","foi"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-10-14 - Welsh Government ATISN 26-0010 - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: OEG.FMCO.FOI@gov.wales\nTo: [redacted]\nCc: OEG.FMCO.FOI@gov.wales\nDate: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 09:59:39 +0000\nSubject: Request for information ATISN 26-0010 - Acknowledgement (Retention & disposal schedules)\n\nDear Requester\n\nFurther to your recent request for information, please find an acknowledgement letter attached.\n\nKind regards\nNatalie\n\nNatalie Chant\nTîm Rheoli Busnes / Business Management Unit\nY Grŵp Effeithiolrwydd Gweithredol / Operational Effectiveness Group\nLlywodraeth Cymru / Welsh Government\nFfôn / Tel: 03000 625022\nE-bost: Natalie.Chant@gov.cymru / E-mail: Natalie.Chant@gov.wales\n\n[Attachment: ATISN 26-0010 - Acknowledgement.odt]\n\n--- Attachment content ---\nSion Buckler\n[redacted]\nOur ref: ATISN 26-0010\nDate: 14 October 2026\n\nDear Requester\n\nATISN 26-0010\n\nThank you for your request which I received on 10 October. You asked for:\n\nThe retention and destruction schedules (or equivalent policy documents) that applied, or still apply, to former Welsh Office / Welsh Government file series covering agricultural tenancies, land ownership or compulsory purchase, rural depopulation, or related policy in the period 1970–1997.\n\nAny recorded information identifying whether files in those series that would have related to Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr / Llandough-juxta-Penarth, or to the companies Western Ground Rents Ltd or BP Properties Ltd, were destroyed, transferred or retained, and if destroyed the date and authority.\n\nConfirmation of the repositories to which any surviving pre-1997 paper files from the relevant series were transferred (e.g. National Assembly / Welsh Government / The National Archives / other).\n\nYou will receive a response by 6 November. If you have any queries about this request, please do not hesitate to contact me at the address below. Please remember to quote the reference number above in any future correspondence.\n\n[Standard privacy and rights notice follows]\n\nYours sincerely\nJamie Jenkins\n"},{"id":"wb-869e36a1","subject":"National archives cas 359317 q8q4r9","name":"2026-10-15 - National Archives CAS-359317-Q8Q4R9 - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","date":"2026-10-15","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Heritage and archives correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-10-15%20-%20National%20Archives%20CAS-359317-Q8Q4R9%20-%20EN%20-%20V0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"national-archives-cas-359317-q8q4r9","scopeTopics":["email","ghf","foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email","ghf","foi"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-10-15 - National Archives CAS-359317-Q8Q4R9 - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"2026-10-15 Re: Response to National Archives enquiry. Case number CAS-359317-Q8Q4R9 - incomplete location response under FOIA from Requester <[redacted]> to enquiry@nationalarchives.gov.uk\n\nFreedom of Information Team,\n\nThank you for the reply of 14 October 2026 under case CAS-359317-Q8Q4R9.\n\nThe reply provides Discovery catalogue search links for Glamorgan XLVII and Penarth field books. It does not answer the location request.\n\nPlease treat this as a follow-up under the Freedom of Information Act 2000 and the s.45 code. The original request was a location request only. I am still not asking for a copy, a screen-room appointment, or production until location and physical-check status are confirmed.\n\nPlease state, for the Valuation Office field book, plan and index covering Ordnance Survey sheet Glamorgan XLVII.6 for the parish of Llandough (Llandough-juxta-Penarth, Glamorgan):\n\n1. The series, piece and item references (IR 58, IR 121, IR 124, IR 125, IR 126, IR 127, IR 128 or successor).\n2. The accession, box, shelf or other store location used by staff.\n3. The Discovery catalogue identifier.\n4. Whether a member of staff has physically checked that the item is in that location: yes, no, or not answered. If yes, who, date, and which store.\n5. Whether the item is open, closed or retained by another department, and if closed the series and FOI route.\n6. The systems searched, and whether a physical file or plan was retrieved rather than a catalogue-only search.\n\nA catalogue-only answer is incomplete unless the systems and physical-check status are stated. Please quote the statutory deadline for this follow-up.\n\nSion Buckler (Ret'd Sergeant)\nRoyal Signals Cyber/Electronic Warfare Professional and Life Member"},{"id":"wb-3d1dd9c8","subject":"National archives FOI hearth tax e179 Llandough juxta penarth","name":"2026-10-15 - National Archives FOI hearth tax E179 Llandough-juxta-Penarth - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","date":"2026-10-15","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Heritage and archives correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-10-15%20-%20National%20Archives%20FOI%20hearth%20tax%20E179%20Llandough-juxta-Penarth%20-%20EN%20-%20V0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"national-archives-foi-hearth-tax-e179-llandough-juxta-penarth","scopeTopics":["email","ghf","foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email","ghf","foi"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-10-15 - National Archives FOI hearth tax E179 Llandough-juxta-Penarth - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nTo: FOIRequests@nationalarchives.gov.uk\nDate: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 16:53:19 -0700\nSubject: FOI — location and content only — Glamorgan hearth tax rolls E 179/221/297 and E 179/221/294 — Llandough-juxta-Penarth\nGmail-Message-Id: 1a141fc0a480f714\nStatus: sent\n\nFreedom of Information Team,\n\nThis is a request under the Freedom of Information Act 2000. It is a location and content request only. It is separate from CAS-347296-M0V4Y1 and its review CAS-356384-B2F5L9.\n\nPLEASE NOTE: DUPLICATE PLACE AND FARM NAMES IN GLAMORGAN\nGlamorgan has two parishes called Llandough, and each had a farm called Great House.\nIN SCOPE: Llandough-juxta-Penarth (Llandough near Penarth and Cardiff), hundred of Dinas Powys. Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr stood beside St Dochdwy's church, approx. ST 168 733.\nOUT OF SCOPE: Llandough-juxta-Cowbridge (Llandough near Cowbridge), and the Great Houses at nearby Llandow and Aberthin.\nPlease do not send or rely on records for the Cowbridge-area places. If an entry says only \"Llandough\" or \"Great House\" and the parish cannot be told, please say so rather than assume.\n\nRecords:\n1. E 179/221/297 — Glamorgan hearth tax assessment, c.1663–1667 (possibly 1666), roll of 105 rotulets.\n2. E 179/221/294 — Glamorgan hearth tax assessment, [1671], roll of 45 rotulets.\n\nPlease tell me:\n(a) For each roll, which rotulet or membrane covers the parish of Llandough-juxta-Penarth (Dinas Powys hundred), if this is recorded in any finding aid, schedule or index you hold.\n(b) Whether any finding aid, index or transcript you hold lists the householders named for Llandough-juxta-Penarth in either roll, and if so, those names and their hearth counts.\n(c) Whether either roll is digitised or available on microfilm, and the cost and route for ordering a copy of only that entry.\n\nIf you consider any of this reasonably accessible by other means under section 21, please say exactly where and how, so I can obtain it.\n\nI am not asking to book a reading room visit.\n\nPublic working evidence catalogue: https://greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com\nTitle and chronology index: https://dartboard.bucklerfamily.estate and https://bpvsbuckler.bucklerfamily.estate\nLong-term archive: https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate\n\nThank you,\n\nSion Buckler (Ret'd Sergeant)\nRoyal Signals Cyber/Electronic Warfare Professional and Life Member"},{"id":"wb-c909b319","subject":"National library of wales FOI herbert survey s7 and bute x.6","name":"2026-10-15 - National Library of Wales FOI Herbert survey S7 and Bute X.6 - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","date":"2026-10-15","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"FOI / EIR correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-10-15%20-%20National%20Library%20of%20Wales%20FOI%20Herbert%20survey%20S7%20and%20Bute%20X.6%20-%20EN%20-%20V0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"national-library-of-wales-foi-herbert-survey-s7-and-bute-x-6","scopeTopics":["email","ghf","foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email","ghf","foi"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-10-15 - National Library of Wales FOI Herbert survey S7 and Bute X.6 - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nTo: enquiry@library.wales\nDate: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 16:53:35 -0700\nSubject: New FOI request (outside FOI/2026/09) — 1610 Herbert survey S7 and Cardiff MSS microfilm of Bute X.6(12) — Llandough-juxta-Penarth\nGmail-Message-Id: 1a141fc481dbd2e5\nStatus: sent\n\nFreedom of Information / Enquiries Service,\n\nThis is a new request under the Freedom of Information Act 2000. It is outside the scope of FOI/2026/09 and its internal review, which concerned Bute series including D153 and D205 and the 1877 transaction. It is also separate from the enquiry of 27 September and follow-up of 10 October 2026 on D153/1–8.\n\nPLEASE NOTE: DUPLICATE PLACE AND FARM NAMES IN GLAMORGAN\nGlamorgan has two parishes called Llandough, and each had a farm called Great House.\nIN SCOPE: Llandough-juxta-Penarth (Llandough near Penarth and Cardiff), hundred of Dinas Powys. Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr stood beside St Dochdwy's church, approx. ST 168 733.\nOUT OF SCOPE: Llandough-juxta-Cowbridge (Llandough near Cowbridge), and the Great Houses at nearby Llandow and Aberthin. For example, \"Manors of Llandough and St Mary Church\" (Penrice and Margam Estate Records, 1619–1704) appears to be the Cowbridge Llandough; please confirm if you know otherwise.\nPlease do not send or rely on records for the Cowbridge-area places. If an entry says only \"Llandough\" or \"Great House\" and the parish cannot be told, please say so rather than assume.\n\nPart 1 — S7, Survey of the lands of William Herbert, kt, dec., in the lordships or manors of Dinas Powys, Cogan, Llandough and others, January 1610.\nPlease tell me:\n(a) Whether S7 contains an entry for Great House or any holding in Llandough-juxta-Penarth, and the folio.\n(b) The name of the holder or tenant recorded, the tenure (freehold, copyhold, leasehold) and any rent or acreage given.\n(c) Whether S7 is digitised or filmed, and the cost of a copy of only those folios.\n\nPart 2 — Cardiff MSS on Microfilm (CARDMFS), filmed 1980–1984.\nGlamorgan Archives (ref 2026/4097b) identified Bute X.6(12), abstracts of payments and receipts by the Bute trustees, 1877 (copy), at Cardiff Library.\nPlease tell me:\n(a) Whether Bute X.6(12), or any Bute X.6 item, is on the CARDMFS microfilm, and the reel and frame reference.\n(b) If so, how I can obtain a copy of the entries relating to Llandough-juxta-Penarth or Daniel Thomas, and the cost.\n\nPart 3 — A lease of Great House said to date from 1667.\nThe family's account (not yet supported by a document) is that the Williams family took Great House, Llandough-juxta-Penarth, in 1667 on a lease for 999 years. Great House is described in published sources as a freehold of the Vaughan family from the 16th to the late 18th century, after which it came to the Bute estate. Please tell me whether the Bute Estate Records, including the Herbert and Windsor family papers within them, or any other NLW collection, hold:\n(a) Any lease, counterpart lease, assignment or enrolment, c.1660–1675, of Great House or any messuage in Llandough-juxta-Penarth, to any person named Williams, or for a term of 999 years (or any other long term).\n(b) Any later deed, abstract of title, schedule or rental that recites or refers to a lease of Great House for 999 years or for a long term.\n(c) The conveyance or other instrument by which the Bute estate (or the Windsor family before it) acquired Great House from the Vaughan family or its heirs, with its date and reference, and whether it recites any lease or tenancy then outstanding.\n(d) Any Vaughan of Llandough family papers, and where they are held if not at NLW.\n\nIf you consider any of this reasonably accessible by other means under section 21, please say exactly where and how.\n\nPublic working evidence catalogue: https://greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com\nTitle and chronology index: https://dartboard.bucklerfamily.estate and https://bpvsbuckler.bucklerfamily.estate\nLong-term archive: https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate\n\nThank you,\n\nSion Buckler (Ret'd Sergeant)\nRoyal Signals Cyber/Electronic Warfare Professional and Life Member"},{"id":"wb-a8cd60f1","subject":"Vale of glamorgan FOI 00210772 internal review chase","name":"2026-10-15 - Vale of Glamorgan FOI 00210772 internal review chase - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","date":"2026-10-15","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Council and local authority correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-10-15%20-%20Vale%20of%20Glamorgan%20FOI%2000210772%20internal%20review%20chase%20-%20EN%20-%20V0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"vale-of-glamorgan-foi-00210772-internal-review-chase","scopeTopics":["email","ghf","foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email","ghf","foi"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-10-15 - Vale of Glamorgan FOI 00210772 internal review chase - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nTo: FoiUnit@valeofglamorgan.gov.uk\nDate: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 16:01:00 -0700\nSubject: Chase — Vale of Glamorgan FOI 00210772 internal review — 15 October 2026\nGmail-Message-Id: 1a141cc245e1dbc5\nStatus: sent\n\nDear FOI Unit,\n\nThis is the scheduled chase for internal review of FOI 00210772, requested on 7 July 2026 and still outstanding.\n\nPlease confirm the review outcome or a firm date. I am also concerned that records relevant to Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr, Llandough (1988 eviction, demolition, planning and Building Preservation Notice files) may be subject to routine disposal. Please suspend any disposal of those records pending the outcome of this review.\n\nPublic working evidence catalogue: https://greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com\nTitle and chronology index: https://dartboard.bucklerfamily.estate and https://bpvsbuckler.bucklerfamily.estate\nLong-term archive: https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nSion Buckler (Ret'd Sergeant)\nRoyal Signals Cyber/Electronic Warfare Professional and Life Member"},{"id":"wb-5d72ec43","subject":"Welsh government atisn 26 0009","name":"2026-10-15 - Welsh Government ATISN 26-0009 - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","date":"2026-10-15","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Heritage and archives correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-10-15%20-%20Welsh%20Government%20ATISN%2026-0009%20-%20EN%20-%20V0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"welsh-government-atisn-26-0009","scopeTopics":["email","ghf","foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email","ghf","foi"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-10-15 - Welsh Government ATISN 26-0009 - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Andy.Wigley@gov.wales\nTo: [redacted]\nCc: CADWMinisterials@gov.wales\nDate: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 13:55:30 +0000\nSubject: RE. ATISN 27021 — existing records behind the 1988 listing-file disposal finding (ATISN 26-0009)\n\nDear Mr Buckler,\n\nATISN 26-0009 - Great House Farm, Llandough\n\nThank you for your latest request which I received on 10 October. You asked for:\n\n1. Any existing file inventory, closure note, archive catalogue entry, transfer list, disposal authority, retention schedule, weeding/destruction record, archival receipt, email/minute/note recording the search or basis for the conclusion, or record showing whether the file remained held or was transferred on/after 6 December 1988.\n2. The recorded date and responsible role/unit for any documented disposal or transfer decision.\n3. If neither 1 or 2 exist, distinguish an actual contemporaneous disposal decision/log from the present-day inability to locate the file, identifying the repositories, indices and systems searched for this focused request.\n\nYou will receive a response by 6 November 2026. If you have any queries about this request, please do not hesitate to contact me at the address below. Please remember to quote the reference number above in any future correspondence.\n\n[Standard Welsh Government privacy notice, GDPR rights, Data Protection Officer contact, ICO contact, and disclosure log note follow.]\n\nYours sincerely\n\nAndy Wigley\n\nDr Andy Wigley\nPrif Arolygydd – Pennaeth Arolygiaeth yr Amgylchedd Hanesyddol / Chief Inspector - Head of Historic Environment Inspectorate\nCadw\nDysgu, Cymunedau a Diwylliant / Learning, Communities and Culture\nLlywodraeth Cymru / Welsh Government\nTŷ Ladywell / Ladywell House\nY Drenewydd / Newtown\nPowys\nSY16 1JB\n\nFfôn / Tel: 0300 025 5375\nFfôn symudol / Mobile: [mobile number omitted]\nE-bost/ Email: andy.wigley@llyw.cymru / andy.wigley@gov.wales\n\n[Welsh/English bilingual footer regarding Welsh correspondence and privacy.]\n"},{"id":"wb-2d6615d6","subject":"Museum wales FOI 2026 024","name":"2026-10-16 - Museum Wales FOI 2026-024 - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","date":"2026-10-16","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"FOI / EIR correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-10-16%20-%20Museum%20Wales%20FOI%202026-024%20-%20EN%20-%20V0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"museum-wales-foi-2026-024","scopeTopics":["email","ghf","foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email","ghf","foi"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-10-16 - Museum Wales FOI 2026-024 - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: FOI Requests <FOI.Requests@museumwales.ac.uk>\nTo: Requester <[redacted]>\nCc: FOI Requests <FOI.Requests@museumwales.ac.uk>\nDate: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 12:35:13 +0000\nSubject: RE: ALLANOL/EXTERNAL - FOI 2026-024 / 032 / 036 – outstanding item 7: recorded basis for treating already-found records as “unrelated / not in scope”\n\nMr Buckler\n\nThe information requested regarding the searches undertaken was provided in our response of 5 October 2026.\n\nAs explained in that response, Collections staff reviewed records identified through references to Great House Farm, Ty Mawr Farm and Llandough, together with records returned through associated place-name searches. Records relating to the wider Llandough area, to other locations sharing the name \"Llandough\", or to other properties bearing the name \"Great House Farm\" were also reviewed.\n\nWe also confirmed that searches of the earliest accession registers encompass the former Cardiff Museum accession records, which became part of the National Museum of Wales collection without re-accessioning.\n\nAmgueddfa Cymru does not hold a separate search log, audit schedule or other recorded document providing further detail beyond that already supplied.\n\nWe therefore have no further recorded information to provide in relation to this matter.\n\nCofion cynnes,\n\nFOI Team\n\nfoi.requests @museumwales.ac.uk\namgueddfa.cymru | museum.wales\nRhif elusen | Charity number 525774\n\n-----Original Message-----\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]>\nSent: 16 October 2026 09:45\nTo: FOI Requests <FOI.Requests@museumwales.ac.uk>\nSubject: Re: ALLANOL/EXTERNAL - FOI 2026-024 / 032 / 036 – outstanding item 7: recorded basis for treating already-found records as “unrelated / not in scope”\n\nThis email comes from outside the organisation.\nDo not click links or open attachments unless it is an email you expected to receive.\nDear Amgueddfa Cymru FOI Team,\n\nThank you for your reply of 12 October. I note that you do not hold a separate recorded basis for treating records as outside the scope of FOI 2026-024. I would be grateful if you could confirm what search terms and systems were used to reach that conclusion, and whether any predecessor records (e.g. from the former National Museum of Wales or GGAT) were included.\n\nRegards,\n\nSion Buckler (Ret'd Sergeant)\nRoyal Signals Cyber/Electronic Warfare Professional and Life Member\n"},{"id":"wb-72078e54","subject":"National library of wales libanswers 9890384 acknowledgement","name":"2026-10-16 - National Library of Wales LibAnswers 9890384 acknowledgement - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","date":"2026-10-16","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"FOI / EIR correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-10-16%20-%20National%20Library%20of%20Wales%20LibAnswers%209890384%20acknowledgement%20-%20EN%20-%20V0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"national-library-of-wales-libanswers-9890384-acknowledgement","scopeTopics":["email","ghf","foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email","ghf","foi"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-10-16 - National Library of Wales LibAnswers 9890384 acknowledgement - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: National Library of Wales <enquiry@llgc.org.uk>\nTo: [redacted]\nDate: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 23:57:21 +0000\nSubject: Your submission to LibAnswers was received.\nGmail-Message-Id: 1a141ffbc39607dd\nStatus: received\nTicket: 9890384\n\nHello Requester,\n\nYour question was submitted successfully to the National Library of Wales enquiries service / Cyflwynwyd eich cwestiwn yn llwyddiannus i Wasanaeth Ymholiadau Llyfrgell Genedlaethol Cymru.\n\nDue to high demand and some resource challenges, there may be some delay in responding to enquiries at present. We apologise for any inconvenience this may cause and will provide an update as soon as possible.\n\nOherwydd lefel uchel y galw a rhai heriau adnoddau, bydd oedi gydag ymateb i ymholiadau ar hyn o bryd. Yr ydym yn ymddiheuro am unrhyw anghyfleuster a achosir gan hyn ac fe fyddwn yn darparu diweddariad cyn gynted ag y medrwn.\n\nThe original question, submitted 16 Oct 2026 12:53am by email, is the FOI request on the 1610 Herbert survey S7 and the Cardiff MSS microfilm of Bute X.6(12) for Llandough-juxta-Penarth. The full request text is filed as:\n2026-10-15 - National Library of Wales FOI Herbert survey S7 and Bute X.6 - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt\n\nThis email is sent from LibAnswers in relation to ticket #9890384."},{"id":"wb-c307bbb3","subject":"Planning file 89 01396 out great house farm Llandough","name":"2026-10-16 - Planning file 89-01396-OUT Great House Farm Llandough - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","date":"2026-10-16","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-10-16%20-%20Planning%20file%2089-01396-OUT%20Great%20House%20Farm%20Llandough%20-%20EN%20-%20V0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"planning-file-89-01396-out-great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":["ghf","planning","llandough"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["ghf","planning","llandough"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-10-16 - Planning file 89-01396-OUT Great House Farm Llandough - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"Catalogue note — planning file for the houses built on the Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr land, Llandough-juxta-Penarth.\n\nThese are the Vale of Glamorgan papers for outline application 89/01396/OUT (also stamped 8901396 OUT). They are filed as PDFs under pdf/planning/ and listed in pdf/_treeview.json.\n\n1. 1989-10-16 — Form D, Town and Country Planning Act 1971.\n   Applicant: BP Properties Ltd, Britannic House, Moor Lane, London EC2Y 9BU.\n   Agent: Debenham Tewson Ltd, 3 Castle Street, Cardiff CF1 2RJ.\n   Land at Leckwith Road, Llandough, South Glamorgan, edged red, site area about 3.4 acres. Applicant's adjoining land edged blue.\n   Proposal: residential development including a new access road. Outline permission. Siting, design, external appearance, means of access and landscaping all reserved.\n   Buildings to be demolished: stated \"None\" (the farm buildings had already been demolished).\n   Certificate A under section 27(1)(a), signed for B.P. Properties Limited, dated 16/10/89: certifies that at the beginning of the 21 days ending with the application nobody except the applicant was the owner of any part of the land. \"Owner\" means a freehold interest or a leasehold with at least 7 years unexpired.\n   Application dated October 1989. Council stamp records receipt.\n\n2. GGAT comments and recommendations on 89/01396/OUT (Glamorgan-Gwent Archaeological Trust Ltd).\n   The church of St Dochdwy and churchyard are treated as an early Christian monastic site, possibly from about 650, with a tenth- or eleventh-century cross shaft.\n   1979 Wales and West Housing Association development south of the church: emergency excavation found part of an Iron Age farmstead, a high-status Roman villa, a dark-age cemetery, a medieval monastic grange of Tewkesbury Abbey, a late medieval village, and a post-medieval mill and farm, none visible before development. Reference: Biglis, Caldicot and Llandough (ed. D. M. Robinson), BAR 188 (1988).\n   Information on Great House Farm itself was limited to the record of the recently demolished property, listed as a county treasure. GGAT asked for a mechanical-excavator assessment under archaeological supervision, estimated at two weeks and £2,000.\n   Policies cited: South Glamorgan Structure Plan (June 1989) EV15; East Vale Local Plan (February 1987) E9; Welsh Office Circular 61/81 section 26 (scheduled or unscheduled monument is a material consideration); SI 1988 No 1812 section 4; Circular 1/85 Appendix B (premature development should be refused).\n   GGAT recommended that the authority not determine the application until BP Properties provided an archaeological assessment by a qualified archaeologist (IFA Directory 1988-9).\n   Negotiations via Debenham Tewson had not produced agreement. Llandough Community Council had offered £100 if BP met the balance. GGAT noted BP were not the developer in the strict sense: they wanted to sell with outline permission.\n   Appendix: extract of GGAT letter to J. Maitland Evans, 1 October 1982. Ty Mawr Farm (H2.1) was a three-acre residential allocation. \"There remains a possibility that occupation continues to the north of the church; provision should be made, therefore, for thorough investigative work prior to the development.\"\n\n3. Manuscript officer report (consultations include further GGAT comment dated 8 February 1990).\n   Site described as about 3.4 / 3.5 acres adjacent to St Dochdwy's parish church. All buildings, including Great House Farm, demolished.\n   Outline application; illustrative layout of 18 dwellings and access to Leckwith Road, matters reserved.\n   Planning history: 8996/3337, 13 houses, same site, approved conditionally 25 July 1972.\n   Llandough Community Council: no objection, but part of the land is a registered village green.\n   Engineering: public foul sewer only; alternative surface-water disposal required.\n   Cardiff Bay Development Corporation: no objection; retain trees on the north-west slope and the public right of way through the site.\n   Welsh Water: applicant to liaise on foul drainage; a connection to the new Eastern District pumping main would not be permitted.\n   Representations from the Barry Preservation Society and a county councillor (a member of GGAT), attached as appendices C and D (those letters are not in this scan).\n   Applicant's agents replied to GGAT; that letter was appendix E (not in this scan).\n   Most of the land allocated for housing in the East Vale Local Plan (policy H2.1). The policy said buildings on the site should be retained; they had been demolished a little while before. The remainder is inside the developed area. Sections are too steep. The site includes a registered village green (a very small area is also the subject of two searches) and is crossed by a public right of way.\n   Recommendation: approve, subject to conditions, including the standard archaeology condition in Appendix B to Welsh Office Circular 1/85.\n   Draft conditions include: structures only on level ground, not on steep or wooded areas; at least 0.1 ha (0.25 acre) beside Leckwith Road and the church boundary kept as village green / amenity; vehicular access at the north-western edge onto the A4055 with 4.5 m x 60 m sightlines; estate roads minimum 5.5 m with 1.8 m footways and 10.5 m kerb radii; public right of way along the north-western boundary not obstructed; retain the stone wall.\n\n4. Decision, 13 March 1990. Vale of Glamorgan Borough Council. Application 89/01396/OUT.\n   Outline permission GRANTED for residential development including a new access road at Land at Great House Farm, Leckwith Road, Llandough, Penarth.\n   Application and plans received 8 November 1989.\n   Reserved matters within 3 years; development begun within 5 years of this permission or 2 years of the last reserved-matter approval, whichever is later.\n   Condition 5: 0.25 acres adjacent to Leckwith Road and the boundary with St Dochdwy's Church to be laid out as a village green / amenity area before beneficial use. Reason: to retain and enhance the existing registered village green.\n   Condition 8: do not obstruct the public right of way along the north-western boundary.\n   Condition 15: retain and make good the existing stone wall fronting the site, except where the access or building operations require otherwise.\n   Conditions 16–18: archaeologist nominated by the authority may observe excavations; no development until a field evaluation and archaeological assessment by a qualified archaeologist, to be carried out within three months of the consent; detail plans must have regard to that assessment.\n\n5. Plans bundle (one PDF, several dates).\n   - Debenham Tewson 1:2500 location plan, identification only, stamped 8901396. Shows St Dochdwy's, the farm group, Llandough Moors and the surrounding streets.\n   - Certified true copy of the register map, Deputy County Solicitor, 18 June 1985. Marks VG 41 and OBJ No 314 (LAND) at Llandough.\n   - Heronhurst Homes exercise layout, Llandough, South Glamorgan, drawn 16.09.91, scale 1:500. Schedule: types E 3, F 3, H 3, W 4, Y 1, total 14. Net area about 2.40 acres. Shows St Dochdwy's Parish Church, village green, Leckwith Road A4055, and a public footpath.\n   - Ideal Homes Wales / Wyn Thomas + Partners, job \"Llandough Farm, Llandough\", site layout 1, drawing 3024, March 1992, scale 1:500. Received by Planning and Development 16 December 1995. House types: Penlan 6, Sutton 9, Budworth 6, total 21. Village green to be maintained by Llandough Community Council and to form part of a pedestrian gateway to the Vale. Area to the north noted as to be maintained by the Forest of Cardiff Group. Penlan Road and a 5 m footpath are shown.\n   - Fax cover sketch from Debenham Tewson, 29 January 1998, a further housing layout on Leckwith Road.\n\nThe outline consent is the planning permission under which the later housing layouts for this land were drawn. The 1972 consent (8996/3337) is the earlier permission on the same site recorded in the 1990 officer report."},{"id":"wb-de88fc02","subject":"Welsh government atisn 26 0010 chase","name":"2026-10-16 - Welsh Government ATISN 26-0010 chase - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","date":"2026-10-16","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Heritage and archives correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-10-16%20-%20Welsh%20Government%20ATISN%2026-0010%20chase%20-%20EN%20-%20V0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"welsh-government-atisn-26-0010-chase","scopeTopics":["email","ghf","foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email","ghf","foi"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-10-16 - Welsh Government ATISN 26-0010 chase - EN - V0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nTo: OEG.FMCO.FOI@gov.wales\nDate: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 01:45:06 -0700\nSubject: Re: ATISN 26-0010 - Acknowledgement (Retention & disposal schedules)\nGmail-Message-Id: 1a143e2e7325eb79\nStatus: sent\n\nDear Natalie / FMCO FOI Team,\n\nThank you for the acknowledgement of ATISN 26-0010 concerning retention and disposal schedules. I would be grateful for the substantive response as soon as possible, particularly in relation to any schedules covering records of Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr, Llandough, 1988–1996.\n\nRegards,\n\nSion Buckler (Ret'd Sergeant)\nRoyal Signals Cyber/Electronic Warfare Professional and Life Member"},{"id":"wb-6914d7af","subject":"Cais am gyngor cyfreithiol Tŷ Mawr Llandough","name":"0000-00-00_cais-am-gyngor-cyfreithiol-ty-mawr-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_cais-am-gyngor-cyfreithiol-ty-mawr-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"cais-am-gyngor-cyfreithiol-t-mawr-llandough","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_cais-am-gyngor-cyfreithiol-ty-mawr-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"[Recovered on 19 Oct 2026 from the original email in the family's Gmail, because this archive file was missing. Matched to this entry by its subject line; where several emails share a subject, the order within the group is not certain. Email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses are redacted.]\n\nSubject: Cais am Gyngor Cyfreithiol – Ty Mawr, Llandough\nFrom: Requester\nDate: Fri, 13 Feb 2026 01:33 UTC\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nAnnwyl Syr/Fadam,\n\nYsgrifennaf i ofyn am gyngor cyfreithiol mewn perthynas â mater cymhleth yn\nymwneud â Thŷ Mawr (Great House Farm), Llandough.\n\nYn gryno, mae’r mater yn codi’r cwestiynau canlynol:\n\n   -\n\n   Diffyg penderfyniad barnwrol ar berchnogaeth cyn achos meddiant yn y\n   1980au;\n   -\n\n   Cofrestru cyntaf yn 1982 a allai gynnwys “camgymeriad” o fewn ystyr\n   Atodlen 4 Deddf Cofrestru Tir 2002;\n   -\n\n   Amnewid neu anghysondeb hunaniaeth yn y disgrifiad cyfreithiol;\n   -\n\n   Cwestiynau ynghylch ystyriaeth a datgeliad treftadaeth archaeolegol.\n\nRwy’n ceisio:\n\n   1.\n\n   Barn gynnar ar hyfywedd unrhyw gais i adolygu neu gywiro’r gofrestr;\n   2.\n\n   Asesiad o’r sefyllfa gyfyngu (limitation);\n   3.\n\n   Cyngor ar gamau cyn-weithredu priodol.\n\nRwyf wedi atodi crynodeb gweithredol byr er hwylustod.\n\nByddwn yn ddiolchgar am gyfle i drefnu ymgynghoriad cychwynnol. Rwy’n barod\ni dalu am gyngor cychwynnol ffurfiol.\n\nYn gywir,\n\nSion Buckler\n[address redacted]\n[phone redacted]\n"},{"id":"wb-0f7fe361","subject":"Cais am gyngor cyfreithiol Tŷ Mawr Llandough","name":"0000-00-00_cais-am-gyngor-cyfreithiol-ty-mawr-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_cais-am-gyngor-cyfreithiol-ty-mawr-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","threadSlug":"cais-am-gyngor-cyfreithiol-t-mawr-llandough","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_cais-am-gyngor-cyfreithiol-ty-mawr-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"[Recovered on 19 Oct 2026 from the original email in the family's Gmail, because this archive file was missing. Matched to this entry by its subject line; where several emails share a subject, the order within the group is not certain. Email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses are redacted.]\n\nSubject:  Cais am Gyngor Cyfreithiol – Ty Mawr, Llandough\nFrom: Rhys Evans\nDate: Fri, 20 Feb 2026 10:23 UTC\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nBoire da Mr Buckler,\n\nDiolch am dy e-bost.\n\nOes modd i chi anfon copi o'r dogfennau i mi, os gwelwch yn dda? Ydych chi ar gael prynhawn yma am 2 o'r gloch am alwad i drafod ymhellach?\n\nDiolch,\nRhys\n\n\n\n\nRhys Evans\nDirector & Head of Rural Practice\nDDI: [phone redacted]\nwww.jcpsolicitors.co.uk\nFor address details please click here\n Privacy - Your privacy is important to us, so we want you to know exactly how we will use your personal data. This information can be found in our privacy notice on our website: https://www.jcpsolicitors.co.uk/site/help/privacy_policy.html. If you would like a hard copy of the notice please contact us.\n  \n BEWARE: Bank Account Fraud - Emails are open communications and so are NOT secure. Please do not EVER send us bank account details by email. If this is unavoidable YOU must telephone US to check we have the right details. Our bank account details NEVER change. If you receive an email or telephone call from us asking you to transfer funds to a different bank account to the one notified to you in hard copy, it is FAKE. Do not send any money but telephone us immediately. Third parties sending funds to us having received bank details by email or telephone, do so at their own risk. We do not accept liability for any losses sustained by anyone responding to fraudulent emails or telephone calls.\n  \n Telephone Number Spoofing - is where criminals use software so that when calls are received the number that comes up is different to the one they are calling from and may show as the number of a firm of solicitors. The criminals will then pretend to be solicitors and ask for payments to be made to a bank account that they control, which can result in large losses for clients. To protect yourself, if you receive a call from someone saying they are calling from JCP Solicitors and they give you bank details over the phone you should:-\n 1.     Call us back on [phone redacted] to check the details BEFORE making any payments or transfers; and\n 2.     If in any doubt DO NOT make the payment or transfer (criminals will often try and rush you by saying it is urgent or must be done immediately) \n  \n Legal Ombudsman - please be advised that the time limits for referring a matter to the Legal Ombudsman have changed. Updated details can be found at https://www.jcpsolicitors.co.uk/site/about/any_concerns/. If you would like a hard copy of this policy please contact us. \n \n Disclaimer and Monitoring Email \n  \n We do not accept any responsibility for viruses and it is your responsibility to scan attachments (if any).\n No contracts may be concluded with us or our clients by means of e­mail communications. \n We do not accept service of proceedings and documents by email. \n We may monitor email traffic data and also the content of e-mail for the purposes of security and staff training.\n \n If you are not the intended recipient of this email you may not forward, copy, or disclose any part of it, If you have received this email in error, please delete it and any copies, and notify us immediately at [email redacted]. The sender does not accept liability for any errors or omissions.\n \n JCP Solicitors is the trading name of JCP Solicitors Limited registered in England and Wales (CRN 9336286) and authorised and regulated by the Solicitors Regulation Authority.\n  \n Registered office - Venture Court, Waterside Business Park, Valley Way, Enterprise Park, Swansea, [postcode redacted]\n \n Directors: \n Keith Thomas, Lynne Morgan, Michael Williams, Hayley Davies*, Matthew Owen, Emma Gilroy, Betsan Powell, Chris Shaw, Richard Howells, Amy Seppman*, Rhys Evans, Andrew Meech, Thomas Rees, Mark Gilroy, Sophie Thomas, Jill Bulteel, Mike Downey, Rhian Jervis*, Denise Warlow*, Jacqui Gower*, David Owen, Ian Rees, Beverley Bowen, Joanna Fenoulhet, Jonathan Flynn, Angela Killa, Nick O'Neill, Jac Staddon, Natalie Jones, Benjamin Davies, Lauren Protheroe, Rhianydd Llewellyn-Thomas, Carys Wilson, Carlos Land, Natalie Corbi*, Eleri Roebuck, Souiful Alam, Angela West, Ian Spender. (*Non-Lawyer).\n  \n Telephone: [phone redacted] Facsimile: [phone redacted]\n  \n Any reference to a Director means a Director of JCP Solicitors Limited or an employee of JCP Solicitors Limited with equivalent standing and qualifications who is not a Director at law.\n \n Please think before you print\n \n \nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSent: 17 February 2026 18:07\nTo: Emily Wellington <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSubject: [External] Re: [EXTERNAL] Cais am Gyngor Cyfreithiol – Ty Mawr, Llandough\n\nHi,\n\nThanks for your reply.\nI can correspond in English, but for the case I'd like it to all be in Welsh.\nI feel that's important.\n\nBut all are welcome to assist my family in this endeavour.\nThis will take, at least, a village.\n\nSion\n\nOn Tue, 17 Feb 2026 at 14:04, Emily Wellington <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>> wrote:\nGood afternoon Mr Buckler,\n\nThank you for your enquiry and voicemail.\n\nFor us to provide a fee quotation we will require sight of some further documents. Please send these through and we can assess if we are able to assist further.\n\nWhilst we do not provide legal advice through the medium of Welsh, my colleague Rhys Evans is a fluent Welsh speaker and is happy to discuss matters through the medium of Welsh.\n\nI look forward to hearing from you.\n\nKind regards,\nEmily\n\n\n​​​​\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSent: 13 February 2026 01:33\nTo: UZZI Consulting <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nCc: [email redacted]<[email redacted]>; Hello <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>; [email redacted]<[email redacted]>; [email redacted]<[email redacted]>\nSubject: [EXTERNAL] Cais am Gyngor Cyfreithiol – Ty Mawr, Llandough\n\n\nAnnwyl Syr/Fadam,\n\nYsgrifennaf i ofyn am gyngor cyfreithiol mewn perthynas â mater cymhleth yn ymwneud â Thŷ Mawr (Great House Farm), Llandough.\n\nYn gryno, mae’r mater yn codi’r cwestiynau canlynol:\n\n  *   Diffyg penderfyniad barnwrol ar berchnogaeth cyn achos meddiant yn y 1980au;\n  *   Cofrestru cyntaf yn 1982 a allai gynnwys “camgymeriad” o fewn ystyr Atodlen 4 Deddf Cofrestru Tir 2002;\n  *   Amnewid neu anghysondeb hunaniaeth yn y disgrifiad cyfreithiol;\n  *   Cwestiynau ynghylch ystyriaeth a datgeliad treftadaeth archaeolegol.\n\nRwy’n ceisio:\n\n  1.  Barn gynnar ar hyfywedd unrhyw gais i adolygu neu gywiro’r gofrestr;\n  2.  Asesiad o’r sefyllfa gyfyngu (limitation);\n  3.  Cyngor ar gamau cyn-weithredu priodol.\n\nRwyf wedi atodi crynodeb gweithredol byr er hwylustod.\n\nByddwn yn ddiolchgar am gyfle i drefnu ymgynghoriad cychwynnol. Rwy’n barod i dalu am gyngor cychwynnol ffurfiol.\n\nYn gywir,\n\nSion Buckler\n[address redacted]\n[phone redacted]\n[Evolve:cb628be1-368d-44fb-9e69-22ad06858d51]\n[Evolve:4d3d4783-3f03-4c1e-852d-a837d47e7a41]\n"},{"id":"wb-98c8d628","subject":"Consortium land registry BP v Buckler","name":"2016-03-23_consortium-land_registry_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2016-03-23","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Land Registry correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2016-03-23_consortium-land_registry_bpvsbuckler__en_v0-0-1.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-land-registry-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2016-03-23_consortium-land_registry_bpvsbuckler__en_v0-0-1.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: hywel apbuckler <[redacted]>\nDate: Wed, 23 Mar 2016 17:03:54 -0300\nSubject: Quiet Title and Adverse Possession\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V7tdLWjHfgNkVs_ovY7_15nr0wfpCv5AMeSrF+_kP_2QQ@mail.gmail.com>\n\nHey,\n\nFYI -  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quiet_title\n\nAwesome for cases of 'Adverse Possession'.\nhttp://swarb.co.uk/bp-properties-ltd-v-buckler-ca-31-jul-1987/\n\nHywel\n"},{"id":"wb-77c1ae3f","subject":"Consortium evidence BP v Buckler","name":"2016-08-16_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2016-08-16","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2016-08-16_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-evidence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2016-08-16_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Hywel Apbuckler <[redacted]>\nDate: Tue, 16 Aug 2016 23:06:41 -0400\nSubject: BP vs Buckler\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V5zJU4p9dGd4G15Mxu=a6VzQUTasys=JYtwOqc=SNqpbg@mail.gmail.com>\n\nI just discovered you. This page\n<https://www.hcrlaw.com/people/jane-mayglothling/> told me about you.\nThis article <https://www.hcrlaw.com/blog/what-is-a-possessory-title/> sent\nme to that page after I Google'd the terms 'possessory title' .\n\nI am the son of William Buckler. Of the 'BP vs Buckler' case of adverse\npossession.\nI am now 31 years old and in Oath to defend rights and freedoms (Gov ID:\n25148537).\n\nNot many people are aware of the true story of this case. I'm now able to\nexpose the truth thanks to skills I acquired in my career as a Cyber\nWarfare/ Security Engineer.\n\nBP did a back hand deal with an archaeological firm who discovered roman\nburials under our family farm land. The 34 year campaign to remove us from\nour land and steal the gold which lay beneath (to sell to museums) was a\nscandal of that time and still not public information. But the scandal runs\ndeeper.\n\nMy father died never fully understanding his inalienable or inherent birth\nrights. I single highhandedly wrote the same amount of code it takes to fly\na US drone or F22 Fighter Jet last year, so reading through and\nunderstanding the Bill of Rights, Magna Carta and the UK's 750 'statute'\nrules wasn't too difficult for me.\n\nWhat I concluded from it all, was that democracy is not voting for the\nleader and rule makers of the society we are members of. Democracy is in\nfact, a free British Subjects right to leave, join or form any society we\nchoose (with any 'statute' rules we consent to be governed by -\nindividually!).\n\nThe 1801 UK \"Society\" (Regime) now closer resembles an Evil, Tyrannical,\nNationwide Cult, which doesn't serve and defend with the allodial title of\nGreat Britain we afford it. Its statute rules, in a lot of cases, actually\nviolate the laws of the land. We are systematically being\nenslaved-from-birth (made members of this society by child abduction and\nforce) and harvested of everything we once had e.g. land, liberty, wealth,\nproperty and even each other.\n\nSo I vow to 'renounce' membership/ citizenship from Britain's new No.1\nEnemy (The 1801 UK \"Society\") and join/form a new British Society. Which I\nhave done!!\n\nThe 2015 United Technocracy Society was founded and financed by myself and\n50 other British Subjects, most in Oath and still in active duty. We have a\nstronghold with a defense force and are unofficially a British Protectorate\n(protecting our societies members from UK Regime oppression, austerity,\ngenocide, interment, touchless torture etc). I will be inviting my\nCommander-In-Chief to give our new Act of Union Her Royal Assent and I will\nseek Royal Charter once we reach 5,000 members.\n\nI never know how much liberty or life I have remaining making this stand\nagainst the UK Regime. As it is one of the most powerful in the world. I\nrecently had a safe house raided by secret services, I've lost two Private\nMilitary Security Contractors and my driver was in a Diana-style RTA. But I\nhonor my Oath and Father and I am determine to see through what I began as\nit is both legal and lawful and fully warranted.\n\nGoogle awarded my Technocratic System of Governance two patents in January\nso we're off to a good start. And we've sold our first 'Neo-Dome' today.\nWhich provides free internet and electricity to those moving Off-Grid. For\neach 5 we sell we're able to create a new mesh network and a 6th dome will\nbe erected for schools, churches and hospitals. I plan to resettle\n(proclaim)back my birth land, once my colonies and defense force have grown\nin strength and numbers.\n\nYes I am a threat to society! The 1801 UK \"Society\" specifically. But it's\nbeen more than a threat to our country, families, wealth and children's\nfuture for many years and someone has to put an end to it! I'm willing and\nable to be the John Gault people needs. I'm a proficient military commander\nand leader, cyber security expect (hacker) and I've made more progress for\nthe freeman movement than Dean Clifford, Roger Hayes or John Harris ever\ndid.\n\nThank you for your time reading this.\nI hope you and others alike will pick the right side when the time comes.\n\nKind Regards,\n\nHywel ApBuckler\nPrime Technocrat\nUnited Technocracy, GB\nskype: hywel.buckler\n"},{"id":"wb-09c03670","subject":"Consortium correspondence BP v Buckler","name":"2024-02-24_consortium-correspondence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2024-02-24","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2024-02-24_consortium-correspondence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-correspondence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2024-02-24_consortium-correspondence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"Subject: Re: [redacted] for Hywel\nMessage ID: 18ddbceab5190d80\nDate: Sat, 24 Feb 2024 15:49:18 +0000\nFrom: Denise Davies <[redacted]>\nTo: Requester <[redacted]>\n============================================================\n\n<html><head><meta http-equiv=\"content-type\" content=\"text/html; charset=utf-8\"></head><body dir=\"auto\">Hi Hywel,&nbsp;<div><br><div>You should write a book! My life is positively boring in comparison. I am so glad to hear about your new family and the addition of little Delyth ( you don’t hear that name very often these days, but there were two girls in my school with that name) are you welsh speaking? My husband asked because of your surname and strong welsh names. I don’t speak Welsh but my husband does.&nbsp;</div><div><br></div><div>I was a staff nurse for 25 years, went into teaching bookkeeping and payroll for 11 years and took redundancy 8 years ago and been working as a dog boarder in my home since and loving every minute. I have a cocker spaniel named Charlie- Brown who is almost 4 years old. I look after my elderly mum (who lives with us) and support my husband who is a lot older than me and is now struggling. But life is good in comparison to many people and my faith in the Bible and our ministry work as one of Jehovah’s Witnesses keeps me very busy which I love. I have been a JW now for almost 40 years! (That shocks me to write that down! Where has the time gone?)&nbsp;</div><div><br></div><div>As part of our ministry we knock doors of course but we also do public cart witnessing, you may have seen us in cities and near pedestrian areas locally and all over the world. Because my mum can no longer do the ministry she stays at home and writes letters. If the weather is particularly bad I will sometimes sit with her and write letters too which is how you came to receive one from me. In order to save her postage costs I volunteer to hand post them through the doors and at this moment in time she is systematically writing to everyone in the area you live.&nbsp;</div><div><br></div><div>We all thoroughly enjoy taking part in this work especially since after covid as many people are so worried about the future and the deterioration of society today and we love to share our hope and knowledge with anyone who is willing to talk with us. &nbsp;I hope you don’t mind but I have attached a short video (just over 3 minutes long) which explains a little about our bible study courses we offer freely to people?</div><div><br></div><div>One of my favourite Bible promises is at psalm 37:10,11 :</div><div>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;‘ just a little while longer and the wicked will be no more….the meek will possess the earth and they will find exquisite delight in the abundance of peace’&nbsp;</div><div><br></div><div>Do you have any interest in the bible yourself? Were you brought up with a religion? I would be very interested to hear your views. I know you will be busy travelling etc. but if you do have time I would like to hear your views.&nbsp;</div><div><br></div><div>Safe journey&nbsp;</div><div>Denise (PS what sort of dog do you have?)&nbsp;</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"display: block;\" class=\"\"><div style=\"-webkit-user-select: all; -webkit-user-drag: element; display: inline-block;\" class=\"apple-rich-link\" draggable=\"true\" role=\"link\" data-url=\"https://www.jw.org/finder?srcid=jwlshare&amp;wtlocale=E&amp;lank=pub-wsb_1_VIDEO\"><a style=\"border-radius:10px;font-family:-apple-system, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;display:block;-webkit-user-select:none;width:228px;user-select:none;-webkit-user-modify:read-only;user-modify:read-only;overflow:hidden;text-decoration:none;\" class=\"lp-rich-link\" rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://www.jw.org/finder?srcid=jwlshare&amp;wtlocale=E&amp;lank=pub-wsb_1_VIDEO\" dir=\"ltr\" role=\"button\" draggable=\"false\" width=\"228\"><table style=\"table-layout:fixed;border-collapse:collapse;width:228px;background-color:#E9E9EB;font-family:-apple-system, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;\" class=\"lp-rich-link-emailBaseTable\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" border=\"0\" width=\"228\"><tbody><tr><td vertical-align=\"center\" align=\"center\"><img style=\"width:228px;filter:brightness(0.97);height:228px;\" width=\"228\" height=\"228\" draggable=\"false\" class=\"lp-rich-link-mediaImage\" alt=\"502013341_univ_sqr_lg.jpg\" src=\"cid:58768A2E-5827-4F09-BA82-1E00C67696A0\"></td></tr><tr><td vertical-align=\"center\"><table bgcolor=\"#E9E9EB\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" width=\"228\" style=\"font-family:-apple-system, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;table-layout:fixed;background-color:rgba(233, 233, 235, 1);\" class=\"lp-rich-link-captionBar\"><tbody><tr><td style=\"padding:8px 0px 8px 0px;\" class=\"lp-rich-link-captionBar-textStackItem\"><div style=\"max-width:100%;margin:0px 16px 0px 16px;overflow:hidden;\" class=\"lp-rich-link-captionBar-textStack\"><div style=\"word-wrap:break-word;font-weight:500;font-size:12px;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;text-align:left;\" class=\"lp-rich-link-captionBar-textStack-topCaption-leading\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://www.jw.org/finder?srcid=jwlshare&amp;wtlocale=E&amp;lank=pub-wsb_1_VIDEO\" style=\"text-decoration: none\" draggable=\"false\"><font color=\"#000000\" style=\"color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 1);\">Why Study the Bible?—Full Length | JW.ORG Videos</font></a></div><div style=\"word-wrap:break-word;font-weight:400;font-size:11px;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;text-align:left;\" class=\"lp-rich-link-captionBar-textStack-bottomCaption-leading\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://www.jw.org/finder?srcid=jwlshare&amp;wtlocale=E&amp;lank=pub-wsb_1_VIDEO\" style=\"text-decoration: none\" draggable=\"false\"><font color=\"#A2A2A9\" style=\"color: rgba(60, 60, 67, 0.6);\">jw.org</font></a></div></div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr></tbody></table></a></div></div><br id=\"lineBreakAtBeginningOfSignature\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Sent from my iPad</div><div dir=\"ltr\"><br><blockquote type=\"cite\">On 23 Feb 2024, at 15:32, Requester &lt;[redacted]&gt; wrote:<br><br></blockquote></div><blockquote type=\"cite\"><div dir=\"ltr\">﻿<div dir=\"auto\"><div dir=\"auto\"><div dir=\"auto\">Hi,</div><div dir=\"auto\"><br></div><div dir=\"auto\">I'm sure we both have tales of life well lived to exchange. I'm perfectly open to making a new friend.&nbsp;</div><div dir=\"auto\"><br></div><div dir=\"auto\">I'm not exactly sure what I've done to deserve the universe conspiring in my favour so much lately. I'm already blessed with 4 incredible best friends (2 men/2 woman) of decades. And a wife and two children.&nbsp;</div><div dir=\"auto\"><br></div><div dir=\"auto\">I'm the grandson of a best actor/ 1993 Wesh Bafta award winner: Ray Smith, from the TV series Dempsey and Makepeace. His son (my uncle) started the band Tigertailz. And all the success originated at our house here on [redacted] as it happens. My father was William Buckler from Great House Farm in Llandough. (See BP Vs Buckler 1987). Our farm was next to the church in Llandough. I believe the kitchen table I grew up eating breakfast on, was the same table Marconi invented the radio while he stayed with our family.&nbsp;</div><div dir=\"auto\"><br></div><div dir=\"auto\">The whole BP Vs Buckler story was local headlines at the time. But take anything you read with a pinch of salt. What I understand to have actually happened, with the war chest of information I inherited from my dad's death, was that my parents and grandparents before them, leased and worked the farm for hundreds of years. We acquired the title deeds in exchange for allowing some quarrying to be done. BP and Cotswold archeologists teamed up to try to get the treasure under our farm by trying to unlawfully evict and remove us from the equation. And after 34 years battling BP we lost our farm in court to them. And after my family and I stood there with the community, watching them bulldose our home down, BP exccevated all the Roman burials under our farm and sold the artifacts and land. My father's heart literally stopped, he fought to defend our home and family to the death.</div><div dir=\"auto\"><br></div><div dir=\"auto\">I grew up. Moved on. I won an assisted place in Llandovery College. And went onto work in military intelligence/ cyber security and warfare.&nbsp;</div><div dir=\"auto\"><br></div><div dir=\"auto\">Until in 2013 I stumbled across some things nobody was meant to see. My 36 day old son got abducted and used to try to ransom me. My options were either be silent or be party to corruption and crimes. I chose exile and refused to return until my son and I got justice.&nbsp;</div><div dir=\"auto\"><br></div><div dir=\"auto\">It was a long road to justice and getting out of exile and returning home to Britain after almost a decade overseas. I immediately got a puppy, caught up with old family and friends and got some work in elderly adult healthcare with the Vale Council.&nbsp;</div><div dir=\"auto\"><br></div><div dir=\"auto\">I refrained from going back up to Yorkshire and kicking that old bees nest last year. I can only hope and pray my son's ok. Instead I stayed out of trouble at my families horse farm (Newmill Farm) in Monknash.&nbsp;</div><div dir=\"auto\"><br></div><div dir=\"auto\">Then unexpectedly a woman I spent my time in exile with (and loved very much), reached out to reciprocate my feelings and announced we were pregnant - And we both couldn't have been happier. She is the love of my life and woman of my dreams.</div><div dir=\"auto\"><br></div><div dir=\"auto\">So last October I flew right back to the Caribbean island which took me a decade to escape, married my now wife (November 27th), then got us a car and villa near her families home. Then on the 27th December our daughter Delyth was born - happy and healthy. After just 3 short hours of labor too. And within a matter of hours we were all home together in our spare room/ nursery, surrounded by our family and friends.&nbsp;</div><div dir=\"auto\"><br></div><div dir=\"auto\">I'm just back in Britain now to get my dog and take him back to the Caribbean with me. I'm right now arranging a ferry to Europe and I'll fly back from there. And I'm trying to arrange obtaining my medals and getting tailored for my army 1's dress/ uniform, ahead of our wedding later this year.</div><div dir=\"auto\"><br></div><div dir=\"auto\">But a fisherman always sees another fisherman from afar. I've worked with intelligence agencies who would love your methods of communication. Feels very revolutionary to hand deliver a letter. I could take some pigeons to the other side of the pond if you want and we can expand that modus operandi.&nbsp;</div><div dir=\"auto\"><br></div><div dir=\"auto\">I look forward to hearing anything you might have to say. My new friend.&nbsp;</div><div dir=\"auto\"><br></div><div dir=\"auto\">Speak soon,</div><div dir=\"auto\"><br></div><div dir=\"auto\">Hywel</div></div><br><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\" dir=\"auto\"><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Fri, 23 Feb 2024, 13:05 Denise Davies, &lt;<a href=\"mailto:[redacted]\" rel=\"noreferrer noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">[redacted]</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"auto\"><div style=\"display:block\"><div style=\"display:inline-block\" role=\"link\"><a style=\"border-radius:10px;font-family:-apple-system,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;display:block;width:228px;overflow:hidden;text-decoration:none\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer noreferrer noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.jw.org/finder?srcid=jwlshare&amp;wtlocale=E&amp;prefer=lang&amp;docid=501100090\" dir=\"ltr\" role=\"button\" width=\"228\" target=\"_blank\"><table style=\"table-layout:fixed;border-collapse:collapse;width:228px;background-color:#e9e9eb;font-family:-apple-system,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" border=\"0\" width=\"228\"><tbody><tr><td align=\"center\"><img style=\"width:228px;height:228px\" width=\"228\" height=\"228\" alt=\"501100090_univ_sqr_lg.jpg\" src=\"cid:B774CE3D-2CF1-4729-BE49-644AB0CC7672\" data-unique-identifier=\"\"></td></tr><tr><td><table bgcolor=\"#E9E9EB\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" width=\"228\" style=\"font-family:-apple-system,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;table-layout:fixed;background-color:rgba(233,233,235,1)\"><tbody><tr><td style=\"padding:8px 0px 8px 0px\"><div style=\"max-width:100%;margin:0px 16px 0px 16px;overflow:hidden\"><div style=\"word-wrap:break-word;font-weight:500;font-size:12px;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;text-align:left\"><a rel=\"nofollow noreferrer noreferrer noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.jw.org/finder?srcid=jwlshare&amp;wtlocale=E&amp;prefer=lang&amp;docid=501100090\" style=\"text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\"><font color=\"#000000\" style=\"color:rgba(0,0,0,1)\">Why Is There So Much Hatred?—What Does the Bible Say?</font></a></div><div style=\"word-wrap:break-word;font-weight:400;font-size:11px;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;text-align:left\"><a rel=\"nofollow noreferrer noreferrer noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.jw.org/finder?srcid=jwlshare&amp;wtlocale=E&amp;prefer=lang&amp;docid=501100090\" style=\"text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\"><font color=\"#A2A2A9\" style=\"color:rgba(60,60,67,0.6)\">jw.org</font></a></div></div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr></tbody></table></a></div></div>Hi Hywel<div><br></div><div>Thank you so much for taking the time to email me, I really appreciate it, it’s good to know that the time we spend writing letters are not for nothing.&nbsp;</div><div><br></div><div>I hope you don’t mind, but I have attached a link to an article on our website <a href=\"http://jw.org\" rel=\"noreferrer noreferrer noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">jw.org</a>, please forgive me for not including the web site in my letter, &nbsp;that was an oversight on my part.&nbsp;</div><div><br></div><div>Hopefully, &nbsp;you will see that I was talking generally about the hatred so prevalent throughout the world and not focusing on one house or street. The Bible’s predictions about its increase is very significant for the days we live in and forms part of a group of signs we are told to look out for. If you wanted to search on the web site for ‘signs of the last days ‘ you will see for yourself what I mean.&nbsp;</div><div><br></div><div>I don’t wish to dwell on negative things all of the time though, so please may I encourage you to also look for ‘what does the future hold’ and you will see the good news that we are trying to help people see in order to strengthen their hope that there are better times soon to come.&nbsp;</div><div><br></div><div>I am intrigued as to why you were in exile but I don’t wish to pry, but glad to hear you are back in the UK and hopefully safe and well.&nbsp;</div><div><br></div><div>If you would like to continue our conversation, I would be very happy to do so through email, text or face to face whatever you are more comfortable with or you can continue to browse the web page and contact me if you have any questions you would like to ask.</div><div><br></div><div>Thanks again for taking the time to read this,</div><div>Hope to hear from you again</div><div>Denise Davies&nbsp;</div><div><br></div><div><br id=\"m_-74707603589583209m_2322105342607661247m_-368831200587330758lineBreakAtBeginningOfSignature\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Sent from my iPad</div><div dir=\"ltr\"><br><blockquote type=\"cite\">On 22 Feb 2024, at 15:03, Requester &lt;<a href=\"mailto:[redacted]\" rel=\"noreferrer noreferrer noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">[redacted]</a>&gt; wrote:<br><br></blockquote></div><blockquote type=\"cite\"><div dir=\"ltr\">﻿<div dir=\"auto\">Denise,<div dir=\"auto\"><br></div><div dir=\"auto\">Thanks for your letter today.&nbsp;</div><div dir=\"auto\">Very kind and inspiring words.</div><div dir=\"auto\">And a very admirable and courageous act.</div><div dir=\"auto\"><br></div><div dir=\"auto\">I'm actually not long back in Britain from 8 years in exile overseas. So this is a very meaningful message.&nbsp;</div><div dir=\"auto\"><br></div><div dir=\"auto\">I am curious - is this letter in response to hatred originating from our house, houses on our street or this area?</div><div dir=\"auto\"><br></div><div dir=\"auto\">You also mention a website.&nbsp;</div><div dir=\"auto\">I will look it up. But what is the URL?</div><div dir=\"auto\"><br></div><div dir=\"auto\">Hywel ApBuckler</div><div dir=\"auto\">[redacted]&nbsp;</div><div dir=\"auto\"><br></div><div dir=\"auto\"><br></div><div dir=\"auto\"><br></div><div dir=\"auto\"><br><br></div></div>\r\n</div></blockquote></div></div></blockquote></div>\r\n</div>\r\n</div></blockquote></div></div></body></html>"},{"id":"wb-e4efdc41","subject":"Fw BP v Buckler","name":"2025-01-01_fw-bp-vs-buckler_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2025-01-01_fw-bp-vs-buckler_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"fw-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/2025-01-01_fw-bp-vs-buckler_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Soin,\r\n\r\nThank you for your email to Kanishka Narayan MP and for passing on your concerns. [location redacted] unfortunately does not fall within the Vale of Glamorgan Parliamentary Constituency and Parliamentary protocol states that Kanishka is unable to assist anyone outside his constituency.\r\n\r\nI would like to recommend contacting your MP, Stephen Doughty. His contact details are:\r\n\r\nPhone Number: 020 7219 5348<tel:020%207219%205348>\r\nEmail Address: stephen.doughty.mp@parliament.uk<mailto:stephen.doughty.mp@parliament.uk>\r\n\r\nI hope they are able to assist you.\r\n\r\nKind regards,\r\nMadelene\r\n\r\n[cid:image001.png@01DC57A8.1BFCB220]\r\nMadelene Larsson\r\nConstituency Support Manager, Kanishka Narayan MP\r\nMember of Parliament for the Vale of Glamorgan\r\nParliamentary Under-Secretary of State in the Department for Science, Innovation and Technology\r\n\r\nUK Parliament Disclaimer: this e-mail is confidential to the intended recipient. If you have received it in error, please notify the sender and delete it from your system. Any unauthorised use, disclosure, or copying is not permitted. This e-mail has been checked for viruses, but no liability is accepted for any damage caused by any virus transmitted by this e-mail. This e-mail address is not secure, is not encrypted and should not be used for sensitive data.\r\n\r\n\r\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]>\r\nSent: 14 November 2025 12:49\r\nTo: NARAYAN, Kanishka (MP) <kanishka.narayan.mp@parliament.uk>\r\nSubject: BP vs Buckler\r\n\r\n\r\nSir/Ma'am,\r\n\r\nI am your constituent, living [location redacted]. I am writing to request your assistance in reviewing a historic legal matter involving my family: BP Properties Ltd v Buckler (1987), relating to Great House Farm, Llandough.\r\n\r\nMy family lived on, cared for and claimed to own that farm for generations. The events surrounding the 1987 case resulted in the loss of our home and land, and left several members of my family deeply affected. Recent research I have undertaken has revealed significant procedural inconsistencies, historical irregularities, unanswered questions regarding corporate identity, unverified service of key documents, and heritage considerations relating to the site.\r\n\r\nI have attached a short report summarising SOME of these issues. I am requesting your help in identifying the appropriate bodies—legal, historical, or governmental—who may be able to independently review the historical record to ensure fairness, accuracy, and transparency.\r\n\r\nMy interest is not solely personal; Great House Farm has a heritage significance to Wales, including documented accounts that Guglielmo Marconi stayed there while experimenting with early radio technology. I believe this should form part of our preserved historical record.\r\n\r\nBy way of background, I am a veteran and have served both in the Armed Forces and recently with the Vale of Glamorgan Council. My intention is simply to seek clarity, proper review, and justice where appropriate. I would be grateful for your guidance on the correct avenues to pursue.\r\n\r\nThank you for your time and consideration. I look forward to your response.\r\n\r\nYours sincerely,\r\n\r\nSion Buckler\r\n[redacted]\r\nTel: 0203 137 7118\r\n\r\nUK Parliament Disclaimer: this e-mail is confidential to the intended recipient. If you have received it in error, please notify the sender and delete it from your system. Any unauthorised use, disclosure, or copying is not permitted. This e-mail has been checked for viruses, but no liability is accepted for any damage caused by any virus transmitted by this e-mail. This e-mail address is not secure, is not encrypted and should not be used for sensitive data.\r\n"},{"id":"wb-c3470a16","subject":"Fwd BP v Buckler 1987 great house farm 20","name":"2025-01-01_fwd-bp-vs-buckler-1987-great-house-farm-20_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2025-01-01_fwd-bp-vs-buckler-1987-great-house-farm-20_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"fwd-bp-v-buckler-1987-great-house-farm-20","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/2025-01-01_fwd-bp-vs-buckler-1987-great-house-farm-20_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"---------- Forwarded message ---------\r\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]>\r\nDate: Sun, 21 Dec 2025, 01:50\r\nSubject: BP vs Buckler 1987 - Great House Farm 2.0\r\nTo: <[redacted]>\r\nCc: <asociacionsandavidcr@gmail.com>, <[redacted]>, <\r\n[redacted]>, <informes@gaiman.gov.ar>, <\r\nproduccion@gaiman.gov.ar>, <cultura@gaiman.gov.ar>, <servicio@gaiman.gov.ar>,\r\n<turismo@dolavon.gob.ar>, <formacionturisticachubutur@gmail.com>\r\n\r\n\r\nSirs/Ma'am,\r\n\r\nI'm a Welsh Christian from Wales. Here in Puerto Madryn. My last employer\r\nwas the Welsh Government. Before that I had a career in the military.\r\n\r\nI grew up in an 800 year old farm near Cardiff in Wales. Called Great House\r\nFarm in Llandough, Cardiff. Which my family occupied for 400 years.\r\n\r\nWe lost possession of this farm to BP, in 1987. After they claimed they\r\nwere the rightful owners. They took possession through the courts, evicted\r\nus, then demolished our farm. My dads appeal was dismissed. His application\r\nto ECHR was blocked. And then he died.\r\n\r\nI am now in Puerto Madryn. I came here with a childhood dream, to rebuild\r\nmy family farm here. I want to construct an exact replica of my families\r\nfarm here. To honour my family.\r\n\r\nFurthermore Marconi was also staying with my family in Wales, at our old\r\nfarm, the day he invited radio. And a lot of Roman burials were exhevated\r\nfrom our family land and placed into museums, after it was demolished.\r\n\r\nWe are only now learning, from advances in AI, that our Welsh farm may have\r\nbeen stolen from us, through lawfare, fraud and concealment. A huge\r\ninjustice and a potentially huge lawsuit against BP and the British\r\nEstablishment, if this is true. It looks to be true.\r\n\r\nIf anyone here would like to help me establish in Argentina and achieve my\r\ngoals here, I would very much appreciate assistance. I am new here. I don't\r\nknow anyone here. I speak a little Spanish. Fluent Welsh. And English.\r\n\r\nI look forward to your replies,\r\n\r\nKind regards,\r\n\r\nSion Hywel Buckler\r\n[redacted]\r\n[redacted] (WhatsApp)\r\n"},{"id":"wb-5f05e401","subject":"Fwd fw BP v Buckler","name":"2025-01-01_fwd-fw-bp-vs-buckler_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2025-01-01_fwd-fw-bp-vs-buckler_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"fwd-fw-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/2025-01-01_fwd-fw-bp-vs-buckler_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"Hi,\r\n\r\nI have something below you maybe interested in.\r\n\r\nI used the latest AI models to unveil consealment and fraud and miscarriage\r\nof just. Which led my family to be forced off our 800 year old farm in the\r\nlate 80's.\r\n\r\nThis means I can re open this case and sue. And I want to. So does my\r\nfamily. The latest AI estimate some £40M in damages for my family if what\r\nI've unveiled can be won in a court of law.\r\n\r\nRegards,\r\n\r\nSion Buckler\r\n[redacted]\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n---------- Forwarded message ---------\r\nFrom: NARAYAN, Kanishka (MP) <kanishka.narayan.mp@parliament.uk>\r\nDate: Mon, 17 Nov 2025, 06:59\r\nSubject: FW: BP vs Buckler\r\nTo: [redacted] <[redacted]>\r\n\r\n\r\nDear Soin,\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nThank you for your email to Kanishka Narayan MP and for passing on your\r\nconcerns. [location redacted] unfortunately does not fall within the Vale of\r\nGlamorgan Parliamentary Constituency and Parliamentary protocol states that\r\nKanishka is unable to assist anyone outside his constituency.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nI would like to recommend contacting your MP, Stephen Doughty. His contact\r\ndetails are:\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nPhone Number: 020 7219 5348\r\n\r\nEmail Address: stephen.doughty.mp@parliament.uk\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nI hope they are able to assist you.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nKind regards,\r\nMadelene\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n*Madelene Larsson Constituency Support Manager, Kanishka Narayan MP*\r\n\r\nMember of Parliament for the Vale of Glamorgan\r\nParliamentary Under-Secretary of State in the Department for Science,\r\nInnovation and Technology\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nUK Parliament Disclaimer: this e-mail is confidential to the intended\r\nrecipient. If you have received it in error, please notify the sender and\r\ndelete it from your system. Any unauthorised use, disclosure, or copying is\r\nnot permitted. This e-mail has been checked for viruses, but no liability\r\nis accepted for any damage caused by any virus transmitted by this e-mail.\r\nThis e-mail address is not secure, is not encrypted and should not be used\r\nfor sensitive data.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n*From:* Requester <[redacted]>\r\n*Sent:* 14 November 2025 12:49\r\n*To:* NARAYAN, Kanishka (MP) <kanishka.narayan.mp@parliament.uk>\r\n*Subject:* BP vs Buckler\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nSir/Ma'am,\r\n\r\nI am your constituent, living [location redacted]. I am writing to request your\r\nassistance in reviewing a historic legal matter involving my family: *BP\r\nProperties Ltd v Buckler (1987)*, relating to Great House Farm, Llandough.\r\n\r\nMy family lived on, cared for and claimed to own that farm for generations.\r\nThe events surrounding the 1987 case resulted in the loss of our home and\r\nland, and left several members of my family deeply affected. Recent\r\nresearch I have undertaken has revealed significant procedural\r\ninconsistencies, historical irregularities, unanswered questions regarding\r\ncorporate identity, unverified service of key documents, and heritage\r\nconsiderations relating to the site.\r\n\r\nI have attached a short report summarising SOME of these issues. I am\r\nrequesting your help in identifying the appropriate bodies—legal,\r\nhistorical, or governmental—who may be able to independently review the\r\nhistorical record to ensure fairness, accuracy, and transparency.\r\n\r\nMy interest is not solely personal; Great House Farm has a heritage\r\nsignificance to Wales, including documented accounts that Guglielmo Marconi\r\nstayed there while experimenting with early radio technology. I believe\r\nthis should form part of our preserved historical record.\r\n\r\nBy way of background, I am a veteran and have served both in the Armed\r\nForces and recently with the Vale of Glamorgan Council. My intention is\r\nsimply to seek clarity, proper review, and justice where appropriate. I\r\nwould be grateful for your guidance on the correct avenues to pursue.\r\n\r\nThank you for your time and consideration. I look forward to your response.\r\n\r\nYours sincerely,\r\n\r\n\r\n*Requester*\r\n[redacted]\r\n[map link redacted]\r\n[location redacted]\r\n[map link redacted]\r\n[redacted]\r\n[map link redacted]\r\nTel: 0203 137 7118\r\nUK Parliament Disclaimer: this e-mail is confidential to the intended\r\nrecipient. If you have received it in error, please notify the sender and\r\ndelete it from your system. Any unauthorised use, disclosure, or copying is\r\nnot permitted. This e-mail has been checked for viruses, but no liability\r\nis accepted for any damage caused by any virus transmitted by this e-mail.\r\nThis e-mail address is not secure, is not encrypted and should not be used\r\nfor sensitive data.\r\n"},{"id":"wb-6b4256c3","subject":"Fwd rc25 0464 information related to great house farm llando","name":"2025-01-01_fwd-rc25-0464-information-related-to-great-house-farm-llando_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2025-01-01_fwd-rc25-0464-information-related-to-great-house-farm-llando_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"fwd-rc25-0464-information-related-to-great-house-farm-llando","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/2025-01-01_fwd-rc25-0464-information-related-to-great-house-farm-llando_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"---------- Forwarded message ---------\r\nFrom: Dr Adam Coward <adam.coward@rcahmw.gov.uk>\r\nDate: Wed, 26 Nov 2025, 09:48\r\nSubject: RC25-0464 - Information related to Great House Farm, Llandough\r\nTo: Requester <[redacted]>\r\n\r\n\r\nGood afternoon,\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nThank you very much for your enquiry. While we cannot advise you on a legal\r\ncase or undertake a review of historical decisions, pleas note that we do\r\nhold information and archives related to Great House Farm, Llandough.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nThe site entry and description for the site in the National Monuments\r\nRecord of Wales can be seen online on Coflein, here:\r\nhttps://coflein.gov.uk/en/site/20182.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nWe hold 14 archives related to the site, a list of which can be seen here:\r\nhttps://coflein.gov.uk/en/site/20182/archives?size=25. These archives can\r\nbe viewed in our Search Room, located in the National Library of Wales,\r\nwhich is open Tuesday through Thursday, 9:30-16:30. If you would like to\r\nview the material in person, I would advise arranging with us beforehand so\r\nthat we can prepare the material ahead of your visit. Some of the material\r\nis on microfiche and as we do not have a microfiche reader in the Search\r\nRoom, we will need to liaise with the National Library Reading Room staff\r\nto try to negotiate the use of their machines ahead of your visit.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nWhile it is possible to order scans of material using the attached order\r\nform, we may be unable to provide research-quality scans or photographs of\r\nsome of the material, such as the material on microfiche, which would\r\nrequire high-resolution scanning at a cost of £20 per scan. Moreover,  many\r\nof these records remain under third-party copyright, and we would only be\r\nable to provide scans or copies for personal, non-commercial research.\r\nOther uses, such as use in a legal case or report would likely require the\r\npermission of the copyright holder(s). I would therefore advise arranging\r\nto view the material in person rather than ordering scans, if possible.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nIn addition to the information and records which we hold, there are several\r\nrelated records in the Historic Environment Record maintained by Heneb,\r\nwhich related to excavations, mostly concerning the cemetery/burial site:\r\n\r\n   -\r\n   https://archwilio.org.uk/arch/query/page.php?watprn=GGAT03584s&dbname=ggat&tbname=core\r\n   -\r\n   https://archwilio.org.uk/arch/query/page.php?watprn=GGAT03583s&dbname=ggat&tbname=core\r\n   -\r\n   https://archwilio.org.uk/arch/query/page.php?watprn=GGAT03586s&dbname=ggat&tbname=core\r\n   -\r\n   https://archwilio.org.uk/arch/query/page.php?watprn=GGAT03585s&dbname=ggat&tbname=core\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nThank you very much again for your enquiry.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nSincerely,\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nAdam Coward\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nDr Adam Coward\r\n Rheolwr Llyfrgell ac Ymholiadau | Library and Enquiries Manager\r\nFfordd Penglais | Penglais Road,  Aberystwyth,  SY23 3BU\r\n+44 (0) 1970 621 216 <+44%20(0)%201970%20621%20216>\r\nadam.coward@cbhc.gov.uk  |  adam.coward@rcahmw.gov.uk\r\nwww.cbhc.gov.uk <https://cbhc.gov.uk/>  |  www.rcahmw.gov.uk\r\n<https://rcahmw.gov.uk/>\r\nNoddir gan Lywodraeth Cymru  |  Sponsored by Welsh Government\r\n[image: Facebook]\r\n<https://www.facebook.com/Royal-Commission-on-the-Ancient-and-Historical-Monuments-of-Wales-146120328739808>\r\n[image: Twitter] <https://www.twitter.com/rcahmwales>\r\nRydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg a Saesneg. Ni fydd gohebu yn\r\nGymraeg yn peri oedi.\r\nWe welcome correspondence in Welsh and English. Corresponding in Welsh\r\nwill not lead to any delay.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nYn unol â Rheoliadau Safonau'r Gymraeg (Rhif 2) 2016, mae gennych hawl i\r\ngyfathrebu a gohebu â Chomisiwn Brenhinol Henebion Cymru yn eich dewis\r\niaith. Er mwyn sicrhau ein bod yn cyflawni’r hawl, rhowch wybod i ni a\r\nydych yn dymuno derbyn gohebiaeth a/neu alwadau ffôn oddi wrthym yn y\r\nGymraeg. Bydd yr wybodaeth hon yn cael ei chofnodi gennym ni, a byddwn yn\r\ndefnyddio'r iaith o’ch dewis ym mhob cyfathrebu yn y dyfodol. Diolch.\r\n​​​​\r\nUnder the Welsh Language Standards (No. 2) Regulations 2016, you have the\r\nright to communicate and correspond with the Royal Commission on the\r\nAncient and Historical Monuments of Wales in your preferred language. To\r\nensure we uphold this right, please let us know whether you wish to receive\r\ncorrespondence and/or telephone calls from us in Welsh. This information\r\nwill be recorded by us, and we will use your preferred language in all\r\nfuture communication. Thank you.\r\n​\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n*From:* Requester <[redacted]>\r\n*Sent:* 14 November 2025 12:49\r\n*To:* kanishka.narayan.mp@parliament.uk\r\n*Subject:* BP vs Buckler\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nYou don't often get email from [redacted]. Learn why this is\r\nimportant <https://aka.ms/LearnAboutSenderIdentification>\r\n\r\nSir/Ma'am,\r\n\r\nI am your constituent, living [location redacted]. I am writing to request your\r\nassistance in reviewing a historic legal matter involving my family: *BP\r\nProperties Ltd v Buckler (1987)*, relating to Great House Farm, Llandough.\r\n\r\nMy family lived on, cared for and claimed to own that farm for generations.\r\nThe events surrounding the 1987 case resulted in the loss of our home and\r\nland, and left several members of my family deeply affected. Recent\r\nresearch I have undertaken has revealed significant procedural\r\ninconsistencies, historical irregularities, unanswered questions regarding\r\ncorporate identity, unverified service of key documents, and heritage\r\nconsiderations relating to the site.\r\n\r\nI have attached a short report summarising SOME of these issues. I am\r\nrequesting your help in identifying the appropriate bodies—legal,\r\nhistorical, or governmental—who may be able to independently review the\r\nhistorical record to ensure fairness, accuracy, and transparency.\r\n\r\nMy interest is not solely personal; Great House Farm has a heritage\r\nsignificance to Wales, including documented accounts that Guglielmo Marconi\r\nstayed there while experimenting with early radio technology. I believe\r\nthis should form part of our preserved historical record.\r\n\r\nBy way of background, I am a veteran and have served both in the Armed\r\nForces and recently with the Vale of Glamorgan Council. My intention is\r\nsimply to seek clarity, proper review, and justice where appropriate. I\r\nwould be grateful for your guidance on the correct avenues to pursue.\r\n\r\nThank you for your time and consideration. I look forward to your response.\r\n\r\nYours sincerely,\r\n\r\n\r\n*Requester*\r\n[redacted]\r\n[map link redacted]\r\n[location redacted]\r\n[map link redacted]\r\n[redacted]\r\n[map link redacted]\r\nTel: 0203 137 7118\r\n"},{"id":"wb-89424bc3","subject":"Re BP v Buckler 1987 documentary","name":"2025-01-01_re-bp-vs-buckler-1987-documentary_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2025-01-01_re-bp-vs-buckler-1987-documentary_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"bp-v-buckler-1987-documentary","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/2025-01-01_re-bp-vs-buckler-1987-documentary_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"Hi Requester\r\n\r\nHope you have a nice day,\r\n\r\nThank you for your proposal.\r\n\r\nWe do not accept unsolicited ideas due to legal reasons; however, I have passed your details onto our Development Team who will contact you with any interest.\r\n\r\nIf you have any queries in the meantime, please do not hesitate to contact me\r\n\r\nBest wishes\r\n\r\n\r\nVickie Lawrence\r\nOffice Manager\r\ntel.        +44 (0)20 7033 2300\r\nmob.     +44 (0) [redacted]\r\nloc.       Zetland House Unit H\r\n            109 Clifton Street London  EC2A 4LD\r\n\r\n[A picture containing logo  Description automatically generated]\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]>\r\nSent: Tuesday, October 28, 2025 11:05 PM\r\nTo: hello@docsociety.org; industry@sheffdocfest.com; info@sheffdocfest.com; dfp@sundance.org; Institute@sundance.org; berthachallenge@berthafoundation.org; storyville@bbc.co.uk; dispatches@channel4.co.uk; wales@itv.com; yourstory@itv.com; Dragonfly Info <info@dragonfly.tv>; enquiries@twentytwenty.tv; contact@cardiffproductions.co.uk\r\nSubject: BP vs Buckler 1987 - Documentary\r\n\r\nHi,\r\n\r\nI'm exploring doing a documentary about legal case BP Vs Buckler 1987.\r\n\r\nI'm the son of the defendant, my late dad William Buckler.\r\n\r\nMy last employer and source of income, for my last 22 years in Oath, was the Vale of Glamorgan council.\r\n\r\nI have a UK company and would like to make a documentary about this case. And I'm now seeking co-financeers for this project.\r\n\r\nPlease email/ call me if you have interest in this project.\r\n\r\nThank you.\r\n\r\nSion Buckler\r\nDirector\r\nDATRO Consortium Ltd\r\n[redacted]\r\n\r\nWant to know more about Banijay UK and its productions? Visit www.banijayuk.com.<https://banijayuk.com/>\r\nAny views or opinions expressed in this e-mail are solely those of the author and do not necessarily represent those of Banijay UK unless specifically stated.\r\nThis email and the information it contains are confidential and intended solely for the use of the individual or entity to which it is addressed. If you have received this email in error please notify us immediately and delete the copy you have received from your system.\r\nYou should not copy it for any purpose, re-transmit it, use it or disclose its contents to any other person. If you suspect the message may have been intercepted or amended please call the sender.\r\n\r\nIf you have sent a CV, Banijay UK may use this CV to consider you for any relevant work opportunities within the Banijay UK Group. See our Recruitment Privacy Notice<https://banijayuk.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/05/BUK-Recruitment-Privacy-Notice-Final.pdf> for more details\r\n\r\nTo make sure we are inclusive and accessible to all, please let us know if you require any reasonable adjustments or have any access/emergency evacuation needs\r\n"},{"id":"wb-65ca727f","subject":"Re BP v Buckler","name":"2025-01-01_re-bp-vs-buckler_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2025-01-01_re-bp-vs-buckler_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/2025-01-01_re-bp-vs-buckler_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"Hi Requester\r\n\r\nThank you for copying me in on this.\r\n\r\nIf you'd like to discuss, please let me know.\r\n\r\nAll the best,\r\n\r\nGeorge\r\n________________________________\r\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]>\r\nSent: Friday, November 14, 2025 12:48:36 PM\r\nTo: kanishka.narayan.mp@parliament.uk <kanishka.narayan.mp@parliament.uk>\r\nSubject: BP vs Buckler\r\n\r\nYou don't often get email from [redacted]. Learn why this is important<https://aka.ms/LearnAboutSenderIdentification>\r\n\r\nSir/Ma'am,\r\n\r\nI am your constituent, living [location redacted]. I am writing to request your assistance in reviewing a historic legal matter involving my family: BP Properties Ltd v Buckler (1987), relating to Great House Farm, Llandough.\r\n\r\nMy family lived on, cared for and claimed to own that farm for generations. The events surrounding the 1987 case resulted in the loss of our home and land, and left several members of my family deeply affected. Recent research I have undertaken has revealed significant procedural inconsistencies, historical irregularities, unanswered questions regarding corporate identity, unverified service of key documents, and heritage considerations relating to the site.\r\n\r\nI have attached a short report summarising SOME of these issues. I am requesting your help in identifying the appropriate bodies—legal, historical, or governmental—who may be able to independently review the historical record to ensure fairness, accuracy, and transparency.\r\n\r\nMy interest is not solely personal; Great House Farm has a heritage significance to Wales, including documented accounts that Guglielmo Marconi stayed there while experimenting with early radio technology. I believe this should form part of our preserved historical record.\r\n\r\nBy way of background, I am a veteran and have served both in the Armed Forces and recently with the Vale of Glamorgan Council. My intention is simply to seek clarity, proper review, and justice where appropriate. I would be grateful for your guidance on the correct avenues to pursue.\r\n\r\nThank you for your time and consideration. I look forward to your response.\r\n\r\nYours sincerely,\r\n\r\nSion Buckler\r\n[redacted]\r\nTel: 0203 137 7118\r\n"},{"id":"wb-df8afa11","subject":"Re BP v Buckler","name":"2025-01-01_re-bp-vs-buckler_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2025-01-01_re_%20bp%20vs%20buckler_19aa03599a014706.txt","threadSlug":"bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/2025-01-01_re_ bp vs buckler_19aa03599a014706.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"Hi Requester,\r\n\r\n \r\n\r\nI regret to inform you that we are unable to assist with the matter you raise.\r\n\r\n \r\n\r\nRegards,\r\n\r\n \r\n\r\nPaul\r\n\r\n \r\n\r\nPaul R. Egan BA, Chartered MCIPD, CiLCA, F. Inst. LM, FIPSM\r\n\r\nClerk to Llandough Community Council,\r\n\r\n63 Woodham Park,\r\n\r\nBarry,\r\n\r\nVale of Glamorgan CF62 8FJ\r\n\r\nTelephone 01446 409294\r\n\r\nWebsite www.llandough-cc.co.uk <http://www.llandough-cc.co.uk/> \r\n\r\n \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nWhen you contact us\r\n\r\nThe information you provide (personal information such as name, address, email address, phone number, organisation) will be processed and stored to enable us to contact you and respond to your correspondence, provide information and/or access our facilities and services. Your personal information will be not shared or provided to any other third party. Your information may however be published in the public domain if you require the Council to discuss the matter raised. \r\n\r\n \r\n\r\nFor further information please see our full privacy notice on our website www.llandough-cc.co.uk <http://www.llandough-cc.co.uk/> \r\n\r\n \r\n\r\nThe Council's mission statement is 'Serving our community, preserving our identity' The Council will always strive to:\r\n\r\n ·  Be open and accessible in all our decision making \r\n\r\n· Be accountable in all our council activities\r\n\r\n· Be responsive to the needs of the community\r\n\r\n· Encourage community participation\r\n\r\n \r\n\r\n \r\n\r\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]> \r\nSent: 14 November 2025 12:49\r\nTo: kanishka.narayan.mp@parliament.uk\r\nSubject: BP vs Buckler\r\n\r\n \r\n\r\nSir/Ma'am,\r\n\r\nI am your constituent, living [location redacted]. I am writing to request your assistance in reviewing a historic legal matter involving my family: BP Properties Ltd v Buckler (1987), relating to Great House Farm, Llandough.\r\n\r\nMy family lived on, cared for and claimed to own that farm for generations. The events surrounding the 1987 case resulted in the loss of our home and land, and left several members of my family deeply affected. Recent research I have undertaken has revealed significant procedural inconsistencies, historical irregularities, unanswered questions regarding corporate identity, unverified service of key documents, and heritage considerations relating to the site.\r\n\r\nI have attached a short report summarising SOME of these issues. I am requesting your help in identifying the appropriate bodies—legal, historical, or governmental—who may be able to independently review the historical record to ensure fairness, accuracy, and transparency.\r\n\r\nMy interest is not solely personal; Great House Farm has a heritage significance to Wales, including documented accounts that Guglielmo Marconi stayed there while experimenting with early radio technology. I believe this should form part of our preserved historical record.\r\n\r\nBy way of background, I am a veteran and have served both in the Armed Forces and recently with the Vale of Glamorgan Council. My intention is simply to seek clarity, proper review, and justice where appropriate. I would be grateful for your guidance on the correct avenues to pursue.\r\n\r\nThank you for your time and consideration. I look forward to your response.\r\n\r\nYours sincerely,\r\n\r\n\r\nSion Buckler\r\n[redacted]\r\nTel: 0203 137 7118\r\n\r\n"},{"id":"wb-1b47bbf8","subject":"Consortium correspondence BP v Buckler","name":"2025-06-25_consortium-correspondence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2025-06-25","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2025-06-25_consortium-correspondence_bpvsbuckler__en_v0-0-1.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-correspondence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2025-06-25_consortium-correspondence_bpvsbuckler__en_v0-0-1.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Wed, 25 Jun 2025 01:28:09 -0300\nSubject: See Attached : Whisleblow A/Sgt Buckler\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V5hu9SWATRV=bAymUJs4ETDZvVJF0wgKJhxOib9yGUeYg@mail.gmail.com>\n\nSirs,\n\nI believe the UK Government is a Terrorist Organization. Not just as\nof late, but has been for at least for 4 decades.\nI believe there's genocide/ hollacost occuring on us Christians,\nAnglo-Saxon British Nationals and our Freedom & Soverignty).\nI have personal accounts of this, with the loss of my land (to BP),\nhome (to Manchester Building Society), child (to Sadists & the State)\nand attempts on my liberty and life (by the Sadists & State).\n\nI also served in Iraq. See attached my affidavit.\nSupporting evidence can be supplied on demand.\n\nRegards,\n\nSion Buckler\n[redacted]\nX.com: @in_visible_ww\nTikTok: @uncle_howell\n\nDocument Updates:\nhttps://docs.google.com/document/d/e/2PACX-1vRZTwzZSyuLsfN2N88eBEZO-RakJaK9i7b30gkF2t1lD7nqumq2kmkL1BBbwZc-Bj68F7Wp0FYhC5zv/pub\n"},{"id":"wb-48bc3c48","subject":"Consortium correspondence BP v Buckler","name":"2025-07-26_consortium-correspondence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2025-07-26","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2025-07-26_consortium-correspondence_bpvsbuckler__en_v0-0-1.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-correspondence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2025-07-26_consortium-correspondence_bpvsbuckler__en_v0-0-1.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Sat, 26 Jul 2025 11:32:42 -0300\nSubject: Re: Grok 4 assesment of my tech\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V6PH7+EoDZnmzYrvKWHnM9gb-M6BdskSCc=PnDXp7HwOQ@mail.gmail.com>\n\nI know you had that with your back, you said weeks ago it was ahead.\nHope it goes well.\n\nDon't write off what I'm saying as crazy though, not about us being in a war.\nThe fact my sim and whatsapp got blocked when I said this, isn't a coincidence.\n\nMy nan's new hips poisoned her blood and vaccines gave her Guillain Barre.\nAnd my dads demise at 40 and even my grandad's at age 55, wasn't all\nmisfortunate. We're in a war.\nThe warfare being used is 5th Generation.\n\nOur heritage and connection to our birthlands make us targets.\nBeing simultaneously White & Anglo more so.\nI wouldn't write off the theft of your quad bike as random.\nI wouldn't write off the police's failure to solve the crime as random either.\n\nI think I can get the money needed to prove my farm case now.\nGrok 4 agrees. It would be a worthwhile investment/ return.\nAnd I think a win on that would go a huge way towards proving the even\nbigger picture I'm talking about.\nAnother reason why we need the comms tech I'm developing.\n\nI think the St Michaels Lay Line will be a kind of Cyprus Green Line.\nI think everything south of it will become Aluya (Islamic Nation).\nSo we have Celt, Anglo,Saxon and now Arab. Wales/N.Ireland/Scotland,\nEngland & Aluya.\nI think the argument this Welsh Settlement founder (Michael Jones)\nmade, in Wales in 1879 (about the \"Decapitation Commission\")) has\nfruitioned.\nThe DEI, Equality Act and SDG is both well intended but also subverted\nto become a paper shield and trojan horse for a depopulation agenda.\n\nMy dads farm, my Welsh (National) Grandads death, nans death (both\nPlaid Cymru), Son situation all makes sense now.\nWe're in war.\n\nI really think it's time ex and off-duty armed forces dust off uniforms,\nattend funerals, and salute any more unexplainable and untimely deaths\n- as civilian casualties of 5th Gen Warfare and this War.\nI don't think it will hurt to do it. But I imagine it'll be a huge\nregret if we don't.\n\nThe St Michael's Lay Line could be the best play to roll out our\nnetwork in Britain first.\nBecause I think the UN would buy it up, or we lease it to them, when\nthis war becomes less hybrid.\nAnd it's evidently becoming less and less cloak/ dagger and silence\nand violence. It will escalate and cruiciendo.\n\nHywel\n\n\nOn Sat, Jul 26, 2025 at 9:05 AM Peter Caines <[redacted]> wrote:\n>\n> Doing okay, I think.\n>\n> I did have four spinal injections on Monday... That stung a little...\n>\n> Has made things a little better, which means more procedures/surgery... which is eerrrr nice...?\n>\n> You disappeared from Whatapp?\n>\n> Kind Regards\n>\n>\n> Peter Caines\n>\n> 0770 9490323\n>\n> ________________________________\n> From: Requester <[redacted]>\n> Sent: 25 July 2025 23:19\n> To: Peter Caines <[redacted]>\n> Subject: Re: Grok 4 assesment of my tech\n>\n> Glad I'm here not UK is what I am.\n> How are you & Lou?\n>\n> Hywel\n>\n> On Fri, 25 Jul 2025, 13:26 Peter Caines, <[redacted]> wrote:\n>\n> How are you getting on?\n>\n>\n>\n> Kind Regards\n>\n>\n> Peter Caines\n>\n> 0770 9490323\n>\n> ________________________________\n> From: Requester <[redacted]>\n> Sent: 15 July 2025 19:30\n> To: Eric Lillibridge <[redacted]>\n> Subject: Grok 4 assesment of my tech\n>\n"},{"id":"wb-9eefb648","subject":"Consortium evidence BP v Buckler","name":"2025-10-05_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2025-10-05","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2025-10-05_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-evidence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2025-10-05_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Sun, 5 Oct 2025 01:50:01 -0300\nSubject: Great House Farm, Llandough\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V4R9_BPnmsiJWMAVDO6Xwca6qZpQ1tBey39Om42LOdL9w@mail.gmail.com>\n\nSirs/Ma'am,\n\nI am seeking representation and initial advice regarding the 1987 case BP\nProperties Ltd v Buckler, which concerned Great House Farm, Llandough —\nproperty long occupied by my family. I believe, among a myriad of\narguments, that the judgment was reached through a procedural inconsistency\namounting to bias or error in law, which may justify reopening under CPR\n52.30 and Limitations Act Section 32.\n\n\n---\n\nSummary of the issue\n\nIn the 1987 case, the court ruled against my grandmother on the basis that\nshe did not formally reject an offer made by BP Properties Ltd, and treated\nthat as tacit acceptance. However, she had already explicitly rejected\nprior licence attempts by BP Pension Trust Ltd, a related BP entity, before\nBP Properties Ltd appeared as claimant.\n\nThe judge ruled that BP Properties Ltd was a distinct legal entity, and\ntherefore her prior rejection of BP Pension Trust Ltd’s offer was legally\nirrelevant.\n\nLater, in my father’s appeal, he argued that the wrong BP company had been\nthe claimant — that BP Pension Trust Ltd, not BP Properties Ltd, held the\nrelevant interest.\nThe appellate court dismissed his appeal, ruling that “BP companies are all\neffectively one and the same” for the purposes of upholding the judgment.\n\nThis is a clear double standard:\n\nWhen it benefited BP, the court treated the companies as separate.\n\nWhen it benefited our family, they were treated as the same entity.\n\n\nThis contradictory reasoning — “heads BP wins, tails the Bucklers lose” —\nrepresents either judicial bias, procedural irregularity, or corruption by\nomission, and undermines the fairness of the original proceedings.\n\n\n---\n\nGrounds for reopening\n\nUnder CPR 52.30, a final judgment can be reopened if necessary to avoid\nreal injustice, in exceptional circumstances, where no alternative remedy\nexists.\nMy argument is that:\n\nThe 1987 decision and subsequent appeal were tainted by inconsistent\napplication of corporate identity principles, creating real injustice.\n\nThe appeal court’s own language evidences bias and unequal treatment\nbetween parties.\n\nThis meets the “exceptional circumstances” threshold.\n\n\nAdditionally, the Supreme Court ruling in Takhar v Gracefield Developments\nLtd [2019] UKSC 13 confirms that a judgment obtained through fraud or\nconcealment can be set aside even decades later — “fraud unravels all.”\n\n\n---\n\nLimitation Act 1980 – Section 32\n\nPursuant to s.32(1)(b):\n\n> “Where any fact relevant to the plaintiff’s right of action has been\ndeliberately concealed by the defendant, the period of limitation shall not\nbegin to run until the plaintiff has discovered the concealment or could\nwith reasonable diligence have discovered it.”\n\n\n\nI have only recently obtained and cross-referenced records revealing these\ncontradictions in corporate treatment and procedural handling — thus,\nlimitation does not bar action.\n\n\n---\n\nRequested scope of representation\n\nAssess the viability of a CPR 52.30 petition to reopen or set aside BP v\nBuckler (1987).\n\nReview the documentary record (Land Registry files, local authority\narchives, excavation permissions, HTV Wales coverage, etc.).\n\nIf viable, initiate a claim for rectification of title and potential\ncultural property recovery of artefacts excavated during BP’s occupation of\nthe disputed land.\n\n\n\n---\n\nAttachments available upon request\n\nLand Registry and microfiche title extracts (pre- and post-1987)\n\nLocal authority and archaeological licensing records (1985–1988)\n\nMedia and correspondence evidence of procedural bias and conflict\n\nChronology of BP company interactions with the Buckler family\n\n\n\n---\n\nPlease confirm conflicts and whether one of your dispute resolution\npartners can offer an initial consultation. I am prepared to provide a full\nevidentiary bundle immediately upon engagement.\n\nThank you for your time and consideration.\n\nKind regards,\n\nSion Buckler\n[redacted]\n"},{"id":"wb-2d41912b","subject":"Consortium evidence BP v Buckler","name":"2025-10-05_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","date":"2025-10-05","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2025-10-05_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-evidence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2025-10-05_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Sun, 5 Oct 2025 19:23:23 -0300\nSubject: Re: Great House Farm, Llandough\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V6L1PkUJ5SrkQLULRSyMuCWfmvGiu-PdsTRcrNM=qst3Q@mail.gmail.com>\n\nThanks Tom.\n\nI think plain & simply the State did a\n\"Heads I win. Tails you lose\".\n\nWhat about the new Hillsborough Law ?\n\nSion\n\nOn Sun, 5 Oct 2025, 18:23 The Editor, <editor@herald.email> wrote:\n\n> Hi Requester,\n>\n> Thank you for your detailed follow-up and for sharing more about your\n> background and the historical context surrounding Great House Farm. I can\n> see how deeply this case matters to you and your family, and it’s clear\n> that you’ve invested considerable time and research into gathering evidence\n> and preserving your family’s story.\n>\n> While developments in AI and digital archives have certainly made it\n> easier to uncover historic material and cross-reference records, the legal\n> framework for reopening a judgment remains essentially unchanged. Courts\n> still apply the same very strict criteria under CPR 52.30 and the\n> Limitation Act 1980, and they would require evidence of fraud, deliberate\n> concealment, or procedural impropriety at the time of the original\n> proceedings — not just new access to historical material or\n> reinterpretation through technology.\n>\n> Even with emerging evidence, after nearly forty years the doctrine of\n> finality in litigation makes it exceptionally difficult to revisit property\n> cases that have long been settled. The courts are unlikely to reopen such a\n> matter unless the new evidence conclusively proves that the original\n> judgment was obtained through deception or corruption.\n>\n> That said, the historical and human side of your family’s story —\n> including the Marconi connection and the loss of the farm — is genuinely\n> fascinating. It may have cultural or documentary value even if it has no\n> remaining legal remedy. If you do decide to pursue a documentary or\n> historical feature, I’d be happy to keep in touch and follow any\n> developments from a reporting standpoint.\n>\n> Thank you again for writing, and I wish you every success in your efforts\n> to document and preserve this important piece of Welsh history.\n>\n> Best regards,\n> Tom Sinclair\n>\n> On Sun, 5 Oct 2025 at 21:30, Requester <[redacted]>\n> wrote:\n>\n>> Tom,\n>>\n>> While I agree with everything you've said there's more.\n>>\n>> AI now exists. We've not had it before.\n>> Which is what is unveiling new evidence.\n>>\n>> There exist evidence still declassified including the exhivate of the\n>> land after our farm was possessed and bulldozed down. There's TV media\n>> coverage still not online. And newspaper achieves behind pay walls. Which\n>> have also only become available. I also have heirlooms with evidence not\n>> online.\n>>\n>> All making this qualify for the limitation act clause.\n>>\n>> My family have known our position on this matter. As you can see\n>> throughout the 87 case, my nan maintained we owned the farm.\n>>\n>> I would not waist anyone's time if I didn't think this wasn't worth\n>> pursuing. I've collated evidence over the years.\n>>\n>> I'm not just the son of William Buckler. I'm a former military cyber\n>> warfare professional, with AI at my fingertips. I would be as much an asset\n>> to a lawyer on this case. I'm also bona fide. I just finished 22 years in\n>> Oath and my last 2 years of employment were under the Welsh Government.\n>>\n>> The latest AI (Grok 4) estimates the case could cost £4M and yield £75M.\n>> Making it a viable endeavour in my estimation.\n>>\n>> Im currently at the Welsh Settlement in Argentina trying to rebuild a\n>> replica of the farm here.\n>>\n>> I would also add, if I didn't already. Marconi was staying with us at our\n>> farm when he invented radio. Literally the first working one. My grandad\n>> took him by horse and cart with his equipment to perform his experiments up\n>> until the day of the first success at Laverock point Penarth. Do you know,\n>> at the housing estate they built over our farm, there's not even a plaque\n>> to commemorate Marconi?\n>>\n>> This would not just be a legal victory. It would be a moral and\n>> historical victory.\n>>\n>> Also the TV footage shows how BP backed off and changed tact, once the\n>> press caught wind that they were bullying my nan. An old lady in a\n>> wheelchair.\n>>\n>> I suspect this will be on Netflix once we're done. And I'm sure Netflix\n>> want story like this right now.\n>>\n>> No matter what angle I look at this from, I see good vibes, triumph &\n>> fortune.\n>>\n>> My mum is still alive and has a law degree. Another asset.\n>>\n>> I could go on.\n>>\n>> Regards,\n>>\n>> Requester\n>> [redacted]\n>>\n>>\n>>\n>>\n>>\n>> On Sun, 5 Oct 2025, 16:59 The Editor, <editor@herald.email> wrote:\n>>\n>>> Dear Requester,\n>>>\n>>> Thank you for getting in touch and for sharing such a detailed account\n>>> of your family’s case concerning Great House Farm, Llandough. I’ve read\n>>> through your summary and understand the points you raise about the way the\n>>> courts treated BP’s different corporate entities.\n>>>\n>>> From a legal perspective, however, it appears extremely unlikely that a\n>>> case dating back to 1987 could now be reopened. The rule you refer to — CPR\n>>> 52.30 — only applies in truly exceptional circumstances, such as where\n>>> there is clear evidence of fraud, corruption, or a fundamental procedural\n>>> defect that undermined the fairness of the original hearing. A disagreement\n>>> over how the law was applied, or inconsistencies in reasoning, would not\n>>> usually be enough to meet that standard.\n>>>\n>>> The other difficulty is the time that has passed. Nearly forty years on,\n>>> the courts place very strong emphasis on finality of litigation,\n>>> particularly in property cases. Land ownership and related interests will\n>>> almost certainly have changed hands since then, and the law is reluctant to\n>>> disturb settled titles unless there is absolute proof of fraud or\n>>> concealment.\n>>>\n>>> While section 32 of the Limitation Act 1980 allows some exceptions where\n>>> facts were deliberately hidden, this applies only if there was intentional\n>>> concealment by a party, not simply later discovery of information that\n>>> might cast doubt on the judgment.\n>>>\n>>> For those reasons, any attempt to reopen the 1987 decision or pursue\n>>> rectification of title would almost certainly be refused by the courts.\n>>>\n>>> I appreciate the time and research you’ve put into this and the\n>>> historical importance it holds for your family, but in practical terms the\n>>> matter is consi