nd.\n>>>>\n>>>>\n>>>>\n>>>> ---\n>>>>\n>>>> Attachments available upon request\n>>>>\n>>>> Land Registry and microfiche title extracts (pre- and post-1987)\n>>>>\n>>>> Local authority and archaeological licensing records (1985–1988)\n>>>>\n>>>> Media and correspondence evidence of procedural bias and conflict\n>>>>\n>>>> Chronology of BP company interactions with the Buckler family\n>>>>\n>>>>\n>>>>\n>>>> ---\n>>>>\n>>>> Please confirm conflicts and whether one of your dispute resolution\n>>>> partners can offer an initial consultation. I am prepared to provide a full\n>>>> evidentiary bundle immediately upon engagement.\n>>>>\n>>>> Thank you for your time and consideration.\n>>>>\n>>>> Kind regards,\n>>>>\n>>>> Requester\n>>>> [redacted]\n>>>>\n>>>>\n>>>>\n>>>>\n>>>\n>>> --\n>>>\n>>>\n>>>\n>>> *Tom Sinclair*Editor | www.herald.wales\n>>>\n>>> T: 01646 454545 ext 202\n>>>\n>>> E: editor@herald.email\n>>>\n>>> A: Carmarthenshire Herald Ltd t/a Herald.Wales, 11 Hamilton Terrace,\n>>> Milford Haven, Pembrokeshire, SA73 3AL\n>>> <https://www.google.com/maps/search/11+Hamilton+Terrace,+Milford+Haven,+Pembrokeshire,+SA73+3AL?entry=gmail&source=g>\n>>>\n>>> [image: facebook icon] <https://www.facebook.com/Herald.Wales>  [image:\n>>> twitter icon] <https://twitter.com/HeraldWales>  [image: instagram icon]\n>>> <https://www.instagram.com/herald.wales/>\n>>> [image: Banner] <https://www.herald.wales/>\n>>>\n>>> Herald.Wales and The Pembrokeshire Herald titles are regulated by the\n>>> Independent Press Standards Organisation Member of BBC Local Democracy News\n>>> Service & BBC News Hub. The Pembrokeshire Herald is registered as a\n>>> newspaper at Companies House pursuant to the Newspaper Libel and\n>>> Registration Act 1881, No. N-5802. The Pembrokeshire Herald and other\n>>> titles are deposited weekly at The National Library of Wales for\n>>> preservation by legal order pursuant to the Legal Deposit Libraries Act 2003\n>>>\n>>\n>\n> --\n>\n>\n>\n> *Tom Sinclair*Editor | www.herald.wales\n>\n> T: 01646 454545 ext 202\n>\n> E: editor@herald.email\n>\n> A: Carmarthenshire Herald Ltd t/a Herald.Wales, 11 Hamilton Terrace,\n> Milford Haven, Pembrokeshire, SA73 3AL\n> <https://www.google.com/maps/search/11+Hamilton+Terrace,+Milford+Haven,+Pembrokeshire,+SA73+3AL?entry=gmail&source=g>\n>\n> [image: facebook icon] <https://www.facebook.com/Herald.Wales>  [image:\n> twitter icon] <https://twitter.com/HeraldWales>  [image: instagram icon]\n> <https://www.instagram.com/herald.wales/>\n> [image: Banner] <https://www.herald.wales/>\n>\n> Herald.Wales and The Pembrokeshire Herald titles are regulated by the\n> Independent Press Standards Organisation Member of BBC Local Democracy News\n> Service & BBC News Hub. The Pembrokeshire Herald is registered as a\n> newspaper at Companies House pursuant to the Newspaper Libel and\n> Registration Act 1881, No. N-5802. The Pembrokeshire Herald and other\n> titles are deposited weekly at The National Library of Wales for\n> preservation by legal order pursuant to the Legal Deposit Libraries Act 2003\n>\n"},{"id":"wb-149429a9","subject":"Consortium correspondence BP v Buckler","name":"2025-11-02_consortium-correspondence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2025-11-02","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2025-11-02_consortium-correspondence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-correspondence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2025-11-02_consortium-correspondence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Sun, 2 Nov 2025 18:02:02 -0300\nSubject: Enquiry re potential acquiring authority route – former family farmland (BP v Buckler 1987)\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V7LmUaOGCrpFnSDLk3GATyH8eYJYRx_jJH9wNHqUnxXxw@mail.gmail.com>\n\nSirs/Ma'am,\n\nI am writing regarding farmland formerly belonging to my family, which\nlater formed part of the litigation known as BP v Buckler (1987).\n\nI now hold a structured list of reasons and supporting evidence which I\nbelieve strongly suggest that the land transfer and subsequent development\nwas procured by fraud and conspiracy involving government actors. The\nevidential threshold I now hold, if correct, would meet the reopening\nconditions described under s.23 of the Limitation Act 1980 – meaning that\nlitigation, rectification and title recovery proceedings may be\nlegitimately initiated.\n\nHowever, before progressing litigation, I wish to formally offer the UK\nGovernment the opportunity to resolve this matter via public law tools\nrather than adversarial reopening. Specifically: I would like to enquire\nwhether an acquiring authority could lawfully consider using compulsory\npurchase powers to acquire the 23 properties that now sit on this former\nfarmland, and transfer title / beneficial ownership back to my family,\nwhere this could be framed as a public interest rectification measure\nrather than a private benefit case.\n\nCould you please:\n\n   - confirm which acquiring authority would be competent jurisdictionally\n   for this scenario,\n   - outline which statutory route would be the correct CPO framework to\n   assess this under, and\n   - confirm any preliminary information requirements you would need from\n   me (title data, timeline, evidential index etc) if this was to be scoped\n   and reviewed.\n\n\nI am willing to provide a full indexed evidence pack for confidential\nreview.\n\nKind regards,\n\nSion Buckler\n[redacted]\n"},{"id":"wb-40372233","subject":"Consortium correspondence BP v Buckler","name":"2025-11-04_consortium-correspondence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2025-11-04","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2025-11-04_consortium-correspondence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-correspondence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2025-11-04_consortium-correspondence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: <PEDW.Casework@gov.wales>\nDate: Tue, 4 Nov 2025 08:56:52 +0000\nSubject: RE: Enquiry re potential acquiring authority route – former family farmland (BP v Buckler 1987)\nMessage-ID: <LO0P123MB5599E9766B81B205827C2CA083C4A@LO0P123MB5599.GBRP123.PROD.OUTLOOK.COM>\n\nGood morning,\n\nPEDW’s role is providing an impartial appeals service, we are not the correct department to comment or provide any advice.\n\nAs there is no appeal before us, it is not in our jurisdiction.\n\nFor planning advice you can go on Planning Aid Wales website which is  https://planningaidwales.org.uk/\n\nI am sorry we cannot be of further help.\n\nKind regards,\n\nLyndsey Dennis\n\nSwddog Gweinyddu Busnes / Business Administration Officer\nPenderfyniadau Cynllunio a’r Amgylchedd / Planning & Environment Decisions Wales\nLlywodraeth Cymru / Welsh Government\nCP2, Parc Cathays, Caerdydd, CF10 3NQ / CP2, Cathays Park, Cardiff, CF10 3NQ\nFfôn / Phone: 0300 025 3338\nE-bost / E-mail:  Pedw.Casework@gov.wales<mailto:Pedw.Casework@gov.wales>\n\n[cid:image001.jpg@01DC4D68.F4BA9B80]\n\n\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]>\nSent: 02 November 2025 21:02\nTo: pcu@levellingup.gov.uk; enquiries@homesengland.gov.uk; enquiries@planninginspectorate.gov.uk; SM-Defra-Helpline, Defra (MCU) <defra.helpline@defra.gov.uk>; PEDW – Gwaith Achos / Casework <PEDW.Casework@gov.wales>; PEDW – Seilwaith / Infrastructure <PEDW.Infrastructure@gov.wales>\nSubject: Enquiry re potential acquiring authority route – former family farmland (BP v Buckler 1987)\n\nSirs/Ma'am,\n\nI am writing regarding farmland formerly belonging to my family, which later formed part of the litigation known as BP v Buckler (1987).\n\nI now hold a structured list of reasons and supporting evidence which I believe strongly suggest that the land transfer and subsequent development was procured by fraud and conspiracy involving government actors. The evidential threshold I now hold, if correct, would meet the reopening conditions described under s.23 of the Limitation Act 1980 – meaning that litigation, rectification and title recovery proceedings may be legitimately initiated.\n\nHowever, before progressing litigation, I wish to formally offer the UK Government the opportunity to resolve this matter via public law tools rather than adversarial reopening. Specifically: I would like to enquire whether an acquiring authority could lawfully consider using compulsory purchase powers to acquire the 23 properties that now sit on this former farmland, and transfer title / beneficial ownership back to my family, where this could be framed as a public interest rectification measure rather than a private benefit case.\n\nCould you please:\n\n  *   confirm which acquiring authority would be competent jurisdictionally for this scenario,\n  *   outline which statutory route would be the correct CPO framework to assess this under, and\n  *   confirm any preliminary information requirements you would need from me (title data, timeline, evidential index etc) if this was to be scoped and reviewed.\n\nI am willing to provide a full indexed evidence pack for confidential review.\n\nKind regards,\n\nSion Buckler\n[redacted]\nSganiwyd y neges hon am bob feirws hysbys wrth iddi adael Llywodraeth Cymru. Mae Llywodraeth Cymru yn cymryd o ddifrif yr angen i ddiogelu eich data. Os cysylltwch â Llywodraeth Cymru, mae ein hysbysiad preifatrwydd<”https://gov.wales/about/welsh-government-privacy-notice/?lang=cy”> yn esbonio sut rydym yn defnyddio eich gwybodaeth a sut rydym yn diogelu eich preifatrwydd. Rydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg. Byddwn yn anfon ateb yn Gymraeg i ohebiaeth a dderbynnir yn Gymraeg ac ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi. ----------------- On leaving the Welsh Government this email was scanned for all known viruses. The Welsh Government takes the protection of your data seriously. If you contact the Welsh Government then our Privacy Notice<https://gov.wales/about/welsh-government-privacy-notice/?lang=en> explains how we use your information and the ways in which we protect your privacy. We welcome receiving correspondence in Welsh. Any correspondence received in Welsh will be answered in Welsh and corresponding in Welsh will not lead to a delay in responding.\n"},{"id":"wb-86411b4f","subject":"Consortium evidence BP v Buckler","name":"2025-11-05_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2025-11-05","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2025-11-05_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-evidence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2025-11-05_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Wed, 5 Nov 2025 02:59:13 -0300\nSubject: BP Properties Ltd v Buckler – Request for Opinion (Limitation Act 1980 s.32)\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V4TuDUpBDFquLAGmqNM_n+-bPUJktgKo6U5kEZphk-uPg@mail.gmail.com>\n\nDear Mr Thomas,\n\nI am seeking a preliminary opinion regarding potential grounds to reopen\nthe case BP Properties Ltd v Buckler (Court of Appeal, 31 July 1987),\nconcerning Great House Farm, Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan.\n\nThere appear to be two material issues:\n\n1. Corporate Party Identification:\nCorrespondence relevant to the dispute was exchanged with BP Pension Trust\nLtd, while litigation and enforcement proceeded under BP Properties Ltd.\nThe Court treated these entities as separate when assessing notice but\neffectively identical in dismissing the appeal and enforcing possession.\nThis inconsistency may constitute a mistake or misrepresentation under\nLimitation Act 1980, Section 32.\n\n\n2. Effect of the 1974 Letters:\nThe Court found that unilateral letters in 1974 granting a licence to\noccupy rent-free for life converted possession from adverse to licensed.\nHowever, I understand there is no evidence that this licence was ever\naccepted. If acceptance did not occur, adverse possession may have\ncontinued and the running of limitation may not have been interrupted as\nthe judgment assumes.\n\n\nI would be grateful for:\n\nYour view on whether these issues may be sufficient to apply to set aside\nor reopen enforcement, or otherwise seek remedy or restitution; and\n\nYour guidance on next procedural steps.\n\n\nI can provide Judgment text and Newspaper records of the demolition (1988)\netc.\n\nThank you for your consideration.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nSion Hywel Buckler\n[redacted]\n"},{"id":"wb-fa5d60ad","subject":"Consortium correspondence BP v Buckler","name":"2025-11-06_consortium-correspondence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2025-11-06","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2025-11-06_consortium-correspondence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-correspondence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2025-11-06_consortium-correspondence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Rachael Beard <Rachael.Beard@communities.gov.uk>\nDate: Thu, 6 Nov 2025 15:29:36 +0000\nSubject: Enquiry re potential acquiring authority route – former family farmland (BP v Buckler 1987)\nMessage-ID: <CWXP123MB5145C30DBEC247EA416461E1C1C2A@CWXP123MB5145.GBRP123.PROD.OUTLOOK.COM>\n\nOFFICIAL\n\nDear Requester\n\nThank you for your email which has been passed to the Planning Casework Unit for a reply.  I’ve provided answers in blue that I hope are helpful, see your email below.\n\nI would advise that you seek your own independent professional advice before contacting the relevant local authority.\n\nRegards Rachael\n\n\n\n\nOFFICIAL\n\n\nOFFICIAL\n\n\nOFFICIAL\n\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]<mailto:[redacted]>>\nSent: 02 November 2025 21:02\nTo: PCU <pcu@levellingup.gov.uk<mailto:pcu@levellingup.gov.uk>>; enquiries@homesengland.gov.uk<mailto:enquiries@homesengland.gov.uk>; enquiries@planninginspectorate.gov.uk<mailto:enquiries@planninginspectorate.gov.uk>; SM-Defra-Helpline, Defra (MCU) <defra.helpline@defra.gov.uk<mailto:defra.helpline@defra.gov.uk>>; PEDW.Casework@gov.wales<mailto:PEDW.Casework@gov.wales>; PEDW.Infrastructure@gov.wales<mailto:PEDW.Infrastructure@gov.wales>\nSubject: Enquiry re potential acquiring authority route – former family farmland (BP v Buckler 1987)\n\nYou don't often get email from [redacted]<mailto:[redacted]>. Learn why this is important<https://aka.ms/LearnAboutSenderIdentification>\nSirs/Ma'am,\n\nI am writing regarding farmland formerly belonging to my family, which later formed part of the litigation known as BP v Buckler (1987).\n\nI now hold a structured list of reasons and supporting evidence which I believe strongly suggest that the land transfer and subsequent development was procured by fraud and conspiracy involving government actors. The evidential threshold I now hold, if correct, would meet the reopening conditions described under s.23 of the Limitation Act 1980 – meaning that litigation, rectification and title recovery proceedings may be legitimately initiated.\n\nHowever, before progressing litigation, I wish to formally offer the UK Government the opportunity to resolve this matter via public law tools rather than adversarial reopening. Specifically: I would like to enquire whether an acquiring authority could lawfully consider using compulsory purchase powers to acquire the 23 properties that now sit on this former farmland, and transfer title / beneficial ownership back to my family, where this could be framed as a public interest rectification measure rather than a private benefit case.\n\nCould you please:\n\n  *   confirm which acquiring authority would be competent jurisdictionally for this scenario, - You would need to approach the Local Planning Authority (LPA) that covers the area the properties come under.\n  *   outline which statutory route would be the correct CPO framework to assess this under, and This would be for the LPA to consider the appropriate powers to use. It would also be for the LPA to consider if use of a CPO power was appropriate. We are unable to give any advice on this matter in case a CPO is later submitted which is to be consider by the department.\n  *   confirm any preliminary information requirements you would need from me (title data, timeline, evidential index etc) if this was to be scoped and reviewed. Should the LPA decide a CPO is appropriate there is guidance on gov.uk about what information they would need to provide to the Secretary of State.\n\nI am willing to provide a full indexed evidence pack for confidential review.\n\nKind regards,\n\nSion Buckler\n[redacted]\n"},{"id":"wb-b6b7f881","subject":"Consortium evidence BP v Buckler","name":"2025-11-14_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2025-11-14","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2025-11-14_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-evidence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2025-11-14_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Fri, 14 Nov 2025 09:48:36 -0300\nSubject: BP vs Buckler\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V5_c=gu0yiBK9n-cA1M3F_7zWbumQhFosobrY2F4CeuBA@mail.gmail.com>\n\nSir/Ma'am,\n\nI am your constituent, living [location redacted]. I am writing to request your\nassistance in reviewing a historic legal matter involving my family: *BP\nProperties Ltd v Buckler (1987)*, relating to Great House Farm, Llandough.\n\nMy family lived on, cared for and claimed to own that farm for generations.\nThe events surrounding the 1987 case resulted in the loss of our home and\nland, and left several members of my family deeply affected. Recent\nresearch I have undertaken has revealed significant procedural\ninconsistencies, historical irregularities, unanswered questions regarding\ncorporate identity, unverified service of key documents, and heritage\nconsiderations relating to the site.\n\nI have attached a short report summarising SOME of these issues. I am\nrequesting your help in identifying the appropriate bodies—legal,\nhistorical, or governmental—who may be able to independently review the\nhistorical record to ensure fairness, accuracy, and transparency.\n\nMy interest is not solely personal; Great House Farm has a heritage\nsignificance to Wales, including documented accounts that Guglielmo Marconi\nstayed there while experimenting with early radio technology. I believe\nthis should form part of our preserved historical record.\n\nBy way of background, I am a veteran and have served both in the Armed\nForces and recently with the Vale of Glamorgan Council. My intention is\nsimply to seek clarity, proper review, and justice where appropriate. I\nwould be grateful for your guidance on the correct avenues to pursue.\n\nThank you for your time and consideration. I look forward to your response.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\n\n*Requester*\n[redacted]\nTel: 0203 137 7118\n"},{"id":"wb-04389584","subject":"Consortium evidence BP v Buckler","name":"2025-11-15_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2025-11-15","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2025-11-15_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-evidence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2025-11-15_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Sat, 15 Nov 2025 03:48:57 -0300\nSubject: Re: BP vs Buckler\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V5NTt-vkTCvj8Gj56N7zbw7ygN=YJ5RL-SUG_dTCA_jEA@mail.gmail.com>\n\nHi,\n\nYes please. Thank you.\n\nIf you have any avaliablity Monday please tell me the best time to call.\nI'm also reachable for scheduled calls on [redacted]\n\nOtherwise I'm on WhatsApp, which you can reach out to me on anytime.\n[redacted]\n\nRegards,\n\nSion Buckler\n\n\n\n\n\n\nOn Fri, 14 Nov 2025, 15:24 Carroll, George D (Cllr), <\ngdcarroll@valeofglamorgan.gov.uk> wrote:\n\n> Hi Requester\n>\n> Thank you for copying me in on this.\n>\n> If you'd like to discuss, please let me know.\n>\n> All the best,\n>\n> George\n> ------------------------------\n> *From:* Requester <[redacted]>\n> *Sent:* Friday, November 14, 2025 12:48:36 PM\n> *To:* kanishka.narayan.mp@parliament.uk <kanishka.narayan.mp@parliament.uk\n> >\n> *Subject:* BP vs Buckler\n>\n> You don't often get email from [redacted]. Learn why this\n> is important <https://aka.ms/LearnAboutSenderIdentification>\n>\n> Sir/Ma'am,\n>\n> I am your constituent, living [location redacted]. I am writing to request your\n> assistance in reviewing a historic legal matter involving my family: *BP\n> Properties Ltd v Buckler (1987)*, relating to Great House Farm, Llandough.\n>\n> My family lived on, cared for and claimed to own that farm for\n> generations. The events surrounding the 1987 case resulted in the loss of\n> our home and land, and left several members of my family deeply affected.\n> Recent research I have undertaken has revealed significant procedural\n> inconsistencies, historical irregularities, unanswered questions regarding\n> corporate identity, unverified service of key documents, and heritage\n> considerations relating to the site.\n>\n> I have attached a short report summarising SOME of these issues. I am\n> requesting your help in identifying the appropriate bodies—legal,\n> historical, or governmental—who may be able to independently review the\n> historical record to ensure fairness, accuracy, and transparency.\n>\n> My interest is not solely personal; Great House Farm has a heritage\n> significance to Wales, including documented accounts that Guglielmo Marconi\n> stayed there while experimenting with early radio technology. I believe\n> this should form part of our preserved historical record.\n>\n> By way of background, I am a veteran and have served both in the Armed\n> Forces and recently with the Vale of Glamorgan Council. My intention is\n> simply to seek clarity, proper review, and justice where appropriate. I\n> would be grateful for your guidance on the correct avenues to pursue.\n>\n> Thank you for your time and consideration. I look forward to your response.\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n>\n> *Requester*\n> [redacted]\n> [map link redacted]\n> [location redacted]\n> [map link redacted]\n> [redacted]\n> [map link redacted]\n> Tel: 0203 137 7118\n>\n"},{"id":"wb-940cf022","subject":"Consortium evidence BP v Buckler","name":"2025-11-17_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2025-11-17","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2025-11-17_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-evidence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2025-11-17_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: \"NARAYAN, Kanishka (MP)\" <kanishka.narayan.mp@parliament.uk>\nDate: Mon, 17 Nov 2025 09:59:29 +0000\nSubject: FW: BP vs Buckler\nMessage-ID: <CWLP123MB6542017D111E6F864941D4F980C9A@CWLP123MB6542.GBRP123.PROD.OUTLOOK.COM>\n\nDear Soin,\n\nThank you for your email to Kanishka Narayan MP and for passing on your concerns. [location redacted] unfortunately does not fall within the Vale of Glamorgan Parliamentary Constituency and Parliamentary protocol states that Kanishka is unable to assist anyone outside his constituency.\n\nI would like to recommend contacting your MP, Stephen Doughty. His contact details are:\n\nPhone Number: 020 7219 5348<tel:020%207219%205348>\nEmail Address: stephen.doughty.mp@parliament.uk<mailto:stephen.doughty.mp@parliament.uk>\n\nI hope they are able to assist you.\n\nKind regards,\nMadelene\n\n[cid:image001.png@01DC57A8.1BFCB220]\nMadelene Larsson\nConstituency Support Manager, Kanishka Narayan MP\nMember of Parliament for the Vale of Glamorgan\nParliamentary Under-Secretary of State in the Department for Science, Innovation and Technology\n\nUK Parliament Disclaimer: this e-mail is confidential to the intended recipient. If you have received it in error, please notify the sender and delete it from your system. Any unauthorised use, disclosure, or copying is not permitted. This e-mail has been checked for viruses, but no liability is accepted for any damage caused by any virus transmitted by this e-mail. This e-mail address is not secure, is not encrypted and should not be used for sensitive data.\n\n\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]>\nSent: 14 November 2025 12:49\nTo: NARAYAN, Kanishka (MP) <kanishka.narayan.mp@parliament.uk>\nSubject: BP vs Buckler\n\n\nSir/Ma'am,\n\nI am your constituent, living [location redacted]. I am writing to request your assistance in reviewing a historic legal matter involving my family: BP Properties Ltd v Buckler (1987), relating to Great House Farm, Llandough.\n\nMy family lived on, cared for and claimed to own that farm for generations. The events surrounding the 1987 case resulted in the loss of our home and land, and left several members of my family deeply affected. Recent research I have undertaken has revealed significant procedural inconsistencies, historical irregularities, unanswered questions regarding corporate identity, unverified service of key documents, and heritage considerations relating to the site.\n\nI have attached a short report summarising SOME of these issues. I am requesting your help in identifying the appropriate bodies—legal, historical, or governmental—who may be able to independently review the historical record to ensure fairness, accuracy, and transparency.\n\nMy interest is not solely personal; Great House Farm has a heritage significance to Wales, including documented accounts that Guglielmo Marconi stayed there while experimenting with early radio technology. I believe this should form part of our preserved historical record.\n\nBy way of background, I am a veteran and have served both in the Armed Forces and recently with the Vale of Glamorgan Council. My intention is simply to seek clarity, proper review, and justice where appropriate. I would be grateful for your guidance on the correct avenues to pursue.\n\nThank you for your time and consideration. I look forward to your response.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nSion Buckler\n[redacted]\nTel: 0203 137 7118\n\nUK Parliament Disclaimer: this e-mail is confidential to the intended recipient. If you have received it in error, please notify the sender and delete it from your system. Any unauthorised use, disclosure, or copying is not permitted. This e-mail has been checked for viruses, but no liability is accepted for any damage caused by any virus transmitted by this e-mail. This e-mail address is not secure, is not encrypted and should not be used for sensitive data.\n"},{"id":"wb-98b10839","subject":"Consortium evidence BP v Buckler","name":"2025-11-17_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","date":"2025-11-17","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2025-11-17_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-evidence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2025-11-17_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: NMR Enquiries <nmr.wales@rcahmw.gov.uk>\nDate: Mon, 17 Nov 2025 14:09:09 +0000\nSubject: RE: BP vs Buckler\nMessage-ID: <AM8PR01MB79065619D539975A04A35819D1C9A@AM8PR01MB7906.eurprd01.prod.exchangelabs.com>\n\nDiolch am eich ymholiad. Byddwn yn ceisio ymateb cyn pen 15 diwrnod gwaith, oni bai bod chi wedi dewis ein Gwasanaeth Blaenoriaeth, wedyn byddwn mewn cysylltiad yn fuan gyda manylion pellach a gwybodaeth am dalu am y gwasanaeth.  Yn y cyfamser, gallai’r wybodaeth isod fod yn ddefnyddiol i chi:\n\nI gael gwybodaeth am safleoedd hanesyddol yng Nghymru a chael manylion am yr hyn sydd yn ein harchifau, ewch i’n cronfa ddata a’n catalog, https://coflein.gov.uk/cy/\n\nOs yn bosibl, rydym yn argymell eich bod yn trefnu apwyntiad i ymweld â’n llyfrgell ac ystafell chwilio<https://cbhc.gov.uk/gwasanaethau/> ein harchif, os hoffech weld deunydd yr archif â’ch llygaid eich hun a thynnu lluniau heb orfod talu amdanynt. Yn gyffredinol, mae gwneud hynny’n gynt ac yn rhatach o lawer nag archebu sganiau o bell.\n\nMae llawer o’n delweddau digidol ar gael i’w lawrlwytho, eu prynu a’u trwyddedu yn syth o Coflein. Mae canllawiau ynghylch sut mae gwneud hynny i’w gweld yn y fideo byr hwn: Lawrlwytho delweddau o Coflein<https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JYJmCNrRFqY> Os nad yw’r ddelwedd yr ydych yn chwilio amdani ar gael drwy’r system hon, y rheswm am hynny’n aml fydd cyfyngiadau hawlfraint, ac efallai y byddwch am chwilio am ddelwedd amgen sydd ar gael, os yw hynny’n bosibl.\n\nOs ydych yn gofyn am set ddata, cofiwch fod y data hwnnw ar gael am ddim yn awr dan y Drwydded Llywodraeth Agored<https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/doc/open-government-licence-cymraeg/version/3/>, ac y gallwch fynd ati eich hun i chwilio am ddata am y tir<https://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nmrw_jan2024_sites> a’r môr<https://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nmrw_jan2024_maritimesites> ar MapDataCymru neu Cymru Hanesyddol<https://historic-wales-rcahmw.hub.arcgis.com/#zoom=0&lat=300000&lon=258000&layers=BFFFFFTFFTTT>.\n\nOs ydych yn cysylltu â ni i ofyn am gael mynediad i safle hanesyddol, cofiwch nad yw’r Comisiwn Brenhinol yn berchen ar unrhyw un o’r safleoedd a gaiff eu rhestru ar Coflein, ac nad oes gennym unrhyw fanylion cyswllt ar gyfer perchnogion safleoedd. Bydd angen i chi gysylltu’n uniongyrchol â pherchnogion safleoedd i ofyn am gael mynediad.\n\nOs ydych yn poeni am gyflwr heneb neu safle hanesyddol, dylech gysylltu â Gwasanaeth Amgylchedd Hanesyddol Llywodraeth Cymru, sef Cadw, neu â’r awdurdod lleol perthnasol. Yn yr un modd, os oes gennych ymholiad am statws statudol safle hanesyddol neu am y broses gynllunio sy’n ymwneud ag Adeiladau Rhestredig neu Henebion Hynafol Cofrestredig, mae ystod o wybodaeth ar gael ar wefan Cadw: Cyngor a Chymorth | Cadw<https://cadw.llyw.cymru/cyngor-a-chymorth?_gl=1*15twz22*_ga*MjAwNzczNTg4MS4xNzExNjMwMjE1*_ga_B2BCVKM874*MTcxMzQ1MDIyNS43LjAuMTcxMzQ1MDIyNS42MC4wLjA.>.\nPrif rôl y Comisiwn Brenhinol yw creu, curadu a rhannu gwybodaeth am yr amgylchedd hanesyddol, ac rydym hefyd yn chwarae rhan fel ymgyngoreion yn y broses gynllunio ar gyfer yr amgylchedd hanesyddol ar sail y wybodaeth honno. Fodd bynnag, er bod yr amgylchedd hanesyddol yng Nghymru yn bwysig i’n staff, nid ydym ni fel corff yn gyfrifol am orfodi deddfwriaeth ym maes cynllunio a threftadaeth nac yn gyfrifol am ddiogelu safleoedd hanesyddol, ac nid ydym chwaith yn gweithredu fel grŵp lobïo.\n\n\n\n\nThank you for your enquiry.  We will endeavour to respond within 15 working days, unless you have selected our Priority Service option in which case we will be in contact with further details and information on paying for this service soon.  In the meantime, you may find the information below helpful:\n\nFor information on historic sites in Wales and details of archive holdings see our database and catalogue, https://coflein.gov.uk/en/\n\nWhere possible we recommend that you make an appointment to visit our archive search room and library<https://rcahmw.gov.uk/services/> to view archive material in person and take photos without charge.  This is generally much quicker and cheaper than ordering scans remotely.\n\nMany of our digital images are available to download, purchase and licence directly from Coflein.  Guidance on how to do this can be seen in this short video: Downloading images from Coflein (youtube.com)<https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gKpL61F1M2w&t=3s>  If the image which you want is not available via this system, this is often due to copyright restrictions and you may wish to identify an alternative image which is available, if possible.\n\nIf you are requesting a dataset, please be advised that this data is now freely available under the Open Government Licence (nationalarchives.gov.uk)<https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/doc/open-government-licence/version/3/> and you can carry out the searches yourself for terrestrial<https://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nmrw_jan2024_sites> and maritime<https://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nmrw_jan2024_maritimesites> data on Data Map Wales and on Historic Wales (arcgis.com)<https://historic-wales-rcahmw.hub.arcgis.com/#zoom=0&lat=300000&lon=258000&layers=BFFFFFTFFTTT>.\n\nIf you are contacting us to request physical access to a historic site please note that the Royal Commission does not own any of the sites listed on Coflein, nor do we hold contact details for site owners.  You will need to contact the site owners directly with your request.\n\nIf you are concerned about the condition of a historic site or ancient monument please contact the Welsh Government’s Historic Environment Service, Cadw or the relevant local authority.  Similarly, if you have a query regarding the statutory status of a historic site or the planning process relating to Listed Buildings or Scheduled Ancient Monuments there is a range of information available on Cadw’s website: Advice & Support | Cadw (gov.wales)<https://cadw.gov.wales/advice-support>\nThe Royal Commission’s primary role is to create, curate and communicate information on the historic environment, we also play a role as consultees in the planning process for the historic environment on the basis of this information.  However, whilst our staff are passionate about the historic environment in Wales, as a body we are not responsible for enforcing planning or heritage legislation, safeguarding historic sites, nor do we act as a lobbying group.\n\n\n\nNMR Enquiries\nYmholiadau Cyhoeddus I Public Enquiries\nFfordd Penglais | Penglais Road, Aberystwyth, SY23 3BU\n+44 (0) 1970 621 200\nchc.cymru@cbhc.gov.uk | nmr.wales@rcahmw.gov.uk\nwww.cbhc.gov.uk | www.rcahmw.gov.uk\nNoddir gan Lywodraeth Cymru | Sponsored by Welsh Government\nRydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg a Saesneg. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn peri oedi.\nWe welcome correspondence in Welsh and English. Corresponding in Welsh will not lead to any delay.\n\nYn unol â Rheoliadau Safonau'r Gymraeg (Rhif 2) 2016, mae gennych hawl i gyfathrebu a gohebu â Chomisiwn Brenhinol Henebion Cymru yn eich dewis iaith. Er mwyn sicrhau ein bod yn cyflawni’r hawl, rhowch wybod i ni a ydych yn dymuno derbyn gohebiaeth a/neu alwadau ffôn oddi wrthym yn y Gymraeg. Bydd yr wybodaeth hon yn cael ei chofnodi gennym ni, a byddwn yn defnyddio'r iaith o’ch dewis ym mhob cyfathrebu yn y dyfodol. Diolch.\nUnder the Welsh Language Standards (No. 2) Regulations 2016, you have the right to communicate and correspond with the Royal Commission on the Ancient and Historical Monuments of Wales in your preferred language. To ensure we uphold this right, please let us know whether you wish to receive correspondence and/or telephone calls from us in Welsh. This information will be recorded by us, and we will use your preferred language in all future communication. Thank you.\n​\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]>\nSent: 14 November 2025 12:49\nTo: kanishka.narayan.mp@parliament.uk\nSubject: BP vs Buckler\n\nYou don't often get email from [redacted]<mailto:[redacted]>. Learn why this is important<https://aka.ms/LearnAboutSenderIdentification>\n\nSir/Ma'am,\n\nI am your constituent, living [location redacted]. I am writing to request your assistance in reviewing a historic legal matter involving my family: BP Properties Ltd v Buckler (1987), relating to Great House Farm, Llandough.\n\nMy family lived on, cared for and claimed to own that farm for generations. The events surrounding the 1987 case resulted in the loss of our home and land, and left several members of my family deeply affected. Recent research I have undertaken has revealed significant procedural inconsistencies, historical irregularities, unanswered questions regarding corporate identity, unverified service of key documents, and heritage considerations relating to the site.\n\nI have attached a short report summarising SOME of these issues. I am requesting your help in identifying the appropriate bodies—legal, historical, or governmental—who may be able to independently review the historical record to ensure fairness, accuracy, and transparency.\n\nMy interest is not solely personal; Great House Farm has a heritage significance to Wales, including documented accounts that Guglielmo Marconi stayed there while experimenting with early radio technology. I believe this should form part of our preserved historical record.\n\nBy way of background, I am a veteran and have served both in the Armed Forces and recently with the Vale of Glamorgan Council. My intention is simply to seek clarity, proper review, and justice where appropriate. I would be grateful for your guidance on the correct avenues to pursue.\n\nThank you for your time and consideration. I look forward to your response.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nSion Buckler\n[redacted]\nTel: 0203 137 7118\n"},{"id":"wb-69a04414","subject":"Consortium evidence BP v Buckler","name":"2025-11-20_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2025-11-20","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2025-11-20_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-evidence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2025-11-20_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Thu, 20 Nov 2025 23:58:34 -0300\nSubject: Enquiry Ref: Marconi and Buckler Family\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V6HxUmyU3Ddn5cdh-k6xV115R9XWvLAYDYQjdNDm0XVtA@mail.gmail.com>\n\nSirs/Ma'am,\n\nI am Requester, the son of William Buckler.\nMy father fought BP in the legal case: BP vs Buckler 1987.\nI wish to notify you of a scandal concerning this case and Marconi.\n\nMarconi was staying with my family at our farm (Great House Farm,\nLlandough, Wales) when he invented the first working radio.\nAfter BP stole our farm via lawfare. And demolished it. Our 800 farm was\nalmost immediately bulldozed down, and covered with 23 houses.\nTo this day there is not even a plaque to commemorate or acknowledge\nMarconi was ever there, or radio was invented from this location.\nAs you can see when I bring this up with my local parish and councillors\nthe response is not cooperation or compassion or just.\n\nThe entire case is suspect. The way the farm was taken from us is suspect.\nWhat was exhevtated from under the ground was suspect.\nMy fathers appeal was dismissed, which was also suspect. Basically when BP\nProperties were the claimant, the judge stated my grandmother's response to\nBP didn't count as her response was to BP Pension Trust Ltd (the alleged\npossessor), which didn't count as them and BP Properties were seperate\ncompanies. Also this was after my nan died, so she was unable to defend her\nposition. But until the day she died she maintained we owned our farm. But\nin my dads appearance, where he raised the issue the claimant was not the\nalleged possessor, it was dismissed citing the two companies were basically\nthe same.\nSo it was a case of 'heads they win, tails we lose' and this entire case is\nriddled with fraud, concealment and scandal.\nMy fathers appeal to the European Court of Human Rights was shut down by\nour Government too. Also suspect.\nHe then died very abruptly and young. The age me and my older brother are\nnow.\n\nI wish to spearhead a lawsuit and sue BP and the British Government.\nUnder the UK Limitation Act clause concerning fraud and concealment I am\nable to re-open this case.\n\nThe Italian government may wish to investigate this by its own accord.\nBut I appear to have made the most ground in this investigation because of\nmy connection to this case.\nAnd my background in military cyber warfare, which helps me utilize\ntechnologies to aggregate the data.\nMy last employer, upon completion of my 22 Years in Oath to the Crown last\nyear, was the Welsh Government.\n\nIf the Italian Government would like to sponsor or endorse my work, I would\nbe grateful.\nI think it would be poetic justice if you were to assist me in this\nendeavour.\n\nRegards,\n\nSion Buckler\n[redacted]\n"},{"id":"wb-8b82a57f","subject":"Consortium evidence BP v Buckler","name":"2025-11-20_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","date":"2025-11-20","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2025-11-20_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-evidence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2025-11-20_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Paul Egan <clerk@llandough-cc.co.uk>\nDate: Thu, 20 Nov 2025 07:40:57 -0000\nSubject: RE: BP vs Buckler\nMessage-ID: <00d201dc59f1$0399e540$0acdafc0$@llandough-cc.co.uk>\n\nHi Requester,\n\n \n\nI regret to inform you that we are unable to assist with the matter you raise.\n\n \n\nRegards,\n\n \n\nPaul\n\n \n\nPaul R. Egan BA, Chartered MCIPD, CiLCA, F. Inst. LM, FIPSM\n\nClerk to Llandough Community Council,\n\n63 Woodham Park,\n\nBarry,\n\nVale of Glamorgan CF62 8FJ\n\nTelephone 01446 409294\n\nWebsite www.llandough-cc.co.uk <http://www.llandough-cc.co.uk/> \n\n \n\n\n\nWhen you contact us\n\nThe information you provide (personal information such as name, address, email address, phone number, organisation) will be processed and stored to enable us to contact you and respond to your correspondence, provide information and/or access our facilities and services. Your personal information will be not shared or provided to any other third party. Your information may however be published in the public domain if you require the Council to discuss the matter raised. \n\n \n\nFor further information please see our full privacy notice on our website www.llandough-cc.co.uk <http://www.llandough-cc.co.uk/> \n\n \n\nThe Council's mission statement is 'Serving our community, preserving our identity' The Council will always strive to:\n\n ·  Be open and accessible in all our decision making \n\n· Be accountable in all our council activities\n\n· Be responsive to the needs of the community\n\n· Encourage community participation\n\n \n\n \n\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]> \nSent: 14 November 2025 12:49\nTo: kanishka.narayan.mp@parliament.uk\nSubject: BP vs Buckler\n\n \n\nSir/Ma'am,\n\nI am your constituent, living [location redacted]. I am writing to request your assistance in reviewing a historic legal matter involving my family: BP Properties Ltd v Buckler (1987), relating to Great House Farm, Llandough.\n\nMy family lived on, cared for and claimed to own that farm for generations. The events surrounding the 1987 case resulted in the loss of our home and land, and left several members of my family deeply affected. Recent research I have undertaken has revealed significant procedural inconsistencies, historical irregularities, unanswered questions regarding corporate identity, unverified service of key documents, and heritage considerations relating to the site.\n\nI have attached a short report summarising SOME of these issues. I am requesting your help in identifying the appropriate bodies—legal, historical, or governmental—who may be able to independently review the historical record to ensure fairness, accuracy, and transparency.\n\nMy interest is not solely personal; Great House Farm has a heritage significance to Wales, including documented accounts that Guglielmo Marconi stayed there while experimenting with early radio technology. I believe this should form part of our preserved historical record.\n\nBy way of background, I am a veteran and have served both in the Armed Forces and recently with the Vale of Glamorgan Council. My intention is simply to seek clarity, proper review, and justice where appropriate. I would be grateful for your guidance on the correct avenues to pursue.\n\nThank you for your time and consideration. I look forward to your response.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\n\nSion Buckler\n[redacted]\nTel: 0203 137 7118\n\n"},{"id":"wb-daaffdef","subject":"Consortium evidence BP v Buckler","name":"2025-11-23_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2025-11-23","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2025-11-23_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler__en_v0-0-1.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-evidence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2025-11-23_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler__en_v0-0-1.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"Assuming I successfully reopen the case under the fraud and concealment exception in the Limitation Act 1980, s 32, win the case, and a court rules that the land, property, and artifacts were acquired by BP Properties Ltd through fraud, my claim and entitlement would be substantial.\n\nJustice in this scenario would aim to place me and my family in the position we would be in, had the fraud not occurred (the principle of equivalence). \nThis would likely involve a combination of the following legal remedies:\n\n1. Recovery of Land and Property (Specific Restitution); The primary entitlement would be the return of the land and property to your family.\nVesting of Title: The court could order the current title holders to transfer the legal title of the land and any structures on it back to us, as the rightful owner. Possession: We would be granted legal possession of the site and the associated land.\n\n2. Recovery of Artifacts (\"Treasure\" and other finds); The court would address the ownership of the artifacts based on the new ruling that the land was always rightfully ours.\nNon-Treasure Items: Artifacts such as pottery, tools, etc., that do not meet the criteria of \"treasure\" would be legally declared our property as the landowner at the time of the find. Museums currently holding them would be ordered to return them to us (restitution).\nTreasure Items: For items that qualify as \"treasure\" (e.g., the Roman coins), ownership legally vests in the Crown. As regards to our entitlement, while we would still not own the treasure outright, as the rightful landowner at the time of discovery, we would likely be entitled to:\n\nFinder's Reward: We may be entitled to a full reward for the items' market value, which is typically split between the finder and the landowner.\nNegotiation for Display/Ownership: A court ruling of fraud could strengthen our position to negotiate with the Crown or museums regarding long-term loans, permanent display with acknowledgment of our families ownership history, or even specific legislation for their return, similar to how historical restitution cases are handled for other cultural objects.\n\n3. Financial Compensation (Damages and Restitution)\nWe would likely be entitled to significant financial remedies to compensate for losses incurred over decades.\nMesne Profits: This is compensation for the period the land has been unlawfully occupied. It would be the reasonable rental value of the property for the entire period of their possession.\nEquitable Compensation: The court could award compensation for losses that were a direct consequence of the fraud and wrongful dispossession.\nAccount of Profits: In cases of fraud, courts can order the fraudulent party to give up any profits they made from the land that they were not entitled to (e.g., profits from its use or development), reversing their unjust enrichment.\nLegal Costs and Expenses: We could recover the substantial legal fees and associated costs incurred by my father and ourselves in challenging this fraud, concealment etc.\nInterest: Statutory interest would be payable on any awarded compensation from the date the loss occurred until payment.\n\n4. Other Potential Legal Actions\nLien: A court could grant a charging order (a form of lien) against the properties or assets of BP to secure the payment of any substantial financial judgment awarded to us.\nMoral Damages: While less common in the UK than in some other jurisdictions, the egregious nature of deliberate fraud and concealment might lead to consideration of damages for mental anguish, distress, and reputational harm, depending on how the case is argued and the evidence presented.\n\nSummary of Justice\n\"Justice\" in this scenario would be a formal acknowledgment by the highest court that a severe historical wrong occurred through corporate fraud, concealment, collusion, conflicts of interest etc - the list is extensive. The practical entitlement would be the physical return of the ancestral land and artifacts, combined with substantial financial restitution and compensation to restore our families standing. This would be achieved not by forceful acts, but through the due process of the UK legal system, following a successful review of the original case.\n"},{"id":"wb-1d69df5e","subject":"Consortium evidence BP v Buckler","name":"2025-11-23_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","date":"2025-11-23","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2025-11-23_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-evidence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2025-11-23_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Sun, 23 Nov 2025 12:06:34 -0300\nSubject: Re: BP vs Buckler\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V6zpqP8ybZsaZe5sCa36WxkgOHPZDejX7q8U0tfqWTBqg@mail.gmail.com>\n\nGeorge,\n\nSee attached.\n\nRegards,\n\nSion Buckler\n[redacted]\n\n\n\nOn Fri, 14 Nov 2025 at 15:24, Carroll, George D (Cllr)\n<gdcarroll@valeofglamorgan.gov.uk> wrote:\n>\n> Hi Requester\n>\n> Thank you for copying me in on this.\n>\n> If you'd like to discuss, please let me know.\n>\n> All the best,\n>\n> George\n> ________________________________\n> From: Requester <[redacted]>\n> Sent: Friday, November 14, 2025 12:48:36 PM\n> To: kanishka.narayan.mp@parliament.uk <kanishka.narayan.mp@parliament.uk>\n> Subject: BP vs Buckler\n>\n> You don't often get email from [redacted]. Learn why this is important\n>\n> Sir/Ma'am,\n>\n> I am your constituent, living [location redacted]. I am writing to request your assistance in reviewing a historic legal matter involving my family: BP Properties Ltd v Buckler (1987), relating to Great House Farm, Llandough.\n>\n> My family lived on, cared for and claimed to own that farm for generations. The events surrounding the 1987 case resulted in the loss of our home and land, and left several members of my family deeply affected. Recent research I have undertaken has revealed significant procedural inconsistencies, historical irregularities, unanswered questions regarding corporate identity, unverified service of key documents, and heritage considerations relating to the site.\n>\n> I have attached a short report summarising SOME of these issues. I am requesting your help in identifying the appropriate bodies—legal, historical, or governmental—who may be able to independently review the historical record to ensure fairness, accuracy, and transparency.\n>\n> My interest is not solely personal; Great House Farm has a heritage significance to Wales, including documented accounts that Guglielmo Marconi stayed there while experimenting with early radio technology. I believe this should form part of our preserved historical record.\n>\n> By way of background, I am a veteran and have served both in the Armed Forces and recently with the Vale of Glamorgan Council. My intention is simply to seek clarity, proper review, and justice where appropriate. I would be grateful for your guidance on the correct avenues to pursue.\n>\n> Thank you for your time and consideration. I look forward to your response.\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n>\n> Requester\n> [redacted]\n> [location redacted]\n> [redacted]\n> Tel: 0203 137 7118\n"},{"id":"wb-6738259e","subject":"Consortium evidence BP v Buckler","name":"2025-11-26_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2025-11-26","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2025-11-26_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-evidence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2025-11-26_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Wed, 26 Nov 2025 13:03:15 -0300\nSubject: Fwd: RC25-0464 - Information related to Great House Farm, Llandough\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V7Q+z2my9hf-AR47k3OrmgviUJ2FssvKRA6WHPAwJ_odw@mail.gmail.com>\n\n---------- Forwarded message ---------\nFrom: Dr Adam Coward <adam.coward@rcahmw.gov.uk>\nDate: Wed, 26 Nov 2025, 09:48\nSubject: RC25-0464 - Information related to Great House Farm, Llandough\nTo: Requester <[redacted]>\n\n\nGood afternoon,\n\n\n\nThank you very much for your enquiry. While we cannot advise you on a legal\ncase or undertake a review of historical decisions, pleas note that we do\nhold information and archives related to Great House Farm, Llandough.\n\n\n\nThe site entry and description for the site in the National Monuments\nRecord of Wales can be seen online on Coflein, here:\nhttps://coflein.gov.uk/en/site/20182.\n\n\n\nWe hold 14 archives related to the site, a list of which can be seen here:\nhttps://coflein.gov.uk/en/site/20182/archives?size=25. These archives can\nbe viewed in our Search Room, located in the National Library of Wales,\nwhich is open Tuesday through Thursday, 9:30-16:30. If you would like to\nview the material in person, I would advise arranging with us beforehand so\nthat we can prepare the material ahead of your visit. Some of the material\nis on microfiche and as we do not have a microfiche reader in the Search\nRoom, we will need to liaise with the National Library Reading Room staff\nto try to negotiate the use of their machines ahead of your visit.\n\n\n\nWhile it is possible to order scans of material using the attached order\nform, we may be unable to provide research-quality scans or photographs of\nsome of the material, such as the material on microfiche, which would\nrequire high-resolution scanning at a cost of £20 per scan. Moreover,  many\nof these records remain under third-party copyright, and we would only be\nable to provide scans or copies for personal, non-commercial research.\nOther uses, such as use in a legal case or report would likely require the\npermission of the copyright holder(s). I would therefore advise arranging\nto view the material in person rather than ordering scans, if possible.\n\n\n\nIn addition to the information and records which we hold, there are several\nrelated records in the Historic Environment Record maintained by Heneb,\nwhich related to excavations, mostly concerning the cemetery/burial site:\n\n   -\n   https://archwilio.org.uk/arch/query/page.php?watprn=GGAT03584s&dbname=ggat&tbname=core\n   -\n   https://archwilio.org.uk/arch/query/page.php?watprn=GGAT03583s&dbname=ggat&tbname=core\n   -\n   https://archwilio.org.uk/arch/query/page.php?watprn=GGAT03586s&dbname=ggat&tbname=core\n   -\n   https://archwilio.org.uk/arch/query/page.php?watprn=GGAT03585s&dbname=ggat&tbname=core\n\n\n\nThank you very much again for your enquiry.\n\n\n\nSincerely,\n\n\n\nAdam Coward\n\n\n\n\nDr Adam Coward\n Rheolwr Llyfrgell ac Ymholiadau | Library and Enquiries Manager\nFfordd Penglais | Penglais Road,  Aberystwyth,  SY23 3BU\n+44 (0) 1970 621 216 <+44%20(0)%201970%20621%20216>\nadam.coward@cbhc.gov.uk  |  adam.coward@rcahmw.gov.uk\nwww.cbhc.gov.uk <https://cbhc.gov.uk/>  |  www.rcahmw.gov.uk\n<https://rcahmw.gov.uk/>\nNoddir gan Lywodraeth Cymru  |  Sponsored by Welsh Government\n[image: Facebook]\n<https://www.facebook.com/Royal-Commission-on-the-Ancient-and-Historical-Monuments-of-Wales-146120328739808>\n[image: Twitter] <https://www.twitter.com/rcahmwales>\nRydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg a Saesneg. Ni fydd gohebu yn\nGymraeg yn peri oedi.\nWe welcome correspondence in Welsh and English. Corresponding in Welsh\nwill not lead to any delay.\n\n\n\nYn unol â Rheoliadau Safonau'r Gymraeg (Rhif 2) 2016, mae gennych hawl i\ngyfathrebu a gohebu â Chomisiwn Brenhinol Henebion Cymru yn eich dewis\niaith. Er mwyn sicrhau ein bod yn cyflawni’r hawl, rhowch wybod i ni a\nydych yn dymuno derbyn gohebiaeth a/neu alwadau ffôn oddi wrthym yn y\nGymraeg. Bydd yr wybodaeth hon yn cael ei chofnodi gennym ni, a byddwn yn\ndefnyddio'r iaith o’ch dewis ym mhob cyfathrebu yn y dyfodol. Diolch.\n​​​​\nUnder the Welsh Language Standards (No. 2) Regulations 2016, you have the\nright to communicate and correspond with the Royal Commission on the\nAncient and Historical Monuments of Wales in your preferred language. To\nensure we uphold this right, please let us know whether you wish to receive\ncorrespondence and/or telephone calls from us in Welsh. This information\nwill be recorded by us, and we will use your preferred language in all\nfuture communication. Thank you.\n​\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n*From:* Requester <[redacted]>\n*Sent:* 14 November 2025 12:49\n*To:* kanishka.narayan.mp@parliament.uk\n*Subject:* BP vs Buckler\n\n\n\nYou don't often get email from [redacted]. Learn why this is\nimportant <https://aka.ms/LearnAboutSenderIdentification>\n\nSir/Ma'am,\n\nI am your constituent, living [location redacted]. I am writing to request your\nassistance in reviewing a historic legal matter involving my family: *BP\nProperties Ltd v Buckler (1987)*, relating to Great House Farm, Llandough.\n\nMy family lived on, cared for and claimed to own that farm for generations.\nThe events surrounding the 1987 case resulted in the loss of our home and\nland, and left several members of my family deeply affected. Recent\nresearch I have undertaken has revealed significant procedural\ninconsistencies, historical irregularities, unanswered questions regarding\ncorporate identity, unverified service of key documents, and heritage\nconsiderations relating to the site.\n\nI have attached a short report summarising SOME of these issues. I am\nrequesting your help in identifying the appropriate bodies—legal,\nhistorical, or governmental—who may be able to independently review the\nhistorical record to ensure fairness, accuracy, and transparency.\n\nMy interest is not solely personal; Great House Farm has a heritage\nsignificance to Wales, including documented accounts that Guglielmo Marconi\nstayed there while experimenting with early radio technology. I believe\nthis should form part of our preserved historical record.\n\nBy way of background, I am a veteran and have served both in the Armed\nForces and recently with the Vale of Glamorgan Council. My intention is\nsimply to seek clarity, proper review, and justice where appropriate. I\nwould be grateful for your guidance on the correct avenues to pursue.\n\nThank you for your time and consideration. I look forward to your response.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\n\n*Requester*\n[redacted]\n[map link redacted]\n[location redacted]\n[map link redacted]\n[redacted]\n[map link redacted]\nTel: 0203 137 7118\n"},{"id":"wb-11b163b9","subject":"Consortium FOI BP v Buckler","name":"2025-12-23_consortium-foi_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2025-12-23","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2025-12-23_consortium-foi_bpvsbuckler__en_v0-0-1.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-foi-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler","email","ghf"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["bpvsbuckler","email","ghf"],"sourcePath":"text/2025-12-23_consortium-foi_bpvsbuckler__en_v0-0-1.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: FOI Unit <FoiUnit@valeofglamorgan.gov.uk>\nDate: Tue, 23 Dec 2025 15:19:01 +0000\nSubject: Information Request - BP Properties Ltd v Buckler (1987) - Request Reference 00209998\nMessage-ID: <LO0P265MB4608A0533E7DA34C3CAF83679BB5A@LO0P265MB4608.GBRP265.PROD.OUTLOOK.COM>\n\nDear Requester\n\nWe are writing to introduce ourselves as the Council's Information Governance Team, who handle requests for information. Your request regarding the above matter has been referred to us by the Council's Legal Services Department.\n\nWe note that your request relates to a historic matter from 1987. As such, it may take some time for us to check records and consider any information that may be held. Please be aware that, due to the time that has passed, the Council may no longer hold information relating to this matter. If this is the case, we will do our best to offer advice that may assist you in continuing your enquiries.\n\nWe will write to you again as soon as possible. In the meantime, if you have any further questions or need clarification, please don't hesitate to get in touch.\nKind regards\n\nInformation Governance Officer / Swyddog Llywodraethu Gwybodaeth\nDirector's Office - Resources / Swyddfa'r Cyfarwyddwr - Adnoddau\nVale of Glamorgan Council / Cyngor Bro Morgannwg\ntel / ffôn: 01446 700111\ne-mail / e-bost: foiunit@valeofglamorgan.gov.uk or dpo@valeofglamorgan.gov.uk<mailto:dpo@valeofglamorgan.gov.uk>\n\nConsider the environment. Please don't print this e-mail unless you really need to.\nYstyriwch yr amgylchedd. Peidiwch ag argraffu'r neges hon oni bai fod gwir angen.\n\nVisit our Website at www.valeofglamorgan.gov.uk<http://www.valeofglamorgan.gov.uk/>\nEwch i'n gwefan yn www.bromorgannwg.gov.uk<http://www.bromorgannwg.gov.uk/>\n\nFind us on Facebook / Cewch ddod o hyd i ni ar Facebook<https://www.facebook.com/valeofglamorgancouncil>\nFollow us on Twitter / Dilynwch ni ar Twitter<http://twitter.com/vogcouncil>\n\nCorrespondence is welcomed in Welsh or English / Croesewir Gohebiaeth yn y Gymraeg neu yn Saesneg.\n\n"},{"id":"wb-1430fd27","subject":"Formal notice for record church view close Llandough bp vs b","name":"2026-01-01_formal-notice-for-record-church-view-close-llandough-bp-vs-b_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-01-01_formal-notice-for-record-church-view-close-llandough-bp-vs-b_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"formal-notice-for-record-church-view-close-llandough-bp-vs-b","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/2026-01-01_formal-notice-for-record-church-view-close-llandough-bp-vs-b_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester,\r\n\r\nThank you for your e-mail of 10 February whose contents are noted. The National Archives does not hold any records relating to your enquiry. The Manorial Documents Register which this department administers is a register for the location of manorial documents and not of the ownership and descent of manorial titles:\r\n\r\nhttps://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/archives-sector/finding-records-in-discovery-and-other-databases/manorial-documents-register/\r\n\r\nA similar position holds for The National Archives responsibilities for public records and concerns about the past management of official records is a matter for the relevant government department such as HM Land Registry.\r\n\r\nYours sincerely\r\n\r\n\r\n[The National Archives logo]\r\nPhilip Gale | Head of the Collections and Cultural Property Team\r\nT: 020 3908 X 9193 | W: nationalarchives.gov.uk<http://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/>\r\nTwitter: @UkNatArchives\r\nThe National Archives, Kew, Richmond, Surrey TW9 4DU\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n-----Original Message-----\r\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]<mailto:[redacted]>>\r\nSent: 10 February 2026 22:15\r\nTo: Archives Sector Leadership <ASL@nationalarchives.gov.uk<mailto:ASL@nationalarchives.gov.uk>>\r\nCc: reportafraud@landregistry.gov.uk<mailto:reportafraud@landregistry.gov.uk>; enquiries@college-of-arms.gov.uk<mailto:enquiries@college-of-arms.gov.uk>; Paul Egan <clerk@llandough-cc.co.uk<mailto:clerk@llandough-cc.co.uk>>; c1v@valeofglamorgan.gov.uk<mailto:c1v@valeofglamorgan.gov.uk>; ukgeneralenquiries@bp.com<mailto:ukgeneralenquiries@bp.com>\r\nSubject: Formal Notice for Record: Church View Close, Llandough / BP vs Buckler 1987\r\n\r\n        You don't often get email from [redacted]<mailto:[redacted]>. Learn why this is important <https://aka.ms/LearnAboutSenderIdentification>\r\n\r\n\r\nEXTERNAL: This email originates from outside of The National Archives.\r\n\r\nFORMAL NOTICE OF RECORD CHALLENGE, ASSERTION OF RIGHT, AND DEMAND FOR ENGAGEMENT\r\n\r\nDear Sir/Madam,\r\n\r\nThis Notice is issued for placement on official record and for reliance. It is not informal correspondence. I urge you not to resort to silence, deflection, or delay, as doing so following receipt of this notice constitutes knowing reliance upon, and maintenance of, records alleged to be materially defective through fraud, concealment, denial of due process, and systemic erasure. Please report any suspicious activity and discrepancies identified.\r\n\r\nThis Notice is issued in pursuit of narrative justice at this stage; formal judicial remedies are expressly reserved. It serves to document our family's side of the story before it is completely erased and history rewritten, ensuring awareness among custodians of land, heritage, heraldry, archaeology, and public records.\r\n\r\nMy elder brother, Rhys Buckler, and I are the sons of the late William Buckler (d. 1991), defendant in the landmark legal case BP Properties Ltd v Buckler (1987), and the grandchildren of the late Mary Williams, whose dispossession, marginalisation, and exclusion from judicial determination remain unresolved matters of record. Mary Williams was repeatedly and materially falsely addressed/mislabeled in legal and administrative records as \"Mrs Buckler,\" a misidentification that was never judicially tested and which directly affected the handling, scope, and outcome of proceedings. The Williams/Buckler family held claim to the land title, having acquired and occupied Great House Farm continuously since 1667, based on family records, historical evidence, and centuries of resistance to adverse claims.\r\n\r\nI place on record that I am the first member of my family able to speak publicly and formally on these matters. Other close family members remain too traumatised and intimidated by the historical conduct of these proceedings-including violent eviction, demolition, and loss of livelihood-to engage, despite the continuing consequences of dispossession. That silence must not be misread as consent, acquiescence, or abandonment of right. There is a material distinction between free silence and silence produced by fear, trauma, and coercive experience.\r\n\r\nThe story, as we have pieced it together, reveals a multi-generational pattern of lawfare and siege: from Norman/English conquests in 1100 granting lordships over ancient Welsh Christian landscapes, through monastic (1215-1539) and Crown seizures (1539 onward), to industrial encroachments (e.g., 1876 Limeworks severance without consent) and modern corporate possession actions. This culminated in the 1988 demolition of an 800-year-old farmhouse amid police standoffs, leaving the family homeless and the site scarred, now buried under Church View Close. Archaeological vindication came too late-1994 excavations revealed over 1,000 Roman and medieval burials, confirming family warnings (e.g., the 1870 Roman soldier discovery under the living room floor, suppressed by authorities)-yet heritage records celebrate the site while omitting our dispossession.\r\n\r\nThe following assertions of right are now formally placed on record. They have never been lawfully adjudicated or extinguished:\r\n\r\n1.      Rhys Buckler may be the rightful Manorial Lord of the Manor of Llandough, by right of primogeniture and continuous hereditary connection arising from our family's occupation of Great House Farm from 1667 until our narrative, legal, and physical dispossession.\r\n2.      I assert a claim to act as Squire of the Manor of Llandough, as executive representative and guardian of the Buckler-Williams estate and matriarchal lineage, with a mandate to pursue correction of record, restitution, and restorative remedies.\r\n\r\nThese are assertions of right, not settled declarations, placed on record pending proper adjudication-which has been historically obstructed through procedural narrowing, missing documents, and avoidance of full hearings.\r\n\r\nIn 1974, Mary Williams was twice prevented from presenting her case and obtaining a judicial determination of superior ownership, despite claims grounded in long occupation, intent, and possession. The court confined inquiry to animus possidendi (intention to possess) and excluded animus domini (intention to own), reclassifying her from putative owner to mere occupier and foreclosing title adjudication. BP sought possession only, not ownership rulings-a choice unexplained and significant. Proceedings were adjourned or quashed without resolution.\r\n\r\nThis occurred despite evidence of ownership intent, including:\r\n\r\n*       Centuries-long occupation and resistance (e.g., failed 1938 auction due to local support; post-1945 rent disputes).\r\n*       Roman-era remains and historical significance (e.g., Guglielmo Marconi's 1897 residence during radio experiments, with family aid erased from records).\r\n*       Unformalized deals like the 1895-1905 Daniel Thomas quarrying permission for promised title, undermined by vanished deeds.\r\n\r\nJudicial ownership examination would have engaged archaeological/heritage protections, incompatible with redevelopment-consequences avoided, raising credibility concerns.\r\n\r\nSerious unresolved anomalies persist, indicating something is still not right with the record:\r\n\r\n*       Licence correspondence dated 31 October 1974 surfacing only on 4 November, potentially interrupting proceedings to prevent hearings.\r\n*       Claims of 1974 benevolent withdrawal under press scrutiny unsupported by verifiable coverage.\r\n*       Land registration before Mary Williams's 1983 death without title determination; disputes possibly lodged/suppressed by Land Registry.\r\n*       Inconsistent treatment of BP entities across proceedings to family detriment.\r\n*       Procedural barriers preventing effective defense (e.g., interrupted essential services post-licence; 1960-1987 court rulings dismissing ancestral title as \"notion\" without evidence).\r\n*       Knowing bypass of archaeological constraints (e.g., 1978 trenches ignored; no contemporaneous 1970s/1990s press on discoveries amid development).\r\n*       Missing key documents (e.g., 1667 conveyance, 1811-1835 Lambert Williams agreements proving status, 1982-84 library deeds vanishing).\r\n*       Risks of further erasure: Heritage databases celebrate \"Llandough Roman villa cemetery\" while omitting our saga; modern residents unaware of the \"battleground\" site.\r\n\r\nThese anomalies suggest systemic theft, from colonial English subjugation (e.g., 1543-1770 orbit shifts) to corporate siege, during Wales' cultural/economic transitions. Authority derived through such means is illegitimate under natural justice, customary Welsh principles, and moral-legal frameworks. The record's distortion secures advantage, but passage of time does not validate it.\r\n\r\nPreliminary assessment indicates cumulative benefits from redevelopment (absent title extinguishment) exceed £100 million, forming a basis for reparations-covering lost land, artifacts, livelihood, and trauma.\r\n\r\nI require relevant public servants, custodians, and office-holders (including Land Registry, heraldry bodies, museums, archaeologists, MPs, historians, and councillors) to cease delay, engage substantively, review/correct/annotate records where defective, and prevent further rewriting of history. Justice delayed is denied, especially with verifiable evidence available.\r\n\r\nThis Notice:\r\n\r\n*       Challenges the record's accuracy, completeness, and legitimacy.\r\n*       Place assertions of title/standing on record pending adjudication.\r\n*       Reserves all rights without prejudice.\r\n*       Puts officials/beneficiaries on notice that silence/reliance may aggravate responsibility.\r\n\r\nFor the avoidance of doubt, His Majesty the King was notified yesterday.\r\n\r\nThis serves as an invitation to engage constructively before reliance becomes untenable.\r\n\r\nRegards, Requester On behalf of the Buckler-Williams family [redacted], Wales. https://bpvsbuckler.datro.xyz/ <https://bpvsbuckler.datro.xyz/>\r\n\r\nPostscript - Disability and Succession (Material Notice): The heir-apparent to the asserted Manorial Lordship is Rhys Buckler's son, a young man with Down syndrome. Any obstruction, marginalisation, or differential treatment affecting his inheritance would engage the Equality Act 2010 and UK international obligations on disability rights. Disability cannot bar succession or recognition. This is recorded to prevent claims of ignorance.\r\nPlease don't print this e-mail unless you really need to. ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- National Archives Disclaimer This email and any files transmitted with it are intended solely for the use of the individual(s) to whom they are addressed. If you are not the intended recipient and have received this email in error, please notify the sender and delete the email. Opinions, conclusions and other information in this message and attachments that do not relate to the official business of The National Archives are neither given nor endorsed by it. ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n"},{"id":"wb-1877f1dd","subject":"Fwd Tŷ Mawr casa grande Llandough gales","name":"2026-01-01_fwd-ty-mawr-casa-grande-llandough-gales_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filena