ed the 10-bedroom manor house before proper heritage\r\nprotections could be applied. I publicly allege that the British state\r\ncontinues to suppress our heritage while they occupy our stolen land.\r\nMy intention is to settle permanently in Patagonia and rebuild an exact\r\nreplica of that 10-bedroom manorial residence. This space will operate as a\r\nmuseum open to the public to celebrate Welsh culture and commemorate a\r\nhistorical milestone of great relevance: the period when Guglielmo Marconi\r\nstayed with us at our original property while conducting the experiments\r\nthat led to the invention of the radio.\r\nTherefore, I formally request that I be granted land of an equivalent size\r\nto the original property in Llandough, along with the legal authority to\r\nname this new settlement Llandough.\r\nI remain entirely at your disposal to submit all corresponding\r\ndocumentation.\r\nSincerely,\r\nSion Buckler Esq. (Ret'd Sergeant)\r\nhttps://bpvsbuckler.datro.xyz https://wa.me/5491176322115\r\n"},{"id":"wb-58a22206","subject":"Fwd Tŷ Mawr casa grande Llandough gales","name":"2026-01-01_fwd-ty-mawr-casa-grande-llandough-gales_en_v0.0.0.00-2.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-01-01_fwd_%20t%C5%B7%20mawr%20%28casa%20grande%29_%20llandough_%20gales_19e86cf14593ddcf.txt","threadSlug":"fwd-t-mawr-casa-grande-llandough-gales","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/2026-01-01_fwd_ tŷ mawr (casa grande)_ llandough_ gales_19e86cf14593ddcf.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"---------- Forwarded message ---------\r\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]>\r\nDate: Tue, 2 Jun 2026, 02:08\r\nSubject: Tŷ Mawr (Casa Grande), Llandough, Gales\r\nTo: <info@mininterior.gob.ar>\r\nCc: <dinarc@mininterior.gob.ar>\r\n\r\n\r\nA la atención del Ministerio del Interior de la Nación:\r\nMe dirijo a usted con el propósito de solicitar formalmente mi radicación\r\nen la región de la Patagonia y presentar una petición para la concesión de\r\ntierras destinadas a un proyecto de preservación histórica y cultural.\r\nActualmente me encuentro en territorio argentino tras haber sido desposeído\r\ny exiliado de mi hogar solariego de 800 años de antigüedad ubicado en\r\nLlandough, Gales. Si bien la propiedad de la tierra sigue bajo disputa\r\nlegal debido a alegaciones de fraude, el Estado británico tomó posesión de\r\nlos terrenos y demolió la residencia señorial de 10 habitaciones antes de\r\nque se pudieran aplicar las debidas protecciones patrimoniales. Denuncio\r\npúblicamente que el Estado británico continúa suprimiendo nuestra herencia\r\ny ocupando tierras robadas.\r\nMi intención es establecerme definitivamente en la Patagonia y reconstruir\r\nuna réplica exacta de dicha residencia de 10 habitaciones. Este espacio\r\nfuncionará como un museo abierto al público para celebrar la cultura galesa\r\ny conmemorar un hito histórico de gran relevancia: la estancia de Guglielmo\r\nMarconi en nuestra propiedad original mientras realizaba los experimentos\r\nque llevaron a la invención de la radio.\r\nPor lo tanto, solicito formalmente que se me otorguen tierras de una\r\nextensión equivalente a la propiedad original en Llandough, con la\r\nautorización legal para bautizar este nuevo asentamiento con el nombre de\r\nLlandough.\r\nQuedo a su entera disposición para presentar la documentación\r\ncorrespondiente.\r\nAtentamente,\r\nSion Buckler Esq. (Ret'd Sergeant)\r\nhttps://bpvsbuckler.datro.xyz https://wa.me/5491176322115\r\n🏴󠁧󠁢󠁷󠁬󠁳󠁿🏴󠁧󠁢󠁷󠁬󠁳󠁿🏴󠁧󠁢󠁷󠁬󠁳󠁿🏴󠁧󠁢󠁷󠁬󠁳󠁿🏴󠁧󠁢󠁷󠁬󠁳󠁿🏴󠁧󠁢󠁷󠁬󠁳󠁿🏴󠁧󠁢󠁷󠁬󠁳󠁿🏴󠁧󠁢󠁷󠁬󠁳󠁿🏴󠁧󠁢󠁷󠁬󠁳󠁿🏴󠁧󠁢󠁷󠁬󠁳󠁿🏴󠁧󠁢󠁷󠁬󠁳󠁿🏴󠁧󠁢󠁷󠁬󠁳󠁿\r\nEr sylw Gweinidogaeth y Materion Mewnol:\r\nYsgrifennaf atoch er mwyn gwneud cais ffurfiol i ymsefydlu yn rhanbarth\r\nPatagonia ac i gyflwyno cais am ddarn o dir ar gyfer prosiect i warchod\r\nhanes a threftadaeth.\r\nRwyf ar hyn o bryd yn yr Ariannin ar ôl cael fy niarddel a mynd i alltud\r\no'm cartref maenoraidd 800 oed yn Llandochau (Llandough), Cymru. Er bod\r\nperchnogaeth y tir yn dal i gael ei herio'n gyfreithiol oherwydd honiadau o\r\ndwyll, cymerodd y wladwriaeth Brydeinig feddiant o'r safle a dymchwel y tŷ\r\nmaenor 10 ystafell wely cyn y gellid gweithredu amddiffyniadau treftadaeth\r\npriodol. Rwyf yn honni bod y wladwriaeth Brydeinig yn parhau i sipresio ein\r\ntreftadaeth tra eu bod yn meddiannu ein tir a ddygwyd oddi arnom.\r\nFy mwriad yw setlo'n barhaol ym Mhatagonia ac adeiladu replica union o'r tŷ\r\nmaenor 10 ystafell wely hwnnw. Bydd y lle hwn yn gweithredu fel amgueddfa\r\nagored i'r cyhoedd i ddathlu diwylliant Cymru ac i goffau carreg filltir\r\nhanesyddol o bwys mawr: cyfnod Guglielmo Marconi yn aros gyda ni yn ein\r\ncartref gwreiddiol tra ei fod yn dyfeisio'r radio.\r\nFelly, rwyf yn gofyn yn ffurfiol i chi roi tir i mi sydd yr un maint â'r\r\nsafle gwreiddiol yn Llandochau, ac i gael yr awdurdod cyfreithiol i enwi'r\r\nsafle hwn yn Llandochau.\r\nRwyf yn parhau i fod ar gael i gyflwyno'r dogfennau perthnasol.\r\nYn gywir,\r\nSion Buckler Esq. (Ret'd Sergeant)\r\nhttps://bpvsbuckler.datro.xyz https://wa.me/5491176322115\r\n🏴󠁧󠁢󠁥󠁮󠁧󠁿🏴󠁧󠁢󠁥󠁮󠁧󠁿🏴󠁧󠁢󠁥󠁮󠁧󠁿🏴󠁧󠁢󠁥󠁮󠁧󠁿🏴󠁧󠁢󠁥󠁮󠁧󠁿🏴󠁧󠁢󠁥󠁮󠁧󠁿🏴󠁧󠁢󠁥󠁮󠁧󠁿🏴󠁧󠁢󠁥󠁮󠁧󠁿🏴󠁧󠁢󠁥󠁮󠁧󠁿🏴󠁧󠁢󠁥󠁮󠁧󠁿🏴󠁧󠁢󠁥󠁮󠁧󠁿🏴󠁧󠁢󠁥󠁮󠁧󠁿🏴󠁧󠁢󠁥󠁮󠁧󠁿🏴󠁧󠁢󠁥󠁮󠁧󠁿🏴󠁧󠁢󠁥󠁮󠁧󠁿🏴󠁧󠁢󠁥󠁮󠁧󠁿\r\nFor the attention of the Ministry of the Interior:\r\nI am writing to you to formally request residency in the Patagonia region\r\nand to submit a petition for a land concession dedicated to a historical\r\nand cultural preservation project.\r\nI am currently located within Argentine territory after being dispossessed\r\nand exiled from my 800-year-old manorial home in Llandough, Wales. While\r\nthe ownership of the land remains under legal dispute due to my allegations\r\nof fraud and abuse of power, the British state took possession of the\r\nproperty and demolished the 10-bedroom manor house before proper heritage\r\nprotections could be applied. I publicly allege that the British state\r\ncontinues to suppress our heritage while they occupy our stolen land.\r\nMy intention is to settle permanently in Patagonia and rebuild an exact\r\nreplica of that 10-bedroom manorial residence. This space will operate as a\r\nmuseum open to the public to celebrate Welsh culture and commemorate a\r\nhistorical milestone of great relevance: the period when Guglielmo Marconi\r\nstayed with us at our original property while conducting the experiments\r\nthat led to the invention of the radio.\r\nTherefore, I formally request that I be granted land of an equivalent size\r\nto the original property in Llandough, along with the legal authority to\r\nname this new settlement Llandough.\r\nI remain entirely at your disposal to submit all corresponding\r\ndocumentation.\r\nSincerely,\r\nSion Buckler Esq. (Ret'd Sergeant)\r\nhttps://bpvsbuckler.datro.xyz https://wa.me/5491176322115\r\n"},{"id":"wb-31214540","subject":"Rc26 0205 great house farm williams buckler family Llandough","name":"2026-01-01_rc26-0205-great-house-farm-williams-buckler-family-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-01-01_rc26-0205-great-house-farm-williams-buckler-family-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"rc26-0205-great-house-farm-williams-buckler-family-llandough","scopeTopics":["morgan"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["morgan"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-01-01_rc26-0205-great-house-farm-williams-buckler-family-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"Good morning,\r\n\r\nThank you for your email. The full list of archives which we hold related to the site can be seen here: https://coflein.gov.uk/en/sites/20182/archives. Fuller information about the archives can be seen by clicking on the red ‘Archive Number’ for each entry in the list, which will take you to the specific entry for it on our catalogue. This will include information such as titles, dates, and descriptions, albeit we may not have information such as dates for all items.\r\n\r\nAll the best,\r\n\r\nAdam\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nDr Adam Coward\r\n Rheolwr Llyfrgell ac Ymholiadau | Library and Enquiries Manager\r\nFfordd Penglais | Penglais Road, Aberystwyth, SY23 3BU\r\n+44 (0) 1970 621 216\r\nadam.coward@cbhc.gov.uk | adam.coward@rcahmw.gov.uk\r\nwww.cbhc.gov.uk | www.rcahmw.gov.uk\r\nNoddir gan Lywodraeth Cymru | Sponsored by Welsh Government\r\nRydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg a Saesneg. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn peri oedi.\r\nWe welcome correspondence in Welsh and English. Corresponding in Welsh will not lead to any delay.\r\n\r\nYn unol â Rheoliadau Safonau'r Gymraeg (Rhif 2) 2016, mae gennych hawl i gyfathrebu a gohebu â Chomisiwn Brenhinol Henebion Cymru yn eich dewis iaith. Er mwyn sicrhau ein bod yn cyflawni’r hawl, rhowch wybod i ni a ydych yn dymuno derbyn gohebiaeth a/neu alwadau ffôn oddi wrthym yn y Gymraeg. Bydd yr wybodaeth hon yn cael ei chofnodi gennym ni, a byddwn yn defnyddio'r iaith o’ch dewis ym mhob cyfathrebu yn y dyfodol. Diolch.\r\nUnder the Welsh Language Standards (No. 2) Regulations 2016, you have the right to communicate and correspond with the Royal Commission on the Ancient and Historical Monuments of Wales in your preferred language. To ensure we uphold this right, please let us know whether you wish to receive correspondence and/or telephone calls from us in Welsh. This information will be recorded by us, and we will use your preferred language in all future communication. Thank you.\r\n​\r\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]>\r\nSent: 05 June 2026 06:04\r\nTo: Dr Adam Coward <adam.coward@rcahmw.gov.uk>\r\nSubject: Re: RC26-0205 - Great House Farm, Williams/ Buckler Family, Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan\r\n\r\nAdam,\r\n\r\nMight I please get the full archive list with titles, dates and descriptions if you don't already have it. Of the 14 files you cited.\r\n\r\nownership\r\noccupiers\r\nestate history\r\nphotographs before demolition\r\nfarm buildings\r\noral history\r\nsurveys undertaken before development\r\n\r\nRegards,\r\n\r\nSion Buckler\r\nOn Thu, 28 May 2026, 11:47 Dr Adam Coward, <adam.coward@rcahmw.gov.uk<mailto:adam.coward@rcahmw.gov.uk>> wrote:\r\nGood afternoon,\r\n\r\nThank you for your enquiry. As noted in my email of 26 November last, while we cannot advise you on a legal case or undertake a review of historical decisions, we do hold information and archives related to Great House Farm, Llandough.\r\n\r\nThe site entry and description for the site in the National Monuments Record of Wales can be seen online on Coflein, here: https://coflein.gov.uk/en/site/20182.\r\n\r\nWe hold 14 archives related to the site, a list of which can be seen here: https://coflein.gov.uk/en/site/20182/archives?size=25. These archives can be viewed in our Search Room, located in the National Library of Wales, which is open Tuesday through Thursday, 9:30-16:30. If you would like to view the material in person, I would advise arranging with us beforehand so that we can prepare the material ahead of your visit. Some of the material is on microfiche and as we do not have a microfiche reader in the Search Room, we will need to liaise with the National Library Reading Room staff to try to negotiate the use of their machines ahead of your visit. However, I have located an electronic copy of Archive Number 6524751, which can be accessed here: https://www.walesher1974.org/her/app/php/herumd.php?group=GGAT&level=3&docid=301361628\r\n\r\nWhile it is possible to order scans of material using the attached order form, we may be unable to provide research-quality scans or photographs of some of the material, such as the material on microfiche, which would require high-resolution scanning at a cost of £20 per scan. Moreover,  many of these records remain under third-party copyright, and we would only be able to provide scans or copies for personal, non-commercial research. Other uses, such as use in a legal case or report would likely require the permission of the copyright holder(s). I would therefore advise arranging to view the material in person rather than ordering scans, if possible.\r\n\r\nFinally, I have confirmed with our Records Manager that we do not hold any correspondence related to the site.\r\n\r\nThank you again for your enquiry.\r\n\r\nSincerely,\r\n\r\nAdam Coward\r\n\r\n\r\n[cid:image001.png@01DCF729.2DDB7870]\r\nDr Adam Coward\r\n Rheolwr Llyfrgell ac Ymholiadau | Library and Enquiries Manager\r\nFfordd Penglais | Penglais Road,\r\n Aberystwyth,\r\n SY23 3BU\r\n+44 (0) 1970 621 216<tel:+44%20(0)%201970%20621%20216>\r\nadam.coward@cbhc.gov.uk<mailto:adam.coward@cbhc.gov.uk>\r\n |\r\nadam.coward@rcahmw.gov.uk<mailto:adam.coward@rcahmw.gov.uk>\r\nwww.cbhc.gov.uk<https://cbhc.gov.uk/>\r\n|\r\n www.rcahmw.gov.uk<https://rcahmw.gov.uk/>\r\nNoddir gan Lywodraeth Cymru\r\n|\r\n Sponsored by Welsh Government\r\n[Facebook]<https://www.facebook.com/Royal-Commission-on-the-Ancient-and-Historical-Monuments-of-Wales-146120328739808>\r\n[Twitter]<https://www.twitter.com/rcahmwales>\r\nRydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg a Saesneg. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn peri oedi.\r\nWe welcome correspondence in Welsh and English. Corresponding in Welsh will not lead to any delay.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nYn unol â Rheoliadau Safonau'r Gymraeg (Rhif 2) 2016, mae gennych hawl i gyfathrebu a gohebu â Chomisiwn Brenhinol Henebion Cymru yn eich dewis iaith. Er mwyn sicrhau ein bod yn cyflawni’r hawl, rhowch wybod i ni a ydych yn dymuno derbyn gohebiaeth a/neu alwadau ffôn oddi wrthym yn y Gymraeg. Bydd yr wybodaeth hon yn cael ei chofnodi gennym ni, a byddwn yn defnyddio'r iaith o’ch dewis ym mhob cyfathrebu yn y dyfodol. Diolch.\r\n​​​​\r\nUnder the Welsh Language Standards (No. 2) Regulations 2016, you have the right to communicate and correspond with the Royal Commission on the Ancient and Historical Monuments of Wales in your preferred language. To ensure we uphold this right, please let us know whether you wish to receive correspondence and/or telephone calls from us in Welsh. This information will be recorded by us, and we will use your preferred language in all future communication. Thank you.\r\n​\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]<mailto:[redacted]>>\r\nSent: 20 May 2026 22:11\r\nTo: unclehowell <[redacted]<mailto:[redacted]>>\r\nSubject: Great House Farm, Williams/ Buckler Family, Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan\r\n\r\n\r\nSir / Madam,\r\n\r\nTo all those whom it concerns, BCC'd into this email.\r\n\r\nI write to request disclosure under the Freedom of Information Act 2000 and, where applicable, the Environmental Information Regulations 2004.\r\n\r\nThis request concerns Great House Farm / Ty Mawr (Great House), Llandough, associated estate lands, historic occupation and related heritage records. My family is Buckler and before that Williams, and we occupied Great House Farm from 1667 until our forceful dispossession of it in 1988.  Without our ownership ever having been estiguished and without BP ever having a ruling of ownership, only possession.\r\n\r\nIt's alledged our fee simple absolute and manorial title was stolen. And when BP registered our lands in their names in 1982, with our stolen title, my nan who challenged this all her life, during the 2 week land registry window to challenge BP's registration, which they achieved via purgergy and document/ identity fraud, she conveniently died. As my father did after his appeal of the 1987 mistake ruling was mistakely dismissed and his ECHR request blocked and home abruptly demolished, which he was prevented from stopping having been hospitalised and arrested without charges by armed force officers and possible SAS.\r\n\r\nhttps://youtu.be/0eXOoj7eUCo?si=hcgSA2pFgEE943HG\r\n\r\nI understand records may exist within the National Monuments Record of Wales, Historic Environment Record archives, archaeological consultations and planning files, including references already identified as:\r\n\r\n• Great House Farm HER site entry – GGAT03586s\r\n• Land at Great House Farm HER event entry – GGATE003872\r\n• The 1990 archaeological assessment commissioned by BP Properties Ltd.\r\n\r\nPlease provide copies of, or access to:\r\n\r\n  1.  All records concerning Great House Farm / Ty Mawr / Great House Estate, Llandough including:\r\n\r\n• surveys\r\n• reports\r\n• site notes\r\n• photographs\r\n• maps\r\n• aerial imagery\r\n• GIS layers\r\n• archive material\r\n• annotations\r\n• title references\r\n• occupancy references\r\n• landscape records\r\n• archaeological observations.\r\n\r\n  1.  All internal and external correspondence involving:\r\n\r\n• RCAHMW / NMRW\r\n• Cadw\r\n• GGAT / Heneb\r\n• Vale of Glamorgan Council\r\n• BP Properties Ltd\r\n• developers\r\n• planning authorities\r\n• landowners, estate representatives or consultants.\r\n\r\n  1.  Any documents discussing:\r\n\r\n• estate ownership\r\n• occupation history\r\n• family occupation or association\r\n• agricultural use\r\n• commemorative tree planting or memorial planting\r\n• estate boundaries\r\n• title history\r\n• acquisition history\r\n• disputes concerning ownership, heritage status or land use\r\n• estate restructuring, extinguishment, compulsory acquisition or title alteration.\r\n\r\n  1.  Copies of all material linked to GGAT03586s and GGATE003872, including the archaeological assessment and associated correspondence.\r\n  2.  Any records identifying historic occupants, proprietors, tenants or family associations, including references to the Williams family where present within archives.\r\n  3.  Metadata identifying file creators, accession history, provenance, dates and retention schedules.\r\n  4.  File indexes, archive references or schedules identifying further related material not already listed.\r\n\r\nPlease provide information electronically where possible.\r\n\r\nIf any material is withheld, please identify the exemption relied upon and provide the public interest assessment.\r\n\r\nRegards,\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nSion Buckler\r\n\r\n[redacted][map link redacted]\r\n"},{"id":"wb-58386659","subject":"Re external cais am gyngor cyfreithiol Tŷ Mawr Llandough","name":"2026-01-01_re-external-cais-am-gyngor-cyfreithiol-ty-mawr-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-01-01_re_%20%5Bexternal%5D%20cais%20am%20gyngor%20cyfreithiol%20%E2%80%93%20ty%20mawr_%20llandough_19c6c8f9b47267cc.txt","threadSlug":"external-cais-am-gyngor-cyfreithiol-t-mawr-llandough","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/2026-01-01_re_ [external] cais am gyngor cyfreithiol – ty mawr_ llandough_19c6c8f9b47267cc.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"Good afternoon Requester,\r\n\r\nThank you for your enquiry and voicemail.\r\n\r\nFor us to provide a fee quotation we will require sight of some further documents. Please send these through and we can assess if we are able to assist further.\r\n\r\nWhilst we do not provide legal advice through the medium of Welsh, my colleague Rhys Evans is a fluent Welsh speaker and is happy to discuss matters through the medium of Welsh.\r\n\r\nI look forward to hearing from you.\r\n\r\nKind regards,\r\nEmily\r\n\r\n\r\n​​​​\r\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]<mailto:[redacted]>>\r\nSent: 13 February 2026 01:33\r\nTo: UZZI Consulting <uzziconsulting@protonmail.com<mailto:uzziconsulting@protonmail.com>>\r\nCc: PMcardiff@hughjames.com<mailto:PMcardiff@hughjames.com>; Hello <hello@jcpsolicitors.co.uk<mailto:hello@jcpsolicitors.co.uk>>; cardiff@blakemorgan.co.uk<mailto:cardiff@blakemorgan.co.uk>; hello@hevans.com<mailto:hello@hevans.com>\r\nSubject: [EXTERNAL] Cais am Gyngor Cyfreithiol – Ty Mawr, Llandough\r\n\r\n\r\nAnnwyl Syr/Fadam,\r\n\r\nYsgrifennaf i ofyn am gyngor cyfreithiol mewn perthynas â mater cymhleth yn ymwneud â Thŷ Mawr (Great House Farm), Llandough.\r\n\r\nYn gryno, mae’r mater yn codi’r cwestiynau canlynol:\r\n\r\n  *   Diffyg penderfyniad barnwrol ar berchnogaeth cyn achos meddiant yn y 1980au;\r\n  *   Cofrestru cyntaf yn 1982 a allai gynnwys “camgymeriad” o fewn ystyr Atodlen 4 Deddf Cofrestru Tir 2002;\r\n  *   Amnewid neu anghysondeb hunaniaeth yn y disgrifiad cyfreithiol;\r\n  *   Cwestiynau ynghylch ystyriaeth a datgeliad treftadaeth archaeolegol.\r\n\r\nRwy’n ceisio:\r\n\r\n  1.  Barn gynnar ar hyfywedd unrhyw gais i adolygu neu gywiro’r gofrestr;\r\n  2.  Asesiad o’r sefyllfa gyfyngu (limitation);\r\n  3.  Cyngor ar gamau cyn-weithredu priodol.\r\n\r\nRwyf wedi atodi crynodeb gweithredol byr er hwylustod.\r\n\r\nByddwn yn ddiolchgar am gyfle i drefnu ymgynghoriad cychwynnol. Rwy’n barod i dalu am gyngor cychwynnol ffurfiol.\r\n\r\nYn gywir,\r\n\r\nSion Buckler\r\n[redacted]\r\n[redacted]\r\n[Evolve:cb628be1-368d-44fb-9e69-22ad06858d51]\r\n"},{"id":"wb-f435547d","subject":"Re EIR FOI request 00210772 great house farm church view clo","name":"2026-01-01_re-eir_foi-request-00210772-great-house-farm-church-view-clo_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"FOI / EIR correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-01-01_re_%20eir_foi%20request%20-%2000210772%20%28great%20house%20farm%20_%20church%20view%20close_%20llandough%29%20%E2%80%93%20further%20clarifica_19ead60b9c35e41b.txt","threadSlug":"eir-foi-request-00210772-great-house-farm-church-view-clo","scopeTopics":["email","ghf"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email","ghf"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-01-01_re_ eir_foi request - 00210772 (great house farm _ church view close_ llandough) – further clarifica_19ead60b9c35e41b.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester\r\n\r\nThank you for the below. We have passed the information you have provided onto our Planning department who are looking into your request.\r\n\r\nKind regards\r\n\r\nInformation Governance Officer / Swyddog Llywodraethu Gwybodaeth\r\nDirector's Office - Resources / Swyddfa’r Cyfarwyddwr - Adnoddau\r\nVale of Glamorgan Council / Cyngor Bro Morgannwg\r\ntel / ffôn: 01446 700111\r\ne-mail / e-bost: foiunit@valeofglamorgan.gov.uk or dpo@valeofglamorgan.gov.uk<mailto:dpo@valeofglamorgan.gov.uk>\r\n\r\nConsider the environment. Please don't print this e-mail unless you really need to.\r\nYstyriwch yr amgylchedd. Peidiwch ag argraffu'r neges hon oni bai fod gwir angen.\r\n\r\nVisit our Website at www.valeofglamorgan.gov.uk<http://www.valeofglamorgan.gov.uk/>\r\nEwch i'n gwefan yn www.bromorgannwg.gov.uk<http://www.bromorgannwg.gov.uk/>\r\n\r\nFind us on Facebook / Cewch ddod o hyd i ni ar Facebook<https://www.facebook.com/valeofglamorgancouncil>\r\nFollow us on Twitter / Dilynwch ni ar Twitter<http://twitter.com/vogcouncil>\r\n\r\nCorrespondence is welcomed in Welsh or English / Croesewir Gohebiaeth yn y Gymraeg neu yn Saesneg.\r\n\r\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]>\r\nSent: 07 June 2026 07:28\r\nTo: FOI Unit <FoiUnit@valeofglamorgan.gov.uk>\r\nCc: DPO <DPO@valeofglamorgan.gov.uk>\r\nSubject: Re: EIR/FOI request - 00210772 (Great House Farm / Church View Close, Llandough) – Further Clarification and Request for Full Disclosure\r\n\r\n\r\nFOI Unit / Evelyn Morgan / Information Governance Team,\r\n\r\nThank you for your email dated 2 June 2026 and for the assistance provided so far in relation to reference 00210772.\r\n\r\nI appreciate the Council's efforts to support this important family matter and note your confirmation that no records are held regarding the private legal proceedings in BP Properties Ltd v Buckler.\r\n\r\nAs background, this case related to the longstanding claims of my Williams/Buckler family to ownership of Great House Farm (Ty Mawr). My grandmother Mary Williams maintained until her conveniently timed death in 1983 (in the 2 week window to challenge BP's registration of our home and land since 1667, in their names) that our family held ownership of the house following arrangements with Daniel Thomas involving the quarry, with the surrounding farmland subject to a lease that defaulted.\r\n\r\nThere was a conveyance for the surrounding land and attempt to merge our farmhouse and garden into that single plot in 1975. Which was challenged by my nan. Something HM Land Registry correspondence (titles WA231076 and WA240304, first registered to BP Properties Limited in 1982/1983) references these conveyances and the Court of Appeal decision, which may assist in narrowing relevant planning and heritage searches.\r\n\r\nTo enable your Planning, Heritage, and other relevant teams to conduct thorough searches without further delay, I confirm that enquiries should cover the historic Great House Farm / Ty Mawr estate in Llandough (adjacent to and including areas now known as Church View Close, north side of Leckwith Road, title WA231076). This includes any planning permissions, heritage assessments, demolition records (circa 1988), tenancy notations, environmental information, or correspondence from 1875 onwards.\r\n\r\nI am keen to receive a full and timely response within the statutory timeframe, drawing on all information the Council holds across departments. This is of profound personal and historic importance to the Williams family descendants as we seek to document and substantiate our family's position. Should any records have been transferred to Glamorgan Archives or handled in any other way, details of this would be most helpful.\r\n\r\nThank you once again for your understanding and support in this sensitive matter. I look forward to your substantive reply at the earliest opportunity.\r\n\r\nKind regards,\r\n\r\nSion Buckler Esq. (Ret'd Sergeant) bpvsbuckler.datro.xyz<http://bpvsbuckler.datro.xyz/> [redacted] (On behalf of the Williams / Buckler family descendants)\r\n"},{"id":"wb-4629a993","subject":"Re FOI","name":"2026-01-01_re-foi_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"FOI / EIR correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-01-01_re-foi_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"foi","scopeTopics":["email"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-01-01_re-foi_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"Morning,\r\n\r\nThank you for your email.\r\nAs you are requesting information which may be held in archived records, we need further information in order to deal with your request.\r\n\r\nIf you haven’t already, please could you provide the full name, full date of birth and any other relevant information e.g. date of conviction/imprisonment, name of prison, prisoner number, region where individual resided before imprisonment. We will have a better chance of finding the information you seek if you include as much detail as possible.\r\n\r\nYou may wish to note that it is a general policy of the Ministry of Justice (MoJ) not to disclose, to a third party, personal information about another living person. This is because the MoJ has obligations under the Data Protection Act and in law generally to protect this information. Your request for personal information has been considered in line with our obligations under the Freedom of Information Act. In the event that we hold the information you request, we will require the written authority of the Data Subject of the information you want the information about. If the individual is now deceased, we will require a copy of official documentation confirming that they are deceased such as death certificate.\r\n\r\nKind regards\r\n\r\n[signature_1893814455]\r\n\r\nDisclosure Team\r\nSecurity and Information Directorate\r\nService Transformation Group\r\nMinistry of Justice\r\n\r\n\r\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]>\r\nSent: 07 June 2026 10:03\r\nTo: Disclosure Team <data.access@Justice.gov.uk>\r\nSubject: FOI\r\n\r\n\r\nSirs,\r\n\r\nPlease will you locate/access probate records for Mary Williams (d. March 1983), Great House Farm, Llandough—any wills, affidavits, or ownership claims referenced?\r\n\r\nThanks,\r\n\r\nSion Buckler Ret'd Sgt\r\n\r\n________________________________\r\nThis e-mail and any attachments is intended only for the attention of the addressee(s). Its unauthorised use, disclosure, storage or copying is not permitted. If you are not the intended recipient, please destroy all copies and inform the sender by return e-mail. Internet e-mail is not a secure medium. Any reply to this message could be intercepted and read by someone else. Please bear that in mind when deciding whether to send material in response to this message by e-mail. This e-mail (whether you are the sender or the recipient) may be monitored, recorded and retained by the Ministry of Justice. Monitoring / blocking software may be used, and e-mail content may be read at any time. You have a responsibility to ensure laws are not broken when composing or forwarding e-mails and their contents.\r\n"},{"id":"wb-e749caf9","subject":"Re great house farm church view close Llandough nr penarth v","name":"2026-01-01_re-great-house-farm-church-view-close-llandough-nr-penarth-v_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Council and local authority correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-01-01_re-great-house-farm-church-view-close-llandough-nr-penarth-v_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"great-house-farm-church-view-close-llandough-nr-penarth-v","scopeTopics":["morgan"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["morgan"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-01-01_re-great-house-farm-church-view-close-llandough-nr-penarth-v_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"Good morning Requester,\r\n\r\nMy name is Mike Weatherhead. My details are at the end of the below email.\r\n\r\nThanks\r\n\r\n-Mike\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]>\r\nSent: 01 June 2026 23:08\r\nTo: Weatherhead, Michael (LCC - Cadw) <Michael.Weatherhead@gov.wales>\r\nSubject: Re: Great House Farm/ Church View Close, Llandough, nr. Penarth, Vale of Glamorgan (ATISN 27021)\r\n\r\nThank you.\r\n\r\nMight I have your name please?\r\n\r\nForce/Stealth & Secrecy is the basis of the concern I'm raising.\r\n\r\nRegards,\r\n\r\nSion Buckler\r\n\r\nOn Mon, 1 Jun 2026, 12:58 , <Michael.Weatherhead@gov.wales<mailto:Michael.Weatherhead@gov.wales>> wrote:\r\nDear Mr Buckler,\r\n\r\nGreat House Farm, Llandough\r\n\r\nThank you for your request which I received on 1 June 2026. You asked for:\r\n\r\n\r\n  1.  Any consideration of the property for statutory listing.\r\n  2.  Any listing assessments, surveys, inspections, recommendations or reports concerning the building.\r\n  3.  Any correspondence between Cadw, Welsh Office officials, local authorities, conservation bodies, archaeological organisations or owners concerning the historic significance, preservation, demolition or protection of the property.\r\n  4.  Any records explaining why the building was not added to the statutory list of buildings of special architectural or historic interest.\r\n  5.  Any records discussing the age, architectural significance, archaeological significance or surviving historic fabric of the building.\r\n  6.  Any records concerning emergency listing, spot-listing, preservation notices or other forms of protection considered for the property.\r\n  7.  Any records held relating to public campaigns, petitions or representations seeking preservation of the building.\r\n  8.  Any photographs, plans, surveys or architectural records held by Cadw or the Welsh Government relating to Great House Farm.\r\n\r\nYou will receive a response by 25 June 2026. If you have any queries about this request, please do not hesitate to contact me at the address below.  Please remember to quote the reference number above in any future correspondence (ATISN 27021).\r\n\r\nThe request you sent me contains personal information about you - for example, your name and address. The Welsh Government will be the data processor for this information and, in accordance with the General Data Protection Regulation, it will be processed in order to fulfil our public task and meet our legal obligations under the Act to provide you with a response. We will only use this personal information to deal with your request and any matters which arise as a result of it. We will keep your personal information and all other information relating to your request for three years from the date on which your request is finally closed.  Your personal information will then be disposed of securely.\r\n\r\nUnder data protection legislation, you have the right:\r\n\r\n\r\n  *   to be informed of the personal data we hold about you and to access it\r\n  *   to require us to rectify inaccuracies in that data\r\n  *   to (in certain circumstances) object to or restrict processing\r\n  *   for (in certain circumstances) your data to be 'erased'\r\n  *   to (in certain circumstances) data portability\r\n  *   to lodge a complaint with the Information Commissioner's Office (ICO) who is our independent regulator for data protection\r\n\r\nFor further information about the information which the Welsh Government holds and its use, or if you wish to exercise your rights under the GDPR, please see contact details below:\r\n\r\nData Protection Officer\r\nWelsh Government\r\nCathays Park\r\nCARDIFF\r\nCF10 3NQ\r\nEmail: DataProtectionOfficer@gov.wales<mailto:DataProtectionOfficer@gov.wales>\r\n\r\nThe contact details for the Information Commissioner's Office are:\r\n\r\nWycliffe House\r\nWater Lane\r\nWilmslow\r\nCheshire\r\nSK9 5AF\r\nWebsite: www.ico.org.uk<http://www.ico.org.uk/>\r\n\r\nAny information released under the Freedom of Information Act 2000 or Environmental Information Regulations 2004 will be listed in the Welsh Government's Disclosure Log (at https://gov.wales/about/open-government/freedom-of-information/responses/?lang=en).\r\n\r\nYours sincerely,\r\n\r\nMike Weatherhead\r\n\r\nArweinydd Tîm Gwaith Achosion / Casework Team Leader\r\nPolisi a Gwarchod / Policy and Protection\r\nArolygiaeth yr Amgylchedd Hanesyddol / Historic Environment Inspectorate\r\nCadw\r\n\r\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]<mailto:[redacted]>>\r\nSent: 28 May 2026 06:29\r\nTo: Freedom of Information Officer <Freedom.ofinformation@gov.wales<mailto:Freedom.ofinformation@gov.wales>>; Listed Buildings / Adeiladau Rhestredig <listedbuildings@gov.wales<mailto:listedbuildings@gov.wales>>\r\nSubject: Great House Farm/ Church View Close, Llandough, nr. Penarth, Vale of Glamorgan\r\n\r\n\r\nDear Freedom of Information Officer,\r\n\r\nI am requesting information held by the Welsh Government and/or Cadw concerning Great House Farm (also known as Great House or Ty Mawr), Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan, prior to its demolition in 1988.\r\n\r\nI request copies of any recorded information held relating to:\r\n\r\n  1.  Any consideration of the property for statutory listing.\r\n  2.  Any listing assessments, surveys, inspections, recommendations or reports concerning the building.\r\n  3.  Any correspondence between Cadw, Welsh Office officials, local authorities, conservation bodies, archaeological organisations or owners concerning the historic significance, preservation, demolition or protection of the property.\r\n  4.  Any records explaining why the building was not added to the statutory list of buildings of special architectural or historic interest.\r\n  5.  Any records discussing the age, architectural significance, archaeological significance or surviving historic fabric of the building.\r\n  6.  Any records concerning emergency listing, spot-listing, preservation notices or other forms of protection considered for the property.\r\n  7.  Any records held relating to public campaigns, petitions or representations seeking preservation of the building.\r\n  8.  Any photographs, plans, surveys or architectural records held by Cadw or the Welsh Government relating to Great House Farm.\r\n\r\nFor the avoidance of doubt, I am seeking recorded information rather than an explanation or opinion created for the purpose of this request.\r\n\r\nIf any of the requested information is held by another public authority, I would be grateful if you could advise which authority is likely to hold the records.\r\n\r\nI would prefer the information in electronic form.\r\n\r\nYours faithfully,\r\n\r\nSion Buckler Esq. (Ret'd Sergeant). Heir & Successors of Mary Williams, Great House Farm, Llandough\r\nSganiwyd y neges hon am bob feirws hysbys wrth iddi adael Llywodraeth Cymru. Mae Llywodraeth Cymru yn cymryd o ddifrif yr angen i ddiogelu eich data. Os cysylltwch â Llywodraeth Cymru, mae ein hysbysiad preifatrwydd yn esbonio sut rydym yn defnyddio eich gwybodaeth a sut rydym yn diogelu eich preifatrwydd. Rydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg. Byddwn yn anfon ateb yn Gymraeg i ohebiaeth a dderbynnir yn Gymraeg ac ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi. ----------------- On leaving the Welsh Government this email was scanned for all known viruses. The Welsh Government takes the protection of your data seriously. If you contact the Welsh Government then our Privacy Notice<https://gov.wales/about/welsh-government-privacy-notice/?lang=en> explains how we use your information and the ways in which we protect your privacy. We welcome receiving correspondence in Welsh. Any correspondence received in Welsh will be answered in Welsh and corresponding in Welsh will not lead to a delay in responding.\r\nSganiwyd y neges hon am bob feirws hysbys wrth iddi adael Llywodraeth Cymru. Mae Llywodraeth Cymru yn cymryd o ddifrif yr angen i ddiogelu eich data. Os cysylltwch â Llywodraeth Cymru, mae ein hysbysiad preifatrwydd<\"https://gov.wales/about/welsh-government-privacy-notice/?lang=cy\"> yn esbonio sut rydym yn defnyddio eich gwybodaeth a sut rydym yn diogelu eich preifatrwydd. Rydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg. Byddwn yn anfon ateb yn Gymraeg i ohebiaeth a dderbynnir yn Gymraeg ac ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi. ----------------- On leaving the Welsh Government this email was scanned for all known viruses. The Welsh Government takes the protection of your data seriously. If you contact the Welsh Government then our Privacy Notice<https://gov.wales/about/welsh-government-privacy-notice/?lang=en> explains how we use your information and the ways in which we protect your privacy. We welcome receiving correspondence in Welsh. Any correspondence received in Welsh will be answered in Welsh and corresponding in Welsh will not lead to a delay in responding.\r\n"},{"id":"wb-852afa33","subject":"Re great house farm williams buckler family Llandough vale o","name":"2026-01-01_re-great-house-farm-williams-buckler-family-llandough-vale-o_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-01-01_re-great-house-farm-williams-buckler-family-llandough-vale-o_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"great-house-farm-williams-buckler-family-llandough-vale-o","scopeTopics":["morgan"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["morgan"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-01-01_re-great-house-farm-williams-buckler-family-llandough-vale-o_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester,\r\n\r\nThank you for your email.  Heneb is not subject to FOI requests as we are a Limited company.  However, if you fill out our Enquiry Form<https://heneb.jotform.com/form/240392792819366> then we will be happy to assist as best we can.\r\n\r\nKind regards,\r\n\r\nCalli\r\n\r\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]>\r\nSent: 20 May 2026 22:11\r\nTo: unclehowell <[redacted]>\r\nSubject: Great House Farm, Williams/ Buckler Family, Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan\r\n\r\n\r\nSir / Madam,\r\n\r\nTo all those whom it concerns, BCC'd into this email.\r\n\r\nI write to request disclosure under the Freedom of Information Act 2000 and, where applicable, the Environmental Information Regulations 2004.\r\n\r\nThis request concerns Great House Farm / Ty Mawr (Great House), Llandough, associated estate lands, historic occupation and related heritage records. My family is Buckler and before that Williams, and we occupied Great House Farm from 1667 until our forceful dispossession of it in 1988.  Without our ownership ever having been estiguished and without BP ever having a ruling of ownership, only possession.\r\n\r\nIt's alledged our fee simple absolute and manorial title was stolen. And when BP registered our lands in their names in 1982, with our stolen title, my nan who challenged this all her life, during the 2 week land registry window to challenge BP's registration, which they achieved via purgergy and document/ identity fraud, she conveniently died. As my father did after his appeal of the 1987 mistake ruling was mistakely dismissed and his ECHR request blocked and home abruptly demolished, which he was prevented from stopping having been hospitalised and arrested without charges by armed force officers and possible SAS.\r\n\r\nhttps://youtu.be/0eXOoj7eUCo?si=hcgSA2pFgEE943HG<https://link.edgepilot.com/x/KyysHp0HzTbbWfvmjPJQhOs?u=https://youtu.be/0eXOoj7eUCo?si=hcgSA2pFgEE943HG>\r\n\r\nI understand records may exist within the National Monuments Record of Wales, Historic Environment Record archives, archaeological consultations and planning files, including references already identified as:\r\n\r\n• Great House Farm HER site entry – GGAT03586s\r\n• Land at Great House Farm HER event entry – GGATE003872\r\n• The 1990 archaeological assessment commissioned by BP Properties Ltd.\r\n\r\nPlease provide copies of, or access to:\r\n\r\n  1.  All records concerning Great House Farm / Ty Mawr / Great House Estate, Llandough including:\r\n\r\n• surveys\r\n• reports\r\n• site notes\r\n• photographs\r\n• maps\r\n• aerial imagery\r\n• GIS layers\r\n• archive material\r\n• annotations\r\n• title references\r\n• occupancy references\r\n• landscape records\r\n• archaeological observations.\r\n\r\n  1.  All internal and external correspondence involving:\r\n\r\n• RCAHMW / NMRW\r\n• Cadw\r\n• GGAT / Heneb\r\n• Vale of Glamorgan Council\r\n• BP Properties Ltd\r\n• developers\r\n• planning authorities\r\n• landowners, estate representatives or consultants.\r\n\r\n  1.  Any documents discussing:\r\n\r\n• estate ownership\r\n• occupation history\r\n• family occupation or association\r\n• agricultural use\r\n• commemorative tree planting or memorial planting\r\n• estate boundaries\r\n• title history\r\n• acquisition history\r\n• disputes concerning ownership, heritage status or land use\r\n• estate restructuring, extinguishment, compulsory acquisition or title alteration.\r\n\r\n  1.  Copies of all material linked to GGAT03586s and GGATE003872, including the archaeological assessment and associated correspondence.\r\n  2.  Any records identifying historic occupants, proprietors, tenants or family associations, including references to the Williams family where present within archives.\r\n  3.  Metadata identifying file creators, accession history, provenance, dates and retention schedules.\r\n  4.  File indexes, archive references or schedules identifying further related material not already listed.\r\n\r\nPlease provide information electronically where possible.\r\n\r\nIf any material is withheld, please identify the exemption relied upon and provide the public interest assessment.\r\n\r\nRegards,\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nSion Buckler\r\n\r\n[redacted]\r\n\r\n\r\nFlotek Group has scanned and approved this email for your delivery - please note all website links have been replaced with a secure link for your own safety and will be scanned again once clicked.\r\n"},{"id":"wb-359ae968","subject":"Re resolution of historical dispossession and fraud great ho","name":"2026-01-01_re-resolution-of-historical-dispossession-and-fraud-great-ho_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-01-01_re_%20resolution%20of%20historical%20dispossession%20and%20fraud%20%E2%80%93%20great%20house%20farm_%20llandough_%20vale%20of%20glamorga_19e21fb4c5a4eaf1.txt","threadSlug":"solution-of-historical-dispossession-and-fraud-great-ho","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/2026-01-01_re_ resolution of historical dispossession and fraud – great house farm_ llandough_ vale of glamorga_19e21fb4c5a4eaf1.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester,\r\n\r\nThank you for your email. I am currently in the process of setting up a constituency office, and will reply to your email in full as soon as possible.\r\n\r\nKind regards,\r\n\r\nJoe Martin MS\r\n\r\n\r\nSent from Outlook for iOS<https://aka.ms/o0ukef>\r\n________________________________\r\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]>\r\nSent: Tuesday, May 12, 2026 12:18:38 PM\r\nTo: Swyddfa Joe Martin AS - Office of Joe Martin MS <Joe.Martin-MS@senedd.wales>; Anna.Brychan-MS@senedd.wales <Anna.Brychan-MS@senedd.wales>\r\nSubject: Resolution of Historical Dispossession and Fraud – Great House Farm, Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan\r\n\r\n[https://senedd.wales/media/obuo0sep/senedd_logo_xs.png]        E-mail protection couldn't recognize this email as this is the first time you received an email from this sender [redacted]\r\n\r\nSirs/ Ma'ams,\r\n\r\nI write on behalf of myself and my siblings, who are the heirs and successors of the late Mary Williams of the Williams family, the rightful owners and occupiers of Great House Farm, Llandough, since 1667 until our unlawful, unconstitution, brutal and inhumane dispossession by armed force in 1988. With press and courts falsly labelling and treating us as squatters in favor of the false narrative of those who stole my disabled, widow nans fee simple absolute title. For which we remain dispossessed, defrauded and without justice or even narrative justice, unto this day. The public also lost a site of historic and archelogical significants and were lied to about what went on. The land looted of artefacts too, which is a loss to our nation and people.\r\n\r\nI apologise if any of this is incoherent. I am the only member of my family willing to speak out; the rest, who are nearly all women (sisters, mum, aunties etc), are petrified of the repercussions, given what happened to our family in the 1980s. Or dead from this. My father, nan, grandad and possibly great grandad and maybe more, all potentially murdered.\r\n\r\nAll this is also chilling in that the playbook used here, could be industrial scale and nationwide and have occured to other families too. Our case might be the foundations for a class action for the public.\r\n\r\nI write to summarise our position regarding the historical and ongoing dispossession of Great House Farm, Llandough, following the landmark legal case *BP v Buckler* [1987], which I allege involved a myriad of fraud, theft, crimes and proceedural unfairness, gaps in records, supressed and concealed records, human rights violations and literally almost every injustice imaginable; not withstanding it was over decades for our family;\r\n\r\n- **Denial of the right to be heard by the Land Registry.** My grandmother, Mary Williams (falsely named \"Mrs Buckler\" on the licence letter produced by BP in 1974, in order to diminish the Williams ancestral superior land claim), conveniently died while our family was in mourning during the limited window granted by the Land Registry to challenge BP's registration of our land and property in their name in 1982/83. Similarly, my grandfather died before he was able to clarify—even to my grandmother—how her fee simple absolute title deed ended up in BP's possession. Until her dying breath, she maintained her ownership claim, despite repeated unlawful attempts over decades to dislodge the firm position she held regarding her and our undisputed ancestral and superior land claim. The official records and narrative are wrong; there are court errors, suppressed evidence, procedural unfairness, fraud, and the list goes on.\r\n\r\nThe *BP v Buckler* [1987] ruling applied the licence my deceased grandmother held to my father and mother. They never had, nor entered into, any such agreement with BP. The ruling also alleged that my grandmother accepted BP's licence letters by non-response, which was untrue. It further stated that any objection should have been directed to the BP Pension Trust rather than the claimant, BP Properties, which were deemed separate companies. Yet in my father's subsequent appeal against the *BP v Buckler* ruling, his appeal was dismissed on the grounds that the BP companies were effectively the same—so \"heads BP wins, tails we lose.\" Our 800-year-old home was then demolished and all money and possessions removed, despite a court injunction ordering that nothing be removed. My father's ECHR application was blocked; he was arrested without charge, hospitalised, and then he conveniently died at the age of 41. Our remaining family, consisting mostly of women and children, have to this day been too terrified to speak about this case because of the decades of crimes committed against our household by the British State. It was synonymous with a battle or war. I say this as a military warfare professional and Iraq War veteran: a certified and experienced military commander and leader. I concluded my military career with honours as an Acting Sergeant. My profession is cyber and electronic warfare.\r\n\r\nAccording to recently declassified, previously suppressed ITV news coverage from 1988, bailiffs occupied our stolen land and property, and even dislocated my mother's shoulder for simply serving them a lawful court injunction. I also question how ITV came to declassify and publish this footage to YouTube a few months ago, after the months I have spent seeking information. That did not go unnoticed. There is liability there, and I do not accept ITV's belief that this quiet release of suppressed news coverage makes the matter right—though the action is appreciated. How did it come to be suppressed in the first place? And why is it cut abruptly, at what appears to be SAS performing a siege on my family home and dislocating my pregnant mother's shoulder?\r\n\r\nWithout my discovery of fraud this year—which has taken me two decades, a career in military cyber and electronic warfare, and recent innovations in AI to discover and unravel—we had no redress or recourse due to the statute of limitations and the passage of time, notwithstanding that we were all too traumatised, like hostages under terrorists, to speak out. We have received no support to this day for what we were subjected to.\r\n\r\nThis whole affair, even to this day, represents scandalous treatment of me and my family. We never surrendered our claim. The manorial titles of the Manor of Llandough are rightfully ours. The fee simple absolute title has been ours for over a century, a fact that is also suppressed and withheld from the defrauded—and implicated—Land Registry.\r\n\r\nI am also a war veteran who left my military career with honours and was employed until 2024 under the Vale of Glamorgan Council / Welsh Government. I completed my 22 years' service oath *bona fide*. I am an indigenous Welsh Briton, and this is my ancestral birthland (established 1667). The disrespect shown by the Vale MP and Llandough Councillor in simply ignoring me and this matter is abhorrent.\r\n\r\nITV coverage (suppressed until recent months) also reports that neighbours described the eviction as an \"SAS-style operation\" to remove us by force in 1988 (myself included). The men in the video do look like SAS. I have submitted an FOI request to the MOD. Shareholders of mine are SAS, so I shall find out one way or another, even if I receive an NCND reply. This matter was also raised in Parliament, who classified and disposed of the records.\r\n\r\nMargaret Thatcher's administration at the time (the British Government) was evidently assisting BP in clawing assets for the UK Government's sale and exit at the time, which might explain how 30 police—and possibly SAS—were used to rip us from our home and land. The human rights violations remain unanswered.\r\n\r\nThe Land Registry ultimately had checks and balances lifted and total free rein during Thatcher's reign, while BP's lawyers evidently committed perjury in their declarations to the Land Registry, withholding information from it and supplying it with false information.\r\n\r\nAll the while, Welsh television was simultaneously interviewing Gaddafi in Libya in 1988, where he accused Thatcher and the British State of being the real terrorists, not him. My oath ended in 2024; I am also a Welsh national. I have no allegiance to the British State. If it acts like my enemy, it is my enemy. I am absolutely a Welsh national and counter-officer now that it is so evident the British State waged civil war upon us indigenous Britons—me and my family included—on our ancestral birthland.\r\n\r\nOther media coverage alleged by UK courts to exist from 1974 seems non-existent or suppressed too. Much is behind paywalls. This whole case needs to be in the public eye. I have put everyone I can think of on notice.\r\n\r\nFraud and procedural unfairness are ultimately rampant in the case of my family's dispossession. Aside from all my family members dying at convenient junctures instrumental in the windows to change the outcome, I still have major concerns regarding how the MOJ took months to respond only to fob me off last week; how the MP fobbed me off and will not provide a proper response; how CADW and Llandough Parish allege this is nothing to do with them, despite CADW failing to list the farmhouse as Grade II so it could be demolished (as per a 1,700-signature petition, which was practically the whole town at the time); the current councillor for Llandough failing to reply properly; and the fact that the National Archives report gaps, missing records, and suppressed records. Notwithstanding that Marconi was staying with us while inventing radio, and the town will not even put a plaque up to commemorate this, which is further erasure of the history and known (but suppressed) archaeological and historic significance of the site. In BP's planning application records, they state that our 800-year-old former courthouse home was merely an old, dilapidated building.\r\n\r\nNotwithstanding the suppression of the 1870 Roman soldier remains we discovered when changing our floor from stone to timber, also suppressed to hide the site's archaeological significance so that housing development could be unlawfully approved. BP did not even seek a ruling on ownership; they only sought possession, most likely so that the discovery and significance of the site's archaeology could be pretended not to be known until the excavation of our land in 1994—which is further evidence of blatant conspiracy and fraud.\r\n\r\nThese are the actions of a crime or terrorist network, seeking to install a terrorist cell or camp. It is worse than simply the British colonising Wales.\r\n\r\nNotwithstanding the child graves secretly moved from the church next door during the excavation, which seemed to take ten years to be properly reported on, and even then their numbers in the report appear to be underrepresented.\r\n\r\nThe scandal continues endlessly. There are court errors too. The fancy Latin words diminish and falsely describe my grandmother's land claim and superior ownership (it was \"older than man can remember,\" not merely a \"claim to the land\"). The language used by the court clearly demonstrates bias prior to the ruling—language implying that my late grandmother, Mrs Williams (*not* Mrs Buckler, as they falsely named her in document fraud), was imagining her ancestral superior land claim, in order to discredit her through carefully selected judicial wording.\r\n\r\nMy siblings and I remain the heirs and successors and landowners. Nothing has lawfully broken or undone that claim. We demand that the fraud, corruption, gaps in records, false records, and myriad crimes committed against us be addressed and not ignored. There should be a Senedd public inquiry into this. My family should not be dispossessed a second longer. We are seriously looking at £100 million in reparations if the truth of this matter becomes known, given the scale of the scandal and injustice.\r\n\r\nIn 1667 we acquired the land and property in a 999-year lease (virtual freehold). In the early 1900s we obtained the superior paper title—free and clear, outright equitable ownership—via the deal with Bute Estate quarryman Daniel Thomas, which led to the tree-planting ceremony to affirm this in history. *(See attached.)*\r\n\r\nFrederick Buckler's transfer of Mrs Williams's equitable title to BP and their predecessors, without her knowledge or consent, is not legitimate and should never have stood, nor does it stand to reason. His inability to clarify the details of the alleged transaction due to his convenient death afterwards does not make it right, as Mary Williams maintained her position after his death. It was never his title to sell, even if he did. My grandmother was left unable to prove her standing, and the 1974 hearing was adjourned. The dismissal of the second hearings in 1974 due to the licence letter issued by BP (resorting to identity and document fraud) does not negate the fact that there was never a ruling stating BP owned our land, despite their purchasing my grandmother's stolen fee simple absolute title deed. BP only ever sought possession. They could not obtain a ruling because it would have meant discovery, unveiling the archaeological significance that would have prevented land and housing development.\r\n\r\nThis was a coordinated campaign of fraud, stealth, and administrative erasure spanning decades, employing force, secrecy, and fraudulent licence. By hook and by crook we were dispossessed of our land. Thatcher—and potentially the SAS—make it, at the least, an allodial/eminent domain seizure, in which case we should still be due compensation.\r\n\r\nI fail to see the proclaimed authority of the other men on my land, alleging themselves to be a government and citizens rather than terrorists, who both ostracised and held my family hostage, if legitimacy does not exist. You need legitimacy to claim authority.\r\n\r\n**Summary of the Fraudulent Dispossession:**\r\n\r\n**The Title Theft:** Evidence supports that a \"Title-for-Quarrying\" agreement existed with Daniel Thomas of the Bute Estate. We obtained this fee simple absolute title lawfully, but it was later secretly and fraudulently taken and sold back to Bute / Western Ground Rents / BP without the knowledge or consent of the rightful owner, Mary Williams. Parliament even stated on record how criminal Western Ground Rents were.\r\n\r\n**Procedural Fraud (The 1974 Licence):** The 1987 Court of Appeal ruling was predicated on a unilateral \"licence letter\" issued by the landlords' solicitors. We reject this ruling. It should be set aside on grounds of fraud.\r\n\r\n**Identity Erasure:** Throughout all legal proceedings, Mary Williams was intentionally misidentified as \"Mrs Buckler.\" This was a calculated tactic to strip her of her ancestral \"Williams\" lineage and the superior land rights and claim associated with it.\r\n\r\n**Exploitation of Mortality:**\r\n- **Frederick Buckler:** Died before the circumstances of how Mary was dispossessed of her title—and how the other parties regained possession after the lawful Daniel Thomas (and/or Anthony Williams) dealings with Bute Estate—could be clarified. This is inconsequential, much like Mary's own death; neither changed her position on the matter, which she did voice, despite what the 1987 ruling falsely alleges.\r\n- **Mary Williams:** Died during the precise two-week window required to rebut the Land Registry's registration of the land in BP's name—a window we believe was exploited to finalise this theft. At minimum, this is the case; it is more likely she was murdered. Her husband before her too. And my father, William Buckler, given the convenient timing of all their deaths—not to mention the unfair dismissal of my father's appeal, the blocking of his ECHR request, the theft and demolition of his property and our home prior to his death.\r\n\r\n**Our Position:**\r\nThe Williams family (now the Buckler family) has never ceased our claim. We maintain that the land was taken through criminal stealth and that the current registered titles are poisoned by this original fraud. We seek full reparations for the loss of ancestral wealth, the destruction of the Roman archaeological site on the property, and the systematic denial of our right to a fair trial.\r\n\r\nPlease provide a formal response and update regarding your position on this matter within 14 days. Please also hold to account those taking steps back into the shadows, as this only makes the matter more clandestine and therefore makes me very jumpy as a military warfare professional. I already fly in and out as if on another tour of the Middle East, and my family remain hostages under terrorists.\r\n\r\nI would also ask that Companies House, who have recently tried to criminalise me, give me some space. They gave me three options to submit ID: a questionnaire with passport and driving licence (which I provided); Post Office verification; or Digital ID. I chose the first option. They will not accept it. But I will not be lured to a location of their choosing, nor will I put any of their tracking ID applications on my devices. I do not trust the regime on that island. Until this and another unnamed matter of similar scale is resolved, I consider the British Government a terrorist organisation and do not recognise its authority. My family there are hostages under said terrorists. And Church View Close, Llandough—23 houses—is a terrorist cell/camp on my land.\r\n\r\nMarconi was staying with us in Llandough when he invented radio. This too was covered up. Cardiff City will not put a plaque naming Marconi as the inventor on the statue of a radio at Cardiff Bay—not even a plaque to commemorate it. Llandough should be a tourist attraction based on this fact alone, rather than the occupying terrorist network's cell of 23 houses, as I see it.\r\n\r\nRegards,\r\n\r\nSion Buckler Esq. (Ret'd Sgt)\r\nOn behalf of (and as one of) the Heirs and Successors of Mary Williams, Great House Farm, Manor of Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan\r\n\r\nCurrent Address: [redacted]\r\n\r\n---\r\n"},{"id":"wb-c5d0c647","subject":"Re subject freedom of information request Tŷ Mawr great hous","name":"2026-01-01_re-subject-freedom-of-information-request-ty-mawr-great-hous_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"FOI / EIR correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-01-01_re-subject-freedom-of-information-request-ty-mawr-great-hous_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"subject-freedom-of-information-request-t-mawr-great-hous","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/2026-01-01_re-subject-freedom-of-information-request-ty-mawr-great-hous_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester\r\n\r\nThank you for your email, however the information you have provided does not assist us in locating the title number.\r\n\r\nWe are unable to proceed with your Freedom of Information request without this information.\r\n\r\nYou may wish to contact our Customer Support Team who are trained to assist you with locating information that is held on the register of title.\r\n\r\nTheir contact details are as follows:\r\n\r\nHM Land Registry\r\nCitizen Centre\r\nPO Box 7806\r\nBilston\r\nWV1 9QR\r\n\r\nEnglish language phone 0300 006 0411\r\nWelsh language phone 0300 006 0422\r\n\r\nTelephone lines are open between 08:00 and 16:30 Monday to Friday. This does not include Bank Holidays.\r\n\r\nWebsite: www.gov.uk/guidance/contact-hm-land-registry<http://www.gov.uk/guidance/contact-hm-land-registry>\r\nOnline contact form: HM Land Registry Contact Us form<https://customerhelp.landregistry.gov.uk/guide-page-external/?stepid=2b31bc48-eb96-eb11-b1ac-002248413956>\r\n\r\nThere is no direct contact e-mail address for Customer Support. Please use the online contact form and any responses will be issued via e-mail.\r\n\r\nYours sincerely\r\n\r\n[logo]\r\nAmanda Whittaker PC.foi\r\nDisclosure Officer\r\nInformation Rights Team\r\nData Group\r\nData and Register Integrity Group\r\nEmail: foi@landregistry.gov.uk<mailto:foi@landregistry.gov.uk> / dataprotection@landregistry.gov.uk<mailto:dataprotection@landregistry.gov.uk>\r\nGOV.UK<https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/land-registry> | @HMLandRegistry<https://twitter.com/HMLandRegistry> | LinkedIn<http://www.linkedin.com/company/land-registry> | Facebook<http://www.facebook.com/pages/Land-Registry/343223435769541>\r\n[cid:image002.png@01DCB2DB.DB38D8B0]           [cid:image003.png@01DCB2DB.DB38D8B0]\r\n\r\n\r\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]>\r\nSent: 12 March 2026 18:44\r\nTo: _FOI email address <FOI@landregistry.gov.uk>\r\nSubject: Re: Subject: Freedom of Information Request - Ty Mawr (Great House), Llandough, near Penarth (1938-1987) F260213\r\n\r\nHi,\r\n\r\nThanks for your reply.\r\nYour agency already responded some weeks ago to say that the registration by BP of Great House Farm, Llandough in 1982, was being scanned.\r\nI have yet to receive a response about that, but that is the land parcel in question. Now known as Church View Close, Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan.\r\n\r\nRegards,\r\n\r\nSion Buckler\r\n\r\nOn Thu, 12 Mar 2026 at 09:21, _FOI email address <FOI@landregistry.gov.uk<mailto:FOI@landregistry.gov.uk>> wrote:\r\nDear Requester\r\n\r\nIn order to proceed with your request we require some further information:\r\n\r\n\r\n  *   Please provide the title number in which you require this information for\r\n\r\nYou may need to apply for a Search of the Index Map to find out if a specific property or area of land is registered and, if it is, the title number(s) of that land. Further information on this service is provided below.\r\n\r\nOnce you have sufficiently clarified the relevant land in which you would like this information for, we will proceed with your request.\r\n\r\nTo assist you in accessing information held by HMLR, please be aware the information you have requested is held within our registers that are open to public inspection and accessible via statutory routes of access. This means that we are unable to provide access to this information via an Environmental Information Regulations (EIR) or a Freedom of Information Act 2000 (FOIA) request and it is likely a refusal notice will be issued for this information.\r\n\r\nInformation about how to apply to inspect or obtain official copies is contained with practice guide 11:\r\nwww.gov.uk/government/publications/inspection-and-application-for-official-copies/practice-guide-11-inspection-and-application-for-official-copies<http://www.gov.uk/government/publications/inspection-and-application-for-official-copies/practice-guide-11-inspection-and-application-for-official-copies>\r\n\r\nOur Customer Support colleagues are trained to handle queries which relate to information that is held on the register of title\r\nIf you require any assistance in completing the application form or to determine which documents to order, please contact our Customer Support Team who will be able to assist you with your query.\r\n\r\nTheir contact details are as follows:\r\n\r\nEnglish language phone 0300 006 0411\r\nWelsh language phone 0300 006 0422\r\n\r\nTelephone lines are open between 08:00 and 17:00 Monday to Friday This does not include Bank Holidays.\r\nWebsite: www.gov.uk/guidance/contact-hm-land-registry<http://www.gov.uk/guidance/contact-hm-land-registry>\r\n\r\nWhat is the Index Map?\r\nHM Land Registry keeps a computerised 'index map' based on the Ordnance Survey map. This computerised map provides an index of the land in every registered title and pending application for first registration.\r\n\r\nApplying for an Index Map Search\r\nYou apply for an official search of the index map using form SIM<https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/index-map-application-for-an-official-search-sim>.\r\nThis will need to be printed, completed and sent to our Standard address<https://www.gov.uk/guidance/hm-land-registry-address-for-applications#standard-addresses>.\r\nYou must attach a plan to show the land that you are interested in, it should:\r\n\r\n\r\n  *        be drawn to scale\r\n  *        be based on the current large scale Ordnance Survey map\r\n  *        indicate scale and north point\r\n  *        show property extent with suitable colouring and position in relation to nearby roads and other features plans of flats and maisonettes\r\n     indicate floor level (if any) of the property.\r\n  *\r\nYou can also search by map on our Search for land and property information<https://www.gov.uk/search-property-information-land-registry> service.\r\n\r\nYours sincerely\r\n\r\n[logo]\r\nAmanda Whittaker PC.foi\r\nDisclosure Officer\r\nInformation Rights Team\r\nData Group\r\nData and Register Integrity Group\r\nEmail: foi@landregistry.gov.uk<mailto:foi@landregistry.gov.uk> / dataprotection@landregistry.gov.uk<mailto:dataprotection@landregistry.gov.uk>\r\nGOV.UK<https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/land-registry> | @HMLandRegistry<https://twitter.com/HMLandRegistry> | LinkedIn<http://www.linkedin.com/company/land-registry> | Facebook<http://www.facebook.com/pages/Land-Registry/343223435769541>\r\n[cid:image002.png@01DCB2DB.DB38D8B0]           [cid:image003.png@01DCB2DB.DB38D8B0]\r\n\r\n\r\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]<mailto:[redacted]>>\r\nSent: 16 February 2026 01:03\r\nTo: _FOI email address <FOI@landregistry.gov.uk<mailto:FOI@landregistry.gov.uk>>; foiunit@valeofglamorgan.gov.uk<mailto:foiunit@valeofglamorgan.gov.uk>\r\nSubject: Subject: Freedom of Information Request - Ty Mawr (Great House), Llandough, near Penarth (1938-1987)\r\n\r\nSome people who received this message don't often get email from [redacted]<mailto:[redacted]>. Learn why this is important<https://aka.ms/LearnAboutSenderIdentification>\r\nSir/Madam,\r\n\r\nI understand you may already be busy, so please don't feel overwhelmed by this FOI request—I'm conducting my own diligence and happy for you to take the full statutory 20 working days to respond.\r\n\r\nUnder the Freedom of Information Act 2000, I request copies of all records, correspondence, memoranda, internal notes, minutes, file notes, land registration applications, objections, or communications held by HM Land Registry from 1 January 1938 to 31 December 1987 relating to:Ty Mawr (Great House), Llandough, near Penarth, South Glamorgan (also known as Great House Farm).\r\n\r\nLand registration applications, title transfers, or dealings involving Mountjoy Ltd, Western Ground Rents Ltd, BP Pension Trust Ltd, and/or BP Properties Ltd (including the 1938 auction/reversion conveyance and 1969 sale).\r\n\r\nInteractions, notices, proceedings, tenancy offers, licences (e.g., 1974 unilateral lifetime licence letters), or disputes involving Mrs Mary Williams (also referred to as Mrs Buckler), Mr Frederick Buckler, or Williams/Buckler family occupiers—particularly the 1962 Cardiff County Court possession proceedings (Dec 11 order by Judge Temple Morris QC).\r\n\r\nAny possession proceedings, adverse possession claims, or title objections connected to the property (e.g., 1955 High Court, 1962 Cardiff County Court, 1974 actions).\r\n\r\nPlease include archived, off-site, or scanned records where applicable.If exemptions apply (e.g., Section 40 personal data), specify which and why. If costs exceed the appropriate limit, advise how to refine (e.g., prioritize 1955-1987 or 1962/1974 events).\r\n\r\nI look forward to your response within 20 working days.\r\n\r\nYours faithfully,\r\n\r\nSion Buckler Esq. Sgt (Ret'd)\r\n\r\nHM Land Registry’s purpose is to protect your land ownership and provide services and data that underpin an efficient and informed property market. Our vision is: “A world-leading property market as part of a thriving economy and a sustainable future”.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nWe check all mail and attachments for known viruses. However, you are advised that you open any attachments at your own risk. If you have received this email and it was not intended for you, please let us know, then delete it.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nWe welcome correspondence in English and Welsh.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nTo see how HM Land Registry treats your personal information, read our Personal information charter: www.gov.uk/government/organisations/land-registry/about/personal-information-charter<http://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/land-registry/about/personal-information-charter>.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nHM Land Registry’s purpose is to protect your land ownership and provide services and data that underpin an efficient and informed property market. Our vision is: “A world-leading property market as part of a thriving economy and a sustainable future”.\r\n\r\nWe check all mail and attachments for known viruses. However, you are advised that you open any attachments at your own risk. If you have received this email and it was not intended for you, please let us know, then delete it.\r\n\r\nWe welcome correspondence in English and Welsh.\r\n\r\nTo see how HM Land Registry treats your personal information, read our Personal information charter: www.gov.uk/government/organisations/land-registry/about/personal-information-charter.\r\n\r\n\r\n"},{"id":"wb-69bfe261","subject":"Consortium evidence BP v Buckler","name":"2026-01-16_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2026-01-16","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-01-16_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-evidence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-01-16_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Fri, 16 Jan 2026 01:35:44 -0300\nSubject: Fwd: FW: BP vs Buckler\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V4PbBZnnjf=AHCZzeLFGhf5rcvAEDENzpz+CBU2BOgoOw@mail.gmail.com>\n\nHi,\n\nI have something below you maybe interested in.\n\nI used the latest AI models to unveil consealment and fraud and miscarriage\nof just. Which led my family to be forced off our 800 year old farm in the\nlate 80's.\n\nThis means I can re open this case and sue. And I want to. So does my\nfamily. The latest AI estimate some £40M in damages for my family if what\nI've unveiled can be won in a court of law.\n\nRegards,\n\nSion Buckler\n[redacted]\n\n\n\n\n\n---------- Forwarded message ---------\nFrom: NARAYAN, Kanishka (MP) <kanishka.narayan.mp@parliament.uk>\nDate: Mon, 17 Nov 2025, 06:59\nSubject: FW: BP vs Buckler\nTo: [redacted] <[redacted]>\n\n\nDear Soin,\n\n\n\nThank you for your email to Kanishka Narayan MP and for passing on your\nconcerns. [location redacted] unfortunately does not fall within the Vale of\nGlamorgan Parliamentary Constituency and Parliamentary protocol states that\nKanishka is unable to assist anyone outside his constituency.\n\n\n\nI would like to recommend contacting your MP, Stephen Doughty. His contact\ndetails are:\n\n\n\nPhone Number: 020 7219 5348\n\nEmail Address: stephen.doughty.mp@parliament.uk\n\n\n\nI hope they are able to assist you.\n\n\n\nKind regards,\nMadelene\n\n\n\n\n*Madelene Larsson Constituency Support Manager, Kanishka Narayan MP*\n\nMember of Parliament for the Vale of Glamorgan\nParliamentary Under-Secretary of State in the Department for Science,\nInnovation and Technology\n\n\n\nUK Parliament Disclaimer: this e-mail is confidential to the intended\nrecipient. If you have received it in error, please notify the sender and\ndelete it from your system. Any unauthorised use, disclosure, or copying is\nnot permitted. This e-mail has been checked for viruses, but no liability\nis accepted for any damage caused by any virus transmitted by this e-mail.\nThis e-mail address is not secure, is not encrypted and should not be used\nfor sensitive data.\n\n\n\n\n\n*From:* Requester <[redacted]>\n*Sent:* 14 November 2025 12:49\n*To:* NARAYAN, Kanishka (MP) <kanishka.narayan.mp@parliament.uk>\n*Subject:* BP vs Buckler\n\n\n\nSir/Ma'am,\n\nI am your constituent, living [location redacted]. I am writing to request your\nassistance in reviewing a historic legal matter involving my family: *BP\nProperties Ltd v Buckler (1987)*, relating to Great House Farm, Llandough.\n\nMy family lived on, cared for and claimed to own that farm for generations.\nThe events surrounding the 1987 case resulted in the loss of our home and\nland, and left several members of my family deeply affected. Recent\nresearch I have undertaken has revealed significant procedural\ninconsistencies, historical irregularities, unanswered questions regarding\ncorporate identity, unverified service of key documents, and heritage\nconsiderations relating to the site.\n\nI have attached a short report summarising SOME of these issues. I am\nrequesting your help in identifying the appropriate bodies—legal,\nhistorical, or governmental—who may be able to independently review the\nhistorical record to ensure fairness, accuracy, and transparency.\n\nMy interest is not solely personal; Great House Farm has a heritage\nsignificance to Wales, including documented accounts that Guglielmo Marconi\nstayed there while experimenting with early radio technology. I believe\nthis should form part of our preserved historical record.\n\nBy way of background, I am a veteran and have served both in the Armed\nForces and recently with the Vale of Glamorgan Council. My intention is\nsimply to seek clarity, proper review, and justice where appropriate. I\nwould be grateful for your guidance on the correct avenues to pursue.\n\nThank you for your time and consideration. I look forward to your response.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\n\n*Requester*\n[redacted]\n[map link redacted]\n[location redacted]\n[map link redacted]\n[redacted]\n[map link redacted]\nTel: 0203 137 7118\nUK Parliament Disclaimer: this e-mail is confidential to the intended\nrecipient. If you have received it in error, please notify the sender and\ndelete it from your system. Any unauthorised use, disclosure, or copying is\nnot permitted. This e-mail has been checked for viruses, but no liability\nis accepted for any damage caused by any virus transmitted by this e-mail.\nThis e-mail address is not secure, is not encrypted and should not be used\nfor sensitive data.\n"},{"id":"wb-ff0c3dbe","subject":"Consortium evidence BP v Buckler","name":"2026-01-28_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2026-01-28","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-01-28_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler__en_v0-0-1.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-evidence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-01-28_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler__en_v0-0-1.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Wed, 28 Jan 2026 03:43:18 -0300\nSubject: For the Attention of Group General Counsel – BP Properties Ltd v Buckler [1987] EWCA Civ 2\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V53nS0SOvgKGEb0wxw_NRdDZbCf5Zs+kaLq6JxchkMThw@mail.gmail.com>\n\nDear Group General Counsel,\n\nI am writing in connection with the historical case of BP Properties Ltd v\nBuckler [1987] EWCA Civ 2, involving my late grandmother, Mrs. Buckler, and\nlater my father as parties.\n\nThis is a courtesy notification rather than the initiation of any formal\nproceedings. In reviewing family records and related documents, I have\nuncovered substantial evidence suggesting material inconsistencies in the\nfacts presented during the original 1974 proceedings and the 1987 appeal.\nThese include questions around BP Properties Ltd's title and capacity to\nissue the 1974 unilateral licence at the time it was granted, as the\nunderlying ownership dispute appears to have been adjourned without\nresolution. Additionally, there are indications of procedural\nirregularities, such as the characterization of Mrs. Buckler's responses\n(including her assertion of ownership), inconsistent treatment of corporate\nentities (BP Pension Trust Ltd and BP Properties Ltd), and potential\nunsubstantiated claims regarding public or media involvement that\ninfluenced the case's direction.\n\nThese findings raise concerns under the Limitation Act 1980 s.32 regarding\ndeliberate concealment or fraud, as well as broader issues of procedural\nfairness that could warrant reopening the matter under CPR 52.30 (Taylor v\nLawrence principles). I am in the process of compiling a full evidential\ndossier and consulting on potential next steps, which may include a formal\napplication to the Court of Appeal.\n\nGiven the case's significance in land law precedent and BP's historical\ncontext as a partially state-owned entity at the time, I recognize this\ncould carry reputational implications today. Before pursuing any public\navenues—such as sharing the story via social media, documentary formats, or\nlegal escalation—I believe it is appropriate to extend BP the opportunity\nfor early, private dialogue. I am open to exploring a mutually agreeable\nresolution, potentially including confidential terms, to address the\nperceived historical injustices without further publicity or litigation.\n\nI would appreciate confirmation of receipt and an indication within 14 days\nas to whether BP is prepared to engage in preliminary discussions. This\nwould allow me to determine the most constructive path forward.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nSion Buckler [redacted]\n"},{"id":"wb-168a5bd1","subject":"Consortium FOI BP v Buckler","name":"2026-01-28_consortium-foi_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2026-01-28","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-01-28_consortium-foi_bpvsbuckler__en_v0-0-1.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-foi-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler","email","ghf"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["bpvsbuckler","email","ghf"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-01-28_consortium-foi_bpvsbuckler__en_v0-0-1.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: do-not-reply-to-this-address@whatdotheyknow.com\nDate: Wed, 28 Jan 2026 06:46:05 +0000\nSubject: New response to your FOI request - Freedom of Information Request – Administrative Records for BP Properties Ltd v Buckler (1974)\nMessage-ID: <6979b0ad1fef4_5dff887088514@mstrwdtkhexa.srv.mysociety.org.mail>\n\nYou have a new response to the Freedom of Information request 'Freedom of Information Request – Administrative Records for BP Properties Ltd v Buckler (1974)' that you made to HM Courts and Tribunals Service.\n\nTo view the response, click on the link below.\n\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/request/freedom_of_information_request_a_2133?nocache=incoming-3289580#incoming-3289580\n\nWhen you get there, please update the status to say if the response contains any useful information.\n\nAlthough all responses are automatically published, we depend on you, the original requester, to evaluate them.\n\n-- the WhatDoTheyKnow team\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nWhatDoTheyKnow is a project of mySociety run by a small team of staff and\ndedicated volunteers.\n\nmySociety is a charity that believes that information should be accessible to everyone.\nIf this is important to you, please consider donating to help us do more:\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/donate\n\nFind out more about how we handle your data at:\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/help/privacy\n\nmySociety is a registered charity in England and Wales (1076346), and a limited\ncompany (03277032)\n"},{"id":"wb-5deba9e8","subject":"Consortium evidence BP v Buckler","name":"2026-01-31_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2026-01-31","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-01-31_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-evidence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-01-31_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Sat, 31 Jan 2026 02:43:10 -0300\nSubject: BP v Buckler 1987 and the 1982 land registration of Great House Farm, Llandough (now Church View Close)\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V6dkG6ADpHuokALuzL1U=KvFWMsFXwiJ-qAQMx1u1znrw@mail.gmail.com>\n\nSirs/Ma'am,\n\nI'm writing in relation to land formerly known as *Great House Farm,\nLlandough (Vale of Glamorgan)*, subsequently developed for residential use\nand historically connected with the proceedings culminating in *BP v\nBuckler (Court of Appeal, 1987)*.\n\nI appreciate that the Local Planning Authority is not responsible for\ndetermining land ownership, nor am I asking it to do so. My purpose is to\nplace on record a *chronological summary of unresolved land-governance\nissues* which may be material to the integrity of the planning history\nrelied upon for that site.\n\nIn brief and as reflected in public records:\n\n   -\n\n   In *July 1974*, proceedings involving my grandmother and BP both\n   asserting ownership, were adjourned and did not result in a judicial final\n   determination.\n   -\n\n   On *31 October 1974*, a further listed hearing did not proceed\n   substantively, following correspondence sent that morning by BP entities.\n   No judgment on title was issued. My nan requested the audience with the\n   judge the day before, with her lawyer. But that got leaked, the press\n   descended on our farm (not that I can find evidence of news articles from\n   said press), and the following morning the two letters from BP arrived to\n   our solicitor, effectively squashing the court hearing and audience with\n   the judge my nan had sought, to make her case of ownership. Her reply to\n   both BP letters (one from BP Pension Trust Ltd and one from BP Properties\n   Ltd), that morning of the 31st of October, was also 'I own the land'.\n   -\n\n   Throughout this period, my grandmother was *excluded from dealings*\n   concerning land we asserted we owned, while the opposing party\ncontinued to *assert\n   ownership*, notwithstanding the absence of a concluded judicial finding.\n   -\n\n   In or around *1982*, the land was registered in BP’s name, despite the\n   earlier proceedings never having resolved the competing claims nor having\n   resulted in findings adverse to my grandmother.\n   -\n\n   My grandmother then died in *1983*, after the registration had taken\n   place, without compensation and without any final adjudication of her claim.\n   -\n\n   In the *1987 appeal*, the Court referred to correspondence as having\n   been replied to (albeit not to the correct corporate entity - which is\n   ultimately why we lost BP vs Buckler 1987), yet in my fathers appeal that\n   followed, whereby he argued that surely BP Pension Trust should have been\n   the claimant not BP Properties, the same judge from the case dismissed the\n   appeal citing that both companies were effectively the same. This is all\n   evidence on the face of the public record of these proceedings.\n   -\n\n   By that stage, and in the absence of a documentary title which had\n   effectively been overtaken by registration, my family was driven to advance\n   arguments in adverse possession as a *fallback position*, which itself\n   should have assured our continued occupancy.\n\nThe cumulative effect was that my family was *displaced*, the land was\ndeveloped, and no compensation was ever paid, despite ownership never\nhaving been conclusively decided in court. Whether intentionally or not,\nthe process operated in a manner where asserted ownership became treated as\nsettled fact.\n\nI am not alleging misconduct, nor am I seeking retrospective remedies from\nthe Authority. I am simply asking whether, given the above sequence, it\nwould be appropriate for the Authority to:\n\n   -\n\n   note that historic planning decisions were taken on the basis of *assumed\n   title*, and\n   -\n\n   record that the underlying ownership dispute was *never finally\n   adjudicated*, for the sake of transparency and future governance\n   integrity.\n\nThis correspondence is intended to be factual, measured, and\nnon-adversarial. I am happy to supply references to the relevant hearings,\nregistrations, or judgments if that assists your records. But what I'm\nhighlighting here is all on public record and in the public eye.\n\n\nYours faithfully,\n\n\nSion Buckler\n"},{"id":"wb-3168db4b","subject":"Consortium evidence BP v Buckler","name":"2026-01-31_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","date":"2026-01-31","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-01-31_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-evidence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-01-31_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Sat, 31 Jan 2026 03:14:04 -0300\nSubject: Systemic governance concern regarding historic land registration and administrative reliance - BP vs Buckler 1987 (inc. BP Land Registry 1982)\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V5-VLx+VwQO5hWEUQz06V-9Jzmi4MuZmZUCwqDC8wnJ9Q@mail.gmail.com>\n\nDear Committee Members,\n\nI am writing to draw your attention to a historical land matter which I\nbelieve may illustrate potential vulnerabilities in public administration,\nrecord-keeping, and the governance of land registration. My purpose is\nsolely to provide factual information for consideration, rather than to\nrequest remedial action.\n\nThe matter concerns land formerly known as *Great House Farm, Llandough,\nVale of Glamorgan*, later developed for residential use and historically\nassociated with *BP v Buckler (Court of Appeal, 1987)*.\n\n*Chronology of relevant events (as reflected in public records):*\n\n   -\n\n   *July 1974:* Court proceedings involving my grandmother were adjourned\n   without determination of ownership.\n   -\n\n   *31 October 1974:* A subsequent hearing did not proceed substantively,\n   following correspondence received from BP entities that same day. No\n   judgment on ownership was issued.\n   -\n\n   Throughout this period, my grandmother was not included in dealings\n   concerning the land, while the opposing party continued to assert ownership.\n   -\n\n   *1982:* The land was registered in the name of BP entities, despite\n   unresolved competing claims and no final judicial adjudication.\n   -\n\n   *1983:* My grandmother passed away after the registration, with\n   ownership never conclusively adjudicated.\n   -\n\n   *1987 Court of Appeal:* The record reflects an apparent inconsistency in\n   the treatment of correspondence and corporate entities. Specifically:\n   -\n\n      The court noted that my grandmother had replied to correspondence on\n      31 October, asserting ownership.\n      -\n\n      The reply was treated as directed to the “wrong” corporation (BP\n      Pensions Trust Ltd rather than BP Properties Ltd).\n      -\n\n      In a later appeal by my father, the same judge acknowledged that both\n      BP entities were effectively treated as the same for the purposes of the\n      ruling.\n      -\n\n      These circumstances resulted in the court upholding the opposing\n      party’s registration despite prior correspondence demonstrating an\n      assertion of ownership.\n      -\n\n   Following these proceedings, my father’s application to the *European\n   Court of Human Rights* was blocked.\n   -\n\n   The family home was demolished, preventing re-entry, even for the\n   retrieval of personal belongings.\n   -\n\n   Despite its historical significance and a petition to protect it, the\n   property was not preserved. The house had prior heritage interest,\n   including documented connections to Marconi.\n   -\n\n   *1991:* My father passed away at just 41.\n   -\n\n   *1994:* The land was subsequently excavated, with items of\n   archaeological interest removed.\n\nTaken together, these events demonstrate a situation in which *land\nregistration and subsequent reliance by public bodies proceeded without\ndefinitive adjudication of ownership*, raising questions regarding\nadministrative safeguards, procedural coherence, and record integrity.\n\nI am submitting this information purely for institutional awareness. I am *not\nmaking allegations of misconduct*, nor requesting investigation. I am able\nto provide a *concise factual timeline and references to public records*\nshould it assist the Committee’s understanding.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\n\nSion Buckler\n"},{"id":"wb-257b6112","subject":"Consortium land registry BP v Buckler","name":"2026-01-31_consortium-land_registry_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2026-01-31","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Land Registry correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-01-31_consortium-land_registry_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-land-registry-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-01-31_consortium-land_registry_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Sat, 31 Jan 2026 02:04:58 -0300\nSubject: Request for review of potential irregularities in 1982 land registration\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V6P+O3_My_D-qowgD10wPgVR4xnx1evMFKS9rMS7mHsYw@mail.gmail.com>\n\nDear HM Land Registry – Fraud Review Team,\n\nSirs/Ma'am,I am writing to request a review of the integrity of a land\nregistration made in or around *1982*, which later underpinned the\nproceedings commonly referred to as *BP v Buckler (1987)*.\n\nMy concern is to raise reasonable grounds of belief that the 1982\nregistration *may have been obtained or maintained through\nmisrepresentation, non-disclosure, or procedural irregularity*, resulting\nin a register entry that did not reflect a title definitively determined by\na court of law.\n\nIn summary:\n\n   1.\n\n   *No definitive judicial determination of ownership existed at the time\n   of registration*\n   Prior to the 1982 registration, the courts had not conclusively\n   determined ownership of the land.\n   -\n\n      Proceedings in *July 1974* were adjourned.\n      -\n\n      An appeal pursued by my grandmother on *31 October 1974* was\n      effectively neutralised following two letters sent by BP-associated\n      entities, rather than by a substantive judicial ruling on title.\n      -\n\n      Ownership was *asserted*, not adjudicated.\n      2.\n\n   *Competing assertions of ownership were unresolved*\n   BP asserted ownership, but so did my grandmother, who maintained\n   possession and occupation until her death in *1983*, after the registry\n   entry had already been filed.\n   At no point prior to her death was there a final court decision\n   extinguishing hers (or our families) claim or possession.\n   3.\n\n   *Inconsistent treatment of BP entities in later proceedings*\n   In the 1987 proceedings, the judge indicated that correspondence had not\n   been replied to because it was sent by a different BP entity (BP Properties\n   rather than BP Trust).\n   However, in my father’s related appeal, the same judge treated *BP Trust\n   and BP Properties as effectively the same entity*.\n   This inconsistency meant that:\n   -\n\n      We were said to have lost because of non-reply, while\n      -\n\n      The judgments simultaneously acknowledge that a reply was in fact\n      made and that the entities were treated as one.\n      This contradiction is material, as it goes directly to whether BP’s\n      asserted title was properly established.\n      4.\n\n   *Possession and long occupation were not displaced by lawful process*\n   My family had occupied the land openly and continuously for generations\n   without challenge. We consistently asserted ownership and, in any event,\n   possession sufficient to ground squatter’s or possessory rights.\n   Our removal was *not voluntary*; it was effected by force and exclusion,\n   not by a final determination of title.\n   5.\n\n   *Subsequent events reinforce the concern*\n   Following the appeal, my father’s application to the European Court of\n   Human Rights was blocked, the farm was demolished preventing re-entry, and\n   he also died thereafter in *1991*.\n   Shortly after his death in 1994, the land was excavated and items of\n   archaeological value were removed, despite prior knowledge of Roman remains\n   on the property (including a Roman soldier previously uncovered within the\n   living structure).\n\nTaken together, these matters raise serious questions as to whether the\n1982 registration accurately reflected the legal position at the time, or\nwhether it relied on assertions and correspondence that had not been\nvalidated by a court and that were later treated inconsistently.\n\nI respectfully request that HM Land Registry review whether:\n\n   -\n\n   The statutory requirements for registration were properly satisfied in\n   1982; and\n   -\n\n   The register may be inaccurate or was obtained by mistake,\n   misrepresentation, or material non-disclosure.\n\nI am able to supply supporting documents, title numbers, correspondence,\nand a concise timeline if required. But everything already on public record\nindicates and supports everything I've highlighted in this email.\n\nRegards,\n\nSion Buckler\n"},{"id":"wb-6257a863","subject":"Consortium FOI BP v Buckler","name":"2026-02-01_consortium-foi_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2026-02-01","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-01_consortium-foi_bpvsbuckler__en_v0-0-1.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-foi-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler","email","ghf"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["bpvsbuckler","email","ghf"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-01_consortium-foi_bpvsbuckler__en_v0-0-1.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: do-not-reply-to-this-address@whatdotheyknow.com\nDate: Sun, 01 Feb 2026 07:32:31 +0000\nSubject: Please update the status of your request - Freedom of Information Request – Administrative Records for BP Properties Ltd v Buckler (1974)\nMessage-ID: <697f018f119ae_17cc988701021db@mstrwdtkhexa.srv.mysociety.org.mail>\n\nTo let everyone know, follow this link and then select the appropriate box.\n\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/profile/sign_in?r=%2Frequest%2Ffreedom_of_information_request_a_2133%23describe_state_form_1\n\nYour request was called 'Freedom of Information Request – Administrative Records for BP Properties Ltd v Buckler (1974)'. Letting everyone know whether you got the information will help us keep tabs on\nHM Courts and Tribunals Service.\n\n-- the WhatDoTheyKnow team\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nWhatDoTheyKnow is a project of mySociety run by a small team of staff and\ndedicated volunteers.\n\nmySociety is a charity that believes that information should be accessible to everyone.\nIf this is important to you, please consider donating to help us do more:\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/donate\n\nFind out more about how we handle your data at:\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/help/privacy\n\nmySociety is a registered charity in England and Wales (1076346), and a limited\ncompany (03277032)\n"},{"id":"wb-4772f64d","subject":"Consortium petition BP v Buckler","name":"2026-02-01_consortium-petition_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2026-02-01","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-01_consortium-petition_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-petition-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-01_consortium-petition_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Sun, 1 Feb 2026 08:06:12 -0300\nSubject: Cais am Ddeiseb ac Ymchwiliad Cyhoeddus: Achos Buckler v BP (1987) a Dinistr Great House Farm, Llandough / Formal Request for Inquiry: The Great House Farm Dispossession (BP vs Buckler)\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V7H4jZ5jLfb+XQoS+4DNAaUgQjgdu4K5_FWFXFdwDDPEA@mail.gmail.com>\n\nAelodau Anrhydeddus y Senedd,\n\nRwy’n ysgrifennu atoch i dynnu eich sylw at yr hyn yr wyf yn ei ystyried yn\nanghyfiawnder hanesyddol difrifol, yn ymwneud â’m teulu, y Bucklers, a\ndinistr Great House Farm yn Llandough — safle fferm a chartref o dros 800\nmlynedd o hanes, gan gynnwys cysylltiadau mynachaidd a chysylltiad\nuniongyrchol â Guglielmo Marconi yn ystod datblygiad cynnar radio.\n\nMae’r mater hwn yn codi cwestiynau difrifol ynghylch hawliau tir,\ngweithdrefnau cyfreithiol, cofrestru tir, a hawliau dynol yng Nghymru.\n\nEr enghraifft, mewn datganiad i’r wasg yn 1974, dywedodd fy nain yn\ngyhoeddus: *“It’s my home.”* Mae’r datganiad hwn, sydd yn fater o gofnod\ncyhoeddus, yn gwrth-ddweud yn uniongyrchol ganfyddiad Llys Apêl 1987 yn *BP\nv Buckler*, lle dywedwyd ei bod wedi bod yn “ddistaw” ac wedi derbyn\ntrwydded BP a gyhoeddwyd yn 1974.\n\nYn yr un achos, gwrthodwyd apêl fy nhad ar y sail bod BP Pension Trust Ltd\na BP Properties Ltd yn “effeithiol yr un cwmni”. Fodd bynnag, yn y\ndyfarniad yr oedd yn ei herio, nododd y barnwr nad oedd ymatebion fy nain i\nBP Pension Trust Ltd yn cyfrif gan mai BP Properties Ltd oedd yr hawlydd,\nac mai cwmnïau ar wahân oeddent. Yn ymarferol, roedd hyn yn arwain at\nsefyllfa “pen y cwmni’n ennill, cynffon y teulu’n colli”.\n\nYn sgil hyn, cafodd fy nhad ei dynnu o’i wely ysbyty, ei garcharu heb\ngyhuddiad, a gosodwyd gorchymyn atal arno i’w atal rhag meddiannu ac\namddiffyn ein fferm 800 oed rhag cael ei dymchwel. Cafodd ei gais i’r Llys\nHawliau Dynol Ewropeaidd ei rwystro cyn iddo farw yn 1991, yn ddim ond 41\noed.\n\nAr y sail hon, rwy’n gofyn yn ffurfiol am *Ddeiseb i sefydlu Ymchwiliad\nCyhoeddus* i’r achos hwn, fel mater o ddwyn tir hanesyddol ac o dorri\nhawliau dynol yng Nghymru.\n\nRwy’n weithiwr proffesiynol ym maes seiber a rhyfela electronig milwrol.\nMae technolegau AI pwerus bellach ar gael i bob un ohonom, ac nid oes\nunrhyw reswm technegol pam na ellid archwilio’r mater hwn yn llawn ac yn\ndrylwyr. Mae’r hyn a ddigwyddodd i’m teulu yn ymddangos fel anghyfiawnder\ndwys, aml-genhedlaethol, ac yn debyg i ffurf ar “lawfare”, lle y trinwyd fy\nnheulu fel llafur di-dâl i gynnal ased tir hyd nes yr oedd yn gyfleus i’w\nariannu. Mae’n arwyddocaol bod bron pob cam yn ein herbyn wedi digwydd ar\nyr eiliad olaf posibl.\n\nCeir honiadau bod sylw sylweddol yn y wasg yn 1974 o blaid fy nain a’n\nteulu, ynghyd â deiseb o tua 1,700 o bobl yn ceisio atal dymchwel y fferm.\nYmhellach, mae’n ymddangos bod CADW wedi methu ag ymyrryd lle dylai’r safle\nfod wedi cael statws Adeilad Rhestredig Gradd II.\n\nNid wyf yn sicr a gofnodwyd erioed yn briodol yn y Gofrestrfa Tir fod y tir\n— a gofrestrwyd gan BP yn 1982 — dan anghydfod. Yn fy nealltwriaeth i,\ncafodd ei drin fel pe na bai mewn anghydfod o gwbl. Nid oes unrhyw lys wedi\npenderfynu’n derfynol mai BP oedd perchnogion cyfreithiol y tir; cafodd\nhynny ei honni yn hytrach na’i brofi.\n\nMae hefyd fater pwysig o hanes diwylliannol. Arhosodd Marconi gyda’n teulu\nyn y fferm wrth iddo ddatblygu radio. Mae cynghorwyr a’r plwyf yn Llandough\nyn gwrthod cydnabod hyn. Rwy’n deall bod Marconi yn cael ei ystyried yn\nwleidyddol eithafol ar y dde, ac, fel y gwelir yn achos y cerflun radio ym\nMae Caerdydd, mae ei rôl hanesyddol yn cael ei hailysgrifennu. Mae’n\nrhesymol tybio bod ein teulu wedi cael ei ddal mewn ymgyrch wleidyddol\nehangach o’r chwith yn erbyn y dde, ac mai am roi llety i Marconi y bu i ni\nwynebu canlyniadau canrif o hyd — problemau nad oedd yn bodoli yn y tair\ncanrif flaenorol yr oeddem wedi byw ar y fferm.\n\nRoedd mam Marconi hefyd yn Jamerson o gwmni wisgi Gwyddelig Jameson. Rwy’n\ngofyn i chi gynnal eich ymchwil eich hun, gwneud eich ymholiadau eich hun,\na dod i’ch casgliadau eich hun — ond yn anad dim, peidiwch â chaniatáu i\nhanes gael ei ailysgrifennu yma.\n\nMae fy nheulu bellach yn archwilio opsiynau ar gyfer siarad cyhoeddus,\nhawliau stori, a dogfennaeth. Yn nhermau cysylltiadau teuluol: fy nhaid\noedd Ray Smith o Dempsey and Makepeace; mae fy modryb yn Shan Cothi; a\ndechreuodd fy ewythr y band Tigertailz.\n\nRwy’n disgrifio’r sefyllfa hon fel gwarthus. Mae fy nheulu’n mynnu\ncyfiawnder am gael ein dadleoli a’n dwyn o’n tir a’n heiddo gan y Sefydliad\nPrydeinig a BP, gyda phrin unrhyw amddiffyniad dan y gyfraith. Rydym yn\ndeulu Cymreig brodorol, Cymraeg eu hiaith, wedi byw yng Nghaerdydd, Bro\nMorgannwg, a’r fferm yn Llandough ers cannoedd o flynyddoedd.\n\nO’r hyn y gallaf ei gasglu, ceir dwsinau o achosion posibl o dwyll a\nchuddio gwybodaeth, sy’n golygu nad yw statud cyfyngiadau yn berthnasol o\nreidrwydd. I’r rhai sy’n cefnogi dad-drefedigaethu Cymru ac annibyniaeth,\nprin y ceir enghraifft gliriach na *BP v Buckler (1987)* o’r hyn a\nddigwyddodd i deulu ffermio Cymreig wrth herio’r Wladwriaeth Brydeinig a\nchorfforaeth fawr. Nid yw eraill wedi codi’r mater hwn, yn bennaf oherwydd\ntrawma cenhedlaethol.\n\nRwyf wedi profi fy hun. Gadewais fy ngyrfa filwrol gydag anrhydedd, gan\ngwblhau fy llw 22 mlynedd. Cefais fy nghyflogi’n bona fide gan Gyngor Bro\nMorgannwg a Llywodraeth Cymru hyd at 2024. Gwasanaethais mewn rhyfeloedd yn\ny Dwyrain Canol. Fodd bynnag, nid yw’r anghyfiawnder fferm yn fater unigol.\n\nYn 2013, cafodd fy mab 36 diwrnod oed ei gipio, ac nid wyf erioed wedi ei\nweld ers hynny. Cafodd tŷ ei ddwyn oddi arnaf (40 Lombard Street, Y Barri).\nYn 2023, cefais fy ngadael i gysgu mewn pabell tra roedd bron i 100 o\nAfghanistaniaid yn cael eu lletya’n dawel wrth ymyl ein pentref o 11 tŷ,\ntra roedd yn rhaid imi aros yno i ofalu am fy nain gan na allai gofalwyr ei\nchyrraedd.\n\nO ganlyniad, rwyf bellach yn byw yn yr hyn a elwir yn Setliad Cymreig yn yr\nAriannin. Rwyf wedi fy nisodli ac wedi fy dadleoli i’r eithaf. Serch hynny,\nrwy’n ddiolchgar fy mod wedi goroesi gyda’m rhyddid, fy mywyd, a’m henw da\nyn gymharol gyfan. Serch hynny, hoffwn fod wedi aros yng Nghymru, fel\nCymro, gyda safon a chynhaliaeth bywyd deg.\n\nMae fy ngwraig a minnau’n amharod i ganiatáu i’n merch hyd yn oed gamu ar\nYnysoedd Prydain o ystyried yr hyn a glywn sy’n digwydd yno. Yn 2024,\nallforiais hyd yn oed fy nghi o Abertawe i’n heiddo yn y Caribî ar ôl i\nunigolyn Afghanistan fod yng ngardd fy nheulu yng Nghymru, gan geisio ei\nddwyn.\n\nGofynnaf i’r Senedd gymryd y mater hwn o ddifrif, ac ystyried sefydlu\nYmchwiliad Cyhoeddus llawn, tryloyw ac annibynnol. Nid er budd fy nheulu yn\nunig y mae hyn, ond er uniondeb hanes, cyfiawnder tir, a hawliau dynol yng\nNghymru.\n\nYn gywir,\n\n*Requester Hywel Buckler*\n\n*----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------*\n\nSirs/Ma'am,\n\nI am writing to you regarding a significant historical injustice involving\nmy family, the Bucklers, and the destruction of Great House Farm (an\n800-year-old site of monastic and Marconi history).\n\nFor example, in a 1974 press statement my grandmother said publically 'It's\nmy home'—that directly contradicts the 1987 Court of Appeal ruling that she\nwas 'silent' and accepted of BP license issued in 1974. My dad's appeal for\nthe BP vs Buckler 1987 case also got dismissed on grounds both BP Pension\nTrust Ltd and BP Properties Ltd were effectively the same company. But in\nthe ruling he was appealing, the judge said that my nans replies to BP\nPensions Trust Ltd didn't count as BP Properties Ltd were the claimant, and\nthe two were seperate companies. So it was heads they win, tails we lose.\nMy dad was ripped from his hospital bed, jailed without charge and slapped\nwith a restraining order to keep him from occupying and defending our 800\nyear old farm from being bulldozed down. His ECHR request blocked before he\ndied in 1991 aged just 41.\n\nI am requesting a *Petition for a Public Inquiry* into this case as a\nmatter of historical land theft and human rights violations in Wales.\n\nI'm a military cyber and electronic warfare professional. We all have very\npowerful AI technology at our disposal which can make light work out of all\nof this. My family suffered an incredible injustice. It also appears to be\nmulti-generational warfare/ lawfare. Whereby we were treated as free labour\nto upkeep the asset of the land and property until it was ripe to be cashed\nin. I say this as every action against us seemed to be last minute.\n\nThere was allegedly press covering the 74 case and supporting my nan and\nfamily and farm. There was a petition of 1700 people trying to defend us\nand the farm from demolishment. CADW looked the other way when it should\nhave been a Grade II listed building. I could go on and on and on.\n\nI'm not even sure the land registry recorded that our land, being\nregistered in 1982 by BP , was in dispute. I believe it was treated as if\nit wasn't dispited. No court actually definitively determined that BP were\nthe landowners. It was just asserted.\n\nMarconi stayed with our family at our farm when inventing radio. The\ncouncillors and parish at Llandough won't even acknowledge this. I\nunderstand Marconi was far right and so, like the radio statute at Cardiff\nBay has confirmed, he is being re-written out of history.  It stands to\nreason that our family was caught in this political campaign of left vs\nright. And for helping Marconi we found ourselves experiencing a century of\nproblems afterwards. Which didn't exist for the 3 centuries we were at our\nfarm before letting far right marconi stay with us. Marconi's mother was\nalso Jamerson of the Irish Whiskey company.\n\nDo your own homework. Make your own enquiries. Draw your own conclusions.\n\n But please do not let history be rewritten here. My family are exploring\noptions for public speaking, story rights, and a documentary etc.\n\nMy grandfather was Ray Smith, of Dempsey and Makepeace. My aunty Shan\nCothi. My uncle started the band tigertailz.\n\nThis is an outrage and my family demands justice for being displaced and\nstolen from, land and property, by the British Establishment/ BP -  with\nlittle to no protections under law. And we're indigenous to the land,\nWelsh, Welsh speaking, Welsh family. Resident in Cardiff / Vale and that\nfarm in Llandough for HUNDREDS OF YEARS.\n\nThere's tens of counts of fraud and concealment from what I can gather.\nWhich means the statute of limitation doesn't apply in this instance. I\nknow some are in favour of Wales decolonising and gaining independence. I\nthink there's no better example of BP vs Buckler 1987 and what has happened\nto my family. Nobody else will bring this up because there's generational\ntrauma. Notwithstanding we, a welsh farming family, are speaking up and\nagainst the British State and BP.\n\nBut I've proven myself. Leaving my military career with honours. Finishing\nmy 22 years Oath, bona fide, employed by the Vale of Glamorgan Council,\nWelsh Government until 2024. I served in the wars in the middle east. I\ndon't know what is going on, on that island. But the farm injustice isn't\nthe only matter. My 36 day old son was abducted in 2013 and I've never seen\nhim since. I had a house stolen (40 Lombard Street, Barry). And in 2023 I\nwas left to sleep in a tent, while almost 100 afghans were secretly housed\nnext to our 11 home village. Where I had to be to care for my grandmother,\nas no carers couldn't reach her. So I'm currently at the Welsh Settlement\nin Argentina. So I'm as displaced and replaced as I can be to cater for the\ninsanity that's going on, on that island. And grateful I've managed to\nsurvive it all with my liberty and life and reputation somewhat still\nintact. But I would have actually liked to have remained in Wales as a\nWelshman. And have some quality and standard of life. My wife and I are\nreluctant for our daughter to even step foot on the British Isles with what\nwe're hearing that's going on there lately. And I even exported my dog from\nSwansea to our place in the Caribbean in 2024 after an afghan was in my\nfamily's garden in Wales, trying to steal him.\n\nI look forward to your reply,\n\nRegards,\n\nSion Hywel Buckler\n"},{"id":"wb-3d8fbf41","subject":"Consortium evidence BP v Buckler","name":"2026-02-04_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2026-02-04","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-04_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-evidence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-04_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Wed, 4 Feb 2026 05:04:14 -0300\nSubject: BP vs Buckler: Great House Farm, Llandough\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V7Q-7MG4GKFVBwF=TEGu62Ei8AXXF3LPUawoaf4OMxKdw@mail.gmail.com>\n\nSirs/Ma'am,\n\nI'm Requester. Son of William Buckler of the landmark legal case BP Vs\nBuckler 1987.\n\nI am at the Welsh settlement in Argentina, attempting to rebuild a replica\nof my families former 800 year old farm here.\n\nOur farm outside Cardiff, which my family occupied since 1667, was where\nMarconi invented radio. It should have been a Grade II listen building. We\nshould have never been forced from it. It should have never been bulldozed\ndown.\n\n\nMy family should have at least been compensated or seen justice. Instead my\nfather's appeal was dismissed. His ECHR request blocked and then he died in\n1991 at age just 41 - my age today.\n\nThere's a number of reasons us indigenous Briton Welsh farmers got forced\nfrom our ancestral birthland of 400 years via force, secrecy/ stealth and\nlicense. When we operated with non of these and remained in honour.\n\n1. Ownership was never determined in a court. Possession was saught but BP\nnever saught clarification on the true owners.\n\nMy nans tried making her case that we were the owners, inspire of our fall\nback legal defence of adverse pissesion.\n\nMy nans audience with the judge in July 1974 was ajourned. Her request for\nan audience with the judge to appeal and make her claim to the land in\nOctober 30th was squashed too, by license letters alledgedly sent by BP\nthat morning, but not received until November 4th. The court stepped back\nin November 1974, alledging the matter was sufficiently de-escelated due to\nBP's license offer. But that was an inadvertent way of ruling in BP's\nfavour without ruling on the matter. So the license offer upheld, my nans\nappeal and claim to land ownership unheard/ blocked yet again.\n\nThe press also appeared around my nan and home after it was leaked our\nfamily solicitor made the appeal to the judge on the 30th October 1974. For\na hearing the next day. Odd coincidence. Where's any of that press\ncoverage? Suppressed? Another odd coincidence. We're they even real press.\nIn any case, real or not, the BP license letters afterwards gave the\nappearance of a retreate by BP, when in actual fact it was forcing a\nlicense, unlawfully and unconditionally onto my one legged, wheelchair\nbound grandmother.\n\nIf ever my nan was silent, it was because the courts, her own solicitor,\nmaybe even husband, and BP were all incrementally deminishing her position.\nHer reason for an amputated legal was a blood clot caused from the stress.\nFurthermore they cut the electric, water, poisoned our river and cows.\n\nThose BP letters of license might have worked with my grandad Frederick\nBuckler. As he did express intent to entertain license offers. But that\nintent, by law, will have died with him in the late 60's. My nan was a\nWilliams. She kept and maintained her maiden name. But the court and press\nand BP would  nevertheless refer to my nan as Mrs Buckler. Since no\nWilliams ever showed intent. Only Frederick Buckler. And so it was\nimportant they force upon my nan the name Buckler, and the narrative the\n\"Buckler's\" expressed intent.\n\nAnd contrary to claims my nan aquiessed and even accepted the license by\nremaining silent, as falsly alleged and ill advised by her solicitor, she\ndid no such thing.\n\n2. I found an article from 1978 showing my nan arranged a petition.\nArranged and open day. And claimed publically \"it's my house\". Our families\nhome since 1667. See attached.\n\nThe press say 'open day' in hyphens, as if to belittle my nans attempts to\nsave our home from possession, demolition and housing development.\n\nSo too, does the judge in the 87 case against my late father, use language\nlike my nan '“Conceived the notion” we held title - suggesting our title\nclaim might be fanciful or ungrounded.\n\nBut recently I have found evidence of the exchange which happened between\nSir Mark Wood and Marquess of Bute as my nan alledged. Whereby we received\ntitle, as it was promised to us in exchange (as agricultural tenants) if we\npermit quarrying to occur on the farmland.\n\nThe deal with us was not honoured. Daniel Thomas is believed to have died\nin 1915. Our title is believed to have been stolen from our home by a house\nguest, not fully known to us. And a copy of the title in our name, stolen\nfrom the public library. Albeit they left the index card.\n\nWhen land registry begun, BP Properties Ltd didn't just race to register in\n1982 (my nan dying age 65 in 1983), despite women in our family living even\nunto 101, but BP and the land registry didn't lodge that the title claim\nwas in dispute as they should have. Nor had they had confirmation by a\ncourt that they were the superior owners. As it was never decided in a\ncourt, it was actually actively avoided as my family held superior title\nand claim which they couldn't challenge. Not by stealing our titles. Not by\ntrying to force, stealth or license.\n\n3. BP should not have been awarded possession of our land in the landmark\nlegal case in 1987. The intent argument was disproven in the appeal my late\nfather filed afterwards. And for reasons I mentioned above. And while the\njudge ruled in BP's favor, citing any rejection my nan made to BP Pensions\nTrust Ltd didn't count, as the claimant and self-proclaimed licensor was BP\nProperties Ltd and the two were separate companies. The same justice\ndismissed my dads appeal citing both companies were effectively the same.\nSo a case of heads they win, tails we lose.\n\nI've tried bringing this up with authorities all my life. And I'm forever\nblocked and ignored, or my enquiries are absorbed and deflected. My family\nwant our justice. And compensation.\n\nI'm a war veteran. Ieft my military career with honours. I finished 22\nyears in Oath. Last 2 employed under the Welsh government (Vale Council)\n2022-24. I am bona fide.\n\nIt's an oborrant situation on British Isles. I've spent most of my adult\nlife abroad because we seem to have just been overrun by extreme\nanti-indigenous leftists, non-indigenous and corporations. It's always\ntotal silence when I bring this matter up. As I've stated, it's so bad I'm\njust rebuilding a replica of my farm at the Welsh settlement in Argentina\nnow. Who care to celebrate the house radio was invented in. And my story\nrights. And me, a living member of this family and whole matter.\n\nIt's evident history is being buried and rewritten by the anti-christian,\nanti-indigenous, anti-welsh Regime occupying British isles. As there's a\nstatute in Cardiff of a radio which expressly won't name the inventor\nMarconi as he was far right. I wonder if that's why a multi generation\nlawfare campaign was waged on my farm and farm where Marconi invented\nradio. Or was it the burials and treasures under our farmland that were\nexhevated in 1994? Or the value of the land overlooking Cardiff bay, which\nwas later sold on to developers.\n\nSomeone even hypothesised that BP and the Brit-ISH/ BritLESS State took\ntheir time with our family so that we would upkeep the farmhouse and\nStewart the land and defend it, until it had reached maturity for value\nextrapolation. Which is plausible all things considered. And would go\nlengths to show you the depth of evil us harvested indigenous Britons are\nfighting.\n\nI am a certified and experienced military commander and leader. And the\nfarm story I've given you here is just one of a long line of unjust events.\nMy house in 40 Lombard Street, Barry was stolen in 2012 in a similar way.\nAnd my 36 day old boy was abducted from my home in Stockeld Park in 2013,\nnever to be seen again. And all that happened since then was failed\nattempts to discredit, frame, kidnap, censor and silence me. So it's like a\ncivil war has been waged on us, which is why I only fly in and our of\nBritain now, like it's another tour of the middle east.\n\nThe danger is real. I keep my daughter and wife off the island now, not\njust my self. I even exported my dog to our place in the Caribbean after I\ncaught an Afghan in my garden in Wales, trying to steal him. One of the 97\nAfghans secretly housed in StAthan, near our 11 home village of Monknash,\nwhich the Government were trying to deny existed.\n\nI hope someone follows up on this story of BP Vs Buckler 1987. And asks all\nthe questions that should have been asked throughout. And to this day.\n\nI won't accept the illegal occupation. At some point as a now counter\nofficer, we Welsh, who are already seeking to decolonise, will need to\nfight back by asserting boundaries upon this trespass. I should not have\nbeen forced off my own ancestral birthland. Out of my own home. Out of my\nson's life. Not as someone in honour and bona fide. As stated I have made\nnew lives in the Caribbean and here in Argentina. But only while justice is\ndelayed back home in Wales. But that delay is now decades. DECADES !! It's\nabsolute insanity.\n\nPlease report !!\nInvestigate.\n\n\nRegards,\n\nSion Buckler\n[redacted]\n"},{"id":"wb-3fd74140","subject":"Consortium petition BP v Buckler","name":"2026-02-04_consortium-petition_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2026-02-04","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-04_consortium-petition_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-petition-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-04_consortium-petition_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Wed, 4 Feb 2026 03:37:11 -0300\nSubject: Cais am Ddeiseb ac Ymchwiliad Cyhoeddus: Achos Buckler v BP (1987) a Dinistr Great House Farm, Llandough / Formal Request for Inquiry: The Great House Farm Dispossession (BP vs Buckler)\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V7t8WfS38swpAqDOQG2V=q+-rWSQ9XScghErErsa-Xkpg@mail.gmail.com>\n\nMP,\n\nFurther to my last email, please would you;\n\n- Pose a Parliamentary Question to the UK Government about the historical\ncase or current land registration policies.\n\n- Request a debate on historical property disputes.\n\n- Refer me to the Parliamentary and Health Service Ombudsman a I have\nevidence of maladministration by a public body (e.g. Land Registry, court,\ncouncil, CADW etc).\n\nRegards,\n\nSion Buckler\n[redacted]\n\nOn Sun, 1 Feb 2026, 09:13 NARAYAN, Kanishka (MP), <\nkanishka.narayan.mp@parliament.uk> wrote:\n\n> Hello,\n>\n> Thank you for reaching out to me. This is just a quick note to confirm\n> that we’ve received your email.\n>\n> As your MP for the Vale of Glamorgan, my priority is supporting local\n> constituents who need help with urgent issues. I receive a high volume of\n> emails every day, so while I will do my best to get back to you as soon as\n> possible, there may be a slight delay—especially if you’re contacting me\n> about policy matters or upcoming legislation. Please know that your message\n> has been logged, and I’ll respond as soon as I can.\n> Constituency Casework\n>\n> If you need my help with a personal case, please make sure your email\n> includes:\n> ✔ Your full name\n> ✔ Your full address (including postcode)\n> ✔ A contact method (email or phone)\n> ✔ Details of your case (including any relevant reference numbers)\n>\n> A member of my casework team will be in touch as soon as possible.\n> Am I Your MP?\n>\n> Due to parliamentary protocol, I can only take up casework for people\n> living in the *Vale of Glamorgan*. If you’re unsure whether I’m your MP,\n> you can check by entering your postcode here:*\n> <http://findyourmp.parliament.uk/>**findyourmp.parliament.uk\n> <http://findyourmp.parliament.uk/>*.\n> Getting in Touch\n>\n> The best way to contact me is by email. My office is open Monday to\n> Friday, 9 AM to 5 PM, and we’ll do our best to respond as soon as we can.\n>\n> You can also keep up with my work here:\n> 📌 *Facebook:* [KanishkafortheVale]\n> 📌 *Instagram:* [@valekanishka]\n> 📌 *X (Twitter):* [@KanishkaNarayan]\n> Emergency Contacts\n>\n> If you need urgent assistance, here are some useful contacts:\n> 📞 *NHS Wales (Non-Emergency Medical Advice):* 111\n> 🚨 *Emergency Services (Police, Fire, Ambulance):* 999\n> 🏥 *Mental Health Support (Samaritans Cymru):* 020 8394 8300\n> 🏠 *Homelessness Support (Shelter Cymru):* 08000 495 495\n> 👮 *South Wales Police (Non-Emergency):* 101\n>\n> Thanks again for getting in touch—I really appreciate hearing from people\n> across our community. My team and I look forward to assisting you.\n>\n> Best wishes,\n> Kanishka\n>\n>\n>\n> *Kanishka Narayan MP*\n>\n> Member of Parliament for the Vale of Glamorgan\n>\n> Minister for AI and Online Safety\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> UK Parliament Disclaimer: this e-mail is confidential to the intended\n> recipient. If you have received it in error, please notify the sender and\n> delete it from your system. Any unauthorised use, disclosure, or copying is\n> not permitted. This e-mail has been checked for viruses, but no liability\n> is accepted for any damage caused by any virus transmitted by this e-mail.\n> This e-mail address is not secure, is not encrypted and should not be used\n> for sensitive data.\n>\n"},{"id":"wb-2f7b6844","subject":"Consortium evidence BP v Buckler","name":"2026-02-05_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2026-02-05","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-05_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-evidence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-05_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: HOC Public Administration and Constitutional Affairs Select Committee <pacac@parliament.uk>\nDate: Thu, 5 Feb 2026 11:40:42 +0000\nSubject: RE: Systemic governance concern regarding historic land registration and administrative reliance - BP vs Buckler 1987 (inc. BP Land Registry 1982)\nMessage-ID: <CWLP123MB225801E5D394513A0E2FA83FD499A@CWLP123MB2258.GBRP123.PROD.OUTLOOK.COM>\n\nDear Requester,\n\nThank you for contacting the Public Administration and Constitutional Affairs Committee.\n\nUnfortunately, this is not a matter within the remit of the Committee.\n\nThe role of the Committee is to examine constitutional issues; the quality and standards of administration provided by Civil Service departments; and the reports of the Parliamentary and Health Service Ombudsman (PHSO).\n\nYou can find out more about the work of the Committee here: Public Administration and Constitutional Affairs Committee - Summary - Committees - UK Parliament<https://committees.parliament.uk/committee/327/public-administration-and-constitutional-affairs-committee/>\n\nBest wishes,\n\nKate\n\nKate Johal\nSecond Clerk\nPublic Administration and Constitutional Affairs Committee (PACAC)\n\nHouse of Commons, Palace of Westminster, London SW1A 0AA\nparliament.uk<http://parliament.uk/>\n[cid:image001.png@01DC9694.40B40CA0]<https://www.parliament.uk/business/committees/>\nThe House of Commons welcomes feedback. If you have any compliments, complaints or comments,\nabout the service that you have received please send an email to feedback@parliament.uk<mailto:feedback@parliament.uk>\n\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]>\nSent: 31 January 2026 06:14\nTo: HOC Public Administration and Constitutional Affairs Select Committee <pacac@parliament.uk>\nSubject: Systemic governance concern regarding historic land registration and administrative reliance - BP vs Buckler 1987 (inc. BP Land Registry 1982)\n\n\nDear Committee Members,\n\nI am writing to draw your attention to a historical land matter which I believe may illustrate potential vulnerabilities in public administration, record-keeping, and the governance of land registration. My purpose is solely to provide factual information for consideration, rather than to request remedial action.\n\nThe matter concerns land formerly known as Great House Farm, Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan, later developed for residential use and historically associated with BP v Buckler (Court of Appeal, 1987).\n\nChronology of relevant events (as reflected in public records):\n\n  *   July 1974: Court proceedings involving my grandmother were adjourned without determination of ownership.\n  *   31 October 1974: A subsequent hearing did not proceed substantively, following correspondence received from BP entities that same day. No judgment on ownership was issued.\n  *   Throughout this period, my grandmother was not included in dealings concerning the land, while the opposing party continued to assert ownership.\n  *   1982: The land was registered in the name of BP entities, despite unresolved competing claims and no final judicial adjudication.\n  *   1983: My grandmother passed away after the registration, with ownership never conclusively adjudicated.\n  *   1987 Court of Appeal: The record reflects an apparent inconsistency in the treatment of correspondence and corporate entities. Specifically:\n\n     *   The court noted that my grandmother had replied to correspondence on 31 October, asserting ownership.\n     *   The reply was treated as directed to the “wrong” corporation (BP Pensions Trust Ltd rather than BP Properties Ltd).\n     *   In a later appeal by my father, the same judge acknowledged that both BP entities were effectively treated as the same for the purposes of the ruling.\n     *   These circumstances resulted in the court upholding the opposing party’s registration despite prior correspondence demonstrating an assertion of ownership.\n\n  *   Following these proceedings, my father’s application to the European Court of Human Rights was blocked.\n  *   The family home was demolished, preventing re-entry, even for the retrieval of personal belongings.\n  *   Despite its historical significance and a petition to protect it, the property was not preserved. The house had prior heritage interest, including documented connections to Marconi.\n  *   1991: My father passed away at just 41.\n  *   1994: The land was subsequently excavated, with items of archaeological interest removed.\n\nTaken together, these events demonstrate a situation in which land registration and subsequent reliance by public bodies proceeded without definitive adjudication of ownership, raising questions regarding administrative safeguards, procedural coherence, and record integrity.\n\nI am submitting this information purely for institutional awareness. I am not making allegations of misconduct, nor requesting investigation. I am able to provide a concise factual timeline and references to public records should it assist the Committee’s understanding.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\nSion Buckler\n\nUK Parliament Disclaimer: this e-mail is confidential to the intended recipient. If you have received it in error, please notify the sender and delete it from your system. Any unauthorised use, disclosure, or copying is not permitted. This e-mail has been checked for viruses, but no liability is accepted for any damage caused by any virus transmitted by this e-mail. This e-mail address is not secure, is not encrypted and should not be used for sensitive data.\n"},{"id":"wb-97cd57ef","subject":"Consortium evidence BP v Buckler","name":"2026-02-05_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","date":"2026-02-05","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-05_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-evidence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-05_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Thu, 5 Feb 2026 12:52:15 -0300\nSubject: Re: Systemic governance concern regarding historic land registration and administrative reliance - BP vs Buckler 1987 (inc. BP Land Registry 1982)\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V5+2w+pxsZNLT=vcqvz-wHathUwhHVmjm0aTKCWv-Vsmw@mail.gmail.com>\n\n*Kate Johal,*\n\nI appreciate your prompt reply and thank you. I assure you I'm not out to\nwaste your departments or anyone's time. I just finished 22 years in Oath\nin 2024 as a public servant, employed at the Vale of Glamorgan Council,\nunder the Welsh Government. I'm not only bona fine, I left my military\ncareer beforehand - with honours. I truly believe I have the most\nbefitting case, that I understand your department specifically exists to\nhandle.\n\nIs it not PACAC's role to ensure that the *machinery of the state*—the\ncourts, the Land Registry, and planning authorities—operates with\ntransparency and protects the constitutional rights of citizens against\nadministrative abuse? My case relates specifically to *maladministration*\nand *constitutional overreach*: it involves the judicial \"erasure\" of a\n320-year ancestral lineage through the tactical misnaming of Mary Williams\nto \"Mrs. Buckler\", the administrative suppression of a known national\nheritage site (the 1888 Roman find) to facilitate demolition permits, and\nthe suspicious disappearance of public title records from state-run\nlibraries. By focusing on how these public institutions failed to maintain\naccurate records and fair procedures, I am  presenting a case of *systemic\nfailure in public administration*, which is the core reason I\nunderstand your Committee to exist.\n\nI must formally contest the assertion that this matter falls outside the\nCommittee’s remit. While the origin of this case involves a dispute with a\nprivate entity (BP), the core of my grievance concerns *constitutional\nissues* and the *quality of administration provided by government-funded\nbodies*, specifically regarding the protection of national heritage and the\nintegrity of land registration.\n\nI ask the Committee to review this matter under the following three pillars\nof your mandate:\n\n*1. Constitutional Issues: The Erasure of Customary and Indigenous Title* The\n1987 Court of Appeal ruling (*BP Properties Ltd v Buckler*) created a\nsignificant legal precedent regarding \"unilateral licenses\". My family\nasserts that the administration of justice in this case allowed for the\ntactical erasure of the \"Williams\" identity—a lineage with de facto\npossession dating back to 1667—to facilitate a corporate land grab. The use\nof the judicial system to overwrite centuries of documented continuity with\na \"paper title\" is a constitutional matter regarding the rights of citizens\nagainst aggressive corporate-state interests.\n\n*2. Standards of Administration: Suppression of Heritage by Public Bodies*\nThe Committee examines the quality of administration. My investigation\nreveals that:\n\n   -\n\n   *Archaeological Suppression:* Evidence of a Roman soldier was discovered\n   in 1888, providing \"actual notice\" of the site's significance. This was\n   reportedly suppressed or ignored by planning authorities and the\n   Glamorgan-Gwent Archaeological Trust (GGAT) to allow for demolition.\n\n   -\n\n   *Misrepresentation in Planning:* The 13th-century Great House Farm was\n   administratively classified as a \"dilapidated utility building\" to bypass\n   heritage protections.\n   -\n\n   *Missing Records:* Vital title evidence and Bute Estate deeds\n   disappeared from the public library system—a failure of public record\n   administration.\n\n\n*3. Accountability of the Land Registry and Parliamentary Oversight* The\nregistration of this land under BP’s title occurred during a period of\ntransition in Welsh land law (c. 1982). If public registries accepted title\nwithout disclosing active disputes or the presence of a known national\nmonument (the Roman/Medieval site), this represents a failure in the\n\"standards of administration\" by the Civil Service. Furthermore, this\nmatter was raised in the House of Commons by MP Ted Rowlands in 1980, yet\nthe administrative failures continued unabated.\n\n*Conclusion* I am not asking the Committee to act as a court of appeal, but\nto investigate the *systemic administrative failures* that allowed a\nhistorically significant site and a family's ancestral rights to be\n\"buried\" through procedural maneuvers and the suppression of evidence.\n\nIf the Committee exists to ensure the Civil Service and constitutional\nframeworks protect the public interest, then the demolition of an\n800-year-old heritage site under a cloud of administrative \"silence\" must\nbe within your purview.\n\n\nI look forward to your reconsidered response.\n\n\nSincerely,\n\n\nSion Buckler\n\nOn Thu, 5 Feb 2026 at 08:40, HOC Public Administration and Constitutional\nAffairs Select Committee <pacac@parliament.uk> wrote:\n\n> Dear Requester,\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you for contacting the Public Administration and Constitutional\n> Affairs Committee.\n>\n>\n>\n> Unfortunately, this is not a matter within the remit of the Committee.\n>\n>\n>\n> The role of the Committee is to examine constitutional issues; the quality\n> and standards of administration provided by Civil Service departments; and\n> the reports of the Parliamentary and Health Service Ombudsman (PHSO).\n>\n>\n>\n> You can find out more about the work of the Committee here: Public\n> Administration and Constitutional Affairs Committee - Summary - Committees\n> - UK Parliament\n> <https://committees.parliament.uk/committee/327/public-administration-and-constitutional-affairs-committee/>\n>\n>\n>\n> Best wishes,\n>\n>\n>\n> Kate\n>\n>\n>\n> *Kate Johal*\n> Second Clerk\n> Public Administration and Constitutional Affairs Committee (PACAC)\n>\n>\n> House of Commons, Palace of Westminster, London SW1A 0AA\n> parliament.uk\n>\n> <https://www.parliament.uk/business/committees/>\n>\n> The House of Commons welcomes feedback. If you have any compliments,\n> complaints or comments,\n> about the service that you have received please send an email to\n> feedback@parliament.uk\n>\n>\n>\n> *From:* Requester <[redacted]>\n> *Sent:* 31 January 2026 06:14\n> *To:* HOC Public Administration and Constitutional Affairs Select\n> Committee <pacac@parliament.uk>\n> *Subject:* Systemic governance concern regarding historic land\n> registration and administrative reliance - BP vs Buckler 1987 (inc. BP Land\n> Registry 1982)\n>\n>\n>\n> Dear Committee Members,\n>\n> I am writing to draw your attention to a historical land matter which I\n> believe may illustrate potential vulnerabilities in public administration,\n> record-keeping, and the governance of land registration. My purpose is\n> solely to provide factual information for consideration, rather than to\n> request remedial action.\n>\n> The matter concerns land formerly known as *Great House Farm, Llandough,\n> Vale of Glamorgan*, later developed for residential use and historically\n> associated with *BP v Buckler (Court of Appeal, 1987)*.\n>\n> *Chronology of relevant events (as reflected in public records):*\n>\n>    - *July 1974:* Court proceedings involving my grandmother were\n>    adjourned without determination of ownership.\n>    - *31 October 1974:* A subsequent hearing did not proceed\n>    substantively, following correspondence received from BP entities that same\n>    day. No judgment on ownership was issued.\n>    - Throughout this period, my grandmother was not included in dealings\n>    concerning the land, while the opposing party continued to assert ownership.\n>    - *1982:* The land was registered in the name of BP entities, despite\n>    unresolved competing claims and no final judicial adjudication.\n>    - *1983:* My grandmother passed away after the registration, with\n>    ownership never conclusively adjudicated.\n>    - *1987 Court of Appeal:* The record reflects an apparent\n>    inconsistency in the treatment of correspondence and corporate entities.\n>    Specifically:\n>\n>\n>    - The court noted that my grandmother had replied to correspondence on\n>       31 October, asserting ownership.\n>       - The reply was treated as directed to the “wrong” corporation (BP\n>       Pensions Trust Ltd rather than BP Properties Ltd).\n>       - In a later appeal by my father, the same judge acknowledged that\n>       both BP entities were effectively treated as the same for the purposes of\n>       the ruling.\n>       - These circumstances resulted in the court upholding the opposing\n>       party’s registration despite prior correspondence demonstrating an\n>       assertion of ownership.\n>\n>\n>    - Following these proceedings, my father’s application to the *European\n>    Court of Human Rights* was blocked.\n>    - The family home was demolished, preventing re-entry, even for the\n>    retrieval of personal belongings.\n>    - Despite its historical significance and a petition to protect it,\n>    the property was not preserved. The house had prior heritage interest,\n>    including documented connections to Marconi.\n>    - *1991:* My father passed away at just 41.\n>    - *1994:* The land was subsequently excavated, with items of\n>    archaeological interest removed.\n>\n> Taken together, these events demonstrate a situation in which *land\n> registration and subsequent reliance by public bodies proceeded without\n> definitive adjudication of ownership*, raising questions regarding\n> administrative safeguards, procedural coherence, and record integrity.\n>\n> I am submitting this information purely for institutional awareness. I am *\n> not making allegations of misconduct*, nor requesting investigation. I am\n> able to provide a *concise factual timeline and references to public\n> records* should it assist the Committee’s understanding.\n>\n> Yours faithfully,\n>\n>\n> Requester\n> UK Parliament Disclaimer: this e-mail is confidential to the intended\n> recipient. If you have received it in error, please notify the sender and\n> delete it from your system. Any unauthorised use, disclosure, or copying is\n> not permitted. This e-mail has been checked for viruses, but no liability\n> is accepted for any damage caused by any virus transmitted by this e-mail.\n> This e-mail address is not secure, is not encrypted and should not be used\n> for sensitive data.\n>\n"},{"id":"wb-f1aca8be","subject":"Consortium land registry BP v Buckler","name":"2026-02-05_consortium-land_registry_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2026-02-05","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Land Registry correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-05_consortium-land_registry_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-land-registry-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-05_consortium-land_registry_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Thu, 5 Feb 2026 13:52:21 -0300\nSubject: Re: FORMAL ALLEGATION OF FRAUD: Ref: 260202-5732372\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V74J7QvSieC--HoCZ30jWJ_SMjzHH86_rjc4Y+caBtJTg@mail.gmail.com>\n\nThat is a very fair point, Bob. You’re right to be cautious—sometimes a\n\"hostile\" tone can cause a civil servant to shut down or hide behind red\ntape rather than helping you.\n\nThe reason it feels like they are being cooperative is that they have\nshifted from saying \"no\" to asking for *evidence*. This is a good sign.\nHowever, the reason we are \"rebutting\" is simply to correct their premise:\nthey think this is a recent fraud, and you are correcting them that the\n\"fraud\" was the *original act of registration in 1982.*\n\nLet’s soften the tone to be *\"Helpful but Firm.\"* We will treat them as an\ninvestigator who needs your guidance to find the truth, rather than an\nenemy.\nAdjusted \"Cooperative\" Draft\n\n*Subject:* Information regarding Registration Irregularities – Great House\nFarm (Ty Mawr) – Ref: 260202-5732372\n\n*Dear S.-G. Haste,*\n\nThank you for your guidance on how to proceed with this report. I\nappreciate the opportunity to clarify the nature of the irregularities I\nhave identified.\n\nAs requested, I have attached a *detailed PDF timeline* which provides the\nfull address and the historical context of the land formerly known as Great\nHouse Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough.\n\nTo assist your investigation into how the 1982 registration may have been\naffected by material non-disclosure, I would like to highlight three\nspecific areas where the \"actual facts\" on the ground differed from the\napplication made to the Registry:\n\n   1.\n\n   *Overriding Interests (The Williams Lineage):* At the time of first\n   registration in 1982, my family had been in continuous possession since\n   1667. I believe the registration was irregular because it misidentified the\n   occupant, Mary Williams, as \"Mrs. Buckler\"—a name she did not\n   use—effectively obscuring a 320-year \"Living Title\" from the Registry’s\n   records.\n   2.\n\n   *Suppression of Material Facts (Archaeology):* The site had \"actual\n   notice\" of national archaeological significance due to a Roman discovery in\n   1888. I am concerned that this was suppressed during the registration and\n   planning phases to facilitate the land's transition to housing.\n   3.\n\n   *Unresolved Ownership Claims:* In 1982, there was an active, adjourned\n   ownership dispute (from 1974) that I believe should have been disclosed to\n   the Land Registry before a clean title was granted to BP Properties Ltd.\n\nI am copying in the relevant heritage and oversight bodies (Cadw, GGAT, and\nthe Ombudsman) not as an act of hostility, but to ensure that all parties\nholding pieces of this historical puzzle are aware that an investigation\ninto the *integrity of the 1982 registration* is now underway.\n\nI have also copied in my MP, the Senedd, and relevant heritage bodies to\nensure that all parties with an interest in Welsh cultural heritage and\npublic record integrity are kept informed as we look into these 1982\nregistration details.\n\nI look forward to your thoughts on how we can review the original 1982\napplication files to see if these interests were disclosed.\n\n\nYours sincerely,\n\n\n*Requester*\n\n*Descendant of the Williams family,*\n\n*of Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough*\n\n\n\nOn Mon, 2 Feb 2026 at 09:01, Customer Support <\ncustomersupport@landregistry.gov.uk> wrote:\n\n> *Enquiry Reference:* 260202-5732372\n>\n> Dear Customer\n>\n> Thank you for your email dated 31 January 2026.\n>\n> Unfortunately, it is not clear from your email what the allegations of\n> fraud/potential irregularities in 1982 land registration are?\n>\n> In order for us to investigate further, please can you provide us with\n> either the full address of the property or the title number concerned.\n>\n> Can you explain /provide full details of what has happened?\n>\n> If the matter has been referred to the Police (please provide police\n> incident number) and advise us, if any legal advice has been taken\n> regarding the matter\n>\n> It is important to note that HM Land Registry can only consider and\n> investigate circumstances in which an alleged fraud relates to actual or\n> planned changes to the register. (registration)\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> Yours faithfully\n>\n> S.-G. Haste\n> HM Land Registry\n> reportafraud@landregistry.gov.uk\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> *We only provide factual information and impartial advice about our\n> procedures. Read more about the advice we give.\n> <https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/hm-land-registry-service-standards/our-service-standards>*\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> HM Land Registry’s purpose is to protect your land ownership and provide services and data that underpin an efficient and informed property market. Our vision is: “A world-leading property market as part of a thriving economy and a sustainable future”.\n>\n> We check all mail and attachments for known viruses. However, you are advised that you open any attachments at your own risk. If you have received this email and it was not intended for you, please let us know, then delete it.\n>\n> We welcome correspondence in English and Welsh.\n>\n> To see how HM Land Registry treats your personal information, read our Personal information charter: www.gov.uk/government/organisations/land-registry/about/personal-information-charter.\n>\n>\n>\n"},{"id":"wb-d03fbd9f","subject":"Consortium evidence BP v Buckler","name":"2026-02-06_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2026-02-06","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-06_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-evidence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-06_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Fri, 6 Feb 2026 02:21:09 -0300\nSubject: Formal Report of Suspected Indigenous Dispossession, Systemic Fraud, and Archaeological Suppression – Great House Farm, Llandough\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V5YSHEWyROtxwbF-YKZXgQSLUADHmPf+=HA1DhGfnc7ZQ@mail.gmail.com>\n\n*To whom it may concern,*\n\nI am writing to formally report a long-standing and systemic case of what I\nsuspect may be dispossession, fraudulent misrepresentation, and\narchaeological suppression concerning my family’s ancestral land, Great\nHouse Farm (historically Tŷ Mawr/Cedfin), Llandough.\n\nI am the descendant of the Williams/Buckler family, who held de facto\npossession and indigenous tenure of this land from 1667 until our forced\ndisplacement in 1988. I am currently the only member of my family who is\nnot too traumatized to speak of these events. My mother (Mary Williams\ndaughter in law), remains to this day too terrified to speak up about this\nmulti-generational \"lawfare\" campaign waged against us. Our family have not\nseen justice or received reperations, nor have we sued or reopened this\ncase under limitations act clauses of fraud and consealment. Yet. But this\nmay not be just a grave injustice upon our family, but our nations\narcheological history and indigenous people on our ancestral birthland. We\nare Welsh Nationals, Welsh speaking, under Welsh flags. This is some\nBritish Colonisation-grade trespass I'm seeing and it's tandamount to a\ndeclaration of civil war on me, my family, community and people.\n\n*The core of this report involves the following suspicions I have of\nconcealment and fraud (see attached for my timeline and notes):*\n\n   1.\n\n   *Archaeological Suppression:* In 1888, my family discovered a Roman\n   soldier in full armor beneath the living room floorboards. This discovery\n   provided \"actual notice\" of the site's national significance a century\n   before its demolition. We allege this notice was systematically suppressed\n   by corporate and state actors to allow for commercial development.\n   2.\n\n   *Fraudulent Misrepresentation in Planning:* During the 1987 planning\n   process, the 13th-century fortified manor was described by developers as a\n   \"dilapidated utility building\" or \"ruined barn\" to secure a demolition\n   permit that would have otherwise been denied under heritage protection laws.\n   3.\n\n   *Legal Identity Falsification:* In 1974, BP Pension Trust issued a\n   \"unilateral license\" intended to bypass our possessory title. This\n   correspondence was falsely addressed to \"Mrs. Buckler\" to bind my\n   grandmother to her deceased husband’s name and legal status, despite her\n   public rejection of their claim. She used her maiden name Williams and her\n   husband Bucklers' intent to accept license died with him. My nan also\n   alledgedly didn't expressly deny the offer and so accepted through her\n   silence. But she was near enough voiceless from screaming for decades that\n   she will not do deals with any of them. That's a different kind of silence,\n   if any. And to have responded to license to Mrs Buckler would have been\n   identify fraud as she was Mrs Williams. And she did in fact reject BPs\n   license offer and advanced, countless times, but publically. I believe\n   these objections were suppressed too. There's allegedly news coverage from\n   October 30th 1974 but I'm yet to find the evidence. But see attached a 1978\n   article I unearthed. My nan clearly maintains her position \"It's my home\".\n\n   4.\n\n   You'll also see in landmark legal case BP Vs Buckler 1987 the judge says\n   that she might have responded to BP, but to BP Pension Trust, but the\n   claimant was BP Properties Ltd which were two separate companies. But in my\n   dads appeal when he challenges that BP Pension Trust Ltd should have been\n   the claimant the same judge dismissed his appeal citing both BP companies\n   are effectively the same. This is clearly a case of heads BP won, Tails we\n   lost.\n   5.\n\n   *Missing Evidence:* Critical title deeds and Bute Estate records from\n   our families deal with Daniel Thomas, for title after quarrying completed,\n   in exchange for our permission to quarry, also disappeared from the loft of\n   our home and the Cardiff Library according to my families accounts,\n   precisely when they were needed to support our ownership claim in the Court\n   of Appeal. Regardless we should have still remained on grounds we'd been\n   there since 1667, and failing that on grounds of adverse possession.\n   6.\n\n   It was evident lawfare to remove us, to get to the burials known about\n   since 1888, BP profited from the land sale, the state and Crown have the\n   exhevated artifacts and my displaced family are left traumatised, cheated\n   and dead. My dad's appeal was dismissed to when it shouldn't have been and\n   his ECHR request blocked, before dying abruptly at age just 41.\n\nThe confirmation in 1994 of a high-status Roman villa and over 1,000\nburials on the exact site of our demolished home proves that the \"official\nnarrative\" used to evict us was predicated on the concealment of known\nfacts.\n\nUnder *Section 32 of the Limitation Act 1980*, the limitation period for\naction does not begin until such fraud or concealment is discovered. As the\nheir to this possessory title, I am seeking a formal investigation into the\ninstitutional failures that permitted this dispossession and the\ndestruction of a national monument.\n\nI have compiled my account and understanding of these events in timeline\norder and I'm prepared to provide further testimony and evidence.\n\nGiven the extreme trauma my family has suffered, I request that all\ncorrespondence be handled with the gravity this human rights violation\ndeserves.\n\nConsidering the lack of cooperation from my traumatised family. And\nauthorities who maybe complicit and implicated. And possible corruption of\nrecords and fraud and consealment, I'm open to the fact my account and\nunderstanding may not be perfect. But if even a single other person or\ngroup looked into this too, even briefly, I'm sure you'll agree there's\nmerrit to the concerns I'm raising with all of this.\n\nI look forward to your response regarding the appropriate next steps for\ninvestigation and restitution.\n\nI do ask for some small sense of urgency in that justice delayed is justice\ndenied. I understand this is ancient news for many, but my family still\nfeel a tremendous sense of disposition and disposition over all of this.\nBringing this all up means to re live it.\n\nI will have been 3 or 4 at the time, but I still remember my toys in our\nhouse, being bulldozed. And my mum handing us rocks to throw at them,\nbulldozing down our home. As my dad was held without charges to prevent him\ndefending us and our home. I'd have to visit him in a hospital bed after\nhis heart attack and see him in a coffin shortly afterwards.\n\nI'm 41 years old now. I'm a certified and experienced military commander\nand leader. I left my military career with honours. I finished 22 years in\nOath in 2024, bond afide, my last 2 employed under the Welsh government.\n\nI kindly request but also require of you, that this matter be layed to rest\ncorrectly.\n\nMy family never surrendered. Never relinquished claim to that land. We\nwe're in honour in that we weren't there with force, stealth/ secrecy or\nlicense. But BP and the British State did use force, stealth/ secrecy and\nlicense.\n\nAll things considered I believe I find myself in honour here, and the\nBritish Establishment in dishonour. This matter must be escalated. I'm duty\nbound.\n\n\nRegards,\n\n\nSion Buckler\n"},{"id":"wb-0af67ace","subject":"Consortium evidence BP v Buckler","name":"2026-02-06_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","date":"2026-02-06","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-06_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-evidence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-06_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: ask <ask@ombudsman.wales>\nDate: Fri, 6 Feb 2026 05:21:57 +0000\nSubject: RE: Formal Report of Suspected Indigenous Dispossession, Systemic Fraud, and Archaeological Suppression – Great House Farm, Llandough\nMessage-ID: <LO6P123MB74122ECF5F4AFCB7E0BB00C7A166A@LO6P123MB7412.GBRP123.PROD.OUTLOOK.COM>\n\nMae hon yn neges ddwyieithog. Sgroliwch i lawr i weld y Gymraeg\n\nThank you for contacting the Ombudsman’s office. We do not accept complaints into this inbox. If you want to complain to our office electronically, please complete our online complaint form<https://complaints.ombudsman.wales/en/pre-complaint>.\n\n\nThe form is easy to use and will help you find out if your complaint is one that we can look into. You can attach additional files at the end of the form.\n\n\nHow else can I complain?\n• By phone: 0300 790 0203. Our phone line is open 10am-12:30pm and 1:30pm-4pm Monday – Friday\n• By post: print out the complaint form<https://www.ombudsman.wales/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Complaint-form-public-services-1.pdf> and return it to us.\n\nWhat if I need support to complain?\n• Use this email address or call us to let us know how we can help you use our service. We will do our best to help.\n• Ask an advocate to help you<https://www.ombudsman.wales/advice-advocates/>.\n\n\n\nAre we the right organisation for you?\nWe look at complaints about public services in Wales or Welsh councillors breaching the Code of Conduct. However, there are other organisations that may be able to help you<https://www.ombudsman.wales/other-organisations-that-may-be-able-to-help-you/>.\n\n\nIf we think we need to respond to your email, we will aim to respond within 7 days.\n\nIf you have copied us into an email, please be aware that we do not act or respond to copy emails.\n\n____________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________\n\n\nDiolch am gysylltu â swyddfa'r Ombwdsmon. Nid ydym yn derbyn cwynion i'r mewnflwch hwn. Os ydych am gwyno i'n swyddfa yn electronig, cwblhewch ein ffurflen gwyno ar-lein<https://complaints.ombwdsmon.cymru/cy/cyn-g%C5%B5yn>.\nMae'r ffurflen yn hawdd i’w defnyddio.ydd yn eich helpu i ganfod a yw eich cwyn yn un y gallwn ymchwilio iddi. Gallwch atodi ffeiliau ychwanegol ar ddiwedd y ffurflen.\n\nSut arall allaf gwyno?\n• Dros y ffôn: 0300 790 0203. Mae ein llinell ffôn ar agor 10am-12:30pm a 1:30pm-4pm o ddydd Llun i ddydd Gwener\n• Trwy'r post: argraffwch y ffurflen gwyno<https://www.ombwdsmon.cymru/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2025/10/Ffurflen-gwyno-gwasanaethau-cyhoeddus.pdf> a'i dychwelyd atom.\n\nBeth os oes angen cefnogaeth arnaf i gwyno?\n• Defnyddiwch y cyfeiriad e-bost hwn neu ffoniwch ni i roi gwybod i ni sut y gallwn eich helpu i ddefnyddio ein gwasanaeth. Gwnawn ein gorau i helpu.\n• Gofyn i eiriolwr eich helpu<https://www.ombwdsmon.cymru/cyrff-cynghori-ac-eirioli/>.\n\n\nAi ni yw'r sefydliad cywir i chi?\nEdrychwn ar gwynion am wasanaethau cyhoeddus yng Nghymru neu gynghorwyr Cymru yn torri'r Cod Ymddygiad. Fodd bynnag, mae sefydliadau eraill a allai eich helpu<https://www.ombwdsmon.cymru/sefydliadau-eraill-a-allai-eich-helpu/>.\n\n\nOs credwn fod angen i ni ymateb i'ch e-bost, byddwn yn anelu at ymateb o fewn 7 diwrnod.\n\n\nOs ydych wedi ein copïo i mewn e-bost, nodwch nad ydym yn gweithredu nac yn ymateb i negeseuon e-bost copi.\n\n________________________________\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]>\nSent: Friday, February 6, 2026 5:21:09 AM\nTo: scheduledmonuments@gov.wales <scheduledmonuments@gov.wales>\nCc: ask <ask@ombudsman.wales>; reportafraud@landregistry.gov.uk <reportafraud@landregistry.gov.uk>; Vikki.Howells@senedd.wales <Vikki.Howells@senedd.wales>\nSubject: Formal Report of Suspected Indigenous Dispossession, Systemic Fraud, and Archaeological Suppression – Great House Farm, Llandough\n\nYou don't often get email from [redacted]. Learn why this is important<https://aka.ms/LearnAboutSenderIdentification>\n\nTo whom it may concern,\n\nI am writing to formally report a long-standing and systemic case of what I suspect may be dispossession, fraudulent misrepresentation, and archaeological suppression concerning my family’s ancestral land, Great House Farm (historically Tŷ Mawr/Cedfin), Llandough.\n\nI am the descendant of the Williams/Buckler family, who held de facto possession and indigenous tenure of this land from 1667 until our forced displacement in 1988. I am currently the only member of my family who is not too traumatized to speak of these events. My mother (Mary Williams daughter in law), remains to this day too terrified to speak up about this multi-generational \"lawfare\" campaign waged against us. Our family have not seen justice or received reperations, nor have we sued or reopened this case under limitations act clauses of fraud and consealment. Yet. But this may not be just a grave injustice upon our family, but our nations archeological history and indigenous people on our ancestral birthland. We are Welsh Nationals, Welsh speaking, under Welsh flags. This is some British Colonisation-grade trespass I'm seeing and it's tandamount to a declaration of civil war on me, my family, community and people.\n\nThe core of this report involves the following suspicions I have of concealment and fraud (see attached for my timeline and notes):\n\n  1.  Archaeological Suppression: In 1888, my family discovered a Roman soldier in full armor beneath the living room floorboards. This discovery provided \"actual notice\" of the site's national significance a century before its demolition. We allege this notice was systematically suppressed by corporate and state actors to allow for commercial development.\n\n  2.  Fraudulent Misrepresentation in Planning: During the 1987 planning process, the 13th-century fortified manor was described by developers as a \"dilapidated utility building\" or \"ruined barn\" to secure a demolition permit that would have otherwise been denied under heritage protection laws.\n\n  3.  Legal Identity Falsification: In 1974, BP Pension Trust issued a \"unilateral license\" intended to bypass our possessory title. This correspondence was falsely addressed to \"Mrs. Buckler\" to bind my grandmother to her deceased husband’s name and legal status, despite her public rejection of their claim. She used her maiden name Williams and her husband Bucklers' intent to accept license died with him. My nan also alledgedly didn't expressly deny the offer and so accepted through her silence. But she was near enough voiceless from screaming for decades that she will not do deals with any of them. That's a different kind of silence, if any. And to have responded to license to Mrs Buckler would have been identify fraud as she was Mrs Williams. And she did in fact reject BPs license offer and advanced, countless times, but publically. I believe these objections were suppressed too. There's allegedly news coverage from October 30th 1974 but I'm yet to find the evidence. But see attached a 1978 article I unearthed. My nan clearly maintains her position \"It's my home\".   [cid:ii_19c31438f12e2e321bc2]\n\n  4.  You'll also see in landmark legal case BP Vs Buckler 1987 the judge says that she might have responded to BP, but to BP Pension Trust, but the claimant was BP Properties Ltd which were two separate companies. But in my dads appeal when he challenges that BP Pension Trust Ltd should have been the claimant the same judge dismissed his appeal citing both BP companies are effectively the same. This is clearly a case of heads BP won, Tails we lost.\n\n  5.  Missing Evidence: Critical title deeds and Bute Estate records from our families deal with Daniel Thomas, for title after quarrying completed, in exchange for our permission to quarry, also disappeared from the loft of our home and the Cardiff Library according to my families accounts, precisely when they were needed to support our ownership claim in the Court of Appeal. Regardless we should have still remained on grounds we'd been there since 1667, and failing that on grounds of adverse possession.\n\n  6.  It was evident lawfare to remove us, to get to the burials known about since 1888, BP profited from the land sale, the state and Crown have the exhevated artifacts and my displaced family are left traumatised, cheated and dead. My dad's appeal was dismissed to when it shouldn't have been and his ECHR request blocked, before dying abruptly at age just 41.\n\nThe confirmation in 1994 of a high-status Roman villa and over 1,000 burials on the exact site of our demolished home proves that the \"official narrative\" used to evict us was predicated on the concealment of known facts.\n\nUnder Section 32 of the Limitation Act 1980, the limitation period for action does not begin until such fraud or concealment is discovered. As the heir to this possessory title, I am seeking a formal investigation into the institutional failures that permitted this dispossession and the destruction of a national monument.\n\nI have compiled my account and understanding of these events in timeline order and I'm prepared to provide further testimony and evidence.\n\nGiven the extreme trauma my family has suffered, I request that all correspondence be handled with the gravity this human rights violation deserves.\n\nConsidering the lack of cooperation from my traumatised family. And authorities who maybe complicit and implicated. And possible corruption of records and fraud and consealment, I'm open to the fact my account and understanding may not be perfect. But if even a single other person or group looked into this too, even briefly, I'm sure you'll agree there's merrit to the concerns I'm raising with all of this.\n\nI look forward to your response regarding the appropriate next steps for investigation and restitution.\n\nI do ask for some small sense of urgency in that justice delayed is justice denied. I understand this is ancient news for many, but my family still feel a tremendous sense of disposition and disposition over all of this. Bringing this all up means to re live it.\n\nI will have been 3 or 4 at the time, but I still remember my toys in our house, being bulldozed. And my mum handing us rocks to throw at them, bulldozing down our home. As my dad was held without charges to prevent him defending us and our home. I'd have to visit him in a hospital bed after his heart attack and see him in a coffin shortly afterwards.\n\nI'm 41 years old now. I'm a certified and experienced military commander and leader. I left my military career with honours. I finished 22 years in Oath in 2024, bond afide, my last 2 employed under the Welsh government.\n\nI kindly request but also require of you, that this matter be layed to rest correctly.\n\nMy family never surrendered. Never relinquished claim to that land. We we're in honour in that we weren't there with force, stealth/ secrecy or license. But BP and the British State did use force, stealth/ secrecy and license.\n\nAll things considered I believe I find myself in honour here, and the British Establishment in dishonour. This matter must be escalated. I'm duty bound.\n\n\nRegards,\n\n\nSion Buckler\n\n--\n\nOmbwdsmon Gwasanaethau Cyhoeddus Cymru/ Public Services Ombudsman for Wales\n\n1 Ffordd yr Hen Gae\n\nPencoed\n\nPen-y-Bont ar Ogwr/ Bridgend/\n\nCF35 5LJ\n\nwww.ombwdsmon-cymru.org.uk<http://www.ombwdsmon-cymru.org.uk> / www.ombudsman.wales<https://www.ombudsman.wales>\n\nhttps://twitter.com/OmbudsmanWales\n\nHybu’r Gymraeg\n\nGallwch ysgrifennu atom yn Gymraeg a byddwn yn ymateb yn Gymraeg. Ni fydd hyn yn arwain at oedi cyn ymateb.\n\nWelsh Language Promotion\n\nYou can write to us in Welsh and we will reply in Welsh.  This will not lead to a delay in responding.\n\nSganiwyd y neges hon am bob feirws hysbys wrth iddi adael Ombwdsmon Gwasanaethau Cyhoeddus Cymru.\n\nMae Ombwdsmon Gwasanaethau Cyhoeddus Cymru yn cymryd o ddifrif yr angen i ddiogelu eich data.\n\nMae ein Hysbysiad Preifatrwydd yn esbonio sut rydym yn defnyddio eich gwybodaeth a sut rydym yn diogelu eich preifatrwydd.\n\nOn leaving the Public Services Ombudsman, this email was scanned for all known viruses.\n\nThe Public Services Ombudsman takes the protection of your data seriously.\n\nOur Privacy Notice explains how we use your information and the ways in which we protect your privacy.\n\nBydd pob galwad yn cael ei recordio ar gyfer dibenion hyfforddi a chyfeirio\n\nAll calls are recorded for training and reference purposes\n\nYstyriwch yr amgylchedd – a oes wir angen i chi argraffu’r neges e-bost hon?\n\nPlease consider the environment - do you really need to print this email?\n\n"},{"id":"wb-04fde097","subject":"Consortium evidence BP v Buckler","name":"2026-02-06_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.002.txt","date":"2026-02-06","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-06_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.002.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-evidence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-06_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.002.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Fri, 6 Feb 2026 14:50:40 -0300\nSubject: Re: Epexio contact us form submission\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V5OUkaWWrQ-rdbWXu=CnZPrKB1qXi_KEDFt1Oxd1ToZQA@mail.gmail.com>\n\nMs Taylor,\n\nThanks for your reply and for outlining the options available to consult\nthe estate plans referenced.\n\nI should clarify my position, as my correspondence is not a routine request\nfor private research access, but part of a wider and serious concern\nregarding possible historical misdescription, suppression, and/or\nconcealment of material facts relating to Great House Farm, Llandough.\n\nI am currently at the Welsh settlement in Argentina, having been displaced\nfrom my ancestral birthplace in Llandough. As such, it is not possible for\nme to attend the searchroom in person so easy as you're 7,500 miles away,\nnor to “pop in” to consult records on site. Requiring physical attendance\nor payment for access is therefore not a practical or equitable option in\nmy circumstances.\n\nMore importantly, my concern is that certain archival descriptions,\nsurveys, and planning-era records may themselves be inaccurate or\nincomplete as a result of earlier failures of disclosure. Specifically, a\nRoman burial reportedly discovered at Great House Farm in 1888 appears to\nhave been treated as a “surprise” in later archaeological surveys\n(1978/1994), and an occupied, c.800-year-old domestic dwelling was\nrepeatedly described in planning documentation as an “old barn” or “utility\nbuilding.” These characterisations materially affected heritage protection,\nculminating in demolition in 1988.\n\nIn that context, I am not seeking to pay for copies of records whose\naccuracy is itself in question, nor to participate financially in processes\nthat may inadvertently perpetuate errors arising from that period. My\nintention is to place relevant public bodies on notice of a potential issue\nof public record integrity and heritage governance, not to obtain documents\nfor private interest. I have written to the Senedd calling for a public\nenquiry too. I've notified the land registry and serious fraud teams too.\n\nI am therefore asking that my correspondence be treated as a formal\nexpression of concern regarding the reliability and completeness of the\narchival record as it relates to this site, and that this concern be noted\naccordingly. I would also be grateful if you could advise which department\nor officer is appropriate for addressing concerns of this nature, where the\nissue is not access but potential historical misrepresentation within\npublic records.\n\nFor the avoidance of doubt, I am not alleging any impropriety on the part\nof current archive staff. My concern relates to historical processes and\ndecisions, and to ensuring that those matters are properly examined rather\nthan inadvertently reinforced.\n\nThank you for your time and consideration.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nSion Buckler\n------------------------------\n\nOn Fri, 6 Feb 2026, 10:44 Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg, <\nGlamro@cardiff.gov.uk> wrote:\n\n> *Ref: 2026/0633*\n>\n>\n>\n> Dear Requester,\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank  you for your email received on 5th February 2026.\n>\n>\n>\n> You would be welcome to visit us to consult the estate plans DB/E/1-2 in\n> our public searchroom and take  your own photographs for free. Our opening\n> hours are Tues-Thurs 9.30am-12.30pm & 1.30pm-4.30pm. We are currently\n> taking bookings from *Tuesday 17th February 2026.* Please note that our\n> public searchroom is *closed* for the week Tues 10th February – Thurs\n> 12th February so that staff can carry out essential work on the\n> collections. We will re-open to the public on Tuesday 17th February 2026.\n>\n>\n>\n> If you have not visited us before, you are required to register. We ask\n> that you bring with you two forms of ID, one showing your name e.g. Driving\n> Licence and the other your current address e.g. current utility bill or\n> official letter.\n>\n>\n>\n> Alternatively, if you are unable to visit us in person, and if you can\n> confirm exactly which pages you require, staff can provide digital copies,\n> for which we charge £15.60 for the first image and £4.80 each for\n> subsequent images, payable in advance. Prices include VAT.\n>\n>\n>\n> You can find a detailed list on the catalogue: Survey of marquis of\n> Bute’s Glamorgan estates, by David Stewart.\n> <https://glamorgan.epexio.com/en/records/DB/E/1-8/1-2> If you would like\n> a quote for digital copies, please let get in touch.\n>\n>\n>\n> We hope this information is of assistance to you.\n>\n>\n>\n> <https://www.google.com/maps/search/Clos+Parc+Morgannwg+%0D%0A+Lecwydd+%2F+Leckwith+%0D%0A+Caerdydd+%2F+Cardiff+%0D%0A+CF11+8AW?entry=gmail&source=g>\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n> <https://www.google.com/maps/search/Clos+Parc+Morgannwg+%0D%0A+Lecwydd+%2F+Leckwith+%0D%0A+Caerdydd+%2F+Cardiff+%0D%0A+CF11+8AW?entry=gmail&source=g>\n>\n>\n>\n> <https://www.google.com/maps/search/Clos+Parc+Morgannwg+%0D%0A+Lecwydd+%2F+Leckwith+%0D%0A+Caerdydd+%2F+Cardiff+%0D%0A+CF11+8AW?entry=gmail&source=g>\n>\n> Melanie Taylor\n>\n> Cynorthwyydd Cofnodion / Records Assistant\n>\n>\n>\n> Archifau Morgannwg / Glamorgan Archives\n>\n> Clos Parc Morgannwg\n> <https://www.google.com/maps/search/Clos+Parc+Morgannwg+%0D%0A+Lecwydd+%2F+Leckwith+%0D%0A+Caerdydd+%2F+Cardiff+%0D%0A+CF11+8AW?entry=gmail&source=g>\n>\n> Lecwydd / Leckwith\n> <https://www.google.com/maps/search/Clos+Parc+Morgannwg+%0D%0A+Lecwydd+%2F+Leckwith+%0D%0A+Caerdydd+%2F+Cardiff+%0D%0A+CF11+8AW?entry=gmail&source=g>\n>\n> Caerdydd / Cardiff\n> <https://www.google.com/maps/search/Clos+Parc+Morgannwg+%0D%0A+Lecwydd+%2F+Leckwith+%0D%0A+Caerdydd+%2F+Cardiff+%0D%0A+CF11+8AW?entry=gmail&source=g>\n>\n> CF11 8AW\n> <https://www.google.com/maps/search/Clos+Parc+Morgannwg+%0D%0A+Lecwydd+%2F+Leckwith+%0D%0A+Caerdydd+%2F+Cardiff+%0D%0A+CF11+8AW?entry=gmail&source=g>\n>\n>\n>\n> 02920872200\n>\n> glamro@cardiff.gov.uk\n>\n> www.archifaumorgannwg.gov.uk / www.glamarchives.gov.uk\n>\n> www.facebook.com/glamarchives\n>\n> www.twitter.com/glamarchives\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> **********************************************************************\n>\n> The Council welcomes correspondence in Welsh, English or bilingually. We\n> will ensure that we communicate with you in the language of your choice, as\n> long as you let us know which you prefer. Corresponding in Welsh will not\n> lead to delay.\n>\n> Privileged/Confidential Information may be contained in this message. If\n> you are not the addressee indicated in this message (or responsible for\n> delivery of the message to such person), you may not copy or deliver this\n> message to anyone. In such case, you should destroy this message and kindly\n> notify the sender by reply email. Please advise immediately if you or your\n> employer does not consent to Internet email for messages of this kind.\n> Opinions, conclusions and other information in this message that do not\n> relate to the official business of the Council of the City and County of\n> Cardiff shall be understood as neither given nor endorsed by it. All e-mail\n> sent to or from this address will be processed by Cardiff County Councils\n> Corporate E-mail system and may be subject to scrutiny by someone other\n> than the addressee.\n>\n> **********************************************************************\n>\n> Mae’r Cyngor yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg, Saesneg neu’n ddwyieithog.\n> Byddwn yn cyfathrebu â chi yn ôl eich dewis, dim ond i chi roi gwybod i ni\n> pa un sydd well gennych. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi.\n>\n> Mae'n bosibl bod gwybodaeth gyfrinachol yn y neges hon. Os na chyfeirir y\n> neges atoch chi'n benodol (neu os nad ydych chi'n gyfrifol am drosglwyddo'r\n> neges i'r person a enwir), yna ni chewch gopio na throsglwyddo'r neges.\n> Mewn achos o'r fath, dylech ddinistrio'r neges a hysbysu'r anfonwr drwy\n> e-bost ar unwaith. Rhowch wybod i'r anfonydd ar unwaith os nad ydych chi\n> neu eich cyflogydd yn caniatau e-bost y Rhyngrwyd am negeseuon fel hon.\n> Rhaid deall nad yw'r safbwyntiau, y casgliadau a'r wybodaeth arall yn y\n> neges hon nad ydynt yn cyfeirio at fusnes swyddogol Cyngor Dinas a Sir\n> Caerdydd yn cynrychioli barn y Cyngor Sir nad yn cael sel ei fendith. Caiff\n> unrhyw negeseuon a anfonir at, neu o'r cyfeiriad e-bost hwn eu prosesu gan\n> system E-bost Gorfforaethol Cyngor Sir Caerdydd a gallant gael eu\n> harchwilio gan rywun heblaw'r person a enwir.\n>\n> **********************************************************************\n>\n"},{"id":"wb-35b53a38","subject":"Consortium evidence BP v Buckler","name":"2026-02-06_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.003.txt","date":"2026-02-06","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-06_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.003.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-evidence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-06_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.003.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Fri, 6 Feb 2026 15:11:06 -0300\nSubject: Re: Response to National Archives case number CAS-313954-Y6C0H3 CRM:022601115\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V6aD_pUEdcqL=ViCxt0Hf47qQfC8CSCDhhkh=ZqO46C1g@mail.gmail.com>\n\nMs Smith,\n\nThanks for your reply and for explaining The National Archives’ position\nregarding annotation and review of records.\n\nI fully accept that archives do not resolve disputes and that records are\noften incomplete or patchwork in character. My purpose in writing is not to\nseek amendment, adjudication, or retrospective commentary, but to raise a\nspecific concern about how absence, silence, and fragmentation within the\narchival record should be understood where material public consequences\nclearly followed.\n\nBy way of clarification, my concern relates to the historical record\nsurrounding Great House Farm, Llandough, and the proceedings culminating in *BP\nProperties Ltd v Buckler* [1987] EWCA Civ 2. In particular:\n\n   - Ownership and occupation of the farmhouse and garden were actively\n   contested in 1974, with a defence asserting adverse possession filed on\n   behalf of Mary Williams, and the matter adjourned without a final\n   determination of title.\n   - Enforcement of earlier possession orders was deferred, a warrant was\n   withdrawn on 31 October 1974, and the occupier remained in possession until\n   her death in 1983.\n   - Despite this, land registration, demolition (1988), and archaeological\n   intervention proceeded without a High Court determination of ownership and\n   without disclosure of earlier, locally known archaeological discoveries.\n   - A Roman burial reportedly discovered beneath the farmhouse in 1888 by\n   the occupying family appears not to have been reflected in later “rescue”\n   excavations (1978–1980) or the major 1994 excavation, which treated Roman\n   and early medieval burials as newly revealed.\n\nTaken together, this produces an archival landscape in which:\n\n   - court files relating to decisive events appear absent or incomplete;\n   - later records imply finality where proceedings were in fact unresolved;\n   - and the destruction of a long-occupied medieval structure occurred\n   before the site’s known archaeological significance was formally\n   acknowledged.\n\nI note and appreciate your confirmation that the principal judicial series\n(J 157, J 83, J 84) contain no relevant material. Given the scale of the\nconsequences flowing from the dispute—forced removal, demolition of an\noccupied dwelling, and loss of heritage fabric—the absence of supporting\ncourt or administrative files is itself significant for researchers.\n\nAccordingly, I would be grateful for guidance on the following procedural\npoints:\n\n   - how researchers are expected to interpret gaps or silence in the\n   archival record where substantial actions plainly occurred;\n   - whether related non-judicial records (for example, departmental,\n   planning, regulatory, or corporate records connected to land registration,\n   planning consent, or archaeological works) are more likely to sit outside\n   the obvious court series; and\n   - whether there is an appropriate mechanism for flagging concerns about\n   record completeness or contextual risk, without seeking alteration of the\n   records themselves.\n\nFor the avoidance of doubt, I am not alleging impropriety on the part of\nThe National Archives or its staff. My concern is to ensure that the\nhistorical record is approached with appropriate caution where absence may\nunintentionally convey certainty or closure that did not exist at the time.\n\nThank you again for your time and professionalism.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nSion Buckler\n\nOn Fri, 6 Feb 2026, 10:07 RESO Records Enquiries, <\nRESORecordsEnquiries@nationalarchives.gov.uk> wrote:\n\n> Dear Requester,\n>\n> Thank you for clarifying the nature of your enquiry.\n>\n> In answer to your main enquiry, in general we would not add notes to, or\n> review, records in light of unresolved disputes or other matters. The\n> records are left to speak for themselves. In fact, those records are often\n> incomplete and patchwork in character, meaning that they may not enable the\n> enquirer to know what the final determination or outcome of any dispute was.\n>\n> Since you refer to a reported Court of Appeal (Civil Division) case, I\n> have conducted a quick search of our, admittedly limited holdings. The main\n> record series in which you might expect to find archival material here are J\n> 157 <https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/r/C9764>, J 83\n> <https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/r/C9691> and J 84\n> <https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/r/C9692>.\n> Unfortunately, none contain records relevant to the case.\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n> Charlotte Smith﻿\n>\n> Principal Records Specialist, Modern Legal Records\n> *www.nationalarchives.gov.uk* <http://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/>\n>\n> *Enquiry:*\n>\n>\n>\n> To clarify my original enquiry, I am specifically concerned with the\n> historical record surrounding the case of BP Properties Ltd v Buckler\n> [1987] EWCA Civ 2, which involved a disputed land dispossession at Great\n> House Farm in Llandough, South Glamorgan. This stemmed from earlier\n> proceedings in 1974, including an adjourned case in July and an appeal\n> attempt on October 31st that was impacted by correspondence from BP the\n> same day. Following the 1987 ruling, there was forced removal, demolition\n> of our family home, and archaeological activity on the site, despite\n> ongoing questions about the adjudication of ownership prior to land\n> registration.\n>\n> My question is not solely about locating ownership or court documents\n> (though any guidance on whether The National Archives holds physical files\n> from these proceedings would be welcome), but rather whether circumstances\n> like these—where ownership was disputed and not fully resolved before\n> registration or excavation—raise any considerations for the integrity or\n> completeness of the archival record. For instance, do such disputes prompt\n> reviews or annotations in historical land records, or are there protocols\n> for archiving related materials from corporate entities like BP or local\n> authorities involved?\n>\n> I understand your resources are focused on national-level holdings, but\n> any further direction would greatly assist my research.\n> Please don't print this e-mail unless you really need to.\n> -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n> National Archives Disclaimer This email and any files transmitted with it\n> are intended solely for the use of the individual(s) to whom they are\n> addressed. If you are not the intended recipient and have received this\n> email in error, please notify the sender and delete the email. Opinions,\n> conclusions and other information in this message and attachments that do\n> not relate to the official business of The National Archives are neither\n> given nor endorsed by it.\n> ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n>\n>\n"},{"id":"wb-852dedf1","subject":"Consortium evidence BP v Buckler","name":"2026-02-06_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.004.txt","date":"2026-02-06","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-06_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.004.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-evidence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-06_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.004.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Fri, 6 Feb 2026 07:49:09 -0300\nSubject: Re: Response to enquiry at The National Archives. Case number: CAS-313954-Y6C0H3 CRM:052900508\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V5DxGhWimcvhMoCy_ZqTQO14ax6Z_v-namAEx=kSqKR6w@mail.gmail.com>\n\nRobert Fleming,\n\nThank you for your prompt response and for providing the helpful research\nguides on land ownership records,\n\nTo clarify my original enquiry, I am specifically concerned with the\nhistorical record surrounding the case of BP Properties Ltd v Buckler\n[1987] EWCA Civ 2, which involved a disputed land dispossession at Great\nHouse Farm in Llandough, South Glamorgan. This stemmed from earlier\nproceedings in 1974, including an adjourned case in July and an appeal\nattempt on October 31st that was impacted by correspondence from BP the\nsame day. Following the 1987 ruling, there was forced removal, demolition\nof our family home, and archaeological activity on the site, despite\nongoing questions about the adjudication of ownership prior to land\nregistration.\n\nMy question is not solely about locating ownership or court documents\n(though any guidance on whether The National Archives holds physical files\nfrom these proceedings would be welcome), but rather whether circumstances\nlike these—where ownership was disputed and not fully resolved before\nregistration or excavation—raise any considerations for the integrity or\ncompleteness of the archival record. For instance, do such disputes prompt\nreviews or annotations in historical land records, or are there protocols\nfor archiving related materials from corporate entities like BP or local\nauthorities involved?\n\nI understand your resources are focused on national-level holdings, but any\nfurther direction would greatly assist my research.\n\nSincerely,\n\nSion Buckler\n\nOn Fri, 6 Feb 2026 at 07:28, RESO Records Enquiries <\nRESORecordsEnquiries@nationalarchives.gov.uk> wrote:\n\n> Dear Requester,\n>\n> Thank you for contacting The National Archives of the United Kingdom.\n>\n> It is unlikely The National Archives holds the records of this type of\n> case, and the Central Land Registry\n> <https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/land-registry>, who hold\n> records of land registration, is a separate body. Some records of land\n> ownership can be found among records at local or county archives.\n>\n> I recommend you consult our guide to searching for records of Land\n> ownership records and deeds: an overview - The National Archives\n> <https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/help-with-your-research/research-guides/land-ownership-records-and-deeds-an-overview/>.\n> Regarding the court cases you mention, I also recommend you consult: Civil\n> court cases: Chancery Division since 1875 - The National Archives\n> <https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/help-with-your-research/research-guides/civil-court-cases-chancery-division-since-1875/>,\n> and you may also find Civil courts: appeal cases after 1875 - The\n> National Archives\n> <https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/help-with-your-research/research-guides/civil-courts-appeal-cases-after-1875/>\n> of use to your research. These guides will help you to use *our catalogue*\n> <https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/>, or other online sources,\n> effectively and focus your search.\n>\n> Records are represented in our online catalogue by a title, a reference\n> and a brief description. When you search our catalogue using keywords you\n> are searching across these titles and descriptions, rather than the\n> contents of the records themselves. By clicking on any search result, you\n> can see whether the record is available online or if you need to visit us\n> to view the original. You can also request copies of records from their\n> catalogue description page.\n>\n> If the record is held by another archive, you will need to contact them\n> directly to find out how you can access it.\n>\n> You can also *watch this video*\n> <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Tz4f--o3zUU> for more advice on using\n> our online catalogue.\n>\n> You are welcome to *visit us*\n> <https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/about/visit-us/> to continue your\n> research here in person, where access to our records is free of charge.\n> Before you visit, please check our website for information regarding our *opening\n> hours* <https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/about/visit-us/opening-times/>\n> and *ID requirements*\n> <https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/about/visit-us/researching-here/do-i-need-a-readers-ticket/>. If\n> you are unable to visit, or would like someone to pursue this research on\n> your behalf, please see the available *paid search options*\n> <https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/help-with-your-research/paying-for-research/>\n> on our website.\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n> Robert Fleming﻿\n>\n> Remote Enquiries Duty Officer\n> *www.nationalarchives.gov.uk* <http://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/>\n>\n> *Enquiry:*\n>\n>\n> -----Original Message-----\n> From: Requester <[redacted]>\n> Sent: 31 January 2026 05:59\n> To: Contact Centre <contactcentre@nationalarchives.gov.uk>\n> Subject: Concern regarding historical record following disputed land\n> dispossession\n>\n>         You don't often get email from [redacted]. Learn\n> why this is important <https://aka.ms/LearnAboutSenderIdentification>\n>\n>\n> EXTERNAL: This email originates from outside of The National Archives.\n>\n> Sirs/Ma'am,\n>\n> I'm writing to raise a concern regarding the integrity of the historical\n> record following disputed dispossession of land i.e. BP vs Buckler 1987\n>\n> After forced removal and demolition of our home, archaeological excavation\n> and removal of items took place on land whose ownership had never been\n> conclusively adjudicated at the time of registration. BP asserted it, but\n> it was never determined in a ruling beforehand as the July 1974 case\n> between BP and my grandmother was adjourned and the October 31st 1974\n> appeal and audience with a judge she made just 24 hours before, was\n> squashed by letters sent by BP that same morning.\n>\n> I am not making allegations here, simply stating facts about this landmark\n> legal case. And asking whether this type of circumstance is relevant to\n> archival or historical record considerations.\n>\n> Yours faithfully,\n>\n>\n> Requester\n>\n>\n> Please don't print this e-mail unless you really need to.\n> -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n> National Archives Disclaimer This email and any files transmitted with it\n> are intended solely for the use of the individual(s) to whom they are\n> addressed. If you are not the intended recipient and have received this\n> email in error, please notify the sender and delete the email. Opinions,\n> conclusions and other information in this message and attachments that do\n> not relate to the official business of The National Archives are neither\n> given nor endorsed by it.\n> ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n>\n>\n"},{"id":"wb-22fb37b2","subject":"Consortium evidence BP v Buckler","name":"2026-02-06_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.005.txt","date":"2026-02-06","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-06_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.005.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-evidence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-06_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.005.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: RESO Records Enquiries <RESORecordsEnquiries@nationalarchives.gov.uk>\nDate: Fri, 6 Feb 2026 13:07:50 +0000\nSubject: Response to National Archives case number CAS-313954-Y6C0H3 CRM:022601115\nMessage-ID: <00E27B941F5E4EBC8320336A5644B4F41DC97699761E@RESORECORDSENQUIRIES.NATIONALARCHIVES.GOV.UK>\n\nDear Requester,\n\nThank you for clarifying the nature of your enquiry.\n\nIn answer to your main enquiry, in general we would not add notes to, or review, records in light of unresolved disputes or other matters. The records are left to speak for themselves. In fact, those records are often incomplete and patchwork in character, meaning that they may not enable the enquirer to know what the final determination or outcome of any dispute was.\n\nSince you refer to a reported Court of Appeal (Civil Division) case, I have conducted a quick search of our, admittedly limited holdings. The main record series in which you might expect to find archival material here are J 157<https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/r/C9764>, J 83<https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/r/C9691> and J 84<https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/r/C9692>. Unfortunately, none contain records relevant to the case.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nCharlotte Smith﻿\n\nPrincipal Records Specialist, Modern Legal Records\nwww.nationalarchives.gov.uk<http://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/>\n\nEnquiry:\n\n\n\nTo clarify my original enquiry, I am specifically concerned with the historical record surrounding the case of BP Properties Ltd v Buckler [1987] EWCA Civ 2, which involved a disputed land dispossession at Great House Farm in Llandough, South Glamorgan. This stemmed from earlier proceedings in 1974, including an adjourned case in July and an appeal attempt on October 31st that was impacted by correspondence from BP the same day. Following the 1987 ruling, there was forced removal, demolition of our family home, and archaeological activity on the site, despite ongoing questions about the adjudication of ownership prior to land registration.\n\nMy question is not solely about locating ownership or court documents (though any guidance on whether The National Archives holds physical files from these proceedings would be welcome), but rather whether circumstances like these—where ownership was disputed and not fully resolved before registration or excavation—raise any considerations for the integrity or completeness of the archival record. For instance, do such disputes prompt reviews or annotations in historical land records, or are there protocols for archiving related materials from corporate entities like BP or local authorities involved?\n\nI understand your resources are focused on national-level holdings, but any further direction would greatly assist my research.\n\nPlease don't print this e-mail unless you really need to. ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- National Archives Disclaimer This email and any files transmitted with it are intended solely for the use of the individual(s) to whom they are addressed. If you are not the intended recipient and have received this email in error, please notify the sender and delete the email. Opinions, conclusions and other information in this message and attachments that do not relate to the official business of The National Archives are neither given nor endorsed by it. ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n"},{"id":"wb-796c0e67","subject":"Consortium land registry BP v Buckler","name":"2026-02-06_consortium-land_registry_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2026-02-06","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Land Registry correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-06_consortium-land_registry_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-land-registry-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-06_consortium-land_registry_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Customer Support <customersupport@landregistry.gov.uk>\nDate: Fri, 6 Feb 2026 10:06:12 +0000\nSubject: Further to Your Enquiry: Ref: 260202-5732372 Title No: WA231076\nMessage-ID: <ECF2277B900B487AB5A347DCC7010C791DC975035024@CUSTOMERSUPPORT.MAIL.LANDREGISTRY.GOV.UK>\n\nEnquiry Reference: 260202-5732372 Title Number: WA231076\n\nDear Customer\n\nThank you for your emails dated 5 and 6 February 2026 regarding the property-Great House Farm (historically Tŷ Mawr/Cedfin), Llandough\n\nThe information provided has been noted and referred to a senior officer.\n\nDue to the complexity of the issues raised please allow enough time for the senior officer to review and investigate your comments and reply to you accordingly.\n\nYours faithfully\n\nS.-G. Haste\nHM Land Registry\nreportafraud@landregistry.gov.uk<mailto:reportafraud@landregistry.gov.uk>\n\n\n\n\n\nWe only provide factual information and impartial advice about our procedures. Read more about the advice we give.<https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/hm-land-registry-service-standards/our-service-standards>\n\n\n\n\n\n\nHM Land Registry’s purpose is to protect your land ownership and provide services and data that underpin an efficient and informed property market. Our vision is: “A world-leading property market as part of a thriving economy and a sustainable future”.\n\nWe check all mail and attachments for known viruses. However, you are advised that you open any attachments at your own risk. If you have received this email and it was not intended for you, please let us know, then delete it.\n\nWe welcome correspondence in English and Welsh.\n\nTo see how HM Land Registry treats your personal information, read our Personal information charter: www.gov.uk/government/organisations/land-registry/about/personal-information-charter.\n\n\n"},{"id":"wb-28148c13","subject":"Consortium formal notice BP v Buckler","name":"2026-02-07_consortium-formal_notice_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2026-02-07","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-07_consortium-formal_notice_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-formal-notice-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-07_consortium-formal_notice_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Mind <info@mind.org.uk>\nDate: Sat, 7 Feb 2026 17:34:50 +0000 (GMT)\nSubject: FORMAL NOTICE: Restorative Justice & Historical Record Correction - Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough [260207-000028]\nMessage-ID: 19c392bb3f86d870\n\nYour question has been received. You should expect a response from us  \nwithin 24 hours.\n\nTo update your question with additional information, click\nhttps://mind.custhelp.com/app/account/questions/detail/i_id/1093426/track/AvNZ3wqbDv8S~RHTGoIe~yL7nOUqji75Mv~a~zj~PP8T/\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------\n\nSubject\n---------------------------------------------------------------\nFORMAL NOTICE: Restorative Justice & Historical Record Correction - Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough\n\nQuestion Reference # 260207-000028\n       Date Created: 07/02/2026 05.34 PM\n  Date Last Updated: 07/02/2026 05.34 PM\n             Status: Unresolved\n\n\n[---002:000576:47685---]\n"},{"id":"wb-05337825","subject":"Consortium evidence BP v Buckler","name":"2026-02-08_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2026-02-08","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-08_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-evidence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-08_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Sun, 8 Feb 2026 11:35:23 -0300\nSubject: Great House Farm - Reperations  V0.5\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V5+3=_OWR6n8HFP1L-CnVStGTWL-pV3yzyhx9j1hgO0FQ@mail.gmail.com>\n\nRene,\n\nThanks,\n\nHywel\n"},{"id":"wb-5fe50090","subject":"Consortium evidence BP v Buckler","name":"2026-02-08_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","date":"2026-02-08","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-08_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-evidence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-08_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Sun, 8 Feb 2026 06:57:06 -0300\nSubject: Re: Response to National Archives case number CAS-313954-Y6C0H3 CRM:022601127\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V6qBoCyHrFAVnUNKmB1u=JBOg=EXuej5EOpHGGw9WEkBA@mail.gmail.com>\n\nMs Smith,\nThank you for your detailed response regarding selection policies and\nretention schedules. I appreciate the procedural clarity you have provided.\nHowever, I must respectfully indicate that your reply does not address the\nsubstantive concerns I have raised, and I am now compelled to escalate this\nmatter formally.\n1. From \"Archival Silence\" to Potential Evidence Tampering\nYou correctly note that archival silences are well-documented in academic\nliterature. However, the circumstances surrounding Great House Farm suggest\nsomething more concerning than routine selection decisions. I am now in\npossession of first-hand testimony (David Buckler, 2024) alleging that:\n\n   - Frederick Buckler (my grandfather) and his eldest son engaged a\n   solicitor privately in the 1960s\n   - Title documents were removed from the family home and allegedly sold\n   - The family was deliberately misled with the phrase \"it's all been\n   dealt with legally\"\n\nThis testimony, combined with the disappearance of the Bute Estate → Daniel\nThomas deed index card from Cardiff Library (reported missing in 1984),\nsuggests potential spoliation of evidence rather than routine archival\nselection. The question is no longer \"why weren't records preserved?\" but\n\"were records deliberately removed to prejudice a 320-year possessory\nclaim?\"\n2. The Computerised Records Question\nYou direct me to search your catalogue. I have done so. However, I now ask\nspecifically:\n\n   - Does The National Archives hold any digitised or microfilmed copies of\n   the Bute Estate records that might contain references to the Daniel\n   Thomas quarrying agreement (circa 1895-1905) or the 1938 auction at the\n   Bear Hotel?\n   - Are there any internal indices or finding aids (particularly from the\n   1970s-1980s) that might reference Great House Farm, Llandough, or the\n   BP/Western Ground Rents acquisition records that are not reflected in the\n   current online catalogue?\n\nThe 1994 archaeological excavation report (Cotswold Archaeological Trust)\nconfirms the site's national significance. The absence of corresponding\nland transaction records from the same period is itself an anomaly\nrequiring explanation.\n3. The BP Corporate Records Question\nYou note that parties are generally responsible for maintaining their own\nrecords. However, BP Properties Ltd and BP Pension Trust Ltd were corporate\nentities with statutory filing obligations. I ask:\n\n   - Were any BP corporate records relating to land acquisition in South\n   Glamorgan (1969-1988) selected for preservation under the Companies Act\n   or as part of the public record?\n   - Has The National Archives ever received or sought records from BP\n   regarding the Great House Farm acquisition, demolition, or the 1987\n   litigation?\n\nThe Court of Appeal judgment [1987] EWCA Civ 2 notes that BP issued letters\non 31 October 1974, not received until November 4th 1974 —letters that were\nallegedly \"accepted by silence.\" The original correspondence would\nconstitute primary evidence of potentially fraudulent misrepresentation. If\nthese were not preserved, I ask under what authority they were destroyed\nand whether this occurred before or after the litigation concluded.\n4. Request for Formal Investigation\nI am not seeking annotation of records. I am seeking:\n\n   1. A formal review of whether records relating to Great House Farm,\n   Llandough (ST 168 733) were selected for preservation, destroyed, or\n   remain non accessioned;\n   2. A search of microfilm, digitised, or legacy finding aid holdings for\n   any reference to the 1938 auction, Western Ground Rents acquisition, or the\n   Daniel Thomas quarrying agreement;\n   3. Confirmation of whether The National Archives has ever received\n   complaints regarding missing Bute Estate records or BP land acquisition\n   documents from other researchers;\n   4. Guidance on the appropriate authority to investigate allegations of\n   document removal from Cardiff Library (1984) and potential fraud in the\n   1982 land registration.\n\n5. Public Interest and Parliamentary Scrutiny\nThis matter involves:\n\n   - The forced removal of a family from land occupied since at least 1667\n   - Demolition of a medieval structure described as a \"dilapidated utility\n   building\" to avoid heritage protection\n   - Archaeological discoveries (1888, 1994) that were known but officially\n   \"forgotten\"\n   - A Court of Appeal decision ([1987] EWCA Civ 2) that may have relied on\n   incomplete or fraudulent evidence\n\nI am copying my MP and the Parliamentary Ombudsman in this correspondence.\nI will also be submitting a Subject Access Request to HM Land Registry\nregarding the 1982 registration and a complaint to the Information\nCommissioner's Office regarding potential destruction of records relevant\nto ongoing property disputes.\nI request a substantive response addressing the specific questions above\nwithin 20 working days. Should The National Archives be unable to assist\nfurther, I request a formal referral to the appropriate body with\njurisdiction over historical record fraud and spoliation.\nYours sincerely,\nSion Buckler\n\n\nOn Sat, 7 Feb 2026 at 07:26, RESO Records Enquiries <\nRESORecordsEnquiries@nationalarchives.gov.uk> wrote:\n\n> Dear Requester,\n>\n> The question about archival silences is one upon which there is a\n> voluminous literature. Practically, however, the best way of making sense\n> of what is and is not in the archives is to refer to the reports which\n> shaped selection policies in the modern era - see especially the Report on\n> Departmental Records (Cmnd 9136) and the Report on Legal Records (Cmnd\n> 3084) - and the resulting retention and disposition schedules - see for\n> example the one for the Court of Appeal (Civil Division) published here\n> <https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/62b47cc9d3bf7f0affd65610/court-appeal-civil-division-rrds.pdf>\n> .\n>\n> What you will see from these is that the presumption for modern records is\n> against permanent preservation. In general, apart from small samples to\n> demonstrate the ordinary functioning of the courts/government departments,\n> records are only preserved if they bear witness to moments of particular\n> legal or policy significance or change. In the case of legal records, the\n> presumption has been that parties would be responsible for maintaining\n> their own records.\n>\n> In terms of how to continue your search, my instinct is that you should\n> consider exploring the holdings of the relevant local archive. You can also\n> try some keyword searches in our catalogue - see, for example, these\n> <https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/results/r?_d=PF%7CWORK%7CBD%7CHLG&_dss=range&_sd=1978&_ed=1988&_ps=60&_hb=tna&_srt=1&_q=archaeological+NOT+bill+NOT+%22PF+309%22>\n> search results which highlight these\n> <https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/results/r?_q=archaeological+NOT+bill+NOT+%22PF+309%22&_d=PF%7CWORK%7CBD%7CHLG&_dss=range&_sd=1978&_ed=1988&_ps=60&_hb=tna&_srt=1&_l=3>\n> series as potentially a relevant source of desired information. You might\n> also wish to search the registered files of the land registry in records\n> series LAR 1 <https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/r/C10222>.\n> You should be advised, however, that departmental records tend to focus\n> more on policy than upon the treatment of individual cases.\n>\n> I hope that this is helpful.\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n> Charlotte Smith﻿\n>\n> Principal Records Specialist, Modern Legal Records\n> *www.nationalarchives.gov.uk* <http://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/>\n>\n> *Enquiry:*\n>\n>\n>\n> I note and appreciate your confirmation that the principal judicial series\n> (J 157, J 83, J 84) contain no relevant material. Given the scale of the\n> consequences flowing from the dispute—forced removal, demolition of an\n> occupied dwelling, and loss of heritage fabric—the absence of supporting\n> court or administrative files is itself significant for researchers.\n>\n> Accordingly, I would be grateful for guidance on the following procedural\n> points:\n>\n>    - how researchers are expected to interpret gaps or silence in the\n>    archival record where substantial actions plainly occurred;\n>    - whether related non-judicial records (for example, departmental,\n>    planning, regulatory, or corporate records connected to land registration,\n>    planning consent, or archaeological works) are more likely to sit outside\n>    the obvious court series; and\n>    - whether there is an appropriate mechanism for flagging concerns\n>    about record completeness or contextual risk, without seeking alteration of\n>    the records themselves.\n>\n> For the avoidance of doubt, I am not alleging impropriety on the part of\n> The National Archives or its staff. My concern is to ensure that the\n> historical record is approached with appropriate caution where absence may\n> unintentionally convey certainty or closure that did not exist at the time.\n> Please don't print this e-mail unless you really need to.\n> -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n> National Archives Disclaimer This email and any files transmitted with it\n> are intended solely for the use of the individual(s) to whom they are\n> addressed. If you are not the intended recipient and have received this\n> email in error, please notify the sender and delete the email. Opinions,\n> conclusions and other information in this message and attachments that do\n> not relate to the official business of The National Archives are neither\n> given nor endorsed by it.\n> ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n>\n>\n"},{"id":"wb-4218c4e8","subject":"Consortium evidence BP v Buckler","name":"2026-02-09_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2026-02-09","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-09_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-evidence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-09_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Mon, 9 Feb 2026 10:27:08 -0300\nSubject: Requester . Nec Vi, Nec Clam, Nec Precario - Heir & Successor to Williams/ Buckler Dynasty, Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V6OByMKHZhie+pE28Y-HK-tWVmMkXgkHuHaDZjA+Chv5A@mail.gmail.com>\n\nSirs / Ma’am,\n\nI write from the Welsh settlement in Patagonia, Argentina, with respect,\nand for the attention of His Majesty King Charles III and the appropriate\nofficers of the Royal Household.\n\nI write as a Welsh national, a father, and a former public servant. I\nconcluded twenty-two years in oath in 2024, including a career in the\nmilitary, also serving in the Middle East and leaving colour service with\nhonours. I recently completed employment under the Welsh Government (Vale\nof Glamorgan Council). I now write in a private capacity, not under oath,\nand not in any adversarial posture, but in good faith and with the\nintention that the historic record be accurately preserved.\n\nMy purpose in writing is to place on record serious concerns regarding the\nhistorical treatment, documentation, and subsequent erasure of my family’s\nancestral estate, Tŷ Mawr (Great House Farm), Llandough, in the Vale of\nGlamorgan.\n\nTŷ Mawr was an historic monastic grange and site of Roman antiquity. From\n1667, it was held in continuous occupation and stewardship by my fathers\nsides maternal family, the Williams family, later intersecting with the\nBuckler line through marriage. For over three centuries, my forebears\noccupied, worked, and maintained this land. I was born there.\n\nPrior to enlisting in Signals Intelligence roles and encountering senior\nnational and royal figures in the course of official duties, I was educated\nin Wales, including at Llandovery College, which I attended under the\nGovernment-run Assisted Places Scheme, having been selected on academic\nmerit. On my mothers side, my late grandfather, the Welsh BAFTA-winning\nactor Ray Smith, carried the same Welsh cultural identity into public life.\nMy wider family has likewise contributed to Welsh cultural life, including\nmy aunt Shan Cothi, the opera singer, and her husband/ my late uncle;\nJustin Smith, founder of the Welsh band Tigertailz and directed a major\n'visit wales' campaign for the tourist board. These details are to\ndemonstrate continuity of Welsh cultural life rooted in this family and\nplace. I'm also a fluent speak Welsh, I named my daugher Delyth, and I do\nmuch for the continuity of Welsh cultural life.\n\nIn 1974, my grandmother on my fathers side; Mary Williams, initiated\nproceedings concerning the title to Tŷ Mawr. Those proceedings were\nadjourned and later that same year effectively extinguished without\nsubstantive resolution. Critically, my grandmother never adopted her\nhusband’s surname; she remained Mary Williams throughout her life. That\nname was not incidental. It was the living link to the 1667 acquisition of\nthe farm and to our family’s long occupation.\n\nDuring later proceedings, the name “Mrs Buckler” was ascribed to her in\ncourt records. This misidentification had material consequences. It severed\nthe documentary continuity of the Williams line and undermined both our de\nfacto occupation and any consideration of adverse possession. This\nmisnaming remains, to my knowledge, uncorrected in the public record.\n\nIn 1988, my family was forcibly removed from Tŷ Mawr, having been\ncharacterised as squatters despite centuries of occupation. Our home — an\n800-year-old farmhouse — was demolished shortly thereafter. I was four\nyears old. I witnessed the destruction alongside my siblings and parents.\nWe were not permitted to retrieve belongings or family records. Centuries\nof family material history were lost.\n\nOf particular concern is that the site had long been associated with Roman\nremains. In 1870, a Roman burial was reportedly uncovered on the land, a\nfact later omitted or minimised in official descriptions of the site.\nFollowing the demolition of the farmhouse, and prior to residential\ndevelopment, it emerged that the land contained what is now understood to\nbe the largest Roman burial site excavated in Wales, with over 1,000\nburials. Had this been properly recorded and acknowledged earlier,\nstatutory archaeological protections should have applied.\n\nThe effect of these events has been the near-total erasure of the historic\nand indigenous Welsh continuity of Tŷ Mawr from public narrative and\nrecord. The land now forms part of a modern housing development, without\nacknowledgment of its significance or of the family that occupied it for\nover three centuries.\n\nI wish to be clear: my primary concern is not confrontation, but correction\n— correction of the historical record, acknowledgment of documented\nirregularities, and preservation of truth for future generations. My family\nhas carried the consequences of these events for decades, including\nsignificant trauma, and many remain reluctant to come forward.\n\nI hold a forensic analysis estimating the financial losses associated with\nTŷ Mawr at approximately £101.2 million, but the greater matter is not\nmonetary. It is the restoration of narrative accuracy and the\nacknowledgment of indigenous Welsh continuity at this site and my families\nstanding in our local community thereafter. The generational trauma is so\ndeep, even last week my mother refused to discuss this, even privately.\n\nI do not write to demand action from His Majesty personally, nor do I\npresume upon the Crown. I write so that it cannot later be said that these\nmatters were unknown or unrecorded. I respectfully request that this\ncorrespondence be retained as a notice of concern regarding historic record\nintegrity, misidentification of parties, and the suppression or omission of\nmaterial archaeological and cultural facts.\n\nMy intention is also to rebuild an exact replica of Great House Farm in\nPatagonia, where the Welsh community here has welcomed the preservation of\nthis history. This project is intended as an act of cultural conservation,\nnot protest.\n\nI remain respectful of the Crown’s constitutional role and of the King\npersonally. My request is simply that the truth of Tŷ Mawr — its history,\nits people, and its erasure — is not allowed to disappear entirely from the\nhistorical record.\n\nI certify that I write in good faith, with full mental capacity, and\nwithout hostility. I seek only truth, acknowledgment, and preservation of\nhistory.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\nSion Hywel Buckler\n[redacted], Wales\n[redacted]\n"},{"id":"wb-6672fd5f","subject":"Consortium formal notice BP v Buckler","name":"2026-02-10_consortium-formal_notice_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2026-02-10","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-10_consortium-formal_notice_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-formal-notice-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-10_consortium-formal_notice_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Tue, 10 Feb 2026 19:15:25 -0300\nSubject: Formal Notice for Record: Church View Close, Llandough / BP vs Buckler 1987\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V4Cf9dTUEsGhwQQYD2W+YrO+3hc9eTZ27jf4+OHrtZQBw@mail.gmail.com>\n\n*FORMAL NOTICE OF RECORD CHALLENGE, ASSERTION OF RIGHT, AND DEMAND FOR\nENGAGEMENT*\n\nDear Sir/Madam,\n\nThis Notice is issued for placement on official record and for reliance. It\nis not informal correspondence. I urge you not to resort to silence,\ndeflection, or delay, as doing so following receipt of this notice\nconstitutes knowing reliance upon, and maintenance of, records alleged to\nbe materially defective through fraud, concealment, denial of due process,\nand systemic erasure. Please report any suspicious activity and\ndiscrepancies identified.\n\nThis Notice is issued in pursuit of narrative justice at this stage; formal\njudicial remedies are expressly reserved. It serves to document our\nfamily's side of the story before it is completely erased and history\nrewritten, ensuring awareness among custodians of land, heritage, heraldry,\narchaeology, and public records.\n\nMy elder brother, Rhys Buckler, and I are the sons of the late William\nBuckler (d. 1991), defendant in the landmark legal case *BP Properties Ltd\nv Buckler* (1987), and the grandchildren of the late Mary Williams, whose\ndispossession, marginalisation, and exclusion from judicial determination\nremain unresolved matters of record. Mary Williams was repeatedly and\nmaterially falsely addressed/mislabeled in legal and administrative records\nas “Mrs Buckler,” a misidentification that was never judicially tested and\nwhich directly affected the handling, scope, and outcome of proceedings.\nThe Williams/Buckler family held claim to the land title, having acquired\nand occupied Great House Farm continuously since 1667, based on family\nrecords, historical evidence, and centuries of resistance to adverse claims.\n\nI place on record that I am the first member of my family able to speak\npublicly and formally on these matters. Other close family members remain\ntoo traumatised and intimidated by the historical conduct of these\nproceedings—including violent eviction, demolition, and loss of\nlivelihood—to engage, despite the continuing consequences of dispossession.\nThat silence must not be misread as consent, acquiescence, or abandonment\nof right. There is a material distinction between free silence and silence\nproduced by fear, trauma, and coercive experience.\n\nThe story, as we have pieced it together, reveals a multi-generational\npattern of lawfare and siege: from Norman/English conquests in 1100\ngranting lordships over ancient Welsh Christian landscapes, through\nmonastic (1215–1539) and Crown seizures (1539 onward), to industrial\nencroachments (e.g., 1876 Limeworks severance without consent) and modern\ncorporate possession actions. This culminated in the 1988 demolition of an\n800-year-old farmhouse amid police standoffs, leaving the family homeless\nand the site scarred, now buried under Church View Close. Archaeological\nvindication came too late—1994 excavations revealed over 1,000 Roman and\nmedieval burials, confirming family warnings (e.g., the 1870 Roman soldier\ndiscovery under the living room floor, suppressed by authorities)—yet\nheritage records celebrate the site while omitting our dispossession.\n\nThe following assertions of right are now formally placed on record. They\nhave never been lawfully adjudicated or extinguished:\n\n   1. Rhys Buckler may be the rightful Manorial Lord of the Manor of\n   Llandough, by right of primogeniture and continuous hereditary connection\n   arising from our family’s occupation of Great House Farm from 1667 until\n   our narrative, legal, and physical dispossession.\n   2. I assert a claim to act as Squire of the Manor of Llandough, as\n   executive representative and guardian of the Buckler–Williams estate and\n   matriarchal lineage, with a mandate to pursue correction of record,\n   restitution, and restorative remedies.\n\nThese are assertions of right, not settled declarations, placed on record\npending proper adjudication—which has been historically obstructed through\nprocedural narrowing, missing documents, and avoidance of full hearings.\n\nIn 1974, Mary Williams was twice prevented from presenting her case and\nobtaining a judicial determination of superior ownership, despite claims\ngrounded in long occupation, intent, and possession. The court confined\ninquiry to *animus possidendi* (intention to possess) and excluded *animus\ndomini* (intention to own), reclassifying her from putative owner to mere\noccupier and foreclosing title adjudication. BP sought possession only, not\nownership rulings—a choice unexplained and significant. Proceedings were\nadjourned or quashed without resolution.\n\nThis occurred despite evidence of ownership intent, including:\n\n   - Centuries-long occupation and resistance (e.g., failed 1938 auction\n   due to local support; post-1945 rent disputes).\n   - Roman-era remains and historical significance (e.g., Guglielmo\n   Marconi's 1897 residence during radio experiments, with family aid erased\n   from records).\n   - Unformalized deals like the 1895–1905 Daniel Thomas quarrying\n   permission for promised title, undermined by vanished deeds.\n\nJudicial ownership examination would have engaged archaeological/heritage\nprotections, incompatible with redevelopment—consequences avoided, raising\ncredibility concerns.\n\nSerious unresolved anomalies persist, indicating something is still not\nright with the record:\n\n   - Licence correspondence dated 31 October 1974 surfacing only on 4\n   November, potentially interrupting proceedings to prevent hearings.\n   - Claims of 1974 benevolent withdrawal under press scrutiny unsupported\n   by verifiable coverage.\n   - Land registration before Mary Williams’s 1983 death without title\n   determination; disputes possibly lodged/suppressed by Land Registry.\n   - Inconsistent treatment of BP entities across proceedings to family\n   detriment.\n   - Procedural barriers preventing effective defense (e.g., interrupted\n   essential services post-licence; 1960–1987 court rulings dismissing\n   ancestral title as \"notion\" without evidence).\n   - Knowing bypass of archaeological constraints (e.g., 1978 trenches\n   ignored; no contemporaneous 1970s/1990s press on discoveries amid\n   development).\n   - Missing key documents (e.g., 1667 conveyance, 1811–1835 Lambert\n   Williams agreements proving status, 1982–84 library deeds vanishing).\n   - Risks of further erasure: Heritage databases celebrate \"Llandough\n   Roman villa cemetery\" while omitting our saga; modern residents unaware of\n   the \"battleground\" site.\n\nThese anomalies suggest systemic theft, from colonial English subjugation\n(e.g., 1543–1770 orbit shifts) to corporate siege, during Wales'\ncultural/economic transitions. Authority derived through such means is\nillegitimate under natural justice, customary Welsh principles, and\nmoral-legal frameworks. The record's distortion secures advantage, but\npassage of time does not validate it.\n\nPreliminary assessment indicates cumulative benefits from redevelopment\n(absent title extinguishment) exceed £100 million, forming a basis for\nreparations—covering lost land, artifacts, livelihood, and trauma.\n\nI require relevant public servants, custodians, and office-holders\n(including Land Registry, heraldry bodies, museums, archaeologists, MPs,\nhistorians, and councillors) to cease delay, engage substantively,\nreview/correct/annotate records where defective, and prevent further\nrewriting of history. Justice delayed is denied, especially with verifiable\nevidence available.\n\nThis Notice:\n\n   - Challenges the record's accuracy, completeness, and legitimacy.\n   - Place assertions of title/standing on record pending adjudication.\n   - Reserves all rights without prejudice.\n   - Puts officials/beneficiaries on notice that silence/reliance may\n   aggravate responsibility.\n\nFor the avoidance of doubt, His Majesty the King was notified yesterday.\n\nThis serves as an invitation to engage constructively before reliance\nbecomes untenable.\n\nRegards, Requester On behalf of the Buckler–Williams family [redacted], Wales. https://bpvsbuckler.datro.xyz\n\n*Postscript — Disability and Succession (Material Notice):* The\nheir-apparent to the asserted Manorial Lordship is Rhys Buckler’s son, a\nyoung man with Down syndrome. Any obstruction, marginalisation, or\ndifferential treatment affecting his inheritance would engage the Equality\nAct 2010 and UK international obligations on disability rights. Disability\ncannot bar succession or recognition. This is recorded to prevent claims of\nignorance.\n"},{"id":"wb-12e72168","subject":"Consortium formal notice BP v Buckler","name