,"dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Land Registry correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-12_consortium-land_registry_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-land-registry-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-12_consortium-land_registry_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: \"Report@sra.org.uk\" <Report@sra.org.uk>\nDate: Thu, 12 Feb 2026 11:11:57 +0000\nSubject: RE: FORMAL ALLEGATION OF FRAUD: Ref: 260202-5732372\nMessage-ID: <LO2P265MB3755E368B8B5BFA9B954FBFCD560A@LO2P265MB3755.GBRP265.PROD.OUTLOOK.COM>\n\nSensitivity: Confidential\n\nDear Requester,\n\nThank you for your email below, which we have been copied into.\n\nThere is information on our website about making a report to us<https://www.sra.org.uk/consumers/problems/report-solicitor/>. If you want to make a report about someone we regulate, please tell us the name and work address of the person and/or firm you have concerns about, along with details of your concerns. When sending this information to us, it would be helpful if you could complete the report form available in the ‘How to make a report to us’ section of our website<https://www.sra.org.uk/consumers/problems/report-solicitor/>.\n\nYou can send your completed report form by post to the address detailed below or by email to our report@sra.org.uk<mailto:report@sra.org.uk> email address. Please note that we currently aim to be back in touch within 45 working days of receipt of a report, or sooner if we consider the matter is urgent.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nAssessment & Early Resolution Team\n\nSolicitors Regulation Authority/Awdurdod Rheoleiddio Cyfreithwyr\n\n•  The Cube, 199 Wharfside Street, Birmingham B1 1RN\ný www.sra.org.uk<http://www.sra.org.uk/>\n\nFor alternative formats, email contactcentre@sra.org.uk <mailto:contactcentre@sra.org.uk> or telephone 0370 606 2555\n\n\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]>\nSent: 05 February 2026 16:52\nTo: Customer Support <customersupport@landregistry.gov.uk>; reportafraud@landregistry.gov.uk\nCc: public.enquiries@sfo.gov.uk; contact@actionfraud.police.uk; wales@lawsociety.org.uk; Report@sra.org.uk; post@museumwales.ac.uk\nSubject: Re: FORMAL ALLEGATION OF FRAUD: Ref: 260202-5732372\n\n**This message originated from outside the SRA- Please treat attachments or links with caution**\n________________________________\nThat is a very fair point, Bob. You’re right to be cautious—sometimes a \"hostile\" tone can cause a civil servant to shut down or hide behind red tape rather than helping you.\n\nThe reason it feels like they are being cooperative is that they have shifted from saying \"no\" to asking for evidence. This is a good sign. However, the reason we are \"rebutting\" is simply to correct their premise: they think this is a recent fraud, and you are correcting them that the \"fraud\" was the original act of registration in 1982.\n\nLet’s soften the tone to be \"Helpful but Firm.\" We will treat them as an investigator who needs your guidance to find the truth, rather than an enemy.\n\nAdjusted \"Cooperative\" Draft\n\nSubject: Information regarding Registration Irregularities – Great House Farm (Ty Mawr) – Ref: 260202-5732372\n\nDear S.-G. Haste,\n\nThank you for your guidance on how to proceed with this report. I appreciate the opportunity to clarify the nature of the irregularities I have identified.\n\nAs requested, I have attached a detailed PDF timeline which provides the full address and the historical context of the land formerly known as Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough.\n\nTo assist your investigation into how the 1982 registration may have been affected by material non-disclosure, I would like to highlight three specific areas where the \"actual facts\" on the ground differed from the application made to the Registry:\n\n  1.  Overriding Interests (The Williams Lineage): At the time of first registration in 1982, my family had been in continuous possession since 1667. I believe the registration was irregular because it misidentified the occupant, Mary Williams, as \"Mrs. Buckler\"—a name she did not use—effectively obscuring a 320-year \"Living Title\" from the Registry’s records.\n  2.  Suppression of Material Facts (Archaeology): The site had \"actual notice\" of national archaeological significance due to a Roman discovery in 1888. I am concerned that this was suppressed during the registration and planning phases to facilitate the land's transition to housing.\n  3.  Unresolved Ownership Claims: In 1982, there was an active, adjourned ownership dispute (from 1974) that I believe should have been disclosed to the Land Registry before a clean title was granted to BP Properties Ltd.\n\nI am copying in the relevant heritage and oversight bodies (Cadw, GGAT, and the Ombudsman) not as an act of hostility, but to ensure that all parties holding pieces of this historical puzzle are aware that an investigation into the integrity of the 1982 registration is now underway.\n\nI have also copied in my MP, the Senedd, and relevant heritage bodies to ensure that all parties with an interest in Welsh cultural heritage and public record integrity are kept informed as we look into these 1982 registration details.\n\nI look forward to your thoughts on how we can review the original 1982 application files to see if these interests were disclosed.\n\n\n\nYours sincerely,\n\n\n\nSion Buckler\n\nDescendant of the Williams family,\n\nof Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough\n\n\n\nOn Mon, 2 Feb 2026 at 09:01, Customer Support <customersupport@landregistry.gov.uk<mailto:customersupport@landregistry.gov.uk>> wrote:\n\nEnquiry Reference: 260202-5732372\n\nDear Customer\nThank you for your email dated 31 January 2026.\n\nUnfortunately, it is not clear from your email what the allegations of fraud/potential irregularities in 1982 land registration are?\n\nIn order for us to investigate further, please can you provide us with either the full address of the property or the title number concerned.\n\nCan you explain /provide full details of what has happened?\n\nIf the matter has been referred to the Police (please provide police incident number) and advise us, if any legal advice has been taken regarding the matter\n\nIt is important to note that HM Land Registry can only consider and investigate circumstances in which an alleged fraud relates to actual or planned changes to the register. (registration)\n\n\n\n\n\n\nYours faithfully\n\nS.-G. Haste\nHM Land Registry\nreportafraud@landregistry.gov.uk<mailto:reportafraud@landregistry.gov.uk>\n\n\n\n\n\nWe only provide factual information and impartial advice about our procedures. Read more about the advice we give.<https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/hm-land-registry-service-standards/our-service-standards>\n\n\n\n\nHM Land Registry’s purpose is to protect your land ownership and provide services and data that underpin an efficient and informed property market. Our vision is: “A world-leading property market as part of a thriving economy and a sustainable future”.\n\n\n\nWe check all mail and attachments for known viruses. However, you are advised that you open any attachments at your own risk. If you have received this email and it was not intended for you, please let us know, then delete it.\n\n\n\nWe welcome correspondence in English and Welsh.\n\n\n\nTo see how HM Land Registry treats your personal information, read our Personal information charter: www.gov.uk/government/organisations/land-registry/about/personal-information-charter<http://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/land-registry/about/personal-information-charter>.\n\n\n\nFor information on how we handle your personal data, see our privacy notice<http://www.sra.org.uk/dpa>.\nThis email is intended for the addressee only. This includes any attachments. Its unauthorised use, further processing, storage or copying is not allowed. If you are not the intended recipient, please let the sender know and then destroy all copies.\nPlease note the sender is not authorised to conclude any contract on behalf of the Solicitors Regulation Authority by email.\nWe are the regulator of solicitors and law firms in England and Wales. The Solicitors Regulation Authority Limited is a company limited by guarantee registered in England and Wales. Our registered offices are: The Cube, 199 Wharfside Street, Birmingham, B1 1RN. Our company registration number is: 12608059.\n"},{"id":"wb-901f5864","subject":"Consortium legal request BP v Buckler","name":"2026-02-12_consortium-legal_request_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2026-02-12","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-12_consortium-legal_request_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-legal-request-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-12_consortium-legal_request_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Thu, 12 Feb 2026 22:33:13 -0300\nSubject: Cais am Gyngor Cyfreithiol – Ty Mawr, Llandough\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V6pd=5nG2Je1x8v2F4oH_rb7r4c6Yt-avhh3hT00+w++g@mail.gmail.com>\n\nAnnwyl Syr/Fadam,\n\nYsgrifennaf i ofyn am gyngor cyfreithiol mewn perthynas â mater cymhleth yn\nymwneud â Thŷ Mawr (Great House Farm), Llandough.\n\nYn gryno, mae’r mater yn codi’r cwestiynau canlynol:\n\n   -\n\n   Diffyg penderfyniad barnwrol ar berchnogaeth cyn achos meddiant yn y\n   1980au;\n   -\n\n   Cofrestru cyntaf yn 1982 a allai gynnwys “camgymeriad” o fewn ystyr\n   Atodlen 4 Deddf Cofrestru Tir 2002;\n   -\n\n   Amnewid neu anghysondeb hunaniaeth yn y disgrifiad cyfreithiol;\n   -\n\n   Cwestiynau ynghylch ystyriaeth a datgeliad treftadaeth archaeolegol.\n\nRwy’n ceisio:\n\n   1.\n\n   Barn gynnar ar hyfywedd unrhyw gais i adolygu neu gywiro’r gofrestr;\n   2.\n\n   Asesiad o’r sefyllfa gyfyngu (limitation);\n   3.\n\n   Cyngor ar gamau cyn-weithredu priodol.\n\nRwyf wedi atodi crynodeb gweithredol byr er hwylustod.\n\nByddwn yn ddiolchgar am gyfle i drefnu ymgynghoriad cychwynnol. Rwy’n barod\ni dalu am gyngor cychwynnol ffurfiol.\n\nYn gywir,\n\nSion Buckler\n[redacted]\n[redacted]\n"},{"id":"wb-61c35f9d","subject":"Consortium evidence BP v Buckler","name":"2026-02-13_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2026-02-13","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-13_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-evidence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-13_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Your Views <yourviews@nationalarchives.gov.uk>\nDate: Fri, 13 Feb 2026 18:38:47 +0000\nSubject: CAS-315609 CRM:009700964\nMessage-ID: <EC275BFDAF4B4759B6499C1B48E96DDD1DC9D17FAD6F@YOURVIEWS.NATIONALARCHIVES.GOV.UK>\n\nDear Requester,\n\nThank you for your reply and correction email.\n\nYour correspondence has been passed through to my team to co-ordinate a response. As such I will be discussing contents of your messages with colleagues and will respond further in due course.\n\nKind regards,\n\nMartin  Willis\n\n\n\nMartin Willis | Quality Manager\nT: 020 8392 5363 | W: nationalarchives.gov.uk<http://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/>\nTwitter: @UkNatArchives |Pronouns: He/Him\nThe National Archives, Kew, Richmond, Surrey TW9 4DU\n\n\n\nCorrection\nIn my previous correspondence, I referenced documents missing from Cardiff Library as \"reported missing in 1984.\" I wish to clarify that this date derives from my family's own chronology and recollection, not from an official report or confirmed archival record. I should not have stated this as an established fact, and I apologise for any confusion caused.\nNew Evidence\nSince my last email, I have obtained a Forensic Evaluation and Assessment of Restitutionary Claims (dated February 8, 2026) regarding the dispossession of the Williams family from Great House Farm, Llandough. This professional assessment provides additional context that may assist in understanding the archival gaps previously identified.\nThe forensic report specifically examines:\n\n  1.\nThe \"Mrs. Buckler\" nomenclature: The analysis characterises the consistent use of \"Mrs. Buckler\" in court records and correspondence as \"strategic misnaming\" rather than clerical error, given that Mary Williams never adopted her husband's surname. This raises questions about whether The National Archives holds any records indicating her formal objection to this identification or her consistent use of \"Mary Williams\" in legal proceedings.\n  2.\nThe 1974 license letters: The report notes that letters dated October/November 1974 were addressed to a \"non-existent legal persona,\" facilitating a judicial finding of \"silence as consent.\" I ask again whether The National Archives holds any corporate records from BP Properties Ltd or BP Pension Trust Ltd regarding this correspondence, or any indication that such records were considered for preservation.\n  3.\nThe 1974 adjournment: The forensic assessment questions why proceedings were adjourned part-heard on July 3, 1974, given your confirmation that principal judicial series contain no relevant material. I request clarification on whether any Cardiff County Court records from 1974-1975 were transferred to The National Archives under subsequent protocols, or whether they remain with the court.\n\nRevised Request\nGiven this additional professional analysis, I renew my request for guidance on:\n\n  *\nWhether The National Archives has received any complaints or inquiries regarding missing Bute Estate records or Great House Farm documents from other researchers;\n  *\nWhether corporate records from BP entities relating to this acquisition were ever selected for preservation; and\n  *\nWhether any mechanism exists for flagging concerns about record completeness where forensic analysis suggests potential irregularities in the historical record.\n\nI remain grateful for your time and professionalism, and I hope this clarification assists in addressing my inquiry.\nYours sincerely,\nSion Buckler\n\n\n\n\nOn Sun, 8 Feb 2026 at 06:57, Requester <[redacted]<mailto:[redacted]>> wrote:\n\nMs Smith,\nThank you for your detailed response regarding selection policies and retention schedules. I appreciate the procedural clarity you have provided. However, I must respectfully indicate that your reply does not address the substantive concerns I have raised, and I am now compelled to escalate this matter formally.\n1. From \"Archival Silence\" to Potential Evidence Tampering\nYou correctly note that archival silences are well-documented in academic literature. However, the circumstances surrounding Great House Farm suggest something more concerning than routine selection decisions. I am now in possession of first-hand testimony (David Buckler, 2024) alleging that:\n\n  *\nFrederick Buckler (my grandfather) and his eldest son engaged a solicitor privately in the 1960s\n  *\nTitle documents were removed from the family home and allegedly sold\n  *\nThe family was deliberately misled with the phrase \"it's all been dealt with legally\"\n\nThis testimony, combined with the disappearance of the Bute Estate → Daniel Thomas deed index card from Cardiff Library (reported missing in 1984), suggests potential spoliation of evidence rather than routine archival selection. The question is no longer \"why weren't records preserved?\" but \"were records deliberately removed to prejudice a 320-year possessory claim?\"\n2. The Computerised Records Question\nYou direct me to search your catalogue. I have done so. However, I now ask specifically:\n\n  *\nDoes The National Archives hold any digitised or microfilmed copies of the Bute Estate records that might contain references to the Daniel Thomas quarrying agreement (circa 1895-1905) or the 1938 auction at the Bear Hotel?\n  *\nAre there any internal indices or finding aids (particularly from the 1970s-1980s) that might reference Great House Farm, Llandough, or the BP/Western Ground Rents acquisition records that are not reflected in the current online catalogue?\n\nThe 1994 archaeological excavation report (Cotswold Archaeological Trust) confirms the site's national significance. The absence of corresponding land transaction records from the same period is itself an anomaly requiring explanation.\n3. The BP Corporate Records Question\nYou note that parties are generally responsible for maintaining their own records. However, BP Properties Ltd and BP Pension Trust Ltd were corporate entities with statutory filing obligations. I ask:\n\n  *\nWere any BP corporate records relating to land acquisition in South Glamorgan (1969-1988) selected for preservation under the Companies Act or as part of the public record?\n  *\nHas The National Archives ever received or sought records from BP regarding the Great House Farm acquisition, demolition, or the 1987 litigation?\n\nThe Court of Appeal judgment [1987] EWCA Civ 2 notes that BP issued letters on 31 October 1974, not received until November 4th 1974 —letters that were allegedly \"accepted by silence.\" The original correspondence would constitute primary evidence of potentially fraudulent misrepresentation. If these were not preserved, I ask under what authority they were destroyed and whether this occurred before or after the litigation concluded.\n4. Request for Formal Investigation\nI am not seeking annotation of records. I am seeking:\n\n  1.\nA formal review of whether records relating to Great House Farm, Llandough (ST 168 733) were selected for preservation, destroyed, or remain non accessioned;\n  2.\nA search of microfilm, digitised, or legacy finding aid holdings for any reference to the 1938 auction, Western Ground Rents acquisition, or the Daniel Thomas quarrying agreement;\n  3.\nConfirmation of whether The National Archives has ever received complaints regarding missing Bute Estate records or BP land acquisition documents from other researchers;\n  4.\nGuidance on the appropriate authority to investigate allegations of document removal from Cardiff Library (1984) and potential fraud in the 1982 land registration.\n\n5. Public Interest and Parliamentary Scrutiny\nThis matter involves:\n\n  *\nThe forced removal of a family from land occupied since at least 1667\n  *\nDemolition of a medieval structure described as a \"dilapidated utility building\" to avoid heritage protection\n  *\nArchaeological discoveries (1888, 1994) that were known but officially \"forgotten\"\n  *\nA Court of Appeal decision ([1987] EWCA Civ 2) that may have relied on incomplete or fraudulent evidence\n\nI am copying my MP and the Parliamentary Ombudsman in this correspondence. I will also be submitting a Subject Access Request to HM Land Registry regarding the 1982 registration and a complaint to the Information Commissioner's Office regarding potential destruction of records relevant to ongoing property disputes.\nI request a substantive response addressing the specific questions above within 20 working days. Should The National Archives be unable to assist further, I request a formal referral to the appropriate body with jurisdiction over historical record fraud and spoliation.\nYours sincerely,\nSion Buckler\n\n\n\n\nOn Sat, 7 Feb 2026 at 07:26, RESO Records Enquiries <RESORecordsEnquiries@nationalarchives.gov.uk<mailto:RESORecordsEnquiries@nationalarchives.gov.uk>> wrote:\n\nDear Requester,\n\nThe question about archival silences is one upon which there is a voluminous literature. Practically, however, the best way of making sense of what is and is not in the archives is to refer to the reports which shaped selection policies in the modern era - see especially the Report on Departmental Records (Cmnd 9136) and the Report on Legal Records (Cmnd 3084) - and the resulting retention and disposition schedules - see for example the one for the Court of Appeal (Civil Division) published here<https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/62b47cc9d3bf7f0affd65610/court-appeal-civil-division-rrds.pdf>.\n\nWhat you will see from these is that the presumption for modern records is against permanent preservation. In general, apart from small samples to demonstrate the ordinary functioning of the courts/government departments, records are only preserved if they bear witness to moments of particular legal or policy significance or change. In the case of legal records, the presumption has been that parties would be responsible for maintaining their own records.\n\nIn terms of how to continue your search, my instinct is that you should consider exploring the holdings of the relevant local archive. You can also try some keyword searches in our catalogue - see, for example, these<https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/results/r?_d=PF%7CWORK%7CBD%7CHLG&_dss=range&_sd=1978&_ed=1988&_ps=60&_hb=tna&_srt=1&_q=archaeological+NOT+bill+NOT+%22PF+309%22> search results which highlight these<https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/results/r?_q=archaeological+NOT+bill+NOT+%22PF+309%22&_d=PF%7CWORK%7CBD%7CHLG&_dss=range&_sd=1978&_ed=1988&_ps=60&_hb=tna&_srt=1&_l=3> series as potentially a relevant source of desired information. You might also wish to search the registered files of the land registry in records series LAR 1<https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/r/C10222>. You should be advised, however, that departmental records tend to focus more on policy than upon the treatment of individual cases.\n\nI hope that this is helpful.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nCharlotte Smith﻿\n\nPrincipal Records Specialist, Modern Legal Records\nwww.nationalarchives.gov.uk<http://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/>\n\nEnquiry:\n\n\n\nI note and appreciate your confirmation that the principal judicial series (J 157, J 83, J 84) contain no relevant material. Given the scale of the consequences flowing from the dispute—forced removal, demolition of an occupied dwelling, and loss of heritage fabric—the absence of supporting court or administrative files is itself significant for researchers.\n\nAccordingly, I would be grateful for guidance on the following procedural points:\n\n  *   how researchers are expected to interpret gaps or silence in the archival record where substantial actions plainly occurred;\n  *   whether related non-judicial records (for example, departmental, planning, regulatory, or corporate records connected to land registration, planning consent, or archaeological works) are more likely to sit outside the obvious court series; and\n  *   whether there is an appropriate mechanism for flagging concerns about record completeness or contextual risk, without seeking alteration of the records themselves.\n\nFor the avoidance of doubt, I am not alleging impropriety on the part of The National Archives or its staff. My concern is to ensure that the historical record is approached with appropriate caution where absence may unintentionally convey certainty or closure that did not exist at the time.\n\nPlease don't print this e-mail unless you really need to. ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- National Archives Disclaimer This email and any files transmitted with it are intended solely for the use of the individual(s) to whom they are addressed. If you are not the intended recipient and have received this email in error, please notify the sender and delete the email. Opinions, conclusions and other information in this message and attachments that do not relate to the official business of The National Archives are neither given nor endorsed by it. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nPlease don't print this e-mail unless you really need to. ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- National Archives Disclaimer This email and any files transmitted with it are intended solely for the use of the individual(s) to whom they are addressed. If you are not the intended recipient and have received this email in error, please notify the sender and delete the email. Opinions, conclusions and other information in this message and attachments that do not relate to the official business of The National Archives are neither given nor endorsed by it. ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n"},{"id":"wb-17c03298","subject":"Consortium evidence BP v Buckler","name":"2026-02-13_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","date":"2026-02-13","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-13_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-evidence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-13_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Fri, 13 Feb 2026 12:42:30 -0300\nSubject: Great House Farm Reparations\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V4Bd3-QQ6FBJuzB+ExK+rYQgf8sCpybMb+ymx4QOqySOw@mail.gmail.com>\n\n\n"},{"id":"wb-2b4d422d","subject":"Consortium evidence BP v Buckler","name":"2026-02-13_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.002.txt","date":"2026-02-13","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-13_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.002.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-evidence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-13_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.002.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Fri, 13 Feb 2026 11:08:10 -0300\nSubject: The Williams Family & Marconi Farm / Ymholiad Cofnodion Teulu: Teulu Williams a Fferm Marconi / Consulta sobre Registros Familiares: La Familia Williams y la Granja Marconi\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V7iZEfN7xms1p9vwUqa-AmWsuZKSDbS8XJgiVtt+9r47w@mail.gmail.com>\n\nWelsh (Cymraeg)\n\n*Annwyl Bugeiliaid a Chyfeillion,*\n\nRwyf yn ysgrifennu atoch i ofyn am eich cymorth. Cyrhaeddais Puerto Madryn\ny llynedd gyda’r nod o ailadeiladu fferm fy nheulu, y teulu Williams.\n\nRoedd y fferm hon yn eiddo i ni ers 1667, ac roedd yn safle hanesyddol lle\nbu Marconi yn dyfeisio’r radio. Yn anffodus, cawsom ein troi allan ym 1988\ngan BP, sydd bellach wedi adeiladu tai ar ein tir. Rydym bellach wedi\ndatgelu twyll a chelach o ran y digwyddiadau hyn. Mae ein cyfreithiwr\nwrthi’n cychwyn achos cyfreithiol i ddarparu iawndal o £200 miliwn am ein\ndiarddel o’r tir.\n\nRwyf yn chwilio am unrhyw gofnodion, tystysgrifau, neu wybodaeth yn eich\narchifau a allai gadarnhau ein llinach a'n hawl i’r tir hwn. Mae hwn yn\nfater o gyfiawnder i’r teulu cyn i’r genhedlaeth hon basio draw.\n\nDiolch yn fawr am eich help.\n\n*Yn gywir,* *Bob [Your Surname]*\n------------------------------\nSpanish (Español)\n\n*Estimados Pastores y Amigos:*\n\nLes escribo para solicitar su ayuda. Llegué a Puerto Madryn el año pasado\ncon el objetivo de reconstruir la granja de mi familia, la familia Williams.\n\nEsta granja fue nuestra propiedad y ocupación desde 1667, y fue el sitio\nhistórico donde Marconi inventó la radio. Lamentablemente, fuimos\ndesalojados en 1988 por la empresa BP, que posteriormente construyó\nviviendas en nuestras tierras. Hemos expuesto el fraude y el ocultamiento\ndetrás de este caso. Nuestro abogado está iniciando procedimientos legales\npara demandar por 200 millones de libras por desposesión.\n\nBusco cualquier registro, certificado o información en sus archivos que\npueda confirmar nuestro linaje y nuestro derecho a estas tierras. Este es\nun acto de justicia para nuestra familia antes de que sea demasiado tarde\npara nuestros mayores.\n\nMuchas gracias por su tiempo y colaboración.\n\n*Atentamente,*\nSion Hywel Buckler\nbpvsbuckler.datro.xyz\n[redacted]\n[redacted]\n\n[image: 492340134_122193631400112242_6487893391323682583_n.jpg][image:\n492889702_122193631334112242_8056921153707903085_n.jpg]\n"},{"id":"wb-93099592","subject":"Consortium evidence BP v Buckler","name":"2026-02-14_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2026-02-14","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-14_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-evidence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-14_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Sat, 14 Feb 2026 07:08:35 -0300\nSubject: Historic Adverse Possession Licence Interruption Cases (1950–2000)\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V6gDkRLzvUwe+0cs26fWzKbcU71fH5dLW5c_fKHDNKwgQ@mail.gmail.com>\n\nSirs/Ma'am,\n\nAny response about my request for a public enquiry of BP Vs Buckler 1987\nand possible fraud & consealment, collusion, conspiracy and deception?\n\nFraud unravels all. And begins when the fraud is discovered, not occured.\nYes?\n\nFurthermore,\n\nI am writing to invite interest in review of a potential coordinated action\nconcerning historic adverse possession cases in England and Wales. As an\nindigenous Welshman. From Wales. Son of the late defendant, the possibly\ndisspossed and victim of fraud; Mr. William Buckler.\n\nMyself and my lawyers and team are investigating circumstances in which\nlong-term occupiers were issued licence letters (often described as\n“rent-free life licences” or similar instruments) during or after\nlimitation periods, and whether in certain instances such licences may not\nhave constituted legally effective interruption of adverse possession.\n\nThe investigation concerns cases primarily between 1950 and 2000 where:\nLong-term occupation had exceeded 12 years;\n\nPossession proceedings were either adjourned or unresolved;\n\nA licence letter was issued and relied upon to defeat a possessory claim;\n\nQuestions may arise regarding identity, acceptance, or material disclosure.\n\nAt this stage, we are seeking:\n\nA preliminary view as to whether a coordinated group litigation strategy\n(GLO or similar) could be viable;\n\nAdvice on limitation, concealment, and fraud thresholds;\n\nGuidance on structured claimant intake if sufficient common issues are\nidentified.\n\nThis enquiry is investigative only and no public allegations are being\nmade.\n\nWe are seeking professional assessment before any formal steps are taken.\n\nIf this is of potential interest, I would welcome a confidential discussion\nat your convenience.\n\nRegards,\n\nSion Buckler Esq. Sgt (Ret'd)\nManor of Llandough\nAnd Constituent at [redacted]\n[redacted]\n"},{"id":"wb-6ada8469","subject":"Consortium evidence BP v Buckler","name":"2026-02-14_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","date":"2026-02-14","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-14_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-evidence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-14_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Sat, 14 Feb 2026 04:31:26 -0300\nSubject: Re: CAS-315609 CRM:009700964\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V75ZGSQ3DMdsNb8g-XmfmkPsZ0YQKP8jJmi1RbuD5F-9w@mail.gmail.com>\n\nThanks Martin.\n\nSion Buckler Esq. Sgt (Ret'd).\nManor of Llandough\n\n\nOn Fri, 13 Feb 2026 at 15:38, Your Views <yourviews@nationalarchives.gov.uk> \nwrote:\n\n> Dear Requester,\n>  \n> Thank you for your reply and correction email. \n>  \n> Your correspondence has been passed through to my team to co-ordinate a \n> response. As such I will be discussing contents of your messages with \n> colleagues and will respond further in due course.\n>  \n> Kind regards,\n>  \n> Martin  Willis\n>\n> [image: The National Archives logo]\n>\n> *Martin Willis *|* Quality Manager*\n> T: 020 8392 5363 | W: nationalarchives.gov.uk \n> <http://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/>\n> Twitter: @UkNatArchives |Pronouns: He/Him\n> The National Archives, Kew, Richmond, Surrey TW9 4DU\n>\n>  \n> Correction\n> In my previous correspondence, I referenced documents missing from Cardiff \n> Library as \"reported missing in 1984.\" I wish to clarify that this date \n> derives from my family's own chronology and recollection, not from an \n> official report or confirmed archival record. I should not have stated this \n> as an established fact, and I apologise for any confusion caused.\n> New Evidence\n> Since my last email, I have obtained a Forensic Evaluation and Assessment \n> of Restitutionary Claims (dated February 8, 2026) regarding the \n> dispossession of the Williams family from Great House Farm, Llandough. This \n> professional assessment provides additional context that may assist in \n> understanding the archival gaps previously identified.\n> The forensic report specifically examines:\n>\n>    1. The \"Mrs. Buckler\" nomenclature: The analysis characterises the \n>    consistent use of \"Mrs. Buckler\" in court records and correspondence as \n>    \"strategic misnaming\" rather than clerical error, given that Mary Williams \n>    never adopted her husband's surname. This raises questions about whether \n>    The National Archives holds any records indicating her formal objection to \n>    this identification or her consistent use of \"Mary Williams\" in legal \n>    proceedings.\n>    2. The 1974 license letters: The report notes that letters dated \n>    October/November 1974 were addressed to a \"non-existent legal persona,\" \n>    facilitating a judicial finding of \"silence as consent.\" I ask again \n>    whether The National Archives holds any corporate records from BP \n>    Properties Ltd or BP Pension Trust Ltd regarding this correspondence, or \n>    any indication that such records were considered for preservation.\n>    3. The 1974 adjournment: The forensic assessment questions why \n>    proceedings were adjourned part-heard on July 3, 1974, given your \n>    confirmation that principal judicial series contain no relevant material. I \n>    request clarification on whether any Cardiff County Court records from \n>    1974-1975 were transferred to The National Archives under subsequent \n>    protocols, or whether they remain with the court.\n>    \n> Revised Request\n> Given this additional professional analysis, I renew my request for \n> guidance on:\n>\n>    - Whether The National Archives has received any complaints or \n>    inquiries regarding missing Bute Estate records or Great House Farm \n>    documents from other researchers;\n>    - Whether corporate records from BP entities relating to this \n>    acquisition were ever selected for preservation; and\n>    - Whether any mechanism exists for flagging concerns about record \n>    completeness where forensic analysis suggests potential irregularities in \n>    the historical record.\n>    \n> I remain grateful for your time and professionalism, and I hope this \n> clarification assists in addressing my inquiry.\n> Yours sincerely,\n> Requester\n>\n>\n>\n>  \n> On Sun, 8 Feb 2026 at 06:57, Requester <[redacted]> \n> wrote:\n>  \n>\n>> Ms Smith,\n>> Thank you for your detailed response regarding selection policies and \n>> retention schedules. I appreciate the procedural clarity you have provided. \n>> However, I must respectfully indicate that your reply does not address the \n>> substantive concerns I have raised, and I am now compelled to escalate this \n>> matter formally.\n>> 1. From \"Archival Silence\" to Potential Evidence Tampering\n>> You correctly note that archival silences are well-documented in academic \n>> literature. However, the circumstances surrounding Great House Farm suggest \n>> something more concerning than routine selection decisions. I am now in \n>> possession of first-hand testimony (David Buckler, 2024) alleging that:\n>>\n>>    - Frederick Buckler (my grandfather) and his eldest son engaged a \n>>    solicitor privately in the 1960s\n>>    - Title documents were removed from the family home and allegedly sold\n>>    - The family was deliberately misled with the phrase \"it's all been \n>>    dealt with legally\"\n>>    \n>> This testimony, combined with the disappearance of the Bute Estate → \n>> Daniel Thomas deed index card from Cardiff Library (reported missing in \n>> 1984), suggests potential spoliation of evidence rather than routine \n>> archival selection. The question is no longer \"why weren't records \n>> preserved?\" but \"were records deliberately removed to prejudice a 320-year \n>> possessory claim?\"\n>> 2. The Computerised Records Question\n>> You direct me to search your catalogue. I have done so. However, I now \n>> ask specifically:\n>>\n>>    - Does The National Archives hold any digitised or microfilmed copies \n>>    of the Bute Estate records that might contain references to the \n>>    Daniel Thomas quarrying agreement (circa 1895-1905) or the 1938 auction at \n>>    the Bear Hotel?\n>>    - Are there any internal indices or finding aids (particularly from \n>>    the 1970s-1980s) that might reference Great House Farm, Llandough, or the \n>>    BP/Western Ground Rents acquisition records that are not reflected in the \n>>    current online catalogue?\n>>    \n>> The 1994 archaeological excavation report (Cotswold Archaeological Trust) \n>> confirms the site's national significance. The absence of corresponding \n>> land transaction records from the same period is itself an anomaly \n>> requiring explanation.\n>> 3. The BP Corporate Records Question\n>> You note that parties are generally responsible for maintaining their own \n>> records. However, BP Properties Ltd and BP Pension Trust Ltd were corporate \n>> entities with statutory filing obligations. I ask:\n>>\n>>    - Were any BP corporate records relating to land acquisition in South \n>>    Glamorgan (1969-1988) selected for preservation under the Companies \n>>    Act or as part of the public record?\n>>    - Has The National Archives ever received or sought records from BP \n>>    regarding the Great House Farm acquisition, demolition, or the 1987 \n>>    litigation?\n>>    \n>> The Court of Appeal judgment [1987] EWCA Civ 2 notes that BP issued \n>> letters on 31 October 1974, not received until November 4th 1974 —letters \n>> that were allegedly \"accepted by silence.\" The original correspondence \n>> would constitute primary evidence of potentially fraudulent \n>> misrepresentation. If these were not preserved, I ask under what authority \n>> they were destroyed and whether this occurred before or after the \n>> litigation concluded.\n>> 4. Request for Formal Investigation\n>> I am not seeking annotation of records. I am seeking:\n>>\n>>    1. A formal review of whether records relating to Great House Farm, \n>>    Llandough (ST 168 733) were selected for preservation, destroyed, or \n>>    remain non accessioned;\n>>    2. A search of microfilm, digitised, or legacy finding aid holdings \n>>    for any reference to the 1938 auction, Western Ground Rents acquisition, or \n>>    the Daniel Thomas quarrying agreement;\n>>    3. Confirmation of whether The National Archives has ever received \n>>    complaints regarding missing Bute Estate records or BP land \n>>    acquisition documents from other researchers;\n>>    4. Guidance on the appropriate authority to investigate allegations \n>>    of document removal from Cardiff Library (1984) and potential fraud in the \n>>    1982 land registration.\n>>    \n>> 5. Public Interest and Parliamentary Scrutiny\n>> This matter involves:\n>>\n>>    - The forced removal of a family from land occupied since at least \n>>    1667\n>>    - Demolition of a medieval structure described as a \"dilapidated \n>>    utility building\" to avoid heritage protection\n>>    - Archaeological discoveries (1888, 1994) that were known but \n>>    officially \"forgotten\"\n>>    - A Court of Appeal decision ([1987] EWCA Civ 2) that may have relied \n>>    on incomplete or fraudulent evidence\n>>    \n>> I am copying my MP and the Parliamentary Ombudsman in this \n>> correspondence. I will also be submitting a Subject Access Request to HM \n>> Land Registry regarding the 1982 registration and a complaint to the \n>> Information Commissioner's Office regarding potential destruction of \n>> records relevant to ongoing property disputes.\n>> I request a substantive response addressing the specific questions above \n>> within 20 working days. Should The National Archives be unable to assist \n>> further, I request a formal referral to the appropriate body with \n>> jurisdiction over historical record fraud and spoliation.\n>> Yours sincerely,\n>> Requester\n>>\n>\n>\n>  \n>\n>> On Sat, 7 Feb 2026 at 07:26, RESO Records Enquiries <\n>> RESORecordsEnquiries@nationalarchives.gov.uk> wrote:\n>>  \n>>\n>>> Dear Requester,\n>>>  \n>>> The question about archival silences is one upon which there is a \n>>> voluminous literature. Practically, however, the best way of making sense \n>>> of what is and is not in the archives is to refer to the reports which \n>>> shaped selection policies in the modern era - see especially the Report on \n>>> Departmental Records (Cmnd 9136) and the Report on Legal Records (Cmnd \n>>> 3084) - and the resulting retention and disposition schedules - see for \n>>> example the one for the Court of Appeal (Civil Division) published here \n>>> <https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/62b47cc9d3bf7f0affd65610/court-appeal-civil-division-rrds.pdf>\n>>> .\n>>>  \n>>> What you will see from these is that the presumption for modern records \n>>> is against permanent preservation. In general, apart from small samples to \n>>> demonstrate the ordinary functioning of the courts/government departments, \n>>> records are only preserved if they bear witness to moments of particular \n>>> legal or policy significance or change. In the case of legal records, the \n>>> presumption has been that parties would be responsible for maintaining \n>>> their own records.\n>>>  \n>>> In terms of how to continue your search, my instinct is that you should \n>>> consider exploring the holdings of the relevant local archive. You can also \n>>> try some keyword searches in our catalogue - see, for example, these \n>>> <https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/results/r?_d=PF%7CWORK%7CBD%7CHLG&_dss=range&_sd=1978&_ed=1988&_ps=60&_hb=tna&_srt=1&_q=archaeological+NOT+bill+NOT+%22PF+309%22> \n>>> search results which highlight these \n>>> <https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/results/r?_q=archaeological+NOT+bill+NOT+%22PF+309%22&_d=PF%7CWORK%7CBD%7CHLG&_dss=range&_sd=1978&_ed=1988&_ps=60&_hb=tna&_srt=1&_l=3> \n>>> series as potentially a relevant source of desired information. You might \n>>> also wish to search the registered files of the land registry in records \n>>> series LAR 1 \n>>> <https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/r/C10222>. You \n>>> should be advised, however, that departmental records tend to focus more on \n>>> policy than upon the treatment of individual cases.\n>>>\n>>> I hope that this is helpful.\n>>>  \n>>> Yours sincerely,\n>>>\n>>> Charlotte Smith﻿\n>>>\n>>> Principal Records Specialist, Modern Legal Records\n>>> *www.nationalarchives.gov.uk* <http://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/>\n>>>  \n>>> *Enquiry:*\n>>>\n>>>  \n>>>\n>>> I note and appreciate your confirmation that the principal judicial \n>>> series (J 157, J 83, J 84) contain no relevant material. Given the scale of \n>>> the consequences flowing from the dispute—forced removal, demolition of an \n>>> occupied dwelling, and loss of heritage fabric—the absence of supporting \n>>> court or administrative files is itself significant for researchers.\n>>>\n>>> Accordingly, I would be grateful for guidance on the following \n>>> procedural points:\n>>>\n>>>    - how researchers are expected to interpret gaps or silence in the \n>>>    archival record where substantial actions plainly occurred;\n>>>    - whether related non-judicial records (for example, departmental, \n>>>    planning, regulatory, or corporate records connected to land registration, \n>>>    planning consent, or archaeological works) are more likely to sit outside \n>>>    the obvious court series; and\n>>>    - whether there is an appropriate mechanism for flagging concerns \n>>>    about record completeness or contextual risk, without seeking alteration of \n>>>    the records themselves.\n>>>\n>>> For the avoidance of doubt, I am not alleging impropriety on the part of \n>>> The National Archives or its staff. My concern is to ensure that the \n>>> historical record is approached with appropriate caution where absence may \n>>> unintentionally convey certainty or closure that did not exist at the time.\n>>>\n>>> Please don't print this e-mail unless you really need to. \n>>> ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- \n>>> National Archives Disclaimer This email and any files transmitted with it \n>>> are intended solely for the use of the individual(s) to whom they are \n>>> addressed. If you are not the intended recipient and have received this \n>>> email in error, please notify the sender and delete the email. Opinions, \n>>> conclusions and other information in this message and attachments that do \n>>> not relate to the official business of The National Archives are neither \n>>> given nor endorsed by it. \n>>> ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n>>>\n>> Please don't print this e-mail unless you really need to. \n> ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- \n> National Archives Disclaimer This email and any files transmitted with it \n> are intended solely for the use of the individual(s) to whom they are \n> addressed. If you are not the intended recipient and have received this \n> email in error, please notify the sender and delete the email. Opinions, \n> conclusions and other information in this message and attachments that do \n> not relate to the official business of The National Archives are neither \n> given nor endorsed by it. \n> ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ \n>\n>\n"},{"id":"wb-4865280a","subject":"Consortium FOI BP v Buckler","name":"2026-02-15_consortium-foi_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2026-02-15","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-15_consortium-foi_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-foi-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler","email","ghf"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["bpvsbuckler","email","ghf"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-15_consortium-foi_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Sun, 15 Feb 2026 22:03:26 -0300\nSubject: Subject: Freedom of Information Request - Ty Mawr (Great House), Llandough, near Penarth (1938-1987)\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V68hvjJMkW3LMix1V=rBJoML+SbZoYxzVasH7nDat_=gw@mail.gmail.com>\n\nSir/Madam,\n\nI understand you may already be busy, so please don't feel overwhelmed by\nthis FOI request—I'm conducting my own diligence and happy for you to take\nthe full statutory 20 working days to respond.\n\nUnder the Freedom of Information Act 2000, I request copies of all records,\ncorrespondence, memoranda, internal notes, minutes, file notes, land\nregistration applications, objections, or communications held by HM Land\nRegistry from 1 January 1938 to 31 December 1987 relating to:Ty Mawr (Great\nHouse), Llandough, near Penarth, South Glamorgan (also known as Great House\nFarm).\n\nLand registration applications, title transfers, or dealings involving\nMountjoy Ltd, Western Ground Rents Ltd, BP Pension Trust Ltd, and/or BP\nProperties Ltd (including the 1938 auction/reversion conveyance and 1969\nsale).\n\nInteractions, notices, proceedings, tenancy offers, licences (e.g., 1974\nunilateral lifetime licence letters), or disputes involving Mrs Mary\nWilliams (also referred to as Mrs Buckler), Mr Frederick Buckler, or\nWilliams/Buckler family occupiers—particularly the 1962 Cardiff County\nCourt possession proceedings (Dec 11 order by Judge Temple Morris QC).\n\nAny possession proceedings, adverse possession claims, or title objections\nconnected to the property (e.g., 1955 High Court, 1962 Cardiff County\nCourt, 1974 actions).\n\nPlease include archived, off-site, or scanned records where applicable.If\nexemptions apply (e.g., Section 40 personal data), specify which and why.\nIf costs exceed the appropriate limit, advise how to refine (e.g.,\nprioritize 1955-1987 or 1962/1974 events).\n\nI look forward to your response within 20 working days.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\nSion Buckler Esq. Sgt (Ret'd)\n"},{"id":"wb-84e13bee","subject":"Consortium land registry BP v Buckler","name":"2026-02-16_consortium-land_registry_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2026-02-16","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Land Registry correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-16_consortium-land_registry_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-land-registry-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-16_consortium-land_registry_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Mon, 16 Feb 2026 14:31:46 -0300\nSubject: Title Fraud and Procedural Concealment – Church View Close, Llandough (formerly Great House Farm/ \"Ty Mawr\")\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V6c+iXa6gGoJzzbHDw13Xe_=baznjfVK902R-XwAzePZg@mail.gmail.com>\n\nCouncillor Carroll & co,\n\nI write regarding potential gaps in the Council's planning and\nrecord-keeping processes relating to Great House Farm, Llandough, which was\ndemolished in 1988 following the 1987 Court of Appeal decision in BP\nProperties Ltd v Buckler.\n\nRecent evidence suggests the Council may wish to examine its own position\non this matter, as it raises questions about procedural compliance and\nrecord integrity that could expose the authority to wider scrutiny.\n\nCORE ISSUES\n\n**1. Identity and Procedural Integrity**\n\nThe 1987 judgment turned on a 1974 licence addressed to \"Mrs Buckler\". My\ngrandmother, Mary Williams, did not use or accept this designation.\nContemporaneous evidence (1978 newspaper article attached) records her\nasserting ownership under her correct name.\n\nIf the 1987 court was not presented with this evidence, the judgment may\nrest on an incomplete factual record. This raises procedural fairness\nconcerns relevant to any future review of the decision.\n\n**2. Heritage Information and Statutory Compliance**\n\nThe site's archaeological significance was documented by 1978 (Roman\nburials, monastic associations). Yet professional excavations in 1994\nrevealed 1,026 early medieval burials and monastic structures—findings that\nshould have prompted evaluation under the Ancient Monuments and\nArchaeological Areas Act 1979.\n\nCritical question: Did the Council conduct or require prior archaeological\nassessment before granting permissions to demolish? If not, this represents\na potential gap in statutory process that may affect the validity of the\npermissions granted.\n\n**3. Land Registry Disclosure**\n\nBP registered title in 1982 based on the disputed licence. The Council\nshould establish:\n- Whether any dispute notice or heritage caution was lodged;\n- Whether the Council disclosed relevant heritage information to the Land\nRegistry;\n- What planning records exist from 1978–1988 regarding the site's known\narchaeological potential.\n\n**4. Corporate Identity Inconsistency**\n\nDuring my father's appeal, the court treated BP Properties Ltd and BP\nPension Trust Ltd inconsistently—as separate entities when convenient, then\nas one when dismissing his claim. This raises questions about how\nthoroughly the litigation was scrutinised and whether the Council's records\nreflect this uncertainty.\n\nWHY THIS MATTERS TO THE COUNCIL\n\nThese issues go beyond a single historical dispute. They touch on:\n\n- **Planning record integrity**: Whether heritage information was\nadequately documented and acted upon;\n- **Procedural compliance**: Whether statutory protections (Ancient\nMonuments Act) were properly applied;\n- **Institutional liability**: Whether decisions were made on incomplete or\nmisleading information;\n- **Precedent risk**: Whether similar vulnerabilities may exist in other\nhistorical planning decisions.\n\nREQUESTS\n\nI ask the Council to:\n\n1. **Commission a review** of the planning permissions granted between\n1978–1988 for Great House Farm, specifically examining what heritage and\narchaeological information was available to decision-makers and how it was\nhandled.\n\n2. **Refer this matter to the Monitoring Officer** for examination of\nprocedural compliance and record integrity.\n\n3. **Establish** what correspondence, cautions, or dispute notices exist in\nrelation to this site's title or heritage status.\n\n4. **Clarify the Council's current protocol** for handling historical title\ndisputes, previously unknown heritage information, or allegations of\nmaterial non-disclosure in past planning processes—to prevent recurrence.\n\nI am not asking the Council to reverse the 1987 judgment or disturb current\nresidents. I am asking it to ensure its own records are accurate and its\nown processes are defensible.\n\nI attach the 1978 article and supporting documents, and am available to\nmeet or provide further information.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\nSion Buckler, Sgt (Ret'd)\nExecutor for the Manor of Llandough\n[redacted]\n"},{"id":"wb-1162a231","subject":"Consortium evidence BP v Buckler","name":"2026-02-17_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2026-02-17","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-17_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-evidence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-17_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Emily Wellington <Emily.Wellington@jcpsolicitors.co.uk>\nDate: Tue, 17 Feb 2026 17:04:28 +0000\nSubject: RE: [EXTERNAL] Cais am Gyngor Cyfreithiol – Ty Mawr, Llandough\nMessage-ID: <LO4P265MB3584BA18E61402EDFFAC80CAC56DA@LO4P265MB3584.GBRP265.PROD.OUTLOOK.COM>\n\nGood afternoon Mr Buckler,\n\nThank you for your enquiry and voicemail.\n\nFor us to provide a fee quotation we will require sight of some further documents. Please send these through and we can assess if we are able to assist further.\n\nWhilst we do not provide legal advice through the medium of Welsh, my colleague Rhys Evans is a fluent Welsh speaker and is happy to discuss matters through the medium of Welsh.\n\nI look forward to hearing from you.\n\nKind regards,\nEmily\n\n\n​​​​\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]<mailto:[redacted]>>\nSent: 13 February 2026 01:33\nTo: UZZI Consulting <uzziconsulting@protonmail.com<mailto:uzziconsulting@protonmail.com>>\nCc: PMcardiff@hughjames.com<mailto:PMcardiff@hughjames.com>; Hello <hello@jcpsolicitors.co.uk<mailto:hello@jcpsolicitors.co.uk>>; cardiff@blakemorgan.co.uk<mailto:cardiff@blakemorgan.co.uk>; hello@hevans.com<mailto:hello@hevans.com>\nSubject: [EXTERNAL] Cais am Gyngor Cyfreithiol – Ty Mawr, Llandough\n\n\nAnnwyl Syr/Fadam,\n\nYsgrifennaf i ofyn am gyngor cyfreithiol mewn perthynas â mater cymhleth yn ymwneud â Thŷ Mawr (Great House Farm), Llandough.\n\nYn gryno, mae’r mater yn codi’r cwestiynau canlynol:\n\n  *   Diffyg penderfyniad barnwrol ar berchnogaeth cyn achos meddiant yn y 1980au;\n  *   Cofrestru cyntaf yn 1982 a allai gynnwys “camgymeriad” o fewn ystyr Atodlen 4 Deddf Cofrestru Tir 2002;\n  *   Amnewid neu anghysondeb hunaniaeth yn y disgrifiad cyfreithiol;\n  *   Cwestiynau ynghylch ystyriaeth a datgeliad treftadaeth archaeolegol.\n\nRwy’n ceisio:\n\n  1.  Barn gynnar ar hyfywedd unrhyw gais i adolygu neu gywiro’r gofrestr;\n  2.  Asesiad o’r sefyllfa gyfyngu (limitation);\n  3.  Cyngor ar gamau cyn-weithredu priodol.\n\nRwyf wedi atodi crynodeb gweithredol byr er hwylustod.\n\nByddwn yn ddiolchgar am gyfle i drefnu ymgynghoriad cychwynnol. Rwy’n barod i dalu am gyngor cychwynnol ffurfiol.\n\nYn gywir,\n\nSion Buckler\n[redacted]\n[redacted]\n[Evolve:cb628be1-368d-44fb-9e69-22ad06858d51]\n"},{"id":"wb-e5be67f4","subject":"Consortium evidence BP v Buckler","name":"2026-02-17_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","date":"2026-02-17","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-17_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-evidence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-17_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Tue, 17 Feb 2026 13:16:32 -0300\nSubject: Re: Historic Adverse Possession Licence Interruption Case(s)\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V5MgYu7R7f1UdNXShe2RJmXr-w3jRFnnrbZ5uZAdLoKvQ@mail.gmail.com>\n\nContact and Assessment Team,\n\nThank you for your email of 17 February 2026 outlining the Bar Standards\nBoard's role and reporting procedures.\n\nBefore submitting a formal report via your online form or other means, I\nwould appreciate any preliminary guidance the Board may be able to provide\non whether the matters I describe below appear to raise arguable issues of\nprofessional conduct under the BSB Handbook that might merit further\nconsideration through your processes.\n\nI write on behalf of my family in connection with the case BP Properties\nLtd v Buckler [1987] EWCA Civ 2, which concerned a claim to adverse\npossession of property at Great House Farm, Llandough (a historic site).\nThe Court of Appeal upheld the effectiveness of a unilateral licence\ngranted in 1974, finding that it ended the adverse nature of possession.\n\nThe concerns I wish to raise relate to the conduct of the barrister(s)\nrepresenting BP Properties Ltd in the 1987 proceedings, specifically:\n\n1. Whether there was adequate consideration or challenge to the identity of\nthe addressee in the 1974 licence letters (addressed to \"Mrs Buckler\"),\ngiven that the occupier was known as Mary Williams and did not formally\nadopt the surname Buckler. She retained her maiden name Williams throughout\nher life, as evidenced by her marriage records (married as Williams in\n1936), court proceedings and dealings prior to 1974 (all referring to her\nas Williams), and her 1983 death record (registered as Mary Williams). The\nlicence letters appear to be the first documents to use \"Mrs Buckler,\" a\nname which arguably carried no equivalent claim to the land as that held by\nMary Williams. This relates to whether the basis for the licence's\neffectiveness was fully addressed in terms of offer/acceptance or meeting\nof minds under relevant principles (e.g., as referenced in cases like Cundy\nv Lindsay on consensus ad idem). The court's use of Latin phrases in the\njudgment may not have fully reflected Mary Williams's actual superior land\nclaim.\n\n2. Whether any relevant information concerning the property's heritage and\narchaeological significance, or potential future demolition plans, was\nknown or should have been disclosed during the proceedings. The property\nwas a 13th-century monastic grange, and a Roman soldier in full armour was\ndiscovered and reported by the family in 1870 beneath the farmhouse floor,\nas emphasized in a 1978 newspaper article where Mary Williams stated, \"This\nis my house.\" The judgment was delivered on 31 July 1987, and the property\nwas demolished in December 1988 despite Cadw's active consideration of\nemergency spot-listing. Subsequent archaeological excavations in 1994\nconfirmed the site as a major early-medieval monastic cemetery of national\nimportance, with over 800 burials.\n\n3. Whether all material facts relating to the circumstances and addressing\nof the 1974 licence letters were fully and properly presented to the court,\nincluding any potential concealment or suppression of evidence regarding\nthe occupier's true identity, the 1667 family tradition of acquisition, or\nthe alleged 1984 removal of a key index card from Cardiff Central Library\nthat may have supported the family's ownership claim.\n\n4. Whether the question of the family's potential superior title or\nownership — derived from centuries of occupation, the 1916 tenancy context,\nand accumulated adverse possession — was adequately addressed or\nchallenged, given that possession proceedings may have deferred or avoided\na full determination of ultimate title.\n\n5. Whether any assessments, surveys, or heritage-impact reports conducted\nbefore the 1987 proceedings were fully disclosed, and if they addressed or\nomitted historic reports of Roman and monastic burials on the site.\n\nI understand that the Board cannot pre-determine the outcome of any\ninvestigation and that complaints involving historical matters may involve\nevidentiary challenges. However, I would be grateful for any indication as\nto whether, on the face of the summarised issues and the attached document,\nthese appear to disclose potential breaches of the Handbook (e.g., duties\nof candour, not to mislead the court, or integrity) that would justify\nproceeding to a formal report.\n\nThis guidance would help ensure any subsequent submission is appropriately\nfocused and substantiated.\n\nThank you for your assistance. I look forward to your response.\n\nRegards,\n\nSion Hywel Buckler Esq./Sgt (Ret'd)\nof the Manor of Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan\nOn behalf of the family and The Restored House of Williams\n"},{"id":"wb-6f0f6a70","subject":"Consortium petition BP v Buckler","name":"2026-02-17_consortium-petition_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2026-02-17","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-17_consortium-petition_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-petition-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-17_consortium-petition_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: \"Petitions Committee | Y Pwyllgor Deisebau\" <petitions@senedd.wales>\nDate: Tue, 17 Feb 2026 12:10:25 +0000\nSubject: RE: Cais am Ddeiseb ac Ymchwiliad Cyhoeddus: Achos Buckler v BP (1987) a Dinistr Great House Farm, Llandough / Formal Request for Inquiry: The Great House Farm Dispossession (BP vs Buckler)\nMessage-ID: <CWLP123MB42277C7D8F8BA8B3E01E96E4816DA@CWLP123MB4227.GBRP123.PROD.OUTLOOK.COM>\n\nDear Requester,\n\nThank you for your message. Senedd petitions are created and managed through an online system<https://petitions.senedd.wales/petitions/check>, and must meet the standards for petitions<https://senedd.wales/senedd-business/petitions/standards-for-petitions/>, and full rules on proper form<https://petitions.senedd.wales/rules>. We would encourage you to check these first, as a Senedd petition may not be the most appropriate channel to pursue your issue.\n\nCofion/Regards,\n\nLara.\n\n[cid:image001.png@01DCA006.653C2280]\nLara Date\nAil Glerc, Pwyllgor Deisebau, Senedd Cymru\nSecond Clerk, Petitions Committee, Welsh Parliament\n________________________________\n[cid:image002.png@01DCA006.653C2280]\n0300 200 6381\n\n[cid:image003.png@01DCA006.653C2280]\nwww.senedd.cymru<http://www.senedd.cymru/>\nwww.senedd.wales<http://www.senedd.wales/>\n[cid:image004.png@01DCA006.653C2280]\n@SeneddDeisebau\n@SeneddPetitions\n[cid:image005.png@01DCA006.653C2280]\nseneddcymru<https://www.facebook.com/seneddcymru>\nseneddwales<https://www.facebook.com/seneddwales/>\n________________________________\nSenedd Cymru yw’r corff sy’n cael ei ethol yn ddemocrataidd i gynrychioli buddiannau Cymru a’i phobl. Mae’r Senedd, fel y’i gelwir, yn deddfu ar gyfer Cymru, yn cytuno ar drethi yng Nghymru, ac yn dwyn Llywodraeth Cymru i gyfrif.\nThe Welsh Parliament is the democratically elected body that represents the interests of Wales and its people. Commonly known as the Senedd, it makes laws for Wales, agrees Welsh taxes and holds the Welsh Government to account.\nCroesewir gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg neu Saesneg | We welcome correspondence in Welsh or English\n\nDylid ystyried unrhyw ddatganiad neu sylw a geir yn y neges hon fel un personol ac nid o reidrwydd yn fynegiant o safbwynt Senedd Cymru, unrhyw ran ohoni neu unrhyw gorff cysylltiedig.\nAny of the statements or comments made above should be regarded as personal and not necessarily those of the Welsh Parliament, any constituent part or connected body.\n\n________________________________\n\n\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]>\nSent: 01 February 2026 11:02\nTo: Petitions Committee | Y Pwyllgor Deisebau <Petitions@senedd.wales>\nSubject: Cais am Ddeiseb ac Ymchwiliad Cyhoeddus: Achos Buckler v BP (1987) a Dinistr Great House Farm, Llandough / Formal Request for Inquiry: The Great House Farm Dispossession (BP vs Buckler)\n\n[https://senedd.wales/media/obuo0sep/senedd_logo_xs.png]\nE-mail protection couldn't recognize this email as this is the first time you received an email from this sender [redacted]\n\n\nAelodau Anrhydeddus y Senedd,\n\nRwy’n ysgrifennu atoch i dynnu eich sylw at yr hyn yr wyf yn ei ystyried yn anghyfiawnder hanesyddol difrifol, yn ymwneud â’m teulu, y Bucklers, a dinistr Great House Farm yn Llandough — safle fferm a chartref o dros 800 mlynedd o hanes, gan gynnwys cysylltiadau mynachaidd a chysylltiad uniongyrchol â Guglielmo Marconi yn ystod datblygiad cynnar radio.\n\nMae’r mater hwn yn codi cwestiynau difrifol ynghylch hawliau tir, gweithdrefnau cyfreithiol, cofrestru tir, a hawliau dynol yng Nghymru.\n\nEr enghraifft, mewn datganiad i’r wasg yn 1974, dywedodd fy nain yn gyhoeddus: “It’s my home.” Mae’r datganiad hwn, sydd yn fater o gofnod cyhoeddus, yn gwrth-ddweud yn uniongyrchol ganfyddiad Llys Apêl 1987 yn BP v Buckler, lle dywedwyd ei bod wedi bod yn “ddistaw” ac wedi derbyn trwydded BP a gyhoeddwyd yn 1974.\n\nYn yr un achos, gwrthodwyd apêl fy nhad ar y sail bod BP Pension Trust Ltd a BP Properties Ltd yn “effeithiol yr un cwmni”. Fodd bynnag, yn y dyfarniad yr oedd yn ei herio, nododd y barnwr nad oedd ymatebion fy nain i BP Pension Trust Ltd yn cyfrif gan mai BP Properties Ltd oedd yr hawlydd, ac mai cwmnïau ar wahân oeddent. Yn ymarferol, roedd hyn yn arwain at sefyllfa “pen y cwmni’n ennill, cynffon y teulu’n colli”.\n\nYn sgil hyn, cafodd fy nhad ei dynnu o’i wely ysbyty, ei garcharu heb gyhuddiad, a gosodwyd gorchymyn atal arno i’w atal rhag meddiannu ac amddiffyn ein fferm 800 oed rhag cael ei dymchwel. Cafodd ei gais i’r Llys Hawliau Dynol Ewropeaidd ei rwystro cyn iddo farw yn 1991, yn ddim ond 41 oed.\n\nAr y sail hon, rwy’n gofyn yn ffurfiol am Ddeiseb i sefydlu Ymchwiliad Cyhoeddus i’r achos hwn, fel mater o ddwyn tir hanesyddol ac o dorri hawliau dynol yng Nghymru.\n\nRwy’n weithiwr proffesiynol ym maes seiber a rhyfela electronig milwrol. Mae technolegau AI pwerus bellach ar gael i bob un ohonom, ac nid oes unrhyw reswm technegol pam na ellid archwilio’r mater hwn yn llawn ac yn drylwyr. Mae’r hyn a ddigwyddodd i’m teulu yn ymddangos fel anghyfiawnder dwys, aml-genhedlaethol, ac yn debyg i ffurf ar “lawfare”, lle y trinwyd fy nheulu fel llafur di-dâl i gynnal ased tir hyd nes yr oedd yn gyfleus i’w ariannu. Mae’n arwyddocaol bod bron pob cam yn ein herbyn wedi digwydd ar yr eiliad olaf posibl.\n\nCeir honiadau bod sylw sylweddol yn y wasg yn 1974 o blaid fy nain a’n teulu, ynghyd â deiseb o tua 1,700 o bobl yn ceisio atal dymchwel y fferm. Ymhellach, mae’n ymddangos bod CADW wedi methu ag ymyrryd lle dylai’r safle fod wedi cael statws Adeilad Rhestredig Gradd II.\n\nNid wyf yn sicr a gofnodwyd erioed yn briodol yn y Gofrestrfa Tir fod y tir — a gofrestrwyd gan BP yn 1982 — dan anghydfod. Yn fy nealltwriaeth i, cafodd ei drin fel pe na bai mewn anghydfod o gwbl. Nid oes unrhyw lys wedi penderfynu’n derfynol mai BP oedd perchnogion cyfreithiol y tir; cafodd hynny ei honni yn hytrach na’i brofi.\n\nMae hefyd fater pwysig o hanes diwylliannol. Arhosodd Marconi gyda’n teulu yn y fferm wrth iddo ddatblygu radio. Mae cynghorwyr a’r plwyf yn Llandough yn gwrthod cydnabod hyn. Rwy’n deall bod Marconi yn cael ei ystyried yn wleidyddol eithafol ar y dde, ac, fel y gwelir yn achos y cerflun radio ym Mae Caerdydd, mae ei rôl hanesyddol yn cael ei hailysgrifennu. Mae’n rhesymol tybio bod ein teulu wedi cael ei ddal mewn ymgyrch wleidyddol ehangach o’r chwith yn erbyn y dde, ac mai am roi llety i Marconi y bu i ni wynebu canlyniadau canrif o hyd — problemau nad oedd yn bodoli yn y tair canrif flaenorol yr oeddem wedi byw ar y fferm.\n\nRoedd mam Marconi hefyd yn Jamerson o gwmni wisgi Gwyddelig Jameson. Rwy’n gofyn i chi gynnal eich ymchwil eich hun, gwneud eich ymholiadau eich hun, a dod i’ch casgliadau eich hun — ond yn anad dim, peidiwch â chaniatáu i hanes gael ei ailysgrifennu yma.\n\nMae fy nheulu bellach yn archwilio opsiynau ar gyfer siarad cyhoeddus, hawliau stori, a dogfennaeth. Yn nhermau cysylltiadau teuluol: fy nhaid oedd Ray Smith o Dempsey and Makepeace; mae fy modryb yn Shan Cothi; a dechreuodd fy ewythr y band Tigertailz.\n\nRwy’n disgrifio’r sefyllfa hon fel gwarthus. Mae fy nheulu’n mynnu cyfiawnder am gael ein dadleoli a’n dwyn o’n tir a’n heiddo gan y Sefydliad Prydeinig a BP, gyda phrin unrhyw amddiffyniad dan y gyfraith. Rydym yn deulu Cymreig brodorol, Cymraeg eu hiaith, wedi byw yng Nghaerdydd, Bro Morgannwg, a’r fferm yn Llandough ers cannoedd o flynyddoedd.\n\nO’r hyn y gallaf ei gasglu, ceir dwsinau o achosion posibl o dwyll a chuddio gwybodaeth, sy’n golygu nad yw statud cyfyngiadau yn berthnasol o reidrwydd. I’r rhai sy’n cefnogi dad-drefedigaethu Cymru ac annibyniaeth, prin y ceir enghraifft gliriach na BP v Buckler (1987) o’r hyn a ddigwyddodd i deulu ffermio Cymreig wrth herio’r Wladwriaeth Brydeinig a chorfforaeth fawr. Nid yw eraill wedi codi’r mater hwn, yn bennaf oherwydd trawma cenhedlaethol.\n\nRwyf wedi profi fy hun. Gadewais fy ngyrfa filwrol gydag anrhydedd, gan gwblhau fy llw 22 mlynedd. Cefais fy nghyflogi’n bona fide gan Gyngor Bro Morgannwg a Llywodraeth Cymru hyd at 2024. Gwasanaethais mewn rhyfeloedd yn y Dwyrain Canol. Fodd bynnag, nid yw’r anghyfiawnder fferm yn fater unigol.\n\nYn 2013, cafodd fy mab 36 diwrnod oed ei gipio, ac nid wyf erioed wedi ei weld ers hynny. Cafodd tŷ ei ddwyn oddi arnaf (40 Lombard Street, Y Barri). Yn 2023, cefais fy ngadael i gysgu mewn pabell tra roedd bron i 100 o Afghanistaniaid yn cael eu lletya’n dawel wrth ymyl ein pentref o 11 tŷ, tra roedd yn rhaid imi aros yno i ofalu am fy nain gan na allai gofalwyr ei chyrraedd.\n\nO ganlyniad, rwyf bellach yn byw yn yr hyn a elwir yn Setliad Cymreig yn yr Ariannin. Rwyf wedi fy nisodli ac wedi fy dadleoli i’r eithaf. Serch hynny, rwy’n ddiolchgar fy mod wedi goroesi gyda’m rhyddid, fy mywyd, a’m henw da yn gymharol gyfan. Serch hynny, hoffwn fod wedi aros yng Nghymru, fel Cymro, gyda safon a chynhaliaeth bywyd deg.\n\nMae fy ngwraig a minnau’n amharod i ganiatáu i’n merch hyd yn oed gamu ar Ynysoedd Prydain o ystyried yr hyn a glywn sy’n digwydd yno. Yn 2024, allforiais hyd yn oed fy nghi o Abertawe i’n heiddo yn y Caribî ar ôl i unigolyn Afghanistan fod yng ngardd fy nheulu yng Nghymru, gan geisio ei ddwyn.\n\nGofynnaf i’r Senedd gymryd y mater hwn o ddifrif, ac ystyried sefydlu Ymchwiliad Cyhoeddus llawn, tryloyw ac annibynnol. Nid er budd fy nheulu yn unig y mae hyn, ond er uniondeb hanes, cyfiawnder tir, a hawliau dynol yng Nghymru.\n\nYn gywir,\n\nSion Buckler\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nSirs/Ma'am,\n\nI am writing to you regarding a significant historical injustice involving my family, the Bucklers, and the destruction of Great House Farm (an 800-year-old site of monastic and Marconi history).\n\nFor example, in a 1974 press statement my grandmother said publically 'It's my home'—that directly contradicts the 1987 Court of Appeal ruling that she was 'silent' and accepted of BP license issued in 1974. My dad's appeal for the BP vs Buckler 1987 case also got dismissed on grounds both BP Pension Trust Ltd and BP Properties Ltd were effectively the same company. But in the ruling he was appealing, the judge said that my nans replies to BP Pensions Trust Ltd didn't count as BP Properties Ltd were the claimant, and the two were seperate companies. So it was heads they win, tails we lose. My dad was ripped from his hospital bed, jailed without charge and slapped with a restraining order to keep him from occupying and defending our 800 year old farm from being bulldozed down. His ECHR request blocked before he died in 1991 aged just 41.\n\nI am requesting a Petition for a Public Inquiry into this case as a matter of historical land theft and human rights violations in Wales.\n\nI'm a military cyber and electronic warfare professional. We all have very powerful AI technology at our disposal which can make light work out of all of this. My family suffered an incredible injustice. It also appears to be multi-generational warfare/ lawfare. Whereby we were treated as free labour to upkeep the asset of the land and property until it was ripe to be cashed in. I say this as every action against us seemed to be last minute.\n\nThere was allegedly press covering the 74 case and supporting my nan and family and farm. There was a petition of 1700 people trying to defend us and the farm from demolishment. CADW looked the other way when it should have been a Grade II listed building. I could go on and on and on.\n\nI'm not even sure the land registry recorded that our land, being registered in 1982 by BP , was in dispute. I believe it was treated as if it wasn't dispited. No court actually definitively determined that BP were the landowners. It was just asserted.\n\nMarconi stayed with our family at our farm when inventing radio. The councillors and parish at Llandough won't even acknowledge this. I understand Marconi was far right and so, like the radio statute at Cardiff Bay has confirmed, he is being re-written out of history.  It stands to reason that our family was caught in this political campaign of left vs right. And for helping Marconi we found ourselves experiencing a century of problems afterwards. Which didn't exist for the 3 centuries we were at our farm before letting far right marconi stay with us. Marconi's mother was also Jamerson of the Irish Whiskey company.\n\nDo your own homework. Make your own enquiries. Draw your own conclusions.\n\n But please do not let history be rewritten here. My family are exploring options for public speaking, story rights, and a documentary etc.\n\nMy grandfather was Ray Smith, of Dempsey and Makepeace. My aunty Shan Cothi. My uncle started the band tigertailz.\n\nThis is an outrage and my family demands justice for being displaced and stolen from, land and property, by the British Establishment/ BP -  with little to no protections under law. And we're indigenous to the land, Welsh, Welsh speaking, Welsh family. Resident in Cardiff / Vale and that farm in Llandough for HUNDREDS OF YEARS.\n\nThere's tens of counts of fraud and concealment from what I can gather. Which means the statute of limitation doesn't apply in this instance. I know some are in favour of Wales decolonising and gaining independence. I think there's no better example of BP vs Buckler 1987 and what has happened to my family. Nobody else will bring this up because there's generational trauma. Notwithstanding we, a welsh farming family, are speaking up and against the British State and BP.\n\nBut I've proven myself. Leaving my military career with honours. Finishing my 22 years Oath, bona fide, employed by the Vale of Glamorgan Council, Welsh Government until 2024. I served in the wars in the middle east. I don't know what is going on, on that island. But the farm injustice isn't the only matter. My 36 day old son was abducted in 2013 and I've never seen him since. I had a house stolen (40 Lombard Street, Barry). And in 2023 I was left to sleep in a tent, while almost 100 afghans were secretly housed next to our 11 home village. Where I had to be to care for my grandmother, as no carers couldn't reach her. So I'm currently at the Welsh Settlement in Argentina. So I'm as displaced and replaced as I can be to cater for the insanity that's going on, on that island. And grateful I've managed to survive it all with my liberty and life and reputation somewhat still intact. But I would have actually liked to have remained in Wales as a Welshman. And have some quality and standard of life. My wife and I are reluctant for our daughter to even step foot on the British Isles with what we're hearing that's going on there lately. And I even exported my dog from Swansea to our place in the Caribbean in 2024 after an afghan was in my family's garden in Wales, trying to steal him.\n\nI look forward to your reply,\n\nRegards,\n\nSion Buckler\n"},{"id":"wb-8ac0cb43","subject":"Consortium petition BP v Buckler","name":"2026-02-17_consortium-petition_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","date":"2026-02-17","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-17_consortium-petition_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-petition-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-17_consortium-petition_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Tue, 17 Feb 2026 10:32:28 -0300\nSubject: Re: Cais am Ddeiseb ac Ymchwiliad Cyhoeddus: Achos Buckler v BP (1987) a Dinistr Great House Farm, Llandough / Formal Request for Inquiry: The Great House Farm Dispossession (BP vs Buckler)\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V7yjb=yoks_hPoo7Lw2+WhTKqUNWYD=F5NG3c07FAPjhw@mail.gmail.com>\n\nLara,\n\nDiolch am eich arweiniad.\n\nRwyf wedi mireinio fy mhethos arfaethedig ymhellach er mwyn ehangu ei\ngwmpas. Mae'n cynnwys nawr alwadau am ymchwiliad i achosion hanesyddol\ntebyg lluosog yng Nghymru, gan gynnwys archwilio problemau systemig posibl\nfel twyll, gorthrymu tystiolaeth, neu anwybyddu/gweithio o amgylch\ngwarchodaethau amaethyddol a threftadaeth mewn anghytundebau meddiannu\nanghyfreithlon—gan o bosibl atal y cloc cyfyngu ar feddiannu anghyfreithlon\nyn anghyfreithlon neu yn anghyfansoddiadol—tra'n defnyddio Fferm Great\nHouse (BP v Buckler 1987) fel prif enghraifft.\n\nMae'r ffocws yn parhau i fod yn gadarn ar faterion datganoledig: polisi\namgylchedd hanesyddol Cymru, gwarchodaethau amaethyddol, rôl CADW, a\ndiwygiadau i fynd i'r afael ag unrhyw fylchau a nodir, heb geisio ailymweld\nâ dyfarniadau llys penodol.\n\nRwy'n cadarnhau bod gennyf gyfeiriad yng Nghymru: [redacted].\nRwy'n etholwr yn Nyffryn Morgannwg ac rwyf wedi ysgrifennu at yr AS\npresennol a'r Cynghorydd Carroll yn Llandochau; maent yn ymwybodol o'r\nmater a chredaf eu bod yn edrych i mewn iddo.\n\nOs caiff ei fframio fel hyn—gan bwysleisio \"dosbarth\" posibl o\nffermydd/teuluoedd Cymreig yr effeithir arnynt a methiannau polisi\nehangach—a ydych chi'n credu y byddai'n bodloni'r safonau'n ddigonol i\nennill sylw'r Pwyllgor a mynd ymlaen i gyflwyno/cyhoeddi?\n\nNid yw twyll byth yn rhy bell yn y gorffennol, gan nad yw'r cyfnod cyfyngu\nyn berthnasol mewn amgylchiadau o'r fath ac fe'i datodolir i gyd yn ôl y\ngyfraith. Mae hefyd yn gyfyngedig o'r dyddiad y cafodd ei ddarganfod—ac\nrwyf newydd ei ddarganfod—ac nid o ddyddiad y twyll honedig.\n\nEr gwaethaf hynny, rydym yn dal yn deulu Cymreig brodorol dadfeddiannol ar\nein tir geni hynafiaid. Rydym yn dal yma.\n\nOs na, pa gamau eraill y byddech chi'n eu hargymell (er enghraifft,\nymgysylltu ag Aelod o'r Senedd, adrannau Llywodraeth Cymru fel CADW, neu\nsianeli eraill ar gyfer codi materion systemig tir/amaethyddol)?\n\nByddai unrhyw gyngor ar gryfhau'r geiriad neu'r dystiolaeth ategol yn cael\nei werthfawrogi'n fawr cyn i mi gyflwyno.\n\nDiolch—mae'r mater hwn o bwysigrwydd sylweddol i gyfanrwydd diwylliannol,\namaethyddol a hanesyddol Cymru.\n\nCofion cynhes,\nSion Buckler\n[redacted]\n\n...................  [SAESNEG/ ENGLISH] .....................\n\nThanks for your guidance.\n\nI've further refined my proposed petition to broaden its scope,\nincorporating calls for an inquiry into multiple similar historical cases\nin Wales. This includes examining the possibility of systemic issues like\nfraud, suppression of evidence, or ignoring/working around agricultural and\nheritage protections in adverse possession disputes—potentially to\nunlawfully or unconstitutionally stop limitation clock on adverse\npossession—while using the Great House Farm (BP v Buckler 1987) as a key\nexample. The focus remains on devolved matters: Welsh historic environment\npolicy, agricultural safeguards, CADW's role, and reforms to address gaps\nwithout revisiting specific court judgments.\n\nAnd yes I have an address, it's [redacted]. I am a constituent\nin the Vale of Glamorgan and I have written to the current MP and\ncouncillor Carroll in Llandough, they are aware and I trust they are\nlooking into it.\n\nIf framed this way—emphasizing a possible \"class\" of affected Welsh\nfarms/families and broader policy failures—do you believe it would meet the\nstandards to gain the Committee's attention and proceed to\nsubmission/publication?\n\nFraud is never too far in the past as the statute of limitation doesn't\napply in such circumstances and furthermore it unravels all according to\nlaw. It is also only limited from the date it was discovered and I recently\ndiscovered, not from the date of occurrence of the alleged fraud.\n\nNotwithstanding we are still an dispossessed indigenous Welsh family on our\nancestral birthland. We are still here.\n\nFailing that, what alternative steps do you recommend (e.g., approaching a\nMember of the Senedd, Welsh Government departments like CADW, or other\nchannels for raising systemic land/agricultural issues)?\n\nAny advice on strengthening wording or evidence would be appreciated before\nsubmission.\n\nThank you—this matter holds significant importance for Welsh cultural,\nagricultural, and historical integrity.\n\nRegards,\n\nSion Buckler [redacted]\n\n>\n"},{"id":"wb-93ccf3d6","subject":"Consortium evidence BP v Buckler","name":"2026-02-18_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2026-02-18","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-18_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-evidence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-18_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: \"Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg\" <Glamro@cardiff.gov.uk>\nDate: Wed, 18 Feb 2026 14:51:11 +0000\nSubject: RE: Epexio contact us form submission\nMessage-ID: <LO6P265MB6345617D895E7594FD5CF0D2CC6AA@LO6P265MB6345.GBRP265.PROD.OUTLOOK.COM>\n\n2026/0672\n\nDear Requester.\n\nThank you for your email, received on 9 February, and for outlining within it your concerns, which we have noted.\n\nGlamorgan Archives holds only the records that we have been given.  We cannot make any assumptions as to whether the content of those records is accurate.  And we have a duty to preserve the records which are presented to us as they are presented to us.  In addition, Glamorgan Archives has an income target and, as such, we aren’t in a position to provide free copies of material in our care.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nRhian Diggins [cid:image002.jpg@01DCA0E6.05398450]\nUwch Archifydd / Senior Archivist\n\nArchifau Morgannwg / Glamorgan Archives\n029 2087 2299\nglamro@cardiff.gov.uk<mailto:glamro@cardiff.gov.uk>\nhttps://glamarchives.gov.uk/\n\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]>\nSent: 06 February 2026 17:51\nTo: Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg <Glamro@cardiff.gov.uk>\nSubject: Re: Epexio contact us form submission\n\nEXTERNAL: This email originated from outside Cardiff Council, take care when clicking links.\nALLANOL: Daw'r e-bost hwn o'r tu allan i Gyngor Caerdydd, cymerwch ofal wrth glicio ar ddolenni.\n\n\nMs Taylor,\n\nThanks for your reply and for outlining the options available to consult the estate plans referenced.\n\nI should clarify my position, as my correspondence is not a routine request for private research access, but part of a wider and serious concern regarding possible historical misdescription, suppression, and/or concealment of material facts relating to Great House Farm, Llandough.\n\nI am currently at the Welsh settlement in Argentina, having been displaced from my ancestral birthplace in Llandough. As such, it is not possible for me to attend the searchroom in person so easy as you're 7,500 miles away, nor to “pop in” to consult records on site. Requiring physical attendance or payment for access is therefore not a practical or equitable option in my circumstances.\n\nMore importantly, my concern is that certain archival descriptions, surveys, and planning-era records may themselves be inaccurate or incomplete as a result of earlier failures of disclosure. Specifically, a Roman burial reportedly discovered at Great House Farm in 1888 appears to have been treated as a “surprise” in later archaeological surveys (1978/1994), and an occupied, c.800-year-old domestic dwelling was repeatedly described in planning documentation as an “old barn” or “utility building.” These characterisations materially affected heritage protection, culminating in demolition in 1988.\n\nIn that context, I am not seeking to pay for copies of records whose accuracy is itself in question, nor to participate financially in processes that may inadvertently perpetuate errors arising from that period. My intention is to place relevant public bodies on notice of a potential issue of public record integrity and heritage governance, not to obtain documents for private interest. I have written to the Senedd calling for a public enquiry too. I've notified the land registry and serious fraud teams too.\n\nI am therefore asking that my correspondence be treated as a formal expression of concern regarding the reliability and completeness of the archival record as it relates to this site, and that this concern be noted accordingly. I would also be grateful if you could advise which department or officer is appropriate for addressing concerns of this nature, where the issue is not access but potential historical misrepresentation within public records.\n\nFor the avoidance of doubt, I am not alleging any impropriety on the part of current archive staff. My concern relates to historical processes and decisions, and to ensuring that those matters are properly examined rather than inadvertently reinforced.\n\nThank you for your time and consideration.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nSion Buckler\n\n________________________________\n\nOn Fri, 6 Feb 2026, 10:44 Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg, <Glamro@cardiff.gov.uk<mailto:Glamro@cardiff.gov.uk>> wrote:\nRef: 2026/0633\nDear Requester,\nThank you for your email received on 5th February 2026.\n\nYou would be welcome to visit us to consult the estate plans DB/E/1-2 in our public searchroom and take your own photographs for free. Our opening hours are Tues-Thurs 9.30am-12.30pm & 1.30pm-4.30pm. We are currently taking bookings from Tuesday 17th February 2026. Please note that our public searchroom is closed for the week Tues 10th February – Thurs 12th February so that staff can carry out essential work on the collections. We will re-open to the public on Tuesday 17th February 2026.\nIf you have not visited us before, you are required to register. We ask that you bring with you two forms of ID, one showing your name e.g. Driving Licence and the other your current address e.g. current utility bill or official letter.\nAlternatively, if you are unable to visit us in person, and if you can confirm exactly which pages you require, staff can provide digital copies, for which we charge £15.60 for the first image and £4.80 each for subsequent images, payable in advance. Prices include VAT.\nYou can find a detailed list on the catalogue: Survey of marquis of Bute’s Glamorgan estates, by David Stewart.<https://glamorgan.epexio.com/en/records/DB/E/1-8/1-2> If you would like a quote for digital copies, please let get in touch.\nWe hope this information is of assistance to you.\nYours sincerely,\nMelanie Taylor [cid:image003.jpg@01DCA0E6.05398450]\nCynorthwyydd Cofnodion / Records Assistant\nArchifau Morgannwg / Glamorgan Archives\nClos Parc Morgannwg<https://www.google.com/maps/search/Clos+Parc+Morgannwg+%0D%0A+Lecwydd+%2F+Leckwith+%0D%0A+Caerdydd+%2F+Cardiff+%0D%0A+CF11+8AW?entry=gmail&source=g>\nLecwydd / Leckwith<https://www.google.com/maps/search/Clos+Parc+Morgannwg+%0D%0A+Lecwydd+%2F+Leckwith+%0D%0A+Caerdydd+%2F+Cardiff+%0D%0A+CF11+8AW?entry=gmail&source=g>\nCaerdydd / Cardiff<https://www.google.com/maps/search/Clos+Parc+Morgannwg+%0D%0A+Lecwydd+%2F+Leckwith+%0D%0A+Caerdydd+%2F+Cardiff+%0D%0A+CF11+8AW?entry=gmail&source=g>\nCF11 8AW<https://www.google.com/maps/search/Clos+Parc+Morgannwg+%0D%0A+Lecwydd+%2F+Leckwith+%0D%0A+Caerdydd+%2F+Cardiff+%0D%0A+CF11+8AW?entry=gmail&source=g>\n02920872200\nglamro@cardiff.gov.uk<mailto:glamro@cardiff.gov.uk>\nwww.archifaumorgannwg.gov.uk<http://www.archifaumorgannwg.gov.uk/> / www.glamarchives.gov.uk<http://www.glamarchives.gov.uk/>\nwww.facebook.com/glamarchives<http://www.facebook.com/glamarchives>\nwww.twitter.com/glamarchives<http://www.twitter.com/glamarchives>\n\n**********************************************************************\n\nThe Council welcomes correspondence in Welsh, English or bilingually. We will ensure that we communicate with you in the language of your choice, as long as you let us know which you prefer. Corresponding in Welsh will not lead to delay.\n\nPrivileged/Confidential Information may be contained in this message. If you are not the addressee indicated in this message (or responsible for delivery of the message to such person), you may not copy or deliver this message to anyone. In such case, you should destroy this message and kindly notify the sender by reply email. Please advise immediately if you or your employer does not consent to Internet email for messages of this kind. Opinions, conclusions and other information in this message that do not relate to the official business of the Council of the City and County of Cardiff shall be understood as neither given nor endorsed by it. All e-mail sent to or from this address will be processed by Cardiff County Councils Corporate E-mail system and may be subject to scrutiny by someone other than the addressee.\n\n**********************************************************************\n\nMae’r Cyngor yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg, Saesneg neu’n ddwyieithog. Byddwn yn cyfathrebu â chi yn ôl eich dewis, dim ond i chi roi gwybod i ni pa un sydd well gennych. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi.\n\nMae'n bosibl bod gwybodaeth gyfrinachol yn y neges hon. Os na chyfeirir y neges atoch chi'n benodol (neu os nad ydych chi'n gyfrifol am drosglwyddo'r neges i'r person a enwir), yna ni chewch gopio na throsglwyddo'r neges. Mewn achos o'r fath, dylech ddinistrio'r neges a hysbysu'r anfonwr drwy e-bost ar unwaith. Rhowch wybod i'r anfonydd ar unwaith os nad ydych chi neu eich cyflogydd yn caniatau e-bost y Rhyngrwyd am negeseuon fel hon. Rhaid deall nad yw'r safbwyntiau, y casgliadau a'r wybodaeth arall yn y neges hon nad ydynt yn cyfeirio at fusnes swyddogol Cyngor Dinas a Sir Caerdydd yn cynrychioli barn y Cyngor Sir nad yn cael sel ei fendith. Caiff unrhyw negeseuon a anfonir at, neu o'r cyfeiriad e-bost hwn eu prosesu gan system E-bost Gorfforaethol Cyngor Sir Caerdydd a gallant gael eu harchwilio gan rywun heblaw'r person a enwir.\n\n**********************************************************************\n"},{"id":"wb-4413dee5","subject":"Consortium evidence BP v Buckler","name":"2026-02-18_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","date":"2026-02-18","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-18_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-evidence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-18_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Wed, 18 Feb 2026 13:52:33 -0300\nSubject: Re: Epexio contact us form submission\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V5fojuq7d2RpKyYdzkM+toTqT9-0rETJYQ3_ub0nFcNzw@mail.gmail.com>\n\nDear Ms Diggins,\n\nThank you for your reply and for clarifying Glamorgan Archives’ position\nregarding access and charges for copies of material. I appreciate the work\nyou do in preserving the records entrusted to your care.\n\nI would like to reiterate that my correspondence is not a routine request\nfor private research access. Rather, it is a formal expression of concern\nregarding the potential historical misdescription or suppression of facts\nrelating to Great House Farm, Llandough, including heritage and\narchaeological records. My concern is about the accuracy and completeness\nof the archival record itself, rather than the provision of copies or\npersonal research access.\n\nAs I am currently residing in Argentina, I am unable to attend in person,\nand my intention is not to request material for private use, but to\nhighlight potential issues with public record integrity. I would therefore\nbe grateful if you could advise which department, officer, or public body I\nshould contact to formally raise concerns regarding historical\nmisrepresentation within public records or archival descriptions, so that\nthese matters may be properly examined.\n\nFor the avoidance of doubt, I am not alleging any impropriety on the part\nof current archive staff. My concern relates solely to historical processes\nand record-keeping.\n\nThank you very much for your time and consideration, and I would be\ngrateful for any guidance you can provide.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\n\n*Requester*\n\nOn Wed, 18 Feb 2026 at 11:51, Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg <\nGlamro@cardiff.gov.uk> wrote:\n\n> 2026/0672\n>\n>\n>\n> Dear Requester.\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you for your email, received on 9 February, and for outlining within\n> it your concerns, which we have noted.\n>\n>\n>\n> Glamorgan Archives holds only the records that we have been given.  We\n> cannot make any assumptions as to whether the content of those records is\n> accurate.  And we have a duty to preserve the records which are presented\n> to us as they are presented to us.  In addition, Glamorgan Archives has an\n> income target and, as such, we aren’t in a position to provide free copies\n> of material in our care.\n>\n>\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n>\n>\n> Rhian Diggins\n>\n> Uwch Archifydd / Senior Archivist\n>\n>\n>\n> Archifau Morgannwg / Glamorgan Archives\n>\n> 029 2087 2299\n>\n> glamro@cardiff.gov.uk\n>\n> https://glamarchives.gov.uk/\n>\n>\n>\n> *From:* Requester <[redacted]>\n> *Sent:* 06 February 2026 17:51\n> *To:* Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg <Glamro@cardiff.gov.uk>\n> *Subject:* Re: Epexio contact us form submission\n>\n>\n>\n> *EXTERNAL:* This email originated from outside Cardiff Council, take care\n> when clicking links.\n> *ALLANOL:* Daw'r e-bost hwn o'r tu allan i Gyngor Caerdydd, cymerwch ofal\n> wrth glicio ar ddolenni.\n>\n>\n>\n> Ms Taylor,\n>\n> Thanks for your reply and for outlining the options available to consult\n> the estate plans referenced.\n>\n> I should clarify my position, as my correspondence is not a routine\n> request for private research access, but part of a wider and serious\n> concern regarding possible historical misdescription, suppression, and/or\n> concealment of material facts relating to Great House Farm, Llandough.\n>\n> I am currently at the Welsh settlement in Argentina, having been displaced\n> from my ancestral birthplace in Llandough. As such, it is not possible for\n> me to attend the searchroom in person so easy as you're 7,500 miles away,\n> nor to “pop in” to consult records on site. Requiring physical attendance\n> or payment for access is therefore not a practical or equitable option in\n> my circumstances.\n>\n> More importantly, my concern is that certain archival descriptions,\n> surveys, and planning-era records may themselves be inaccurate or\n> incomplete as a result of earlier failures of disclosure. Specifically, a\n> Roman burial reportedly discovered at Great House Farm in 1888 appears to\n> have been treated as a “surprise” in later archaeological surveys\n> (1978/1994), and an occupied, c.800-year-old domestic dwelling was\n> repeatedly described in planning documentation as an “old barn” or “utility\n> building.” These characterisations materially affected heritage protection,\n> culminating in demolition in 1988.\n>\n> In that context, I am not seeking to pay for copies of records whose\n> accuracy is itself in question, nor to participate financially in processes\n> that may inadvertently perpetuate errors arising from that period. My\n> intention is to place relevant public bodies on notice of a potential issue\n> of public record integrity and heritage governance, not to obtain documents\n> for private interest. I have written to the Senedd calling for a public\n> enquiry too. I've notified the land registry and serious fraud teams too.\n>\n> I am therefore asking that my correspondence be treated as a formal\n> expression of concern regarding the reliability and completeness of the\n> archival record as it relates to this site, and that this concern be noted\n> accordingly. I would also be grateful if you could advise which department\n> or officer is appropriate for addressing concerns of this nature, where the\n> issue is not access but potential historical misrepresentation within\n> public records.\n>\n> For the avoidance of doubt, I am not alleging any impropriety on the part\n> of current archive staff. My concern relates to historical processes and\n> decisions, and to ensuring that those matters are properly examined rather\n> than inadvertently reinforced.\n>\n> Thank you for your time and consideration.\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n> Requester\n> ------------------------------\n>\n>\n>\n> On Fri, 6 Feb 2026, 10:44 Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg, <\n> Glamro@cardiff.gov.uk> wrote:\n>\n> *Ref: 2026/0633*\n>\n> Dear Requester,\n>\n> Thank you for your email received on 5th February 2026.\n>\n> You would be welcome to visit us to consult the estate plans DB/E/1-2 in\n> our public searchroom and take your own photographs for free. Our opening\n> hours are Tues-Thurs 9.30am-12.30pm & 1.30pm-4.30pm. We are currently\n> taking bookings from *Tuesday 17th February 2026.* Please note that our\n> public searchroom is *closed* for the week Tues 10th February – Thurs\n> 12th February so that staff can carry out essential work on the\n> collections. We will re-open to the public on Tuesday 17th February 2026.\n>\n> If you have not visited us before, you are required to register. We ask\n> that you bring with you two forms of ID, one showing your name e.g. Driving\n> Licence and the other your current address e.g. current utility bill or\n> official letter.\n>\n> Alternatively, if you are unable to visit us in person, and if you can\n> confirm exactly which pages you require, staff can provide digital copies,\n> for which we charge £15.60 for the first image and £4.80 each for\n> subsequent images, payable in advance. Prices include VAT.\n>\n> You can find a detailed list on the catalogue: Survey of marquis of\n> Bute’s Glamorgan estates, by David Stewart.\n> <https://glamorgan.epexio.com/en/records/DB/E/1-8/1-2> If you would like\n> a quote for digital copies, please let get in touch.\n>\n> We hope this information is of assistance to you.\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n> Melanie Taylor\n>\n> Cynorthwyydd Cofnodion / Records Assistant\n>\n> Archifau Morgannwg / Glamorgan Archives\n>\n> Clos Parc Morgannwg\n> <https://www.google.com/maps/search/Clos+Parc+Morgannwg+%0D%0A+Lecwydd+%2F+Leckwith+%0D%0A+Caerdydd+%2F+Cardiff+%0D%0A+CF11+8AW?entry=gmail&source=g>\n>\n> Lecwydd / Leckwith\n> <https://www.google.com/maps/search/Clos+Parc+Morgannwg+%0D%0A+Lecwydd+%2F+Leckwith+%0D%0A+Caerdydd+%2F+Cardiff+%0D%0A+CF11+8AW?entry=gmail&source=g>\n>\n> Caerdydd / Cardiff\n> <https://www.google.com/maps/search/Clos+Parc+Morgannwg+%0D%0A+Lecwydd+%2F+Leckwith+%0D%0A+Caerdydd+%2F+Cardiff+%0D%0A+CF11+8AW?entry=gmail&source=g>\n>\n> CF11 8AW\n> <https://www.google.com/maps/search/Clos+Parc+Morgannwg+%0D%0A+Lecwydd+%2F+Leckwith+%0D%0A+Caerdydd+%2F+Cardiff+%0D%0A+CF11+8AW?entry=gmail&source=g>\n>\n> 02920872200\n>\n> glamro@cardiff.gov.uk\n>\n> www.archifaumorgannwg.gov.uk / www.glamarchives.gov.uk\n>\n> www.facebook.com/glamarchives\n>\n> www.twitter.com/glamarchives\n>\n> **********************************************************************\n>\n> The Council welcomes correspondence in Welsh, English or bilingually. We\n> will ensure that we communicate with you in the language of your choice, as\n> long as you let us know which you prefer. Corresponding in Welsh will not\n> lead to delay.\n>\n> Privileged/Confidential Information may be contained in this message. If\n> you are not the addressee indicated in this message (or responsible for\n> delivery of the message to such person), you may not copy or deliver this\n> message to anyone. In such case, you should destroy this message and kindly\n> notify the sender by reply email. Please advise immediately if you or your\n> employer does not consent to Internet email for messages of this kind.\n> Opinions, conclusions and other information in this message that do not\n> relate to the official business of the Council of the City and County of\n> Cardiff shall be understood as neither given nor endorsed by it. All e-mail\n> sent to or from this address will be processed by Cardiff County Councils\n> Corporate E-mail system and may be subject to scrutiny by someone other\n> than the addressee.\n>\n> **********************************************************************\n>\n> Mae’r Cyngor yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg, Saesneg neu’n ddwyieithog.\n> Byddwn yn cyfathrebu â chi yn ôl eich dewis, dim ond i chi roi gwybod i ni\n> pa un sydd well gennych. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi.\n>\n> Mae'n bosibl bod gwybodaeth gyfrinachol yn y neges hon. Os na chyfeirir y\n> neges atoch chi'n benodol (neu os nad ydych chi'n gyfrifol am drosglwyddo'r\n> neges i'r person a enwir), yna ni chewch gopio na throsglwyddo'r neges.\n> Mewn achos o'r fath, dylech ddinistrio'r neges a hysbysu'r anfonwr drwy\n> e-bost ar unwaith. Rhowch wybod i'r anfonydd ar unwaith os nad ydych chi\n> neu eich cyflogydd yn caniatau e-bost y Rhyngrwyd am negeseuon fel hon.\n> Rhaid deall nad yw'r safbwyntiau, y casgliadau a'r wybodaeth arall yn y\n> neges hon nad ydynt yn cyfeirio at fusnes swyddogol Cyngor Dinas a Sir\n> Caerdydd yn cynrychioli barn y Cyngor Sir nad yn cael sel ei fendith. Caiff\n> unrhyw negeseuon a anfonir at, neu o'r cyfeiriad e-bost hwn eu prosesu gan\n> system E-bost Gorfforaethol Cyngor Sir Caerdydd a gallant gael eu\n> harchwilio gan rywun heblaw'r person a enwir.\n>\n> **********************************************************************\n>\n>\n"},{"id":"wb-a388d5b9","subject":"Consortium FOI BP v Buckler","name":"2026-02-18_consortium-foi_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2026-02-18","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-18_consortium-foi_bpvsbuckler__en_v0-0-1.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-foi-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler","email","ghf"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["bpvsbuckler","email","ghf"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-18_consortium-foi_bpvsbuckler__en_v0-0-1.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: do-not-reply-to-this-address@whatdotheyknow.com\nDate: Wed, 18 Feb 2026 16:03:10 +0000\nSubject: New response to your FOI request - Freedom of Information Request – Administrative Records for BP Properties Ltd v Buckler (1974)\nMessage-ID: <6995e2be25643_14bddd870726db@mstrwdtkhexa.srv.mysociety.org.mail>\n\nYou have a new response to the Freedom of Information request 'Freedom of Information Request – Administrative Records for BP Properties Ltd v Buckler (1974)' that you made to HM Courts and Tribunals Service.\n\nTo view the response, click on the link below.\n\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/request/freedom_of_information_request_a_2133?nocache=incoming-3315104#incoming-3315104\n\nWhen you get there, please update the status to say if the response contains any useful information.\n\nAlthough all responses are automatically published, we depend on you, the original requester, to evaluate them.\n\n-- the WhatDoTheyKnow team\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nWhatDoTheyKnow is a project of mySociety run by a small team of staff and\ndedicated volunteers.\n\nmySociety is a charity that believes that information should be accessible to everyone.\nIf this is important to you, please consider donating to help us do more:\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/donate\n\nFind out more about how we handle your data at:\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/help/privacy\n\nmySociety is a registered charity in England and Wales (1076346), and a limited\ncompany (03277032)\n"},{"id":"wb-74357817","subject":"Consortium formal notice BP v Buckler","name":"2026-02-18_consortium-formal_notice_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2026-02-18","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-18_consortium-formal_notice_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-formal-notice-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-18_consortium-formal_notice_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Philip Gale <Philip.Gale@nationalarchives.gov.uk>\nDate: Wed, 18 Feb 2026 17:30:26 +0000\nSubject: Formal Notice for Record: Church View Close, Llandough / BP vs Buckler 1987\nMessage-ID: <CWLP265MB4931555D4979FD545C641AF6B06AA@CWLP265MB4931.GBRP265.PROD.OUTLOOK.COM>\n\nDear Mr Buckler,\n\nThank you for your e-mail of 10 February whose contents are noted. The National Archives does not hold any records relating to your enquiry. The Manorial Documents Register which this department administers is a register for the location of manorial documents and not of the ownership and descent of manorial titles:\n\nhttps://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/archives-sector/finding-records-in-discovery-and-other-databases/manorial-documents-register/\n\nA similar position holds for The National Archives responsibilities for public records and concerns about the past management of official records is a matter for the relevant government department such as HM Land Registry.\n\nYours sincerely\n\n\n[The National Archives logo]\nPhilip Gale | Head of the Collections and Cultural Property Team\nT: 020 3908 X 9193 | W: nationalarchives.gov.uk<http://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/>\nTwitter: @UkNatArchives\nThe National Archives, Kew, Richmond, Surrey TW9 4DU\n\n\n\n-----Original Message-----\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]<mailto:[redacted]>>\nSent: 10 February 2026 22:15\nTo: Archives Sector Leadership <ASL@nationalarchives.gov.uk<mailto:ASL@nationalarchives.gov.uk>>\nCc: reportafraud@landregistry.gov.uk<mailto:reportafraud@landregistry.gov.uk>; enquiries@college-of-arms.gov.uk<mailto:enquiries@college-of-arms.gov.uk>; Paul Egan <clerk@llandough-cc.co.uk<mailto:clerk@llandough-cc.co.uk>>; c1v@valeofglamorgan.gov.uk<mailto:c1v@valeofglamorgan.gov.uk>; ukgeneralenquiries@bp.com<mailto:ukgeneralenquiries@bp.com>\nSubject: Formal Notice for Record: Church View Close, Llandough / BP vs Buckler 1987\n\n        You don't often get email from [redacted]<mailto:[redacted]>. Learn why this is important <https://aka.ms/LearnAboutSenderIdentification>\n\n\nEXTERNAL: This email originates from outside of The National Archives.\n\nFORMAL NOTICE OF RECORD CHALLENGE, ASSERTION OF RIGHT, AND DEMAND FOR ENGAGEMENT\n\nDear Sir/Madam,\n\nThis Notice is issued for placement on official record and for reliance. It is not informal correspondence. I urge you not to resort to silence, deflection, or delay, as doing so following receipt of this notice constitutes knowing reliance upon, and maintenance of, records alleged to be materially defective through fraud, concealment, denial of due process, and systemic erasure. Please report any suspicious activity and discrepancies identified.\n\nThis Notice is issued in pursuit of narrative justice at this stage; formal judicial remedies are expressly reserved. It serves to document our family's side of the story before it is completely erased and history rewritten, ensuring awareness among custodians of land, heritage, heraldry, archaeology, and public records.\n\nMy elder brother, Rhys Buckler, and I are the sons of the late William Buckler (d. 1991), defendant in the landmark legal case BP Properties Ltd v Buckler (1987), and the grandchildren of the late Mary Williams, whose dispossession, marginalisation, and exclusion from judicial determination remain unresolved matters of record. Mary Williams was repeatedly and materially falsely addressed/mislabeled in legal and administrative records as \"Mrs Buckler,\" a misidentification that was never judicially tested and which directly affected the handling, scope, and outcome of proceedings. The Williams/Buckler family held claim to the land title, having acquired and occupied Great House Farm continuously since 1667, based on family records, historical evidence, and centuries of resistance to adverse claims.\n\nI place on record that I am the first member of my family able to speak publicly and formally on these matters. Other close family members remain too traumatised and intimidated by the historical conduct of these proceedings-including violent eviction, demolition, and loss of livelihood-to engage, despite the continuing consequences of dispossession. That silence must not be misread as consent, acquiescence, or abandonment of right. There is a material distinction between free silence and silence produced by fear, trauma, and coercive experience.\n\nThe story, as we have pieced it together, reveals a multi-generational pattern of lawfare and siege: from Norman/English conquests in 1100 granting lordships over ancient Welsh Christian landscapes, through monastic (1215-1539) and Crown seizures (1539 onward), to industrial encroachments (e.g., 1876 Limeworks severance without consent) and modern corporate possession actions. This culminated in the 1988 demolition of an 800-year-old farmhouse amid police standoffs, leaving the family homeless and the site scarred, now buried under Church View Close. Archaeological vindication came too late-1994 excavations revealed over 1,000 Roman and medieval burials, confirming family warnings (e.g., the 1870 Roman soldier discovery under the living room floor, suppressed by authorities)-yet heritage records celebrate the site while omitting our dispossession.\n\nThe following assertions of right are now formally placed on record. They have never been lawfully adjudicated or extinguished:\n\n1.      Rhys Buckler may be the rightful Manorial Lord of the Manor of Llandough, by right of primogeniture and continuous hereditary connection arising from our family's occupation of Great House Farm from 1667 until our narrative, legal, and physical dispossession.\n2.      I assert a claim to act as Squire of the Manor of Llandough, as executive representative and guardian of the Buckler-Williams estate and matriarchal lineage, with a mandate to pursue correction of record, restitution, and restorative remedies.\n\nThese are assertions of right, not settled declarations, placed on record pending proper adjudication-which has been historically obstructed through procedural narrowing, missing documents, and avoidance of full hearings.\n\nIn 1974, Mary Williams was twice prevented from presenting her case and obtaining a judicial determination of superior ownership, despite claims grounded in long occupation, intent, and possession. The court confined inquiry to animus possidendi (intention to possess) and excluded animus domini (intention to own), reclassifying her from putative owner to mere occupier and foreclosing title adjudication. BP sought possession only, not ownership rulings-a choice unexplained and significant. Proceedings were adjourned or quashed without resolution.\n\nThis occurred despite evidence of ownership intent, including:\n\n*       Centuries-long occupation and resistance (e.g., failed 1938 auction due to local support; post-1945 rent disputes).\n*       Roman-era remains and historical significance (e.g., Guglielmo Marconi's 1897 residence during radio experiments, with family aid erased from records).\n*       Unformalized deals like the 1895-1905 Daniel Thomas quarrying permission for promised title, undermined by vanished deeds.\n\nJudicial ownership examination would have engaged archaeological/heritage protections, incompatible with redevelopment-consequences avoided, raising credibility concerns.\n\nSerious unresolved anomalies persist, indicating something is still not right with the record:\n\n*       Licence correspondence dated 31 October 1974 surfacing only on 4 November, potentially interrupting proceedings to prevent hearings.\n*       Claims of 1974 benevolent withdrawal under press scrutiny unsupported by verifiable coverage.\n*       Land registration before Mary Williams's 1983 death without title determination; disputes possibly lodged/suppressed by Land Registry.\n*       Inconsistent treatment of BP entities across proceedings to family detriment.\n*       Procedural barriers preventing effective defense (e.g., interrupted essential services post-licence; 1960-1987 court rulings dismissing ancestral title as \"notion\" without evidence).\n*       Knowing bypass of archaeological constraints (e.g., 1978 trenches ignored; no contemporaneous 1970s/1990s press on discoveries amid development).\n*       Missing key documents (e.g., 1667 conveyance, 1811-1835 Lambert Williams agreements proving status, 1982-84 library deeds vanishing).\n*       Risks of further erasure: Heritage databases celebrate \"Llandough Roman villa cemetery\" while omitting our saga; modern residents unaware of the \"battleground\" site.\n\nThese anomalies suggest systemic theft, from colonial English subjugation (e.g., 1543-1770 orbit shifts) to corporate siege, during Wales' cultural/economic transitions. Authority derived through such means is illegitimate under natural justice, customary Welsh principles, and moral-legal frameworks. The record's distortion secures advantage, but passage of time does not validate it.\n\nPreliminary assessment indicates cumulative benefits from redevelopment (absent title extinguishment) exceed £100 million, forming a basis for reparations-covering lost land, artifacts, livelihood, and trauma.\n\nI require relevant public servants, custodians, and office-holders (including Land Registry, heraldry bodies, museums, archaeologists, MPs, historians, and councillors) to cease delay, engage substantively, review/correct/annotate records where defective, and prevent further rewriting of history. Justice delayed is denied, especially with verifiable evidence available.\n\nThis Notice:\n\n*       Challenges the record's accuracy, completeness, and legitimacy.\n*       Place assertions of title/standing on record pending adjudication.\n*       Reserves all rights without prejudice.\n*       Puts officials/beneficiaries on notice that silence/reliance may aggravate responsibility.\n\nFor the avoidance of doubt, His Majesty the King was notified yesterday.\n\nThis serves as an invitation to engage constructively before reliance becomes untenable.\n\nRegards, Requester On behalf of the Buckler-Williams family [redacted], Wales. https://bpvsbuckler.datro.xyz/ <https://bpvsbuckler.datro.xyz/>\n\nPostscript - Disability and Succession (Material Notice): The heir-apparent to the asserted Manorial Lordship is Rhys Buckler's son, a young man with Down syndrome. Any obstruction, marginalisation, or differential treatment affecting his inheritance would engage the Equality Act 2010 and UK international obligations on disability rights. Disability cannot bar succession or recognition. This is recorded to prevent claims of ignorance.\nPlease don't print this e-mail unless you really need to. ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- National Archives Disclaimer This email and any files transmitted with it are intended solely for the use of the individual(s) to whom they are addressed. If you are not the intended recipient and have received this email in error, please notify the sender and delete the email. Opinions, conclusions and other information in this message and attachments that do not relate to the official business of The National Archives are neither given nor endorsed by it. ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n"},{"id":"wb-09f8ccf6","subject":"Consortium formal notice BP v Buckler","name":"2026-02-18_consortium-formal_notice_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","date":"2026-02-18","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-18_consortium-formal_notice_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-formal-notice-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-18_consortium-formal_notice_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Wed, 18 Feb 2026 14:49:54 -0300\nSubject: Re: Formal Notice for Record: Church View Close, Llandough / BP vs Buckler 1987\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V5Kv0e0YdiGKFy38ZArAjnzjAmnKy__RPeQ3eeR5eMjHw@mail.gmail.com>\n\nDear Mr Gale,\n\nThank you for your reply of 12 February. I would like to clarify and narrow\nmy request to ensure there is no misunderstanding regarding the records I\nam trying to locate.\n\nMy correspondence is *not* seeking an adjudication of ownership or title —\nI fully accept the stated remit boundaries of the Manorial Documents\nRegister (MDR). Rather, my enquiry concerns the existence of *any archival\nreference, index card, catalogue entry, or pointer to a document\nhistorically known as the “Williams Title”*, which was, according to\ncontemporaneous family testimony, at one point:\n\n   1.\n\n   Held in the *local public library* in the first half of the twentieth\n   century; and\n   2.\n\n   Indexed in some manner (e.g., an index card or catalogue reference) that\n   persisted even after the original document could not be located.\n\nMy understanding of the MDR — as described on your own website — is that it\nexists to provide brief descriptions of manorial records and details of\ntheir locations:\n\n“An official index of English and Welsh manorial records providing brief\ndescriptions of documents and details of their locations… It does *not*\ninclude title deeds or conveyances, and is *not* a register of ownership of\nmanorial lordships or rights.”\nhttps://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/manor-search\n<https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/manor-search?utm_source=chatgpt.com>\n\nBased on that description, I would be grateful if you could confirm whether:\n\n   1.\n\n   *Any catalogue entries, index cards, reference pointers, or archival\n   descriptions* relating to the *Manor of Llandough* or the *Williams\n   family title* exist in the MDR or related archival catalogues.\n   2.\n\n   If so, the *reference identifiers* and information on how these can be\n   consulted in person or via digitised records.\n\nTo be precise, I am looking for historical references to the *index card or\nlisting* for the Williams Title — whether held in a library, local record\noffice, or other institutional archive — rather than a claim for ownership\njudgment itself.\n\nIf such a reference does not currently appear in the MDR, I would greatly\nappreciate guidance on where any known catalogue cards or entry lists for\nmanorial documents from *Glamorgan / Llandough* prior to 1950 might now\nreside (e.g., regional archives, National Archives holdings, or affiliated\nlocal repository catalogues).\n\nAnyway I anticipate your forgiveness, if I have erred in writing to you\nearlier.\n\nThank you again and in advance for your assistance.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\n\nSion Buckler\nOn Wed, 18 Feb 2026 at 14:30, Philip Gale <\nPhilip.Gale@nationalarchives.gov.uk> wrote:\n\n> Dear Requester,\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you for your e-mail of 10 February whose contents are noted. The\n> National Archives does not hold any records relating to your enquiry. The\n> Manorial Documents Register which this department administers is a register\n> for the location of manorial documents and not of the ownership and descent\n> of manorial titles:\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/archives-sector/finding-records-in-discovery-and-other-databases/manorial-documents-register/\n>\n>\n>\n> A similar position holds for The National Archives responsibilities for\n> public records and concerns about the past management of official records\n> is a matter for the relevant government department such as HM Land\n> Registry.\n>\n>\n>\n> Yours sincerely\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> [image: The National Archives logo]\n>\n> *Philip Gale *|* Head of the Collections and Cultural Property Team*\n> T: 020 3908 X 9193 | W: nationalarchives.gov.uk\n> <http://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/>\n> Twitter: @UkNatArchives\n> The National Archives, Kew, Richmond, Surrey TW9 4DU\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> -----Original Message-----\n>\n> From: Requester <[redacted]>\n>\n> Sent: 10 February 2026 22:15\n>\n> To: Archives Sector Leadership <ASL@nationalarchives.gov.uk>\n>\n> Cc: reportafraud@landregistry.gov.uk; enquiries@college-of-arms.gov.uk;\n> Paul Egan <clerk@llandough-cc.co.uk>; c1v@valeofglamorgan.gov.uk;\n> ukgeneralenquiries@bp.com\n>\n> Subject: Formal Notice for Record: Church View Close, Llandough / BP vs\n> Buckler 1987\n>\n>\n>\n>         You don't often get email from [redacted]. Learn\n> why this is important <https://aka.ms/LearnAboutSenderIdentification>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> EXTERNAL: This email originates from outside of The National Archives.\n>\n>\n>\n> FORMAL NOTICE OF RECORD CHALLENGE, ASSERTION OF RIGHT, AND DEMAND FOR\n> ENGAGEMENT\n>\n>\n>\n> Dear Sir/Madam,\n>\n>\n>\n> This Notice is issued for placement on official record and for reliance.\n> It is not informal correspondence. I urge you not to resort to silence,\n> deflection, or delay, as doing so following receipt of this notice\n> constitutes knowing reliance upon, and maintenance of, records alleged to\n> be materially defective through fraud, concealment, denial of due process,\n> and systemic erasure. Please report any suspicious activity and\n> discrepancies identified.\n>\n>\n>\n> This Notice is issued in pursuit of narrative justice at this stage;\n> formal judicial remedies are expressly reserved. It serves to document our\n> family's side of the story before it is completely erased and history\n> rewritten, ensuring awareness among custodians of land, heritage, heraldry,\n> archaeology, and public records.\n>\n>\n>\n> My elder brother, Rhys Buckler, and I are the sons of the late William\n> Buckler (d. 1991), defendant in the landmark legal case BP Properties Ltd v\n> Buckler (1987), and the grandchildren of the late Mary Williams, whose\n> dispossession, marginalisation, and exclusion from judicial determination\n> remain unresolved matters of record. Mary Williams was repeatedly and\n> materially falsely addressed/mislabeled in legal and administrative records\n> as “Mrs Buckler,” a misidentification that was never judicially tested and\n> which directly affected the handling, scope, and outcome of proceedings.\n> The Williams/Buckler family held claim to the land title, having acquired\n> and occupied Great House Farm continuously since 1667, based on family\n> records, historical evidence, and centuries of resistance to adverse claims.\n>\n>\n>\n> I place on record that I am the first member of my family able to speak\n> publicly and formally on these matters. Other close family members remain\n> too traumatised and intimidated by the historical conduct of these\n> proceedings—including violent eviction, demolition, and loss of\n> livelihood—to engage, despite the continuing consequences of dispossession.\n> That silence must not be misread as consent, acquiescence, or abandonment\n> of right. There is a material distinction between free silence and silence\n> produced by fear, trauma, and coercive experience.\n>\n>\n>\n> The story, as we have pieced it together, reveals a multi-generational\n> pattern of lawfare and siege: from Norman/English conquests in 1100\n> granting lordships over ancient Welsh Christian landscapes, through\n> monastic (1215–1539) and Crown seizures (1539 onward), to industrial\n> encroachments (e.g., 1876 Limeworks severance without consent) and modern\n> corporate possession actions. This culminated in the 1988 demolition of an\n> 800-year-old farmhouse amid police standoffs, leaving the family homeless\n> and the site scarred, now buried under Church View Close. Archaeological\n> vindication came too late—1994 excavations revealed over 1,000 Roman and\n> medieval burials, confirming family warnings (e.g., the 1870 Roman soldier\n> discovery under the living room floor, suppressed by authorities)—yet\n> heritage records celebrate the site while omitting our dispossession.\n>\n>\n>\n> The following assertions of right are now formally placed on record. They\n> have never been lawfully adjudicated or extinguished:\n>\n>\n>\n> 1.      Rhys Buckler may be the rightful Manorial Lord of the Manor of\n> Llandough, by right of primogeniture and continuous hereditary connection\n> arising from our family’s occupation of Great House Farm from 1667 until\n> our narrative, legal, and physical dispossession.\n>\n> 2.      I assert a claim to act as Squire of the Manor of Llandough, as\n> executive representative and guardian of the Buckler–Williams estate and\n> matriarchal lineage, with a mandate to pursue correction of record,\n> restitution, and restorative remedies.\n>\n>\n>\n> These are assertions of right, not settled declarations, placed on record\n> pending proper adjudication—which has been historically obstructed through\n> procedural narrowing, missing documents, and avoidance of full hearings.\n>\n>\n>\n> In 1974, Mary Williams was twice prevented from presenting her case and\n> obtaining a judicial determination of superior ownership, despite claims\n> grounded in long occupation, intent, and possession. The court confined\n> inquiry to animus possidendi (intention to possess) and excluded animus\n> domini (intention to own), reclassifying her from putative owner to mere\n> occupier and foreclosing title adjudication. BP sought possession only, not\n> ownership rulings—a choice unexplained and significant. Proceedings were\n> adjourned or quashed without resolution.\n>\n>\n>\n> This occurred despite evidence of ownership intent, including:\n>\n>\n>\n> *       Centuries-long occupation and resistance (e.g., failed 1938\n> auction due to local support; post-1945 rent disputes).\n>\n> *       Roman-era remains and historical significance (e.g., Guglielmo\n> Marconi's 1897 residence during radio experiments, with family aid erased\n> from records).\n>\n> *       Unformalized deals like the 1895–1905 Daniel Thomas quarrying\n> permission for promised title, undermined by vanished deeds.\n>\n>\n>\n> Judicial ownership examination would have engaged archaeological/heritage\n> protections, incompatible with redevelopment—consequences avoided, raising\n> credibility concerns.\n>\n>\n>\n> Serious unresolved anomalies persist, indicating something is still not\n> right with the record:\n>\n>\n>\n> *       Licence correspondence dated 31 October 1974 surfacing only on 4\n> November, potentially interrupting proceedings to prevent hearings.\n>\n> *       Claims of 1974 benevolent withdrawal under press scrutiny\n> unsupported by verifiable coverage.\n>\n> *       Land registration before Mary Williams’s 1983 death without title\n> determination; disputes possibly lodged/suppressed by Land Registry.\n>\n> *       Inconsistent treatment of BP entities across proceedings to family\n> detriment.\n>\n> *       Procedural barriers preventing effective defense (e.g.,\n> interrupted essential services post-licence; 1960–1987 court rulings\n> dismissing ancestral title as \"notion\" without evidence).\n>\n> *       Knowing bypass of archaeological constraints (e.g., 1978 trenches\n> ignored; no contemporaneous 1970s/1990s press on discoveries amid\n> development).\n>\n> *       Missing key documents (e.g., 1667 conveyance, 1811–1835 Lambert\n> Williams agreements proving status, 1982–84 library deeds vanishing).\n>\n> *       Risks of further erasure: Heritage databases celebrate \"Llandough\n> Roman villa cemetery\" while omitting our saga; modern residents unaware of\n> the \"battleground\" site.\n>\n>\n>\n> These anomalies suggest systemic theft, from colonial English subjugation\n> (e.g., 1543–1770 orbit shifts) to corporate siege, during Wales'\n> cultural/economic transitions. Authority derived through such means is\n> illegitimate under natural justice, customary Welsh principles, and\n> moral-legal frameworks. The record's distortion secures advantage, but\n> passage of time does not validate it.\n>\n>\n>\n> Preliminary assessment indicates cumulative benefits from redevelopment\n> (absent title extinguishment) exceed £100 million, forming a basis for\n> reparations—covering lost land, artifacts, livelihood, and trauma.\n>\n>\n>\n> I require relevant public servants, custodians, and office-holders\n> (including Land Registry, heraldry bodies, museums, archaeologists, MPs,\n> historians, and councillors) to cease delay, engage substantively,\n> review/correct/annotate records where defective, and prevent further\n> rewriting of history. Justice delayed is denied, especially with verifiable\n> evidence available.\n>\n>\n>\n> This Notice:\n>\n>\n>\n> *       Challenges the record's accuracy, completeness, and legitimacy.\n>\n> *       Place assertions of title/standing on record pending adjudication.\n>\n> *       Reserves all rights without prejudice.\n>\n> *       Puts officials/beneficiaries on notice that silence/reliance may\n> aggravate responsibility.\n>\n>\n>\n> For the avoidance of doubt, His Majesty the King was notified yesterday.\n>\n>\n>\n> This serves as an invitation to engage constructively before reliance\n> becomes untenable.\n>\n>\n>\n> Regards, Requester On behalf of the Buckler–Williams family [redacted],\n> Dinas Powys, Vale of Glamorgan, Wales. https://bpvsbuckler.datro.xyz/ <\n> https://bpvsbuckler.datro.xyz/>\n>\n>\n>\n> Postscript — Disability and Succession (Material Notice): The\n> heir-apparent to the asserted Manorial Lordship is Rhys Buckler’s son, a\n> young man with Down syndrome. Any obstruction, marginalisation, or\n> differential treatment affecting his inheritance would engage the Equality\n> Act 2010 and UK international obligations on disability rights. Disability\n> cannot bar succession or recognition. This is recorded to prevent claims of\n> ignorance.\n> Please don't print this e-mail unless you really need to.\n> -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n> National Archives Disclaimer This email and any files transmitted with it\n> are intended solely for the use of the individual(s) to whom they are\n> addressed. If you are not the intended recipient and have received this\n> email in error, please notify the sender and delete the email. Opinions,\n> conclusions and other information in this message and attachments that do\n> not relate to the official business of The National Archives are neither\n> given nor endorsed by it.\n> ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n>\n>\n"},{"id":"wb-8a119093","subject":"Consortium evidence BP v Buckler","name":"2026-02-19_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2026-02-19","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-19_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-evidence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-19_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: \"Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg\" <Glamro@cardiff.gov.uk>\nDate: Thu, 19 Feb 2026 09:05:23 +0000\nSubject: RE: Epexio contact us form submission\nMessage-ID: <LO6P265MB6345B6F097F96613BD8BA6B1CC6BA@LO6P265MB6345.GBRP265.PROD.OUTLOOK.COM>\n\nDear Requester.\n\nThank you for your email.\n\nCan I suggest that you contact the National Archives<https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/>?  They hold responsibility for public records in England and Wales and may be best placed to advise you.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nRhian Diggins [cid:image001.jpg@01DCA17E.E0B5BBF0]\nUwch Archifydd / Senior Archivist\n\nArchifau Morgannwg / Glamorgan Archives\n029 2087 2299\nglamro@cardiff.gov.uk<mailto:glamro@cardiff.gov.uk>\nhttps://glamarchives.gov.uk/\n\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]>\nSent: 18 February 2026 16:53\nTo: Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg <Glamro@cardiff.gov.uk>\nSubject: Re: Epexio contact us form submission\n\nEXTERNAL: This email originated from outside Cardiff Council, take care when clicking links.\nALLANOL: Daw'r e-bost hwn o'r tu allan i Gyngor Caerdydd, cymerwch ofal wrth glicio ar ddolenni.\n\n\nDear Ms Diggins,\n\nThank you for your reply and for clarifying Glamorgan Archives’ position regarding access and charges for copies of material. I appreciate the work you do in preserving the records entrusted to your care.\n\nI would like to reiterate that my correspondence is not a routine request for private research access. Rather, it is a formal expression of concern regarding the potential historical misdescription or suppression of facts relating to Great House Farm, Llandough, including heritage and archaeological records. My concern is about the accuracy and completeness of the archival record itself, rather than the provision of copies or personal research access.\n\nAs I am currently residing in Argentina, I am unable to attend in person, and my intention is not to request material for private use, but to highlight potential issues with public record integrity. I would therefore be grateful if you could advise which department, officer, or public body I should contact to formally raise concerns regarding historical misrepresentation within public records or archival descriptions, so that these matters may be properly examined.\n\nFor the avoidance of doubt, I am not alleging any impropriety on the part of current archive staff. My concern relates solely to historical processes and record-keeping.\n\nThank you very much for your time and consideration, and I would be grateful for any guidance you can provide.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nSion Buckler\n\nOn Wed, 18 Feb 2026 at 11:51, Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg <Glamro@cardiff.gov.uk<mailto:Glamro@cardiff.gov.uk>> wrote:\n2026/0672\nDear Requester.\nThank you for your email, received on 9 February, and for outlining within it your concerns, which we have noted.\nGlamorgan Archives holds only the records that we have been given. We cannot make any assumptions as to whether the content of those records is accurate. And we have a duty to preserve the records which are presented to us as they are presented to us. In addition, Glamorgan Archives has an income target and, as such, we aren’t in a position to provide free copies of material in our care.\nYours sincerely,\nRhian Diggins [cid:image001.jpg@01DCA17E.E0B5BBF0]\nUwch Archifydd / Senior Archivist\nArchifau Morgannwg / Glamorgan Archives\n029 2087 2299\nglamro@cardiff.gov.uk<mailto:glamro@cardiff.gov.uk>\nhttps://glamarchives.gov.uk/\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]<mailto:[redacted]>>\nSent: 06 February 2026 17:51\nTo: Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg <Glamro@cardiff.gov.uk<mailto:Glamro@cardiff.gov.uk>>\nSubject: Re: Epexio contact us form submission\nEXTERNAL: This email originated from outside Cardiff Council, take care when clicking links.\nALLANOL: Daw'r e-bost hwn o'r tu allan i Gyngor Caerdydd, cymerwch ofal wrth glicio ar ddolenni.\n\nMs Taylor,\n\nThanks for your reply and for outlining the options available to consult the estate plans referenced.\n\nI should clarify my position, as my correspondence is not a routine request for private research access, but part of a wider and serious concern regarding possible historical misdescription, suppression, and/or concealment of material facts relating to Great House Farm, Llandough.\n\nI am currently at the Welsh settlement in Argentina, having been displaced from my ancestral birthplace in Llandough. As such, it is not possible for me to attend the searchroom in person so easy as you're 7,500 miles away, nor to “pop in” to consult records on site. Requiring physical attendance or payment for access is therefore not a practical or equitable option in my circumstances.\n\nMore importantly, my concern is that certain archival descriptions, surveys, and planning-era records may themselves be inaccurate or incomplete as a result of earlier failures of disclosure. Specifically, a Roman burial reportedly discovered at Great House Farm in 1888 appears to have been treated as a “surprise” in later archaeological surveys (1978/1994), and an occupied, c.800-year-old domestic dwelling was repeatedly described in planning documentation as an “old barn” or “utility building.” These characterisations materially affected heritage protection, culminating in demolition in 1988.\n\nIn that context, I am not seeking to pay for copies of records whose accuracy is itself in question, nor to participate financially in processes that may inadvertently perpetuate errors arising from that period. My intention is to place relevant public bodies on notice of a potential issue of public record integrity and heritage governance, not to obtain documents for private interest. I have written to the Senedd calling for a public enquiry too. I've notified the land registry and serious fraud teams too.\n\nI am therefore asking that my correspondence be treated as a formal expression of concern regarding the reliability and completeness of the archival record as it relates to this site, and that this concern be noted accordingly. I would also be grateful if you could advise which department or officer is appropriate for addressing concerns of this nature, where the issue is not access but potential historical misrepresentation within public records.\n\nFor the avoidance of doubt, I am not alleging any impropriety on the part of current archive staff. My concern relates to historical processes and decisions, and to ensuring that those matters are properly examined rather than inadvertently reinforced.\n\nThank you for your time and consideration.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nSion Buckler\n\n________________________________\nOn Fri, 6 Feb 2026, 10:44 Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg, <Glamro@cardiff.gov.uk<mailto:Glamro@cardiff.gov.uk>> wrote:\nRef: 2026/0633\nDear Requester,\nThank you for your email received on 5th February 2026.\n\nYou would be welcome to visit us to consult the estate plans DB/E/1-2 in our public searchroom and take your own photographs for free. Our opening hours are Tues-Thurs 9.30am-12.30pm & 1.30pm-4.30pm. We are currently taking bookings from Tuesday 17th February 2026. Please note that our public searchroom is closed for the week Tues 10th February – Thurs 12th February so that staff can carry out essential work on the collections. We will re-open to the public on Tuesday 17th February 2026.\nIf you have not visited us before, you are required to register. We ask that you bring with you two forms of ID, one showing your name e.g. Driving Licence and the other your current address e.g. current utility bill or official letter.\nAlternatively, if you are unable to visit us in person, and if you can confirm exactly which pages you require, staff can provide digital copies, for which we charge £15.60 for the first image and £4.80 each for subsequent images, payable in advance. Prices include VAT.\nYou can find a detailed list on the catalogue: Survey of marquis of Bute’s Glamorgan estates, by David Stewart.<https://glamorgan.epexio.com/en/records/DB/E/1-8/1-2> If you would like a quote for digital copies, please let get in touch.\nWe hope this information is of assistance to you.\nYours sincerely,\nMelanie Taylor [cid:image002.jpg@01DCA17E.E0B5BBF0]\nCynorthwyydd Cofnodion / Records Assistant\nArchifau Morgannwg / Glamorgan Archives\nClos Parc Morgannwg<https://www.google.com/maps/search/Clos+Parc+Morgannwg+%0D%0A+Lecwydd+%2F+Leckwith+%0D%0A+Caerdydd+%2F+Cardiff+%0D%0A+CF11+8AW?entry=gmail&source=g>\nLecwydd / Leckwith<https://www.google.com/maps/search/Clos+Parc+Morgannwg+%0D%0A+Lecwydd+%2F+Leckwith+%0D%0A+Caerdydd+%2F+Cardiff+%0D%0A+CF11+8AW?entry=gmail&source=g>\nCaerdydd / Cardiff<https://www.google.com/maps/search/Clos+Parc+Morgannwg+%0D%0A+Lecwydd+%2F+Leckwith+%0D%0A+Caerdydd+%2F+Cardiff+%0D%0A+CF11+8AW?entry=gmail&source=g>\nCF11 8AW<https://www.google.com/maps/search/Clos+Parc+Morgannwg+%0D%0A+Lecwydd+%2F+Leckwith+%0D%0A+Caerdydd+%2F+Cardiff+%0D%0A+CF11+8AW?entry=gmail&source=g>\n02920872200\nglamro@cardiff.gov.uk<mailto:glamro@cardiff.gov.uk>\nwww.archifaumorgannwg.gov.uk<http://www.archifaumorgannwg.gov.uk/> / www.glamarchives.gov.uk<http://www.glamarchives.gov.uk/>\nwww.facebook.com/glamarchives<http://www.facebook.com/glamarchives>\nwww.twitter.com/glamarchives<http://www.twitter.com/glamarchives>\n\n**********************************************************************\n\nThe Council welcomes correspondence in Welsh, English or bilingually. We will ensure that we communicate with you in the language of your choice, as long as you let us know which you prefer. Corresponding in Welsh will not lead to delay.\n\nPrivileged/Confidential Information may be contained in this message. If you are not the addressee indicated in this message (or responsible for delivery of the message to such person), you may not copy or deliver this message to anyone. In such case, you should destroy this message and kindly notify the sender by reply email. Please advise immediately if you or your employer does not consent to Internet email for messages of this kind. Opinions, conclusions and other information in this message that do not relate to the official business of the Council of the City and County of Cardiff shall be understood as neither given nor endorsed by it. All e-mail sent to or from this address will be processed by Cardiff County Councils Corporate E-mail system and may be subject to scrutiny by someone other than the addressee.\n\n**********************************************************************\n\nMae’r Cyngor yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg, Saesneg neu’n ddwyieithog. Byddwn yn cyfathrebu â chi yn ôl eich dewis, dim ond i chi roi gwybod i ni pa un sydd well gennych. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi.\n\nMae'n bosibl bod gwybodaeth gyfrinachol yn y neges hon. Os na chyfeirir y neges atoch chi'n benodol (neu os nad ydych chi'n gyfrifol am drosglwyddo'r neges i'r person a enwir), yna ni chewch gopio na throsglwyddo'r neges. Mewn achos o'r fath, dylech ddinistrio'r neges a hysbysu'r anfonwr drwy e-bost ar unwaith. Rhowch wybod i'r anfonydd ar unwaith os nad ydych chi neu eich cyflogydd yn caniatau e-bost y Rhyngrwyd am negeseuon fel hon. Rhaid deall nad yw'r safbwyntiau, y casgliadau a'r wybodaeth arall yn y neges hon nad ydynt yn cyfeirio at fusnes swyddogol Cyngor Dinas a Sir Caerdydd yn cynrychioli barn y Cyngor Sir nad yn cael sel ei fendith. Caiff unrhyw negeseuon a anfonir at, neu o'r cyfeiriad e-bost hwn eu prosesu gan system E-bost Gorfforaethol Cyngor Sir Caerdydd a gallant gael eu harchwilio gan rywun heblaw'r person a enwir.\n\n**********************************************************************\n"},{"id":"wb-22a8d59a","subject":"Consortium evidence BP v Buckler","name":"2026-02-19_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","date":"2026-02-19","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-19_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-evidence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-19_consortium-evidence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Thu, 19 Feb 2026 07:34:01 -0300\nSubject: Re: Epexio contact us form submission\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V5f6jO418vYa5NBzxyoRPZxsi9foaMyx5iChUQpzYcANw@mail.gmail.com>\n\nDear Ms Diggins,\n\nI would be grateful for clarification on a specific point.\n\nIt has long been understood within my family that an index card existed\nreferring to the title deeds of Great House Farm, Llandough. While the\ndeeds themselves reportedly went missing, the index reference was said to\nremain at one time.\n\nMy question is simply this:\n\nIf an index card or catalogue entry once existed within Glamorgan Archives\n(or its predecessor systems), would a record of that entry survive\nfollowing digitisation, even if the underlying document was withdrawn,\ntransferred, or missing?\n\nIn other words, are superseded or removed catalogue references retained\nwithin archival metadata systems?\n\nI am not alleging wrongdoing. I am trying to establish whether any trace of\nsuch an index entry would ordinarily remain within your records management\nframework.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\n\nSion Buckler\n\n\nOn Thu, 19 Feb 2026 at 06:05, Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg <\nGlamro@cardiff.gov.uk> wrote:\n\n> Dear Requester.\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you for your email.\n>\n>\n>\n> Can I suggest that you contact the National Archives\n> <https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/>?  They hold responsibility for\n> public records in England and Wales and may be best placed to advise you.\n>\n>\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n>\n>\n> Rhian Diggins\n>\n> Uwch Archifydd / Senior Archivist\n>\n>\n>\n> Archifau Morgannwg / Glamorgan Archives\n>\n> 029 2087 2299\n>\n> glamro@cardiff.gov.uk\n>\n> https://glamarchives.gov.uk/\n>\n>\n>\n> *From:* Requester <[redacted]>\n> *Sent:* 18 February 2026 16:53\n> *To:* Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg <Glamro@cardiff.gov.uk>\n> *Subject:* Re: Epexio contact us form submission\n>\n>\n>\n> *EXTERNAL:* This email originated from outside Cardiff Council, take care\n> when clicking links.\n> *ALLANOL:* Daw'r e-bost hwn o'r tu allan i Gyngor Caerdydd, cymerwch ofal\n> wrth glicio ar ddolenni.\n>\n>\n>\n> Dear Ms Diggins,\n>\n> Thank you for your reply and for clarifying Glamorgan Archives’ position\n> regarding access and charges for copies of material. I appreciate the work\n> you do in preserving the records entrusted to your care.\n>\n> I would like to reiterate that my correspondence is not a routine request\n> for private research access. Rather, it is a formal expression of concern\n> regarding the potential historical misdescription or suppression of facts\n> relating to Great House Farm, Llandough, including heritage and\n> archaeological records. My concern is about the accuracy and completeness\n> of the archival record itself, rather than the provision of copies or\n> personal research access.\n>\n> As I am currently residing in Argentina, I am unable to attend in person,\n> and my intention is not to request material for private use, but to\n> highlight potential issues with public record integrity. I would therefore\n> be grateful if you could advise which department, officer, or public body I\n> should contact to formally raise concerns regarding historical\n> misrepresentation within public records or archival descriptions, so that\n> these matters may be properly examined.\n>\n> For the avoidance of doubt, I am not alleging any impropriety on the part\n> of current archive staff. My concern relates solely to historical processes\n> and record-keeping.\n>\n> Thank you very much for your time and consideration, and I would be\n> grateful for any guidance you can provide.\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n>\n> *Requester*\n>\n>\n>\n> On Wed, 18 Feb 2026 at 11:51, Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg <\n> Glamro@cardiff.gov.uk> wrote:\n>\n> 2026/0672\n>\n> Dear Requester.\n>\n> Thank you for your email, received on 9 February, and for outlining within\n> it your concerns, which we have noted.\n>\n> Glamorgan Archives holds only the records that we have been given. We\n> cannot make any assumptions as to whether the content of those records is\n> accurate. And we have a duty to preserve the records which are presented to\n> us as they are presented to us. In addition, Glamorgan Archives has an\n> income target and, as such, we aren’t in a position to provide free copies\n> of material in our care.\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n> Rhian Diggins\n>\n> Uwch Archifydd / Senior Archivist\n>\n> Archifau Morgannwg / Glamorgan Archives\n>\n> 029 2087 2299\n>\n> glamro@cardiff.gov.uk\n>\n> https://glamarchives.gov.uk/\n>\n> *From:* Requester <[redacted]>\n> *Sent:* 06 February 2026 17:51\n> *To:* Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg <Glamro@cardiff.gov.uk>\n> *Subject:* Re: Epexio contact us form submission\n>\n> *EXTERNAL:* This email originated from outside Cardiff Council, take care\n> when clicking links.\n> *ALLANOL:* Daw'r e-bost hwn o'r tu allan i Gyngor Caerdydd, cymerwch ofal\n> wrth glicio ar ddolenni.\n>\n> Ms Taylor,\n>\n> Thanks for your reply and for outlining the options available to consult\n> the estate plans referenced.\n>\n> I should clarify my position, as my correspondence is not a routine\n> request for private research access, but part of a wider and serious\n> concern regarding possible historical misdescription, suppression, and/or\n> concealment of material facts relating to Great House Farm, Llandough.\n>\n> I am currently at the Welsh settlement in Argentina, having been displaced\n> from my ancestral birthplace in Llandough. As such, it is not possible for\n> me to attend the searchroom in person so easy as you're 7,500 miles away,\n> nor to “pop in” to consult records on site. Requiring physical attendance\n> or payment for access is therefore not a practical or equitable option in\n> my circumstances.\n>\n> More importantly, my concern is that certain archival descriptions,\n> surveys, and planning-era records may themselves be inaccurate or\n> incomplete as a result of earlier failures of disclosure. Specifically, a\n> Roman burial reportedly discovered at Great House Farm in 1888 appears to\n> have been treated as a “surprise” in later archaeological surveys\n> (1978/1994), and an occupied, c.800-year-old domestic dwelling was\n> repeatedly described in planning documentation as an “old barn” or “utility\n> building.” These characterisations materially affected heritage protection,\n> culminating in demolition in 1988.\n>\n> In that context, I am not seeking to pay for copies of records whose\n> accuracy is itself in question, nor to participate financially in processes\n> that may inadvertently perpetuate errors arising from that period. My\n> intention is to place relevant public bodies on notice of a potential issue\n> of public record integrity and heritage governance, not to obtain documents\n> for private interest. I have written to the Senedd calling for a public\n> enquiry too. I've notified the land registry and serious fraud teams too.\n>\n> I am therefore asking that my correspondence be treated as a formal\n> expression of concern regarding the reliability and completeness of the\n> archival record as it relates to this site, and that this concern be noted\n> accordingly. I would also be grateful if you could advise which department\n> or officer is appropriate for addressing concerns of this nature, where the\n> issue is not access but potential historical misrepresentation within\n> public records.\n>\n> For the avoidance of doubt, I am not alleging any impropriety on the part\n> of current archive staff. My concern relates to historical processes and\n> decisions, and to ensuring that those matters are properly examined rather\n> than inadvertently reinforced.\n>\n> Thank you for your time and consideration.\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n> Requester\n> ------------------------------\n>\n> On Fri, 6 Feb 2026, 10:44 Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg, <\n> Glamro@cardiff.gov.uk> wrote:\n>\n> *Ref: 2026/0633*\n>\n> Dear Requester,\n>\n> Thank you for your email received on 5th February 2026.\n>\n> You would be welcome to visit us to consult the estate plans DB/E/1-2 in\n> our public searchroom and take your own photographs for free. Our opening\n> hours are Tues-Thurs 9.30am-12.30pm & 1.30pm-4.30pm. We are currently\n> taking bookings from *Tuesday 17th February 2026.* Please note that our\n> public searchroom is *closed* for the week Tues 10th February – Thurs\n> 12th February so that staff can carry out essential work on the\n> collections. We will re-open to the public on Tuesday 17th February 2026.\n>\n> If you have not visited us before, you are required to register. We ask\n> that you bring with you two forms of ID, one showing your name e.g. Driving\n> Licence and the other your current address e.g. current utility bill or\n> official letter.\n>\n> Alternatively, if you are unable to visit us in person, and if you can\n> confirm exactly which pages you require, staff can provide digital copies,\n> for which we charge £15.60 for the first image and £4.80 each for\n> subsequent images, payable in advance. Prices include VAT.\n>\n> You can find a detailed list on the catalogue: Survey of marquis of\n> Bute’s Glamorgan estates, by David Stewart.\n> <https://glamorgan.epexio.com/en/records/DB/E/1-8/1-2> If you would like\n> a quote for digital copies, please let get in touch.\n>\n> We hope this information is of assistance to you.\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n> Melanie Taylor\n>\n> Cynorthwyydd Cofnodion / Records Assistant\n>\n> Archifau Morgannwg / Glamorgan Archives\n>\n> Clos Parc Morgannwg\n> <https://www.google.com/maps/search/Clos+Parc+Morgannwg+%0D%0A+Lecwydd+%2F+Leckwith+%0D%0A+Caerdydd+%2F+Cardiff+%0D%0A+CF11+8AW?entry=gmail&source=g>\n>\n> Lecwydd / Leckwith\n> <https://www.google.com/maps/search/Clos+Parc+Morgannwg+%0D%0A+Lecwydd+%2F+Leckwith+%0D%0A+Caerdydd+%2F+Cardiff+%0D%0A+CF11+8AW?entry=gmail&source=g>\n>\n> Caerdydd / Cardiff\n> <https://www.google.com/maps/search/Clos+Parc+Morgannwg+%0D%0A+Lecwydd+%2F+Leckwith+%0D%0A+Caerdydd+%2F+Cardiff+%0D%0A+CF11+8AW?entry=gmail&source=g>\n>\n> CF11 8AW\n> <https://www.google.com/maps/search/Clos+Parc+Morgannwg+%0D%0A+Lecwydd+%2F+Leckwith+%0D%0A+Caerdydd+%2F+Cardiff+%0D%0A+CF11+8AW?entry=gmail&source=g>\n>\n> 02920872200\n>\n> glamro@cardiff.gov.uk\n>\n> www.archifaumorgannwg.gov.uk / www.glamarchives.gov.uk\n>\n> www.facebook.com/glamarchives\n>\n> www.twitter.com/glamarchives\n>\n> **********************************************************************\n>\n> The Council welcomes correspondence in Welsh, English or bilingually. We\n> will ensure that we communicate with you in the language of your choice, as\n> long as you let us know which you prefer. Corresponding in Welsh will not\n> lead to delay.\n>\n> Privileged/Confidential Information may be contained in this message. If\n> you are not the addressee indicated in this message (or responsible for\n> delivery of the message to such person), you may not copy or deliver this\n> message to anyone. In such case, you should destroy this message and kindly\n> notify the sender by reply email. Please advise immediately if you or your\n> employer does not consent to Internet email for messages of this kind.\n> Opinions, conclusions and other information in this message that do not\n> relate to the official business of the Council of the City and County of\n> Cardiff shall be understood as neither given nor endorsed by it. All e-mail\n> sent to or from this address will be processed by Cardiff County Councils\n> Corporate E-mail system and may be subject to scrutiny by someone other\n> than the addressee.\n>\n> **********************************************************************\n>\n> Mae’r Cyngor yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg, Saesneg neu’n ddwyieithog.\n> Byddwn yn cyfathrebu â chi yn ôl eich dewis, dim ond i chi roi gwybod i ni\n> pa un sydd well gennych. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi.\n>\n> Mae'n bosibl bod gwybodaeth gyfrinachol yn y neges hon. Os na chyfeirir y\n> neges atoch chi'n benodol (neu os nad ydych chi'n gyfrifol am drosglwyddo'r\n> neges i'r person a enwir), yna ni chewch gopio na throsglwyddo'r neges.\n> Mewn achos o'r fath, dylech ddinistrio'r neges a hysbysu'r anfonwr drwy\n> e-bost ar unwaith. Rhowch wybod i'r anfonydd ar unwaith os nad ydych chi\n> neu eich cyflogydd yn caniatau e-bost y Rhyngrwyd am negeseuon fel hon.\n> Rhaid deall nad yw'r safbwyntiau, y casgliadau a'r wybodaeth arall yn y\n> neges hon nad ydynt yn cyfeirio at fusnes swyddogol Cyngor Dinas a Sir\n> Caerdydd yn cynrychioli barn y Cyngor Sir nad yn cael sel ei fendith. Caiff\n> unrhyw negeseuon a anfonir at, neu o'r cyfeiriad e-bost hwn eu prosesu gan\n> system E-bost Gorfforaethol Cyngor Sir Caerdydd a gallant gael eu\n> harchwilio gan rywun heblaw'r person a enwir.\n>\n> **********************************************************************\n>\n>\n"},{"id":"wb-3cf5966e","subject":"Consortium correspondence BP v Buckler","name":"2026-02-20_consortium-correspondence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","date":"2026-02-20","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-20_consortium-correspondence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-correspondence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-20_consortium-correspondence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: \"Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg\" <Glamro@cardiff.gov.uk>\nDate: Fri, 20 Feb 2026 09:00:26 +0000\nSubject: RE: Epexio contact us form submission\nMessage-ID: <LO6P265MB63452BE8458AF6E8E1AA6ABBCC68A@LO6P265MB6345.GBRP265.PROD.OUTLOOK.COM>\n\n2026/0859\n\nDear Mr Buckler.\n\nThank you for your email.\n\nGlamorgan Archives had at one time a large number of paper catalogues, along with card indexes.  The data from these was transferred into our catalogue when we moved from analogue to digital.  The original catalogues and cards were not retained.\n\nOur catalogue only records descriptions of documents that are held within our collection.  If a document is permanently withdrawn from the collection, we would remove the description from the catalogue. Many of our records are private records held on deposit on behalf of the owner.  We have no control over what they do or don’t give us.  The owner retains the right to withdraw these documents if they wish.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nRhian Diggins [cid:image001.jpg@01DCA247.5A36F0C0]\nUwch Archifydd / Senior Archivist\n\nArchifau Morgannwg / Glamorgan Archives\n029 2087 2299\nglamro@cardiff.gov.uk<mailto:glamro@cardiff.gov.uk>\nhttps://glamarchives.gov.uk/\n\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]>\nSent: 19 February 2026 10:34\nTo: Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg <Glamro@cardiff.gov.uk>\nSubject: Re: Epexio contact us form submission\n\nEXTERNAL: This email originated from outside Cardiff Council, take care when clicking links.\nALLANOL: Daw'r e-bost hwn o'r tu allan i Gyngor Caerdydd, cymerwch ofal wrth glicio ar ddolenni.\n\n\nDear Ms Diggins,\n\nI would be grateful for clarification on a specific point.\n\nIt has long been understood within my family that an index card existed referring to the title deeds of Great House Farm, Llandough. While the deeds themselves reportedly went missing, the index reference was said to remain at one time.\n\nMy question is simply this:\n\nIf an index card or catalogue entry once existed within Glamorgan Archives (or its predecessor systems), would a record of that entry survive following digitisation, even if the underlying document was withdrawn, transferred, or missing?\n\nIn other words, are superseded or removed catalogue references retained within archival metadata systems?\n\nI am not alleging wrongdoing. I am trying to establish whether any trace of such an index entry would ordinarily remain within your records management framework.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nSion Buckler\n\n\nOn Thu, 19 Feb 2026 at 06:05, Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg <Glamro@cardiff.gov.uk<mailto:Glamro@cardiff.gov.uk>> wrote:\nDear Requester.\nThank you for your email.\nCan I suggest that you contact the National Archives<https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/>? They hold responsibility for public records in England and Wales and may be best placed to advise you.\nYours sincerely,\nRhian Diggins [cid:image001.jpg@01DCA247.5A36F0C0]\nUwch Archifydd / Senior Archivist\nArchifau Morgannwg / Glamorgan Archives\n029 2087 2299\nglamro@cardiff.gov.uk<mailto:glamro@cardiff.gov.uk>\nhttps://glamarchives.gov.uk/\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]<mailto:[redacted]>>\nSent: 18 February 2026 16:53\nTo: Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg <Glamro@cardiff.gov.uk<mailto:Glamro@cardiff.gov.uk>>\nSubject: Re: Epexio contact us form submission\nEXTERNAL: This email originated from outside Cardiff Council, take care when clicking links.\nALLANOL: Daw'r e-bost hwn o'r tu allan i Gyngor Caerdydd, cymerwch ofal wrth glicio ar ddolenni.\n\nDear Ms Diggins,\n\nThank you for your reply and for clarifying Glamorgan Archives’ position regarding access and charges for copies of material. I appreciate the work you do in preserving the records entrusted to your care.\n\nI would like to reiterate that my correspondence is not a routine request for private research access. Rather, it is a formal expression of concern regarding the potential historical misdescription or suppression of facts relating to Great House Farm, Llandough, including heritage and archaeological records. My concern is about the accuracy and completeness of the archival record itself, rather than the provision of copies or personal research access.\n\nAs I am currently residing in Argentina, I am unable to attend in person, and my intention is not to request material for private use, but to highlight potential issues with public record integrity. I would therefore be grateful if you could advise which department, officer, or public body I should contact to formally raise concerns regarding historical misrepresentation within public records or archival descriptions, so that these matters may be properly examined.\n\nFor the avoidance of doubt, I am not alleging any impropriety on the part of current archive staff. My concern relates solely to historical processes and record-keeping.\n\nThank you very much for your time and consideration, and I would be grateful for any guidance you can provide.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nSion Buckler\nOn Wed, 18 Feb 2026 at 11:51, Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg <Glamro@cardiff.gov.uk<mailto:Glamro@cardiff.gov.uk>> wrote:\n2026/0672\nDear Requester.\nThank you for your email, received on 9 February, and for outlining within it your concerns, which we have noted.\nGlamorgan Archives holds only the records that we have been given. We cannot make any assumptions as to whether the content of those records is accurate. And we have a duty to preserve the records which are presented to us as they are presented to us. In addition, Glamorgan Archives has an income target and, as such, we aren’t in a position to provide free copies of material in our care.\nYours sincerely,\nRhian Diggins [cid:image001.jpg@01DCA247.5A36F0C0]\nUwch Archifydd / Senior Archivist\nArchifau Morgannwg / Glamorgan Archives\n029 2087 2299\nglamro@cardiff.gov.uk<mailto:glamro@cardiff.gov.uk>\nhttps://glamarchives.gov.uk/\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]<mailto:[redacted]>>\nSent: 06 February 2026 17:51\nTo: Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg <Glamro@cardiff.gov.uk<mailto:Glamro@cardiff.gov.uk>>\nSubject: Re: Epexio contact us form submission\nEXTERNAL: This email originated from outside Cardiff Council, take care when clicking links.\nALLANOL: Daw'r e-bost hwn o'r tu allan i Gyngor Caerdydd, cymerwch ofal wrth glicio ar ddolenni.\n\nMs Taylor,\n\nThanks for your reply and for outlining the options available to consult the estate plans referenced.\n\nI should clarify my position, as my correspondence is not a routine request for private research access, but part of a wider and serious concern regarding possible historical misdescription, suppression, and/or concealment of material facts relating to Great House Farm, Llandough.\n\nI am currently at the Welsh settlement in Argentina, having been displaced from my ancestral birthplace in Llandough. As such, it is not possible for me to attend the searchroom in person so easy as you're 7,500 miles away, nor to “pop in” to consult records on site. Requiring physical attendance or payment for access is therefore not a practical or equitable option in my circumstances.\n\nMore importantly, my concern is that certain archival descriptions, surveys, and planning-era records may themselves be inaccurate or incomplete as a result of earlier failures of disclosure. Specifically, a Roman burial reportedly discovered at Great House Farm in 1888 appears to have been treated as a “surprise” in later archaeological surveys (1978/1994), and an occupied, c.800-year-old domestic dwelling was repeatedly described in planning documentation as an “old barn” or “utility building.” These characterisations materially affected heritage protection, culminating in demolition in 1988.\n\nIn that context, I am not seeking to pay for copies of records whose accuracy is itself in question, nor to participate financially in processes that may inadvertently perpetuate errors arising from that period. My intention is to place relevant public bodies on notice of a potential issue of public record integrity and heritage governance, not to obtain documents for private interest. I have written to the Senedd calling for a public enquiry too. I've notified the land registry and serious fraud teams too.\n\nI am therefore asking that my correspondence be treated as a formal expression of concern regarding the reliability and completeness of the archival record as it relates to this site, and that this concern be noted accordingly. I would also be grateful if you could advise which department or officer is appropriate for addressing concerns of this nature, where the issue is not access but potential historical misrepresentation within public records.\n\nFor the avoidance of doubt, I am not alleging any impropriety on the part of current archive staff. My concern relates to historical processes and decisions, and to ensuring that those matters are properly examined rather than inadvertently reinforced.\n\nThank you for your time and consideration.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nSion Buckler\n\n________________________________\nOn Fri, 6 Feb 2026, 10:44 Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg, <Glamro@cardiff.gov.uk<mailto:Glamro@cardiff.gov.uk>> wrote:\nRef: 2026/0633\nDear Requester,\nThank you for your email received on 5th February 2026.\n\nYou would be welcome to visit us to consult the estate plans DB/E/1-2 in our public searchroom and take your own photographs for free. Our opening hours are Tues-Thurs 9.30am-12.30pm & 1.30pm-4.30pm. We are currently taking bookings from Tuesday 17th February 2026. Please note that our public searchroom is closed for the week Tues 10th February – Thurs 12th February so that staff can carry out essential work on the collections. We will re-open to the public on Tuesday 17th February 2026.\nIf you have not visited us before, you are required to register. We ask that you bring with you two forms of ID, one showing your name e.g. Driving Licence and the other your current address e.g. current utility bill or official letter.\nAlternatively, if you are unable to visit us in person, and if you can confirm exactly which pages you require, staff can provide digital copies, for which we charge £15.60 for the first image and £4.80 each for subsequent images, payable in advance. Prices include VAT.\nYou can find a detailed list on the catalogue: Survey of marquis of Bute’s Glamorgan estates, by David Stewart.<https://glamorgan.epexio.com/en/records/DB/E/1-8/1-2> If you would like a quote for digital copies, please let get in touch.\nWe hope this information is of assistance to you.\nYours sincerely,\nMelanie Taylor [cid:image002.jpg@01DCA247.5A36F0C0]\nCynorthwyydd Cofnodion / Records Assistant\nArchifau Morgannwg / Glamorgan Archives\nClos Parc Morgannwg<https://www.google.com/maps/search/Clos+Parc+Morgannwg+%0D%0A+Lecwydd+%2F+Leckwith+%0D%0A+Caerdydd+%2F+Cardiff+%0D%0A+CF11+8AW?entry=gmail&source=g>\nLecwydd / Leckwith<https://www.google.com/maps/search/Clos+Parc+Morgannwg+%0D%0A+Lecwydd+%2F+Leckwith+%0D%0A+Caerdydd+%2F+Cardiff+%0D%0A+CF11+8AW?entry=gmail&source=g>\nCaerdydd / Cardiff<https://www.google.com/maps/search/Clos+Parc+Morgannwg+%0D%0A+Lecwydd+%2F+Leckwith+%0D%0A+Caerdydd+%2F+Cardiff+%0D%0A+CF11+8AW?entry=gmail&source=g>\nCF11 8AW<https://www.google.com/maps/search/Clos+Parc+Morgannwg+%0D%0A+Lecwydd+%2F+Leckwith+%0D%0A+Caerdydd+%2F+Cardiff+%0D%0A+CF11+8AW?entry=gmail&source=g>\n02920872200\nglamro@cardiff.gov.uk<mailto:glamro@cardiff.gov.uk>\nwww.archifaumorgannwg.gov.uk<http://www.archifaumorgannwg.gov.uk/> / www.glamarchives.gov.uk<http://www.glamarchives.gov.uk/>\nwww.facebook.com/glamarchives<http://www.facebook.com/glamarchives>\nwww.twitter.com/glamarchives<http://www.twitter.com/glamarchives>\n\n**********************************************************************\n\nThe Council welcomes correspondence in Welsh, English or bilingually. We will ensure that we communicate with you in the language of your choice, as long as you let us know which you prefer. Corresponding in Welsh will not lead to delay.\n\nPrivileged/Confidential Information may be contained in this message. If you are not the addressee indicated in this message (or responsible for delivery of the message to such person), you may not copy or deliver this message to anyone. In such case, you should destroy this message and kindly notify the sender by reply email. Please advise immediately if you or your employer does not consent to Internet email for messages of this kind. Opinions, conclusions and other information in this message that do not relate to the official business of the Council of the City and County of Cardiff shall be understood as neither given nor endorsed by it. All e-mail sent to or from this address will be processed by Cardiff County Councils Corporate E-mail system and may be subject to scrutiny by someone other than the addressee.\n\n**********************************************************************\n\nMae’r Cyngor yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg, Saesneg neu’n ddwyieithog. Byddwn yn cyfathrebu â chi yn ôl eich dewis, dim ond i chi roi gwybod i ni pa un sydd well gennych. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi.\n\nMae'n bosibl bod gwybodaeth gyfrinachol yn y neges hon. Os na chyfeirir y neges atoch chi'n benodol (neu os nad ydych chi'n gyfrifol am drosglwyddo'r neges i'r person a enwir), yna ni chewch gopio na throsglwyddo'r neges. Mewn achos o'r fath, dylech ddinistrio'r neges a hysbysu'r anfonwr drwy e-bost ar unwaith. Rhowch wybod i'r anfonydd ar unwaith os nad ydych chi neu eich cyflogydd yn caniatau e-bost y Rhyngrwyd am negeseuon fel hon. Rhaid deall nad yw'r safbwyntiau, y casgliadau a'r wybodaeth arall yn y neges hon nad ydynt yn cyfeirio at fusnes swyddogol Cyngor Dinas a Sir Caerdydd yn cynrychioli barn y Cyngor Sir nad yn cael sel ei fendith. Caiff unrhyw negeseuon a anfonir at, neu o'r cyfeiriad e-bost hwn eu prosesu gan system E-bost Gorfforaethol Cyngor Sir Caerdydd a gallant gael eu harchwilio gan rywun heblaw'r person a enwir.\n\n**********************************************************************\n"},{"id":"wb-45a02ccd","subject":"Consortium correspondence BP v Buckler","name":"2026-02-20_consortium-correspondence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","date":"2026-02-20","dateStatus":"filename-date-unverified","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/2026-02-20_consortium-correspondence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","threadSlug":"consortium-correspondence-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":["bpvsbuckler"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["bpvsbuckler"],"sourcePath":"text/2026-02-20_consortium-correspondence_bpvsbuckler_en_v0.0.1.001.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"From: Requester <[redacted]>\nDate: Fri, 20 Feb 2026 13:00:32 -0300\nSubject: Re: Epexio contact us form submission\nMessage-ID: <CAAuJ3V5ypJ8yTfnQUVouZhFJUer-wGP4eyudRUTHhoMLgtzasw@mail.gmail.com>\n\nDear Ms Diggins,\n\nThank you for your clarification. You are being most helpful.\n\nI understand that if a document is permanently withdrawn from the\ncollection, its catalogue description is removed.\n\nMay I ask a procedural question:\n\nWhen privately deposited records are withdrawn, does Glamorgan Archives\nretain any internal administrative record of:\n\n   -\n\n   the fact that the material was once held,\n   -\n\n   the identity of the depositor,\n   -\n\n   and the date of withdrawal?\n\nIn other words, while the public catalogue entry may be removed, is there\nan accession register or internal record that preserves evidence that the\nmaterial once formed part of the collection?\n\nI am not asking you to adjudicate ownership. I am simply trying to\nestablish whether archival systems ordinarily retain a trace of withdrawn\ndeposits.\n\nThank you for your assistance.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\n\nSion Buckler\n\nOn Fri, 20 Feb 2026 at 06:00, Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg <\nGlamro@cardiff.gov.uk> wrote:\n\n> 2026/0859\n>\n>\n>\n> Dear Requester.\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you for your email.\n>\n>\n>\n> Glamorgan Archives had at one time a large number of paper catalogues,\n> along with card indexes.  The data from these was transferred into our\n> catalogue when we moved from analogue to digital.  The original catalogues\n> and cards were not retained.\n>\n>\n>\n> Our catalogue only records descriptions of documents that are held within\n> our collection.  If a document is permanently withdrawn from the\n> collection, we would remove the description from the catalogue. Many of our\n> records are private records held on deposit on behalf of the owner.  We\n> have no control over what they do or don’t give us.  The owner retains the\n> right to withdraw these documents if they wish.\n>\n>\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n>\n>\n> Rhian Diggins\n>\n> Uwch Archifydd / Senior Archivist\n>\n>\n>\n> Archifau Morgannwg / Glamorgan Archives\n>\n> 029 2087 2299\n>\n> glamro@cardiff.gov.uk\n>\n> https://glamarchives.gov.uk/\n>\n>\n>\n> *From:* Requester <[redacted]>\n> *Sent:* 19 February 2026 10:34\n> *To:* Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg <Glamro@cardiff.gov.uk>\n> *Subject:* Re: Epexio contact us form submission\n>\n>\n>\n> *EXTERNAL:* This email originated from outside Cardiff Council, take care\n> when clicking links.\n> *ALLANOL:* Daw'r e-bost hwn o'r tu allan i Gyngor Caerdydd, cymerwch ofal\n> wrth glicio ar ddolenni.\n>\n>\n>\n> Dear Ms Diggins,\n>\n> I would be grateful for clarification on a specific point.\n>\n> It has long been understood within my family that an index card existed\n> referring to the title deeds of Great House Farm, Llandough. While the\n> deeds themselves reportedly went missing, the index reference was said to\n> remain at one time.\n>\n> My question is simply this:\n>\n> If an index card or catalogue entry once existed within Glamorgan Archives\n> (or its predecessor systems), would a record of that entry survive\n> following digitisation, even if the underlying document was withdrawn,\n> transferred, or missing?\n>\n> In other words, are superseded or removed catalogue references retained\n> within archival metadata systems?\n>\n> I am not alleging wrongdoing. I am trying to establish whether any trace\n> of such an index entry would ordinarily remain within your records\n> management framework.\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n>\n> Requester\n>\n>\n>\n> On Thu, 19 Feb 2026 at 06:05, Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg <\n> Glamro@cardiff.gov.uk> wrote:\n>\n> Dear Requester.\n>\n> Thank you for your email.\n>\n> Can I suggest that you contact the National Archives\n> <https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/>? They hold responsibility for\n> public records in England and Wales and may be best placed to advise you.\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n> Rhian Diggins\n>\n> Uwch Archifydd / Senior Archivist\n>\n> Archifau Morgannwg / Glamorgan Archives\n>\n> 029 2087 2299\n>\n> glamro@cardiff.gov.uk\n>\n> https://glamarchives.gov.uk/\n>\n> *From:* Requester <[redacted]>\n> *Sent:* 18 February 2026 16:53\n> *To:* Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg <Glamro@cardiff.gov.uk>\n> *Subject:* Re: Epexio contact us form submission\n>\n> *EXTERNAL:* This email originated from outside Cardiff Council, take care\n> when clicking links.\n> *ALLANOL:* Daw'r e-bost hwn o'r tu allan i Gyngor Caerdydd, cymerwch ofal\n> wrth glicio ar ddolenni.\n>\n> Dear Ms Diggins,\n>\n> Thank you for your reply and for clarifying Glamorgan Archives’ position\n> regarding access and charges for copies of material. I appreciate the work\n> you do in preserving the records entrusted to your care.