nspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"[Recovered on 19 Oct 2026 from the original email in the family's Gmail, because this archive file was missing. Matched to this entry by its subject line; where several emails share a subject, the order within the group is not certain. Email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses are redacted.]\n\nSubject: Enquiry\nFrom: Requester\nDate: Thu, 29 Feb 2024 18:23 UTC\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nI'm getting an overnight Ferry from Harwich, England to Hook of Holland.\nThen a train to a Brussels airport hotel. Then the follow day I fly from\nBrussels Airport to Punta Cana in the Dominican Republic.\n\nEveryone I speak to at the vets, APHA, the Dominican Republic Embassy in\nLondon and Belgium and Netherlands officials all tell me conflicting\ninformation.\n\nPlease can your department tell me what I need and soon, because my\ndeparture date is 10th March.\n\nI look forward to your reply,\n\nSion Buckler\n[phone redacted]\n"},{"id":"wb-a5a5ce98","subject":"Enquiry","name":"0000-00-00_enquiry_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_enquiry_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","threadSlug":"enquiry","scopeTopics":["email"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email"],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_enquiry_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"[Recovered on 19 Oct 2026 from the original email in the family's Gmail, because this archive file was missing. Matched to this entry by its subject line; where several emails share a subject, the order within the group is not certain. Email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses are redacted.]\n\nSubject: Enquiry\nFrom: Requester\nDate: Fri, 01 Mar 2024 14:08 UTC\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nI am struggling to find an OV for Export Health Certificate 3899.\n\nI was hoping to secure an OV today before applying for my application form\nonline. Because it asks online who is my OV.\n\nUnfortunately my local vets in Penarth Road, Cardiff and Barry and Bridgend\naren't able to help.\n\nI depart on the 11th March, so this is my first day of the timeframe to try\nto arrange this. I did spend the last week or two trying to get ahead of\nthis but there was lots of incorrect and misinformation and fraudsters and\nmy MP Alun Cairns is working to correct the issues now.\n\nI look forward to your reply,\n\nSion Buckler\n[phone redacted]\n"},{"id":"wb-b932de2d","subject":"Enquiry","name":"0000-00-00_enquiry_en_v0.0.0.00-2.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_enquiry_en_v0.0.0.00-2.txt","threadSlug":"enquiry","scopeTopics":["email"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email"],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_enquiry_en_v0.0.0.00-2.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"[Recovered on 19 Oct 2026 from the original email in the family's Gmail, because this archive file was missing. Matched to this entry by its subject line; where several emails share a subject, the order within the group is not certain. Email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses are redacted.]\n\nSubject: Enquiry\nFrom: Requester\nDate: Sat, 09 Mar 2024 20:58 UTC\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nCould we have a call tomorrow?\nIt's about APHA Deregation letters.\n\nRegards,\n\nSion Buckler\n[phone redacted]\n"},{"id":"wb-809037ae","subject":"Enquiry","name":"0000-00-00_enquiry_en_v0.0.0.00-3.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_enquiry_en_v0.0.0.00-3.txt","threadSlug":"enquiry","scopeTopics":["email"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email"],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_enquiry_en_v0.0.0.00-3.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"[Recovered on 19 Oct 2026 from the original email in the family's Gmail, because this archive file was missing. Matched to this entry by its subject line; where several emails share a subject, the order within the group is not certain. Email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses are redacted.]\n\nSubject: Enquiry\nFrom: Requester\nDate: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 21:05 UTC\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nDo you do free credit?\nI'm working on what was a family pet project:\nhttps://eathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com\n\nIt's possible AI may have discovered fraud, concealment and government\ninvolvement in a case from the 80's. It transpires we still seem to own\nland even the ECHR said was BPs not ours.\n\nI need all the help I can get cracking this case. If I'm right my family\ncan reopen the case and seek reporations for a major injustice.\n\nIt began as a project to preserve our history and herrirage. It turned into\na monster thing that's a lot for us to handle without support where we can\nget it.\n\nRegards,\n\nSion Buckler (Ret'd Sergeant)\nRoyal Signals Cyber/Electronic Warfare Professional and Life Member\n"},{"id":"wb-22d2e945","subject":"Foir incident 1235 05 03 2024","name":"0000-00-00_foir-incident-1235-05_03_2024_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"FOI / EIR correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_foir-incident-1235-05_03_2024_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"foir-incident-1235-05-03-2024","scopeTopics":["email"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email"],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_foir-incident-1235-05_03_2024_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"[Recovered on 19 Oct 2026 from the original email in the family's Gmail, because this archive file was missing. Matched to this entry by its subject line; where several emails share a subject, the order within the group is not certain. Email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses are redacted.]\n\nSubject: FOIR - Incident 1235 05/03/2024\nFrom: Requester\nDate: Tue, 05 Mar 2024 16:44 UTC\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nSirs/Ma'am,\n\nSee enclosed my FOI request.\nIt concerns me and my vehicle in Cardiff earlier today.\n\nI enclosed a payment link in the form, or if you would like I can pay the\n£10 fee by phone or bank transfer.\n\nI also spoke with Donna at South Wales Police - incident number 527 circa\n1240 (05/03/2024)\n\nI trust you'll be able to pass the footage to my insurance company.\n\nRegards,\n\nSion Buckler\n[phone redacted]\n"},{"id":"wb-a2307267","subject":"FOI","name":"0000-00-00_foi_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"FOI / EIR correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_foi_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"foi","scopeTopics":["email"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email"],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_foi_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"Sirs,\r\n\r\nPlease will you locate/access probate records for Mary Williams (d. March\r\n1983), Great House Farm, Llandough—any wills, affidavits, or ownership\r\nclaims referenced?\r\n\r\nThanks,\r\n\r\nSion Buckler Ret'd Sgt\r\n"},{"id":"wb-f845b91f","subject":"FOI EIR request case ref 00210772","name":"0000-00-00_foi_eir-request-case-ref-00210772_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"FOI / EIR correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_foi_eir-request-case-ref-00210772_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"foi-eir-request-case-ref-00210772","scopeTopics":["email"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email"],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_foi_eir-request-case-ref-00210772_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nDear Requester\r\n\r\nFREEDOM OF INFORMATION ACT 2000/ENVIRONMENTAL INFORMATION REGULATIONS 2004 - INFORMATION REQUEST\r\n\r\nThank you for your request for information about: Great House Farm, Williams/ Buckler Family, Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan\r\n\r\nYour request was received on 20/05/2026 and is being considered under the terms of the Freedom of Information Act 2000 and the Environmental Information Regulations 2004. Your query has been given the reference 00210772.\r\n\r\nIn some circumstances a fee may be payable and if that is the case we will let you know. A fees notice will be issued to you, and you will be required to pay before we will proceed to deal with your request.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nYou should receive the information requested within 20 working days unless the Council does not hold the information or there is a reason for it to be withheld. We will write to you in any event.\r\n\r\nIf you have any requirements regarding the format any information should be supplied in, e.g. the language to be used, audio, large print and so on, then if you have not already done so please let us know. \r\n\r\nIf you have any queries or concerns then please do not hesitate to contact us. Please remember to quote the reference number above in any future communications.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nFurther information about your rights is also available from the Information\r\nCommissioner at:\r\n\r\n\r\nInformation Commissioner's Office - Wales\r\n\r\nSecond Floor, Churchill House\r\n\r\nChurchill Way,\r\n\r\nCardiff\r\n\r\nCF10 2HH\r\n\r\n029 20678400 \r\n\r\nwales@ico.org.uk \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nYours sincerely,\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nInformation Governance Officer/ Swyddog Llywodraethu Gwybodaeth\r\n\r\nDirector's Office - Resources / Swyddfa'r Cyfarwyddwr - Adnoddau\r\n\r\nVale of Glamorgan Council / Cyngor Bro Morgannwg\r\n\r\ntel / ffôn: 01446 700111\r\n\r\ne-mail / e-bost: foiunit@valeofglamorgan.gov.uk or dpo@valeofglamorgan.gov.uk\r\n\r\n \r\n\r\nConsider the environment. Please don't print this e-mail unless you really need to.\r\n\r\nYstyriwch yr amgylchedd. Peidiwch ag argraffu'r neges hon oni bai fod gwir angen.\r\n\r\n \r\n\r\nVisit our Website at www.valeofglamorgan.gov.uk\r\n\r\nEwch i'n gwefan yn www.bromorgannwg.gov.uk\r\n\r\n \r\n\r\nFind us on Facebook / Cewch ddod o hyd i ni ar Facebook\r\n\r\nFollow us on Twitter /Dilynwch ni ar Twitter\r\n\r\n \r\n\r\nCorrespondence is welcomed in Welsh or English / Croesewir Gohebiaeth yn y Gymraeg neu yn Saesneg. "},{"id":"wb-8942bd56","subject":"Formal notice state sanctioned dispossession of great house","name":"0000-00-00_formal-notice-state-sanctioned-dispossession-of-great-house_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_formal-notice-state-sanctioned-dispossession-of-great-house_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"formal-notice-state-sanctioned-dispossession-of-great-house","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_formal-notice-state-sanctioned-dispossession-of-great-house_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"Sirs/Mesdames,\r\n\r\nI am writing on behalf of the heirs of Mary Williams concerning the state-sanctioned dispossession of Great House Farm, Llandough. Our investigation has uncovered that the family's superior manorial title was undermined through a series of \"Death of a Thousand Cuts\" (DoaTC) events, involving public institutions.\r\n\r\nWe hereby put the State on notice regarding:\r\n1. The deliberate stalling of the Cadw emergency listing process in 1988, which directly facilitated the demolition of the farm.\r\n2. The removal of Williams/Buckler title documentation from Cardiff Library in 1984, which remains an unexplained erasure of public records.\r\n3. The procedural unfairness of the 1987 Court of Appeal judgment which denied the family's rightful claim through inconsistent judicial logic.\r\n\r\nWe are formally requesting the production of all records held by the National Archives, Cadw, and Vale of Glamorgan Council regarding these events. We maintain that the title to Great House Farm remains rightfully with the family and has never been lawfully surrendered.\r\n\r\nRegards,\r\n\r\nSion Buckler\r\n"},{"id":"wb-c89a0f14","subject":"Formal notice state sanctioned dispossession of great house","name":"0000-00-00_formal-notice-state-sanctioned-dispossession-of-great-house_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_formal-notice-state-sanctioned-dispossession-of-great-house_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","threadSlug":"formal-notice-state-sanctioned-dispossession-of-great-house","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_formal-notice-state-sanctioned-dispossession-of-great-house_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"not-recovered","text":null,"textNotice":"Original text not yet recovered: the archived source file for this record is missing. See sourceUrl."},{"id":"wb-4a433999","subject":"Formal notice state sanctioned dispossession of great house","name":"0000-00-00_formal-notice-state-sanctioned-dispossession-of-great-house_en_v0.0.0.00-2.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_formal-notice-state-sanctioned-dispossession-of-great-house_en_v0.0.0.00-2.txt","threadSlug":"formal-notice-state-sanctioned-dispossession-of-great-house","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_formal-notice-state-sanctioned-dispossession-of-great-house_en_v0.0.0.00-2.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"not-recovered","text":null,"textNotice":"Original text not yet recovered: the archived source file for this record is missing. See sourceUrl."},{"id":"wb-e873e6df","subject":"Formal notice unlawful dispossession of great house farm for","name":"0000-00-00_formal-notice-unlawful-dispossession-of-great-house-farm-for_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_formal-notice-unlawful-dispossession-of-great-house-farm-for_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"formal-notice-unlawful-dispossession-of-great-house-farm-for","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_formal-notice-unlawful-dispossession-of-great-house-farm-for_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Legal Team,\n\nI am writing as the rightful executor of the estate, administering my family trust and estate, concerning the unlawful dispossession of Great House Farm, Llandough. Our investigation has confirmed that our fee simple absolute manorial title (which is distinct from the virtual freehold 999-year lease we acquired in 1667) was never sold, surrendered, or extinguished.\n\nOur title was stolen, and we put you on notice that the statute of limitations has not passed, as it commences from the discovery of the fraud, not the occurrence of it. We maintain that the 'Death of a Thousand Cuts' we suffered—a combination of procedural unfairness, abuse of power, fraud, concealment, perjury, gaps in records, and court mistakes—is evidence of a conspiracy to dispossess us of our lawful inheritance.\n\nWe put you on formal notice of the following findings:\n1. The 1916 'tenancy' was a legal fiction used to facilitate land seizure.\n2. The 1987 BP v Buckler judgment relied on judicial manipulation where BP Pension Trust and BP Properties were inconsistently ruled as separate and identical entities to suit BP's interest (\"Heads/Tails\" fraud).\n3. The 1988 demolition of Great House Farm was a bad-faith act to destroy evidence.\n4. Essential title documents were stolen from public custody in 1984.\n\nWe hereby demand the production of:\n- All internal records regarding the 1938 Western Ground Rents reversion conveyance.\n- All internal BP/BP Properties documentation regarding the 1974 'Licence to Occupy' operation.\n\nWe await your response within 14 days.\n\n\nSion Buckler Esq. (Ret'd Sergeant)\nManor of Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan\nhttps://bpvsbuckler.datro.xyz\n[redacted]\n"},{"id":"wb-46181871","subject":"Formal notice unlawful dispossession of great house farm for","name":"0000-00-00_formal-notice-unlawful-dispossession-of-great-house-farm-for_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_formal-notice-unlawful-dispossession-of-great-house-farm-for_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","threadSlug":"formal-notice-unlawful-dispossession-of-great-house-farm-for","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_formal-notice-unlawful-dispossession-of-great-house-farm-for_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"not-recovered","text":null,"textNotice":"Original text not yet recovered: the archived source file for this record is missing. See sourceUrl."},{"id":"wb-0b4e52ac","subject":"Formal notice unlawful dispossession of great house farm for","name":"0000-00-00_formal-notice-unlawful-dispossession-of-great-house-farm-for_en_v0.0.0.00-2.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_formal-notice-unlawful-dispossession-of-great-house-farm-for_en_v0.0.0.00-2.txt","threadSlug":"formal-notice-unlawful-dispossession-of-great-house-farm-for","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_formal-notice-unlawful-dispossession-of-great-house-farm-for_en_v0.0.0.00-2.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"not-recovered","text":null,"textNotice":"Original text not yet recovered: the archived source file for this record is missing. See sourceUrl."},{"id":"wb-372cf9a4","subject":"Fw BP v Buckler","name":"0000-00-00_fw-bp-vs-buckler_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_fw-bp-vs-buckler_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"fw-bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_fw-bp-vs-buckler_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"not-recovered","text":null,"textNotice":"Original text not yet recovered: the archived source file for this record is missing. See sourceUrl."},{"id":"wb-7c1e9ad9","subject":"Fw great house farm Llandough aka Tŷ Mawr","name":"0000-00-00_fw-great-house-farm-llandough-aka-ty-mawr_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_fw-great-house-farm-llandough-aka-ty-mawr_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"fw-great-house-farm-llandough-aka-t-mawr","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_fw-great-house-farm-llandough-aka-ty-mawr_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"Good afternoon Requester,\r\n\r\nThank you for your email of 17 May regarding Great House Farm, Llandough, and also for your email of 20 May.\r\n\r\nI have searched our electronic records relating to Great House Farm and Barn and I can confirm they were assessed and rejected for listing in the 1988 community survey of Llandough. I have attached two photos, dated 29 July 1988. The \"YYY\" marking on the rear of the photo indicates that the buildings were considered marginally below the bar for listing. We hold no other electronic records.\r\n\r\nI note the SW Echo report suggesting that Cadw inspectors would visit again in December 1988, but I can find no record (as-yet) of this. The one paper file we have located, dated 2002, makes no mention of Great House Farm. I have asked a colleague to search for other related paper files, including the Welsh Office files, and I will write to you again when I have the result of the search. This may take a couple of weeks, and I must advise that is quite likely that at least some of these files will have been destroyed, but I will seek to disclose any records we recover to you.\r\n\r\nIn the meantime, would suggest contacting the following archives services, if you haven't already done so, as they may hold relevant records or be able to offer further assistance -\r\n\r\nGlamorgan Archives<https://glamarchives.gov.uk/>\r\nNational Library of Wales<https://www.library.wales/catalogues-searching/about-our-collections/archives>\r\nThe Royal Commission on the Ancient & Historical Monuments of Wales<https://coflein.gov.uk/en/contact>, including their Coflein<https://coflein.gov.uk/en/> catalogue of historic buildings.\r\n\r\nThese mapping services may also be of use to you:\r\nTithe Maps of Wales<https://places.library.wales/> (1840s)\r\nHistoric Ordnance Survey Maps<https://maps.nls.uk/geo/find/#zoom=5.0&lat=56.00000&lon=-4.00000&layers=102&b=1&z=0&point=0,0> (1820s-1960s)\r\n\r\nThe following research guide may too also be of interest - Land ownership records and deeds: an overview - The National Archives<https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/help-with-your-research/research-guides/land-ownership-records-and-deeds-an-overview/>.\r\n\r\nAnd finally for your records, you may wish to note this record about Great House Farm, held by the Royal Commission (Details | 20182 | Sites | Coflein<https://coflein.gov.uk/en/sites/20182>), this Historic Environment Record (GGAT02038s<https://archwilio.org.uk/her/chi3/report/page.php?watprn=GGAT02038s&dbname=ggat&tbname=core&sessid=CHI4idg6jee&queryid=Q528087001779371396>), and the attached Welsh Archaeological Trust report.\r\n\r\nYours Sincerely,\r\n\r\nMike Weatherhead\r\n\r\nArweinydd Tîm Gwaith Achosion / Casework Team Leader\r\nPolisi a Gwarchod / Policy and Protection\r\nArolygiaeth yr Amgylchedd Hanesyddol / Historic Environment Inspectorate\r\nCadw\r\n\r\n\r\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]>\r\nSent: 17 May 2026 05:11\r\nTo: Scheduled Monuments <scheduledmonuments@gov.wales>\r\nCc: Correspondence.Eluned.Morgan@gov.wales; Cadw Planning <cadwplanning@gov.wales>; cadw@gov.wales; Culture Division / Adran Diwylliant <culture@gov.wales>; Correspondence.Jack.Sargeant@gov.wales\r\nSubject: BP vs Buckler, Great House Farm, Llandough\r\n\r\n\r\nTo whom it may concern,\r\n\r\nI am writing as the child of Branwen and William (\"Billy\") Buckler regarding the destruction of Great House Farm, Llandough, and the long-running dispute surrounding BP Properties Ltd v Buckler [1987].\r\n\r\nI have compiled a substantial historical archive, including newspaper reports, court references, family testimony, archaeological material, estate history, and public records relating to Great House Farm (\"Ty Mawr\"), a site associated with medieval, monastic, and Roman occupation.\r\n\r\nThe archive is currently being made into a website at:\r\n\r\nbpvsbuckler.datro.xyz<http://bpvsbuckler.datro.xyz/>\r\n\r\nThe material raises serious unresolved questions regarding:\r\n\r\n* The treatment of us Williams/Buckler family's longstanding occupation and asserted ownership claims. * The repeated use of possession proceedings without a full adjudication of superior title. * Missing or allegedly suppressed title documentation connected to Bute estate transfers and later transactions. * The demolition of an allegedly medieval structure during an active heritage controversy. * Whether the archaeological significance of the site was fully disclosed during planning and demolition processes. * The wider public interest and cultural significance of the site and its destruction.\r\n\r\nIt's looking to me like my family actually did obtain fee simple absolute and manorial title. Then it was stolen and attempts were made by the possessor of the stolen title, to make my family lessee's / tenants as we were when we were on the virtual freehold (999 year lease) aquired in 1667. Thereafter it appears to be a death of a thousand cuts of proceedural unfairness, audacity, fraud, court \"mistakes\" , purgery and more.\r\n\r\nhttps://youtu.be/4GXmR7mAas0?feature=shared\r\n\r\nContemporaneous newspaper coverage from the 1970s and 1980s demonstrates that these concerns were publicly known at the time, including heritage objections, petitions, and public claims of ancestral ownership.\r\n\r\nI am requesting:\r\n\r\n  1.  Review and preservation consideration of all surviving records connected to Great House Farm.\r\n  2.  Clarification as to whether any formal heritage review or investigation into the demolition process was ever completed.\r\n  3.  Guidance regarding access to archival and planning materials relevant to the site.\r\n  4.  Consideration of whether this matter warrants renewed historical or heritage examination.\r\n\r\nI appreciate that some claims remain disputed or unproven, and I am not asking recipients to accept every allegation as established fact. However, I believe the totality of the historical material warrants serious review rather than dismissal.\r\n\r\nI would be grateful for acknowledgement of receipt and guidance on the appropriate next steps.\r\n\r\nKind regards,\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nSion Buckler Esq. (Ret'd Sergeant)\r\n\r\nManor of Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nSganiwyd y neges hon am bob feirws hysbys wrth iddi adael Llywodraeth Cymru. Mae Llywodraeth Cymru yn cymryd o ddifrif yr angen i ddiogelu eich data. Os cysylltwch â Llywodraeth Cymru, mae ein hysbysiad preifatrwydd<\"https://gov.wales/about/welsh-government-privacy-notice/?lang=cy\"> yn esbonio sut rydym yn defnyddio eich gwybodaeth a sut rydym yn diogelu eich preifatrwydd. Rydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg. Byddwn yn anfon ateb yn Gymraeg i ohebiaeth a dderbynnir yn Gymraeg ac ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi. ----------------- On leaving the Welsh Government this email was scanned for all known viruses. The Welsh Government takes the protection of your data seriously. If you contact the Welsh Government then our Privacy Notice<https://gov.wales/about/welsh-government-privacy-notice/?lang=en> explains how we use your information and the ways in which we protect your privacy. We welcome receiving correspondence in Welsh. Any correspondence received in Welsh will be answered in Welsh and corresponding in Welsh will not lead to a delay in responding.\r\n"},{"id":"wb-62b6481e","subject":"Fw manor of Llandough mdr entries","name":"0000-00-00_fw-manor-of-llandough-mdr-entries_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_fw-manor-of-llandough-mdr-entries_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"fw-manor-of-llandough-mdr-entries","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_fw-manor-of-llandough-mdr-entries_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester,\r\n\r\nI have had an opportunity to view the file of original returns to the Manorial Documents Committee (Ref. HMC9/311) and the single return for the Manor of Llandough (see the attached image), and it provides less information than the existing entries on the on-line MDR. The information on the other manors in the return is equally sparse This may be because many of the surviving manorial records were not in custody of the National Library of Wales at the time of the original survey.\r\n\r\nThe best course of action is to search the archive service catalogues, and particularly the on-line catalogue of the National Library of Wales. Is it difficult to advise you further without more information, for example do you have rough date for the document called ‘Williams’s Title? A particularly  relevant archive collection appears to be the Penrice and Margam Estate Records, [c. 1147]-[19 cent.] / Penrice and Margam estate. - National Library of Wales<https://discover.library.wales/discovery/fulldisplay?docid=alma99193071102419&context=L&vid=44WHELF_NLW:44WHELF_NLW_NUI&lang=en&search_scope=In_The_Library&adaptor=Local%20Search%20Engine&tab=In_The_Library&query=any,contains,Talbots%20of%20margam%20archives&offset=0> as it includes the surviving manorial records for Llandough.\r\n\r\nThere is useful guidance on the different types of title deeds published by the University of Nottingham which might be helpful when searching catalogues and consulting original property records:\r\n\r\nIntroduction to deeds in depth - The University of Nottingham<https://www.nottingham.ac.uk/manuscriptsandspecialcollections/researchguidance/deedsindepth/introduction.aspx>\r\n\r\nI am sorry not to be able to provide any additional sources for your continuing research.\r\n\r\nKind regards\r\n\r\n\r\n[The National Archives logo]\r\nPhilip Gale | Head of the Collections and Cultural Property Team\r\nT: 020 3908 X 9193 | W: nationalarchives.gov.uk<http://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/>\r\nTwitter: @UkNatArchives\r\nThe National Archives, Kew, Richmond, Surrey TW9 4DU\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]>\r\nSent: 24 February 2026 17:42\r\nTo: Philip Gale <Philip.Gale@nationalarchives.gov.uk>\r\nSubject: Re: Manor of Llandough - MDR Entries\r\n\r\nEXTERNAL: This email originates from outside of The National Archives.\r\nYou are a delight.\r\nMy family thanks you.\r\nThis means a great deal.\r\n\r\nSion\r\n\r\nOn Tue, 24 Feb 2026 at 13:50, Philip Gale <Philip.Gale@nationalarchives.gov.uk<mailto:Philip.Gale@nationalarchives.gov.uk>> wrote:\r\nDear Mr Buckler,\r\n\r\nThank you for your e-mail and for clarifying the subject of your enquiry.\r\n\r\nAs you know the Manorial Documents Register (MDR) only gives general descriptions of the content, extent and location of the manorial records recorded in the register: LLANDOUGH MANOR | The National Archives<https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/c/F236775> The manorial records for the Manor of Llandough are held at the National Library of Wales, Glamorgan Archives and West Glamorgan Archives, and their catalogues can be accessed on-line : NLW Catalogue<https://discover.library.wales/discovery/search?vid=44WHELF_NLW:44WHELF_NLW_NUI> , Glamorgan Archives Catalogue<https://canfod.glamarchives.gov.uk/en/> and Archive Collections - Swansea<https://www.swansea.gov.uk/archivecollections?lang=en>\r\n\r\nThe Welsh entries in the online MDR were added in the 1990’s were based on the survey returns to the Manorial Documents Committee received from institutions and private owners during the 1920’s. These were subsequently passed to the custody of the Historical Manuscripts Commission in 1959 which amalgamated with the Public Record Office to form The National Archives in 2003. I have requested to see the original returns for Manor of Llandough to see if they can provide any additional information on the documentation relating to the manor (Ref. HMC 9/311: https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/r/C5879336 )\r\n\r\nThere are several other manors with a similar name in Glamorgan and a MDR search using the search term “Llandough” gives the following result: Search results: Llandough Glamorgan | The National Archives<https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/results/c?_q=Llandough&_sf=text_man&_ocn=Glamorgan&_naet=M&_st=mdrc>   Sometimes the administration of different manors were brought together and this appears to have occurred  with the Llandough and St May’s Church Manors : https://archives.library.wales/index.php/manors-of-llandough-and-st-mary-church\r\n\r\nI will contact you again once I have had an opportunity to look at the original returns sent to Manorial Documents Committee. I hope this preliminary information is of assistance.\r\n\r\nYours sincerely\r\n\r\n[The National Archives logo]\r\nPhilip Gale | Head of the Collections and Cultural Property Team\r\nT: 020 3908 X 9193 | W: nationalarchives.gov.uk<http://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/>\r\nTwitter: @UkNatArchives\r\nThe National Archives, Kew, Richmond, Surrey TW9 4DU\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n________________________________\r\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]<mailto:[redacted]>>\r\nSent: Wednesday, February 18, 2026 5:49:54 PM\r\nTo: Philip Gale <Philip.Gale@nationalarchives.gov.uk<mailto:Philip.Gale@nationalarchives.gov.uk>>\r\nSubject: Re: Formal Notice for Record: Church View Close, Llandough / BP vs Buckler 1987\r\n\r\n\r\nEXTERNAL: This email originates from outside of The National Archives.\r\n\r\nDear Mr Gale,\r\n\r\nThank you for your reply of 12 February. I would like to clarify and narrow my request to ensure there is no misunderstanding regarding the records I am trying to locate.\r\n\r\nMy correspondence is not seeking an adjudication of ownership or title — I fully accept the stated remit boundaries of the Manorial Documents Register (MDR). Rather, my enquiry concerns the existence of any archival reference, index card, catalogue entry, or pointer to a document historically known as the “Williams Title”, which was, according to contemporaneous family testimony, at one point:\r\n\r\n  1.  Held in the local public library in the first half of the twentieth century; and\r\n  2.  Indexed in some manner (e.g., an index card or catalogue reference) that persisted even after the original document could not be located.\r\n\r\nMy understanding of the MDR — as described on your own website — is that it exists to provide brief descriptions of manorial records and details of their locations:\r\n\r\n“An official index of English and Welsh manorial records providing brief descriptions of documents and details of their locations… It does not include title deeds or conveyances, and is not a register of ownership of manorial lordships or rights.”\r\nhttps://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/manor-search<https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/manor-search?utm_source=chatgpt.com>\r\n\r\nBased on that description, I would be grateful if you could confirm whether:\r\n\r\n  1.  Any catalogue entries, index cards, reference pointers, or archival descriptions relating to the Manor of Llandough or the Williams family title exist in the MDR or related archival catalogues.\r\n  2.  If so, the reference identifiers and information on how these can be consulted in person or via digitised records.\r\n\r\nTo be precise, I am looking for historical references to the index card or listing for the Williams Title — whether held in a library, local record office, or other institutional archive — rather than a claim for ownership judgment itself.\r\n\r\nIf such a reference does not currently appear in the MDR, I would greatly appreciate guidance on where any known catalogue cards or entry lists for manorial documents from Glamorgan / Llandough prior to 1950 might now reside (e.g., regional archives, National Archives holdings, or affiliated local repository catalogues).\r\n\r\nAnyway I anticipate your forgiveness, if I have erred in writing to you earlier.\r\n\r\nThank you again and in advance for your assistance.\r\n\r\nYours sincerely,\r\n\r\nSion Buckler\r\nOn Wed, 18 Feb 2026 at 14:30, Philip Gale <Philip.Gale@nationalarchives.gov.uk<mailto:Philip.Gale@nationalarchives.gov.uk>> wrote:\r\n\r\nDear Mr Buckler,\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nThank you for your e-mail of 10 February whose contents are noted. The National Archives does not hold any records relating to your enquiry. The Manorial Documents Register which this department administers is a register for the location of manorial documents and not of the ownership and descent of manorial titles:\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nhttps://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/archives-sector/finding-records-in-discovery-and-other-databases/manorial-documents-register/\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nA similar position holds for The National Archives responsibilities for public records and concerns about the past management of official records is a matter for the relevant government department such as HM Land Registry.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nYours sincerely\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n[The National Archives logo]\r\n\r\nPhilip Gale | Head of the Collections and Cultural Property Team\r\nT: 020 3908 X 9193 | W: nationalarchives.gov.uk<http://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/>\r\nTwitter: @UkNatArchives\r\nThe National Archives, Kew, Richmond, Surrey TW9 4DU\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n-----Original Message-----\r\n\r\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]<mailto:[redacted]>>\r\n\r\nSent: 10 February 2026 22:15\r\n\r\nTo: Archives Sector Leadership <ASL@nationalarchives.gov.uk<mailto:ASL@nationalarchives.gov.uk>>\r\n\r\nCc: reportafraud@landregistry.gov.uk<mailto:reportafraud@landregistry.gov.uk>; enquiries@college-of-arms.gov.uk<mailto:enquiries@college-of-arms.gov.uk>; Paul Egan <clerk@llandough-cc.co.uk<mailto:clerk@llandough-cc.co.uk>>; c1v@valeofglamorgan.gov.uk<mailto:c1v@valeofglamorgan.gov.uk>; ukgeneralenquiries@bp.com<mailto:ukgeneralenquiries@bp.com>\r\n\r\nSubject: Formal Notice for Record: Church View Close, Llandough / BP vs Buckler 1987\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n        You don't often get email from [redacted]<mailto:[redacted]>. Learn why this is important <https://aka.ms/LearnAboutSenderIdentification>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nEXTERNAL: This email originates from outside of The National Archives.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nFORMAL NOTICE OF RECORD CHALLENGE, ASSERTION OF RIGHT, AND DEMAND FOR ENGAGEMENT\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nDear Sir/Madam,\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nThis Notice is issued for placement on official record and for reliance. It is not informal correspondence. I urge you not to resort to silence, deflection, or delay, as doing so following receipt of this notice constitutes knowing reliance upon, and maintenance of, records alleged to be materially defective through fraud, concealment, denial of due process, and systemic erasure. Please report any suspicious activity and discrepancies identified.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nThis Notice is issued in pursuit of narrative justice at this stage; formal judicial remedies are expressly reserved. It serves to document our family's side of the story before it is completely erased and history rewritten, ensuring awareness among custodians of land, heritage, heraldry, archaeology, and public records.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nMy elder brother, Rhys Buckler, and I are the sons of the late William Buckler (d. 1991), defendant in the landmark legal case BP Properties Ltd v Buckler (1987), and the grandchildren of the late Mary Williams, whose dispossession, marginalisation, and exclusion from judicial determination remain unresolved matters of record. Mary Williams was repeatedly and materially falsely addressed/mislabeled in legal and administrative records as “Mrs Buckler,” a misidentification that was never judicially tested and which directly affected the handling, scope, and outcome of proceedings. The Williams/Buckler family held claim to the land title, having acquired and occupied Great House Farm continuously since 1667, based on family records, historical evidence, and centuries of resistance to adverse claims.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nI place on record that I am the first member of my family able to speak publicly and formally on these matters. Other close family members remain too traumatised and intimidated by the historical conduct of these proceedings—including violent eviction, demolition, and loss of livelihood—to engage, despite the continuing consequences of dispossession. That silence must not be misread as consent, acquiescence, or abandonment of right. There is a material distinction between free silence and silence produced by fear, trauma, and coercive experience.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nThe story, as we have pieced it together, reveals a multi-generational pattern of lawfare and siege: from Norman/English conquests in 1100 granting lordships over ancient Welsh Christian landscapes, through monastic (1215–1539) and Crown seizures (1539 onward), to industrial encroachments (e.g., 1876 Limeworks severance without consent) and modern corporate possession actions. This culminated in the 1988 demolition of an 800-year-old farmhouse amid police standoffs, leaving the family homeless and the site scarred, now buried under Church View Close. Archaeological vindication came too late—1994 excavations revealed over 1,000 Roman and medieval burials, confirming family warnings (e.g., the 1870 Roman soldier discovery under the living room floor, suppressed by authorities)—yet heritage records celebrate the site while omitting our dispossession.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nThe following assertions of right are now formally placed on record. They have never been lawfully adjudicated or extinguished:\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n1.      Rhys Buckler may be the rightful Manorial Lord of the Manor of Llandough, by right of primogeniture and continuous hereditary connection arising from our family’s occupation of Great House Farm from 1667 until our narrative, legal, and physical dispossession.\r\n\r\n2.      I assert a claim to act as Squire of the Manor of Llandough, as executive representative and guardian of the Buckler–Williams estate and matriarchal lineage, with a mandate to pursue correction of record, restitution, and restorative remedies.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nThese are assertions of right, not settled declarations, placed on record pending proper adjudication—which has been historically obstructed through procedural narrowing, missing documents, and avoidance of full hearings.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nIn 1974, Mary Williams was twice prevented from presenting her case and obtaining a judicial determination of superior ownership, despite claims grounded in long occupation, intent, and possession. The court confined inquiry to animus possidendi (intention to possess) and excluded animus domini (intention to own), reclassifying her from putative owner to mere occupier and foreclosing title adjudication. BP sought possession only, not ownership rulings—a choice unexplained and significant. Proceedings were adjourned or quashed without resolution.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nThis occurred despite evidence of ownership intent, including:\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n*       Centuries-long occupation and resistance (e.g., failed 1938 auction due to local support; post-1945 rent disputes).\r\n\r\n*       Roman-era remains and historical significance (e.g., Guglielmo Marconi's 1897 residence during radio experiments, with family aid erased from records).\r\n\r\n*       Unformalized deals like the 1895–1905 Daniel Thomas quarrying permission for promised title, undermined by vanished deeds.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nJudicial ownership examination would have engaged archaeological/heritage protections, incompatible with redevelopment—consequences avoided, raising credibility concerns.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nSerious unresolved anomalies persist, indicating something is still not right with the record:\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n*       Licence correspondence dated 31 October 1974 surfacing only on 4 November, potentially interrupting proceedings to prevent hearings.\r\n\r\n*       Claims of 1974 benevolent withdrawal under press scrutiny unsupported by verifiable coverage.\r\n\r\n*       Land registration before Mary Williams’s 1983 death without title determination; disputes possibly lodged/suppressed by Land Registry.\r\n\r\n*       Inconsistent treatment of BP entities across proceedings to family detriment.\r\n\r\n*       Procedural barriers preventing effective defense (e.g., interrupted essential services post-licence; 1960–1987 court rulings dismissing ancestral title as \"notion\" without evidence).\r\n\r\n*       Knowing bypass of archaeological constraints (e.g., 1978 trenches ignored; no contemporaneous 1970s/1990s press on discoveries amid development).\r\n\r\n*       Missing key documents (e.g., 1667 conveyance, 1811–1835 Lambert Williams agreements proving status, 1982–84 library deeds vanishing).\r\n\r\n*       Risks of further erasure: Heritage databases celebrate \"Llandough Roman villa cemetery\" while omitting our saga; modern residents unaware of the \"battleground\" site.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nThese anomalies suggest systemic theft, from colonial English subjugation (e.g., 1543–1770 orbit shifts) to corporate siege, during Wales' cultural/economic transitions. Authority derived through such means is illegitimate under natural justice, customary Welsh principles, and moral-legal frameworks. The record's distortion secures advantage, but passage of time does not validate it.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nPreliminary assessment indicates cumulative benefits from redevelopment (absent title extinguishment) exceed £100 million, forming a basis for reparations—covering lost land, artifacts, livelihood, and trauma.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nI require relevant public servants, custodians, and office-holders (including Land Registry, heraldry bodies, museums, archaeologists, MPs, historians, and councillors) to cease delay, engage substantively, review/correct/annotate records where defective, and prevent further rewriting of history. Justice delayed is denied, especially with verifiable evidence available.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nThis Notice:\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n*       Challenges the record's accuracy, completeness, and legitimacy.\r\n\r\n*       Place assertions of title/standing on record pending adjudication.\r\n\r\n*       Reserves all rights without prejudice.\r\n\r\n*       Puts officials/beneficiaries on notice that silence/reliance may aggravate responsibility.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nFor the avoidance of doubt, His Majesty the King was notified yesterday.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nThis serves as an invitation to engage constructively before reliance becomes untenable.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nRegards, Requester On behalf of the Buckler–Williams family [redacted], Wales. https://bpvsbuckler.datro.xyz/ <https://bpvsbuckler.datro.xyz/>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nPostscript — Disability and Succession (Material Notice): The heir-apparent to the asserted Manorial Lordship is Rhys Buckler’s son, a young man with Down syndrome. Any obstruction, marginalisation, or differential treatment affecting his inheritance would engage the Equality Act 2010 and UK international obligations on disability rights. Disability cannot bar succession or recognition. This is recorded to prevent claims of ignorance.\r\nPlease don't print this e-mail unless you really need to. ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- National Archives Disclaimer This email and any files transmitted with it are intended solely for the use of the individual(s) to whom they are addressed. If you are not the intended recipient and have received this email in error, please notify the sender and delete the email. Opinions, conclusions and other information in this message and attachments that do not relate to the official business of The National Archives are neither given nor endorsed by it. ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\nPlease don't print this e-mail unless you really need to. ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- National Archives Disclaimer This email and any files transmitted with it are intended solely for the use of the individual(s) to whom they are 