ch Room, located in the National Library of Wales, which is open Tuesday through Thursday, 9:30-16:30. If you would like to view the material in person, I would advise arranging with us beforehand so that we can prepare the material ahead of your visit. Some of the material is on microfiche and as we do not have a microfiche reader in the Search Room, we will need to liaise with the National Library Reading Room staff to try to negotiate the use of their machines ahead of your visit. However, I have located an electronic copy of Archive Number 6524751, which can be accessed here: https://www.walesher1974.org/her/app/php/herumd.php?group=GGAT&level=3&docid=301361628\n\nWhile it is possible to order scans of material using the attached order form, we may be unable to provide research-quality scans or photographs of some of the material, such as the material on microfiche, which would require high-resolution scanning at a cost of £20 per scan. Moreover,  many of these records remain under third-party copyright, and we would only be able to provide scans or copies for personal, non-commercial research. Other uses, such as use in a legal case or report would likely require the permission of the copyright holder(s). I would therefore advise arranging to view the material in person rather than ordering scans, if possible.\n\nFinally, I have confirmed with our Records Manager that we do not hold any correspondence related to the site.\n\nThank you again for your enquiry.\n\nSincerely,\n\nAdam Coward\n\n\n\nDr Adam Coward\n Rheolwr Llyfrgell ac Ymholiadau | Library and Enquiries Manager\nFfordd Penglais | Penglais Road, Aberystwyth, [postcode redacted]\n[phone redacted]\n[email redacted] | [email redacted]\nwww.cbhc.gov.uk | www.rcahmw.gov.uk\nNoddir gan Lywodraeth Cymru | Sponsored by Welsh Government\nRydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg a Saesneg. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn peri oedi.\nWe welcome correspondence in Welsh and English. Corresponding in Welsh will not lead to any delay.\n\nYn unol â Rheoliadau Safonau'r Gymraeg (Rhif 2) 2016, mae gennych hawl i gyfathrebu a gohebu â Chomisiwn Brenhinol Henebion Cymru yn eich dewis iaith. Er mwyn sicrhau ein bod yn cyflawni’r hawl, rhowch wybod i ni a ydych yn dymuno derbyn gohebiaeth a/neu alwadau ffôn oddi wrthym yn y Gymraeg. Bydd yr wybodaeth hon yn cael ei chofnodi gennym ni, a byddwn yn defnyddio'r iaith o’ch dewis ym mhob cyfathrebu yn y dyfodol. Diolch.\nUnder the Welsh Language Standards (No. 2) Regulations 2016, you have the right to communicate and correspond with the Royal Commission on the Ancient and Historical Monuments of Wales in your preferred language. To ensure we uphold this right, please let us know whether you wish to receive correspondence and/or telephone calls from us in Welsh. This information will be recorded by us, and we will use your preferred language in all future communication. Thank you.\n​\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]>\nSent: 20 May 2026 22:11\nTo: unclehowell <[email redacted]>\nSubject: Great House Farm, Williams/ Buckler Family, Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan\n\n\nSir / Madam,\n\nTo all those whom it concerns, BCC'd into this email.\n\nI write to request disclosure under the Freedom of Information Act 2000 and, where applicable, the Environmental Information Regulations 2004.\n\nThis request concerns Great House Farm / Ty Mawr (Great House), Llandough, associated estate lands, historic occupation and related heritage records. My family is Buckler and before that Williams, and we occupied Great House Farm from 1667 until our forceful dispossession of it in 1988.  Without our ownership ever having been estiguished and without BP ever having a ruling of ownership, only possession.\n\nIt's alledged our fee simple absolute and manorial title was stolen. And when BP registered our lands in their names in 1982, with our stolen title, my nan who challenged this all her life, during the 2 week land registry window to challenge BP's registration, which they achieved via purgergy and document/ identity fraud, she conveniently died. As my father did after his appeal of the 1987 mistake ruling was mistakely dismissed and his ECHR request blocked and home abruptly demolished, which he was prevented from stopping having been hospitalised and arrested without charges by armed force officers and possible SAS.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/0eXOoj7eUCo?si=hcgSA2pFgEE943HG\n\nI understand records may exist within the National Monuments Record of Wales, Historic Environment Record archives, archaeological consultations and planning files, including references already identified as:\n\n* Great House Farm HER site entry - GGAT03586s\n* Land at Great House Farm HER event entry - GGATE003872\n* The 1990 archaeological assessment commissioned by BP Properties Ltd.\n\nPlease provide copies of, or access to:\n\n  1.  All records concerning Great House Farm / Ty Mawr / Great House Estate, Llandough including:\n\n* surveys\n* reports\n* site notes\n* photographs\n* maps\n* aerial imagery\n* GIS layers\n* archive material\n* annotations\n* title references\n* occupancy references\n* landscape records\n* archaeological observations.\n\n  1.  All internal and external correspondence involving:\n\n* RCAHMW / NMRW\n* Cadw\n* GGAT / Heneb\n* Vale of Glamorgan Council\n* BP Properties Ltd\n* developers\n* planning authorities\n* landowners, estate representatives or consultants.\n\n  1.  Any documents discussing:\n\n* estate ownership\n* occupation history\n* family occupation or association\n* agricultural use\n* commemorative tree planting or memorial planting\n* estate boundaries\n* title history\n* acquisition history\n* disputes concerning ownership, heritage status or land use\n* estate restructuring, extinguishment, compulsory acquisition or title alteration.\n\n  1.  Copies of all material linked to GGAT03586s and GGATE003872, including the archaeological assessment and associated correspondence.\n  2.  Any records identifying historic occupants, proprietors, tenants or family associations, including references to the Williams family where present within archives.\n  3.  Metadata identifying file creators, accession history, provenance, dates and retention schedules.\n  4.  File indexes, archive references or schedules identifying further related material not already listed.\n\nPlease provide information electronically where possible.\n\nIf any material is withheld, please identify the exemption relied upon and provide the public interest assessment.\n\nRegards,\n\n\n\nSion Buckler\n\n[address redacted]\n"},{"id":"wb-f0ecae17","subject":"Rc26 0205 great house farm williams buckler family Llandough","name":"0000-00-00_rc26-0205-great-house-farm-williams-buckler-family-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00-2.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_rc26-0205-great-house-farm-williams-buckler-family-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00-2.txt","threadSlug":"rc26-0205-great-house-farm-williams-buckler-family-llandough","scopeTopics":["morgan","family"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["morgan","family"],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_rc26-0205-great-house-farm-williams-buckler-family-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00-2.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"not-recovered","text":null,"textNotice":"Original text not yet recovered: the archived source file for this record is missing. See sourceUrl."},{"id":"wb-eb6bf2d8","subject":"Re BP v Buckler 1987 documentary","name":"0000-00-00_re-bp-vs-buckler-1987-documentary_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-bp-vs-buckler-1987-documentary_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"bp-v-buckler-1987-documentary","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-bp-vs-buckler-1987-documentary_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"Hi Simon,\r\n\r\nThank you for your inquiry regarding funding for your project. While we\r\nunfortunately do not offer direct funding, I would like to invite you to\r\nconsider applying for the Meetmarket\r\n<https://www.sheffdocfest.com/meetmarket>. If selected, you would have\r\nthe opportunity to meet with international funders, broadcasters,\r\ndistributors, festival programmers and international sales agents.\r\nApplications will open from the 5th of November 2025 to the 28th of January\r\n2026. All the information is on our website, but if you have any further\r\nquestions, please don't hesitate to get in touch.\r\n\r\nBest wishes,\r\n\r\nCamila\r\n-- \r\n\r\n*Camila Arriaga Torres *(she/her)\r\n\r\n*Industry Programme Producer*\r\n\r\n------------------------------------\r\n\r\nSheffield DocFest\r\n\r\nThe Workstation\r\n\r\n15 Paternoster Row\r\n\r\nSheffield S1 2BX\r\n\r\n+44 (0)114 276 5141\r\n\r\nTwitter <https://twitter.com/sheffdocfest> | Instagram\r\n<https://www.instagram.com/sheffdocfest> | Facebook\r\n<https://www.facebook.com/sheffdocfest> | Linkedin\r\n<https://www.linkedin.com/company/sheffield-doc-fest/> | Newsletter\r\n<https://sheffdocfest.us6.list-manage.com/subscribe?u=26cafef22345355298a3cbe9e&id=7912e8bdab>\r\n\r\n2026 Passes on Sale Now <https://www.sheffdocfest.com/attend/industry-pass>\r\n\r\nInternational Documentary Festival Sheffield (Sheffield DocFest) is a\r\ncharitable incorporated organisation registered in England & Wales, charity\r\nnumber: 1184849.\r\n\r\nThis email and any attachments to it are intended solely for the use of the\r\nindividual to whom it is addressed. Any views or opinions expressed are\r\nsolely those of the author and do not necessarily represent those of\r\nSheffield DocFest.\r\n\r\n ----------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n♽ Please consider the environment before printing\r\n\r\n\r\nOn Tue, 28 Oct 2025 at 23:05, Requester <[redacted]> wrote:\r\n\r\n> Hi,\r\n>\r\n> I'm exploring doing a documentary about legal case BP Vs Buckler 1987.\r\n>\r\n> I'm the son of the defendant, my late dad William Buckler.\r\n>\r\n> My last employer and source of income, for my last 22 years in Oath, was\r\n> the Vale of Glamorgan council.\r\n>\r\n> I have a UK company and would like to make a documentary about this case.\r\n> And I'm now seeking co-financeers for this project.\r\n>\r\n> Please email/ call me if you have interest in this project.\r\n>\r\n> Thank you.\r\n>\r\n> Requester\r\n> Director\r\n> DATRO Consortium Ltd\r\n> [redacted]\r\n>\r\n>\r\n"},{"id":"wb-351b8b22","subject":"Re BP v Buckler","name":"0000-00-00_re-bp-vs-buckler_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-bp-vs-buckler_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-bp-vs-buckler_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"Jane,\r\n\r\nI recently acquired the top level domain .scottishbay\r\nThe Scottish Bay is the 70km of Caribbean coastline where myself and other\r\nBritish Subject defectors live.\r\n\r\nWe replaced the nomenclature 'United Technocracy' with the term:\r\nDecentralized Autonomous Society (DAS),\r\nwhich is a delineation of a Decentralized Autonomous Organisation (DAO).\r\n\r\nRegards,\r\n\r\nSion Buckler\r\nFounder\r\ndatro.xyz\r\n\r\nOn Tue, Aug 16, 2016 at 11:06 PM Hywel Apbuckler <[redacted]>\r\nwrote:\r\n\r\n> I just discovered you. This page\r\n> <https://www.hcrlaw.com/people/jane-mayglothling/> told me about you.\r\n> This article <https://www.hcrlaw.com/blog/what-is-a-possessory-title/>\r\n> sent me to that page after I Google'd the terms 'possessory title' .\r\n>\r\n> I am the son of William Buckler. Of the 'BP vs Buckler' case of adverse\r\n> possession.\r\n> I am now 31 years old and in Oath to defend rights and freedoms (Gov ID:\r\n> 25148537).\r\n>\r\n> Not many people are aware of the true story of this case. I'm now able to\r\n> expose the truth thanks to skills I acquired in my career as a Cyber\r\n> Warfare/ Security Engineer.\r\n>\r\n> BP did a back hand deal with an archaeological firm who discovered roman\r\n> burials under our family farm land. The 34 year campaign to remove us from\r\n> our land and steal the gold which lay beneath (to sell to museums) was a\r\n> scandal of that time and still not public information. But the scandal runs\r\n> deeper.\r\n>\r\n> My father died never fully understanding his inalienable or inherent birth\r\n> rights. I single highhandedly wrote the same amount of code it takes to fly\r\n> a US drone or F22 Fighter Jet last year, so reading through and\r\n> understanding the Bill of Rights, Magna Carta and the UK's 750 'statute'\r\n> rules wasn't too difficult for me.\r\n>\r\n> What I concluded from it all, was that democracy is not voting for the\r\n> leader and rule makers of the society we are members of. Democracy is in\r\n> fact, a free British Subjects right to leave, join or form any society we\r\n> choose (with any 'statute' rules we consent to be governed by -\r\n> individually!).\r\n>\r\n> The 1801 UK \"Society\" (Regime) now closer resembles an Evil, Tyrannical,\r\n> Nationwide Cult, which doesn't serve and defend with the allodial title of\r\n> Great Britain we afford it. Its statute rules, in a lot of cases, actually\r\n> violate the laws of the land. We are systematically being\r\n> enslaved-from-birth (made members of this society by child abduction and\r\n> force) and harvested of everything we once had e.g. land, liberty, wealth,\r\n> property and even each other.\r\n>\r\n> So I vow to 'renounce' membership/ citizenship from Britain's new No.1\r\n> Enemy (The 1801 UK \"Society\") and join/form a new British Society. Which I\r\n> have done!!\r\n>\r\n> The 2015 United Technocracy Society was founded and financed by myself and\r\n> 50 other British Subjects, most in Oath and still in active duty. We have a\r\n> stronghold with a defense force and are unofficially a British Protectorate\r\n> (protecting our societies members from UK Regime oppression, austerity,\r\n> genocide, interment, touchless torture etc). I will be inviting my\r\n> Commander-In-Chief to give our new Act of Union Her Royal Assent and I will\r\n> seek Royal Charter once we reach 5,000 members.\r\n>\r\n> I never know how much liberty or life I have remaining making this stand\r\n> against the UK Regime. As it is one of the most powerful in the world. I\r\n> recently had a safe house raided by secret services, I've lost two Private\r\n> Military Security Contractors and my driver was in a Diana-style RTA. But I\r\n> honor my Oath and Father and I am determine to see through what I began as\r\n> it is both legal and lawful and fully warranted.\r\n>\r\n> Google awarded my Technocratic System of Governance two patents in January\r\n> so we're off to a good start. And we've sold our first 'Neo-Dome' today.\r\n> Which provides free internet and electricity to those moving Off-Grid. For\r\n> each 5 we sell we're able to create a new mesh network and a 6th dome will\r\n> be erected for schools, churches and hospitals. I plan to resettle\r\n> (proclaim)back my birth land, once my colonies and defense force have grown\r\n> in strength and numbers.\r\n>\r\n> Yes I am a threat to society! The 1801 UK \"Society\" specifically. But it's\r\n> been more than a threat to our country, families, wealth and children's\r\n> future for many years and someone has to put an end to it! I'm willing and\r\n> able to be the John Gault people needs. I'm a proficient military commander\r\n> and leader, cyber security expect (hacker) and I've made more progress for\r\n> the freeman movement than Dean Clifford, Roger Hayes or John Harris ever\r\n> did.\r\n>\r\n> Thank you for your time reading this.\r\n> I hope you and others alike will pick the right side when the time comes.\r\n>\r\n> Kind Regards,\r\n>\r\n> Hywel ApBuckler\r\n> Prime Technocrat\r\n> United Technocracy, GB\r\n> skype: hywel.buckler\r\n>\r\n>\r\n"},{"id":"wb-f51d0498","subject":"Re BP v Buckler","name":"0000-00-00_re-bp-vs-buckler_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-bp-vs-buckler_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","threadSlug":"bp-v-buckler","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-bp-vs-buckler_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"[Recovered on 19 Oct 2026 from the original email in the family's Gmail, because this archive file was missing. Matched to this entry by its subject line; where several emails share a subject, the order within the group is not certain. Email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses are redacted.]\n\nSubject: Re: BP vs Buckler\nFrom: Requester\nDate: Sat, 15 Nov 2025 06:48 UTC\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nHi,\n\nYes please. Thank you.\n\nIf you have any avaliablity Monday please tell me the best time to call.\nI'm also reachable for scheduled calls on [phone redacted]\n\nOtherwise I'm on WhatsApp, which you can reach out to me on anytime.\n[phone redacted]\n\nRegards,\n\nSion Buckler\n\n\n\n\n\n\nOn Fri, 14 Nov 2025, 15:24 Carroll, George D (Cllr), <\n[email redacted]> wrote:\n\n> Hi Requester\n>\n> Thank you for copying me in on this.\n>\n> If you'd like to discuss, please let me know.\n>\n> All the best,\n>\n> George\n> ------------------------------\n> *From:* Requester <[email redacted]>\n> *Sent:* Friday, November 14, 2025 12:48:36 PM\n> *To:* [email redacted] <[email redacted]\n> >\n> *Subject:* BP vs Buckler\n>\n> You don't often get email from [email redacted]. Learn why this\n> is important <https://aka.ms/LearnAboutSenderIdentification>\n>\n> Sir/Ma'am,\n>\n> I am your constituent, living [location redacted]. I am writing to request your\n> assistance in reviewing a historic legal matter involving my family: *BP\n> Properties Ltd v Buckler (1987)*, relating to Great House Farm, Llandough.\n>\n> My family lived on, cared for and claimed to own that farm for\n> generations. The events surrounding the 1987 case resulted in the loss of\n> our home and land, and left several members of my family deeply affected.\n> Recent research I have undertaken has revealed significant procedural\n> inconsistencies, historical irregularities, unanswered questions regarding\n> corporate identity, unverified service of key documents, and heritage\n> considerations relating to the site.\n>\n> I have attached a short report summarising SOME of these issues. I am\n> requesting your help in identifying the appropriate bodies—legal,\n> historical, or governmental—who may be able to independently review the\n> historical record to ensure fairness, accuracy, and transparency.\n>\n> My interest is not solely personal; Great House Farm has a heritage\n> significance to Wales, including documented accounts that Guglielmo Marconi\n> stayed there while experimenting with early radio technology. I believe\n> this should form part of our preserved historical record.\n>\n> By way of background, I am a veteran and have served both in the Armed\n> Forces and recently with the Vale of Glamorgan Council. My intention is\n> simply to seek clarity, proper review, and justice where appropriate. I\n> would be grateful for your guidance on the correct avenues to pursue.\n>\n> Thank you for your time and consideration. I look forward to your response.\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n>\n> *Requester*\n> [address redacted]\n> [map link redacted]\n> [location redacted]\n> [map link redacted]\n> [postcode redacted]\n> [map link redacted]\n> Tel: [phone redacted]\n>\n"},{"id":"wb-2110b0a9","subject":"Re cais am ddeiseb ac ymchwiliad cyhoeddus achos buckler v b","name":"0000-00-00_re-cais-am-ddeiseb-ac-ymchwiliad-cyhoeddus-achos-buckler-v-b_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-cais-am-ddeiseb-ac-ymchwiliad-cyhoeddus-achos-buckler-v-b_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"cais-am-ddeiseb-ac-ymchwiliad-cyhoeddus-achos-buckler-v-b","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-cais-am-ddeiseb-ac-ymchwiliad-cyhoeddus-achos-buckler-v-b_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester,\r\n\r\nThank you for your message. Senedd petitions are created and managed through an online system<https://petitions.senedd.wales/petitions/check>, and must meet the standards for petitions<https://senedd.wales/senedd-business/petitions/standards-for-petitions/>, and full rules on proper form<https://petitions.senedd.wales/rules>. We would encourage you to check these first, as a Senedd petition may not be the most appropriate channel to pursue your issue.\r\n\r\nCofion/Regards,\r\n\r\nLara.\r\n\r\n[cid:image001.png@01DCA006.653C2280]\r\nLara Date\r\nAil Glerc, Pwyllgor Deisebau, Senedd Cymru\r\nSecond Clerk, Petitions Committee, Welsh Parliament\r\n________________________________\r\n[cid:image002.png@01DCA006.653C2280]\r\n0300 200 6381\r\n\r\n[cid:image003.png@01DCA006.653C2280]\r\nwww.senedd.cymru<http://www.senedd.cymru/>\r\nwww.senedd.wales<http://www.senedd.wales/>\r\n[cid:image004.png@01DCA006.653C2280]\r\n@SeneddDeisebau\r\n@SeneddPetitions\r\n[cid:image005.png@01DCA006.653C2280]\r\nseneddcymru<https://www.facebook.com/seneddcymru>\r\nseneddwales<https://www.facebook.com/seneddwales/>\r\n________________________________\r\nSenedd Cymru yw’r corff sy’n cael ei ethol yn ddemocrataidd i gynrychioli buddiannau Cymru a’i phobl. Mae’r Senedd, fel y’i gelwir, yn deddfu ar gyfer Cymru, yn cytuno ar drethi yng Nghymru, ac yn dwyn Llywodraeth Cymru i gyfrif.\r\nThe Welsh Parliament is the democratically elected body that represents the interests of Wales and its people. Commonly known as the Senedd, it makes laws for Wales, agrees Welsh taxes and holds the Welsh Government to account.\r\nCroesewir gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg neu Saesneg | We welcome correspondence in Welsh or English\r\n\r\nDylid ystyried unrhyw ddatganiad neu sylw a geir yn y neges hon fel un personol ac nid o reidrwydd yn fynegiant o safbwynt Senedd Cymru, unrhyw ran ohoni neu unrhyw gorff cysylltiedig.\r\nAny of the statements or comments made above should be regarded as personal and not necessarily those of the Welsh Parliament, any constituent part or connected body.\r\n\r\n________________________________\r\n\r\n\r\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]>\r\nSent: 01 February 2026 11:02\r\nTo: Petitions Committee | Y Pwyllgor Deisebau <Petitions@senedd.wales>\r\nSubject: Cais am Ddeiseb ac Ymchwiliad Cyhoeddus: Achos Buckler v BP (1987) a Dinistr Great House Farm, Llandough / Formal Request for Inquiry: The Great House Farm Dispossession (BP vs Buckler)\r\n\r\n[https://senedd.wales/media/obuo0sep/senedd_logo_xs.png]\r\nE-mail protection couldn't recognize this email as this is the first time you received an email from this sender [redacted]\r\n\r\n\r\nAelodau Anrhydeddus y Senedd,\r\n\r\nRwy’n ysgrifennu atoch i dynnu eich sylw at yr hyn yr wyf yn ei ystyried yn anghyfiawnder hanesyddol difrifol, yn ymwneud â’m teulu, y Bucklers, a dinistr Great House Farm yn Llandough — safle fferm a chartref o dros 800 mlynedd o hanes, gan gynnwys cysylltiadau mynachaidd a chysylltiad uniongyrchol â Guglielmo Marconi yn ystod datblygiad cynnar radio.\r\n\r\nMae’r mater hwn yn codi cwestiynau difrifol ynghylch hawliau tir, gweithdrefnau cyfreithiol, cofrestru tir, a hawliau dynol yng Nghymru.\r\n\r\nEr enghraifft, mewn datganiad i’r wasg yn 1974, dywedodd fy nain yn gyhoeddus: “It’s my home.” Mae’r datganiad hwn, sydd yn fater o gofnod cyhoeddus, yn gwrth-ddweud yn uniongyrchol ganfyddiad Llys Apêl 1987 yn BP v Buckler, lle dywedwyd ei bod wedi bod yn “ddistaw” ac wedi derbyn trwydded BP a gyhoeddwyd yn 1974.\r\n\r\nYn yr un achos, gwrthodwyd apêl fy nhad ar y sail bod BP Pension Trust Ltd a BP Properties Ltd yn “effeithiol yr un cwmni”. Fodd bynnag, yn y dyfarniad yr oedd yn ei herio, nododd y barnwr nad oedd ymatebion fy nain i BP Pension Trust Ltd yn cyfrif gan mai BP Properties Ltd oedd yr hawlydd, ac mai cwmnïau ar wahân oeddent. Yn ymarferol, roedd hyn yn arwain at sefyllfa “pen y cwmni’n ennill, cynffon y teulu’n colli”.\r\n\r\nYn sgil hyn, cafodd fy nhad ei dynnu o’i wely ysbyty, ei garcharu heb gyhuddiad, a gosodwyd gorchymyn atal arno i’w atal rhag meddiannu ac amddiffyn ein fferm 800 oed rhag cael ei dymchwel. Cafodd ei gais i’r Llys Hawliau Dynol Ewropeaidd ei rwystro cyn iddo farw yn 1991, yn ddim ond 41 oed.\r\n\r\nAr y sail hon, rwy’n gofyn yn ffurfiol am Ddeiseb i sefydlu Ymchwiliad Cyhoeddus i’r achos hwn, fel mater o ddwyn tir hanesyddol ac o dorri hawliau dynol yng Nghymru.\r\n\r\nRwy’n weithiwr proffesiynol ym maes seiber a rhyfela electronig milwrol. Mae technolegau AI pwerus bellach ar gael i bob un ohonom, ac nid oes unrhyw reswm technegol pam na ellid archwilio’r mater hwn yn llawn ac yn drylwyr. Mae’r hyn a ddigwyddodd i’m teulu yn ymddangos fel anghyfiawnder dwys, aml-genhedlaethol, ac yn debyg i ffurf ar “lawfare”, lle y trinwyd fy nheulu fel llafur di-dâl i gynnal ased tir hyd nes yr oedd yn gyfleus i’w ariannu. Mae’n arwyddocaol bod bron pob cam yn ein herbyn wedi digwydd ar yr eiliad olaf posibl.\r\n\r\nCeir honiadau bod sylw sylweddol yn y wasg yn 1974 o blaid fy nain a’n teulu, ynghyd â deiseb o tua 1,700 o bobl yn ceisio atal dymchwel y fferm. Ymhellach, mae’n ymddangos bod CADW wedi methu ag ymyrryd lle dylai’r safle fod wedi cael statws Adeilad Rhestredig Gradd II.\r\n\r\nNid wyf yn sicr a gofnodwyd erioed yn briodol yn y Gofrestrfa Tir fod y tir — a gofrestrwyd gan BP yn 1982 — dan anghydfod. Yn fy nealltwriaeth i, cafodd ei drin fel pe na bai mewn anghydfod o gwbl. Nid oes unrhyw lys wedi penderfynu’n derfynol mai BP oedd perchnogion cyfreithiol y tir; cafodd hynny ei honni yn hytrach na’i brofi.\r\n\r\nMae hefyd fater pwysig o hanes diwylliannol. Arhosodd Marconi gyda’n teulu yn y fferm wrth iddo ddatblygu radio. Mae cynghorwyr a’r plwyf yn Llandough yn gwrthod cydnabod hyn. Rwy’n deall bod Marconi yn cael ei ystyried yn wleidyddol eithafol ar y dde, ac, fel y gwelir yn achos y cerflun radio ym Mae Caerdydd, mae ei rôl hanesyddol yn cael ei hailysgrifennu. Mae’n rhesymol tybio bod ein teulu wedi cael ei ddal mewn ymgyrch wleidyddol ehangach o’r chwith yn erbyn y dde, ac mai am roi llety i Marconi y bu i ni wynebu canlyniadau canrif o hyd — problemau nad oedd yn bodoli yn y tair canrif flaenorol yr oeddem wedi byw ar y fferm.\r\n\r\nRoedd mam Marconi hefyd yn Jamerson o gwmni wisgi Gwyddelig Jameson. Rwy’n gofyn i chi gynnal eich ymchwil eich hun, gwneud eich ymholiadau eich hun, a dod i’ch casgliadau eich hun — ond yn anad dim, peidiwch â chaniatáu i hanes gael ei ailysgrifennu yma.\r\n\r\nMae fy nheulu bellach yn archwilio opsiynau ar gyfer siarad cyhoeddus, hawliau stori, a dogfennaeth. Yn nhermau cysylltiadau teuluol: fy nhaid oedd Ray Smith o Dempsey and Makepeace; mae fy modryb yn Shan Cothi; a dechreuodd fy ewythr y band Tigertailz.\r\n\r\nRwy’n disgrifio’r sefyllfa hon fel gwarthus. Mae fy nheulu’n mynnu cyfiawnder am gael ein dadleoli a’n dwyn o’n tir a’n heiddo gan y Sefydliad Prydeinig a BP, gyda phrin unrhyw amddiffyniad dan y gyfraith. Rydym yn deulu Cymreig brodorol, Cymraeg eu hiaith, wedi byw yng Nghaerdydd, Bro Morgannwg, a’r fferm yn Llandough ers cannoedd o flynyddoedd.\r\n\r\nO’r hyn y gallaf ei gasglu, ceir dwsinau o achosion posibl o dwyll a chuddio gwybodaeth, sy’n golygu nad yw statud cyfyngiadau yn berthnasol o reidrwydd. I’r rhai sy’n cefnogi dad-drefedigaethu Cymru ac annibyniaeth, prin y ceir enghraifft gliriach na BP v Buckler (1987) o’r hyn a ddigwyddodd i deulu ffermio Cymreig wrth herio’r Wladwriaeth Brydeinig a chorfforaeth fawr. Nid yw eraill wedi codi’r mater hwn, yn bennaf oherwydd trawma cenhedlaethol.\r\n\r\nRwyf wedi profi fy hun. Gadewais fy ngyrfa filwrol gydag anrhydedd, gan gwblhau fy llw 22 mlynedd. Cefais fy nghyflogi’n bona fide gan Gyngor Bro Morgannwg a Llywodraeth Cymru hyd at 2024. Gwasanaethais mewn rhyfeloedd yn y Dwyrain Canol. Fodd bynnag, nid yw’r anghyfiawnder fferm yn fater unigol.\r\n\r\nYn 2013, cafodd fy mab 36 diwrnod oed ei gipio, ac nid wyf erioed wedi ei weld ers hynny. Cafodd tŷ ei ddwyn oddi arnaf (40 Lombard Street, Y Barri). Yn 2023, cefais fy ngadael i gysgu mewn pabell tra roedd bron i 100 o Afghanistaniaid yn cael eu lletya’n dawel wrth ymyl ein pentref o 11 tŷ, tra roedd yn rhaid imi aros yno i ofalu am fy nain gan na allai gofalwyr ei chyrraedd.\r\n\r\nO ganlyniad, rwyf bellach yn byw yn yr hyn a elwir yn Setliad Cymreig yn yr Ariannin. Rwyf wedi fy nisodli ac wedi fy dadleoli i’r eithaf. Serch hynny, rwy’n ddiolchgar fy mod wedi goroesi gyda’m rhyddid, fy mywyd, a’m henw da yn gymharol gyfan. Serch hynny, hoffwn fod wedi aros yng Nghymru, fel Cymro, gyda safon a chynhaliaeth bywyd deg.\r\n\r\nMae fy ngwraig a minnau’n amharod i ganiatáu i’n merch hyd yn oed gamu ar Ynysoedd Prydain o ystyried yr hyn a glywn sy’n digwydd yno. Yn 2024, allforiais hyd yn oed fy nghi o Abertawe i’n heiddo yn y Caribî ar ôl i unigolyn Afghanistan fod yng ngardd fy nheulu yng Nghymru, gan geisio ei ddwyn.\r\n\r\nGofynnaf i’r Senedd gymryd y mater hwn o ddifrif, ac ystyried sefydlu Ymchwiliad Cyhoeddus llawn, tryloyw ac annibynnol. Nid er budd fy nheulu yn unig y mae hyn, ond er uniondeb hanes, cyfiawnder tir, a hawliau dynol yng Nghymru.\r\n\r\nYn gywir,\r\n\r\nSion Buckler\r\n\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n\r\nSirs/Ma'am,\r\n\r\nI am writing to you regarding a significant historical injustice involving my family, the Bucklers, and the destruction of Great House Farm (an 800-year-old site of monastic and Marconi history).\r\n\r\nFor example, in a 1974 press statement my grandmother said publically 'It's my home'—that directly contradicts the 1987 Court of Appeal ruling that she was 'silent' and accepted of BP license issued in 1974. My dad's appeal for the BP vs Buckler 1987 case also got dismissed on grounds both BP Pension Trust Ltd and BP Properties Ltd were effectively the same company. But in the ruling he was appealing, the judge said that my nans replies to BP Pensions Trust Ltd didn't count as BP Properties Ltd were the claimant, and the two were seperate companies. So it was heads they win, tails we lose. My dad was ripped from his hospital bed, jailed without charge and slapped with a restraining order to keep him from occupying and defending our 800 year old farm from being bulldozed down. His ECHR request blocked before he died in 1991 aged just 41.\r\n\r\nI am requesting a Petition for a Public Inquiry into this case as a matter of historical land theft and human rights violations in Wales.\r\n\r\nI'm a military cyber and electronic warfare professional. We all have very powerful AI technology at our disposal which can make light work out of all of this. My family suffered an incredible injustice. It also appears to be multi-generational warfare/ lawfare. Whereby we were treated as free labour to upkeep the asset of the land and property until it was ripe to be cashed in. I say this as every action against us seemed to be last minute.\r\n\r\nThere was allegedly press covering the 74 case and supporting my nan and family and farm. There was a petition of 1700 people trying to defend us and the farm from demolishment. CADW looked the other way when it should have been a Grade II listed building. I could go on and on and on.\r\n\r\nI'm not even sure the land registry recorded that our land, being registered in 1982 by BP , was in dispute. I believe it was treated as if it wasn't dispited. No court actually definitively determined that BP were the landowners. It was just asserted.\r\n\r\nMarconi stayed with our family at our farm when inventing radio. The councillors and parish at Llandough won't even acknowledge this. I understand Marconi was far right and so, like the radio statute at Cardiff Bay has confirmed, he is being re-written out of history.  It stands to reason that our family was caught in this political campaign of left vs right. And for helping Marconi we found ourselves experiencing a century of problems afterwards. Which didn't exist for the 3 centuries we were at our farm before letting far right marconi stay with us. Marconi's mother was also Jamerson of the Irish Whiskey company.\r\n\r\nDo your own homework. Make your own enquiries. Draw your own conclusions.\r\n\r\n But please do not let history be rewritten here. My family are exploring options for public speaking, story rights, and a documentary etc.\r\n\r\nMy grandfather was Ray Smith, of Dempsey and Makepeace. My aunty Shan Cothi. My uncle started the band tigertailz.\r\n\r\nThis is an outrage and my family demands justice for being displaced and stolen from, land and property, by the British Establishment/ BP -  with little to no protections under law. And we're indigenous to the land, Welsh, Welsh speaking, Welsh family. Resident in Cardiff / Vale and that farm in Llandough for HUNDREDS OF YEARS.\r\n\r\nThere's tens of counts of fraud and concealment from what I can gather. Which means the statute of limitation doesn't apply in this instance. I know some are in favour of Wales decolonising and gaining independence. I think there's no better example of BP vs Buckler 1987 and what has happened to my family. Nobody else will bring this up because there's generational trauma. Notwithstanding we, a welsh farming family, are speaking up and against the British State and BP.\r\n\r\nBut I've proven myself. Leaving my military career with honours. Finishing my 22 years Oath, bona fide, employed by the Vale of Glamorgan Council, Welsh Government until 2024. I served in the wars in the middle east. I don't know what is going on, on that island. But the farm injustice isn't the only matter. My 36 day old son was abducted in 2013 and I've never seen him since. I had a house stolen (40 Lombard Street, Barry). And in 2023 I was left to sleep in a tent, while almost 100 afghans were secretly housed next to our 11 home village. Where I had to be to care for my grandmother, as no carers couldn't reach her. So I'm currently at the Welsh Settlement in Argentina. So I'm as displaced and replaced as I can be to cater for the insanity that's going on, on that island. And grateful I've managed to survive it all with my liberty and life and reputation somewhat still intact. But I would have actually liked to have remained in Wales as a Welshman. And have some quality and standard of life. My wife and I are reluctant for our daughter to even step foot on the British Isles with what we're hearing that's going on there lately. And I even exported my dog from Swansea to our place in the Caribbean in 2024 after an afghan was in my family's garden in Wales, trying to steal him.\r\n\r\nI look forward to your reply,\r\n\r\nRegards,\r\n\r\nSion Buckler\r\n"},{"id":"wb-a1730f45","subject":"Re EIR FOI request 00210772 great house farm church view clo","name":"0000-00-00_re-eir_foi-request-00210772-great-house-farm-church-view-clo_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"FOI / EIR correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-eir_foi-request-00210772-great-house-farm-church-view-clo_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"eir-foi-request-00210772-great-house-farm-church-view-clo","scopeTopics":["email","ghf"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email","ghf"],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-eir_foi-request-00210772-great-house-farm-church-view-clo_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"[Recovered on 19 Oct 2026 from the original email in the family's Gmail, because this archive file was missing. Matched to this entry by its subject line; where several emails share a subject, the order within the group is not certain. Email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses are redacted.]\n\nSubject: Re: EIR/FOI request - 00210772 (Great House Farm / Church View Close, Llandough) – Further Clarification and Request for Full Disclosure\nFrom: Requester\nDate: Sun, 07 Jun 2026 06:28 UTC\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nFOI Unit / Evelyn Morgan / Information Governance Team,\n\nThank you for your email dated 2 June 2026 and for the assistance provided\nso far in relation to reference 00210772.\n\nI appreciate the Council's efforts to support this important family matter\nand note your confirmation that no records are held regarding the private\nlegal proceedings in *BP Properties Ltd v Buckler*.\n\nAs background, this case related to the longstanding claims of my\nWilliams/Buckler family to ownership of Great House Farm (Ty Mawr). My\ngrandmother Mary Williams maintained until her conveniently timed death in\n1983 (in the 2 week window to challenge BP's registration of our home and\nland since 1667, in their names) that our family held ownership of the\nhouse following arrangements with Daniel Thomas involving the quarry, with\nthe surrounding farmland subject to a lease that defaulted.\n\nThere was a conveyance for the surrounding land and attempt to merge our\nfarmhouse and garden into that single plot in 1975. Which was challenged by\nmy nan. Something HM Land Registry correspondence (titles WA231076 and\nWA240304, first registered to BP Properties Limited in 1982/1983)\nreferences these conveyances and the Court of Appeal decision, which may\nassist in narrowing relevant planning and heritage searches.\n\nTo enable your Planning, Heritage, and other relevant teams to conduct\nthorough searches without further delay, I confirm that enquiries should\ncover the historic Great House Farm / Ty Mawr estate in Llandough (adjacent\nto and including areas now known as Church View Close, north side of\nLeckwith Road, title WA231076). This includes any planning permissions,\nheritage assessments, demolition records (circa 1988), tenancy notations,\nenvironmental information, or correspondence from 1875 onwards.\n\nI am keen to receive a full and timely response within the statutory\ntimeframe, drawing on all information the Council holds across departments.\nThis is of profound personal and historic importance to the Williams family\ndescendants as we seek to document and substantiate our family's position.\nShould any records have been transferred to Glamorgan Archives or handled\nin any other way, details of this would be most helpful.\n\nThank you once again for your understanding and support in this sensitive\nmatter. I look forward to your substantive reply at the earliest\nopportunity.\n\nKind regards,\n\n*Requester Esq. (Ret'd Sergeant)* bpvsbuckler.datro.xyz [address redacted],\n[location redacted] [postcode redacted] (On behalf of the Williams / Buckler family\ndescendants)\n"},{"id":"wb-22a6701e","subject":"Re enquiry","name":"0000-00-00_re-enquiry_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-enquiry_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"enquiry","scopeTopics":["email"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email"],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-enquiry_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"[Recovered on 19 Oct 2026 from the original email in the family's Gmail, because this archive file was missing. Matched to this entry by its subject line; where several emails share a subject, the order within the group is not certain. Email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses are redacted.]\n\nSubject: Re: Enquiry\nFrom: Valley Vets Hospital Reception\nDate: Wed, 28 Feb 2024 11:50 UTC\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nGood morning,\n\nI hope you're well.\n\nI would contact your vets and ask them to find all the information for you directly.  It may also be worth researching yourself as to what documents may be needed for Europe.\n\nYour vets should be able to assist you with this, unfortunately we are unable to as we are an emergency and referral hospital only.\n\nKind regards,\n\n\nValley Veterinary Hospital\n\nVetPartners Practices Limited T/A Valley Vets\n\n\n\nT: [phone redacted]\n\nE: [email redacted]\n\nwww.valleyvets.net<http://www.valleyvets.net/>\n\n\n\n[cid:[email redacted]41050]\n\n\n\nVetPartners Practices Ltd trading as Valley Vets\n\nRegistered Office: Spitfire House, Aviator Ct, York [postcode redacted]\n\nRegistered in England & Wales No 10084952 VAT Registration No: 228 9288 65\n\n\n\nThe information contained in this e-mail and any attachments is confidential and may be the subject of legal, professional or another privilege. It is intended only for the named addressee(s) and may not be disclosed to anyone without the consent of Valley Vets. If you are not the named addressee, you must not use, disclose, distribute, copy, print or rely on the contents of the e-mail. In such an event, please contact us and destroy the e-mail immediately.\n\nComputer Viruses: We make reasonable attempts to exclude any virus or any other defect which might affect any computer or IT system, from this email and any attachments, but it is the responsibility of the recipient to ensure that they are virus free and we accept no liability for the loss or damage arising in any way from their receipt or use.\n\nOpinions Expressed in E-mail: These are the opinions of the individual expressing them and are not necessarily the opinions of Valley Vets.\n\n________________________________\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]>\nSent: 28 February 2024 11:39\nTo: Valley Vets Hospital Reception <[email redacted]>\nSubject: Enquiry\n\nI want to take my dog to Dominican republic. on the 10th March I want to get the ferry to Holland. Train ride down to Brussels. And fly from Brussels airport the next morning.\n\nI believe I need an export certificate for my country of destination (form 3899ehc). Although it does say for Great Britain and I'm flying from Europe.\n\nI'm very confused. I reached out to my nearest vets and it's been a week and I'm still confused. They say they'll do the EU health certificate, but I'm sure I need this export health certificate too.\n\nIt's not a holiday. I'm moving him to wife and my villa out there.\n\nLook forward to your reply,\n\nRegards,\n\nSion\n[phone redacted]\n"},{"id":"wb-127805bb","subject":"Re FOI","name":"0000-00-00_re-foi_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"FOI / EIR correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-foi_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"foi","scopeTopics":["email"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email"],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-foi_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"[Recovered on 19 Oct 2026 from the original email in the family's Gmail, because this archive file was missing. Matched to this entry by its subject line; where several emails share a subject, the order within the group is not certain. Email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses are redacted.]\n\nSubject: Re: FOI\nFrom: Requester\nDate: Mon, 27 Jul 2026 18:05 UTC\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nDisclosure Team,\n\nSee attached.\n\nI look forward to your reply,\n\nRegards,\n\nSion Buckler (Ret'd Sergeant)\nRoyal Signals Cyber & Electronic Warfare Professional and Life Member\nWilliams/Buckler Family Heir & Successor\n[phone redacted]\n\nOn Mon, 8 Jun 2026, 04:10 Disclosure Team, <[email redacted]>\nwrote:\n\n> Morning,\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you for your email.\n>\n> As you are requesting information which may be held in archived records,\n> we need further information in order to deal with your request.\n>\n>\n>\n> If you haven’t already, please could you provide the full name, full date\n> of birth and any other relevant information e.g. date of\n> conviction/imprisonment, name of prison, prisoner number, region where\n> individual resided before imprisonment. We will have a better chance of\n> finding the information you seek if you include as much detail as possible.\n>\n>\n>\n> You may wish to note that it is a general policy of the Ministry of\n> Justice (MoJ) not to disclose, to a third party, personal information about\n> another living person. This is because the MoJ has obligations under the\n> Data Protection Act and in law generally to protect this information. Your\n> request for personal information has been considered in line with our\n> obligations under the Freedom of Information Act. In the event that we hold\n> the information you request, we will require the written authority of the\n> Data Subject of the information you want the information about. If the\n> individual is now deceased, we will require a copy of official\n> documentation confirming that they are deceased such as death certificate.\n>\n>\n>\n> Kind regards\n>\n>\n>\n> [image: signature_1893814455]\n>\n>\n>\n> *Disclosure Team*\n>\n> Security and Information Directorate\n>\n> Service Transformation Group\n>\n> Ministry of Justice\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> *From:* Requester <[email redacted]>\n> *Sent:* 07 June 2026 10:03\n> *To:* Disclosure Team <[email redacted]>\n> *Subject:* FOI\n>\n>\n>\n> Sirs,\n>\n> Please will you locate/access probate records for Mary Williams (d. March\n> 1983), Great House Farm, Llandough—any wills, affidavits, or ownership\n> claims referenced?\n>\n> Thanks,\n>\n> Requester Ret'd Sgt\n> ------------------------------\n> This e-mail and any attachments is intended only for the attention of the\n> addressee(s). Its unauthorised use, disclosure, storage or copying is not\n> permitted. If you are not the intended recipient, please destroy all copies\n> and inform the sender by return e-mail. Internet e-mail is not a secure\n> medium. Any reply to this message could be intercepted and read by someone\n> else. Please bear that in mind when deciding whether to send material in\n> response to this message by e-mail. This e-mail (whether you are the sender\n> or the recipient) may be monitored, recorded and retained by the Ministry\n> of Justice. Monitoring / blocking software may be used, and e-mail content\n> may be read at any time. You have a responsibility to ensure laws are not\n> broken when composing or forwarding e-mails and their contents.\n>\n"},{"id":"wb-0b7537ea","subject":"Re fw manor of Llandough mdr entries","name":"0000-00-00_re-fw-manor-of-llandough-mdr-entries_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-fw-manor-of-llandough-mdr-entries_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"fw-manor-of-llandough-mdr-entries","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-fw-manor-of-llandough-mdr-entries_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Mr. Gale,\r\n\r\nThank you again for your helpful reply and for viewing the original\r\nManorial Documents Committee returns (HMC9/311). I entirely understand that\r\nit is difficult to advise further without more specifics, and I am grateful\r\nfor the steer toward the Penrice and Margam Estate Records and the NLW\r\ncatalogue.\r\n\r\nTo answer your question directly: the document historically referred to\r\nwithin the family as the *“Williams Title”* (or the equitable interest\r\nconveyance that underpinned it) dates to the period *c. 1900–1915*. It\r\narises from an agreement between John Williams (occupier of Great House\r\nFarm / Ty Mawr / Cedfin) and Daniel Thomas & Son (Bute Estate quarry\r\noperators / limestoners). In essence, the Williams family granted\r\npermission for quarrying on part of the farm in return for equitable title\r\n/ permanent security of tenure once quarrying was complete. Daniel Thomas\r\nis said to have formalised his side by a land-swap deed with the Bute\r\nEstate (copy formerly held in Cardiff Library, now reported missing along\r\nwith its index card in 1984).\r\n\r\nThis places the key transaction squarely in the early 20th-century Bute\r\nestate rationalisation period, immediately before the 1916 agricultural\r\ntenancy to John Williams and the 1915 death of Daniel Thomas (after which\r\nBute attempted eviction, only for the equitable interest to be asserted\r\nsuccessfully at the time). Family testimony, supported by the 1876\r\nlimeworks severance (Glamorgan Archives D153/4-8) and the known industrial\r\nquarrying context, points to this narrow window as the origin of the modern\r\ntitle dispute.\r\n\r\n*Why this matters now* The 1987 Court of Appeal decision (*BP Properties\r\nLtd v Buckler*) turned on the technical effect of 1974 unilateral licence\r\nletters addressed to “Mrs Buckler” – a name my grandmother Mary Williams\r\n(née Williams) never used in her marriage or death records. By addressing\r\nher under this legal fiction, the proceedings bypassed any adjudication of\r\nthe Williams equitable title that had been recognised as late as 1915. As\r\nthe principle “fraud unravels all” makes clear, once the foundational\r\nmisnomer is identified, the entire chain of subsequent possession orders,\r\nlicences and registration falls to be re-examined. My grandmother and her\r\nheirs therefore retain the superior land claim; the 1988 dispossession and\r\ndemolition of the 800-year-old farmhouse constitute actionable loss for\r\nwhich reparations are now sought.\r\n\r\nTo strengthen the evidential base I have prepared a one-page interactive\r\ntimeline (hosted at [insert your hosted link here after uploading]) that\r\nhighlights every event directly relevant to the “Williams Title” period.\r\nWith your elevated archival access, you will immediately see where to focus\r\nsearches in the Penrice and Margam or Bute collections (e.g., 1895–1916\r\nleases, conveyances, limeworks/quarrying files, and any Daniel Thomas\r\nreferences).\r\n\r\nI would be most grateful if you could now guide me to the precise\r\nsub-series or box references within those collections that cover Llandough\r\nmanorial transactions in the 1900–1916 window. Any surviving index cards,\r\nrental entries, or correspondence mentioning John Williams / Daniel Thomas\r\nin connection with Great House Farm would be transformative.\r\n\r\nThank you once more for your time and expertise. I look forward to your\r\nfurther assistance.\r\n\r\nKind regards\r\n\r\nSion Buckler\r\n\r\n\r\nOn Thu, 26 Feb 2026 at 14:44, Philip Gale <\r\nPhilip.Gale@nationalarchives.gov.uk> wrote:\r\n\r\n> Dear Requester,\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> I have had an opportunity to view the file of original returns to the\r\n> Manorial Documents Committee (Ref. *HMC9/311*) and the single return for\r\n> the Manor of Llandough (see the attached image), and it provides less\r\n> information than the existing entries on the on-line MDR. The information\r\n> on the other manors in the return is equally sparse This may be because\r\n> many of the surviving manorial records were not in custody of the National\r\n> Library of Wales at the time of the original survey.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> The best course of action is to search the archive service catalogues, and\r\n> particularly the on-line catalogue of the National Library of Wales. Is it\r\n> difficult to advise you further without more information, for example do\r\n> you have rough date for the document called ‘Williams’s Title? A\r\n> particularly  relevant archive collection appears to be the Penrice and\r\n> Margam Estate Records, [c. 1147]-[19 cent.] / Penrice and Margam estate. -\r\n> National Library of Wales\r\n> <https://discover.library.wales/discovery/fulldisplay?docid=alma99193071102419&context=L&vid=44WHELF_NLW:44WHELF_NLW_NUI&lang=en&search_scope=In_The_Library&adaptor=Local%20Search%20Engine&tab=In_The_Library&query=any,contains,Talbots%20of%20margam%20archives&offset=0>\r\n> as it includes the surviving manorial records for Llandough.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> There is useful guidance on the different types of title deeds published\r\n> by the University of Nottingham which might be helpful when searching\r\n> catalogues and consulting original property records:\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Introduction to deeds in depth - The University of Nottingham\r\n> <https://www.nottingham.ac.uk/manuscriptsandspecialcollections/researchguidance/deedsindepth/introduction.aspx>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> I am sorry not to be able to provide any additional sources for your\r\n> continuing research.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Kind regards\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> [image: The National Archives logo]\r\n>\r\n> *Philip Gale *|* Head of the Collections and Cultural Property Team*\r\n> T: 020 3908 X 9193 | W: nationalarchives.gov.uk\r\n> <http://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/>\r\n> Twitter: @UkNatArchives\r\n> The National Archives, Kew, Richmond, Surrey TW9 4DU\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> *From:* Requester <[redacted]>\r\n> *Sent:* 24 February 2026 17:42\r\n> *To:* Philip Gale <Philip.Gale@nationalarchives.gov.uk>\r\n> *Subject:* Re: Manor of Llandough - MDR Entries\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> *EXTERNAL*: This email originates from outside of The National Archives.\r\n>\r\n> You are a delight.\r\n>\r\n> My family thanks you.\r\n>\r\n> This means a great deal.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Requester\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> On Tue, 24 Feb 2026 at 13:50, Philip Gale <\r\n> Philip.Gale@nationalarchives.gov.uk> wrote:\r\n>\r\n> Dear Requester,\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Thank you for your e-mail and for clarifying the subject of your enquiry.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> As you know the Manorial Documents Register (MDR) only gives general\r\n> descriptions of the content, extent and location of the manorial records\r\n> recorded in the register: LLANDOUGH MANOR | The National Archives\r\n> <https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/c/F236775> The\r\n> manorial records for the Manor of Llandough are held at the National\r\n> Library of Wales, Glamorgan Archives and West Glamorgan Archives, and their\r\n> catalogues can be accessed on-line : NLW Catalogue\r\n> <https://discover.library.wales/discovery/search?vid=44WHELF_NLW:44WHELF_NLW_NUI>\r\n> , Glamorgan Archives Catalogue <https://canfod.glamarchives.gov.uk/en/>\r\n> and Archive Collections - Swansea\r\n> <https://www.swansea.gov.uk/archivecollections?lang=en>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> The Welsh entries in the online MDR were added in the 1990’s were based on\r\n> the survey returns to the Manorial Documents Committee received from\r\n> institutions and private owners during the 1920’s. These were subsequently\r\n> passed to the custody of the Historical Manuscripts Commission in 1959\r\n> which amalgamated with the Public Record Office to form The National\r\n> Archives in 2003. I have requested to see the original returns for Manor of\r\n> Llandough to see if they can provide any additional information on the\r\n> documentation relating to the manor (Ref. *HMC 9/311*:\r\n> https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/r/C5879336 )\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> There are several other manors with a similar name in Glamorgan and a MDR\r\n> search using the search term “Llandough” gives the following result: Search\r\n> results: Llandough Glamorgan | The National Archives\r\n> <https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/results/c?_q=Llandough&_sf=text_man&_ocn=Glamorgan&_naet=M&_st=mdrc>\r\n> Sometimes the administration of different manors were brought together and\r\n> this appears to have occurred  with the Llandough and St May’s Church\r\n> Manors :\r\n> https://archives.library.wales/index.php/manors-of-llandough-and-st-mary-church\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> I will contact you again once I have had an opportunity to look at the\r\n> original returns sent to Manorial Documents Committee. I hope this\r\n> preliminary information is of assistance.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Yours sincerely\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> [image: The National Archives logo]\r\n>\r\n> *Philip Gale *|* Head of the Collections and Cultural Property Team*\r\n> T: 020 3908 X 9193 | W: nationalarchives.gov.uk\r\n> <http://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/>\r\n> Twitter: @UkNatArchives\r\n> The National Archives, Kew, Richmond, Surrey TW9 4DU\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> ------------------------------\r\n>\r\n> *From:* Requester <[redacted]>\r\n> *Sent:* Wednesday, February 18, 2026 5:49:54 PM\r\n> *To:* Philip Gale <Philip.Gale@nationalarchives.gov.uk>\r\n> *Subject:* Re: Formal Notice for Record: Church View Close, Llandough /\r\n> BP vs Buckler 1987\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> *EXTERNAL*: This email originates from outside of The National Archives.\r\n>\r\n> Dear Mr Gale,\r\n>\r\n> Thank you for your reply of 12 February. I would like to clarify and\r\n> narrow my request to ensure there is no misunderstanding regarding the\r\n> records I am trying to locate.\r\n>\r\n> My correspondence is *not* seeking an adjudication of ownership or title\r\n> — I fully accept the stated remit boundaries of the Manorial Documents\r\n> Register (MDR). Rather, my enquiry concerns the existence of *any\r\n> archival reference, index card, catalogue entry, or pointer to a document\r\n> historically known as the “Williams Title”*, which was, according to\r\n> contemporaneous family testimony, at one point:\r\n>\r\n>    1. Held in the *local public library* in the first half of the\r\n>    twentieth century; and\r\n>    2. Indexed in some manner (e.g., an index card or catalogue reference)\r\n>    that persisted even after the original document could not be located.\r\n>\r\n> My understanding of the MDR — as described on your own website — is that\r\n> it exists to provide brief descriptions of manorial records and details of\r\n> their locations:\r\n>\r\n> “An official index of English and Welsh manorial records providing brief\r\n> descriptions of documents and details of their locations… It does *not*\r\n> include title deeds or conveyances, and is *not* a register of ownership\r\n> of manorial lordships or rights.”\r\n> https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/manor-search\r\n> <https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/manor-search?utm_source=chatgpt.com>\r\n>\r\n> Based on that description, I would be grateful if you could confirm\r\n> whether:\r\n>\r\n>    1. *Any catalogue entries, index cards, reference pointers, or\r\n>    archival descriptions* relating to the *Manor of Llandough* or the *Williams\r\n>    family title* exist in the MDR or related archival catalogues.\r\n>    2. If so, the *reference identifiers* and information on how these can\r\n>    be consulted in person or via digitised records.\r\n>\r\n> To be precise, I am looking for historical references to the *index card\r\n> or listing* for the Williams Title — whether held in a library, local\r\n> record office, or other institutional archive — rather than a claim for\r\n> ownership judgment itself.\r\n>\r\n> If such a reference does not currently appear in the MDR, I would greatly\r\n> appreciate guidance on where any known catalogue cards or entry lists for\r\n> manorial documents from *Glamorgan / Llandough* prior to 1950 might now\r\n> reside (e.g., regional archives, National Archives holdings, or affiliated\r\n> local repository catalogues).\r\n>\r\n> Anyway I anticipate your forgiveness, if I have erred in writing to you\r\n> earlier.\r\n>\r\n> Thank you again and in advance for your assistance.\r\n>\r\n> Yours sincerely,\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Requester\r\n>\r\n> On Wed, 18 Feb 2026 at 14:30, Philip Gale <\r\n> Philip.Gale@nationalarchives.gov.uk> wrote:\r\n>\r\n> Dear Requester,\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Thank you for your e-mail of 10 February whose contents are noted. The\r\n> National Archives does not hold any records relating to your enquiry. The\r\n> Manorial Documents Register which this department administers is a register\r\n> for the location of manorial documents and not of the ownership and descent\r\n> of manorial titles:\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/archives-sector/finding-records-in-discovery-and-other-databases/manorial-documents-register/\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> A similar position holds for The National Archives responsibilities for\r\n> public records and concerns about the past management of official records\r\n> is a matter for the relevant government department such as HM Land\r\n> Registry.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Yours sincerely\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> [image: The National Archives logo]\r\n>\r\n> *Philip Gale *|* Head of the Collections and Cultural Property Team*\r\n> T: 020 3908 X 9193 | W: nationalarchives.gov.uk\r\n> <http://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/>\r\n> Twitter: @UkNatArchives\r\n> The National Archives, Kew, Richmond, Surrey TW9 4DU\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> -----Original Message-----\r\n>\r\n> From: Requester <[redacted]>\r\n>\r\n> Sent: 10 February 2026 22:15\r\n>\r\n> To: Archives Sector Leadership <ASL@nationalarchives.gov.uk>\r\n>\r\n> Cc: reportafraud@landregistry.gov.uk; enquiries@college-of-arms.gov.uk;\r\n> Paul Egan <clerk@llandough-cc.co.uk>; c1v@valeofglamorgan.gov.uk;\r\n> ukgeneralenquiries@bp.com\r\n>\r\n> Subject: Formal Notice for Record: Church View Close, Llandough / BP vs\r\n> Buckler 1987\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>         You don't often get email from [redacted]. Learn\r\n> why this is important <https://aka.ms/LearnAboutSenderIdentification>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> EXTERNAL: This email originates from outside of The National Archives.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> FORMAL NOTICE OF RECORD CHALLENGE, ASSERTION OF RIGHT, AND DEMAND FOR\r\n> ENGAGEMENT\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Dear Sir/Madam,\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> This Notice is issued for placement on official record and for reliance.\r\n> It is not informal correspondence. I urge you not to resort to silence,\r\n> deflection, or delay, as doing so following receipt of this notice\r\n> constitutes knowing reliance upon, and maintenance of, records alleged to\r\n> be materially defective through fraud, concealment, denial of due process,\r\n> and systemic erasure. Please report any suspicious activity and\r\n> discrepancies identified.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> This Notice is issued in pursuit of narrative justice at this stage;\r\n> formal judicial remedies are expressly reserved. It serves to document our\r\n> family's side of the story before it is completely erased and history\r\n> rewritten, ensuring awareness among custodians of land, heritage, heraldry,\r\n> archaeology, and public records.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> My elder brother, Rhys Buckler, and I are the sons of the late William\r\n> Buckler (d. 1991), defendant in the landmark legal case BP Properties Ltd v\r\n> Buckler (1987), and the grandchildren of the late Mary Williams, whose\r\n> dispossession, marginalisation, and exclusion from judicial determination\r\n> remain unresolved matters of record. Mary Williams was repeatedly and\r\n> materially falsely addressed/mislabeled in legal and administrative records\r\n> as “Mrs Buckler,” a misidentification that was never judicially tested and\r\n> which directly affected the handling, scope, and outcome of proceedings.\r\n> The Williams/Buckler family held claim to the land title, having acquired\r\n> and occupied Great House Farm continuously since 1667, based on family\r\n> records, historical evidence, and centuries of resistance to adverse claims.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> I place on record that I am the first member of my family able to speak\r\n> publicly and formally on these matters. Other close family members remain\r\n> too traumatised and intimidated by the historical conduct of these\r\n> proceedings—including violent eviction, demolition, and loss of\r\n> livelihood—to engage, despite the continuing consequences of dispossession.\r\n> That silence must not be misread as consent, acquiescence, or abandonment\r\n> of right. There is a material distinction between free silence and silence\r\n> produced by fear, trauma, and coercive experience.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> The story, as we have pieced it together, reveals a multi-generational\r\n> pattern of lawfare and siege: from Norman/English conquests in 1100\r\n> granting lordships over ancient Welsh Christian landscapes, through\r\n> monastic (1215–1539) and Crown seizures (1539 onward), to industrial\r\n> encroachments (e.g., 1876 Limeworks severance without consent) and modern\r\n> corporate possession actions. This culminated in the 1988 demolition of an\r\n> 800-year-old farmhouse amid police standoffs, leaving the family homeless\r\n> and the site scarred, now buried under Church View Close. Archaeological\r\n> vindication came too late—1994 excavations revealed over 1,000 Roman and\r\n> medieval burials, confirming family warnings (e.g., the 1870 Roman soldier\r\n> discovery under the living room floor, suppressed by authorities)—yet\r\n> heritage records celebrate the site while omitting our dispossession.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> The following assertions of right are now formally placed on record. They\r\n> have never been lawfully adjudicated or extinguished:\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> 1.      Rhys Buckler may be the rightful Manorial Lord of the Manor of\r\n> Llandough, by right of primogeniture and continuous hereditary connection\r\n> arising from our family’s occupation of Great House Farm from 1667 until\r\n> our narrative, legal, and physical dispossession.\r\n>\r\n> 2.      I assert a claim to act as Squire of the Manor of Llandough, as\r\n> executive representative and guardian of the Buckler–Williams estate and\r\n> matriarchal lineage, with a mandate to pursue correction of record,\r\n> restitution, and restorative remedies.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> These are assertions of right, not settled declarations, placed on record\r\n> pending proper adjudication—which has been historically obstructed through\r\n> procedural narrowing, missing documents, and avoidance of full hearings.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> In 1974, Mary Williams was twice prevented from presenting her case and\r\n> obtaining a judicial determination of superior ownership, despite claims\r\n> grounded in long occupation, intent, and possession. The court confined\r\n> inquiry to animus possidendi (intention to possess) and excluded animus\r\n> domini (intention to own), reclassifying her from putative owner to mere\r\n> occupier and foreclosing title adjudication. BP sought possession only, not\r\n> ownership rulings—a choice unexplained and significant. Proceedings were\r\n> adjourned or quashed without resolution.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> This occurred despite evidence of ownership intent, including:\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> *       Centuries-long occupation and resistance (e.g., failed 1938\r\n> auction due to local support; post-1945 rent disputes).\r\n>\r\n> *       Roman-era remains and historical significance (e.g., Guglielmo\r\n> Marconi's 1897 residence during radio experiments, with family aid erased\r\n> from records).\r\n>\r\n> *       Unformalized deals like the 1895–1905 Daniel Thomas quarrying\r\n> permission for promised title, undermined by vanished deeds.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Judicial ownership examination would have engaged archaeological/heritage\r\n> protections, incompatible with redevelopment—consequences avoided, raising\r\n> credibility concerns.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Serious unresolved anomalies persist, indicating something is still not\r\n> right with the record:\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> *       Licence correspondence dated 31 October 1974 surfacing only on 4\r\n> November, potentially interrupting proceedings to prevent hearings.\r\n>\r\n> *       Claims of 1974 benevolent withdrawal under press scrutiny\r\n> unsupported by verifiable coverage.\r\n>\r\n> *       Land registration before Mary Williams’s 1983 death without title\r\n> determination; disputes possibly lodged/suppressed by Land Registry.\r\n>\r\n> *       Inconsistent treatment of BP entities across proceedings to family\r\n> detriment.\r\n>\r\n> *       Procedural barriers preventing effective defense (e.g.,\r\n> interrupted essential services post-licence; 1960–1987 court rulings\r\n> dismissing ancestral title as \"notion\" without evidence).\r\n>\r\n> *       Knowing bypass of archaeological constraints (e.g., 1978 trenches\r\n> ignored; no contemporaneous 1970s/1990s press on discoveries amid\r\n> development).\r\n>\r\n> *       Missing key documents (e.g., 1667 conveyance, 1811–1835 Lambert\r\n> Williams agreements proving status, 1982–84 library deeds vanishing).\r\n>\r\n> *       Risks of further erasure: Heritage databases celebrate \"Llandough\r\n> Roman villa cemetery\" while omitting our saga; modern residents unaware of\r\n> the \"battleground\" site.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> These anomalies suggest systemic theft, from colonial English subjugation\r\n> (e.g., 1543–1770 orbit shifts) to corporate siege, during Wales'\r\n> cultural/economic transitions. Authority derived through such means is\r\n> illegitimate under natural justice, customary Welsh principles, and\r\n> moral-legal frameworks. The record's distortion secures advantage, but\r\n> passage of time does not validate it.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Preliminary assessment indicates cumulative benefits from redevelopment\r\n> (absent title extinguishment) exceed £100 million, forming a basis for\r\n> reparations—covering lost land, artifacts, livelihood, and trauma.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> I require relevant public servants, custodians, and office-holders\r\n> (including Land Registry, heraldry bodies, museums, archaeologists, MPs,\r\n> historians, and councillors) to cease delay, engage substantively,\r\n> review/correct/annotate records where defective, and prevent further\r\n> rewriting of history. Justice delayed is denied, especially with verifiable\r\n> evidence available.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> This Notice:\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> *       Challenges the record's accuracy, completeness, and legitimacy.\r\n>\r\n> *       Place assertions of title/standing on record pending adjudication.\r\n>\r\n> *       Reserves all rights without prejudice.\r\n>\r\n> *       Puts officials/beneficiaries on notice that silence/reliance may\r\n> aggravate responsibility.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> For the avoidance of doubt, His Majesty the King was notified yesterday.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> This serves as an invitation to engage constructively before reliance\r\n> becomes untenable.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Regards, Requester On behalf of the Buckler–Williams family [redacted],\r\n> [location redacted], Vale of Glamorgan, Wales. https://bpvsbuckler.datro.xyz/ <\r\n> https://bpvsbuckler.datro.xyz/>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Postscript — Disability and Succession (Material Notice): The\r\n> heir-apparent to the asserted Manorial Lordship is Rhys Buckler’s son, a\r\n> young man with Down syndrome. Any obstruction, marginalisation, or\r\n> differential treatment affecting his inheritance would engage the Equality\r\n> Act 2010 and UK international obligations on disability rights. Disability\r\n> cannot bar succession or recognition. This is recorded to prevent claims of\r\n> ignorance.\r\n>\r\n> Please don't print this e-mail unless you really need to.\r\n> -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n> National Archives Disclaimer This email and any files transmitted with it\r\n> are intended solely for the use of the individual(s) to whom they are\r\n> addressed. If you are not the intended recipient and have received this\r\n> email in error, please notify the sender and delete the email. Opinions,\r\n> conclusions and other information in this message and attachments that do\r\n> not relate to the official business of The National Archives are neither\r\n> given nor endorsed by it.\r\n> ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Please don't print this e-mail unless you really need to.\r\n> -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n> National Archives Disclaimer This email and any files transmitted with it\r\n> are intended solely for the use of the individual(s) to whom they are\r\n> addressed. If you are not the intended recipient and have received this\r\n> email in error, please notify the sender and delete the email. Opinions,\r\n> conclusions and other information in this message and attachments that do\r\n> not relate to the official business of The National Archives are neither\r\n> given nor endorsed by it.\r\n> ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Please don't print this e-mail unless you really need to.\r\n> -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n> National Archives Disclaimer This email and any files transmitted with it\r\n> are intended solely for the use of the individual(s) to whom they are\r\n> addressed. If you are not the intended recipient and have received this\r\n> email in error, please notify the sender and delete the email. Opinions,\r\n> conclusions and other information in this message and attachments that do\r\n> not relate to the official business of The National Archives are neither\r\n> given nor endorsed by it.\r\n> ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n>\r\n>\r\n"},{"id":"wb-f1edae0e","subject":"Re formal report of suspected indigenous dispossession syste","name":"0000-00-00_re-formal-report-of-suspected-indigenous-dispossession-syste_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-formal-report-of-suspected-indigenous-dispossession-syste_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"formal-report-of-suspected-indigenous-dispossession-syste","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-formal-report-of-suspected-indigenous-dispossession-syste_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"Mae hon yn neges ddwyieithog. Sgroliwch i lawr i weld y Gymraeg\r\n\r\nThank you for contacting the Ombudsman’s office. We do not accept complaints into this inbox. If you want to complain to our office electronically, please complete our online complaint form<https://complaints.ombudsman.wales/en/pre-complaint>.\r\n\r\n\r\nThe form is easy to use and will help you find out if your complaint is one that we can look into. You can attach additional files at the end of the form.\r\n\r\n\r\nHow else can I complain?\r\n• By phone: 0300 790 0203. Our phone line is open 10am-12:30pm and 1:30pm-4pm Monday – Friday\r\n• By post: print out the complaint form<https://www.ombudsman.wales/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Complaint-form-public-services-1.pdf> and return it to us.\r\n\r\nWhat if I need support to complain?\r\n• Use this email address or call us to let us know how we can help you use our service. We will do our best to help.\r\n• Ask an advocate to help you<https://www.ombudsman.wales/advice-advocates/>.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nAre we the right organisation for you?\r\nWe look at complaints about public services in Wales or Welsh councillors breaching the Code of Conduct. However, there are other organisations that may be able to help you<https://www.ombudsman.wales/other-organisations-that-may-be-able-to-help-you/>.\r\n\r\n\r\nIf we think we need to respond to your email, we will aim to respond within 7 days.\r\n\r\nIf you have copied us into an email, please be aware that we do not act or respond to copy emails.\r\n\r\n____________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________\r\n\r\n\r\nDiolch am gysylltu â swyddfa'r Ombwdsmon. Nid ydym yn derbyn cwynion i'r mewnflwch hwn. Os ydych am gwyno i'n swyddfa yn electronig, cwblhewch ein ffurflen gwyno ar-lein<https://complaints.ombwdsmon.cymru/cy/cyn-g%C5%B5yn>.\r\nMae'r ffurflen yn hawdd i’w defnyddio.ydd yn eich helpu i ganfod a yw eich cwyn yn un y gallwn ymchwilio iddi. Gallwch atodi ffeiliau ychwanegol ar ddiwedd y ffurflen.\r\n\r\nSut arall allaf gwyno?\r\n• Dros y ffôn: 0300 790 0203. Mae ein llinell ffôn ar agor 10am-12:30pm a 1:30pm-4pm o ddydd Llun i ddydd Gwener\r\n• Trwy'r post: argraffwch y ffurflen gwyno<https://www.ombwdsmon.cymru/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2025/10/Ffurflen-gwyno-gwasanaethau-cyhoeddus.pdf> a'i dychwelyd atom.\r\n\r\nBeth os oes angen cefnogaeth arnaf i gwyno?\r\n• Defnyddiwch y cyfeiriad e-bost hwn neu ffoniwch ni i roi gwybod i ni sut y gallwn eich helpu i ddefnyddio ein gwasanaeth. Gwnawn ein gorau i helpu.\r\n• Gofyn i eiriolwr eich helpu<https://www.ombwdsmon.cymru/cyrff-cynghori-ac-eirioli/>.\r\n\r\n\r\nAi ni yw'r sefydliad cywir i chi?\r\nEdrychwn ar gwynion am wasanaethau cyhoeddus yng Nghymru neu gynghorwyr Cymru yn torri'r Cod Ymddygiad. Fodd bynnag, mae sefydliadau eraill a allai eich helpu<https://www.ombwdsmon.cymru/sefydliadau-eraill-a-allai-eich-helpu/>.\r\n\r\n\r\nOs credwn fod angen i ni ymateb i'ch e-bost, byddwn yn anelu at ymateb o fewn 7 diwrnod.\r\n\r\n\r\nOs ydych wedi ein copïo i mewn e-bost, nodwch nad ydym yn gweithredu nac yn ymateb i negeseuon e-bost copi.\r\n\r\n________________________________\r\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]>\r\nSent: Friday, February 6, 2026 5:21:09 AM\r\nTo: scheduledmonuments@gov.wales <scheduledmonuments@gov.wales>\r\nCc: ask <ask@ombudsman.wales>; reportafraud@landregistry.gov.uk <reportafraud@landregistry.gov.uk>; Vikki.Howells@senedd.wales <Vikki.Howells@senedd.wales>\r\nSubject: Formal Report of Suspected Indigenous Dispossession, Systemic Fraud, and Archaeological Suppression – Great House Farm, Llandough\r\n\r\nYou don't often get email from [redacted]. Learn why this is important<https://aka.ms/LearnAboutSenderIdentification>\r\n\r\nTo whom it may concern,\r\n\r\nI am writing to formally report a long-standing and systemic case of what I suspect may be dispossession, fraudulent misrepresentation, and archaeological suppression concerning my family’s ancestral land, Great House Farm (historically Tŷ Mawr/Cedfin), Llandough.\r\n\r\nI am the descendant of the Williams/Buckler family, who held de facto possession and indigenous tenure of this land from 1667 until our forced displacement in 1988. I am currently the only member of my family who is not too traumatized to speak of these events. My mother (Mary Williams daughter in law), remains to this day too terrified to speak up about this multi-generational \"lawfare\" campaign waged against us. Our family have not seen justice or received reperations, nor have we sued or reopened this case under limitations act clauses of fraud and consealment. Yet. But this may not be just a grave injustice upon our family, but our nations archeological history and indigenous people on our ancestral birthland. We are Welsh Nationals, Welsh speaking, under Welsh flags. This is some British Colonisation-grade trespass I'm seeing and it's tandamount to a declaration of civil war on me, my family, community and people.\r\n\r\nThe core of this report involves the following suspicions I have of concealment and fraud (see attached for my timeline and notes):\r\n\r\n  1.  Archaeological Suppression: In 1888, my family discovered a Roman soldier in full armor beneath the living room floorboards. This discovery provided \"actual notice\" of the site's national significance a century before its demolition. We allege this notice was systematically suppressed by corporate and state actors to allow for commercial development.\r\n\r\n  2.  Fraudulent Misrepresentation in Planning: During the 1987 planning process, the 13th-century fortified manor was described by developers as a \"dilapidated utility building\" or \"ruined barn\" to secure a demolition permit that would have otherwise been denied under heritage protection laws.\r\n\r\n  3.  Legal Identity Falsification: In 1974, BP Pension Trust issued a \"unilateral license\" intended to bypass our possessory title. This correspondence was falsely addressed to \"Mrs. Buckler\" to bind my grandmother to her deceased husband’s name and legal status, despite her public rejection of their claim. She used her maiden name Williams and her husband Bucklers' intent to accept license died with him. My nan also alledgedly didn't expressly deny the offer and so accepted through her silence. But she was near enough voiceless from screaming for decades that she will not do deals with any of them. That's a different kind of silence, if any. And to have responded to license to Mrs Buckler would have been identify fraud as she was Mrs Williams. And she did in fact reject BPs license offer and advanced, countless times, but publically. I believe these objections were suppressed too. There's allegedly news coverage from October 30th 1974 but I'm yet to find the evidence. But see attached a 1978 article I unearthed. My nan clearly maintains her position \"It's my home\".   [cid:ii_19c31438f12e2e321bc2]\r\n\r\n  4.  You'll also see in landmark legal case BP Vs Buckler 1987 the judge says that she might have responded to BP, but to BP Pension Trust, but the claimant was BP Properties Ltd which were two separate companies. But in my dads appeal when he challenges that BP Pension Trust Ltd should have been the claimant the same judge dismissed his appeal citing both BP companies are effectively the same. This is clearly a case of heads BP won, Tails we lost.\r\n\r\n  5.  Missing Evidence: Critical title deeds and Bute Estate records from our families deal with Daniel Thomas, for title after quarrying completed, in exchange for our permission to quarry, also disappeared from the loft of our home and the Cardiff Library according to my families accounts, precisely when they were needed to support our ownership claim in the Court of Appeal. Regardless we should have still remained on grounds we'd been there since 1667, and failing that on grounds of adverse possession.\r\n\r\n  6.  It was evident lawfare to remove us, to get to the burials known about since 1888, BP profited from the land sale, the state and Crown have the exhevated artifacts and my displaced family are left traumatised, cheated and dead. My dad's appeal was dismissed to when it shouldn't have been and his ECHR request blocked, before dying abruptly at age just 41.\r\n\r\nThe confirmation in 1994 of a high-status Roman villa and over 1,000 burials on the exact site of our demolished home proves that the \"official narrative\" used to evict us was predicated on the concealment of known facts.\r\n\r\nUnder Section 32 of the Limitation Act 1980, the limitation period for action does not begin until such fraud or concealment is discovered. As the heir to this possessory title, I am seeking a formal investigation into the institutional failures that permitted this dispossession and the destruction of a national monument.\r\n\r\nI have compiled my account and understanding of these events in timeline order and I'm prepared to provide further testimony and evidence.\r\n\r\nGiven the extreme trauma my family has suffered, I request that all correspondence be handled with the gravity this human rights violation deserves.\r\n\r\nConsidering the lack of cooperation from my traumatised family. And authorities who maybe complicit and implicated. And possible corruption of records and fraud and consealment, I'm open to the fact my account and understanding may not be perfect. But if even a single other person or group looked into this too, even briefly, I'm sure you'll agree there's merrit to the concerns I'm raising with all of this.\r\n\r\nI look forward to your response regarding the appropriate next steps for investigation and restitution.\r\n\r\nI do ask for some small sense of urgency in that justice delayed is justice denied. I understand this is ancient news for many, but my family still feel a tremendous sense of disposition and disposition over all of this. Bringing this all up means to re live it.\r\n\r\nI will have been 3 or 4 at the time, but I still remember my toys in our house, being bulldozed. And my mum handing us rocks to throw at them, bulldozing down our home. As my dad was held without charges to prevent him defending us and our home. I'd have to visit him in a hospital bed after his heart attack and see him in a coffin shortly afterwards.\r\n\r\nI'm 41 years old now. I'm a certified and experienced military commander and leader. I left my military career with honours. I finished 22 years in Oath in 2024, bond afide, my last 2 employed under the Welsh government.\r\n\r\nI kindly request but also require of you, that this matter be layed to rest correctly.\r\n\r\nMy family never surrendered. Never relinquished claim to that land. We we're in honour in that we weren't there with force, stealth/ secrecy or license. But BP and the British State did use force, stealth/ secrecy and license.\r\n\r\nAll things considered I believe I find myself in honour here, and the British Establishment in dishonour. This matter must be escalated. I'm duty bound.\r\n\r\n\r\nRegards,\r\n\r\n\r\nSion Buckler\r\n\r\n--\r\n\r\nOmbwdsmon Gwasanaethau Cyhoeddus Cymru/ Public Services Ombudsman for Wales\r\n\r\n1 Ffordd yr Hen Gae\r\n\r\nPencoed\r\n\r\nPen-y-Bont ar Ogwr/ Bridgend/\r\n\r\nCF35 5LJ\r\n\r\nwww.ombwdsmon-cymru.org.uk<http://www.ombwdsmon-cymru.org.uk> / www.ombudsman.wales<https://www.ombudsman.wales>\r\n\r\nhttps://twitter.com/OmbudsmanWales\r\n\r\nHybu’r Gymraeg\r\n\r\nGallwch ysgrifennu atom yn Gymraeg a byddwn yn ymateb yn Gymraeg. Ni fydd hyn yn arwain at oedi cyn ymateb.\r\n\r\nWelsh Language Promotion\r\n\r\nYou can write to us in Welsh and we will reply in Welsh.  This will not lead to a delay in responding.\r\n\r\nSganiwyd y neges hon am bob feirws hysbys wrth iddi adael Ombwdsmon Gwasanaethau Cyhoeddus Cymru.\r\n\r\nMae Ombwdsmon Gwasanaethau Cyhoeddus Cymru yn cymryd o ddifrif yr angen i ddiogelu eich data.\r\n\r\nMae ein Hysbysiad Preifatrwydd yn esbonio sut rydym yn defnyddio eich gwybodaeth a sut rydym yn diogelu eich preifatrwydd.\r\n\r\nOn leaving the Public Services Ombudsman, this email was scanned for all known viruses.\r\n\r\nThe Public Services Ombudsman takes the protection of your data seriously.\r\n\r\nOur Privacy Notice explains how we use your information and the ways in which we protect your privacy.\r\n\r\nBydd pob galwad yn cael ei recordio ar gyfer dibenion hyfforddi a chyfeirio\r\n\r\nAll calls are recorded for training and reference purposes\r\n\r\nYstyriwch yr amgylchedd – a oes wir angen i chi argraffu’r neges e-bost hon?\r\n\r\nPlease consider the environment - do you really need to print this email?\r\n\r\n"},{"id":"wb-f177a23c","subject":"Re formal report of suspected indigenous dispossession syste","name":"0000-00-00_re-formal-report-of-suspected-indigenous-dispossession-syste_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-formal-report-of-suspected-indigenous-dispossession-syste_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","threadSlug":"formal-report-of-suspected-indigenous-dispossession-syste","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-formal-report-of-suspected-indigenous-dispossession-syste_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"[Recovered on 19 Oct 2026 from the original email in the family's Gmail, because this archive file was missing. Matched to this entry by its subject line; where several emails share a subject, the order within the group is not certain. Email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses are redacted.]\n\nSubject: RE: Formal Report of Suspected Indigenous Dispossession, Systemic Fraud, and Archaeological Suppression – Great House Farm, Llandough\nFrom: (name not shown; gov.wales address)\nDate: Wed, 25 Feb 2026 11:37 UTC\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nDear Requester,\n\nThank you for your email regarding Great House Farm, Llandough. However, I must inform you that much of the content in your correspondence falls outside the scope of Cadw’s work.\n\nIn relation to the claim of 'archaeological suppression' involving the discovery of a Roman soldier in full armour in 1888, any such find would typically have been reported in the antiquarian press at either the national or local level. It is possible that records of this nature may be held by local museums. It is worth noting that the first Ancient Monuments Act was passed in 1882, focusing solely on bringing threatened ancient monuments into guardianship. The legal recognition of nationally important archaeology did not come about until the Ancient Monuments Consolidation and Amendment Act of 1913.\n\nUpon reviewing the Cof Cymru database, I found that there are no designated historic assets relevant to this topic. However, Archwilio contains two records that document the involvement of the Glamorgan-Gwent Archaeological Trust (GGAT):\n\n1. Great House Farm HER site entry - GGAT03586s\n2. Land at Great House Farm HER event entry - GGATE003872, which includes GGAT's 1990 archaeological assessment report commissioned by BP Properties Ltd.\n\nAny advice or correspondence that Cadw may have provided should be preserved in the Royal Commission on the Ancient and Historical Monuments of Wales (RCAHMW) National Monuments Record of Wales (NMRW). I recommend you contact the NMRW to access any relevant documents. Additionally, you may want to reach out to Heneb and the Vale of Glamorgan Local Planning Authority (LPA) to explore what records they might hold concerning this matter.\n\nKind regards,\n\nJenna Arnold\n\n[cid:image001.png@01DCA64B.15168E30]\n\n\nJenna Arnold\nArolygiaeth yr Amgylchedd Hanesyddol / Historic Environment Inspectorate\nLlywodraeth Cymru / Welsh Government\nCadw, Rhydycar Business Park | Parc Business Rhydycar, Merthyr Tydfil | Merthyr Tudful [postcode redacted]\n\n\n\n[https://i.ibb.co/CVwfkw2/Cadw-er-lles-pawb.png]<http://www.aelodaethcadw.gwasanaeth.llyw.cymru/>\n[https://i.ibb.co/NxJvcfW/For-us-all-to-keep.png]<http://www.cadwmembership.service.gov.wales/>\n\nYmunwch â Cadw<https://www.aelodaethcadw.gwasanaeth.llyw.cymru/>\nJoin Cadw<https://www.cadwmembership.service.gov.wales/>\n\n\n\n\n\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSent: 06 February 2026 05:21\nTo: Scheduled Monuments <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nCc: [email redacted]<[email redacted]>; [email redacted]<[email redacted]>; [email redacted]<[email redacted]>\nSubject: Formal Report of Suspected Indigenous Dispossession, Systemic Fraud, and Archaeological Suppression – Great House Farm, Llandough\n\n\nTo whom it may concern,\n\nI am writing to formally report a long-standing and systemic case of what I suspect may be dispossession, fraudulent misrepresentation, and archaeological suppression concerning my family’s ancestral land, Great House Farm (historically Tŷ Mawr/Cedfin), Llandough.\n\nI am the descendant of the Williams/Buckler family, who held de facto possession and indigenous tenure of this land from 1667 until our forced displacement in 1988. I am currently the only member of my family who is not too traumatized to speak of these events. My mother (Mary Williams daughter in law), remains to this day too terrified to speak up about this multi-generational \"lawfare\" campaign waged against us. Our family have not seen justice or received reperations, nor have we sued or reopened this case under limitations act clauses of fraud and consealment. Yet. But this may not be just a grave injustice upon our family, but our nations archeological history and indigenous people on our ancestral birthland. We are Welsh Nationals, Welsh speaking, under Welsh flags. This is some British Colonisation-grade trespass I'm seeing and it's tandamount to a declaration of civil war on me, my family, community and people.\n\nThe core of this report involves the following suspicions I have of concealment and fraud (see attached for my timeline and notes):\n\n  1.  Archaeological Suppression: In 1888, my family discovered a Roman soldier in full armor beneath the living room floorboards. This discovery provided \"actual notice\" of the site's national significance a century before its demolition. We allege this notice was systematically suppressed by corporate and state actors to allow for commercial development.\n  2.  Fraudulent Misrepresentation in Planning: During the 1987 planning process, the 13th-century fortified manor was described by developers as a \"dilapidated utility building\" or \"ruined barn\" to secure a demolition permit that would have otherwise been denied under heritage protection laws.\n  3.  Legal Identity Falsification: In 1974, BP Pension Trust issued a \"unilateral license\" intended to bypass our possessory title. This correspondence was falsely addressed to \"Mrs. Buckler\" to bind my grandmother to her deceased husband’s name and legal status, despite her public rejection of their claim. She used her maiden name Williams and her husband Bucklers' intent to accept license died with him. My nan also alledgedly didn't expressly deny the offer and so accepted through her silence. But she was near enough voiceless from screaming for decades that she will not do deals with any of them. That's a different kind of silence, if any. And to have responded to license to Mrs Buckler would have been identify fraud as she was Mrs Williams. And she did in fact reject BPs license offer and advanced, countless times, but publically. I believe these objections were suppressed too. There's allegedly news coverage from October 30th 1974 but I'm yet to find the evidence. But see attached a 1978 article I unearthed. My nan clearly maintains her position \"It's my home\". [cid:image004.jpg@01DCA64B.15168E30]\n  4.  You'll also see in landmark legal case BP Vs Buckler 1987 the judge says that she might have responded to BP, but to BP Pension Trust, but the claimant was BP Properties Ltd which were two separate companies. But in my dads appeal when he challenges that BP Pension Trust Ltd should have been the claimant the same judge dismissed his appeal citing both BP companies are effectively the same. This is clearly a case of heads BP won, Tails we lost.\n  5.  Missing Evidence: Critical title deeds and Bute Estate records from our families deal with Daniel Thomas, for title after quarrying completed, in exchange for our permission to quarry, also disappeared from the loft of our home and the Cardiff Library according to my families accounts, precisely when they were needed to support our ownership claim in the Court of Appeal. Regardless we should have still remained on grounds we'd been there since 1667, and failing that on grounds of adverse possession.\n  6.  It was evident lawfare to remove us, to get to the burials known about since 1888, BP profited from the land sale, the state and Crown have the exhevated artifacts and my displaced family are left traumatised, cheated and dead. My dad's appeal was dismissed to when it shouldn't have been and his ECHR request blocked, before dying abruptly at age just 41.\n\nThe confirmation in 1994 of a high-status Roman villa and over 1,000 burials on the exact site of our demolished home proves that the \"official narrative\" used to evict us was predicated on the concealment of known facts.\n\nUnder Section 32 of the Limitation Act 1980, the limitation period for action does not begin until such fraud or concealment is discovered. As the heir to this possessory title, I am seeking a formal investigation into the institutional failures that permitted this dispossession and the destruction of a national monument.\n\nI have compiled my account and understanding of these events in timeline order and I'm prepared to provide further testimony and evidence.\n\nGiven the extreme trauma my family has suffered, I request that all correspondence be handled with the gravity this human rights violation deserves.\n\nConsidering the lack of cooperation from my traumatised family. And authorities who maybe complicit and implicated. And possible corruption of records and fraud and consealment, I'm open to the fact my account and understanding may not be perfect. But if even a single other person or group looked into this too, even briefly, I'm sure you'll agree there's merrit to the concerns I'm raising with all of this.\n\nI look forward to your response regarding the appropriate next steps for investigation and restitution.\n\nI do ask for some small sense of urgency in that justice delayed is justice denied. I understand this is ancient news for many, but my family still feel a tremendous sense of disposition and disposition over all of this. Bringing this all up means to re live it.\n\nI will have been 3 or 4 at the time, but I still remember my toys in our house, being bulldozed. And my mum handing us rocks to throw at them, bulldozing down our home. As my dad was held without charges to prevent him defending us and our home. I'd have to visit him in a hospital bed after his heart attack and see him in a coffin shortly afterwards.\n\nI'm 41 years old now. I'm a certified and experienced military commander and leader. I left my military career with honours. I finished 22 years in Oath in 2024, bond afide, my last 2 employed under the Welsh government.\n\nI kindly request but also require of you, that this matter be layed to rest correctly.\n\nMy family never surrendered. Never relinquished claim to that land. We we're in honour in that we weren't there with force, stealth/ secrecy or license. But BP and the British State did use force, stealth/ secrecy and license.\n\nAll things considered I believe I find myself in honour here, and the British Establishment in dishonour. This matter must be escalated. I'm duty bound.\n\n\n\nRegards,\n\n\n\nSion Buckler\n\nSganiwyd y neges hon am bob feirws hysbys wrth iddi adael Llywodraeth Cymru. Mae Llywodraeth Cymru yn cymryd o ddifrif yr angen i ddiogelu eich data. Os cysylltwch â Llywodraeth Cymru, mae ein hysbysiad preifatrwydd<”https://gov.wales/about/welsh-government-privacy-notice/?lang=cy”> yn esbonio sut rydym yn defnyddio eich gwybodaeth a sut rydym yn diogelu eich preifatrwydd. Rydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg. Byddwn yn anfon ateb yn Gymraeg i ohebiaeth a dderbynnir yn Gymraeg ac ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi. ----------------- On leaving the Welsh Government this email was scanned for all known viruses. The Welsh Government takes the protection of your data seriously. If you contact the Welsh Government then our Privacy Notice<https://gov.wales/about/welsh-government-privacy-notice/?lang=en> explains how we use your information and the ways in which we protect your privacy. We welcome receiving correspondence in Welsh. Any correspondence received in Welsh will be answered in Welsh and corresponding in Welsh will not lead to a delay in responding.\n"},{"id":"wb-f40b9725","subject":"Re great house farm Llandough nr penarth vale of glamorgan","name":"0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm-llandough-nr-penarth-vale-of-glamorgan_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Council and local authority correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm-llandough-nr-penarth-vale-of-glamorgan_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"great-house-farm-llandough-nr-penarth-vale-of-glamorgan","scopeTopics":["morgan"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["morgan"],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm-llandough-nr-penarth-vale-of-glamorgan_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"Ref 2026/2336\r\n\r\nDear Requester\r\n\r\nMany thanks for your email received onto our system on 27 May 2026\r\n\r\nI have taken a look at our catalogue and we do not have a petition relating to ‘Great House’ or Ty Mawr’ farm. If you are interested in finding other documents that relate you can carry out searches on our catalogue, Glamorgan Archives Catalogue<https://glamorgan.epexio.com/en/>.\r\n\r\nIf you do find something that you would like to see, you are welcome to view it for free at Glamorgan Archives. We are open Tuesday-Thursday, 0930-1230 and 1330-1630. Please book an appointment in advance to ensure that there is space to accommodate you in the searchroom.\r\n\r\nPlease let me know if you have any further queries\r\n\r\nBest wishes\r\n\r\nKatie\r\n\r\n\r\nKatie Finn\r\nArchifydd | Archivist\r\n\r\nArchifau Morgannwg, Clos Parc Morgannwg, Lecwydd, Caerdydd CF11 8AW | Glamorgan Archives, Clos Parc Morgannwg, Leckwith, Cardiff CF11 8AW\r\n( 029 2087 2299\r\nwww.archifaumorgannwg.gov.uk / www.glamarchives.gov.uk<http://www.glamro.gov.uk/>\r\nwww.facebook.com/glamarchives<http://www.facebook.com/glamarchives>\r\nwww.twitter.com/glamarchives\r\nInstagram: glamarchives\r\n\r\nBydd Archifau Morgannwg yn sicrhau ein bod yn cyfathrebu â chi yn yr iaith o'ch dewis, boed hynny'n Gymraeg, yn Saesneg neu'n ddwyieithog cyn belled ag y byddwch yn rhoi gwybod i ni pa well gennych. Rydych yn derbyn y wybodaeth hon yn y Saesneg gan fod hyn wedi ei gofnodi fel eich dewis iaith. Os hoffech chi ddiweddaru'r wybodaeth hon neu ddewis peidio â derbyn y negeseuon e-bost hyn, cysylltwch â ni: glamro@cardiff.gov.uk<mailto:glamro@cardiff.gov.uk>. | Glamorgan Archives will ensure that we communicate with you in the language of your choice, whether that’s English, Welsh or bilingual as long as you let us know which you prefer. You are receiving this information in English as this is recorded as your language preference. If you would like to update this information or opt out of receiving these emails please contact us at glamro@cardiff.gov.uk<mailto:glamro@cardiff.gov.uk>.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]>\r\nSent: 26 May 2026 17:42\r\nTo: Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg <Glamro@cardiff.gov.uk>\r\nSubject: Great House Farm, Llandough, Nr Penarth, Vale of Glamorgan\r\n\r\nEXTERNAL: This email originated from outside Cardiff Council, take care when clicking links.\r\nALLANOL: Daw'r e-bost hwn o'r tu allan i Gyngor Caerdydd, cymerwch ofal wrth glicio ar ddolenni.\r\n\r\nSirs/Ma'am, Please cite the link displaying the record of petition of demolition of great house farm in Llandough near Penarth. I have a newspaper article from 1978 citing the petition. It says 1,000 signatures, in other references it says 1700. Regards, Requester\r\n\r\n**********************************************************************\r\nThe Council welcomes correspondence in Welsh, English or bilingually. We will ensure that we communicate with you in the language of your choice, as long as you let us know which you prefer. Corresponding in Welsh will not lead to delay.\r\n\r\nPrivileged/Confidential Information may be contained in this message. If you are not the addressee indicated in this message (or responsible for delivery of the message to such person), you may not copy or deliver this message to anyone. In such case, you should destroy this message and kindly notify the sender by reply email. Please advise immediately if you or your employer does not consent to Internet email for messages of this kind.  Opinions, conclusions and other information in this message that do not relate to the official business of the Council of the City and County of Cardiff shall be understood as neither given nor endorsed by it.  All e-mail sent to or from this address will be processed by Cardiff County Councils Corporate E-mail system and may be subject to scrutiny by someone other than the addressee.\r\n**********************************************************************\r\nMae’r Cyngor yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg, Saesneg neu’n ddwyieithog. Byddwn yn cyfathrebu â chi yn ôl eich dewis, dim ond i chi roi gwybod i ni pa un sydd well gennych. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi.\r\n\r\nMae'n bosibl bod gwybodaeth gyfrinachol yn y neges hon. Os na chyfeirir y neges atoch chi'n benodol (neu os nad ydych chi'n gyfrifol am drosglwyddo'r neges i'r person a enwir), yna ni chewch gopio na throsglwyddo'r neges. Mewn achos o'r fath, dylech ddinistrio'r neges a hysbysu'r anfonwr drwy e-bost ar unwaith. Rhowch wybod i'r anfonydd ar unwaith os nad ydych chi neu eich cyflogydd yn caniatau e-bost y Rhyngrwyd am negeseuon fel hon. Rhaid deall nad yw'r safbwyntiau, y casgliadau a'r wybodaeth arall yn y neges hon nad ydynt yn cyfeirio at fusnes swyddogol Cyngor Dinas a Sir Caerdydd yn cynrychioli barn y Cyngor Sir nad yn cael sel ei fendith. Caiff unrhyw negeseuon a anfonir at, neu o'r cyfeiriad e-bost hwn eu prosesu gan system E-bost Gorfforaethol Cyngor Sir Caerdydd a gallant gael eu harchwilio gan rywun heblaw'r person a enwir.\r\n**********************************************************************\r\n\r\n"},{"id":"wb-dbc5f107","subject":"Re great house farm Llandough nr penarth vale of glamorgan","name":"0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm-llandough-nr-penarth-vale-of-glamorgan_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Council and local authority correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm-llandough-nr-penarth-vale-of-glamorgan_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","threadSlug":"great-house-farm-llandough-nr-penarth-vale-of-glamorgan","scopeTopics":["morgan"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["morgan"],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm-llandough-nr-penarth-vale-of-glamorgan_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"[Recovered on 19 Oct 2026 from the original email in the family's Gmail, because this archive file was missing. Matched to this entry by its subject line; where several emails share a subject, the order within the group is not certain. Email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses are redacted.]\n\nSubject: Re: Great House Farm, Llandough, Nr Penarth, Vale of Glamorgan\nFrom: Requester\nDate: Thu, 28 May 2026 20:50 UTC\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nThanks Katie,\nI am at the Welsh Settlement in Argentina. Where I will be safer, seeing as\nevery member in my family which challenged the official narrative here are\ndead. Any assistance appreciated. I believe my disabled widow nan inherited\nthe fee simple absolute (and Manorial) title from the Marquess  of Bute, a\ndeal family before us made with Daniel Thomas and the Marquess of Bute. I\nbelieve Marconi staying with us while inventing the radio or boxer\n'Peerless' Jim Driscoll fighting his first boxing match at our barns, or\nwhen we did a tree planting ceremony (customary when obtaining land) with\nthe whole town there to witness and photo evidence in the nations\ncollection of wales, it was because we'd become the absolute and manorial\ntitle holders. My nan maintained this position in all court proceedings and\nin the local press. Like a 1978 interview and article which was suppressed.\nI also noticed ITV quietly declassified covering this 1988 story. Much like\nlittle to no evidence of the 1974 news coverage exists (which the courts\ndocument as having occured, wherby the public and press sided with my nan).\nAnd more suppressed evidence exists which I am trying to get declassified\ntoo. It appears her title was stolen and ended up in the hands of Western\nGround Rents and later BP who bought it from them. Who then tried and\nfailed to get my widow pensioner nan to play the role of her tenant and pay\nthem. Failing this they appear to have replaced us and the 800 year old\nfarmhouse they demolished, with 23 houses and people who would pay their\namortizations and virtual/ lifetime freeholds.   I am simply trying to put\ntogether this story which is evidently supressed and fragmented and has\ngaps. This is not just for my family , but the injustice to us indigenous\nand our history and herritage by the British State and BP is horrific. We\nappear to have been erazed of the face of the planet, but we're not . We're\nstill here Yma o Hyd and we seek answers. So if you could kindly do\neverything in your power to assist, so that this matter is put to rest I'd\nappreciate. Regards, Requester\n\n>\n>\n> Dear Requester\n>\n>\n>\n> Many thanks for your email received onto our system on 27 May 2026\n>\n>\n>\n> I have taken a look at our catalogue and we do not have a petition\n> relating to ‘Great House’ or Ty Mawr’ farm. If you are interested in\n> finding other documents that relate you can carry out searches on our\n> catalogue, Glamorgan Archives Catalogue <https://glamorgan.epexio.com/en/>.\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> If you do find something that you would like to see, you are welcome to\n> view it for free at Glamorgan Archives. We are open Tuesday-Thursday,\n> 0930-1230 and 1330-1630. Please book an appointment in advance to ensure\n> that there is space to accommodate you in the searchroom.\n>\n>\n>\n> Please let me know if you have any further queries\n>\n>\n>\n> Best wishes\n>\n>\n>\n> Katie\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> Katie Finn\n>\n> Archifydd | Archivist\n>\n>\n>\n> Archifau Morgannwg, Clos Parc Morgannwg, Lecwydd, Caerdydd [postcode redacted] | Glamorgan\n> Archives, Clos Parc Morgannwg, Leckwith, Cardiff [postcode redacted]\n>\n> ( [phone redacted]\n>\n> www.archifaumorgannwg.gov.uk / www.glamarchives.gov.uk\n> <http://www.glamro.gov.uk/>\n>\n> www.facebook.com/glamarchives\n>\n> www.twitter.com/glamarchives\n>\n> Instagram: glamarchives\n>\n>\n>\n> *Bydd Archifau Morgannwg yn sicrhau ein bod yn cyfathrebu â chi yn yr\n> iaith o'ch dewis, boed hynny'n Gymraeg, yn Saesneg neu'n ddwyieithog cyn\n> belled ag y byddwch yn rhoi gwybod i ni pa well gennych. Rydych yn derbyn y\n> wybodaeth hon yn y Saesneg gan fod hyn wedi ei gofnodi fel eich dewis\n> iaith. Os hoffech chi ddiweddaru'r wybodaeth hon neu ddewis peidio â derbyn\n> y negeseuon e-bost hyn, cysylltwch â ni: [email redacted]\n> <[email redacted]>. *| *Glamorgan Archives will ensure that we\n> communicate with you in the language of your choice, whether that’s\n> English, Welsh or bilingual as long as you let us know which you prefer.\n> You are receiving this information in English as this is recorded as your\n> language preference. If you would like to update this information or opt\n> out of receiving these emails please contact us at [email redacted]\n> <[email redacted]>.*\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> *From:* Requester <[email redacted]>\n> *Sent:* 26 May 2026 17:42\n> *To:* Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg <[email redacted]>\n> *Subject:* Great House Farm, Llandough, Nr Penarth, Vale of Glamorgan\n>\n>\n>\n> *EXTERNAL:* This email originated from outside Cardiff Council, take care\n> when clicking links.\n> *ALLANOL:* Daw'r e-bost hwn o'r tu allan i Gyngor Caerdydd, cymerwch ofal\n> wrth glicio ar ddolenni.\n>\n>\n>\n> Sirs/Ma'am, Please cite the link displaying the record of petition of\n> demolition of great house farm in Llandough near Penarth. I have a\n> newspaper article from 1978 citing the petition. It says 1,000 signatures,\n> in other references it says 1700. Regards, Requester\n>\n> **********************************************************************\n>\n> The Council welcomes correspondence in Welsh, English or bilingually. We\n> will ensure that we communicate with you in the language of your choice, as\n> long as you let us know which you prefer. Corresponding in Welsh will not\n> lead to delay.\n>\n> Privileged/Confidential Information may be contained in this message. If\n> you are not the addressee indicated in this message (or responsible for\n> delivery of the message to such person), you may not copy or deliver this\n> message to anyone. In such case, you should destroy this message and kindly\n> notify the sender by reply email. Please advise immediately if you or your\n> employer does not consent to Internet email for messages of this kind.\n> Opinions, conclusions and other information in this message that do not\n> relate to the official business of the Council of the City and County of\n> Cardiff shall be understood as neither given nor endorsed by it. All e-mail\n> sent to or from this address will be processed by Cardiff County Councils\n> Corporate E-mail system and may be subject to scrutiny by someone other\n> than the addressee.\n>\n> **********************************************************************\n>\n> Mae’r Cyngor yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg, Saesneg neu’n ddwyieithog.\n> Byddwn yn cyfathrebu â chi yn ôl eich dewis, dim ond i chi roi gwybod i ni\n> pa un sydd well gennych. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi.\n>\n> Mae'n bosibl bod gwybodaeth gyfrinachol yn y neges hon. Os na chyfeirir y\n> neges atoch chi'n benodol (neu os nad ydych chi'n gyfrifol am drosglwyddo'r\n> neges i'r person a enwir), yna ni chewch gopio na throsglwyddo'r neges.\n> Mewn achos o'r fath, dylech ddinistrio'r neges a hysbysu'r anfonwr drwy\n> e-bost ar unwaith. Rhowch wybod i'r anfonydd ar unwaith os nad ydych chi\n> neu eich cyflogydd yn caniatau e-bost y Rhyngrwyd am negeseuon fel hon.\n> Rhaid deall nad yw'r safbwyntiau, y casgliadau a'r wybodaeth arall yn y\n> neges hon nad ydynt yn cyfeirio at fusnes swyddogol Cyngor Dinas a Sir\n> Caerdydd yn cynrychioli barn y Cyngor Sir nad yn cael sel ei fendith. Caiff\n> unrhyw negeseuon a anfonir at, neu o'r cyfeiriad e-bost hwn eu prosesu gan\n> system E-bost Gorfforaethol Cyngor Sir Caerdydd a gallant gael eu\n> harchwilio gan rywun heblaw'r person a enwir.\n>\n> **********************************************************************\n>\n"},{"id":"wb-8f08a91d","subject":"Re great house farm Llandough","name":"0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"Hi Requester,\r\n\r\nThank you for your detailed follow-up and for sharing more about your\r\nbackground and the historical context surrounding Great House Farm. I can\r\nsee how deeply this case matters to you and your family, and it’s clear\r\nthat you’ve invested considerable time and research into gathering evidence\r\nand preserving your family’s story.\r\n\r\nWhile developments in AI and digital archives have certainly made it easier\r\nto uncover historic material and cross-reference records, the legal\r\nframework for reopening a judgment remains essentially unchanged. Courts\r\nstill apply the same very strict criteria under CPR 52.30 and the\r\nLimitation Act 1980, and they would require evidence of fraud, deliberate\r\nconcealment, or procedural impropriety at the time of the original\r\nproceedings — not just new access to historical material or\r\nreinterpretation through technology.\r\n\r\nEven with emerging evidence, after nearly forty years the doctrine of\r\nfinality in litigation makes it exceptionally difficult to revisit property\r\ncases that have long been settled. The courts are unlikely to reopen such a\r\nmatter unless the new evidence conclusively proves that the original\r\njudgment was obtained through deception or corruption.\r\n\r\nThat said, the historical and human side of your family’s story — including\r\nthe Marconi connection and the loss of the farm — is genuinely fascinating.\r\nIt may have cultural or documentary value even if it has no remaining legal\r\nremedy. If you do decide to pursue a documentary or historical feature, I’d\r\nbe happy to keep in touch and follow any developments from a reporting\r\nstandpoint.\r\n\r\nThank you again for writing, and I wish you every success in your efforts\r\nto document and preserve this important piece of Welsh history.\r\n\r\nBest regards,\r\nTom Sinclair\r\n\r\nOn Sun, 5 Oct 2025 at 21:30, Requester <[redacted]> wrote:\r\n\r\n> Tom,\r\n>\r\n> While I agree with everything you've said there's more.\r\n>\r\n> AI now exists. We've not had it before.\r\n> Which is what is unveiling new evidence.\r\n>\r\n> There exist evidence still declassified including the exhivate of the land\r\n> after our farm was possessed and bulldozed down. There's TV media coverage\r\n> still not online. And newspaper achieves behind pay walls. Which have also\r\n> only become available. I also have heirlooms with evidence not online.\r\n>\r\n> All making this qualify for the limitation act clause.\r\n>\r\n> My family have known our position on this matter. As you can see\r\n> throughout the 87 case, my nan maintained we owned the farm.\r\n>\r\n> I would not waist anyone's time if I didn't think this wasn't worth\r\n> pursuing. I've collated evidence over the years.\r\n>\r\n> I'm not just the son of William Buckler. I'm a former military cyber\r\n> warfare professional, with AI at my fingertips. I would be as much an asset\r\n> to a lawyer on this case. I'm also bona fide. I just finished 22 years in\r\n> Oath and my last 2 years of employment were under the Welsh Government.\r\n>\r\n> The latest AI (Grok 4) estimates the case could cost £4M and yield £75M.\r\n> Making it a viable endeavour in my estimation.\r\n>\r\n> Im currently at the Welsh Settlement in Argentina trying to rebuild a\r\n> replica of the farm here.\r\n>\r\n> I would also add, if I didn't already. Marconi was staying with us at our\r\n> farm when he invented radio. Literally the first working one. My grandad\r\n> took him by horse and cart with his equipment to perform his experiments up\r\n> until the day of the first success at Laverock point Penarth. Do you know,\r\n> at the housing estate they built over our farm, there's not even a plaque\r\n> to commemorate Marconi?\r\n>\r\n> This would not just be a legal victory. It would be a moral and historical\r\n> victory.\r\n>\r\n> Also the TV footage shows how BP backed off and changed tact, once the\r\n> press caught wind that they were bullying my nan. An old lady in a\r\n> wheelchair.\r\n>\r\n> I suspect this will be on Netflix once we're done. And I'm sure Netflix\r\n> want story like this right now.\r\n>\r\n> No matter what angle I look at this from, I see good vibes, triumph &\r\n> fortune.\r\n>\r\n> My mum is still alive and has a law degree. Another asset.\r\n>\r\n> I could go on.\r\n>\r\n> Regards,\r\n>\r\n> Requester\r\n> [redacted]\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> On Sun, 5 Oct 2025, 16:59 The Editor, <editor@herald.email> wrote:\r\n>\r\n>> Dear Requester,\r\n>>\r\n>> Thank you for getting in touch and for sharing such a detailed account of\r\n>> your family’s case concerning Great House Farm, Llandough. I’ve read\r\n>> through your summary and understand the points you raise about the way the\r\n>> courts treated BP’s different corporate entities.\r\n>>\r\n>> From a legal perspective, however, it appears extremely unlikely that a\r\n>> case dating back to 1987 could now be reopened. The rule you refer to — CPR\r\n>> 52.30 — only applies in truly exceptional circumstances, such as where\r\n>> there is clear evidence of fraud, corruption, or a fundamental procedural\r\n>> defect that undermined the fairness of the original hearing. A disagreement\r\n>> over how the law was applied, or inconsistencies in reasoning, would not\r\n>> usually be enough to meet that standard.\r\n>>\r\n>> The other difficulty is the time that has passed. Nearly forty years on,\r\n>> the courts place very strong emphasis on finality of litigation,\r\n>> particularly in property cases. Land ownership and related interests will\r\n>> almost certainly have changed hands since then, and the law is reluctant to\r\n>> disturb settled titles unless there is absolute proof of fraud or\r\n>> concealment.\r\n>>\r\n>> While section 32 of the Limitation Act 1980 allows some exceptions where\r\n>> facts were deliberately hidden, this applies only if there was intentional\r\n>> concealment by a party, not simply later discovery of information that\r\n>> might cast doubt on the judgment.\r\n>>\r\n>> For those reasons, any attempt to reopen the 1987 decision or pursue\r\n>> rectification of title would almost certainly be refused by the courts.\r\n>>\r\n>> I appreciate the time and research you’ve put into this and the\r\n>> historical importance it holds for your family, but in practical terms the\r\n>> matter is considered legally closed.\r\n>>\r\n>> With best wishes,\r\n>> Tom Sinclair\r\n>> Editor\r\n>>\r\n>> On Sun, 5 Oct 2025 at 05:50, Requester <[redacted]>\r\n>> wrote:\r\n>>\r\n>>> Sirs/Ma'am,\r\n>>>\r\n>>> I am seeking representation and initial advice regarding the 1987 case\r\n>>> BP Properties Ltd v Buckler, which concerned Great House Farm, Llandough —\r\n>>> property long occupied by my family. I believe, among a myriad of\r\n>>> arguments, that the judgment was reached through a procedural inconsistency\r\n>>> amounting to bias or error in law, which may justify reopening under CPR\r\n>>> 52.30 and Limitations Act Section 32.\r\n>>>\r\n>>>\r\n>>> ---\r\n>>>\r\n>>> Summary of the issue\r\n>>>\r\n>>> In the 1987 case, the court ruled against my grandmother on the basis\r\n>>> that she did not formally reject an offer made by BP Properties Ltd, and\r\n>>> treated that as tacit acceptance. However, she had already explicitly\r\n>>> rejected prior licence attempts by BP Pension Trust Ltd, a related BP\r\n>>> entity, before BP Properties Ltd appeared as claimant.\r\n>>>\r\n>>> The judge ruled that BP Properties Ltd was a distinct legal entity, and\r\n>>> therefore her prior rejection of BP Pension Trust Ltd’s offer was legally\r\n>>> irrelevant.\r\n>>>\r\n>>> Later, in my father’s appeal, he argued that the wrong BP company had\r\n>>> been the claimant — that BP Pension Trust Ltd, not BP Properties Ltd, held\r\n>>> the relevant interest.\r\n>>> The appellate court dismissed his appeal, ruling that “BP companies are\r\n>>> all effectively one and the same” for the purposes of upholding the\r\n>>> judgment.\r\n>>>\r\n>>> This is a clear double standard:\r\n>>>\r\n>>> When it benefited BP, the court treated the companies as separate.\r\n>>>\r\n>>> When it benefited our family, they were treated as the same entity.\r\n>>>\r\n>>>\r\n>>> This contradictory reasoning — “heads BP wins, tails the Bucklers lose”\r\n>>> — represents either judicial bias, procedural irregularity, or corruption\r\n>>> by omission, and undermines the fairness of the original proceedings.\r\n>>>\r\n>>>\r\n>>> ---\r\n>>>\r\n>>> Grounds for reopening\r\n>>>\r\n>>> Under CPR 52.30, a final judgment can be reopened if necessary to avoid\r\n>>> real injustice, in exceptional circumstances, where no alternative remedy\r\n>>> exists.\r\n>>> My argument is that:\r\n>>>\r\n>>> The 1987 decision and subsequent appeal were tainted by inconsistent\r\n>>> application of corporate identity principles, creating real injustice.\r\n>>>\r\n>>> The appeal court’s own language evidences bias and unequal treatment\r\n>>> between parties.\r\n>>>\r\n>>> This meets the “exceptional circumstances” threshold.\r\n>>>\r\n>>>\r\n>>> Additionally, the Supreme Court ruling in Takhar v Gracefield\r\n>>> Developments Ltd [2019] UKSC 13 confirms that a judgment obtained through\r\n>>> fraud or concealment can be set aside even decades later — “fraud unravels\r\n>>> all.”\r\n>>>\r\n>>>\r\n>>> ---\r\n>>>\r\n>>> Limitation Act 1980 – Section 32\r\n>>>\r\n>>> Pursuant to s.32(1)(b):\r\n>>>\r\n>>> > “Where any fact relevant to the plaintiff’s right of action has been\r\n>>> deliberately concealed by the defendant, the period of limitation shall not\r\n>>> begin to run until the plaintiff has discovered the concealment or could\r\n>>> with reasonable diligence have discovered it.”\r\n>>>\r\n>>>\r\n>>>\r\n>>> I have only recently obtained and cross-referenced records revealing\r\n>>> these contradictions in corporate treatment and procedural handling — thus,\r\n>>> limitation does not bar action.\r\n>>>\r\n>>>\r\n>>> ---\r\n>>>\r\n>>> Requested scope of representation\r\n>>>\r\n>>> Assess the viability of a CPR 52.30 petition to reopen or set aside BP v\r\n>>> Buckler (1987).\r\n>>>\r\n>>> Review the documentary record (Land Registry files, local authority\r\n>>> archives, excavation permissions, HTV Wales coverage, etc.).\r\n>>>\r\n>>> If viable, initiate a claim for rectification of title and potential\r\n>>> cultural property recovery of artefacts excavated during BP’s occupation of\r\n>>> the disputed land.\r\n>>>\r\n>>>\r\n>>>\r\n>>> ---\r\n>>>\r\n>>> Attachments available upon request\r\n>>>\r\n>>> Land Registry and microfiche title extracts (pre- and post-1987)\r\n>>>\r\n>>> Local authority and archaeological licensing records (1985–1988)\r\n>>>\r\n>>> Media and correspondence evidence of procedural bias and conflict\r\n>>>\r\n>>> Chronology of BP company interactions with the Buckler family\r\n>>>\r\n>>>\r\n>>>\r\n>>> ---\r\n>>>\r\n>>> Please confirm conflicts and whether one of your dispute resolution\r\n>>> partners can offer an initial consultation. I am prepared to provide a full\r\n>>> evidentiary bundle immediately upon engagement.\r\n>>>\r\n>>> Thank you for your time and consideration.\r\n>>>\r\n>>> Kind regards,\r\n>>>\r\n>>> Requester\r\n>>> [redacted]\r\n>>>\r\n>>>\r\n>>>\r\n>>>\r\n>>\r\n>> --\r\n>>\r\n>>\r\n>>\r\n>> *Tom Sinclair*Editor | www.herald.wales\r\n>>\r\n>> T: 01646 454545 ext 202\r\n>>\r\n>> E: editor@herald.email\r\n>>\r\n>> A: Carmarthenshire Herald Ltd t/a Herald.Wales, 11 Hamilton Terrace,\r\n>> Milford Haven, Pembrokeshire, SA73 3AL\r\n>> <https://www.google.com/maps/search/11+Hamilton+Terrace,+Milford+Haven,+Pembrokeshire,+SA73+3AL?entry=gmail&source=g>\r\n>>\r\n>> [image: facebook icon] <https://www.facebook.com/Herald.Wales>  [image:\r\n>> twitter icon] <https://twitter.com/HeraldWales>  [image: instagram icon]\r\n>> <https://www.instagram.com/herald.wales/>\r\n>> [image: Banner] <https://www.herald.wales/>\r\n>>\r\n>> Herald.Wales and The Pembrokeshire Herald titles are regulated by the\r\n>> Independent Press Standards Organisation Member of BBC Local Democracy News\r\n>> Service & BBC News Hub. The Pembrokeshire Herald is registered as a\r\n>> newspaper at Companies House pursuant to the Newspaper Libel and\r\n>> Registration Act 1881, No. N-5802. The Pembrokeshire Herald and other\r\n>> titles are deposited weekly at The National Library of Wales for\r\n>> preservation by legal order pursuant to the Legal Deposit Libraries Act 2003\r\n>>\r\n>\r\n\r\n-- \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n*Tom Sinclair*Editor | www.herald.wales\r\n\r\nT: 01646 454545 ext 202\r\n\r\nE: editor@herald.email\r\n\r\nA: Carmarthenshire Herald Ltd t/a Herald.Wales, 11 Hamilton Terrace,\r\nMilford Haven, Pembrokeshire, SA73 3AL\r\n\r\n[image: facebook icon] <https://www.facebook.com/Herald.Wales>  [image:\r\ntwitter icon] <https://twitter.com/HeraldWales>  [image: instagram icon]\r\n<https://www.instagram.com/herald.wales/>\r\n[image: Banner] <https://www.herald.wales/>\r\n\r\nHerald.Wales and The Pembrokeshire Herald titles are regulated by the\r\nIndependent Press Standards Organisation Member of BBC Local Democracy News\r\nService & BBC News Hub. The Pembrokeshire Herald is registered as a\r\nnewspaper at Companies House pursuant to the Newspaper Libel and\r\nRegistration Act 1881, No. N-5802. The Pembrokeshire Herald and other\r\ntitles are deposited weekly at The National Library of Wales for\r\npreservation by legal order pursuant to the Legal Deposit Libraries Act 2003\r\n"},{"id":"wb-3f2b97ab","subject":"Re great house farm williams buckler family Llandough vale o","name":"0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm-williams-buckler-family-llandough-vale-o_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm-williams-buckler-family-llandough-vale-o_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"great-house-farm-williams-buckler-family-llandough-vale-o","scopeTopics":["morgan","family"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["morgan","family"],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm-williams-buckler-family-llandough-vale-o_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"Diolch am eich ymholiad. Byddwn yn ymateb cyn pen 20 diwrnod gwaith, oni bai bod chi wedi dewis ein Gwasanaeth Blaenoriaeth, wedyn byddwn mewn cysylltiad yn fuan gyda manylion pellach a gwybodaeth am dalu am y gwasanaeth.  Yn y cyfamser, gallai’r wybodaeth isod fod yn ddefnyddiol i chi:\r\nI gael gwybodaeth am safleoedd hanesyddol yng Nghymru a chael manylion am yr hyn sydd yn ein harchifau, ewch i’n cronfa ddata a’n catalog, https://coflein.gov.uk/cy/\r\nOs yn bosibl, rydym yn argymell eich bod yn trefnu apwyntiad i ymweld â’n llyfrgell ac ystafell chwilio<https://cbhc.gov.uk/gwasanaethau/> ein harchif, os hoffech weld deunydd yr archif â’ch llygaid eich hun a thynnu lluniau heb orfod talu amdanynt. Yn gyffredinol, mae gwneud hynny’n gynt ac yn rhatach o lawer nag archebu sganiau o bell.\r\nMae llawer o’n delweddau digidol ar gael i’w lawrlwytho, eu prynu a’u trwyddedu yn syth o Coflein. Mae canllawiau ynghylch sut mae gwneud hynny i’w gweld yn y fideo byr hwn: Lawrlwytho delweddau o Coflein<https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JYJmCNrRFqY> Os nad yw’r ddelwedd yr ydych yn chwilio amdani ar gael drwy’r system hon, y rheswm am hynny’n aml fydd cyfyngiadau hawlfraint, ac efallai y byddwch am chwilio am ddelwedd amgen sydd ar gael, os yw hynny’n bosibl.\r\nOs ydych yn gofyn am set ddata, cofiwch fod y data hwnnw ar gael am ddim yn awr dan y Drwydded Llywodraeth Agored<https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/doc/open-government-licence-cymraeg/version/3/>, ac y gallwch fynd ati eich hun i chwilio am ddata am y tir<https://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nmrw_jan2024_sites> a’r môr<https://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nmrw_jan2024_maritimesites> ar MapDataCymru neu Cymru Hanesyddol<https://historic-wales-rcahmw.hub.arcgis.com/#zoom=0&lat=300000&lon=258000&layers=BFFFFFTFFTTT>.\r\nOs ydych yn cysylltu â ni i ofyn am gael mynediad i safle hanesyddol, cofiwch nad yw’r Comisiwn Brenhinol yn berchen ar unrhyw un o’r safleoedd a gaiff eu rhestru ar Coflein, ac nad oes gennym unrhyw fanylion cyswllt ar gyfer perchnogion safleoedd. Bydd angen i chi gysylltu’n uniongyrchol â pherchnogion safleoedd i ofyn am gael mynediad.\r\nOs ydych yn poeni am gyflwr heneb neu safle hanesyddol, dylech gysylltu â Gwasanaeth Amgylchedd Hanesyddol Llywodraeth Cymru, sef Cadw, neu â’r awdurdod lleol perthnasol. Yn yr un modd, os oes gennych ymholiad am statws statudol safle hanesyddol neu am y broses gynllunio sy’n ymwneud ag Adeiladau Rhestredig neu Henebion Hynafol Cofrestredig, mae ystod o wybodaeth ar gael ar wefan Cadw: Cyngor a Chymorth | Cadw<https://cadw.llyw.cymru/cyngor-a-chymorth?_gl=1*15twz22*_ga*MjAwNzczNTg4MS4xNzExNjMwMjE1*_ga_B2BCVKM874*MTcxMzQ1MDIyNS43LjAuMTcxMzQ1MDIyNS42MC4wLjA.>.\r\nPrif rôl y Comisiwn Brenhinol yw creu, curadu a rhannu gwybodaeth am yr amgylchedd hanesyddol, ac rydym hefyd yn chwarae rhan fel ymgyngoreion yn y broses gynllunio ar gyfer yr amgylchedd hanesyddol ar sail y wybodaeth honno. Fodd bynnag, er bod yr amgylchedd hanesyddol yng Nghymru yn bwysig i’n staff, nid ydym ni fel corff yn gyfrifol am orfodi deddfwriaeth ym maes cynllunio a threftadaeth nac yn gyfrifol am ddiogelu safleoedd hanesyddol, ac nid ydym chwaith yn gweithredu fel grŵp lobïo.\r\n\r\n\r\nThank you for your enquiry.  We will respond within 20 working days, unless you have selected our Priority Service option in which case we will be in contact with further details and information on paying for this service soon.  In the meantime, you may find the information below helpful:\r\n\r\nFor information on historic sites in Wales and details of archive holdings see our database and catalogue, https://coflein.gov.uk/en/\r\n\r\nWhere possible we recommend that you make an appointment to visit our archive search room and library<https://rcahmw.gov.uk/services/> to view archive material in person and take photos without charge.  This is generally much quicker and cheaper than ordering scans remotely.\r\n\r\nMany of our digital images are available to download, purchase and licence directly from Coflein.  Guidance on how to do this can be seen in this short video: Downloading images from Coflein (youtube.com)<https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gKpL61F1M2w&t=3s>  If the image which you want is not available via this system, this is often due to copyright restrictions and you may wish to identify an alternative image which is available, if possible.\r\n\r\nIf you are requesting a dataset, please be advised that this data is now freely available under the Open Government Licence (nationalarchives.gov.uk)<https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/doc/open-government-licence/version/3/> and you can carry out the searches yourself for terrestrial<https://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nmrw_jan2024_sites> and maritime<https://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nmrw_jan2024_maritimesites> data on Data Map Wales and on Historic Wales (arcgis.com)<https://historic-wales-rcahmw.hub.arcgis.com/#zoom=0&lat=300000&lon=258000&layers=BFFFFFTFFTTT>.\r\n\r\nIf you are contacting us to request physical access to a historic site please note that the Royal Commission does not own any of the sites listed on Coflein, nor do we hold contact details for site owners.  You will need to contact the site owners directly with your request.\r\n\r\nIf you are concerned about the condition of a historic site or ancient monument please contact the Welsh Government’s Historic Environment Service, Cadw or the relevant local authority.  Similarly, if you have a query regarding the statutory status of a historic site or the planning process relating to Listed Buildings or Scheduled Ancient Monuments there is a range of information available on Cadw’s website: Advice & Support | Cadw (gov.wales)<https://cadw.gov.wales/advice-support>\r\nThe Royal Commission’s primary role is to create, curate and communicate information on the historic environment, we also play a role as consultees in the planning process for the historic environment on the basis of this information.  However, whilst our staff are passionate about the historic environment in Wales, as a body we are not responsible for enforcing planning or heritage legislation, safeguarding historic sites, nor do we act as a lobbying group.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nNMR Enquiries\r\nYmholiadau Cyhoeddus I Public Enquiries\r\nFfordd Penglais | Penglais Road, Aberystwyth, SY23 3BU\r\n+44 (0) 1970 621 200\r\nchc.cymru@cbhc.gov.uk | nmr.wales@rcahmw.gov.uk\r\nwww.cbhc.gov.uk | www.rcahmw.gov.uk\r\nNoddir gan Lywodraeth Cymru | Sponsored by Welsh Government\r\nRydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg a Saesneg. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn peri oedi.\r\nWe welcome correspondence in Welsh and English. Corresponding in Welsh will not lead to any delay.\r\n\r\nYn unol â Rheoliadau Safonau'r Gymraeg (Rhif 2) 2016, mae gennych hawl i gyfathrebu a gohebu â Chomisiwn Brenhinol Henebion Cymru yn eich dewis iaith. Er mwyn sicrhau ein bod yn cyflawni’r hawl, rhowch wybod i ni a ydych yn dymuno derbyn gohebiaeth a/neu alwadau ffôn oddi wrthym yn y Gymraeg. Bydd yr wybodaeth hon yn cael ei chofnodi gennym ni, a byddwn yn defnyddio'r iaith o’ch dewis ym mhob cyfathrebu yn y dyfodol. Diolch.\r\nUnder the Welsh Language Standards (No. 2) Regulations 2016, you have the right to communicate and correspond with the Royal Commission on the Ancient and Historical Monuments of Wales in your preferred language. To ensure we uphold this right, please let us know whether you wish to receive correspondence and/or telephone calls from us in Welsh. This information will be recorded by us, and we will use your preferred language in all future communication. Thank you.\r\n​\r\nFrom: Requester <[redacted]>\r\nSent: 20 May 2026 22:11\r\nTo: unclehowell <[redacted]>\r\nSubject: Great House Farm, Williams/ Buckler Family, Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan\r\n\r\n\r\nSir / Madam,\r\n\r\nTo all those whom it concerns, BCC'd into this email.\r\n\r\nI write to request disclosure under the Freedom of Information Act 2000 and, where applicable, the Environmental Information Regulations 2004.\r\n\r\nThis request concerns Great House Farm / Ty Mawr (Great House), Llandough, associated estate lands, historic occupation and related heritage records. My family is Buckler and before that Williams, and we occupied Great House Farm from 1667 until our forceful dispossession of it in 1988.  Without our ownership ever having been estiguished and without BP ever having a ruling of ownership, only possession.\r\n\r\nIt's alledged our fee simple absolute and manorial title was stolen. And when BP registered our lands in their names in 1982, with our stolen title, my nan who challenged this all her life, during the 2 week land registry window to challenge BP's registration, which they achieved via purgergy and document/ identity fraud, she conveniently died. As my father did after his appeal of the 1987 mistake ruling was mistakely dismissed and his ECHR request blocked and home abruptly demolished, which he was prevented from stopping having been hospitalised and arrested without charges by armed force officers and possible SAS.\r\n\r\nhttps://youtu.be/0eXOoj7eUCo?si=hcgSA2pFgEE943HG\r\n\r\nI understand records may exist within the National Monuments Record of Wales, Historic Environment Record archives, archaeological consultations and planning files, including references already identified as:\r\n\r\n• Great House Farm HER site entry – GGAT03586s\r\n• Land at Great House Farm HER event entry – GGATE003872\r\n• The 1990 archaeological assessment commissioned by BP Properties Ltd.\r\n\r\nPlease provide copies of, or access to:\r\n\r\n  1.  All records concerning Great House Farm / Ty Mawr / Great House Estate, Llandough including:\r\n\r\n• surveys\r\n• reports\r\n• site notes\r\n• photographs\r\n• maps\r\n• aerial imagery\r\n• GIS layers\r\n• archive material\r\n• annotations\r\n• title references\r\n• occupancy references\r\n• landscape records\r\n• archaeological observations.\r\n\r\n  1.  All internal and external correspondence involving:\r\n\r\n• RCAHMW / NMRW\r\n• Cadw\r\n• GGAT / Heneb\r\n• Vale of Glamorgan Council\r\n• BP Properties Ltd\r\n• developers\r\n• planning authorities\r\n• landowners, estate representatives or consultants.\r\n\r\n  1.  Any documents discussing:\r\n\r\n• estate ownership\r\n• occupation history\r\n• family occupation or association\r\n• agricultural use\r\n• commemorative tree planting or memorial planting\r\n• estate boundaries\r\n• title history\r\n• acquisition history\r\n• disputes concerning ownership, heritage status or land use\r\n• estate restructuring, extinguishment, compulsory acquisition or title alteration.\r\n\r\n  1.  Copies of all material linked to GGAT03586s and GGATE003872, including the archaeological assessment and associated correspondence.\r\n  2.  Any records identifying historic occupants, proprietors, tenants or family associations, including references to the Williams family where present within archives.\r\n  3.  Metadata identifying file creators, accession history, provenance, dates and retention schedules.\r\n  4.  File indexes, archive references or schedules identifying further related material not already listed.\r\n\r\nPlease provide information electronically where possible.\r\n\r\nIf any material is withheld, please identify the exemption relied upon and provide the public interest assessment.\r\n\r\nRegards,\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nSion Buckler\r\n\r\n[redacted]\r\n"},{"id":"wb-6b172705","subject":"Re great house farm williams buckler family Llandough vale o","name":"0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm-williams-buckler-family-llandough-vale-o_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm-williams-buckler-family-llandough-vale-o_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","threadSlug":"great-house-farm-williams-buckler-family-llandough-vale-o","scopeTopics":["morgan","family"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["morgan","family"],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm-williams-buckler-family-llandough-vale-o_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original s