landough\r\n> Roman villa cemetery\" while omitting our saga; modern residents unaware of\r\n> the \"battleground\" site.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> These anomalies suggest systemic theft, from colonial English subjugation\r\n> (e.g., 1543–1770 orbit shifts) to corporate siege, during Wales'\r\n> cultural/economic transitions. Authority derived through such means is\r\n> illegitimate under natural justice, customary Welsh principles, and\r\n> moral-legal frameworks. The record's distortion secures advantage, but\r\n> passage of time does not validate it.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Preliminary assessment indicates cumulative benefits from redevelopment\r\n> (absent title extinguishment) exceed £100 million, forming a basis for\r\n> reparations—covering lost land, artifacts, livelihood, and trauma.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> I require relevant public servants, custodians, and office-holders\r\n> (including Land Registry, heraldry bodies, museums, archaeologists, MPs,\r\n> historians, and councillors) to cease delay, engage substantively,\r\n> review/correct/annotate records where defective, and prevent further\r\n> rewriting of history. Justice delayed is denied, especially with verifiable\r\n> evidence available.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> This Notice:\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> *       Challenges the record's accuracy, completeness, and legitimacy.\r\n>\r\n> *       Place assertions of title/standing on record pending adjudication.\r\n>\r\n> *       Reserves all rights without prejudice.\r\n>\r\n> *       Puts officials/beneficiaries on notice that silence/reliance may\r\n> aggravate responsibility.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> For the avoidance of doubt, His Majesty the King was notified yesterday.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> This serves as an invitation to engage constructively before reliance\r\n> becomes untenable.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Regards, Requester On behalf of the Buckler–Williams family [redacted],\r\n> [location redacted], Vale of Glamorgan, Wales. https://bpvsbuckler.datro.xyz/ <\r\n> https://bpvsbuckler.datro.xyz/>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Postscript — Disability and Succession (Material Notice): The\r\n> heir-apparent to the asserted Manorial Lordship is Rhys Buckler’s son, a\r\n> young man with Down syndrome. Any obstruction, marginalisation, or\r\n> differential treatment affecting his inheritance would engage the Equality\r\n> Act 2010 and UK international obligations on disability rights. Disability\r\n> cannot bar succession or recognition. This is recorded to prevent claims of\r\n> ignorance.\r\n> Please don't print this e-mail unless you really need to.\r\n> -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n> National Archives Disclaimer This email and any files transmitted with it\r\n> are intended solely for the use of the individual(s) to whom they are\r\n> addressed. If you are not the intended recipient and have received this\r\n> email in error, please notify the sender and delete the email. Opinions,\r\n> conclusions and other information in this message and attachments that do\r\n> not relate to the official business of The National Archives are neither\r\n> given nor endorsed by it.\r\n> ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n>\r\n>\r\n"},{"id":"wb-fb0b403e","subject":"Re great house farm Llandough nr penarth vale of glamorgan","name":"0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm-llandough-nr-penarth-vale-of-glamorgan_en_v0.0.0.00-2.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Council and local authority correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm-llandough-nr-penarth-vale-of-glamorgan_en_v0.0.0.00-2.txt","threadSlug":"great-house-farm-llandough-nr-penarth-vale-of-glamorgan","scopeTopics":["morgan"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["morgan"],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm-llandough-nr-penarth-vale-of-glamorgan_en_v0.0.0.00-2.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"[Recovered on 19 Oct 2026 from the original email in the family's Gmail, because this archive file was missing. Matched to this entry by its subject line; where several emails share a subject, the order within the group is not certain. Email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses are redacted.]\n\nSubject: RE: Great House Farm, Llandough, Nr Penarth, Vale of Glamorgan\nFrom: Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg\nDate: Fri, 29 May 2026 10:33 UTC\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nRef: 2026/2370\n\nDear Requester,\n\nThank you for your further email.\n\nFull details of our holdings can be found on our catalogue so you are welcome to search the records we hold to see if anything may be of use to you: Glamorgan Archives Catalogue<https://canfod.glamarchives.gov.uk/en/>. If you come across anything that you think might help we would be pleased to provide a quote for producing digital copies of the records for you.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nLouise Hunt\nArchifydd / Archivist\n\nArchifau Morgannwg / Glamorgan Archives\nClos Parc Morgannwg\nLecwydd / Leckwith\nCaerdydd / Cardiff\n[postcode redacted]\n\n[phone redacted]\n[email redacted]<[email redacted]>\n\nwww.archifaumorgannwg.gov.uk/<http://www.archifaumorgannwg.gov.uk/> www.glamarchives.gov.uk<http://www.glamarchives.gov.uk/>\nwww.facebook.com/glamarchives<http://www.facebook.com/glamarchives>\nwww.instagram.com/glamarchives<https://www.instagram.com/glamarchives/>\n\n**********************************************************************\nThe Council welcomes correspondence in Welsh, English or bilingually. We will ensure that we communicate with you in the language of your choice, as long as you let us know which you prefer. Corresponding in Welsh will not lead to delay.\n\nPrivileged/Confidential Information may be contained in this message. If you are not the addressee indicated in this message (or responsible for delivery of the message to such person), you may not copy or deliver this message to anyone. In such case, you should destroy this message and kindly notify the sender by reply email. Please advise immediately if you or your employer does not consent to Internet email for messages of this kind.  Opinions, conclusions and other information in this message that do not relate to the official business of the Council of the City and County of Cardiff shall be understood as neither given nor endorsed by it.  All e-mail sent to or from this address will be processed by Cardiff County Councils Corporate E-mail system and may be subject to scrutiny by someone other than the addressee.\n**********************************************************************\nMae’r Cyngor yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg, Saesneg neu’n ddwyieithog. Byddwn yn cyfathrebu â chi yn ôl eich dewis, dim ond i chi roi gwybod i ni pa un sydd well gennych. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi.\n\nMae'n bosibl bod gwybodaeth gyfrinachol yn y neges hon. Os na chyfeirir y neges atoch chi'n benodol (neu os nad ydych chi'n gyfrifol am drosglwyddo'r neges i'r person a enwir), yna ni chewch gopio na throsglwyddo'r neges. Mewn achos o'r fath, dylech ddinistrio'r neges a hysbysu'r anfonwr drwy e-bost ar unwaith. Rhowch wybod i'r anfonydd ar unwaith os nad ydych chi neu eich cyflogydd yn caniatau e-bost y Rhyngrwyd am negeseuon fel hon. Rhaid deall nad yw'r safbwyntiau, y casgliadau a'r wybodaeth arall yn y neges hon nad ydynt yn cyfeirio at fusnes swyddogol Cyngor Dinas a Sir Caerdydd yn cynrychioli barn y Cyngor Sir nad yn cael sel ei fendith. Caiff unrhyw negeseuon a anfonir at, neu o'r cyfeiriad e-bost hwn eu prosesu gan system E-bost Gorfforaethol Cyngor Sir Caerdydd a gallant gael eu harchwilio gan rywun heblaw'r person a enwir.\n**********************************************************************\n"},{"id":"wb-4b336acf","subject":"Re great house farm Llandough","name":"0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","threadSlug":"great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"[Recovered on 19 Oct 2026 from the original email in the family's Gmail, because this archive file was missing. Matched to this entry by its subject line; where several emails share a subject, the order within the group is not certain. Email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses are redacted.]\n\nSubject: RE: Great House Farm, Llandough\nFrom: NMR Enquiries\nDate: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 09:10 UTC\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nDiolch am eich ymholiad. Byddwn yn ceisio ymateb cyn pen 15 diwrnod gwaith. Rydym wedi gohirio ein gwasanaeth chwilio a blaenoriaeth ar hyn o bryd oherwydd prinder staff. Ymddiheuriadau am unrhyw anghyfleustra a achosir.\n\nYn y cyfamser, gallai’r wybodaeth isod fod yn ddefnyddiol i chi:\n\nI gael gwybodaeth am safleoedd hanesyddol yng Nghymru a chael manylion am yr hyn sydd yn ein harchifau, ewch i’n cronfa ddata a’n catalog, https://coflein.gov.uk/cy/\n\nOs yn bosibl, rydym yn argymell eich bod yn trefnu apwyntiad i ymweld â’n llyfrgell ac ystafell chwilio<https://cbhc.gov.uk/gwasanaethau/> ein harchif, os hoffech weld deunydd yr archif â’ch llygaid eich hun a thynnu lluniau heb orfod talu amdanynt. Yn gyffredinol, mae gwneud hynny’n gynt ac yn rhatach o lawer nag archebu sganiau o bell.\n\nMae llawer o’n delweddau digidol ar gael i’w lawrlwytho, eu prynu a’u trwyddedu yn syth o Coflein. Mae canllawiau ynghylch sut mae gwneud hynny i’w gweld yn y fideo byr hwn: Lawrlwytho delweddau o Coflein<https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JYJmCNrRFqY> Os nad yw’r ddelwedd yr ydych yn chwilio amdani ar gael drwy’r system hon, y rheswm am hynny’n aml fydd cyfyngiadau hawlfraint, ac efallai y byddwch am chwilio am ddelwedd amgen sydd ar gael, os yw hynny’n bosibl.\n\n\nThank you for your enquiry. We will endeavour to respond within 15 working days. Please note, we have currently suspended our Priority Enquiry Service due to staff shortage. We apologise for any inconvenience caused.\n\nIn the meantime, you may find the information below helpful:\n\nFor information on historic sites in Wales and details of archive holdings see our database and catalogue, https://coflein.gov.uk/en/\n\nWhere possible we recommend that you make an appointment to visit our archive search room and library<https://rcahmw.gov.uk/services/> to view archive material in person and take photos without charge.  This is generally much quicker and cheaper than ordering scans remotely.\n\nMany of our digital images are available to download, purchase and licence directly from Coflein.  Guidance on how to do this can be seen in this short video: Downloading images from Coflein (youtube.com)<https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gKpL61F1M2w&t=3s>  If the image which you want is not available via this system, this is often due to copyright restrictions and you may wish to identify an alternative image which is available, if possible.\n\n\n\nNMR Enquiries\nYmholiadau Cyhoeddus I Public Enquiries\nFfordd Penglais | Penglais Road, Aberystwyth, [postcode redacted]\n[phone redacted]\n[email redacted] | [email redacted]\nwww.cbhc.gov.uk | www.rcahmw.gov.uk\nNoddir gan Lywodraeth Cymru | Sponsored by Welsh Government\nRydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg a Saesneg. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn peri oedi.\nWe welcome correspondence in Welsh and English. Corresponding in Welsh will not lead to any delay.\n\nYn unol â Rheoliadau Safonau'r Gymraeg (Rhif 2) 2016, mae gennych hawl i gyfathrebu a gohebu â Chomisiwn Brenhinol Henebion Cymru yn eich dewis iaith. Er mwyn sicrhau ein bod yn cyflawni’r hawl, rhowch wybod i ni a ydych yn dymuno derbyn gohebiaeth a/neu alwadau ffôn oddi wrthym yn y Gymraeg. Bydd yr wybodaeth hon yn cael ei chofnodi gennym ni, a byddwn yn defnyddio'r iaith o’ch dewis ym mhob cyfathrebu yn y dyfodol. Diolch.\nUnder the Welsh Language Standards (No. 2) Regulations 2016, you have the right to communicate and correspond with the Royal Commission on the Ancient and Historical Monuments of Wales in your preferred language. To ensure we uphold this right, please let us know whether you wish to receive correspondence and/or telephone calls from us in Welsh. This information will be recorded by us, and we will use your preferred language in all future communication. Thank you.\n​\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]>\nSent: 17 September 2026 04:24\nTo: NMR Enquiries <[email redacted]>\nSubject: Great House Farm, Llandough\n\n\nRoyal Commission,\n\nI am researching the demolition of Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough-juxta-Penarth, in December 1988 (HER reference GGAT02038s / ST 1681 7331).\n\nI am seeking to establish whether the Royal Commission was consulted or notified before the farmhouse was demolished.\n\nContemporary reports from 3 December 1988 state that Cadw was considering emergency protection/listing of Great House Farm and that a Cadw inspection was being arranged, including discussion with the Royal Commission for Ancient and Historical Monuments.\n\nThe farmhouse was then demolished on 6 December 1988.\n\nI understand that the statutory framework at the time included provisions concerning the recording of historic buildings before authorised demolition. I would therefore be grateful if you could check your records and confirm:\n\n  1.  Whether the Royal Commission received any notification, consultation, referral or request for advice concerning Great House Farm before its demolition in December 1988.\n  2.  Whether the Royal Commission was asked to inspect, photograph, record or otherwise document the building before demolition.\n  3.  Whether any correspondence exists between the Royal Commission, Cadw, the local planning authority, BP Properties Ltd, or their representatives concerning the proposed demolition.\n  4.  Whether the Royal Commission was given an opportunity to complete or supplement its earlier recording of the farmhouse before demolition.\n  5.  Whether your records contain any photographs, measured drawings, reports, field notes or other documentation relating specifically to the farmhouse immediately before its demolition.\n  6.  If records exist, I would be grateful for the relevant reference numbers and, where possible, copies.\n\nFor context, the Royal Commission had already recorded Great House Farm architecturally in 1974. Contemporary material indicates that the building was subsequently under consideration for emergency listing shortly before its destruction.\n\nThere is also an important chronology concerning the occupation and possession of the property: my late father William Buckler had lodged European Commission of Human Rights application 14464/88 on 3 May 1988; BP obtained possession on 29 November 1988; demolition began on 6 December 1988; and the Strasbourg application was registered on 12 December 1988.\n\nI am not asking the Royal Commission to express an opinion on the ownership dispute or the legality of the demolition. I am simply seeking to establish, from your records, whether the Commission was notified or consulted and what opportunity, if any, it was given to record the building (our home since 1667) before it was destroyed?\n\nGiven the historical and archaeological significance subsequently identified at the site, I would particularly appreciate confirmation of whether any pre-demolition recording was requested or undertaken.\n\nRegards\n[cid:image001.jpg@01DD468C.C3547200]\n\n,\n\n\n\nSion Buckler (Ret'd Sergeant)\n\nRoyal Signals Cyber/ Electronic Warfare Professional and Life Member\n"},{"id":"wb-85476d46","subject":"Re great house farm Llandough","name":"0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00-2.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00-2.txt","threadSlug":"great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00-2.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"[Recovered on 19 Oct 2026 from the original email in the family's Gmail, because this archive file was missing. Matched to this entry by its subject line; where several emails share a subject, the order within the group is not certain. Email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses are redacted.]\n\nSubject: Re: Great House Farm, Llandough\nFrom: David Noble\nDate: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 17:26 UTC\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nHi Requester\nThank you for your email.\nI have spoken with a past Chair of Penarth Civic Society and she has\nconfirmed that we had no involvement with the planning process regarding\nthe farmhouse, our interests have always stopped at Cogan.\nI wonder if the Civic Trust was a different organisation operating at the\ntime?\n\nSorry we can’t be more helpful\nRegards\nDave Noble\nTrustee\n\nOn Thu, 17 Sep 2026 at 03:45, Requester <[email redacted]> wrote:\n\n> Dear Civic Trust,\n>\n> I am researching the 1989 planning application for the development of\n> Church View Close, Llandough-juxta-Penarth, and in particular the treatment\n> of the historic Great House Farm farmhouse.\n>\n> The surviving planning officer's report records that representations were\n> received from the *Civic Trust* and the *Barry Preservation Society*, but\n> the report does not set out the substance of those representations.\n>\n> I therefore request any records held by the Civic Trust concerning the\n> 1989 planning application, including:\n>\n>    - the letter, objection, representation or submission made by the\n>    Civic Trust concerning the application;\n>    - any comments specifically concerning the retention, demolition,\n>    alteration or preservation of the *Great House Farm farmhouse*;\n>    - any photographs, plans, notes, minutes, correspondence or internal\n>    records relating to the farmhouse or the application;\n>    - correspondence with Vale of Glamorgan/Barry Council, the developer,\n>    GGAT, Cadw or any other body concerning the historic or archaeological\n>    significance of the farmhouse/site;\n>    - the date on which the Trust became aware of the proposal and the\n>    date and method by which its representation was submitted; and\n>    - any subsequent correspondence concerning the fate or demolition of\n>    the farmhouse.\n>\n> I am particularly interested in establishing *what the Civic Trust said\n> in 1989 about whether Great House Farm farmhouse should be retained*, and\n> the evidence or reasons given for that position.\n>\n> The planning application concerned the site at Llandough-juxta-Penarth\n> subsequently developed as Church View Close. The relevant planning records\n> describe the site as formerly containing Great House Farm.\n>\n> Please provide copies of any records you hold, preferably as scanned PDFs\n> or images. If the original records are no longer held, please state that\n> and, if known, identify where they were transferred or deposited.\n>\n> This is a historical research request concerning records from 1989.\n>\n> Regards,\n>\n> Requester\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n"},{"id":"wb-23a1d7e5","subject":"Re great house farm Llandough","name":"0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00-3.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00-3.txt","threadSlug":"great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00-3.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"[Recovered on 19 Oct 2026 from the original email in the family's Gmail, because this archive file was missing. Matched to this entry by its subject line; where several emails share a subject, the order within the group is not certain. Email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses are redacted.]\n\nSubject: Re: Great House Farm, Llandough\nFrom: Requester\nDate: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 23:05 UTC\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nDave,\n\n​Thank you for checking with the past Chair and getting back to me so\nquickly.\n\n​You were spot on—the \"Civic Trust\" referenced in the 1989 report was\nindeed a separate national body: *The Civic Trust for Wales* (later Civic\nTrust Cymru), which operated as the umbrella organization for civic\nsocieties across Wales at the time.\n\n​That completely clears up the confusion and explains the geographical\nboundary stopping at Cogan. Because the national Trust ceased operations\naround 2016, I'll direct my search toward the Glamorgan Archives, where\ntheir historic records were deposited.\n\n​Thanks again for your time and for helping clear that up!\n\n​Best regards,\n\n​Requester\n\nOn Fri, 18 Sept 2026, 18:26 David Noble, <[email redacted]> wrote:\n\n> Hi Requester\n> Thank you for your email.\n> I have spoken with a past Chair of Penarth Civic Society and she has\n> confirmed that we had no involvement with the planning process regarding\n> the farmhouse, our interests have always stopped at Cogan.\n> I wonder if the Civic Trust was a different organisation operating at the\n> time?\n>\n> Sorry we can’t be more helpful\n> Regards\n> Dave Noble\n> Trustee\n>\n> On Thu, 17 Sep 2026 at 03:45, Requester <[email redacted]>\n> wrote:\n>\n>> Dear Civic Trust,\n>>\n>> I am researching the 1989 planning application for the development of\n>> Church View Close, Llandough-juxta-Penarth, and in particular the treatment\n>> of the historic Great House Farm farmhouse.\n>>\n>> The surviving planning officer's report records that representations were\n>> received from the *Civic Trust* and the *Barry Preservation Society*,\n>> but the report does not set out the substance of those representations.\n>>\n>> I therefore request any records held by the Civic Trust concerning the\n>> 1989 planning application, including:\n>>\n>>    - the letter, objection, representation or submission made by the\n>>    Civic Trust concerning the application;\n>>    - any comments specifically concerning the retention, demolition,\n>>    alteration or preservation of the *Great House Farm farmhouse*;\n>>    - any photographs, plans, notes, minutes, correspondence or internal\n>>    records relating to the farmhouse or the application;\n>>    - correspondence with Vale of Glamorgan/Barry Council, the developer,\n>>    GGAT, Cadw or any other body concerning the historic or archaeological\n>>    significance of the farmhouse/site;\n>>    - the date on which the Trust became aware of the proposal and the\n>>    date and method by which its representation was submitted; and\n>>    - any subsequent correspondence concerning the fate or demolition of\n>>    the farmhouse.\n>>\n>> I am particularly interested in establishing *what the Civic Trust said\n>> in 1989 about whether Great House Farm farmhouse should be retained*,\n>> and the evidence or reasons given for that position.\n>>\n>> The planning application concerned the site at Llandough-juxta-Penarth\n>> subsequently developed as Church View Close. The relevant planning records\n>> describe the site as formerly containing Great House Farm.\n>>\n>> Please provide copies of any records you hold, preferably as scanned PDFs\n>> or images. If the original records are no longer held, please state that\n>> and, if known, identify where they were transferred or deposited.\n>>\n>> This is a historical research request concerning records from 1989.\n>>\n>> Regards,\n>>\n>> Requester\n>>\n>>\n>>\n>>\n>>\n"},{"id":"wb-473fe4a4","subject":"Re great house farm Llandough","name":"0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00-4.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00-4.txt","threadSlug":"great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00-4.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"[Recovered on 19 Oct 2026 from the original email in the family's Gmail, because this archive file was missing. Matched to this entry by its subject line; where several emails share a subject, the order within the group is not certain. Email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses are redacted.]\n\nSubject: RE: Great House Farm, Llandough\nFrom: Pwyllgor Diwylliant, Cyfathrebu, y Gymraeg a Chwaraeon - Culture, Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee\nDate: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 07:13 UTC\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nDear Mr Buckler,\n\nYour correction is noted with thanks.\n\nKind regards,\n\nHaidee\n\n[cid:image001.png@01DD4F21.379E9E00]\nHaidee James[cid:image002.png@01DD4F21.379E9E00]\nAil Glerc, Pwyllgor Diwylliant, Cyfathrebu, y Gymraeg a Chwaraeon, Senedd Cymru\nSecond Clerk, Culture, Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee, Welsh Parliament\n[phone redacted] Rhagenwau|Pronouns: hi – she/her\n\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]>\nSent: 27 September 2026 05:12\nTo: Wales.OfficeMail <[email redacted]>\nCc: Coventry.OfficeMail <[email redacted]>\nSubject: Great House Farm, Llandough\n\nSirs/ Ma'ams,\n\nI need to correct a date I have used in earlier correspondence about Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr, Llandough, titles WA231076 and WA240304.\n\nI had treated 1983 as the year the two titles were merged. That was wrong. HM Land Registry’s own letter of 23 March 2026 (Lisa Griffiths, ref N610CGV) separates the two acts.\n1982–83 was first registration of two live titles, not a merger.\n30 November 1982: application to first-register WA231076. The Court of Appeal later treated BP Properties Ltd as registered proprietors of “the farm” from November 1982.\n23 February 1983: application to first-register a second title, WA240304.\n26 March 1983: Mary Williams died. On the family’s case, notice of those applications did not reach us until after her death, while the family was in mourning. She had no window in which to object. The family was not in a position to use any window that arrived after that.\nThose two titles remained separate through first instance.\n24 July 1986: Hollis J. Both titles were still live. The judgment language treats Great House Farm as one holding. It had not been one holding on the register. On the family’s case it had not been one holding since the 1877 split: Parcel A (house, buildings, garden/yard and about 10 acres, the Thomas/Williams line) and Parcel B (the eastern fields retained by Bute and later WGR/BP).\n2 February 1987: application to amalgamate the two titles. WA240304 was then closed and registration continued under WA231076. That is the merge. It was made after the 1986 ruling and before the Court of Appeal gave judgment on 31 July 1987.\nSo 1983 is silent first registration of a second title while Mary was still in Parcel A, or had only just died. 1987 is the administrative merger, carried out while the appeal was still open.\nThis was not a dispute about a single land parcel that BP already owned. Mary was on Parcel A, which the family says she owned, with occupation on that house parcel running in the family from at least 1667. BP, and WGR before it from 1949, were the neighbouring interest on Parcel B. From 1949 onwards the family’s case is that those neighbours ran a decades-long series of tenancy offers, “whole farm” wording, possession orders and the 1974 letters to make her occupation of her own parcel look permissive, and to make both parcels look like one estate they had acquired. They never produced a conveyance of Parcel A. What they had was an unfounded claim that her occupancy was permissive.\nPlease treat any earlier letter from me that said the titles merged in 1983 as corrected by this email. The dates that matter are:\n1982/83 — first registration of two titles, without the occupier being heard;\n24 July 1986 — first-instance judgment describing one farm while two titles still stood;\n2 February 1987 — amalgamation, before the appeal ended;\n31 July 1987 — Court of Appeal.\nYours faithfully,\n\nSion Buckler\nRefs where relevant: N610CGV; 260919-6726246; titles WA231076 and WA240304.\nSion Buckler (Ret'd Sergeant)\ngreathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com<http://greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com/>\n"},{"id":"wb-a2e62cf3","subject":"Re great house farm Llandough","name":"0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00-5.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00-5.txt","threadSlug":"great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00-5.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates witho