eam\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> *Consider the environment. Please don't print this e-mail unless you\n> really need to.*\n>\n> *Ystyriwch yr amgylchedd. Peidiwch ag argraffu'r neges hon oni bai fod\n> gwir angen.*\n>\n>\n>\n> Visit our Website at www.valeofglamorgan.gov.uk\n>\n> Ewch i'n gwefan yn www.bromorgannwg.gov.uk\n>\n>\n>\n> Find us on Facebook / Cewch ddod o hyd i ni ar Facebook\n> <http://en-gb.facebook.com/valeofglamorganlife>\n>\n> Follow us on Instagram / Dilynwch ni ar Instagram\n> <https://www.instagram.com/vog_council>\n>\n> Follow us on LinkedIn / Dilynwch ni ar LinkedIn\n> <https://www.linkedin.com/company/vale-of-glamorgan-council>\n>\n>\n>\n> Correspondence is welcomed in Welsh or English / Croesewir Gohebiaeth yn y\n> Gymraeg neu yn Saesneg.\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> <https://www.valeofglamorgan.gov.uk/en/our_council/achieving_our_vision/Corporate-Plan.aspx>\n>\n>\n>\n"},{"id":"wb-f1213062","subject":"Re great house farm church view close Llandough nr penarth v","name":"0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm-church-view-close-llandough-nr-penarth-v_en_v0.0.0.00-2.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Council and local authority correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm-church-view-close-llandough-nr-penarth-v_en_v0.0.0.00-2.txt","threadSlug":"great-house-farm-church-view-close-llandough-nr-penarth-v","scopeTopics":["morgan"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["morgan"],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm-church-view-close-llandough-nr-penarth-v_en_v0.0.0.00-2.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"[Recovered on 19 Oct 2026 from the original email in the family's Gmail, because this archive file was missing. Matched to this entry by its subject line; where several emails share a subject, the order within the group is not certain. Email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses are redacted.]\n\nSubject: RE: Great House Farm/ Church View Close, Llandough, nr. Penarth, Vale of Glamorgan (ATISN 27021)\nFrom: (name not shown; gov.wales address)\nDate: Thu, 11 Jun 2026 15:54 UTC\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nDear Mr Buckler,\n\nThank you for your request which I received on 1 June 2026, and your follow-up request received on 8 June 2026.\n\nPlease see attached response and documents.\n\nThank you,\n\nMike Weatherhead\n\nArweinydd Tîm Gwaith Achosion / Casework Team Leader\nPolisi a Gwarchod / Policy and Protection\nArolygiaeth yr Amgylchedd Hanesyddol / Historic Environment Inspectorate\nCadw\n\n\n\n\n\nFrom: Weatherhead, Michael (LCC - Cadw)\nSent: 01 June 2026 16:59\nTo: 'Requester' <[email redacted]>\nSubject: RE: Great House Farm/ Church View Close, Llandough, nr. Penarth, Vale of Glamorgan (ATISN 27021)\n\nDear Mr Buckler,\n\nGreat House Farm, Llandough\n\nThank you for your request which I received on 1 June 2026. You asked for:\n\n\n  1.  Any consideration of the property for statutory listing.\n  2.  Any listing assessments, surveys, inspections, recommendations or reports concerning the building.\n  3.  Any correspondence between Cadw, Welsh Office officials, local authorities, conservation bodies, archaeological organisations or owners concerning the historic significance, preservation, demolition or protection of the property.\n  4.  Any records explaining why the building was not added to the statutory list of buildings of special architectural or historic interest.\n  5.  Any records discussing the age, architectural significance, archaeological significance or surviving historic fabric of the building.\n  6.  Any records concerning emergency listing, spot-listing, preservation notices or other forms of protection considered for the property.\n  7.  Any records held relating to public campaigns, petitions or representations seeking preservation of the building.\n  8.  Any photographs, plans, surveys or architectural records held by Cadw or the Welsh Government relating to Great House Farm.\n\nYou will receive a response by 25 June 2026. If you have any queries about this request, please do not hesitate to contact me at the address below.  Please remember to quote the reference number above in any future correspondence (ATISN 27021).\n\nThe request you sent me contains personal information about you - for example, your name and address. The Welsh Government will be the data processor for this information and, in accordance with the General Data Protection Regulation, it will be processed in order to fulfil our public task and meet our legal obligations under the Act to provide you with a response. We will only use this personal information to deal with your request and any matters which arise as a result of it. We will keep your personal information and all other information relating to your request for three years from the date on which your request is finally closed.  Your personal information will then be disposed of securely.\n\nUnder data protection legislation, you have the right:\n\n\n  *   to be informed of the personal data we hold about you and to access it\n  *   to require us to rectify inaccuracies in that data\n  *   to (in certain circumstances) object to or restrict processing\n  *   for (in certain circumstances) your data to be ‘erased’\n  *   to (in certain circumstances) data portability\n  *   to lodge a complaint with the Information Commissioner’s Office (ICO) who is our independent regulator for data protection\n\nFor further information about the information which the Welsh Government holds and its use, or if you wish to exercise your rights under the GDPR, please see contact details below:\n\nData Protection Officer\nWelsh Government\nCathays Park\nCARDIFF\n[postcode redacted]\nEmail: [email redacted]<[email redacted]>\n\nThe contact details for the Information Commissioner’s Office are:\n\nWycliffe House\nWater Lane\nWilmslow\nCheshire\n[postcode redacted]\nWebsite: www.ico.org.uk<http://www.ico.org.uk/>\n\nAny information released under the Freedom of Information Act 2000 or Environmental Information Regulations 2004 will be listed in the Welsh Government’s Disclosure Log (at https://gov.wales/about/open-government/freedom-of-information/responses/?lang=en).\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nMike Weatherhead\n\nArweinydd Tîm Gwaith Achosion / Casework Team Leader\nPolisi a Gwarchod / Policy and Protection\nArolygiaeth yr Amgylchedd Hanesyddol / Historic Environment Inspectorate\nCadw\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSent: 28 May 2026 06:29\nTo: Freedom of Information Officer <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>; Listed Buildings / Adeiladau Rhestredig <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSubject: Great House Farm/ Church View Close, Llandough, nr. Penarth, Vale of Glamorgan\n\n\nDear Freedom of Information Officer,\n\nI am requesting information held by the Welsh Government and/or Cadw concerning Great House Farm (also known as Great House or Ty Mawr), Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan, prior to its demolition in 1988.\n\nI request copies of any recorded information held relating to:\n\n  1.  Any consideration of the property for statutory listing.\n  2.  Any listing assessments, surveys, inspections, recommendations or reports concerning the building.\n  3.  Any correspondence between Cadw, Welsh Office officials, local authorities, conservation bodies, archaeological organisations or owners concerning the historic significance, preservation, demolition or protection of the property.\n  4.  Any records explaining why the building was not added to the statutory list of buildings of special architectural or historic interest.\n  5.  Any records discussing the age, architectural significance, archaeological significance or surviving historic fabric of the building.\n  6.  Any records concerning emergency listing, spot-listing, preservation notices or other forms of protection considered for the property.\n  7.  Any records held relating to public campaigns, petitions or representations seeking preservation of the building.\n  8.  Any photographs, plans, surveys or architectural records held by Cadw or the Welsh Government relating to Great House Farm.\n\nFor the avoidance of doubt, I am seeking recorded information rather than an explanation or opinion created for the purpose of this request.\n\nIf any of the requested information is held by another public authority, I would be grateful if you could advise which authority is likely to hold the records.\n\nI would prefer the information in electronic form.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\nSion Buckler Esq. (Ret'd Sergeant). Heir & Successors of Mary Williams, Great House Farm, Llandough\n\nSganiwyd y neges hon am bob feirws hysbys wrth iddi adael Llywodraeth Cymru. Mae Llywodraeth Cymru yn cymryd o ddifrif yr angen i ddiogelu eich data. Os cysylltwch â Llywodraeth Cymru, mae ein hysbysiad preifatrwydd<”https://gov.wales/about/welsh-government-privacy-notice/?lang=cy”> yn esbonio sut rydym yn defnyddio eich gwybodaeth a sut rydym yn diogelu eich preifatrwydd. Rydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg. Byddwn yn anfon ateb yn Gymraeg i ohebiaeth a dderbynnir yn Gymraeg ac ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi. ----------------- On leaving the Welsh Government this email was scanned for all known viruses. The Welsh Government takes the protection of your data seriously. If you contact the Welsh Government then our Privacy Notice<https://gov.wales/about/welsh-government-privacy-notice/?lang=en> explains how we use your information and the ways in which we protect your privacy. We welcome receiving correspondence in Welsh. Any correspondence received in Welsh will be answered in Welsh and corresponding in Welsh will not lead to a delay in responding.\n"},{"id":"wb-e4c68210","subject":"Re great house farm","name":"0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"great-house-farm","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"Thanks Paul,\r\n\r\nOn Wed, 20 May 2026 at 15:25, Paul Egan <clerk@llandough-cc.co.uk> wrote:\r\n\r\n> Dear Requester,\r\n>\r\n> Thank you for your Freedom of Information request dated 18 May 2026 which \r\n> I will respond to within the statutory time limit.\r\n>\r\n> Regards,\r\n>\r\n> Paul\r\n> ---\r\n> Paul R. Egan BA, Chartered MCIPD, CiLCA, F. Inst. LM, FIPSM\r\n>\r\n> Clerk to Llandough Community Council,\r\n>\r\n> 63 Woodham Park,\r\n>\r\n> Barry,\r\n>\r\n> Vale of Glamorgan CF62 8FJ\r\n>\r\n> Telephone 01446 409294\r\n>\r\n> Website www.llandough-cc.co.uk\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>  \r\n>\r\n> When you contact us\r\n>\r\n> The information you provide (personal information such as name, address, \r\n> email address, phone number, organisation) will be processed and stored \r\n> to enable us to contact you and respond to your correspondence, provide \r\n> information and/or access our facilities and services. \r\n>\r\n> Your personal information will be not shared or provided to any other \r\n> third party. Your information may however be published in the public domain \r\n> if you require the Council to discuss the matter raised.\r\n>\r\n>  \r\n>\r\n> For further information please see our full privacy notice on our website \r\n> www.llandough-cc.co.uk\r\n>\r\n>  \r\n>\r\n> The Council's mission statement is 'Serving our community, preserving our \r\n> identity' The Council will always strive to:\r\n>\r\n>  ·  Be open and accessible in all our decision making \r\n>\r\n> · Be accountable in all our council activities\r\n>\r\n> · Be responsive to the needs of the community\r\n>\r\n> · Encourage community participation\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> On 2026-05-18 04:12, Requester wrote:\r\n>\r\n> Mr Egan,\r\n>\r\n> Further to my previous correspondence, I understand that matters \r\n> concerning the property and family were discussed by Llandough Community \r\n> Council in or around 1988 and that my late father William \"Billy\" Buckler \r\n> addressed the Council.\r\n>\r\n> I am attempting to reconstruct the historical chain of occupation, \r\n> ownership, administration and public record concerning the property, family \r\n> and associated farm lands. \r\n>\r\n> https://bpvsbuckler.datro.xyz\r\n>\r\n> Accordingly, I request disclosure of all records held by Llandough \r\n> Community Council, regardless of date, concerning:\r\n>\r\n>    - Ty Mawr (Great House), Llandough; \r\n>    - the associated farm lands and any connected parcels; \r\n>    - the Williams family and successors in occupation; \r\n>    - Billy Buckler, Branwen Buckler and members of the Buckler family; \r\n>    - historical ownership, occupation, tenancy, possession, planning, \r\n>    access, acquisition, disposal, development, disputes or representations; \r\n>    - BP v Buckler and related matters; \r\n>    - any meetings in which Billy Buckler addressed the Council. \r\n>\r\n> This request includes, but is not limited to:\r\n>\r\n>    - minute books; \r\n>    - agendas; \r\n>    - correspondence; \r\n>    - maps; \r\n>    - plans; \r\n>    - asset registers; \r\n>    - committee papers; \r\n>    - land schedules; \r\n>    - photographs; \r\n>    - memoranda; \r\n>    - clerk notes; \r\n>    - archived files; \r\n>    - transferred records; \r\n>    - records held by third-party archives on the Council's behalf. \r\n>\r\n> If records predating the present Council are not held, please confirm:\r\n>\r\n>    1. Whether older records were transferred elsewhere; \r\n>    2. The archive location; \r\n>    3. Whether records were destroyed and under what retention policy; \r\n>    4. The earliest records held concerning the property. \r\n>\r\n> My understanding is that family occupation and acquisition history extends \r\n> substantially earlier, potentially to the seventeenth century, and I am \r\n> seeking to establish the documentary chain.\r\n>\r\n> I am confident that our claim to the fee simple absolute manorial title \r\n> still stands and no amount of time, fraud, purgergy, court \"mistakes\", gaps \r\n> in records, demolition of our home, erasure of history (like Marconi \r\n> staying with us while inventing radio), acts of terrorism, human rights \r\n> violations, lawfare, nor proceedural unfairness and supression or \r\n> witholding of information and possible murder of members of my family has \r\n> changed that fact. It appears our title was stolen and this is a position \r\n> grandmother held until 1982 when she conveniently died in the 2 week window \r\n> she had to challenge BP's registrstion of our land in their name with the \r\n> land registry. \r\n>\r\n> ITV also recently declassified and published coverage too: \r\n>\r\n> https://youtu.be/4GXmR7mAas0?feature=shared\r\n>\r\n> This is a terrorist/ hostage situation so far as I see. My dispossessed \r\n> family are your hostages not constituents. They are too frightened to even \r\n> speak up about this matter. I require of your department some effort to \r\n> revisit your postion. \r\n>\r\n> My research tells me that the councillor at the time said, prior to my \r\n> father's death, that the situation with BP would result in a death. I \r\n> believe that was concerning the conflict with Bailiffs, Police and possibly \r\n> SAS. \r\n>\r\n> I have submitted a FOI to the MOD too. The Ministry of Justice are also on \r\n> notice and imply this matter is being investigated. \r\n>\r\n> If not me, who? If not now, when?\r\n>\r\n> My address is [redacted]. If \r\n> you care to visit or write or deliver items, please do so to that address. \r\n>\r\n> Kind regards,\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Requester Esq. (Ret'd Sergeant)\r\n>\r\n> On Thu, 20 Nov 2025, 05:42 Requester, <[redacted]> wrote:\r\n>\r\n> Paul, \r\n>  \r\n> I understand.  \r\n> Thanks for your reply nevertheless. \r\n>  \r\n> Requester\r\n> [redacted]\r\n>\r\n> On Thu, 20 Nov 2025, 04:40 Paul Egan, <clerk@llandough-cc.co.uk> wrote:\r\n>\r\n> Hi Requester,\r\n>\r\n>  \r\n>\r\n> I regret to inform you that we are unable to assist with the matter you \r\n> raise.\r\n>\r\n>  \r\n>\r\n> Regards,\r\n>\r\n>  \r\n>\r\n> Paul\r\n>\r\n>  \r\n>\r\n> Paul R. Egan BA, Chartered MCIPD, CiLCA, F. Inst. LM, FIPSM\r\n>\r\n> Clerk to Llandough Community Council,\r\n>\r\n> 63 Woodham Park \r\n> <https://www.google.com/maps/search/63+Woodham+Park?entry=gmail&source=g>,\r\n>\r\n> Barry,\r\n>\r\n> Vale of Glamorgan CF62 8FJ\r\n>\r\n> Telephone 01446 409294\r\n>\r\n> Website www.llandough-cc.co.uk\r\n>\r\n>  \r\n>\r\n> When you contact us\r\n>\r\n> The information you provide (personal information such as name, address, \r\n> email address, phone number, organisation) will be processed and stored to \r\n> enable us to contact you and respond to your correspondence, provide \r\n> information and/or access our facilities and services. Your personal \r\n> information will be not shared or provided to any other third party. Your \r\n> information may however be published in the public domain if you require \r\n> the Council to discuss the matter raised. \r\n>\r\n>  \r\n>\r\n> For further information please see our full privacy notice on our website \r\n> www.llandough-cc.co.uk\r\n>\r\n>  \r\n>\r\n> The Council's mission statement is 'Serving our community, preserving our \r\n> identity' The Council will always strive to:\r\n>\r\n>  ·  *Be open and accessible in all our decision making* \r\n>\r\n> · *Be accountable in all our council activities*\r\n>\r\n> · *Be responsive to the needs of the community*\r\n>\r\n> · *Encourage community participation*\r\n>\r\n>  \r\n>\r\n>  \r\n>\r\n> *From:* Requester <[redacted]> \r\n> *Sent:* 14 November 2025 12:49\r\n> *To:* kanishka.narayan.mp@parliament.uk\r\n> *Subject:* BP vs Buckler\r\n>\r\n>  \r\n>\r\n> Sir/Ma'am,\r\n>\r\n> I am your constituent, living [location redacted]. I am writing to request your \r\n> assistance in reviewing a historic legal matter involving my family: *BP \r\n> Properties Ltd v Buckler (1987)*, relating to Great House Farm, Llandough.\r\n>\r\n> My family lived on, cared for and claimed to own that farm for \r\n> generations. The events surrounding the 1987 case resulted in the loss of \r\n> our home and land, and left several members of my family deeply affected. \r\n> Recent research I have undertaken has revealed significant procedural \r\n> inconsistencies, historical irregularities, unanswered questions regarding \r\n> corporate identity, unverified service of key documents, and heritage \r\n> considerations relating to the site.\r\n>\r\n> I have attached a short report summarising SOME of these issues. I am \r\n> requesting your help in identifying the appropriate bodies—legal, \r\n> historical, or governmental—who may be able to independently review the \r\n> historical record to ensure fairness, accuracy, and transparency.\r\n>\r\n> My interest is not solely personal; Great House Farm has a heritage \r\n> significance to Wales, including documented accounts that Guglielmo Marconi \r\n> stayed there while experimenting with early radio technology. I believe \r\n> this should form part of our preserved historical record.\r\n>\r\n> By way of background, I am a veteran and have served both in the Armed \r\n> Forces and recently with the Vale of Glamorgan Council. My intention is \r\n> simply to seek clarity, proper review, and justice where appropriate. I \r\n> would be grateful for your guidance on the correct avenues to pursue.\r\n>\r\n> Thank you for your time and consideration. I look forward to your response.\r\n>\r\n> Yours sincerely,\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> *Requester*\r\n> [redacted] \r\n> [map link redacted]\r\n> [location redacted] \r\n> [map link redacted]\r\n> [redacted] \r\n> [map link redacted]\r\n> Tel: 0203 137 7118\r\n>\r\n>\r\n"},{"id":"wb-fef356a6","subject":"Re great house farm","name":"0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","threadSlug":"great-house-farm","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"not-recovered","text":null,"textNotice":"Original text not yet recovered: the archived source file for this record is missing. See sourceUrl."},{"id":"wb-a919c92f","subject":"Re great house farm","name":"0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm_en_v0.0.0.00-2.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm_en_v0.0.0.00-2.txt","threadSlug":"great-house-farm","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-great-house-farm_en_v0.0.0.00-2.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"not-recovered","text":null,"textNotice":"Original text not yet recovered: the archived source file for this record is missing. See sourceUrl."},{"id":"wb-715a1757","subject":"Re information request bp properties ltd v buckler 1987 requ","name":"0000-00-00_re-information-request-bp-properties-ltd-v-buckler-1987-requ_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-information-request-bp-properties-ltd-v-buckler-1987-requ_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"information-request-bp-properties-ltd-v-buckler-1987-requ","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-information-request-bp-properties-ltd-v-buckler-1987-requ_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"[Recovered on 19 Oct 2026 from the original email in the family's Gmail, because this archive file was missing. Matched to this entry by its subject line; where several emails share a subject, the order within the group is not certain. Email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses are redacted.]\n\nSubject: Re: Information Request - BP Properties Ltd v Buckler (1987) - Request Reference 00209998\nFrom: Requester\nDate: Tue, 23 Dec 2025 22:31 UTC\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nHi,\n\nThanks.\n\nRegards,\n\nSion\n\nOn Tue, 23 Dec 2025, 12:19 FOI Unit, <[email redacted]> wrote:\n\n> Dear Requester\n>\n> We are writing to introduce ourselves as the Council’s Information\n> Governance Team, who handle requests for information. Your request\n> regarding the above matter has been referred to us by the Council’s Legal\n> Services Department.\n>\n> We note that your request relates to a historic matter from 1987. As such,\n> it may take some time for us to check records and consider any information\n> that may be held. Please be aware that, due to the time that has passed,\n> the Council may no longer hold information relating to this matter. If this\n> is the case, we will do our best to offer advice that may assist you in\n> continuing your enquiries.\n>\n> We will write to you again as soon as possible. In the meantime, if you\n> have any further questions or need clarification, please don’t hesitate to\n> get in touch.\n>\n> Kind regards\n>\n>\n>\n> Information Governance Officer / Swyddog Llywodraethu Gwybodaeth\n>\n> Director's Office - Resources / Swyddfa’r Cyfarwyddwr - Adnoddau\n>\n> Vale of Glamorgan Council / Cyngor Bro Morgannwg\n>\n> tel / ffôn: [phone redacted]\n>\n> e-mail / e-bost: [email redacted] or\n> [email redacted]\n>\n>\n>\n> *Consider the environment. Please don't print this e-mail unless you\n> really need to.*\n>\n> *Ystyriwch yr amgylchedd. Peidiwch ag argraffu'r neges hon oni bai fod\n> gwir angen.*\n>\n>\n>\n> Visit our Website at www.valeofglamorgan.gov.uk\n>\n> Ewch i'n gwefan yn www.bromorgannwg.gov.uk\n>\n>\n>\n> Find us on Facebook / Cewch ddod o hyd i ni ar Facebook\n> <https://www.facebook.com/valeofglamorgancouncil>\n>\n> Follow us on Twitter / Dilynwch ni ar Twitter\n> <http://twitter.com/vogcouncil>\n>\n>\n>\n> Correspondence is welcomed in Welsh or English / Croesewir Gohebiaeth yn y\n> Gymraeg neu yn Saesneg.\n>\n>\n>\n"},{"id":"wb-3d1a9d7a","subject":"Re manor of Llandough mdr entries","name":"0000-00-00_re-manor-of-llandough-mdr-entries_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-manor-of-llandough-mdr-entries_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","threadSlug":"manor-of-llandough-mdr-entries","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-manor-of-llandough-mdr-entries_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"[Recovered on 19 Oct 2026 from the original email in the family's Gmail, because this archive file was missing. Matched to this entry by its subject line; where several emails share a subject, the order within the group is not certain. Email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses are redacted.]\n\nSubject: RE: Manor of Llandough - MDR Entries\nFrom: Philip Gale\nDate: Tue, 24 Feb 2026 16:50 UTC\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nDear Mr Buckler,\n\nThank you for your e-mail and for clarifying the subject of your enquiry.\n\nAs you know the Manorial Documents Register (MDR) only gives general descriptions of the content, extent and location of the manorial records recorded in the register: LLANDOUGH MANOR | The National Archives<https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/c/F236775> The manorial records for the Manor of Llandough are held at the National Library of Wales, Glamorgan Archives and West Glamorgan Archives, and their catalogues can be accessed on-line : NLW Catalogue<https://discover.library.wales/discovery/search?vid=44WHELF_NLW:44WHELF_NLW_NUI> , Glamorgan Archives Catalogue<https://canfod.glamarchives.gov.uk/en/> and Archive Collections - Swansea<https://www.swansea.gov.uk/archivecollections?lang=en>\n\nThe Welsh entries in the online MDR were added in the 1990’s were based on the survey returns to the Manorial Documents Committee received from institutions and private owners during the 1920’s. These were subsequently passed to the custody of the Historical Manuscripts Commission in 1959 which amalgamated with the Public Record Office to form The National Archives in 2003. I have requested to see the original returns for Manor of Llandough to see if they can provide any additional information on the documentation relating to the manor (Ref. HMC 9/311: https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/r/C5879336 )\n\nThere are several other manors with a similar name in Glamorgan and a MDR search using the search term “Llandough” gives the following result: Search results: Llandough Glamorgan | The National Archives<https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/results/c?_q=Llandough&_sf=text_man&_ocn=Glamorgan&_naet=M&_st=mdrc>   Sometimes the administration of different manors were brought together and this appears to have occurred  with the Llandough and St May’s Church Manors : https://archives.library.wales/index.php/manors-of-llandough-and-st-mary-church\n\nI will contact you again once I have had an opportunity to look at the original returns sent to Manorial Documents Committee. I hope this preliminary information is of assistance.\n\nYours sincerely\n\n[The National Archives logo]\nPhilip Gale | Head of the Collections and Cultural Property Team\nT: 020 3908 X 9193 | W: nationalarchives.gov.uk<http://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/>\nTwitter: @UkNatArchives\nThe National Archives, Kew, Richmond, Surrey [postcode redacted]\n\n\n\n\n________________________________\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSent: Wednesday, February 18, 2026 5:49:54 PM\nTo: Philip Gale <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSubject: Re: Formal Notice for Record: Church View Close, Llandough / BP vs Buckler 1987\n\n\nEXTERNAL: This email originates from outside of The National Archives.\n\nDear Mr Gale,\n\nThank you for your reply of 12 February. I would like to clarify and narrow my request to ensure there is no misunderstanding regarding the records I am trying to locate.\n\nMy correspondence is not seeking an adjudication of ownership or title — I fully accept the stated remit boundaries of the Manorial Documents Register (MDR). Rather, my enquiry concerns the existence of any archival reference, index card, catalogue entry, or pointer to a document historically known as the “Williams Title”, which was, according to contemporaneous family testimony, at one point:\n\n  1.  Held in the local public library in the first half of the twentieth century; and\n  2.  Indexed in some manner (e.g., an index card or catalogue reference) that persisted even after the original document could not be located.\n\nMy understanding of the MDR — as described on your own website — is that it exists to provide brief descriptions of manorial records and details of their locations:\n\n“An official index of English and Welsh manorial records providing brief descriptions of documents and details of their locations… It does not include title deeds or conveyances, and is not a register of ownership of manorial lordships or rights.”\nhttps://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/manor-search<https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/manor-search?utm_source=chatgpt.com>\n\nBased on that description, I would be grateful if you could confirm whether:\n\n  1.  Any catalogue entries, index cards, reference pointers, or archival descriptions relating to the Manor of Llandough or the Williams family title exist in the MDR or related archival catalogues.\n  2.  If so, the reference identifiers and information on how these can be consulted in person or via digitised records.\n\nTo be precise, I am looking for historical references to the index card or listing for the Williams Title — whether held in a library, local record office, or other institutional archive — rather than a claim for ownership judgment itself.\n\nIf such a reference does not currently appear in the MDR, I would greatly appreciate guidance on where any known catalogue cards or entry lists for manorial documents from Glamorgan / Llandough prior to 1950 might now reside (e.g., regional archives, National Archives holdings, or affiliated local repository catalogues).\n\nAnyway I anticipate your forgiveness, if I have erred in writing to you earlier.\n\nThank you again and in advance for your assistance.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nSion Buckler\nOn Wed, 18 Feb 2026 at 14:30, Philip Gale <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>> wrote:\n\nDear Mr Buckler,\n\n\n\nThank you for your e-mail of 10 February whose contents are noted. The National Archives does not hold any records relating to your enquiry. The Manorial Documents Register which this department administers is a register for the location of manorial documents and not of the ownership and descent of manorial titles:\n\n\n\nhttps://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/archives-sector/finding-records-in-discovery-and-other-databases/manorial-documents-register/\n\n\n\nA similar position holds for The National Archives responsibilities for public records and concerns about the past management of official records is a matter for the relevant government department such as HM Land Registry.\n\n\n\nYours sincerely\n\n\n\n\n\n[The National Archives logo]\n\nPhilip Gale | Head of the Collections and Cultural Property Team\nT: 020 3908 X 9193 | W: nationalarchives.gov.uk<http://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/>\nTwitter: @UkNatArchives\nThe National Archives, Kew, Richmond, Surrey [postcode redacted]\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n-----Original Message-----\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\n\nSent: 10 February 2026 22:15\n\nTo: Archives Sector Leadership <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\n\nCc: [email redacted]<[email redacted]>; [email redacted]<[email redacted]>; Paul Egan <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>; [email redacted]<[email redacted]>; [email redacted]<[email redacted]>\n\nSubject: Formal Notice for Record: Church View Close, Llandough / BP vs Buckler 1987\n\n\n\n        You don't often get email from [email redacted]<[email redacted]>. Learn why this is important <https://aka.ms/LearnAboutSenderIdentification>\n\n\n\n\n\nEXTERNAL: This email originates from outside of The National Archives.\n\n\n\nFORMAL NOTICE OF RECORD CHALLENGE, ASSERTION OF RIGHT, AND DEMAND FOR ENGAGEMENT\n\n\n\nDear Sir/Madam,\n\n\n\nThis Notice is issued for placement on official record and for reliance. It is not informal correspondence. I urge you not to resort to silence, deflection, or delay, as doing so following receipt of this notice constitutes knowing reliance upon, and maintenance of, records alleged to be materially defective through fraud, concealment, denial of due process, and systemic erasure. Please report any suspicious activity and discrepancies identified.\n\n\n\nThis Notice is issued in pursuit of narrative justice at this stage; formal judicial remedies are expressly reserved. It serves to document our family's side of the story before it is completely erased and history rewritten, ensuring awareness among custodians of land, heritage, heraldry, archaeology, and public records.\n\n\n\nMy elder brother, Rhys Buckler, and I are the sons of the late William Buckler (d. 1991), defendant in the landmark legal case BP Properties Ltd v Buckler (1987), and the grandchildren of the late Mary Williams, whose dispossession, marginalisation, and exclusion from judicial determination remain unresolved matters of record. Mary Williams was repeatedly and materially falsely addressed/mislabeled in legal and administrative records as “Mrs Buckler,” a misidentification that was never judicially tested and which directly affected the handling, scope, and outcome of proceedings. The Williams/Buckler family held claim to the land title, having acquired and occupied Great House Farm continuously since 1667, based on family records, historical evidence, and centuries of resistance to adverse claims.\n\n\n\nI place on record that I am the first member of my family able to speak publicly and formally on these matters. Other close family members remain too traumatised and intimidated by the historical conduct of these proceedings—including violent eviction, demolition, and loss of livelihood—to engage, despite the continuing consequences of dispossession. That silence must not be misread as consent, acquiescence, or abandonment of right. There is a material distinction between free silence and silence produced by fear, trauma, and coercive experience.\n\n\n\nThe story, as we have pieced it together, reveals a multi-generational pattern of lawfare and siege: from Norman/English conquests in 1100 granting lordships over ancient Welsh Christian landscapes, through monastic (1215–1539) and Crown seizures (1539 onward), to industrial encroachments (e.g., 1876 Limeworks severance without consent) and modern corporate possession actions. This culminated in the 1988 demolition of an 800-year-old farmhouse amid police standoffs, leaving the family homeless and the site scarred, now buried under Church View Close. Archaeological vindication came too late—1994 excavations revealed over 1,000 Roman and medieval burials, confirming family warnings (e.g., the 1870 Roman soldier discovery under the living room floor, suppressed by authorities)—yet heritage records celebrate the site while omitting our dispossession.\n\n\n\nThe following assertions of right are now formally placed on record. They have never been lawfully adjudicated or extinguished:\n\n\n\n1.      Rhys Buckler may be the rightful Manorial Lord of the Manor of Llandough, by right of primogeniture and continuous hereditary connection arising from our family’s occupation of Great House Farm from 1667 until our narrative, legal, and physical dispossession.\n\n2.      I assert a claim to act as Squire of the Manor of Llandough, as executive representative and guardian of the Buckler–Williams estate and matriarchal lineage, with a mandate to pursue correction of record, restitution, and restorative remedies.\n\n\n\nThese are assertions of right, not settled declarations, placed on record pending proper adjudication—which has been historically obstructed through procedural narrowing, missing documents, and avoidance of full hearings.\n\n\n\nIn 1974, Mary Williams was twice prevented from presenting her case and obtaining a judicial determination of superior ownership, despite claims grounded in long occupation, intent, and possession. The court confined inquiry to animus possidendi (intention to possess) and excluded animus domini (intention to own), reclassifying her from putative owner to mere occupier and foreclosing title adjudication. BP sought possession only, not ownership rulings—a choice unexplained and significant. Proceedings were adjourned or quashed without resolution.\n\n\n\nThis occurred despite evidence of ownership intent, including:\n\n\n\n*       Centuries-long occupation and resistance (e.g., failed 1938 auction due to local support; post-1945 rent disputes).\n\n*       Roman-era remains and historical significance (e.g., Guglielmo Marconi's 1897 residence during radio experiments, with family aid erased from records).\n\n*       Unformalized deals like the 1895–1905 Daniel Thomas quarrying permission for promised title, undermined by vanished deeds.\n\n\n\nJudicial ownership examination would have engaged archaeological/heritage protections, incompatible with redevelopment—consequences avoided, raising credibility concerns.\n\n\n\nSerious unresolved anomalies persist, indicating something is still not right with the record:\n\n\n\n*       Licence correspondence dated 31 October 1974 surfacing only on 4 November, potentially interrupting proceedings to prevent hearings.\n\n*       Claims of 1974 benevolent withdrawal under press scrutiny unsupported by verifiable coverage.\n\n*       Land registration before Mary Williams’s 1983 death without title determination; disputes possibly lodged/suppressed by Land Registry.\n\n*       Inconsistent treatment of BP entities across proceedings to family detriment.\n\n*       Procedural barriers preventing effective defense (e.g., interrupted essential services post-licence; 1960–1987 court rulings dismissing ancestral title as \"notion\" without evidence).\n\n*       Knowing bypass of archaeological constraints (e.g., 1978 trenches ignored; no contemporaneous 1970s/1990s press on discoveries amid development).\n\n*       Missing key documents (e.g., 1667 conveyance, 1811–1835 Lambert Williams agreements proving status, 1982–84 library deeds vanishing).\n\n*       Risks of further erasure: Heritage databases celebrate \"Llandough Roman villa cemetery\" while omitting our saga; modern residents unaware of the \"battleground\" site.\n\n\n\nThese anomalies suggest systemic theft, from colonial English subjugation (e.g., 1543–1770 orbit shifts) to corporate siege, during Wales' cultural/economic transitions. Authority derived through such means is illegitimate under natural justice, customary Welsh principles, and moral-legal frameworks. The record's distortion secures advantage, but passage of time does not validate it.\n\n\n\nPreliminary assessment indicates cumulative benefits from redevelopment (absent title extinguishment) exceed £100 million, forming a basis for reparations—covering lost land, artifacts, livelihood, and trauma.\n\n\n\nI require relevant public servants, custodians, and office-holders (including Land Registry, heraldry bodies, museums, archaeologists, MPs, historians, and councillors) to cease delay, engage substantively, review/correct/annotate records where defective, and prevent further rewriting of history. Justice delayed is denied, especially with verifiable evidence available.\n\n\n\nThis Notice:\n\n\n\n*       Challenges the record's accuracy, completeness, and legitimacy.\n\n*       Place assertions of title/standing on record pending adjudication.\n\n*       Reserves all rights without prejudice.\n\n*       Puts officials/beneficiaries on notice that silence/reliance may aggravate responsibility.\n\n\n\nFor the avoidance of doubt, His Majesty the King was notified yesterday.\n\n\n\nThis serves as an invitation to engage constructively before reliance becomes untenable.\n\n\n\nRegards, Requester On behalf of the Buckler–Williams family [address redacted], Vale of Glamorgan, Wales. https://bpvsbuckler.datro.xyz/ <https://bpvsbuckler.datro.xyz/>\n\n\n\nPostscript — Disability and Succession (Material Notice): The heir-apparent to the asserted Manorial Lordship is Rhys Buckler’s son, a young man with Down syndrome. Any obstruction, marginalisation, or differential treatment affecting his inheritance would engage the Equality Act 2010 and UK international obligations on disability rights. Disability cannot bar succession or recognition. This is recorded to prevent claims of ignorance.\nPlease don't print this e-mail unless you really need to. ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- National Archives Disclaimer This email and any files transmitted with it are intended solely for the use of the individual(s) to whom they are addressed. If you are not the intended recipient and have received this email in error, please notify the sender and delete the email. Opinions, conclusions and other information in this message and attachments that do not relate to the official business of The National Archives are neither given nor endorsed by it. ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPlease don't print this e-mail unless you really need to. ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- National Archives Disclaimer This email and any files transmitted with it are intended solely for the use of the individual(s) to whom they are addressed. If you are not the intended recipient and have received this email in error, please notify the sender and delete the email. Opinions, conclusions and other information in this message and attachments that do not relate to the official business of The National Archives are neither given nor endorsed by it. ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n"},{"id":"wb-e2d6608d","subject":"Re rc25 0464 information related to great house farm llandou","name":"0000-00-00_re-rc25-0464-information-related-to-great-house-farm-llandou_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-rc25-0464-information-related-to-great-house-farm-llandou_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"rc25-0464-information-related-to-great-house-farm-llandou","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-rc25-0464-information-related-to-great-house-farm-llandou_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"Thanks for your prompt and detailed response. Most diligent of you.\r\nSincerely appreciated. Good drills.\r\n\r\nRegards,\r\n\r\nSion Buckler\r\n[redacted]\r\n\r\n\r\nOn Wed, 26 Nov 2025, 09:48 Dr Adam Coward, <adam.coward@rcahmw.gov.uk>\r\nwrote:\r\n\r\n> Good afternoon,\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Thank you very much for your enquiry. While we cannot advise you on a\r\n> legal case or undertake a review of historical decisions, pleas note that\r\n> we do hold information and archives related to Great House Farm, Llandough.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> The site entry and description for the site in the National Monuments\r\n> Record of Wales can be seen online on Coflein, here:\r\n> https://coflein.gov.uk/en/site/20182.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> We hold 14 archives related to the site, a list of which can be seen here:\r\n> https://coflein.gov.uk/en/site/20182/archives?size=25. These archives can\r\n> be viewed in our Search Room, located in the National Library of Wales,\r\n> which is open Tuesday through Thursday, 9:30-16:30. If you would like to\r\n> view the material in person, I would advise arranging with us beforehand so\r\n> that we can prepare the material ahead of your visit. Some of the material\r\n> is on microfiche and as we do not have a microfiche reader in the Search\r\n> Room, we will need to liaise with the National Library Reading Room staff\r\n> to try to negotiate the use of their machines ahead of your visit.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> While it is possible to order scans of material using the attached order\r\n> form, we may be unable to provide research-quality scans or photographs of\r\n> some of the material, such as the material on microfiche, which would\r\n> require high-resolution scanning at a cost of £20 per scan. Moreover,  many\r\n> of these records remain under third-party copyright, and we would only be\r\n> able to provide scans or copies for personal, non-commercial research.\r\n> Other uses, such as use in a legal case or report would likely require the\r\n> permission of the copyright holder(s). I would therefore advise arranging\r\n> to view the material in person rather than ordering scans, if possible.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> In addition to the information and records which we hold, there are\r\n> several related records in the Historic Environment Record maintained by\r\n> Heneb, which related to excavations, mostly concerning the cemetery/burial\r\n> site:\r\n>\r\n>    -\r\n>    https://archwilio.org.uk/arch/query/page.php?watprn=GGAT03584s&dbname=ggat&tbname=core\r\n>    -\r\n>    https://archwilio.org.uk/arch/query/page.php?watprn=GGAT03583s&dbname=ggat&tbname=core\r\n>    -\r\n>    https://archwilio.org.uk/arch/query/page.php?watprn=GGAT03586s&dbname=ggat&tbname=core\r\n>    -\r\n>    https://archwilio.org.uk/arch/query/page.php?watprn=GGAT03585s&dbname=ggat&tbname=core\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Thank you very much again for your enquiry.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Sincerely,\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Adam Coward\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Dr Adam Coward\r\n>  Rheolwr Llyfrgell ac Ymholiadau | Library and Enquiries Manager\r\n> Ffordd Penglais | Penglais Road,  Aberystwyth,  SY23 3BU\r\n> +44 (0) 1970 621 216 <+44%20(0)%201970%20621%20216>\r\n> adam.coward@cbhc.gov.uk  |  adam.coward@rcahmw.gov.uk\r\n> www.cbhc.gov.uk <https://cbhc.gov.uk/>  |  www.rcahmw.gov.uk\r\n> <https://rcahmw.gov.uk/>\r\n> Noddir gan Lywodraeth Cymru  |  Sponsored by Welsh Government\r\n> [image: Facebook]\r\n> <https://www.facebook.com/Royal-Commission-on-the-Ancient-and-Historical-Monuments-of-Wales-146120328739808>\r\n> [image: Twitter] <https://www.twitter.com/rcahmwales>\r\n>\r\n> Rydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg a Saesneg. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn peri oedi.\r\n>\r\n> We welcome correspondence in Welsh and English. Corresponding in Welsh will not lead to any delay.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Yn unol â Rheoliadau Safonau'r Gymraeg (Rhif 2) 2016, mae gennych hawl i\r\n> gyfathrebu a gohebu â Chomisiwn Brenhinol Henebion Cymru yn eich dewis\r\n> iaith. Er mwyn sicrhau ein bod yn cyflawni’r hawl, rhowch wybod i ni a\r\n> ydych yn dymuno derbyn gohebiaeth a/neu alwadau ffôn oddi wrthym yn y\r\n> Gymraeg. Bydd yr wybodaeth hon yn cael ei chofnodi gennym ni, a byddwn yn\r\n> defnyddio'r iaith o’ch dewis ym mhob cyfathrebu yn y dyfodol. Diolch.\r\n> ​​​​\r\n> Under the Welsh Language Standards (No. 2) Regulations 2016, you have the\r\n> right to communicate and correspond with the Royal Commission on the\r\n> Ancient and Historical Monuments of Wales in your preferred language. To\r\n> ensure we uphold this right, please let us know whether you wish to receive\r\n> correspondence and/or telephone calls from us in Welsh. This information\r\n> will be recorded by us, and we will use your preferred language in all\r\n> future communication. Thank you.\r\n> ​\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> *From:* Requester <[redacted]>\r\n> *Sent:* 14 November 2025 12:49\r\n> *To:* kanishka.narayan.mp@parliament.uk\r\n> *Subject:* BP vs Buckler\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> You don't often get email from [redacted]. Learn why this\r\n> is important <https://aka.ms/LearnAboutSenderIdentification>\r\n>\r\n> Sir/Ma'am,\r\n>\r\n> I am your constituent, living [location redacted]. I am writing to request your\r\n> assistance in reviewing a historic legal matter involving my family: *BP\r\n> Properties Ltd v Buckler (1987)*, relating to Great House Farm, Llandough.\r\n>\r\n> My family lived on, cared for and claimed to own that farm for\r\n> generations. The events surrounding the 1987 case resulted in the loss of\r\n> our home and land, and left several members of my family deeply affected.\r\n> Recent research I have undertaken has revealed significant procedural\r\n> inconsistencies, historical irregularities, unanswered questions regarding\r\n> corporate identity, unverified service of key documents, and heritage\r\n> considerations relating to the site.\r\n>\r\n> I have attached a short report summarising SOME of these issues. I am\r\n> requesting your help in identifying the appropriate bodies—legal,\r\n> historical, or governmental—who may be able to independently review the\r\n> historical record to ensure fairness, accuracy, and transparency.\r\n>\r\n> My interest is not solely personal; Great House Farm has a heritage\r\n> significance to Wales, including documented accounts that Guglielmo Marconi\r\n> stayed there while experimenting with early radio technology. I believe\r\n> this should form part of our preserved historical record.\r\n>\r\n> By way of background, I am a veteran and have served both in the Armed\r\n> Forces and recently with the Vale of Glamorgan Council. My intention is\r\n> simply to seek clarity, proper review, and justice where appropriate. I\r\n> would be grateful for your guidance on the correct avenues to pursue.\r\n>\r\n> Thank you for your time and consideration. I look forward to your response.\r\n>\r\n> Yours sincerely,\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> *Requester*\r\n> [redacted]\r\n> [map link redacted]\r\n> [location redacted]\r\n> [map link redacted]\r\n> [redacted]\r\n> [map link redacted]\r\n> Tel: 0203 137 7118\r\n>\r\n"},{"id":"wb-c1d07b46","subject":"Re rc26 0205 great house farm williams buckler family llando","name":"0000-00-00_re-rc26-0205-great-house-farm-williams-buckler-family-llando_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-rc26-0205-great-house-farm-williams-buckler-family-llando_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"rc26-0205-great-house-farm-williams-buckler-family-llando","scopeTopics":["morgan","family"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["morgan","family"],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-rc26-0205-great-house-farm-williams-buckler-family-llando_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Dr Coward,\r\n\r\nThank you for your assistance to date and for providing access to the\r\nCoflein archive listings relating to Great House Farm, Llandough.\r\n\r\nHaving reviewed your correspondence carefully, I note that RCAHMW holds\r\nfourteen archive records associated with the site.\r\n\r\nTo assist my research, I would be grateful if you could provide, for each\r\nof the fourteen archive records, any information held regarding:\r\n\r\n• full archive title; • archive number; • creator or originating body; •\r\naccession source; • accession date; • provenance; • date range covered; •\r\nphysical format (photograph, report, microfiche, survey, map, drawing,\r\netc.); • extent (number of pages, images, sheets, frames, etc.); •\r\ndescriptive notes not visible on the public catalogue; • related archive\r\nreferences.\r\n\r\nIn addition, I would be grateful if you could confirm whether any of the\r\nfourteen archives contain references to:\r\n\r\n• ownership of Great House Farm; • occupiers or tenants; • the Williams\r\nfamily; • Daniel Thomas; • Bute Estate land transactions; • estate\r\nboundaries; • farm buildings before demolition; • archaeological surveys; •\r\nBP Properties Ltd; • planning consultations; • oral history; •\r\ncommemorative tree planting; • historic photographs of the farm and\r\nsurrounding land.\r\n\r\nYou previously confirmed that RCAHMW does not hold correspondence relating\r\nto the site. Could you also confirm whether any related material exists\r\noutside the fourteen listed archives, including:\r\n\r\n• aerial photographs; • survey photographs; • field notebooks; •\r\nunpublished reports; • HER-linked records; • transferred archives; •\r\naccession files; • provenance files; • curator notes; • associated\r\ncollections indexed under alternative site names.\r\n\r\nAs part of my research into the history, occupation and documentary record\r\nof Great House Farm, I would also be grateful if you could advise whether\r\nany archive accession records identify the person, organisation or\r\nauthority that deposited the material with RCAHMW.\r\n\r\nFinally, if any material has been transferred to another repository, please\r\nidentify the repository and any relevant catalogue references.\r\n\r\nMany thanks for your assistance.\r\n\r\nKind regards,\r\n\r\nSion Buckler (Ret'd Sgt) | bpvsbuckler.datro.xyz\r\n\r\nOn Mon, 8 Jun 2026, 05:30 Dr Adam Coward, <adam.coward@rcahmw.gov.uk> wrote:\r\n\r\n> Good morning,\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Thank you for your email. The full list of archives which we hold related\r\n> to the site can be seen here:\r\n> https://coflein.gov.uk/en/sites/20182/archives. Fuller information about\r\n> the archives can be seen by clicking on the red ‘Archive Number’ for each\r\n> entry in the list, which will take you to the specific entry for it on our\r\n> catalogue. This will include information such as titles, dates, and\r\n> descriptions, albeit we may not have information such as dates for all\r\n> items.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> All the best,\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Adam\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Dr Adam Coward\r\n>  Rheolwr Llyfrgell ac Ymholiadau | Library and Enquiries Manager\r\n> Ffordd Penglais | Penglais Road,  Aberystwyth,  SY23 3BU\r\n> +44 (0) 1970 621 216 <+44%20(0)%201970%20621%20216>\r\n> adam.coward@cbhc.gov.uk  |  adam.coward@rcahmw.gov.uk\r\n> www.cbhc.gov.uk <https://cbhc.gov.uk/>  |  www.rcahmw.gov.uk\r\n> <https://rcahmw.gov.uk/>\r\n> Noddir gan Lywodraeth Cymru  |  Sponsored by Welsh Government\r\n> [image: Facebook]\r\n> <https://www.facebook.com/Royal-Commission-on-the-Ancient-and-Historical-Monuments-of-Wales-146120328739808>\r\n> [image: Twitter] <https://www.twitter.com/rcahmwales>\r\n>\r\n> Rydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg a Saesneg. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn peri oedi.\r\n>\r\n> We welcome correspondence in Welsh and English. Corresponding in Welsh will not lead to any delay.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Yn unol â Rheoliadau Safonau'r Gymraeg (Rhif 2) 2016, mae gennych hawl i\r\n> gyfathrebu a gohebu â Chomisiwn Brenhinol Henebion Cymru yn eich dewis\r\n> iaith. Er mwyn sicrhau ein bod yn cyflawni’r hawl, rhowch wybod i ni a\r\n> ydych yn dymuno derbyn gohebiaeth a/neu alwadau ffôn oddi wrthym yn y\r\n> Gymraeg. Bydd yr wybodaeth hon yn cael ei chofnodi gennym ni, a byddwn yn\r\n> defnyddio'r iaith o’ch dewis ym mhob cyfathrebu yn y dyfodol. Diolch.\r\n> ​​​​\r\n> Under the Welsh Language Standards (No. 2) Regulations 2016, you have the\r\n> right to communicate and correspond with the Royal Commission on the\r\n> Ancient and Historical Monuments of Wales in your preferred language. To\r\n> ensure we uphold this right, please let us know whether you wish to receive\r\n> correspondence and/or telephone calls from us in Welsh. This information\r\n> will be recorded by us, and we will use your preferred language in all\r\n> future communication. Thank you.\r\n> ​\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> *From:* Requester <[redacted]>\r\n> *Sent:* 05 June 2026 06:04\r\n> *To:* Dr Adam Coward <adam.coward@rcahmw.gov.uk>\r\n> *Subject:* Re: RC26-0205 - Great House Farm, Williams/ Buckler Family,\r\n> Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Adam,\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Might I please get the full archive list with titles, dates and\r\n> descriptions if you don't already have it. Of the 14 files you cited.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> ownership\r\n>\r\n> occupiers\r\n>\r\n> estate history\r\n>\r\n> photographs before demolition\r\n>\r\n> farm buildings\r\n>\r\n> oral history\r\n>\r\n> surveys undertaken before development\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Regards,\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Requester\r\n>\r\n> On Thu, 28 May 2026, 11:47 Dr Adam Coward, <adam.coward@rcahmw.gov.uk>\r\n> wrote:\r\n>\r\n> Good afternoon,\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Thank you for your enquiry. As noted in my email of 26 November last,\r\n> while we cannot advise you on a legal case or undertake a review of\r\n> historical decisions, we do hold information and archives related to Great\r\n> House Farm, Llandough.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> The site entry and description for the site in the National Monuments\r\n> Record of Wales can be seen online on Coflein, here:\r\n> https://coflein.gov.uk/en/site/20182.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> We hold 14 archives related to the site, a list of which can be seen here:\r\n> https://coflein.gov.uk/en/site/20182/archives?size=25. These archives can\r\n> be viewed in our Search Room, located in the National Library of Wales,\r\n> which is open Tuesday through Thursday, 9:30-16:30. If you would like to\r\n> view the material in person, I would advise arranging with us beforehand so\r\n> that we can prepare the material ahead of your visit. Some of the material\r\n> is on microfiche and as we do not have a microfiche reader in the Search\r\n> Room, we will need to liaise with the National Library Reading Room staff\r\n> to try to negotiate the use of their machines ahead of your visit. However,\r\n> I have located an electronic copy of Archive Number 6524751, which can be\r\n> accessed here:\r\n> https://www.walesher1974.org/her/app/php/herumd.php?group=GGAT&level=3&docid=301361628\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> While it is possible to order scans of material using the attached order\r\n> form, we may be unable to provide research-quality scans or photographs of\r\n> some of the material, such as the material on microfiche, which would\r\n> require high-resolution scanning at a cost of £20 per scan. Moreover,  many\r\n> of these records remain under third-party copyright, and we would only be\r\n> able to provide scans or copies for personal, non-commercial research.\r\n> Other uses, such as use in a legal case or report would likely require the\r\n> permission of the copyright holder(s). I would therefore advise arranging\r\n> to view the material in person rather than ordering scans, if possible.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Finally, I have confirmed with our Records Manager that we do not hold any\r\n> correspondence related to the site.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Thank you again for your enquiry.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Sincerely,\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Adam Coward\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> *Dr Adam Coward*\r\n>\r\n> * Rheolwr Llyfrgell ac Ymholiadau | Library and Enquiries Manager*\r\n>\r\n> Ffordd Penglais | Penglais Road,\r\n>\r\n>  Aberystwyth,\r\n>\r\n>  SY23 3BU\r\n>\r\n> +44 (0) 1970 621 216 <+44%20(0)%201970%20621%20216>\r\n>\r\n> adam.coward@cbhc.gov.uk\r\n>\r\n>  |\r\n>\r\n> adam.coward@rcahmw.gov.uk\r\n>\r\n> www.cbhc.gov.uk <https://cbhc.gov.uk/>\r\n>\r\n> |\r\n>\r\n>  www.rcahmw.gov.uk <https://rcahmw.gov.uk/>\r\n>\r\n> *Noddir gan Lywodraeth Cymru *\r\n>\r\n> |\r\n>\r\n> * Sponsored by Welsh Government*\r\n>\r\n> [image: Facebook]\r\n> <https://www.facebook.com/Royal-Commission-on-the-Ancient-and-Historical-Monuments-of-Wales-146120328739808>\r\n>\r\n> [image: Twitter] <https://www.twitter.com/rcahmwales>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Rydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg a Saesneg. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn peri oedi.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> We welcome correspondence in Welsh and English. Corresponding in Welsh will not lead to any delay.\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Yn unol â Rheoliadau Safonau'r Gymraeg (Rhif 2) 2016, mae gennych hawl i\r\n> gyfathrebu a gohebu â Chomisiwn Brenhinol Henebion Cymru yn eich dewis\r\n> iaith. Er mwyn sicrhau ein bod yn cyflawni’r hawl, rhowch wybod i ni a\r\n> ydych yn dymuno derbyn gohebiaeth a/neu alwadau ffôn oddi wrthym yn y\r\n> Gymraeg. Bydd yr wybodaeth hon yn cael ei chofnodi gennym ni, a byddwn yn\r\n> defnyddio'r iaith o’ch dewis ym mhob cyfathrebu yn y dyfodol. Diolch.\r\n>\r\n> ​​​​\r\n>\r\n> Under the Welsh Language Standards (No. 2) Regulations 2016, you have the\r\n> right to communicate and correspond with the Royal Commission on the\r\n> Ancient and Historical Monuments of Wales in your preferred language. To\r\n> ensure we uphold this right, please let us know whether you wish to receive\r\n> correspondence and/or telephone calls from us in Welsh. This information\r\n> will be recorded by us, and we will use your preferred language in all\r\n> future communication. Thank you.\r\n> ​\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> *From:* Requester <[redacted]>\r\n> *Sent:* 20 May 2026 22:11\r\n> *To:* unclehowell <[redacted]>\r\n> *Subject:* Great House Farm, Williams/ Buckler Family, Llandough, Vale of\r\n> Glamorgan\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Sir / Madam,\r\n>\r\n> To all those whom it concerns, BCC'd into this email.\r\n>\r\n> I write to request disclosure under the Freedom of Information Act 2000\r\n> and, where applicable, the Environmental Information Regulations 2004.\r\n>\r\n> This request concerns Great House Farm / Ty Mawr (Great House), Llandough,\r\n> associated estate lands, historic occupation and related heritage records.\r\n> My family is Buckler and before that Williams, and we occupied Great House\r\n> Farm from 1667 until our forceful dispossession of it in 1988.  Without our\r\n> ownership ever having been estiguished and without BP ever having a ruling\r\n> of ownership, only possession.\r\n>\r\n> It's alledged our fee simple absolute and manorial title was stolen. And\r\n> when BP registered our lands in their names in 1982, with our stolen title,\r\n> my nan who challenged this all her life, during the 2 week land registry\r\n> window to challenge BP's registration, which they achieved via purgergy and\r\n> document/ identity fraud, she conveniently died. As my father did after his\r\n> appeal of the 1987 mistake ruling was mistakely dismissed and his ECHR\r\n> request blocked and home abruptly demolished, which he was prevented from\r\n> stopping having been hospitalised and arrested without charges by armed\r\n> force officers and possible SAS.\r\n>\r\n> https://youtu.be/0eXOoj7eUCo?si=hcgSA2pFgEE943HG\r\n>\r\n> I understand records may exist within the National Monuments Record of\r\n> Wales, Historic Environment Record archives, archaeological consultations\r\n> and planning files, including references already identified as:\r\n>\r\n> • Great House Farm HER site entry – GGAT03586s\r\n> • Land at Great House Farm HER event entry – GGATE003872\r\n> • The 1990 archaeological assessment commissioned by BP Properties Ltd.\r\n>\r\n> Please provide copies of, or access to:\r\n>\r\n>    1. All records concerning Great House Farm / Ty Mawr / Great House\r\n>    Estate, Llandough including:\r\n>\r\n> • surveys\r\n> • reports\r\n> • site notes\r\n> • photographs\r\n> • maps\r\n> • aerial imagery\r\n> • GIS layers\r\n> • archive material\r\n> • annotations\r\n> • title references\r\n> • occupancy references\r\n> • landscape records\r\n> • archaeological observations.\r\n>\r\n>    2. All internal and external correspondence involving:\r\n>\r\n> • RCAHMW / NMRW\r\n> • Cadw\r\n> • GGAT / Heneb\r\n> • Vale of Glamorgan Council\r\n> • BP Properties Ltd\r\n> • developers\r\n> • planning authorities\r\n> • landowners, estate representatives or consultants.\r\n>\r\n>    3. Any documents discussing:\r\n>\r\n> • estate ownership\r\n> • occupation history\r\n> • family occupation or association\r\n> • agricultural use\r\n> • commemorative tree planting or memorial planting\r\n> • estate boundaries\r\n> • title history\r\n> • acquisition history\r\n> • disputes concerning ownership, heritage status or land use\r\n> • estate restructuring, extinguishment, compulsory acquisition or title\r\n> alteration.\r\n>\r\n>    4. Copies of all material linked to GGAT03586s and GGATE003872,\r\n>    including the archaeological assessment and associated correspondence.\r\n>    5. Any records identifying historic occupants, proprietors, tenants or\r\n>    family associations, including references to the Williams family where\r\n>    present within archives.\r\n>    6. Metadata identifying file creators, accession history, provenance,\r\n>    dates and retention schedules.\r\n>    7. File indexes, archive references or schedules identifying further\r\n>    related material not already listed.\r\n>\r\n> Please provide information electronically where possible.\r\n>\r\n> If any material is withheld, please identify the exemption relied upon and\r\n> provide the public interest assessment.\r\n>\r\n> Regards,\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Requester\r\n>\r\n> [redacted]\r\n> [map link redacted]\r\n>\r\n>\r\n"},{"id":"wb-2ffa0a24","subject":"Re rc26 0205 great house farm williams buckler family llando","name":"0000-00-00_re-rc26-0205-great-house-farm-williams-buckler-family-llando_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-rc26-0205-great-house-farm-williams-buckler-family-llando_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","threadSlug":"rc26-0205-great-house-farm-williams-buckler-family-llando","scopeTopics":["morgan","family"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["morgan","family"],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-rc26-0205-great-house-farm-williams-buckler-family-llando_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"[Recovered on 19 Oct 2026 from the original email in the family's Gmail, because this archive file was missing. Matched to this entry by its subject line; where several emails share a subject, the order within the group is not certain. Email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses are redacted.]\n\nSubject: Re: RC26-0205 - Great House Farm, Williams/ Buckler Family, Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan\nFrom: Requester\nDate: Fri, 29 May 2026 22:56 UTC\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nThanks Adam.\n\nHaving recently completed my 2022 years in Oath as a public servant.\nEmployed under the welsh government/ Vale of Glamorgan for the last 2 of\nit. And left my military career with honours before that. I always prided\nmyself serving as a public servant.\n\nBut I am outraged at what occured to my family and its a huge scandal. And\nanyone in office should not be treating this like an us/them situation\nwhere I'm going up against my Welsh Govenrment and fudiciaries.\n\nThere's a lot of women in my family too terrified to even speak up against\nthis. My father alledged fraud. He died. My grandad died before being able\nto explain how BP got our title. My nan died in the window she had to\nchallenge BP's registration of our land in their names. I am the only one\nwho is willing to speak up and out about this.\n\nIts not I wasn't frightened like they were. Its that statute of limitation\napplied and there wasn't much that could be done. But I suspect fraud as of\nthis year and this means this can and should be reopened.\n\nI am horrified that the MP and Councillor for Llandough are remaining\nsilent. Not responding. But its possible they might need time to take a\nposition on this. So far as I see the British (Thatcher and BP) seem\nimplicated. And us indigenous welsh on our ancestral birthland home since\n1667, makes this very bad for them.\n\nSo if there's any Welsh still in Wales, that care that our herritage and\nhistory could be looted and history covered up as this appears to have, I\nhope someone else will make noise about this too. And not just let it rest.\n\nMarconi stayed with us while inventing radio and there's not even a plaque\nto commemorate Church View Close, Llandough as the birthplace of radio.\nThis is a huge scandal. I understand many Brits are liberal left and\nMarconi was right wing. Thats what Cardiff City Council say about why\nthere's no plaque to commemorate marconi on the statute of a radio at\nCardiff Bay. But this perversion and supression of history and facts is so\nunacceptable. I have nephews and children and the fact they can't see any\ntrace of our 320 years in Llandough , up until 1988 is an outrage. The fact\nthe press all labelled us squatters and thats what they will grow up\nreading, when it was our home since 1667 is also an outrage. They're\nclearly hostages under terrorists and there's nothing democratic about any\nof this.\n\nSo far as I see what happened to our family in 1988 , in how we were\ndisplaced, isn't just tyranny or british colonising. It's a literally act\nof terrorism. Which I reported to police yesterday as such. And I consider\nmy family there in Wales hostages under said terrorists. The situation is\nuntenable. We require remedy. Narrative justice. Correction of records.\nSome press release. Something to amend this situation.\n\nThe fact ITV quietly just declassified footage from 1988 after 38 years was\ncowardly. They cannot expect to get away with that.  The footage clearly\nsays neighbours report SAS used and the footage shows SAS and there's\nmention of the land being prime Real Estate overlooking cardiff bay. But\nmore so is the fact that my old best friends late mum, Nichola Hayward\nThomas, who is the news anchor, never mentioned this footage to me and took\nthis to her grave. And in the clip she fails to mention the assault on my\nmum, while serving the injuction to the illegal occupiers terrorists,\nproperly SAS. Didn't just dislocate her shoulder. But she was pregnant with\nthree toddlers. So even the declassified footage after 38 years supressed,\nis itself carefully scripted to show bias. And there's more on that. Like\nthe fact the edit/ cut is so abrupt when the SAS looking man or mention of\nreal estate value being prime.\n\nWe scattered to the wind, looted and half dead in 1988 after decades of\nlawfare and this terrorist attack and siege on our home. We want justice.\nBecause what we've said all along seems to have been the plausible story.\nAnd what the press, records and courts ruled seems to be the fraud.\n\nWithout legitimacy there's no authority here. It's important as fudiciaries\nyou be legitimate.\n\nI'm horrified at the replies I have received thus far. Like CADW saying\nthey had nothing to do with this or land registry converting my query into\na complaint only to squash is. And failing that some other cowardly reply.\n\nThere's institutional failure. There's court mistakes. Theres proceedural\nunfairness.\nPurgergy and fraud. I expect better from the Welsh Govenrment to fix this.\n\nPlease do everything in your power.\nThis is all I ask.\n\nRegards,\n\nSion Buckler\n[address redacted], Vale of Glamorgan\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nOn Thu, 28 May 2026, 11:47 Dr Adam Coward, <[email redacted]>\nwrote:\n\n> Good afternoon,\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you for your enquiry. As noted in my email of 26 November last,\n> while we cannot advise you on a legal case or undertake a review of\n> historical decisions, we do hold information and archives related to Great\n> House Farm, Llandough.\n>\n>\n>\n> The site entry and description for the site in the National Monuments\n> Record of Wales can be seen online on Coflein, here:\n> https://coflein.gov.uk/en/site/20182.\n>\n>\n>\n> We hold 14 archives related to the site, a list of which can be seen here:\n> https://coflein.gov.uk/en/site/20182/archives?size=25. These archives can\n> be viewed in our Search Room, located in the National Library of Wales,\n> which is open Tuesday through Thursday, 9:30-16:30. If you would like to\n> view the material in person, I would advise arranging with us beforehand so\n> that we can prepare the material ahead of your visit. Some of the material\n> is on microfiche and as we do not have a microfiche reader in the Search\n> Room, we will need to liaise with the National Library Reading Room staff\n> to try to negotiate the use of their machines ahead of your visit. However,\n> I have located an electronic copy of Archive Number 6524751, which can be\n> accessed here:\n> https://www.walesher1974.org/her/app/php/herumd.php?group=GGAT&level=3&docid=301361628\n>\n>\n>\n> While it is possible to order scans of material using the attached order\n> form, we may be unable to provide research-quality scans or photographs of\n> some of the material, such as the material on microfiche, which would\n> require high-resolution scanning at a cost of £20 per scan. Moreover,  many\n> of these records remain under third-party copyright, and we would only be\n> able to provide scans or copies for personal, non-commercial research.\n> Other uses, such as use in a legal case or report would likely require the\n> permission of the copyright holder(s). I would therefore advise arranging\n> to view the material in person rather than ordering scans, if possible.\n>\n>\n>\n> Finally, I have confirmed with our Records Manager that we do not hold any\n> correspondence related to the site.\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you again for your enquiry.\n>\n>\n>\n> Sincerely,\n>\n>\n>\n> Adam Coward\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> Dr Adam Coward\n>  Rheolwr Llyfrgell ac Ymholiadau | Library and Enquiries Manager\n> Ffordd Penglais | Penglais Road,  Aberystwyth,  [postcode redacted]\n> [phone redacted] <+44%20(0)%201970%20621%20216>\n> [email redacted]  |  [email redacted]\n> www.cbhc.gov.uk <https://cbhc.gov.uk/>  |  www.rcahmw.gov.uk\n> <https://rcahmw.gov.uk/>\n> Noddir gan Lywodraeth Cymru  |  Sponsored by Welsh Government\n> [image: Facebook]\n> <https://www.facebook.com/Royal-Commission-on-the-Ancient-and-Historical-Monuments-of-Wales-146120328739808>\n> [image: Twitter] <https://www.twitter.com/rcahmwales>\n>\n> Rydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg a Saesneg. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn peri oedi.\n>\n> We welcome correspondence in Welsh and English. Corresponding in Welsh will not lead to any delay.\n>\n>\n>\n> Yn unol â Rheoliadau Safonau'r Gymraeg (Rhif 2) 2016, mae gennych hawl i\n> gyfathrebu a gohebu â Chomisiwn Brenhinol Henebion Cymru yn eich dewis\n> iaith. Er mwyn sicrhau ein bod yn cyflawni’r hawl, rhowch wybod i ni a\n> ydych yn dymuno derbyn gohebiaeth a/neu alwadau ffôn oddi wrthym yn y\n> Gymraeg. Bydd yr wybodaeth hon yn cael ei chofnodi gennym ni, a byddwn yn\n> defnyddio'r iaith o’ch dewis ym mhob cyfathrebu yn y dyfodol. Diolch.\n> ​​​​\n> Under the Welsh Language Standards (No. 2) Regulations 2016, you have the\n> right to communicate and correspond with the Royal Commission on the\n> Ancient and Historical Monuments of Wales in your preferred language. To\n> ensure we uphold this right, please let us know whether you wish to receive\n> correspondence and/or telephone calls from us in Welsh. This information\n> will be recorded by us, and we will use your preferred language in all\n> future communication. Thank you.\n> ​\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> *From:* Requester <[email redacted]>\n> *Sent:* 20 May 2026 22:11\n> *To:* unclehowell <[email redacted]>\n> *Subject:* Great House Farm, Williams/ Buckler Family, Llandough, Vale of\n> Glamorgan\n>\n>\n>\n> Sir / Madam,\n>\n> To all those whom it concerns, BCC'd into this email.\n>\n> I write to request disclosure under the Freedom of Information Act 2000\n> and, where applicable, the Environmental Information Regulations 2004.\n>\n> This request concerns Great House Farm / Ty Mawr (Great House), Llandough,\n> associated estate lands, historic occupation and related heritage records.\n> My family is Buckler and before that Williams, and we occupied Great House\n> Farm from 1667 until our forceful dispossession of it in 1988.  Without our\n> ownership ever having been estiguished and without BP ever having a ruling\n> of ownership, only possession.\n>\n> It's alledged our fee simple absolute and manorial title was stolen. And\n> when BP registered our lands in their names in 1982, with our stolen title,\n> my nan who challenged this all her life, during the 2 week land registry\n> window to challenge BP's registration, which they achieved via purgergy and\n> document/ identity fraud, she conveniently died. As my father did after his\n> appeal of the 1987 mistake ruling was mistakely dismissed and his ECHR\n> request blocked and home abruptly demolished, which he was prevented from\n> stopping having been hospitalised and arrested without charges by armed\n> force officers and possible SAS.\n>\n> https://youtu.be/0eXOoj7eUCo?si=hcgSA2pFgEE943HG\n>\n> I understand records may exist within the National Monuments Record of\n> Wales, Historic Environment Record archives, archaeological consultations\n> and planning files, including references already identified as:\n>\n> • Great House Farm HER site entry – GGAT03586s\n> • Land at Great House Farm HER event entry – GGATE003872\n> • The 1990 archaeological assessment commissioned by BP Properties Ltd.\n>\n> Please provide copies of, or access to:\n>\n>    1. All records concerning Great House Farm / Ty Mawr / Great House\n>    Estate, Llandough including:\n>\n> • surveys\n> • reports\n> • site notes\n> • photographs\n> • maps\n> • aerial imagery\n> • GIS layers\n> • archive material\n> • annotations\n> • title references\n> • occupancy references\n> • landscape records\n> • archaeolo