s?size=25. These archives can\n> be viewed in our Search Room, located in the National Library of Wales,\n> which is open Tuesday through Thursday, 9:30-16:30. If you would like to\n> view the material in person, I would advise arranging with us beforehand so\n> that we can prepare the material ahead of your visit. Some of the material\n> is on microfiche and as we do not have a microfiche reader in the Search\n> Room, we will need to liaise with the National Library Reading Room staff\n> to try to negotiate the use of their machines ahead of your visit. However,\n> I have located an electronic copy of Archive Number 6524751, which can be\n> accessed here:\n> https://www.walesher1974.org/her/app/php/herumd.php?group=GGAT&level=3&docid=301361628\n>\n>\n>\n> While it is possible to order scans of material using the attached order\n> form, we may be unable to provide research-quality scans or photographs of\n> some of the material, such as the material on microfiche, which would\n> require high-resolution scanning at a cost of £20 per scan. Moreover,  many\n> of these records remain under third-party copyright, and we would only be\n> able to provide scans or copies for personal, non-commercial research.\n> Other uses, such as use in a legal case or report would likely require the\n> permission of the copyright holder(s). I would therefore advise arranging\n> to view the material in person rather than ordering scans, if possible.\n>\n>\n>\n> Finally, I have confirmed with our Records Manager that we do not hold any\n> correspondence related to the site.\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you again for your enquiry.\n>\n>\n>\n> Sincerely,\n>\n>\n>\n> Adam Coward\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> *Dr Adam Coward*\n>\n> * Rheolwr Llyfrgell ac Ymholiadau | Library and Enquiries Manager*\n>\n> Ffordd Penglais | Penglais Road,\n>\n>  Aberystwyth,\n>\n>  [postcode redacted]\n>\n> [phone redacted] <+44%20(0)%201970%20621%20216>\n>\n> [email redacted]\n>\n>  |\n>\n> [email redacted]\n>\n> www.cbhc.gov.uk <https://cbhc.gov.uk/>\n>\n> |\n>\n>  www.rcahmw.gov.uk <https://rcahmw.gov.uk/>\n>\n> *Noddir gan Lywodraeth Cymru *\n>\n> |\n>\n> * Sponsored by Welsh Government*\n>\n> [image: Facebook]\n> <https://www.facebook.com/Royal-Commission-on-the-Ancient-and-Historical-Monuments-of-Wales-146120328739808>\n>\n> [image: Twitter] <https://www.twitter.com/rcahmwales>\n>\n>\n> Rydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg a Saesneg. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn peri oedi.\n>\n>\n> We welcome correspondence in Welsh and English. Corresponding in Welsh will not lead to any delay.\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> Yn unol â Rheoliadau Safonau'r Gymraeg (Rhif 2) 2016, mae gennych hawl i\n> gyfathrebu a gohebu â Chomisiwn Brenhinol Henebion Cymru yn eich dewis\n> iaith. Er mwyn sicrhau ein bod yn cyflawni’r hawl, rhowch wybod i ni a\n> ydych yn dymuno derbyn gohebiaeth a/neu alwadau ffôn oddi wrthym yn y\n> Gymraeg. Bydd yr wybodaeth hon yn cael ei chofnodi gennym ni, a byddwn yn\n> defnyddio'r iaith o’ch dewis ym mhob cyfathrebu yn y dyfodol. Diolch.\n>\n> ​​​​\n>\n> Under the Welsh Language Standards (No. 2) Regulations 2016, you have the\n> right to communicate and correspond with the Royal Commission on the\n> Ancient and Historical Monuments of Wales in your preferred language. To\n> ensure we uphold this right, please let us know whether you wish to receive\n> correspondence and/or telephone calls from us in Welsh. This information\n> will be recorded by us, and we will use your preferred language in all\n> future communication. Thank you.\n> ​\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> *From:* Requester <[email redacted]>\n> *Sent:* 20 May 2026 22:11\n> *To:* unclehowell <[email redacted]>\n> *Subject:* Great House Farm, Williams/ Buckler Family, Llandough, Vale of\n> Glamorgan\n>\n>\n>\n> Sir / Madam,\n>\n> To all those whom it concerns, BCC'd into this email.\n>\n> I write to request disclosure under the Freedom of Information Act 2000\n> and, where applicable, the Environmental Information Regulations 2004.\n>\n> This request concerns Great House Farm / Ty Mawr (Great House), Llandough,\n> associated estate lands, historic occupation and related heritage records.\n> My family is Buckler and before that Williams, and we occupied Great House\n> Farm from 1667 until our forceful dispossession of it in 1988.  Without our\n> ownership ever having been estiguished and without BP ever having a ruling\n> of ownership, only possession.\n>\n> It's alledged our fee simple absolute and manorial title was stolen. And\n> when BP registered our lands in their names in 1982, with our stolen title,\n> my nan who challenged this all her life, during the 2 week land registry\n> window to challenge BP's registration, which they achieved via purgergy and\n> document/ identity fraud, she conveniently died. As my father did after his\n> appeal of the 1987 mistake ruling was mistakely dismissed and his ECHR\n> request blocked and home abruptly demolished, which he was prevented from\n> stopping having been hospitalised and arrested without charges by armed\n> force officers and possible SAS.\n>\n> https://youtu.be/0eXOoj7eUCo?si=hcgSA2pFgEE943HG\n>\n> I understand records may exist within the National Monuments Record of\n> Wales, Historic Environment Record archives, archaeological consultations\n> and planning files, including references already identified as:\n>\n> • Great House Farm HER site entry – GGAT03586s\n> • Land at Great House Farm HER event entry – GGATE003872\n> • The 1990 archaeological assessment commissioned by BP Properties Ltd.\n>\n> Please provide copies of, or access to:\n>\n>    1. All records concerning Great House Farm / Ty Mawr / Great House\n>    Estate, Llandough including:\n>\n> • surveys\n> • reports\n> • site notes\n> • photographs\n> • maps\n> • aerial imagery\n> • GIS layers\n> • archive material\n> • annotations\n> • title references\n> • occupancy references\n> • landscape records\n> • archaeological observations.\n>\n>    2. All internal and external correspondence involving:\n>\n> • RCAHMW / NMRW\n> • Cadw\n> • GGAT / Heneb\n> • Vale of Glamorgan Council\n> • BP Properties Ltd\n> • developers\n> • planning authorities\n> • landowners, estate representatives or consultants.\n>\n>    3. Any documents discussing:\n>\n> • estate ownership\n> • occupation history\n> • family occupation or association\n> • agricultural use\n> • commemorative tree planting or memorial planting\n> • estate boundaries\n> • title history\n> • acquisition history\n> • disputes concerning ownership, heritage status or land use\n> • estate restructuring, extinguishment, compulsory acquisition or title\n> alteration.\n>\n>    4. Copies of all material linked to GGAT03586s and GGATE003872,\n>    including the archaeological assessment and associated correspondence.\n>    5. Any records identifying historic occupants, proprietors, tenants or\n>    family associations, including references to the Williams family where\n>    present within archives.\n>    6. Metadata identifying file creators, accession history, provenance,\n>    dates and retention schedules.\n>    7. File indexes, archive references or schedules identifying further\n>    related material not already listed.\n>\n> Please provide information electronically where possible.\n>\n> If any material is withheld, please identify the exemption relied upon and\n> provide the public interest assessment.\n>\n> Regards,\n>\n>\n>\n> Requester\n>\n> [address redacted]\n> [map link redacted]\n>\n>\n"},{"id":"wb-33ed904f","subject":"Re rc26 0205 great house farm williams buckler family llando","name":"0000-00-00_re-rc26-0205-great-house-farm-williams-buckler-family-llando_en_v0.0.0.00-4.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-rc26-0205-great-house-farm-williams-buckler-family-llando_en_v0.0.0.00-4.txt","threadSlug":"rc26-0205-great-house-farm-williams-buckler-family-llando","scopeTopics":["morgan","family"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["morgan","family"],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-rc26-0205-great-house-farm-williams-buckler-family-llando_en_v0.0.0.00-4.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"[Recovered on 19 Oct 2026 from the original email in the family's Gmail, because this archive file was missing. Matched to this entry by its subject line; where several emails share a subject, the order within the group is not certain. Email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses are redacted.]\n\nSubject: Re: RC26-0205 - Great House Farm, Williams/ Buckler Family, Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan - Further Details of Archives\nFrom: Requester\nDate: Wed, 10 Jun 2026 19:39 UTC\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nOn Wed, 10 Jun 2026, 10:06 Dr Adam Coward, <[email redacted]>\nwrote:\n\n> Good afternoon,\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you for your further enquiry. I have checked the accessions\n> information for the archive items which we hold related to the site.\n> Unfortunately, there is no accessions data for the items in the NMR Site\n> File collection. This is because most of the information was either created\n> (in the case of notes and drawing from site visits) or collected (in the\n> case of the newspaper cuttings) by RCAHMW staff in the course of their\n> duties. Having examined the records themselves, however, I do note that\n> Archive Number 6048942 *may* have come from a Mrs Buckler (‘Mrs Buckler?’\n> is handwritten under the account). There is also a note at the bottom of\n> Archive Number 6048945 that ‘Correspondence between C.N. Johns and Mrs\n> Buckler, Great House Farm, has been lost since 1986/87’, although I have\n> been unable to find any other record of this correspondence either in our\n> records or our archive catalogue, having carried out both digital and\n> physical searches, including searching through the RCAHMW administrative\n> files in the archive (some of which are not yet open to the public).\n>\n>\n>\n> With regard to the first three records in the list, I include what\n> accessions details are available below:\n>\n> Archive Number\n>\n> creator or originating body\n>\n> accession source\n>\n> accession date\n>\n> 6524751\n>\n> Glamorgan-Gwent Archaeological Trust\n>\n> Donation\n>\n> 20/03/2019\n>\n> 6416853\n>\n>  Cotswold Archaeology\n>\n> Donation\n>\n> 30/05/2014\n>\n> 6500905\n>\n> Dr Katherine Watson\n>\n> Transferred from Historic England as part of an exchange of material\n> between the two organisations\n>\n> 15/01/2018\n>\n>\n>\n> With regards to which records mention things like ownership and the\n> dispute, most of the newspaper article clippings naturally discuss it.\n> Archive Number 6048942 provides a short history of the site and notes the\n> family’s residence there and Archive Number 6048945 notes that ‘The house\n> remained partially recorded because of problems created by an ownership\n> dispute. The house and farm buildings were suddenly and completely\n> destroyed by B. P. Properties Ltd. on 6th December 1988 amid considerable\n> local controversy’. If you would like further details about the content of\n> these records, they can be viewed in our Search Room, located in the\n> National Library of Wales, which is open Tuesday through Thursday. Scans of\n> records can also be requested, using the attached order form. We also do\n> offer a limited document summary service. Please see our price list for\n> details.\n>\n>\n>\n> In addition to these 14 records, we may hold some historical aerial\n> photography which may include the site. This would likely include vertical\n> aerial photography taken from a high altitude by the Royal Air Force and/or\n> Ordnance survey from the 1940s to the turn of the 21st century. If you\n> would like to request a search of our historic aerial photography\n> collections, please provide details for your search, such as any location\n> or time-period parameters. Please note that we do charge for these\n> searches. Please see the attached price list for details.\n>\n>\n>\n> Sincerely,\n>\n>\n>\n> Adam Coward\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> Dr Adam Coward\n>  Rheolwr Llyfrgell ac Ymholiadau | Library and Enquiries Manager\n> Ffordd Penglais | Penglais Road,  Aberystwyth,  [postcode redacted]\n> [phone redacted] <+44%20(0)%201970%20621%20216>\n> [email redacted]  |  [email redacted]\n> www.cbhc.gov.uk <https://cbhc.gov.uk/>  |  www.rcahmw.gov.uk\n> <https://rcahmw.gov.uk/>\n> Noddir gan Lywodraeth Cymru  |  Sponsored by Welsh Government\n> [image: Facebook]\n> <https://www.facebook.com/Royal-Commission-on-the-Ancient-and-Historical-Monuments-of-Wales-146120328739808>\n> [image: Twitter] <https://www.twitter.com/rcahmwales>\n>\n> Rydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg a Saesneg. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn peri oedi.\n>\n> We welcome correspondence in Welsh and English. Corresponding in Welsh will not lead to any delay.\n>\n>\n>\n> Yn unol â Rheoliadau Safonau'r Gymraeg (Rhif 2) 2016, mae gennych hawl i\n> gyfathrebu a gohebu â Chomisiwn Brenhinol Henebion Cymru yn eich dewis\n> iaith. Er mwyn sicrhau ein bod yn cyflawni’r hawl, rhowch wybod i ni a\n> ydych yn dymuno derbyn gohebiaeth a/neu alwadau ffôn oddi wrthym yn y\n> Gymraeg. Bydd yr wybodaeth hon yn cael ei chofnodi gennym ni, a byddwn yn\n> defnyddio'r iaith o’ch dewis ym mhob cyfathrebu yn y dyfodol. Diolch.\n> ​​​​\n> Under the Welsh Language Standards (No. 2) Regulations 2016, you have the\n> right to communicate and correspond with the Royal Commission on the\n> Ancient and Historical Monuments of Wales in your preferred language. To\n> ensure we uphold this right, please let us know whether you wish to receive\n> correspondence and/or telephone calls from us in Welsh. This information\n> will be recorded by us, and we will use your preferred language in all\n> future communication. Thank you.\n> ​\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n"},{"id":"wb-ff843eb0","subject":"Re resolution of historical dispossession and fraud great ho","name":"0000-00-00_re-resolution-of-historical-dispossession-and-fraud-great-ho_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-resolution-of-historical-dispossession-and-fraud-great-ho_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"solution-of-historical-dispossession-and-fraud-great-ho","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-resolution-of-historical-dispossession-and-fraud-great-ho_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"[Recovered on 19 Oct 2026 from the original email in the family's Gmail, because this archive file was missing. Matched to this entry by its subject line; where several emails share a subject, the order within the group is not certain. Email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses are redacted.]\n\nSubject: Re: Resolution of Historical Dispossession and Fraud – Great House Farm, Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan\nFrom: Swyddfa Joe Martin AS - Office of Joe Martin MS\nDate: Wed, 13 May 2026 15:36 UTC\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nDear Requester,\n\nThank you for your email. I am currently in the process of setting up a constituency office, and will reply to your email in full as soon as possible.\n\nKind regards,\n\nJoe Martin MS\n\n\nSent from Outlook for iOS<https://aka.ms/o0ukef>\n________________________________\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]>\nSent: Tuesday, May 12, 2026 12:18:38 PM\nTo: Swyddfa Joe Martin AS - Office of Joe Martin MS <[email redacted]>; [email redacted] <[email redacted]>\nSubject: Resolution of Historical Dispossession and Fraud – Great House Farm, Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan\n\n[https://senedd.wales/media/obuo0sep/senedd_logo_xs.png]        E-mail protection couldn't recognize this email as this is the first time you received an email from this sender [email redacted]\n\nSirs/ Ma'ams,\n\nI write on behalf of myself and my siblings, who are the heirs and successors of the late Mary Williams of the Williams family, the rightful owners and occupiers of Great House Farm, Llandough, since 1667 until our unlawful, unconstitution, brutal and inhumane dispossession by armed force in 1988. With press and courts falsly labelling and treating us as squatters in favor of the false narrative of those who stole my disabled, widow nans fee simple absolute title. For which we remain dispossessed, defrauded and without justice or even narrative justice, unto this day. The public also lost a site of historic and archelogical significants and were lied to about what went on. The land looted of artefacts too, which is a loss to our nation and people.\n\nI apologise if any of this is incoherent. I am the only member of my family willing to speak out; the rest, who are nearly all women (sisters, mum, aunties etc), are petrified of the repercussions, given what happened to our family in the 1980s. Or dead from this. My father, nan, grandad and possibly great grandad and maybe more, all potentially murdered.\n\nAll this is also chilling in that the playbook used here, could be industrial scale and nationwide and have occured to other families too. Our case might be the foundations for a class action for the public.\n\nI write to summarise our position regarding the historical and ongoing dispossession of Great House Farm, Llandough, following the landmark legal case *BP v Buckler* [1987], which I allege involved a myriad of fraud, theft, crimes and proceedural unfairness, gaps in records, supressed and concealed records, human rights violations and literally almost every injustice imaginable; not withstanding it was over decades for our family;\n\n- **Denial of the right to be heard by the Land Registry.** My grandmother, Mary Williams (falsely named \"Mrs Buckler\" on the licence letter produced by BP in 1974, in order to diminish the Williams ancestral superior land claim), conveniently died while our family was in mourning during the limited window granted by the Land Registry to challenge BP's registration of our land and property in their name in 1982/83. Similarly, my grandfather died before he was able to clarify—even to my grandmother—how her fee simple absolute title deed ended up in BP's possession. Until her dying breath, she maintained her ownership claim, despite repeated unlawful attempts over decades to dislodge the firm position she held regarding her and our undisputed ancestral and superior land claim. The official records and narrative are wrong; there are court errors, suppressed evidence, procedural unfairness, fraud, and the list goes on.\n\nThe *BP v Buckler* [1987] ruling applied the licence my deceased grandmother held to my father and mother. They never had, nor entered into, any such agreement with BP. The ruling also alleged that my grandmother accepted BP's licence letters by non-response, which was untrue. It further stated that any objection should have been directed to the BP Pension Trust rather than the claimant, BP Properties, which were deemed separate companies. Yet in my father's subsequent appeal against the *BP v Buckler* ruling, his appeal was dismissed on the grounds that the BP companies were effectively the same—so \"heads BP wins, tails we lose.\" Our 800-year-old home was then demolished and all money and possessions removed, despite a court injunction ordering that nothing be removed. My father's ECHR application was blocked; he was arrested without charge, hospitalised, and then he conveniently died at the age of 41. Our remaining family, consisting mostly of women and children, have to this day been too terrified to speak about this case because of the decades of crimes committed against our household by the British State. It was synonymous with a battle or war. I say this as a military warfare professional and Iraq War veteran: a certified and experienced military commander and leader. I concluded my military career with honours as an Acting Sergeant. My profession is cyber and electronic warfare.\n\nAccording to recently declassified, previously suppressed ITV news coverage from 1988, bailiffs occupied our stolen land and property, and even dislocated my mother's shoulder for simply serving them a lawful court injunction. I also question how ITV came to declassify and publish this footage to YouTube a few months ago, after the months I have spent seeking information. That did not go unnoticed. There is liability there, and I do not accept ITV's belief that this quiet release of suppressed news coverage makes the matter right—though the action is appreciated. How did it come to be suppressed in the first place? And why is it cut abruptly, at what appears to be SAS performing a siege on my family home and dislocating my pregnant mother's shoulder?\n\nWithout my discovery of fraud this year—which has taken me two decades, a career in military cyber and electronic warfare, and recent innovations in AI to discover and unravel—we had no redress or recourse due to the statute of limitations and the passage of time, notwithstanding that we were all too traumatised, like hostages under terrorists, to speak out. We have received no support to this day for what we were subjected to.\n\nThis whole affair, even to this day, represents scandalous treatment of me and my family. We never surrendered our claim. The manorial titles of the Manor of Llandough are rightfully ours. The fee simple absolute title has been ours for over a century, a fact that is also suppressed and withheld from the defrauded—and implicated—Land Registry.\n\nI am also a war veteran who left my military career with honours and was employed until 2024 under the Vale of Glamorgan Council / Welsh Government. I completed my 22 years' service oath *bona fide*. I am an indigenous Welsh Briton, and this is my ancestral birthland (established 1667). The disrespect shown by the Vale MP and Llandough Councillor in simply ignoring me and this matter is abhorrent.\n\nITV coverage (suppressed until recent months) also reports that neighbours described the eviction as an \"SAS-style operation\" to remove us by force in 1988 (myself included). The men in the video do look like SAS. I have submitted an FOI request to the MOD. Shareholders of mine are SAS, so I shall find out one way or another, even if I receive an NCND reply. This matter was also raised in Parliament, who classified and disposed of the records.\n\nMargaret Thatcher's administration at the time (the British Government) was evidently assisting BP in clawing assets for the UK Government's sale and exit at the time, which might explain how 30 police—and possibly SAS—were used to rip us from our home and land. The human rights violations remain unanswered.\n\nThe Land Registry ultimately had checks and balances lifted and total free rein during Thatcher's reign, while BP's lawyers evidently committed perjury in their declarations to the Land Registry, withholding information from it and supplying it with false information.\n\nAll the while, Welsh television was simultaneously interviewing Gaddafi in Libya in 1988, where he accused Thatcher and the British State of being the real terrorists, not him. My oath ended in 2024; I am also a Welsh national. I have no allegiance to the British State. If it acts like my enemy, it is my enemy. I am absolutely a Welsh national and counter-officer now that it is so evident the British State waged civil war upon us indigenous Britons—me and my family included—on our ancestral birthland.\n\nOther media coverage alleged by UK courts to exist from 1974 seems non-existent or suppressed too. Much is behind paywalls. This whole case needs to be in the public eye. I have put everyone I can think of on notice.\n\nFraud and procedural unfairness are ultimately rampant in the case of my family's dispossession. Aside from all my family members dying at convenient junctures instrumental in the windows to change the outcome, I still have major concerns regarding how the MOJ took months to respond only to fob me off last week; how the MP fobbed me off and will not provide a proper response; how CADW and Llandough Parish allege this is nothing to do with them, despite CADW failing to list the farmhouse as Grade II so it could be demolished (as per a 1,700-signature petition, which was practically the whole town at the time); the current councillor for Llandough failing to reply properly; and the fact that the National Archives report gaps, missing records, and suppressed records. Notwithstanding that Marconi was staying with us while inventing radio, and the town will not even put a plaque up to commemorate this, which is further erasure of the history and known (but suppressed) archaeological and historic significance of the site. In BP's planning application records, they state that our 800-year-old former courthouse home was merely an old, dilapidated building.\n\nNotwithstanding the suppression of the 1870 Roman soldier remains we discovered when changing our floor from stone to timber, also suppressed to hide the site's archaeological significance so that housing development could be unlawfully approved. BP did not even seek a ruling on ownership; they only sought possession, most likely so that the discovery and significance of the site's archaeology could be pretended not to be known until the excavation of our land in 1994—which is further evidence of blatant conspiracy and fraud.\n\nThese are the actions of a crime or terrorist network, seeking to install a terrorist cell or camp. It is worse than simply the British colonising Wales.\n\nNotwithstanding the child graves secretly moved from the church next door during the excavation, which seemed to take ten years to be properly reported on, and even then their numbers in the report appear to be underrepresented.\n\nThe scandal continues endlessly. There are court errors too. The fancy Latin words diminish and falsely describe my grandmother's land claim and superior ownership (it was \"older than man can remember,\" not merely a \"claim to the land\"). The language used by the court clearly demonstrates bias prior to the ruling—language implying that my late grandmother, Mrs Williams (*not* Mrs Buckler, as they falsely named her in document fraud), was imagining her ancestral superior land claim, in order to discredit her through carefully selected judicial wording.\n\nMy siblings and I remain the heirs and successors and landowners. Nothing has lawfully broken or undone that claim. We demand that the fraud, corruption, gaps in records, false records, and myriad crimes committed against us be addressed and not ignored. There should be a Senedd public inquiry into this. My family should not be dispossessed a second longer. We are seriously looking at £100 million in reparations if the truth of this matter becomes known, given the scale of the scandal and injustice.\n\nIn 1667 we acquired the land and property in a 999-year lease (virtual freehold). In the early 1900s we obtained the superior paper title—free and clear, outright equitable ownership—via the deal with Bute Estate quarryman Daniel Thomas, which led to the tree-planting ceremony to affirm this in history. *(See attached.)*\n\nFrederick Buckler's transfer of Mrs Williams's equitable title to BP and their predecessors, without her knowledge or consent, is not legitimate and should never have stood, nor does it stand to reason. His inability to clarify the details of the alleged transaction due to his convenient death afterwards does not make it right, as Mary Williams maintained her position after his death. It was never his title to sell, even if he did. My grandmother was left unable to prove her standing, and the 1974 hearing was adjourned. The dismissal of the second hearings in 1974 due to the licence letter issued by BP (resorting to identity and document fraud) does not negate the fact that there was never a ruling stating BP owned our land, despite their purchasing my grandmother's stolen fee simple absolute title deed. BP only ever sought possession. They could not obtain a ruling because it would have meant discovery, unveiling the archaeological significance that would have prevented land and housing development.\n\nThis was a coordinated campaign of fraud, stealth, and administrative erasure spanning decades, employing force, secrecy, and fraudulent licence. By hook and by crook we were dispossessed of our land. Thatcher—and potentially the SAS—make it, at the least, an allodial/eminent domain seizure, in which case we should still be due compensation.\n\nI fail to see the proclaimed authority of the other men on my land, alleging themselves to be a government and citizens rather than terrorists, who both ostracised and held my family hostage, if legitimacy does not exist. You need legitimacy to claim authority.\n\n**Summary of the Fraudulent Dispossession:**\n\n**The Title Theft:** Evidence supports that a \"Title-for-Quarrying\" agreement existed with Daniel Thomas of the Bute Estate. We obtained this fee simple absolute title lawfully, but it was later secretly and fraudulently taken and sold back to Bute / Western Ground Rents / BP without the knowledge or consent of the rightful owner, Mary Williams. Parliament even stated on record how criminal Western Ground Rents were.\n\n**Procedural Fraud (The 1974 Licence):** The 1987 Court of Appeal ruling was predicated on a unilateral \"licence letter\" issued by the landlords' solicitors. We reject this ruling. It should be set aside on grounds of fraud.\n\n**Identity Erasure:** Throughout all legal proceedings, Mary Williams was intentionally misidentified as \"Mrs Buckler.\" This was a calculated tactic to strip her of her ancestral \"Williams\" lineage and the superior land rights and claim associated with it.\n\n**Exploitation of Mortality:**\n- **Frederick Buckler:** Died before the circumstances of how Mary was dispossessed of her title—and how the other parties regained possession after the lawful Daniel Thomas (and/or Anthony Williams) dealings with Bute Estate—could be clarified. This is inconsequential, much like Mary's own death; neither changed her position on the matter, which she did voice, despite what the 1987 ruling falsely alleges.\n- **Mary Williams:** Died during the precise two-week window required to rebut the Land Registry's registration of the land in BP's name—a window we believe was exploited to finalise this theft. At minimum, this is the case; it is more likely she was murdered. Her husband before her too. And my father, William Buckler, given the convenient timing of all their deaths—not to mention the unfair dismissal of my father's appeal, the blocking of his ECHR request, the theft and demolition of his property and our home prior to his death.\n\n**Our Position:**\nThe Williams family (now the Buckler family) has never ceased our claim. We maintain that the land was taken through criminal stealth and that the current registered titles are poisoned by this original fraud. We seek full reparations for the loss of ancestral wealth, the destruction of the Roman archaeological site on the property, and the systematic denial of our right to a fair trial.\n\nPlease provide a formal response and update regarding your position on this matter within 14 days. Please also hold to account those taking steps back into the shadows, as this only makes the matter more clandestine and therefore makes me very jumpy as a military warfare professional. I already fly in and out as if on another tour of the Middle East, and my family remain hostages under terrorists.\n\nI would also ask that Companies House, who have recently tried to criminalise me, give me some space. They gave me three options to submit ID: a questionnaire with passport and driving licence (which I provided); Post Office verification; or Digital ID. I chose the first option. They will not accept it. But I will not be lured to a location of their choosing, nor will I put any of their tracking ID applications on my devices. I do not trust the regime on that island. Until this and another unnamed matter of similar scale is resolved, I consider the British Government a terrorist organisation and do not recognise its authority. My family there are hostages under said terrorists. And Church View Close, Llandough—23 houses—is a terrorist cell/camp on my land.\n\nMarconi was staying with us in Llandough when he invented radio. This too was covered up. Cardiff City will not put a plaque naming Marconi as the inventor on the statue of a radio at Cardiff Bay—not even a plaque to commemorate it. Llandough should be a tourist attraction based on this fact alone, rather than the occupying terrorist network's cell of 23 houses, as I see it.\n\nRegards,\n\nSion Buckler Esq. (Ret'd Sgt)\nOn behalf of (and as one of) the Heirs and Successors of Mary Williams, Great House Farm, Manor of Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan\n\nCurrent Address: [address redacted]\n\n---\n"},{"id":"wb-1d3f93d7","subject":"Re response to national archives case number cas 313954 y6c0","name":"0000-00-00_re-response-to-national-archives-case-number-cas-313954-y6c0_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Heritage and archives correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-response-to-national-archives-case-number-cas-313954-y6c0_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"sponse-to-national-archives-case-number-cas-313954-y6c0","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-response-to-national-archives-case-number-cas-313954-y6c0_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"[Recovered on 19 Oct 2026 from the original email in the family's Gmail, because this archive file was missing. Matched to this entry by its subject line; where several emails share a subject, the order within the group is not certain. Email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses are redacted.]\n\nSubject: Re: Response to National Archives case number CAS-313954-Y6C0H3 CRM:022601115\nFrom: Requester\nDate: Fri, 06 Feb 2026 18:11 UTC\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nMs Smith,\n\nThanks for your reply and for explaining The National Archives’ position\nregarding annotation and review of records.\n\nI fully accept that archives do not resolve disputes and that records are\noften incomplete or patchwork in character. My purpose in writing is not to\nseek amendment, adjudication, or retrospective commentary, but to raise a\nspecific concern about how absence, silence, and fragmentation within the\narchival record should be understood where material public consequences\nclearly followed.\n\nBy way of clarification, my concern relates to the historical record\nsurrounding Great House Farm, Llandough, and the proceedings culminating in *BP\nProperties Ltd v Buckler* [1987] EWCA Civ 2. In particular:\n\n   - Ownership and occupation of the farmhouse and garden were actively\n   contested in 1974, with a defence asserting adverse possession filed on\n   behalf of Mary Williams, and the matter adjourned without a final\n   determination of title.\n   - Enforcement of earlier possession orders was deferred, a warrant was\n   withdrawn on 31 October 1974, and the occupier remained in possession until\n   her death in 1983.\n   - Despite this, land registration, demolition (1988), and archaeological\n   intervention proceeded without a High Court determination of ownership and\n   without disclosure of earlier, locally known archaeological discoveries.\n   - A Roman burial reportedly discovered beneath the farmhouse in 1888 by\n   the occupying family appears not to have been reflected in later “rescue”\n   excavations (1978–1980) or the major 1994 excavation, which treated Roman\n   and early medieval burials as newly revealed.\n\nTaken together, this produces an archival landscape in which:\n\n   - court files relating to decisive events appear absent or incomplete;\n   - later records imply finality where proceedings were in fact unresolved;\n   - and the destruction of a long-occupied medieval structure occurred\n   before the site’s known archaeological significance was formally\n   acknowledged.\n\nI note and appreciate your confirmation that the principal judicial series\n(J 157, J 83, J 84) contain no relevant material. Given the scale of the\nconsequences flowing from the dispute—forced removal, demolition of an\noccupied dwelling, and loss of heritage fabric—the absence of supporting\ncourt or administrative files is itself significant for researchers.\n\nAccordingly, I would be grateful for guidance on the following procedural\npoints:\n\n   - how researchers are expected to interpret gaps or silence in the\n   archival record where substantial actions plainly occurred;\n   - whether related non-judicial records (for example, departmental,\n   planning, regulatory, or corporate records connected to land registration,\n   planning consent, or archaeological works) are more likely to sit outside\n   the obvious court series; and\n   - whether there is an appropriate mechanism for flagging concerns about\n   record completeness or contextual risk, without seeking alteration of the\n   records themselves.\n\nFor the avoidance of doubt, I am not alleging impropriety on the part of\nThe National Archives or its staff. My concern is to ensure that the\nhistorical record is approached with appropriate caution where absence may\nunintentionally convey certainty or closure that did not exist at the time.\n\nThank you again for your time and professionalism.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nSion Buckler\n\nOn Fri, 6 Feb 2026, 10:07 RESO Records Enquiries, <\n[email redacted]> wrote:\n\n> Dear Requester,\n>\n> Thank you for clarifying the nature of your enquiry.\n>\n> In answer to your main enquiry, in general we would not add notes to, or\n> review, records in light of unresolved disputes or other matters. The\n> records are left to speak for themselves. In fact, those records are often\n> incomplete and patchwork in character, meaning that they may not enable the\n> enquirer to know what the final determination or outcome of any dispute was.\n>\n> Since you refer to a reported Court of Appeal (Civil Division) case, I\n> have conducted a quick search of our, admittedly limited holdings. The main\n> record series in which you might expect to find archival material here are J\n> 157 <https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/r/C9764>, J 83\n> <https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/r/C9691> and J 84\n> <https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/r/C9692>.\n> Unfortunately, none contain records relevant to the case.\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n> Charlotte Smith﻿\n>\n> Principal Records Specialist, Modern Legal Records\n> *www.nationalarchives.gov.uk* <http://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/>\n>\n> *Enquiry:*\n>\n>\n>\n> To clarify my original enquiry, I am specifically concerned with the\n> historical record surrounding the case of BP Properties Ltd v Buckler\n> [1987] EWCA Civ 2, which involved a disputed land dispossession at Great\n> House Farm in Llandough, South Glamorgan. This stemmed from earlier\n> proceedings in 1974, including an adjourned case in July and an appeal\n> attempt on October 31st that was impacted by correspondence from BP the\n> same day. Following the 1987 ruling, there was forced removal, demolition\n> of our family home, and archaeological activity on the site, despite\n> ongoing questions about the adjudication of ownership prior to land\n> registration.\n>\n> My question is not solely about locating ownership or court documents\n> (though any guidance on whether The National Archives holds physical files\n> from these proceedings would be welcome), but rather whether circumstances\n> like these—where ownership was disputed and not fully resolved before\n> registration or excavation—raise any considerations for the integrity or\n> completeness of the archival record. For instance, do such disputes prompt\n> reviews or annotations in historical land records, or are there protocols\n> for archiving related materials from corporate entities like BP or local\n> authorities involved?\n>\n> I understand your resources are focused on national-level holdings, but\n> any further direction would greatly assist my research.\n> Please don't print this e-mail unless you really need to.\n> -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n> National Archives Disclaimer This email and any files transmitted with it\n> are intended solely for the use of the individual(s) to whom they are\n> addressed. If you are not the intended recipient and have received this\n> email in error, please notify the sender and delete the email. Opinions,\n> conclusions and other information in this message and attachments that do\n> not relate to the official business of The National Archives are neither\n> given nor endorsed by it.\n> ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n>\n>\n"},{"id":"wb-12e92c39","subject":"Re response to national archives case number cas 313954 y6c0","name":"0000-00-00_re-response-to-national-archives-case-number-cas-313954-y6c0_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Heritage and archives correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-response-to-national-archives-case-number-cas-313954-y6c0_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","threadSlug":"sponse-to-national-archives-case-number-cas-313954-y6c0","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-response-to-national-archives-case-number-cas-313954-y6c0_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"[Recovered on 19 Oct 2026 from the original email in the family's Gmail, because this archive file was missing. Matched to this entry by its subject line; where several emails share a subject, the order within the group is not certain. Email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses are redacted.]\n\nSubject: Re: Response to National Archives case number CAS-313954-Y6C0H3 CRM:022601127\nFrom: Requester\nDate: Sun, 08 Feb 2026 09:57 UTC\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nMs Smith,\nThank you for your detailed response regarding selection policies and\nretention schedules. I appreciate the procedural clarity you have provided.\nHowever, I must respectfully indicate that your reply does not address the\nsubstantive concerns I have raised, and I am now compelled to escalate this\nmatter formally.\n1. From \"Archival Silence\" to Potential Evidence Tampering\nYou correctly note that archival silences are well-documented in academic\nliterature. However, the circumstances surrounding Great House Farm suggest\nsomething more concerning than routine selection decisions. I am now in\npossession of first-hand testimony (David Buckler, 2024) alleging that:\n\n   - Frederick Buckler (my grandfather) and his eldest son engaged a\n   solicitor privately in the 1960s\n   - Title documents were removed from the family home and allegedly sold\n   - The family was deliberately misled with the phrase \"it's all been\n   dealt with legally\"\n\nThis testimony, combined with the disappearance of the Bute Estate → Daniel\nThomas deed index card from Cardiff Library (reported missing in 1984),\nsuggests potential spoliation of evidence rather than routine archival\nselection. The question is no longer \"why weren't records preserved?\" but\n\"were records deliberately removed to prejudice a 320-year possessory\nclaim?\"\n2. The Computerised Records Question\nYou direct me to search your catalogue. I have done so. However, I now ask\nspecifically:\n\n   - Does The National Archives hold any digitised or microfilmed copies of\n   the Bute Estate records that might contain references to the Daniel\n   Thomas quarrying agreement (circa 1895-1905) or the 1938 auction at the\n   Bear Hotel?\n   - Are there any internal indices or finding aids (particularly from the\n   1970s-1980s) that might reference Great House Farm, Llandough, or the\n   BP/Western Ground Rents acquisition records that are not reflected in the\n   current online catalogue?\n\nThe 1994 archaeological excavation report (Cotswold Archaeological Trust)\nconfirms the site's national significance. The absence of corresponding\nland transaction records from the same period is itself an anomaly\nrequiring explanation.\n3. The BP Corporate Records Question\nYou note that parties are generally responsible for maintaining their own\nrecords. However, BP Properties Ltd and BP Pension Trust Ltd were corporate\nentities with statutory filing obligations. I ask:\n\n   - Were any BP corporate records relating to land acquisition in South\n   Glamorgan (1969-1988) selected for preservation under the Companies Act\n   or as part of the public record?\n   - Has The National Archives ever received or sought records from BP\n   regarding the Great House Farm acquisition, demolition, or the 1987\n   litigation?\n\nThe Court of Appeal judgment [1987] EWCA Civ 2 notes that BP issued letters\non 31 October 1974, not received until November 4th 1974 —letters that were\nallegedly \"accepted by silence.\" The original correspondence would\nconstitute primary evidence of potentially fraudulent misrepresentation. If\nthese were not preserved, I ask under what authority they were destroyed\nand whether this occurred before or after the litigation concluded.\n4. Request for Formal Investigation\nI am not seeking annotation of records. I am seeking:\n\n   1. A formal review of whether records relating to Great House Farm,\n   Llandough (ST 168 733) were selected for preservation, destroyed, or\n   remain non accessioned;\n   2. A search of microfilm, digitised, or legacy finding aid holdings for\n   any reference to the 1938 auction, Western Ground Rents acquisition, or the\n   Daniel Thomas quarrying agreement;\n   3. Confirmation of whether The National Archives has ever received\n   complaints regarding missing Bute Estate records or BP land acquisition\n   documents from other researchers;\n   4. Guidance on the appropriate authority to investigate allegations of\n   document removal from Cardiff Library (1984) and potential fraud in the\n   1982 land registration.\n\n5. Public Interest and Parliamentary Scrutiny\nThis matter involves:\n\n   - The forced removal of a family from land occupied since at least 1667\n   - Demolition of a medieval structure described as a \"dilapidated utility\n   building\" to avoid heritage protection\n   - Archaeological discoveries (1888, 1994) that were known but officially\n   \"forgotten\"\n   - A Court of Appeal decision ([1987] EWCA Civ 2) that may have relied on\n   incomplete or fraudulent evidence\n\nI am copying my MP and the Parliamentary Ombudsman in this correspondence.\nI will also be submitting a Subject Access Request to HM Land Registry\nregarding the 1982 registration and a complaint to the Information\nCommissioner's Office regarding potential destruction of records relevant\nto ongoing property disputes.\nI request a substantive response addressing the specific questions above\nwithin 20 working days. Should The National Archives be unable to assist\nfurther, I request a formal referral to the appropriate body with\njurisdiction over historical record fraud and spoliation.\nYours sincerely,\nSion Buckler\n\n\nOn Sat, 7 Feb 2026 at 07:26, RESO Records Enquiries <\n[email redacted]> wrote:\n\n> Dear Requester,\n>\n> The question about archival silences is one upon which there is a\n> voluminous literature. Practically, however, the best way of making sense\n> of what is and is not in the archives is to refer to the reports which\n> shaped selection policies in the modern era - see especially the Report on\n> Departmental Records (Cmnd 9136) and the Report on Legal Records (Cmnd\n> 3084) - and the resulting retention and disposition schedules - see for\n> example the one for the Court of Appeal (Civil Division) published here\n> <https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/62b47cc9d3bf7f0affd65610/court-appeal-civil-division-rrds.pdf>\n> .\n>\n> What you will see from these is that the presumption for modern records is\n> against permanent preservation. In general, apart from small samples to\n> demonstrate the ordinary functioning of the courts/government departments,\n> records are only preserved if they bear witness to moments of particular\n> legal or policy significance or change. In the case of legal records, the\n> presumption has been that parties would be responsible for maintaining\n> their own records.\n>\n> In terms of how to continue your search, my instinct is that you should\n> consider exploring the holdings of the relevant local archive. You can also\n> try some keyword searches in our catalogue - see, for example, these\n> <https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/results/r?_d=PF%7CWORK%7CBD%7CHLG&_dss=range&_sd=1978&_ed=1988&_ps=60&_hb=tna&_srt=1&_q=archaeological+NOT+bill+NOT+%22PF+309%22>\n> search results which highlight these\n> <https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/results/r?_q=archaeological+NOT+bill+NOT+%22PF+309%22&_d=PF%7CWORK%7CBD%7CHLG&_dss=range&_sd=1978&_ed=1988&_ps=60&_hb=tna&_srt=1&_l=3>\n> series as potentially a relevant source of desired information. You might\n> also wish to search the registered files of the land registry in records\n> series LAR 1 <https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/r/C10222>.\n> You should be advised, however, that departmental records tend to focus\n> more on policy than upon the treatment of individual cases.\n>\n> I hope that this is helpful.\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n> Charlotte Smith﻿\n>\n> Principal Records Specialist, Modern Legal Records\n> *www.nationalarchives.gov.uk* <http://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/>\n>\n> *Enquiry:*\n>\n>\n>\n> I note and appreciate your confirmation that the principal judicial series\n> (J 157, J 83, J 84) contain no relevant material. Given the scale of the\n> consequences flowing from the dispute—forced removal, demolition of an\n> occupied dwelling, and loss of heritage fabric—the absence of supporting\n> court or administrative files is itself significant for researchers.\n>\n> Accordingly, I would be grateful for guidance on the following procedural\n> points:\n>\n>    - how researchers are expected to interpret gaps or silence in the\n>    archival record where substantial actions plainly occurred;\n>    - whether related non-judicial records (for example, departmental,\n>    planning, regulatory, or corporate records connected to land registration,\n>    planning consent, or archaeological works) are more likely to sit outside\n>    the obvious court series; and\n>    - whether there is an appropriate mechanism for flagging concerns\n>    about record completeness or contextual risk, without seeking alteration of\n>    the records themselves.\n>\n> For the avoidance of doubt, I am not alleging impropriety on the part of\n> The National Archives or its staff. My concern is to ensure that the\n> historical record is approached with appropriate caution where absence may\n> unintentionally convey certainty or closure that did not exist at the time.\n> Please don't print this e-mail unless you really need to.\n> -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n> National Archives Disclaimer This email and any files transmitted with it\n> are intended solely for the use of the individual(s) to whom they are\n> addressed. If you are not the intended recipient and have received this\n> email in error, please notify the sender and delete the email. Opinions,\n> conclusions and other information in this message and attachments that do\n> not relate to the official business of The National Archives are neither\n> given nor endorsed by it.\n> ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n>\n>\n"},{"id":"wb-61d55734","subject":"Re response to national archives case number cas 313954 y6c0","name":"0000-00-00_re-response-to-national-archives-case-number-cas-313954-y6c0_en_v0.0.0.00-2.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Heritage and archives correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-response-to-national-archives-case-number-cas-313954-y6c0_en_v0.0.0.00-2.txt","threadSlug":"sponse-to-national-archives-case-number-cas-313954-y6c0","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-response-to-national-archives-case-number-cas-313954-y6c0_en_v0.0.0.00-2.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"[Recovered on 19 Oct 2026 from the original email in the family's Gmail, because this archive file was missing. Matched to this entry by its subject line; where several emails share a subject, the order within the group is not certain. Email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses are redacted.]\n\nSubject: Re: Response to National Archives case number CAS-313954-Y6C0H3 CRM:022601127 | Correction |  Forensic Evaluation and Assessment of Restitutionary Claims\nFrom: Requester\nDate: Sun, 08 Feb 2026 14:22 UTC\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nCorrection\nIn my previous correspondence, I referenced documents missing from Cardiff\nLibrary as \"reported missing in 1984.\" I wish to clarify that this date\nderives from my family's own chronology and recollection, not from an\nofficial report or confirmed archival record. I should not have stated this\nas an established fact, and I apologise for any confusion caused.\nNew Evidence\nSince my last email, I have obtained a Forensic Evaluation and Assessment\nof Restitutionary Claims (dated February 8, 2026) regarding the\ndispossession of the Williams family from Great House Farm, Llandough. This\nprofessional assessment provides additional context that may assist in\nunderstanding the archival gaps previously identified.\nThe forensic report specifically examines:\n\n   1. The \"Mrs. Buckler\" nomenclature: The analysis characterises the\n   consistent use of \"Mrs. Buckler\" in court records and correspondence as\n   \"strategic misnaming\" rather than clerical error, given that Mary Williams\n   never adopted her husband's surname. This raises questions about whether\n   The National Archives holds any records indicating her formal objection to\n   this identification or her consistent use of \"Mary Williams\" in legal\n   proceedings.\n   2. The 1974 license letters: The report notes that letters dated\n   October/November 1974 were addressed to a \"non-existent legal persona,\"\n   facilitating a judicial finding of \"silence as consent.\" I ask again\n   whether The National Archives holds any corporate records from BP\n   Properties Ltd or BP Pension Trust Ltd regarding this correspondence, or\n   any indication that such records were considered for preservation.\n   3. The 1974 adjournment: The forensic assessment questions why\n   proceedings were adjourned part-heard on July 3, 1974, given your\n   confirmation that principal judicial series contain no relevant material. I\n   request clarification on whether any Cardiff County Court records from\n   1974-1975 were transferred to The National Archives under subsequent\n   protocols, or whether they remain with the court.\n\nRevised Request\nGiven this additional professional analysis, I renew my request for\nguidance on:\n\n   - Whether The National Archives has received any complaints or inquiries\n   regarding missing Bute Estate records or Great House Farm documents from\n   other researchers;\n   - Whether corporate records from BP entities relating to this\n   acquisition were ever selected for preservation; and\n   - Whether any mechanism exists for flagging concerns about record\n   completeness where forensic analysis suggests potential irregularities in\n   the historical record.\n\nI remain grateful for your time and professionalism, and I hope this\nclarification assists in addressing my inquiry.\nYours sincerely,\nSion Buckler\n\n\nOn Sun, 8 Feb 2026 at 06:57, Requester <[email redacted]> wrote:\n\n> Ms Smith,\n> Thank you for your detailed response regarding selection policies and\n> retention schedules. I appreciate the procedural clarity you have provided.\n> However, I must respectfully indicate that your reply does not address the\n> substantive concerns I have raised, and I am now compelled to escalate this\n> matter formally.\n> 1. From \"Archival Silence\" to Potential Evidence Tampering\n> You correctly note that archival silences are well-documented in academic\n> literature. However, the circumstances surrounding Great House Farm suggest\n> something more concerning than routine selection decisions. I am now in\n> possession of first-hand testimony (David Buckler, 2024) alleging that:\n>\n>    - Frederick Buckler (my grandfather) and his eldest son engaged a\n>    solicitor privately in the 1960s\n>    - Title documents were removed from the family home and allegedly sold\n>    - The family was deliberately misled with the phrase \"it's all been\n>    dealt with legally\"\n>\n> This testimony, combined with the disappearance of the Bute Estate →\n> Daniel Thomas deed index card from Cardiff Library (reported missing in\n> 1984), suggests potential spoliation of evidence rather than routine\n> archival selection. The question is no longer \"why weren't records\n> preserved?\" but \"were records deliberately removed to prejudice a 320-year\n> possessory claim?\"\n> 2. The Computerised Records Question\n> You direct me to search your catalogue. I have done so. However, I now ask\n> specifically:\n>\n>    - Does The National Archives hold any digitised or microfilmed copies\n>    of the Bute Estate records that might contain references to the Daniel\n>    Thomas quarrying agreement (circa 1895-1905) or the 1938 auction at the\n>    Bear Hotel?\n>    - Are there any internal indices or finding aids (particularly from\n>    the 1970s-1980s) that might reference Great House Farm, Llandough, or the\n>    BP/Western Ground Rents acquisition records that are not reflected in the\n>    current online catalogue?\n>\n> The 1994 archaeological excavation report (Cotswold Archaeological Trust)\n> confirms the site's national significance. The absence of corresponding\n> land transaction records from the same period is itself an anomaly\n> requiring explanation.\n> 3. The BP Corporate Records Question\n> You note that parties are generally responsible for maintaining their own\n> records. However, BP Properties Ltd and BP Pension Trust Ltd were corporate\n> entities with statutory filing obligations. I ask:\n>\n>    - Were any BP corporate records relating to land acquisition in South\n>    Glamorgan (1969-1988) selected for preservation under the Companies\n>    Act or as part of the public record?\n>    - Has The National Archives ever received or sought records from BP\n>    regarding the Great House Farm acquisition, demolition, or the 1987\n>    litigation?\n>\n> The Court of Appeal judgment [1987] EWCA Civ 2 notes that BP issued\n> letters on 31 October 1974, not received until November 4th 1974 —letters\n> that were allegedly \"accepted by silence.\" The original correspondence\n> would constitute primary evidence of potentially fraudulent\n> misrepresentation. If these were not preserved, I ask under what authority\n> they were destroyed and whether this occurred before or after the\n> litigation concluded.\n> 4. Request for Formal Investigation\n> I am not seeking annotation of records. I am seeking:\n>\n>    1. A formal review of whether records relating to Great House Farm,\n>    Llandough (ST 168 733) were selected for preservation, destroyed, or\n>    remain non accessioned;\n>    2. A search of microfilm, digitised, or legacy finding aid holdings\n>    for any reference to the 1938 auction, Western Ground Rents acquisition, or\n>    the Daniel Thomas quarrying agreement;\n>    3. Confirmation of whether The National Archives has ever received\n>    complaints regarding missing Bute Estate records or BP land\n>    acquisition documents from other researchers;\n>    4. Guidance on the appropriate authority to investigate allegations of\n>    document removal from Cardiff Library (1984) and potential fraud in the\n>    1982 land registration.\n>\n> 5. Public Interest and Parliamentary Scrutiny\n> This matter involves:\n>\n>    - The forced removal of a family from land occupied since at least 1667\n>    - Demolition of a medieval structure described as a \"dilapidated\n>    utility building\" to avoid heritage protection\n>    - Archaeological discoveries (1888, 1994) that were known but\n>    officially \"forgotten\"\n>    - A Court of Appeal decision ([1987] EWCA Civ 2) that may have relied\n>    on incomplete or fraudulent evidence\n>\n> I am copying my MP and the Parliamentary Ombudsman in this correspondence.\n> I will also be submitting a Subject Access Request to HM Land Registry\n> regarding the 1982 registration and a complaint to the Information\n> Commissioner's Office regarding potential destruction of records relevant\n> to ongoing property disputes.\n> I request a substantive response addressing the specific questions above\n> within 20 working days. Should The National Archives be unable to assist\n> further, I request a formal referral to the appropriate body with\n> jurisdiction over historical record fraud and spoliation.\n> Yours sincerely,\n> Requester\n>\n>\n> On Sat, 7 Feb 2026 at 07:26, RESO Records Enquiries <\n> [email redacted]> wrote:\n>\n>> Dear Requester,\n>>\n>> The question about archival silences is one upon which there is a\n>> voluminous literature. Practically, however, the best way of making sense\n>> of what is and is not in the archives is to refer to the reports which\n>> shaped selection policies in the modern era - see especially the Report on\n>> Departmental Records (Cmnd 9136) and the Report on Legal Records (Cmnd\n>> 3084) - and the resulting retention and disposition schedules - see for\n>> example the one for the Court of Appeal (Civil Division) published here\n>> <https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/62b47cc9d3bf7f0affd65610/court-appeal-civil-division-rrds.pdf>\n>> .\n>>\n>> What you will see from these is that the presumption for modern records\n>> is against permanent preservation. In general, apart from small samples to\n>> demonstrate the ordinary functioning of the courts/government departments,\n>> records are only preserved if they bear witness to moments of particular\n>> legal or policy significance or change. In the case of legal records, the\n>> presumption has been that parties would be responsible for maintaining\n>> their own records.\n>>\n>> In terms of how to continue your search, my instinct is that you should\n>> consider exploring the holdings of the relevant local archive. You can also\n>> try some keyword searches in our catalogue - see, for example, these\n>> <https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/results/r?_d=PF%7CWORK%7CBD%7CHLG&_dss=range&_sd=1978&_ed=1988&_ps=60&_hb=tna&_srt=1&_q=archaeological+NOT+bill+NOT+%22PF+309%22>\n>> search results which highlight these\n>> <https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/results/r?_q=archaeological+NOT+bill+NOT+%22PF+309%22&_d=PF%7CWORK%7CBD%7CHLG&_dss=range&_sd=1978&_ed=1988&_ps=60&_hb=tna&_srt=1&_l=3>\n>> series as potentially a relevant source of desired information. You might\n>> also wish to search the registered files of the land registry in records\n>> series LAR 1 <https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/r/C10222>.\n>> You should be advised, however, that departmental records tend to focus\n>> more on policy than upon the treatment of individual cases.\n>>\n>> I hope that this is helpful.\n>>\n>> Yours sincerely,\n>>\n>> Charlotte Smith﻿\n>>\n>> Principal Records Specialist, Modern Legal Records\n>> *www.nationalarchives.gov.uk* <http://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/>\n>>\n>> *Enquiry:*\n>>\n>>\n>>\n>> I note and appreciate your confirmation that the principal judicial\n>> series (J 157, J 83, J 84) contain no relevant material. Given the scale of\n>> the consequences flowing from the dispute—forced removal, demolition of an\n>> occupied dwelling, and loss of heritage fabric—the absence of supporting\n>> court or administrative files is itself significant for researchers.\n>>\n>> Accordingly, I would be grateful for guidance on the following procedural\n>> points:\n>>\n>>    - how researchers are expected to interpret gaps or silence in the\n>>    archival record where substantial actions plainly occurred;\n>>    - whether related non-judicial records (for example, departmental,\n>>    planning, regulatory, or corporate records connected to land registration,\n>>    planning consent, or archaeological works) are more likely to sit outside\n>>    the obvious court series; and\n>>    - whether there is an appropriate mechanism for flagging concerns\n>>    about record completeness or contextual risk, without seeking alteration of\n>>    the records themselves.\n>>\n>> For the avoidance of doubt, I am not alleging impropriety on the part of\n>> The National Archives or its staff. My concern is to ensure that the\n>> historical record is approached with appropriate caution where absence may\n>> unintentionally convey certainty or closure that did not exist at the time.\n>> Please don't print this e-mail unless you really need to.\n>> -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n>> National Archives Disclaimer This email and any files transmitted with it\n>> are intended solely for the use of the individual(s) to whom they are\n>> addressed. If you are not the intended recipient and have received this\n>> email in error, please notify the sender and delete the email. Opinions,\n>> conclusions and other information in this message and attachments that do\n>> not relate to the official business of The National Archives are neither\n>> given nor endorsed by it.\n>> ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n>>\n>>\n>\n"},{"id":"wb-ca5a550e","subject":"Re response to enquiry at the national archives case number","name":"0000-00-00_re-response-to-enquiry-at-the-national-archives-case-number_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Heritage and archives correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-response-to-enquiry-at-the-national-archives-case-number_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"sponse-to-enquiry-at-the-national-archives-case-number","scopeTopics":["email"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["email"],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-response-to-enquiry-at-the-national-archives-case-number_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"[Recovered on 19 Oct 2026 from the original email in the family's Gmail, because this archive file was missing. Matched to this entry by its subject line; where several emails share a subject, the order within the group is not certain. Email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses are redacted.]\n\nSubject: Re: Response to enquiry at The National Archives. Case number: CAS-313954-Y6C0H3 CRM:052900508\nFrom: Requester\nDate: Fri, 06 Feb 2026 10:49 UTC\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nRobert Fleming,\n\nThank you for your prompt response and for providing the helpful research\nguides on land ownership records,\n\nTo clarify my original enquiry, I am specifically concerned with the\nhistorical record surrounding the case of BP Properties Ltd v Buckler\n[1987] EWCA Civ 2, which involved a disputed land dispossession at Great\nHouse Farm in Llandough, South Glamorgan. This stemmed from earlier\nproceedings in 1974, including an adjourned case in July and an appeal\nattempt on October 31st that was impacted by correspondence from BP the\nsame day. Following the 1987 ruling, there was forced removal, demolition\nof our family home, and archaeological activity on the site, despite\nongoing questions about the adjudication of ownership prior to land\nregistration.\n\nMy question is not solely about locating ownership or court documents\n(though any guidance on whether The National Archives holds physical files\nfrom these proceedings would be welcome), but rather whether circumstances\nlike these—where ownership was disputed and not fully resolved before\nregistration or excavation—raise any considerations for the integrity or\ncompleteness of the archival record. For instance, do such disputes prompt\nreviews or annotations in historical land records, or are there protocols\nfor archiving related materials from corporate entities like BP or local\nauthorities involved?\n\nI understand your resources are focused on national-level holdings, but any\nfurther direction would greatly assist my research.\n\nSincerely,\n\nSion Buckler\n\nOn Fri, 6 Feb 2026 at 07:28, RESO Records Enquiries <\n[email redacted]> wrote:\n\n> Dear Requester,\n>\n> Thank you for contacting The National Archives of the United Kingdom.\n>\n> It is unlikely The National Archives holds the records of this type of\n> case, and the Central Land Registry\n> <https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/land-registry>, who hold\n> records of land registration, is a separate body. Some records of land\n> ownership can be found among records at local or county archives.\n>\n> I recommend you consult our guide to searching for records of Land\n> ownership records and deeds: an overview - The National Archives\n> <https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/help-with-your-research/research-guides/land-ownership-records-and-deeds-an-overview/>.\n> Regarding the court cases you mention, I also recommend you consult: Civil\n> court cases: Chancery Division since 1875 - The National Archives\n> <https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/help-with-your-research/research-guides/civil-court-cases-chancery-division-since-1875/>,\n> and you may also find Civil courts: appeal cases after 1875 - The\n> National Archives\n> <https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/help-with-your-research/research-guides/civil-courts-appeal-cases-after-1875/>\n> of use to your research. These guides will help you to use *our catalogue*\n> <https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/>, or other online sources,\n> effectively and focus your search.\n>\n> Records are represented in our online catalogue by a title, a reference\n> and a brief description. When you search our catalogue using keywords you\n> are searching across these titles and descriptions, rather than the\n> contents of the records themselves. By clicking on any search result, you\n> can see whether the record is available online or if you need to visit us\n> to view the original. You can also request copies of records from their\n> catalogue description page.\n>\n> If the record is held by another archive, you will need to contact them\n> directly to find out how you can access it.\n>\n> You can also *watch this video*\n> <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Tz4f--o3zUU> for more advice on using\n> our online catalogue.\n>\n> You are welcome to *visit us*\n> <https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/about/visit-us/> to continue your\n> research here in person, where access to our records is free of charge.\n> Before you visit, please check our website for information regarding our *opening\n> hours* <https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/about/visit-us/opening-times/>\n> and *ID requirements*\n> <https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/about/visit-us/researching-here/do-i-need-a-readers-ticket/>. If\n> you are unable to visit, or would like someone to pursue this research on\n> your behalf, please see the available *paid search options*\n> <https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/help-with-your-research/paying-for-research/>\n> on our website.\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n> Robert Fleming﻿\n>\n> Remote Enquiries Duty Officer\n> *www.nationalarchives.gov.uk* <http://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/>\n>\n> *Enquiry:*\n>\n>\n> -----Original Message-----\n> From: Requester <[email redacted]>\n> Sent: 31 January 2026 05:59\n> To: Contact Centre <[email redacted]>\n> Subject: Concern regarding historical record following disputed land\n> dispossession\n>\n>         You don't often get email from [email redacted]. Learn\n> why this is important <https://aka.ms/LearnAboutSenderIdentification>\n>\n>\n> EXTERNAL: This email originates from outside of The National Archives.\n>\n> Sirs/Ma'am,\n>\n> I'm writing to raise a concern regarding the integrity of the historical\n> record following disputed dispossession of land i.e. BP vs Buckler 1987\n>\n> After forced removal and demolition of our home, archaeological excavation\n> and removal of items took place on land whose ownership had never been\n> conclusively adjudicated at the time of registration. BP asserted it, but\n> it was never determined in a ruling beforehand as the July 1974 case\n> between BP and my grandmother was adjourned and the October 31st 1974\n> appeal and audience with a judge she made just 24 hours before, was\n> squashed by letters sent by BP that same morning.\n>\n> I am not making allegations here, simply stating facts about this landmark\n> legal case. And asking whether this type of circumstance is relevant to\n> archival or historical record considerations.\n>\n> Yours faithfully,\n>\n>\n> Requester\n>\n>\n> Please don't print this e-mail unless you really need to.\n> -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n> National Archives Disclaimer This email and any files transmitted with it\n> are intended solely for the use of the individual(s) to whom they are\n> addressed. If you are not the intended recipient and have received this\n> email in error, please notify the sender and delete the email. Opinions,\n> conclusions and other information in this message and attachments that do\n> not relate to the official business of The National Archives are neither\n> given nor endorsed by it.\n> ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n>\n>\n"},{"id":"wb-d4662e98","subject":"Re setting aside BP v Buckler 1987 on grounds of bp with ou","name":"0000-00-00_re-setting-aside-bp-vs-buckler-1987-on-grounds-of-bp-with-ou_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-setting-aside-bp-vs-buckler-1987-on-grounds-of-bp-with-ou_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"setting-aside-bp-v-buckler-1987-on-grounds-of-bp-with-ou","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-setting-aside-bp-vs-buckler-1987-on-grounds-of-bp-with-ou_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"Councillor of Llandough,\r\n\r\nHave you seen this footage?  https://youtu.be/4GXmR7mAas0?feature=shared\r\n\r\nI'm a military cyber warfare professional.\r\nNot much gets past me.\r\nI was the best hacker in our Government and military in 2005.\r\nIn fact I already note this footage was cut and censored to hide key facts.\r\nNot to mention this and other press coverage and footage supressed. Even\r\nunto this day.\r\n\r\nMore interestingly than my mum's appearance at the end of the clip, is the\r\nnews anchor who was my old best friends mum. That same best friend is now\r\nan executive of press monitoring company Meltwater Media in Cardiff City.\r\n\r\nThere's plenty more press coverage of this whole war waged on my family,\r\nland & home. And it's all going into a Netflix documentry along with the\r\nhandling of this matter which you still all try to bury and cover up. Like\r\nthe hundreds of child graves surrounding this case. You are all committing\r\ncareer and political suicide.\r\n\r\nI'm still waiting for you and the MP to respond to this matter.  For months\r\nnow.\r\nMoJ won't comment as they say its ongoing. See attached. Which doesn't make\r\nsense as I've not begun legal proceedings and this hasn't been touched in\r\ndecades.\r\nThey also took half a year just to reply.\r\n\r\nLand Registry made my query into a complaint and shut it down instantly.\r\nCadw falsely allege they have nothing to do with it when they were pivotal\r\nin it. They should have listen it as a grade II.\r\n\r\nI'm not rolling up with tanks, but I'm not going away. We need to\r\nstraighten this all out.\r\n\r\nYou know the clause with harassment? I.e. If I have reason to pursue I may\r\npersist. And so I will persist. But I have no desire to make you feel\r\nharassed. So please just all do your jobs. And diligently.\r\n\r\nMy family are still all terrorised into silence about this, even decades\r\nlater. It's a hostage/ terrorist situation in my estimation (As a military\r\nwarfare professional).\r\n\r\nI find dealing with my former community and Govenrment 50/50 at the moment.\r\nWhich is good enough odds for me to refrain from any direct action on this\r\nmatter. For now.\r\n\r\nI'm actually still giving you and the MP the benefit of the doubt you're\r\nbusy and/or mulling this over still, before taking a position on the\r\nmatter. But justice delayed is justice denied. Like the MoJ taking half a\r\nyear to reply only to fob me off. Tick tick.\r\n\r\nIt's not just my family being held hostage there, but my people seem\r\ndemoralised and its no way for them to all live. I already fly in and out\r\nlike its another military tour of Iraq. I was shot at in Barry and an\r\nAfghan tried stealing my dog last time I was back.\r\n\r\nI will feel my villages warmth one way or another. When it's our ancestral\r\nhome for centuries. How dare we be ignored, disregarded and exiled. Or shot\r\nat and shunned. Next time I fly home I want it to feel like home. Not\r\nanother tour of the middle east where I had to get behind a GPMG in SF role\r\nfrom a helicopter.\r\n\r\nThe situation is untenable. We can't go on like this another decade, with\r\nyou Brit'ish all on our stolen land without a care in the world, and us all\r\ndispossessed, butchered and forced out by force, secrecy and fraudulent\r\nlicense.\r\n\r\nYou don't seem to fathom the ongoing harm, injury and loss here. This\r\ndispossession and disposition, tantamount to a terrorist/ hostage situation\r\nhas been ongoing almost 4 decades. My families silence from being\r\nterorrised into silence and the years its taken for me to discover the\r\nfraud, due to the stealth, secretly and complexity of it, does not put this\r\nin the past but very much in our faces today.\r\n\r\nOur family demand of you and anyone other installed persons, proclaiming\r\nlegitimate authority of our lands, immediately address this matter. I'm not\r\nthe kind to hold a gun to your head and demand attention. I'm being polite,\r\ncourteous and professional here. And patient and understanding. But it\r\nwould not be sane or reasonable for me to allow you or the MP to cause me\r\nlet or hiderence.\r\n\r\nIn my experience if you won't tackle this in the open I have to presume you\r\nare going  clandestine and into the shadows and thus pose risk of further\r\nthreat to me and my family. As the British State.\r\n\r\nThere's gaps in records. Court mistakes. There's supressed, redacted,\r\ncensored media coverage. Procedural unfairness. Fraud. Theft. And the\r\nsilence and lack of co operation rectifying records and narratives only\r\ndiminishes your legitimacy and so your authority.\r\n\r\nYou wouldn't need to be clandestine and abuse force to try to handle men\r\nlike me and mine, if you'd just all straighten out this matter. Then you'd\r\nhave my trust and fieldty.\r\n\r\nYou know at the WCIT in Dubai with the help of David Cameron in 2012, it\r\ntook a few weeks to exit UK from the treaty. Which is something I had to\r\nbring up with my bank the other day who took twice as long to process a\r\nrefund after a dispute because of all the beurocracy. Similarly with this\r\nwe have no barriers to communicate, we have A.I, we have digital systems\r\nand digital records yet this is being made into a slow, tedious, confusing,\r\ncumbersom, challenging, obstructive process.\r\n\r\nDon't weaponise time. Justice delayed is justice denied. I am accustom to\r\ntime being abused so that other hybrid warfare war crime avenues can be\r\nexplored like trying to kidnap and silence me. I know the British State\r\nacts like a terrorist organisation even by its own definition of one,\r\nbecause I was one of you terorrists. In an illegal war. On another\r\nindigenous population. I also spent most of my adult life in exile in latin\r\namerican now so I can draw all the parallels between the regime on British\r\nIsles and cartel/ crime syndicates on other banana republics. Ours stink\r\nand is no different to other islands. So let's not fancy our regime or\r\nactors in it, as any better or more benevolant or honest than others.\r\n\r\nMy postion, proportionate to the situation, seems correct. In that a multi\r\ngenerational civil war has been waged on my family, home and people, as\r\nindigenous Welsh Britons, on our ancestral birthland. And having completed\r\nmy 22 years in oath in 2024, under the employ of the Vale council, I no\r\nlonger have the restraint of allegiance to the Brit'ish State. I am\r\nnaturally leaning towards being a counter officer and pro Wales fully\r\ndecolonising. Bearing in mind this war being waged isn't us ontowards the\r\nBrit'ish State, but the exact opposite. And there's no requirement on our\r\npart we participate willingly or make it any easier than is neccesary to\r\nsoft genocide us. After already dispossesing, displacing, looting,\r\nbutchering and holding us hostage or exiling us.\r\n\r\nIn any case I believe my families standing, title and estate was stolen.\r\nVia fraud and armed force. Once the cover up and falsified records are\r\nrectified it will be more apparent why restoration and reperations should\r\noccur. Because it's already very clear to me. As a cyber and electronic\r\nmilitary warfare professional and certified and experienced military\r\ncommander and leader. As well as me being the son and grandson of the man\r\nand his mum butchered in the process of this fraud and cover up attempt of\r\nthe crimes relating to this terrorist siege on our land and property of\r\ncenturies.\r\n\r\nWe Britons do not negotiate with terrorists.\r\nI'm not SAS. I'm who JSHQ Northwood sent to break the SAS out of the Iraq\r\nprison days before they got executed. I setup the covert drone surveillance\r\nvia satellies between Iraq and London. Now it's the inverse situation where\r\nyou're regime is on my lands, have executed my family members, hold my\r\nfamily hostage and still act like terrorists. Silence does not work in your\r\nfavour with me. I just presume you are otherwise engaging in clandestine\r\nwarfare with me.\r\n\r\nI'm hoping for the best. I really am.\r\nPeace, due process, dialouge etc.\r\n\r\nThis isn't even our generations fight. It's an inherited scandal and mess,\r\npassed down to us. The responsibility to rectify this matter is a fudiciary\r\none. And I'm just the one in my family daring to simply speak about this.\r\nNo wonder none of the hostages there speak. It just becomes another\r\nopportunity for you to all reaffirm our people are hostages under\r\nterrorists.\r\n\r\n\r\nI've seen recently others speaking out and speaking up being separated from\r\ntheir evidence, charges fabricated and them subjected to interment. I\r\nmyself already had to have the support of a US Marine officer when attempts\r\nwere made to frame and extradite and silence me. The defence correspondent\r\nfor the mail on Sunday was also contracted to destroy my reputation in a\r\nnationwide campaign before. Which I also managed to stop in time. So I know\r\nthe lengths that the regime on that island will go to, to cover up crimes\r\nand fraud instead of remedy.\r\n\r\n I doubt a lot of you are even human at this point. Not because of my\r\nactual military attachment to the underground UFO city in Coresham. But\r\nbecause a mars attack type alien invasion and occupancy and rule is exactly\r\nthe vibes I get when I'm back on my motherland. Citizenship seems to now\r\nmean to be hostages whereby people are gagged, hands tied behind their\r\nbacks and on their knees. That is the exact situation I see people in, on\r\nBritish Isles. Any moment due to have our throats slit.\r\n\r\nNot for me thank you. I much preferred being behind the machine gun in a\r\nhelicopter pointing it at the inhumane terrorists treating humans this way.\r\n\r\nI spoke to my sister earlier about an article stating that bailiffs stole,\r\nagainst the explicit injunction instructions, £30,000 of my dads after\r\nevictim him from his home. In 1988. She told me my family there haven't\r\never experienced having £30,000 laying around the house in the almost 40\r\nyears since. If that doesn't tell me they're like Jews of nazi Germany\r\nunder the occupancy and rule of the tyranny and terrorism there i don't\r\nknow what does.\r\n\r\nSo it is a hostage / terorrist situation so far as I see. We're not\r\nforgotten about. I've made it very clear for months and I'm just ignored\r\nand rebuttled and dismissed. So  clearly someone believes we're not all\r\nequal under God. Or law. That's disappointing.\r\n\r\nRegards,\r\n\r\nSion Buckler Esq (Ret'd Sgt)\r\nRightful Executor of Manor of Llandough\r\n\r\nOn Fri, 14 Nov 2025, 15:24 Carroll, George D (Cllr), <\r\ngdcarroll@valeofglamorgan.gov.uk> wrote:\r\n\r\n> Hi Requester\r\n>\r\n> Thank you for copying me in on this.\r\n>\r\n> If you'd like to discuss, please let me know.\r\n>\r\n> All the best,\r\n>\r\n> George\r\n> ------------------------------\r\n> *From:* Requester <[redacted]>\r\n> *Sent:* Friday, November 14, 2025 12:48:36 PM\r\n> *To:* kanishka.narayan.mp@parliament.uk <kanishka.narayan.mp@parliament.uk\r\n> >\r\n> *Subject:* BP vs Buckler\r\n>\r\n> You don't often get email from [redacted]. Learn why this\r\n> is important <https://aka.ms/LearnAboutSenderIdentification>\r\n>\r\n> Sir/Ma'am,\r\n>\r\n> I am your constituent, living [location redacted]. I am writing to request your\r\n> assistance in reviewing a historic legal matter involving my family: *BP\r\n> Properties Ltd v Buckler (1987)*, relating to Great House Farm, Llandough.\r\n>\r\n> My family lived on, cared for and claimed to own that farm for\r\n> generations. The events surrounding the 1987 case resulted in the loss of\r\n> our home and land, and left several members of my family deeply affected.\r\n> Recent research I have undertaken has revealed significant procedural\r\n> inconsistencies, historical irregularities, unanswered questions regarding\r\n> corporate identity, unverified service of key documents, and heritage\r\n> considerations relating to the site.\r\n>\r\n> I have attached a short report summarising SOME of these issues. I am\r\n> requesting your help in identifying the appropriate bodies—legal,\r\n> historical, or governmental—who may be able to independently review the\r\n> historical record to ensure fairness, accuracy, and transparency.\r\n>\r\n> My interest is not solely personal; Great House Farm has a heritage\r\n> significance to Wales, including documented accounts that Guglielmo Marconi\r\n> stayed there while experimenting with early radio technology. I believe\r\n> this should form part of our preserved historical record.\r\n>\r\n> By way of background, I am a veteran and have served both in the Armed\r\n> Forces and recently with the Vale of Glamorgan Council. My intention is\r\n> simply to seek clarity, proper review, and justice where appropriate. I\r\n> would be grateful for your guidance on the correct avenues to pursue.\r\n>\r\n> Thank you for your time and consideration. I look forward to your response.\r\n>\r\n> Yours sincerely,\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> *Requester*\r\n> [redacted]\r\n> [map link redacted]\r\n> [location redacted]\r\n> [map link redacted]\r\n> [redacted]\r\n> [map link redacted]\r\n> Tel: 0203 137 7118\r\n>\r\n"},{"id":"wb-444f12d0","subject":"Re setting aside BP v Buckler 1987 on grounds of fraud","name":"0000-00-00_re-setting-aside-bp-vs-buckler-1987-on-grounds-of-fraud_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-setting-aside-bp-vs-buckler-1987-on-grounds-of-fraud_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"setting-aside-bp-v-buckler-1987-on-grounds-of-fraud","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-setting-aside-bp-vs-buckler-1987-on-grounds-of-fraud_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"Hi Requester,\r\n\r\nSorry for the delay in response. The above is really interesting, and a\r\ntravesty. However, we aren't sure how we would be able to be of help to you.\r\n\r\nA good idea may be contacting the journalist Will Hayward , his contact\r\ndetails should be online.\r\n\r\nBest wishes and the best of luck,\r\nBronwen\r\n\r\nOn Mon, 18 May 2026 at 16:55, Requester <[redacted]> wrote:\r\n\r\n> Hwyl Bronwen,\r\n>\r\n> I speak fluent Welsh but will keep this in English as I'm a military cyber\r\n> and electronic warfare professional and use A.I which is learning from this\r\n> case for a possible class action lawsuit scaling.\r\n>\r\n> I'm writing to raise a long-standing family grievance concerning the\r\n> events surrounding BP v Buckler (1987), and what I believe to be the\r\n> dispossession and marginalisation of my indigenous Welsh family from our\r\n> ancestral birthland and heritage. Our title appears to have been stolen\r\n> after Marconi stayed with us while inventing radio, then for the last\r\n> century we appear to have suffered decades of supressed info, court\r\n> \"mistakes\" , proceedural unfairness, conveniently timed deaths, gaps in\r\n> records, abuse of power, human rights violations and what I now see to be\r\n> fraud. Fraud unravels all. And limitation applies from discovery not\r\n> occurance of the fraud.\r\n>\r\n> My family history concerns Ty Mawr (Great House), Llandough, and the\r\n> Buckler / Williams lineage connected to the property and surrounding lands.\r\n> From family records, historical material, local reporting, and documents\r\n> referenced through my research, I believe there are unresolved questions\r\n> regarding title history, possession, treatment of the family, and the\r\n> handling of allegations and complaints raised over the years.\r\n>\r\n> My concern is not only the legal events themselves, but the broader\r\n> historical context. The period during and following the Thatcher era saw\r\n> major industrial, land, and economic changes in Wales. From my perspective,\r\n> these changes disproportionately affected Welsh communities and families\r\n> with long-standing local roots and historical connections to land.\r\n>\r\n> I believe BP v Buckler (1987) should be revisited as part of a wider\r\n> historical review of:\r\n>\r\n> • Chain of custody and title history relating to the property and lands\r\n> • Historical ownership claims and succession issues\r\n> • Any council records, correspondence, reports, or discussions concerning\r\n> the matter\r\n> • Allegations reportedly raised but not fully investigated\r\n> • The treatment and displacement of local Welsh families connected to the\r\n> land\r\n>\r\n> I respectfully request access to all available records, archives, minutes,\r\n> correspondence, title documents, and related materials concerning the\r\n> property and associated disputes. I am being ignored by the MP and\r\n> Councillor and Land Registry converted my query to a complaint only to mark\r\n> it as solved and squash it. Ministry of Justice took 6 months to reply only\r\n> to dismiss me. ITV ignore me but quietly declassified supressed footage\r\n> recently:\r\n>\r\n> https://youtu.be/4GXmR7mAas0?feature=shared\r\n>\r\n> I am working on a website to put together the full timeline and full\r\n> evidence for a Netflix documentry. Or a Welsh studio and producer if\r\n> possible. My family want narrative and legal justice. They are terrified\r\n> into silence. I am 7000 miles away in Patagonia just to bring this matter\r\n> up, since the British State has already tried Force, Stealth/ Secrecy and\r\n> fraudulent License and other clandestine strikes from the shadows, instead\r\n> of addressing this out in the open. I also require of the Senedd a public\r\n> enquiry. Our Nation and Herritage was defrauded, not just my family.\r\n>\r\n> My intention is historical clarification, transparency, and preservation\r\n> of Welsh heritage and family history.\r\n>\r\n> Yours faithfully,\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> Requester Esq. (Ret'd Sergeant)\r\n>\r\n> [redacted]\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n"},{"id":"wb-3e46c5ec","subject":"Re subject freedom of information request Tŷ Mawr great hous","name":"0000-00-00_re-subject-freedom-of-information-request-ty-mawr-great-hous_en_v0.0.0.00-2.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"FOI / EIR correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-subject-freedom-of-information-request-ty-mawr-great-hous_en_v0.0.0.00-2.txt","threadSlug":"subject-freedom-of-information-request-t-mawr-great-hous","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-subject-freedom-of-information-request-ty-mawr-great-hous_en_v0.0.0.00-2.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"[Recovered on 19 Oct 2026 from the original email in the family's Gmail, because this archive file was missing. Matched to this entry by its subject line; where several emails share a subject, the order within the group is not certain. Email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses are redacted.]\n\nSubject: Re: Subject: Freedom of Information Request - Ty Mawr (Great House), Llandough, near Penarth (1938-1987) F260213\nFrom: Requester\nDate: Sat, 14 Mar 2026 09:32 UTC\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nThanks for your reply,\n\nSee attached the letter to which I am referring.\nI trust this helps clarify the plot in question.\n\nRegards,\n\nSion Buckler\n\nOn Fri, 13 Mar 2026 at 08:27, _FOI email address <[email redacted]>\nwrote:\n\n> Dear Requester\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you for your email, however the information you have provided does\n> not assist us in locating the title number.\n>\n>\n>\n> We are unable to proceed with your Freedom of Information request without\n> this information.\n>\n>\n>\n> You may wish to contact our Customer Support Team who are trained to\n> assist you with locating information that is held on the register of title.\n>\n>\n>\n> Their contact details are as follows:\n>\n>\n>\n> HM Land Registry\n> Citizen Centre\n> PO Box 7806\n> Bilston\n> [postcode redacted]\n>\n>\n>\n> English language phone [phone redacted]\n>\n> Welsh language phone [phone redacted]\n>\n>\n>\n> Telephone lines are open between 08:00 and 16:30 Monday to Friday. This\n> does not include Bank Holidays.\n>\n>\n>\n> Website: www.gov.uk/guidance/contact-hm-land-registry\n>\n> Online contact form: HM Land Registry Contact Us form\n> <https://customerhelp.landregistry.gov.uk/guide-page-external/?stepid=2b31bc48-eb96-eb11-b1ac-002248413956>\n>\n>\n>\n> There is no direct contact e-mail address for Customer Support. Please use\n> the online contact form and any responses will be issued via e-mail.\n>\n>\n>\n> Yours sincerely\n>\n>\n>\n> [image: logo]\n>\n> *Amanda Whittaker *\n> *PC.foi Disclosure Officer*\n>\n> *Information Rights Team*\n>\n> Data Group\n>\n> Data and Register Integrity Group\n>\n> Email: [email redacted] / [email redacted]\n> GOV.UK <https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/land-registry> |\n> @HMLandRegistry <https://twitter.com/HMLandRegistry> | LinkedIn\n> <http://www.linkedin.com/company/land-registry> | Facebook\n> <http://www.facebook.com/pages/Land-Registry/343223435769541>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> *From:* Requester <[email redacted]>\n> *Sent:* 12 March 2026 18:44\n> *To:* _FOI email address <[email redacted]>\n> *Subject:* Re: Subject: Freedom of Information Request - Ty Mawr (Great\n> House), Llandough, near Penarth (1938-1987) F260213\n>\n>\n>\n> Hi,\n>\n>\n>\n> Thanks for your reply.\n>\n> Your agency already responded some weeks ago to say that the registration\n> by BP of Great House Farm, Llandough in 1982, was being scanned.\n>\n> I have yet to receive a response about that, but that is the land parcel\n> in question. Now known as Church View Close, Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan.\n>\n>\n>\n> Regards,\n>\n>\n>\n> Requester\n>\n>\n>\n> On Thu, 12 Mar 2026 at 09:21, _FOI email address <[email redacted]>\n> wrote:\n>\n> Dear Requester\n>\n>\n>\n> In order to proceed with your request we require some further information:\n>\n>\n>\n>    - Please provide the title number in which you require this\n>    information for\n>\n>\n>\n> You may need to apply for a Search of the Index Map to find out if a\n> specific property or area of land is registered and, if it is, the title\n> number(s) of that land. Further information on this service is provided\n> below.\n>\n>\n>\n> Once you have sufficiently clarified the relevant land in which you would\n> like this information for, we will proceed with your request.\n>\n>\n>\n> To assist you in accessing information held by HMLR, please be aware the\n> information you have requested is held within our registers that are open\n> to public inspection and accessible via statutory routes of access. This\n> means that we are unable to provide access to this information via an\n> Environmental Information Regulations (EIR) or a Freedom of Information Act\n> 2000 (FOIA) request and it is likely a refusal notice will be issued for\n> this information.\n>\n>\n>\n> Information about how to apply to inspect or obtain official copies is\n> contained with practice guide 11:\n>\n>\n> www.gov.uk/government/publications/inspection-and-application-for-official-copies/practice-guide-11-inspection-and-application-for-official-copies\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> Our Customer Support colleagues are trained to handle queries which relate\n> to information that is held on the register of title\n>\n> If you require any assistance in completing the application form or to\n> determine which documents to order, please contact our Customer Support\n> Team who will be able to assist you with your query.\n>\n>\n>\n> Their contact details are as follows:\n>\n>\n>\n> English language phone [phone redacted]\n>\n> Welsh language phone [phone redacted]\n>\n>\n>\n> Telephone lines are open between 08:00 and 17:00 Monday to Friday This\n> does not include Bank Holidays.\n>\n> Website: www.gov.uk/guidance/contact-hm-land-registry\n>\n>\n>\n> *What is the Index Map?*\n>\n> HM Land Registry keeps a computerised 'index map' based on the Ordnance\n> Survey map. This computerised map provides an index of the land in every\n> registered title and pending application for first registration.\n>\n>\n>\n> *Applying for an Index Map Search*\n>\n> You apply for an official search of the index map using form SIM\n> <https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/index-map-application-for-an-official-search-sim>\n> .\n>\n> This will need to be printed, completed and sent to our Standard address\n> <https://www.gov.uk/guidance/hm-land-registry-address-for-applications#standard-addresses>\n> .\n>\n> You must attach a plan to show the land that you are interested in, it\n> should:\n>\n>\n>\n>    -      be drawn to scale\n>    -      be based on the current large scale Ordnance Survey map\n>    -      indicate scale and north point\n>    -      show property extent with suitable colouring and position in\n>    relation to nearby roads and other features plans of flats and maisonettes\n>         indicate floor level (if any) of the property.\n>    -\n>\n> You can also search by map on our Search for land and property information\n> <https://www.gov.uk/search-property-information-land-registry> service.\n>\n>\n>\n> Yours sincerely\n>\n>\n>\n> [image: logo]\n>\n> *Amanda Whittaker *\n> *PC.foi Disclosure Officer*\n>\n> *Information Rights Team*\n>\n> Data Group\n>\n> Data and Register Integrity Group\n>\n> Email: [email redacted] / [email redacted]\n> GOV.UK <https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/land-registry> |\n> @HMLandRegistry <https://twitter.com/HMLandRegistry> | LinkedIn\n> <http://www.linkedin.com/company/land-registry> | Facebook\n> <http://www.facebook.com/pages/Land-Registry/343223435769541>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> *From:* Requester <[email redacted]>\n> *Sent:* 16 February 2026 01:03\n> *To:* _FOI email address <[email redacted]>;\n> [email redacted]\n> *Subject:* Subject: Freedom of Information Request - Ty Mawr (Great\n> House), Llandough, near Penarth (1938-1987)\n>\n>\n>\n> Some people who received this message don't often get email from\n> [email redacted]. Learn why this is important\n> <https://aka.ms/LearnAboutSenderIdentification>\n>\n> Sir/Madam,\n>\n>\n>\n> I understand you may already be busy, so please don't feel overwhelmed by\n> this FOI request—I'm conducting my own diligence and happy for you to take\n> the full statutory 20 working days to respond.\n>\n>\n>\n> Under the Freedom of Information Act 2000, I request copies of all\n> records, correspondence, memoranda, internal notes, minutes, file notes,\n> land registration applications, objections, or communications held by HM\n> Land Registry from 1 January 1938 to 31 December 1987 relating to:Ty Mawr\n> (Great House), Llandough, near Penarth, South Glamorgan (also known as\n> Great House Farm).\n>\n>\n>\n> Land registration applications, title transfers, or dealings involving\n> Mountjoy Ltd, Western Ground Rents Ltd, BP Pension Trust Ltd, and/or BP\n> Properties Ltd (including the 1938 auction/reversion conveyance and 1969\n> sale).\n>\n>\n>\n> Interactions, notices, proceedings, tenancy offers, licences (e.g., 1974\n> unilateral lifetime licence letters), or disputes involving Mrs Mary\n> Williams (also referred to as Mrs Buckler), Mr Frederick Buckler, or\n> Williams/Buckler family occupiers—particularly the 1962 Cardiff County\n> Court possession proceedings (Dec 11 order by Judge Temple Morris QC).\n>\n>\n>\n> Any possession proceedings, adverse possession claims, or title objections\n> connected to the property (e.g., 1955 High Court, 1962 Cardiff County\n> Court, 1974 actions).\n>\n>\n>\n> Please include archived, off-site, or scanned records where applicable.If\n> exemptions apply (e.g., Section 40 personal data), specify which and why.\n> If costs exceed the appropriate limit, advise how to refine (e.g.,\n> prioritize 1955-1987 or 1962/1974 events).\n>\n>\n>\n> I look forward to your response within 20 working days.\n>\n>\n>\n> Yours faithfully,\n>\n>\n>\n> Requester Esq. Sgt (Ret'd)\n>\n> HM Land Registry’s purpose is to protect your land ownership and provide services and data that underpin an efficient and informed property market. Our vision is: “A world-leading property market as part of a thriving economy and a sustainable future”.\n>\n>\n>\n> We check all mail and attachments for known viruses. However, you are advised that you open any attachments at your own risk. If you have received this email and it was not intended for you, please let us know, then delete it.\n>\n>\n>\n> We welcome correspondence in English and Welsh.\n>\n>\n>\n> To see how HM Land Registry treats your personal information, read our Personal information charter: www.gov.uk/government/organisations/land-registry/about/personal-information-charter.\n>\n>\n>\n> HM Land Registry’s purpose is to protect your land ownership and provide services and data that underpin an efficient and informed property market. Our vision is: “A world-leading property market as part of a thriving economy and a sustainable future”.\n>\n> We check all mail and attachments for known viruses. However, you are advised that you open any attachments at your own risk. If you have received this email and it was not intended for you, please let us know, then delete it.\n>\n> We welcome correspondence in English and Welsh.\n>\n> To see how HM Land Registry treats your personal information, read our Personal information charter: www.gov.uk/government/organisations/land-registry/about/personal-information-charter.\n>\n>\n>\n"},{"id":"wb-a0a08b9b","subject":"Re subject freedom of information request Tŷ Mawr great hous","name":"0000-00-00_re-subject-freedom-of-information-request-ty-mawr-great-hous_en_v0.0.0.00-3.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"FOI / EIR correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-subject-freedom-of-information-request-ty-mawr-great-hous_en_v0.0.0.00-3.txt","threadSlug":"subject-freedom-of-information-request-t-mawr-great-hous","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-subject-freedom-of-information-request-ty-mawr-great-hous_en_v0.0.0.00-3.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"not-recovered","text":null,"textNotice":"Original text not yet recovered: the archived source file for this record is missing. See sourceUrl."},{"id":"wb-951ef9bd","subject":"Re subject stage two complaint title WA231076 alleged mistak","name":"0000-00-00_re-subject-stage-two-complaint-title-wa231076-alleged-mistak_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Land Registry correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_re-subject-stage-two-complaint-title-wa231076-alleged-mistak_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"subject-stage-two-complaint-title-wa231076-alleged-mistak","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_re-subject-stage-two-complaint-title-wa231076-alleged-mistak_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"[Recovered on 19 Oct 2026 from the original email in the family's Gmail, because this archive file was missing. Matched to this entry by its subject line; where several emails share a subject, the order within the group is not certain. Email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses are redacted.]\n\nSubject: Re: Subject: Stage Two Complaint – Title WA231076 – Alleged Mistake in the Register\nFrom: Requester\nDate: Sun, 03 May 2026 16:10 UTC\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nDear Sir/Madam,\n\nI write to append further evidence and clarification to my ongoing\ncomplaint regarding the registration of title relating to Great House Farm,\nLlandough (also known as Ty Mawr / Great House).\n\nhttps://youtu.be/4GXmR7mAas0?feature=shared\n\nThis submission sets out a series of facts which I can evidence and which,\ntaken together, raise serious concerns of fraud, concealment, and wrongful\nregistration that have materially affected the rights of the Williams\nfamily and their heirs.\n\n1. Unauthorised transfer of title (circa 1965)\nI can evidence that the title to the property was transferred or sold in or\naround 1965 by Frederick Buckler without the knowledge or consent of Mrs\nMary Williams, who maintained a rightful claim to the property. This raises\nthe issue that the disposition may have been made without lawful authority.\n\n2. Death of key party and concealment (1968)\nFrederick Buckler died in 1968. Any knowledge he held regarding the\ncircumstances of the transfer died with him, preventing proper scrutiny or\nchallenge at the time.\n\n3. Inability to prove ownership in court (1974)\nMrs Mary Williams was subsequently unable to prove her claim in court\nproceedings in 1974. This inability must be viewed in the context of the\nprior undisclosed transfer and the absence of key evidence and testimony.\n\n4. Knowledge of defective title by acquiring party\nThere is a credible basis to assert that the acquiring party (BP and/or its\nassociated property entities) knew, or ought reasonably to have known, that\nthe title they acquired was defective. This point is central and I am\ncompiling supporting material demonstrating notice and/or constructive\nknowledge.\n\n5. Registration following death of claimant\nMrs Mary Williams died prior to the land being formally registered in the\nname of the acquiring party. As a result, she was denied any opportunity to\nrebut, challenge, or correct the position before registration was effected.\n\n6. Death of additional disputing party\nMr Buckler also died during the period in which he was engaged in disputing\nthe matter. The cumulative effect is that all primary individuals with\ndirect knowledge were deceased before the issue could be fully and fairly\nresolved.\n\n7. Ongoing family claim\nDespite these events, the claim of the Williams family has never been\nrelinquished. The position remains that the title was wrongfully\ntransferred and subsequently registered on the basis of that defective\ntransfer.\n\n---\n\nRequest for Action\n\nIn light of the above, I respectfully request that:\n\n1. This evidence be formally appended to my existing complaint file.\n2. The register be reviewed in accordance with provisions relating to\nfraud, mistake, and/or rectification.\n3. Consideration be given to whether the registration was obtained through\nor affected by fraud or concealment.\n4. I be informed of the appropriate process for seeking rectification\nand/or indemnity.\n\nI am prepared to provide supporting documents, including historical\nrecords, press reports, and family documentation, upon request or via\nsecure submission.\n\nGiven the seriousness of the matters raised, I would be grateful for\nwritten confirmation that this submission has been received and attached to\nmy complaint, along with an outline of the next steps.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\n\nSion Buckler\n\nOn Sun, 19 Apr 2026, 16:29 Requester, <[email redacted]> wrote:\n\n>  Sir or Madam,\n>\n> I write to ask that my complaint regarding title number WA231076 (Land on\n> the North Side of Leckwith Road, Llandough) be escalated to **stage two**\n> of HM Land Registry’s Complaints Procedure. This email sets out why I\n> consider the stage‑one decision to be incorrect, identifies the mistake I\n> say exists in the register, and explains the decision I seek.\n>\n> ### 1. My core contention\n>\n> My core contention is that BP Properties Limited were registered as\n> proprietors on the basis of a factual and legal error regarding my\n> grandmother’s status on the land. My grandmother did not occupy under any\n> genuine licence granted to her and did not relinquish any claim. Instead,\n> any licence relied on by BP related to a different name/person. As her\n> heir, I have succeeded to her claim; I therefore say that registering BP as\n> proprietors was a “mistake in the register” for the purposes of the Land\n> Registration Act 2002.\n>\n> ### 2. Identity mismatch and alleged “licence”\n>\n> Your stage‑one letter records that HM Land Registry relies on the Court of\n> Appeal decision in BP Properties Ltd v Buckler  EWCA Civ 2, and treats my\n> grandmother as having occupied under a licence granted by the documentary\n> title holder, so that her possession was no longer adverse.\n>\n> However, the alleged licence and related correspondence did not use my\n> grandmother’s true legal name. My grandmother was Mrs Williams (supported\n> by her birth, marriage and death certificates), not “Mrs Buckler”. The\n> “licensee” referred to in BP’s narrative and in the 1987 litigation is\n> therefore not the same legal person as my grandmother. There is, in other\n> words, a fundamental mis‑identification or identity fraud at the heart of\n> the story that HM Land Registry and the Court have relied upon.\n>\n> If the licence relied on by BP was addressed to, or granted in respect of,\n> a different named individual, it cannot properly be said that my\n> grandmother’s occupation was under that licence. In that case her\n> occupation remained adverse to BP’s paper title, and any finding that she\n> was a “licensee” rests on a factual premise that is demonstrably wrong.\n>\n> ### 3. Timing – “cart before the horse”\n>\n> Your letter explains that the applications for first registration of\n> WA231076 (and the related title WA240304) were made in 1982–83 and that BP\n> demonstrated a documentary root of title back to 1969/1975 by way of\n> conveyances and a 1971 abstract of title.\n>\n> You also confirm that the Court of Appeal only gave its decision in BP\n> Properties Ltd v Buckler in 1987, holding that an offer by BP to let my\n> grandmother remain created a licence and brought her adverse possession to\n> an end.\n>\n> This highlights a fundamental sequencing problem:\n>\n> - At the date of first registration (1982–83), whether there was a valid\n> licence capable of defeating my grandmother’s adverse possession was a\n> live, unresolved dispute.\n> - BP nevertheless presented themselves as entitled to be registered as\n> proprietors and their solicitors certified on form FR1 that there was no\n> question or doubt affecting the title.\n>\n> - HM Land Registry, relying on that certification, registered BP before\n> any court had determined whether my grandmother’s possession had\n> extinguished BP’s title.\n> - Only in 1987 did the Court of Appeal retrospectively treat the alleged\n> licence as bringing adverse possession to an end, and that decision was\n> itself based on the mis‑identification described above.\n>\n> In simple terms, the effect of the alleged licence (if any) was judicially\n> determined **after** first registration, and on the basis of the wrong\n> person being treated as the “licensee”. BP and their solicitors put the\n> cart before the horse, and HM Land Registry accepted that position.\n>\n> ### 4. The specific “mistake in the register”\n>\n> Your stage‑one response concludes that there is “no evidence of a mistake\n> in the register” and therefore no power to alter the register and no right\n> to indemnity. I say this is incorrect.\n>\n> The mistake I allege is as follows:\n>\n> 1. On the true facts, my grandmother’s occupation of the land was adverse\n> to BP’s documentary title and was not under any valid licence granted to\n> her.\n>\n> 2. Her adverse possession had, or may have, extinguished BP’s title by the\n> time of first registration in 1982–83, meaning BP were not entitled to be\n> registered as proprietors at that time.\n>\n> 3. HM Land Registry nevertheless registered BP as proprietors on the basis\n> of:\n>    - a licence attributed to the wrong person (identity error/fraud), and\n>    - a solicitor’s FR1 certificate stating there was no question or doubt\n> affecting title, despite a long‑running dispute and pending litigation.\n>\n> 4. Subsequent reliance on the 1987 Court of Appeal decision does not cure\n> this, because that decision itself proceeded on the mis‑identification of\n> my grandmother and did not and could not retrospectively validate a licence\n> that never related to her.\n>\n> On that analysis, the register shows BP as proprietors when, in law, my\n> grandmother’s adverse possession should have extinguished BP’s title and\n> given rise to a transmissible proprietary interest which I, as her heir,\n> now claim. The register therefore does not accurately reflect the true\n> legal position and contains a mistake capable of rectification.\n>\n> ### 5. Why the stage‑one reasoning is inadequate\n>\n> In your stage‑one letter you state that:\n>\n> - HM Land Registry has an administrative function and cannot “look behind\n> or challenge decisions of the Court”. [1]\n> - My identity‑fraud claims are “not relevant to the issue of title to the\n> land” because they relate to the dispute heard by the Court.\n>\n> - There is no evidence of negligence or maladministration and no evidence\n> of a mistake in the register.\n>\n> With respect, this reasoning is incomplete:\n>\n> - Once HM Land Registry is presented with clear evidence that the person\n> named as the “licensee” in the litigation and licence documentation is not\n> the same legal person as the occupier (my grandmother), that goes directly\n> to whether the Court of Appeal’s factual premise remains reliable and\n> whether the register is accurate.\n>\n> - Recognising that there is a serious question as to the correctness of\n> the register is not the same as “overturning” the Court of Appeal. HM Land\n> Registry has statutory powers to alter the register where there is a\n> mistake, and it can, where appropriate, refer disputes about rectification\n> to the tribunal or the courts.\n>\n> - To simply say that identity‑fraud concerns are “not relevant to title”\n> is, I say, an error of law and/or an unreasonable application of policy. It\n> disregards new evidence going to the foundation of BP’s claimed entitlement.\n>\n> ### 6. The decision and outcome I seek\n>\n> For stage two, I ask that a Land Registrar (Senior Officer) reconsider the\n> complaint and:\n>\n> 1. Accept that the combination of:\n>    - the identity mismatch (licence/name vs my grandmother’s true legal\n> name), and\n>    - the timing (registration in 1982–83 before the 1987 decision) gives\n> rise to at least a **serious question** as to whether the register is\n> mistaken in showing BP/ Companies thereafter, as proprietors.\n>\n> 2. Treat this as evidence of a potential “mistake in the register” for the\n> purposes of your alteration powers and Schedule 8, rather than as a mere\n> disagreement with the 1987 judgment.\n>\n> 3. Either:\n>\n>    - (a) commence the process of rectification, recognising that my\n> grandmother’s adverse possession may have extinguished BP’s title and that\n> I, as her heir, now have a claim to be registered, or\n>\n>    - (b) formally refer the dispute over the correctness of the register\n> to the appropriate tribunal/court, making clear that HM Land Registry\n> recognises that the identity‑fraud evidence raises a real issue about\n> whether the register is accurate.\n>\n> 4. Confirm that, if it is ultimately determined that the register is\n> mistaken and cannot be rectified without prejudice to third parties, HM\n> Land Registry will treat me as having a potential claim for indemnity under\n> Schedule 8.\n>\n> Please also confirm any further documents you require (for example, copies\n> of the licence, the BP v Buckler pleadings/judgment, and my grandmother’s\n> certificates) so that the stage‑two review can consider the identity\n> evidence in full.\n>\n> Fraud unravels all. The limitation act also had a clause for fraud.\n> Furthermore any limitation begins from discovery of fraud not occurrence of\n> it.\n>\n> The convenient and timely death of my nan after her two obstructed\n> attempts to make her case in 1974, followed by my father's convenient and\n> timely death after his appeal of the 1987 case was dismissed and his ECHR\n> obstructed, along with our forceful removal by 30 police and bailiffs in\n> 1988. And the press reporting us as squatters on our ancestral birthland\n> since 1667, did unlawfully dispossess and disposition us, but it doesn't\n> change the facts.\n>\n> I see this case as instrumental in Wales' decolonisation. Whereby us\n> indigneous Welsh Nationals on our ancestral birthland are victim to a\n> crime. And I am the only one willing to speak up and out as my family of\n> mostly women, are all too traumatised by this crime.\n>\n> It's evident illicit profits and gains. Identity fraud. And negligence and\n> incompetence. I absolutely see the Brit-ish Establishment that now occupy\n> and rule through force, stealth/ secrecy and licence as terrorists and us\n> your hostages. As a direct result of this case.\n>\n> This is now a hostage terrorist situation on our lands. Well, my family\n> are your hostages, I'm more of a counter officer soldier. So put it right.\n> I will not stand down.\n>\n> Regards,\n>\n> Requester Esq. (Ret'd Sgt)\n> Rightful Squire & executor of Manon of Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan, Wales.\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> On Sun, 19 Apr 2026, 07:41 Wales.OfficeMail, <\n> [email redacted]> wrote:\n>\n>> The document attached is an official requisition, reminder or letter from\n>> HM Land Registry. It is not a circular and relates either to an application\n>> you have lodged with us or a property in which you have an interest. No\n>> paper copy of the item of correspondence will be sent to you.\n>>\n>> The document is in 'Portable Document Format' (PDF) which replicates the\n>> appearance of the hard copy version. You will need Adobe Acrobat Reader to\n>> open the document. If you do not already have Adobe Acrobat Reader it can\n>> be instal