 act to reopen the case. My nan fought off the alleged possessor, BP Pensions Trust Ltd, for many years - and  maintained equitable title ownership of our 800 year old farm. But the judge ruled that BP Properties Ltd, the claimant in the 1987 case, were ignored. And the BP companies were seperate, so rejecting one doesn't inherently meaning rejecting the other. And so their letter of offer, a license to stay at \"their\" farm until she died, upheld and stopped the clock on adverse possession. Which was the position of our family (since our title deeds had gone missing from our home and copy gone from the public library during all this multi generational lawfare).\n\nBut in my dads appeal (point 3/3) he argued BP Pensions Trust should have been the claimant, not BP Properties Ltd. To which the judges dismissed the appeal citing - both BP companies are basically the same.\n\nSo a blatant case of \"Heads they win, tails we lose\". Not to mention the relationship between British Government and BP in those years being a huge bias.\n\nFurthermore my nan wasn't alive to defend her position in 87. And no evidence was presented of the alleged letter from BP to stop the clock of adverse possession. Yet the case was won on the fact a letter of rejection, from a now dead woman, wasn't able to be presented. It is insane how BP took possession of our family farm of 400 years of occupancy.\n\nOur farm was prime real estate, overlooking Cardiff bay. This is an Erin Brokovitch scale scandal. It gets more wild. In my research I discovered Marconi stayed with us while inventing Radio. My great grandad took him via horse & cart every day, to Lavenock Point Penarth, to conduct his radio experiments - including the day of the first successful broadcast. There's not even a plaque there today to commemorate that Llandough is the birthplace of radio. I'm also a decorated Royal Signals veteran (honourable discharge). Just 23 houses built over the site now. CADW expressly rejected a petition at the time too, which would afford our 800 year old farm protection from demolishment. Not only was the farm demolished, it was done during the night and early morning after the lawfare in the court which is an evident act of  desperation and relentlessness.\n\nMy family would like justice. I believe we have a strong case. The councillor of Llandough is very interested. I just need legal representation to take this forward.\n\nI look forward to your reply on that matter too if you wouldn't mind. I did call and leave a message about it last week as well.\n\n\nRegards,\n\nSion Buckler\n[phone redacted]\n\n[cid:image007.jpg@01DC6914.E3AF9670]\n\n\n\nOn Mon, 8 Dec 2025, 07:07 Jessica Williams, <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>> wrote:\nGood morning Requester,\n\nI write in response to your website enquiry received by me this morning – apologies for the delay in reverting to you. Unfortunately, this does not appear to be a matter we would be able to assist with as we do not have a Criminal Law department.\n\nYou may be able to find assistance through Forces Law<https://forceslaw.com/contact/>. They are essentially a switchboard of multiple different firms of solicitors, all with military knowledge. Alternatively, Op NOVA<https://www.forcesemployment.org.uk/programmes/op-nova/contact/> may be able to assist. They are a charity who support veterans in contact with the justice system.\n\nThank you for your enquiry, and apologies we cannot be of assistance at this time.\n\nBest wishes,\nJess\n[cid:image002.png@01DC6914.E3AF9670]\n\n​​​​\nJessica Williams\nLegal Assistant\nDDI\nEmail\n[phone redacted]\n[email redacted]<[email redacted]>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nwilkinchapmanrollits.co.uk<http://wilkinchapmanrollits.co.uk>\n\nThe Maltings, 11‑15[map link redacted] Brayford[map link redacted] Wharf[map link redacted] East,[map link redacted] Lincoln,[map link redacted] LN5[map link redacted] 7AY[map link redacted]\n\n[LinkedIn]<https://www.linkedin.com/company/wilkinchapmanrollits/>\n