hments\n>\n> ​created by Wilkin Chapman LLP) belongs to Wilkin Chapman LLP which asserts the right to be identified as such and hereby objects to\n> ​any misuse thereof.\n>\n> *From:* Requester <[email redacted]>\n> *Sent:* 08 December 2025 12:38\n> *To:* Jessica Williams <[email redacted]>\n> *Subject:* [EXTERNAL] Re: WCR Website Enquiry\n>\n>\n>\n> *This Message originated outside your organisation.*\n> ------------------------------\n>\n> No problem about Martin's case, understood, thanks for your prompt reply\n> Jess.\n>\n>\n>\n> I don't mean to appear litigious, but the other matter I have is BP vs\n> Buckler 1987. My dad appealed it, it was dismissed. He then sought ECHR,\n> blocked. He then died. The trauma on our family frightened and immobilised\n> everyone from seeking justice. Even unto this day.\n>\n>\n>\n> I found a lot of fraud & concealment in this case. There's also a lot of\n> media coverage in archives. I wondered if the clause in the limitations act\n> could allow me to reopen the case on these grounds. The museum confirmed to\n> be last week all the items exhevated from under our farm after it was\n> stolen from us.\n>\n>\n>\n> I'll give you one of the 12 arguments I have where it concerns clauses in\n> the limitation act to reopen the case. My nan fought off the alleged\n> possessor, BP Pensions Trust Ltd, for many years - and  maintained\n> equitable title ownership of our 800 year old farm. But the judge ruled\n> that BP Properties Ltd, the claimant in the 1987 case, were ignored. And\n> the BP companies were seperate, so rejecting one doesn't inherently meaning\n> rejecting the other. And so their letter of offer, a license to stay at\n> \"their\" farm until she died, upheld and stopped the clock on adverse\n> possession. Which was the position of our family (since our title deeds had\n> gone missing from our home and copy gone from the public library during all\n> this multi generational lawfare).\n>\n>\n>\n> But in my dads appeal (point 3/3) he argued BP Pensions Trust should have\n> been the claimant, not BP Properties Ltd. To which the judges dismissed the\n> appeal citing - both BP companies are basically the same.\n>\n>\n>\n> So a blatant case of \"Heads they win, tails we lose\". Not to mention the\n> relationship between British Government and BP in those years being a huge\n> bias.\n>\n>\n>\n> Furthermore my nan wasn't alive to defend her position in 87. And no\n> evidence was presented of the alleged letter from BP to stop the clock of\n> adverse possession. Yet the case was won on the fact a letter of rejection,\n> from a now dead woman, wasn't able to be presented. It is insane how BP\n> took possession of our family farm of 400 years of occupancy.\n>\n>\n>\n> Our farm was prime real estate, overlooking Cardiff bay. This is an Erin\n> Brokovitch scale scandal. It gets more wild. In my research I discovered\n> Marconi stayed with us while inventing Radio. My great grandad took him via\n> horse & cart every day, to Lavenock Point Penarth, to conduct his radio\n> experiments - including the day of the first successful broadcast. There's\n> not even a plaque there today to commemorate that Llandough is the\n> birthplace of radio. I'm also a decorated Royal Signals veteran (honourable\n> discharge). Just 23 houses built over the site now. CADW expressly rejected\n> a petition at the time too, which would afford our 800 year old farm\n> protection from demolishment. Not only was the farm demolished, it was done\n> during the night and early morning after the lawfare in the court which is\n> an evident act of  desperation and relentlessness.\n>\n>\n>\n> My family would like justice. I believe we have a strong case. The\n> councillor of Llandough is very interested. I just need legal\n> representation to take this forward.\n>\n>\n>\n> I look forward to your reply on that matter too if you wouldn't mind. I\n> did call and leave a message about it last week as well.\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> Regards,\n>\n>\n>