eStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Council and local authority correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_great-house-farm-church-view-close-llandough-nr-penarth-vale_en_v0.0.0.00-2.txt","threadSlug":"great-house-farm-church-view-close-llandough-nr-penarth-vale","scopeTopics":["morgan"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["morgan"],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_great-house-farm-church-view-close-llandough-nr-penarth-vale_en_v0.0.0.00-2.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"[Recovered on 19 Oct 2026 from the original email in the family's Gmail, because this archive file was missing. Matched to this entry by its subject line; where several emails share a subject, the order within the group is not certain. Email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses are redacted.]\n\nSubject: Fwd: Great House Farm/ Church View Close, Llandough, nr. Penarth, Vale of Glamorgan (ATISN 27021)\nFrom: Requester\nDate: Thu, 11 Jun 2026 19:40 UTC\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\n---------- Forwarded message ---------\nFrom: <[email redacted]>\nDate: Thu, 11 Jun 2026, 12:54\nSubject: RE: Great House Farm/ Church View Close, Llandough, nr. Penarth,\nVale of Glamorgan (ATISN 27021)\nTo: <[email redacted]>\n\n\nDear Mr Buckler,\n\n\n\nThank you for your request which I received on 1 June 2026, and your\nfollow-up request received on 8 June 2026.\n\n\n\nPlease see attached response and documents.\n\n\n\nThank you,\n\n\n\nMike Weatherhead\n\n\nArweinydd Tîm Gwaith Achosion / Casework Team Leader\n\nPolisi a Gwarchod / Policy and Protection\n\nArolygiaeth yr Amgylchedd Hanesyddol / Historic Environment Inspectorate\n\nCadw\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n*From:* Weatherhead, Michael (LCC - Cadw)\n*Sent:* 01 June 2026 16:59\n*To:* 'Requester' <[email redacted]>\n*Subject:* RE: Great House Farm/ Church View Close, Llandough, nr. Penarth,\nVale of Glamorgan (ATISN 27021)\n\n\n\nDear Mr Buckler,\n\n\n\n*Great House Farm, Llandough*\n\n\n\nThank you for your request which I received on 1 June 2026. You asked for:\n\n\n\n   1. Any consideration of the property for statutory listing.\n   2. Any listing assessments, surveys, inspections, recommendations or\n   reports concerning the building.\n   3. Any correspondence between Cadw, Welsh Office officials, local\n   authorities, conservation bodies, archaeological organisations or owners\n   concerning the historic significance, preservation, demolition or\n   protection of the property.\n   4. Any records explaining why the building was not added to the\n   statutory list of buildings of special architectural or historic interest.\n   5. Any records discussing the age, architectural significance,\n   archaeological significance or surviving historic fabric of the building.\n   6. Any records concerning emergency listing, spot-listing, preservation\n   notices or other forms of protection considered for the property.\n   7. Any records held relating to public campaigns, petitions or\n   representations seeking preservation of the building.\n   8. Any photographs, plans, surveys or architectural records held by Cadw\n   or the Welsh Government relating to Great House Farm.\n\n\n\nYou will receive a response by 25 June 2026. If you have any queries about\nthis request, please do not hesitate to contact me at the address below.\nPlease remember to quote the reference number above in any future\ncorrespondence *(ATISN 27021).*\n\n\n\nThe request you sent me contains personal information about you - for\nexample, your name and address. The Welsh Government will be the data\nprocessor for this information and, in accordance with the General Data\nProtection Regulation, it will be processed in order to fulfil our public\ntask and meet our legal obligations under the Act to provide you with a\nresponse. We will only use this personal information to deal with your\nrequest and any matters which arise as a result of it. We will keep your\npersonal information and all other information relating to your request for\nthree years from the date on which your request is finally closed.  Your\npersonal information will then be disposed of securely.\n\n\n\nUnder data protection legislation, you have the right:\n\n\n\n   - to be informed of the personal data we hold about you and to access it\n   - to require us to rectify inaccuracies in that data\n   - to (in certain circumstances) object to or restrict processing\n   - for (in certain circumstances) your data to be ‘erased’\n   - to (in certain circumstances) data portability\n   - to lodge a complaint with the Information Commissioner’s Office (ICO)\n   who is our independent regulator for data protection\n\n\n\nFor further information about the information which the Welsh Government\nholds and its use, or if you wish to exercise your rights under the GDPR,\nplease see contact details below:\n\n\n\nData Protection Officer\n\nWelsh Government\n\nCathays Park\n\nCARDIFF\n\n[postcode redacted]\n\nEmail: [email redacted]\n\n\n\nThe contact details for the Information Commissioner’s Office are:\n\n\n\nWycliffe House\n\nWater Lane\n\nWilmslow\n\nCheshire\n\n[postcode redacted]\n\nWebsite: www.ico.org.uk\n\n\n\nAny information released under the Freedom of Information Act 2000 or\nEnvironmental Information Regulations 2004 will be listed in the Welsh\nGovernment’s Disclosure Log (at\nhttps://gov.wales/about/open-government/freedom-of-information/responses/?lang=en\n).\n\n\n\nYours sincerely,\n\n\n\nMike Weatherhead\n\n\nArweinydd Tîm Gwaith Achosion / Casework Team Leader\n\nPolisi a Gwarchod / Policy and Protection\n\nArolygiaeth yr Amgylchedd Hanesyddol / Historic Environment Inspectorate\n\nCadw\n\n\n\n*From:* Requester <[email redacted]>\n*Sent:* 28 May 2026 06:29\n*To:* Freedom of Information Officer <[email redacted]>;\nListed Buildings / Adeiladau Rhestredig <[email redacted]>\n*Subject:* Great House Farm/ Church View Close, Llandough, nr. Penarth,\nVale of Glamorgan\n\n\n\nDear Freedom of Information Officer,\n\nI am requesting information held by the Welsh Government and/or Cadw\nconcerning Great House Farm (also known as Great House or Ty Mawr),\nLlandough, Vale of Glamorgan, prior to its demolition in 1988.\n\nI request copies of any recorded information held relating to:\n\n   1. Any consideration of the property for statutory listing.\n   2. Any listing assessments, surveys, inspections, recommendations or\n   reports concerning the building.\n   3. Any correspondence between Cadw, Welsh Office officials, local\n   authorities, conservation bodies, archaeological organisations or owners\n   concerning the historic significance, preservation, demolition or\n   protection of the property.\n   4. Any records explaining why the building was not added to the\n   statutory list of buildings of special architectural or historic interest.\n   5. Any records discussing the age, architectural significance,\n   archaeological significance or surviving historic fabric of the building.\n   6. Any records concerning emergency listing, spot-listing, preservation\n   notices or other forms of protection considered for the property.\n   7. Any records held relating to public campaigns, petitions or\n   representations seeking preservation of the building.\n   8. Any photographs, plans, surveys or architectural records held by Cadw\n   or the Welsh Government relating to Great House Farm.\n\nFor the avoidance of doubt, I am seeking recorded information rather than\nan explanation or opinion created for the purpose of this request.\n\nIf any of the requested information is held by another public authority, I\nwould be grateful if you could advise which authority is likely to hold the\nrecords.\n\nI would prefer the information in electronic form.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\nSion Buckler Esq. (Ret'd Sergeant). Heir & Successors of Mary Williams,\nGreat House Farm, Llandough\nSganiwyd y neges hon am bob feirws hysbys wrth iddi adael Llywodraeth\nCymru. Mae Llywodraeth Cymru yn cymryd o ddifrif yr angen i ddiogelu eich\ndata. Os cysylltwch â Llywodraeth Cymru, mae ein hysbysiad preifatrwydd yn\nesbonio sut rydym yn defnyddio eich gwybodaeth a sut rydym yn diogelu eich\npreifatrwydd. Rydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg. Byddwn yn anfon ateb\nyn Gymraeg i ohebiaeth a dderbynnir yn Gymraeg ac ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg\nyn arwain at oedi. ----------------- On leaving the Welsh Government this\nemail was scanned for all known viruses. The Welsh Government takes the\nprotection of your data seriously. If you contact the Welsh Government then\nour Privacy Notice\n<https://gov.wales/about/welsh-government-privacy-notice/?lang=en> explains\nhow we use your information and the ways in which we protect your privacy.\nWe welcome receiving correspondence in Welsh. Any correspondence received\nin Welsh will be answered in Welsh and corresponding in Welsh will not lead\nto a delay in responding.\n"},{"id":"wb-72aaf0c1","subject":"Great house farm church view close Llandough nr penarth vale","name":"0000-00-00_great-house-farm-church-view-close-llandough-nr-penarth-vale_en_v0.0.0.00-3.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Council and local authority correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_great-house-farm-church-view-close-llandough-nr-penarth-vale_en_v0.0.0.00-3.txt","threadSlug":"great-house-farm-church-view-close-llandough-nr-penarth-vale","scopeTopics":["morgan"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["morgan"],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_great-house-farm-church-view-close-llandough-nr-penarth-vale_en_v0.0.0.00-3.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"not-recovered","text":null,"textNotice":"Original text not yet recovered: the archived source file for this record is missing. See sourceUrl."},{"id":"wb-a9e153bb","subject":"Great house farm Llandough nr penarth vale of glamorgan","name":"0000-00-00_great-house-farm-llandough-nr-penarth-vale-of-glamorgan_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Council and local authority correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_great-house-farm-llandough-nr-penarth-vale-of-glamorgan_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","threadSlug":"great-house-farm-llandough-nr-penarth-vale-of-glamorgan","scopeTopics":["morgan"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["morgan"],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_great-house-farm-llandough-nr-penarth-vale-of-glamorgan_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"not-recovered","text":null,"textNotice":"Original text not yet recovered: the archived source file for this record is missing. See sourceUrl."},{"id":"wb-a06bdc74","subject":"Great house farm Llandough wales","name":"0000-00-00_great-house-farm-llandough-wales_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_great-house-farm-llandough-wales_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","threadSlug":"great-house-farm-llandough-wales","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_great-house-farm-llandough-wales_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"not-recovered","text":null,"textNotice":"Original text not yet recovered: the archived source file for this record is missing. See sourceUrl."},{"id":"wb-28006c58","subject":"Great house farm Llandough","name":"0000-00-00_great-house-farm-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00-4.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_great-house-farm-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00-4.txt","threadSlug":"great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_great-house-farm-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00-4.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"[Recovered on 19 Oct 2026 from the original email in the family's Gmail, because this archive file was missing. Matched to this entry by its subject line; where several emails share a subject, the order within the group is not certain. Email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses are redacted.]\n\nSubject: Great House Farm, Llandough\nFrom: Requester\nDate: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 04:19 UTC\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nCustomer Relations / Planning Complaints Officer,\n\nI wish to make a formal complaint concerning the Vale of Glamorgan\nCouncil's Dear Customer Relations / Planning Complaints Officer,\n\nI wish to make a formal complaint concerning the Vale of Glamorgan\nCouncil's handling of Great House Farm, Llandough-juxta-Penarth,\nparticularly the circumstances surrounding its demolition in December 1988\nand the subsequent planning process.\n\n\nPlease register this as a formal complaint under the Council's corporate\ncomplaints procedure and provide me with a complaint reference number.\n\n\nThis complaint concerns the Council's administration and exercise of its\nplanning and heritage responsibilities. It is not intended to constitute an\nappeal against a properly made historic planning decision.\n\n\n1. Great House Farm, Llandough (Cardiff not Cowbridge) was recognised as a\nbuilding of historic importance\n\n\nThe planning and archaeological documentation concerning the site\nidentifies Great House Farm as a building of heritage significance.\n\n\nIn particular, the archaeological material identifies the former building\nas a \"county treasure\" associated with SMR 2038s, while earlier\narchitectural recording by RCAHMW describes a substantial historic\nfarmhouse with surviving historic fabric.\n\n\nThe later 1990 archaeological assessment states:\n\n\n«\"Owing to an ownership dispute and the subsequent sudden destruction of\nthe property in 1988 the building was incompletely understood.\"»\n\n\nI regard that statement as highly significant because it records,\ncontemporaneously with the subsequent planning process, that the building\nhad been destroyed suddenly and that its archaeological and architectural\nhistory had therefore not been adequately understood or recorded.\n\n\n2. The Council's apparent failure to take protective action before the 1988\ndemolition\n\n\nI ask the Council to investigate what action, if any, its planning and\nheritage officers took when Great House Farm was at risk of demolition.\n\n\nIn particular, I ask the Council to establish:\n\n\n1. Whether the Council knew that Great House Farm was at risk of demolition\nbefore December 1988.\n\n2. Whether the Council received any notification, complaint, application,\ncorrespondence or other warning concerning the proposed demolition.\n\n3. Whether the building had been considered for statutory listing or other\nstatutory protection.\n\n4. Whether consideration was given to the use of a Building Preservation\nNotice or any other available statutory or administrative mechanism capable\nof preventing or delaying demolition pending assessment.\n\n5. Whether the Council's heritage officers, planning officers or\narchaeological advisers were consulted.\n\n6. Whether any inspection or photographic/architectural record was\nundertaken immediately before or after the demolition.\n\n7. Why, given the building's recognised historic significance, the Council\ndid not secure a comprehensive standing-building record before the building\nwas lost.\n\n\nI am not asserting at this stage that a particular statutory power\nnecessarily had to be exercised. I am asking the Council to investigate\nwhat powers and procedures were available in 1988 and why they were, or\nwere not, used in this case.\n\n\n3. The \"sudden destruction\" of the building requires investigation\n\n\nThe 1990 archaeological assessment expressly attributes the incomplete\nunderstanding of the building to its \"subsequent sudden destruction ... in\n1988\".\n\n\nI therefore ask the Council to establish from its records:\n\n\n- the date on which it first became aware of the proposed or actual\ndemolition;\n\n- the identity of the relevant officers;\n\n- whether the Council received complaints or reports concerning demolition\nactivity;\n\n- whether any site visit took place;\n\n- whether any notice, warning, enforcement action, preservation action or\nother intervention was considered;\n\n- and whether the Council has retained records explaining why the\ndemolition was allowed to proceed.\n\n\nThis is particularly important because the building was subsequently\ntreated within the planning process as an already-cleared site.\n\n\n4. The 1989 planning application appears to contain a significant\nhistorical discrepancy\n\n\nThe outline planning application submitted by BP Properties Ltd in 1989\nstates, in the section asking which buildings were to be demolished:\n\n\n«\"None.\"»\n\n\nThe same application describes the site as \"Disused\" and the previous use\nas \"Not known\".\n\n\nThis requires explanation because the application followed the demolition\nof Great House Farm in December 1988 by approximately one year.\n\n\nI ask the Council to investigate whether its planning officers:\n\n\n- were aware of the former Great House Farm building when the 1989\napplication was processed;\n\n- knew when and how it had been demolished;\n\n- checked the accuracy of the applicant's statement that no buildings were\nto be demolished;\n\n- identified the former farmhouse in the planning history and supporting\ndocumentation;\n\n- and considered whether the demolition itself raised any planning,\nheritage or enforcement issue requiring investigation.\n\n\nI am particularly concerned that the planning process appears to have\nproceeded on the basis of a cleared site without first establishing the\ncircumstances in which the historic building had disappeared.\n\n\n5. Failure to create an adequate record of the lost building\n\n\nThe subsequent archaeological documentation states that the building was\n\"incompletely understood\".\n\n\nI ask the Council to explain what mitigation, recording or investigation it\nrequired in consequence.\n\n\nThe documentary evidence presently available indicates that substantial\nhistoric fabric and multiple phases of the building existed, yet the\nbuilding was destroyed without a complete standing-building survey having\nbeen secured.\n\n\nI therefore ask the Council to identify:\n\n\n- all surveys undertaken before demolition;\n\n- all surveys undertaken after demolition;\n\n- all archaeological or architectural advice obtained by the Council;\n\n- all photographs held by the Council;\n\n- all correspondence concerning the building's architectural or historic\nimportance;\n\n- and the Council's policy or practice in 1988 concerning the recording of\nsignificant unlisted historic buildings at risk.\n\n\n6. Subsequent planning treatment of the site\n\n\nFollowing demolition, the planning process concentrated heavily on\nbelow-ground archaeology, ultimately resulting in archaeological\ninvestigation of the cemetery and associated remains.\n\n\nI do not suggest that archaeological excavation was inappropriate. My\nconcern is that the destruction of the standing historic building appears\nto have been treated as a fait accompli rather than as an event requiring\naccountability and an explanation of how the Council's heritage\nresponsibilities had been discharged.\n\n\nThe later planning documentation should therefore be reviewed alongside the\nrecords relating to the 1988 demolition.\n\n\nIn particular, I ask the Council to identify precisely when the planning\nauthority first recorded the former farmhouse as demolished and what\nexplanation was placed on the planning file for its loss.\n\n\n7. Matters I ask the Council to investigate\n\n\nPlease investigate and provide a substantive response to the following\nquestions:\n\n\nA. What did Vale of Glamorgan Council know about the impending demolition\nof Great House Farm before December 1988?\n\n\nB. What statutory and non-statutory heritage protections were available to\nthe Council at the time?\n\n\nC. Were any of those mechanisms considered or discussed?\n\n\nD. Were any Building Preservation Notice, listing, enforcement, urgent\nworks, archaeological recording or other protective measures considered?\n\n\nE. Why was the building apparently destroyed without a comprehensive\nstanding-building survey?\n\n\nF. What records does the Council hold concerning the demolition itself?\n\n\nG. Why did the 1989 planning application proceed despite the recent\ndemolition of a building identified in the archaeological record as a\n\"county treasure\"?\n\n\nH. Why was the applicant's answer \"None\" given to the question concerning\nbuildings to be demolished, and did the Council investigate the apparent\ndiscrepancy?\n\n\nI. Did any Council officer consider whether the 1988 demolition constituted\na breach of planning control or otherwise required investigation?\n\n\nJ. Did the Council investigate whether the building had been demolished\nlawfully?\n\n\nK. What heritage assessment was undertaken before the subsequent\ndevelopment permissions were processed?\n\n\nL. What lessons has the Council subsequently incorporated into its\nprocedures for County Treasures and other locally significant historic\nbuildings?\n\n\n8. Records requested as part of the investigation\n\n\nPlease preserve and review all relevant records, including planning files,\nenforcement records, heritage files, committee papers, officer reports,\ninternal correspondence, site inspection notes, photographs, archaeological\ncorrespondence and consultation records concerning:\n\n\n- Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr / Cedfin / Cydfin;\n\n- SMR/HER reference 2038s / GGAT02038s;\n\n- the December 1988 demolition;\n\n- the 1989 outline planning application;\n\n- the 1990 archaeological assessment;\n\n- and the subsequent reserved matters/development applications.\n\n\nI would also ask that the Council identify any material which has been\ndestroyed or cannot now be located, together with the applicable\nretention/disposal record where one exists.\n\n\n9. Outcome sought\n\n\nI am seeking a formal investigation and a reasoned written explanation of\nthe Council's actions and omissions.\n\n\nIn particular, I ask the Council to determine:\n\n\n1. Whether its officers discharged the heritage and planning\nresponsibilities applicable at the time.\n\n2. Whether any failure occurred in relation to the identification,\nprotection, recording or investigation of Great House Farm.\n\n3. Whether the Council's handling of the 1989 planning application\nappropriately addressed the recent demolition of the building.\n\n4. Whether any relevant records have been lost or destroyed and, if so,\nwhen and under what retention arrangements.\n\n5. What procedural lessons arise from the case.\n\n\nI also request disclosure of the relevant non-exempt records identified\nduring the complaint investigation, where these are not otherwise\navailable. I recognise that requests for specific recorded information may\nneed to be dealt with separately under the appropriate information-access\nregime.\n\n\nI have only recently been able to examine and correlate the surviving\nplanning, archaeological and heritage documentation sufficiently to\nidentify these issues. I therefore ask that the Council accepts the\ncomplaint for investigation notwithstanding the age of the events concerned.\n\n\nPlease acknowledge receipt and confirm the complaint reference and the\nofficer responsible for its investigation.\n\n\nYours faithfully,\n\n\nSion Buckler of Great House Farm, Llandough-juxta-Penarth, particularly the\ncircumstances surrounding its demolition in December 1988 and the\nsubsequent planning process.\n\nPlease register this as a formal complaint under the Council's corporate\ncomplaints procedure and provide me with a complaint reference number.\n\nThis complaint concerns the Council's administration and exercise of its\nplanning and heritage responsibilities. It is not intended to constitute an\nappeal against a properly made historic planning decision.\n1. Great House Farm was recognised as a building of historic importance\n\nThe planning and archaeological documentation concerning the site\nidentifies Great House Farm as a building of heritage significance.\n\nIn particular, the archaeological material identifies the former building\nas a \"county treasure\" associated with SMR 2038s, while earlier\narchitectural recording by RCAHMW describes a substantial historic\nfarmhouse with surviving historic fabric.\n\nThe later 1990 archaeological assessment states:\n\n\"Owing to an ownership dispute and the subsequent sudden destruction of the\nproperty in 1988 the building was incompletely understood.\"\n\nI regard that statement as highly significant because it records,\ncontemporaneously with the subsequent planning process, that the building\nhad been destroyed suddenly and that its archaeological and architectural\nhistory had therefore not been adequately understood or recorded.\n2. The Council's apparent failure to take protective action before the 1988\ndemolition\n\nI ask the Council to investigate what action, if any, its planning and\nheritage officers took when Great House Farm was at risk of demolition.\n\nIn particular, I ask the Council to establish:\n\n   1. Whether the Council knew that Great House Farm was at risk of\n   demolition before December 1988.\n   2. Whether the Council received any notification, complaint,\n   application, correspondence or other warning concerning the proposed\n   demolition.\n   3. Whether the building had been considered for statutory listing or\n   other statutory protection.\n   4. Whether consideration was given to the use of a Building Preservation\n   Notice or any other available statutory or administrative mechanism capable\n   of preventing or delaying demolition pending assessment.\n   5. Whether the Council's heritage officers, planning officers or\n   archaeological advisers were consulted.\n   6. Whether any inspection or photographic/architectural record was\n   undertaken immediately before or after the demolition.\n   7. Why, given the building's recognised historic significance, the\n   Council did not secure a comprehensive standing-building record before the\n   building was lost.\n\nI am not asserting at this stage that a particular statutory power\nnecessarily had to be exercised. I am asking the Council to investigate\nwhat powers and procedures were available in 1988 and why they were, or\nwere not, used in this case.\n3. The \"sudden destruction\" of the building requires investigation\n\nThe 1990 archaeological assessment expressly attributes the incomplete\nunderstanding of the building to its \"subsequent sudden destruction ... in\n1988\".\n\nI therefore ask the Council to establish from its records:\n\n   - the date on which it first became aware of the proposed or actual\n   demolition;\n   - the identity of the relevant officers;\n   - whether the Council received complaints or reports concerning\n   demolition activity;\n   - whether any site visit took place;\n   - whether any notice, warning, enforcement action, preservation action\n   or other intervention was considered;\n   - and whether the Council has retained records explaining why the\n   demolition was allowed to proceed.\n\nThis is particularly important because the building was subsequently\ntreated within the planning process as an already-cleared site.\n4. The 1989 planning application appears to contain a significant\nhistorical discrepancy\n\nThe outline planning application submitted by BP Properties Ltd in 1989\nstates, in the section asking which buildings were to be demolished:\n\n\"None.\"\n\nThe same application describes the site as \"Disused\" and the previous use\nas \"Not known\".\n\nThis requires explanation because the application followed the demolition\nof Great House Farm in December 1988 by approximately one year.\n\nI ask the Council to investigate whether its planning officers:\n\n   - were aware of the former Great House Farm building when the 1989\n   application was processed;\n   - knew when and how it had been demolished;\n   - checked the accuracy of the applicant's statement that no buildings\n   were to be demolished;\n   - identified the former farmhouse in the planning history and supporting\n   documentation;\n   - and considered whether the demolition itself raised any planning,\n   heritage or enforcement issue requiring investigation.\n\nI am particularly concerned that the planning process appears to have\nproceeded on the basis of a cleared site without first establishing the\ncircumstances in which the historic building had disappeared.\n5. Failure to create an adequate record of the lost building\n\nThe subsequent archaeological documentation states that the building was\n\"incompletely understood\".\n\nI ask the Council to explain what mitigation, recording or investigation it\nrequired in consequence.\n\nThe documentary evidence presently available indicates that substantial\nhistoric fabric and multiple phases of the building existed, yet the\nbuilding was destroyed without a complete standing-building survey having\nbeen secured.\n\nI therefore ask the Council to identify:\n\n   - all surveys undertaken before demolition;\n   - all surveys undertaken after demolition;\n   - all archaeological or architectural advice obtained by the Council;\n   - all photographs held by the Council;\n   - all correspondence concerning the building's architectural or historic\n   importance;\n   - and the Council's policy or practice in 1988 concerning the recording\n   of significant unlisted historic buildings at risk.\n\n6. Subsequent planning treatment of the site\n\nFollowing demolition, the planning process concentrated heavily on\nbelow-ground archaeology, ultimately resulting in archaeological\ninvestigation of the cemetery and associated remains.\n\nI do not suggest that archaeological excavation was inappropriate. My\nconcern is that the destruction of the standing historic building appears\nto have been treated as a fait accompli rather than as an event requiring\naccountability and an explanation of how the Council's heritage\nresponsibilities had been discharged.\n\nThe later planning documentation should therefore be reviewed alongside the\nrecords relating to the 1988 demolition.\n\nIn particular, I ask the Council to identify precisely when the planning\nauthority first recorded the former farmhouse as demolished and what\nexplanation was placed on the planning file for its loss.\n7. Matters I ask the Council to investigate\n\nPlease investigate and provide a substantive response to the following\nquestions:\n\nA. What did Vale of Glamorgan Council know about the impending demolition\nof Great House Farm before December 1988?\n\nB. What statutory and non-statutory heritage protections were available to\nthe Council at the time?\n\nC. Were any of those mechanisms considered or discussed?\n\nD. Were any Building Preservation Notice, listing, enforcement, urgent\nworks, archaeological recording or other protective measures considered?\n\nE. Why was the building apparently destroyed without a comprehensive\nstanding-building survey?\n\nF. What records does the Council hold concerning the demolition itself?\n\nG. Why did the 1989 planning application proceed despite the recent\ndemolition of a building identified in the archaeological record as a\n\"county treasure\"?\n\nH. Why was the applicant's answer \"None\" given to the question concerning\nbuildings to be demolished, and did the Council investigate the apparent\ndiscrepancy?\n\nI. Did any Council officer consider whether the 1988 demolition constituted\na breach of planning control or otherwise required investigation?\n\nJ. Did the Council investigate whether the building had been demolished\nlawfully?\n\nK. What heritage assessment was undertaken before the subsequent\ndevelopment permissions were processed?\n\nL. What lessons has the Council subsequently incorporated into its\nprocedures for County Treasures and other locally significant historic\nbuildings?\n8. Records requested as part of the investigation\n\nPlease preserve and review all relevant records, including planning files,\nenforcement records, heritage files, committee papers, officer reports,\ninternal correspondence, site inspection notes, photographs, archaeological\ncorrespondence and consultation records concerning:\n\n   - Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr / Cedfin / Cydfin;\n   - SMR/HER reference 2038s / GGAT02038s;\n   - the December 1988 demolition;\n   - the 1989 outline planning application;\n   - the 1990 archaeological assessment;\n   - and the subsequent reserved matters/development applications.\n\nI would also ask that the Council identify any material which has been\ndestroyed or cannot now be located, together with the applicable\nretention/disposal record where one exists.\n9. Outcome sought\n\nI am seeking a formal investigation and a reasoned written explanation of\nthe Council's actions and omissions.\n\nIn particular, I ask the Council to determine:\n\n   1. Whether its officers discharged the heritage and planning\n   responsibilities applicable at the time.\n   2. Whether any failure occurred in relation to the identification,\n   protection, recording or investigation of Great House Farm.\n   3. Whether the Council's handling of the 1989 planning application\n   appropriately addressed the recent demolition of the building.\n   4. Whether any relevant records have been lost or destroyed and, if so,\n   when and under what retention arrangements.\n   5. What procedural lessons arise from the case.\n\nI also request disclosure of the relevant non-exempt records identified\nduring the complaint investigation, where these are not otherwise\navailable. I recognise that requests for specific recorded information may\nneed to be dealt with separately under the appropriate information-access\nregime.\n\nI have only recently been able to examine and correlate the surviving\nplanning, archaeological and heritage documentation sufficiently to\nidentify these issues. I therefore ask that the Council accepts the\ncomplaint for investigation notwithstanding the age of the events concerned.\n\nPlease acknowledge receipt and confirm the complaint reference and the\nofficer responsible for its investigation.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\n\n\n\nSion Buckler (Ret'd Sergeant)\n\nRoyal Signals Cyber/Electronic Warfare Professional and Life Member\n"},{"id":"wb-c7dcd537","subject":"Great house farm Llandough","name":"0000-00-00_great-house-farm-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00-5.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_great-house-farm-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00-5.txt","threadSlug":"great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_great-house-farm-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00-5.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"[Recovered on 19 Oct 2026 from the original email in the family's Gmail, because this archive file was missing. Matched to this entry by its subject line; where several emails share a subject, the order within the group is not certain. Email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses are redacted.]\n\nSubject: Great House Farm, Llandough\nFrom: Requester\nDate: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 19:32 UTC\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nMr Wigley,\n\nI am writing to make a *formal complaint to Cadw* concerning the handling\nand substantive response to my correspondence regarding Great House Farm\n(Tŷ Mawr), Llandough, and particularly Cadw's 1988 consideration of whether\nthe building should be protected by listing.\n\nI am making this complaint directly to Cadw at this stage because I\nconsider that Cadw should be given a proper opportunity to review and\naddress the matters before any escalation to the wider Welsh Government\ncomplaints process.\n\nFor the avoidance of doubt, I am *not making a further Freedom of\nInformation request* and I am not simply asking Cadw to produce records\nwhich it has already said no longer exist. I accept that Cadw has stated\nthat all records it currently holds concerning the 1988 listing decision\nhave been released.\n\nMy complaint concerns what can properly be established from the surviving\nevidence, whether the responses I have received have adequately addressed\nthat evidence, and whether the historical record can now be described\naccurately where the underlying file has been lost.\n1. The newly identified 1982 evidence\n\nI have now identified a 1 October 1982 consultation letter from the\nGlamorgan-Gwent Archaeological Trust concerning the East Vale Local Plan\nand the proposed Ty Maw Farm site, identified as *H2.1*.\n\nThe letter states:\n\n“Ty Maw Farm (H2.1) is designated as a three-acre residential site. As you\nwill see from the enclosed information the settlement at Llandough\ndeveloped considerably from farming and fishing... Recent excavations at\nLlandough by the Trust revealed early occupation, part of the villa and\nmedieval grange buildings. There remains a possibility that occupation\ncontinues to the north of the church; provision should be made, therefore,\nfor thorough investigative work prior to the development.”\n\nI do not suggest that this document, by itself, establishes that the\nstanding farmhouse was required to be listed.\n\nIts significance is narrower and more demonstrable: *six years before the\n1988 demolition, the archaeological and planning system already contained\nsite-specific information identifying Ty Maw Farm as a proposed development\nsite and recording substantial archaeological sensitivity in the immediate\nLlandough area, including possible continued occupation north of the\nchurch.*\n\nThat materially affects the historical context in which Cadw's 1988\nassessment needs to be understood.\n\nI therefore ask Cadw to address:\n\n   1.\n\n   Whether this 1982 information was held by, available to, or capable of\n   being obtained by Cadw in the period leading to the 1988 decision.\n   2.\n\n   Whether Cadw has any surviving evidence showing what archaeological,\n   planning or historic-building information was available to the inspectors\n   or decision-makers in 1988.\n   3.\n\n   Whether there was communication between Cadw and the relevant\n   planning/archaeological authorities concerning the proposed development of\n   Ty Maw Farm and the need for investigation or protection.\n   4.\n\n   If it is no longer possible to establish whether this evidence was\n   actually considered, whether Cadw will expressly acknowledge that\n   uncertainty rather than treating the absence of the historic file as\n   evidence that the relevant information was absent.\n\n2. The 1974 RCAHMW survey\n\nI have previously drawn Cadw's attention to the 26 April 1974 Royal\nCommission survey by H.J. Thomas at ST16817331.\n\nThat survey recorded Great House Farm as a historic building, including\nfeatures such as the late seventeenth-century character of the house, its\ncross-passage arrangement, adze-worked beams, ogee stops and barrel-vaulted\ncellar.\n\nCadw has stated that only two photographs from its relevant historic\nmaterial survive and that it cannot now establish whether the 1974 survey\nwas taken into account in the 1988 listing consideration.\n\nI accept that this may now be incapable of being proved from the surviving\nfile.\n\nHowever, there is an important distinction between:\n\n*“Cadw cannot now establish whether the information was considered”*\n\nand\n\n*“there is no evidence that the information was available or relevant.”*\n\nThe surviving RCAHMW material demonstrates that professional\nhistoric-building recording existed well before the 1988 decision.\n\nI therefore ask Cadw to address specifically whether that material was\navailable to Cadw through the inter-agency heritage system at the time and,\nif so, what can now be established about its availability.\n3. Cadw's own evidence that listing was actively being considered in 1988\n\nThe surviving contemporary material indicates that in December 1988 Cadw\nwas actively considering emergency protection/spot-listing of Great House\nFarm, including inspection and consultation concerning the building's\nhistoric significance.\n\nThat is important because it demonstrates that this was not retrospectively\ninvented as a heritage issue decades later. Cadw itself was considering\nprotection immediately before the building was demolished.\n\nCadw's present position is that the historical listing file no longer\nsurvives and that its retention arrangements at the time cannot now be\nestablished with certainty.\n\nI am not asking Cadw to speculate about what a destroyed file contained.\n\nI am asking Cadw to distinguish clearly between:\n\n   - what the surviving evidence positively establishes;\n   - what information was demonstrably available by 1988;\n   - what actions by Cadw in 1988 can still be established;\n   - what cannot now be established because the historic record has been\n   lost; and\n   - any conclusions Cadw considers cannot responsibly be drawn.\n\nThat distinction is particularly important when assessing whether the\nsurviving record provides a sufficiently reliable account of what happened.\n4. The planning authority and temporary protection\n\nI also wish to clarify the position concerning temporary protection.\n\nI am not suggesting that Cadw itself necessarily possessed the statutory\npower to serve a Building Preservation Notice. I understand that such\nprotection was a matter for the relevant local planning authority under the\nlegislation then in force.\n\nThe question for Cadw is therefore whether, during the 1988\nconsideration, *Cadw\nadvised, discussed or communicated with the relevant planning authority\nconcerning any available temporary protection mechanism or the need to\nprevent demolition pending consideration of listing.*\n\nIf Cadw cannot establish what was discussed because the records no longer\nsurvive, that should be stated expressly.\n5. The later description of Great House Farm as “a listed building”\n\nI have also identified later archaeological/planning documentation in which\nthe former site is described in terms including:\n\n“The site formerly contained Great House Farm (a listed building).”\n\nI am not asserting that this wording conclusively proves that Great House\nFarm was formally listed at that time.\n\nI am asking Cadw to explain, insofar as the surviving evidence permits,\nwhether there is any known provenance for that description and whether Cadw\nconsiders it accurate, erroneous, or simply impossible now to determine.\n\nThis is relevant because it illustrates the existence of potentially\nconflicting historical accounts concerning the building's protection status.\n6. The significance of the loss of the 1988 record\n\nCadw has understandably stated that the historic file cannot now be\nproduced.\n\nHowever, the loss of that file creates a historical evidential limitation;\nit should not automatically resolve questions arising from the surviving\nevidence in Cadw's favour.\n\nThe appropriate conclusion may simply be that some aspects of the 1988\ndecision *cannot now be reconstructed with certainty*.\n\nI would therefore like Cadw to consider whether its previous responses have\ndrawn that distinction sufficiently clearly.\n7. What I am asking Cadw to do\n\nI am asking Cadw to treat this as a formal complaint and provide a\nsubstantive response addressing the following:\n\n*A.* Whether the newly identified 1982 GGAT evidence materially changes\nCadw's understanding of the information environment surrounding Ty Maw Farm\nbefore the 1988 demolition.\n\n*B.* Whether Cadw can establish that the 1974 RCAHMW survey was available\nto it in 1988.\n\n*C.* Whether Cadw can establish what archaeological, planning and\nhistoric-building information was available to its officers when the\nbuilding was being considered for protection.\n\n*D.* What can now be established about Cadw's communications with the local\nplanning authority and other heritage bodies during the 1988 listing\nconsideration.\n\n*E.* Whether temporary protection was discussed or advised upon and, if not\ncapable of being established, whether Cadw will state that plainly.\n\n*F.* What can be established about the inspection of Great House Farm and\nthe ultimate listing decision.\n\n*G.* Whether Cadw accepts that the loss of the historic file means that\nsome aspects of the 1988 decision can no longer be reconstructed reliably.\n\n*H.* Whether Cadw considers the surviving documentary evidence, including\nthe 1974 survey and 1982 archaeological/planning evidence, should be\nreflected in any relevant contemporary heritage record or historical\naccount.\n\n*I.* Whether Cadw can explain the provenance, insofar as it is capable of\ndoing so, of the later description of the former site as containing “a\nlisted building.”\n\n*J.* Whether Cadw considers that its previous responses have adequately\ndistinguished between the absence of surviving evidence and evidence that\nsomething did not happen.\n8. Scope of this complaint\n\nI want to be clear that I am not asking Cadw to determine my family's wider\nproperty or title claims, nor am I asking Cadw to reach a predetermined\nconclusion about the historical ownership of the land.\n\nThose matters are separate.\n\nThis complaint is confined to *Cadw's own heritage-related role, the 1988\nprotection/listing process, the information that can now be established as\nhaving existed, and the adequacy and accuracy of Cadw's present explanation\nof that history.*\n\nI am also not asking Cadw to speculate about missing documents. On the\ncontrary, I ask that the absence of those documents be treated as an\nevidential limitation and expressly identified as such.\n9. Requested outcome\n\nI would like Cadw to:\n\n   1.\n\n   Register this as a formal complaint and provide a complaint reference.\n   2.\n\n   Have the matter reviewed by an appropriate person who has not simply\n   repeated the previous FOI position.\n   3.\n\n   Provide a point-by-point response to the matters above, taking account\n   of the newly identified 1982 evidence.\n   4.\n\n   Correct or clarify any part of Cadw's previous correspondence that is\n   found to have been incomplete, inaccurate or insufficiently qualified.\n   5.\n\n   State clearly which matters can now be established, which cannot, and\n   which remain uncertain because the historical records no longer survive.\n\nI regard that as a proportionate and reasonable opportunity for Cadw to\naddress the matter directly before I consider whether escalation through\nthe Welsh Government complaints process is necessary.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\n*Requester (Ret'd Sergeant)*\nRoyal Signals Cyber/Electronic Warfare Professional and Life Member\n"},{"id":"wb-e722246e","subject":"Great house farm Llandough","name":"0000-00-00_great-house-farm-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00-6.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_great-house-farm-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00-6.txt","threadSlug":"great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_great-house-farm-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00-6.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"[Recovered on 19 Oct 2026 from the original email in the family's Gmail, because this archive file was missing. Matched to this entry by its subject line; where several emails share a subject, the order within the group is not certain. Email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses are redacted.]\n\nSubject: Great House Farm, Llandough\nFrom: Requester\nDate: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 04:12 UTC\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nSirs/ Ma'ams,\n\nI need to correct a date I have used in earlier correspondence about Great\nHouse Farm / Tŷ Mawr, Llandough, titles WA231076 and WA240304.\n\nI had treated 1983 as the year the two titles were merged. That was wrong.\nHM Land Registry’s own letter of 23 March 2026 (Lisa Griffiths, ref\nN610CGV) separates the two acts.\n1982–83 was first registration of two live titles, not a merger.\n30 November 1982: application to first-register WA231076. The Court of\nAppeal later treated BP Properties Ltd as registered proprietors of “the\nfarm” from November 1982.\n23 February 1983: application to first-register a second title, WA240304.\n26 March 1983: Mary Williams died. On the family’s case, notice of those\napplications did not reach us until after her death, while the family was\nin mourning. She had no window in which to object. The family was not in a\nposition to use any window that arrived after that.\nThose two titles remained separate through first instance.\n24 July 1986: Hollis J. Both titles were still live. The judgment language\ntreats Great House Farm as one holding. It had not been one holding on the\nregister. On the family’s case it had not been one holding since the 1877\nsplit: Parcel A (house, buildings, garden/yard and about 10 acres, the\nThomas/Williams line) and Parcel B (the eastern fields retained by Bute and\nlater WGR/BP).\n2 February 1987: application to amalgamate the two titles. WA240304 was\nthen closed and registration continued under WA231076. That is the merge.\nIt was made after the 1986 ruling and before the Court of Appeal gave\njudgment on 31 July 1987.\nSo 1983 is silent first registration of a second title while Mary was still\nin Parcel A, or had only just died. 1987 is the administrative merger,\ncarried out while the appeal was still open.\nThis was not a dispute about a single land parcel that BP already owned.\nMary was on Parcel A, which the family says she owned, with occupation on\nthat house parcel running in the family from at least 1667. BP, and WGR\nbefore it from 1949, were the neighbouring interest on Parcel B. From 1949\nonwards the family’s case is that those neighbours ran a decades-long\nseries of tenancy offers, “whole farm” wording, possession orders and the\n1974 letters to make her occupation of her own parcel look permissive, and\nto make both parcels look like one estate they had acquired. They never\nproduced a conveyance of Parcel A. What they had was an unfounded claim\nthat her occupancy was permissive.\nPlease treat any earlier letter from me that said the titles merged in 1983\nas corrected by this email. The dates that matter are:\n1982/83 — first registration of two titles, without the occupier being\nheard;\n24 July 1986 — first-instance judgment describing one farm while two titles\nstill stood;\n2 February 1987 — amalgamation, before the appeal ended;\n31 July 1987 — Court of Appeal.\nYours faithfully,\n\nSion Buckler\nRefs where relevant: N610CGV; 260919-6726246; titles WA231076 and WA240304.\nSion Buckler (Ret'd Sergeant)\ngreathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com\n"},{"id":"wb-391312ed","subject":"Great house farm Llandough","name":"0000-00-00_great-house-farm-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00-7.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_great-house-farm-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00-7.txt","threadSlug":"great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_great-house-farm-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00-7.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"[Recovered on 19 Oct 2026 from the original email in the family's Gmail, because this archive file was missing. Matched to this entry by its subject line; where several emails share a subject, the order within the group is not certain. Email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses are redacted.]\n\nSubject: Great House Farm, Llandough\nFrom: Requester\nDate: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 17:29 UTC\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nChris,\n\nI flew back, I'm even at Mamgus old house in Dinas from time to time.\n\nHere's a website AI generated and maintains which articulates my grievance.\nhttps://greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com\n\nI shared with the charity who own the woodland. They're immidiate response\nwas to do what everyone has always done to us and ignore our ownership\nclaim and assertion and acknowledge someone else as the owner.\n\nMany and myself are still investigating. But in a nutshell a synthetic\nparcel appears to have been created, based on enough years of attempts to\nmake our occupancy appear permissive, so that the land registry can accept\nthe story. So 1974 licence letters was also a failed attempt and premis my\nfamily rejected, in a long line of failed attempts spanning back to at\nleast 1949.\n\nThe registration then made the 1987 ruling possible. And that ruling is\nwhat the land registry depend on to justify its decision to allow BP to\nregister a synthetic mimic of our parcel in their names, without notifying\nus until long after the appeal of that registration had ended. In which my\nnan conveniently died. Equally that 1987 ruling depends on the 1983\nregistration of our land parcel (or mimic synthetic version of it, built\nfrom a falsehood of a chain that didn't have root title and was not and\nnever was successful attempts to make our occupancy permissive.\n\nFurthermore the \"whole farm\" being treated as one parcel, was not merged\nback into one parcel (a synthetic version of our parcel too, not ours),\nuntil after the 1986/1987 case.\n\nSo my nan Mary Williams didn't get a fair opportunity to reject the licence\nletter in 1974 between it being offered and quashing the 1962 possession\norder for our land parcel. Which itself was never enforced and arguably not\nlegally enforceable as it was based on ambuguity from the 1955 order which\nmade our occupancy of our parcel simply appear permissive with concealment\nof the fact my nans resistence was due to her ownership claim. And\nconcealment of the fact \"whole farm\" excluded our parcel of land and was\nnot in the 1939 tenancy agreement. So not conveyed to Western Ground Rents\nin 1938.\n\nFurther to that my nans ownership claim, even though unable to be proven\ndue her title being stolen in 1950/52, still means having not paid anyone\nfor anything on her parcel since 1928 she will have already qualified for\nadverse possession by 1940.\n\nWe didn't get fair opportunity to appeal the 83 registration either. We\nalso didn't get opportunity to appeal CADWs decision to not list our county\ntreasure 800 year old home as a grade II before BP demolished it at 4am the\nnext day.\n\nThen the council didn't hold them to any account when they returned in 1989\nto get planning permission. The council are also under the microscope for\nmany failures. Failure to issue a demolition block order. Also two annexes\nare missing from those planning docs which are local historic societies\ntestemony to petition demolition citing our old ancestral homes\nsignificants (home since 1667 and my birth home I remind you).\n\nPlease just look at the evidence.\nhttps://greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com\n\nMy family are living like hostages under terrorists and have done for 38\nyears. It's not acceptable. This demonic realm and spell has to be fought\noff.\n\nDo what you can please. Even if its a small prayer for us. I thank you in\nadvance.\n\nSee attached a few images from my military service. I served this country.\nI'm still trying to here. I left my career with full honours. My last\nemployer in the Vale Council. I hope this betrayal will end. I'm more than\ndisappointed in local authories here, who don't appear any less hostile to\nmy family and our interest in our land in Llandough.\n\nI can't play the role of hostage or prisoner of war under this regime. My\nfamily are mostly all women and children, I am not. It's an untenable\nsituation Chris. The Senedd should have at least done a public enquiry of\nthis.\n\nRegards,\n\nSion Hywel Buckler (Ret'd Sergeant)\nRoyal Signals Cyber/ Electronic Warfare Professional and Life Member\n[phone redacted]\n"},{"id":"wb-9e7b904b","subject":"Great house farm williams buckler family Llandough vale of g","name":"0000-00-00_great-house-farm-williams-buckler-family-llandough-vale-of-g_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_great-house-farm-williams-buckler-family-llandough-vale-of-g_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","threadSlug":"great-house-farm-williams-buckler-family-llandough-vale-of-g","scopeTopics":["morgan"],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":["morgan"],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_great-house-farm-williams-buckler-family-llandough-vale-of-g_en_v0.0.0.00-1.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"[Recovered on 19 Oct 2026 from the original email in the family's Gmail, because this archive file was missing. Matched to this entry by its subject line; where several emails share a subject, the order within the group is not certain. Email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses are redacted.]\n\nSubject: Great House Farm, Williams/ Buckler Family, Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan\nFrom: Huggins, Helen\nDate: Fri, 12 Jun 2026 09:19 UTC\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nDear Requester,\n\nThank you for your emails, requesting information on the above mentioned site. I can confirm, I have done a search on the site and the two applications that may be of relevance, for the information you are seeking are noted below.  Please be advised that this information is available via our