sting public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"not-recovered","text":null,"textNotice":"Original text not yet recovered: the archived source file for this record is missing. See sourceUrl."},{"id":"wb-8a1a6c1d","subject":"Great house farm","name":"0000-00-00_great-house-farm_en_v0.0.0.00-4.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_great-house-farm_en_v0.0.0.00-4.txt","threadSlug":"great-house-farm","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_great-house-farm_en_v0.0.0.00-4.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"not-recovered","text":null,"textNotice":"Original text not yet recovered: the archived source file for this record is missing. See sourceUrl."},{"id":"wb-cd5242d4","subject":"Hey kiro here s more on BP v Buckler 1987","name":"0000-00-00_hey-kiro-here_s-more-on-bp-vs-buckler-1987_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Court and legal correspondence","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/text/0000-00-00_hey-kiro-here_s-more-on-bp-vs-buckler-1987_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","threadSlug":"hey-kiro-here-s-more-on-bp-v-buckler-1987","scopeTopics":[],"recordType":"related-case-source","tags":[],"sourcePath":"text/0000-00-00_hey-kiro-here_s-more-on-bp-vs-buckler-1987_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","classificationNote":"Indexed from the public Wayback text catalogue by case-related filename and/or existing public catalogue tags. The classification is a finding aid, not proof that every record is an email; inspect the original source before relying on metadata. Filename dates are not asserted as sent dates without confirmation from the source.","textStatus":"available","text":"This is a chat I had with kimi.com ai\n\nWhat UK Courts Will Reject\n\nThe language of \"legal fiction,\" \"identity fraud,\" and refusing to engage with a name that \"isn't hers\" sits within the freeman-on-the-land / strawman territory that UK courts have consistently dismissed as pseudo-legal nonsense. Judges have held repeatedly that a person's legal name is not a separate corporate entity, and that arguing otherwise is an abuse of process. If she was lawfully married to Frederick Buckler, then she was Mrs Buckler as a matter of fact, regardless of what other names or titles she may also have held.\nSo the argument that \"Mrs Buckler\" was a fabricated legal fiction BP conjured into existence will not fly in any English or Welsh court.\n---------------------------------------------------------------\n\nWhat Might Have Legal Merit\n\nHowever, underneath that framing is a legitimate procedural observation that could be powerful if you have documents to support it:\n\n1. The 1987 Case Was Only About Possession, Not Title\n\nYou are factually correct: BP Properties Ltd v Buckler was a possession action, not a claim for a declaration of title.\n\nThe court was not asked to trace the chain of title from Bute Estate → Western Ground Rents → BP and determine whether that chain was valid. It was only asked whether the Buckler family had acquired title by adverse possession.\nBecause the defendant (the son) argued adverse possession and lost on the licence point, the court never adjudicated whether BP held good title against the true owner. It simply held that the son had not acquired title by squatter's rights. This leaves a gap: if Mary Williams (or her heirs) held an independent, superior title that predated BP's paper title, that claim was never litigated and never decided.\n\n2. If She Held Title as \"Mrs Williams,\" the Licence May Have Been with the Wrong Person\n\nUnder the Married Women's Property Act 1882, a married woman could hold real property independently of her husband. If Mary Williams inherited superior title from her father in her own name — as Mary Williams or Mrs Williams — and BP's 1974 letters were addressed only to \"Mrs Buckler\" (the wife of a squatter/occupier), then BP may have granted a licence to the wrong legal person.\nThe Court of Appeal held that the 1974 letters created a unilateral licence that stopped adverse possession time running.\n\nBut a licence is a personal permission. If it was granted to Mrs Buckler (as BP perceived her — a trespasser's wife) and not to Mrs Williams (as the independent title-holder), then:\n\n- The person who actually held the equitable or legal title never received the licence.\n- The person who received the licence may not have had authority to accept on behalf of the title-holder.\n- Therefore, the true owner's possession may never have been converted into a licence.\n\nThis is not a \"legal fiction\" argument. It is a misnomer / wrong party argument: the litigation finger was pointed at the occupier's wife, not at the independent title-holder.\n\n3. The 1962 Possession Order May Have Been Against the Wrong Party\n\nYou mentioned that in 1962 Western Ground Rents obtained a possession order against Mary Williams.\n\nIf that is true — if the 1962 order named Mary Williams — but the 1974 letters and the 1987 case proceeded against Mrs Buckler, then there is a discontinuity. The person against whom the original possession order was obtained was not the same person BP later treated as their licensee. A possession order against Mary Williams does not automatically bind Mrs Buckler, and a licence granted to Mrs Buckler does not automatically affect Mary Williams' independent rights.\n\n4. Possession Does Not Prove Title\n\nA possession order is not a declaration that the claimant has the best title in the kingdom. It only determines who has the immediate right to possession against the defendant. If the defendant never raised a competing title, the court does not investigate the claimant's root of title.\nThis means BP's 1987 victory was narrow: they proved they had a better right than a squatter who failed to complete 12 years of adverse possession. They did not prove they had better title than Mary Williams (or her heirs) if she held an independent pre-existing title that was never put before the court.\n---------------------------------------------------------------\n\nThe Evidence You Would Need\n\nFor this to be more than a procedural curiosity, you must produce:\nTable\n\nEvidence Needed\tWhy It Matters\nMary Williams' marriage certificate (or lack thereof)\tIf she never married Frederick Buckler, \"Mrs Buckler\" was indeed the wrong person. If she did marry him, she was Mrs Buckler as well as Mrs Williams, and the name argument weakens.\nDeeds or wills showing Mary Williams inherited title from her father\tEstablishes the independent title chain you describe.\nThe 1962 possession order\tShows who was actually named as defendant. If it was Mary Williams, and later proceedings were against Mrs Buckler, that's a procedural break.\nThe 1974 letters\tShows exactly what name BP used. If they wrote to \"Mrs Buckler\" and not \"Mary Williams,\" and if she held title independently, the licence may be defective.\nAny document showing Mr Buckler conveyed her title to Western Ground Rents without her consent\tThis is the fraud that would allow you to challenge the root of BP's title.\n---------------------------------------------------------------\n\nThe Bottom Line\n\nYour instinct is correct in one crucial respect: BP never got a ruling that they held good title against the true owner. They only got a ruling that they could evict a man who failed to prove adverse possession. If Mary Williams held an independent, superior title that was never presented in 1987, that title was not adjudicated away.\nBut to reopen this, you cannot rely on \"legal fiction\" or \"identity fraud\" theories. You must instead argue:\n\n- Misnomer / wrong party: The proceedings targeted Mrs Buckler the occupier, not Mrs Williams the title-holder.\n- Fraud in the root of title: Western Ground Rents (or Mr Buckler) conveyed title that belonged to Mary Williams without her knowledge or consent.\n- Rectification of the register: Under the Land Registration Act 2002, the register can be corrected where a registered proprietor obtained registration through fraud or where a prior estate was omitted.\n\nIf you have the deeds proving Mary Williams held independent title, and you can show BP's chain derives from a fraudulent conveyance of her property, you would apply to the Land Registry for rectification — not adverse possession. That is a completely different claim from the one that failed in 1987, and it might not be barred by the previous judgment because it was never raised.\nBut without those deeds, the court will treat BP v Buckler as the final word on who possesses Great House Farm.\n\nReference\n\n'\n\nConsider this other agents responses.\n\nSion Buckler\n\nSent from [Proton Mail](https://proton.me/mail/home) for Android."},{"id":"wb-259bc0fb","subject":"Hywel you re now a member of george carroll for Llandough","name":"0000-00-00_hywel-you_re-now-a-member-of-george-carroll-for-llandough_en_v0.0.0.00.txt","date":null,"dateStatus":"date-not-stated-or-filename-placeholder","category":"Case correspondence / related source","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"DATRO Wayback public text archive","sourceUrl":