-CrossTenant-OriginalAttributedTenantConnectingIp: TenantId=770a2450-0227-4c62-90c7-4e38537f1102;Ip=[40.81.153.120];Helo=[smtp.int.prod.defra.gov.uk]\nX-MS-Exchange-CrossTenant-AuthSource:\n        DU2PEPF00028D0C.eurprd03.prod.outlook.com\nX-MS-Exchange-CrossTenant-AuthAs: Anonymous\nX-MS-Exchange-CrossTenant-FromEntityHeader: HybridOnPrem\nX-MS-Exchange-Transport-CrossTenantHeadersStamped: AM7PR05MB6789\nX-OrganizationHeadersPreserved: AM7PR05MB6789.eurprd05.prod.outlook.com\nX-CrossPremisesHeadersFilteredByDsnGenerator:\n        AM7PR05MB6789.eurprd05.prod.outlook.com\n\n\nFinal-Recipient: rfc822;[email redacted]\nAction: failed\nStatus: 5.4.12\nDiagnostic-Code: smtp;554 5.4.12 SMTP; Hop count exceeded - possible mail loop detected on message id <[email redacted]>\nRemote-MTA: dns;PRDDWPEXCSR1602.DT2.local\n\n\nWarning: The sender of this message could not be validated. Please use caution when opening any message content such as attachments or links\n\n\n\nDear Defra Information Rights team,\n\nThis is a request under the Freedom of Information Act 2000 and, where\nthey apply, the Environmental Information Regulations 2004. It\nconcerns Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr, Llandough-juxta-Penarth, Vale of\nGlamorgan (then Glamorgan), a farm occupied by my relatives, the\nWilliams family, and the records of the Ministry of Agriculture and\nFisheries (MAF) and its Glamorgan committees, to which Defra is the\nsuccessor. It is separate from my request 2026/1425E, which is on a\ndifferent subject. It is linked to my enquiries with The National\nArchives, CAS-347296-M0V4Y1 and CAS-344046-K1S1R2.\n\nWestern Ground Rents Ltd (company no. 00342092), to which the Bute\nEstate conveyed its interest by a conveyance dated 25 December 1938,\nobtained a possession order on 4 July 1955. I am trying to establish\nfrom the records how the farm was dealt with between 1936 and 1955,\nwho took each decision, and on what record.\n\nPlease tell me whether Defra holds, and provide, the following for\nGreat House Farm for 1936–1955:\n1. any National Farm Survey or other MAF farm record for the holding,\nincluding the occupier, the owner and the acreage recorded;\n2. any record of the Glamorgan War Agricultural Executive Committee\n(or its successor, the Glamorgan Agricultural Executive Committee)\nabout the farm: inspections, gradings, cultivation or other\ndirections, supervision orders, or any possession, requisition or\ntermination of tenancy, with the date and the committee or officer (by\nrole) who made each decision;\n3. any application for, or decision on, the Minister's consent to the\noperation of a notice to quit the holding (Agricultural Holdings Act\n1948), with the date, the decision and who made it;\n4. any compensation claim or payment for the land under the\nCompensation (Defence) Act 1939 or the Agriculture Acts;\n5. any correspondence with Western Ground Rents Ltd, the Bute Estate\nor their agents about the farm, or about the possession order of 4\nJuly 1955 and the land it covered.\n\nIf records in scope have been transferred to The National Archives\n(for example MAF 32, the National Farm Survey, or the MAF 80 series of\ncommittee minutes), please give the reference rather than treating\nthat part as not held. If any record in scope was destroyed, please\ngive the retention or disposal schedule that applied and, where\nrecorded, the date and the authority for its destruction. If any part\nis held by the Welsh Government rather than by Defra, please say so.\n\nPlease reply within 20 working days, by 17 November 2026. If you hold\nno information for any part, please say so; if anything is withheld,\nplease give the exemption relied on. If any part cannot be answered as\nframed, I would be grateful for advice and assistance (section 16\nFOIA; regulation 9 EIR).\n\nPlease also make sure that no record relating to Great House Farm / Tŷ\nMawr is destroyed while these enquiries are open.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\nRequester \n\nhttps://eur03.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fgreathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com%2F&data=05%7C02%7Cfoi%40defra.gov.uk%7Cee77c817209c4b17e01c08df2e41a56d%7C770a245002274c6290c74e38537f1102%7C0%7C0%7C639280532226468250%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C80000%7C%7C%7C&sdata=ScBey3wVDLeuJGYoMo6kcQeTKANoQl2UhT0I6TIlNUk%3D&reserved=0"},{"id":"gm-1a156b9a5d8a0e20","subject":"Preservation and disclosure request — Church View Close / Great House Farm, Llandough — titles WA231076 / WA240304","name":"Preservation and disclosure request — Church View Close / Great House Farm, Llandough — titles WA231076 / WA240304","date":"2026-10-20T00:32:49Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a156b9a5d8a0e20.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0e3b180d95f228-preservation-and-disclosure-request-church-view-close-great-house-farm-llandough-titles-wa231076-wa240304","scopeTopics":["persimmon"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["persimmon"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0e3b180d95f228","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"persimmon","name":"Persimmon Homes"}],"refs":["WA231076","WA240304"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a156b9a5d8a0e20.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Persimmon Homes Legal Department,<br><br>Church View Close, Llandough (formerly Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr) – title WA231076 (and former title WA240304)<br><br>I wrote to you on 27 September 2026 asking that records about this land be preserved. I have received only automatic replies, so I would be grateful if you could confirm that the preservation request has been passed to the right team.<br><br>I now write with a short, specific request for records. As I understand it, BP Properties Ltd transferred the land to Ideal Homes in 1993, Church View Close was then built, and Persimmon Homes (Wales) Ltd is now the registered proprietor of WA231076. I am trying to establish, from the records, what was known and decided about the land when it was bought and developed, and by whom.<br><br>Please tell me whether Persimmon (including as successor to Ideal Homes Wales) holds any of the following, and provide copies or a description:<br>1. the 1993 transfer from BP Properties Ltd, its plan, and the contract or conditions of sale, including any warranty, indemnity or condition given by BP about title, occupation, the 1987 Court of Appeal decision in BP Properties Ltd v Buckler, or the demolition of the farmhouse in 1988;<br>2. the report on title, pre-contract enquiries and requisitions on title (and BP's replies) for the purchase, so far as they mention the occupation of the farmhouse, the 1987 decision or any claim to the land;<br>3. any title indemnity or defective-title insurance taken out for the purchase or the development, with the date and the risk it covered;<br>4. the internal approval for the purchase (for example, the land acquisition or board approval), with its date and the role of the person who approved it;<br>5. any archaeological report or correspondence about the 1994 excavation on the site (published by Holbrook and Thomas in Medieval Archaeology 49, 2005), and any licence or record for the removal, reburial or retention of human remains found during the development, with where they were reinterred or deposited;<br>6. any planning condition, section 52 or section 106 agreement, or correspondence with the Vale of Glamorgan Council about village green VG41 or the woodland, linked to planning applications 89/01396/OUT and 92/00671/RES;<br>7. if any of these records have been destroyed or passed to another party (for example, solicitors or a successor company), the date, the retention policy applied and who holds them now.<br><br>I understand that Persimmon is not subject to the Freedom of Information Act 2000, so this is a request for your voluntary help; I am not making any allegation against Persimmon. If you hold nothing, a short note saying so would be equally helpful. A reply by 17 November 2026 would be much appreciated.<br><br>Yours faithfully,<br><br>Requester <br><br>https://greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com\n<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div class=\"gmail_attr\">On Sun, Sep 27, 2026 at 05:39 PM, Requester &lt;<a href=\"[email redacted]>[email redacted]</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"auto\"><p>Sirs/Madam,</p><p>I write concerning the land now comprising Church View Close, Llandough, formerly Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr, including title WA231076 and former title WA240304.</p><p>Persimmon Homes (Wales) Ltd is the current registered proprietor of WA231076. Persimmon is therefore a present link in the conveyancing chain arising from the registration of this land in BP Properties Ltd&#39;s name in 1982–83, BP&#39;s subsequent transfer to Ideal Homes in 1993, and the later development history.</p><p>I am conducting a documented investigation into the legitimacy of that title chain. This is not a speculative historical enquiry. The issues arise from identified Land Registry, conveyancing, court, planning and public-authority records, and from specific documentary gaps which remain unresolved.</p><p>The central issue is the historic identity and ownership of the land.</p><p>The evidence presently assembled indicates two historically distinct parcels:</p><p>• Parcel A: the farmhouse, garden, buildings, yard and associated woodland, which the Williams family maintains was acquired out of the Bute estate through the Daniel Thomas transaction and subsequently became the family&#39;s freehold interest; and</p><p>• Parcel B: the eastern agricultural fields, whose subsequent passage from Bute to Western Ground Rents and then to BP is not presently disputed by the family.</p><p>The significance of that distinction is that later documents repeatedly use the expression “whole farm”, although the surviving 1939 tenancy plan is understood to show the eastern fields only. The 1938 Bute-to-Western Ground Rents conveyance is therefore a material title-chain question: what estate was actually capable of being conveyed by Bute in 1938, and did that conveyance include Parcel A?</p><p>There is further evidence requiring examination of the period from 1949 to 1974, including repeated attempts to characterise Mary&#39;s occupation of the farmhouse parcel as tenancy or permission; the loss of the family&#39;s title papers in about 1950–52; the 1955 possession order expressed in “whole farm” terms but enforced everywhere except the farmhouse and garden; the separate 1962 possession proceedings concerning the farmhouse and garden; Mary&#39;s express assertion of ownership; and the 1974 proceedings, which were adjourned part-heard and were never finally adjudicated on the family&#39;s historic ownership claim.</p><p>The Court of Appeal&#39;s decision in BP Properties Ltd v Buckler [1987] EWCA Civ 2 subsequently concerned possession, limitation and the legal effect of the October 1974 letters. The judgment records Mary&#39;s asserted ownership and the absence of the documents she said supported it. The present investigation therefore distinguishes carefully between what that judgment decided concerning possession and limitation and the separate question of whether the documentary root and extent of WGR/BP&#39;s title to Parcel A were ever actually established.</p><p>That distinction becomes particularly important in relation to first registration.</p><p>HM Land Registry records show BP&#39;s first-registration applications for WA231076 and WA240304 in 1982–83. The surviving registration material also identifies a 31 December 1969 conveyance from Western Ground Rents to BP Pension Trust as a root of title, but that conveyance is not presently included in the 30 July 2026 schedule of documents surviving on the relevant first-registration files. The 1975 conveyance and plan, the 1982–83 registration material, the extent of the two titles and their later amalgamation therefore require direct reconciliation against the underlying documentary title.</p><p>There is also an unresolved procedural issue concerning notice and opportunity to object during the 1982–83 first-registration process. Mary Williams remained in occupation of the farmhouse. The family evidence recorded on the evidence index is that notification was not received until after her death on 26 March 1983. I am obtaining the primary notice, service, application and registration records to establish exactly what occurred.</p><p>The matters under investigation consequently include possible registration mistake, procedural unfairness, material non-disclosure and, depending upon what the primary records establish, possible fraud or deliberate concealment. There are also unresolved public-authority record questions involving HM Land Registry, court administration and other authorities. These are identified as evidential questions, not asserted as established findings against Persimmon or any individual.</p><p>Persimmon&#39;s position is therefore directly relevant because its present registered title derives from the historic chain now being examined.</p><p>Please provide a substantive response to the following:</p><ol><li>\n<p><strong>Title chain:</strong> What documentary chain does Persimmon say establishes its predecessor&#39;s title to the whole of WA231076, including the farmhouse, garden and associated land now comprising Church View Close?</p>\n</li><li>\n<p><strong>BP / Ideal Homes due diligence:</strong> What title, acquisition and due-diligence documents were supplied to and reviewed by Persimmon in connection with the acquisition from Ideal Homes, including deeds, plans, title abstracts, searches, requisitions, Land Registry correspondence and enquiries concerning the extent of WA231076/WA240304?</p>\n</li><li>\n<p><strong>Earlier title documents:</strong> Does Persimmon hold, or has it ever held, copies of the 1969 WGR-to-BP Pension Trust conveyance, the 1975 BP Pension Trust-to-BP Properties conveyance and plan, the 1982–83 first-registration application/bundle, or documents explaining the inclusion of the farmhouse parcel within the registered extent?</p>\n</li><li>\n<p><strong>1996 correspondence:</strong> Does Persimmon retain the correspondence from March 1996 with HM Land Registry concerning WA231076, identified in the surviving title chronology as the last dealing on the file? If so, please provide a copy, subject to any properly applicable exemption or privilege.</p>\n</li><li>\n<p><strong>Parcel A:</strong> Has Persimmon ever reviewed the underlying documentary title sufficiently to satisfy itself that the farmhouse/garden parcel was actually comprised in the title acquired by BP, rather than being brought within the registered extent through the later “whole farm” description?</p>\n</li><li>\n<p><strong>Present position:</strong> Having regard to the matters above, does Persimmon maintain that its title to the farmhouse/garden parcel is supported by an identifiable documentary root independent of the possession and limitation history considered in BP Properties Ltd v Buckler?</p>\n</li></ol><p>If Persimmon maintains that the documentary title to Parcel A is established, please identify the instrument or instruments on which that position depends and the land those instruments actually comprise.</p><p>I am seeking voluntary or pre-action disclosure of non-privileged material where possible. If any requested document is withheld, please identify the document or category concerned and the basis upon which it is withheld.</p><p><strong>Preservation notice</strong></p><p>Please treat this correspondence as formal notice to preserve all documents and electronically stored information within Persimmon&#39;s possession, custody or control that may be relevant to the title, acquisition, registration, extent, occupation, planning or development of this land.</p><p>This includes historical material inherited from BP Properties, Ideal Homes or any other predecessor, together with deeds, conveyances, plans, title abstracts, searches, due-diligence files, acquisition memoranda, legal/property correspondence, Land Registry correspondence, planning records and communications with public authorities.</p><p>Please ensure that no routine retention, deletion, destruction, disposal or overwriting process removes potentially relevant material. This preservation request includes electronic files, archived mailboxes and other electronically stored information where applicable.</p><p>The public evidence index and supporting record are maintained at:</p><p><a href=\"https://greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com/\">https://greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com/</a></p><p>Nothing in this correspondence abandons, limits or prejudices any claim concerning title, registration, fraud, deliberate concealment, procedural unfairness, public-authority involvement, limitation, reopening of historic proceedings, rectification/alteration of the register, compensation or reparations. All rights and remedies are expressly reserved.</p><p>This correspondence is not a claim form and does not constitute service of proceedings.</p><p>Please acknowledge receipt and refer this correspondence to Persimmon&#39;s legal/property team. I request a substantive response addressing the questions above.</p><p>Yours faithfully,</p><p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</p><p>Requester\n</p></div>\n</div></blockquote></div>"},{"id":"gm-1a156b90b31efeca","subject":"Re: Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough — request for information under FOIA 2000 / EIR 2004","name":"Re: Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough — request for information under FOIA 2000 / EIR 2004","date":"2026-10-20T00:32:09Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a156b90b31efeca.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0c1e36871ebfd1-great-house-farm-t-mawr-llandough-request-for-information-under-foia-2000-eir-2004","scopeTopics":["llandough"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["llandough"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0c1e36871ebfd1","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"llandough","name":"Llandough Community Council"}],"refs":["EIR 2004"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a156b90b31efeca.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Mr Egan,<br><br>New, narrower request under the Freedom of Information Act 2000 (and the Environmental Information Regulations 2004 where they apply)<br><br>Thank you for your response of 11 October 2026. This is a new, narrower request. It is separate from my request of 13 October for an internal review of that response, and it is not meant to reopen or pre-empt the review.<br><br>Your response explained that the cost estimate was driven by the need to search \"all minutes of the council and its committees for a period of 14 years\", and by the \"significant amount of documents on file from the period 2002-2016 where minutes are in documents rather than published on the website or in archives\". To keep within the limit, this request does not ask you to search the council's minutes or minute books for any period. It asks only for any file or record the council itself holds that is specifically about the following, concerning Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr and the land beside Leckwith Road, Llandough:<br><br>1. village green VG41 beside Leckwith Road: any record of its registration in 1970 (including whether the council, or the parish council before it, applied for or supported the registration, and who signed on its behalf), and any later proposal to extend or alter it;<br>2. any comment, objection or consultation response by the council on planning applications 89/01396/OUT and 92/00671/RES, and any correspondence about them;<br>3. any correspondence between the council and the Vale of Glamorgan Council, BP Properties Ltd or its agents, or the developers of Church View Close about Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr;<br>4. where the council's own records from before 2002 are now held (for example, which archive, under what reference, and when they were deposited), and any record of the destruction or disposal of council files on the matters in items 1 to 3, with the date and who authorised it.<br><br>If you consider that any item would still exceed the cost limit, please tell me which, and I will narrow it further (section 16). If the council holds nothing for an item, please say so. If any records are held by someone else, please say where.<br><br>Please reply within 20 working days, by 17 November 2026.<br><br>Yours faithfully,<br><br>Requester <br><br>https://greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com\n<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div class=\"gmail_attr\">On Tue, Oct 13, 2026 at 01:07 AM, Requester &lt;<a href=\"[email redacted]>[email redacted]</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"auto\"><div dir=\"auto\"><p>Mr Egan,</p>\n<p>Thanks for your email of 11 October 2026. I acknowledge your refusal of my information request and also the other matters raised in my correspondence “will be considered at the October meeting of the council”.</p>\n<p>Not to sound like a stickler but your email doesn&#39;t identify the date, time, venue, public-access arrangements or agenda for that meeting, or direct me to a published notice containing those particulars. This omission prevents me from verifying when the promised consideration is to take place, whether the relevant matters are scheduled for consideration, and whether I can attend or monitor the proceedings. Please provide those particulars immediately or identify where the Council has published them.</p>\n<p>Below is my position, formal requests an internal review, and further request for recorded information, and constitutes a subject access request for my personal data. Please record and process each part under the applicable procedure. None is intended to withdraw, narrow or replace the others.</p>\n<h3>1. Position concerning Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr</h3>\n<p>As stated previously and repeatedly, my research of this case concerns the legitimacy of my families historic title and the treatment of Great House Farm, its buildings and associated land. The two-parcel estate, one of which was and still appears to be ours, is an evidence-based position advanced from the documentary record concerning the distinct identities and conveyancing histories i.e. Parcel A—the farmhouse, farm buildings, garden, yard,  associated woodland and what is now church view close green —and Parcel B: the eastern agricultural land, east of our Parcel A and what was WGR and BP&#39;s parcel and the focus on their possession. My argument is, again - that our parcel of land and home since 1667, was treated as one and the same as Parcel B under the blanket term &quot;whole farm&quot;. </p><p>Our Parcel A (or a synthetic mimic of it, born from a long running and  rejected string of attempts to make our occupancy appear permissive) was stealthfully registered by BP in 1983 and 