gory":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a1363b3030d0960.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a136032cedc03e6-automatic-reply-foi-1264-26-non-personal-operational-records-concerning-great-house-farm-t-mawr-llandough-november-decem","scopeTopics":["swp"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["swp"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a136032cedc03e6","direction":"received","from":"Freedom of Information","institutions":[{"id":"swp","name":"South Wales Police"}],"refs":["FOI1264"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a1363b3030d0960.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Diolch am gysylltu â'r Tîm Rhyddid Gwybodaeth yn Heddlu De Cymru. Dyma neges i gadarnhau ein bod wedi cael eich gohebiaeth. Peidiwch ag ymateb i'r neges e-bost hon.\n\nRydym yn delio â nifer mawr o geisiadau ar hyn o bryd, yn ogystal ag ôl-groniad. Er ein bod yn gwneud pob ymdrech i ymateb i geisiadau o fewn y terfyn amser o 20 diwrnod gwaith, gall fod oedi wrth ddelio â rhai o'r ceisiadau o ganlyniad i hyn.\n\nMae'n ddrwg gennym am unrhyw anghyfleustra a achoswyd.\n\nThank you for contacting the Freedom of Information (FOI) Team at South Wales Police. This message is to confirm that we have received your correspondence. Please do not reply to this email.\n\nPlease be advised that we are currently dealing with a high volume of requests as well as a backlog. Whilst we make every effort to respond to all requests within the 20 working day deadline, there may be delays in the handling some of the requests as a result.\n\nWe apologise for any inconvenience caused.\nYmateb nad yw'n Argyfwng\\ Non-Emergency Response\n\nOes angen i chi siarad â'r heddlu ond nid oes angen ymateb brys arnoch? Gallwch roi gwybod am fater drwy ein gwefan https://www.heddlu-de-cymru.police.uk, neu ffonio 101. Gallwch ddefnyddio 101 i roi gwybod am achosion nad ydynt yn rhai brys i unrhyw heddlu yng Nghymru a Lloegr. Mewn argyfwng, ffoniwch 999 bob amser.\n\nDo you need to speak to the police but don’t require an emergency response? You can make a report via our website https://www.south-wales.police.uk, or call 101. 101 can be used to report a non-emergency to any force in Wales and England. In an emergency always dial 999.\n\nCymorth Iaith Gymraeg\\Welsh Language Support\n\nMae Heddlu De Cymru yn croesawu derbyn gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg a Saesneg. Byddwn yn ateb gohebiaeth a dderbynnir yn Gymraeg yn Gymraeg ac ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi.\n\nSouth Wales Police welcomes receiving correspondence in Welsh and English. Any correspondence received in Welsh will be answered in Welsh and corresponding in Welsh will not lead to a delay in responding.\n\nYmwadiad Heddlu De Cymru\\South Wales Police Disclaimer\n\nGallai defnyddio neu ddatgelu’r neges e-bost hon heb awdurdod fod yn anghyfreithlon. Gallwch weld ein datganiad cyfrinachedd yn: https://www.heddlu-de-cymru.police.uk/cy-GB/heddluoedd/heddlu-de-cymru/ardaloedd/amdanom-ni/amdanom-ni/datganiad-cyfrinachedd-e-bost/\".\n\nUnauthorised use or disclosure of this email may be unlawful. View our confidentiality statement at https://www.south-wales.police.uk/police-forces/south-wales-police/areas/about-us/about-us/email-confidentiality-statement/."},{"id":"gm-1a1363a8a12eaf81","subject":"Re: FOI 1264/26 - non-personal operational records concerning Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr, Llandough, November–December 1988","name":"Re: FOI 1264/26 - non-personal operational records concerning Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr, Llandough, November–December 1988","date":"2026-10-13T17:06:08Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a1363a8a12eaf81.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a12fa8ff526287b-foi-1264-26-non-personal-operational-records-concerning-great-house-farm-t-mawr-llandough-november-december-1988","scopeTopics":["swp"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["swp"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a12fa8ff526287b","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"swp","name":"South Wales Police"}],"refs":["1264/26","FOI1264"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a1363a8a12eaf81.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Further to my clarification sent today, for the avoidance of doubt: William\nBeverly Buckler, my late father, died at Llandough Hospital on 15 May 1992.\nInformation about a deceased person is not personal data under the Data\nProtection Act 2018, s.3(2), so recorded information about him falls within\nthe scope of this FOI request.\n\nI add, as a specific search lead, that he was reportedly taken from his\nhospital bed at Llandough Hospital on 3 December 1988 to be charged with\nassaulting two bailiffs, criminal damage, and wanton or furious driving.\n\nRequester \n\n\n\n\nOn Tue, 13 Oct 2026, 17:05 Requester, <[email redacted]> wrote:\n\n> Dear Mr Byrne,\n>\n> Thank you for your email of 13 October 2026 concerning Freedom of\n> Information request 1264/26.\n>\n> I provide the clarification and additional search identifiers below. The\n> purpose is to help South Wales Police identify and locate existing recorded\n> information responsive to my original request. I am not seeking to extend\n> the period covered, require the creation of new information, or ask the\n> Force to investigate or determine the underlying property dispute.\n>\n> For clarity, the requested period remains 1 November to 31 December 1988\n> inclusive. My request remains for existing, non-personal recorded\n> information only. Personal data is excluded from this FOI request and is\n> being pursued separately through the appropriate subject-access route.\n>\n> 1. Location and alternative site names\n>\n> The property concerned is Great House Farm, also referred to as Great\n> House and Tŷ Mawr / Ty Mawr, at Llandough-juxta-Penarth in the Vale of\n> Glamorgan, adjoining St Dochdwy’s Church.\n>\n> The following identifiers may assist searches of occurrence, incident,\n> control-room, deployment or other operational records:\n>\n> - Site: Great House Farm / Great House / Tŷ Mawr / Ty Mawr.\n> - Locality: Llandough-juxta-Penarth, Vale of Glamorgan, near Penarth.\n> - Approximate National Grid Reference: ST 1681 7331; the less precise\n> reference ST 168 733 may also appear.\n> - Distinctive context: the family farmhouse and associated buildings that\n> were the subject of the possession dispute and were demolished in December\n> 1988.\n>\n> The locality description is intentional: I refer to\n> Llandough-juxta-Penarth, not the separate Llandough near Cowbridge.\n>\n> 2. Names and organisational identifiers\n>\n> The principal individual identifier is:\n>\n> William Beverly Buckler, also referred to in contemporary reporting as\n> William Buckler, Bill Buckler or Billy Buckler.\n>\n> Relevant corporate or property-related search terms include:\n>\n> - B.P. Properties Ltd / BP Properties Ltd, the company identified in the\n> property litigation and associated with the demolition.\n> - BP Pension Trust Ltd and Western Ground Rents Ltd, which appear in the\n> wider historical property context. I include these names only as\n> supplementary indexing terms; I do not assert that either organisation was\n> involved in, or communicated with police about, an event during the\n> requested period.\n> - Vale of Glamorgan Borough Council and South Glamorgan County Council,\n> the relevant local-government names for the period.\n> - Court enforcement personnel, bailiffs and any public authority or\n> official recorded as contacting, corresponding with, or requesting\n> assistance from the police in relation to the property.\n>\n> The following case identifiers may also be useful if records were indexed\n> by litigation or proceedings rather than by address:\n>\n> - B.P. Properties Ltd v Buckler [1987] EWCA Civ 2.\n> - Buckler v United Kingdom, European Commission of Human Rights\n> application 14464/88, registered on 12 December 1988.\n>\n> These case identifiers are offered to assist retrieval, not as an\n> assertion that the Force necessarily held records under either reference.\n>\n> I am supplying names and case references as historical search identifiers.\n> I am not asking for personal data about living individuals. Where a\n> responsive record contains personal data, please consider whether\n> non-personal information can be disclosed separately or after appropriate\n> redaction.\n>\n> 3. Chronology and specific search leads\n>\n> The following events provide context for identifying any corresponding\n> police records. They are search leads drawn from historical reporting, the\n> heritage record and the family archive; they should not be treated as proof\n> of police involvement.\n>\n> 29–30 November 1988 — possession and eviction\n>\n> The historical chronology records possession on 29 November 1988. A\n> contemporaneous South Wales Echo report dated 30 November, titled “Angry\n> scenes as farmer evicted” and attributed to Peter Bibby, describes the\n> eviction sequence and reports that bailiffs entered the property and that\n> the roof of an outbuilding had already been partly removed. The chronology\n> also associates demolition of outbuildings with 30 November.\n>\n> There is therefore a date distinction between the recorded possession date\n> and the newspaper’s account of the eviction. I cannot presently reconcile\n> the dates. Please search across both 29 and 30 November, rather than\n> treating either as an exclusive date for the relevant incident.\n>\n> 2–5 December 1988 — court proceedings and proposed access to belongings\n>\n> Contemporary reporting describes further court proceedings in the days\n> before the farmhouse was demolished, including an arrangement under which\n> access to collect belongings was reportedly permitted subject to conditions\n> and pending a further hearing.\n>\n> The precise terms and procedural details should be verified from the\n> original records rather than inferred from press summaries. This chronology\n> may nevertheless help identify any police liaison, requests for assistance,\n> public-order concerns or communications concerning access to the premises.\n>\n> 6 December 1988 — demolition\n>\n> The heritage record identifies 6 December 1988 as the demolition date. The\n> archived historical chronology reports that a demolition team arrived in\n> the early hours and that the farmhouse was demolished that morning.\n>\n> I emphasise that the reported arrival time is not evidence that police\n> were present at that time. I am asking whether responsive police records\n> exist, not inviting an assumption that an attendance or deployment took\n> place.\n>\n> 9–13 December 1988 — discrepancy requiring verification\n>\n> A South Wales Echo report dated 10 December 1988 appears to state that\n> William Buckler was released on bail the previous day, which would be 9\n> December.\n>\n> Separately, a notebook attributed to William Buckler and retained in the\n> family archive contains an account referring to police having “locked me up\n> for 2 weeks” and to a search involving family antiques and the taking of\n> documents. The entry is a family-held historical account; it is not, by\n> itself, independently verified proof of detention, a search, seizure or the\n> precise dates involved.\n>\n> If the notebook’s reference to two weeks from 29 November is literal, it\n> would point to a date around 13 December. That does not readily reconcile\n> with the newspaper’s reported release date of 9 December. I do not ask the\n> Force to accept either account in preference to its own records. The\n> discrepancy is a reason to check the relevant dates and any associated\n> records.\n>\n> Accordingly, please include 29 November to 13 December 1988 when checking\n> for any potentially relevant custody, detention, search, property-seizure,\n> operational or cross-referenced occurrence records, while retaining the\n> original overall FOI period of 1 November to 31 December 1988. I seek only\n> information falling within the original request’s non-personal scope,\n> including any separable non-personal operational information.\n>\n> 4. Earlier event offered solely as an indexing lead\n>\n> The archived historical chronology also describes a separate incident at\n> Great House Farm on 29 April 1988, involving bailiffs and a police presence\n> during a stand-off. The account attributes to South Wales Police the\n> position that the officers were there to prevent a breach of the peace,\n> rather than to assist bailiffs.\n>\n> That event is outside the requested period. I am not asking you to\n> disclose records from April 1988 under this request, and I do not suggest\n> that the earlier incident establishes police attendance in November or\n> December. I mention it only in case any occurrence number, location index\n> or related reference appearing in the records from the requested period\n> would be easier to identify against the same property or dispute.\n>\n> 5. Records sought and search categories\n>\n> With those identifiers in mind, the records sought remain the existing\n> records described in my original request, insofar as they fall within the\n> period and are held by South Wales Police. In particular, please search for\n> responsive:\n>\n> 1. Incident and control-room records: incident or occurrence logs, calls\n> for assistance, control-room entries, dispatch records and associated\n> incident references relating to the site or the events described above.\n>\n> 2. Police attendance and operational records: deployment, duty,\n> operational or event logs recording any police attendance, requested\n> attendance, planned response or recorded decision concerning the site, its\n> occupants, bailiffs, enforcement personnel or demolition activity.\n>\n> 3. Communications and liaison: recorded correspondence, messages,\n> telephone notes or other communications between police and local-authority\n> officers, court or enforcement personnel, the landowner or developer, their\n> representatives, or other public bodies concerning the property or events\n> during the requested period.\n>\n> 4. Requests for police assistance and public-order matters: records\n> concerning requests for assistance, access to or removal from the premises,\n> the enforcement of possession, complaints, reported disorder, obstruction,\n> public-order concerns, or action proposed or taken in connection with the\n> eviction or demolition.\n>\n> 5. Custody, search and property-related records: to the extent that\n> responsive non-personal information is held and falls within the original\n> scope, any operational references or separable non-personal information\n> concerning a possible detention, premises search, or taking or retention of\n> documents or other property during the relevant dates.\n>\n> 6. Records-management information: where a responsive record is no longer\n> held, any existing record-transfer, retention, destruction or disposal\n> entry sufficient to establish whether the record existed and, if recorded,\n> what became of it.\n>\n> I am not asserting that each category necessarily generated a record. The\n> purpose of the list is to identify the likely record types and help make\n> the search practicable.\n>\n> If the Force’s systems use different terminology, location codes,\n> occurrence categories or operational record series, please use the\n> equivalent available indexing terms where reasonably possible.\n>\n> 6. Historical reference material\n>\n> For convenience, the relevant historical chronology and case background\n> are available here:\n>\n> - Archived Great House Farm Wiki homepage:\n> https://web.archive.org/web/20260927001835/https://greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com/\n> - Archived demolition chronology:\n> https://web.archive.org/web/20261006042123/https://greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com/demolition-1988/\n> - B.P. Properties Ltd v Buckler case page:\n> https://greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com/bp-properties-v-buckler/\n>\n> These pages are provided as contextual aids and sources of possible search\n> terms, not as substitutes for the Force’s own records or as claims that any\n> particular police record exists.\n>\n> 7. Confirmation of scope and statutory timetable\n>\n> I trust the above supplies the clarification you require to identify and\n> locate the information requested.\n>\n> Please confirm receipt and whether this clarification is sufficient for\n> the Force to proceed with its searches. Please also confirm the date from\n> which you calculate the applicable 20-working-day response period and the\n> resulting response deadline.\n>\n> For the avoidance of doubt:\n>\n> - the date range remains 1 November–31 December 1988 inclusive;\n> - the request remains confined to existing recorded information;\n> - personal data remains excluded from this FOI request and is being\n> pursued separately through the appropriate subject-access route;\n> - the historical accounts and discrepancies above are search leads, not\n> demands that the Force accept disputed facts; and\n> - I am not asking the Force to reach conclusions about ownership, the\n> validity of court orders, the conduct of any party, or the truth of any\n> allegation.\n>\n> Thank you for your assistance.\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n> Requester \n> "},{"id":"gm-1a1362dbffa34acd","subject":"Ty Mawr Llandoche","name":"Ty Mawr Llandoche","date":"2026-10-13T16:51:58Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a1362dbffa34acd.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a1362dbffa34acd-ty-mawr-llandoche","scopeTopics":["itv"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["itv"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a1362dbffa34acd","direction":"received","from":"Owain Meredith","institutions":[{"id":"itv","name":"ITV Archive"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a1362dbffa34acd.json","textStatus":"available","text":"SHwmae Requester\nMae eich cais wedi ei ddanfon ataf i\n\nOdych chi ar gael am sgwrs ar y ffon?\n\n\n\nOwain Meredith\n\nArchifydd ITV Cymru\nArchivist ITV Wales\n\n[phone redacted]\n[phone redacted]\n\n-- \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nITV plc (Registration No. 4967001) (ITV) is incorporated in England \nand Wales with its registered office at ITV White City, 201 Wood Lane, \nLondon [postcode redacted]. Please visit www.itv.com   for further \ninformation.\n\nThis email and any attachments are intended solely for the \naddressee and may contain confidential, copyrighted and/or privileged \ninformation.  If you have received this message in error, please notify us \nand delete it.  Recipients should scan the email and any attachments for \ntheir own protection. \n\nThis email does not conclude a binding agreement. \nIf there is commercial information in this email it remains subject to \ncontract and management approval. The views or opinions presented in this \nemail do not necessarily represent those of ITV.\n\nWe reserve the right to \nmonitor all e-mail communications, as permitted by applicable law and \nregulations.\n\nFor details of how we process personal data, please see the \nprivacy notices for each of ITV's business areas at \nhttp://www.itv.com/groupprivacy"},{"id":"gm-1a136032cedc03e6","subject":"Automatic reply: FOI 1264/26 - non-personal operational records concerning Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr, Llandough, November–December 1988","name":"Automatic reply: FOI 1264/26 - non-personal operational records concerning Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr, Llandough, November–December 1988","date":"2026-10-13T16:05:36Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a136032cedc03e6.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a136032cedc03e6-automatic-reply-foi-1264-26-non-personal-operational-records-concerning-great-house-farm-t-mawr-llandough-november-decem","scopeTopics":["swp"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["swp"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a136032cedc03e6","direction":"received","from":"Freedom of Information","institutions":[{"id":"swp","name":"South Wales Police"}],"refs":["FOI1264"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a136032cedc03e6.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Diolch am gysylltu â'r Tîm Rhyddid Gwybodaeth yn Heddlu De Cymru. Dyma neges i gadarnhau ein bod wedi cael eich gohebiaeth. Peidiwch ag ymateb i'r neges e-bost hon.\n\nRydym yn delio â nifer mawr o geisiadau ar hyn o bryd, yn ogystal ag ôl-groniad. Er ein bod yn gwneud pob ymdrech i ymateb i geisiadau o fewn y terfyn amser o 20 diwrnod gwaith, gall fod oedi wrth ddelio â rhai o'r ceisiadau o ganlyniad i hyn.\n\nMae'n ddrwg gennym am unrhyw anghyfleustra a achoswyd.\n\nThank you for contacting the Freedom of Information (FOI) Team at South Wales Police. This message is to confirm that we have received your correspondence. Please do not reply to this email.\n\nPlease be advised that we are currently dealing with a high volume of requests as well as a backlog. Whilst we make every effort to respond to all requests within the 20 working day deadline, there may be delays in the handling some of the requests as a result.\n\nWe apologise for any inconvenience caused.\nYmateb nad yw'n Argyfwng\\ Non-Emergency Response\n\nOes angen i chi siarad â'r heddlu ond nid oes angen ymateb brys arnoch? Gallwch roi gwybod am fater drwy ein gwefan https://www.heddlu-de-cymru.police.uk, neu ffonio 101. Gallwch ddefnyddio 101 i roi gwybod am achosion nad ydynt yn rhai brys i unrhyw heddlu yng Nghymru a Lloegr. Mewn argyfwng, ffoniwch 999 bob amser.\n\nDo you need to speak to the police but don’t require an emergency response? You can make a report via our website https://www.south-wales.police.uk, or call 101. 101 can be used to report a non-emergency to any force in Wales and England. In an emergency always dial 999.\n\nCymorth Iaith Gymraeg\\Welsh Language Support\n\nMae Heddlu De Cymru yn croesawu derbyn gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg a Saesneg. Byddwn yn ateb gohebiaeth a dderbynnir yn Gymraeg yn Gymraeg ac ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi.\n\nSouth Wales Police welcomes receiving correspondence in Welsh and English. Any correspondence received in Welsh will be answered in Welsh and corresponding in Welsh will not lead to a delay in responding.\n\nYmwadiad Heddlu De Cymru\\South Wales Police Disclaimer\n\nGallai defnyddio neu ddatgelu’r neges e-bost hon heb awdurdod fod yn anghyfreithlon. Gallwch weld ein datganiad cyfrinachedd yn: https://www.heddlu-de-cymru.police.uk/cy-GB/heddluoedd/heddlu-de-cymru/ardaloedd/amdanom-ni/amdanom-ni/datganiad-cyfrinachedd-e-bost/\".\n\nUnauthorised use or disclosure of this email may be unlawful. View our confidentiality statement at https://www.south-wales.police.uk/police-forces/south-wales-police/areas/about-us/about-us/email-confidentiality-statement/."},{"id":"gm-1a13602f78dfa3d1","subject":"RE: FOI 1264/26 - non-personal operational records concerning Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr, Llandough, November–December 1988","name":"RE: FOI 1264/26 - non-personal operational records concerning Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr, Llandough, November–December 1988","date":"2026-10-13T16:05:26Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a13602f78dfa3d1.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a12fa8ff526287b-foi-1264-26-non-personal-operational-records-concerning-great-house-farm-t-mawr-llandough-november-december-1988","scopeTopics":["swp"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["swp"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a12fa8ff526287b","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"swp","name":"South Wales Police"}],"refs":["1264/26","FOI1264"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a13602f78dfa3d1.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Mr Byrne,\n\nThank you for your email of 13 October 2026 concerning Freedom of Information request 1264/26.\n\nI provide the clarification and additional search identifiers below. The purpose is to help South Wales Police identify and locate existing recorded information responsive to my original request. I am not seeking to extend the period covered, require the creation of new information, or ask the Force to investigate or determine the underlying property dispute.\n\nFor clarity, the requested period remains 1 November to 31 December 1988 inclusive. My request remains for existing, non-personal recorded information only. Personal data is excluded from this FOI request and is being pursued separately through the appropriate subject-access route.\n\n1. Location and alternative site names\n\nThe property concerned is Great House Farm, also referred to as Great House and Tŷ Mawr / Ty Mawr, at Llandough-juxta-Penarth in the Vale of Glamorgan, adjoining St Dochdwy’s Church.\n\nThe following identifiers may assist searches of occurrence, incident, control-room, deployment or other operational records:\n\n- Site: Great House Farm / Great House / Tŷ Mawr / Ty Mawr.\n- Locality: Llandough-juxta-Penarth, Vale of Glamorgan, near Penarth.\n- Approximate National Grid Reference: ST 1681 7331; the less precise reference ST 168 733 may also appear.\n- Distinctive context: the family farmhouse and associated buildings that were the subject of the possession dispute and were demolished in December 1988.\n\nThe locality description is intentional: I refer to Llandough-juxta-Penarth, not the separate Llandough near Cowbridge.\n\n2. Names and organisational identifiers\n\nThe principal individual identifier is:\n\nWilliam Beverly Buckler, also referred to in contemporary reporting as William Buckler, Bill Buckler or Billy Buckler.\n\nRelevant corporate or property-related search terms include:\n\n- B.P. Properties Ltd / BP Properties Ltd, the company identified in the property litigation and associated with the demolition.\n- BP Pension Trust Ltd and Western Ground Rents Ltd, which appear in the wider historical property context. I include these names only as supplementary indexing terms; I do not assert that either organisation was involved in, or communicated with police about, an event during the requested period.\n- Vale of Glamorgan Borough Council and South Glamorgan County Council, the relevant local-government names for the period.\n- Court enforcement personnel, bailiffs and any public authority or official recorded as contacting, corresponding with, or requesting assistance from the police in relation to the property.\n\nThe following case identifiers may also be useful if records were indexed by litigation or proceedings rather than by address:\n\n- B.P. Properties Ltd v Buckler [1987] EWCA Civ 2.\n- Buckler v United Kingdom, European Commission of Human Rights application 14464/88, registered on 12 December 1988.\n\nThese case identifiers are offered to assist retrieval, not as an assertion that the Force necessarily held records under either reference.\n\nI am supplying names and case references as historical search identifiers. I am not asking for personal data about living individuals. Where a responsive record contains personal data, please consider whether non-personal information can be disclosed separately or after appropriate redaction.\n\n3. Chronology and specific search leads\n\nThe following events provide context for identifying any corresponding police records. They are search leads drawn from historical reporting, the heritage record and the family archive; they should not be treated as proof of police involvement.\n\n29–30 November 1988 — possession and eviction\n\nThe historical chronology records possession on 29 November 1988. A contemporaneous South Wales Echo report dated 30 November, titled “Angry scenes as farmer evicted” and attributed to Peter Bibby, describes the eviction sequence and reports that bailiffs entered the property and that the roof of an outbuilding had already been partly removed. The chronology also associates demolition of outbuildings with 30 November.\n\nThere is therefore a date distinction between the recorded possession date and the newspaper’s account of the eviction. I cannot presently reconcile the dates. Please search across both 29 and 30 November, rather than treating either as an exclusive date for the relevant incident.\n\n2–5 December 1988 — court proceedings and proposed access to belongings\n\nContemporary reporting describes further court proceedings in the days before the farmhouse was demolished, including an arrangement under which access to collect belongings was reportedly permitted subject to conditions and pending a further hearing.\n\nThe precise terms and procedural details should be verified from the original records rather than inferred from press summaries. This chronology may nevertheless help identify any police liaison, requests for assistance, public-order concerns or communications concerning access to the premises.\n\n6 December 1988 — demolition\n\nThe heritage record identifies 6 December 1988 as the demolition date. The archived historical chronology reports that a demolition team arrived in the early hours and that the farmhouse was demolished that morning.\n\nI emphasise that the reported arrival time is not evidence that police were present at that time. I am asking whether responsive police records exist, not inviting an assumption that an attendance or deployment took place.\n\n9–13 December 1988 — discrepancy requiring verification\n\nA South Wales Echo report dated 10 December 1988 appears to state that William Buckler was released on bail the previous day, which would be 9 December.\n\nSeparately, a notebook attributed to William Buckler and retained in the family archive contains an account referring to police having “locked me up for 2 weeks” and to a search involving family antiques and the taking of documents. The entry is a family-held historical account; it is not, by itself, independently verified proof of detention, a search, seizure or the precise dates involved.\n\nIf the notebook’s reference to two weeks from 29 November is literal, it would point to a date around 13 December. That does not readily reconcile with the newspaper’s reported release date of 9 December. I do not ask the Force to accept either account in preference to its own records. The discrepancy is a reason to check the relevant dates and any associated records.\n\nAccordingly, please include 29 November to 13 December 1988 when checking for any potentially relevant custody, detention, search, property-seizure, operational or cross-referenced occurrence records, while retaining the original overall FOI period of 1 November to 31 December 1988. I seek only information falling within the original request’s non-personal scope, including any separable non-personal operational information.\n\n4. Earlier event offered solely as an indexing lead\n\nThe archived historical chronology also describes a separate incident at Great House Farm on 29 April 1988, involving bailiffs and a police presence during a stand-off. The account attributes to South Wales Police the position that the officers were there to prevent a breach of the peace, rather than to assist bailiffs.\n\nThat event is outside the requested period. I am not asking you to disclose records from April 1988 under this request, and I do not suggest that the earlier incident establishes police attendance in November or December. I mention it only in case any occurrence number, location index or related reference appearing in the records from the requested period would be easier to identify against the same property or dispute.\n\n5. Records sought and search categories\n\nWith those identifiers in mind, the records sought remain the existing records described in my original request, insofar as they fall within the period and are held by South Wales Police. In particular, please search for responsive:\n\n1. Incident and control-room records: incident or occurrence logs, calls for assistance, control-room entries, dispatch records and associated incident references relating to the site or the events described above.\n\n2. Police attendance and operational records: deployment, duty, operational or event logs recording any police attendance, requested attendance, planned response or recorded decision concerning the site, its occupants, bailiffs, enforcement personnel or demolition activity.\n\n3. Communications and liaison: recorded correspondence, messages, telephone notes or other communications between police and local-authority officers, court or enforcement personnel, the landowner or developer, their representatives, or other public bodies concerning the property or events during the requested period.\n\n4. Requests for police assistance and public-order matters: records concerning requests for assistance, access to or removal from the premises, the enforcement of possession, complaints, reported disorder, obstruction, public-order concerns, or action proposed or taken in connection with the eviction or demolition.\n\n5. Custody, search and property-related records: to the extent that responsive non-personal information is held and falls within the original scope, any operational references or separable non-personal information concerning a possible detention, premises search, or taking or retention of documents or other property during the relevant dates.\n\n6. Records-management information: where a responsive record is no longer held, any existing record-transfer, retention, destruction or disposal entry sufficient to establish whether the record existed and, if recorded, what became of it.\n\nI am not asserting that each category necessarily generated a record. The purpose of the list is to identify the likely record types and help make the search practicable.\n\nIf the Force’s systems use different terminology, location codes, occurrence categories or operational record series, please use the equivalent available indexing terms where reasonably possible.\n\n6. Historical reference material\n\nFor convenience, the relevant historical chronology and case background are available here:\n\n- Archived Great House Farm Wiki homepage: https://web.archive.org/web/20260927001835/https://greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com/\n- Archived demolition chronology: https://web.archive.org/web/20261006042123/https://greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com/demolition-1988/\n- B.P. Properties Ltd v Buckler case page: https://greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com/bp-properties-v-buckler/\n\nThese pages are provided as contextual aids and sources of possible search terms, not as substitutes for the Force’s own records or as claims that any particular police record exists.\n\n7. Confirmation of scope and statutory timetable\n\nI trust the above supplies the clarification you require to identify and locate the information requested.\n\nPlease confirm receipt and whether this clarification is sufficient for the Force to proceed with its searches. Please also confirm the date from which you calculate the applicable 20-working-day response period and the resulting response deadline.\n\nFor the avoidance of doubt:\n\n- the date range remains 1 November–31 December 1988 inclusive;\n- the request remains confined to existing recorded information;\n- personal data remains excluded from this FOI request and is being pursued separately through the appropriate subject-access route;\n- the historical accounts and discrepancies above are search leads, not demands that the Force accept disputed facts; and\n- I am not asking the Force to reach conclusions about ownership, the validity of court orders, the conduct of any party, or the truth of any allegation.\n\nThank you for your assistance.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nRequester \n"},{"id":"gm-1a135bdc3ab000b2","subject":"RC26-0375 – thank you and three remaining points & Order for scans","name":"RC26-0375 – thank you and three remaining points & Order for scans","date":"2026-10-13T14:49:40Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a135bdc3ab000b2.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a135bdc3ab000b2-rc26-0375-thank-you-and-three-remaining-points-order-for-scans","scopeTopics":["rcahmw"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["rcahmw"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a135bdc3ab000b2","direction":"received","from":"NMR Enquiries","institutions":[{"id":"rcahmw","name":"RCAHMW (Royal Commission)"}],"refs":["RC26-0375"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a135bdc3ab000b2.json","attachments":[{"name":"LES08 Order Form 2021.docx","title":"LES08 Order Form 2021.docx","url":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/pdf/foi/2026-05-28_rcahmw_rc26-0205_les08-order-form-2021.docx","mime":"application/octet-stream","bytes":142468,"sha256":"291e3be8baf22b8f7e538ffd565e2a16462b66f5679c94ac3d72093816a18335"},{"name":"LES16 Price List 2026.pdf","title":"LES16 Price List 2026.pdf","url":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/pdf/evidence/2026-10-13_rcahmw_les16-price-list-2026.pdf","mime":"application/pdf","bytes":324233,"sha256":"141cafc6cd2b1d21902b55e3c91db2a6622afeea58fba237a0ae844c2280a3bb"}],"textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester,\n\nWith regard to the three remaining points noted below:\n\n1) If you would like to order scans of these items, please complete and return the attached order form. While I cannot confirm a quote for this order before receiving a completed order form, an order of these three items would likely require four research-quality scans. As per the attached price list, we waive fees for orders requiring fewer than five research-quality scans for personal, non-commercial use.\n\n2) As has been noted, our searches have not been limited to the registered files. No further information related to this is held beyond what is noted in the archive catalogue.\n\n3) As noted in my emails of 29 September and 6 October, the item Archive Number 6364275 from the Emergency Recording Collection has been physically examined and does not relate to Great House Farm, Llandough. As noted (correctly) in the archive catalogue, it relates to Great House, Bonvilston.\n\nAll the best,\n\nAdam Coward\n\n\n\nNMR Enquiries\nYmholiadau Cyhoeddus I Public Enquiries\nFfordd Penglais | Penglais Road, Aberystwyth, [postcode redacted]\n[phone redacted]\n[email redacted] | [email redacted]\nwww.cbhc.gov.uk | www.rcahmw.gov.uk\nNoddir gan Lywodraeth Cymru | Sponsored by Welsh Government\nRydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg a Saesneg. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn peri oedi.\nWe welcome correspondence in Welsh and English. Corresponding in Welsh will not lead to any delay.\n\nYn unol â Rheoliadau Safonau'r Gymraeg (Rhif 2) 2016, mae gennych hawl i gyfathrebu a gohebu â Chomisiwn Brenhinol Henebion Cymru yn eich dewis iaith. Er mwyn sicrhau ein bod yn cyflawni’r hawl, rhowch wybod i ni a ydych yn dymuno derbyn gohebiaeth a/neu alwadau ffôn oddi wrthym yn y Gymraeg. Bydd yr wybodaeth hon yn cael ei chofnodi gennym ni, a byddwn yn defnyddio'r iaith o’ch dewis ym mhob cyfathrebu yn y dyfodol. Diolch.\nUnder the Welsh Language Standards (No. 2) Regulations 2016, you have the right to communicate and correspond with the Royal Commission on the Ancient and Historical Monuments of Wales in your preferred language. To ensure we uphold this right, please let us know whether you wish to receive correspondence and/or telephone calls from us in Welsh. This information will be recorded by us, and we will use your preferred language in all future communication. Thank you.\n​\n-----Original Message-----\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]> \nSent: 10 October 2026 20:11\nTo: NMR Enquiries <[email redacted]>\nSubject: RC26-0375 – thank you and three remaining points\n\nDear National Monuments Record team,\n\nThank you for the consolidated reply of 29 September / 6 October 2026\n(RC26-0375) and for confirming that the requests of 8, 15, 17 and 20 September are now treated as fully answered.\n\nThree points remain:\n\n1. Please supply the free scans of NMR 6048945, 6048943 and 6048941 as indicated.\n2. Confirm whether any accession data, transfer note or provenance record exists for the material held under NPRN 20182 (beyond the statement that none is held in registered files).\n3. Confirm whether the emergency-recording case previously catalogued under the wrong place-name (Bonvilston) has now been correctly linked.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\nRequester \n"},{"id":"gm-1a134f768d97f0eb","subject":"Re: Mary Doreen Williams — death registration — request passed from Vale of Glamorgan Registration Service on 30 September 2026","name":"Re: Mary Doreen Williams — death registration — request passed from Vale of Glamorgan Registration Service on 30 September 2026","date":"2026-10-13T11:13:11Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a134f768d97f0eb.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a11a41ac938b1a5-mary-doreen-williams-death-registration-request-passed-from-vale-of-glamorgan-registration-service-on-30-september-2026","scopeTopics":["glamorgan"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["glamorgan"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a11a41ac938b1a5","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"glamorgan","name":"Glamorgan Archives"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a134f768d97f0eb.json","textStatus":"available","text":"MoJ Ref 260904007\n\nThanks Karen.\n\nYes I tried the website.\nIt seems to allow me to place the order.\n\nI don't know what website I was on before that wouldn't allow me to enter\nthe year 1983, but it looks to be working today.\n\nThanks for all your help.\n\nMoJ, see below\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester \n\n\n\n\n\nOn Tue, 13 Oct 2026, 09:48 Denham, Karen (Registrars), <\n[email redacted]> wrote:\n\n> Hi Requester\n>\n> I have checked with the Glamorgan Archives to see what districts would\n> have come under South Glamorgan in 1983 and they give me Bridgend.\n> Pontypridd and Merthyr Tydfil. I have checked with all these districts and\n> they do not hold a death record for Mary Doreen Williams in the March qtr\n> of 1983. I have also checked with Thornhill Crematorium to see if they had\n> a burial or cremation record here in Cardiff and they don’t hold any record\n> either. They also checked Glyntaff Crematorium and Coychurch Crematorium\n> and they don’t hold any record of the burial or cremation there either. As\n> previously discussed with you if you quote VOLUME 28 PAGE NO 3029 to our\n> head office they will do a copy of the certificate from there for you.\n> Their contact telephone number is [phone redacted] or alternatively you can\n> email Order a birth, death, marriage or civil partnership certificate -\n> GOV.UK  .\n> There is nothing more that I can do for you with this request as we do not\n> hold the record here in Cardiff. I am sorry that I cannot be of more help\n> to you and good luck in your search.\n>\n>\n>\n> Kind Regards\n>\n> Karen\n>\n>\n>\n> *Karen Denham*\n>\n> Dirprwy Gofrestrydd a Swyddog Dinasyddiaeth / Deputy Registrar &\n> Nationality Officer\n>\n>\n>\n> Ebost/Email: [email redacted] / [email redacted]\n>\n> Ffon/Tel: [phone redacted]\n>\n> Y Swyddfa Gofrestru, Archifau Morgannwg, Clos Parc Morgannwg. Lecwydd,\n> Caerdydd [postcode redacted]\n>\n> The Register Office, Glamorgan Archives, Clos Parc Morgannwg, Leckwith,\n> Cardiff [postcode redacted]\n>\n> www.cardiffregisteroffice.co.uk\n>\n>\n>\n> *From:* Requester  \n> *Sent:* 12 October 2026 16:20\n> *To:* Denham, Karen (Registrars)  \n> *Subject:* Re: Mary Doreen Williams — death registration — request passed\n> from Vale of Glamorgan Registration Service on 30 September 2026\n>\n>\n>\n>  *EXTERNAL:* This email originated from outside Cardiff Council, take\n> care when clicking links.\n>  *ALLANOL:* Daw'r e-bost hwn o'r tu allan i Gyngor Caerdydd, cymerwch\n> ofal wrth glicio ar ddolenni.\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> Yes, please pass on.\n>\n> Thank you.\n>\n>\n>\n> Requester\n>\n>\n>\n> On Mon, 12 Oct 2026, 09:35 Denham, Karen (Registrars), <\n> [email redacted]> wrote:\n>\n> Dear Requester\n>\n> Thank you for your email regarding Mary Doreen Williams\n>\n>\n>\n> 1 Cardiff Register Office does not hold the registration of death for Mary\n> Doreen Williams\n>\n> 2 The indexes that were search were FREEBMD where the reference number\n> VOLUME 28 PAGE NO 3029 comes up. Note. If you quote this to our head office\n> based in Southport they can do the certificate from there for you\n>\n>\n>\n> In 1983 South Glamorgan was a vast area so the registration could be in\n> any district. If you would like I will pass this on to our team at the\n> Glamorgan Archives and they can tell you which register office that you\n> would need to contact but advised previously it is better to get a copy of\n> the certificate from our head office as we definitely do not hold the\n> record for Mary Doreen Williams here with us in Cardiff.\n>\n>\n>\n> Kind Regards\n>\n> Karen\n>\n>\n>\n> *Karen Denham*\n>\n> Dirprwy Gofrestrydd a Swyddog Dinasyddiaeth / Deputy Registrar &\n> Nationality Officer\n>\n> Ebost/Email: [email redacted] / [email redacted]\n>\n> Ffon/Tel: [phone redacted]\n>\n> www.cardiffregisteroffice.co.uk\n>\n>\n>\n> *From:* Requester  \n> *Sent:* 10 October 2026 20:14\n> *To:* Denham, Karen (Registrars)  \n> *Subject:* Mary Doreen Williams — death registration — request passed\n> from Vale of Glamorgan Registration Service on 30 September 2026\n>\n>\n>\n>  *EXTERNAL:* This email originated from outside Cardiff Council, take\n> care when clicking links.\n>  *ALLANOL:* Daw'r e-bost hwn o'r tu allan i Gyngor Caerdydd, cymerwch\n> ofal wrth glicio ar ddolenni.\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> Dear Ms Denham,\n>\n> Mary Doreen Williams: death registration\n>\n> On 30 September 2026 the Vale of Glamorgan Registration Service told me\n> they could not find a death record for Mary Doreen Williams in the Vale,\n> and that they had passed my request to Cardiff. I have not had a reply from\n> Cardiff.\n>\n> Please could you:\n>\n> 1. confirm whether Cardiff Register Office holds a registration of death\n> for Mary Doreen Williams;\n> 2. if it does, tell me how I can obtain a certified copy and the fee; and\n> 3. if it does not, tell me which indexes and registers were searched, so\n> that I can search the GRO death index for the correct registration district.\n>\n> If you need further details to carry out the search (approximate date or\n> place of death, date of birth, last address), please tell me what you need\n> and I will supply it. I am asking as a family member and I need the\n> registration for estate administration.\n>\n> Thank you for your help.\n>\n> Regards,\n>\n> Requester \n> \n>\n> **********************************************************************\n>\n> The Council welcomes correspondence in Welsh, English or bilingually. We\n> will ensure that we communicate with you in the language of your choice, as\n> long as you let us know which you prefer. Corresponding in Welsh will not\n> lead to delay.\n>\n> Privileged/Confidential Information may be contained in this message. If\n> you are not the addressee indicated in this message (or responsible for\n> delivery of the message to such person), you may not copy or deliver this\n> message to anyone. In such case, you should destroy this message and kindly\n> notify the sender by reply email. Please advise immediately if you or your\n> employer does not consent to Internet email for messages of this kind.\n> Opinions, conclusions and other information in this message that do not\n> relate to the official business of the Council of the City and County of\n> Cardiff shall be understood as neither given nor endorsed by it. All e-mail\n> sent to or from this address will be processed by Cardiff County Councils\n> Corporate E-mail system and may be subject to scrutiny by someone other\n> than the addressee.\n>\n> **********************************************************************\n>\n> Mae’r Cyngor yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg, Saesneg neu’n ddwyieithog.\n> Byddwn yn cyfathrebu â chi yn ôl eich dewis, dim ond i chi roi gwybod i ni\n> pa un sydd well gennych. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi.\n>\n> Mae'n bosibl bod gwybodaeth gyfrinachol yn y neges hon. Os na chyfeirir y\n> neges atoch chi'n benodol (neu os nad ydych chi'n gyfrifol am drosglwyddo'r\n> neges i'r person a enwir), yna ni chewch gopio na throsglwyddo'r neges.\n> Mewn achos o'r fath, dylech ddinistrio'r neges a hysbysu'r anfonwr drwy\n> e-bost ar unwaith. Rhowch wybod i'r anfonydd ar unwaith os nad ydych chi\n> neu eich cyflogydd yn caniatau e-bost y Rhyngrwyd am negeseuon fel hon.\n> Rhaid deall nad yw'r safbwyntiau, y casgliadau a'r wybodaeth arall yn y\n> neges hon nad ydynt yn cyfeirio at fusnes swyddogol Cyngor Dinas a Sir\n> Caerdydd yn cynrychioli barn y Cyngor Sir nad yn cael sel ei fendith. Caiff\n> unrhyw negeseuon a anfonir at, neu o'r cyfeiriad e-bost hwn eu prosesu gan\n> system E-bost Gorfforaethol Cyngor Sir Caerdydd a gallant gael eu\n> harchwilio gan rywun heblaw'r person a enwir.\n>\n> **********************************************************************\n>\n>"},{"id":"gm-1a13472b496a2845","subject":"RE: Mary Doreen Williams — death registration — request passed from Vale of Glamorgan Registration Service on 30 September 2026","name":"RE: Mary Doreen Williams — death registration — request passed from Vale of Glamorgan Registration Service on 30 September 2026","date":"2026-10-13T08:48:10Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a13472b496a2845.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a11a41ac938b1a5-mary-doreen-williams-death-registration-request-passed-from-vale-of-glamorgan-registration-service-on-30-september-2026","scopeTopics":["glamorgan"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["glamorgan"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a11a41ac938b1a5","direction":"received","from":"Denham, Karen (Registrars)","institutions":[{"id":"glamorgan","name":"Glamorgan Archives"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a13472b496a2845.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Hi Requester\nI have checked with the Glamorgan Archives to see what districts would have come under South Glamorgan in 1983 and they give me Bridgend. Pontypridd and Merthyr Tydfil. I have checked with all these districts and they do not hold a death record for Mary Doreen Williams in the March qtr of 1983. I have also checked with Thornhill Crematorium to see if they had a burial or cremation record here in Cardiff and they don’t hold any record either. They also checked Glyntaff Crematorium and Coychurch Crematorium and they don’t hold any record of the burial or cremation there either. As previously discussed with you if you quote VOLUME 28 PAGE NO 3029 to our head office they will do a copy of the certificate from there for you. Their contact telephone number is [phone redacted] or alternatively you can email Order a birth, death, marriage or civil partnership certificate - GOV.UK<https://www.gov.uk/order-copy-birth-death-marriage-certificate>. There is nothing more that I can do for you with this request as we do not hold the record here in Cardiff. I am sorry that I cannot be of more help to you and good luck in your search.\n\nKind Regards\nKaren\n\nKaren Denham\nDirprwy Gofrestrydd a Swyddog Dinasyddiaeth / Deputy Registrar & Nationality Officer\n\nEbost/Email: [email redacted]<[email redacted]> / [email redacted]<[email redacted]>\nFfon/Tel: [phone redacted]\nY Swyddfa Gofrestru, Archifau Morgannwg, Clos Parc Morgannwg. Lecwydd, Caerdydd [postcode redacted]\nThe Register Office, Glamorgan Archives, Clos Parc Morgannwg, Leckwith, Cardiff [postcode redacted]\nwww.cardiffregisteroffice.co.uk<http://www.cardiffregisteroffice.co.uk/>\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]>\nSent: 12 October 2026 16:20\nTo: Denham, Karen (Registrars) <[email redacted]>\nSubject: Re: Mary Doreen Williams — death registration — request passed from Vale of Glamorgan Registration Service on 30 September 2026\n\n EXTERNAL: This email originated from outside Cardiff Council, take care when clicking links.\n ALLANOL: Daw'r e-bost hwn o'r tu allan i Gyngor Caerdydd, cymerwch ofal wrth glicio ar ddolenni.\n\n\nYes, please pass on.\nThank you.\n\nRequester\n\nOn Mon, 12 Oct 2026, 09:35 Denham, Karen (Registrars), <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>> wrote:\nDear Requester\nThank you for your email regarding Mary Doreen Williams\n\n1 Cardiff Register Office does not hold the registration of death for Mary Doreen Williams\n2 The indexes that were search were FREEBMD where the reference number VOLUME 28 PAGE NO 3029 comes up. Note. If you quote this to our head office based in Southport they can do the certificate from there for you\n\nIn 1983 South Glamorgan was a vast area so the registration could be in any district. If you would like I will pass this on to our team at the Glamorgan Archives and they can tell you which register office that you would need to contact but advised previously it is better to get a copy of the certificate from our head office as we definitely do not hold the record for Mary Doreen Williams here with us in Cardiff.\n\nKind Regards\nKaren\n\nKaren Denham\nDirprwy Gofrestrydd a Swyddog Dinasyddiaeth / Deputy Registrar & Nationality Officer\nEbost/Email: [email redacted]<[email redacted]> / [email redacted]<[email redacted]>\nFfon/Tel: [phone redacted]\nwww.cardiffregisteroffice.co.uk<http://www.cardiffregisteroffice.co.uk/>\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSent: 10 October 2026 20:14\nTo: Denham, Karen (Registrars) <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSubject: Mary Doreen Williams — death registration — request passed from Vale of Glamorgan Registration Service on 30 September 2026\n\n EXTERNAL: This email originated from outside Cardiff Council, take care when clicking links.\n ALLANOL: Daw'r e-bost hwn o'r tu allan i Gyngor Caerdydd, cymerwch ofal wrth glicio ar ddolenni.\n\n\nDear Ms Denham,\n\nMary Doreen Williams: death registration\n\nOn 30 September 2026 the Vale of Glamorgan Registration Service told me they could not find a death record for Mary Doreen Williams in the Vale, and that they had passed my request to Cardiff. I have not had a reply from Cardiff.\n\nPlease could you:\n\n1. confirm whether Cardiff Register Office holds a registration of death for Mary Doreen Williams;\n2. if it does, tell me how I can obtain a certified copy and the fee; and\n3. if it does not, tell me which indexes and registers were searched, so that I can search the GRO death index for the correct registration district.\n\nIf you need further details to carry out the search (approximate date or place of death, date of birth, last address), please tell me what you need and I will supply it. I am asking as a family member and I need the registration for estate administration.\n\nThank you for your help.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester \n\n\n**********************************************************************\n\nThe Council welcomes correspondence in Welsh, English or bilingually. We will ensure that we communicate with you in the language of your choice, as long as you let us know which you prefer. Corresponding in Welsh will not lead to delay.\n\nPrivileged/Confidential Information may be contained in this message. If you are not the addressee indicated in this message (or responsible for delivery of the message to such person), you may not copy or deliver this message to anyone. In such case, you should destroy this message and kindly notify the sender by reply email. Please advise immediately if you or your employer does not consent to Internet email for messages of this kind. Opinions, conclusions and other information in this message that do not relate to the official business of the Council of the City and County of Cardiff shall be understood as neither given nor endorsed by it. All e-mail sent to or from this address will be processed by Cardiff County Councils Corporate E-mail system and may be subject to scrutiny by someone other than the addressee.\n\n**********************************************************************\n\nMae’r Cyngor yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg, Saesneg neu’n ddwyieithog. Byddwn yn cyfathrebu â chi yn ôl eich dewis, dim ond i chi roi gwybod i ni pa un sydd well gennych. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi.\n\nMae'n bosibl bod gwybodaeth gyfrinachol yn y neges hon. Os na chyfeirir y neges atoch chi'n benodol (neu os nad ydych chi'n gyfrifol am drosglwyddo'r neges i'r person a enwir), yna ni chewch gopio na throsglwyddo'r neges. Mewn achos o'r fath, dylech ddinistrio'r neges a hysbysu'r anfonwr drwy e-bost ar unwaith. Rhowch wybod i'r anfonydd ar unwaith os nad ydych chi neu eich cyflogydd yn caniatau e-bost y Rhyngrwyd am negeseuon fel hon. Rhaid deall nad yw'r safbwyntiau, y casgliadau a'r wybodaeth arall yn y neges hon nad ydynt yn cyfeirio at fusnes swyddogol Cyngor Dinas a Sir Caerdydd yn cynrychioli barn y Cyngor Sir nad yn cael sel ei fendith. Caiff unrhyw negeseuon a anfonir at, neu o'r cyfeiriad e-bost hwn eu prosesu gan system E-bost Gorfforaethol Cyngor Sir Caerdydd a gallant gael eu harchwilio gan rywun heblaw'r person a enwir.\n\n**********************************************************************"},{"id":"gm-1a1344cdc78c3aaa","subject":"RE: FOI 1264/26 - non-personal operational records concerning Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr, Llandough, November–December 1988","name":"RE: FOI 1264/26 - non-personal operational records concerning Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr, Llandough, November–December 1988","date":"2026-10-13T08:06:49Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a1344cdc78c3aaa.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a12fa8ff526287b-foi-1264-26-non-personal-operational-records-concerning-great-house-farm-t-mawr-llandough-november-december-1988","scopeTopics":["swp"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["swp"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a12fa8ff526287b","direction":"received","from":"Freedom of Information","institutions":[{"id":"swp","name":"South Wales Police"}],"refs":["FOI1264"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a1344cdc78c3aaa.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Good morning,\n\nI write in relation to your request requesting:\n\n\n“Please treat this as a request under the Freedom of Information Act\n\n2000 for recorded information, excluding personal data and excluding any material for which the appropriate handling route is the separate subject-access request already open. The site is Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr, Llandough-juxta-Penarth, adjoining St Dochdwy’s Church, Cardiff (approx. ST 168 733), and the period is 1 November to 31 December 1988 inclusive.\n\n\n\nPlease search the relevant South Wales Constabulary / predecessor-force and successor holdings, including any transferred records available to the Force, for existing non-personal records that refer to the site or the events associated with its possession and demolition. Relevant record types, if held, include:\n\n1. incident or occurrence logs and control-room records; 2. deployment, duty or operational logs and any record identifying whether officers attended; 3. correspondence between police, the local authority, landowner/developer, court enforcement officers or other public bodies about the site; 4. any recorded liaison or request for police assistance connected with possession, eviction, demolition, public order or the handling of complaints; and 5. records-transfer, retention or disposal entries for the relevant operational record series, if the responsive records are no longer held…”\n\n\nIt is not possible to progress your request at this time, as clarification is needed from you to enable us to locate the information you have asked for.\n\nPlease could you provide some information as to the individuals or entities involved in the occurrences at Ty Mawr from 01/11/1988 – 31/12/1988. We are currently liaising with our legacy crime team and they require this information to carry out a search on their records. Useful information would include any names, dates of birth, or addresses of any persons involved.\n\nIn line with the Freedom of Information Act 2000 Section 10(6), the 20 working day timescale will be suspended until the required clarification has been received, restarting on the date when the information is received.  If we do not receive clarification from you then your request will be closed 40 working days from the date of this email.\n\nKind regards,\n\n\n[cid:image001.png@01DD5AF1.4B1A83F0]<https://www.south-wales.police.uk/>\n\n\nOwen Byrne\nSwyddog Cymorth Ymchwil a Datgelu| Research and Data Quality Officer\nRheolwr Rheoli Data a Datgeliadau | Data Management and Disclosure Unit\nPencadlys Heddlu De Cymru | South Wales Police Headquarters\n•: [phone redacted] | Ext: 26660\n[cid:image002.png@01DD5AF1.4B1A83F0]<http://fb.me/swpolice>\n[cid:image003.png@01DD5AF1.4B1A83F0]<https://www.instagram.com/swpolice/>\n[cid:image004.png@01DD5AF1.4B1A83F0]<https://twitter.com/swpolice>\n[cid:image005.png@01DD5AF1.4B1A83F0]<https://www.youtube.com/user/swptv>\n\n\nYmateb nad yw'n Argyfwng\\ Non-Emergency Response\n\nOes angen i chi siarad â'r heddlu ond nid oes angen ymateb brys arnoch? Gallwch roi gwybod am fater drwy ein gwefan https://www.heddlu-de-cymru.police.uk<https://www.heddlu-de-cymru.police.uk/>, neu ffonio 101. Gallwch ddefnyddio 101 i roi gwybod am achosion nad ydynt yn rhai brys i unrhyw heddlu yng Nghymru a Lloegr. Mewn argyfwng, ffoniwch 999 bob amser.\n\nDo you need to speak to the police but don’t require an emergency response? You can make a report via our website https://www.south-wales.police.uk<https://www.south-wales.police.uk/>, or call 101. 101 can be used to report a non-emergency to any force in Wales and England. In an emergency always dial 999.\n\nCymorth Iaith Gymraeg\\Welsh Language Support\n\nMae Heddlu De Cymru yn croesawu derbyn gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg a Saesneg. Byddwn yn ateb gohebiaeth a dderbynnir yn Gymraeg yn Gymraeg ac ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi.\n\nSouth Wales Police welcomes receiving correspondence in Welsh and English. Any correspondence received in Welsh will be answered in Welsh and corresponding in Welsh will not lead to a delay in responding.\n\nYmwadiad Heddlu De Cymru\\South Wales Police Disclaimer\n\nGallai defnyddio neu ddatgelu’r neges e-bost hon heb awdurdod fod yn anghyfreithlon. Gallwch weld ein datganiad cyfrinachedd yn: https://www.heddlu-de-cymru.police.uk/cy-GB/heddluoedd/heddlu-de-cymru/ardaloedd/amdanom-ni/amdanom-ni/datganiad-cyfrinachedd-e-bost/\".\n\nUnauthorised use or disclosure of this email may be unlawful. View our confidentiality statement at https://www.south-wales.police.uk/police-forces/south-wales-police/areas/about-us/about-us/email-confidentiality-statement/."},{"id":"gm-1a133f3c7760862d","subject":"Please update the status of your request - BP vs Buckler 1987","name":"Please update the status of your request - BP vs Buckler 1987","date":"2026-10-13T06:29:36Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a133f3c7760862d.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a133f3c7760862d-please-update-the-status-of-your-request-bp-vs-buckler-1987","scopeTopics":["nactso"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["nactso"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a133f3c7760862d","direction":"received","from":"(name not shown; whatdotheyknow.com)","institutions":[{"id":"nactso","name":"Counter Terrorism Policing (NaCTSO)"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a133f3c7760862d.json","textStatus":"available","text":"To let everyone know, follow this link and then select the appropriate box.\n\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/profile/sign_in?r=%2Frequest%2Fbp_vs_buckler_1987_1988%23describe_state_form_1\n\nYour request was called 'BP vs Buckler 1987'. Letting everyone know whether you got the information will help us keep tabs on\nNational Counter Terrorism Security Office.\n\n-- the WhatDoTheyKnow team\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nWhatDoTheyKnow is a project of mySociety run by a small team of staff and\ndedicated volunteers.\n\nmySociety is a charity that believes that information should be accessible to everyone.\nIf this is important to you, please consider donating to help us do more:\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/donate\n\nFind out more about how we handle your data at:\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/help/privacy\n\nmySociety is a registered charity in England and Wales (1076346), and a limited\ncompany (03277032)"},{"id":"gm-1a1334ac831c541a","subject":"Apologies Gentlemen - Disregard Last Email Por Favor","name":"Apologies Gentlemen - Disregard Last Email Por Favor","date":"2026-10-13T03:25:01Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a1334ac831c541a.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a13347b9572d705-apologies-gentlemen-disregard-last-email-por-favor","scopeTopics":["llandough"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["llandough"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a13347b9572d705","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"llandough","name":"Llandough Community Council"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a1334ac831c541a.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Apologies Gentlemen,\n\nI am on a night excercise and my attention was elsewhere trying to respond\nto your email. It has my full attention now. Please disregard my last email\nin lieu of this one. Thank you in advance for your patience and\nunderstanding.\n\nMr Egan (& cc Mo),\n\nThank you for your email of 11 October 2026. I acknowledge your refusal of\nmy information request and your statement that the other matters raised in\nmy correspondence “will be considered at the October meeting of the\ncouncil”.\n\nNot to sound like a stickler, but your email does not identify the date,\ntime, venue, public-access arrangements or agenda for that meeting, nor\ndoes it direct me to a published notice containing those particulars. This\nomission prevents me from verifying when the promised consideration is to\ntake place, whether the relevant matters are scheduled for consideration,\nand whether I can attend or monitor the proceedings. Please provide those\nparticulars immediately or identify where the Council has published them.\n\nBelow are my position, formal request for an internal review, further\nrequest for recorded information and subject access request for my personal\ndata. Please record and process each part under the applicable procedure.\nNone is intended to withdraw, narrow or replace any of the others.\n\n*1. Position concerning Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr*\n\nAs stated previously and repeatedly, my research into this case concerns\nthe legitimacy of my family's historic title and the treatment of Great\nHouse Farm, its buildings and associated land.\n\nThe two-parcel estate, one parcel of which was and still appears to be\nours, is an evidence-based position advanced from the documentary record\nconcerning the distinct identities and conveyancing histories of the land.\nParcel A comprised the farmhouse, farm buildings, garden, yard, associated\nwoodland and what is now the green at Church View Close. Parcel B comprised\nthe eastern agricultural land, east of our Parcel A, which was WGR's and\nBP's parcel and the focus of their possession claim.\n\nMy argument, again, is that our parcel of land and home, dating back to\n1667, was treated as one and the same as Parcel B under the blanket term\n“whole farm”.\n\nOur Parcel A—or a synthetic imitation of it, arising from a long-running\nand rejected series of attempts to make our occupancy appear permissive—was\nstealthily registered by BP in 1983 and merged into Parcel B in 1987. My\ngrandmother died at a particularly convenient point in this sequence, and\nno notice of the registration was given during the appeal window. The\nparcels were merged in 1987 before my late father's appeal against the 1987\nruling had concluded.\n\nOur county's treasured home was also demolished in 1988, before our appeal\nhad even begun, let alone concluded, following Cadw's decision not to list\nit as a Grade II building, leaving it open to demolition.\n\nNone of this should be downgraded to speculative theory simply because the\nCouncil has not adjudicated it. The chronology of the evidence is painting\na damning picture of what appears to be everything needed to reopen this\ncase: fraud, concealment and government involvement. These are matters I am\ninvestigating, and I encourage everyone involved to conduct their own\nresearch and investigation.\n\nBefore returning to the formal substance of this communication, I will give\nyou the courtesy of some colloquialism, so that you have the courtesy from\nme of being treated like a human being and understand that you are also\ndealing with one. I have received some very gracious emails from Chris\nFranks, the coroners, and other family and community members whose families\nhave been allied with mine for decades and, in some instances, centuries.\n\nFirst, I wholeheartedly reject what I regard as a terrorist/hostage\nsituationship with local authorities here. I also reject the transformation\nof our town, county and country into one giant corporate concentration work\ncamp, with voters being imported by traitors to keep themselves in power. I\nserved under Tony Blair's orders, and I called David Cameron into an\ninternational treaty process that I helped stop at WCIT 2012. I know a\ngreat deal about what the other mammals on this island get up to.\n\nWhat happened to our stronghold, and the destruction of our ancient\nhistory, heritage and family, is tantamount to ethnic cleansing and a\ndeclaration of war on everything I hold sacred: my family, home, land,\nhistory, reality and faith. The situation was untenable enough back then.\nThe more the full picture of what happened in this case becomes apparent,\nthe less legitimacy I can accept in the occupation and rule of my\nbirthplace by the authorities currently exercising power over it.\n\nUnless I am mistaken and this has all been one big misunderstanding, with\nnobody to blame, that needs to be demonstrated through evidence and clear\nanswers. I am not receiving much reassurance from any of you that I am\nmistaken.\n\nI understood you to have already retired, which was a misunderstanding on\nmy part. Much like I misunderstood the 1987 merger of the parcels to have\noccurred in 1983 until recently, this is a complex case. I do not claim to\nunderstand every aspect of it myself. It will take a village, and I am here\nhoping to feel my village's warmth, with three others due to arrive shortly\nafter me this month.\n\nLegitimacy is the only authority I will recognise here. Two of my\nclose-protection officers are three-times-decorated Gulf War veterans and\nformer police officers, and I have shared this matter with them. If the\nlocal authorities will not cooperate, I have no issue drawing on my own\nsecurity group and resources in Britain and America. I am giving the local\nauthorities here the opportunity to answer for what you all did to my\nfamily over the past 40 years.\n\nAs you may be able to tell, I have more or less abandoned the idea that\nthis is a democracy or that a lawful system of government operates here. I\nknow the British State is a terrorist organisation because I have been part\nof the forces carrying out comparable operations to the siege and assault\non my home and family in Llandough in 1988. I was behind a machine gun in a\nhelicopter during the illegal invasion of Iraq. So, while you may fancy\nyourselves crazy here, holding my family hostage under a web of deceit, I\nmay actually be a little bit worse.\n\nMy flight back to Cardiff last month was synonymous with an earlier\nmilitary tour of the Middle East in 2005, when two SAS personnel were held\nhostage by Iraqi police and were about to be decapitated. We had to smash\ndown the prison walls and get them out. I am here to see that my family\ngets out of the captivity they are being held in. The walls of this\nterrorist/hostage situationship are not physical; they are more like some\ndemonic realm, a spell everyone has been kept under. Perhaps it is the same\nsource as whatever is going into the water that is making frogs turn gay.\n\nThe circus this case appears to be, if not rectified, will surely damn and\ncurse that small, pale village to hell. This is no small matter. The\narmed-forces world I know is nothing compared with the forces being messed\nwith in this case. None of us can now claim innocence or ignorance of the\nfacts being brought forward. My older brother, sisters, mum, aunties and\ncousins are also seeing this for the charade and circus it is. I am\nbringing the matter to everyone's attention to give you all the opportunity\nto respond. So far, all I have experienced is barriers, delays, walls,\npaywalls and excuses.\n\nYou yourself kindly pointed us towards things such as the 1939 agreement,\nwhich excludes our parcel. But let us not forget that your initial response\nwas an unwillingness to become involved.\n\nUnderstand that I bring this matter to the local authorities—whose\nlegitimacy in this case I dispute—after considerable personal trauma, a\ndifficult personal trajectory, misinformation, fragmented information and\nexpense to myself. I attended private boarding school, and my career began\nin military intelligence, but we were nevertheless knocked into a different\nlife after 1988.\n\nMore recently, I left my wife and daughter at our villa in the Caribbean\nand spent the past year in a cabin in southern Argentina, trying to\nunderstand this case. While you are all taking your merry old time dragging\nthis out for months, that has contributed to the end of my marriage and\nanother family separation. I do not know where you all imagine I am going\neach time you give me the finger.\n\nI am a military warfare professional, and I am very committed to\nunderstanding this. That is why I flew 7,000 miles to your doorstep. If\nthis is my family's land, how did it come to be that you are all on it? I\nam not convinced by the answers you keep providing.\n\nPerhaps those answers sufficed when I was an infant, my mum was pregnant\nand my dad was terminally ill. But my disabled, widowed, pensioner\ngrandmother, Mary Williams, had seven children, who had many other\nchildren, and generation on generation has now produced a small and very\nhungry army. I have more than 50 former soldiers in my US-based private\nsecurity group, with a satellite office here.\n\nI say this because my family here are a lot bigger and uglier. I am no\nlonger based here with any of you. I am resigned to the conclusion that the\nBritish State is ethnically cleansing Welsh nationals in Wales. That is why\nI am now based in Patagonia, Argentina, where Welsh nationals, our history\nand our heritage are celebrated, and where I am pursuing legal personhood\narrangements for my AI agents.\n\nI am only here to light the metaphorical fuse of this powder keg. The\naftermath will be between the local authorities and everyone affected by\nwhat has happened. I am quietly gathering evidence, particularly for the\nreplica of Great House Farm I am constructing in Patagonia as a museum and\nthird home. If I uncover fraud, concealment and government involvement in\nthat process, so be it. The case reopens.\n\nhttps://bpvsbuckler.bucklerfamily.estate\n\nMy wife, soon to be my ex-wife, and I will not even allow our unregistered\ndaughter to set foot in Wales until it is decolonised or the British\nEstablishment and UK Government have been overthrown.\n\nI also exported my dog from our livery yard in Monknash in 2024 after an\nAfghan migrant entered our horse yard and indoor riding school, allegedly\nattempting to steal and eat him.\n\nMy first child was abducted at 36 days old in 2013 and has never been seen\nby me again. It feels as though a demonic realm is ruling this island and\neveryone is under its spell.\n\nThe reason my business associates, former soldiers and family members are\ngathering around this matter and flying into the region is that, if I am\nright about this case and corruption in the local authorities, it is the\ncatalyst and nexus event of the land-market bubble we are now experiencing.\nI am not saying that this case alone meets the threshold for reopening; I\nam saying that it may be one of the earliest, possibly the first, of many\nparallel cases nationwide.\n\nThat means there is a very real possibility of something like what we saw\nin Cyprus, in which I was directly involved while serving as guard\ncommander at RAF Akrotiri. In that context, the United Nations in Nicosia\nwould have to award land back to its native owners after decades of\noccupation or provide reparations. Decolonisation is not all political and\nfigurative; it is very literal, Paul. It is logistical and operational. You\nwill soon see that through this case or the increasing number of others now\nemerging nationwide.\n\nHaving recently worked on setting up the car-finance reparations claims\nscheme and its information systems, against the background of the FCA's\nwork and last year's court rulings, I see a definite parallel here as more\ncases resembling my family's experience in Llandough emerge nationally and\nglobally.\n\nDo you see the parallel? The following websites illustrate it. In my view,\nneither underlying system should be able to function as it currently does\nwithout scrutiny and accountability.\n\nhttps://car.financecheque.uk\n\nhttps://bucklerfamily.estate\n\nBut I digress. To continue from my earlier point, I am not asking Llandough\nCommunity Council to adjudicate title or pronounce on matters outside its\nlegal remit. The Council's institutional authority is not itself proof of\nthe legitimacy of a title position. Its responsibility is to account\naccurately for its own records, knowledge, representations, decisions and\nreferrals, and to disclose the recorded information it holds.\n\nThe question of legitimacy must be assessed against the underlying\ndocuments and relevant legal acts, rather than presumed from administrative\nstatus or the repetition of an earlier account. I expect the Council to\ndistinguish primary documentary evidence, recorded statements, family\nevidence and inference when dealing with these matters.\n\n*2. Formal request for internal review — refusal dated 11 October 2026*\n\nPlease treat this section as my formal request for an internal review of\nthe refusal of my original request, submitted on 21 September 2026.\n\nThat request covered:\n\n   1. Records created after 2002 concerning Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr,\n   Church View Close, the green—including references to VG41—and the roadside\n   verge beside the churchyard wall.\n   2. Photographs, postcards and other images, of any date, depicting the\n   farmhouse, farm buildings, green or surrounding church area.\n   3. Records concerning material transferred from Penarth Town Council\n   during the 1982 local-government reorganisation, and information held by\n   the Council about the whereabouts or deposit of earlier Llandough community\n   records.\n\nYour refusal explains that searching minutes and related documentation\nacross a period of approximately 2002–2016 would exceed the appropriate\ncost limit. It refers to 224 sets of records and estimates 20 minutes to\nread each set and a further 10 minutes to assess it, but gives a total of\n2,240 minutes, or approximately 37 hours.\n\nThose figures are internally inconsistent. At 30 minutes per set, 224 sets\nwould amount to 6,720 minutes, or 112 hours. The stated 2,240 minutes\nequates to 10 minutes per set. More importantly, the response does not\nexplain precisely which activities have been included in the estimate.\n\nPlease have the reviewer consider the following matters:\n\n   - How the estimate was calculated, including the number and nature of\n   the records assumed to require examination.\n   - Whether the estimate was applied to every part of the request,\n   including photographic material and records concerning the 1982 transfer,\n   rather than treating all categories as if they required the same search.\n   - Whether existing indexes, catalogues, minute headings, document\n   descriptions, filing systems or other finding aids permit targeted searches\n   before individual records are examined.\n   - Whether a targeted search using the place names, former names, dates\n   and subjects identified in my request could provide a materially less\n   costly route to the information.\n   - Whether the requested environmental information must be considered\n   under the Environmental Information Regulations 2004, rather than being\n   refused solely by reference to the Freedom of Information Act cost limit.\n\nPlease identify how the stated 20 minutes for reading and 10 minutes for\nassessment per set correspond to the permitted activities under regulation\n4(3) of the Freedom of Information and Data Protection (Appropriate Limit\nand Fees) Regulations 2004. In particular, distinguish time spent\ndetermining whether the requested information is held, locating, retrieving\nor extracting it from time spent on activities that cannot lawfully be\nincluded in the section 12 estimate.\n\nIf the Council relies on regulation 12(4)(b) of the Environmental\nInformation Regulations, please identify that exception expressly, provide\nthe relevant reasoning and carry out the required public-interest\nassessment, including the presumption in favour of disclosure. Do not treat\nthe FOIA section 12 estimate as a substitute for determining which legal\nregime applies to each category.\n\nPlease also address the duty to provide reasonable advice and assistance\nunder section 16 FOIA and, where applicable, regulation 9 of the\nEnvironmental Information Regulations. Your suggestion that I narrow the\ndate range to 2002–2006 is noted, but I ask the reviewer to consider what\ntargeted searches or available indexes could reasonably be used to address\nthe original request before concluding that the information cannot be\nprovided.\n\nPlease confirm receipt of this internal review request, identify who will\nconduct the review, and provide the anticipated response date. Where\nreasonably practicable, the review should be undertaken by someone\nindependent of the original refusal.\n\n*3. Further request for recorded information — 1986–1990 and associated\nland matters*\n\nIn addition to the original request, please treat this section as a new\nrequest for recorded information under FOIA and, where applicable, the\nEnvironmental Information Regulations. It is directed to records held by\nthe Council and does not presume that any particular document exists.\n\n*A. Great House Farm: occupation, title, development and demolition*\n\nPlease disclose minutes, agendas, reports, correspondence, letters, notes,\ncomplaints, representations, referrals and other recorded information\ncreated between 1 January 1986 and 31 December 1990 concerning Great House\nFarm / Tŷ Mawr, including its occupation, title, tenure, proposed\ndevelopment, removal of the occupants, or demolition of the farmhouse and\nassociated buildings.\n\nThis includes records of communications with Vale of Glamorgan Council,\nplanning authorities, developers, solicitors, landowners, police, heritage\nbodies or other public authorities concerning those subjects.\n\nPlease include any records containing statements attributed to my late\nfather, William “Billy” Buckler, about the property, occupation, title or\nalleged fraud. References to alleged fraud identify a subject for the\nsearch; they are not a claim that a Council record or finding establishing\nfraud necessarily exists.\n\n*B. Planning records and annexes*\n\nPlease identify and disclose the Council-held planning records, plans,\nsupporting documents, annexes, correspondence and associated material\nrelating to application 89/01396/OUT and any related consideration of the\nredevelopment of the site.\n\nI have been unable to locate certain associated annexes in my copy. Please\nconfirm whether the Council holds them and, if not, whether its records\nidentify another custodian or archive.\n\n*C. Green, verge, trees and woodland*\n\nPlease disclose records held by the Council concerning the history, status,\nboundaries, maintenance, planting, ownership representations or public use\nof the green, the roadside verge beside the churchyard wall, and the\nwoodland associated with the former farm.\n\nThis includes relevant correspondence, complaints, maps, plans, site-visit\nrecords, referrals and communications with landowners, residents,\ncharities, Vale of Glamorgan Council or other responsible bodies.\n\nI have also received information raising a concern that some gardens at\nChurch View Close may extend beyond their recorded boundaries into\nadjoining woodland. I identify this as an unverified concern requiring\ndocumentary clarification, not as an established finding of encroachment or\nwrongdoing. Please disclose any Council-held records relating to the\nboundaries, complaints or enquiries about this area.\n\nI recognise that the official VG41 registration file is understood to be\nheld by Vale of Glamorgan Council. I am requesting records held by\nLlandough Community Council, including any copies, correspondence,\nrepresentations or referrals concerning the green or its registration. I am\nnot suggesting that this Council is the custodian of another authority's\nfile.\n\nPlease search relevant paper and electronic records, including\ncorrespondence and records held in archived or transferred collections that\nremain accessible to the Council. If any part of this request is considered\ntoo broad, please identify the specific part and explain what narrower\nsearch is practicable. If records are not held, please say so and identify\nany known transfer or deposit location where possible.\n\n*4. Subject access request — my personal data*\n\nSeparately, I make a subject access request under Article 15 of the UK GDPR\nand the Data Protection Act 2018 for personal data about me, Requester,\nheld by the Council.\n\nThe request covers personal data concerning my childhood association with\nGreat House Farm, the events surrounding the removal of my family from the\nfarmhouse and its demolition in 1988, and subsequent Council records\nreferring to me or recording information about me in connection with these\nevents.\n\nPlease search relevant Council-held correspondence, minutes, reports,\nnotes, referrals, photographs and other records, whether paper or\nelectronic. This request concerns my personal data; it is not a request for\nunrestricted disclosure of every record concerning my late father or the\nproperty.\n\nPlease acknowledge this as a subject access request and deal with it under\nthe applicable data-protection procedure. If you require proportionate\nidentification information to verify my identity, please specify what is\nrequired.\n\n*5. Matters said to be for the “October meeting”*\n\nYour email of 11 October states that other matters raised in my\ncorrespondence “will be considered at the October meeting of the council”\nand that you will provide the Council's views afterwards.\n\nPlease confirm whether that meeting has already taken place.\n\nIf it has, please provide the exact date, the relevant agenda and papers,\nthe minutes or other formal record of consideration, and any decision or\naction arising in relation to the matters I raised.\n\nIf it has not yet taken place, please provide the confirmed date, time,\nvenue and public-access arrangements, together with the draft or published\nagenda showing which of my matters are scheduled for consideration.\n\nThose matters include:\n\n   - The Council's historic knowledge of Great House Farm, its surroundings\n   and the green, and any representations or records it holds concerning these\n   matters.\n   - The available documentary and photographic record relating to the site\n   and its redevelopment.\n   - The concerns raised about the adjoining woodland and garden\n   boundaries, ins