y route to the information.\n   - Whether the requested environmental information must be considered\n   under the Environmental Information Regulations 2004, rather than being\n   refused solely by reference to the Freedom of Information Act cost limit.\n\nPlease identify how the stated 20 minutes for reading and 10 minutes for\nassessment per set correspond to the permitted activities under regulation\n4(3) of the Freedom of Information and Data Protection (Appropriate Limit\nand Fees) Regulations 2004. In particular, distinguish time spent\ndetermining whether the requested information is held, locating, retrieving\nor extracting it from time spent on activities that cannot lawfully be\nincluded in the section 12 estimate.\n\nIf the Council relies on regulation 12(4)(b) of the Environmental\nInformation Regulations, please identify that exception expressly, provide\nthe relevant reasoning and carry out the required public-interest\nassessment, including the presumption in favour of disclosure. Do not treat\nthe FOIA section 12 estimate as a substitute for determining which legal\nregime applies to each category.\n\nPlease also address the duty to provide reasonable advice and assistance\nunder section 16 FOIA and, where applicable, regulation 9 of the\nEnvironmental Information Regulations. Your suggestion that I narrow the\ndate range to 2002–2006 is noted, but I ask the reviewer to consider what\ntargeted searches or available indexes could reasonably be used to address\nthe original request before concluding that the information cannot be\nprovided.\n\nPlease confirm receipt of this internal review request, identify who will\nconduct the review, and provide the anticipated response date. Where\nreasonably practicable, the review should be undertaken by someone\nindependent of the original refusal.\n3. Further request for recorded information — 1986–1990 and associated land\nmatters\n\nIn addition to the original request, please treat this section as a new\nreq