our actions can have on some of the most vulnerable members of our community.\nThose responsible showed no such regard.\n\nThe images above, captured by Peter Griffiths of Vale Sports Arena, demonstrate the extent of the problem.\n\nThey show both the number of fireworks set off and the number of people involved.\n\nIt also shows authorities must treat stopping this behaviour as a priority.\n\nThe noise caused is distressing for many people, including the elderly and those with small children.\n\nIt's also distressing for animals, which is a particular problem with Cardiff Dogs Home located within the vicinity.\n\nhttps://www.facebook.com/share/v/1F4JUgcVux/\n\nI've contacted both South Wales Police and the Vale of Glamorgan Council to request the following things:\n* Effective surveillance equipment be installed urgently to identify those responsible;\n* Regular nighttime police patrols in the area to stop and apprehend those causing these disturbances;\n* Access to the area to be limited to prevent people using it to set off fireworks.\n\nIt is a criminal offence to set off fireworks past 11pm at night.\n\nThere are also many other laws and regulations that may have been broken.\n\nWhile Thursday's incident commenced at around 10.30pm, there have been several previous instances in which the law has clearly been breached.\n\nIt is punishable by an unlimited fine and up to six months imprisonment.\n\nIt is time for the law to be enforced.\n\nThose responsible for causing distress to the vulnerable in Llandough cannot be allowed to think the law does not apply to them.\n\nIf you any information about this incident, Requester, please come forward. It will be treated in the utmost confidence.\n\n\n**\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nNEXT WEEK:\nRedlands Road diversion\n\nRedlands Road will be closed between 9th and 11th October to vehicles travelling towards Dinas Powys towards Llandough.\n\nThis is because the right hand turn will be closed to allow gas works to be carried out safely.\n\nDetails of the diversion can be found above.\n\nAs you'll see, drivers are being told to travel past the Merrie Harrier and the Barons Court to the Cardiff Bay roundabout, where they will be able to turn around and travel back towards Penarth, before using the left hand turn to access Redlands Road.\n\nThe route for vehicles travelling in the opposite direction is unaffected.\n\nWhile this is unlikely to be particularly problematic for Llandough residents, I'm concerned some drivers may attempt to use the Dochdwy estate or the Merrie Harrier car park to turn around, potentially causing a bottleneck.\n\nI've therefore put together a short video, explaining the diversion route in place, which avoids our village.\n\nIt would be helpful if you would share it on social media, Requester.\n\nTo do so, you can click below now.\nhttps://www.facebook.com/share/r/1CJhqM9XTD/\n\nRoad markings:\nPen-y-turnpike Road\n\nThe road markings at the junction between Pen-y-turnpike Road and Leckwith Road have now been repainted.\n\nWe secured these works together, by pressuring the Council to get them done.\n\nIt may seem like a small thing, but making it clear to drivers they need to give way will help stop accidents.\n\nThank you to everyone across Llandough, Leckwith and Michaelston-le-Pit who helped.\nhttps://www.facebook.com/share/r/1EaRtbbAVJ/\n\nPreventing overdevelopment\n\nWhen I'm out and about in our community, many residents express concerns about overdevelopment and the way it threatens our village's distinct identity and character.\n\nThe proposals to build 250 houses on Eastbrook Road are a good example.\n\nWhile outside our village, the site is located just down from the Merrie Harrier, on green fields that form a natural boundary between Llandough and Dinas Powys.\n\nThis boundary is crucial in ensuring both villages remain separate, unique entities.\n\nYou can watch my comments at the last Full Council meeting below.\n\nhttps://www.facebook.com/share/r/1Fff7ZbyuK/\n\nDochdwy Road pavement defects\n\nHuge thanks to the resident who got in touch about pavement defects on Dochdwy Road.\n\nAs you'll see, these have now all been marked up for repair.\n\nBy getting in touch, the resident helped ensure the issue was taken forward quickly.\n\nWhen you contact me about things you spot, you're not causing a bother.\n\nTo the contrary, you're playing a crucial role in our team, as we work to improve things in our village.\n\nThat's what Our Plan for Llandough is all about!\n\nhttps://www.facebook.com/share/r/1Hy7k64Xw5/\n\nCommunity drop-in event:\n20th October\n\nOur next community drop-in event takes place at the Merrie Harrier on 20th October.\n\nLike the last one, it'll start at 7.00pm.\n\nThe pub is a key asset to our community.\n\nTracey and the team do a lot for us - help return the favour by lending them your support.\n\nYou can see some of their midweek offers below!\n\nhttps://www.facebook.com/share/r/1EM9z5wbMs/\n\nOur Plan for Llandough!\n\n\nAs your councillor, I'm working with you all to secure a better future for Llandough. Together, we have a plan to deliver for the village.\n\nOur plan is making Llandough better by:\n\n1. Sorting out the parking problems\n2. Stamping out dog fouling\n3. Repairing our roads\n4. Stopping littering and flytipping\n5. Delivering better sports facilities\n6. Keeping the whole community involved\n\nIf you have any suggestions of what you'd like to see in Our Plan, please get in touch!\n\nhttp://www.facebook.com/sharer/sharer.php?u=https%3A%2F%2Fmailchi.mp%2F04504f486a28%2Fin-touch-with-llandough-17666248 Share (http://www.facebook.com/sharer/sharer.php?u=https%3A%2F%2Fmailchi.mp%2F04504f486a28%2Fin-touch-with-llandough-17666248)\nhttps://us19.forward-to-friend.com/forward?u=7b7a08284cdbc1f69f5b0afd8&id=5279f843b3&e=1429c2c1bb Forward (https://us19.forward-to-friend.com/forward?u=7b7a08284cdbc1f69f5b0afd8&id=5279f843b3&e=1429c2c1bb)\n\n============================================================\nI'm hoping to reach as many people as possible with this newsletter, so please feel free to forward it to friends and neighbours.\n\nIf this email has been forwarded to you, and you would like to sign up to my newsletter, please click ** here ([email redacted]\n.\nThis email was sent to you because your email address is on my subscribers list. If you don't want to receive any more messages from me, please click below to unsubscribe.\n\nYour consent was previously given during an online or offline interaction with me or the Conservatives. If you feel this is incorrect or would like to comment, please contact me. I do not buy email lists.\n\nPromoted by Chris Kirkham on behalf of George Carroll, both of Vale of Glamorgan Conservatives, Holton House, 50 Holton Road, Barry [postcode redacted].\n\nThis email was sent to [email redacted] ([email redacted]\nwhy did I get this? (https://facebook.us19.list-manage.com/about?u=7b7a08284cdbc1f69f5b0afd8&id=daad00f7e3&e=1429c2c1bb&c=5279f843b3)     unsubscribe from this list (***REDACTED***     update subscription preferences (https://facebook.us19.list-manage.com/profile?u=7b7a08284cdbc1f69f5b0afd8&id=daad00f7e3&e=1429c2c1bb&c=5279f843b3)\nPolitician . Unit 5 Pro Copy Business Centre . Parc Ty Glas . Cardiff, Crf [postcode redacted] . USA"},{"id":"gm-1a115341cd46142d","subject":"RE: FOI 2026-036 – Great House Farm, Llandough – filtered schedule still not supplied; unanswered points from FOI 2026-032 correction","name":"RE: FOI 2026-036 – Great House Farm, Llandough – filtered schedule still not supplied; unanswered points from FOI 2026-032 correction","date":"2026-10-07T07:11:32Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a115341cd46142d.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a1149cc3b8ce394-foi-2026-036-great-house-farm-llandough-filtered-schedule-still-not-supplied-unanswered-points-from-foi-2026-032-correct","scopeTopics":["museum"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["museum"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a1149cc3b8ce394","direction":"received","from":"FOI Requests","institutions":[{"id":"museum","name":"Amgueddfa Cymru - Museum Wales"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a115341cd46142d.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester\n\nThank you for your email.\n\nIn relation to the filtered schedule, Amgueddfa Cymru does not hold a separate recorded document identifying, item by item, which search results were excluded following review. Search results were reviewed by staff to determine whether they related to the location specified in your request, and the outcome of that review was reflected in our response of 5 October 2026. We do not hold a separate audit schedule of those decisions.\n\nIn relation to MS 3360/3, the relevant page was previously supplied in response to FOI 2026-032. That page records that \"Great House\" formed part of the Llandough Estate sale particulars of 1924, was occupied by W.G. Thomas & Sons, comprised approximately 202 acres, was situated in the Parish of Llanfair, and describes the homestead as being situated in the Village of Llandough. No additional recorded information identifying the holding has been identified within the material reviewed.\n\nIn relation to Ref 13.2, the collections record describes the item as a \"Waterwheel from Llandough Farm (opposite hospital)\". No further location information has been identified within the record.\n\nWe trust this clarifies the position.\n\nCofion cynnes,\n\nFOI Team\n[email redacted]\namgueddfa.cymru | museum.wales\n \nRhif elusen | Charity number 525774\n\n\n\n-----Original Message-----\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]> \nSent: 07 October 2026 05:29\nTo: FOI Requests <[email redacted]>\nSubject: RE: FOI 2026-036 – Great House Farm, Llandough – filtered schedule still not supplied; unanswered points from FOI 2026-032 correction\n\nThis email comes from outside the organisation.\nDo not click links or open attachments unless it is an email you expected to receive.\nDear FOI Team,\n\nFOI 2026-036 – response of 5 October 2026\n\nThank you for the letter. I note the additional records identified (Ref 13.2 waterwheel and the page of MS 3360/3).\n\nWhat remains unanswered\nOn 27 September I corrected the place names in the FOI 2026-032 pack, identifying at least PAS 2022.18.1 (NGR SS9934672805) and a separate Great House Farm entry at Llandow as belonging to different properties. Your reply of 28 September accepted the distinction: the collections system does not group by similar names, no recorded cross-attribution is held, and those hits arose from the search rather than catalogue overlap.\n\nThe 5 October letter does not supply a cleaned / filtered schedule showing which of the FOI 2026-032 items survive that filter. Until that is provided, the cross-reference still carries the Cowbridge and Llandow material. That is the point left open on 28 September and still open now.\n\nWhat you have not said\nYou have not stated, item by item, which of the earlier schedule entries are now excluded as relating to Great House Farm near Cowbridge or Great House Farm, Llandow. You have not confirmed the parish of the page of MS 3360/3 that you identify as relating to Great House Farm (which Llandough, and any vendor / tenant / acreage / parcel detail that identifies the holding). You have not confirmed whether Ref 13.2 (waterwheel collected 1 April 1979 from “Llandough Farm”, donor Wales and West Housing Association) is recorded as the Penarth site opposite the hospital, or as the farmhouse demolished by BP on 6 December 1988.\n\nPlease therefore supply:\n1. A filtered schedule of the FOI 2026-032 records that remain after exclusion of all items relating to Great House Farm / Llandough near Cowbridge and to Great House Farm, Llandow. For each surviving entry:\naccession or archive number, title, date, and one-line description.\n2. Explicit parish confirmation for the page of MS 3360/3 identified as relating to Great House Farm: which Llandough, and any vendor / tenant / acreage / parcel detail that identifies the holding.\n3. Confirmation whether Ref 13.2 is recorded as the Penarth site opposite the hospital, or as the farmhouse demolished by BP on 6 December 1988.\n\nThese are requests for recorded information already within the searches undertaken. I am not seeking new searches or opinions. The unanswered point is the filter that you accepted was necessary on 28 September but have not yet applied in a disclosed schedule.\n\nCofion,\n\nRequester \n"},{"id":"gm-1a1149f6236df4ed","subject":"RE: FOI 2026-036 – Great House Farm, Llandough – filtered schedule still not supplied; unanswered points from FOI 2026-032 correction","name":"RE: FOI 2026-036 – Great House Farm, Llandough – filtered schedule still not supplied; unanswered points from FOI 2026-032 correction","date":"2026-10-07T04:29:12Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a1149f6236df4ed.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a1149cc3b8ce394-foi-2026-036-great-house-farm-llandough-filtered-schedule-still-not-supplied-unanswered-points-from-foi-2026-032-correct","scopeTopics":["museum"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["museum"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a1149cc3b8ce394","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"museum","name":"Amgueddfa Cymru - Museum Wales"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a1149f6236df4ed.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear FOI Team,\n\nFOI 2026-036 – response of 5 October 2026\n\nThank you for the letter. I note the additional records identified (Ref 13.2 waterwheel and the page of MS 3360/3).\n\nWhat remains unanswered\nOn 27 September I corrected the place names in the FOI 2026-032 pack, identifying at least PAS 2022.18.1 (NGR SS9934672805) and a separate Great House Farm entry at Llandow as belonging to different properties. Your reply of 28 September accepted the distinction: the collections system does not group by similar names, no recorded cross-attribution is held, and those hits arose from the search rather than catalogue overlap.\n\nThe 5 October letter does not supply a cleaned / filtered schedule showing which of the FOI 2026-032 items survive that filter. Until that is provided, the cross-reference still carries the Cowbridge and Llandow material. That is the point left open on 28 September and still open now.\n\nWhat you have not said\nYou have not stated, item by item, which of the earlier schedule entries are now excluded as relating to Great House Farm near Cowbridge or Great House Farm, Llandow. You have not confirmed the parish of the page of MS 3360/3 that you identify as relating to Great House Farm (which Llandough, and any vendor / tenant / acreage / parcel detail that identifies the holding). You have not confirmed whether Ref 13.2 (waterwheel collected 1 April 1979 from “Llandough Farm”, donor Wales and West Housing Association) is recorded as the Penarth site opposite the hospital, or as the farmhouse demolished by BP on 6 December 1988.\n\nPlease therefore supply:\n1. A filtered schedule of the FOI 2026-032 records that remain after exclusion of all items relating to Great House Farm / Llandough near Cowbridge and to Great House Farm, Llandow. For each surviving entry: accession or archive number, title, date, and one-line description.\n2. Explicit parish confirmation for the page of MS 3360/3 identified as relating to Great House Farm: which Llandough, and any vendor / tenant / acreage / parcel detail that identifies the holding.\n3. Confirmation whether Ref 13.2 is recorded as the Penarth site opposite the hospital, or as the farmhouse demolished by BP on 6 December 1988.\n\nThese are requests for recorded information already within the searches undertaken. I am not seeking new searches or opinions. The unanswered point is the filter that you accepted was necessary on 28 September but have not yet applied in a disclosed schedule.\n\nCofion,\n\nRequester \n"},{"id":"gm-1a1149f606c1eb09","subject":"Re: Great House Farm, Llandough – Request 00210772 – Internal Review – Formal Chase – unanswered points from 30 September and 11 September","name":"Re: Great House Farm, Llandough – Request 00210772 – Internal Review – Formal Chase – unanswered points from 30 September and 11 September","date":"2026-10-07T04:29:11Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a1149f606c1eb09.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a1149cf594b0667-great-house-farm-llandough-request-00210772-internal-review-formal-chase-unanswered-points-from-30-september-and-11-sept","scopeTopics":["vale-foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["vale-foi"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a1149cf594b0667","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"vale-foi","name":"Vale of Glamorgan Council"}],"refs":["00210772"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a1149f606c1eb09.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Information Governance Team\ncc: Monitoring Officer; Data Protection Officer\n\nCase Ref 00210772 — Internal Review requested 7 July 2026\n\nThis is a formal chase of unanswered points already put to you. It is not a new request and must not be used to restart any clock.\n\nOn 11 September 2026 you wrote that service areas had found further historical documentation, that you were still sifting it, and that because some of it is “not necessarily Council documentation but is nonetheless in the Council’s possession” you were considering whether to consult third parties. You said you would try to give a clearer timetable by the end of the following week (i.e. by 18 September 2026).\n\nOn 30 September 2026 I asked, by Tuesday 6 October 2026, for:\n1. A firm date by which the completed internal review decision will be issued;\n2. A non-disclosive description of the further documentation located (date range, creator/supplier, how it came into the Council’s possession, approximate volume);\n3. Confirmation that the newly located documentation is being preserved without alteration, destruction or disposal; and\n4. Immediate release of any material that can properly be disclosed without awaiting third-party consultation.\n\nNone of those four points has been answered.\n\nWhat you have not said\nYou have not identified a single document found in the September sift. You have not named any third-party consultee class, the date any consultation was sent, or the date by which a reply was required. You have not stated whether the material is held under EIR or FOIA. You have not addressed the child / personal-data point previously raised. You have not confirmed a preservation hold. Silence on each of these points is itself a recorded position.\n\nWhat is now required (unchanged from the unanswered requests)\n(a) Issue the internal review decision. On any view the ICO’s recommended maximum of 40 working days from the request for review (7 July 2026) has long passed. If you contend that EIR does not apply, say so expressly and identify the statutory basis for treating the request under FOIA alone.\n\n(b) Attach a schedule of every document found in the further search: date, description, format, holding service, whether Council or third-party record in the Council’s possession, and the decision on each (disclose / part-disclose / withhold with the precise regulation or section).\n\n(c) If any third-party consultation has occurred or is proposed, name the class of consultee, the date the consultation was sent, and the deadline set for reply. A consultee’s silence is not a ground to withhold.\n\n(d) Confirm in writing that every document in the sift, and every related planning, enforcement, legal, committee, land, finance and correspondence record (including predecessor-authority material), is under a formal preservation hold and will not be destroyed, weeded, transferred or disposed of while 00210772 and related requests remain open.\n\n(e) Address the child / personal-data point: if any record has been withheld or is being considered for withholding because it mentions a child, identify the record and the exemption. If no such withholding exists, say so expressly. Treat the personal-data element also as a subject-access request under UK GDPR for Requester (child occupant in 1988).\n\nThe 7 July 2026 answer treated the matter as a planning-register query. That was too narrow. The sift must cover the full list previously set out (planning files including unpublished appendices, enforcement, building control, environmental health, demolition, legal-services files, member/officer correspondence, land/estates files, finance records, and predecessor-authority transfers to Glamorgan Archives).\n\nPublic-accountability questions (to be answered from the documents, including those found in September)\n1. Was any Council or predecessor expenditure, fee, cost, grant or works associated with the occupation, demolition or redevelopment?\n2. Was any Council or predecessor land, right, permission or undertaking involved?\n3. Were officers or members told of anything that contradicted or qualified the ownership, occupation, heritage or demolition account then being used?\n4. What accounting, audit, legal, planning, committee and correspondence records survive?\n5. Was any irregularity, conflict, material error or control failure identified, reported, or formally accounted for?\n6. If the Council’s position is that there is nothing to examine, state that expressly and identify the documents relied on.\n\nBy return\nWithin 5 working days: the document schedule, the preservation confirmation, and either the child-exemption notice or a clear statement that none exists.\nWithin 10 working days: the internal review decision with copies of everything you are able to disclose.\n\nThis email records that the four points put to you on 30 September 2026 remain unanswered as of 6 October 2026. If no substantive response is received, the continuing failure to complete the internal review will be referred to the Information Commissioner without further notice.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester \n"},{"id":"gm-1a1149f5fe45fa0d","subject":"N610CGV — WA231076 / WA240304 — 1924–1930 instruments and any closed parent title","name":"N610CGV — WA231076 / WA240304 — 1924–1930 instruments and any closed parent title","date":"2026-10-07T04:29:11Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a1149f5fe45fa0d.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a1149d11bef0045-n610cgv-wa231076-wa240304-1924-1930-instruments-and-any-closed-parent-title","scopeTopics":["hmlr"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["hmlr"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a1149d11bef0045","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"hmlr","name":"HM Land Registry"}],"refs":["N610CGV"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a1149f5fe45fa0d.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Mr Haste / Customer Support\n\nReference: N610CGV\nYour letter: 30 July 2026\nAlso: 260918-6720625; 260919-6726246; 260929-6766714\nTitles: WA231076 and WA240304 (closed)\nProperty: land on the north side of Leckwith Road, Llandough (Great House Farm / Tÿ Mawr)\n\nThis is not a complaint, not a request to reopen the stage 2 review of 25 May 2026, and not a request that you determine ownership.\n\nI am asking you to search the historic files you have already retrieved and to say whether a 1924–1930 instrument for the house plot is on them.\n\nFamily evidence states that John Williams registered the house and garden parcel in 1928, and that an agreement of 15 May 1924 is on the WA231076 file. Llandough was not a compulsory-registration area in 1928. I need to know whether any voluntary first registration, or any filed conveyance or agreement of that date, exists.\n\nPlease answer these points, and send copies by email to [email redacted] where the document is held:\n\n1. Any title number opened between 1924 and 1930 for this land, including a voluntary first registration in the name of John Williams, and any closed title later absorbed into WA231076 or WA240304. If none, please say none.\n\n2. Historical editions of the register and title plan for WA231076 and WA240304 (form HC1), including the edition of 23 February 1983 already listed in your 30 July letter.\n\n3. The agreement of 15 May 1924, if filed on WA231076 or any parent title, with its plan.\n\n4. Any other filed instrument dated 1924–1930, and any entry for such an instrument on the 1971 abstract of title or the A13-DL list of documents lodged with first registration.\n\n5. If a document in 3 or 4 is not held, please say it is not held, rather than omit it.\n\nIf email copies will not be issued, confirm each item is or is not held and give the OC2 / HC1 wording and the postal address so the forms can go in without a further inventory letter.\n\nPlease reply to this email.\n\nRequester \n"},{"id":"gm-1a1149f5f9fe3964","subject":"Internal review — CAS-347296-M0V4Y1 / CRM:007302479 — Great House Farm / Tÿ Mawr, Llandough","name":"Internal review — CAS-347296-M0V4Y1 / CRM:007302479 — Great House Farm / Tÿ Mawr, Llandough","date":"2026-10-07T04:29:11Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a1149f5f9fe3964.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a1149d274a72440-internal-review-cas-347296-m0v4y1-crm-007302479-great-house-farm-t-mawr-llandough","scopeTopics":["tna"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["tna"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a1149d274a72440","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"tna","name":"The National Archives"}],"refs":["CAS-347296"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a1149f5f9fe3964.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Quality Manager\nThe National Archives\n[email redacted]\ncc: [email redacted]\n\nSirs/Madam,\n\nInternal review of CAS-347296-M0V4Y1 / CRM:007302479, disclosure dated 23 September 2026.\nRequester: Requester.\n\nI am not asking TNA to decide title. I am asking whether the disclosure answered the request.\n\nThe request asked whether records relating to Great House Farm / Tÿ Mawr and to BP Properties Ltd v Buckler exist in your systems, where they came from, and whether they were transferred, retained, destroyed or disposed of. A finding that TNA does not itself hold a court file is not a finding that no transfer, accession or destruction entry exists.\n\nPlease determine, on review:\n\n1. Which catalogues, transfer lists and destruction registers were searched.\n2. Whether any WELSH OFFICE, court-administration, HO or DEFE series was considered and excluded, and why.\n3. Whether the destroyed European Commission file in Buckler v United Kingdom, Application 14464/88 (file destroyed in 2000; decision of 14 April 1989 remains on HUDOC), has any TNA counterpart or transfer note.\n4. What, if anything, is held about the existence or disposal of those records, as distinct from the records themselves.\n\nThe public evidence index is https://greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com/ . The 1987 Court of Appeal decided possession, limitation and the October 1974 letters. It did not decide the documentary root of the farmhouse parcel. Transfer and destruction entries go to that separate question. They are evidential questions, not findings against TNA.\n\nPlease preserve anything in scope while the review is open. Nothing in this letter abandons any claim. This is not a claim form.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester \n"},{"id":"gm-1a11178b91ab138c","subject":"RC26-0375 (gan gynnwys RC26-0353, -0368, a -0371) - Ty Mawr/ Great House, Llandough, Cardiff - Further response","name":"RC26-0375 (gan gynnwys RC26-0353, -0368, a -0371) - Ty Mawr/ Great House, Llandough, Cardiff - Further response","date":"2026-10-06T13:47:33Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a11178b91ab138c.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a11178b91ab138c-rc26-0375-gan-gynnwys-rc26-0353-0368-a-0371-ty-mawr-great-house-llandough-cardiff-further-response","scopeTopics":["rcahmw"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["rcahmw"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a11178b91ab138c","direction":"received","from":"NMR Enquiries","institutions":[{"id":"rcahmw","name":"RCAHMW (Royal Commission)"}],"refs":["RC26-0353","RC26-0375"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a11178b91ab138c.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester,\n\nWith regards to your email of 30th September, I note that many of the queries which you have set have already been addressed in our previous correspondence. In particular, please note that our searches have been extensive, including searches of any possibly relevant registered files, the only administrative and operational records and materials held in the long term, and the NMRW archive, including both term-based and spatial searches in the archive catalogue, and a manual search for any additional, uncatalogued material. This will be more fully addressed in point 1 as I take your questions in order, below.\n\n1) Our searches have been extensive. This has included searches of any possibly relevant registered files, the only administrative records and materials held in the long term. As I have noted, we do not appear to hold any records or correspondence in these records relating to your enquiries, or to Great House Farm more generally, including the circumstances of its demolition. Our searches have also included full searches of the NMRW archive, including both term-based and spatial searches in the archive catalogue, and a manual search for any additional, uncatalogued material. The term-based searches have included all relevant site names and related terms, as you have enumerated them. These searches have therefore included searches for all of the types of records which you have enumerated, with the exception of our historic aerial photography collections, which searches would incur a charge (see point 6, below).\n\n2) As has been noted in my previous correspondence, no record of this correspondence or allusion to the same is extant in our records, with the exception of the brief note in Archive 6048945. For the scope of our comprehensive searches undertaken to determine this, see point 1, above.\n\n3) Regarding Archive Number 6048945, as I noted in my email of 29 September, if you would like to order a scan of this record, please complete the attached order form. The record comprises one page, front and back, and would therefore require two scans. Please note that we waive fees for small orders of fewer than 5 research-quality scans for personal, non-commercial research. The record is part of our NMR Site Files Collection, for details of which, please see the collection-level entry in our archive catalogue, here: https://coflein.gov.uk/en/archives/6202405. As I further noted in my email of 29 September, there is no surviving record giving the date or purpose of that visit beyond the description currently catalogued, nor any associated site note, field sheet, photograph, sketch or correspondence.\n\n4) Searches of our internal records relating to these accessions numbers have revealed no further information beyond that which is available in the publicly accessible archive catalogue and noted in my email of 29th September.\n\n5) These records have been thoroughly, physically reviewed and it has been determined that they relate to the sites stated in the Archive Catalogue. They do not relate or allude to Great House Farm, Llandough in any way.\n\n6) With regard to aerial photography, as noted in my email of 29 September, a search of our aerial photography collections, including to determine whether there is aerial photography coverage of the site, would incur a charge (see attached price list). As was further noted in my email of 29 September, if any aerial photographs covering the area of the site do exist, these would likely be vertical aerial photography, taken from a high altitude during routine recording of the landscape and would be unlikely to show the site in any detail. There is no further photographic record of the site in our collection.\n\n7) As noted in previous correspondence, the 1974 ‘investigator's notes for Great House Farm, Llandough, including sketch plan showing periods of construction’ by H. J. Thomas is Archive Number 6048943 (https://coflein.gov.uk/en/archives/6048943). The ‘ink drawing showing plan of Great House Farm, Llandough, showing periods of construction. Scale 1:100. Additions made 12/12/1988’ is Archive Number 6048941 (https://coflein.gov.uk/en/archives/6048941). A scan of the item Archive Number 6048943 can be ordered using the attached order form, while, as noted in my emails of 28 May and 29 September, a copy of Archive Number 6048941 is included in the report available here: https://www.walesher1974.org/her/app/php/herumd.php?group=GGAT&level=3&docid=301361628. As I have noted throughout our correspondence, we do not hold anything beyond the material already visible on Coflein.\n\n8) There is no record of this.\n\n9) We hold no such providence information.\n\n10) As noted in my email of 29 September and in point 3, above, we do not hold any information relating to the circumstances of the December 1988 visit outside of Archive Number 6048945.\n\n11) See point 1, above. Physical searches were undertaken of all relevant Registered Files and key collections, such as the Site Files, which may have contained uncatalogued material. In both cases nothing of any relevance was identified beyond the records enumerated in the online archive catalogue here: https://coflein.gov.uk/en/sites/20182/archives. Comprehensive searches were carried out on both Coflein and Historic Wales, as well as in our internal archive catalogue and details of these searches are noted in point 1, above. All identified archive material has been carefully examined and read by our enquiries staff. All relevant records and archive staff have been consulted. No potentially relevant material has been excluded for any reason.\n\n12) In accordance with the suggestion contained in your email of 20 September, my email of 29 September fully addressed, and was in reply to, all enquiries of 8, 15, 17, and 20 September, and I therefore consider all of these enquiries to have now received a full response. We have established that we hold no internal, administrative, or operational records related to your enquiries, nor any which relate to Great House Farm, and that the only records of any relevance which we hold are publicly accessible records in the National Monuments Record of Wales archives. No information is being withheld. All enquiries therefore relate to publicly available records and should be considered as ‘publicly accessible to the applicant by other means’, namely our public enquiries service: https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2000/36/section/21.\n\nOur FOI policy can be seen here: https://rcahmw.gov.uk/about-us/corporate-information/policies/freedom-of-information-policy/.\n\nSincerely,\n\nAdam Coward\n\n\n\n\nNMR Enquiries\nYmholiadau Cyhoeddus I Public Enquiries\nFfordd Penglais | Penglais Road, Aberystwyth, [postcode redacted]\n[phone redacted]\n[email redacted] | [email redacted]\nwww.cbhc.gov.uk | www.rcahmw.gov.uk\nNoddir gan Lywodraeth Cymru | Sponsored by Welsh Government\nRydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg a Saesneg. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn peri oedi.\nWe welcome correspondence in Welsh and English. Corresponding in Welsh will not lead to any delay.\n\nYn unol â Rheoliadau Safonau'r Gymraeg (Rhif 2) 2016, mae gennych hawl i gyfathrebu a gohebu â Chomisiwn Brenhinol Henebion Cymru yn eich dewis iaith. Er mwyn sicrhau ein bod yn cyflawni’r hawl, rhowch wybod i ni a ydych yn dymuno derbyn gohebiaeth a/neu alwadau ffôn oddi wrthym yn y Gymraeg. Bydd yr wybodaeth hon yn cael ei chofnodi gennym ni, a byddwn yn defnyddio'r iaith o’ch dewis ym mhob cyfathrebu yn y dyfodol. Diolch.\nUnder the Welsh Language Standards (No. 2) Regulations 2016, you have the right to communicate and correspond with the Royal Commission on the Ancient and Historical Monuments of Wales in your preferred language. To ensure we uphold this right, please let us know whether you wish to receive correspondence and/or telephone calls from us in Welsh. This information will be recorded by us, and we will use your preferred language in all future communication. Thank you.\n​\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]>\nSent: 30 September 2026 01:12\nTo: NMR Enquiries <[email redacted]>\nSubject: Re: RC26-0375 (gan gynnwys RC26-0353, -0368, a -0371) - Ty Mawr/ Great House, Llandough, Cardiff\n\nDear Adam,\n\nThank you for your detailed response of 29 September (RC26-0375). It is particularly helpful to have your confirmation that Archive 6048945 records Richard Suggett's visit to the site after its demolition in December 1988, and for identifying Archives 6048941, 6048942 and 6048944.\n\nI have considered your response alongside the Commission's catalogue entries and the other records now available. Before any of the outstanding September requests are treated as concluded, I need to clarify one distinction.\n\nYou state (as in your emails of 28 May, 10 June and 11 June) that there are no letters or records concerning Tŷ Mawr Farm, Llandough, in the Commission's registered files, which you describe as the only campaign records kept in the long term.\n\nI note that answer. My requests were not confined to registered campaign files. They concern recorded information held by the Commission relating to the site, including records which your own archive identifies as existing, lost, received or created in connection with it.\n\nI am happy for the points below to be answered in whatever order is most convenient, provided the FOIA/EIR status and original dates of the September requests are preserved. Where a point has already been answered in full, a short confirmation that nothing further is held will suffice; I do not wish the Commission to repeat itself.\n\n1. Scope of the search\n\nPlease confirm whether the search has extended beyond registered files to other relevant NMRW collections and systems, including (where applicable):\n\n- Inventory working papers, drafts, record cards and historic index cards;\n- field and survey notes;\n- measured drawings, sketches, plans and elevations;\n- maps and plan collections;\n- photographic collections, negatives and indexes;\n- vertical and oblique aerial photography and its indexes;\n- grey literature and unpublished reports;\n- special, personal or specialist collections;\n- background records;\n- historic administrative records;\n- digital and legacy catalogue systems;\n- accession and deposit records;\n- transfer, withdrawal, de-accession or disposal records; and\n- uncatalogued or separately catalogued material reasonably capable of containing information about the site.\n\nPlease also confirm whether the search used the principal known identifiers and variants, including:\n\nGreat House Farm; Great House; Tŷ Mawr; Ty Mawr Farm; Llandough; Llandochau; Llandough-juxta-Penarth; NPRN 20182; ST 1681 7331; ST16707335; GGAT02038s; Inventory site 425; NA/GL/88/49; NA/GL/89/208; RCS2/1/1777; RCS2/1/1776; 6048941; 6048942; 6048944; 6048945; C.N. Johns; CN Johns; and Mrs Buckler.\n\nIf any of the categories above were not searched, a simple statement to that effect is sufficient.\n\n2. The lost C.N. Johns / Mrs Buckler correspondence\n\nThe surviving Suggett record (Archive 6048945) identifies correspondence between C.N. Johns and Mrs Buckler as having been lost from the file since 1986/87.\n\nYour response establishes that the correspondence itself cannot now be located. Please therefore confirm whether the Commission has also searched for any residual records about that correspondence, including:\n\n- a file cover or wrapper;\n- enclosure or contents list;\n- accession or receipt entry;\n- issue, loan or handling record;\n- reader-request record;\n- catalogue or index card;\n- duplicate copy;\n- draft, extract or summary;\n- contemporary note concerning its absence;\n- later note concerning its loss;\n- correspondence concerning its loss;\n- transfer or return record; or\n- any other record identifying when or how the correspondence ceased to be with the file.\n\nIf none of those records exists, please say so expressly. The question is no longer simply whether the correspondence survives, but whether the surviving archive contains any documentary trace of its provenance and disappearance.\n\n3. Archive 6048945\n\nPlease provide the complete surviving contents of Archive 6048945 (every page, card, continuation sheet, annotation and enclosure).\n\nPlease also confirm whether 6048945 is a complete standalone record, part of a larger file or collection, an extract separated from a parent record, or linked in any catalogue, accession or administrative system to other records concerning Great House Farm/Tŷ Mawr.\n\nYou have confirmed that Suggett visited the site after demolition. Please also confirm whether there is any surviving record giving the date or purpose of that visit beyond the description currently catalogued, or any associated site note, field sheet, photograph, sketch or correspondence.\n\n4. NA/GL/88/49 and NA/GL/89/208\n\nThank you for explaining the relationship between those admission references and Archives 6048945 and 6048941.\n\nPlease confirm whether the Commission retains any admission register entry, receipt, accession sheet, contents list, file wrapper, box/listing record, catalogue amendment, transfer record or other metadata showing what was originally received under NA/GL/88/49 and NA/GL/89/208.\n\nIn particular, if 6048941 is the only surviving record received under NA/GL/89/208, please confirm whether that conclusion comes from an original admission record or simply from what is currently visible in the catalogue.\n\n5. RCS2/1/1777 and RCS2/1/1776\n\nI appreciate the clarification concerning Archive Numbers 6364275 and 6364276.\n\nThe separate question concerning RCS2/1/1777 remains open. Please confirm whether the contents of RCS2/1/1777 have actually been inspected (including location plan, photographs, captions and associated recording material) sufficiently to establish whether the site is Bonvilston or Llandough.\n\nIf it is Bonvilston, I will record that as resolved. If it is Llandough, please explain whether the catalogue has been or will be corrected or linked to NPRN 20182, and provide the contents and copies requested earlier.\n\nPlease also confirm whether RCS2/1/1776 has been checked for the same reason.\n\n6. Photographs and aerial photography\n\nI note your explanation that searching the aerial collection may involve a charge.\n\nBefore deciding whether to commission a paid search, please tell me what the Commission's existing indexes or catalogue systems establish about aerial coverage of the relevant location:\n\n- Is there any aerial coverage containing the site?\n- Is there any vertical or oblique coverage for the relevant period?\n- Is there any photograph taken during or after the demolition?\n- Are there catalogue entries for photographs which are no longer held?\n- Does the Commission have any image connected to NPRN 20182 which is not presently visible on Coflein?\n\nThis is relevant to Suggett's statement that there was apparently no photographic record of the site. I would like to distinguish “Suggett could identify no photographic record at the time” from “the Commission has searched its photographic collections and holds no photograph of the site”. A nil return following a defined search of the photographic and aerial indexes would itself be a useful result.\n\n7. The 1974 survey and the ownership dispute\n\nYour quotation of Archive 6048945 now establishes (as a matter of the Commission's own file) that the house remained partially recorded because of problems created by an ownership dispute. That point is therefore settled.\n\nPlease confirm whether the Commission holds anything beyond the material already visible on Coflein concerning:\n\n- H.J. Thomas's 26 April 1974 survey;\n- the 1974 site notes;\n- the 12 December 1988 additions to the plan;\n- measured drawings;\n- field notes;\n- correspondence concerning access; or\n- any decision about what could or could not be recorded because of the ownership dispute.\n\nI am not asking the Commission to adjudicate the ownership dispute. I am seeking only to establish what recording opportunities existed and what records were actually made. (For context, the Commission's post-demolition addition to the 1974 plan on 12 December 1988 is the only institutional building-recording event identified in the immediate aftermath of the demolition.)\n\n8. Transfers, retention and disposal\n\nPlease confirm whether any records concerning Great House Farm/Tŷ Mawr were ever transferred to another repository, received from another repository, returned to a depositor, withdrawn, de-accessioned, destroyed, or otherwise removed from the Commission's custody. Where such an event occurred, please provide the recorded date, description, authority/reference and destination where held.\n\nIn particular, please confirm whether relevant material was ever transferred to or received from Cadw/Welsh Office/Welsh Government, Glamorgan Archives, The National Archives, Amgueddfa Cymru, GGAT/Heneb or another institution. (I note that the public HER entry GGAT02038s now incorporates material derived from the Commission's 1988 records; any residual administrative link or transfer record relating to that would be useful.)\n\n9. The nineteenth-century deposit\n\nThank you for identifying Archives 6048942 and 6048944.\n\nI am not asking the Commission to endorse the historical account. I would like the provenance of the RCAHMW record established, so far as it can be:\n\n- what source was used to create Archive 6048942;\n- whether that source was contemporary, retrospective or copied from another publication;\n- whether RCAHMW ever examined the alleged objects itself;\n- whether any accession, transfer or correspondence record connected the account with Amgueddfa Cymru; and\n- whether the attribution that material was held by the National Museum of Wales appears elsewhere in the Commission's records.\n\nIf the Commission has no such provenance information, please say so. (For completeness, Amgueddfa Cymru has separately stated that it holds no record of such an acquisition; I raise the point only to establish the documentary trail within the Commission's own holdings.)\n\n10. The demolition sequence\n\nYour confirmation that Suggett visited after demolition makes the following question particularly important.\n\nPlease confirm whether the Commission holds any record of notification of the proposed demolition, notification during the demolition, a request for (or consideration of, or decision not to undertake) emergency recording, a site visit before, during or immediately after demolition, or contact with Cadw, GGAT, the relevant local authority, BP Properties or its agents, or Mrs Buckler or any other family member.\n\nI am aware that the Commission may not have been under a statutory duty to receive notice merely because the building was unlisted. I am not assuming otherwise. The question is simply whether any such recorded information exists. The Commission's own addition to the plan on 12 December 1988 is the only institutional recording event so far identified in the immediate post-demolition period; any residual notes or correspondence from that visit would therefore be of particular value.\n\n11. Search statement\n\nBecause the principal issue is now the distinction between “not in the registered files” and “not held anywhere by the Commission”, I would be grateful for a short factual search statement identifying:\n\n- which physical record series were searched;\n- which electronic/catalogue systems were searched;\n- the principal names, site identifiers or reference numbers used;\n- whether relevant archive staff or custodians were consulted; and\n- whether any potentially relevant material was excluded because it was uncatalogued, separately indexed, deposited or otherwise outside the registered-file series.\n\nA concise factual description is sufficient; I am not asking for an elaborate report.\n\n12. Statutory status\n\nFinally, I do not want the distinction between the Commission's public enquiry/research service and its statutory information-rights function to become blurred.\n\nMy requests of 8, 15, 17 and 20 September 2026 remain as made. I am content for the Commission to answer them together where that is administratively easier, but I am not withdrawing them or converting them into paid research enquiries.\n\nPlease therefore confirm:\n\n1. which request or requests your present email is intended to answer;\n2. whether each outstanding request remains open;\n3. whether your present email is intended to constitute a formal FOIA/EIR response;\n4. where you say information is not held, the scope of the search supporting that conclusion;\n5. where information is being withheld rather than not held, the relevant statutory basis; and\n6. the applicable internal-review procedure and the name/contact details of the officer responsible for information rights.\n\nI appreciate the distinction you have drawn between material the Commission currently holds and material which may once have existed. That distinction is precisely why I am trying to establish the documentary chain carefully rather than draw conclusions from gaps in the archive.\n\nI am deliberately approaching this as a records-reconstruction exercise. I am not asking the Commission to reach a conclusion about ownership, the legality of the demolition, or the conduct of any person or institution.\n\nMy aim is to establish what records were created, what survives, what entered or left the Commission's custody, what was transferred or disposed of, what cannot now be located, and what searches have actually been undertaken.\n\nThank you again for your assistance. I would be grateful if the outstanding September requests could remain open while these points of clarification are addressed.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester \n"},{"id":"gm-1a110a1e67c69f50","subject":"RE: Enquiry — does D153/1–8 contain a c.1875–1880 Llandough-juxta-Penarth instrument?","name":"RE: Enquiry — does D153/1–8 contain a c.1875–1880 Llandough-juxta-Penarth instrument?","date":"2026-10-06T09:53:24Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a110a1e67c69f50.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0ac815c712ae7c-enquiry-does-d153-1-8-contain-a-c-1875-1880-llandough-juxta-penarth-instrument","scopeTopics":["glamorgan"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["glamorgan"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0ac815c712ae7c","direction":"received","from":"Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg","institutions":[{"id":"glamorgan","name":"Glamorgan Archives"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a110a1e67c69f50.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Ref: 2026/4274\n\nDear Requester,\n\nThank you for your email received into our enquiries system 17th September.\n\nThe majority of your email relates to document reference D153/1–8.  As discussed in previous emails, this is a reference for material held at the National Library of Wales so you should contact them about these records.  Detailed descriptions can be found on their online catalogue: Deeds relating to premises in p. Llandough, co. Glam., comprising a dwelling house adjoining the churchyard, 1811 (D153/1-2), land (19 ..., - National Library of Wales Archives and Manuscripts<https://archives.library.wales/index.php/deeds-relating-to-premises-in-p-llandough-co-glam-comprising-dwelling-house-adjoining-churchyard-1811-d153-1-2-land-19>\n\nThe other part of your email quotes a document reference that appears to come from our collections at Glamorgan Archives but we are not sure if it is the correct reference for what you are researching.  D/DRa 11/120 is an old way of formatting our reference DRA/11/120.  This forms part of the series DRA/11/1-293 which comprises lawsuits, instructions to counsel, opinions etc. relating to undermining of house property, flooding, contravention of colliery regulations, 1856-1909 in respect of Elder’s Navigation Colliery, Maesteg.  This doesn’t seem relevant to your research and doesn’t relate to Tynywain either.  Can you point us to where you found this reference so that we can assist further with your questions about this particular item?\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nLouise Hunt\nArchifydd / Archivist\n\nArchifau Morgannwg / Glamorgan Archives\nClos Parc Morgannwg\nLecwydd / Leckwith\nCaerdydd / Cardiff\n[postcode redacted]\n\n[phone redacted]\n[email redacted]<[email redacted]>\n\nwww.archifaumorgannwg.gov.uk/<http://www.archifaumorgannwg.gov.uk/> www.glamarchives.gov.uk<http://www.glamarchives.gov.uk/>\nwww.facebook.com/glamarchives<http://www.facebook.com/glamarchives>\nwww.instagram.com/glamarchives<https://www.instagram.com/glamarchives/>\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]>\nSent: 17 September 2026 00:21\nTo: Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg <[email redacted]>\nSubject: Enquiry — does D153/1–8 contain a c.1875–1880 Llandough-juxta-Penarth instrument?\n\nEXTERNAL: This email originated from outside Cardiff Council, take care when clicking links.\nALLANOL: Daw'r e-bost hwn o'r tu allan i Gyngor Caerdydd, cymerwch ofal wrth glicio ar ddolenni.\n\nArchivist,\nPlease would you check whether D153/1–8 contains any document dated c.1875–1880 relating to Great House Farm / Ty Mawr, Llandough-juxta-Penarth (ST 168 733), adjoining St Dochdwy’s churchyard.\nThis is not Great House Farm, Llandough near Cowbridge.\n\nI am looking for an agreement, lease, counterpart, award, conveyance or related plan involving any of:\n\nDaniel Thomas / D. Thomas & Son\n\nLlandough Limeworks\n\nJohn Williams / Thomas Williams\n\nthe Bute Estate or the Trustees of the Marquess of Bute\n\nquarrying, minerals, lime, sidings, compensation or reparation\n\nthe dwelling adjoining the churchyard\nPlease inspect the full sequence D153/1–8, not only items already described as D153/4–8.\n\nCatalogue titles may not name the parties. Endorsements, dockets and plans should be checked.\n\nIf such an item exists, please give:\n\nthe exact reference\ndate\ndocument type\nparties as written\nproperty description and acreage\nwhether a plan is attached\nwhether a copy can be supplied, and at what cost\n\nAlso is there any item in D153/1–8 dated 1875–1880 naming Thomas Williams, John Williams, Daniel Thomas, Llandough Limeworks, or the dwelling by St Dochdwy’s?\n\nPlease confirm D/DRa 11/120 is Tynywain, Llanedeyrne, and not Llandough-juxta-Penarth/ Llandough Cardiff.\n\nI do not need a searchroom booking at this stage.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\nRequester\n\n**********************************************************************\nThe Council welcomes correspondence in Welsh, English or bilingually. We will ensure that we communicate with you in the language of your choice, as long as you let us know which you prefer. Corresponding in Welsh will not lead to delay.\n\nPrivileged/Confidential Information may be contained in this message. If you are not the addressee indicated in this message (or responsible for delivery of the message to such person), you may not copy or deliver this message to anyone. In such case, you should destroy this message and kindly notify the sender by reply email. Please advise immediately if you or your employer does not consent to Internet email for messages of this kind.  Opinions, conclusions and other information in this message that do not relate to the official business of the Council of the City and County of Cardiff shall be understood as neither given nor endorsed by it.  All e-mail sent to or from this address will be processed by Cardiff County Councils Corporate E-mail system and may be subject to scrutiny by someone other than the addressee.\n**********************************************************************\nMae’r Cyngor yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg, Saesneg neu’n ddwyieithog. Byddwn yn cyfathrebu â chi yn ôl eich dewis, dim ond i chi roi gwybod i ni pa un sydd well gennych. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi.\n\nMae'n bosibl bod gwybodaeth gyfrinachol yn y neges hon. Os na chyfeirir y neges atoch chi'n benodol (neu os nad ydych chi'n gyfrifol am drosglwyddo'r neges i'r person a enwir), yna ni chewch gopio na throsglwyddo'r neges. Mewn achos o'r fath, dylech ddinistrio'r neges a hysbysu'r anfonwr drwy e-bost ar unwaith. Rhowch wybod i'r anfonydd ar unwaith os nad ydych chi neu eich cyflogydd yn caniatau e-bost y Rhyngrwyd am negeseuon fel hon. Rhaid deall nad yw'r safbwyntiau, y casgliadau a'r wybodaeth arall yn y neges hon nad ydynt yn cyfeirio at fusnes swyddogol Cyngor Dinas a Sir Caerdydd yn cynrychioli barn y Cyngor Sir nad yn cael sel ei fendith. Caiff unrhyw negeseuon a anfonir at, neu o'r cyfeiriad e-bost hwn eu prosesu gan system E-bost Gorfforaethol Cyngor Sir Caerdydd a gallant gael eu harchwilio gan rywun heblaw'r person a enwir.\n**********************************************************************"},{"id":"gm-1a1103d9d380a3b6","subject":"RE: Great House Farm, Llandough, Cardiff","name":"RE: Great House Farm, Llandough, Cardiff","date":"2026-10-06T08:03:48Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case corr