om \n\nI shared with the charity who own the woodland. They're immidiate response was to do what everyone has always done to us and ignore our ownership claim and assertion and acknowledge someone else as the owner.\n\nMany and myself are still investigating. But in a nutshell a synthetic parcel appears to have been created, based on enough years of attempts to make our occupancy appear permissive, so that the land registry can accept the story. So 1974 licence letters was also a failed attempt and premis my family rejected, in a long line of failed attempts spanning back to at least 1949.\n\nThe registration then made the 1987 ruling possible. And that ruling is what the land registry depend on to justify its decision to allow BP to register a synthetic mimic of our parcel in their names, without notifying us until long after the appeal of that registration had ended. In which my nan conveniently died. Equally that 1987 ruling depends on the 1983 registration of our land parcel (or mimic synthetic version of it, built from a falsehood of a chain that didn't have root title and was not and never was successful attempts to make our occupancy permissive.\n\nFurthermore the \"whole farm\" being treated as one parcel, was not merged back into one parcel (a synthetic version of our parcel too, not ours), until after the 1986/1987 case.\n\nSo my nan Mary Williams didn't get a fair opportunity to reject the licence letter in 1974 between it being offered and quashing the 1962 possession order for our land parcel. Which itself was never enforced and arguably not legally enforceable as it was based on ambuguity from the 1955 order which made our occupancy of our parcel simply appear permissive with concealment of the fact my nans resistence was due to her ownership claim. And concealment of the fact \"whole farm\" excluded our parcel of land and was not in the 1939 tenancy agreement. So not conveyed to Western Ground Rents in 1938.\n\nFurther to that my nans ownership claim, even though unable to be proven due her title being stolen in 1950/52, still means having not paid anyone for anything on her parcel since 1928 she will have already qualified for adverse possession by 1940.\n\nWe didn't get fair opportunity to appeal the 83 registration either. We also didn't get opportunity to appeal CADWs decision to not list our county treasure 800 year old home as a grade II before BP demolished it at 4am the next day.\n\nThen the council didn't hold them to any account when they returned in 1989 to get planning permission. The council are also under the microscope for many failures. Failure to issue a demolition block order. Also two annexes are missing from those planning docs which are local historic societies testemony to petition demolition citing our old ancestral homes significants (home since 1667 and my birth home I remind you).\n\nPlease just look at the evidence.\nhttps://greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com \n\nMy family are living like hostages under terrorists and have done for 38 years. It's not acceptable. This demonic realm and spell has to be fought off.\n\nDo what you can please. Even if its a small prayer for us. I thank you in advance.\n\nSee attached a few images from my military service. I served this country. I'm still trying to here. I left my career with full honours. My last employer in the Vale Council. I hope this betrayal will end. I'm more than disappointed in local authories here, who don't appear any less hostile to my family and our interest in our land in Llandough.\n\nI can't play the role of hostage or prisoner of war under this regime. My family are mostly all women and children, I am not. It's an untenable situation Chris. The Senedd should have at least done a public enquiry of this.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester \n\n[phone redacted]"},{"id":"gm-1a10c2ac609d288e","subject":"FW: 2026-036- Great House Farm, Llandough, Cardiff","name":"FW: 2026-036- Great House Farm, Llandough, Cardiff","date":"2026-10-05T13:04:42Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a10c2ac609d288e.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a10c2ac609d288e-fw-2026-036-great-house-farm-llandough-cardiff","scopeTopics":["museum"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["museum"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a10c2ac609d288e","direction":"received","from":"FOI Requests","institutions":[{"id":"museum","name":"Amgueddfa Cymru - Museum Wales"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a10c2ac609d288e.json","attachments":[{"name":"63.24.1.pdf","title":"FOI 2026-036: record 63.24/1 rowel spur","url":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/pdf/foi/2026-10-05_museum-wales_foi-2026-036_63-24-1-rowel-spur.pdf","mime":"application/pdf","bytes":69945,"sha256":"c1c0fa5e25d57937dcd57302369ba2f8e5318e8c74f979c2791c7799b8460cff"},{"name":"63.24.2.pdf","title":"FOI 2026-036: record 63.24/2 spearhead","url":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/pdf/foi/2026-10-05_museum-wales_foi-2026-036_63-24-2-spearhead.pdf","mime":"application/pdf","bytes":65742,"sha256":"cfadaeb03c944108278e7436c7b90cf0adae0ca929084a2a41821a7812063c8c"},{"name":"63-24 letter.pdf","title":"FOI 2026-036: letter of 22 Nov 1931, file 63.24","url":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/pdf/foi/2026-10-05_museum-wales_foi-2026-036_63-24-letter-1931.pdf","mime":"application/pdf","bytes":80368,"sha256":"34a54e6f76e54c20e9cf304b5c24f7444d5f1e72502fa96c09c535120e67c47d"}],"textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester\n\nThank you for your request.\n\nAmgueddfa Cymru has previously responded to requests concerning Great House Farm / Ty Mawr / Llandough Farm, Llandough, including FOI 2026-024 and FOI 2026-032. In responding to the present request, further enquiries were undertaken with Archaeology, Industry and Social History Collections. These enquiries built upon the searches already undertaken for the earlier requests.\n\nCollections staff reviewed records identified through references to Great House Farm, Ty Mawr and Llandough Farm, Llandough (Vale of Glamorgan), together with records returned through associated place-name searches. Records relating to the wider Llandough area, to other locations sharing the name \"Llandough\", or to other properties bearing the name \"Great House Farm\" were reviewed but were not considered to relate to the location specified in your request.\n\nQuestion 1\nA schedule of every object, archive file, accession, loan, enquiry or correspondence record that Amgueddfa Cymru holds which is indexed, provenanced or otherwise recorded as from Great House Farm / Ty Mawr / Llandough Farm, Llandough (Penarth / Vale of Glamorgan).\nThe schedules provided in response to FOI 2026-032 contained the records previously identified as relating to the location specified in your request.\nThe additional enquiries undertaken for this request identified the following records:\nReference\nTitle\nDate\nDescription\nRef 13.2\nWaterwheel from Llandough Farm\nCollected 1 April 1979\nWaterwheel collected from Llandough Farm. Museum records state that the item was donated by Wales and West Housing Association in connection with development of the site. The item was never formally accessioned and has subsequently been deaccessioned.\nMS 3360/3\nParticulars, Conditions and Plans of the Sale of Nine Well-Known Farms in the Vale of Glamorgan forming part of the Llandough Estate\n1924\nPhotostat copy. Collections staff identified a page relating to Great House Farm within the document. Provided in FOI: 2026-032\nNo further records relating specifically to Great House Farm / Ty Mawr / Llandough Farm, Llandough were identified.\n\nQuestion 2\nThe same schedule for Church Farm, Llandough, but only insofar as records were retrieved during the searches undertaken for FOI 2026-024 and can be identified without a new full search.\nThe record identified is:\nReference\nTitle\nDescription\n63.24\nArchaeological accession\nDetails attached\nNo further records were identified from the material retrieved during the searches undertaken for FOI 2026-024.\n\nQuestion 3\nAny accession, register entry, day-book, donations book or correspondence in the collections of Amgueddfa Cymru or of its predecessor Cardiff Museum / Welsh Museum of Natural History / National Museum of Wales, dated 1875-1915, recording ironwork, armour, a visor, a lance, a helmet, \"armour from Llandough\", or a find from Great House / Llandough Farm.\nNo such records were identified.\n\nFor clarification, the accession registers maintained by Cardiff Museum form the earliest accession registers of the National Museum of Wales collection. Objects held by Cardiff Museum were not re-accessioned when responsibility for the collections transferred to the National Museum of Wales and the accession numbering sequence continued uninterrupted. Searches of the earliest accession registers therefore encompass the former Cardiff Museum accession records.\n\nAs part of the enquiries undertaken in response to this request and previous requests concerning the same location, accession records and associated collections documentation were reviewed for the objects and locations specified above. No accession record, register entry, day-book entry, donations book entry or correspondence dated between 1875 and 1915 relating to finds from Great House Farm, Ty Mawr or Llandough Farm was identified.\n\nQuestion 4\nAny object record for Llandough ironwork that is not labelled \"visor\" or \"lance\" but is or was associated with the farmhouse floor or rear wing / dairy.\nNo such records were identified.\n\nArchaeology Collections have confirmed that they hold no record of any additional ironwork from Llandough associated with the farmhouse floor, rear wing or dairy beyond material already considered in previous responses concerning this location.\n\n\n\n\nI hope this information is helpful.\n\nIf you are dissatisfied with the way your request has been handled, you may request an internal review. Requests for an internal review should be submitted within 40 working days of the date of this response.\n\nIf you remain dissatisfied following the outcome of the internal review, you have the right to apply directly to the Information Commissioner's Office (ICO) for a decision. The ICO can be contacted at www.ico.org.uk.\n\nCofion cynnes,\n\n[A picture containing text, tableware, plate, dishware  Description automatically generated]\nFOI Team\n[cid:image003.png@01DD54BD.2E027CD0]\n[email redacted]<[email redacted]>\namgueddfa.cymru<applewebdata://288FE56C-5BFC-474C-ADB3-A1017EF25E69/amgueddfa.cymru> | museum.wales<applewebdata://288FE56C-5BFC-474C-ADB3-A1017EF25E69/museum.wales>\n\nRhif elusen | Charity number 525774\n\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSent: 08 September 2026 04:05\nTo: FOI Requests <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSubject: ALLANOL/EXTERNAL - Great House Farm, Llandough, Cardiff\n\n\nThis email comes from outside the organisation.\n\nDo not click links or open attachments unless it is an email you expected to receive.\nFOI Team,\n\nThis is a new request under the Freedom of Information Act 2000. It is not a repeat of FOI 2026-024 and it is not a comment on the internal review of that request.\n\nIn your letter of 25 August 2026 (FOI 2026-024) you stated:\n\n  \"For completeness, our searches identified records relating to other archaeological finds from Church Farm, Llandough and to other archaeological discoveries recorded at Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough. These records do not relate to, nor are they otherwise associated with, the discovery described in your request. We have therefore not considered them to fall within the scope of this request.\"\n\nI now request those records.\n\nPlease provide:\n\n1. A schedule of every object, archive file, accession, loan, enquiry or correspondence record that Amgueddfa Cymru holds which is indexed, provenanced or otherwise recorded as from Great House Farm / Ty Mawr / Llandough Farm, Llandough (Penarth / Vale of Glamorgan). For each entry: accession or archive number, object or file title, date of acquisition or creation, donor or excavator if recorded, and a one-line description.\n\n2. The same schedule for Church Farm, Llandough, but only so far as the records were retrieved in the 2026-024 search and can be identified without a new full trawl.\n\n3. Any accession, register entry, day-book, donations book or correspondence in the collections of Amgueddfa Cymru or of its predecessor Cardiff Museum / Welsh Museum of Natural History / National Museum of Wales, dated 1875-1915, recording ironwork, armour, a visor, a lance, a helmet, \"armour from Llandough\", or a find from Great House / Llandough Farm.\n\n   Published sources which state that such ironwork is in the National Museum, and which therefore make a search of predecessor registers reasonable, are:\n\n   * GGAT, Land at Great House Farm, Llandough: An Archaeological Assessment, August 1990 (planning file 89/01396/OUT and 92/00671/RES): \"it is likely that some medieval ironwork which is held by the National Museum of Wales was discovered under the rear wing c 1880 (RCAHMW 1988).\"\n\n   * Holbrook & Thomas, Medieval Archaeology 49 (2005), p. 70 n. 142: \"medieval ironwork was discovered under the rear wing of Great House Farm c. 1880. It is now in the collections of the National Museums & Galleries of Wales.\"\n\n   I note that the National Museum of Wales was not founded until 1907. If a find of c.1880 entered a public collection in Cardiff it would have done so through the earlier municipal museum. Please search those predecessor accession registers, not only the post-1907 NMW database.\n\n4. Any object record for Llandough ironwork that is not labelled \"visor\" or \"lance\" but is or was associated with the farmhouse floor or rear wing / dairy (the room identified on H. J. Thomas's 1974 RCAHMW plan).\n\nPlease treat this as a request for recorded information only.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester\n[email redacted]<[email redacted]>\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\nAttachments (3)\n- 63.24.1.pdf (application/pdf, 68 KB): https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/pdf/foi/2026-10-05_museum-wales_foi-2026-036_63-24-1-rowel-spur.pdf\n- 63.24.2.pdf (application/pdf, 64 KB): https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/pdf/foi/2026-10-05_museum-wales_foi-2026-036_63-24-2-spearhead.pdf\n- 63-24 letter.pdf (application/pdf, 78 KB): https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/pdf/foi/2026-10-05_museum-wales_foi-2026-036_63-24-letter-1931.pdf"},{"id":"gm-1a10ac1941cfaedc","subject":"Please update the status of your request - BP vs Buckler 1987","name":"Please update the status of your request - BP vs Buckler 1987","date":"2026-10-05T06:30:20Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a10ac1941cfaedc.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a10ac1941cfaedc-please-update-the-status-of-your-request-bp-vs-buckler-1987","scopeTopics":["nactso"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["nactso"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a10ac1941cfaedc","direction":"received","from":"(name not shown; whatdotheyknow.com)","institutions":[{"id":"nactso","name":"Counter Terrorism Policing (NaCTSO)"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a10ac1941cfaedc.json","textStatus":"available","text":"To let everyone know, follow this link and then select the appropriate box.\n\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/profile/sign_in?r=%2Frequest%2Fbp_vs_buckler_1987_1988%23describe_state_form_1\n\nYour request was called 'BP vs Buckler 1987'. Letting everyone know whether you got the information will help us keep tabs on\nNational Counter Terrorism Security Office.\n\n-- the WhatDoTheyKnow team\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nWhatDoTheyKnow is a project of mySociety run by a small team of staff and\ndedicated volunteers.\n\nmySociety is a charity that believes that information should be accessible to everyone.\nIf this is important to you, please consider donating to help us do more:\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/donate\n\nFind out more about how we handle your data at:\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/help/privacy\n\nmySociety is a registered charity in England and Wales (1076346), and a limited\ncompany (03277032)"},{"id":"gm-1a10812c4dfa50fc","subject":"In Touch with Llandough!","name":"In Touch with Llandough!","date":"2026-10-04T18:00:06Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a10812c4dfa50fc.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a10812c4dfa50fc-in-touch-with-llandough","scopeTopics":["vale-scrutiny"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["vale-scrutiny"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a10812c4dfa50fc","direction":"received","from":"Cllr George Carroll","institutions":[{"id":"vale-scrutiny","name":"Vale of Glamorgan Council (Scrutiny)"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a10812c4dfa50fc.json","textStatus":"available","text":"View this email in your browser (https://mailchi.mp/8d75f0aee649/in-touch-with-llandough-17666242?e=1429c2c1bb)\nFIREWORKS: WESTPOINT INDUSTRIAL ESTATE\n\n\n**\n------------------------------------------------------------\nDear Requester\n\nThursday night's incident, in which fireworks were set off again at WestPoint Industrial Estate, was completely unacceptable.\n\nPeople from outside Llandough coming into our community and behaving in such a selfish and inconsiderate manner is becoming an increasing problem.\n\nIt shows no regard for the way we do things in Llandough.\n\nWe rightly think of others, recognising the impacts our actions can have on some of the most vulnerable members of our community.\nThose responsible showed no such regard.\n\nThe images above, captured by Peter Griffiths of Vale Sports Arena, demonstrate the extent of the problem.\n\nThey show both the number of fireworks set off and the number of people involved.\n\nIt also shows authorities must treat stopping this behaviour as a priority.\n\nThe noise caused is distressing for many people, including the elderly and those with small children.\n\nIt's also distressing for animals, which is a particular problem with Cardiff Dogs Home located within the vicinity.\n\nhttps://www.facebook.com/share/v/1F4JUgcVux/\n\nI've contacted both South Wales Police and the Vale of Glamorgan Council to request the following things:\n* Effective surveillance equipment be installed urgently to identify those responsible;\n* Regular nighttime police patrols in the area to stop and apprehend those causing these disturbances;\n* Access to the area to be limited to prevent people using it to set off fireworks.\n\nIt is a criminal offence to set off fireworks past 11pm at night.\n\nThere are also many other laws and regulations that may have been broken.\n\nWhile Thursday's incident commenced at around 10.30pm, there have been several previous instances in which the law has clearly been breached.\n\nIt is punishable by an unlimited fine and up to six months imprisonment.\n\nIt is time for the law to be enforced.\n\nThose responsible for causing distress to the vulnerable in Llandough cannot be allowed to think the law does not apply to them.\n\nIf you any information about this incident, Requester, please come forward. It will be treated in the utmost confidence.\n\n\n**\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nNEXT WEEK:\nRedlands Road diversion\n\nRedlands Road will be closed between 9th and 11th October to vehicles travelling towards Dinas Powys towards Llandough.\n\nThis is because the right hand turn will be closed to allow gas works to be carried out safely.\n\nDetails of the diversion can be found above.\n\nAs you'll see, drivers are being told to travel past the Merrie Harrier and the Barons Court to the Cardiff Bay roundabout, where they will be able to turn around and travel back towards Penarth, before using the left hand turn to access Redlands Road.\n\nThe route for vehicles travelling in the opposite direction is unaffected.\n\nWhile this is unlikely to be particularly problematic for Llandough residents, I'm concerned some drivers may attempt to use the Dochdwy estate or the Merrie Harrier car park to turn around, potentially causing a bottleneck.\n\nI've therefore put together a short video, explaining the diversion route in place, which avoids our village.\n\nIt would be helpful if you would share it on social media, Requester.\n\nTo do so, you can click below now.\nhttps://www.facebook.com/share/r/1CJhqM9XTD/\n\nRoad markings:\nPen-y-turnpike Road\n\nThe road markings at the junction between Pen-y-turnpike Road and Leckwith Road have now been repainted.\n\nWe secured these works together, by pressuring the Council to get them done.\n\nIt may seem like a small thing, but making it clear to drivers they need to give way will help stop accidents.\n\nThank you to everyone across Llandough, Leckwith and Michaelston-le-Pit who helped.\nhttps://www.facebook.com/share/r/1EaRtbbAVJ/\n\nPreventing overdevelopment\n\nWhen I'm out and about in our community, many residents express concerns about overdevelopment and the way it threatens our village's distinct identity and character.\n\nThe proposals to build 250 houses on Eastbrook Road are a good example.\n\nWhile outside our village, the site is located just down from the Merrie Harrier, on green fields that form a natural boundary between Llandough and Dinas Powys.\n\nThis boundary is crucial in ensuring both villages remain separate, unique entities.\n\nYou can watch my comments at the last Full Council meeting below.\n\nhttps://www.facebook.com/share/r/1Fff7ZbyuK/\n\nDochdwy Road pavement defects\n\nHuge thanks to the resident who got in touch about pavement defects on Dochdwy Road.\n\nAs you'll see, these have now all been marked up for repair.\n\nBy getting in touch, the resident helped ensure the issue was taken forward quickly.\n\nWhen you contact me about things you spot, you're not causing a bother.\n\nTo the contrary, you're playing a crucial role in our team, as we work to improve things in our village.\n\nThat's what Our Plan for Llandough is all about!\n\nhttps://www.facebook.com/share/r/1Hy7k64Xw5/\n\nCommunity drop-in event:\n20th October\n\nOur next community drop-in event takes place at the Merrie Harrier on 20th October.\n\nLike the last one, it'll start at 7.00pm.\n\nThe pub is a key asset to our community.\n\nTracey and the team do a lot for us - help return the favour by lending them your support.\n\nYou can see some of their midweek offers below!\n\nhttps://www.facebook.com/share/r/1EM9z5wbMs/\n\nOur Plan for Llandough!\n\n\nAs your councillor, I'm working with you all to secure a better future for Llandough. Together, we have a plan to deliver for the village.\n\nOur plan is making Llandough better by:\n\n1. Sorting out the parking problems\n2. Stamping out dog fouling\n3. Repairing our roads\n4. Stopping littering and flytipping\n5. Delivering better sports facilities\n6. Keeping the whole community involved\n\nIf you have any suggestions of what you'd like to see in Our Plan, please get in touch!\n\nhttp://www.facebook.com/sharer/sharer.php?u=https%3A%2F%2Fmailchi.mp%2F8d75f0aee649%2Fin-touch-with-llandough-17666242 Share (http://www.facebook.com/sharer/sharer.php?u=https%3A%2F%2Fmailchi.mp%2F8d75f0aee649%2Fin-touch-with-llandough-17666242)\nhttps://us19.forward-to-friend.com/forward?u=7b7a08284cdbc1f69f5b0afd8&id=9a0d3c73ab&e=1429c2c1bb Forward (https://us19.forward-to-friend.com/forward?u=7b7a08284cdbc1f69f5b0afd8&id=9a0d3c73ab&e=1429c2c1bb)\n\n============================================================\nI'm hoping to reach as many people as possible with this newsletter, so please feel free to forward it to friends and neighbours.\n\nIf this email has been forwarded to you, and you would like to sign up to my newsletter, please click ** here ([email redacted]\n.\nThis email was sent to you because your email address is on my subscribers list. If you don't want to receive any more messages from me, please click below to unsubscribe.\n\nYour consent was previously given during an online or offline interaction with me or the Conservatives. If you feel this is incorrect or would like to comment, please contact me. I do not buy email lists.\n\nPromoted by Chris Kirkham on behalf of George Carroll, both of Vale of Glamorgan Conservatives, Holton House, 50 Holton Road, Barry [postcode redacted].\n\nThis email was sent to [email redacted] ([email redacted]\nwhy did I get this? (https://facebook.us19.list-manage.com/about?u=7b7a08284cdbc1f69f5b0afd8&id=daad00f7e3&e=1429c2c1bb&c=9a0d3c73ab)     unsubscribe from this list (***REDACTED***     update subscription preferences (https://facebook.us19.list-manage.com/profile?u=7b7a08284cdbc1f69f5b0afd8&id=daad00f7e3&e=1429c2c1bb&c=9a0d3c73ab)\nPolitician . Unit 5 Pro Copy Business Centre . Parc Ty Glas . Cardiff, Crf [postcode redacted] . USA"},{"id":"gm-1a0fd363100cc68b","subject":"Extension - Our reference:  260904007 SAR IR","name":"Extension - Our reference:  260904007 SAR IR","date":"2026-10-02T15:22:58Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0fd363100cc68b.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0fd363100cc68b-extension-our-reference-260904007-sar-ir","scopeTopics":["moj"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["moj"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0fd363100cc68b","direction":"received","from":"NSUKilo","institutions":[{"id":"moj","name":"Ministry of Justice / HMCTS"}],"refs":["260904007"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0fd363100cc68b.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester,\nPlease see attached the extension letter for your request (our reference: 260904007).\n\n\n\n\n\nYours sincerely,\n\n\n\n\n\nKnowledge and Information Liaison Officer\n\nNational Support Unit\n\nHM Courts and Tribunals Service\n\n\n[cid:268c8766-3eec-496c-86f8-9c995529bc59]\n\n\n\n________________________________\nThis e-mail and any attachments is intended only for the attention of the addressee(s). Its unauthorised use, disclosure, storage or copying is not permitted. If you are not the intended recipient, please destroy all copies and inform the sender by return e-mail. Internet e-mail is not a secure medium. Any reply to this message could be intercepted and read by someone else. Please bear that in mind when deciding whether to send material in response to this message by e-mail. This e-mail (whether you are the sender or the recipient) may be monitored, recorded and retained by the Ministry of Justice. Monitoring / blocking software may be used, and e-mail content may be read at any time. You have a responsibility to ensure laws are not broken when composing or forwarding e-mails and their contents."},{"id":"gm-1a0fb4e443150e10","subject":"Please update the status of your request - 2020 01590 HYB","name":"Please update the status of your request - 2020 01590 HYB","date":"2026-10-02T06:30:05Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0fb4e443150e10.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0fb4e443150e10-please-update-the-status-of-your-request-2020-01590-hyb","scopeTopics":["vale-foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["vale-foi"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0fb4e443150e10","direction":"received","from":"(name not shown; whatdotheyknow.com)","institutions":[{"id":"vale-foi","name":"Vale of Glamorgan Council"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0fb4e443150e10.json","textStatus":"available","text":"To let everyone know, follow this link and then select the appropriate box.\n\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/profile/sign_in?r=%2Frequest%2F2020_01590_hyb%23describe_state_form_1\n\nYour request was called '2020 01590 HYB'. Letting everyone know whether you got the information will help us keep tabs on\nVale of Glamorgan Council.\n\n-- the WhatDoTheyKnow team\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nWhatDoTheyKnow is a project of mySociety run by a small team of staff and\ndedicated volunteers.\n\nmySociety is a charity that believes that information should be accessible to everyone.\nIf this is important to you, please consider donating to help us do more:\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/donate\n\nFind out more about how we handle your data at:\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/help/privacy\n\nmySociety is a registered charity in England and Wales (1076346), and a limited\ncompany (03277032)"},{"id":"gm-1a0f81abba064237","subject":"Re: FOI — Welsh Office / Cadw papers, July–December 1988 — Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough — following 26FOI 103 (ATISN 27589)","name":"Re: FOI — Welsh Office / Cadw papers, July–December 1988 — Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough — following 26FOI 103 (ATISN 27589)","date":"2026-10-01T15:34:56Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0f81abba064237.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0f7dbf9dec61a9-foi-welsh-office-cadw-papers-july-december-1988-great-house-farm-ty-mawr-llandough-following-26foi-103-atisn-27589","scopeTopics":["cadw"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["cadw"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0f7dbf9dec61a9","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"cadw","name":"Welsh Government / Cadw"}],"refs":["26FOI103","ATISN27589"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0f81abba064237.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Dr Wigley,\n\nThank you for your acknowledgement of ATISN 27589 and confirmation that a\nresponse is due by 26 October 2026.\n\nFor the avoidance of doubt, I should clarify the two historical dates\nreproduced in your acknowledgement:\n\n“Possession was taken on 29 November 1988” and “Demolition of the farmhouse\nbegan on 6 December 1988” were included in my request as contextual\ninformation identifying the period and events to which the requested\nrecords relate. Their repetition in the acknowledgement should not be\ntreated as an acceptance by me that either date, or any legal\ncharacterisation of possession or demolition, has been established by the\nWelsh Government or by me. This whole situation maybe unprecedented. It was\nalready a landmark legal case in 1987, without this added layer  we may now\nbe understanding.\n\nMy request is for recorded information held by the Welsh Government/Cadw\nconcerning the matters specified in points 1–3. I am not asking Cadw to\ndetermine title, possession, limitation or any other legal issue, and\nnothing in this FOI correspondence is intended to constitute an admission,\nwaiver, settlement or abandonment of any rights or position concerning\nthose matters.\n\nPlease also expressly confirm that records falling within the scope of\nATISN 27589, including any relevant historic listing, inspection,\nphotographic, administrative and file-disposal records, will be preserved\npending completion of the request and any applicable internal review or\ncomplaint process.\n\nI look forward to the substantive response by 26 October. And sincerely\nthank you for your time and attention in this matter. You are appreciated.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester  \n\nOn Thu, 1 Oct 2026, 15:26 , <[email redacted]> wrote:\n\n> Dear Requester,\n>\n>\n>\n> *ATISN 27589 - Great House Farm, Llandough*\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you for your request which I received on 29 September.  You asked\n> for:\n>\n>\n>\n>    1. Any consideration, between 1 July and 31 December 1988, of listing,\n>    spot-listing or emergency listing of Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr,\n>    Llandough-juxta-Penarth, adjoining St Dochdwy's (approx. ST 168 733).\n>    2. Any inspection, photograph, minute or note of the building, or of\n>    its demolition, in that period. Possession was taken on 29 November 1988.\n>    Demolition of the farmhouse began on 6 December 1988.\n>    3. Any transfer, destruction or disposal of those files, with the\n>    schedule used and the date.\n>\n>\n>\n> You will receive a response by 26 October 2026.  If you have any queries\n> about this request, please do not hesitate to contact me at the address\n> below.  Please remember to quote the reference number above in any future\n> correspondence.\n>\n>\n>\n> The request you sent me contains personal information about you - for\n> example, your name and address. The Welsh Government will be the data\n> processor for this information and, in accordance with the General Data\n> Protection Regulation, it will be processed in order to fulfil our public\n> task and meet our legal obligations under the Act to provide you with a\n> response. We will only use this personal information to deal with your\n> request and any matters which arise as a result of it. We will keep your\n> personal information and all other information relating to your request for\n> three years from the date on which your request is finally closed.  Your\n> personal information will then be disposed of securely.\n>\n>\n>\n> Under data protection legislation, you have the right:\n>\n>\n>\n> •              to be informed of the personal data we hold about you and\n> to access it\n>\n> •              to require us to rectify inaccuracies in that data\n>\n> •              to (in certain circumstances) object to or restrict\n> processing\n>\n> •              for (in certain circumstances) your data to be ‘erased’\n>\n> •              to (in certain circumstances) data portability\n>\n> •              to lodge a complaint with the Information Commissioner’s\n> Office (ICO) who is our independent regulator for data protection\n>\n>\n>\n> For further information about the information which the Welsh Government\n> holds and its use, or if you wish to exercise your rights under the GDPR,\n> please see contact details below:\n>\n>\n>\n> Data Protection Officer\n>\n> Welsh Government\n>\n> Cathays Park\n>\n> CARDIFF