 will handle your request in accordance with your rights under UK GDPR and the Data Protection Act 2018.\n\nOur statutory deadline for reply is 28 October 2026, although we will endeavour to respond as soon as possible.\n\nYour reference is D260928.\n\nYours sincerely\n\n\nKat Wignall\nEO Disclosure Officer\nInformation Rights Team\nData and Register Integrity Group\nEmail- [email redacted] / [email redacted]\n\n\n\n-----Original Message-----\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]>\nSent: 28 September 2026 22:42\nTo: _DataProtection <[email redacted]>\nCc: Wales.OfficeMail <[email redacted]>; _FOI email address <[email redacted]>\nSubject: Subject access request — WA231076 / WA240304 — Great House Farm, Llandough — Williams / Buckler\n\n[You don't often get email from [email redacted]. Learn why this is important at https://aka.ms/LearnAboutSenderIdentification ]\n\nData Protection Officer\nHM Land Registry\n[email redacted]\ncc: [email redacted]; [email redacted] Refs already with you: N610CGV; letter of SG Haste, 30 July 2026\n\nSirs/Madam,\n\nSubject access request under UK GDPR Article 15.\nRequester: Requester.\nTitles: WA231076 and WA240304 (closed). Land north of Leckwith Road, Llandough (Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr).\n\nThis is not a request for an official copy of the register. It is a request for personal data in the working file in which I am identifiable, including customer-complaint files, the pack sent to Stephen Doughty MP, and any note of my correspondence on N610CGV.\n\nPlease also say whether the first-registration file contains personal data about Mary Doreen Williams (died 26 March 1983) or William Buckler, so far as you can disclose it to me as a family representative, and what identity you require before the month starts.\n\nThe matters to which that file goes, so you can search properly, are these. They are questions, not findings against Land Registry:\n\n1. First-registration applications: WA231076 on 30 November 1982 (form 1E); WA240304 on 23 February 1983 (form 1C); later amalgamation and closure of WA240304.\n2. A 31 December 1969 conveyance, Western Ground Rents to BP Pension Trust, identified as a root, and whether it is on the 30 July 2026 schedule of documents surviving on the file.\n3. Notice and opportunity to object in 1982–83, while Mary Williams remained in occupation of the farmhouse. Family evidence is that notification was not received until after her death on 26 March 1983.\nI am seeking the notice and service record.\n4. What the 1987 Court of Appeal decided (possession, limitation, October 1974 letters) and what it did not decide (documentary root of the farmhouse and garden parcel).\n\nThe public evidence index is https://greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com/ .\n\nPlease preserve the first-registration file and the complaint file. Do not start the statutory month until you have said what identification you need. Nothing in this letter abandons any claim concerning title, registration, concealment, limitation, reopening or alteration of the register. This is not a claim form and not service.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\nRequester \n\n[email redacted]\n\n\nHM Land Registry’s purpose is to protect your land ownership and provide services and data that underpin an efficient and informed property market. Our vision is: “A world-leading property market as part of a thriving economy and a sustainable future”.\n\nWe check all mail and attachments for known viruses. However, you are advised that you open any attachments at your own risk. If you have received this email and it was not intended for you, please let us know, then delete it.\n\nWe welcome correspondence in English and Welsh.\n\nTo see how HM Land Registry treats your personal information, read our Personal information charter: www.gov.uk/government/organisations/land-registry/about/personal-information-charter."},{"id":"gm-1a0f752fb0575943","subject":"RE: Freedom of Information Request: Reference CAS-347296-M0V4Y1 — clarification","name":"RE: Freedom of Information Request: Reference CAS-347296-M0V4Y1 — clarification","date":"2026-10-01T11:56:40Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0f752fb0575943.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0ed914794badec-freedom-of-information-request-reference-cas-347296-m0v4y1-clarification","scopeTopics":["tna"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["tna"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0ed914794badec","direction":"received","from":"FOI Requests","institutions":[{"id":"tna","name":"The National Archives"}],"refs":["CAS-344046","CAS-347296","CAS-356384"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0f752fb0575943.json","attachments":[{"name":"Item 01 - Release Schedule_On Discovery_Redacted.csv","title":"Item 01 - Release Schedule_On Discovery_Redacted.csv","url":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/pdf/foi/2026-09-23_tna_cas-347296_item01-release-schedule-on-discovery.csv","mime":"text/csv","bytes":549,"sha256":"7050146d5c76fd542fc174736d0591dba7e69a40be9aca1ff3d5850e4c8a8df4"},{"name":"Item 02 - Release Schedule_High Level Delivery Schedule_Redacted.csv","title":"Item 02 - Release Schedule_High Level Delivery Schedule_Redacted.csv","url":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/pdf/foi/2026-09-23_tna_cas-347296_item02-high-level-delivery-schedule.csv","mime":"text/csv","bytes":176,"sha256":"2fa452194df3dc863cb4f4ff2b377f12b98e0f756ec9ed22f9f3800d1775f3aa"},{"name":"Item 03 - Collect 27 Series 11_Manifest_Redacted.csv","title":"Item 03 - Collect 27 Series 11_Manifest_Redacted.csv","url":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/pdf/foi/2026-09-23_tna_cas-347296_item03-collect27-series11-manifest.csv","mime":"text/csv","bytes":93,"sha256":"10681e85f7a96fc29959599f757aed67ffb745f264d89139e2dd73f315d5daab"}],"textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester,\n\nThank you for your email, dated 30th September.\n\nTo clarify, you provided The National Archives with clarification to your FOI request on the 2nd September 2026 and we provided a final response on 23rd September 2026.\n\nAlso on 23rd September 2026, you requested an internal review into the handling of your FOI request. This case reference for this internal review is CAS-356384.\n\nFor ease of reference, please find attached the final response to your original request which contains details of your original request and the clarification you provided.\n\nPlease note, the request with case reference CAS-344046 relates to your original enquiry to The National Archives. We decided to handle this as an FOI request, with the new case reference CAS-347296.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nFOI Manager\nFreedom of Information Centre\nGovernment and Archives Leadership\nThe National Archives\n\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSent: 30 September 2026 14:19\nTo: FOI Requests <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSubject: RE: Freedom of Information Request: Reference CAS-347296-M0V4Y1 — clarification\n\nEXTERNAL: This email originates from outside of The National Archives.\nFOI Centre,\n\nReference CAS-347296-M0V4Y1\n\nThank you for your email of 20 August asking me to clarify scope. I apologise for the delay in replying. Please take the request off hold on the following basis.\n\n1. The FOI request (CAS-347296) is limited to the six numbered questions in my email of 13 August: whether the searches were limited to the PRO and RW series and four place-name terms; whether the named companies, court proceedings and application references were searched; whether TNA holds any transfer, accession, retention, destruction or disposal information for records concerning the property, companies or litigation; the reason for any \"not held\" answer; whether HMC9/311 was checked; and whether there are central-government, court or departmental records on the litigation or entities that would not be found by a place-name search.\n\n2. My email of 20 August about the National Farm Survey and the Glamorgan War Agricultural Executive Committee (1936–1950) is not part of this FOI request. Please treat it as a records enquiry under the existing case CAS-344046-K1S1R2. My chase of that case on 20 August bounced from [email redacted]<[email redacted]>, so I would be grateful if you could pass it to the Records Enquiries team.\n\nI am not asking TNA to research the contents of uncatalogued records, only to tell me what recorded information it holds about the searches, the catalogue and the transfer or disposal of relevant records.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester \n\nPlease don't print this e-mail unless you really need to. ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- National Archives Disclaimer This email and any files transmitted with it are intended solely for the use of the individual(s) to whom they are addressed. If you are not the intended recipient and have received this email in error, please notify the sender and delete the email. Opinions, conclusions and other information in this message and attachments that do not relate to the official business of The National Archives are neither given nor endorsed by it. ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nDear Requester,\n\nThank you for your enquiry in which you asked for the information concerning the existence, provenance, transfer, retention, destruction or disposal of records relating to Ty Mawr / Great House Farm.\n\nYour request has been handled under the Freedom of Information (FOI) Act 2000. The FOI Act gives you the right to know whether we hold the information you want and to have it communicated to you, subject to any exemptions which may apply.\n\nI can confirm that The National Archives holds some information relevant to your request. We are pleased to be able to provide some of this information to you.\n\nOur response\n\nThe initial response from our records enquiries team looked for the four terms (“Great House Farm”, “Ty Mawr”, “Llandough” or “Cedfin”) within the PRO and RW series and did not find any relevant information. We have now tried those terms and the further twelve terms that you have specified in a wider catalogue search. We have provided the results of our search below.\n\nThe crux of your query is local land ownership dispute in Glamorgan and the demolition of some buildings by a private company; generally speaking, these are not matters dealt with by central government and we would not expect to see anything within our collections. As such, there would have been no records to transfer. The exception is the court case relating to BP properties, which generated some papers. Links to the catalogue pages for these records can be seen below.\n\nThere are likely to be records of the farm in the 1940s as part of the National Farm Survey series but these cannot all currently be searched online by farm.\n\nWe are unable to conduct this research on the archival collections on your behalf without charging fees to conduct research on your behalf (Paid Search). A public authority is allowed to charge fees under section 9 of the Freedom of Information Act and within this section of the Act it states: \"provision is made by or under any enactment as to the fee may be charged by the public authority for the disclosure of the information\" (S9(5) FOIA).\n\nThe Public Records Act (1958) enables a statutory instrument to be made setting out the fees which may be made in connection with the supply of information from The National Archives. The prices we charge are set out by our Fees Regulations<https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2025/1243/made>.\n\nIf you are interested in paying for research to be conducted on your behalf, then details of our Paid Search service and other options for paying for research are available here: Paying for research - The National Archives<https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/help-with-your-research/paying-for-research/>\n\nIf you would prefer to conduct this research yourself then you may find the following research guide to be of assistance: National Farm Survey of England and Wales 1941-1943 - The National Archives<https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/help-with-your-research/research-guides/national-farm-survey-england-wales-1941-1943/>\n\nThere are Agricultural Executive Committee minutes and papers for Glamorgan; but again, these cannot be searched online by name of farm and we are unable to conduct this research without charging a fee: Browse records of other archives | The National Archives<https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/browse/r/h/C42711>\n\nWe can provide the following advice regarding the search terms you have specified:\n\n\n  1.  Western Ground Rents Ltd\n\n      Relevant information not found.\n\n\n  2.  BP Pension Trust Ltd\n\n      Relevant information not found.\n\n\n  3.  BP Properties Ltd\n\n      Judicial Papers A-C 1-63 | The National Archives<https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/r/74c935f9-563a-4c1b-8643-ed3eed68ad98> (House of Lords as court of appeal)\n\n\n  4.  Mountjoy Ltd\n\n      Relevant information not found.\n\n\n  5.  Proceedings in BP Properties Ltd v Buckler [1987] EWCA Civ 2\n\n      Judicial Papers A-C 1-63 | The National Archives<https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/r/74c935f9-563a-4c1b-8643-ed3eed68ad98> (House of Lords as court of appeal)\n\n\n  6.  1982–1983 registration and associated government or administrative records\n\n      Relevant information not found.\n\n\n  7.  1980–1988 public campaign and governmental correspondence\n\n      Relevant information not found.\n\n\n  8.  1988 eviction and demolition\n\n      Relevant information not found.\n\n\n  9.  Cadw or central-government communications concerning the property\n\n      Relevant information not found.\n\n\n  10. European Commission of Human Rights Application 14464/88\n\n      Relevant information not found.\n\n\n  11. Records concerning transfer, destruction or disposal of potentially relevant material\n\n      Within the results of our search, it revealed a record within the parliamentary collection.\n\n      This is Judicial Papers A-C 1-63<https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/r/74c935f9-563a-4c1b-8643-ed3eed68ad98> . The parliamentary records and the provision of a service for them by The National Archives, was done in phases, with their collections loaded into Discovery in stages. The series that this record formed part of were released on our catalogue on Discovery on 20th May 2026.\n\n      This is an open record and available to the public here at The National Archives, Kew. To note, this record requires preparation for production and will take four working days to prepare\n\n      TNA holds transfer paperwork for YHL/PO/JO/10/11/2536 - Judicial Papers A-C 1-63<https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/r/74c935f9-563a-4c1b-8643-ed3eed68ad98>. The below table is a schedule of the documents that we have attached to this response.\n\nDocument Title  Document Description    TNA’s Notes\nItem 01 Release Schedule: On Discovery\nThis schedule shows the release date of 20th May.\n        Relevant information to request provided. Some of the information is additionally redacted as ‘out of scope’ and not relevant to the request.\nItem 02 Release Schedule: High Level Delivery Schedule\nThis schedule shows that it was scheduled for release in May    Relevant information to request provided. Some of the information is additionally redacted as ‘out of scope’ and not relevant to the request.\nItem 03 Collect 27 Series 11 Spreadsheet: Manifest\nThis spreadsheet is the manifest TNA received from the Parliamentary Archives   Relevant information to request provided. Some of the information is additionally redacted as ‘out of scope’ and not relevant to the request.\n\n\n\n  12. HMC9/311 or related archival references\n\n      This is Manorial Documents register returns the county of Glamorgan organised by Manor alphabetically. The Manorial Documents Register<https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/manor-search> can now be searched online, by manor or parish and the results display in Discovery.  This search summary says it lists records 1189-1925 held at National Library of Wales, Glamorgan Archives and West Glamorgan Archive Service: Search results: llandough | The National Archives<https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/results/c?_q=llandough&_sf=text_man&_naet=M&_st=mdrc>. This date range is too early, individual farm names are not listed in the titles, and the records are not held at TNA.\n\nThis is the limit of what our catalogue metadata and corporate records allow us to confirm.\n\nThis concludes our response to your request.\n\nIf you are dissatisfied with the handling of your request or the decision which has been reached, you have the right to ask for an internal review.  Internal review requests must be submitted within two months of the date of this response and should be addressed to:\n\nQuality Manager\nVisitor Experience\nThe National Archives\nKew\nRichmond\nSurrey\n[postcode redacted]\n[email redacted]<[email redacted]>\n\nPlease mark your complaint clearly. You have the right to ask the Information Commissioner (ICO) to investigate any aspect of your complaint. However, please note that the ICO is likely to expect internal complaints procedures to have been exhausted before beginning his investigation.\n\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nFOI Assessor\nThe Freedom of Information Centre\nGovernment and Archives Leadership\nThe National Archives\n\n\n\n\n\nYour request\n\nOriginal email dated 13/08/2026:\n\nMy request was deliberately framed more broadly than a simple catalogue search for the words “Great House Farm”, “Ty Mawr”, “Llandough” or “Cedfin”. I specifically asked The National Archives to identify recorded information concerning the existence, transfer, retention, destruction, disposal or absence of records, including records that might not be catalogued under the property's later or local names.\n\nYour response confirms only that you searched the PRO and RW series using:\n\n“Great House Farm” OR “Ty Mawr” OR Llandough OR Cedfin\n\ncombined with “Glamorgan”, and that this produced no catalogue results.\n\nYou also correctly state that Discovery searches catalogue titles and descriptions rather than the contents of records. That qualification is important, because it means the search described cannot establish that relevant records did not exist, were never transferred, or were destroyed.\n\nIn particular, your response does not appear to address the other categories expressly identified in my request, including searches or recorded information relating to:\n\n\n   *   Western Ground Rents Ltd;\n   *   BP Pension Trust Ltd;\n   *   BP Properties Ltd;\n   *   Mountjoy Ltd;\n   *   the proceedings in BP Properties Ltd v Buckler [1987] EWCA Civ 2;\n   *   the 1982–1983 registration and associated government or administrative records;\n   *   the 1980–1988 public campaign and governmental correspondence;\n   *   the 1988 eviction and demolition;\n   *   Cadw or central-government communications concerning the property;\n   *   European Commission of Human Rights Application 14464/88;\n   *   records concerning transfer, destruction or disposal of potentially relevant material; and\n   *   HMC9/311 or related archival references.\n\nThese alternative lines of enquiry are intentional. The purpose is to establish whether relevant information can be identified through the government bodies, corporate entities, court proceedings, administrative processes and archival transfers associated with the property, rather than relying solely upon the modern name by which the property is known.\n\nI am conducting corresponding enquiries with other repositories and public bodies. This is specifically intended to triangulate the documentary history where records may have been catalogued under a different creator, department, legal proceeding, estate, corporate body or administrative function.\n\nI therefore ask that you do not treat the absence of a Discovery keyword hit as an answer to the substantive questions raised.\n\nPlease clarify:\n\n\n  1.  Whether the searches undertaken were limited to the PRO and RW catalogue series and the four property/location search terms identified in your email.\n\n\n  2.  Whether The National Archives searched for the named corporate bodies, court proceedings and application references identified in my request, rather than only searching the property names.\n\n\n  3.  Whether The National Archives holds any recorded transfer, accession, disposal, destruction or retention information indicating that records concerning the relevant property, estate, companies, litigation or government involvement were transferred elsewhere, destroyed, or otherwise disposed of.\n\n\n  4.  If such information is not held, whether this is because no such records are held, because no relevant records were ever transferred to TNA, or because TNA's catalogue/search systems contain no discoverable description of them.\n\n\n  5.  Whether HMC9/311 or related series have been checked, or whether that reference falls outside the search undertaken.\n\n\n  6.  Whether there are central-government, court, departmental or administrative records relating to the litigation and entities identified above which may contain references to the property without using “Great House Farm”, “Ty Mawr”, “Llandough” or “Cedfin” in the catalogue description.\n\nFor the avoidance of doubt, I am not asking The National Archives to infer the contents of uncatalogued records or to undertake an unlimited research project. I am asking for confirmation of the recorded information held by TNA concerning the existence, provenance, transfer, retention, destruction or disposal of potentially relevant records, and for the searches undertaken to locate that information.\n\nThat distinction is particularly important because a catalogue search returning no result is not equivalent to a finding that no relevant historical, governmental or archival records existed.\n\nI would therefore be grateful if this could be reconsidered as a response to the scope of the original FOI enquiry rather than treated solely as a request for assistance with a Discovery keyword search.\n\nI am continuing parallel enquiries with the National Library of Wales, Glamorgan Archives, Cadw, RCAHMW, HM Land Registry and other relevant bodies. The purpose of these enquiries is to establish the documentary chain independently and to identify where records were created, transferred, retained or potentially destroyed.\n\nFollow-up email dated 20/08/2026\n\nIn addition, please expand the scope of the search to include any records held relating to the Glamorgan War Agricultural Executive Committee (“War Ag” / CWAEC) and Great House Farm / the relevant land at Llandough, covering approximately 1936–1950 (with particular focus on 1939–1945). This should include, where held:\n\n\n   *   Any orders, notices or decisions taking possession of, directing cultivation of, or otherwise requisitioning land forming part of Great House Farm (or the specific parcel now associated with Llandough Motor Sales / Llandough Trading Estate).\n   *   Any compensation claims, assessments, payments or settlements under the Compensation (Defence) Act 1939 or related wartime legislation, including the identity of the recipient(s).\n   *   Relevant minutes, correspondence or case files of the Glamorgan CWAEC concerning the farm or land in question.\n   *   Any related material in the MAF series that links the National Farm Survey entries with wartime agricultural possession or compensation.\n\nI hold original documents recording compensation payments in connection with this land and am seeking to identify the wartime status of the holding and the party recognised as entitled to compensation.\n\nClarification email dated 02/09/2026\n\nIn summary, I am seeking recorded information held by The National Archives concerning:\n\n\n   *   Existence, transfer, retention, destruction or disposal of records relating to Great House Farm / Ty Mawr (and earlier descriptions) and associated entities, litigation and administrative processes (as previously detailed).\n\n\n   *   National Farm Survey / MAFF records for the holding.\n\n\n   *   Any Glamorgan CWAEC / War Ag records, possession/requisition orders, cultivation directions, and compensation claims/payments under the Compensation (Defence) Act 1939 or related wartime legislation in respect of the land (or the parcel now associated with Llandough Motor Sales / Llandough Trading Estate), including identity of any compensation recipients.\n\nI am not asking for an open-ended research exercise. I am asking for confirmation of what recorded information (including transfer, accession, disposal or destruction records) is held, the series searched, and the results of those searches."},{"id":"gm-1a0f7435f1e7cf6d","subject":"New response to your FOI request - BP vs Buckler 1987","name":"New response to your FOI request - BP vs Buckler 1987","date":"2026-10-01T11:39:42Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0f7435f1e7cf6d.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0f7428edff878c-new-response-to-your-foi-request-bp-vs-buckler-1987","scopeTopics":["nactso"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["nactso"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email