","date":"2026-10-01T05:12:09Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0f5e0903321107.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0f5e0903321107-delayed-response-to-your-foi-request-great-house-farm-llandough-cardiff-bp-vs-buckler-1987","scopeTopics":["tna"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["tna"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0f5e0903321107","direction":"received","from":"(name not shown; whatdotheyknow.com)","institutions":[{"id":"tna","name":"The National Archives"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0f5e0903321107.json","textStatus":"available","text":"National Archives have delayed.\n\nThey have not replied to your FOI request 'Great House Farm, Llandough, Cardiff (BP vs Buckler 1987)' promptly, as normally required by law.\n\nClick on the link below to send a message to National Archives reminding them to reply to your request. You can also refer your request to the Information Commissioner once the statutory deadline has passed.\n\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/request/great_house_farm_llandough_cardi/followups/new/3540231\n\n\n-- the WhatDoTheyKnow team\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nWhatDoTheyKnow is a project of mySociety run by a small team of staff and\ndedicated volunteers.\n\nmySociety is a charity that believes that information should be accessible to everyone.\nIf this is important to you, please consider donating to help us do more:\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/donate\n\nFind out more about how we handle your data at:\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/help/privacy\n\nmySociety is a registered charity in England and Wales (1076346), and a limited\ncompany (03277032)"},{"id":"gm-1a0f4cb2e18c1a58","subject":"Re: FOI — Mary Williams probate records — death certificate enclosed","name":"Re: FOI — Mary Williams probate records — death certificate enclosed","date":"2026-10-01T00:09:11Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0f4cb2e18c1a58.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0e0b7ebcf0f20f-foi-mary-williams-probate-records-death-certificate-enclosed","scopeTopics":["moj"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["moj"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0e0b7ebcf0f20f","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"moj","name":"Ministry of Justice / HMCTS"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0f4cb2e18c1a58.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Sirs/Ma'am,\n\nI did receive a response on this from one of you lot — i.e. Brit'ish —\nyesterday. There's a glimmer of legitimacy and good governance in the\nhandling of this case. Possibly even as much as 2%, in my estimation so far.\n\nAs I may have already stated, I'm already resigned to the idea that my\nWelsh family in Wales has been practically ethnically cleansed, which this\nBP vs Buckler case alone attests to. Hence why I'm looking to rebuild and\nresume our home life at the Welsh settlement in Argentina, where they\ncelebrate instead of exterminate and replace us - as the Brit'ish are doing\nto us Britons in Britain.\n\nI'm the first of my family to speak out about this case because of the\nimperialist trauma already suffered. Everyone else in my family who has\nchallenged the narrative died at all-too-convenient moments — like my\ngrandmother Mary, during the appeal window of BP registering our land\nparcel in their names.\n\nI was also at our home during our dispossession in 1988, which, according\nto ITV News reports, may have involved SAS personnel under cover as BP\nbailiffs. One of whom threw my pregnant mother over the gate and into a\npuddle when she attempted to get medicine for my father in hospital during\nhis heart attack. She was also a young mother, 22 years old at the time.\nThis is why her arm is in a sling as ITV interviewed her, but they fail to\nreport her pregnancy or that there were three infant children in the house\nat the time the pickaxes came smashing through our home's east-wing roof\nand windows.\n\nSo, if I have erred in writing to you, let my emotions get the better of\nme, I anticipate your forgiveness — as Gandhi once said to Hitler. But I am\nraising all these concerns of fraud, concealment and government involvement\nfrom a place of deep trauma, having had no trauma counselling or post-event\nsupport during what happened to our family for decades in this dispute.\n\nI'm also left to investigate because police or CPS won't, as yet. Land\nRegistry won't accept that they made any mistake, not to me or in a reply\nto the MP. The MP took months to respond, but is now involved. The\ncouncillor for Llandough won't respond, and those responding to my\ncomplaint about that refuse to hold him to account for a non-response. His\ncouncil clerk took retirement the day he responded to my FOI, after\ninitially refusing to participate and trying to fob me off. The Vale of\nGlamorgan Council are also so suspicious from both ends that I can't tell\nwhere the scandal begins or ends with them.\n\nI would like to think the government, and synonymous BP, have evolved since\ntheir siege on our family and home for practically the whole of the last\ncentury. BP won't show me records after 1955, which is another huge scandal\nin itself.\n\nSomething is still very rotten in Denmark. Except not in Denmark, but South\nWales.\n\nI'm not surprised or horrified anymore. I just think that, until proven\notherwise, the Brit'ish State is now a rogue state and terrorist\norganisation which Britons should stop harbouring on our small pale island.\nIts networks/cells are occupying our stolen land with officer or\nPoW/hostage housing, having committed its terrorist act and ethnic\ncleansing to dispossess us.\n\nMy language on this may appear disproportionate and even absurd. But just\nstudy the case, the history and heritage, and you may see what I see.\n\nI was with the UN in Cyprus, when I was RAF Akrotiri guard commander. I\ntook my soldiers there for tours I would arrange. They had to return stolen\noccupied land to the indigenous Greeks.\n\nI understand synthetic land parcels can be created where there's a dog's\ndinner made of the previous administration of title and estate. I know this\nvery well from almost a decade, on and off, in the Dominican Republic over\nthe last decade, where my wife and daughter are.\n\nBut the missing title deed and confusion which led to BP's synthetic land\nparcel doesn't appear to be naturally occurring in BP v Buckler 1987. The\ncase cites \"the whole farm\", treating it as one, then afterwards, as my\nfather was mid-appeal, they clandestinely merge the parcels back together\nto create the \"whole farm\" alleged single land parcel, but with a synthetic\nversion of our Parcel A.\n\nAnd all while we were still alive, on our land since 1667, asserting\nownership, and a family and family home.\n\nWe got buried alive. With our legitimate land-parcel title. Albeit the\npaper stolen. And in lieu of a mimic synthetic parcel, born from an\narguably permissive-occupancy allegation, not permissive occupancy. And\nafter a long line of failed ploys to make our occupancy permissive.\n\nNotwithstanding that, even deprived of our paper title from 1950/52, we\nhadn't paid rent from when we got the freehold to our parcel in 1928,\nmeaning our occupancy will already have qualified for adverse possession by\n1940. The issue was that the two parcels were falsely treated as the same\nprior to them actually merging into one, and with a clandestine\nregistration of our parcel in BP's name in 1983.\n\nIf I'm mistaken — which I surely am about parts of this — I will\nwholeheartedly accept that. But there's no smoke without fire. There is\nsomething to this case that stinks. And much was known at the time, but\nconcealed. I understand the threshold for fraud is high, and I am still\ninvestigating and gathering evidence.\n\nBut my 22 years in Oath ended in 2024, employed under the Vale of Glamorgan\nCouncil, of all entities. I'm no longer a public servant. And I should not\nbe encountering resistance or obstacles like I am, nor should I be the only\none intrigued by this case.\n\nIf there's more your office can do on this case, would you just do it? — so\nthat the Brit'ish State at least resembles a legitimate regime instead of\nfulfilling its own definition of what a terrorist organisation is, with one\nof your networks/cells' camps illegitimately occupying our family's literal\nancestral birthland, and for several straight decades.\n\nIt appears to be pure theatre why the UK Regime's on our land in Wales,\nlike an actual trespassing circus trick and circus. This is a dog's dinner.\n\nAlso, I flew Colombia to Chile earlier this month and was reminded of the\nvastness of land on this planet, and Britain's 0.21% of the landmass of\nthis planet. And the intensity of the claws digging into our land is\nabsurd. The United Kingdom should revisit its position on this matter. I\nrequire that of you. I also wrote to the Royal Family.\n\nI'm also fairly sure the reason the unicorn on the Royal Family Coat of\nArms is now chained down, as of 2024, is my campaigning to the Home Office\nsince 2015. So I do hold some hope that the circus that is the UK regime\ndoes and can alter direction, albeit slowly.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester\n\n\nWilliams/Buckler Family — Great House Farm, Llandough\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nOn Wed, 30 Sept 2026, 14:50 Disclosure Team, <[email redacted]>\nwrote:\n\n> OFFICIAL\n>\n> Good afternoon,\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you for your email. As you are requesting probate records, please\n> use following link for access to Probate records from the Probate Registry\n> - https://www.gov.uk/search-will-probate.\n>\n>\n>\n> Kind regards,\n>\n>\n>\n> [image: signature_1893814455]\n>\n>\n>\n> *Disclosure Team*\n>\n> Security and Information Directorate\n>\n> Service Transformation Group\n>\n> Ministry of Justice\n>\n>\n>\n> OFFICIAL\n>\n> *From:* Requester <[email redacted]>\n> *Sent:* 27 September 2026 05:09\n> *To:* Disclosure Team <[email redacted]>\n> *Subject:* Re: FOI — Mary Williams probate records — death certificate\n> enclosed\n>\n>\n>\n> Disclosure Team,\n>\n>\n>\n> Further to your email of 28 July 2026 requesting a copy of the death\n> certificate for Mary Williams before you can process my FOI request\n> concerning her probate records, please find the death certificate attached.\n>\n>\n>\n> Mary Williams (also known as Mary Buckler following her marriage to\n> Frederick Buckler — see attached extracts from her statement) died in March\n> 1983. She was the occupier and asserted owner of Great House Farm (Ty Mawr\n> alias Cedfin aka Cedwyn), Llandough-juxta-Penarth, Cardiff.\n>\n>\n>\n> My request, submitted 7 June 2026, seeks access to any probate records,\n> wills, affidavits, or ownership claim documents referenced in court\n> proceedings relating to Mary Williams and Great House Farm, Llandough,\n> particularly in connection with the proceedings recorded in BP Properties\n> Ltd v Buckler [1987] EWCA Civ 2.\n>\n>\n>\n> Please can you confirm receipt and advise the expected response date.\n>\n>\n>\n> With talk of civil war on the horizon and already a lot of government\n> fiduciaries in less possession of records than I am in possession of, due\n> to so much destruction of property and records and gaps in records by the\n> government in this case, I am keen to salvage what remains of my family's\n> history and heritage so that the historians and I at the Welsh settlement\n> in Argentina can celebrate my family's homes and strongholds, Welsh history\n> and heritage, rather than waiting more than the almost years we have\n> already waited for the British State to stop its endless war it has waged\n> on us to ethnically cleanse us indigenous Britons from our ancestral\n> birthland in Wales. We'll just return if/when someone overthrows this\n> terrorist organisation, the British State we now seem to be harbouring on\n> the British Isles. All fear and respect for the United Kingdom government\n> has gone for our family. Few care if you now bring guns and finish the job\n> of erasing us, as they have lived four decades in what is effectively the\n> concentration work-camp conditions our towns and cities have been turned\n> into.\n>\n>\n>\n> But before our county treasure, the former courthouse manorial 10-bedroom\n> courthouse, was demolished, or looted of all our furniture from Cardiff by\n> what appears to be SAS under cover as BP bailiffs, I do remember a\n> different realm from the one now blocking out the sun on the British Isles.\n> A realm less demonic, on our sacred Welsh Christian stronghold, upon which\n> the suppressed history and heritage once stood: the Roman villa and\n> medieval burial site and Celtic 2,200-year-old roundhouse and monastery.\n> And Marconi staying with us while inventing radio, or world champion boxer\n> Peerless Jim Driscoll fighting his first fight in our barns. Today there is\n> no history tourist-board plaque, no park-bench plaque. Just 20\n> characterless houses, 13 of which are on our stolen occupied land.\n>\n>\n>\n> As a bona fide, certified and experienced military commander and leader\n> and warfare professional and war veteran, who was subject of this siege on\n> our land and home in 1988, I have to say it is important to call a kettle\n> black and call this what it was and is. This is all synonymous with a\n> terrorist organisation, network or cell, committing an act of terrorism.\n> Establishing a terrorist camp for its officers or PoW/hostages. Following\n> an act of terrorism (my pregnant mum also thrown over our gate, into a\n> puddle, collarbone dislocated, just for seeking medicine for my father who\n> lay in the hospital bed after police left him in a cell and ignored him,\n> complaining of a heart attack for six hours).\n>\n>\n>\n> My family have lived as terrified hostage-like prisoners of war under the\n> British State in Wales. Too frightened to speak up or out about any of\n> this. Since everyone who has challenged acquiescing in our own replacement\n> in my family, on our land since 1667, seems to have died at very convenient\n> timings.\n>\n>\n>\n> I don't fully understand how deep this all goes. I suspect it was a\n> military operation disguised as a civilian action. Perhaps hybrid warfare\n> annexure of our land as some of the last in Wales to fall victim to British\n> State imperialsim. Our home was literally looted of our furniture from\n> Cardiff castle. Our lands were literally looted of medieval burials.\n> Including roman coins and so forth. Much of which ended up in a suspicious\n> fire in Neath.\n>\n>\n>\n> I have called for a public enquiry from the Senedd. But it seems the fight\n> to keep burying us alive is a never ending one.\n>\n>\n>\n> I wish you all the best with the civil war you've waged on us indigenous\n> Britons on our ancestral birthland. I see the British State for what it is\n> now. A terrorist organisation my countrymen will surely soon stop\n> harbouring on our island.\n>\n>\n>\n> I think there's a small window of opportunity in our families case for\n> your regime to even just take a break from trying to make us into striped\n> pjamad hostages. And allow us to restore even parts of our dignity and\n> minor gentry manorial 800 year old home and 4 century of occupancy before\n> you stole and destroyed it by force.\n>\n>\n>\n> We don't even seek to try to enjoy our language, history and herritage\n> under this terrorist anarcho tyrant ethnic cleansing demonic realm regime\n> in Wales anymore. We're committed to rebuilding the replica of our former\n> home in Patagonia where our existence and culture and language and\n> herritage as a race and ethnicity, isn't being actively exterminated and\n> concealed and supressed.\n>\n>\n>\n> I did my 22 years in Oath. Served in war. I'm bona fide, left my military\n> career with full honours. I'm a warfare professional and certified and\n> experienced military commander and leader. And I can't even get back basic\n> FOI's, after almost a year from many branches of your government. And I\n> don't think its all negigence or incompetence.\n>\n>\n>\n> I think there's concealment and fraud and government involvement and your\n> government are in dishonour and are a disgrace in this whole case and\n> treatment of my family and our home and history and herritage.\n>\n>\n>\n> I feel having brought all this to everyone's attention. Which was also\n> very difficult and sensitive to do considering nobody in my family will\n> even speak up or out about it. To be met with such negligence and\n> incompetence and obtuse, hostile, unco-operative, railroading and\n> gatekeeping and delay is a disgrace.\n>\n>\n>\n> This is nothing short of a nightmare under a demonic realm in a\n> concentration work camp where we're being ethnically cleansed and ignored.\n> There is no democracy here. There is no peace here.\n>\n>\n>\n> Church View Close Llandough was once our lands of centuries. Its been\n> nothing short of a crime scene since BP arrived onto the scene in 1969.\n> It's only in these last few decades of the beautiful undisturbed centuries\n> of life on our land overlooking Cardiff, WHICH WE STILL OWN, despite you\n> lot illegally occupying it - its only in the last few decades we've seen\n> all this ethnic cleansing British imperialism turned inwards on us.\n>\n>\n>\n> The evidence is all in this case. The words I use to describe this are\n> strong, but are actually proportionate to what has happened in this case.\n> And is ongoing because nothing and nobody is doing a thing to undo this\n> dogs dinner of a society.\n>\n>\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n>\n>\n> Requester \n>\n> \n>\n> Williams/Buckler Family — Great House Farm, Llandough\n> ------------------------------\n> This e-mail and any attachments is intended only for the attention of the\n> addressee(s). Its unauthorised use, disclosure, storage or copying is not\n> permitted. If you are not the intended recipient, please destroy all copies\n> and inform the sender by return e-mail. Internet e-mail is not a secure\n> medium. Any reply to this message could be intercepted and read by someone\n> else. Please bear that in mind when deciding whether to send material in\n> response to this message by e-mail. This e-mail (whether you are the sender\n> or the recipient) may be monitored, recorded and retained by the Ministry\n> of Justice. Monitoring / blocking software may be used, and e-mail content\n> may be read at any time. You have a responsibility to ensure laws are not\n> broken when composing or forwarding e-mails and their contents.\n>"},{"id":"gm-1a0f30f76be4af0f","subject":"[NLW English] Great House Farm, Llandough | Cardiff Castle Furniture","name":"[NLW English] Great House Farm, Llandough | Cardiff Castle Furniture","date":"2026-09-30T16:04:29Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0f30f76be4af0f.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19f1c45aa603989e-nlw-english-great-house-farm-llandough-cardiff-castle-furniture","scopeTopics":["nlw"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["nlw"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19f1c45aa603989e","direction":"received","from":"National Library of Wales","institutions":[{"id":"nlw","name":"National Library of Wales"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0f30f76be4af0f.json","textStatus":"available","text":"--# Teipiwch eich ateb uwchben y llinell hon #--\n------------------\n\nOwain Roberts\n\nSep 30 2026, 05:04PM via System\n\nPlease refer to previous correspondence for details of the searches conducted on your behalf.\n------------------\n\nRequester\n\nJul 30 2026, 04:27PM via Email\nDear Dr Roberts,\n\nThank you for your response and for confirming the internal review outcome.\n\nI acknowledge the clarification provided regarding the searches undertaken,\nincluding reference to the Bute Estate series D153, D205 and D219, and the\napplication of section 21 in relation to publicly accessible catalogue\nmaterial.\n\nHowever, I would like to clarify one remaining point for the record.\n\nMy concern throughout this request has not been whether the Library holds\nphysical furniture or objects as collection items. My request was\nspecifically for recorded information relating to furniture, furnishings,\nfixtures, household effects and movable property connected with Great House\nFarm (Ty Mawr), Llandough.\n\nThe questions I sought answers to were therefore about the existence of\nrecords such as inventories, valuations, estate correspondence, fixture\nrecords, transfers, disposals, provenance information and references within\nrelevant archival collections.\n\nTo avoid any misunderstanding, could you please confirm specifically:\n\n1. Whether the Bute Estate series D153, D205 and D219 were searched only\nfor references to the property name (for example \"Ty Mawr\", \"Great House\nFarm\" or \"Llandough\"), or whether searches were also undertaken for related\nterms such as:\n\n\n- inventory;\n- valuation;\n- contents;\n- furniture;\n- furnishings;\n- fixtures;\n- household effects;\n- sale;\n- transfer;\n- disposal.\n\n\n2.\n\nWhether any archival collections other than the identified Bute Estate\nseries were considered relevant and searched.\n3.\n\nWhether the Library holds, or has confirmed it does not hold, any:\n\n\n- household inventories;\n- estate valuations;\n- tenant records;\n- correspondence regarding contents or fixtures;\n- records of removal, disposal or transfer of items;\n- photographs or descriptions of household contents connected with Ty\nMawr/Llandough.\n\n\n4. If no such records were identified, please confirm that this\nconclusion is based on searches undertaken across the relevant archival\ncollections, rather than solely on the absence of catalogue descriptions\ncontaining the property name.\n\nI appreciate that archival catalogues do not always describe individual\nitems or subjects in detail. However, the purpose of this clarification is\nto establish precisely what searches were undertaken and what records were\nconsidered, so that there is a clear and accurate record of the Library’s\nposition.\n\nI am grateful for the assistance provided so far and would appreciate a\nspecific response to each point above.\n\nKind regards,\n\nRequester \n\n ------------------\n\nOwain Roberts\n\nJul 30 2026, 02:41PM via System\n\nDear Requester\n\nThank you for your message of acknowledgement.\n\nKind regards and best wishes,\n\nDr Owain Rhys Roberts\n------------------\n\nRequester\n\nJul 29 2026, 02:10PM via Email\nDear Dr Roberts,\nThank you for the detailed internal review outcome of FOI/2026/09 dated 29\nJuly 2026.\nI note the clarification of the searches undertaken (including Bute Estate\nseries D153, D205 and D219), the rationale for limiting terms to the\nproperty name, and the application of section 21 to the publicly accessible\ncatalogue and Reading Room material.\nThis closes the internal review from my side. I will proceed via the\nLibrary’s normal catalogue and Reading Room services for the identified\nestate records. ------------------\n\nOwain Roberts\n\nJul 29 2026, 11:21AM via System\n\nDear Requester,\n\nPlease find attached the outcome of the internal review that you requested on the 20 July 2026. I can confirm that the internal review considered the clarification that you provided on the 20 July 2026.\n\nRegards,\nDr Owain Rhys Roberts\n\nAttached Files\n\n-FOI2026-09-Internal_Review_Outcome.pdf <https://national-library-wales.libanswers.com/loaderTicket?fid=4534050&type=0&key=f13df66d89dd9bb9040b52659665094b>\n\n------------------\n\nRequester\n\nJul 20 2026, 08:37PM via Email\nMs Daniel,\n\nFurther to my internal review request, I would like to provide a short\nclarification regarding the scope of my original FOI request.\n\nHaving reviewed my original request submitted on 3 July 2026, I wish to\nemphasise that the request was expressly for recorded information, not\nmerely for physical objects held within NLW collections.\n\nThe original request specifically sought:\ninventories, catalogues and asset registers;\nacquisition, disposal, loan and transfer records;\nprovenance files and ownership records;\nphotographs;\ncorrespondence concerning identification, recovery, ownership or location;\ninternal reports, legal advice and memoranda;\ncorrespondence involving the Buckler or Williams families;\ncorrespondence with Bute Estate, Cardiff Castle, museums and other public\nbodies.\n\nTherefore, my concern is not that NLW does not hold furniture as part of a\ncollection. I understand that point. My concern is whether relevant\ndocumentary records were searched across appropriate archival collections\nwhere references to such items may exist.\n\nFor example, where NLW holds Bute Estate material relating to Ty\nMawr/Llandough, I would appreciate confirmation as to whether those\ncollections were searched for incidental references to:\nhousehold inventories;\nfixtures;\nvaluations;\nestate correspondence;\ntransfers or disposals;\nprovenance information;\nreferences to occupants or contents of the farmhouse.\n\nThis clarification is provided to assist the internal review and ensure the\noriginal request is considered in its full context.\nI appreciate the catalogue references already provided and look forward to\nconfirmation of the searches undertaken. ------------------\n\nRequester\n\nJul 20 2026, 08:35PM via Email\nMs Daniel,\n\nThank you for your response dated 20 July 2026 regarding FOI/2026/09 and\nfor identifying the catalogue references relating to Ty Mawr/Llandough\nwithin the Bute Estate collections.\n\nI would like to request an internal review, as I believe the response may\nhave interpreted the scope of my request too narrowly.\n\nMy request was for:\n\n\"All recorded information held concerning furniture, furnishings, antiques,\nfixtures, heirlooms and other movable property originating from Great House\nFarm (Ty Mawr), Llandough, including any and all items received from Bute\nEstate, Cardiff Castle, Cardiff Council.\"\n\nI appreciate that the National Library of Wales does not collect furniture\nas a category of object and therefore may not hold the physical items\nthemselves. However, my request was not limited to physical objects or\nacquisition files. It sought *all recorded information* relating to such\nitems.\n\nThis could include, for example:\n\n- inventories;\n- valuations;\n- estate correspondence;\n- fixture schedules;\n- probate references;\n- sale catalogues;\n- photographs;\n- tenancy records;\n- references within Bute Estate papers;\n- records of transfer, removal or disposal.\n\nThe response confirms that NLW holds significant Bute Estate material\nrelating to Ty Mawr, including maps, leases and deeds. However, it does not\nclarify whether those wider collections were searched for references to the\nfarmhouse contents, fixtures, or related estate administration records.\n\nCould the internal review please clarify:\n\n1.\n\nWhat collections and record series were searched in responding to this\nrequest?\n2.\n\nWere searches undertaken beyond acquisition files, including within Bute\nEstate papers and related archival collections?\n3.\n\nWere terms such as:\n\n\n- Great House Farm;\n- Ty Mawr;\n- Llandough;\n- fixtures;\n- inventory;\n- valuation;\n- contents;\n- furnishings;\n- sale;\n- transfer;\n\nused as part of the search?\n\n4. If no relevant records are held, could you please confirm the\nsearches undertaken and the basis for that conclusion?\n\nThe reason this distinction matters is that my wider research concerns the\ndocumentary history of the farmhouse and garden plot following changes\naffecting Ty Mawr/Llandough after the 19th century. Records relating to\nfixtures, valuations, inventories or estate administration may provide\nimportant historical context, regardless of whether the physical items\nthemselves remain.\n\nI am not asking NLW to determine ownership or provide a legal opinion. I am\nseeking confirmation that all relevant archival holdings have been\nconsidered and that the request has been searched against the appropriate\ncollections.\n\nThank you for your assistance. I look forward to the outcome of the\ninternal review. ------------------\n\nIwan ap Dafydd\n\nJul 20 2026, 05:10PM via System\n\nDear Requester,\n\nAttached is the response to your recent Freedom of Information request.\n\nRegards,\n\nIwan ap Dafydd\n\nAttached Files\n\n-FOI2026-09.pdf <https://national-library-wales.libanswers.com/loaderTicket?fid=4517166&type=0&key=5afe8f6fd61ea76cddc027c015f0bfb2>\n\n------------------\n\nIwan ap Dafydd\n\nJul 03 2026, 11:45AM via System\n\nDear Requester,\n\nI am writing in response to your recent application for information held by the Library\n \n(Ref FOI/2026/09) received here at the Library on 3 July, 2026\n \nYou will receive a reply within 20 working days, being 31 July, 2026\n\nBest wishes,\n\nIwan ap Dafydd\n\n------------------\n\nCwestiwn gwreiddiol\n\nJul 02 2026, 08:51PM via Email\nGreat House Farm, Llandough | Cardiff Castle Furniture\n\nFreedom of Information Act 2000 Request\n\nSir/Madam,\n\nI am making this request under the Freedom of Information Act 2000.\n\nI seek all recorded information held concerning furniture, furnishings, antiques, fixtures, heirlooms and other movable property originating from Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough, including any and all items we received from Bute Estate, Cardiff Castle, Cardiff Council.  Weather it be museums, archives or any other public body. I know museums have artifacts from our land in Llandough. \n\nSpecifically, I request copies of all:\n-Inventories, catalogues and asset registers.\n-Acquisition, disposal, loan and transfer records.\n-Provenance files and ownership records.\n-Photographs of items identified as originating from Great House Farm.\n-Correspondence concerning the identification, recovery, ownership or location of such items.\n-Internal reports, legal advice and memoranda relating to Great House Farm furniture or household effects.\n-Correspondence mentioning the Buckler or Williams families in connection with these items.\n-Records concerning any efforts by Cardiff Council or Cardiff Castle to trace, recover or secure items believed to have originated from Great House Farm.\n-Correspondence with the Bute Estate, museums, the Department for Culture, Media and Sport, the Reviewing Committee on the Export of Works of Art and Objects of Cultural Interest (RCEWA), Arts Council England or any other public authority regarding objects from Great House Farm.\n\nFor context, I am allegeing that my family was unlawfully dispossessed of Great House Farm through fraud, and that furniture and other historic possessions belonging to the family were consequently dispersed. It's possible the bailiffs from BP were also SAS soldiers. We are still gathering actionable intelligence. \n\nI appreciate that the Freedom of Information Act does not require the authority to determine the merits of that allegation. However, it is directly relevant to understanding why I seek the recorded information.\n\nI am currently developing a full-scale replica of Great House Farm in Patagonia, Argentina as a museum dedicated to preserving the history of the house, its occupants and its contents. Should records identify surviving items from the farm, whether held by Cardiff Council, Cardiff Castle, museums or other public institutions, I intend to pursue their lawful return or transfer so they may ultimately be reunited with the reconstructed Great House Farm museum. This statement is provided solely as context for my request and does not seek a determination under the Freedom of Information Act.\n\nIf any information is withheld, please identify each exemption relied upon and explain why it applies, including the outcome of any public interest test where required.\n\nIf another public authority is more likely to hold the requested information, I would be grateful if you would transfer the request or advise me accordingly.\n\nI look forward to your response within the statutory time limit.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\nRequester \n\n\n\n\n\n------------------\n\nThank you for using the National Library of Wales Enquiry Service.\n\n\n\nThis email is sent from LibAnswers in relationship to ticket #9603180.\n\nRead our privacy policy. <https://springshare.com/privacy.html>"},{"id":"gm-1a0f2f0f54a36630","subject":"RE: Ty Mawr, Llandough, Cardiff","name":"RE: Ty Mawr, Llandough, Cardiff","date":"2026-09-30T15:31:07Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0f2f0f54a36630.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a02ad4c2f0fd6e3-ty-mawr-llandough-cardiff","scopeTopics":["glamorgan"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["glamorgan"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a02ad4c2f0fd6e3","direction":"received","from":"Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg","institutions":[{"id":"glamorgan","name":"Glamorgan Archives"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0f2f0f54a36630.json","attachments":[{"name":"Bute 10.doc","title":"Bute 10 catalogue document (resent under 2026/4097b)","url":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/pdf/foi/2026-09-30_glamorgan-archives_bute-10.doc","mime":"application/msword","bytes":133120,"sha256":"1e3aaf941bbeb767d2cb8bc6534ffc817c93541d8838a0ad87a5dbb63e02e870"}],"textStatus":"available","text":"Ref: 2026/4097b\n\nDear Requester,\n\nThank you for your additional email that was received on 8th September.  Please accept our apologies for the delay in responding; we have recently been receiving a large number of enquiries.\n\nIn response to your questions below:\n\n\n  1.  Daniel Thomas 1877 deed: we do not appear to hold any deeds relating to this transaction.  A search of our catalogue has been carried out and we cannot suggest any documents that would be worth searching to find record of the transaction you are seeking information on that are within our collections.  There are some records in the Cardiff Library Bute collection that may be relevant though, see point 3 below.\n  2.  D696 Western Ground Rents: please see our catalogue for descriptions of the three volumes included in this collection: D696/1<https://canfod.glamarchives.gov.uk/en/records/D696/1>, D696/2<https://canfod.glamarchives.gov.uk/en/records/D696/2>, D696/3<https://canfod.glamarchives.gov.uk/en/records/D696/3>.  As mentioned previously, a member of our team could search these records on your behalf at a charge, or they can be accessed for free in our public searchroom.  Only once the records have been searched would we be able to confirm whether any information is held within them in relation to land in Llandough and Ty Mawr / Great House Farm.\n  3.  Cardiff Library Bute Estate records: one possible record that could be worth searching in this collection is document reference Bute X.6(12) which contains abstracts of payments and receipts by the trustees of the Marquis of Bute's properties, 1877 (copy).  Other similar records in Bute X might also be of interest (see attached catalogue).  These records are currently still with Cardiff Library so you should contact them about access in the short-term.\n  4.  Land Valuation 1910 maps at National Archives: please the National Archives’ research guide on the land valuation records for guidance on how to find the maps: Land use, value and ownership: Valuation Office Survey 1910-1915 - The National Archives<https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/help-with-your-research/research-guides/valuation-office-survey-land-value-ownership-1910-1915/#4-how-to-find-plans-maps>.  When enquiring with the National Archives, ensure you are asking specifically about the 1910 land valuation survey maps.  The Ordnance Survey sheet that is relevant to your search is Glamorgan XLVII.6.\n\nWe hope that this information is of use to you.  We will answer your remaining emails in due course.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nLouise Hunt\nArchifydd / Archivist\n\nArchifau Morgannwg / Glamorgan Archives\nClos Parc Morgannwg\nLecwydd / Leckwith\nCaerdydd / Cardiff\n[postcode redacted]\n\n[phone redacted]\n[email redacted]<[email redacted]>\n\nwww.archifaumorgannwg.gov.uk/<http://www.archifaumorgannwg.gov.uk/> www.glamarchives.gov.uk<http://www.glamarchives.gov.uk/>\nwww.facebook.com/glamarchives<http://www.facebook.com/glamarchives>\nwww.instagram.com/glamarchives<https://www.instagram.com/glamarchives/>\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]>\nSent: 07 September 2026 20:55\nTo: Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg <[email redacted]>\nSubject: Re: Ty Mawr, Llandough, Cardiff\n\nEXTERNAL: This email originated from outside Cardiff Council, take care when clicking links.\nALLANOL: Daw'r e-bost hwn o'r tu allan i Gyngor Caerdydd, cymerwch ofal wrth glicio ar ddolenni.\n\n\nLouise,\n\nThanks for your detailed and prompt reply and for the additional information concerning the Bute Estate records and D696.\n\nI just want to clarify one important point in relation to my original enquiry, as I think I may have caused a slight misunderstanding about the particular transaction I am trying to establish.\n\nMy principal question is not whether there are deeds between the Bute Estate and Alfred Thomas. The key transaction I am trying to locate and verify is the 1877 transaction concerning Daniel Thomas and the Bute Estate, which I understand from the surviving family evidence to have concerned a defined portion of Great House Farm, including the farmhouse and surrounding land. I see an 1877 agreement between both parties, for that year, but for a different property in Cardiff. I wondered if it was mis labelled and if you could validify its content, particularly any map data. So as the integrity of the descriptor of the record is assured or revisited.\n\nThe issue is whether the Bute Estate conveyed, granted or otherwise dealt with that defined parcel in favour of Daniel Thomas, and, crucially, what the instrument and its plan actually show about the boundaries and the nature of the interest created. In 1877.\n\nThis is important because the present documentary reconstruction I am preparing treats the 1877 instrument as the potential starting point for the separate House Plot chain. The working case is that the instrument dealt with the farmhouse land separately from the eastern agricultural/quarrying parcel, with provisions connected to quarrying and the subsequent passing of the house land to the occupying Williams family when quarrying ceased. That proposition is expressly being treated as a matter requiring verification against the original instrument and plan, rather than as an established fact.\n\nI therefore wonder whether the catalogue search could be reconsidered specifically on the following basis:\n\nBute Estate → Daniel Thomas → defined land at Llandough / Great House Farm, approximately 1876–1878\n\nincluding instruments which may not themselves be catalogued under “Great House Farm” or “Daniel Thomas”, but which might appear under:\n\n  *   the name of the property or former field name;\n  *   the Marquess of Bute or his trustees as grantor;\n  *   Daniel Thomas as builder, quarryman, lime-burner or occupier;\n  *   a lease, conveyance, agreement, surrender or counterpart;\n  *   quarrying or mineral rights;\n  *   a plan accompanying an instrument;\n  *   or a transaction recorded in a Bute Estate rental, ledger or estate correspondence rather than in the deed catalogue.\n\nI appreciate that your catalogue may simply contain no surviving record of this transaction. If that is the position, that is useful information in itself. What I am particularly trying to establish is whether the 1877 transaction has been positively searched for and not found, as opposed to there being no later catalogue entry linking Daniel or Alfred Thomas to the Bute Estate.\n\nD696 and the later Western Ground Rents material\n\nThank you also for pointing me towards D696. Please do let me know which item(s) within D696 contain the book plans and rental books for Western Ground Rents Ltd c.1938–1944, particularly anything relating to Great House Farm, Llandough or the land occupied by John Williams.\n\nThis is potentially very relevant because the documentary issue I am investigating is whether the later WGR estate corresponded geographically with the parcel dealt with by the Bute Estate in 1877, or instead with the separate agricultural/quarrying parcel.\n\nBute Estate finding aid\n\nI would also be grateful if you could retain my enquiry against the Bute Estate material being transferred from Cardiff Library. In particular, I would be interested in any items concerning:\n\n  *   Llandough / Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr;\n  *   Daniel Thomas;\n  *   the 1870s Bute Estate conveyances or leases;\n  *   estate plans showing parcel boundaries;\n  *   quarrying or lime works;\n  *   rentals distinguishing the farm from the farmhouse/cottages; and\n  *   subsequent dealings with the relevant parcels.\n\n1910 Land Valuation map\n\nFinally, thank you for identifying DVAL/1/33. If Glamorgan Archives has any information enabling me to identify the corresponding 1910 valuation map held by The National Archives, I would be grateful for the relevant reference. I am trying to make a targeted request rather than simply asking The National Archives to search broadly for a Llandough map.\n\nThank you again for your assistance. The clarification regarding what is and is not held at Glamorgan Archives is useful, and I appreciate your help in narrowing down the surviving documentary trail.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nRequester \n\n\n**********************************************************************\nThe Council welcomes correspondence in Welsh, English or bilingually. We will ensure that we communicate with you in the language of your choice, as long as you let us know which you prefer. Corresponding in Welsh will not lead to delay.\n\nPrivileged/Confidential Information may be contained in this message. If you are not the addressee indicated in this message (or responsible for delivery of the message to such person), you may not copy or deliver this message to anyone. In such case, you should destroy this message and kindly notify the sender by reply email. Please advise immediately if you or your employer does not consent to Internet email for messages of this kind.  Opinions, conclusions and other information in this message that do not relate to the official business of the Council of the City and County of Cardiff shall be understood as neither given nor endorsed by it.  All e-mail sent to or from this address will be processed by Cardiff County Councils Corporate E-mail system and may be subject to scrutiny by someone other than the addressee.\n**********************************************************************\nMae’r Cyngor yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg, Saesneg neu’n ddwyieithog. Byddwn yn cyfathrebu â chi yn ôl eich dewis, dim ond i chi roi gwybod i ni pa un sydd well gennych. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi.\n\nMae'n bosibl bod gwybodaeth gyfrinachol yn y neges hon. Os na chyfeirir y neges atoch chi'n benodol (neu os nad ydych chi'n gyfrifol am drosglwyddo'r neges i'r person a enwir), yna ni chewch gopio na throsglwyddo'r neges. Mewn achos o'r fath, dylech ddinistrio'r neges a hysbysu'r anfonwr drwy e-bost ar unwaith. Rhowch wybod i'r anfonydd ar unwaith os nad ydych chi neu eich cyflogydd yn caniatau e-bost y Rhyngrwyd am negeseuon fel hon. Rhaid deall nad yw'r safbwyntiau, y casgliadau a'r wybodaeth arall yn y neges hon nad ydynt yn cyfeirio at fusnes swyddogol Cyngor Dinas a Sir Caerdydd yn cynrychioli barn y Cyngor Sir nad yn cael sel ei fendith. Caiff unrhyw negeseuon a anfonir at, neu o'r cyfeiriad e-bost hwn eu prosesu gan system E-bost Gorfforaethol Cyngor Sir Caerdydd a gallant gael eu harchwilio gan rywun heblaw'r person a enwir.\n**********************************************************************\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\nAttachments (1)\n- Bute 10.doc (application/msword, 130 KB): https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/pdf/foi/2026-09-30_glamorgan-archives_bute-10.doc"},{"id":"gm-1a0f2d7e013f1d97","subject":"FOI/EIR request - Case Ref/ 00211557","name":"FOI/EIR request - Case Ref/ 00211557","date":"2026-09-30T15:03:44Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0f2d7e013f1d97.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0f2d7e013f1d97-foi-eir-request-case-ref-00211557","scopeTopics":["vale-foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["vale-foi"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0f2d7e013f1d97","direction":"received","from":"(name not shown; valeofglamorgan.gov.uk)","institutions":[{"id":"vale-foi","name":"Vale of Glamorgan Council"}],"refs":["00211557"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0f2d7e013f1d97.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester\n\nFREEDOM OF INFORMATION ACT 2000/ENVIRONMENTAL INFORMATION REGULATIONS 2004 - INFORMATION REQUEST\n\nThank you for your request for information about: Great House Farm / Church View Close — s.52/land charges, planning register, EA screening, condition 16–18 chain, 92/00671/RES\n\nYour request was received on 28/09/2026 and is being considered under the terms of the Freedom of Information Act 2000 and the Environmental Information Regulations 2004. Your query has been given the reference 00211557.\n\nIn some circumstances a fee may be payable and if that is the case we will let you know. A fees notice will be issued to you, and you will be required to pay before we will proceed to deal with your request.\n\n\n\n\nYou should receive the information requested within 20 working days unless the Council does not hold the information or there is a reason for it to be withheld. We will write to you in any event.\n\nIf you have any requirements regarding the format any information should be supplied in, e.g. the language to be used, audio, large print and so on, then if you have not already done so please let us know. \n\nIf you have any queries or concerns then please do not hesitate to contact us. Please remember to quote the reference number above in any future communications.\n\n\n\nFurther information about your rights is also available from the Information\nCommissioner at:\n\n\nInformation Commissioner's Office - Wales\n\nSecond Floor, Churchill House\n\nChurchill Way,\n\nCardiff\n\n[postcode redacted]\n\n[phone redacted] \n\n[email redacted] \n\n\n\nYours sincerely,\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nInformation Governance Officer/ Swyddog Llywodraethu Gwybodaeth\n\nDirector's Office - Resources / Swyddfa'r Cyfarwyddwr - Adnoddau\n\nVale of Glamorgan Council / Cyngor Bro Morgannwg\n\ntel / ffôn: [phone redacted]\n\ne-mail / e-bost: [email redacted] or [email redacted]\n\n \n\nConsider the environment. Please don't print this e-mail unless you really need to.\n\nYstyriwch yr amgylchedd. Peidiwch ag argraffu'r neges hon oni bai fod gwir angen.\n\n \n\nVisit our Website at www.valeofglamorgan.gov.uk\n\nEwch i'n gwefan yn www.bromorgannwg.gov.uk\n\n \n\nFind us on Facebook / Cewch ddod o hyd i ni ar Facebook\n\nFollow us on Twitter /Dilynwch ni ar Twitter\n\n \n\nCorrespondence is welcomed in Welsh or English / Croesewir Gohebiaeth yn y Gymraeg neu yn Saesneg."},{"id":"gm-1a0f297acf478271","subject":"RE: [3] Re: Formal complaint — GGAT / Heneb: Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough — HER identifiers and the 1978–1995 record","name":"RE: [3] Re: Formal complaint — GGAT / Heneb: Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough — HER identifiers and the 1978–1995 record","date":"2026-09-30T13:53:30Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0f297acf478271.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0ed989b1263c57-3-re-formal-complaint-ggat-heneb-great-house-farm-t-mawr-llandough-her-identifiers-and-the-1978-1995-record","scopeTopics":["heneb"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["heneb"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0ed989b1263c57","direction":"received","from":"Steven Foulston","institutions":[{"id":"heneb","name":"Heneb (formerly GGAT) / Historic Environment Record"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0f297acf478271.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester, I acknowledge receipt of your supplementary information.\n\nBest regards\n\nSteven Foulston\n\nFrom: Requester  \nSent: 30 September 2026 13:57\nTo: Steven Foulston  \nSubject: [3] Re: Formal complaint — GGAT / Heneb: Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough — HER identifiers and the 1978–1995 record\n\nMr Foulston,\n\nThank you for acknowledging the complaint of 27 September 2026 and for confirming you are the investigating officer. I note the five-week target.\n\nPlease treat this email as a supplement to that complaint. The attached is my report of GGATs involvement to the extent I know and a current consolidated account of GGAT’s role as curator, advisor, contractor and record-holder at Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough, and of the record now held by Heneb.\n\nAttachment\nGGAT — Forensic Critique, Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough, Version 4.0.3 (Final), 30 September 2026.\n\nMy AI agent generated report based on the info I hold thus far, does attempt to  distinguish established facts, reasonable inferences, matters that cannot be established, and facts that cut against the criticism. I took care not to allege dishonesty, bad faith or criminal conduct by named officers. It records nine defects: the standing building; the 1982–1988 SMR gap; an evaluation method mismatched to GGAT’s own predictive model; specify-price-bid-execute on the same application; a missing representation from a County Councillor identified as a member of GGAT; access converted into a “non-existent to low” zoning; failure to answer the Disused Burial Grounds question GGAT itself raised; a still-misclassified and inconsistently identified HER; and the absence, until your appointment, of any substantive institutional answer.\n\nWhat I ask Heneb to do on this complaint\n\n1. Give me a complaint reference and confirm the target date for a written response.\n\n2. Confirm which legal entity now holds GGAT’s records for this site (Glamorgan-Gwent Archaeological Trust Limited, company 01276976 / charity 505609 / Heneb operationally).\n\n3. Correct or formally review HER GGAT02038s, which remains classified as “POST MEDIEVAL – Farm” despite the 1994 cemetery and the 2005 publication of 1,026 burials as the largest early-medieval burial population then recovered in Wales.\n\n4. Reconcile the HER identifiers already supplied to the family (including GGAT02038s, GGAT03586s and GGATE003872) and supply the source file, not a public summary.\n\n5. Search and disclose, or certify as not held: file S387; the 1 October 1982 letter; the 1989 consultee report on 89/01396/OUT; the 8 February 1990 further comments (Appendix B to the officer’s report); the 1990 assessment archive; any emergency building record, watching brief or fabric report on the December 1988 demolition; and any conflicts register or trustee minute for the 1989–90 commission.\n\n6. Address the nine criticisms in the attached report in writing.\n\nOur family’s wider case, so the complaint is not read as a cataloguing dispute only\n\nSeparately from the archaeological record:\n\nI believe there has been fraud, concealment and government involvement resulting in the dispossession of our home and land. The family has occupied and claimed Great House Farm since at least 1667. In 1988, after BP Properties Ltd v Buckler, we were removed. The farmhouse was demolished on 6 December 1988. About twenty houses now stand on that ground. The narrative that followed treated us as peasant squatters evicted from an old farmhouse. That narrative is false.\n\nIn reconstructing the house as a Welsh heritage museum in Patagonia I found that our ownership of the house-and-garden parcel was never extinguished. The working case is two parcels: Parcel A (house and garden) belonging to Mary Williams; Parcel B treated as if it were the whole farm; a synthetic title built by decades of false assertion that occupation of Parcel A was permissive. Limitation is postponed if concealment is made out.\n\nThat title case is for the Land Registry, the courts and the other bodies already on notice. It is not for Heneb to adjudicate. It is why the archaeological record matters. The same ground GGAT evaluated as largely empty contained inhumations a few centimetres below the topsoil. RCAHMW’s own file records that the standing building remained partially recorded because of the ownership dispute, and was then destroyed.\n\nOur family has suffered shame, dispossession, ostracisation, exile, reputational harm, and the loss of our ancestral home, land and an incompletely recorded building. There were, on our case, serious human-rights failures in how that was done too. Such as my pregnant mum being thrown over the gateway of our property into a puddle, which is why she is in a sling with a dislocated collar bone on ITV News Wales footage afterwards. She was trying to get medicine for my father who lay in hospital after a heart attack. I watched helpless, as a child, while our home was demolished in front of us.\n\nI am giving each institution that held a public function over this site the opportunity to remedy what it accepts. For Heneb that means putting the record right, producing what it holds, and saying plainly where GGAT’s 1978–1995 conduct and the present HER fail the standards that applied then and apply now. Remedy from Heneb is correction of the record, disclosure, and an accountable written position — not a finding on title.\n\nPlease confirm receipt of the attached report and that it forms part of the complaint file you are investigating.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester \n"},{"id":"gm-1a0f294c9b3320c1","subject":"RE: FOI — Mary Williams probate records — death certificate enclosed","name":"RE: FOI — Mary Williams probate records — death certificate enclosed","date":"2026-09-30T13:50:21Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0f294c9b3320c1.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0e0b7ebcf0f20f-foi-mary-williams-probate-records-death-certificate-enclosed","scopeTopics":["moj"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["moj"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0e0b7ebcf0f20f","direction":"received","from":"Disclosure Team","institutions":[{"id":"moj","name":"Ministry of Justice / HMCTS"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0f294c9b3320c1.json","textStatus":"available","text":"OFFICIAL\n\n\nGood afternoon,\n\n\n\nThank you for your email. As you are requesting probate records, please use following link for access to Probate records from the Probate Registry - https://www.gov.uk/search-will-probate.\n\nKind regards,\n\n[signature_1893814455]\n\nDisclosure Team\nSecurity and Information Directorate\nService Transformation Group\nMinistry of Justice\n\n\n\nOFFICIAL\nFrom: Requester  >\nSent: 27 September 2026 05:09\nTo: Disclosure Team  >\nSubject: Re: FOI — Mary Williams probate records — death certificate enclosed\n\nDisclosure Team,\n\nFurther to your email of 28 July 2026 requesting a copy of the death certificate for Mary Williams before you can process my FOI request concerning her probate records, please find the death certificate attached.\n\nMary Williams (also known as Mary Buckler following her marriage to Frederick Buckler — see attached extracts from her statement) died in March 1983. She was the occupier and asserted owner of Great House Farm (Ty Mawr alias Cedfin aka Cedwyn), Llandough-juxta-Penarth, Cardiff.\n\nMy request, submitted 7 June 2026, seeks access to any probate records, wills, affidavits, or ownership claim documents referenced in court proceedings relating to Mary Williams and Great House Farm, Llandough, particularly in connection with the proceedings recorded in BP Properties Ltd v Buckler [1987] EWCA Civ 2.\n\nPlease can you confirm receipt and advise the expected response date.\n\nWith talk of civil war on the horizon and already a lot of government fiduciaries in less possession of records than I am in possession of, due to so much destruction of property and records and gaps in records by the government in this case, I am keen to salvage what remains of my family's history and heritage so that the historians and I at the Welsh settlement in Argentina can celebrate my family's homes and strongholds, Welsh history and heritage, rather than waiting more than the almost years we have already waited for the British State to stop its endless war it has waged on us to ethnically cleanse us indigenous Britons from our ancestral birthland in Wales. We'll just return if/when someone overthrows this terrorist organisation, the British State we now seem to be harbouring on the British Isles. All fear and respect for the United Kingdom government has gone for our family. Few care if you now bring guns and finish the job of erasing us, as they have lived four decades in what is effectively the concentration work-camp conditions our towns and cities have been turned into.\n\nBut before our county treasure, the former courthouse manorial 10-bedroom courthouse, was demolished, or looted of all our furniture from Cardiff by what appears to be SAS under cover as BP bailiffs, I do remember a different realm from the one now blocking out the sun on the British Isles. A realm less demonic, on our sacred Welsh Christian stronghold, upon which the suppressed history and heritage once stood: the Roman villa and medieval burial site and Celtic 2,200-year-old roundhouse and monastery. And Marconi staying with us while inventing radio, or world champion boxer Peerless Jim Driscoll fighting his first fight in our barns. Today there is no history tourist-board plaque, no park-bench plaque. Just 20 characterless houses, 13 of which are on our stolen occupied land.\n\nAs a bona fide, certified and experienced military commander and leader and warfare professional and war veteran, who was subject of this siege on our land and home in 1988, I have to say it is important to call a kettle black and call this what it was and is. This is all synonymous with a terrorist organisation, network or cell, committing an act of terrorism. Establishing a terrorist camp for its officers or PoW/hostages. Following an act of terrorism (my pregnant mum also thrown over our gate, into a puddle, collarbone dislocated, just for seeking medicine for my father who lay in the hospital bed after police left him in a cell and ignored him, complaining of a heart attack for six hours).\n\nMy family have lived as terrified hostage-like prisoners of war under the British State in Wales. Too frightened to speak up or out about any of this. Since everyone who has challenged acquiescing in our own replacement in my family, on our land since 1667, seems to have died at very convenient timings.\n\nI don't fully understand how deep this all goes. I suspect it was a military operation disguised as a civilian action. Perhaps hybrid warfare annexure of our land as some of the last in Wales to fall victim to British State imperialsim. Our home was literally looted of our furniture from Cardiff castle. Our lands were literally looted of medieval burials. Including roman coins and so forth. Much of which ended up in a suspicious fire in Neath.\n\nI have called for a public enquiry from the Senedd. But it seems the fight to keep burying us alive is a never ending one.\n\nI wish you all the best with the civil war you've waged on us indigenous Britons on our ancestral birthland. I see the British State for what it is now. A terrorist organisation my countrymen will surely soon stop harbouring on our island.\n\nI think there's a small window of opportunity in our families case for your regime to even just take a break from trying to make us into striped pjamad hostages. And allow us to restore even parts of our dignity and minor gentry manorial 800 year old home and 4 century of occupancy before you stole and destroyed it by force.\n\nWe don't even seek to try to enjoy our language, history and herritage under this terrorist anarcho tyrant ethnic cleansing demonic realm regime in Wales anymore. We're committed to rebuilding the replica of our former home in Patagonia where our existence and culture and language and herritage as a race and ethnicity, isn't being actively exterminated and concealed and supressed.\n\nI did my 22 years in Oath. Served in war. I'm bona fide, left my military career with full honours. I'm a warfare professional and certified and experienced military commander and leader. And I can't even get back basic FOI's, after almost a year from many branches of your government. And I don't think its all negigence or incompetence.\n\nI think there's concealment and fraud and government involvement and your government are in dishonour and are a disgrace in this whole case and treatment of my family and our home and history and herritage.\n\nI feel having brought all this to everyone's attention. Which was also very difficult and sensitive to do considering nobody in my family will even speak up or out about it. To be met with such negligence and incompetence and obtuse, hostile, unco-operative, railroading and gatekeeping and delay is a disgrace.\n\nThis is nothing short of a nightmare under a demonic realm in a concentration work camp where we're being ethnically cleansed and ignored. There is no democracy here. There is no peace here.\n\nChurch View Close Llandough was once our lands of centuries. Its been nothing short of a crime scene since BP arrived onto the scene in 1969. It's only in these last few decades of the beautiful undisturbed centuries of life on our land overlooking Cardiff, WHICH WE STILL OWN, despite you lot illegally occupying it - its only in the last few decades we've seen all this ethnic cleansing British imperialism turned inwards on us.\n\nThe evidence is all in this case. The words I use to describe this are strong, but are actually proportionate to what has happened in this case. And is ongoing because nothing and nobody is doing a thing to undo this dogs dinner of a society.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nRequester \n\nWilliams/Buckler Family — Great House Farm, Llandough\n________________________________\nThis e-mail and any attachments is intended only for the attention of the addressee(s). Its unauthorised use, disclosure, storage or copying is not permitted. If you are not the intended recipient, please destroy all copies and inform the sender by return e-mail. Internet e-mail is not a secure medium. Any reply to this message could be intercepted and read by someone else. Please bear that in mind when deciding whether to send material in response to this message by e-mail. This e-mail (whether you are the sender or the recipient) may be monitored, recorded and retained by the Ministry of Justice. Monitoring / blocking software may be used, and e-mail content may be read at any time. You have a responsibility to ensure laws are not broken when composing or forwarding e-mails and their contents."},{"id":"gm-1a0f2926630689c3","subject":"Subject Access Request","name":"Subject Access Request","date":"2026-09-30T13:47:44Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0",