o complaint re-served 20 September 2026 — Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough","name":"RE: Supplement to complaint re-served 20 September 2026 — Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough","date":"2026-09-30T08:41:32Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0f179fea76284a.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0c990bf55b4ddd-supplement-to-complaint-re-served-20-september-2026-great-house-farm-t-mawr-llandough","scopeTopics":["cadw"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["cadw"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0c990bf55b4ddd","direction":"received","from":"(name not shown; gov.wales)","institutions":[{"id":"cadw","name":"Welsh Government / Cadw"}],"refs":["ATISN27021"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0f179fea76284a.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Good morning Requester,\n\nYour correspondence has been received by the Welsh Government Complaints advice team.\n\nThe complaints policy allows us to consider complaints about Welsh Government’s administrative actions (i.e. maladministration) or service failure by us. The policy is available by post on request and can also be found on our website: How to make a complaint about Welsh Government [HTML] | GOV.WALES .\n\nThe Policy does not allow us to consider complaints about a decision which has not involved maladministration.\n\nYour concerns will be addressed by the relevant officials within Welsh Government, who will respond to you directly and as soon as possible.\n\nBest Regards,\n\nSteffan Herdman\nRheolwr Cwynion / Complaints Manager\nY Tîm Cynghori ar Gwynion / Complaints Advice Team\nGweithrediadau a Chyflenwi AD / HR Operations and Delivery\nCyfarwyddiaeth Pobl a Lleoedd  / People and Places Directorate\nLlywodraeth Cymru / Welsh Government\n\nFrom: Requester  \nSent: 22 September 2026 16:51\nTo: Wigley, Andy (LCC - Cadw)  \nCc: Complaints - Cwynion  ; Cadw Planning  \nSubject: Supplement to complaint re-served 20 September 2026 — Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough\n\nDr Wigley,\n\nThis is a supplement to the complaint re-served on 20 September, not a chaser. I do not expect a reply before the stage-one response. It adds seven matters under the existing headings at section 7, and one addition to the outcomes at section 9, set out at the end.\n\nThe public interest is engaged alongside my family's. Great House Farm stood beside the Grade I church at Llandough, was recorded by the Royal Commission in 1974, sat within a known archaeological site, and was described in the post-demolition planning record as a \"county treasure\". I do not ask Cadw to treat the 1994 discoveries as knowledge it had in 1988. They show what was lost; the pre-1988 record is the measure of Cadw's process.\n\nOn my family's account and the contemporary press report, protection was being sought in the first days of December 1988 and the house was demolished almost immediately afterwards. I do not ask Cadw to determine responsibility for the demolition. The grievance is narrower: protection was sought at the critical moment, and Cadw cannot now show what it considered.\n\n1. The adopted development plan (matter A)\n\nThe planning officer's report on application 89/01396/OUT records that the supporting information to Policy H.2 of the East Vale Local Plan \"states that buildings on the site should be retained, however were unfortunately demolished a little while ago.\" I do not say this bound Cadw or required listing. I ask:\n(a) was Cadw aware of it in December 1988;\n(b) did the planning authority draw it to Cadw's attention; and\n(c) if neither can be established, will Cadw say so expressly.\n\n2. The search undertaken on 21 May (matters D and F)\n\nMr Weatherhead wrote that he had asked a colleague to search other paper files, \"including the Welsh Office files\", and would write again with the result, while fairly warning that some files had quite likely been destroyed. I have not received that letter. Please confirm whether the search was carried out, which series were searched, what was found, and whether Cadw regards the ATISN 27021 response of 11 June as its report of that search.\n\n3. The meaning of \"YYY\" (matter J)\n\nCadw has given two accounts of the marking — that the buildings were \"marginally below the bar for listing\" (21 May) and that the building did not meet the criteria (10 July) — and one inference, that it did not meet the criteria \"as it was not included on the list\" (29 July). I ask for one settled meaning, the documentary source for it, and, if there is none, a statement to that effect.\n\n4. The retention period (matter J)\n\nYour email of 27 August said that no records of the historic retention arrangements are available, and that the three-year period is what current colleagues advised from their knowledge of the systems then in place. I ask only that the complaint response confirm expressly that this is an account of general practice, not evidence that the Great House Farm file was in fact destroyed after three years.\n\n5. Professional archaeological knowledge of this site before December 1988 (matter C)\n\nOn 22 September 2026 Amgueddfa Cymru disclosed to me, under FOI 2026-032, its collections records for two accessions from this site. The first, 82.44H, is the material from the Glamorgan-Gwent Archaeological Trust's excavation of 1979 on land formerly part of Great House Farm, carried out in advance of housing development. The museum's records give the findspot as ST 168 733, the donor as Wales & West Housing Association Ltd, and the published report as Owen-John 1988. The accession runs to several hundred entries and includes Roman coins, samian, glass, a bone scabbard slide and querns, together with medieval pottery and ironwork.\n\nThis is not archaeology discovered after the event. By the time Cadw considered this building, the site had been professionally excavated, the finds had been accessioned by the national museum under a 1982 accession number, and the excavation report had been published. I therefore ask, as an addition to matter C:\n(a) whether Cadw held, or had access to, the 1979 excavation record or its published report when it considered the buildings in 1988;\n(b) whether that material was drawn to its attention by GGAT, the planning authority or the Royal Commission; and\n(c) if neither can now be established, that Cadw say so in terms.\n\nI do not say this material required the building to be listed. I say that the conclusion recorded in December 1988, that the site was not of sufficient archaeological interest, was reached against a professional record that already existed and is still held by the national museum today.\n\n6. The National Museum attribution (matter H)\n\nThe GGAT assessment of August 1990, which Cadw supplied to me on 21 May, states that \"it is likely that some medieval ironwork which is held by the National Museum of Wales was discovered under the rear wing c 1880 (RCAHMW 1988).\"\n\nThe museum's disclosure allows that sentence to be tested, and the position is more precise than either of us has previously put it. The museum does hold medieval ironwork from this site — knife blades, arrowheads, a buckle and a pair of compasses within accession 82.44H — but as excavated material from 1979, not as an 1880 discovery beneath the rear wing. Its searches found no accession, donation or deposit answering the c.1880 provenance at all. The museum's own explanation, given in its disclosure, is that objects recorded as found at Church Farm, Llandough in 1858 and donated in 1963 under accession 63.24 \"could well be the basis for such a local story/tradition\".\n\nI therefore no longer ask Cadw to accept that the attribution is uncorroborated in the round. I ask, more narrowly, whether Cadw holds anything supporting the c.1880 rear-wing provenance beyond the GGAT sentence it forwarded, and whether it will ask that the derived record note the museum's findings rather than continue to carry the 1880 attribution unqualified.\n\n7. Correcting the record (matter H)\n\nYou directed me to Heneb as manager of the HER, which was reasonable. The only substantive reply I have had from Heneb is its statement of 22 May that it is not subject to FOI as a limited company; a schedule I sent on 19 September remains outstanding.\n\nThe museum's disclosure now gives a firm anchor for what the record should show. Accession 95.56H comprises the material from the Cotswold Archaeological Trust excavation of 1994, donated by Ideal Homes Wales Ltd, and its locality is recorded in the museum's own collections database as \"Great House Farm Llandough\" at ST 1681 7331 — the grid reference carried by the historic environment record for this site. That accession runs to several hundred sets of early medieval human remains from this ground.\n\nAs the statutory duty to keep historic environment records rests with the Welsh Ministers, I ask:\n(a) which body is responsible for correcting an inaccurate or incomplete historic environment record;\n(b) what Cadw's own role is where the record is managed by Heneb; and\n(c) what Cadw will do if the entry for this site (GGAT02038s) is not corrected to reflect the 1979 and 1994 excavations and the accessions arising from them.\n\nCorrection to my letter of 20 September\n\nI said [email redacted]  had rejected my correspondence on three occasions. Having checked properly, it rejected messages on at least eight occasions between January and September 2026: 23 January; 9 May (twice); 17 May; 10 August; 11 August; 29 August; and 17 September. Separate failures on 29 August and 17 September came from the retired address [email redacted] . I correct this because I have asked Cadw to distinguish what is established from what is inferred, and the same standard applies to me.\n\nAddition to the outcomes at section 9\n\n6. A written acknowledgement that the 21 May undertaking was not reported on, and that Cadw's accounts of the 1988 assessment have varied.\n7. A single statement of what can and cannot now be established about the 1988 decisions, which my family can rely on and cite.\n8. That Cadw write to Heneb and the Royal Commission asking that their records for this site reflect that statement and the museum's accession records.\n9. Consideration, under the Welsh Government complaints policy, of redress for the time and trouble my family has spent since May pursuing questions that Cadw's own records could not answer.\n\nI am content to supply the museum's disclosure to Cadw if that would assist, and my offer of 20 September stands: any 1988 Cadw correspondence recovered from the Royal Commission or Heneb will be sent to you. I remain grateful for the courtesy you and your colleagues have shown me.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nRequester \n\nSganiwyd y neges hon am bob feirws hysbys wrth iddi adael Llywodraeth Cymru. Mae Llywodraeth Cymru yn cymryd o ddifrif yr angen i ddiogelu eich data. Os cysylltwch â Llywodraeth Cymru, mae ein hysbysiad preifatrwydd<”https://gov.wales/about/welsh-government-privacy-notice/?lang=cy”> yn esbonio sut rydym yn defnyddio eich gwybodaeth a sut rydym yn diogelu eich preifatrwydd. Rydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg. Byddwn yn anfon ateb yn Gymraeg i ohebiaeth a dderbynnir yn Gymraeg ac ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi. ----------------- On leaving the Welsh Government this email was scanned for all known viruses. The Welsh Government takes the protection of your data seriously. If you contact the Welsh Government then our Privacy Notice  explains how we use your information and the ways in which we protect your privacy. We welcome receiving correspondence in Welsh. Any correspondence received in Welsh will be answered in Welsh and corresponding in Welsh will not lead to a delay in responding."},{"id":"gm-1a0f079948f7f7d7","subject":"Re: Statutory notice under Historic Environment (Wales) Act 2023 ss.194–195 — HER GGAT02038s still classifies Wales’s largest excavated early-medieval cemetery as “POST MEDIEVAL – Farm”","name":"Re: Statutory notice under Historic Environment (Wales) Act 2023 ss.194–195 — HER GGAT02038s still classifies Wales’s largest excavated early-medieval cemetery as “POST MEDIEVAL – Farm”","date":"2026-09-30T04:01:33Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0f079948f7f7d7.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0f0785e97d9a3e-statutory-notice-under-historic-environment-wales-act-2023-ss-194-195-her-ggat02038s-still-classifies-wales-s-largest-ex","scopeTopics":["cadw"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["cadw"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0f0785e97d9a3e","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"cadw","name":"Welsh Government / Cadw"}],"refs":["RC26-0375"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0f079948f7f7d7.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Cabinet Minister for Culture and Sport,\n\nThis is a short supplement to my letter of 20 September 2026 on the\nhistoric environment record for Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr, Llandough (NPRN\n20182; HER GGAT02038s). It does not replace that letter. It does not ask\nyou to adjudicate title.\n\nThree corrections to the letter of 20 September\n\n1. A sentence in the middle of that letter was left unfinished. Please\ndisregard it. Nothing in the requests depends on it.\n\n2. The HM Land Registry letter forwarded by the constituency MP was dated\n18 September 2026, not “yesterday”. On 27 September I wrote separately to\nHM Land Registry to correct a year I had used in earlier correspondence\nabout titles WA231076 and WA240304. That correction is not a request to you.\n\n3. I asked whether guidance under section 196 had been issued. Cadw’s\npublished page now states that Historic Environment Records in Wales:\nCompilation and Use (2017) is issued as statutory guidance under section\n196 of the 2023 Act. What I still need is confirmation whether that 2017\ntext has been substantively reviewed since sections 194 to 196 came into\nforce on 4 November 2024, and the date on which the current guidance was\nissued under section 196 and laid before Senedd Cymru. I would also be\ngrateful for confirmation whether the current guidance addresses primary\nclassification of records and the behaviour of the public interface.\n\nWhat has happened since 20 September, relevant to the duties\n\nOn 27 September I made a formal complaint to Heneb concerning the HER\nidentifiers, classification and the 1978–1995 record. Steven Foulston\nacknowledged it on 29 September as investigating officer, with a five-week\ntarget. That is Heneb’s own process. It does not discharge the Ministers’\nduties under sections 194 and 195.\n\nOn 29 September the Royal Commission (RC26-0375) supplied the core\ndescription it says is used for GGAT02038s on Archwilio. That description\nis of a post-medieval farmhouse and barn. It records medieval finds, the\n1974 survey and the demolition of 6 December 1988. The live HER also links\nthe site to the separate 1994 excavation event, GGATE003875, which contains\nthe excavation details and findings.\n\nThe adequacy question in my letter of 20 September can therefore be put\nmore precisely: whether the HER, taken as a whole, provides the details of\nrelevant investigations and their findings required by section 194(2)(j),\nand whether the way those records are classified, linked and presented\npublicly is sufficient for that purpose.\n\nCadw’s stage-one complaint on the 1988 listing decision remains open\n(ticket #628635887). That is a separate file. I mention it only so the two\nare not confused. The latest position is that the complaints investigation\nhas commenced.\n\nThe five requests in the letter of 20 September stand. The one that can be\ntested immediately remains the Archwilio silent-substitution question:\nwhether an unresolved HER reference can silently resolve to a different\nrecord without warning.\n\nI will work to the published seventeen-working-day response aim from 20\nSeptember. If a substantive response will take longer, I would be grateful\nsimply to be told.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester \n"},{"id":"gm-1a0efc00fee23928","subject":"Automatic reply: [2] Re: Supplement to complaint re-served 20 September 2026 — Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough","name":"Automatic reply: [2] Re: Supplement to complaint re-served 20 September 2026 — Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough","date":"2026-09-30T00:38:53Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0efc00fee23928.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0efc00fee23928-automatic-reply-2-re-supplement-to-complaint-re-served-20-september-2026-great-house-farm-t-mawr-llandough","scopeTopics":["cadw"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["cadw"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0efc00fee23928","direction":"received","from":"(name not shown; gov.wales)","institutions":[{"id":"cadw","name":"Welsh Government / Cadw"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0efc00fee23928.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Diolch am eich neges.\nRydw allan o'r Swyddfa.\nByddaf yn delio â’m he-byst ar ôl dychwelyd (1/10/26).\nAm unrhyw beth brys cysylltwch â [email redacted] \n\nThank you for your message.\nI am out of the office.\nI will respond to my e-mails on my return (1/10/26).\nIf your email is urgent please contact [email redacted] \n\n\n\nSganiwyd y neges hon am bob feirws hysbys wrth iddi adael Llywodraeth Cymru. Mae Llywodraeth Cymru yn cymryd o ddifrif yr angen i ddiogelu eich data. Os cysylltwch â Llywodraeth Cymru, mae ein hysbysiad preifatrwydd<”https://gov.wales/about/welsh-government-privacy-notice/?lang=cy”> yn esbonio sut rydym yn defnyddio eich gwybodaeth a sut rydym yn diogelu eich preifatrwydd. Rydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg. Byddwn yn anfon ateb yn Gymraeg i ohebiaeth a dderbynnir yn Gymraeg ac ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi. ----------------- On leaving the Welsh Government this email was scanned for all known viruses. The Welsh Government takes the protection of your data seriously. If you contact the Welsh Government then our Privacy Notice  explains how we use your information and the ways in which we protect your privacy. We welcome receiving correspondence in Welsh. Any correspondence received in Welsh will be answered in Welsh and corresponding in Welsh will not lead to a delay in responding."},{"id":"gm-1a0efbfbb19ced15","subject":"[2] Re: Supplement to complaint re-served 20 September 2026 — Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough","name":"[2] Re: Supplement to complaint re-served 20 September 2026 — Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough","date":"2026-09-30T00:38:34Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0efbfbb19ced15.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0ed9851244fa3a-2-re-supplement-to-complaint-re-served-20-september-2026-great-house-farm-t-mawr-llandough","scopeTopics":["cadw"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["cadw"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0ed9851244fa3a","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"cadw","name":"Welsh Government / Cadw"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0efbfbb19ced15.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dr Wigley / Cadw Complaints Manager,\n\nThis is not a new complaint and not a chase. It is notice for the stage-one\ncomplaint already opened under ticket #628635887: complaint of 17 September\n2026, re-served on 20 September after delivery to [email redacted] was\nrejected, with a supplement sent on 22 September.\n\nThe complaint is that Cadw’s 1988 assessment of Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr),\nLlandough-juxta-Penarth (approximately ST 1681 7331; later HER GGAT02038s),\ndid not adequately address the historic fabric, archaeological context and\ninformation then available, and that the building was demolished in\nDecember 1988 before any listing protection was secured.\n\nI am not asking Cadw to determine title. I am asking Cadw to answer for its\nown role, decisions and professional handling of the 1988 assessment.\n\nWhat I seek from this complaint, if Cadw is willing to resolve it without\nreferral to the Public Services Ombudsman for Wales, is:\n\n1. A written stage-one decision on whether Cadw’s 1988 assessment was\nadequate having regard to the material then available and the criteria and\nprocess applicable at the time, including the relevant 1979 GGAT excavation\nrecord concerning the Roman villa and associated early-medieval archaeology\non land formerly within the farm holding.\n\n2. If Cadw concludes that the assessment was inadequate, a written\nacknowledgement of that finding, confined to Cadw’s own role and without\ndetermination of title or third-party liability.\n\n3. Cadw’s confirmation of whether it will ask Heneb to review HER entry\nGGAT02038s and, where necessary, correct or supplement the entry so that\nthe relevant excavated archaeology is accurately recorded, rather than\nleaving the site classified simply as “POST MEDIEVAL – Farm” where that\ndescription is incomplete.\n\n4. Confirmation of what records from the 1988 assessment Cadw still holds,\nwhat material was destroyed, disposed of or not retained, and the\napplicable retention schedule or rule governing the 1988 file.\n\nI do not expect Cadw to rebuild the house or determine the separate title\ndispute involving HM Land Registry or any successor in title. Those matters\nbelong to other processes.\n\nI will await the stage-one response. I will not add further correspondence\nto this complaint unless Cadw asks a question or the published response\ntimescale expires.\n\nPlease treat ticket #628635887 together with the emails of 17, 20 and 22\nSeptember 2026 as the complaint. This letter adds no new grounds.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester \n"},{"id":"gm-1a0efbe877bbd17f","subject":"[1] RE: Great House Farm, Llandough – Request 00210772 – Internal Review – Formal Chase","name":"[1] RE: Great House Farm, Llandough – Request 00210772 – Internal Review – Formal Chase","date":"2026-09-30T00:37:15Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0efbe877bbd17f.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0ed9825c0118bc-1-re-great-house-farm-llandough-request-00210772-internal-review-formal-chase","scopeTopics":["vale-foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["vale-foi"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0ed9825c0118bc","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"vale-foi","name":"Vale of Glamorgan Council"}],"refs":["00210772"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0efbe877bbd17f.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Information Governance Team,\n\n*Case Ref 00210772 — internal review requested 7 July 2026*\n\nThank you for your email of 11 September. You said that you would try to\nupdate me by the end of the following week with a clearer timeframe. I have\nnot received that update.\n\nUnder regulation 11(4) of the Environmental Information Regulations 2004,\nan internal review should be completed as soon as possible and no later\nthan 40 working days after receipt of the request for reconsideration. On\nmy calculation, that period expired on 2 September 2026. The review is\ntherefore now overdue.\n\nI appreciate that further historical documentation has been located and\nthat you may need to consult third parties. I am not asking you to\ncompromise any proper consultation process. However, I ask that you now\nprovide the following by *Tuesday 6 October 2026*:\n\n   1.\n\n   *A firm date* by which the completed internal review decision will be\n   issued.\n   2.\n\n   *A non-disclosive description of the further documentation located*,\n   including, so far as presently known:\n   - its approximate date or date range;\n      - who created or supplied it;\n      - how it came into the Council's possession; and\n      - approximately how many documents are involved.\n\n   I am asking for a description of the material and its provenance, rather\n   than disclosure of its substantive contents.\n   3.\n\n   *Confirmation that the newly located documentation is being preserved in\n   its present form*, without alteration, destruction or disposal, while\n   the internal review and any subsequent complaint to the Information\n   Commissioner remain outstanding.\n   4.\n\n   *Release now of any material that can properly be disclosed without\n   awaiting third-party consultation*, where the Council has already\n   established that no exemption or other restriction prevents disclosure.\n\nIf I do not receive a substantive response by *6 October 2026*, I will\nrefer the continuing delay to the Information Commissioner's Office without\nfurther notice.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester \n"},{"id":"gm-1a0efa79faafb260","subject":"Re: RC26-0375 (gan gynnwys RC26-0353, -0368, a -0371) - Ty Mawr/ Great House, Llandough, Cardiff","name":"Re: RC26-0375 (gan gynnwys RC26-0353, -0368, a -0371) - Ty Mawr/ Great House, Llandough, Cardiff","date":"2026-09-30T00:12:14Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0efa79faafb260.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0efa60db17120c-rc26-0375-gan-gynnwys-rc26-0353-0368-a-0371-ty-mawr-great-house-llandough-cardiff","scopeTopics":["rcahmw"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["rcahmw"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0efa60db17120c","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"rcahmw","name":"RCAHMW (Royal Commission)"}],"refs":["RC26-0353","RC26-0375"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0efa79faafb260.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Adam,\n\nThank you for your detailed response of 29 September (RC26-0375). It is\nparticularly helpful to have your confirmation that Archive 6048945 records\nRichard Suggett's visit to the site after its demolition in December 1988,\nand for identifying Archives 6048941, 6048942 and 6048944.\n\nI have considered your response alongside the Commission's catalogue\nentries and the other records now available. Before any of the outstanding\nSeptember requests are treated as concluded, I need to clarify one\ndistinction.\n\nYou state (as in your emails of 28 May, 10 June and 11 June) that there are\nno letters or records concerning Tŷ Mawr Farm, Llandough, in the\nCommission's registered files, which you describe as the only campaign\nrecords kept in the long term.\n\nI note that answer. My requests were not confined to registered campaign\nfiles. They concern recorded information held by the Commission relating to\nthe site, including records which your own archive identifies as existing,\nlost, received or created in connection with it.\n\nI am happy for the points below to be answered in whatever order is most\nconvenient, provided the FOIA/EIR status and original dates of the\nSeptember requests are preserved. Where a point has already been answered\nin full, a short confirmation that nothing further is held will suffice; I\ndo not wish the Commission to repeat itself.\n\n1. Scope of the search\n\nPlease confirm whether the search has extended beyond registered files to\nother relevant NMRW collections and systems, including (where applicable):\n\n- Inventory working papers, drafts, record cards and historic index cards;\n- field and survey notes;\n- measured drawings, sketches, plans and elevations;\n- maps and plan collections;\n- photographic collections, negatives and indexes;\n- vertical and oblique aerial photography and its indexes;\n- grey literature and unpublished reports;\n- special, personal or specialist collections;\n- background records;\n- historic administrative records;\n- digital and legacy catalogue systems;\n- accession and deposit records;\n- transfer, withdrawal, de-accession or disposal records; and\n- uncatalogued or separately catalogued material reasonably capable of\ncontaining information about the site.\n\nPlease also confirm whether the search used the principal known identifiers\nand variants, including:\n\nGreat House Farm; Great House; Tŷ Mawr; Ty Mawr Farm; Llandough;\nLlandochau; Llandough-juxta-Penarth; NPRN 20182; ST 1681 7331; ST16707335;\nGGAT02038s; Inventory site 425; NA/GL/88/49; NA/GL/89/208; RCS2/1/1777;\nRCS2/1/1776; 6048941; 6048942; 6048944; 6048945; C.N. Johns; CN Johns; and\nMrs Buckler.\n\nIf any of the categories above were not searched, a simple statement to\nthat effect is sufficient.\n\n2. The lost C.N. Johns / Mrs Buckler correspondence\n\nThe surviving Suggett record (Archive 6048945) identifies correspondence\nbetween C.N. Johns and Mrs Buckler as having been lost from the file since\n1986/87.\n\nYour response establishes that the correspondence itself cannot now be\nlocated. Please therefore confirm whether the Commission has also searched\nfor any residual records about that correspondence, including:\n\n- a file cover or wrapper;\n- enclosure or contents list;\n- accession or receipt entry;\n- issue, loan or handling record;\n- reader-request record;\n- catalogue or index card;\n- duplicate copy;\n- draft, extract or summary;\n- contemporary note concerning its absence;\n- later note concerning its loss;\n- correspondence concerning its loss;\n- transfer or return record; or\n- any other record identifying when or how the correspondence ceased to be\nwith the file.\n\nIf none of those records exists, please say so expressly. The question is\nno longer simply whether the correspondence survives, but whether the\nsurviving archive contains any documentary trace of its provenance and\ndisappearance.\n\n3. Archive 6048945\n\nPlease provide the complete surviving contents of Archive 6048945 (every\npage, card, continuation sheet, annotation and enclosure).\n\nPlease also confirm whether 6048945 is a complete standalone record, part\nof a larger file or collection, an extract separated from a parent record,\nor linked in any catalogue, accession or administrative system to other\nrecords concerning Great House Farm/Tŷ Mawr.\n\nYou have confirmed that Suggett visited the site after demolition. Please\nalso confirm whether there is any surviving record giving the date or\npurpose of that visit beyond the description currently catalogued, or any\nassociated site note, field sheet, photograph, sketch or correspondence.\n\n4. NA/GL/88/49 and NA/GL/89/208\n\nThank you for explaining the relationship between those admission\nreferences and Archives 6048945 and 6048941.\n\nPlease confirm whether the Commission retains any admission register entry,\nreceipt, accession sheet, contents list, file wrapper, box/listing record,\ncatalogue amendment, transfer record or other metadata showing what was\noriginally received under NA/GL/88/49 and NA/GL/89/208.\n\nIn particular, if 6048941 is the only surviving record received under\nNA/GL/89/208, please confirm whether that conclusion comes from an original\nadmission record or simply from what is currently visible in the catalogue.\n\n5. RCS2/1/1777 and RCS2/1/1776\n\nI appreciate the clarification concerning Archive Numbers 6364275 and\n6364276.\n\nThe separate question concerning RCS2/1/1777 remains open. Please confirm\nwhether the contents of RCS2/1/1777 have actually been inspected (including\nlocation plan, photographs, captions and associated recording material)\nsufficiently to establish whether the site is Bonvilston or Llandough.\n\nIf it is Bonvilston, I will record that as resolved. If it is Llandough,\nplease explain whether the catalogue has been or will be corrected or\nlinked to NPRN 20182, and provide the contents and copies requested earlier.\n\nPlease also confirm whether RCS2/1/1776 has been checked for the same\nreason.\n\n6. Photographs and aerial photography\n\nI note your explanation that searching the aerial collection may involve a\ncharge.\n\nBefore deciding whether to commission a paid search, please tell me what\nthe Commission's existing indexes or catalogue systems establish about\naerial coverage of the relevant location:\n\n- Is there any aerial coverage containing the site?\n- Is there any vertical or oblique coverage for the relevant period?\n- Is there any photograph taken during or after the demolition?\n- Are there catalogue entries for photographs which are no longer held?\n- Does the Commission have any image connected to NPRN 20182 which is not\npresently visible on Coflein?\n\nThis is relevant to Suggett's statement that there was apparently no\nphotographic record of the site. I would like to distinguish “Suggett could\nidentify no photographic record at the time” from “the Commission has\nsearched its photographic collections and holds no photograph of the site”.\nA nil return following a defined search of the photographic and aerial\nindexes would itself be a useful result.\n\n7. The 1974 survey and the ownership dispute\n\nYour quotation of Archive 6048945 now establishes (as a matter of the\nCommission's own file) that the house remained partially recorded because\nof problems created by an ownership dispute. That point is therefore\nsettled.\n\nPlease confirm whether the Commission holds anything beyond the material\nalready visible on Coflein concerning:\n\n- H.J. Thomas's 26 April 1974 survey;\n- the 1974 site notes;\n- the 12 December 1988 additions to the plan;\n- measured drawings;\n- field notes;\n- correspondence concerning access; or\n- any decision about what could or could not be recorded because of the\nownership dispute.\n\nI am not asking the Commission to adjudicate the ownership dispute. I am\nseeking only to establish what recording opportunities existed and what\nrecords were actually made. (For context, the Commission's post-demolition\naddition to the 1974 plan on 12 December 1988 is the only institutional\nbuilding-recording event identified in the immediate aftermath of the\ndemolition.)\n\n8. Transfers, retention and disposal\n\nPlease confirm whether any records concerning Great House Farm/Tŷ Mawr were\never transferred to another repository, received from another repository,\nreturned to a depositor, withdrawn, de-accessioned, destroyed, or otherwise\nremoved from the Commission's custody. Where such an event occurred, please\nprovide the recorded date, description, authority/reference and destination\nwhere held.\n\nIn particular, please confirm whether relevant material was ever\ntransferred to or received from Cadw/Welsh Office/Welsh Government,\nGlamorgan Archives, The National Archives, Amgueddfa Cymru, GGAT/Heneb or\nanother institution. (I note that the public HER entry GGAT02038s now\nincorporates material derived from the Commission's 1988 records; any\nresidual administrative link or transfer record relating to that would be\nuseful.)\n\n9. The nineteenth-century deposit\n\nThank you for identifying Archives 6048942 and 6048944.\n\nI am not asking the Commission to endorse the historical account. I would\nlike the provenance of the RCAHMW record established, so far as it can be:\n\n- what source was used to create Archive 6048942;\n- whether that source was contemporary, retrospective or copied from\nanother publication;\n- whether RCAHMW ever examined the alleged objects itself;\n- whether any accession, transfer or correspondence record connected the\naccount with Amgueddfa Cymru; and\n- whether the attribution that material was held by the National Museum of\nWales appears elsewhere in the Commission's records.\n\nIf the Commission has no such provenance information, please say so. (For\ncompleteness, Amgueddfa Cymru has separately stated that it holds no record\nof such an acquisition; I raise the point only to establish the documentary\ntrail within the Commission's own holdings.)\n\n10. The demolition sequence\n\nYour confirmation that Suggett visited after demolition makes the following\nquestion particularly important.\n\nPlease confirm whether the Commission holds any record of notification of\nthe proposed demolition, notification during the demolition, a request for\n(or consideration of, or decision not to undertake) emergency recording, a\nsite visit before, during or immediately after demolition, or contact with\nCadw, GGAT, the relevant local authority, BP Properties or its agents, or\nMrs Buckler or any other family member.\n\nI am aware that the Commission may not have been under a statutory duty to\nreceive notice merely because the building was unlisted. I am not assuming\notherwise. The question is simply whether any such recorded information\nexists. The Commission's own addition to the plan on 12 December 1988 is\nthe only institutional recording event so far identified in the immediate\npost-demolition period; any residual notes or correspondence from that\nvisit would therefore be of particular value.\n\n11. Search statement\n\nBecause the principal issue is now the distinction between “not in the\nregistered files” and “not held anywhere by the Commission”, I would be\ngrateful for a short factual search statement identifying:\n\n- which physical record series were searched;\n- which electronic/catalogue systems were searched;\n- the principal names, site identifiers or reference numbers used;\n- whether relevant archive staff or custodians were consulted; and\n- whether any potentially relevant material was excluded because it was\nuncatalogued, separately indexed, deposited or otherwise outside the\nregistered-file series.\n\nA concise factual description is sufficient; I am not asking for an\nelaborate report.\n\n12. Statutory status\n\nFinally, I do not want the distinction between the Commission's public\nenquiry/research service and its statutory information-rights function to\nbecome blurred.\n\nMy requests of 8, 15, 17 and 20 September 2026 remain as made. I am content\nfor the Commission to answer them together where that is administratively\neasier, but I am not withdrawing them or converting them into paid research\nenquiries.\n\nPlease therefore confirm:\n\n1. which request or requests your present email is intended to answer;\n2. whether each outstanding request remains open;\n3. whether your present email is intended to constitute a formal FOIA/EIR\nresponse;\n4. where you say information is not held, the scope of the search\nsupporting that conclusion;\n5. where information is being withheld rather than not held, the relevant\nstatutory basis; and\n6. the applicable internal-review procedure and the name/contact details of\nthe officer responsible for information rights.\n\nI appreciate the distinction you have drawn between material the Commission\ncurrently holds and material which may once have existed. That distinction\nis precisely why I am trying to establish the documentary chain carefully\nrather than draw conclusions from gaps in the archive.\n\nI am deliberately approaching this as a records-reconstruction exercise. I\nam not asking the Commission to reach a conclusion about ownership, the\nlegality of the demolition, or the conduct of any person or institution.\n\nMy aim is to establish what records were created, what survives, what\nentered or left the Commission's custody, what was transferred or disposed\nof, what cannot now be located, and what searches have actually been\nundertaken.\n\nThank you again for your assistance. I would be grateful if the outstanding\nSeptember requests could remain open while these points of clarification\nare addressed.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester \n"},{"id":"gm-1a0ee493c00fb442","subject":"Great House Farm / suspected historic fraud","name":"Great House Farm / suspected historic fraud","date":"2026-09-29T17:49:12Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0ee493c00fb442.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0ee493c00fb442-great-house-farm-suspected-historic-fraud","scopeTopics":["cps"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["cps"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0ee493c00fb442","direction":"received","from":"Victim Liaison Cymru-Wales","institutions":[{"id":"cps","name":"Crown Prosecution Service"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0ee493c00fb442.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester\n\nThank you for your email dated 28 September 2026.\n\nUnfortunately, I have been unable to locate a case file based on the information provided. Our reference numbers are known as Unique Reference Numbers (URNs) and follow a format similar to: 62HA... followed by seven digits.\n\nCould you please contact the officer in the case to request the CPS reference number, if it has been issued.\n\nWe look forward to receiving the necessary information so that we can assist you with your enquiry. Please provide this by 13 October 2026.\n\nYours sincerely\n\nL Rutter\nRheolwr Cyswllt Dioddefwyr / Victim Liaison Officer\nUned Cyswllt Dioddefwyr / Victim Liaison Unit\n[Gwasanaeth Erlyn y Goron / Crown Prosecution Service]\nCrown Prosecution Service, CPS Wales Area, Welsh Government Offices, Cathays Park, Cardiff [postcode redacted]. Gwasanaeth Erlyn y Goron, Ardal CPS Cymru, Llywodraeth Cymru, Parc Cathays, Caerdydd [postcode redacted].\nFfôn/Telephone: [phone redacted]\nwww.cps.gov.uk<http://www.cps.gov.uk/>"},{"id":"gm-1a0edea4e0bcbd8a","subject":"Re: Subject Access Request","name":"Re: Subject Access Request","date":"2026-09-29T16:05:49Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0edea4e0bcbd8a.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0edc455b623659-subject-access-request","scopeTopic