R Enquiries","institutions":[{"id":"rcahmw","name":"RCAHMW (Royal Commission)"}],"refs":["RC26-0353","RC26-0375"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0ed5d3ada10e6b.json","attachments":[{"name":"LES08 Ffurflen Archebu 2023.docx","title":"RC26-0375 order form (Welsh)","url":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/pdf/foi/2026-09-29_rcahmw_rc26-0375_les08-order-form-2023.docx","mime":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document","bytes":137851,"sha256":"8c01766df737e9e588d1dcf72ce0c53fa9de4cf4cfd3f923f31833fe1a1391b6"},{"name":"LES16 Rhestr Brisiau 2026.pdf","title":"RC26-0375 price list 2026 (Welsh)","url":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/pdf/foi/2026-09-29_rcahmw_rc26-0375_les16-price-list-2026.pdf","mime":"application/pdf","bytes":310379,"sha256":"9aa42a9dc07019e5b3f159b6798006cdb22e6d40c9da9d77e36bd97525f1b781"}],"textStatus":"available","text":"Annwyl Requester,\n\nDiolch yn fawr am eich ymholiadau o 8, 15, 17 ac 20 Medi. Fel y awgrymir yn eich e-bost o 20 Medi, gwnaf ateb eich ymholiadau gyda’i gilydd.\n\nFel y dwedir yn fy e-byst o 28 Mai , 10 Mehefin, a 11 Mehefin, nid oes unrhyw lythyrau na chofnodion ynglŷn â’r safle hon, sef Fferm Tŷ Mawr, Llandochau Fach, yn ein ffeiliau cofrestredig, sef yr unig gofnodion ymgyrchol rydem eu cadw yn y tymor hir. Nid oes unrhyw gofnodion wedi dod i’r amlwg yn ystod y cyfnod ers hynny. Yn sgil hynny, yr unig wybodaeth sydd gennem ynglŷn â’r safle, gan gynnwys ei dymchwel, yw’r cofnodion yn ein harchif, sef Cofnod Henebion Cenedlaethol Cymru. Gellir gweld rhestr lawn ohonynt yma: https://coflein.gov.uk/en/sites/20182/archives. Ynglŷn ag awyrluniau, oherwydd cymhlethdod y casgliad hwn, byddai rhaid talu ar gyfer y chwiliadau hyn (gwelir y rhestr brisiau, a atodir, ar gyfer manylion). Petasai awyrluniau sydd yn cynnwys y safle yn bodoli, y mae’n debygol y byddent yn awyrluniau fertigol wedi’u tynnu o uchder uchel fel rhan o waith arolwg y tirlun arferol ac nid fyddent yn canolbwyntio ar y safle, na ei ddangos yn fanwl.\n\nYnglŷn â’ch ymholiad ynglŷn â’r arwisg a darganfuwyd yn ystod y bedwaredd ganrif ar bymtheg hwyr, nid oes sylw arno yn yr adroddiad gan Richard Suggett heb ei chyhoeddi, sef Rhif Archif 6048945 (https://coflein.gov.uk/en/archives/6048945). Gyda llaw, mae rhan fwyaf o’r cofnod hon wedi’i thanysgrifennu fel disgrifiad y safle gan Heneb, GGAT02038s (https://archwilio.org.uk/her/chi3/report/page.php?watprn=GGAT02038s&dbname=ggat&tbname=core&sessid=CHI4nj4fn3q&queryid=Q383581001790598378). Yr wyf yn gynnwys eu disgrifiad isod, wedi’i gwirio er mwyn adlewyrchu y testun gwreiddiol:\n‘Great House Farmhouse and barn formed an attractive group, built of local lias limestone, and occupied an historic site next to the parish church. Finds of medieval ironwork and pottery have been reported from the site. Great House was the chief freehold farm of the parish and was occupied by the Vaughan's, a minor gentry family, from the mid C16 until the latter part of the C18. Several memorial inscriptions relating to the family are preserved in the church and churchyard. In the early C19 the freehold was acquired by the Bute Estate and the manorial courts for Llandough and Leckwith were held in Great House.\n\n‘Externally both farmhouse and barn appeared to be of nineteenth-century date. Internally, however, surviving vernacular detail- a blocked stone fireplace stair and agee-stopped beams- suggested a seventeenth-century date for the house. The ground floor of the house was surveyed in unusual circumstances in 1974 by H.J.T. Much detail was then concealed and it was not possible to examine the first floor and roof trusses. The house remained partially recorded because of problems of access created by an ownership dispute. The house and farm buildings were suddenly and completely demolished by B.P. Properties Ltd. on 6th December 1988 amid considerable local controversy.\n\n‘The plan of Great House was not published in the Inventory of Farmhouses & Cottages because of the incompleteness of the available information but it was classified as a sub-medieval house of direct-entry type (site no. 425). However, certain features suggested that the core of the house may have been medieval. The clearly-defined cross-passage end-chimney plan with masonry partitions and external wall batter (on the unreconstructed N side) had clear affinities with Brynwell, a medieval farmhouse in the neighbouring parish of Leckwith, Brynwell was built as a direct-entry, end-chimney, storeyed pre-Reformation house (Inventory, pp.187, 192). Brynwell, like Great House, was modernised in the C19th by the Bute Estate but the medieval date of the house is clearly shown by a small first-floor cusped-head window and the remodelled roof trusses. Unfortunately it was not possible to examine the fabric of Great House before the demolition of the house. Examination of the rubble brought to light no new features except an ogee-stopped beam with torus, a form of decoration that is not particularly common in Glamorgan (Inventory, Map 50).’\n\nEr hynny, y mae dau gofnod sydd yn cyfeirio at ddarganfod yr arwisg, sef Rhif Archif 6048942 (https://coflein.gov.uk/en/archives/6048942), le y nodir ‘At one time it was reputed to be the stable of a Castle, which could be verified by the finding of the remains of a soldier in armour, his horse, lance and small shield under the dining room floor’, a Rhif Archif 6048944 (https://coflein.gov.uk/en/archives/6048944), sef erthygl o’r Daily Telegraph, 16 Ebrill 1974, le y nodir ‘Its history has never been fully chronicled but a theory that it was originally a coaching house near a Castle was supported by the discovery benieth the dining room floor in about 1870 of the remains of a soldier in armour, his horse, a lance and shield’. Gyda llaw, canys sefydlwyd y CBHC ac Amgueddfa Cymru yn yr ugeinfed ganrif gynnar a darganfuwyd yr arwisg yn y bedwaredd ganrif ar bymtheg, mae’n annhebyg y byddai’r Comisiwn Brenhinol yn rhan o rodiad yr arfwisg i’r Amgueddfa.\n\nGellir archebu sganiau'r tri chofnod hyn gan ddefnyddio’r ffurflen archebu a atodir. Wrth i ni hepgor ffioedd ar gyfer archebion bychain (llai na pum sgan cyfraniad ymchwil),  y mae’n debygol byddai darparir yr archeb hon am ddim.\n\nYnglŷn â’ch ymholiad ynglŷn â’r cofnodion Rhifau Archif 6364275 (https://coflein.gov.uk/en/archives/6364275)  a 6364276 (https://coflein.gov.uk/en/archives/6364276), yr wyf wedi ymgynghori’r ddau. Gallaf gadarnhau y mae’r ddau yn berthnasol i’r ddau safle y adnabyddir yn ein catalog archif, sef Tŷ Mawr, Tresimwn, a 33 Whitecross Street, Fynwy, ac nid Tŷ Mawr, Llandochau Fach.\n\nYnglŷn â’ch ymholiadau o 11 Mehefin, fel y dwedir uchod, nid oes unrhyw lythyrau na chofnodion ynglŷn â’r safle hon, sef Fferm Tŷ Mawr, Llandochau Fach, yn ein ffeiliau cofrestredig, sef yr unig gofnodion ymgyrchol rydem eu cadw yn y tymor hir. Nid ydy unrhyw gofnodion wedi dod yn amlwg yn ystod y cyfnod ers hynny. Er hynny, y mae’n amlwg bod aelod o’n tîm arolwg, sef Richard Suggett, wedi ymweld â’r safle yn fyr ar ôl ei dymchwel yn fis Rhagfyr 1988 (sef cynnwys y cofnod Rhif Archif 6048945), ond nid chofnodir rheswm yr ymweliad hwn.\n\nYnglŷn â’ch ymholiadau bellach o 20 Medi, fel y dwedir uchod, nid oes unrhyw lythyrau na chofnodion ynglŷn â’r safle hon, sef Fferm Tŷ Mawr, Llandochau Fach, yn ein ffeiliau cofrestredig, sef yr unig gofnodion ymgyrchol rydem eu cadw yn y tymor hir. Canys hynny, nid oes gennem lythyrau na chofnodion ynglŷn â’r llythyrau rhwng ‘C.N. Johns a Mrs Buckler, Great House Farm’, fel y dwedais yn fy e-bost o 10 Mehefin, na unrhyw amgylchiadau eraill ynglŷn â dymchwel y safle.\n\nY mae’r rhifau cofnodion a nodir yn eich e-bost o 20 Medi, sef NA/GL/88/49 a NA/GL/89/208 yn edrych yn debyg at rifau cofnodion derbyniadau. NA/GL/88/49 yw’r rhifyn derbyniad ar gyfer y disgrifiad gan Richard Suggett, sef Rhif Archif 6048945, fel uchod. Yr unig gofnod a dderbynid fel rhan o NA/GL/89/208, ar ôl ein cofnodion, oedd Rhif Archif 6048941 (https://coflein.gov.uk/en/archives/6048941). Gyda llaw, mae’r cofnod hon wedi cael ei gynnwys yn yr adroddiad GGAT ‘Land at Great House Farm, Llandough. An Archaeological Assessment’ (1990), sef Rhif Archif 6524751 (https://coflein.gov.uk/en/archives/6524751). Fel y dwedais yn fy e-bost o 28 Mai, gellir gweld copi digidol o’r adroddiad hon yma: https://www.walesher1974.org/her/app/php/herumd.php?group=GGAT&level=3&docid=301361628.\n\nFel y sefydlir nid oes gennem gofnodion ynglŷn â’r mater hon heblaw am y rhai sydd yn ein harchif, sydd ar gael i’r cyhoedd trwy ein Gwasanaeth Ymholiadau cyhoeddus, mi fydd pob ymholiad pellach ynglŷn â’r mater hon yn cael ei thrin fel ymholiad cyhoeddus. Canys hynny, mae’n bosibl bydd rhaid talu ffioedd ar gyfer ymholiadau cymhleth o’r math hyn yn y dyfodol (gwelir y Rhestr Brisiau, a atodir, ar gyfer manylion).\n\nYr eiddoch yn gywir,\n\nAdam Coward\n\n\n\nNMR Enquiries\nYmholiadau Cyhoeddus I Public Enquiries\nFfordd Penglais | Penglais Road, Aberystwyth, [postcode redacted]\n[phone redacted]\n[email redacted] | [email redacted]\nwww.cbhc.gov.uk | www.rcahmw.gov.uk\nNoddir gan Lywodraeth Cymru | Sponsored by Welsh Government\nRydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg a Saesneg. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn peri oedi.\nWe welcome correspondence in Welsh and English. Corresponding in Welsh will not lead to any delay.\n\nYn unol â Rheoliadau Safonau'r Gymraeg (Rhif 2) 2016, mae gennych hawl i gyfathrebu a gohebu â Chomisiwn Brenhinol Henebion Cymru yn eich dewis iaith. Er mwyn sicrhau ein bod yn cyflawni’r hawl, rhowch wybod i ni a ydych yn dymuno derbyn gohebiaeth a/neu alwadau ffôn oddi wrthym yn y Gymraeg. Bydd yr wybodaeth hon yn cael ei chofnodi gennym ni, a byddwn yn defnyddio'r iaith o’ch dewis ym mhob cyfathrebu yn y dyfodol. Diolch.\nUnder the Welsh Language Standards (No. 2) Regulations 2016, you have the right to communicate and correspond with the Royal Commission on the Ancient and Historical Monuments of Wales in your preferred language. To ensure we uphold this right, please let us know whether you wish to receive correspondence and/or telephone calls from us in Welsh. This information will be recorded by us, and we will use your preferred language in all future communication. Thank you.\n​\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]>\nSent: 20 September 2026 08:07\nTo: NMR Enquiries <[email redacted]>\nSubject: Ty Mawr/ Great House, Llandough, Cardiff\n\nDear Rhodri, dear colleagues,\n>\n> Diolch am eich cydnabyddiaeth ar 8 Medi. On the Welsh Language Standards point raised in your signature — I am Welsh and I would be glad to receive correspondence in Welsh, but please use whichever language is quicker for you. I do not want language to delay anything, and I will not treat a Welsh reply as needing translation.\n>\n> This is a request under section 1 of the Freedom of Information Act 2000 and, so far as the records concern the historic environment, under the Environmental Information Regulations 2004.\n>\n> **First, a point of housekeeping. Nothing in this email amends, clarifies, narrows or withdraws my requests of 8, 15 or 17 September.** Those stand exactly as made and their statutory periods run from their own dates. This is an additional request, and I would not want it to be treated as restarting any clock. On my reckoning:\n>\n> - **8 September 2026** — FOI/EIR on R. F. Suggett's December 1988 unpublished records, H. J. Thomas's 1974 survey, the source of the \"held by the National Museum of Wales\" attribution, and copy correspondence with Amgueddfa Cymru, GGAT, Cadw and the Vale. Acknowledged by you the same day, twenty working days. **Due Tuesday 6 October 2026.** No reference number issued; nothing yet supplied.\n> - **15 September 2026** — catalogue check on Emergency Recording case **RCS2/1/1777**, filed under Great House (Ty Mawr), Bonvilston, where the material described is the pattern one would expect for the Llandough demolition. **Due Tuesday 13 October 2026.**\n> - **17 September 2026** — whether the Commission was notified, consulted or asked to record the farmhouse before its demolition on 6 December 1988. **Due Thursday 15 October 2026.**\n>\n> The second and third received only the automated enquiry acknowledgement, which cites fifteen working days and notes that the priority search service is suspended. I understand the staffing position and I am not complaining about it. But both emails ask for recorded information, and a request for recorded information is a request under section 1 whether or not it is labelled as one. **Please confirm that all three are logged as FOIA / EIR requests and let me have the reference numbers.** If they are currently sitting in the research-enquiry queue rather than the information-rights queue, that is easily fixed now and much harder to fix later.\n>\n> **On the number of requests.** I am conscious that this is my fifth email in twelve days. It is not an attempt to burden the Commission. Each request arose from a document I only found after sending the last one — the GGAT 1990 assessment led me to Suggett, Suggett's own note led me to the lost correspondence, and a Coflein search led me to RCS2/1/1777. I am content for all of them to be answered together in a single response if that is more efficient for you, and if the whole exceeds the appropriate limit under section 12 I will happily tell you which items to do first rather than have anything refused outright. Please just ask.\n>\n> **The site.** Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr, Llandough (Llandochau) juxta Penarth, Vale of Glamorgan — the community of St Dochdwy's Church and Llandough Hospital. **Not** Llandough near Cowbridge, and **not** Ty Mawr, Bonvilston (Cadw 13603), which is a different and still-standing house. NPRN 20182. ST 1681 7331. HER GGAT02038s. Now Church View Close. Outbuildings demolished 30 November 1988; farmhouse 6 December 1988.\n>\n> Holdings I am aware of include file references **NA/GL/88/49** and **NA/GL/89/208**, NMR site file material catalogued on Coflein (a plan with additions dated 12 December 1988, a descriptive account, a press cutting), Inventory working papers for Glamorgan Inventory site 425, and the disputed ERC bundle RCS2/1/1777.\n>\n> **Why this matters to me.** The farm was my family's. The immediate reason for this request is a line in your own records: R. F. Suggett's December 1988 assessment notes that *\"Correspondence between C. N. Johns and Mrs Buckler, Great House Farm, has been lost from the file since 1986/87\"*, and that *\"There is apparently no photographic record of the site.\"* Mrs Buckler was my relative. I want the correspondence if it survives, the record of how it came to be lost if it does not, and the rest of the file either way. I am not asking the Commission for a view on title or on the legality of the demolition, and I would not expect one.\n>\n> **Information requested**\n>\n> Please supply copies, so far as held:\n>\n> 1. A full contents list or file schedule for **NA/GL/88/49** and **NA/GL/89/208**, itemising every document on each file, whether or not the documents themselves are disclosed.\n> 2. Copies of all documents on those two files.\n> 3. **The file cover, front sheet or wrapper of each file**, and any enclosure list, loans register, issue slip, reader-request record or file-tracking entry attached to or kept for them.\n> 4. Every record concerning the **C. N. Johns / Mrs Buckler correspondence** recorded as lost since 1986/87: any surviving copy, draft, extract, summary, index entry or catalogue stub; any contemporaneous or later note of its loss; any record of a search for it; any record of who consulted, borrowed or handled the file between 1 January 1985 and 31 December 1988; and any internal or external correspondence about its absence. If no such record exists, please say so expressly rather than leaving it unaddressed.\n> 5. The Commission's **retention and disposal schedule** applicable to site files and correspondence files in the 1980s and today, and confirmation whether any records relating to this site have been destroyed under it, and if so when and under what authority.\n> 6. Whether the files listed above have been **reviewed for transfer to, or retention from, The National Archives or the Glamorgan Archives**, and the outcome of any such review.\n> 7. All **Inventory working papers, drafts, card-index entries and record cards** for the site, including those for Glamorgan Inventory site 425.\n> 8. All **photographs** of the site in any format — prints, negatives, transparencies, digital images, and any oblique or vertical aerial coverage of Llandough showing the farm at any date — whether or not catalogued, with their catalogue entries. Given Suggett's 1988 note that there is apparently no photographic record, **please state expressly whether the Commission holds none**, and whether any images sit within RCS2/1/1777 or in uncatalogued Emergency Recording material.\n> 9. Confirmation, from the location plan, map copies and photographs inside **RCS2/1/1777**, whether that bundle is Bonvilston or Llandough. If Llandough, please re-link it to NPRN 20182 and supply a contents list and copies. Please also check adjacent case **RCS2/1/1776**. *(This is my request of 15 September, restated here for completeness only — it stands on its own date.)*\n> 10. Any record of a site visit, watching brief, recording exercise or emergency recording carried out or considered before, during or after the demolition; any notification of the proposed demolition, from whom and on what date; and any decision **not** to record the building together with the reasons recorded for it. The building was not listed — Cadw inspected on 29 July 1988 and did not list it — so I make no assumption that the Commission was entitled to notice. What I would like to know is what the Commission's own criteria for initiating Emergency Recording were at the time, and whether this site was considered against them. Contemporary reports of 3 December 1988 stated that Cadw was considering emergency listing and that discussion with the Commission was being arranged. The house came down three days later.\n> 11. The **1974 survey by H. J. Thomas**, his site notes of 26 April 1974 and 12 December 1988, and any measured drawings, sketches, plans or elevations, published or unpublished.\n> 12. **R. F. Suggett's December 1988 records in full** — every page, card, continuation sheet, annotation and enclosure — not only the passages concerning the c.1880 find.\n> 13. Any record of the source of the statement that the c.1880 ironwork \"is held by the National Museum of Wales\", and any later file that confirms, corrects or withdraws that attribution.\n> 14. All correspondence between the Commission and any of the following concerning this site, 1970–1995: Cadw or the Welsh Office; the Glamorgan-Gwent Archaeological Trust; Vale of Glamorgan Borough Council; BP Properties Limited or its agents, including Debenham Tewson & Chinnocks; Persimmon Homes (Wales) Limited; Ideal Homes Wales Limited; Amgueddfa Cymru; any Member of Parliament; and any member of my family.\n> 15. Any accession records for material deposited with the Commission by GGAT, Cadw, Cotswold Archaeology or any other body relating to this site.\n>\n> **Practicalities.** Please reply by email to this address. I cannot attend in person and I am not seeking anything that attracts a charge. Scans are fine. Where an item is already on Coflein, a stable reference and link is enough **provided** you confirm the online record is complete. If any item is held elsewhere — The National Archives, Glamorgan Archives, Cadw, Heneb or Amgueddfa Cymru — please name the body and reference so I can apply there directly, which I will.\n>\n> Finally, so that it is on record from the outset rather than raised later: please let me have the name and contact details of the officer responsible for information rights at the Commission, and confirmation of the internal review procedure, so that I know where to direct any review request if one becomes necessary.\n>\n> With thanks for your patience with a long list.\n>\n> Yn gywir / Yours faithfully,\n>\n> Requester\n> [email redacted]<[email redacted]>\n\n--\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\nAttachments (2)\n- LES08 Ffurflen Archebu 2023.docx (application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document, 135 KB): https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/pdf/foi/2026-09-29_rcahmw_rc26-0375_les08-order-form-2023.docx\n- LES16 Rhestr Brisiau 2026.pdf (application/pdf, 303 KB): https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/pdf/foi/2026-09-29_rcahmw_rc26-0375_les16-price-list-2026.pdf"},{"id":"gm-1a0ed4541d097398","subject":"Automatic reply: Great House Farm, Llandough","name":"Automatic reply: Great House Farm, Llandough","date":"2026-09-29T13:05:31Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0ed4541d097398.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0e110bed09b687-automatic-reply-great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":["hmlr"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["hmlr"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0e110bed09b687","direction":"received","from":"_Notice Response","institutions":[{"id":"hmlr","name":"HM Land Registry"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0ed4541d097398.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Thank you for your recent communication.　\n\nYour email and any attachments have been passed to an HM Land Registry caseworker for consideration.\n\nIf you would like to discuss this correspondence or require it in an alternate format, please contact us at [email redacted]\n\n\n\n\nHM Land Registry’s purpose is to protect your land ownership and provide services and data that underpin an efficient and informed property market. Our vision is: “A world-leading property market as part of a thriving economy and a sustainable future”.\n\nWe check all mail and attachments for known viruses. However, you are advised that you open any attachments at your own risk. If you have received this email and it was not intended for you, please let us know, then delete it.\n\nWe welcome correspondence in English and Welsh.\n\nTo see how HM Land Registry treats your personal information, read our Personal information charter: www.gov.uk/government/organisations/land-registry/about/personal-information-charter."},{"id":"gm-1a0ed44f180b42bb","subject":"Re: Great House Farm, Llandough","name":"Re: Great House Farm, Llandough","date":"2026-09-29T13:05:12Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0ed44f180b42bb.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0e03138143e763-great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":["hmlr"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["hmlr"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0e03138143e763","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"hmlr","name":"HM Land Registry"}],"refs":["N610CGV"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0ed44f180b42bb.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Reference number 260929-6766714\n\nThanks,\n\nRequester \n\n\nOn Tue, 29 Sept 2026, 10:47 _Notice Response, <\n[email redacted]> wrote:\n\n> Dear Customer,\n>\n>\n>\n> This email address is not the correct method of contact for general\n> enquiries.  Therefore, no action will be taken with this enquiry.\n>\n>\n>\n> Our Contact HM Land Registry page\n>   has comprehensive\n> help pages for most enquiries.\n>\n>\n>\n> Please resubmit your enquiry via our online contact form.\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> *From:* Requester  \n> *Sent:* 27 September 2026 05:12\n> *To:* Wales.OfficeMail  \n> *Cc:* Coventry.OfficeMail  \n> *Subject:* Great House Farm, Llandough\n>\n>\n>\n> Sirs/ Ma'ams,\n>\n>\n>\n> I need to correct a date I have used in earlier correspondence about Great\n> House Farm / Tŷ Mawr, Llandough, titles WA231076 and WA240304.\n>\n>\n>\n> I had treated 1983 as the year the two titles were merged. That was wrong.\n> HM Land Registry’s own letter of 23 March 2026 (Lisa Griffiths, ref\n> N610CGV) separates the two acts.\n>\n> 1982–83 was first registration of two live titles, not a merger.\n>\n> 30 November 1982: application to first-register WA231076. The Court of\n> Appeal later treated BP Properties Ltd as registered proprietors of “the\n> farm” from November 1982.\n>\n> 23 February 1983: application to first-register a second title, WA240304.\n>\n> 26 March 1983: Mary Williams died. On the family’s case, notice of those\n> applications did not reach us until after her death, while the family was\n> in mourning. She had no window in which to object. The family was not in a\n> position to use any window that arrived after that.\n>\n> Those two titles remained separate through first instance.\n>\n> 24 July 1986: Hollis J. Both titles were still live. The judgment language\n> treats Great House Farm as one holding. It had not been one holding on the\n> register. On the family’s case it had not been one holding since the 1877\n> split: Parcel A (house, buildings, garden/yard and about 10 acres, the\n> Thomas/Williams line) and Parcel B (the eastern fields retained by Bute and\n> later WGR/BP).\n>\n> 2 February 1987: application to amalgamate the two titles. WA240304 was\n> then closed and registration continued under WA231076. That is the merge.\n> It was made after the 1986 ruling and before the Court of Appeal gave\n> judgment on 31 July 1987.\n>\n> So 1983 is silent first registration of a second title while Mary was\n> still in Parcel A, or had only just died. 1987 is the administrative\n> merger, carried out while the appeal was still open.\n>\n> This was not a dispute about a single land parcel that BP already owned.\n> Mary was on Parcel A, which the family says she owned, with occupation on\n> that house parcel running in the family from at least 1667. BP, and WGR\n> before it from 1949, were the neighbouring interest on Parcel B. From 1949\n> onwards the family’s case is that those neighbours ran a decades-long\n> series of tenancy offers, “whole farm” wording, possession orders and the\n> 1974 letters to make her occupation of her own parcel look permissive, and\n> to make both parcels look like one estate they had acquired. They never\n> produced a conveyance of Parcel A. What they had was an unfounded claim\n> that her occupancy was permissive.\n>\n> Please treat any earlier letter from me that said the titles merged in\n> 1983 as corrected by this email. The dates that matter are:\n>\n> 1982/83 — first registration of two titles, without the occupier being\n> heard;\n>\n> 24 July 1986 — first-instance judgment describing one farm while two\n> titles still stood;\n>\n> 2 February 1987 — amalgamation, before the appeal ended;\n>\n> 31 July 1987 — Court of Appeal.\n>\n> Yours faithfully,\n>\n>\n>\n> Requester\n>\n> Refs where relevant: N610CGV; 260919-6726246; titles WA231076 and WA240304.\n>\n> Requester \n>\n> greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> HM Land Registry’s purpose is to protect your land ownership and provide services and data that underpin an efficient and informed property market. Our vision is: “A world-leading property market as part of a thriving economy and a sustainable future”.\n>\n> We check all mail and attachments for known viruses. However, you are advised that you open any attachments at your own risk. If you have received this email and it was not intended for you, please let us know, then delete it.\n>\n> We welcome correspondence in English and Welsh.\n>\n> To see how HM Land Registry treats your personal information, read our Personal information charter: www.gov.uk/government/organisations/land-registry/about/personal-information-charter.\n>\n>\n>"},{"id":"gm-1a0ed265386659cd","subject":"Formal complaint — GGAT / Heneb: Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough — HER identifiers and the 1978–1995 record","name":"Formal complaint — GGAT / Heneb: Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough — HER identifiers and the 1978–1995 record","date":"2026-09-29T12:31:39Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0ed265386659cd.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0ed265386659cd-formal-complaint-ggat-heneb-great-house-farm-t-mawr-llandough-her-identifiers-and-the-1978-1995-record","scopeTopics":["heneb"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["heneb"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0ed265386659cd","direction":"received","from":"Steven Foulston","institutions":[{"id":"heneb","name":"Heneb (formerly GGAT) / Historic Environment Record"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0ed265386659cd.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester,\n\nI acknowledge receipt of your formal complaint dated 27 September 2026.\n\nI have been appointed as the investigating officer in this matter.\n\nI aim to respond to you within five weeks.  If that is not possible I will provide you with an update on the timeframe.\n\nBest regards\n\nSteven Foulston\nHeneb\n\n[cid:image001.png@01DD5016.D85032A0]\n​​​​\nSteven Foulston FCIPD\nRheolwr Adnoddau Dynol| HR Manager\n[phone redacted]\n[email redacted] \n​www.heneb.org.uk\n​\nYmddiriedolaeth Archaeoleg Cymru | The Trust for Welsh Archaeology​\n​Cadeirydd | Chair: Dr Carol Bell          PSG | CEO: Richard Nicholls\n\nMae Heneb yn Sefydliad Cofrestredig gyda’r Chartered Institute for Archaeologists\n​Heneb is a Registered Organisation with the Chartered Institute for Archaeologists\n​​\n[cid:image002.png@01DD5016.D85032A0]\n[cid:image003.png@01DD5016.D85032A0]\nSwyddfa Gofrestrig: Ty Cornel, 6 Stryd Caerfyrddin, Llandeilo, Sir Gaerfyrddin, [postcode redacted]\n​Registered Office: Corner House, 6 Carmarthen Street, Llandeilo, Carmarthenshire, [postcode redacted]\n​\nPrivacy Policy - Heneb"},{"id":"gm-1a0ecdadf39a0c93","subject":"RE: Preservation notice — Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough — do not destroy, transfer or dispose","name":"RE: Preservation notice — Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough — do not destroy, transfer or dispose","date":"2026-09-29T11:09:11Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0ecdadf39a0c93.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0e9e1e5a4586bf-preservation-notice-great-house-farm-t-mawr-llandough-do-not-destroy-transfer-or-dispose","scopeTopics":["rcahmw"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["rcahmw"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0e9e1e5a4586bf","direction":"received","from":"NMR Enquiries","institutions":[{"id":"rcahmw","name":"RCAHMW (Royal Commission)"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0ecdadf39a0c93.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Prynhawn da,\n\nDiolch yn fawr am eich e-bost; yr wyf wedi’i anfon ymlaen at ein Rheolwr Cofnodion, Gareth Edwards.\n\nGyda llaw, gobeithiaf ymateb i’ch ymholiadau eraill nes ymlaen heddiw.\n\nCofion gorau,\n\nAdam Coward\n\n\nNMR Enquiries\nYmholiadau Cyhoeddus I Public Enquiries\nFfordd Penglais | Penglais Road, Aberystwyth, [postcode redacted]\n[phone redacted]\n[email redacted] | [email redacted]\nwww.cbhc.gov.uk | www.rcahmw.gov.uk\nNoddir gan Lywodraeth Cymru | Sponsored by Welsh Government\nRydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg a Saesneg. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn peri oedi.\nWe welcome correspondence in Welsh and English. Corresponding in Welsh will not lead to any delay.\n\nYn unol â Rheoliadau Safonau'r Gymraeg (Rhif 2) 2016, mae gennych hawl i gyfathrebu a gohebu â Chomisiwn Brenhinol Henebion Cymru yn eich dewis iaith. Er mwyn sicrhau ein bod yn cyflawni’r hawl, rhowch wybod i ni a ydych yn dymuno derbyn gohebiaeth a/neu alwadau ffôn oddi wrthym yn y Gymraeg. Bydd yr wybodaeth hon yn cael ei chofnodi gennym ni, a byddwn yn defnyddio'r iaith o’ch dewis ym mhob cyfathrebu yn y dyfodol. Diolch.\nUnder the Welsh Language Standards (No. 2) Regulations 2016, you have the right to communicate and correspond with the Royal Commission on the Ancient and Historical Monuments of Wales in your preferred language. To ensure we uphold this right, please let us know whether you wish to receive correspondence and/or telephone calls from us in Welsh. This information will be recorded by us, and we will use your preferred language in all future communication. Thank you.\n​\n-----Original Message-----\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]>\nSent: 28 September 2026 22:42\nTo: [email redacted]; [email redacted]; [email redacted]; [email redacted]; [email redacted]; [email redacted]; NMR Enquiries <[email redacted]>; [email redacted]; [email redacted]; [email redacted]; [email redacted]; [email redacted]; [email redacted]; [email redacted]\nSubject: Preservation notice — Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough — do not destroy, transfer or dispose\n\nTo each recipient, in your own capacity as holder or likely holder of records. Please do not treat this as a joint reply. Answer only for your own holdings.\n\nSirs/Madam,\n\nThis is a preservation notice. It is not a claim form and not service of proceedings. It does not replace any FOI, review or subject access request already with you.\n\nLand: Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr, Llandough-juxta-Penarth, adjoining St Dochdwy's. Titles WA231076, WA240304 (closed), WA513690, WA735527, and any related title. Planning refs 89/01396/OUT and 92/00671/RES.\n\nPlease preserve, and do not destroy, transfer out of your custody without a written receipt, or dispose of, any file, plan, photograph, tape or electronic record in your possession, custody or control relating to:\n\n1. The 1877 split, the 1916 and 1939 tenancies, and the 1938 Bute to Western Ground Rents conveyance.\n2. The 1955 and 1962 possession orders, the 1974 proceedings, and BP Properties Ltd v Buckler [1987] EWCA Civ 2.\n3. First registration: WA231076 application 30 November 1982; WA240304 application 23 February 1983; later amalgamation.\n4. The 1988 possession (29 November) and demolition (begun 6 December), including listing, planning, police and any liaison papers.\n5. Mary Doreen Williams (died 26 March 1983) and William Buckler, so far as your file concerns this land.\n\nAlso preserve, if you hold them, the following planning and land-charge classes. Absence from a 2026 file disclosure is not proof they never existed:\n\n6. Any section 52 Town and Country Planning Act 1971 agreement, later section 106 instrument, deed, undertaking or other agreement regulating the development or use of this land, and any corresponding local-land-charge entry.\n7. The historic planning-register entries required by the Town and Country Planning General Development Order 1988 for 89/01396/OUT and 92/00671/RES (applications, reserved matters, plans and drawings, and decision information).\n8. The officer's report annexes to 89/01396/OUT, including Appendix B (further GGAT comments after discussions with the Trust), and Appendices C–E.\n9. The condition chain on 89/01396/OUT: conditions 16–18; the Council's October 1990 confirmation of condition 17; GGAT's July 1992 archaeological warning; the September 1992 reserved-matters approval; and the 1993–94 papers on whether condition 18 had been discharged.\n10. Any preliminary environmental-assessment / screening determination for the outline application (site described as about 3.4 acres / 1.38 ha).\n11. Any record of the statutory consultation route used because the land was, or was treated as, a site of archaeological interest / SMR entry under the 1988 Order.\n12. The complete 92/00671/RES file, and any sale, option, conditional-contract or transfer paper on the planning files covering BP Properties to Ideal Homes Wales, including the recorded transfer of\n3 December 1993.\n13. The application-receipt record for 89/01396/OUT (form dated October 1989; reconstructed Council receipt 8 November 1989).\n\nPlease stop routine deletion, including archived mailboxes, for anything in that scope.\n\nThe public evidence index is https://greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com/\n. The investigation distinguishes the farmhouse parcel from the eastern fields. The 1987 Court of Appeal decided possession, limitation and the October 1974 letters. It did not decide the documentary root of the farmhouse parcel. These records go to that question. This notice is not a finding against any of you.\n\nPlease acknowledge, name the officer responsible for the hold, and say what you hold in scope. If you hold nothing, say what you searched.\n\nNothing in this letter abandons any claim concerning title, registration, concealment, limitation, reopening or alteration of the register.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\nRequester \n\n[email redacted]"},{"id":"gm-1a0ecc942f0fb115","subject":"Your complaint about HM Land Registry - reference ICR/090/26","name":"Your complaint about HM Land Registry - reference ICR/090/26","date":"2026-09-29T10:50:05Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0ecc942f0fb115.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0ecc942f0fb115-your-complaint-about-hm-land-registry-reference-icr-090-26","scopeTopics":["hmlr-icr"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["hmlr-icr"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0ecc942f0fb115","direction":"received","from":"ICR Office Inbox","institutions":[{"id":"hmlr-icr","name":"HM Land Registry (Independent Complaints Reviewer)"}],"refs":["ICR/090/26"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0ecc942f0fb115.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester\n\nI am writing to acknowledge receipt of the following further emails from you:\n\n1.) Your email of 28 September, sent 21:31, titled “Re: Your complaint about HM Land Registry - referral to ICR Office” with three attachments.\n\nYou have asked that the four further points you make in this email be placed on file for consideration by the investigation officer alongside the information you have already provided about your complaint (ICR/090/26). I can confirm that this has been done.\n\n2.) Your email of 28 September, sent 22:40, titled “Preservation notice — Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough — do not destroy records — Matter of public interest”\n\nYours sincerely\n\nVicky Applegate\nOffice Administrator\nIndependent Complaints Reviewers Office\n10 South Colonnade\nCanary Wharf\nLondon\n[postcode redacted]\n\n[email redacted]<[email redacted]>\n[phone redacted]"},{"id":"gm-1a0eca2b89512fe9","subject":"RE: Preservation notice — Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough — do not destroy records — Matter of public interest","name":"RE: Preservation notice — Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough — do not destroy records — Matter of public interest","date":"2026-09-29T10:07:51Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0eca2b89512fe9.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0e9e9889be4e6a-preservation-notice-great-house-farm-t-mawr-llandough-do-not-destroy-records-matter-of-public-interest","scopeTopics":["linklaters"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["linklaters"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0e9e9889be4e6a","direction":"received","from":"Data Protection","institutions":[{"id":"linklaters","name":"Linklaters"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0eca2b89512fe9.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester,\n\nthank you for contacting us; we will assess your request in due course where, and to the extent that, it concerns personal data for which Linklaters acts as data controller.\nKind regards,\nRegulatory Compliance Team\nLinklaters LLP  |  20 Ropemaker Street  |  London [postcode redacted]     |  www.linklaters.com \n\nFrom: Requester  \nSent: poniedziałek, 28 września 2026 23:40\nTo: Data Protection  ; [email redacted]; [email redacted]; [email redacted]; BP Archive  ; FOI Requests  ; Cardiff County, Enquiries  ; [email redacted]\nCc: [email redacted]; [email redacted]; Connor McGilly  ; MRC_Archive, Resource  ; [email redacted]; [email redacted]; [email redacted]; [email redacted]; [email redacted]; [email redacted]; [email redacted]; [email redacted]; [email redacted]; Disclosure Team  ; NSUKilo  ; Paul Egan  ; [email redacted]; [email redacted]; [email redacted]; [email redacted]; Julian Wilkes  ; [email redacted]; _FOI email address  ; [email redacted]; Customer Support  ; Davidson, Victoria  ; [email redacted]; [email redacted]; [email redacted]; [email redacted]; Contact OneVale  ; [email redacted]; DOUGHTY, Stephen  ; CAIRNS Alun  ; [email redacted]; Pwyllgor Diwylliant, Cyfathrebu, y Gymraeg a Chwaraeon - Culture, Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee  ; [email redacted]; [email redacted]; FOI / Rhyddid Gwybodaeth  ; [email redacted]; [email redacted]\nSubject: Preservation notice — Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough — do not destroy records — Matter of public interest\n\nCaution: External email.\n\nFORMAL PRESERVATION NOTICE\nGreat House Farm / Tŷ Mawr, Llandough-juxta-Penarth\nTitles: WA231076, WA240304, WA513690 and WA735527\nPlanning references: 89/01396/OUT and 92/00671/RES\n\nURL: greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com \n\nTo each recipient separately, in your own capacity as a current or potential holder, custodian or controller of relevant records:\n\nThis notice is addressed to each recipient individually. It is not a joint request, and it does not ask any recipient to speak for, investigate on behalf of, or answer in place of any other person or organisation. Each recipient is asked to respond only in respect of records within that recipient's own possession, custody or control, including records inherited from a predecessor organisation or retained on its behalf.\n\n1. Nature and purpose of this notice\n\nThis is a formal preservation notice concerning documentary material relating to Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr, Llandough-juxta-Penarth, adjoining St Dochdwy's, and the subsequent development of the site, including the area now associated with Church View Close.\n\nIt is not a claim form, is not service of proceedings, and is not intended to constitute or replace any FOI request, internal review, complaint, subject-access request or other statutory process already made to any recipient.\n\nThe purpose of this notice is to require the preservation of potentially relevant material while the documentary history, title chain, registration history, planning history, possession proceedings, demolition and subsequent development are being reconstructed.\n\nA separate first-stage preservation notice has been issued or is being issued to Cadw / Welsh Government FOI, HM Land Registry Wales Office and DPO, Heneb / GGAT HER, RCAHMW, Vale of Glamorgan FOI Unit and DPO, Glamorgan Archives, South Wales Police FOI, The National Archives, the Wales Office FOI team, and the National Library of Wales. Persimmon Homes (Wales) Ltd has received a separate preservation letter.\n\nYou are receiving this notice separately because the published documentary record places you, or a predecessor whose records may remain in your custody or control, within the documentary, legal, professional, administrative or development chain concerning this land.\n\n2. Land and documentary scope\n\nPlease preserve, and do not destroy, alter, overwrite, discard, transfer out of custody without a recorded handover, or otherwise dispose of, any record within your possession, custody or control relating to the land or matters identified below.\n\nThe scope includes, without limitation, originals, copies, working papers, drafts, annotations, plans, photographs, negatives, films, tapes, maps, registers, indexes, correspondence, emails and attachments, electronic files, archived mailboxes, databases, metadata, internal notes, meeting records, instructions, client or matter files, survey material and other associated records.\n\nThe material requested includes:\n\n1. Historical ownership, occupation and conveyancing\n\nThe 1877 split; the 1916 and 1939 tenancies; the 1938 Bute to Western Ground Rents conveyance; the 1969 Western Ground Rents to BP Pension Trust conveyance; and any plans, schedules, abstracts, searches, correspondence or other documents relied upon in relation to those transactions.\n\n2. Possession proceedings and subsequent litigation\n\nThe 1955 High Court and 1962 Cardiff County Court possession orders; the 1974 proceedings; BP Properties Ltd v Buckler [1987] EWCA Civ 2; House of Lords leave papers, including YHL/PO/JO/10/11/2536; and Buckler v United Kingdom (1989), including associated correspondence, instructions, evidence, plans and working papers.\n\n3. Land registration\n\nThe first registration of WA231076, including the application dated 30 November 1982; the first registration of WA240304, including the application dated 23 February 1983; subsequent amalgamation, transfer or closure; and every plan, conveyance, abstract, search, application, correspondence trail or professional file relied upon in connection with those registrations or changes.\n\n4. 1988 possession and demolition\n\nThe taking of possession on or about 29 November 1988 and the demolition commenced on or about 6 December 1988, including any planning, listed-building, heritage, police, enforcement, media, contractor, survey, photographic or inter-agency liaison material.\n\n5. Planning and subsequent development, 1989–1996\n\nPlanning application 89/01396/OUT; reserved matters application 92/00671/RES; conditions 16–18; GGAT / Cotswold Archaeology material; records concerning any section 52, section 106, planning obligation, local land charge or equivalent instrument; and the transfer from BP to Ideal Homes dated 3 December 1993, together with all associated plans, correspondence, approvals, agreements, searches and completion material.\n\n6. Family records\n\nRecords concerning Mary Doreen Williams (died 1983) and William Buckler, insofar as those records relate to Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr, the land described above, its occupation, ownership, possession, disposal, registration, development or demolition.\n\n3. Predecessor and inherited files\n\nWhere you hold, or may hold, a file originating with a predecessor, successor, merged practice, affiliated practice, client or other entity, that material is also within scope.\n\nThis includes, without limitation, records originating from:\n\nLoosemores of Cardiff; Robbins Olivey & Blake Lapthorn / Blake Morgan; Linklaters & Paines; Debenham Tewson / DTZ / Cushman & Wakefield; Wyn Thomas + Partners; Ideal Homes Wales; BP Properties; BP Pension Trust; Cotswold Archaeological Trust; Cardiff Bay Development Corporation; and South Glamorgan County Council.\n\nThe reference to a predecessor file does not assert that you necessarily hold such a file. It identifies material for which a reasonable custody or succession inquiry should be made where applicable.\n\n4. Preservation steps\n\nPlease take reasonable steps to prevent the routine destruction, deletion, alteration or overwriting of potentially relevant material, including material held in archived mailboxes, legacy systems, off-site storage, document-management systems, backup repositories and other electronic or physical stores.\n\nWhere relevant material has been transferred to another repository, archive, successor organisation or third party, please preserve the information necessary to identify the transfer and its current location, including any accession, file, box, reference or custody details.\n\nNothing in this notice requires the creation of records that do not exist. The request concerns the preservation of records already held or controlled by you.\n\n5. Purpose and legal position\n\nThis notice is not a finding against any recipient, and I am not asking any recipient to determine title or adjudicate the underlying dispute.\n\nThe 1987 Court of Appeal judgment is relied upon here only for matters actually determined by that judgment, including the issues concerning possession, limitation and the October 1974 letters. This preservation notice does not treat that judgment as having determined the complete documentary root of title to the farmhouse parcel.\n\nThe underlying documentary history, including the relationship between the farmhouse parcel and the agricultural land subsequently dealt with through Western Ground Rents and BP, remains a matter requiring reconstruction from the primary records.\n\nAccordingly, preservation is requested precisely so that the documentary record can be established from the records themselves rather than through assumptions based on later registration, later occupation, later planning treatment or secondary descriptions.\n\n6. Acknowledgement and identification of records\n\nPlease acknowledge receipt of this notice and, in your own capacity, confirm:\n\n(a) the officer, partner, records manager or other person responsible for implementing the preservation hold;\n\n(b) what records within your possession, custody or control you have identified as falling within the scope above; and\n\n(c) where you identify no records within scope, what relevant systems, files, repositories or predecessor records you searched in reaching that conclusion.\n\nA substantive assessment of title is not requested from you. The immediate requirement is preservation and identification of the documentary material you hold.\n\nThe public documentary index and developing evidence record is available at:\n\nhttps://greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com/ \n\nPlease monitor that site for subsequent identification of documents, references and specific schedules relevant to your organisation's records.\n\nWhere a reliable public email address is unavailable, a postal preservation notice will also be sent. For example, a postal notice will follow to Loosemores, Alliance House, 18–19 High Street, Cardiff [postcode redacted].\n\nNothing in this notice constitutes an abandonment, waiver or limitation of any claim, position or remedy concerning title, registration, concealment, limitation, reopening, rectification or alteration of the register, or any associated matter.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester \n\n\nAny business communication sent by or on behalf of Linklaters LLP or one of its affiliated firms or other entities (together \"Linklaters\") is confidential and may be privileged or otherwise protected. If you receive it in error please inform us and then delete it immediately from your system. You should not copy it or disclose its contents to anyone. Please be aware that messages sent to and from Linklaters may be monitored for reasons of security, to protect our business, and to ensure compliance with legal and regulatory obligations and our internal policies. Emails are not a secure method of communication, can be intercepted and cannot be guaranteed to be error free. Anyone who communicates with us by email is taken to understand and accept the above.\n\nLinklaters LLP is a limited liability partnership registered in England and Wales with registered number OC326345. It is a law firm authorised and regulated by the Solicitors Regulation Authority (www.sra.org.uk), with SRA ID: 460682. The term partner in relation to Linklaters LLP is used to refer to a member of Linklaters LLP or an employee or consultant of Linklaters LLP or any of its affiliated firms or entities with equivalent standing and qualifications. Please refer to www.linklaters.com/regulation for important information on our regulatory position. A list of Linklaters LLP members together with a list of those non-members who are designated as partners and their professional qualifications, may be inspected at our registered office, 20 Ropemaker St, London [postcode redacted] and such persons are either solicitors or registered foreign lawyers.\n\nWe take your privacy seriously. For information about how we process your personal data, please see our Global Privacy Notice at www.linklaters.com/en/legal-notices/privacy-notice."},{"id":"gm-1a0ec902d29c3087","subject":"RE: Great House Farm, Llandough","name":"RE: Great House Farm, Llandough","date":"2026-09-29T09:47:41Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0ec902d29c3087.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0e03138143e763-great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":["hmlr"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["hmlr"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0e03138143e763","direction":"received","from":"_Notice Response","institutions":[{"id":"hmlr","name":"HM Land Registry"}],"refs":["N610CGV"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0ec902d29c3087.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Customer,\n\nThis email address is not the correct method of contact for general enquiries.  Therefore, no action will be taken with this enquiry.\n\nOur Contact HM Land Registry page  has comprehensive help pages for most enquiries.\n\nPlease resubmit your enquiry via our online contact form.\n\n\nFrom: Requester  \nSent: 27 September 2026 05:12\nTo: Wales.OfficeMail  \nCc: Coventry.OfficeMail  \nSubject: Great House Farm, Llandough\n\nSirs/ Ma'ams,\n\nI need to correct a date I have used in earlier correspondence about Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr, Llandough, titles WA231076 and WA240304.\n\nI had treated 1983 as the year the two titles were merged. That was wrong. HM Land Registry’s own letter of 23 March 2026 (Lisa Griffiths, ref N610CGV) separates the two acts.\n1982–83 was first registration of two live titles, not a merger.\n30 November 1982: application to first-register WA231076. The Court of Appeal later treated BP Properties Ltd as registered proprietors of “the farm” from November 1982.\n23 February 1983: application to first-register a second title, WA240304.\n26 March 1983: Mary Williams died. On the family’s case, notice of those applications did not reach us until after her death, while the family was in mourning. She had no window in which to object. The family was not in a position to use any window that arrived after that.\nThose two titles remained separate through first instance.\n24 July 1986: Hollis J. Both titles were still live. The judgment language treats Great House Farm as one holding. It had not been one holding on the register. On the family’s case it had not been one holding since the 1877 split: Parcel A (house, buildings, garden/yard and about 10 acres, the Thomas/Williams line) and Parcel B (the eastern fields retained by Bute and later WGR/BP).\n2 February 1987: application to amalgamate the two titles. WA240304 was then closed and registration continued under WA231076. That is the merge. It was made after the 1986 ruling and before the Court of Appeal gave judgment on 31 July 1987.\nSo 1983 is silent first registration of a second title while Mary was still in Parcel A, or had only just died. 1987 is the administrative merger, carried out while the appeal was still open.\nThis was not a dispute about a single land parcel that BP already owned. Mary was on Parcel A, which the family says she owned, with occupation on that house parcel running in the family from at least 1667. BP, and WGR before it from 1949, were the neighbouring interest on Parcel B. From 1949 onwards the family’s case is that those neighbours ran a decades-long series of tenancy offers, “whole farm” wording, possession orders and the 1974 letters to make her occupation of her own parcel look permissive, and to make both parcels look like one estate they had acquired. They never produced a conveyance of Parcel A. What they had was an unfounded claim that her occupancy was permissive.\nPlease treat any earlier letter from me that said the titles merged in 1983 as corrected by this email. The dates that matter are:\n1982/83 — first registration of two titles, without the occupier being heard;\n24 July 1986 — first-instance judgment describing one farm while two titles still stood;\n2 February 1987 — amalgamation, before the appeal ended;\n31 July 1987 — Court of Appeal.\nYours faithfully,\n\nRequester\nRefs where relevant: N610CGV; 260919-6726246; titles WA231076 and WA240304.\nRequester \ngreathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com \n\n\n\n\n\nHM Land Registry’s purpose is to protect your land ownership and provide services and data that underpin an efficient and informed property market. Our vision is: “A world-leading property market as part of a thriving economy and a sustainable future”.\n\nWe check all mail and attachments for known viruses. However, you are advised that you open any attachments at your own risk. If you have received this email and it was not intended for you, please let us know, then delete it.\n\nWe welcome correspondence in English and Welsh.\n\nTo see how HM Land Registry treats your personal information, read our Personal information charter: www.gov.uk/government/organisations/land-registry/about/personal-information-charter."},{"id":"gm-1a0ebef6ac854529","subject":"Please update the status of your request - BP vs Buckler 1987","name":"Please update the status of your request - BP vs Buckler 1987","date":"2026-09-29T06:52:10Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0ebef6ac854529.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0ebef6ac854529-please-update-the-status-of-your-request-bp-vs-buckler-1987","scopeTopics":["nactso"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["nactso"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0ebef6ac854529","directi