ne numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0e9e9889be4e6a","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"bp","name":"BP"},{"id":"cotswold","name":"Cotswold Archaeology"},{"id":"linklaters","name":"Linklaters"},{"id":"moj","name":"Ministry of Justice / HMCTS"},{"id":"museum","name":"Amgueddfa Cymru - Museum Wales"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0e9f58d1f3cf15.json","textStatus":"available","text":"*FORMAL PRESERVATION NOTICE*\n*Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr, Llandough-juxta-Penarth*\n*Titles: WA231076, WA240304, WA513690 and WA735527*\n*Planning references: 89/01396/OUT and 92/00671/RES*\n\n*URL: **greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com\n *\n\n*To each recipient separately, in your own capacity as a current or\npotential holder, custodian or controller of relevant records:*\n\nThis notice is addressed to each recipient *individually*. It is *not a\njoint request*, and it does not ask any recipient to speak for, investigate\non behalf of, or answer in place of any other person or organisation. Each\nrecipient is asked to respond only in respect of records within that\nrecipient's own possession, custody or control, including records inherited\nfrom a predecessor organisation or retained on its behalf.\n1. Nature and purpose of this notice\n\nThis is a *formal preservation notice* concerning documentary material\nrelating to Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr, Llandough-juxta-Penarth, adjoining\nSt Dochdwy's, and the subsequent development of the site, including the\narea now associated with Church View Close.\n\nIt is *not a claim form, is not service of proceedings, and is not intended\nto constitute or replace any FOI request, internal review, complaint,\nsubject-access request or other statutory process already made to any\nrecipient.*\n\nThe purpose of this notice is to require the preservation of potentially\nrelevant material while the documentary history, title chain, registration\nhistory, planning history, possession proceedings, demolition and\nsubsequent development are being reconstructed.\n\nA separate first-stage preservation notice has been issued or is being\nissued to Cadw / Welsh Government FOI, HM Land Registry Wales Office and\nDPO, Heneb / GGAT HER, RCAHMW, Vale of Glamorgan FOI Unit and DPO,\nGlamorgan Archives, South Wales Police FOI, The National Archives, the\nWales Office FOI team, and the National Library of Wales. Persimmon Homes\n(Wales) Ltd has received a separate preservation letter.\n\n*You are receiving this notice separately because the published documentary\nrecord places you, or a predecessor whose records may remain in your\ncustody or control, within the documentary, legal, professional,\nadministrative or development chain concerning this land.*\n2. Land and documentary scope\n\nPlease preserve, and do not destroy, alter, overwrite, discard, transfer\nout of custody without a recorded handover, or otherwise dispose of, any\nrecord within your possession, custody or control relating to the land or\nmatters identified below.\n\nThe scope includes, without limitation, *originals, copies, working papers,\ndrafts, annotations, plans, photographs, negatives, films, tapes, maps,\nregisters, indexes, correspondence, emails and attachments, electronic\nfiles, archived mailboxes, databases, metadata, internal notes, meeting\nrecords, instructions, client or matter files, survey material and other\nassociated records.*\n\nThe material requested includes:\n\n*1. Historical ownership, occupation and conveyancing*\n\nThe 1877 split; the 1916 and 1939 tenancies; the 1938 Bute to Western\nGround Rents conveyance; the 1969 Western Ground Rents to BP Pension Trust\nconveyance; and any plans, schedules, abstracts, searches, correspondence\nor other documents relied upon in relation to those transactions.\n\n*2. Possession proceedings and subsequent litigation*\n\nThe 1955 High Court and 1962 Cardiff County Court possession orders; the\n1974 proceedings; *BP Properties Ltd v Buckler [1987] EWCA Civ 2*; House of\nLords leave papers, including *YHL/PO/JO/10/11/2536*; and *Buckler v United\nKingdom (1989)*, including associated correspondence, instructions,\nevidence, plans and working papers.\n\n*3. Land registration*\n\nThe first registration of *WA231076*, including the application dated 30\nNovember 1982; the first registration of *WA240304*, including the\napplication dated 23 February 1983; subsequent amalgamation, transfer or\nclosure; and every plan, conveyance, abstract, search, application,\ncorrespondence trail or professional file relied upon in connection with\nthose registrations or changes.\n\n*4. 1988 possession and demolition*\n\nThe taking of possession on or about *29 November 1988* and the demolition\ncommenced on or about *6 December 1988*, including any planning,\nlisted-building, heritage, police, enforcement, media, contractor, survey,\nphotographic or inter-agency liaison material.\n\n*5. Planning and subsequent development, 1989–1996*\n\nPlanning application *89/01396/OUT*; reserved matters application\n*92/00671/RES*; conditions 16–18; GGAT / Cotswold Archaeology material;\nrecords concerning any *section 52, section 106, planning obligation, local\nland charge or equivalent instrument*; and the transfer from BP to Ideal\nHomes dated *3 December 1993*, together with all associated plans,\ncorrespondence, approvals, agreements, searches and completion material.\n\n*6. Family records*\n\nRecords concerning *Mary Doreen Williams (died 1983)* and *William Buckler*,\ninsofar as those records relate to Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr, the land\ndescribed above, its occupation, ownership, possession, disposal,\nregistration, development or demolition.\n3. Predecessor and inherited files\n\nWhere you hold, or may hold, a file originating with a predecessor,\nsuccessor, merged practice, affiliated practice, client or other entity,\nthat material is also within scope.\n\nThis includes, without limitation, records originating from:\n\n*Loosemores of Cardiff; Robbins Olivey & Blake Lapthorn / Blake Morgan;\nLinklaters & Paines; Debenham Tewson / DTZ / Cushman & Wakefield; Wyn\nThomas + Partners; Ideal Homes Wales; BP Properties; BP Pension Trust;\nCotswold Archaeological Trust; Cardiff Bay Development Corporation; and\nSouth Glamorgan County Council.*\n\nThe reference to a predecessor file does not assert that you necessarily\nhold such a file. It identifies material for which a reasonable custody or\nsuccession inquiry should be made where applicable.\n4. Preservation steps\n\nPlease take reasonable steps to prevent the routine destruction, deletion,\nalteration or overwriting of potentially relevant material, including\nmaterial held in *archived mailboxes, legacy systems, off-site storage,\ndocument-management systems, backup repositories and other electronic or\nphysical stores*.\n\nWhere relevant material has been transferred to another repository,\narchive, successor organisation or third party, please preserve the\ninformation necessary to identify the transfer and its current location,\nincluding any accession, file, box, reference or custody details.\n\nNothing in this notice requires the creation of records that do not exist.\nThe request concerns the preservation of records already held or controlled\nby you.\n5. Purpose and legal position\n\nThis notice is *not a finding against any recipient*, and I am not asking\nany recipient to determine title or adjudicate the underlying dispute.\n\nThe *1987 Court of Appeal judgment* is relied upon here only for matters\nactually determined by that judgment, including the issues concerning\npossession, limitation and the October 1974 letters. This preservation\nnotice does not treat that judgment as having determined the complete\ndocumentary root of title to the farmhouse parcel.\n\nThe underlying documentary history, including the relationship between the\nfarmhouse parcel and the agricultural land subsequently dealt with through\nWestern Ground Rents and BP, remains a matter requiring reconstruction from\nthe primary records.\n\nAccordingly, preservation is requested precisely so that the documentary\nrecord can be established from the records themselves rather than through\nassumptions based on later registration, later occupation, later planning\ntreatment or secondary descriptions.\n6. Acknowledgement and identification of records\n\nPlease acknowledge receipt of this notice and, in your own capacity,\nconfirm:\n\n*(a)* the officer, partner, records manager or other person responsible for\nimplementing the preservation hold;\n\n*(b)* what records within your possession, custody or control you have\nidentified as falling within the scope above; and\n\n*(c)* where you identify no records within scope, what relevant systems,\nfiles, repositories or predecessor records you searched in reaching that\nconclusion.\n\nA substantive assessment of title is not requested from you. The immediate\nrequirement is preservation and identification of the documentary material\nyou hold.\n\nThe public documentary index and developing evidence record is available at:\n\n*https://greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com/\n *\n\nPlease monitor that site for subsequent identification of documents,\nreferences and specific schedules relevant to your organisation's records.\n\nWhere a reliable public email address is unavailable, a postal preservation\nnotice will also be sent. For example, a postal notice will follow to\n*Loosemores,\nAlliance House, 18–19 High Street, Cardiff [postcode redacted]*.\n\nNothing in this notice constitutes an abandonment, waiver or limitation of\nany claim, position or remedy concerning *title, registration, concealment,\nlimitation, reopening, rectification or alteration of the register, or any\nassociated matter.*\n\nRegards,\n\n*Requester *\n**"},{"id":"gm-1a0e9b71dfbd3bfa","subject":"Re: Your complaint about HM Land Registry - referral to ICR Office","name":"Re: Your complaint about HM Land Registry - referral to ICR Office","date":"2026-09-28T20:31:26Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0e9b71dfbd3bfa.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0e992fe9e3dc6a-your-complaint-about-hm-land-registry-referral-to-icr-office","scopeTopics":["hmlr-icr"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["hmlr-icr"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0e992fe9e3dc6a","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"hmlr-icr","name":"HM Land Registry (Independent Complaints Reviewer)"}],"refs":["ICR/090/26","N610CGV"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0e9b71dfbd3bfa.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Ms Applegate,\n\nThank you for your letter of 28 September 2026 acknowledging my emails of\n26 and 27 September, and for allocating reference ICR/090/26. I note that\nan investigation officer will contact me within ten working days.\n\nI would like to place four further points on the file for the investigation\nofficer, in addition to those already set out in my emails.\n\n1. Public record\n\nPlease treat https://greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com/ as part of the\nmaterial already cited.\n\nThe landing page sets out, diagrammatically and with links to the\nunderlying evidence, the family’s case that Parcel A — the farmhouse,\ngarden and associated land on the Williams/Thomas line — was included\nwithin a whole-farm registered title even though the documentary root by\nwhich BP acquired that parcel remains unresolved.\n\nOur paper title deed being stolen from our home resulting in us not able to\nbe present in court in 1974 (and so ownership adjudication was ajourned and\nour ownership assertion was weakened (not exstiguished) we understand. But\nthe stretch of the imagine it takes to ever believe it was Western Ground\nRents or BPs is all based on absurdity after absurdity and does not in my\nestimation, meet the criteria of what a sane, reasonable person would do.\n\nThe website greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com\ndeliberately distinguishes documentary records from family evidence,\nreconstruction and unresolved inference. I am therefore not asking ICR to\naccept every proposition on the site as established fact; I am asking that\nthe underlying records and the specific questions identified there be\nconsidered.\n\n2. Chronology now recorded by HM Land Registry\n\nI had earlier written as if the two titles merged in 1983. That was wrong.\nHM Land Registry’s letter of 23 March 2026 (Lisa Griffiths, ref N610CGV)\nseparates the first-registration events from the later amalgamation. My\ncorrection of 27 September is already on your file.\n\nThe chronology that now matters is:\n\n• 30 November 1982 — application to first-register WA231076.\n\n• 23 February 1983 — application to first-register WA240304.\n\n• 26 March 1983 — Mary Williams died. Our family’s account is that notice\nof BP’s registration was received only after her death, during/ after that\nmourning period. Whether that account can be established from the notice,\nservice and correspondence records is itself a question for investigation.\n\n• 24 July 1986 — Hollis J. The historical title material still showed both\ntitles live.\n\n• 2 February 1987 — an application to amalgamate the two titles was\nrecorded. WA240304 was subsequently closed and registration continued under\nWA231076. This is the amalgamation event, distinct from the 1982/83 first\nregistrations. It occurred while the BP Properties Ltd v Buckler litigation\nwas still live and before the Court of Appeal judgment of 31 July 1987.\n\n• *14 April 1989 — Buckler v United Kingdom was declared inadmissible by\nthe European Commission of Human Rights.*\n\nI regard this as a further institutional failure that now requires\nreconsideration. The decision treated BP as having rights in our land\nparcel and referred to BP as the owners, although BP had been awarded\npossession, not ownership, and ownership of Parcel A had never been\nadjudicated in BP’s favour. The distinction is fundamental. Possession is\nnot the same thing as ownership, and an order for possession did not itself\ndetermine that BP had acquired the underlying proprietary interest in\nParcel A.\n\nOn the family’s case, our ownership claim to Parcel A was never\nextinguished. The question therefore is not simply whether BP obtained\npossession through the proceedings, but whether any legal process ever\ntransferred, extinguished or superseded the family’s underlying proprietary\ninterest. If it did not, the basis upon which BP was subsequently treated\nas having superior rights in the parcel requires examination.\n\nThat issue also goes directly to the later assertion that the family’s\noccupation became “permissive” under BP’s 1974 licence letters. A party\ncannot ordinarily confer a proprietary right it does not itself possess. If\nBP did not own Parcel A, it is necessary to establish what legal interest\nBP actually held that enabled it to grant my grandmother, or anyone else,\npermissive occupation of that parcel — particularly where there had already\nbeen a long succession of earlier attempts to establish a permissive basis\nfor the family’s occupation.\nThe point is especially significant because the person whose occupation was\nsupposedly made permissive was herself the long-standing occupier and, on\nthe family’s case, the superior title holder of the parcel, with the\nfamily’s occupation extending back centuries. I therefore ask that the\ninvestigation distinguish carefully between *possession awarded to BP,\nownership of Parcel A, and any alleged power to license the family’s\noccupation*. Those are not interchangeable propositions, and the\ndocumentary basis for treating them as such requires examination.\n\n3. What I am asking ICR to review\n\nHM Land Registry has told my MP, and said in its stage-two decision of 25\nMay 2026, that the title was registered correctly and that there is no\nevidence of a mistake. That is the institutional position my family has\nencountered for decades.\n\nI am not asking ICR to determine title. I am asking whether that conclusion\nwas reached after a proper examination of the first-registration files and,\nin particular:\n\n• the documentary basis and extent claimed for Parcel A in the\nfirst-registration applications;\n\n• the missing 31 December 1969 conveyance and the 1971 abstract of title;\n\n• the notice and service records, including what notice was given to the\noccupier, when, and what effective opportunity existed to object;\n\n• the three inconsistent closure dates now appearing in the HMLR record for\nWA240304; and\n\n• the 2 February 1987 amalgamation application, including what was actually\namalgamated, on what plans and documents, and what title information\nexisted at that point in the still-live litigation.\n\nThe concern is therefore not simply one of hindsight. Much of the relevant\nmaterial was created contemporaneously with the events in question. That is\nwhy I raise mistake, concealment and procedural unfairness as matters\nrequiring investigation, rather than as findings I am asking you to make at\nthis acknowledgement stage.\n\n4. Why the record was reconstructed from Y Wladfa\n\nFor almost two years I have been in the Welsh Settlement in Patagonia,\nrebuilding a replica of the farmhouse in which I was born.\n\nThat reconstruction prompted me to revisit the documentary history of Great\nHouse Farm and, in doing so, to compare the family evidence with the\nsurviving land-registration, court and archival records. That work is what\nbrought the title chronology and the unresolved Parcel A question back into\nfocus.\n\nThe replica is also a way of preserving the history of a house that was\ndemolished in December 1988. I mention this not as an argument on the\nmerits, but to explain why a historical record that had remained largely\nunexamined for decades was reconstructed now, and why I have pursued the\nunderlying documents rather than relying solely on inherited family\naccounts.\n\nY Wladfa itself exists because Michael D. Jones and those who followed him\njudged that Welsh language, history, heritage, people, landscape and\nbuildings were being overwritten in Wales — the process they cited as the\nwork of a “decapitation committee”. Which makes it interesting 99% of UK\nMPs refused to sign an EDM in 2025 condemning us Christians being\ndecapitated. Our land parcel was clearly a Christian stronghold and holy\nsacred lands and we were clearly stewards of it for centuries.\n\nThe Patagonia Welsh settlement is where those things are instead kept,\ncelebrated and respected. The replica stands there for the same reason the\noriginal house cannot stand on Parcel A.\n\nI will wait to hear from the investigation officer.\n\nI also take this opportunity to draw your attention to the enclosed PDF,\nwhich contains photographs relating to a tree-planting ceremony on the\nsouthernmost part of our claimed Parcel A and subsequent photographs\nshowing the tree being demolished. The ceremony is part of the family’s\nevidence of its assertion of ownership: my great-grandfather is recorded as\nplanting the tree to commemorate that ownership. The photographs also\npreserve contemporaneous visual evidence, including identifiable\nindividuals and machinery vehicle registration numbers, which may assist in\nestablishing who carried out the later destruction and in what\ncircumstances.\n\nThere is a further land-history issue which may be relevant to the wider\nquestion of how our claimed parcel came to be treated as no longer\nbelonging to the family. The woodland to the north of our home, on our\nclaimed parcel, was taken at a time when my grandmother had undergone a leg\namputation in 1955 and had become wheelchair-bound. I understand that this\nwoodland was subsequently gifted to a charity, which has itself contacted\nme recently; I enclose that correspondence with the present material.\n\nSimilarly, I understand that land to the south of the farmhouse was also\ntaken, notwithstanding that the 1955 possession order appears, on the\ndocumentary material presently available to me, to have concerned the\nparcel to the east of ours. The Council subsequently made the southern land\ninto a small green or park and, in the process, the commemorative tree and\nother physical evidence of the family’s assertion of ownership were\ndestroyed. I appreciate that this moves beyond the immediate registration\nquestion, but the destruction of physical evidence bearing directly upon\nthe family's historic claim may be relevant to understanding the subsequent\nevidential position.\n\nThere is also a further matter which I mention cautiously because I do not\nyet know its full significance. It has been alleged to me that the\nlong-serving Council Clerk for Llandough played a role in the destruction\nof this evidence. When I initially raised the matter through FOI, his\nresponse was, as I understand it, an attempt not to become involved. When\nsubsequently required to respond, he retired after approximately 35 years'\nservice. I am not asserting that these events establish misconduct or that\nhis retirement was connected with this matter; I do not presently know the\nextent, if any, of the connection.\n\nI include the material because I believe it may have evidential relevance,\nwhether to the immediate investigation or, if not, at a later stage when\nthe wider history of the land and the disappearance of evidence is\nconsidered. The tree ceremony, its subsequent destruction, the photographs,\nand the associated correspondence provide material evidence which I do not\nthink should be disregarded simply because some aspects of it may\nultimately fall outside the immediate scope of this review.\n\nThere is also an important cultural dimension to the evidence. The tree was\nnot merely a physical object: its planting appears to have been an act of\nasserting and commemorating ownership according to the family’s\nlongstanding traditions and understanding of the land. I recognise that\nquestions of ancient custom and tradition may not fall within the immediate\nlegal scope of ICR/090/26. I mention them because the destruction of the\nphysical manifestation of that assertion removed evidence of the very claim\nwhose documentary survival is now being investigated.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester \n\n\nICR/090/26\nTitles WA231076 / WA240304 (closed)\nHMLR refs: N610CGV; 260919-6726246\nhttps://greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com/"},{"id":"gm-1a0e91087e035256","subject":"Great House Farm, Llandough","name":"Great House Farm, Llandough","date":"2026-09-28T17:29:29Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0e91087e035256.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0e8f80ef591718-great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":["vale-scrutiny"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["vale-scrutiny"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0e8f80ef591718","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"vale-scrutiny","name":"Vale of Glamorgan Council (Scrutiny)"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0e91087e035256.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Chris,\n\nI flew back, I'm even at Mamgus old house in Dinas from time to time.\n\nHere's a website AI generated and maintains which articulates my grievance.\nhttps://greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com\n\nI shared with the charity who own the woodland. They're immidiate response\nwas to do what everyone has always done to us and ignore our ownership\nclaim and assertion and acknowledge someone else as the owner.\n\nMany and myself are still investigating. But in a nutshell a synthetic\nparcel appears to have been created, based on enough years of attempts to\nmake our occupancy appear permissive, so that the land registry can accept\nthe story. So 1974 licence letters was also a failed attempt and premis my\nfamily rejected, in a long line of failed attempts spanning back to at\nleast 1949.\n\nThe registration then made the 1987 ruling possible. And that ruling is\nwhat the land registry depend on to justify its decision to allow BP to\nregister a synthetic mimic of our parcel in their names, without notifying\nus until long after the appeal of that registration had ended. In which my\nnan conveniently died. Equally that 1987 ruling depends on the 1983\nregistration of our land parcel (or mimic synthetic version of it, built\nfrom a falsehood of a chain that didn't have root title and was not and\nnever was successful attempts to make our occupancy permissive.\n\nFurthermore the \"whole farm\" being treated as one parcel, was not merged\nback into one parcel (a synthetic version of our parcel too, not ours),\nuntil after the 1986/1987 case.\n\nSo my nan Mary Williams didn't get a fair opportunity to reject the licence\nletter in 1974 between it being offered and quashing the 1962 possession\norder for our land parcel. Which itself was never enforced and arguably not\nlegally enforceable as it was based on ambuguity from the 1955 order which\nmade our occupancy of our parcel simply appear permissive with concealment\nof the fact my nans resistence was due to her ownership claim. And\nconcealment of the fact \"whole farm\" excluded our parcel of land and was\nnot in the 1939 tenancy agreement. So not conveyed to Western Ground Rents\nin 1938.\n\nFurther to that my nans ownership claim, even though unable to be proven\ndue her title being stolen in 1950/52, still means having not paid anyone\nfor anything on her parcel since 1928 she will have already qualified for\nadverse possession by 1940.\n\nWe didn't get fair opportunity to appeal the 83 registration either. We\nalso didn't get opportunity to appeal CADWs decision to not list our county\ntreasure 800 year old home as a grade II before BP demolished it at 4am the\nnext day.\n\nThen the council didn't hold them to any account when they returned in 1989\nto get planning permission. The council are also under the microscope for\nmany failures. Failure to issue a demolition block order. Also two annexes\nare missing from those planning docs which are local historic societies\ntestemony to petition demolition citing our old ancestral homes\nsignificants (home since 1667 and my birth home I remind you).\n\nPlease just look at the evidence.\nhttps://greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com\n\nMy family are living like hostages under terrorists and have done for 38\nyears. It's not acceptable. This demonic realm and spell has to be fought\noff.\n\nDo what you can please. Even if its a small prayer for us. I thank you in\nadvance.\n\nSee attached a few images from my military service. I served this country.\nI'm still trying to here. I left my career with full honours. My last\nemployer in the Vale Council. I hope this betrayal will end. I'm more than\ndisappointed in local authories here, who don't appear any less hostile to\nmy family and our interest in our land in Llandough.\n\nI can't play the role of hostage or prisoner of war under this regime. My\nfamily are mostly all women and children, I am not. It's an untenable\nsituation Chris. The Senedd should have at least done a public enquiry of\nthis.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester \n\n[phone redacted]"},{"id":"gm-1a0e8ab4fd4c25fd","subj