 apply to the Information Commissioner; and \n> regulation 11 gives a right to an internal review of any refusal under \n> the EIR.\n> \n> \"We have nothing more to add\" does not meet those requirements. If the \n> period passes without a compliant response I will have no option but to \n> complain to the Information Commissioner.\n> \n> PART TWO -- MATTERS FOR THE COUNCIL'S DISCRETION\n> \n> These are not part of the request above and carry no deadline. They are \n> simply things I would be grateful for.\n> \n> A. I am not asking for the name or contact details of any individual. I \n> am asking whether the Council would be willing to pass my details to \n> any former member, former clerk or long-standing resident who might \n> remember the village between the 1950s and the 1990s, leaving it \n> entirely to them whether to contact me. I appreciate the Council may \n> have data protection or administrative reasons for declining, and I \n> would not press it.\n> \n> B. Whether the Council would be willing to table this correspondence at \n> a meeting and minute it. I am content for it to be tabled in full.\n> \n> C. Whether the Council would consider, in due course, whether an \n> interpretation board or a plaque near the green would be appropriate. \n> The excavation on the land beneath Church View Close recorded 1,026 \n> burials, described in the published report as by far the largest early \n> medieval burial population so far recovered from Wales, above a Roman \n> villa and an Iron Age roundhouse. That history belongs to Llandough as \n> much as to my family, and there is nothing on the ground that records \n> it. I would be glad to give the Council or the community a short talk \n> on the documentary record, without charge, if that would be useful.\n> \n> I should be grateful for a response to items 1 to 3 by 16 October 2026.\n> \n> Regards,\n> \n> Requester \n> \n> \n> On Sun, 20 Sept 2026, 18:31 Mo Misra, <[email redacted]> \n> wrote:\n> \n>> Dear Requester,\n>> \n>> Thanks for your email of 20th September 2026.\n>> \n>> I note that our Clerk, Paul Egan, has already directed you to all the\n>> information we have on the matter as indicated in paragraph 6 of your\n>> email.\n>> \n>> We have nothing more to add.\n>> \n>> Best regards\n>> \n>> Dr M C Misra,\n>> Chair Llandough Community Council"},{"id":"gm-1a0e6e0a094fc1cd","subject":"RE: FOI - 2026-032 - Great House Farm, Llandough","name":"RE: FOI - 2026-032 - Great House Farm, Llandough","date":"2026-09-28T07:17:48Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0e6e0a094fc1cd.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0c8b62d56ff57c-foi-2026-032-great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":["museum"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["museum"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0c8b62d56ff57c","direction":"received","from":"FOI Requests","institutions":[{"id":"museum","name":"Amgueddfa Cymru - Museum Wales"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0e6e0a094fc1cd.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Apologies, regarding FOI: 2026-036, we aim to provide a response by 6th October.\n\nCofion cynnes,\n\n[A picture containing text, tableware, plate, dishware  Description automatically generated]\nFOI Team\n[cid:image002.png@01DD4F21.D8BA3560]\n[email redacted] \namgueddfa.cymru  | museum.wales \n\nRhif elusen | Charity number 525774\n\n\nFrom: FOI Requests  \nSent: 28 September 2026 08:13\nTo: Requester  \nCc: FOI Requests  \nSubject: RE: FOI - 2026-032 - Great House Farm, Llandough\n\nRequester\n\nThank you for your email.\n\nJust to clarify, our collections management system records items individually and does not group records together based on similar place names. We are not aware of any recorded information showing that records for Great House Farm, Llandough (near Cowbridge) or Great House Farm, Llandow were ever catalogued or treated within the collections system as relating to Great House Farm, Llandough (near Penarth/Cardiff).   The references to similarly named locations arose from our searches for information relevant to your request, rather than from any overlap within the collections records themselves.\n\nIn relation to item 7 of your email of 26 August, we do not hold any separate recorded information explaining the basis on which staff concluded that the identified records were unrelated to the reported nineteenth-century discovery. That conclusion was reached through staff assessment of the available records, and no further recorded information is held.\n\nI hope this helps clarify our position.\n\nCofion cynnes,\n\n[A picture containing text, tableware, plate, dishware  Description automatically generated]\nFOI Team\n[cid:image002.png@01DD4F21.D8BA3560]\n[email redacted] \namgueddfa.cymru  | museum.wales \n\nRhif elusen | Charity number 525774\n\n\nFrom: Requester  >\nSent: 27 September 2026 05:16\nTo: FOI Requests  >\nSubject: RE: FOI - 2026-032 - Great House Farm, Llandough\n\n\nThis email comes from outside the organisation.\n\nDo not click links or open attachments unless it is an email you expected to receive.\nDear FOI Team,\n\nFOI 2026-032\n\nDiolch yn fawr. Thank you for the records, the descriptors and the published references. This is a thorough response and it is genuinely useful.\n\nOne correction of place is required.\n\nThe request concerns Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough near Penarth / Cardiff. Among the disclosed PAS material is at least one record for a different property of the same name: Great House Farm, Llandough near Cowbridge (including 2022.18.1, NGR SS9934672805, medieval lead Romanesque mount). There is also a Great House farm entry at Llandow. Those are not the site named in the request. Two Llandoughs, and more than one Great House Farm, sit in the same county. The homonym is easy to miss and material.\n\nPlease treat the Cowbridge / Llandow entries as out of scope for this request unless you hold recorded information showing they were, at any time, indexed, cited, or treated as if they related to Great House Farm, Llandough (Penarth / Cardiff). If any such cross-attribution exists in historic files, catalogues, correspondence, or search notes, that recorded information is within scope and I ask that it be disclosed.\n\nIf labels or findspot fields are now amended to distinguish the two farms, please do not overwrite the original wording without retaining the prior form. I ask that any correction be recorded as a dated amendment, with the earlier descriptor preserved, so the sequence of how the records stood when searches were first run remains inspectable. Please do not destroy, weed, or replace the versions as disclosed on 22 September 2026.\n\nOne small point from my email of 26 August also remains. As item 7 I asked for any recorded information showing the basis on which the Museum concluded that the identified records were unrelated to the reported nineteenth-century discovery. If that conclusion was reached by staff judgement and not written down, please simply confirm that no recorded information is held on that point. I am not asking for a new opinion.\n\nCould you also confirm the date by which you expect to respond to FOI 2026-036?\n\nCofion,\n\nRequester\ngreathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com"},{"id":"gm-1a0e6dd07a8cfe15","subject":"RE: Great House Farm, Llandough","name":"RE: Great House Farm, Llandough","date":"2026-09-28T07:13:42Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0e6dd07a8cfe15.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0e03138143e763-great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":["senedd"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["senedd"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0e03138143e763","direction":"received","from":"Pwyllgor Diwylliant, Cyfathrebu, y Gymraeg a Chwaraeon - Culture, Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee","institutions":[{"id":"senedd","name":"Senedd"}],"refs":["N610CGV"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0e6dd07a8cfe15.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester,\n\nYour correction is noted with thanks.\n\nKind regards,\n\nHaidee\n\n[cid:image001.png@01DD4F21.379E9E00]\nHaidee James[cid:image002.png@01DD4F21.379E9E00]\nAil Glerc, Pwyllgor Diwylliant, Cyfathrebu, y Gymraeg a Chwaraeon, Senedd Cymru\nSecond Clerk, Culture, Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee, Welsh Parliament\n[phone redacted] Rhagenwau|Pronouns: hi – she/her\n\n\nFrom: Requester  \nSent: 27 September 2026 05:12\nTo: Wales.OfficeMail  \nCc: Coventry.OfficeMail  \nSubject: Great House Farm, Llandough\n\nSirs/ Ma'ams,\n\nI need to correct a date I have used in earlier correspondence about Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr, Llandough, titles WA231076 and WA240304.\n\nI had treated 1983 as the year the two titles were merged. That was wrong. HM Land Registry’s own letter of 23 March 2026 (Lisa Griffiths, ref N610CGV) separates the two acts.\n1982–83 was first registration of two live titles, not a merger.\n30 November 1982: application to first-register WA231076. The Court of Appeal later treated BP Properties Ltd as registered proprietors of “the farm” from November 1982.\n23 February 1983: application to first-register a second title, WA240304.\n26 March 1983: Mary Williams died. On the family’s case, notice of those applications did not reach us until after her death, while the family was in mourning. She had no window in which to object. The family was not in a position to use any window that arrived after that.\nThose two titles remained separate through first instance.\n24 July 1986: Hollis J. Both titles were still live. The judgment language treats Great House Farm as one holding. It had not been one holding on the register. On the family’s case it had not been one holding since the 1877 split: Parcel A (house, buildings, garden/yard and about 10 acres, the Thomas/Williams line) and Parcel B (the eastern fields retained by Bute and later WGR/BP).\n2 February 1987: application to amalgamate the two titles. WA240304 was then closed and registration continued under WA231076. That is the merge. It was made after the 1986 ruling and before the Court of Appeal gave judgment on 31 July 1987.\nSo 1983 is silent first registration of a second title while Mary was still in Parcel A, or had only just died. 1987 is the administrative merger, carried out while the appeal was still open.\nThis was not a dispute about a single land parcel that BP already owned. Mary was on Parcel A, which the family says she owned, with occupation on that house parcel running in the family from at least 1667. BP, and WGR before it from 1949, were the neighbouring interest on Parcel B. From 1949 onwards the family’s case is that those neighbours ran a decades-long series of tenancy offers, “whole farm” wording, possession orders and the 1974 letters to make her occupation of her own parcel look permissive, and to make both parcels look like one estate they had acquired. They never produced a conveyance of Parcel A. What they had was an unfounded claim that her occupancy was permissive.\nPlease treat any earlier letter from me that said the titles merged in 1983 as corrected by this email. The dates that matter are:\n1982/83 — first registration of two titles, without the occupier being heard;\n24 July 1986 — first-instance judgment describing one farm while two titles still stood;\n2 February 1987 — amalgamation, before the appeal ended;\n31 July 1987 — Court of Appeal.\nYours faithfully,\n\nRequester\nRefs where relevant: N610CGV; 260919-6726246; titles WA231076 and WA240304.\nRequester \ngreathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com"},{"id":"gm-1a0e6dc8c61aeba3","subject":"RE: FOI - 2026-032 - Great House Farm, Llandough","name":"RE: FOI - 2026-032 - Great House Farm, Llandough","date":"2026-09-28T07:13:17Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0e6dc8c61aeba3.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0c8b62d56ff57c-foi-2026-032-great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":["museum"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["museum"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0c8b62d56ff57c","direction":"received","from":"FOI Requests","institutions":[{"id":"museum","name":"Amgueddfa Cymru - Museum Wales"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0e6dc8c61aeba3.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Requester\n\nThank you for your email.\n\nJust to clarify, our collections management system records items individually and does not group records together based on similar place names. We are not aware of any recorded information showing that records for Great House Farm, Llandough (near Cowbridge) or Great House Farm, Llandow were ever catalogued or treated within the collections system as relating to Great House Farm, Llandough (near Penarth/Cardiff).   The references to similarly named locations arose from our searches for information relevant to your request, rather than from any overlap within the collections records themselves.\n\nIn relation to item 7 of your email of 26 August, we do not hold any separate recorded information explaining the basis on which staff concluded that the identified records were unrelated to the reported nineteenth-century discovery. That conclusion was reached through staff assessment of the available records, and no further recorded information is held.\n\nI hope this helps clarify our position.\n\nCofion cynnes,\n\n[A picture containing text, tableware, plate, dishware  Description automatically generated]\nFOI Team\n[cid:image002.png@01DD4F21.36E14170]\n[email redacted]<[email redacted]>\namgueddfa.cymru<applewebdata://288FE56C-5BFC-474C-ADB3-A1017EF25E69/amgueddfa.cymru> | museum.wales<applewebdata://288FE56C-5BFC-474C-ADB3-A1017EF25E69/museum.wales>\n\nRhif elusen | Charity number 525774\n\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]>\nSent: 27 September 2026 05:16\nTo: FOI Requests <[email redacted]>\nSubject: RE: FOI - 2026-032 - Great House Farm, Llandough\n\n\nThis email comes from outside the organisation.\n\nDo not click links or open attachments unless it is an email you expected to receive.\nDear FOI Team,\n\nFOI 2026-032\n\nDiolch yn fawr. Thank you for the records, the descriptors and the published references. This is a thorough response and it is genuinely useful.\n\nOne correction of place is required.\n\nThe request concerns Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough near Penarth / Cardiff. Among the disclosed PAS material is at least one record for a different property of the same name: Great House Farm, Llandough near Cowbridge (including 2022.18.1, NGR SS9934672805, medieval lead Romanesque mount). There is also a Great House farm entry at Llandow. Those are not the site named in the request. Two Llandoughs, and more than one Great House Farm, sit in the same county. The homonym is easy to miss and material.\n\nPlease treat the Cowbridge / Llandow entries as out of scope for this request unless you hold recorded information showing they were, at any time, indexed, cited, or treated as if they related to Great House Farm, Llandough (Penarth / Cardiff). If any such cross-attribution exists in historic files, catalogues, correspondence, or search notes, that recorded information is within scope and I ask that it be disclosed.\n\nIf labels or findspot fields are now amended to distinguish the two farms, please do not overwrite the original wording without retaining the prior form. I ask that any correction be recorded as a dated amendment, with the earlier descriptor preserved, so the sequence of how the records stood when searches were first run remains inspectable. Please do not destroy, weed, or replace the versions as disclosed on 22 September 2026.\n\nOne small point from my email of 26 August also remains. As item 7 I asked for any recorded information showing the basis on which the Museum concluded that the identified records were unrelated to the reported nineteenth-century discovery. If that conclusion was reached by staff judgement and not written down, please simply confirm that no recorded information is held on that point. I am not asking for a new opinion.\n\nCould you also confirm the date by which you expect to respond to FOI 2026-036?\n\nCofion,\n\nRequester\ngreathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com<http://greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com/>"},{"id":"gm-1a0e406403d49830","subject":"In Touch with Llandough!","name":"In Touch with Llandough!","date":"2026-09-27T18:00:05Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0e406403d49830.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0e406403d49830-in-touch-with-llandough","scopeTopics":["vale-scrutiny"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["vale-scrutiny"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0e406403d49830","direction":"received","from":"Cllr George Carroll","institutions":[{"id":"vale-scrutiny","name":"Vale of Glamorgan Council (Scrutiny)"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0e406403d49830.json","textStatus":"available","text":"View this email in your browser (https://mailchi.mp/609fe58781ea/in-touch-with-llandough-17666141?e=1429c2c1bb)\nNEXT SUNDAY: ROAD CLOSURES\nFOR CARDIFF HALF MARATHON\n\n\n**\n------------------------------------------------------------\nDear Requester\n\nRoad closures will affect our community on the morning of Sunday 4th October for the Cardiff Half Marathon.\n\nPenarth Road, Barry Road and Cogan Spur will all be closed, as the marathon course runs along these roads.\n\nLlandough Hill will also be closed, to prevent vehicles diverted via Penlan Road from inadvertently trying to access Penarth Road while it's shut.\nDiversions will be in place, and you can see the full road closure map above.\n\nThe closures will operate between 9am and 12pm.\n\nFurther details can be found at the Cardiff Half Marathon website here.  (https://www.cardiffhalfmarathon.co.uk/event-info/road-closures/)\n\nhttps://www.facebook.com/share/r/1BzkRccTvm/\n\nIf you have further questions, Requester, please let me know.\n\n\n**\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nNext community litter pick\n\nOur last community litter pick has had great feedback.\n\nI've even had a couple of messages asking when the next one is taking place!\n\nLlandough Community Council organise these events throughout the year, but they usually put them on hold during the winter, due to lack of interest.\n\nBut given the enthusiasm, I thought it best to harness it!\n\nWe discussed it at last week's Community Council meeting, and I'm exploring organising another in the coming weeks.\n\nI'll need to liaise with the Vale Council to arrange equipment, but the Community Council are on board and have said they'll help.\n\nIf you're interested in taking part, Requester, drop me a message!\nhttps://www.facebook.com/share/v/1KDWx1SUjX/\n\nLicensing Service of\nRevd Phil Maudsley\n\nI had a wonderful evening on Tuesday at the Licensing Service of Reverend Phil Maudsley, the new Penarth Ministry Area lead.\n\nIt was great to see many members of our community there, including Llandough Community Council Chair Mo Misra, Reverend Susannah Griffin and lots of other residents.\n\nPhil and his family have recently moved to South Wales from North Yorkshire, and I look forward to working with him.\n\nI know everyone in Llandough will join me in welcoming them to our community.\n\nGreenway Close verge\n\nWhen I was out in our village recently, a Greenway Close resident raised the overgrowth on the nearby verge.\n\nAs you'll see from the first picture, it was in need of attention.\n\nI raised with the Council. And I'm pleased to report it's now been cut back.\n\nThanks so much to the resident for coming over and raising this.\n\nIt helped ensure it was cleared.\n\nAs I always say, we're a team in Llandough.\n\nBy all chipping in, we make our village better for everyone.\n\nIf you ever spot anything that needs attention, Requester, do let me know!\n\nPreventing car thefts\n\nI spoke with two people who've recently purchased Faraday boxes while I was out meeting residents this week.\n\nWith attempted car thefts reported in our village in recent months, police have advised everyone with keyless fobs to keep them in a Faraday pouch or box as a security measure.\n\nIt's a sensible precaution and a small expense to protect your car.\n\nIt was good to see people following the advice.\n\nIf you require further information, please drop me a message.\n\nCommunity drop-in event:\n20th October\n\nOur next community drop-in event takes place at the Merrie Harrier on 20th October.\n\nLike the last one, it'll start at 7.00pm.\n\nThe pub is a key asset to our community.\n\nTracey and the team do a lot for us - help return the favour by lending them your support.\n\nYou can see some of their midweek offers below!\n\nhttps://www.facebook.com/share/r/1EM9z5wbMs/\n\nOur Plan for Llandough!\n\n\nAs your councillor, I'm working with you all to secure a better future for Llandough. Together, we have a plan to deliver for the village.\n\nOur plan is making Llandough better by:\n\n1. Sorting out the parking problems\n2. Stamping out dog fouling\n3. Repairing our roads\n4. Stopping littering and flytipping\n5. Delivering better sports facilities\n6. Keeping the whole community involved\n\nIf you have any suggestions of what you'd like to see in Our Plan, please get in touch!\n\nhttp://www.facebook.com/sharer/sharer.php?u=https%3A%2F%2Fmailchi.mp%2F609fe58781ea%2Fin-touch-with-llandough-17666141 Share (http://www.facebook.com/sharer/sharer.php?u=https%3A%2F%2Fmailchi.mp%2F609fe58781ea%2Fin-touch-with-llandough-17666141)\nhttps://us19.forward-to-friend.com/forward?u=7b7a08284cdbc1f69f5b0afd8&id=a99cf470b6&e=1429c2c1bb Forward (https://us19.forward-to-friend.com/forward?u=7b7a08284cdbc1f69f5b0afd8&id=a99cf470b6&e=1429c2c1bb)\n\n============================================================\nI'm hoping to reach as many people as possible with this newsletter, so please feel free to forward it to friends and neighbours.\n\nIf this email has been forwarded to you, and you would like to sign up to my newsletter, please click ** here ([email redacted]\n.\nThis email was sent to you because your email address is on my subscribers list. If you don't want to receive any more messages from me, please click below to unsubscribe.\n\nYour consent was previously given during an online or offline interaction with me or the Conservatives. If you feel this is incorrect or would like to comment, please contact me. I do not buy email lists.\n\nPromoted by Chris Kirkham on behalf of George Carroll, both of Vale of Glamorgan Conservatives, Holton House, 50 Holton Road, Barry [postcode redacted].\n\nThis email was sent to [email redacted] ([email redacted]\nwhy did I get this? (https://facebook.us19.list-manage.com/about?u=7b7a08284cdbc1f69f5b0afd8&id=daad00f7e3&e=1429c2c1bb&c=a99cf470b6)     unsubscribe from this list (***REDACTED***     update subscription preferences (https://facebook.us19.list-manage.com/profile?u=7b7a08284cdbc1f69f5b0afd8&id=daad00f7e3&e=1429c2c1bb&c=a99cf470b6)\nPolitician . Unit 5 Pro Copy Business Centre . Parc Ty Glas . Cardiff, Crf [postcode redacted] . USA"},{"id":"gm-1a0e3f540e4cde99","subject":"Automatic reply: Preservation and disclosure request — Church View Close / Great House Farm, Llandough — titles WA231076 / WA240304","name":"Automatic reply: Preservation and disclosure request — Church View Close / Great House Farm, Llandough — titles WA231076 / WA240304","date":"2026-09-27T17:41:32Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0e3f540e4cde99.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0e3f4adfc33bec-automatic-reply-preservation-and-disclosure-request-church-view-close-great-house-farm-llandough-titles-wa231076-wa24030","scopeTopics":["persimmon"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["persimmon"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0e3f4adfc33bec","direction":"received","from":"EWAL Customer Care","institutions":[{"id":"persimmon","name":"Persimmon Homes"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0e3f540e4cde99.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Thank you for your enquiry to Persimmon Homes East Wales Customer Care team.\n\nPlease be advised that your email will be responded to