gland number 1818486. The registered office for these companies is Persimmon House, Fulford, York [postcode redacted]."},{"id":"gm-1a0e3f4adfc33bec","subject":"Automatic reply: Preservation and disclosure request — Church View Close / Great House Farm, Llandough — titles WA231076 / WA240304","name":"Automatic reply: Preservation and disclosure request — Church View Close / Great House Farm, Llandough — titles WA231076 / WA240304","date":"2026-09-27T17:40:54Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0e3f4adfc33bec.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0e3f4adfc33bec-automatic-reply-preservation-and-disclosure-request-church-view-close-great-house-farm-llandough-titles-wa231076-wa24030","scopeTopics":["persimmon"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["persimmon"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0e3f4adfc33bec","direction":"received","from":"Legal Enquiries","institutions":[{"id":"persimmon","name":"Persimmon Homes"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0e3f4adfc33bec.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Your email has been received. The email will now be forwarded to the appropriate person to deal and you will be contacted directly by the person dealing in due course. Please do not respond to this email.\n\n[https://images.persimmoncloud.com/Disclaimer/Banner_PH_700x150.png]\n[https://images.persimmoncloud.com/Disclaimer/HBF2026_5star_red_whitebg.png]\nDisclaimer\nThe information in this email is confidential and may be legally privileged. It is intended solely for the addressee. Access to this email by anyone else is unauthorised. If you are not the intended recipient, any disclosure, copying, distribution or any action taken or omitted in reliance on it is prohibited and may be unlawful. If you are not the intended recipient please notify the sender and delete this email.\n\nOur privacy policies for our customers, employees and job applicants are available at Persimmon privacy policies <https://www.persimmonhomes.com/corporate/sustainability/policies-and-statements> .\n\nPersimmon Homes Limited is registered in England number 4108747, Charles Church Developments Limited is registered in England number 1182689 and Space4 Limited is registered in England number 3702606.\n\nThese companies are wholly owned subsidiaries of Persimmon Plc registered in England number 1818486. The registered office for these companies is Persimmon House, Fulford, York [postcode redacted]."},{"id":"gm-1a0e3f34cc06c477","subject":"Preservation and disclosure request — Church View Close / Great House Farm, Llandough — titles WA231076 / WA240304","name":"Preservation and disclosure request — Church View Close / Great House Farm, Llandough — titles WA231076 / WA240304","date":"2026-09-27T17:39:27Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0e3f34cc06c477.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0e3b180d95f228-preservation-and-disclosure-request-church-view-close-great-house-farm-llandough-titles-wa231076-wa240304","scopeTopics":["persimmon"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["persimmon"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0e3b180d95f228","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"persimmon","name":"Persimmon Homes"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0e3f34cc06c477.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Sirs/Madam,\n\nI write concerning the land now comprising Church View Close, Llandough,\nformerly Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr, including title WA231076 and former\ntitle WA240304.\n\nPersimmon Homes (Wales) Ltd is the current registered proprietor of\nWA231076. Persimmon is therefore a present link in the conveyancing chain\narising from the registration of this land in BP Properties Ltd's name in\n1982–83, BP's subsequent transfer to Ideal Homes in 1993, and the later\ndevelopment history.\n\nI am conducting a documented investigation into the legitimacy of that\ntitle chain. This is not a speculative historical enquiry. The issues arise\nfrom identified Land Registry, conveyancing, court, planning and\npublic-authority records, and from specific documentary gaps which remain\nunresolved.\n\nThe central issue is the historic identity and ownership of the land.\n\nThe evidence presently assembled indicates two historically distinct\nparcels:\n\n• Parcel A: the farmhouse, garden, buildings, yard and associated woodland,\nwhich the Williams family maintains was acquired out of the Bute estate\nthrough the Daniel Thomas transaction and subsequently became the family's\nfreehold interest; and\n\n• Parcel B: the eastern agricultural fields, whose subsequent passage from\nBute to Western Ground Rents and then to BP is not presently disputed by\nthe family.\n\nThe significance of that distinction is that later documents repeatedly use\nthe expression “whole farm”, although the surviving 1939 tenancy plan is\nunderstood to show the eastern fields only. The 1938 Bute-to-Western Ground\nRents conveyance is therefore a material title-chain question: what estate\nwas actually capable of being conveyed by Bute in 1938, and did that\nconveyance include Parcel A?\n\nThere is further evidence requiring examination of the period from 1949 to\n1974, including repeated attempts to characterise Mary's occupation of the\nfarmhouse parcel as tenancy or permission; the loss of the family's title\npapers in about 1950–52; the 1955 possession order expressed in “whole\nfarm” terms but enforced everywhere except the farmhouse and garden; the\nseparate 1962 possession proceedings concerning the farmhouse and garden;\nMary's express assertion of ownership; and the 1974 proceedings, which were\nadjourned part-heard and were never finally adjudicated on the family's\nhistoric ownership claim.\n\nThe Court of Appeal's decision in BP Properties Ltd v Buckler [1987] EWCA\nCiv 2 subsequently concerned possession, limitation and the legal effect of\nthe October 1974 letters. The judgment records Mary's asserted ownership\nand the absence of the documents she said supported it. The present\ninvestigation therefore distinguishes carefully between what that judgment\ndecided concerning possession and limitation and the separate question of\nwhether the documentary root and extent of WGR/BP's title to Parcel A were\never actually established.\n\nThat distinction becomes particularly important in relation to first\nregistration.\n\nHM Land Registry records show BP's first-registration applications for\nWA231076 and WA240304 in 1982–83. The surviving registration material also\nidentifies a 31 December 1969 conveyance from Western Ground Rents to BP\nPension Trust as a root of title, but that conveyance is not presently\nincluded in the 30 July 2026 schedule of documents surviving on the\nrelevant first-registration files. The 1975 conveyance and plan, the\n1982–83 registration material, the extent of the two titles and their later\namalgamation therefore require direct reconciliation against the underlying\ndocumentary title.\n\nThere is also an unresolved procedural issue concerning notice and\nopportunity to object during the 1982–83 first-registration process. Mary\nWilliams remained in occupation of the farmhouse. The family evidence\nrecorded on the evidence index is that notification was not received until\nafter her death on 26 March 1983. I am obtaining the primary notice,\nservice, application and registration records to establish exactly what\noccurred.\n\nThe matters under investigation consequently include possible registration\nmistake, procedural unfairness, material non-disclosure and, depending upon\nwhat the primary records establish, possible fraud or deliberate\nconcealment. There are also unresolved public-authority record questions\ninvolving HM Land Registry, court administration and other authorities.\nThese are identified as evidential questions, not asserted as established\nfindings against Persimmon or any individual.\n\nPersimmon's position is therefore directly relevant because its present\nregistered title derives from the historic chain now being examined.\n\nPlease provide a substantive response to the following:\n\n   1.\n\n   *Title chain:* What documentary chain does Persimmon say establishes its\n   predecessor's title to the whole of WA231076, including the farmhouse,\n   garden and associated land now comprising Church View Close?\n   2.\n\n   *BP / Ideal Homes due diligence:* What title, acquisition and\n   due-diligence documents were supplied to and reviewed by Persimmon in\n   connection with the acquisition from Ideal Homes, including deeds, plans,\n   title abstracts, searches, requisitions, Land Registry correspondence and\n   enquiries concerning the extent of WA231076/WA240304?\n   3.\n\n   *Earlier title documents:* Does Persimmon hold, or has it ever held,\n   copies of the 1969 WGR-to-BP Pension Trust conveyance, the 1975 BP Pension\n   Trust-to-BP Properties conveyance and plan, the 1982–83 first-registration\n   application/bundle, or documents explaining the inclusion of the farmhouse\n   parcel within the registered extent?\n   4.\n\n   *1996 correspondence:* Does Persimmon retain the correspondence from\n   March 1996 with HM Land Registry concerning WA231076, identified in the\n   surviving title chronology as the last dealing on the file? If so, please\n   provide a copy, subject to any properly applicable exemption or privilege.\n   5.\n\n   *Parcel A:* Has Persimmon ever reviewed the underlying documentary title\n   sufficiently to satisfy itself that the farmhouse/garden parcel was\n   actually comprised in the title acquired by BP, rather than being brought\n   within the registered extent through the later “whole farm” description?\n   6.\n\n   *Present position:* Having regard to the matters above, does Persimmon\n   maintain that its title to the farmhouse/garden parcel is supported by an\n   identifiable documentary root independent of the possession and limitation\n   history considered in BP Properties Ltd v Buckler?\n\nIf Persimmon maintains that the documentary title to Parcel A is\nestablished, please identify the instrument or instruments on which that\nposition depends and the land those instruments actually comprise.\n\nI am seeking voluntary or pre-action disclosure of non-privileged material\nwhere possible. If any requested document is withheld, please identify the\ndocument or category concerned and the basis upon which it is withheld.\n\n*Preservation notice*\n\nPlease treat this correspondence as formal notice to preserve all documents\nand electronically stored information within Persimmon's possession,\ncustody or control that may be relevant to the title, acquisition,\nregistration, extent, occupation, planning or development of this land.\n\nThis includes historical material inherited from BP Properties, Ideal Homes\nor any other predecessor, together with deeds, conveyances, plans, title\nabstracts, searches, due-diligence files, acquisition memoranda,\nlegal/property correspondence, Land Registry correspondence, planning\nrecords and communications with public authorities.\n\nPlease ensure that no routine retention, deletion, destruction, disposal or\noverwriting process removes potentially relevant material. This\npreservation request includes electronic files, archived mailboxes and\nother electronically stored information where applicable.\n\nThe public evidence index and supporting record are maintained at:\n\nhttps://greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com/\n\nNothing in this correspondence abandons, limits or prejudices any claim\nconcerning title, registration, fraud, deliberate concealment, procedural\nunfairness, public-authority involvement, limitation, reopening of historic\nproceedings, rectification/alteration of the register, compensation or\nreparations. All rights and remedies are expressly reserved.\n\nThis correspondence is not a claim form and does not constitute service of\nproceedings.\n\nPlease acknowledge receipt and refer this correspondence to Persimmon's\nlegal/property team. I request a substantive response addressing the\nquestions above.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\nRequester  "},{"id":"gm-1a0e14ca6951632f","subject":"Further to Your Enquiry: Ref: 260918-6720625 Title No: WA240304","name":"Further to Your Enquiry: Ref: 260918-6720625 Title No: WA240304","date":"2026-09-27T05:18:08Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0e14ca6951632f.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0e14ca6951632f-further-to-your-enquiry-ref-260918-6720625-title-no-wa240304","scopeTopics":["hmlr"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["hmlr"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0e14ca6951632f","direction":"received","from":"_Data Services","institutions":[{"id":"hmlr","name":"HM Land Registry"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0e14ca6951632f.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Enquiry Reference: 260918-6720625 Title Number: WA240304\n\nDear Customer\n\nThank you for your enquiry. I have referred it for consideration by the appropriate team.\n\n\n\nHM Land Registry: processing times - GOV.UK  may assist you. It provides information on how we are delivering our services.\n\n\n\nTo contact us\n\nIf you are an HM Land Registry Portal customer:\n\nPlease use “Reply to Requisition” or the View Applications “Respond” function. This will attach your correspondence to the application.\n\nPlease use the View Applications  function to view and manage your applications.\n\n\n\nIf you are not a Portal customer / if you are a citizen customer:\n\nPlease reply to this email.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nYours faithfully\n\nSara\nHM Land Registry\n\nWe only provide factual information and impartial advice about our procedures. Read more about the advice we give. \n\nEasy and free support to help you work with HM Land Registry:\nGuidance for members of the public - GOV.UK \nGuidance for property professionals - GOV.UK \n\n\n\nHM Land Registry’s purpose is to protect your land ownership and provide services and data that underpin an efficient and informed property market. Our vision is: “A world-leading property market as part of a thriving economy and a sustainable future”.\n\nWe check all mail and attachments for known viruses. However, you are advised that you open any attachments at your own risk. If you have received this email and it was not intended for you, please let us know, then delete it.\n\nWe welcome correspondence in English and Welsh.\n\nTo see how HM Land Registry treats your personal information, read our Personal information charter: www.gov.uk/government/organisations/land-registry/about/personal-information-charter."},{"id":"gm-1a0e115941c12253","subject":"Automatic reply: Internal review of 719/26 (no records held) and overdue request of 20 August 2026 — Great House Farm, Llandough, 1988","name":"Automatic reply: Internal review of 719/26 (no records held) and overdue request of 20 August 2026 — Great House Farm, Llandough, 1988","date":"2026-09-27T04:17:59Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0e115941c12253.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0e115941c12253-automatic-reply-internal-review-of-719-26-no-records-held-and-overdue-request-of-20-august-2026-great-house-farm-llandou","scopeTopics":["swp"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["swp"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0e115941c12253","direction":"received","from":"Freedom of Information","institutions":[{"id":"swp","name":"South Wales Police"}],"refs":["719/26"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0e115941c12253.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Diolch am gysylltu â'r Tîm Rhyddid Gwybodaeth yn Heddlu De Cymru. Dyma neges i gadarnhau ein bod wedi cael eich gohebiaeth. Peidiwch ag ymateb i'r neges e-bost hon.\n\nRydym yn delio â nifer mawr o geisiadau ar hyn o bryd, yn ogystal ag ôl-groniad. Er ein bod yn gwneud pob ymdrech i ymateb i geisiadau o fewn y terfyn amser o 20 diwrnod gwaith, gall fod oedi wrth ddelio â rhai o'r ceisiadau o ganlyniad i hyn.\n\nMae'n ddrwg gennym am unrhyw anghyfleustra a achoswyd.\n\nThank you for contacting the Freedom of Information (FOI) Team at South Wales Police. This message is to confirm that we have received your correspondence. Please do not reply to this email.\n\nPlease be advised that we are currently dealing with a high volume of requests as well as a backlog. Whilst we make every effort to respond to all requests within the 20 working day deadline, there may be delays in the handling some of the requests as a result.\n\nWe apologise for any inconvenience caused.\nYmateb nad yw'n Argyfwng\\ Non-Emergency Response\n\nOes angen i chi siarad â'r heddlu ond nid oes angen ymateb brys arnoch? Gallwch roi gwybod am fater drwy ein gwefan https://www.heddlu-de-cymru.police.uk, neu ffonio 101. Gallwch ddefnyddio 101 i roi gwybod am achosion nad ydynt yn rhai brys i unrhyw heddlu yng Nghymru a Lloegr. Mewn argyfwng, ffoniwch 999 bob amser.\n\nDo you need to speak to the police but don’t require an emergency response? You can make a report via our website https://www.south-wales.police.uk, or call 101. 101 can be used to report a non-emergency to any force in Wales and England. In an emergency always dial 999.\n\nCymorth Iaith Gymraeg\\Welsh Language Support\n\nMae Heddlu De Cymru yn croesawu derbyn gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg a Saesneg. Byddwn yn ateb gohebiaeth a dderbynnir yn Gymraeg yn Gymraeg ac ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi.\n\nSouth Wales Police welcomes receiving correspondence in Welsh and English. Any correspondence received in Welsh will be answered in Welsh and corresponding in Welsh will not lead to a delay in responding.\n\nYmwadiad Heddlu De Cymru\\South Wales Police Disclaimer\n\nGallai defnyddio neu ddatgelu’r neges e-bost hon heb awdurdod fod yn anghyfreithlon. Gallwch weld ein datganiad cyfrinachedd yn: https://www.heddlu-de-cymru.police.uk/cy-GB/heddluoedd/heddlu-de-cymru/ardaloedd/amdanom-ni/amdanom-ni/datganiad-cyfrinachedd-e-bost/\".\n\nUnauthorised use or disclosure of this email may be unlawful. View our confidentiality statement at https://www.south-wales.police.uk/police-forces/south-wales-police/areas/about-us/about-us/email-confidentiality-statement/."},{"id":"gm-1a0e115453ac927d","subject":"Internal review of 719/26 (no records held) and overdue request of 20 August 2026 — Great House Farm, Llandough, 1988","name":"Internal review of 719/26 (no records held) and overdue request of 20 August 2026 — Great House Farm, Llandough, 1988","date":"2026-09-27T04:17:42Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0e115453ac927d.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0cab8f7b455a11-internal-review-of-719-26-no-records-held-and-overdue-request-of-20-august-2026-great-house-farm-llandough-1988","scopeTopics":["swp"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["swp"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0cab8f7b455a11","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"swp","name":"South Wales Police"}],"refs":["719/26"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0e115453ac927d.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Freedom of Information / Data Management Team,\n\nRequest 719/26, and my request of 20 August 2026 (deaths of William Buckler\nand Mary Doreen Williams)\n\n1. Internal review of 719/26\n\nI understand the Force's position is that it holds no records. I ask for an\ninternal review of that conclusion, because the search does not appear\ncapable of finding what the request describes.\n\nSouth Wales Constabulary officers were present at the eviction and\ndemolition at Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough, between 29 November\nand 6 December 1988. This is not a matter of recollection. It appears in\nITV Wales footage from the time, now public on ITV's archive channel, and\nin contemporary newspaper photographs and reports, which I can supply. A\npolice presence on that scale would have generated records: a duty or\ndeployment record, an operational order or briefing, an occurrence or\nincident log, liaison with the Under-Sheriff or bailiffs, press office\nmaterial, and possibly photographs.\n\nFor the review, please confirm in writing:\n\n(a) Which systems, archives and paper holdings were searched, including\npre-1990s paper records of the former Penarth/Barry division and any South\nGlamorgan command records.\n(b) The search terms used. Records of that period may be indexed under\n\"Leckwith Road\", \"Llandough\", \"BP Properties\", \"Buckler\", \"Williams\", \"Ty\nMawr\", \"eviction\", \"breach of the peace\" or the date, rather than \"Great\nHouse Farm\".\n(c) Whether records of that type from 1988 were destroyed under a retention\nschedule, and if so which schedule and whether any disposal record survives.\n(d) Whether any records were transferred to Glamorgan Archives or the South\nWales Police Heritage Centre, with the reference if known.\n\nIf records were destroyed, a record of their destruction is itself\ninformation I am asking for. If they are held elsewhere, please tell me\nwhere, under your duty to advise and assist.\n\nI was present at the eviction as a child. So far as any record contains my\npersonal data, please also treat this as a subject access request under\nArticle 15 UK GDPR.\n\n2. Request of 20 August 2026\n\nThis request was due on 18 September 2026 and I have had no response beyond\nthe automatic acknowledgement. Please give me its reference and the\nresponse. If the records are not held, please say whether they were\ndestroyed or passed to the Coroner.\n\nIf I do not receive the internal review outcome within 20 working days, I\nwill complain to the Information Commissioner.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\nRequester \ngreathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com"},{"id":"gm-1a0e11539bc7a9aa","subject":"Automatic reply: Referral to the Independent Complaints Reviewer — HM Land Registry, titles WA231076 / WA240304 (Llandough)","name":"Automatic reply: Referral to the Independent Complaints Reviewer — HM Land Registry, titles WA231076 / WA240304 (Llandough)","date":"2026-09-27T04:17:30Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0e11539bc7a9aa.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0e11539bc7a9aa-automatic-reply-referral-to-the-independent-complaints-reviewer-hm-land-registry-titles-wa231076-wa240304-llandough","scopeTopics":["mp"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["mp"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0e11539bc7a9aa","direction":"received","from":"DOUGHTY, Stephen","institutions":[{"id":"mp","name":"Member of Parliament"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0e11539bc7a9aa.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Hello,\n\nThank you for your email. Please read this automatic response, which serves as notice that your message has been received.\n\nI receive hundreds of emails every week, and although I intend to reply as quickly as I can to all, I prioritise those that raise immediate problems and urgent cases.\n\n\nCasework\n\nThere is a strict protocol that means Members of Parliament may only provide assistance to their own constituents. This means you must live in the constituency of Cardiff South and Penarth in order for me to assist.\nPlease therefore ensure that you have included:\n\n• Your full name\n\n• Your full postal address\n\n• Your contact telephone number\n\n• Any relevant reference numbers\n\n\nParliamentary Business\n\nIf your email relates to my work in Parliament, it will be dealt with as appropriate.\n\n\nMinisterial Business\n\nIf you are contacting me in my capacity as a Minister at the FCDO, you will need to redirect your email to to [email redacted]<[email redacted]>, or alternatively contact your own MP – you can find who that is at https://members.parliament.uk/FindYourMP\n\n\nThank you for taking the time to contact me.\n\nFor information about how we use your personal data, see our Privacy Notice<https://www.stephendoughty.wales/privacynotice>.\n\n\nYours sincerely,\n\n\nStephen\n\n\nStephen Doughty MP / AS\nMember of Parliament for Cardiff South and Penarth // Aelod Seneddol dros Dde Caerdydd a Phenarth\nHouse of Commons, Westminster, London, [postcode redacted]\nTel: [phone redacted] | Email: [email redacted]\n\n\nUK Parliament Disclaimer: this e-mail is confidential to the intended recipient. If you have received it in error, please notify the sender and delete it from your system. Any unauthorised use, disclosure, or copying is not permitted. This e-mail has been checked for viruses, but no liability is accepted for any damage caused by any virus transmitted by this e-mail. This e-mail address is not secure, is not encrypted and should not be used for sensitive data."},{"id":"gm-1a0e114a14151ccb","subject":"Referral to the Independent Complaints Reviewer — HM Land Registry, titles WA231076 / WA240304 (Llandough)","name":"Referral to the Independent Complaints Reviewer — HM Land Registry, titles WA231076 / WA240304 (Llandough)","date":"2026-09-27T04:17:00Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0e114a14151ccb.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0cab93bfe29b3d-ferral-to-the-independent-complaints-reviewer-hm-land-registry-titles-wa231076-wa240304-llandough","scopeTopics":["hmlr-icr"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["hmlr-icr"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0cab93bfe29b3d","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"hmlr-icr","name":"HM Land Registry (Independent Complaints Reviewer)"}],"refs":["N610CGV"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0e114a14151ccb.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Independent Complaints Reviewer,\n\nComplaint about HM Land Registry — titles WA231076 and WA240304 (closed),\nland north of Leckwith Road, Llandough (Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr). HMLR\nreferences: N610CGV, 260919-6726246.\n\nI ask you to review how HM Land Registry handled my complaint. HMLR gave\nits final decision on 25 May 2026 that the title was \"registered\ncorrectly\". I understand you cannot review that registration decision\nitself. My complaint is about the way it was reached and handled:\n\n1. The decision came before the file was examined. HMLR gave its final\ndecision on 25 May 2026, but did not list what the first-registration files\nactually contain until 30 July 2026. A final complaint decision cannot\nproperly rest on documents that had not yet been identified.\n\n2. The decision relies on a document that does not appear on the file.\nHMLR's letter of 19 April 2026 described a 15-year root of title. The root\nrelied on, a conveyance of 31 December 1969, was under 13 years old at\nfirst registration in 1982. Neither that conveyance nor the 1971 abstract\nsaid to prove it appears on the 30 July 2026 file list, and HMLR has not\nexplained this.\n\n3. HMLR has given three different dates for the closure of title WA240304:\n1983 (file correspondence of 12 April 1983), 1987 (an amalgamation\napplication of 2 February 1987, made while my family's appeal was pending)\nand 2005 (an email of 21 September 2026). It has not reconciled them.\n\n4. The stage 1 response relied on a form FR1 certificate, although the\napplications were on forms 1E and 1C.\n\n5. The file holds examination material (form D23 and a letter of 8 April\n1983) and two 1994 applications \"to correct the title\". HMLR has not\nexplained any of them.\n\n6. My written questions, including through my MP Stephen Doughty, have been\nredirected to a property search service instead of being answered.\n\nThe outcome I seek is a numbered written answer to these points,\nconfirmation that all first-registration, amalgamation and correction\nrecords for both titles are being preserved, and acknowledgement of any\nfailure in how the complaint was handled.\n\nI am separately taking advice on whether to apply to alter the register. I\nunderstand that is outside your remit.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\nRequester \ngreathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com"},{"id":"gm-1a0e1146d5796081","subject":"South Wales Constabulary records, November–December 1988 — Great House Farm, Llandough","name":"South Wales Constabulary records, November–December 1988 — Great House Farm, Llandough","date":"2026-09-27T04:16:47Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0e1146d5796081.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0cab95d9806099-south-wales-constabulary-records-november-december-1988-great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":["glamorgan"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["glamorgan"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0cab95d9806099","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"glamorgan","name":"Glamorgan Archives"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0e1146d5796081.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Glamorgan Archives Team,\n\nI am researching the eviction and demolition at Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr),\nLlandough, between 29 November and 6 December 1988. South Wales\nConstabulary officers were present. I understand from the South Wales\nPolice Heritage Centre that Glamorgan Archives holds records of South Wales\nPolice and its predecessor forces.\n\nCould you tell me whether you hold any of the following for late 1988, and\ntheir references and access conditions:\n\n1. Occurrence books, station or duty records for the division covering\nLlandough (Penarth, Barry or Cardiff).\n2. Operational orders or files for police attendance at evictions,\nsheriff's or bailiffs' enforcement, or breach-of-the-peace deployments.\n3. Press office or public relations files for November–December 1988.\n4. Any deposit list or transfer schedule for South Wales Constabulary\nrecords covering 1985–1990, so I can see what was and was not deposited.\n\nI also hold references ACA/8/2 (your ref 2026–3941) and 2026/3893 from\nearlier enquiries, if that helps connect this to my existing file.\n\nIf some records are closed, please tell me the closure period and whether I\ncan apply for access to the parts that concern my family.\n\nDiolch,\n\nRequester \ngreathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com"},{"id":"gm-1a0e1142c3b0174a","subject":"Your submission to LibAnswers was received.","name":"Your submission to LibAnswers was received.","date":"2026-09-27T04:16:30Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0e1142c3b0174a.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0e1142c3b0174a-your-submission-to-libanswers-was-received","scopeTopics":["nlw"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["nlw"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0e1142c3b0174a","direction":"received","from":"National Library of Wales","institutions":[{"id":"nlw","name":"National Library of Wales"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0e1142c3b0174a.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Hello Requester,\n\nYour question was submitted successfully to the National Library of Wales enquiries service / Cyflwynwyd eich cwestiwn yn llwyddiannus i Wasanaeth Ymholiadau Llyfrgell Genedlaethol Cymru\n\nDue to high demand and some resource challenges, there may be some delay in responding to enquiries at present. We apologise for any inconvenience this may cause and will provide an update as soon as possible.\n\nOherwydd lefel uchel y galw a rhai heriau adnoddau, bydd oedi gydag ymateb i ymholiadau ar hyn o bryd. Yr ydym yn ymddiheuro am unrhyw anghyfleuster a achosir gan hyn ac fe fyddwn yn darparu diweddariad cyn gynted ag y medrwn.\n\nWe will contact you as soon as possible. In the meantime, perhaps you can visit our FAQs <http://national-library-wales.libanswers.com>\nByddwn yn cysylltu â chi cyn gynted â phosibl! Yn y cyfamser, efallai yr hoffech chi chwlio ein  FAQs  <http://national-library-wales.libanswers.com> perthnasol gall ateb eich cwestiwn.\n\n\n\nWe welcome calls and correspondence in Welsh. You will receive a response in Welsh and correspondence in Welsh will not result in a delay./ Rydym yn croesawu galwadau a gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg. Byddwch yn derbyn ymateb yn Gymraeg ac ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi.\n------------------\n\nCwestiwn gwreiddiol\n\nSep 27 2026, 05:15AM via Email\nResearch enquiry — Bute Estate records GB 0210 BUTE / D153 / D219: Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough-juxta-Penarth — 1877 split and 1916 tenancy\nEnquiries Service\nThe National Library of Wales\n\nI am researching the title history of Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough-juxta-Penarth, Glamorgan, adjoining St Dochdwy’s churchyard (approx. ST 167 733).\n\nGlamorgan Archives (Louise Hunt, ref. 2026/4097, 24 September 2026) has confirmed that the following references are yours, not theirs, and has directed me to you:\n\n1. D153 — Deeds relating to premises in p. Llandough, co. Glam., comprising a dwelling house adjoining the churchyard, 1811 (D153/1–2), and land (D153 onwards, including items sometimes cited as D153/4–8).\n2. D219 — Llandough manorial leases and agreements.\n3. The main Bute Estate deposit, GB 0210 BUTE (deeds to 1937; Glamorgan / Cardiff rentals).\n\nI do not ask you to adjudicate title. I ask only whether the following instruments exist in your holdings, and for catalogue references, dates, parties, acreages, and whether a plan is attached.\n\nA. The 1877 division\nDoes the Bute deposit contain a conveyance, agreement, or other instrument of about 1876–1877 by which the Marquess of Bute (or trustees) conveyed or contracted to convey the farmhouse, buildings and about 10 acres adjoining the churchyard at Llandough to Daniel Thomas (or Thomas and another), while retaining about 9 acres of agricultural land to the east?\n\nB. The 1916 agricultural tenancy\nDoes the deposit contain a yearly agricultural tenancy, or counterpart, of 1916 from the Marquess of Bute to John Williams of Great House Farm, Llandough?\nIf yes:\n- does the parcels clause describe the whole farm, or only the land Bute still owned after 1877?\n- is there a plan?\n- what acreage is stated?\n\nC. Rentals 1877–1936 for this farm\nDo Glamorgan / Cardiff / Llandough rentals in GB 0210 BUTE distinguish, after c.1877, between:\n- a house/buildings holding (Thomas / later Williams), and\n- a residual Bute field holding later passed to Western Ground Rents in 1938?\n\nD. Anything that would show the two holdings were treated as one unit again before 1938.\n\nPurpose. HM Land Registry first-registered titles WA231076 (30 November 1982) and WA240304 (23 February 1983; later amalgamated). The 1982/83 files treat the land as one farm. The family case is that Parcel A (house and about 10 acres) had already left Bute in 1877 and was not the land let in 1916, so the 1982 merged title cannot be the same unit as the 1916 tenancy. Your deeds and rentals are the estate-side evidence that can confirm or deny that.\n\nI live in Patagonia and cannot visit Aberystwyth in person in the short term. I would be grateful for:\n- catalogue references and a short description of any matching items;\n- whether copies can be supplied, and the cost;\n- if the 1916 tenancy is not in GB 0210 BUTE, any note of where Bute tenancy counterparts after 1910 were deposited.\n\nThis is a new enquiry. It is not a repeat of FOI/2026/11. Glamorgan Archives hold the 1938–44 Western Ground Rents plan/rental book (D696) and have already answered on their own Bute material.\n\nRequester Requester\n[email redacted] <[email redacted]>\n[phone redacted]\n\nPublic chronology and parcel statement:\nhttps://greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com/the-two-parcels/\nhttps://greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com/1916-tenancy/\nhttps://greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com/land-registry-titles/\n\n------------------\n\n\n\n\nThis email is sent from LibAnswers in relationship to ticket #9816877.\n\nRead our privacy policy. <https://springshare.com/privacy.html>"},{"id":"gm-1a0e1141d1bbf552","subject":"RE: FOI - 2026-032 - Great House Farm, Llandough","name":"RE: FOI - 2026-032 - Great House Farm, Llandough","date":"2026-09-27T04:16:26Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0e1141d1bbf552.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0c8b62d56ff57c-foi-2026-032-great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":["museum"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["museum"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0c8b62d56ff57c","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"museum","name":"Amgueddfa Cymru - Museum Wales"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0e1141d1bbf552.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear FOI Team,\n\nFOI 2026-032\n\nDiolch yn fawr. Thank you for the records, the descriptors and the\npublished references. This is a thorough response and it is genuinely\nuseful.\n\nOne correction of place is required.\n\nThe request concerns Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough near Penarth /\nCardiff. Among the disclosed PAS material is at least one record for a\ndifferent property of the same name: Great House Farm, Llandough near\nCowbridge (including 2022.18.1, NGR SS9934672805, medieval lead Romanesque\nmount). There is also a Great House farm entry at Llandow. Those are not\nthe site named in the request. Two Llandoughs, and more than one Great\nHouse Farm, sit in the same county. The homonym is easy to miss and\nmaterial.\n\nPlease treat the Cowbridge / Llandow entries as out of scope for this\nrequest unless you hold recorded information showing they were, at any\ntime, indexed, cited, or treated as if they related to Great House Farm,\nLlandough (Penarth / Cardiff). If any such cross-attribution exists in\nhistoric files, catalogues, correspondence, or search notes, that recorded\ninformation is within scope and I ask that it be disclosed.\n\nIf labels or findspot fields are now amended to distinguish the two farms,\nplease do not overwrite the original wording without retaining the prior\nform. I ask that any correction be recorded as a dated amendment, with the\nearlier descriptor preserved, so the sequence of how the records stood when\nsearches were first run remains inspectable. Please do not destroy, weed,\nor replace the versions as disclosed on 22 September 2026.\n\nOne small point from my email of 26 August also remains. As item 7 I asked\nfor any recorded information showing the basis on which the Museum\nconcluded that the identified records were unrelated to the reported\nnineteenth-century discovery. If that conclusion was reached by staff\njudgement and not written down, please simply confirm that no recorded\ninformation is held on that point. I am not asking for a new opinion.\n\nCould you also confirm the date by which you expect to respond to FOI\n2026-036?\n\nCofion,\n\nRequester\ngreathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com"},{"id":"gm-1a0e11391052951a","subject":"Formal complaint — GGAT / Heneb: Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough — HER identifiers and the 1978–1995 record","name":"Formal complaint — GGAT / Heneb: Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough — HER identifiers and the 1978–1995 record","date":"2026-09-27T04:15:50Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0e11391052951a.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0ceb50ec8ec412-formal-complaint-ggat-heneb-great-house-farm-t-mawr-llandough-her-identifiers-and-the-1978-1995-record","scopeTopics":["heneb"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["heneb"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0ceb50ec8ec412","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"heneb","name":"Heneb (formerly GGAT) / Historic Environment Record"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0e11391052951a.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Chief Executive / Complaints Officer,\n\nThis is a formal complaint to Heneb: The Trust for Welsh Archaeology, as\noperational successor to the Glamorgan-Gwent Archaeological Trust, and to\nGlamorgan-Gwent Archaeological Trust Limited (The), company 01276976, which\nremains an active company with its registered office at Heneb's address.\n\nIt concerns Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr, Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan (near\nPenarth / Cardiff); ST 1681 7331; NPRN 20182. Cadw and RCAHMW have supplied\nthree unreconciled HER references for the same site: GGAT02038s, GGAT03586s\nand GGATE003872.\n\nThis is not a chaser of my records request of 8 September 2026 or the\nschedule of 19 September (ref GHF/HENEB/2026-09-20). That request remains\nopen and is due a response by 6 October. Please treat this complaint as a\nseparate file and give it a complaint reference.\n\nWhat this is not. It is not an allegation that GGAT demolished the house,\nadvised that it be demolished, or decided that it should not be listed. It\nis not an allegation of dishonesty, bad faith or criminal conduct. It is\nnot a title claim and I do not ask you to adjudicate BP v Buckler. GGAT's\nletters of 13 and 15 December 1993, recommending a Breach of Condition\nNotice when Condition 18 had not been met, were the Trust doing its\ncuratorial job. That is recorded here so it is not lost in what follows.\n\nThe charge, put narrowly. GGAT was the public curator of the regional\nrecord; the adviser who told the planning authority it could not determine\n89/01396/OUT without an archaeological assessment; the body which, in the\nsame document, specified machine-trench evaluation, quoted £2,000 and two\nweeks, and recorded that it was already negotiating that work with BP\nProperties; the contractor who then executed that evaluation on a\nchurchside monastic site its own 1978–80 excavations and its own 1982\nletter had already flagged; and the author of a zoning map which treated as\nempty ground in which, four years later, inhumations were found a few\ncentimetres below the topsoil. Disclosure of the dual role was not\nmanagement of it. The public record still does not reliably carry what was\nfound. Heneb's entire substantive reply since May is one sentence about\ncorporate form.\n\nI enclose GGAT — Forensic Critique, Version 4.0.0 (21 September 2026). It\ndistinguishes established fact, reasonable inference, what cannot be\nestablished, and facts that cut against the criticism. Where earlier\nversions were wrong, the corrections are printed and kept. The critique is\nthe sourcing. This letter is the complaint. Please answer the letter. Treat\nthe critique as the schedule of sources, not as a substitute for numbered\nanswers.\n\n1. I have not seen the HER\n\nI do not assert the wording of GGAT02038s, because I have not been allowed\nto read it. Cadw has said the content of the HER is managed by Heneb.\nRCAHMW and Cadw have given three different identifiers for the same site.\nTests of Archwilio on 20 September 2026 did not return an error for\nunresolved records. They returned a different record, from a different\ntrust, for a different site, with no warning:\n\nGGAT02038s → GAT46242 (Gwynedd desk-based assessment)\nGGAT03586s → GAT92911 (farmstead, Warwick Place, Gwynedd)\nGGAT02038s, alternative path, three attempts → E001657 each time\n\nA control query for an indexed record returned correctly. Please test those\nthree queries, correct the substitution if confirmed, and tell me the\noutcome. Then permit inspection of the source file and supply an index of\nevery linked site and event record with its correct reference.\n\n2. \"Partially recorded\" is RCAHMW's phrase, not a finding I have verified\non the HER\n\nOn 10 June 2026 Dr Adam Coward quoted NMR 6048945: \"The house remained\npartially recorded because of problems created by an ownership dispute. The\nhouse and farm buildings were suddenly and completely destroyed by B. P.\nProperties Ltd. on 6th December 1988 amid considerable local controversy.\"\n\nThat is the Royal Commission's file. I hold H. J. Thomas's measured\nground-floor plan of 26 April 1974, at 1:100, identifying the principal\nrooms including the Dairy and recording the beams. GGAT reproduced that\nplan as Figure 2 of its 1990 assessment, by kind permission. A measured\nground-floor survey that could not reach the first floor and roof is not,\nwithout more, a failed survey.\n\nPlease state, from records you hold:\n(a) whether the HER uses the phrase \"partially recorded\", and if so from\nwhich source;\n(b) what parts of the building the 1974 survey measured, and what it did\nnot;\n(c) what contemporaneous document, if any beyond NMR 6048945, establishes\nthat access was restricted by the ownership dispute, by whom, on what date,\nand to whom;\n(d) whether Trench 7 of 1990 (north-east corner / former dairy, stone\nfloor, mortared partition) was ever reconciled against the 1974 plan, and\nif the Trench 7 drawings, photographs and grid survive.\n\n3. What GGAT held in 1988, and what it did not do\n\nOn 1 October 1982 GGAT told the Vale that occupation might continue north\nof the church and that thorough investigative work should precede\ndevelopment. Between that letter and November 1988 no GGAT act concerning\nthis site has been identified. RCAHMW added to its 1974 plan on 12 December\n1988, six days after the demolition. There is no equivalent GGAT event. The\n1990 report notes architectural features \"observed in the rubble\" and then\nbegins GGAT's own account with the words \"Following the demolition… GGAT\nentered into discussion with Debenham Tewson\".\n\nPlease state what the SMR entry contained immediately before December 1988;\nwhether the 1982 advice was on it; whether any emergency building record,\nwatching brief on the demolition or the rubble, or post-demolition fabric\nreport exists; and where the carved stone and other architectural material\nlater referred to now is.\n\n4. \"County treasure\"\n\nGGAT 1989 §2.3: \"At present our information on the Great House Farm area is\nlimited to the records of the recently demolished property which was listed\nas a 'county treasure'.\" The phrase conferred no statutory protection.\nIdentify the list or card, the date, the criteria, and what information\nabout the building accompanied it. If you do not hold that document, say so.\n\n5. Specify, price, bid, execute\n\nIn one 1989 consultee report GGAT advised that 89/01396/OUT should not be\ndetermined without an assessment; specified machine-excavator trial\ntrenching; quoted two weeks at £2,000; and recorded that negotiations with\nBP Properties via Debenham Tewson were already under way. BP then\ncommissioned GGAT. The 1990 report disclosed a \"dual role\" and invited\neither party to go elsewhere. Disclosure is recorded. It is not the same as\nmanagement.\n\nThe IFA Code of approved practice for contractual arrangements was adopted\non 17 September 1990, after the assessment was delivered. It does not bind\nthat commission. The Welsh Archaeological Trusts' Code of Practice dates\nfrom November 1997 and is not applied retrospectively. What did bind the\n1989–90 arrangement, as contemporaneous standards rather than as a pleaded\ncause of action: the IFA Code of Conduct in force from June 1985; the BADLG\nCode, which GGAT expressly endorsed in the 1989 report; section 13 of the\nSupply of Goods and Services Act 1982 as the implied standard of care\nbetween GGAT and BP (the family was not a party); and the fiduciary duty of\ncharity trustees to identify and manage conflicts, not merely to notify two\ncommercial counterparties.\n\nOn 18 May 1994 GGAT's Curatorial Section wrote, of another contractor: \"An\narchaeological contractor should never be in the position of writing his\nown Brief as well as carrying out the work.\" That principle was not met by\nGGAT's own 1989–90 arrangement.\n\nPlease identify who wrote the 1989 recommendation, who accepted the\ncommission, whether those functions were separated, whether trustees or\nCadw were told, and who wrote the 1990 brief. If you hold Appendix \"B\" to\nthe officer's report (GGAT's further comments of 8 February 1990), Appendix\n\"D\" (the representation from a County Councillor identified as a member of\nGGAT), or Appendix \"E\" (the applicant's agents' written response to GGAT's\nreport), produce them. None of those three is in the planning file released\nin 2026. Appendix \"A\" is. I do not assert that GGAT removed them. I ask\nwhether copies survive in GGAT/Heneb files.\n\n6. The 1990 method against GGAT's own model\n\nGGAT had excavated an early-medieval cemetery, a Roman villa and a\nTewkesbury Abbey grange immediately south of the same church, on the same\nalkaline Liassic geology, and had written that occupation might continue\nnorth of it. It chose eight machine-cut trenches, some placed \"at random\",\nand then rated uninvestigated eastern ground \"non existent to low\"\npotential. That rating converted a logistical limit into an empirical\nclaim. Cotswold's December 1994 preliminary report recorded that burials\ncontinued into that zone and \"lay only a few centimetres below the topsoil\".\n\nI do not say a 1990 evaluation had to predict 1,026 burials. I ask whether\nthe method was capable of detecting shallow inhumations on ground where\nGGAT's own experience predicted excellent bone survival, and whether the\n1990 evaluation has ever been reviewed against the 1994 results. If it has\nnot, will Heneb review it now.\n\n7. Human remains and the 1981 Act\n\nGGAT identified the Disused Burial Grounds (Amendment) Act 1981 and found\ndisarticulated human bone by the churchyard wall. That Act turns on whether\nthe land is a burial ground. A colour-coded potential map cannot answer it.\nWhat legal advice was obtained, when, and from whom? What evidence set the\nburial-ground boundary? Was a review undertaken after 1994?\n\n8. Two burial figures, both correct\n\nCotswold's December 1994 preliminary report, §4.2: 858 inhumations and 152\ndisarticulated bone groups. Holbrook and Thomas, Medieval Archaeology 49\n(2005): 1,026 burials comprising 814 articulated inhumations and 212\ndisarticulated bone groups, of which 801 skeletons received full\nosteological analysis; \"by far the largest Early-medieval burial population\nso far recovered from Wales\". They are the same excavation counted twice.\nPlease confirm which figure the HER now carries, and that both stages are\nidentified as such.\n\n9. The c.1880 ironwork sentence, and what Amgueddfa Cymru now holds\n\nGGAT 1990: \"it is likely that some medieval ironwork which is held by the\nNational Museum of Wales was discovered under the rear wing c 1880 (RCAHMW\n1988).\" By 2005 that hedged sentence had become flat assertion in print.\n\nAmgueddfa Cymru's disclosures of 25 August 2026 (FOI 2026-024 and internal\nreview 22 September 2026) and 22 September 2026 (FOI 2026-032) establish\nthe following, so far as the Museum holds recorded information:\n\n(a) No accession, donation, transfer or deposit answering a visor, lance,\nhelmet, armour, soldier, horse, or nineteenth-century discovery under the\nfarmhouse floor.\n(b) Accession 82.44H is the 1979 GGAT excavation assemblage. Collected by\nGlamorgan-Gwent Archaeological Trust Ltd, 1979; donated 14 June 1982 by\nWales & West Housing Association Ltd; bibliographic reference Owen-John\n1988. The collections database locality field is \"Llandough Roman Villa\",\nNGR ST 168 733 — not \"Great House Farm\".\n(c) Accession 95.56H is the 1994 Cotswold Archaeological Trust cemetery\nassemblage. Collected by CAT, 1994; donated 28 July 1994 by Ideal Homes\nWales Ltd. Locality field: \"Great House Farm Llandough\", NGR ST 1681 7331.\nPeriod on the human-remains entries: Early Medieval (c.400–c.1070).\n(d) Accession 63.24 is objects stated to have been found at Church Farm,\nLlandough, in 1858. The Museum's explanatory note of 22 September 2026\nrecords that this is the only paperwork on that file, and that staff\nconsider it possible those objects are the basis of the later local story.\nThat is the Museum's observation, not a finding I ask you to adopt as fact.\n\nQualify or withdraw the 1880 sentence if you hold no source beyond the 1990\nwording. Carry 82.44H and 95.56H on the principal HER description, with\ncollector, donor, donation date, locality field as catalogued, and NGR. If\nthe HER still points a professional user first to a post-medieval dwelling,\nthat is a record defect against what both excavations produced and what the\nnational museum now catalogues.\n\nPlease also confirm whether GGAT retained copies of the 1980 donation\ncorrespondence with the National Museum (letters of 14 and 24 April 1980\nare on the public record) and of any transfer file for 82.44H.\n\n10. What the public record must now say\n\nIf GGAT02038s, or its successor identifier, still leads as a post-medieval\nfarm or dwelling, it does not satisfy the function of a working historic\nenvironment record, and it is difficult to reconcile with section 194(2)(j)\nof the Historic Environment (Wales) Act 2023. I have not seen the source\nfile, so I put that as a request for correction if the classification is as\ndescribed by Cadw officers, not as a finding I have verified on the face of\nthe record.\n\nA professional user needs, in the principal description and not only in a\nlater narrative paragraph: the 1974 measured survey; GGAT's 1978–80 work\nand 1982 advice; that the 1990 evaluation followed demolition and found\nhuman bone; that the 1994 excavation recovered the assemblage above,\nincluding in the 1990 low-potential zone; museum accessions 82.44H and\n95.56H with their catalogued localities; and a note that the c.1880 NMW\nironwork attribution is uncorroborated by the institution said to hold the\nobject.\n\nPlease also reconcile the NGRs in use for the farmhouse, the 1990 trenches,\nthe 1994 excavation, the churchyard and the development parcels.\nDistinguish this site from Great House Farm, Llandough near Cowbridge, and\nfrom Llandow. Two Llandoughs sit in the same historic county. Homonym error\nis a live records risk.\n\nWhat I ask now\n\nA. A complaint reference, the name of the person who will determine it, the\nprocedure you will apply, the timescale, and any independent review route.\nPreserve all surviving Great House Farm records pending that determination.\n\nB. Confirm which legal entity holds the records and functions formerly of\nGGAT Ltd, and who handles HER corrections.\n\nC. Answer sections 1 to 10 from the documents you hold. Where a document\ndoes not survive, say when it left the file, under what retention rule, and\nby whose authority.\n\nD. Correct the HER so that the 1979 and 1994 investigations and accessions\n82.44H and 95.56H sit in the principal description, the 1880 attribution is\nqualified, the 1990 low-potential zone is annotated against the 1994\nresult, the three identifiers are reconciled, and Archwilio does not\nsilently substitute another trust's site.\n\nE. Test and, if confirmed, correct the Archwilio substitution.\n\nF. If you accept any part of this complaint, say what remedy you offer:\nrecord correction, release of surviving files or an index, a written\naccount of the 1989–90 dual-role arrangement, and a methodological note on\nthe 1990 evaluation against the 1994 excavation.\n\nI do not ask you to adjudicate title. I do ask you to account for what GGAT\nknew, what it recorded, what it advised, what it then sold and executed,\nwhat was found four years later, what the national museum now catalogues\nfrom GGAT's own 1979 work, and whether the public record now tells the\ntruth of that sequence.\n\nThis letter is sent so that Heneb and GGAT Ltd have a fair opportunity to\ninvestigate, answer, and offer remedy before any further process. Please\nanswer each numbered section. A general assurance that the organisation\nacted appropriately will not do.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester \n\n[email redacted]\n\nPlease visit\ngreathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com\n\nEnc: GGAT — Forensic Critique, Version 4.0.0, 21 September 2026\n(to be attached before sending)"},{"id":"gm-1a0e11318e426166","subject":"Automatic reply: Great House Farm, Llandough","name":"Automatic reply: Great House Farm, Llandough","date":"2026-09-27T04:15:17Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0e11318e426166.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0e110bed09b687-automatic-reply-great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":["heneb"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["heneb"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0e110bed09b687","direction":"received","from":"Barbara Rees","institutions":[{"id":"heneb","name":"Heneb (formerly GGAT) / Historic Environment Record"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0e11318e426166.json","textStatus":"available","text":"My next working day is Wednesday, 30th September.\n\nDiolch."},{"id":"gm-1a0e112fa9acda07","subject":"Research enquiry — Bute Estate records GB 0210 BUTE / D153 / D219: Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough-juxta-Penarth — 1877 split and 1916 tenancy","name":"Research enquiry — Bute Estate records GB 0210 BUTE / D153 / D219: Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough-juxta-Penarth — 1877 split and 1916 tenancy","date":"2026-09-27T04:15:12Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0e112fa9acda07.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0d4d42318ac190-search-enquiry-bute-estate-records-gb-0210-bute-d153-d219-great-house-farm-t-mawr-llandough-juxta-penarth-1877-split-and","scopeTopics":["nlw"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["nlw"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0d4d42318ac190","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"nlw","name":"National Library of Wales"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0e112fa9acda07.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Enquiries Service\nThe National Library of Wales\n\nI am researching the title history of Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr),\nLlandough-juxta-Penarth, Glamorgan, adjoining St Dochdwy’s churchyard\n(approx. ST 167 733).\n\nGlamorgan Archives (Louise Hunt, ref. 2026/4097, 24 September 2026) has\nconfirmed that the following references are yours, not theirs, and has\ndirected me to you:\n\n1. D153 — Deeds relating to premises in p. Llandough, co. Glam., comprising\na dwelling house adjoining the churchyard, 1811 (D153/1–2), and land (D153\nonwards, including items sometimes cited as D153/4–8).\n2. D219 — Llandough manorial leases and agreements.\n3. The main Bute Estate deposit, GB 0210 BUTE (deeds to 1937; Glamorgan /\nCardiff rentals).\n\nI do not ask you to adjudicate title. I ask only whether the following\ninstruments exist in your holdings, and for catalogue references, dates,\nparties, acreages, and whether a plan is attached.\n\nA. The 1877 division\nDoes the Bute deposit contain a conveyance, agreement, or other instrument\nof about 1876–1877 by which the Marquess of Bute (or trustees) conveyed or\ncontracted to convey the farmhouse, buildings and about 10 acres adjoining\nthe churchyard at Llandough to Daniel Thomas (or Thomas and another), while\nretaining about 9 acres of agricultural land to the east?\n\nB. The 1916 agricultural tenancy\nDoes the deposit contain a yearly agricultural tenancy, or counterpart, of\n1916 from the Marquess of Bute to John Williams of Great House Farm,\nLlandough?\nIf yes:\n- does the parcels clause describe the whole farm, or only the land Bute\nstill owned after 1877?\n- is there a plan?\n- what acreage is stated?\n\nC. Rentals 1877–1936 for this farm\nDo Glamorgan / Cardiff / Llandough rentals in GB 0210 BUTE distinguish,\nafter c.1877, between:\n- a house/buildings holding (Thomas / later Williams), and\n- a residual Bute field holding later passed to Western Ground Rents in\n1938?\n\nD. Anything that would show the two holdings were treated as one unit again\nbefore 1938.\n\nPurpose. HM Land Registry first-registered titles WA231076 (30 November\n1982) and WA240304 (23 February 1983; later amalgamated). The 1982/83 files\ntreat the land as one farm. The family case is that Parcel A (house and\nabout 10 acres) had already left Bute in 1877 and was not the land let in\n1916, so the 1982 merged title cannot be the same unit as the 1916 tenancy.\nYour deeds and rentals are the estate-side evidence that can confirm or\ndeny that.\n\nI live in Patagonia and cannot visit Aberystwyth in person in the short\nterm. I would be grateful for:\n- catalogue references and a short description of any matching items;\n- whether copies can be supplied, and the cost;\n- if the 1916 tenancy is not in GB 0210 BUTE, any note of where Bute\ntenancy counterparts after 1910 were deposited.\n\nThis is a new enquiry. It is not a repeat of FOI/2026/11. Glamorgan\nArchives hold the 1938–44 Western Ground Rents plan/rental book (D696) and\nhave already answered on their own Bute material.\n\nRequester Requester\n[email redacted]\n[phone redacted]\n\nPublic chronology and parcel statement:\nhttps://greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com/the-two-parcels/\nhttps://greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com/1916-tenancy/\nhttps://greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com/land-registry-titles/"},{"id":"gm-1a0e112511993a85","subject":"Re: Epexio contact us form submission","name":"Re: Epexio contact us form submission","date":"2026-09-27T04:14:28Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0e112511993a85.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a07b3bf0b65e56c-epexio-contact-us-form-submission","scopeTopics":["glamorgan"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["glamorgan"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a07b3bf0b65e56c","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"glamorgan","name":"Glamorgan Archives"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0e112511993a85.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Louise,\n\nThank you again for your very detailed response under reference 2026/4097.\n\nYou have been extremely helpful throughout this research, and I am very\nconscious of that. I do travel back and forth to the UK and will visit\nLeckwith in person, where I look forward to meeting you and the team and\nthanking you personally.\n\nI wanted to reply because I think I can now explain more precisely what I\nam trying to establish from the records.\n\nI am trying to reconstruct what happened historically which led my late\ngrandmother and father, over many decades, to continue asserting that our\nfamily owned the land at Great House Farm, while that position was\nrepeatedly contested and ultimately possession was awarded to BP in *BP\nProperties Ltd v Buckler*.\n\nWhat has caused me to revisit the documentary history is that the later\nposition is not as straightforward as I had previously assumed. Land\nRegistry has now told me that its position is not that it determined that\nmy family did not own the land; but it insists that no registration mistake\nhas been identified, which is what the MP forwarded me (they're fobbing him\noff too).\n\nLand registry will wake up to this soon I'm sure. Or I'm wrong and I need\nto wake up. But I am a military warfare professional, and while I make\nmistakes, or AI makes mistakes on my behalf, I have no issue holding my\nhands up. I'm just trying to understand what happened to us and our home\nand our land as children in the 80's, and why it all happened.\n\nAt the same time, I have so far found no convincing documentary evidence of\nmy family paying rent for the particular parcel which we occupied and\nclaimed as our own after the quarrying arrangement appears to have come to\nan end.\n\nI have therefore had one of my AI agents published my current\nreconstruction, including the documentary sources behind it, here:\n\nGreat House Farm research <https://reference-url-citation.invalid/3>\n\nI am not asking you to accept that reconstruction. It's clearly hit and\nmiss as an early technology. I am asking for your help in testing it\nagainst the records held by Glamorgan Archives.\nThe central question\n\nMy present understanding is that what had originally been treated as one\nagricultural holding can, when the surviving documents are examined\nchronologically, be distinguished into two areas.\n\nFor simplicity, I have called them *Parcel A* and *Parcel B*:\n\n   - *Parcel A* — the farmhouse, house plot, garden and associated land\n   north/west of St Dochdwy's Church, occupied by my family; and\n   - *Parcel B* — the agricultural land to the east which was subject to\n   the agricultural tenancy and subsequently associated with Western Ground\n   Rents.\n\nI appreciate that “Parcel A” and “Parcel B” are my labels, not historical\ndescriptions appearing in the documents. But they aren't entirely just my\nlabels, parcel B is clearly what is in the 1939 map, which you hold, which\nthe council clerk for Llandough directed me to earlier this year, on his\nday of retirement.\n\nAlso in 1983 two parcels were merged at the land registry, by BP. Which\nconfirms two parcels. I have purchased all those records avaliable and the\nmaps do confirm what I suspect.\n\nThe important question is whether the *underlying documents themselves\nsupport the physical distinction*.\n\nMy working reconstruction is that the 1877 Daniel Thomas/Williams\narrangements ultimately resulted in my family acquiring the freehold\ninterest in the land around the farmhouse when the quarrying arrangement\ncame to an end, with the last machinery leaving in 1928.\n\nIf that is correct, it would explain something which otherwise appears very\ndifficult to reconcile.\n\nThe 1916 agricultural tenancy may have covered both parcels of the farm as\na whole at that time. But if part of that holding subsequently ceased to be\npart of the agricultural tenancy because of my family's interest in the\nparcel A crystallising into our freehold of parcel A, then the 1938 Western\nGround Rents acquisition and the *1939 revised agricultural tenancy* would\nnot necessarily concern our farmhouse land. Despite decades of it being\ntreated as though it did and our occupancy being allegedly permissive,\nwhich is a narrative forced upon us to believe, when it most likely wasn't\nthe case. And why my nan rejected the premis and my dad too. Until their\ndying breaths. I inherit this interest in this land parcel A as heir and\nsuccessor.\n\nThat is why the 1939 plan was so important and helpful.\nThe point I would particularly like you to confirm\n\n*When you look at the actual 1939 Western Ground Rents revised tenancy and\nits plan, I presume you see the same thing I see?*\n\nMy reading is that the plan depicts *Parcel B, the eastern agricultural\nland, but does not depict or include Parcel A containing the farmhouse and\nthe land immediately associated with it.*\n\nIf that is what the plan shows, I would be very grateful if you could\nsimply confirm that.\n\nIf I am wrong, I would equally value you telling me that I am wrong and,\nideally, pointing me to the feature of the plan or accompanying document\nwhich demonstrates that.\n\nI am not asking you to determine what that means in law. Only whether *we\nare looking at the same physical information in the same historical\ndocument*.\n\nThat distinction is crucial because I am increasingly concerned that the\nlater history may have proceeded on the assumption that there was only one\nundivided parcel, when the contemporary records may actually distinguish\ntwo different interests.\nI would like to do the same exercise with the other records\n\nYour response itself says:\n\n“With all historical research of this nature, it is necessary to piece\ntogether information from surviving records that have been deposited with\nus.”\n\nThat is precisely what I am now trying to do.\n\nI understand the scientific process demands you look at the evidence and\ntell what it tells. But I've done that, I'm making my case. And I'm\nactually drawing the conclusion South Wales might be hexed under a spell\nnot seeing what I see. We also have hallucination with AI which is why I\nask for this specific clarification of the evidence you hold against my\ninterpretation of it.\n\nBecause in one hand I have the narrative, from court documents and news\narticles saying we were squatters. In the other hand I have credible\nevidence we had no issue relinquishing their parcel. The problem was all\nwith us on our parcel. And Western Ground Rents and BP don't appear to ever\nhad our parcel to be able to assert we were on it permissively. And the\n1982 registration of their parcel in their name, is paradoxically based on\nan alledged no contest our  occupancy was permissive. When its all we've\ndone since the 40's is challenge it. Almost a century.\n\nI would therefore be grateful if, where reasonably possible, you could look\nat the relevant surviving records and tell me whether the documentary\nevidence supports, contradicts, or simply does not establish the following:\n\n*1. 1916*\n\nI think the 1916 agricultural tenancy appears to encompass the whole farm\nas it then stood. Weather it was 2 seperate parcels then I don't know.\n\n*2. 1877 / 1928*\n\nDo the surviving Thomas/Williams/quarrying records support or contradict\nthe proposition that the farmhouse parcel was treated differently from the\neastern agricultural land, and that the relevant quarrying arrangement had\nan effect on the family's interest in that land by 1928?\n\n*3. 1938*\n\nDoes the Western Ground Rents conveyance or associated material show that\nWGR acquired the farmhouse/house plot itself, or does it appear to concern\nonly the agricultural interest which subsequently appears in the 1939\ntenancy?\n\n*4. 1939*\n\nDoes the revised WGR tenancy plan contain only the eastern agricultural\nparcel, excluding the farmhouse and its immediate land?\n\nThis is the point on which I would particularly appreciate a direct\nconfirmation or correction.\n\n*5. 1955 / 1962 / 1974*\n\nDo the surviving records contain any express documentary act which actually\ntransferred, extinguished or otherwise conclusively disposed of the\nfamily's claimed interest in the farmhouse parcel?\n\nI am deliberately not asking you to determine the legal effect of those\nevents. I am asking whether such an express documentary act appears in the\nrecords.\n\n*6. Later title history*\n\nDo the surviving Glamorgan Archives records contain documentary evidence\nshowing how the farmhouse parcel itself passed from the earlier family\ninterest into Western Ground Rents, BP or another owner before the 1982/83\nregistration?\n\nOr is that documentary chain simply not present in the surviving archive?\nI would genuinely welcome correction\n\nI want to emphasise this because I do not want this correspondence to\nbecome an exercise in asking the Archive to validate my theory.\n\nIf I have misunderstood the records, *please tell me plainly*.\n\nIf the records support what I am seeing, please tell me that plainly too.\n\nAnd if the surviving material cannot establish the point either way, that\nis also a useful and perfectly acceptable answer.\n\nSomething as simple as:\n\n*Supported by the surviving record*\n*Not supported / contradicted by the surviving record*\n*Cannot be determined from the surviving record*\n\nwould be enormously helpful to me, together with the relevant reference\nwhere possible.\n\nI am particularly keen to establish this because the research has already\ndemonstrated the value of going back to the underlying documents rather\nthan relying upon later descriptions of them. The 1877 material is a good\nexample: what initially appeared to be an inconsistency has been capable of\nbeing reconsidered once the underlying research and records were examined.\n\nThat has made me realise that I need to make sure that I am not making the\nsame mistake in reverse.\n\nI do not want to build an elaborate theory around a document which I have\nsimply misunderstood.\nI am not asking the Archive to become a party to the dispute\n\nI completely understand the distinction between an archive holding a record\nand a court or Land Registry determining legal title.\n\nI am not asking Glamorgan Archives to adjudicate the dispute, give me legal\nadvice, determine ownership, or take sides against any present landowner or\npublic authority.\n\nI am asking for something considerably narrower:\n\n*I want to know whether the historical documentary evidence held by the\nArchive actually shows what I think it shows.*\n\nThat seems to me to be a legitimate research question for an archive\nservice, particularly where the answer can be expressed simply by reference\nto what is physically depicted or expressly recorded in the documents.\n\nI also appreciate that the statutory FOI duty itself does not necessarily\nrequire an archive to create a new interpretation of information merely\nbecause a requester asks for one. I am not trying to turn this into such a\ndemand. I am asking for professional assistance and dialogue in\nunderstanding the historical material, which seems consistent with\nGlamorgan Archives' own published access principles concerning open two-way\ncommunication, active participation by users and responsiveness to the\ncommunity it serves. The wider Archive Service Accreditation framework\nlikewise recognises access and engagement with communities as a core part\nof the function of an archive service.\n\nI think that distinction is important.\nWhy this matters to me\n\nI am trying to establish what actually happened to my late grandmother and\nfather's claimed ownership of their home and land.\n\nMy family maintained that claim for decades. The claim was contested. The\nmatter eventually resulted in the loss of possession and the subsequent\ndemolition of the farmhouse.\n\nI completed 22 years of military service in 2024, leaving my career with\nhonour. I am now approaching this as a researcher trying to establish the\ndocumentary truth of a family and community history.\n\nI am not asking anyone to believe my family merely because it is our family\nhistory.\n\nQuite the opposite.\n\n*I want the records to decide what can and cannot properly be said.*\n\nThat is why I would value an open exchange with the people who hold those\nrecords.\n\nIf you look at the 1939 plan and say, “No, Requester, I don't think it excludes\nthe farmhouse parcel, and this is why,” I would consider that extremely\nuseful.\n\nIf you say, “Yes, I can see exactly what you are seeing — the plan depicts\nthe eastern agricultural land and does not include the farmhouse parcel,”\nthat is equally important.\n\nAnd if the surviving records simply do not allow the point to be\ndetermined, I would rather have that stated clearly than have either of us\ninfer something which the evidence cannot sustain.\n\nI think that is the most constructive way for us to proceed.\n\nThere is a shared interest here in getting the history right. These are\nrecords of the community in which we all live, and I think their greatest\nvalue is realised when the surviving evidence can be examined openly,\ncritically and collaboratively.\n\nI would therefore be very grateful if you could engage with the question at\nthat documentary level and tell me, as plainly as possible, *whether you\nsee what I see*.\n\nThank you again for all your assistance so far. I genuinely appreciate it.\n\nKind regards,\n\nRequester \n\n\ngreathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nOn Thu, 24 Sept 2026, 16:56 Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg, <\n[email redacted]> wrote:\n\n> *Ref: 2026/4097*\n>\n>\n>\n> Dear Requester,\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you for your email received into our enquiries system 8th\n> September.  Please find below answers to your questions in turn.  Please\n> note that prices quoted are without VAT based on the fact that you are in\n> Patagonia.\n>\n>\n>\n> **26 May 2026 (2026/2370)**\n>\n> 1. 1974 demolition petition, Llandough juxta Penarth — ref or not held.\n>\n> There is not a petition held within our collection. You would need to know\n> who the petition was submitted to in order to search for any further\n> reference to it.  If it was submitted to the planning authority there may\n> be mention in the Planning Committee minutes and reports – see point 6\n> below.\n>\n>\n>\n> **29 May 2026 FOIA/EIR (2026/2372)**\n>\n>\n>\n> 2. Williams/Buckler as owners, tenants, or reporters of the 1880 floor\n> discovery.\n>\n> One source of information about ownership would be rate books. UC/73/1 is\n> a rate book for 1882 – this is the nearest we have to 1880.  A search of\n> this (and later volumes UC/73/1-5 covers 1882-1902 and then UDPE/T/30 and\n> UDPE/T/32 cover dates after 1900) could be undertaken by staff on your\n> behalf at a charge of *£50 *which would include copies of relevant\n> entries.\n>\n>\n>\n> 3. Gaps / missing volumes / withdrawals 1950–1989.\n>\n> We have never had a full set of records that would show the full\n> tenancy/ownership history of Great House / Ty Mawr Farm. With all\n> historical research of this nature, it is necessary to piece together\n> information from surviving records that have been deposited with us.  There\n> are not any records that have been withdrawn from our collections that\n> would have provided additional information to this search for the period\n> 1950-1989.\n>\n>\n>\n> 4. Why the fabric was not scheduled before 1988 (or “Cadw holds this; we\n> hold nothing”).\n>\n> Not held in our collections – suggest contacting Cadw and the Royal\n> Commission on the Ancient and Historical Monuments of Wales.\n>\n>\n>\n> 5. Internal preservation assessments.\n>\n> As above - suggest contacting Cadw and the Royal Commission on the Ancient\n> and Historical Monuments of Wales.\n>\n>\n>\n> 6. Planning files for this acreage / demolition / Church View Close — file\n> refs, not class names.\n>\n> We do not hold planning files (it is possible some files may still be held\n> by the Vale of Glamorgan Council Planning Department), but we do hold\n> copies of the Planning Committee minutes and reports.  Mary Buckler started\n> looking at the Planning Committee minutes and reports on her last visit to\n> the searchroom on 13th August.  The document references to start with for\n> these would be DCVG/C/4/36/76-77 and DCVG/C/4/36/94 these are for the\n> planning applications 1989/01396/OUT and 1992/00671/RES.  It is possible\n> that the minutes may make reference to any earlier applications or\n> discussions.  A search of these records and anything else that they lead to\n> could be carried out by staff at a charge of *£50 *to include copies of\n> any relevant pages found.\n>\n>\n>\n> 7. When the known archaeology was raised in planning.\n>\n> If any record of this is in our collections it would appear in the\n> Planning Committee records described above in point 6.\n>\n>\n>\n> 8. EIAs / HER / developer briefs 1980–1990.\n>\n> The HER for Wales is searchable via the Heneb website: Historic\n> Environment Records (HER) - Heneb\n> <https://heneb.org.uk/historic-environment-records-her/>.  You could also\n> contact the Glamorgan Gwent Archaeological Trust to see if they have any\n> relevant information.\n>\n>\n>\n> **27 July / 5 August 2026 (2026/3309)**\n>\n> 9. DB/E/1–2 Stewart survey / Cedfin.\n>\n> This was consulted and photographed by Mary Buckler in our public\n> searchroom.  If you would like to receive copies of the pages relating to\n> Ty Mawr this would be *two images* (one from DB/E/1 the survey and one\n> from DB/E/2 the terrier).  Total cost *£17* (£13 for the first image in\n> the order and £4 for the next image).\n>\n>\n>\n> 10. Lambert Williams–Bute agreements 1818–1835 (not the D160 maps already\n> seen).\n>\n> Not held. Possibly at the National Library of Wales?\n>\n>\n>\n> 11. Material related to NLW D219.\n>\n> Not held – this is a National Library of Wales reference: Llandough\n> manorial leases and agreements, - National Library of Wales Archives and\n> Manuscripts\n> <https://archives.library.wales/index.php/llandough-manorial-leases-and-agreements>\n> You should contact the NLW for access or copies.\n>\n>\n>\n> 12. D153/4–8 1876 limeworks severance — not seen.\n>\n> Not held – this is a National Library of Wales reference: Deeds relating\n> to premises in p. Llandough, co. Glam., comprising a dwelling house\n> adjoining the churchyard, 1811 (D153/1-2), land (19 ..., - National Library\n> of Wales Archives and Manuscripts\n> <https://archives.library.wales/index.php/deeds-relating-to-premises-in-p-llandough-co-glam-comprising-dwelling-house-adjoining-churchyard-1811-d153-1-2-land-19>.\n> You should contact the NLW for access or copies.\n>\n>\n>\n> 13. DXJF/64/27–32 full sketch bundle — notes seen; confirm sketches and\n> refs.\n>\n> We believe that the specific references within the bundle 27-32 that would\n> be of interest to you are items DXJF/64/30-31: sketch of former layout of\n> part of village of Llandough-juxta-Penarth by Mrs. Nancy Buckler of great\n> house farm, Llandough, with notes c1950 [1 card, 1 paper]. Is that what you\n> have already seen?  Other items in the bundle do not relate to Llandough.\n>\n>\n>\n> 14. DCOWHS on Buckler of Penarth (not DCO/WHS/2).\n>\n> DCOWHS/2 is the only part of the DCOWHS (Cowbridge and District Local\n> History Society) that relates to Buckler of Penarth. No other records held.\n>\n>\n>\n> 15. Exact ref of the Nov 1877 counterpart already photographed; separate\n> July 1877 counterpart?\n>\n> I think the items described below have been discussed previously – are\n> these what you are seeking?  Please confirm and we can provide a quote for\n> producing copies if required.\n>\n>\n>\n> *Document reference*\n>\n>    - DBDT/1/3\n>\n> *Title*\n>\n>    - Adamsdown Square leases from the trustees of the will of the late\n>    Marquess of Bute <https://glamorgan.epexio.com/en/records/DBDT/1-110/1>\n>    . Lease to Mr John Williams\n>\n> *Level of description*\n>\n>    - Item\n>\n> *Date*\n>\n>    - Jul 1877\n>\n> *Extent*\n>\n>    - 1 parchment; plan in margin\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> *Document reference*\n>\n>    - DBDT/18/1\n>\n> *Title*\n>\n>    - Lease (counterpart) of land and premises situate in Clyde Street,\n>    Roath from the trustees of the Will of the late Marquess of Bute to Mr John\n>    Williams\n>\n> *Level of description*\n>\n>    - Item\n>\n> *Date*\n>\n>    - Nov 1877\n>\n> *Extent*\n>\n>    - 1 parchment; plan in margin\n>\n>\n>\n> 16. Cardiff RDC records for this farm.\n>\n> No records found. The area was not covered by Cardiff Rural District\n> Council but rather Penarth Urban District Council.\n>\n>\n>\n> 17. DRA 418–420 / DRA 5/31 if this site.\n>\n> Neither of these are complete document references from our collection.\n> Perhaps you could check these references or let us know what you think they\n> should be and we will be happy to search our catalogue.\n>\n>\n>\n> 18. UDPE/S/1/68.\n>\n> This was viewed by Mary Buckler on 6th August and is a set of plans for a\n> pair of semi-detached houses on the road between Dinas Powys and Llandough.\n> If you would like copies of these plans 5 images will be required at a\n> charge of *£20.*\n>\n>\n>\n> 19. Penarth estate/tithe/OS/boundary plans of the house plot (not\n> Cowbridge 1924/38).\n>\n> The relevant estate map would be the Bute Estate survey which is referred\n> to in point 9 above.  You can view the tithe map online here: Tithe Maps\n> of Wales\n> <https://places.library.wales/viewer/4622411#?cv=&h=95&xywh=10530%2C8509%2C3549%2C1196>\n> along with the accompanying apportionment: Tithe Maps of Wales\n> <https://places.library.wales/viewer/4542756#?cv=1&h=95&xywh=-507%2C-68%2C2508%2C897>.\n> Many historic Ordnance Survey maps can also be viewed online, for example\n> through the National Library of Scotland website: Map Finder - with\n> Outlines - National Library of Scotland\n> <https://maps.nls.uk/geo/find/#zoom=15.3&lat=51.45225&lon=-3.20017&layers=101&b=1&z=0&point=51.44624,-3.20044&i=135196558>\n> .\n>\n>\n>\n> 20. WGR / Mountjoy / BP Pension Trust / BP Properties papers.\n>\n> The only relevant papers we have are those mentioned in point 5.4 in my\n> email of 7th September: collection reference D696 – book of plans and\n> rental books for land owned by Western Ground Rents Ltd c1938-1944.  A\n> search of these records could be carried out on your behalf at a charge of\n> *£50.*\n>\n>\n>\n> 21. Associated bundles stored separately.\n>\n> All catalogues have been searched for your enquiry.\n>\n>\n>\n> 22. Uncatalogued / recent deposits 1875–1988.\n>\n> A search of our accessions database, which records all records received\n> into our collection even if they haven’t yet been catalogued, has not\n> revealed any additional records.\n>\n>\n>\n> 23. Photography for copies to Patagonia — rules.\n>\n> Please find attached the digital photography order form which applies\n> wherever you are based.  As mentioned above, prices quoted do not include\n> VAT due to your location.  You need to sign and agree to the declaration on\n> the order form.\n>\n>\n>\n> **22 August 2026 — no reply**\n>\n>\n>\n> 24–29. DBDT10/19 and 10/20 identification of Daniel Thomas; Bute–Thomas\n> search at Llandough juxta Penarth; quarry/lime/reversion instruments\n> (poor-rate occupation already seen); later title; research cost.\n>\n> These points were answered in my email of 7th September.\n>\n>\n>\n> **26 August 2026 — no reply**\n>\n> 30–35. Physical examination of original **DBDT79/1** (catalogued May 1877,\n> Pembroke Terrace). Treat as a different instrument from the Nov 1877\n> Llandough counterpart unless you confirm they are the same.\n>\n> These points were answered in my email of 14th September.\n>\n>\n>\n> We hope that this information is of use to you.  We will respond to other\n> outstanding emails in due course.\n>\n>\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n>\n>\n> Louise Hunt\n>\n> *Archifydd / Archivist*\n>\n>\n>\n> [map link redacted]\n>\n> *Archifau Morgannwg / Glamorgan Archives*\n>\n> [map link redacted]\n>\n> *Clos Parc Morgannwg\n> [map link redacted]*\n>\n> [map link redacted]\n>\n> *Lecwydd / Leckwith\n> [map link redacted]*\n>\n> *Caerdydd / Cardiff\n> [map link redacted]*\n>\n> *[postcode redacted]\n> [map link redacted]*\n>\n>\n>\n> [phone redacted]\n>\n> *[email redacted]* <[email redacted]>\n>\n>\n>\n> *www.archifaumorgannwg.gov.uk/* <http://www.archifaumorgannwg.gov.uk/>\n> *www.glamarchives.gov.uk* <http://www.glamarchives.gov.uk/>\n>\n> www.facebook.com/glamarchives\n>\n> www.instagram.com/glamarchives\n>\n> **********************************************************************\n>\n> The Council welcomes correspondence in Welsh, English or bilingually. We\n> will ensure that we communicate with you in the language of your choice, as\n> long as you let us know which you prefer. Corresponding in Welsh will not\n> lead to delay.\n>\n> Privileged/Confidential Information may be contained in this message. If\n> you are not the addressee indicated in this message (or responsible for\n> delivery of the message to such person), you may not copy or deliver this\n> message to anyone. In such case, you should destroy this message and kindly\n> notify the sender by reply email. Please advise immediately if you or your\n> employer does not consent to Internet email for messages of this kind.\n> Opinions, conclusions and other information in this message that do not\n> relate to the official business of the Council of the City and County of\n> Cardiff shall be understood as neither given nor endorsed by it. All e-mail\n> sent to or from this address will be processed by Cardiff County Councils\n> Corporate E-mail system and may be subject to scrutiny by someone other\n> than the addressee.\n>\n> **********************************************************************\n>\n> Mae’r Cyngor yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg, Saesneg neu’n ddwyieithog.\n> Byddwn yn cyfathrebu â chi yn ôl eich dewis, dim ond i chi roi gwybod i ni\n> pa un sydd well gennych. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi.\n>\n> Mae'n bosibl bod gwybodaeth gyfrinachol yn y neges hon. Os na chyfeirir y\n> neges atoch chi'n benodol (neu os nad ydych chi'n gyfrifol am drosglwyddo'r\n> neges i'r person a enwir), yna ni chewch gopio na throsglwyddo'r neges.\n> Mewn achos o'r fath, dylech ddinistrio'r neges a hysbysu'r anfonwr drwy\n> e-bost ar unwaith. Rhowch wybod i'r anfonydd ar unwaith os nad ydych chi\n> neu eich cyflogydd yn caniatau e-bost y Rhyngrwyd am negeseuon fel hon.\n> Rhaid deall nad yw'r safbwyntiau, y casgliadau a'r wybodaeth arall yn y\n> neges hon nad ydynt yn cyfeirio at fusnes swyddogol Cyngor Dinas a Sir\n> Caerdydd yn cynrychioli barn y Cyngor Sir nad yn cael sel ei fendith. Caiff\n> unrhyw negeseuon a anfonir at, neu o'r cyfeiriad e-bost hwn eu prosesu gan\n> system E-bost Gorfforaethol Cyngor Sir Caerdydd a gallant gael eu\n> harchwilio gan rywun heblaw'r person a enwir.\n>\n> **********************************************************************\n>"},{"id":"gm-1a0e110fccdc4819","subject":"Automatic reply: Great House Farm, Llandough","name":"Automatic reply: Great House Farm, Llandough","date":"2026-09-27T04:12:44Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0e110fccdc4819.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0e110bed09b687-automatic-reply-great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":["mp"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["mp"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0e110bed09b687","direction":"received","from":"DOUGHTY, Stephen","institutions":[{"id":"mp","name":"Member of Parliament"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0e110fccdc4819.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Hello,\n\nThank you for your email. Please read this automatic response, which serves as notice that your message has been received.\n\nI receive hundreds of emails every week, and although I intend to reply as quickly as I can to all, I prioritise those that raise immediate problems and urgent cases.\n\n\nCasework\n\nThere is a strict protocol that means Members of Parliament may only provide assistance to their own constituents. This means you must live in the constituency of Cardiff South and Penarth in order for me to assist.\nPlease therefore ensure that you have included:\n\n• Your full name\n\n• Your full postal address\n\n• Your contact telephone number\n\n• Any relevant reference numbers\n\n\nParliamentary Business\n\nIf your email relates to my work in Parliament, it will be dealt with as appropriate.\n\n\nMinisterial Business\n\nIf you are contacting me in my capacity as a Minister at the FCDO, you will need to redirect your email to to [email redacted]<[email redacted]>, or alternatively contact your own MP – you can find who that is at https://members.parliament.uk/FindYourMP\n\n\nThank you for taking the time to contact me.\n\nFor information about how we use your personal data, see our Privacy Notice<https://www.stephendoughty.wales/privacynotice>.\n\n\nYours sincerely,\n\n\nStephen\n\n\nStephen Doughty MP / AS\nMember of Parliament for Cardiff South and Penarth // Aelod Seneddol dros Dde Caerdydd a Phenarth\nHouse of Commons, Westminster, London, [postcode redacted]\nTel: [phone redacted] | Email: [email redacted]\n\n\nUK Parliament Disclaimer: this e-mail is confidential to the intended recipient. If you have received it in error, please notify the sender and delete it from your system. Any unauthorised use, disclosure, or copying is not permitted. This e-mail has been checked for viruses, but no liability is accepted for any damage caused by any virus transmitted by this e-mail. This e-mail address is not secure, is not encrypted and should not be used for sensitive data."},{"id":"gm-1a0e110bed09b687","subject":"Automatic reply: Great House Farm, Llandough","name":"Automatic reply: Great House Farm, Llandough","date":"2026-09-27T04:12:42Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0e110bed09b687.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0e110bed09b687-automatic-reply-great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":["cadw"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["cadw"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0e110bed09b687","direction":"received","from":"(name not shown; gov.wales)","institutions":[{"id":"cadw","name":"Welsh Government / Cadw"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0e110bed09b687.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Diolch am eich e-bost i Lywodraeth Cymru.\n\n\n  1.\nYmateb awtomatig yw hwn i ddiolch ichi am eich e-bost i Lywodraeth Cymru. Gan ein bod wedi derbyn eich gohebiaeth yn electronig, ni fydd angen ichi anfon copi papur atom.\n\n\n\n  1.\nRydym yn croesawu derbyn gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg. Byddwn yn ateb yr ohebiaeth yn Gymraeg ac ni fydd hynny’n achosi oedi.\n\n\n\n  1.\nOs ydych yn ysgrifennu atom ar ran rhywun arall, gallai fod yn ofynnol o dan y ddeddfwriaeth diogelu data inni ofyn i’r unigolyn gadarnhau ei fod yn fodlon inni brosesu a storio ei ddata personol.\n\n\n\n  1.\nNi fyddwn fel arfer yn ymateb i unigolion sy’n anfon copïau parod o ohebiaeth neu ddeisebau sy’n ymwneud ag ymgyrchoedd. Byddwn fel arfer yn anfon un ymateb at drefnwyr yr ymgyrch. Os mai wedi cael ein copïo i neges y byddwn, fel arfer bydd gohebiaeth o’r fath yn cael ei darllen a’i chadw ond ni fydd yn cael cydnabyddiaeth bellach.\n\n\n\n  1.\nOs ydym yn bwriadu ymateb, fe anfonwn gydnabyddiaeth atoch a fydd yn nodi erbyn pa ddyddiad y gallwch ddisgwyl cael ateb.\n\n\nAm ragor o wybodaeth ynghylch sut rydym yn prosesu gohebiaeth i’r Gweinidogion, gweler: https://llyw.cymru/ysgrifennu-weinidogion\nThank you for your e-mail to the Welsh Government.\n\n\n  1.\nThis is an automated response to thank you for your correspondence to the Welsh Government. As we have received your correspondence electronically, there is no need to send us a hard copy.\n\n  1.\nWe welcome receiving correspondence in the Welsh language, and please be assured that such correspondence will be answered in Welsh and will not lead to a delay in responding.\n  2.\nIf you are writing to us on behalf of someone else, then data protection legislation may require us to seek consent from them to confirm they are content for their personal data to be processed and stored by us.\n  3.\nWe will not generally respond to individual senders of pre-generated campaign correspondence or petitions, instead we generally issue a single response to the campaign organisers only. Copy correspondence is generally read and retained but receives no further acknowledgment.\n  4.\nWhere we will be responding, we will issue an acknowledgment giving the date by which we aim to reply.\n\n\nFor further information on how we process correspondence to Ministers, see here: https://gov.wales/writing-ministers\n\nSganiwyd y neges hon am bob feirws hysbys wrth iddi adael Llywodraeth Cymru. Mae Llywodraeth Cymru yn cymryd o ddifrif yr angen i ddiogelu eich data. Os cysylltwch â Llywodraeth Cymru, mae ein hysbysiad preifatrwydd<”https://gov.wales/about/welsh-government-privacy-notice/?lang=cy”> yn esbonio sut rydym yn defnyddio eich gwybodaeth a sut rydym yn diogelu eich preifatrwydd. Rydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg. Byddwn yn anfon ateb yn Gymraeg i ohebiaeth a dderbynnir yn Gymraeg ac ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi. ----------------- On leaving the Welsh Government this email was scanned for all known viruses. The Welsh Government takes the protection of your data seriously. If you contact the Welsh Government then our Privacy Notice<https://gov.wales/about/welsh-government-privacy-notice/?lang=en> explains how we use your information and the ways in which we protect your privacy. We welcome receiving correspondence in Welsh. Any correspondence received in Welsh will be answered in Welsh and corresponding in Welsh will not lead to a delay in responding."},{"id":"gm-1a0e110be3cadc42","subject":"Automatic reply: Great House Farm, Llandough","name":"Automatic reply: Great House Farm, Llandough","date":"2026-09-27T04:12:42Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0e110be3cadc42.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0e110bed09b687-automatic-reply-great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":["hmlr"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["hmlr"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0e110bed09b687","direction":"received","from":"_FOI email address","institutions":[{"id":"hmlr","name":"HM Land Registry"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0e110be3cadc42.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Thank you for contacting HM Land Registry. This is an automated response to confirm that we have received your correspondence. We aim to formally provide an acknowledgement within three working days. If you have any special requirements that mean you would like us to communicate with you in a specific way, please let us know and we will make adjustments if we can.\n\n\nIf you are contacting us in respect of any matters relating to property registration or have a general customer enquiry – Our team are unable to respond to any general registration enquiries or provide updates on any property applications. Please redirect your customer enquiry to our Customer Support colleagues, using any of the methods below:\n\n\n  *   Online contact form: HM Land Registry Contact Us form<https://customerhelp.landregistry.gov.uk/guide-page-external/?stepid=2b31bc48-eb96-eb11-b1ac-002248413956>\n  *   Website - www.gov.uk/guidance/contact-hm-land-registry<http://www.gov.uk/guidance/contact-hm-land-registry>\n  *   Telephone\n  *   English language [phone redacted]\n  *   Welsh language [phone redacted]\n\n\nTelephone lines are open between 08:00 and 17:00 Monday to Thursday. This does not include Bank Holidays.\n\n\n  *   Post\n\nHM Land Registry Citizen Centre\nPO Box 74\nGloucester\n[postcode redacted]\n\nThere is no direct contact e-mail address for Customer Support. Please use the online contact form and any responses will be issued via e-mail.\n\nIf you have made a request under the Freedom of Information Act or Environmental Information Regulations – the statutory timescale for response is 20 working days following receipt of your request.\n\nIf you have made a request in relation to your data rights, for example, a subject access request for personal data – the statutory timescale for response is one calendar month following receipt of your request.\n\nPlease note that where personal data is held on our register, it is exempt from the right of subject access under UK GDPR.\n\nWhere any personal data is held on record for a registered title, the exemption provided at Schedule 2, Part 1, Paragraph 5(1) of the Data Protection Act 2018 would be engaged.\n\nThis states:\n\n“5(1) The listed GDPR provisions do not apply to personal data consisting of information that the controller is obliged by an enactment to make available to the public, to the extent that the application of those provisions would prevent the controller from complying with that obligation.”\n\nPlease see the section below titled 'Register information and your data protection rights' for further explanation.\n\nIf you have only copied your correspondence to us - we will not respond.\n\nYours sincerely\n\nInformation Rights Team\nHM Land Registry\n\nAccessing information held by HMLR on our register of title\n\nTo assist you in accessing information held by HMLR, please be aware most of the information held within our registers is open to public inspection and accessible via statutory routes of access. This means that we may be unable to provide access to this information via a Freedom of Information Act 2000 (FOIA) request.\n\nStatutory Routes of Access to information held on Public Registers\n\nThe Land Registration Act 2002 is the principal legislation governing land registration in England and Wales. It sets out provisions for keeping a register of title to land and property, and in accordance with Section 66 & 67 of the Land Registration Act, it is a requirement that the Chief Land Registrar makes this information (including any documents held on the register of title) available for public inspection.\n\nHow to access information held on our Registers\n\nThe information you have requested is available to access under our statutory protocol under section 66 & 67 of the Land Registration Act 2002.\n\nSearch for Land and Property\n\nPlease visit our website for further information about our registers:\n\nwww.gov.uk/get-information-about-property-and-land<http://www.gov.uk/get-information-about-property-and-land>\n\nOfficial Copies\n\nFor information about how to apply for official copies of documents held in relation to registered titles, and information about the types of documents available, please refer to practice guide 11:\n\nwww.gov.uk/government/publications/inspection-and-application-for-official-copies/practice-guide-11-inspection-and-application-for-official-copies<http://www.gov.uk/government/publications/inspection-and-application-for-official-copies/practice-guide-11-inspection-and-application-for-official-copies>\n\nIndex of proprietors’ names\n\nUnder rule 11 of the Land Registration Rules 2003 the registrar is required to keep an index of proprietors’ names which shows for each individual register the proprietor of the registered estate and the proprietor of any registered charge together with the title number. When you search the index of proprietors’ names, you will receive a list of titles that are owned by, or mortgaged to, the name that you have searched against.\n\nFor further information about this please refer to practice guide 74:\n\nwww.gov.uk/government/publications/searches-of-the-index-of-proprietors-names/practice-guide-74-searches-of-the-index-of-proprietors-names<http://www.gov.uk/government/publications/searches-of-the-index-of-proprietors-names/practice-guide-74-searches-of-the-index-of-proprietors-names>\n\nSection 21 FOIA exemption: Information accessible to the applicant by other means\n\nWhere information held on the register of title is available for inspection through these statutory routes of access it is exempt from disclosure under section 21(2)(b) of FOIA.\n\nSection 21 states:\n\n“21 Information accessible to applicant by other means.\n\n(1) Information which is reasonably accessible to the applicant otherwise than under section 1 is exempt information.\n\n(2) For the purposes of subsection (1)—\n\n(a) information may be reasonably accessible to the applicant even though it is accessible only on payment, and\n\n(b) information is to be taken to be reasonably accessible to the applicant if it is information which the public authority or any other person is obliged by or under any enactment to communicate (otherwise than by making the information available for inspection) to members of the public on request, whether free of charge or on payment.”\n\nTherefore, we are unable to provide information held on our register through FOIA, as it covered by this exemption.\n\nAdvice and assistance\n\nUnder Section 16 of FOIA and in accordance with the Section 45 FOIA code of practice, we have a duty to provide you with reasonable advice and assistance.\n\nPlease note that the ICO has previously reviewed HM Land Registry’s use of the section 21 exemption. Relevant decision notices can be accessed here<https://icosearch.ico.org.uk/s/search.html?collection=ico-meta&profile=decisions&query&query=&f.By+authority%7CpublicAuthority=HM%20Land%20Registry&f.By+section%7CsectionOfLegislation=FOI%2021> including IC-173302-N4J1<https://ico.org.uk/media/action-weve-taken/decision-notices/2022/4022883/ic-173302-n4j1.pdf>, FS50685812<https://ico.org.uk/media/action-weve-taken/decision-notices/2017/2172810/fs50685812.pdf> and FER0454056<https://ico.org.uk/media/action-weve-taken/decision-notices/2012/780474/fer_0454056.pdf>.\n\nThe ICO has agreed that this position is long established, and the Commissioner is satisfied that section 21 would be engaged for this type of request.\n\nFor assistance on how to obtain official copies of documents you can contact our Customer Support Centre by telephone between 8am and 5pm, Monday to Thursday on [phone redacted] or online through our Contact Us<https://customerhelp.landregistry.gov.uk/guide-page-external/?stepid=2b31bc48-eb96-eb11-b1ac-002248413956> form.\n\nOur Customer Support colleagues are trained to handle queries which relate to information that is held on the register of title.\n\nInformation about how to apply to inspect or obtain official copies is contained with practice guide 11:\n\nwww.gov.uk/government/publications/inspection-and-application-for-official-copies/practice-guide-11-inspection-and-application-for-official-copies<http://www.gov.uk/government/publications/inspection-and-application-for-official-copies/practice-guide-11-inspection-and-application-for-official-copies>\n\nIf you need any support completing the application form for official copies or to determine which documents to order, you may contact our Customer Support colleagues:\n\nwww.gov.uk/guidance/contact-hm-land-registry<http://www.gov.uk/guidance/contact-hm-land-registry>\n\nYou may also refer to our Publication Scheme which includes information about how to access information that is held on the register of title under the heading ‘Request information’:\n\nwww.gov.uk/government/organisations/land-registry/about/publication-scheme#request-information<http://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/land-registry/about/publication-scheme#request-information>\n\nPlease refer to HMLR’s Transaction Data webpage which contains information in respect of commonly used abbreviations:\n\nwww.gov.uk/guidance/hm-land-registry-transaction-data#abbreviations-used-in-transaction-data<http://www.gov.uk/guidance/hm-land-registry-transaction-data#abbreviations-used-in-transaction-data>\n\nIf you need any independent advice about individual’s rights under information legislation you can contact the Information Commissioner’s Office (ICO). The ICO is the UK’s independent authority set up to uphold information rights in the public interest, promoting openness by public bodies and data privacy for individuals.\n\nThe contact details for the ICO are detailed below. There is useful information on the ICO website which explains the rights of individuals:\n\nwww.ico.org.uk/for-the-public<http://www.ico.org.uk/for-the-public>\n\nPlease refer to the top tips:\n\nwww.ico.org.uk/for-the-public/official-information<http://www.ico.org.uk/for-the-public/official-information/>\n\nRegister information and your data protection rights\n\nData protection law permits HM Land Registry to use personal data for our official purposes.\n\nThe lawful basis for HM Land Registry processing personal data under the United Kingdom (UK) General Data Protection Regulation (UK GDPR) is that it is necessary to enable us to perform our public task of the registration of land and property (Article 6(1)(e) of UK GDPR). This is defined in the Land Registration Act 2002.\n\nParagraph 5 of Schedule 2 Part 1 of the Data Protection Act 2018 provides the following exemption from the specified UK GDPR provisions:\n\n“The listed UK GDPR provisions do not apply to personal data consisting of information that the controller is obliged by an enactment to make available to the public, to the extent that the application of those provisions would prevent the controller from complying with that obligation.”\n\nThe Land Registration Act 2002 is the principal legislation governing land registration in England and Wales. It sets out provisions for the keeping of a register of title to land and requirements for its administration by HM Land Registry under a Chief Land Registrar.\n\nThe Chief Land Registrar is required by the Land Registration Act 2002 and to make register information (including any documents held on the register of title) available to the public, therefore the following data protection provisions do not apply:\n\n\n  *   the requirement to provide ‘privacy notices’ to individuals\n  *   the requirement to provide personal data in response to subject access requests\n  *   the requirement to rectification\n  *   the requirement to comply with requests to erasure (right to be ‘forgotten’)\n  *   most of the principles of UK GDPR\n\n\nPlease visit our website for further information about our registers:\n\nwww.gov.uk/get-information-about-property-and-land<https://www.gov.uk/get-information-about-property-and-land>\n\nFor further information in relation to your rights and how HMLR processes personal data please refer to our Personal Information Charter:\n\nwww.gov.uk/government/organisations/land-registry/about/personal-information-charter<http://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/land-registry/about/personal-information-charter>\n\n\n\n\nHM Land Registry’s purpose is to protect your land ownership and provide services and data that underpin an efficient and informed property market. Our vision is: “A world-leading property market as part of a thriving economy and a sustainable future”.\n\nWe check all mail and attachments for known viruses. However, you are advised that you open any attachments at your own risk. If you have received this email and it was not intended for you, please let us know, then delete it.\n\nWe welcome correspondence in English and Welsh.\n\nTo see how HM Land Registry treats your personal information, read our Personal information charter: www.gov.uk/government/organisations/land-registry/about/personal-information-charter."},{"id":"gm-1a0e110665ebcb5c","subject":"Great House Farm, Llandough","name":"Great House Farm, Llandough","date":"2026-09-27T04:12:23Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0e110665ebcb5c.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0e03138143e763-great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":["hmlr"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["hmlr"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0e03138143e763","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"hmlr","name":"HM Land Registry"}],"refs":["N610CGV"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0e110665ebcb5c.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Sirs/ Ma'ams,\n\nI need to correct a date I have used in earlier correspondence about Great\nHouse Farm / Tŷ Mawr, Llandough, titles WA231076 and WA240304.\n\nI had treated 1983 as the year the two titles were merged. That was wrong.\nHM Land Registry’s own letter of 23 March 2026 (Lisa Griffiths, ref\nN610CGV) separates the two acts.\n1982–83 was first registration of two live titles, not a merger.\n30 November 1982: application to first-register WA231076. The Court of\nAppeal later treated BP Properties Ltd as registered proprietors of “the\nfarm” from November 1982.\n23 February 1983: application to first-register a second title, WA240304.\n26 March 1983: Mary Williams died. On the family’s case, notice of those\napplications did not reach us until after her death, while the family was\nin mourning. She had no window in which to object. The family was not in a\nposition to use any window that arrived after that.\nThose two titles remained separate through first instance.\n24 July 1986: Hollis J. Both titles were still live. The judgment language\ntreats Great House Farm as one holding. It had not been one holding on the\nregister. On the family’s case it had not been one holding since the 1877\nsplit: Parcel A (house, buildings, garden/yard and about 10 acres, the\nThomas/Williams line) and Parcel B (the eastern fields retained by Bute and\nlater WGR/BP).\n2 February 1987: application to amalgamate the two titles. WA240304 was\nthen closed and registration continued under WA231076. That is the merge.\nIt was made after the 1986 ruling and before the Court of Appeal gave\njudgment on 31 July 1987.\nSo 1983 is silent first registration of a second title while Mary was still\nin Parcel A, or had only just died. 1987 is the administrative merger,\ncarried out while the appeal was still open.\nThis was not a dispute about a single land parcel that BP already owned.\nMary was on Parcel A, which the family says she owned, with occupation on\nthat house parcel running in the family from at least 1667. BP, and WGR\nbefore it from 1949, were the neighbouring interest on Parcel B. From 1949\nonwards the family’s case is that those neighbours ran a decades-long\nseries of tenancy offers, “whole farm” wording, possession orders and the\n1974 letters to make her occupation of her own parcel look permissive, and\nto make both parcels look like one estate they had acquired. They never\nproduced a conveyance of Parcel A. What they had was an unfounded claim\nthat her occupancy was permissive.\nPlease treat any earlier letter from me that said the titles merged in 1983\nas corrected by this email. The dates that matter are:\n1982/83 — first registration of two titles, without the occupier being\nheard;\n24 July 1986 — first-instance judgment describing one farm while two titles\nstill stood;\n2 February 1987 — amalgamation, before the appeal ended;\n31 July 1987 — Court of Appeal.\nYours faithfully,\n\nRequester\nRefs where relevant: N610CGV; 260919-6726246; titles WA231076 and WA240304.\nRequester \ngreathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com"},{"id":"gm-1a0e10fdcfb4fd43","subject":"Automatic reply: Great House Farm / N610CGV — chase: MoJ response and HMLR first-registration file","name":"Automatic reply: Great House Farm / N610CGV — chase: MoJ response and HMLR first-registration file","date":"2026-09-27T04:11:42Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0e10fdcfb4fd43.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0e10fdcfb4fd43-automatic-reply-great-house-farm-n610cgv-chase-moj-response-and-hmlr-first-registration-file","scopeTopics":["mp"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["mp"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0e10fdcfb4fd43","direction":"received","from":"DOUGHTY, Stephen","institutions":[{"id":"mp","name":"Member of Parliament"}],"refs":["N610CGV"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0e10fdcfb4fd43.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Hello,\n\nThank you for your email. Please read this automatic response, which serves as notice that your message has been received.\n\nI receive hundreds of emails every week, and although I intend to reply as quickly as I can to all, I prioritise those that raise immediate problems and urgent cases.\n\n\nCasework\n\nThere is a strict protocol that means Members of Parliament may only provide assistance to their own constituents. This means you must live in the constituency of Cardiff South and Penarth in order for me to assist.\nPlease therefore ensure that you have included:\n\n• Your full name\n\n• Your full postal address\n\n• Your contact telephone number\n\n• Any relevant reference numbers\n\n\nParliamentary Business\n\nIf your email relates to my work in Parliament, it will be dealt with as appropriate.\n\n\nMinisterial Business\n\nIf you are contacting me in my capacity as a Minister at the FCDO, you will need to redirect your email to to [email redacted]<[email redacted]>, or alternatively contact your own MP – you can find who that is at https://members.parliament.uk/FindYourMP\n\n\nThank you for taking the time to contact me.\n\nFor information about how we use your personal data, see our Privacy Notice<https://www.stephendoughty.wales/privacynotice>.\n\n\nYours sincerely,\n\n\nStephen\n\n\nStephen Doughty MP / AS\nMember of Parliament for Cardiff South and Penarth // Aelod Seneddol dros Dde Caerdydd a Phenarth\nHouse of Commons, Westminster, London, [postcode redacted]\nTel: [phone redacted] | Email: [email redacted]\n\n\nUK Parliament Disclaimer: this e-mail is confidential to the intended recipient. If you have received it in error, please notify the sender and delete it from your system. Any unauthorised use, disclosure, or copying is not permitted. This e-mail has been checked for viruses, but no liability is accepted for any damage caused by any virus transmitted by this e-mail. This e-mail address is not secure, is not encrypted and should not be used for sensitive data."},{"id":"gm-1a0e10f6d03bd817","subject":"Great House Farm / N610CGV — chase: MoJ response and HMLR first-registration file","name":"Great House Farm / N610CGV — chase: MoJ response and HMLR first-registration file","date":"2026-09-27T04:11:19Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0e10f6d03bd817.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0e0b198a3c842a-great-house-farm-n610cgv-chase-moj-response-and-hmlr-first-registration-file","scopeTopics":["mp"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["mp"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0e0b198a3c842a","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"mp","name":"Member of Parliament"}],"refs":["N610CGV"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0e10f6d03bd817.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Mr Doughty\n\nLand Registry need holding to account Sir.\nASAP.\n\nRequester \ngreathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com"},{"id":"gm-1a0e10e33f795cac","subject":"Chase — submission of 3 September 2026: Great House Farm / suspected historic fraud","name":"Chase — submission of 3 September 2026: Great House Farm / suspected historic fraud","date":"2026-09-27T04:09:58Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0e10e33f795cac.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0e0b1a2b1ebbc3-chase-submission-of-3-september-2026-great-house-farm-suspected-historic-fraud","scopeTopics":["cps"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["cps"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0e0b1a2b1ebbc3","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"cps","name":"Crown Prosecution Service"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0e10e33f795cac.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Victim Liaison Cymru\n\nI wrote on 3 September 2026 (subject: Re-Opening of BP vs Buckler 1987)\nplacing before CPS Cymru material concerning suspected historic fraud and\nrelated conduct arising from the registration and later dispossession at\nGreat House Farm, Llandough.\n\nI have had no acknowledgement.\n\nPlease confirm:\n1. that the 3 September submission was received;\n2. the reference number allocated; and\n3. whether CPS Cymru will consider the material, or whether I should\ninstead report to Action Fraud / South Wales Police for a crime reference\nnumber.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester \ngreathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com"},{"id":"gm-1a0e10cfed13b010","subject":"Re: FOI — Mary Williams probate records — death certificate enclosed","name":"Re: FOI — Mary Williams probate records — death certificate enclosed","date":"2026-09-27T04:08:39Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0e10cfed13b010.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0e0b7ebcf0f20f-foi-mary-williams-probate-records-death-certificate-enclosed","scopeTopics":["moj"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["moj"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0e0b7ebcf0f20f","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"moj","name":"Ministry of Justice / HMCTS"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0e10cfed13b010.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Disclosure Team,\n\nFurther to your email of 28 July 2026 requesting a copy of the death\ncertificate for Mary Williams before you can process my FOI request\nconcerning her probate records, please find the death certificate attached.\n\nMary Williams (also known as Mary Buckler following her marriage to\nFrederick Buckler — see attached extracts from her statement) died in March\n1983. She was the occupier and asserted owner of Great House Farm (Ty Mawr\nalias Cedfin aka Cedwyn), Llandough-juxta-Penarth, Cardiff.\n\nMy request, submitted 7 June 2026, seeks access to any probate records,\nwills, affidavits, or ownership claim documents referenced in court\nproceedings relating to Mary Williams and Great House Farm, Llandough,\nparticularly in connection with the proceedings recorded in BP Properties\nLtd v Buckler [1987] EWCA Civ 2.\n\nPlease can you confirm receipt and advise the expected response date.\n\nWith talk of civil war on the horizon and already a lot of government\nfiduciaries in less possession of records than I am in possession of, due\nto so much destruction of property and records and gaps in records by the\ngovernment in this case, I am keen to salvage what remains of my family's\nhistory and heritage so that the historians and I at the Welsh settlement\nin Argentina can celebrate my family's homes and strongholds, Welsh history\nand heritage, rather than waiting more than the almost years we have\nalready waited for the British State to stop its endless war it has waged\non us to ethnically cleanse us indigenous Britons from our ancestral\nbirthland in Wales. We'll just return if/when someone overthrows this\nterrorist organisation, the British State we now seem to be harbouring on\nthe British Isles. All fear and respect for the United Kingdom government\nhas gone for our family. Few care if you now bring guns and finish the job\nof erasing us, as they have lived four decades in what is effectively the\nconcentration work-camp conditions our towns and cities have been turned\ninto.\n\nBut before our county treasure, the former courthouse manorial 10-bedroom\ncourthouse, was demolished, or looted of all our furniture from Cardiff by\nwhat appears to be SAS under cover as BP bailiffs, I do remember a\ndifferent realm from the one now blocking out the sun on the British Isles.\nA realm less demonic, on our sacred Welsh Christian stronghold, upon which\nthe suppressed history and heritage once stood: the Roman villa and\nmedieval burial site and Celtic 2,200-year-old roundhouse and monastery.\nAnd Marconi staying with us while inventing radio, or world champion boxer\nPeerless Jim Driscoll fighting his first fight in our barns. Today there is\nno history tourist-board plaque, no park-bench plaque. Just 20\ncharacterless houses, 13 of which are on our stolen occupied land.\n\nAs a bona fide, certified and experienced military commander and leader and\nwarfare professional and war veteran, who was subject of this siege on our\nland and home in 1988, I have to say it is important to call a kettle black\nand call this what it was and is. This is all synonymous with a terrorist\norganisation, network or cell, committing an act of terrorism. Establishing\na terrorist camp for its officers or PoW/hostages. Following an act of\nterrorism (my pregnant mum also thrown over our gate, into a puddle,\ncollarbone dislocated, just for seeking medicine for my father who lay in\nthe hospital bed after police left him in a cell and ignored him,\ncomplaining of a heart attack for six hours).\n\nMy family have lived as terrified hostage-like prisoners of war under the\nBritish State in Wales. Too frightened to speak up or out about any of\nthis. Since everyone who has challenged acquiescing in our own replacement\nin my family, on our land since 1667, seems to have died at very convenient\ntimings.\n\nI don't fully understand how deep this all goes. I suspect it was a\nmilitary operation disguised as a civilian action. Perhaps hybrid warfare\nannexure of our land as some of the last in Wales to fall victim to British\nState imperialsim. Our home was literally looted of our furniture from\nCardiff castle. Our lands were literally looted of medieval burials.\nIncluding roman coins and so forth. Much of which ended up in a suspicious\nfire in Neath.\n\nI have called for a public enquiry from the Senedd. But it seems the fight\nto keep burying us alive is a never ending one.\n\nI wish you all the best with the civil war you've waged on us indigenous\nBritons on our ancestral birthland. I see the British State for what it is\nnow. A terrorist organisation my countrymen will surely soon stop\nharbouring on our island.\n\nI think there's a small window of opportunity in our families case for your\nregime to even just take a break from trying to make us into striped pjamad\nhostages. And allow us to restore even parts of our dignity and minor\ngentry manorial 800 year old home and 4 century of occupancy before you\nstole and destroyed it by force.\n\nWe don't even seek to try to enjoy our language, history and herritage\nunder this terrorist anarcho tyrant ethnic cleansing demonic realm regime\nin Wales anymore. We're committed to rebuilding the replica of our former\nhome in Patagonia where our existence and culture and language and\nherritage as a race and ethnicity, isn't being actively exterminated and\nconcealed and supressed.\n\nI did my 22 years in Oath. Served in war. I'm bona fide, left my military\ncareer with full honours. I'm a warfare professional and certified and\nexperienced military commander and leader. And I can't even get back basic\nFOI's, after almost a year from many branches of your government. And I\ndon't think its all negigence or incompetence.\n\nI think there's concealment and fraud and government involvement and your\ngovernment are in dishonour and are a disgrace in this whole case and\ntreatment of my family and our home and history and herritage.\n\nI feel having brought all this to everyone's attention. Which was also very\ndifficult and sensitive to do considering nobody in my family will even\nspeak up or out about it. To be met with such negligence and incompetence\nand obtuse, hostile, unco-operative, railroading and gatekeeping and delay\nis a disgrace.\n\nThis is nothing short of a nightmare under a demonic realm in a\nconcentration work camp where we're being ethnically cleansed and ignored.\nThere is no democracy here. There is no peace here.\n\nChurch View Close Llandough was once our lands of centuries. Its been\nnothing short of a crime scene since BP arrived onto the scene in 1969.\nIt's only in these last few decades of the beautiful undisturbed centuries\nof life on our land overlooking Cardiff, WHICH WE STILL OWN, despite you\nlot illegally occupying it - its only in the last few decades we've seen\nall this ethnic cleansing British imperialism turned inwards on us.\n\nThe evidence is all in this case. The words I use to describe this are\nstrong, but are actually proportionate to what has happened in this case.\nAnd is ongoing because nothing and nobody is doing a thing to undo this\ndogs dinner of a society.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nRequester \n\nWilliams/Buckler Family — Great House Farm, Llandough"},{"id":"gm-1a0df1f896c4122a","subject":"Great House Farm, Llandough","name":"Great House Farm, Llandough","date":"2026-09-26T19:09:40Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0df1f896c4122a.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0d618398424383-great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":["hmlr-icr"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["hmlr-icr"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0d618398424383","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"hmlr-icr","name":"HM Land Registry (Independent Complaints Reviewer)"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0df1f896c4122a.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Sirs,\n\nI am seeking an independent review of HM Land Registry's handling of my\ncomplaint concerning the historic registration of land belonging to the\nWilliams/Buckler family at Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr, Llandough, Cardiff,\nand the subsequent registered title history, including WA231076, WA240304\nand the later woodland title WA735527.\n\nHM Land Registry has now replied to my MP and has maintained, in essence,\nthat it does not accept that a mistake was made.\n\nMy concern is not simply that HM Land Registry reached a conclusion with\nwhich I disagree. It is that the conclusion appears to leave fundamental\ndocumentary questions unanswered and relies heavily upon historical\nproceedings which did not determine the underlying documentary root of the\nfamily's ownership claim.\n\nI have spent approximately twenty years researching this matter and have\nassembled a substantial body of documentary, archival, court, Land Registry\nand family evidence. I am not asking ICR to determine ownership itself. I\nam asking it to review whether HM Land Registry properly investigated and\nanswered the complaint put before it, whether it addressed the evidence I\nsupplied, and whether the reasons given for its conclusion adequately\nexplain how the disputed registration was accepted and subsequently\nmaintained.\n1. The underlying family ownership claim\n\nThe family's position is that our historic Parcel A was a distinct parcel\nof land around the farmhouse and woodland and that our underlying freehold\ninterest was never lawfully extinguished.\n\nMy grandmother, Mary Williams, consistently asserted ownership of the\nfamily parcel. The documentary history presently available to me raises a\nsubstantial question as to whether Parcel A was ever conveyed to Western\nGround Rents in the manner subsequently assumed.\n\nThere is an important distinction between:\n\n   - the family-occupied Parcel A surrounding the farmhouse; and\n   - the eastern agricultural Parcel B which was the subject of the later\n   agricultural tenancy arrangements.\n\nThe 1939 revised agricultural tenancy documentation which I have located\nappears to concern Parcel B and its associated land. The accompanying plan\ndoes not appear to include the family-occupied Parcel A.\n\nThis raises a basic documentary question:\n\nWhat instrument actually conveyed Parcel A to Western Ground Rents, if it\nwas ever conveyed to them at all?\n\nI have not yet obtained a document which satisfactorily answers that\nquestion.\n2. The 1928 / 1938 / 1939 documentary sequence\n\nThe present reconstruction begins with the 1877 arrangements involving the\nMarquess of Bute and Daniel Thomas & Son and the subsequent quarrying\narrangements.\n\nMy understanding from the evidence presently assembled is that the relevant\nquarrying arrangements were completed by 1928 and that the family\nthereafter continued in occupation of Parcel A as its own land.\n\nThe later 1938 conveyancing from the Bute estate to Western Ground Rents\ntherefore requires close examination as to precisely what land was conveyed.\n\nThis is particularly important because the 1939 revised agricultural\ntenancy record naming Western Ground Rents as landlord appears, from its\nplan, to concern Parcel B rather than Parcel A.\n\nThat record was brought to my attention by the clerk for Llandough Town,\nwho directed me to the archive material shortly before his retirement\nearlier this year.\n\nIf Parcel A was not conveyed to Western Ground Rents in 1938, and was not\nincluded in the 1939 agricultural tenancy, the subsequent legal narrative\nby which the family's occupation came to be treated as permissive requires\nconsiderably more examination than HM Land Registry's present response\nappears to have given it.\n3. The successive attempts to characterise the family occupation as\npermissive\n\nThe historical evidence reveals a sequence of events which I believe needs\nto be examined as a whole rather than in isolation.\n\nThese include:\n\n*1949* — an attempted tenancy arrangement concerning Parcel B which appears\nto have sought to include my grandmother in the tenancy and thereby connect\nher occupation of Parcel A with the agricultural tenancy of Parcel B;\n\n*1951* — the disappearance of important family papers, including the\nfamily's title documentation, which my grandmother reported as stolen;\n\n*1955* — possession proceedings in which the language concerning the “whole\nfarm” requires comparison with the actual definition of the agricultural\nholding and its separate parcels. The order also expressly excluded the\n“house and garden” and referred to my grandmother's “condition and\nresistance”;\n\n*1959* — an offer of a tenancy of the farmhouse and garden, which my\ngrandmother rejected while continuing to assert an ownership claim;\n\n*1962* — a possession order concerning the farmhouse and garden, the legal\neffect and subsequent enforcement history of which require examination;\n\n*1965* — a further attempt to place “Mrs Williams” into a weekly tenancy,\nagain rejected;\n\n*1974* — an ownership action was adjourned; leave to enforce the 1962\npossession order was obtained but the warrant was not pursued; and BP\nsubsequently relied upon the two letters of 31 October 1974 as establishing\na licence relationship.\n\nThe family interpretation is that these were not independent, isolated\nevents but a succession of attempts to convert or present an allegedly\nproprietary occupation as permissive.\n\nI appreciate that this is an interpretation requiring documentary testing.\nThat is precisely why I believe HM Land Registry should have engaged with\nthe underlying documents rather than treating the later registered position\nand the 1987 Court of Appeal judgment as effectively resolving the matter.\n4. The 1987 judgment does not answer the documentary provenance question\n\nA particularly important part of my complaint concerns the way HM Land\nRegistry has relied upon *BP Properties Ltd v Buckler* [1987] EWCA Civ 2.\n\nI do not dispute that this was an important Court of Appeal decision.\n\nHowever, the case concerned the adverse-possession and possession issues\nconcerning the farmhouse and garden. The Court considered the\nregistered/paper-title position relied upon by BP, the previous possession\nproceedings and the legal effect of the 1974 letters upon the character of\nMrs Buckler's occupation.\n\nThe judgment itself records that Mary Williams had asserted that she had a\ntitle to Great House Farm through her grandfather and believed that\ndocuments existed establishing that title, but that such documents were not\nproduced.\n\nThe point is therefore not that the Court of Appeal “never touched title”.\nIt plainly considered BP's registered/paper-title position.\n\nThe point is narrower and, in my view, fundamental:\n\nThe 1987 judgment is not itself the conveyance, plan, root deed or other\ndocumentary instrument establishing how the woodland and the family's\nhistoric Parcel A entered BP's registered title.\n\nNor did it adjudicate the validity of Forest of Cardiff's separate 1994\nacquisition of the woodland.\n\nI am concerned that HM Land Registry's present response treats the 1987\njudgment as though it answers a documentary provenance question which it\ndid not determine.\n5. The 1969 conveyance and 1971 abstract\n\nHM Land Registry has also told me that BP supplied a title chain extending\nback to 1969.\n\nThat raises another substantial documentary issue.\n\nThere were only thirteen years between the 1969 conveyance and BP's first\nregistration in 1982.\n\nThe 1969 conveyance from Western Ground Rents to BP Pension Trust therefore\nrequires examination as to exactly what proprietary interest was being\nconveyed.\n\nI understand the later title documentation to recite the 1969 conveyance as\na root document, while the 1971 abstract refers to the 1962 possession\norder and an OS plan.\n\nIf the underlying title to Parcel A was never conveyed to Western Ground\nRents, the critical question is how an asserted interest arising from\npossession proceedings could subsequently be treated as though it were\ndocumentary title to the parcel itself.\n\nThe 1971 abstract is therefore particularly important.\n\nHM Land Registry's own correspondence has recorded that such an abstract\nwas lodged. The 30 July 2026 schedule of surviving first-registration\ndocuments does not identify the 31 December 1969 conveyance itself, nor\ndoes it resolve the evidential gap concerning the 1971 abstract.\n\nI am continuing to pursue those underlying records.\n6. The 1982–83 registrations\n\nMy principal concern is that BP may have registered not the family's actual\nhistoric Parcel A, supported by a lawful root title, but a later\nconstructed or “synthetic” parcel derived from the succession of\npossession, tenancy and licence documents which had been used to\ncharacterise the family occupation as permissive.\n\nI use “synthetic parcel” as a description of the issue I am investigating,\nnot as a statement that this is already legally established.\n\nThe essential question is:\n\nWhat precise land did BP apply to register in 1982–83, what documentary\ninstrument entitled BP to register that land, and did that land correspond\nto the land actually conveyed to BP's predecessors?\n\nThe later amalgamation of the BP titles is also significant because what\nmay have originated as separate parcels or interests appears subsequently\nto have been brought together within the registered estate.\n\nIt is necessary to establish whether that process merely recorded an\nexisting lawful title or whether it caused an underlying proprietary\nboundary to disappear within a later registered estate.\n7. HM Land Registry's response\n\nHM Land Registry has now maintained that it was correct to register the\nland and has relied, in part, upon the 1987 judgment.\n\nI respectfully submit that this does not answer the complaint.\n\nAn administrative conclusion that a registration was “correct” cannot\nitself reconstruct a missing conveyance, establish the precise boundary of\nland conveyed decades earlier, or demonstrate that a later registered\nproprietor had documentary title to a distinct parcel where the underlying\nconveyancing remains disputed.\n\nNor does the existence of the 1987 judgment establish the documentary\nprovenance of WA735527 or the validity of the separate 1994 transfer to\nForest of Cardiff.\n\nI am particularly concerned that HM Land Registry appears to regard its\nreliance upon the 1987 judgment as effectively validating the 1982–83\nregistration, when the registration itself pre-dated the judgment.\n\nThe chronology matters.\n\nThe registration came first.\n\nThe litigation came afterwards.\n\nI therefore cannot see how a later judgment concerning possession and\nadverse possession can, without further analysis, resolve whether the\nearlier registration accurately represented the documentary title existing\nat the date of registration.\n8. Notification and my grandmother's death\n\nAnother issue which requires consideration is notification.\n\nTo my knowledge, the family was not notified that BP had applied to\nregister the relevant land in 1982.\n\nMy grandmother died on 14 March 1983, shortly after the first registration\nand within the period in which the registration would have been capable of\nbeing challenged had she known of it.\n\nShe had spent decades rejecting attempts to characterise her occupation as\npermissive and consistently maintaining an ownership claim.\n\nThe fact that she died shortly after the registration is not itself proof\nof wrongdoing. It is, however, a material chronological fact when\nconsidering whether she had a meaningful opportunity to challenge the\nregistration and whether HM Land Registry appropriately considered the\nnotification history.\n\nI ask the ICR to consider whether HM Land Registry has adequately addressed:\n\n   - what notice was given of the 1982 application;\n   - to whom it was given;\n   - what information was supplied to the persons potentially affected;\n   - whether any competing ownership claim was disclosed by the applicant;\n   - and what records exist showing how HM Land Registry assessed those\n   matters.\n\n9. Possible fraud, concealment and institutional involvement\n\nThrough my research I have also identified matters which I consider may\nwarrant investigation for possible fraud, deliberate concealment,\nmisrepresentation, mistake and institutional involvement.\n\nI want to be precise about this.\n\nI am not asking the ICR to find that fraud occurred.\n\nI am asking the ICR to consider whether HM Land Registry adequately\ninvestigated evidence raising those questions, particularly where the\nevidence concerns missing historic documents, the distinction between\nParcel A and Parcel B, the provenance of the 1969 title, the 1971 abstract,\nthe 1982–83 registrations and the subsequent amalgamation of titles.\n\nI have deliberately distinguished established documentary facts from\nmatters which remain hypotheses requiring investigation.\n\nThere are also serious questions concerning the destruction or absence of\nhistorical records and the later treatment of the land. Those matters are\nbeing pursued through separate archival and documentary channels.\n10. The human and public-interest dimension\n\nThis is not merely an abstract registration dispute.\n\nMy grandmother was a disabled widow and amputee who maintained an ownership\nclaim to the family land through decades of litigation and repeated\nattempts to characterise her occupation as permissive.\n\nThe family was ultimately removed from the property in 1988. The historic\nfarmhouse, which had been our family home and which we believe had\nsignificant architectural and historic importance, was subsequently\ndemolished.\n\nI was a child when we were removed.\n\nFamily possessions and historic material were lost or dispersed, while the\nland was subsequently developed and altered.\n\nThe family's connection with the site is recorded as extending back to the\nseventeenth century, and I was born at Great House Farm.\n\nThere are also wider heritage and archaeological questions concerning the\nsite and its subsequent development, which I am investigating separately.\n\nI mention these matters because they explain why I have continued the\ninvestigation for approximately twenty years and why the accuracy of the\nunderlying land record matters so profoundly to my family.\n\nMy professional background is in military cyber and electronic warfare, and\nmuch of my research has involved reconstructing fragmented documentary\nhistories and identifying discrepancies between records. I have approached\nthis as an evidential investigation rather than simply as a family\ngrievance.\n11. Why I am approaching the ICR\n\nI therefore ask the ICR to review HM Land Registry's handling of my\ncomplaint and, in particular, whether it:\n\n   1.\n\n   adequately investigated the distinction between historic Parcel A and\n   Parcel B;\n   2.\n\n   adequately considered the 1938 conveyance and 1939 revised tenancy plan;\n   3.\n\n   adequately addressed the documentary provenance of the 1969 conveyance\n   and the 1971 abstract;\n   4.\n\n   adequately examined the precise land comprised in the 1982–83\n   registration applications;\n   5.\n\n   relied upon the 1987 *BP v Buckler* judgment for a proposition which the\n   judgment itself did not determine;\n   6.\n\n   adequately considered the chronology whereby the 1982–83 registration\n   preceded the 1987 judgment;\n   7.\n\n   adequately investigated the notification given to persons asserting\n   ownership of the land;\n   8.\n\n   adequately addressed the missing or unavailable historic documents and\n   the evidential gaps I have identified;\n   9.\n\n   adequately considered the evidence I supplied rather than simply\n   reaffirming the existing registered position; and\n   10.\n\n   provided reasons sufficient to explain why, notwithstanding those\n   unresolved documentary questions, HM Land Registry considers that no\n   mistake or registration error occurred.\n\nI am not asking the ICR to adjudicate the family's ultimate title claim.\n\nI am asking for an independent examination of whether HM Land Registry's\ninvestigation and reasoning were adequate, proportionate and properly\nresponsive to the evidence and issues actually raised.\n12. Documents and evidence\n\nI can provide the relevant correspondence with HM Land Registry, the\nresponse to my MP, registration material, historical conveyancing, court\nrecords, archival plans, family evidence and other documents relied upon in\nmy complaint.\n\nI also maintain a detailed chronology of the matter, including:\n\n1916 — agricultural tenancy;\n\n1928 — completion of the relevant quarrying arrangements;\n\n1938 — Bute / Western Ground Rents conveyancing;\n\n1939 — revised agricultural tenancy and plan;\n\n1949 — tenancy attempt;\n\n1951 — missing/stolen title documentation issue;\n\n1955 — possession proceedings;\n\n1959 — farmhouse and garden tenancy offer;\n\n1962 — possession order;\n\n1965 — further tenancy attempt;\n\n1969 — Western Ground Rents → BP Pension Trust conveyance;\n\n1971 — abstract and plan material;\n\n1974 — ownership proceedings, possession enforcement history and licence\nletters;\n\n1975 — BP Pension Trust → BP Properties transfer;\n\n1982 — first registration;\n\n1983 — subsequent registration and title dealings;\n\n1987 — *BP Properties Ltd v Buckler* Court of Appeal judgment;\n\n1988 — final removal from the farmhouse and demolition; and\n\n1994 — subsequent acquisition of the woodland by Forest of Cardiff.\n\nThe chronology is significant because it demonstrates why I do not consider\nthe 1987 judgment, standing alone, capable of answering the earlier\nregistration question.\n13. What I am asking for\n\nI respectfully ask the ICR to accept this as a request for independent\nreview of HM Land Registry's handling of the complaint.\n\nIn particular, I ask that the reviewer consider whether HM Land Registry\nhas actually answered the documentary questions raised, rather than merely\nreaffirming the registered position.\n\nI would also ask that the underlying Land Registry records relevant to the\n1982–83 registration, notification, supporting title documents, plans, the\n1969 conveyance and the 1971 abstract be considered where they remain\navailable.\n\nI am not seeking a finding against individual Land Registry staff.\n\nI am seeking an independent assessment of whether the institution's\nhandling of this matter has been adequate and whether the reasons given for\nrefusing to recognise a possible mistake are properly supported by the\nunderlying record.\n\nThe issue remains live because the disputed land is now being dealt with\nthrough subsequent titles and, in the case of the woodland, is presently\nsubject to a proposed disposal by Forest of Cardiff.\n\nI therefore respectfully ask the ICR to consider this matter on the basis\nof the documentary chronology and evidence rather than treating the\nexistence of the current register as dispositive of the historical question.\n\nI am willing to provide a structured evidence bundle and chronology to\nassist the review.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\nRequester \n"},{"id":"gm-1a0d677d2f33f0ab","subject":"Re: Freedom of Information Request: Reference CAS-347296-M0V4Y1 CRM:007302479","name":"Re: Freedom of Information Request: Reference CAS-347296-M0V4Y1 CRM:007302479","date":"2026-09-25T02:49:31Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0d677d2f33f0ab.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0cf0a5c1c2a0a0-freedom-of-information-request-reference-cas-347296-m0v4y1-crm-007302479","scopeTopics":["tna"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["tna"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0cf0a5c1c2a0a0","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"tna","name":"The National Archives"}],"refs":["CAS-347296"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0d677d2f33f0ab.json","textStatus":"available","text":"FOI Team,\n\nI write in response to your disclosure dated 23 September 2026 concerning\nmy Freedom of Information request regarding Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr and\nrecords relating to BP Properties Ltd v Buckler.\n\nI appreciate the additional catalogue references and the release\ninformation provided. However, your response also identifies a number of\nmatters concerning the scope, timing and methodology of the searches\nundertaken which require clarification.\n\nPlease therefore treat this email as a *formal request for internal review*,\nas well as a request for clarification of the matters set out below.\n1. Physical accession/transfer of YHL/PO/JO/10/11/2536\n\nYour response states that:\n\n“The series that this record formed part of were released on our catalogue\non Discovery on 20th May 2026.”\n\nYou also state that TNA *holds transfer paperwork* for:\n\n*YHL/PO/JO/10/11/2536 – Judicial Papers A-C 1-63*\n\nPlease therefore confirm, insofar as the information is recorded in TNA's\ntransfer/accession records:\n\n   - the date on which the physical records comprising YHL/PO/JO/10/11/2536\n   were transferred into the custody of The National Archives;\n   - the accession/transfer reference associated with that transfer, if\n   recorded;\n   - whether the transfer paperwork identifies the transferring body or\n   institution;\n   - whether the physical records were already in TNA's custody during my\n   earlier enquiries in August 2026, notwithstanding that their catalogue\n   description was only released on Discovery on 20 May 2026; and\n   - whether the *20 May 2026* date refers solely to publication/release of\n   catalogue metadata on Discovery, rather than the date on which the physical\n   records entered TNA custody.\n\nI am particularly seeking to distinguish *physical custody/accession*\nfrom *catalogue\npublication*, as these are not necessarily the same event.\n2. Scope and methodology of the initial search\n\nYour response states:\n\n“The initial response from our records enquiries team looked for the four\nterms (‘Great House Farm’, ‘Ty Mawr’, ‘Llandough’ or ‘Cedfin’) within the\nPRO and RW series and did not find any relevant information.”\n\nMy original request, however, expressly identified a number of alternative\nsearch routes, including:\n\n   - Western Ground Rents Ltd;\n   - BP Pension Trust Ltd;\n   - BP Properties Ltd;\n   - Mountjoy Ltd;\n   - BP Properties Ltd v Buckler;\n   - the 1982–1983 registration;\n   - the 1980–1988 government/public campaign;\n   - the 1988 eviction and demolition;\n   - Cadw and central-government communications;\n   - ECHR Application 14464/88;\n   - transfer, destruction or disposal records; and\n   - HMC9/311 and related archival references.\n\nYour present response confirms that a wider catalogue search was\nsubsequently undertaken and that searching *BP Properties Ltd* produced the\nHouse of Lords judicial record.\n\nPlease therefore clarify:\n\n   - whether the *initial search* was in fact limited to the four\n   property/location terms and the PRO/RW series stated in your response;\n   - why the named corporate litigant *BP Properties Ltd*, expressly\n   identified in my request, was not included in that initial search;\n   - whether any search log, enquiry record, search worksheet or other\n   recorded audit trail exists showing the terms, series and dates searched at\n   each stage; and\n   - whether the later wider search was materially different from the\n   initial search in a way that explains why the judicial record was not\n   identified earlier.\n\nI recognise that TNA cannot necessarily undertake an unlimited historical\nresearch exercise. My question here is narrower: *what searches were\nactually undertaken, when, and against which terms/series?*\n3. Search undertaken after 20 May 2026\n\nYour response states that the relevant parliamentary series had been\nreleased onto Discovery on *20 May 2026*, yet the subsequent correspondence\nrecords a negative result until the search parameters were expanded to\ninclude the named legal/corporate party.\n\nPlease explain, insofar as this is recorded:\n\n   - whether any searches of Discovery or relevant TNA catalogue data\n   concerning this matter were conducted between *20 May 2026 and 13 August\n   2026*;\n   - if so, whether those searches included “BP Properties Ltd” or the case\n   title *BP Properties Ltd v Buckler*; and\n   - if no such search was undertaken before 13 August 2026, please confirm\n   that fact.\n\nThis is important because the chronology appears to be:\n\n*20 May 2026 – catalogue release of the relevant series*\n*earlier enquiries – negative result*\n*13 August 2026 – explicit instruction to search the named corporate/legal\nparty*\n*subsequent wider search – judicial record identified*\n\nI am seeking to establish the documentary search history accurately, rather\nthan infer the reason for the earlier negative result.\n4. Transfer, retention, destruction or disposal records\n\nYour response states that TNA holds transfer paperwork for\nYHL/PO/JO/10/11/2536 and has disclosed three documents concerning its\nrelease/manifest arrangements.\n\nPlease confirm whether those records contain, or whether TNA otherwise\nholds, information showing:\n\n   - the date of transfer or accession;\n   - the previous custody/location of the records;\n   - whether the records were transferred directly from the Parliamentary\n   Archives or another repository;\n   - whether any associated material was excluded, withheld, destroyed,\n   disposed of or retained elsewhere; and\n   - whether the transfer documentation identifies any earlier repository\n   or custodial history.\n\nI am not asking TNA to reconstruct the contents of uncatalogued material. I\nam asking what *recorded provenance and custody information* TNA holds\nconcerning the records now identified.\n\nI would also take this opportunity to ask again, if you hold any more\ninformation I've asked for, not disclosed to me, please disclose in your\nnext reply. This case went before ECHR (Buckler vs United Kingdom 1989) and\ntheir window to reopen that ruling based on such matters as concealment, is\nas I understand, 6 months. It would be a grave injustice to deny or delay\nor tortioustly interfere with our quest for justice, anymore than has\nalready been the case for my heavily dispositioned family by this whole\nalready very prolonged, very  traumatising matter.\n5. Internal-review outcome\n\nPlease review the original response in light of the above and confirm\nwhether TNA considers that the searches undertaken adequately addressed the\nscope of my original request.\n\nWhere TNA considers that information is not held, please distinguish where\npossible between:\n\n*(a)* information establishing that no such records were ever transferred\nto TNA;\n*(b)* information showing that records were transferred elsewhere;\n*(c)* information showing that records were destroyed/disposed of; and\n*(d)* the absence of a discoverable catalogue reference or search hit.\n\nThese are materially different conclusions for the purposes of my\ndocumentary research.\n\nI would be grateful for the internal-review response within 14 working days.\n\nRegards,\n\n\nRequester \n\n\n\nOn Wed, 23 Sept 2026, 17:12 FOI Requests, <\n[email redacted]> wrote:\n\n> Dear Requester,\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you for your enquiry in which you asked for the information\n> concerning the existence, provenance, transfer, retention, destruction or\n> disposal of records relating to Ty Mawr / Great House Farm.\n>\n>\n>\n> Your request has been handled under the Freedom of Information (FOI) Act\n> 2000. The FOI Act gives you the right to know whether we hold the\n> information you want and to have it communicated to you, subject to any\n> exemptions which may apply.\n>\n>\n>\n> I can confirm that The National Archives holds some information relevant\n> to your request. We are pleased to be able to provide some of this\n> information to you.\n>\n>\n>\n> *Our response *\n>\n>\n>\n> The initial response from our records enquiries team looked for the four\n> terms (*“Great House Farm”, “Ty Mawr”, “Llandough” or “Cedfin”*) within\n> the PRO and RW series and did not find any relevant information. We have\n> now tried those terms and the further twelve terms that you have specified\n> in a wider catalogue search. We have provided the results of our search\n> below.\n>\n>\n>\n> The crux of your query is local land ownership dispute in Glamorgan and\n> the demolition of some buildings by a private company; generally speaking,\n> these are not matters dealt with by central government and we would not\n> expect to see anything within our collections. As such, there would have\n> been no records to transfer. The exception is the court case relating to BP\n> properties, which generated some papers. Links to the catalogue pages for\n> these records can be seen below.\n>\n>\n>\n> There are likely to be records of the farm in the 1940s as part of the\n> National Farm Survey series but these cannot all currently be searched\n> online by farm.\n>\n>\n>\n> We are unable to conduct this research on the archival collections on your\n> behalf without charging fees to conduct research on your behalf (Paid\n> Search). A public authority is allowed to charge fees under section 9 of\n> the Freedom of Information Act and within this section of the Act it\n> states: *\"provision is made by or under any enactment as to the fee may\n> be charged by the public authority for the disclosure of the information\"\n> (S9(5) FOIA). *\n>\n>\n>\n> The Public Records Act (1958) enables a statutory instrument to be made\n> setting out the fees which may be made in connection with the supply of\n> information from The National Archives. The prices we charge are set out by\n> our Fees Regulations <https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2025/1243/made>.\n>\n>\n>\n> If you are interested in paying for research to be conducted on your\n> behalf, then details of our Paid Search service and other options for\n> paying for research are available here: Paying for research - The\n> National Archives\n> <https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/help-with-your-research/paying-for-research/>\n>\n>\n>\n> If you would prefer to conduct this research yourself then you may find\n> the following research guide to be of assistance: National Farm Survey of\n> England and Wales 1941-1943 - The National Archives\n> <https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/help-with-your-research/research-guides/national-farm-survey-england-wales-1941-1943/>\n>\n>\n>\n> There are Agricultural Executive Committee minutes and papers for\n> Glamorgan; but again, these cannot be searched online by name of farm and\n> we are unable to conduct this research without charging a fee: Browse\n> records of other archives | The National Archives\n> <https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/browse/r/h/C42711>\n>\n>\n>\n> We can provide the following advice regarding the search terms you have\n> specified:\n>\n>\n>\n>    1.\n>\n>    *Western Ground Rents Ltd*\n>\n>\n>\n> Relevant information not found.\n>\n>\n>\n>    2.\n>\n>    *BP Pension Trust Ltd*\n>\n>\n>\n> Relevant information not found.\n>\n>\n>\n>    3.\n>\n>    *BP Properties Ltd*\n>\n>\n>\n> Judicial Papers A-C 1-63 | The National Archives\n> <https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/r/74c935f9-563a-4c1b-8643-ed3eed68ad98>\n> (House of Lords as court of appeal)\n>\n>\n>\n>    4.\n>\n>    *Mountjoy Ltd*\n>\n>\n>\n> Relevant information not found.\n>\n>\n>\n>    5.\n>\n>    *Proceedings in BP Properties Ltd v Buckler [1987] EWCA Civ 2*\n>\n>\n>\n> Judicial Papers A-C 1-63 | The National Archives\n> <https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/r/74c935f9-563a-4c1b-8643-ed3eed68ad98>\n> (House of Lords as court of appeal)\n>\n>\n>\n>    6.\n>\n>    *1982–1983 registration and associated government or administrative\n>    records*\n>\n>\n>\n> Relevant information not found.\n>\n>\n>\n>    7.\n>\n>    *1980–1988 public campaign and governmental correspondence*\n>\n>\n>\n> Relevant information not found.\n>\n>\n>\n>    8.\n>\n>    *1988 eviction and demolition*\n>\n>\n>\n> Relevant information not found.\n>\n>\n>\n>    9.\n>\n>    *Cadw or central-government communications concerning the property*\n>\n>\n>\n> Relevant information not found.\n>\n>\n>\n>    10.\n>\n>    *European Commission of Human Rights Application 14464/88*\n>\n>\n>\n> Relevant information not found.\n>\n>\n>\n>    11.\n>\n>    *Records concerning transfer, destruction or disposal of potentially\n>    relevant material*\n>\n>\n>\n> Within the results of our search, it revealed a record within the\n> parliamentary collection.\n>\n>\n>\n> This is *Judicial Papers A-C 1-63*\n> <https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/r/74c935f9-563a-4c1b-8643-ed3eed68ad98>\n>  . The parliamentary records and the provision of a service for them by\n> The National Archives, was done in phases, with their collections loaded\n> into Discovery in stages. The series that this record formed part of were\n> released on our catalogue on Discovery on 20th May 2026.\n>\n>\n>\n> This is an open record and available to the public here at The National\n> Archives, Kew. To note, this record requires preparation for production and\n> will take four working days to prepare\n>\n>\n>\n> TNA holds transfer paperwork for *YHL/PO/JO/10/11/2536 - Judicial Papers\n> A-C 1-63*\n> <https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/r/74c935f9-563a-4c1b-8643-ed3eed68ad98>.\n> The below table is a schedule of the documents that we have attached to\n> this response.\n>\n> *Document Title*\n>\n> *Document Description*\n>\n> *TNA’s Notes*\n>\n> Item 01\n>\n> *Release Schedule: On Discovery*\n>\n> This schedule shows the release date of 20th May.\n>\n>\n>\n> Relevant information to request provided. Some of the information is\n> additionally redacted as ‘out of scope’ and not relevant to the request.\n>\n> Item 02\n>\n> *Release Schedule: High Level Delivery Schedule*\n>\n> This schedule shows that it was scheduled for release in May\n>\n> Relevant information to request provided. Some of the information is\n> additionally redacted as ‘out of scope’ and not relevant to the request.\n>\n> Item 03\n>\n> *Collect 27 Series 11 Spreadsheet: Manifest*\n>\n> This spreadsheet is the manifest TNA received from the Parliamentary\n> Archives\n>\n> Relevant information to request provided. Some of the information is\n> additionally redacted as ‘out of scope’ and not relevant to the request.\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>    12.\n>\n>    *HMC9/311 or related archival references*\n>\n>\n>\n> This is Manorial Documents register returns the county of Glamorgan\n> organised by Manor alphabetically. The Manorial Documents Register\n> <https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/manor-search> can now be\n> searched online, by manor or parish and the results display in Discovery.\n> This search summary says it lists records 1189-1925 held at National\n> Library of Wales, Glamorgan Archives and West Glamorgan Archive Service: Search\n> results: llandough | The National Archives\n> <https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/results/c?_q=llandough&_sf=text_man&_naet=M&_st=mdrc>.\n> This date range is too early, individual farm names are not listed in the\n> titles, and the records are not held at TNA.\n>\n>\n>\n> This is the limit of what our catalogue metadata and corporate records\n> allow us to confirm.\n>\n>\n>\n> This concludes our response to your request.\n>\n>\n>\n> If you are dissatisfied with the handling of your request or the decision\n> which has been reached, you have the right to ask for an internal review.\n> Internal review requests must be submitted within two months of the date\n> of this response and should be addressed to:\n>\n>\n>\n> Quality Manager\n>\n> Visitor Experience\n>\n> The National Archives\n>\n> Kew\n>\n> Richmond\n>\n> Surrey\n>\n> [postcode redacted]\n>\n> [email redacted]\n>\n>\n>\n> Please mark your complaint clearly. You have the right to ask the\n> Information Commissioner (ICO) to investigate any aspect of your complaint.\n> However, please note that the ICO is likely to expect internal complaints\n> procedures to have been exhausted before beginning his investigation.\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n>\n>\n> *FOI Assessor*\n>\n> The Freedom of Information Centre\n> Government and Archives Leadership\n> The National Archives\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> *Your request*\n>\n>\n>\n> *Original email dated 13/08/2026:*\n>\n>\n>\n> *My request was deliberately framed more broadly than a simple catalogue\n> search for the words “Great House Farm”, “Ty Mawr”, “Llandough” or\n> “Cedfin”. I specifically asked The National Archives to identify recorded\n> information concerning the existence, transfer, retention, destruction,\n> disposal or absence of records, including records that might not be\n> catalogued under the property's later or local names.*\n>\n>\n>\n> *Your response confirms only that you searched the PRO and RW series\n> using:*\n>\n>\n>\n> *“Great House Farm” OR “Ty Mawr” OR Llandough OR Cedfin*\n>\n>\n>\n> *combined with “Glamorgan”, and that this produced no catalogue results.*\n>\n>\n>\n> *You also correctly state that Discovery searches catalogue titles and\n> descriptions rather than the contents of records. That qualification is\n> important, because it means the search described cannot establish that\n> relevant records did not exist, were never transferred, or were destroyed.*\n>\n>\n>\n> *In particular, your response does not appear to address the other\n> categories expressly identified in my request, including searches or\n> recorded information relating to:*\n>\n>\n>\n>    -\n>\n>    *Western Ground Rents Ltd;*\n>    -\n>\n>    *BP Pension Trust Ltd;*\n>    -\n>\n>    *BP Properties Ltd;*\n>    -\n>\n>    *Mountjoy Ltd;*\n>    -\n>\n>    *the proceedings in BP Properties Ltd v Buckler [1987] EWCA Civ 2;*\n>    -\n>\n>    *the 1982–1983 registration and associated government or\n>    administrative records;*\n>    -\n>\n>    *the 1980–1988 public campaign and governmental correspondence;*\n>    -\n>\n>    *the 1988 eviction and demolition;*\n>    -\n>\n>    *Cadw or central-government communications concerning the property;*\n>    -\n>\n>    *European Commission of Human Rights Application 14464/88;*\n>    -\n>\n>    *records concerning transfer, destruction or disposal of potentially\n>    relevant material; and*\n>    -\n>\n>    *HMC9/311 or related archival references.*\n>\n>\n>\n> *These alternative lines of enquiry are intentional. The purpose is to\n> establish whether relevant information can be identified through the\n> government bodies, corporate entities, court proceedings, administrative\n> processes and archival transfers associated with the property, rather than\n> relying solely upon the modern name by which the property is known.*\n>\n>\n>\n> *I am conducting corresponding enquiries with other repositories and\n> public bodies. This is specifically intended to triangulate the documentary\n> history where records may have been catalogued under a different creator,\n> department, legal proceeding, estate, corporate body or administrative\n> function.*\n>\n>\n>\n> *I therefore ask that you do not treat the absence of a Discovery keyword\n> hit as an answer to the substantive questions raised.*\n>\n>\n>\n> *Please clarify:*\n>\n>\n>\n>    1.\n>\n>    *Whether the searches undertaken were limited to the PRO and RW\n>    catalogue series and the four property/location search terms identified in\n>    your email.*\n>\n>\n>\n>    2.\n>\n>    *Whether The National Archives searched for the named corporate\n>    bodies, court proceedings and application references identified in my\n>    request, rather than only searching the property names.*\n>\n>\n>\n>    3.\n>\n>    *Whether The National Archives holds any recorded transfer, accession,\n>    disposal, destruction or retention information indicating that records\n>    concerning the relevant property, estate, companies, litigation or\n>    government involvement were transferred elsewhere, destroyed, or otherwise\n>    disposed of.*\n>\n>\n>\n>    4.\n>\n>    *If such information is not held, whether this is because no such\n>    records are held, because no relevant records were ever transferred to TNA,\n>    or because TNA's catalogue/search systems contain no discoverable\n>    description of them.*\n>\n>\n>\n>    5.\n>\n>    *Whether HMC9/311 or related series have been checked, or whether that\n>    reference falls outside the search undertaken.*\n>\n>\n>\n>    6.\n>\n>    *Whether there are central-government, court, departmental or\n>    administrative records relating to the litigation and entities identified\n>    above which may contain references to the property without using “Great\n>    House Farm”, “Ty Mawr”, “Llandough” or “Cedfin” in the catalogue\n>    description.*\n>\n>\n>\n> *For the avoidance of doubt, I am not asking The National Archives to\n> infer the contents of uncatalogued records or to undertake an unlimited\n> research project. I am asking for confirmation of the recorded information\n> held by TNA concerning the existence, provenance, transfer, retention,\n> destruction or disposal of potentially relevant records, and for the\n> searches undertaken to locate that information.*\n>\n>\n>\n> *That distinction is particularly important because a catalogue search\n> returning no result is not equivalent to a finding that no relevant\n> historical, governmental or archival records existed.*\n>\n>\n>\n> *I would therefore be grateful if this could be reconsidered as a response\n> to the scope of the original FOI enquiry rather than treated solely as a\n> request for assistance with a Discovery keyword search.*\n>\n>\n>\n> *I am continuing parallel enquiries with the National Library of Wales,\n> Glamorgan Archives, Cadw, RCAHMW, HM Land Registry and other relevant\n> bodies. The purpose of these enquiries is to establish the documentary\n> chain independently and to identify where records were created,\n> transferred, retained or potentially destroyed.*\n>\n>\n>\n> *Follow-up email dated 20/08/2026*\n>\n>\n>\n> *In addition, please expand the scope of the search to include any records\n> held relating to the Glamorgan War Agricultural Executive Committee (“War\n> Ag” / CWAEC) and Great House Farm / the relevant land at Llandough,\n> covering approximately 1936–1950 (with particular focus on 1939–1945). This\n> should include, where held:*\n>\n>\n>\n>    -\n>\n>    *Any orders, notices or decisions taking possession of, directing\n>    cultivation of, or otherwise requisitioning land forming part of Great\n>    House Farm (or the specific parcel now associated with Llandough Motor\n>    Sales / Llandough Trading Estate).*\n>    -\n>\n>    *Any compensation claims, assessments, payments or settlements under\n>    the Compensation (Defence) Act 1939 or related wartime legislation,\n>    including the identity of the recipient(s).*\n>    -\n>\n>    *Relevant minutes, correspondence or case files of the Glamorgan CWAEC\n>    concerning the farm or land in question.*\n>    -\n>\n>    *Any related material in the MAF series that links the National Farm\n>    Survey entries with wartime agricultural possession or compensation.*\n>\n>\n>\n> *I hold original documents recording compensation payments in connection\n> with this land and am seeking to identify the wartime status of the holding\n> and the party recognised as entitled to compensation.*\n>\n>\n>\n> *Clarification email dated 02/09/2026*\n>\n>\n>\n> *In summary, I am seeking recorded information held by The National\n> Archives concerning:*\n>\n>\n>\n>    -\n>\n>    *Existence, transfer, retention, destruction or disposal of records\n>    relating to Great House Farm / Ty Mawr (and earlier descriptions) and\n>    associated entities, litigation and administrative processes (as previously\n>    detailed).*\n>\n>\n>\n>    -\n>\n>    *National Farm Survey / MAFF records for the holding.*\n>\n>\n>\n>    -\n>\n>    *Any Glamorgan CWAEC / War Ag records, possession/requisition orders,\n>    cultivation directions, and compensation claims/payments under the\n>    Compensation (Defence) Act 1939 or related wartime legislation in respect\n>    of the land (or the parcel now associated with Llandough Motor Sales /\n>    Llandough Trading Estate), including identity of any compensation\n>    recipients.*\n>\n>\n>\n> *I am not asking for an open-ended research exercise. I am asking for\n> confirmation of what recorded information (including transfer, accession,\n> disposal or destruction records) is held, the series searched, and the\n> results of those searches.*\n>\n>\n> Please don't print this e-mail unless you really need to.\n> -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n> National Archives Disclaimer This email and any files transmitted with it\n> are intended solely for the use of the individual(s) to whom they are\n> addressed. If you are not the intended recipient and have received this\n> email in error, please notify the sender and delete the email. Opinions,\n> conclusions and other information in this message and attachments that do\n> not relate to the official business of The National Archives are neither\n> given nor endorsed by it.\n> ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n>\n>"},{"id":"gm-1a0d548fc34cb6b4","subject":"Introducing Great House Farm Wiki: Archival & Historical Record","name":"Introducing Great House Farm Wiki: Archival & Historical Record","date":"2026-09-24T21:18:45Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0d548fc34cb6b4.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0d548fc34cb6b4-introducing-great-house-farm-wiki-archival-historical-record","scopeTopics":["mp-vale"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["mp-vale"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0d548fc34cb6b4","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"mp-vale","name":"Member of Parliament (Vale of Glamorgan)"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0d548fc34cb6b4.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Hello,\n\nI would like to share a public documentary archive and historical research project: Great House Farm Wiki (Ty Mawr).\n\nWebsite: [Insert Great House Farm Wiki Link]\n\nThe archive documents the 150-year history of Great House Farm in Llandough-juxta-Penarth, South Wales. It brings together primary records from Glamorgan Archives, tithe apportionments, historical Ordnance Survey maps, lease instruments, and family records.\n\nKey highlights of the repository include:\n- A structured Two-Parcel Analysis of the land and historical titles.\n- Primary evidence records cross-referencing Bute Estate and Western Ground Rents tenancy documents.\n- A machine-readable index and evidence library for legal and local history research.\n\nIf you are interested in South Wales history, land archival records, or local heritage, please feel free to explore the wiki and share it within your network.\n\nBest regards,\nBob"},{"id":"gm-1a0d421cdc687541","subject":"RE: Epexio contact us form submission","name":"RE: Epexio contact us form submission","date":"2026-09-24T15:56:15Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0d421cdc687541.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a07b3bf0b65e56c-epexio-contact-us-form-submission","scopeTopics":["glamorgan"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["glamorgan"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a07b3bf0b65e56c","direction":"received","from":"Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg","institutions":[{"id":"glamorgan","name":"Glamorgan Archives"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0d421cdc687541.json","attachments":[{"name":"digital photography order form 2025.docx","title":"digital photography order form 2025.docx","url":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/pdf/foi/2026-09-07_glamorgan-archives_digital-photography-order-form-2025.docx","mime":"application/octet-stream","bytes":62339,"sha256":"6df6162232f5916a61446e394fc52207302fd79f8bacb0d41340320b0ef26131"}],"textStatus":"available","text":"Ref: 2026/4097\n\nDear Requester,\n\nThank you for your email received into our enquiries system 8th September.  Please find below answers to your questions in turn.  Please note that prices quoted are without VAT based on the fact that you are in Patagonia.\n\n**26 May 2026 (2026/2370)**\n1. 1974 demolition petition, Llandough juxta Penarth — ref or not held.\nThere is not a petition held within our collection. You would need to know who the petition was submitted to in order to search for any further reference to it.  If it was submitted to the planning authority there may be mention in the Planning Committee minutes and reports – see point 6 below.\n\n**29 May 2026 FOIA/EIR (2026/2372)**\n\n2. Williams/Buckler as owners, tenants, or reporters of the 1880 floor discovery.\nOne source of information about ownership would be rate books. UC/73/1 is a rate book for 1882 – this is the nearest we have to 1880.  A search of this (and later volumes UC/73/1-5 covers 1882-1902 and then UDPE/T/30 and UDPE/T/32 cover dates after 1900) could be undertaken by staff on your behalf at a charge of £50 which would include copies of relevant entries.\n\n3. Gaps / missing volumes / withdrawals 1950–1989.\nWe have never had a full set of records that would show the full tenancy/ownership history of Great House / Ty Mawr Farm. With all historical research of this nature, it is necessary to piece together information from surviving records that have been deposited with us.  There are not any records that have been withdrawn from our collections that would have provided additional information to this search for the period 1950-1989.\n\n4. Why the fabric was not scheduled before 1988 (or “Cadw holds this; we hold nothing”).\nNot held in our collections – suggest contacting Cadw and the Royal Commission on the Ancient and Historical Monuments of Wales.\n\n5. Internal preservation assessments.\nAs above - suggest contacting Cadw and the Royal Commission on the Ancient and Historical Monuments of Wales.\n\n6. Planning files for this acreage / demolition / Church View Close — file refs, not class names.\nWe do not hold planning files (it is possible some files may still be held by the Vale of Glamorgan Council Planning Department), but we do hold copies of the Planning Committee minutes and reports.  Mary Buckler started looking at the Planning Committee minutes and reports on her last visit to the searchroom on 13th August.  The document references to start with for these would be DCVG/C/4/36/76-77 and DCVG/C/4/36/94 these are for the planning applications 1989/01396/OUT and 1992/00671/RES.  It is possible that the minutes may make reference to any earlier applications or discussions.  A search of these records and anything else that they lead to could be carried out by staff at a charge of £50 to include copies of any relevant pages found.\n\n7. When the known archaeology was raised in planning.\nIf any record of this is in our collections it would appear in the Planning Committee records described above in point 6.\n\n8. EIAs / HER / developer briefs 1980–1990.\nThe HER for Wales is searchable via the Heneb website: Historic Environment Records (HER) - Heneb<https://heneb.org.uk/historic-environment-records-her/>.  You could also contact the Glamorgan Gwent Archaeological Trust to see if they have any relevant information.\n\n**27 July / 5 August 2026 (2026/3309)**\n9. DB/E/1–2 Stewart survey / Cedfin.\nThis was consulted and photographed by Mary Buckler in our public searchroom.  If you would like to receive copies of the pages relating to Ty Mawr this would be two images (one from DB/E/1 the survey and one from DB/E/2 the terrier).  Total cost £17 (£13 for the first image in the order and £4 for the next image).\n\n10. Lambert Williams–Bute agreements 1818–1835 (not the D160 maps already seen).\nNot held. Possibly at the National Library of Wales?\n\n11. Material related to NLW D219.\nNot held – this is a National Library of Wales reference: Llandough manorial leases and agreements, - National Library of Wales Archives and Manuscripts<https://archives.library.wales/index.php/llandough-manorial-leases-and-agreements> You should contact the NLW for access or copies.\n\n12. D153/4–8 1876 limeworks severance — not seen.\nNot held – this is a National Library of Wales reference: Deeds relating to premises in p. Llandough, co. Glam., comprising a dwelling house adjoining the churchyard, 1811 (D153/1-2), land (19 ..., - National Library of Wales Archives and Manuscripts<https://archives.library.wales/index.php/deeds-relating-to-premises-in-p-llandough-co-glam-comprising-dwelling-house-adjoining-churchyard-1811-d153-1-2-land-19>.  You should contact the NLW for access or copies.\n\n13. DXJF/64/27–32 full sketch bundle — notes seen; confirm sketches and refs.\nWe believe that the specific references within the bundle 27-32 that would be of interest to you are items DXJF/64/30-31: sketch of former layout of part of village of Llandough-juxta-Penarth by Mrs. Nancy Buckler of great house farm, Llandough, with notes c1950 [1 card, 1 paper]. Is that what you have already seen?  Other items in the bundle do not relate to Llandough.\n\n14. DCOWHS on Buckler of Penarth (not DCO/WHS/2).\nDCOWHS/2 is the only part of the DCOWHS (Cowbridge and District Local History Society) that relates to Buckler of Penarth. No other records held.\n\n15. Exact ref of the Nov 1877 counterpart already photographed; separate July 1877 counterpart?\nI think the items described below have been discussed previously – are these what you are seeking?  Please confirm and we can provide a quote for producing copies if required.\n\nDocument reference\n\n  *   DBDT/1/3\nTitle\n\n  *   Adamsdown Square leases from the trustees of the will of the late Marquess of Bute<https://glamorgan.epexio.com/en/records/DBDT/1-110/1>. Lease to Mr John Williams\nLevel of description\n\n  *   Item\nDate\n\n  *   Jul 1877\nExtent\n\n  *   1 parchment; plan in margin\n\n\nDocument reference\n\n  *   DBDT/18/1\nTitle\n\n  *   Lease (counterpart) of land and premises situate in Clyde Street, Roath from the trustees of the Will of the late Marquess of Bute to Mr John Williams\nLevel of description\n\n  *   Item\nDate\n\n  *   Nov 1877\nExtent\n\n  *   1 parchment; plan in margin\n\n16. Cardiff RDC records for this farm.\nNo records found. The area was not covered by Cardiff Rural District Council but rather Penarth Urban District Council.\n\n17. DRA 418–420 / DRA 5/31 if this site.\nNeither of these are complete document references from our collection.  Perhaps you could check these references or let us know what you think they should be and we will be happy to search our catalogue.\n\n18. UDPE/S/1/68.\nThis was viewed by Mary Buckler on 6th August and is a set of plans for a pair of semi-detached houses on the road between Dinas Powys and Llandough. If you would like copies of these plans 5 images will be required at a charge of £20.\n\n19. Penarth estate/tithe/OS/boundary plans of the house plot (not Cowbridge 1924/38).\nThe relevant estate map would be the Bute Estate survey which is referred to in point 9 above.  You can view the tithe map online here: Tithe Maps of Wales<https://places.library.wales/viewer/4622411#?cv=&h=95&xywh=10530%2C8509%2C3549%2C1196> along with the accompanying apportionment: Tithe Maps of Wales<https://places.library.wales/viewer/4542756#?cv=1&h=95&xywh=-507%2C-68%2C2508%2C897>.  Many historic Ordnance Survey maps can also be viewed online, for example through the National Library of Scotland website: Map Finder - with Outlines - National Library of Scotland<https://maps.nls.uk/geo/find/#zoom=15.3&lat=51.45225&lon=-3.20017&layers=101&b=1&z=0&point=51.44624,-3.20044&i=135196558>.\n\n20. WGR / Mountjoy / BP Pension Trust / BP Properties papers.\nThe only relevant papers we have are those mentioned in point 5.4 in my email of 7th September: collection reference D696 – book of plans and rental books for land owned by Western Ground Rents Ltd c1938-1944.  A search of these records could be carried out on your behalf at a charge of £50.\n\n21. Associated bundles stored separately.\nAll catalogues have been searched for your enquiry.\n\n22. Uncatalogued / recent deposits 1875–1988.\nA search of our accessions database, which records all records received into our collection even if they haven’t yet been catalogued, has not revealed any additional records.\n\n23. Photography for copies to Patagonia — rules.\nPlease find attached the digital photography order form which applies wherever you are based.  As mentioned above, prices quoted do not include VAT due to your location.  You need to sign and agree to the declaration on the order form.\n\n**22 August 2026 — no reply**\n\n24–29. DBDT10/19 and 10/20 identification of Daniel Thomas; Bute–Thomas search at Llandough juxta Penarth; quarry/lime/reversion instruments (poor-rate occupation already seen); later title; research cost.\nThese points were answered in my email of 7th September.\n\n**26 August 2026 — no reply**\n30–35. Physical examination of original **DBDT79/1** (catalogued May 1877, Pembroke Terrace). Treat as a different instrument from the Nov 1877 Llandough counterpart unless you confirm they are the same.\nThese points were answered in my email of 14th September.\n\nWe hope that this information is of use to you.  We will respond to other outstanding emails in due course.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nLouise Hunt\nArchifydd / Archivist\n\nArchifau Morgannwg / Glamorgan Archives\nClos Parc Morgannwg\nLecwydd / Leckwith\nCaerdydd / Cardiff\n[postcode redacted]\n\n[phone redacted]\n[email redacted]<[email redacted]>\n\nwww.archifaumorgannwg.gov.uk/<http://www.archifaumorgannwg.gov.uk/> www.glamarchives.gov.uk<http://www.glamarchives.gov.uk/>\nwww.facebook.com/glamarchives<http://www.facebook.com/glamarchives>\nwww.instagram.com/glamarchives<https://www.instagram.com/glamarchives/>\n\n**********************************************************************\nThe Council welcomes correspondence in Welsh, English or bilingually. We will ensure that we communicate with you in the language of your choice, as long as you let us know which you prefer. Corresponding in Welsh will not lead to delay.\n\nPrivileged/Confidential Information may be contained in this message. If you are not the addressee indicated in this message (or responsible for delivery of the message to such person), you may not copy or deliver this message to anyone. In such case, you should destroy this message and kindly notify the sender by reply email. Please advise immediately if you or your employer does not consent to Internet email for messages of this kind.  Opinions, conclusions and other information in this message that do not relate to the official business of the Council of the City and County of Cardiff shall be understood as neither given nor endorsed by it.  All e-mail sent to or from this address will be processed by Cardiff County Councils Corporate E-mail system and may be subject to scrutiny by someone other than the addressee.\n**********************************************************************\nMae’r Cyngor yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg, Saesneg neu’n ddwyieithog. Byddwn yn cyfathrebu â chi yn ôl eich dewis, dim ond i chi roi gwybod i ni pa un sydd well gennych. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi.\n\nMae'n bosibl bod gwybodaeth gyfrinachol yn y neges hon. Os na chyfeirir y neges atoch chi'n benodol (neu os nad ydych chi'n gyfrifol am drosglwyddo'r neges i'r person a enwir), yna ni chewch gopio na throsglwyddo'r neges. Mewn achos o'r fath, dylech ddinistrio'r neges a hysbysu'r anfonwr drwy e-bost ar unwaith. Rhowch wybod i'r anfonydd ar unwaith os nad ydych chi neu eich cyflogydd yn caniatau e-bost y Rhyngrwyd am negeseuon fel hon. Rhaid deall nad yw'r safbwyntiau, y casgliadau a'r wybodaeth arall yn y neges hon nad ydynt yn cyfeirio at fusnes swyddogol Cyngor Dinas a Sir Caerdydd yn cynrychioli barn y Cyngor Sir nad yn cael sel ei fendith. Caiff unrhyw negeseuon a anfonir at, neu o'r cyfeiriad e-bost hwn eu prosesu gan system E-bost Gorfforaethol Cyngor Sir Caerdydd a gallant gael eu harchwilio gan rywun heblaw'r person a enwir.\n**********************************************************************"},{"id":"gm-1a0d404548a13a54","subject":"Re: FOI \"Llandough Roman villa & medieval house finds were destroyed in a 1983 fire\" #628912926","name":"Re: FOI \"Llandough Roman villa & medieval house finds were destroyed in a 1983 fire\" #628912926","date":"2026-09-24T15:24:08Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0d404548a13a54.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0d404548a13a54-foi-llandough-roman-villa-medieval-house-finds-were-destroyed-in-a-1983-fire-628912926","scopeTopics":["tfw"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["tfw"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0d404548a13a54","direction":"received","from":"(name not shown; customersupport.tfw.wales)","institutions":[{"id":"tfw","name":"Transport for Wales"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0d404548a13a54.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dydd da,Diolch yn fawr am eich e-bost. Mae hwn yn gadarnhad ein bod wedi’i dderbyn, ac mae wedi’i anfon ymlaen at yr adran berthnasol er mwyn iddynt ei ystyried.Diolch,LloydAelod o Dîm Ymholiadau E-bost CadwMember of the Cadw Email Enquiry TeamGood day,Thank you for your email. This is to confirm that it has been received and has been forwarded to the relevant department for their attention.DiolchLloydAelod o Dîm Ymholiadau E-bost CadwMember of the Cadw Email Enquiry Team \n\nOn Thu, Sep 24, 2026 at 4:05 PM, Requester <[email redacted]> wrote:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nYou don't often get email from [email redacted]. \nLearn why this is important \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nEXTERNAL EMAIL WARNING\n\nThis email originated from outside of the organisation. You should take extra care and make sure this email is genuine before taking any action. If this email appears suspicious, use the\nReport button function in Outlook\nIf you've already interacted with a suspicious or malicious email, please contact TfW IT Service Desk\n\n\n\n\nDear Heneb: The Trust for Welsh Archaeology,\nI am writing to request access to recorded information concerning the March 1983 fire at the former Glamorgan-Gwent Archaeological Trust (GGAT) warehouse in Swansea, particularly the reported destruction of archaeological finds, excavation records and photographic\n material from Llandough, together with records concerning the subsequent custody, preservation, transfer and integrity of archaeological records relating to Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough.\nI recognise that Heneb is an independent private company limited by guarantee and registered charity rather than a body ordinarily subject to the Freedom of Information Act 2000.\nHowever, Heneb now maintains the statutory Historic Environment Records for Wales on behalf of the Welsh Ministers. Sections 194 and 195 of the Historic Environment (Wales) Act 2023 require the Welsh Ministers to maintain publicly accessible Historic Environment\n Records, including information concerning archaeological investigations and their findings.\nI therefore ask that this request be considered under any applicable statutory information-access regime, including the Environmental Information Regulations 2004 insofar as Heneb considers that it falls within their scope.\nIf Heneb considers that the EIR do not apply to any or all of the information requested, please state that expressly and identify the legal basis for that position. I would nevertheless be grateful if Heneb would consider the request voluntarily as an information-access\n request, particularly where the information forms part of, or supports, the statutory Historic Environment Record or associated archaeological archive maintained on behalf of the Welsh Ministers.\nI have copied Cadw and the Royal Commission on the Ancient and Historical Monuments of Wales because the request concerns the statutory HER system and records said to have been transferred or accessioned into the National Monuments Record of Wales.\nThe request is for existing recorded information only. I am not asking Heneb to create a new report or undertake fresh archaeological interpretation.\nPurpose and evidential context\nThis request arises from a wider forensic examination of the surviving documentary record concerning Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough.\nTwo companion forensic critiques, dated 21 September 2026, have been prepared concerning respectively Cadw and GGAT/Heneb. They are not presented as proof of dishonesty, bad faith, corruption or deliberate destruction. Rather, they identify a series of documentary\n gaps, inconsistent records and questions which cannot properly be resolved without access to the underlying primary records.\nIn particular, the GGAT critique identifies questions concerning the Trust's historical roles as archaeological adviser, contractor, investigator, curator and record-holder, and concerning the provenance and subsequent custody of the 1990 Great House Farm\n assessment and associated material.\nThe purpose of this request is therefore to establish, from the records themselves, what material existed, what was lost, what survived, where it was held, when it was transferred, and whether the present HER and archive accurately represent the historical\n record.\nThe two distinct archaeological areas at Llandough\nFor the avoidance of doubt, I ask that the following be treated as separate investigations.\n1. Llandough Farm / Roman villa area south of St Dochdwy's church\nGGAT undertook excavation in this area in 1978–79, with the HER identifying event GGATE001109 as \"Llandough Farm\". The associated site record concerns an Iron Age/Roman villa complex with later early-medieval burial activity.\n2. Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr) area north of St Dochdwy's church\nGGAT undertook an archaeological assessment/evaluation of Great House Farm in 1990, followed by the substantially larger Cotswold Archaeological Trust excavation in 1994.\nThese should not be conflated in the records or in any response to this request.\nPart A — March 1983 warehouse fire\nI have identified contemporaneous newspaper reports concerning the March 1983 fire at the GGAT Swansea warehouse.\nThose reports state that substantial quantities of archaeological material were destroyed and specifically identify finds from Llandough among the losses. They also report the destruction of a photographic studio/darkroom and a substantial quantity of photographic\n material.\nI therefore request:\n1. Any surviving inventory, catalogue, accession register, storage list, box list, find list, archive schedule or other contemporaneous record identifying archaeological material held at the Swansea warehouse immediately before the fire.\n2. Any surviving inventory, assessment or other record identifying material from the 1978–79 Llandough Farm excavation which was destroyed, lost, damaged or rendered unrecoverable in the fire.\n3. Any records identifying Llandough photographic material lost in the fire, including, where recorded:\n\noriginal negatives;contact sheets;prints;transparencies/slides;photographic registers or catalogues;site photography logs; andduplicate or working copies.\n4. Any post-fire salvage, recovery, reconciliation or damage-assessment records concerning archaeological material or photographic material recovered from the warehouse.\n5. Any insurance claim, loss schedule, valuation, stock reconciliation, internal audit, board/minute record, memorandum or other contemporaneous document assessing the scale or nature of the archaeological and photographic losses.\n6. Any correspondence concerning the fire or its archaeological losses with Cadw/Welsh Historic Monuments, the Welsh Office/Welsh Government, Amgueddfa Cymru/National Museum Wales, RCAHMW, local authorities, funding bodies, insurers, fire\n authorities or other archaeological organisations.\n7. Any GGAT annual report, internal report, memorandum or other record discussing the fire, the losses, salvage, replacement of records, reconstruction of catalogues or subsequent archive-management arrangements.\nPart B — What survived from the 1978–79 Llandough excavation\n8. Any surviving GGAT records showing what material from the Llandough Farm excavation survived the 1983 fire, including finds, site records, plans, drawings, photographs, notebooks, context records, catalogues, reports and publication material.\n9. Any surviving records showing where surviving Llandough material was subsequently stored, transferred, deposited or otherwise held.\n10. Any transfer, accession or deposit records identifying material from the Llandough excavation now held by Heneb, Amgueddfa Cymru/National Museum Wales, RCAHMW, the Archaeology Data Service or another repository.\n11. If Heneb no longer holds material formerly held by GGAT from this excavation, please provide any recorded information showing when and how that material ceased to be held by GGAT or its successor organisations.\nPart C — Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr): the 1990 GGAT assessment\n12. Any records showing the complete archival history and custody of the GGAT 1990 archaeological assessment of Great House Farm, including the report associated with HER record GGAT02038s.\n13. Please provide any surviving records showing:\n\nthe date on which the assessment was completed;the date on which it was received or entered into GGAT records;its original file or archive reference;where the original and working copies were held;any subsequent movement between GGAT offices, stores or repositories;any transfer or deposit to RCAHMW/the National Monuments Record of Wales;the date and reference of any such accession; andthe identity and present location of associated archive material.\nI understand that the 1990 assessment was accessioned into the National Monuments Record of Wales on\n20 March 2019.\nPlease confirm whether that date is correct and provide the underlying accession, transfer, deposit or cataloguing record.\n14. If the assessment was not accessioned into the National Monuments Record until 2019, please provide any recorded information showing where the assessment and associated archive material were held between completion of the assessment\n and that accession.\n15. Please provide any surviving pre-1990 or 1990 GGAT material relating to Great House Farm/Tŷ Mawr, including correspondence, field notes, photographs, plans, drawings, draft reports, site notebooks, assessment notes and archaeological\n planning correspondence.\n16. Please specifically provide any surviving material corresponding to, or identified as,\nAppendix B and Appendix E of the 1990 GGAT assessment, together with any records showing whether either appendix has been lost, separated from the principal report, transferred elsewhere, superseded, or otherwise disposed of.\n17. Any records concerning the preparation, internal review, approval, issue and circulation of the 1990 assessment and its appendices.\n18. Any records concerning the commissioning, procurement, quotation, specification, fee, contract, invoice or other administrative arrangements under which GGAT undertook the 1990 Great House Farm assessment.\n19. Any records showing what information supplied by GGAT about Great House Farm was entered into, amended within, cross-referenced within, or omitted from the then South Glamorgan/West Glamorgan Sites and Monuments Record.\n20. Any audit trail, change history, correction record or equivalent metadata showing subsequent amendments to the relevant HER records and references for Great House Farm/Tŷ Mawr.\nPart D — Relationship between the 1990 assessment and the 1994 excavation\n21. Any records showing what information or archive material from the 1990 GGAT assessment was supplied to, transferred to, or made available to Cotswold Archaeological Trust in connection with the 1994 excavation.\n22. Any correspondence between GGAT and Cotswold Archaeological Trust concerning the archaeological potential, results, interpretation, extent or recording of the Great House Farm site.\n23. Any records showing whether the 1990 GGAT trench plan, archaeological potential map, appendices or conclusions were used to determine the location, extent or methodology of the 1994 excavation.\n24. Any records identifying any material change between GGAT's 1990 assessment and the findings of the 1994 excavation, including any contemporary acknowledgment within GGAT's records that the 1994 findings differed materially from the conclusions\n or zoning of the 1990 assessment.\nPart E — The 1994 Cotswold Archaeological Trust excavation archive\n25. Any records held by Heneb concerning the surviving archive of the 1994 Great House Farm excavation, including information on where the archive was deposited or transferred.\n26. Any records showing the date, destination and scope of any transfer or deposit of the 1994 excavation archive, including:\n\nfinds;human-remains records;context sheets;plans and drawings;photographs;catalogues;databases; andspecialist reports.\n27. The published excavation record states that 1,026 inhumation burials were examined, whereas the current Heneb HER record states\n858 inhumations.\nPlease provide any records held by Heneb which explain the difference between these figures, including whether they represent different stages of excavation, different archaeological categories, different counting methodologies, or subsequent correction\n or revision of the HER.\n28. Any records showing whether material from the 1994 excavation remains with Heneb/GGAT, Cotswold Archaeology, Amgueddfa Cymru/National Museum Wales, RCAHMW, the Archaeology Data Service or another repository.\n29. I understand that archive material relating to the 1994 excavation was deposited in or around 2014. Please confirm whether this is correct and, if so, provide the relevant accession/deposit information and identify the repository concerned.\nPart F — Archive management following the 1983 fire\n30. Any records concerning the archive-management arrangements implemented by GGAT following the 1983 fire for the preservation, cataloguing, duplication, storage and eventual deposition of excavation archives, photographs and associated\n records.\n31. Any records showing whether records affected by the fire were reconstructed, recreated, copied, replaced or otherwise restored, and which archaeological projects were affected by any such work.\n32. Any records concerning subsequent reviews of the completeness of excavation archives affected by the fire.\n33. Any retention schedules, disposal schedules, archive policies, accession procedures or records-management guidance used by GGAT during the relevant period which governed the retention or disposal of archaeological project records and\n photographs.\nPart G — Present completeness and custody of the archaeological record\n34. Any surviving GGAT/Heneb records identifying missing, lost, destroyed, separated or otherwise unaccounted-for archive components relating to either the Llandough Farm excavation or Great House Farm/Tŷ Mawr.\n35. Any records showing whether the 1978–79 Llandough excavation archive, the 1990 Great House Farm assessment and the 1994 Great House Farm excavation archive were ever cross-referenced, reconciled or treated as related archaeological records.\n36. Any records identifying discrepancies between the historical GGAT records, the present Heneb HER, the National Monuments Record of Wales and the published archaeological reports concerning these sites.\nPart H — Heneb's statutory HER role and custody of information\n37. Please identify the legal and administrative basis on which Heneb maintains the relevant Historic Environment Record on behalf of the Welsh Ministers, including any agreement, contract, service specification, grant agreement, memorandum\n of understanding or other instrument governing that arrangement.\n38. Does Heneb consider that information forming part of the statutory Historic Environment Record is held:\na. by Heneb in its own capacity;\nb. by Heneb on behalf of the Welsh Ministers; or\nc. on some other legal or administrative basis?\nPlease explain.\n39. Where historical GGAT material now held by Heneb forms evidence supporting a statutory HER record, please identify whether the underlying source archive is regarded as part of the HER itself, associated reference material, or a separate\n archaeological archive.\n40. If any of the information requested above is held by Heneb solely on behalf of the Welsh Ministers, please identify the relevant Welsh Government/Cadw body responsible for access to that information and the applicable access regime.\nSearches and records no longer held\nI appreciate that some historical records may no longer survive.\nWhere information requested above is no longer held, please state that clearly and, where recorded information exists concerning its destruction, disposal, transfer, loss or separation, please provide that information.\nFor each material category where the original record is no longer held, please provide any surviving record showing:\n\nwhen it ceased to be held;why it ceased to be held;whether it was destroyed, lost, transferred or otherwise disposed of;whether any replacement, copy or reconstruction was made; andthe repository or organisation to which it was transferred, where applicable.\nIf Heneb's searches establish that a record formerly known to exist cannot now be located, please identify the relevant search terms, archive locations or record systems searched and the outcome of those searches, insofar as such information is itself recorded.\nWhere records have been transferred to another body, please identify that body together with any accession, archive or reference number known to Heneb.\nScope and cost\nIf Heneb considers that this request is too broad or that responding to it would exceed any applicable statutory cost limit, please identify the specific questions or categories causing the difficulty and advise how I can narrow the request while retaining\n the central questions concerning:\n\nthe scale and nature of the archaeological and photographic losses in the March 1983 warehouse fire;what material from the 1978–79 Llandough excavation survived;the complete custody history of the 1990 Great House Farm assessment and its appendices;the relationship between that assessment and the 1994 excavation;the whereabouts and completeness of the 1994 excavation archive; andthe present accuracy and provenance of the relevant HER records.\nIf some information is already publicly available or is held by another body, please identify the relevant repository and, where possible, provide the relevant archive, accession or catalogue reference rather than simply directing me to the organisation\n generally.\nThis request is made in the public interest and is intended to establish the documentary record. It should not be read as an allegation that any individual or institution acted dishonestly, in bad faith, corruptly, or deliberately destroyed or concealed\n evidence.\nI would be grateful for confirmation of receipt and for the request to be dealt with under the appropriate applicable information-access regime.\nRegards,\n\nRequester \n\n"},{"id":"gm-1a0d3f30a932fcc1","subject":"FOI \"Llandough Roman villa & medieval house finds were destroyed in a 1983 fire\"","name":"FOI \"Llandough Roman villa & medieval house finds were destroyed in a 1983 fire\"","date":"2026-09-24T15:05:15Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0d3f30a932fcc1.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0d3d85a7ef4715-foi-llandough-roman-villa-medieval-house-finds-were-destroyed-in-a-1983-fire","scopeTopics":["heneb"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["heneb"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0d3d85a7ef4715","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"heneb","name":"Heneb (formerly GGAT) / Historic Environment Record"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0d3f30a932fcc1.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Heneb: The Trust for Welsh Archaeology,\n\nI am writing to request access to recorded information concerning the March\n1983 fire at the former Glamorgan-Gwent Archaeological Trust (GGAT)\nwarehouse in Swansea, particularly the reported destruction of\narchaeological finds, excavation records and photographic material from\nLlandough, together with records concerning the subsequent custody,\npreservation, transfer and integrity of archaeological records relating to\nGreat House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough.\n\nI recognise that Heneb is an independent private company limited by\nguarantee and registered charity rather than a body ordinarily subject to\nthe Freedom of Information Act 2000.\n\nHowever, Heneb now maintains the statutory Historic Environment Records for\nWales on behalf of the Welsh Ministers. Sections 194 and 195 of the\nHistoric Environment (Wales) Act 2023 require the Welsh Ministers to\nmaintain publicly accessible Historic Environment Records, including\ninformation concerning archaeological investigations and their findings.\n\nI therefore ask that this request be considered under any applicable\nstatutory information-access regime, including the Environmental\nInformation Regulations 2004 insofar as Heneb considers that it falls\nwithin their scope.\n\nIf Heneb considers that the EIR do not apply to any or all of the\ninformation requested, please state that expressly and identify the legal\nbasis for that position. I would nevertheless be grateful if Heneb would\nconsider the request voluntarily as an information-access request,\nparticularly where the information forms part of, or supports, the\nstatutory Historic Environment Record or associated archaeological archive\nmaintained on behalf of the Welsh Ministers.\n\nI have copied Cadw and the Royal Commission on the Ancient and Historical\nMonuments of Wales because the request concerns the statutory HER system\nand records said to have been transferred or accessioned into the National\nMonuments Record of Wales.\n\nThe request is for existing recorded information only. I am not asking\nHeneb to create a new report or undertake fresh archaeological\ninterpretation.\nPurpose and evidential context\n\nThis request arises from a wider forensic examination of the surviving\ndocumentary record concerning Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough.\n\nTwo companion forensic critiques, dated 21 September 2026, have been\nprepared concerning respectively Cadw and GGAT/Heneb. They are not\npresented as proof of dishonesty, bad faith, corruption or deliberate\ndestruction. Rather, they identify a series of documentary gaps,\ninconsistent records and questions which cannot properly be resolved\nwithout access to the underlying primary records.\n\nIn particular, the GGAT critique identifies questions concerning the\nTrust's historical roles as archaeological adviser, contractor,\ninvestigator, curator and record-holder, and concerning the provenance and\nsubsequent custody of the 1990 Great House Farm assessment and associated\nmaterial.\n\nThe purpose of this request is therefore to establish, from the records\nthemselves, what material existed, what was lost, what survived, where it\nwas held, when it was transferred, and whether the present HER and archive\naccurately represent the historical record.\nThe two distinct archaeological areas at Llandough\n\nFor the avoidance of doubt, I ask that the following be treated as separate\ninvestigations.\n\n*1. Llandough Farm / Roman villa area south of St Dochdwy's church*\n\nGGAT undertook excavation in this area in 1978–79, with the HER identifying\nevent GGATE001109 as \"Llandough Farm\". The associated site record concerns\nan Iron Age/Roman villa complex with later early-medieval burial activity.\n\n*2. Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr) area north of St Dochdwy's church*\n\nGGAT undertook an archaeological assessment/evaluation of Great House Farm\nin 1990, followed by the substantially larger Cotswold Archaeological Trust\nexcavation in 1994.\n\nThese should not be conflated in the records or in any response to this\nrequest.\nPart A — March 1983 warehouse fire\n\nI have identified contemporaneous newspaper reports concerning the March\n1983 fire at the GGAT Swansea warehouse.\n\nThose reports state that substantial quantities of archaeological material\nwere destroyed and specifically identify finds from Llandough among the\nlosses. They also report the destruction of a photographic studio/darkroom\nand a substantial quantity of photographic material.\n\nI therefore request:\n\n*1.* Any surviving inventory, catalogue, accession register, storage list,\nbox list, find list, archive schedule or other contemporaneous record\nidentifying archaeological material held at the Swansea warehouse\nimmediately before the fire.\n\n*2.* Any surviving inventory, assessment or other record identifying\nmaterial from the 1978–79 Llandough Farm excavation which was destroyed,\nlost, damaged or rendered unrecoverable in the fire.\n\n*3.* Any records identifying Llandough photographic material lost in the\nfire, including, where recorded:\n\n   - original negatives;\n   - contact sheets;\n   - prints;\n   - transparencies/slides;\n   - photographic registers or catalogues;\n   - site photography logs; and\n   - duplicate or working copies.\n\n*4.* Any post-fire salvage, recovery, reconciliation or damage-assessment\nrecords concerning archaeological material or photographic material\nrecovered from the warehouse.\n\n*5.* Any insurance claim, loss schedule, valuation, stock reconciliation,\ninternal audit, board/minute record, memorandum or other contemporaneous\ndocument assessing the scale or nature of the archaeological and\nphotographic losses.\n\n*6.* Any correspondence concerning the fire or its archaeological losses\nwith Cadw/Welsh Historic Monuments, the Welsh Office/Welsh Government,\nAmgueddfa Cymru/National Museum Wales, RCAHMW, local authorities, funding\nbodies, insurers, fire authorities or other archaeological organisations.\n\n*7.* Any GGAT annual report, internal report, memorandum or other record\ndiscussing the fire, the losses, salvage, replacement of records,\nreconstruction of catalogues or subsequent archive-management arrangements.\nPart B — What survived from the 1978–79 Llandough excavation\n\n*8.* Any surviving GGAT records showing what material from the Llandough\nFarm excavation survived the 1983 fire, including finds, site records,\nplans, drawings, photographs, notebooks, context records, catalogues,\nreports and publication material.\n\n*9.* Any surviving records showing where surviving Llandough material was\nsubsequently stored, transferred, deposited or otherwise held.\n\n*10.* Any transfer, accession or deposit records identifying material from\nthe Llandough excavation now held by Heneb, Amgueddfa Cymru/National Museum\nWales, RCAHMW, the Archaeology Data Service or another repository.\n\n*11.* If Heneb no longer holds material formerly held by GGAT from this\nexcavation, please provide any recorded information showing when and how\nthat material ceased to be held by GGAT or its successor organisations.\nPart C — Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr): the 1990 GGAT assessment\n\n*12.* Any records showing the complete archival history and custody of the\nGGAT 1990 archaeological assessment of Great House Farm, including the\nreport associated with HER record GGAT02038s.\n\n*13.* Please provide any surviving records showing:\n\n   - the date on which the assessment was completed;\n   - the date on which it was received or entered into GGAT records;\n   - its original file or archive reference;\n   - where the original and working copies were held;\n   - any subsequent movement between GGAT offices, stores or repositories;\n   - any transfer or deposit to RCAHMW/the National Monuments Record of\n   Wales;\n   - the date and reference of any such accession; and\n   - the identity and present location of associated archive material.\n\nI understand that the 1990 assessment was accessioned into the National\nMonuments Record of Wales on *20 March 2019*.\n\nPlease confirm whether that date is correct and provide the underlying\naccession, transfer, deposit or cataloguing record.\n\n*14.* If the assessment was not accessioned into the National Monuments\nRecord until 2019, please provide any recorded information showing where\nthe assessment and associated archive material were held between completion\nof the assessment and that accession.\n\n*15.* Please provide any surviving pre-1990 or 1990 GGAT material relating\nto Great House Farm/Tŷ Mawr, including correspondence, field notes,\nphotographs, plans, drawings, draft reports, site notebooks, assessment\nnotes and archaeological planning correspondence.\n\n*16.* Please specifically provide any surviving material corresponding to,\nor identified as, *Appendix B* and *Appendix E* of the 1990 GGAT\nassessment, together with any records showing whether either appendix has\nbeen lost, separated from the principal report, transferred elsewhere,\nsuperseded, or otherwise disposed of.\n\n*17.* Any records concerning the preparation, internal review, approval,\nissue and circulation of the 1990 assessment and its appendices.\n\n*18.* Any records concerning the commissioning, procurement, quotation,\nspecification, fee, contract, invoice or other administrative arrangements\nunder which GGAT undertook the 1990 Great House Farm assessment.\n\n*19.* Any records showing what information supplied by GGAT about Great\nHouse Farm was entered into, amended within, cross-referenced within, or\nomitted from the then South Glamorgan/West Glamorgan Sites and Monuments\nRecord.\n\n*20.* Any audit trail, change history, correction record or equivalent\nmetadata showing subsequent amendments to the relevant HER records and\nreferences for Great House Farm/Tŷ Mawr.\nPart D — Relationship between the 1990 assessment and the 1994 excavation\n\n*21.* Any records showing what information or archive material from the\n1990 GGAT assessment was supplied to, transferred to, or made available to\nCotswold Archaeological Trust in connection with the 1994 excavation.\n\n*22.* Any correspondence between GGAT and Cotswold Archaeological Trust\nconcerning the archaeological potential, results, interpretation, extent or\nrecording of the Great House Farm site.\n\n*23.* Any records showing whether the 1990 GGAT trench plan, archaeological\npotential map, appendices or conclusions were used to determine the\nlocation, extent or methodology of the 1994 excavation.\n\n*24.* Any records identifying any material change between GGAT's 1990\nassessment and the findings of the 1994 excavation, including any\ncontemporary acknowledgment within GGAT's records that the 1994 findings\ndiffered materially from the conclusions or zoning of the 1990 assessment.\nPart E — The 1994 Cotswold Archaeological Trust excavation archive\n\n*25.* Any records held by Heneb concerning the surviving archive of the\n1994 Great House Farm excavation, including information on where the\narchive was deposited or transferred.\n\n*26.* Any records showing the date, destination and scope of any transfer\nor deposit of the 1994 excavation archive, including:\n\n   - finds;\n   - human-remains records;\n   - context sheets;\n   - plans and drawings;\n   - photographs;\n   - catalogues;\n   - databases; and\n   - specialist reports.\n\n*27.* The published excavation record states that *1,026 inhumation burials\nwere examined*, whereas the current Heneb HER record states *858\ninhumations*.\n\nPlease provide any records held by Heneb which explain the difference\nbetween these figures, including whether they represent different stages of\nexcavation, different archaeological categories, different counting\nmethodologies, or subsequent correction or revision of the HER.\n\n*28.* Any records showing whether material from the 1994 excavation remains\nwith Heneb/GGAT, Cotswold Archaeology, Amgueddfa Cymru/National Museum\nWales, RCAHMW, the Archaeology Data Service or another repository.\n\n*29.* I understand that archive material relating to the 1994 excavation\nwas deposited in or around 2014. Please confirm whether this is correct\nand, if so, provide the relevant accession/deposit information and identify\nthe repository concerned.\nPart F — Archive management following the 1983 fire\n\n*30.* Any records concerning the archive-management arrangements\nimplemented by GGAT following the 1983 fire for the preservation,\ncataloguing, duplication, storage and eventual deposition of excavation\narchives, photographs and associated records.\n\n*31.* Any records showing whether records affected by the fire were\nreconstructed, recreated, copied, replaced or otherwise restored, and which\narchaeological projects were affected by any such work.\n\n*32.* Any records concerning subsequent reviews of the completeness of\nexcavation archives affected by the fire.\n\n*33.* Any retention schedules, disposal schedules, archive policies,\naccession procedures or records-management guidance used by GGAT during the\nrelevant period which governed the retention or disposal of archaeological\nproject records and photographs.\nPart G — Present completeness and custody of the archaeological record\n\n*34.* Any surviving GGAT/Heneb records identifying missing, lost,\ndestroyed, separated or otherwise unaccounted-for archive components\nrelating to either the Llandough Farm excavation or Great House Farm/Tŷ\nMawr.\n\n*35.* Any records showing whether the 1978–79 Llandough excavation archive,\nthe 1990 Great House Farm assessment and the 1994 Great House Farm\nexcavation archive were ever cross-referenced, reconciled or treated as\nrelated archaeological records.\n\n*36.* Any records identifying discrepancies between the historical GGAT\nrecords, the present Heneb HER, the National Monuments Record of Wales and\nthe published archaeological reports concerning these sites.\nPart H — Heneb's statutory HER role and custody of information\n\n*37.* Please identify the legal and administrative basis on which Heneb\nmaintains the relevant Historic Environment Record on behalf of the Welsh\nMinisters, including any agreement, contract, service specification, grant\nagreement, memorandum of understanding or other instrument governing that\narrangement.\n\n*38.* Does Heneb consider that information forming part of the statutory\nHistoric Environment Record is held:\n\na. by Heneb in its own capacity;\n\nb. by Heneb on behalf of the Welsh Ministers; or\n\nc. on some other legal or administrative basis?\n\nPlease explain.\n\n*39.* Where historical GGAT material now held by Heneb forms evidence\nsupporting a statutory HER record, please identify whether the underlying\nsource archive is regarded as part of the HER itself, associated reference\nmaterial, or a separate archaeological archive.\n\n*40.* If any of the information requested above is held by Heneb solely on\nbehalf of the Welsh Ministers, please identify the relevant Welsh\nGovernment/Cadw body responsible for access to that information and the\napplicable access regime.\nSearches and records no longer held\n\nI appreciate that some historical records may no longer survive.\n\nWhere information requested above is no longer held, please state that\nclearly and, where recorded information exists concerning its destruction,\ndisposal, transfer, loss or separation, please provide that information.\n\nFor each material category where the original record is no longer held,\nplease provide any surviving record showing:\n\n   - when it ceased to be held;\n   - why it ceased to be held;\n   - whether it was destroyed, lost, transferred or otherwise disposed of;\n   - whether any replacement, copy or reconstruction was made; and\n   - the repository or organisation to which it was transferred, where\n   applicable.\n\nIf Heneb's searches establish that a record formerly known to exist cannot\nnow be located, please identify the relevant search terms, archive\nlocations or record systems searched and the outcome of those searches,\ninsofar as such information is itself recorded.\n\nWhere records have been transferred to another body, please identify that\nbody together with any accession, archive or reference number known to\nHeneb.\nScope and cost\n\nIf Heneb considers that this request is too broad or that responding to it\nwould exceed any applicable statutory cost limit, please identify the\nspecific questions or categories causing the difficulty and advise how I\ncan narrow the request while retaining the central questions concerning:\n\n   1. the scale and nature of the archaeological and photographic losses in\n   the March 1983 warehouse fire;\n   2. what material from the 1978–79 Llandough excavation survived;\n   3. the complete custody history of the 1990 Great House Farm assessment\n   and its appendices;\n   4. the relationship between that assessment and the 1994 excavation;\n   5. the whereabouts and completeness of the 1994 excavation archive; and\n   6. the present accuracy and provenance of the relevant HER records.\n\nIf some information is already publicly available or is held by another\nbody, please identify the relevant repository and, where possible, provide\nthe relevant archive, accession or catalogue reference rather than simply\ndirecting me to the organisation generally.\n\nThis request is made in the public interest and is intended to establish\nthe documentary record. It should not be read as an allegation that any\nindividual or institution acted dishonestly, in bad faith, corruptly, or\ndeliberately destroyed or concealed evidence.\n\nI would be grateful for confirmation of receipt and for the request to be\ndealt with under the appropriate applicable information-access regime.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester \n\n\n"},{"id":"gm-1a0cf0a5c1c2a0a0","subject":"Freedom of Information Request: Reference CAS-347296-M0V4Y1 CRM:007302479","name":"Freedom of Information Request: Reference CAS-347296-M0V4Y1 CRM:007302479","date":"2026-09-23T16:12:31Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0cf0a5c1c2a0a0.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0cf0a5c1c2a0a0-freedom-of-information-request-reference-cas-347296-m0v4y1-crm-007302479","scopeTopics":["tna"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["tna"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0cf0a5c1c2a0a0","direction":"received","from":"FOI Requests","institutions":[{"id":"tna","name":"The National Archives"}],"refs":["CAS-347296"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0cf0a5c1c2a0a0.json","attachments":[{"name":"Item 01 - Release Schedule_On Discovery_Redacted.csv","title":"CAS-347296 item 01: release schedule (on Discovery)","url":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/pdf/foi/2026-09-23_tna_cas-347296_item01-release-schedule-on-discovery.csv","mime":"text/csv","bytes":549,"sha256":"7050146d5c76fd542fc174736d0591dba7e69a40be9aca1ff3d5850e4c8a8df4"},{"name":"Item 02 - Release Schedule_High Level Delivery Schedule_Redacted.csv","title":"CAS-347296 item 02: high-level delivery schedule","url":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/pdf/foi/2026-09-23_tna_cas-347296_item02-high-level-delivery-schedule.csv","mime":"text/csv","bytes":176,"sha256":"2fa452194df3dc863cb4f4ff2b377f12b98e0f756ec9ed22f9f3800d1775f3aa"},{"name":"Item 03 - Collect 27 Series 11_Manifest_Redacted.csv","title":"CAS-347296 item 03: Collection 27 series 11 manifest","url":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/pdf/foi/2026-09-23_tna_cas-347296_item03-collect27-series11-manifest.csv","mime":"text/csv","bytes":93,"sha256":"10681e85f7a96fc29959599f757aed67ffb745f264d89139e2dd73f315d5daab"}],"textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester,\n\n\n\nThank you for your enquiry in which you asked for the information concerning the existence, provenance, transfer, retention, destruction or disposal of records relating to Ty Mawr / Great House Farm.\n\n\n\nYour request has been handled under the Freedom of Information (FOI) Act 2000. The FOI Act gives you the right to know whether we hold the information you want and to have it communicated to you, subject to any exemptions which may apply.\n\n\n\nI can confirm that The National Archives holds some information relevant to your request. We are pleased to be able to provide some of this information to you.\n\n\n\nOur response\n\n\n\nThe initial response from our records enquiries team looked for the four terms (“Great House Farm”, “Ty Mawr”, “Llandough” or “Cedfin”) within the PRO and RW series and did not find any relevant information. We have now tried those terms and the further twelve terms that you have specified in a wider catalogue search. We have provided the results of our search below.\n\n\n\nThe crux of your query is local land ownership dispute in Glamorgan and the demolition of some buildings by a private company; generally speaking, these are not matters dealt with by central government and we would not expect to see anything within our collections. As such, there would have been no records to transfer. The exception is the court case relating to BP properties, which generated some papers. Links to the catalogue pages for these records can be seen below.\n\n\n\nThere are likely to be records of the farm in the 1940s as part of the National Farm Survey series but these cannot all currently be searched online by farm.\n\n\n\nWe are unable to conduct this research on the archival collections on your behalf without charging fees to conduct research on your behalf (Paid Search). A public authority is allowed to charge fees under section 9 of the Freedom of Information Act and within this section of the Act it states: \"provision is made by or under any enactment as to the fee may be charged by the public authority for the disclosure of the information\" (S9(5) FOIA).\n\n\n\nThe Public Records Act (1958) enables a statutory instrument to be made setting out the fees which may be made in connection with the supply of information from The National Archives. The prices we charge are set out by our Fees Regulations<https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2025/1243/made>.\n\n\n\nIf you are interested in paying for research to be conducted on your behalf, then details of our Paid Search service and other options for paying for research are available here: Paying for research - The National Archives<https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/help-with-your-research/paying-for-research/>\n\n\n\nIf you would prefer to conduct this research yourself then you may find the following research guide to be of assistance: National Farm Survey of England and Wales 1941-1943 - The National Archives<https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/help-with-your-research/research-guides/national-farm-survey-england-wales-1941-1943/>\n\n\n\nThere are Agricultural Executive Committee minutes and papers for Glamorgan; but again, these cannot be searched online by name of farm and we are unable to conduct this research without charging a fee: Browse records of other archives | The National Archives<https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/browse/r/h/C42711>\n\n\n\nWe can provide the following advice regarding the search terms you have specified:\n\n\n\n  1.  Western Ground Rents Ltd\n\n\n\nRelevant information not found.\n\n\n\n  1.  BP Pension Trust Ltd\n\n\n\nRelevant information not found.\n\n\n\n  1.  BP Properties Ltd\n\n\n\nJudicial Papers A-C 1-63 | The National Archives<https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/r/74c935f9-563a-4c1b-8643-ed3eed68ad98> (House of Lords as court of appeal)\n\n\n\n  1.  Mountjoy Ltd\n\n\n\nRelevant information not found.\n\n\n\n  1.  Proceedings in BP Properties Ltd v Buckler [1987] EWCA Civ 2\n\n\n\nJudicial Papers A-C 1-63 | The National Archives<https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/r/74c935f9-563a-4c1b-8643-ed3eed68ad98> (House of Lords as court of appeal)\n\n\n\n  1.  1982–1983 registration and associated government or administrative records\n\n\n\nRelevant information not found.\n\n\n\n  1.  1980–1988 public campaign and governmental correspondence\n\n\n\nRelevant information not found.\n\n\n\n  1.  1988 eviction and demolition\n\n\n\nRelevant information not found.\n\n\n\n  1.  Cadw or central-government communications concerning the property\n\n\n\nRelevant information not found.\n\n\n\n  1.  European Commission of Human Rights Application 14464/88\n\n\n\nRelevant information not found.\n\n\n\n  1.  Records concerning transfer, destruction or disposal of potentially relevant material\n\n\n\nWithin the results of our search, it revealed a record within the parliamentary collection.\n\n\n\nThis is Judicial Papers A-C 1-63<https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/r/74c935f9-563a-4c1b-8643-ed3eed68ad98> . The parliamentary records and the provision of a service for them by The National Archives, was done in phases, with their collections loaded into Discovery in stages. The series that this record formed part of were released on our catalogue on Discovery on 20th May 2026.\n\n\n\nThis is an open record and available to the public here at The National Archives, Kew. To note, this record requires preparation for production and will take four working days to prepare\n\n\n\nTNA holds transfer paperwork for YHL/PO/JO/10/11/2536 - Judicial Papers A-C 1-63<https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/r/74c935f9-563a-4c1b-8643-ed3eed68ad98>. The below table is a schedule of the documents that we have attached to this response.\n\nDocument Title\n\nDocument Description\n\nTNA’s Notes\n\nItem 01\n\nRelease Schedule: On Discovery\n\nThis schedule shows the release date of 20th May.\n\n\n\nRelevant information to request provided. Some of the information is additionally redacted as ‘out of scope’ and not relevant to the request.\n\nItem 02\n\nRelease Schedule: High Level Delivery Schedule\n\nThis schedule shows that it was scheduled for release in May\n\nRelevant information to request provided. Some of the information is additionally redacted as ‘out of scope’ and not relevant to the request.\n\nItem 03\n\nCollect 27 Series 11 Spreadsheet: Manifest\n\nThis spreadsheet is the manifest TNA received from the Parliamentary Archives\n\nRelevant information to request provided. Some of the information is additionally redacted as ‘out of scope’ and not relevant to the request.\n\n\n\n\n\n  1.  HMC9/311 or related archival references\n\n\n\nThis is Manorial Documents register returns the county of Glamorgan organised by Manor alphabetically. The Manorial Documents Register<https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/manor-search> can now be searched online, by manor or parish and the results display in Discovery.  This search summary says it lists records 1189-1925 held at National Library of Wales, Glamorgan Archives and West Glamorgan Archive Service: Search results: llandough | The National Archives<https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/results/c?_q=llandough&_sf=text_man&_naet=M&_st=mdrc>. This date range is too early, individual farm names are not listed in the titles, and the records are not held at TNA.\n\n\n\nThis is the limit of what our catalogue metadata and corporate records allow us to confirm.\n\n\n\nThis concludes our response to your request.\n\n\n\nIf you are dissatisfied with the handling of your request or the decision which has been reached, you have the right to ask for an internal review.  Internal review requests must be submitted within two months of the date of this response and should be addressed to:\n\n\n\nQuality Manager\n\nVisitor Experience\n\nThe National Archives\n\nKew\n\nRichmond\n\nSurrey\n\n[postcode redacted]\n\n[email redacted]<[email redacted]>\n\n\n\nPlease mark your complaint clearly. You have the right to ask the Information Commissioner (ICO) to investigate any aspect of your complaint. However, please note that the ICO is likely to expect internal complaints procedures to have been exhausted before beginning his investigation.\n\n\n\n\n\nYours sincerely,\n\n\n\nFOI Assessor\n\nThe Freedom of Information Centre\nGovernment and Archives Leadership\nThe National Archives\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nYour request\n\n\n\nOriginal email dated 13/08/2026:\n\n\n\nMy request was deliberately framed more broadly than a simple catalogue search for the words “Great House Farm”, “Ty Mawr”, “Llandough” or “Cedfin”. I specifically asked The National Archives to identify recorded information concerning the existence, transfer, retention, destruction, disposal or absence of records, including records that might not be catalogued under the property's later or local names.\n\n\n\nYour response confirms only that you searched the PRO and RW series using:\n\n\n\n“Great House Farm” OR “Ty Mawr” OR Llandough OR Cedfin\n\n\n\ncombined with “Glamorgan”, and that this produced no catalogue results.\n\n\n\nYou also correctly state that Discovery searches catalogue titles and descriptions rather than the contents of records. That qualification is important, because it means the search described cannot establish that relevant records did not exist, were never transferred, or were destroyed.\n\n\n\nIn particular, your response does not appear to address the other categories expressly identified in my request, including searches or recorded information relating to:\n\n\n\n  *   Western Ground Rents Ltd;\n\n  *   BP Pension Trust Ltd;\n\n  *   BP Properties Ltd;\n\n  *   Mountjoy Ltd;\n\n  *   the proceedings in BP Properties Ltd v Buckler [1987] EWCA Civ 2;\n\n  *   the 1982–1983 registration and associated government or administrative records;\n\n  *   the 1980–1988 public campaign and governmental correspondence;\n\n  *   the 1988 eviction and demolition;\n\n  *   Cadw or central-government communications concerning the property;\n\n  *   European Commission of Human Rights Application 14464/88;\n\n  *   records concerning transfer, destruction or disposal of potentially relevant material; and\n\n  *   HMC9/311 or related archival references.\n\n\n\nThese alternative lines of enquiry are intentional. The purpose is to establish whether relevant information can be identified through the government bodies, corporate entities, court proceedings, administrative processes and archival transfers associated with the property, rather than relying solely upon the modern name by which the property is known.\n\n\n\nI am conducting corresponding enquiries with other repositories and public bodies. This is specifically intended to triangulate the documentary history where records may have been catalogued under a different creator, department, legal proceeding, estate, corporate body or administrative function.\n\n\n\nI therefore ask that you do not treat the absence of a Discovery keyword hit as an answer to the substantive questions raised.\n\n\n\nPlease clarify:\n\n\n\n  1.  Whether the searches undertaken were limited to the PRO and RW catalogue series and the four property/location search terms identified in your email.\n\n\n\n  1.  Whether The National Archives searched for the named corporate bodies, court proceedings and application references identified in my request, rather than only searching the property names.\n\n\n\n  1.  Whether The National Archives holds any recorded transfer, accession, disposal, destruction or retention information indicating that records concerning the relevant property, estate, companies, litigation or government involvement were transferred elsewhere, destroyed, or otherwise disposed of.\n\n\n\n  1.  If such information is not held, whether this is because no such records are held, because no relevant records were ever transferred to TNA, or because TNA's catalogue/search systems contain no discoverable description of them.\n\n\n\n  1.  Whether HMC9/311 or related series have been checked, or whether that reference falls outside the search undertaken.\n\n\n\n  1.  Whether there are central-government, court, departmental or administrative records relating to the litigation and entities identified above which may contain references to the property without using “Great House Farm”, “Ty Mawr”, “Llandough” or “Cedfin” in the catalogue description.\n\n\n\nFor the avoidance of doubt, I am not asking The National Archives to infer the contents of uncatalogued records or to undertake an unlimited research project. I am asking for confirmation of the recorded information held by TNA concerning the existence, provenance, transfer, retention, destruction or disposal of potentially relevant records, and for the searches undertaken to locate that information.\n\n\n\nThat distinction is particularly important because a catalogue search returning no result is not equivalent to a finding that no relevant historical, governmental or archival records existed.\n\n\n\nI would therefore be grateful if this could be reconsidered as a response to the scope of the original FOI enquiry rather than treated solely as a request for assistance with a Discovery keyword search.\n\n\n\nI am continuing parallel enquiries with the National Library of Wales, Glamorgan Archives, Cadw, RCAHMW, HM Land Registry and other relevant bodies. The purpose of these enquiries is to establish the documentary chain independently and to identify where records were created, transferred, retained or potentially destroyed.\n\n\n\nFollow-up email dated 20/08/2026\n\n\n\nIn addition, please expand the scope of the search to include any records held relating to the Glamorgan War Agricultural Executive Committee (“War Ag” / CWAEC) and Great House Farm / the relevant land at Llandough, covering approximately 1936–1950 (with particular focus on 1939–1945). This should include, where held:\n\n\n\n  *   Any orders, notices or decisions taking possession of, directing cultivation of, or otherwise requisitioning land forming part of Great House Farm (or the specific parcel now associated with Llandough Motor Sales / Llandough Trading Estate).\n\n  *   Any compensation claims, assessments, payments or settlements under the Compensation (Defence) Act 1939 or related wartime legislation, including the identity of the recipient(s).\n\n  *   Relevant minutes, correspondence or case files of the Glamorgan CWAEC concerning the farm or land in question.\n\n  *   Any related material in the MAF series that links the National Farm Survey entries with wartime agricultural possession or compensation.\n\n\n\nI hold original documents recording compensation payments in connection with this land and am seeking to identify the wartime status of the holding and the party recognised as entitled to compensation.\n\n\n\nClarification email dated 02/09/2026\n\n\n\nIn summary, I am seeking recorded information held by The National Archives concerning:\n\n\n\n  *   Existence, transfer, retention, destruction or disposal of records relating to Great House Farm / Ty Mawr (and earlier descriptions) and associated entities, litigation and administrative processes (as previously detailed).\n\n\n\n  *   National Farm Survey / MAFF records for the holding.\n\n\n\n  *   Any Glamorgan CWAEC / War Ag records, possession/requisition orders, cultivation directions, and compensation claims/payments under the Compensation (Defence) Act 1939 or related wartime legislation in respect of the land (or the parcel now associated with Llandough Motor Sales / Llandough Trading Estate), including identity of any compensation recipients.\n\n\n\nI am not asking for an open-ended research exercise. I am asking for confirmation of what recorded information (including transfer, accession, disposal or destruction records) is held, the series searched, and the results of those searches.\n\n\n\nPlease don't print this e-mail unless you really need to. ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- National Archives Disclaimer This email and any files transmitted with it are intended solely for the use of the individual(s) to whom they are addressed. If you are not the intended recipient and have received this email in error, please notify the sender and delete the email. Opinions, conclusions and other information in this message and attachments that do not relate to the official business of The National Archives are neither given nor endorsed by it. ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\nAttachments (3)\n- Item 01 - Release Schedule_On Discovery_Redacted.csv (text/csv, 1 KB): https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/pdf/foi/2026-09-23_tna_cas-347296_item01-release-schedule-on-discovery.csv\n- Item 02 - Release Schedule_High Level Delivery Schedule_Redacted.csv (text/csv, 1 KB): https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/pdf/foi/2026-09-23_tna_cas-347296_item02-high-level-delivery-schedule.csv\n- Item 03 - Collect 27 Series 11_Manifest_Redacted.csv (text/csv, 1 KB): https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/pdf/foi/2026-09-23_tna_cas-347296_item03-collect27-series11-manifest.csv"},{"id":"gm-1a0ca4852ba8b88a","subject":"RE: Acknowledgement from the Culture, Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee","name":"RE: Acknowledgement from the Culture, Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee","date":"2026-09-22T18:02:02Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0ca4852ba8b88a.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19ff079027ee0614-acknowledgement-from-the-culture-communications-cymraeg-and-sport-committee","scopeTopics":["senedd"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["senedd"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19ff079027ee0614","direction":"received","from":"Pwyllgor Diwylliant, Cyfathrebu, y Gymraeg a Chwaraeon - Culture, Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee","institutions":[{"id":"senedd","name":"Senedd"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0ca4852ba8b88a.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester,\n\nThank you for your email. As previously advised, Parliamentary committees do not have a role in reviewing or overseeing day-to-day operational decisions taken in individual cases. Accordingly, the Committee is unable to take forward the matters raised in your correspondence. I have enclosed a link to information explaining the criteria for submitting a petition to the Senedd.\n\nHow petitions work \n\nI have also explained that the appropriate channel for pursuing your complaint is with the public body concerned and thereafter with the Public Services Ombudsman for Wales.\n\nKind regards,\n\nHaidee\n\n[cid:image001.png@01DD4ABC.6D312FB0]\nHaidee James[cid:image002.png@01DD4ABC.6D312FB0]\nAil Glerc, Pwyllgor Diwylliant, Cyfathrebu, y Gymraeg a Chwaraeon, Senedd Cymru\nSecond Clerk, Culture, Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee, Welsh Parliament\n[phone redacted] Rhagenwau|Pronouns: hi – she/her\n\n\nFrom: Requester  \nSent: 17 September 2026 17:25\nTo: Pwyllgor Diwylliant, Cyfathrebu, y Gymraeg a Chwaraeon - Culture, Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee  \nSubject: Re: Acknowledgement from the Culture, Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee\n\n\nHaidee,\n\nThanks.\n\nI am also asking the Senedd to consider whether the circumstances surrounding the demolition of Great House Farm, Llandough-juxta-Penarth, and the subsequent planning and heritage history of the site warrant independent scrutiny and, ultimately, a public inquiry or Senedd committee inquiry.\n\nI am not asking the Senedd to determine a private property dispute or to decide legal questions that properly belong before the courts. My concern is the conduct, decision-making, record-keeping and accountability of public bodies responsible for heritage protection, planning and local government.\n\nThe surviving evidence presents a series of unusually important questions.\n\nGreat House Farm was professionally surveyed by the Royal Commission on the Ancient and Historical Monuments of Wales in 1974. In December 1988, Cadw was considering the building for emergency spot-listing, with a contemporary report stating that inspectors were due to visit and consider the case. The European Commission of Human Rights records that the Convention application had been introduced on 3 May 1988; BP obtained possession on 29 November 1988; demolition began on 6 December 1988; and the application was not registered by the Commission until 12 December 1988.\n\nThe evidence therefore establishes a very compressed sequence in which emergency heritage protection was being considered, domestic interim relief was refused, possession was obtained and the building was then destroyed within days.\n\nThat chronology does not, by itself, establish coordination, deliberate suppression or unlawful conduct. It does, however, warrant investigation.\n\nThere are several specific questions which I believe require answers from an authority independent of the present local authority:\n\n  1.  What information and records were actually considered by Cadw when the 1988 spot-listing decision was made?\n  2.  Was the 1974 RCAHMW architectural survey considered, and if not, why not?\n  3.  What communications took place between Cadw, South Glamorgan County Council, BP Properties Ltd, the courts and any other public bodies during the critical period immediately preceding demolition?\n  4.  What consideration was given by the local planning authority to the available statutory powers of preservation in circumstances where demolition was imminent?\n  5.  Why was the building destroyed before there could be an independent retrospective architectural examination of the fabric?\n  6.  How should the later archaeological evidence, including the discovery of approximately 1,026 burials in 1994, be understood in assessing the historic significance of the site and the adequacy of the earlier decision-making? I recognise that this later discovery cannot retrospectively prove that the 1988 listing decision was unlawful.\n  7.  A 1989 planning document appears to describe the site as having formerly contained Great House Farm “(a listed building)”. I ask that this apparent discrepancy be investigated and reconciled with the 1988 Cadw decision.\n  8.  What records relating to these decisions still exist, where are they held, and have reasonable steps been taken to preserve and disclose them?\n\nI am particularly concerned that the destruction of the physical building in December 1988 permanently removed primary architectural evidence which could otherwise have been examined independently.\n\nI also ask the Senedd to consider the wider public-interest question: whether the present mechanisms for accountability are adequate where a historic building is destroyed during a period of rapidly developing legal, planning and heritage proceedings and significant questions remain about what information was available to decision-makers.\n\nI would therefore ask the Petitions Committee to consider accepting a petition on this subject and, where appropriate, referring the matter to the relevant Senedd committee or committees for investigation. I would also welcome consideration of whether an independent public inquiry, committee inquiry or other form of external investigation would be appropriate.\n\nI am prepared to provide the underlying documents, chronology, court material, heritage records, planning documents and correspondence supporting these questions.\n\nI am not asking the Senedd to adopt my conclusions. I am asking that the evidence and unanswered questions be examined independently and that the relevant public bodies be required to account for what they did, what they knew, and what records exist.\n\nPlease confirm the appropriate route for submitting this formally to the Senedd and whether the material can be referred to the Petitions Committee for consideration.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\nRequester \n\n\n\n\nOn Wed, 12 Aug 2026, 10:13 Pwyllgor Diwylliant, Cyfathrebu, y Gymraeg a Chwaraeon - Culture, Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee,  > wrote:\nDear Requester,\n\nThank you for your further correspondence and for clarifying the purpose of your submission.\n\nI understand the distinction you draw between seeking a review of the original 1988 decision and raising broader concerns about record-keeping, archival practice, and public accountability. However, the Committee's role is to scrutinise and hold the Welsh Government and associated public bodies to account in relation to policy, governance, and the use of public resources.\n\nWhile I appreciate the broader administrative concerns you have identified, the issues raised in your correspondence arise from a specific historical case. The Committee does not have a role in reviewing or overseeing day-to-day operational decisions taken in individual cases. Accordingly, the Committee is unable to take forward the matters raised in your correspondence.\n\nIf you wish to pursue concerns regarding the management, retention or preservation of historical records, these matters are most appropriately raised with the relevant public authority or archival body responsible for those records. Similarly, concerns regarding the administration of a public body should be pursued through that body's complaints procedure and, where appropriate, with the Public Services Ombudsman for Wales.\n\nThank you for taking the time to write to the Committee and to set out your concerns in detail.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nHaidee\n\n[cid:image001.png@01DD4ABC.6D312FB0]\nHaidee James[cid:image002.png@01DD4ABC.6D312FB0]\nAil Glerc, Pwyllgor Diwylliant, Cyfathrebu, y Gymraeg a Chwaraeon, Senedd Cymru\nSecond Clerk, Culture, Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee, Welsh Parliament\n[phone redacted] Rhagenwau|Pronouns: hi – she/her\n\n\nFrom: Requester  >\nSent: 11 August 2026 16:57\nTo: Pwyllgor Diwylliant, Cyfathrebu, y Gymraeg a Chwaraeon - Culture, Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee  >\nSubject: Re: Acknowledgement from the Culture, Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee\n\n\nDear Ms James,\n\nThank you for your considered response.\n\nI understand and accept that the Committee does not have a remit to adjudicate upon an individual dispute, determine whether a historical decision was lawful, or direct Cadw to reopen a particular case. My submission was deliberately framed so as not to ask the Committee to do any of those things.\n\nI would, however, respectfully clarify one point, because I am concerned that the nature of the request may have been characterised more narrowly than intended.\n\nThe attached report expressly states that it is not a legal challenge, a forensic proof of wrongdoing, a complaint seeking adjudication, or a demand for reversal of the 1988 decision. Its stated purpose is historical record correction, archival explanation and administrative acknowledgement. In particular, it asks for an explanation of why almost none of the records concerning the 1988 assessment survive, whether that reflects the applicable record-retention arrangements, and whether the surviving independent evidence should be properly reflected in the permanent public record.\n\nThe central issue therefore extends beyond the merits of the individual 1988 listing assessment.\n\nCadw's 2026 FOI response confirms that an assessment took place, but that only two exterior photographs now survive; the assessment report, reasons for refusal, site notes, internal recommendations and consultation correspondence are no longer available. The report does not assert that this proves wrongdoing. Rather, it identifies a straightforward public-administration question: how can the adequacy and evidential basis of an irreversible public heritage decision be understood or scrutinised when the underlying administrative record no longer survives?\n\nThat seems potentially relevant to the Committee's stated function of scrutinising the administration and accountability of Welsh Government and associated public bodies, even if the Committee cannot itself determine whether the 1988 decision was correct.\n\nI am therefore not asking the Committee to investigate the Buckler family's historical dispute with BP, adjudicate upon the 1988 decision, or direct Cadw to reverse anything. The specific administrative questions raised in the report are instead:\n\n  1.  What record-retention arrangements applied to the 1988 assessment?\n  2.  Why do the assessment report, reasons, site notes and consultation records no longer survive?\n  3.  Were the records destroyed under an applicable retention policy or other authority, and were they considered for transfer to the appropriate archival institution?\n  4.  Is the present position consistent with Welsh Government/Cadw expectations for the preservation of records concerning significant heritage decisions?\n  5.  Are current arrangements sufficient to ensure that comparable heritage assessments, particularly where a building is under imminent threat of demolition, are properly documented and retained?\n\nThese are not questions that require the Committee to determine the rights of either party to the original dispute. They concern administrative practice, institutional record-keeping and the ability of the Senedd and the public to scrutinise decisions taken by public bodies. The report specifically asks Cadw to explain the archival position and review its current record-keeping practices for this reason.\n\nI would therefore be grateful if the Committee could reconsider whether the broader administrative and accountability issue falls within its scrutiny function, even if the Committee cannot examine the underlying individual dispute.\n\nIf the Committee considers that it nevertheless cannot engage with the matter, I would appreciate clarification as to which Senedd or Welsh Government scrutiny, oversight, archival or complaints mechanism is considered appropriate for raising a documented concern about the absence of the administrative record of a historical Cadw decision and the implications this has for public accountability.\n\nFor completeness, I should also emphasise that the report itself deliberately recognises the limits of the surviving evidence. It does not claim that Cadw acted unlawfully or in bad faith, and it expressly acknowledges that the absence of the 1988 working papers means that the assessment cannot now be reconstructed definitively.\n\nMy objective is therefore quite limited: to ensure that the surviving evidence is preserved, that the archival gap is properly explained, and that the public record accurately distinguishes what is established from what can no longer be verified.\n\nI hope that clarification assists, and I would be grateful if you could confirm whether the Committee is able to consider the matter from that wider public-administration and accountability perspective.\n\nKind regards,\n\nRequester\n\n\nOn Tue, 11 Aug 2026, 07:58 Pwyllgor Diwylliant, Cyfathrebu, y Gymraeg a Chwaraeon - Culture, Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee,  > wrote:\nDear Requester,\n\nThank you for your correspondence and for sharing the forensic review of Cadw's 1988 assessment of Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough. I acknowledge the strength of feeling expressed in your letter and recognise the longstanding concerns that you and your family have raised.\n\nThe Committee’s role is to scrutinise the work of the Welsh Government and associated public bodies, including holding ministers and public authorities to account for their policies, administration and use of public resources. However, the Committee does not have a remit to investigate, adjudicate upon, or oversee individual complaints or dispute. Nor does it have the powers to reopen historical decisions or direct public bodies to undertake investigations into specific matters.\n\nThe Committee is therefore unable to take on an oversight role in relation to the issues you raise or to intervene in these matters.\n\nThe matters you raise appear principally to concern the actions and decision-making processes of Cadw in relation to a specific historical assessment. Cadw are responsible for responding to requests for information about their records and decision-making processes, and for considering any representations made regarding the accuracy or completeness of the historical record.\n\nThank you again for writing to the Committee.\n\nKind regards,\n\nHaidee\n\n[cid:image001.png@01DD4ABC.6D312FB0]\nHaidee James[cid:image002.png@01DD4ABC.6D312FB0]\nAil Glerc, Pwyllgor Diwylliant, Cyfathrebu, y Gymraeg a Chwaraeon, Senedd Cymru\nSecond Clerk, Culture, Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee, Welsh Parliament\n[phone redacted] Rhagenwau|Pronouns: hi – she/her\n\n\nFrom: Requester  >\nSent: 10 August 2026 22:46\nTo: [email redacted] \nCc: [email redacted] ; [email redacted] ; DOUGHTY, Stephen  >\nSubject: Re: CADW failures – Revised Report (10 August 2026)\n\nSirs/Ma'ams,\n\nI write further to my email of 9 August 2026 submitting a review concerning Cadw’s 1988 assessment of Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough.\n\nPlease find attached (and linked below) a revised edition of the report, dated 10 August 2026. This version has been carefully restructured to present the same evidence in a more measured and administratively focused form. It is offered as a request for historical record correction, archival explanation, and administrative acknowledgement rather than as a forensic challenge.\n\nThe revised document:\n\n• Clearly states what it is and is not;\n• Sets out the surviving independent evidence (principally the H.J. Thomas RCAHMW survey of 1974);\n• Notes the near-total absence of Cadw’s 1988 working papers confirmed in ATISN 27021;\n• Asks only for realistic, achievable administrative steps: explanation of the archival position, reflection of the surviving evidence in the Historic Environment Record, acknowledgement of the records gap, and consideration of a local heritage marker.\n\nIt does not claim unlawfulness, seek compensation from Cadw, or demand a public inquiry.\n\nI hope this revised presentation is more palatable and will assist the relevant bodies in providing a constructive response.\n\nI look forward to your substantive response.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester\n\n[email redacted] \n[phone redacted]"},{"id":"gm-1a0c9d0a9a2d583f","subject":"Supplement to complaint re-served 20 September 2026 — Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough","name":"Supplement to complaint re-served 20 September 2026 — Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough","date":"2026-09-22T15:51:29Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0c9d0a9a2d583f.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0c990bf55b4ddd-supplement-to-complaint-re-served-20-september-2026-great-house-farm-t-mawr-llandough","scopeTopics":["cadw"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["cadw"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0c990bf55b4ddd","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"cadw","name":"Welsh Government / Cadw"}],"refs":["ATISN27021"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0c9d0a9a2d583f.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dr Wigley,\n\nThis is a supplement to the complaint re-served on 20 September, not a\nchaser. I do not expect a reply before the stage-one response. It adds\nseven matters under the existing headings at section 7, and one addition to\nthe outcomes at section 9, set out at the end.\n\nThe public interest is engaged alongside my family's. Great House Farm\nstood beside the Grade I church at Llandough, was recorded by the Royal\nCommission in 1974, sat within a known archaeological site, and was\ndescribed in the post-demolition planning record as a \"county treasure\". I\ndo not ask Cadw to treat the 1994 discoveries as knowledge it had in 1988.\nThey show what was lost; the pre-1988 record is the measure of Cadw's\nprocess.\n\nOn my family's account and the contemporary press report, protection was\nbeing sought in the first days of December 1988 and the house was\ndemolished almost immediately afterwards. I do not ask Cadw to determine\nresponsibility for the demolition. The grievance is narrower: protection\nwas sought at the critical moment, and Cadw cannot now show what it\nconsidered.\n\n1. The adopted development plan (matter A)\n\nThe planning officer's report on application 89/01396/OUT records that the\nsupporting information to Policy H.2 of the East Vale Local Plan \"states\nthat buildings on the site should be retained, however were unfortunately\ndemolished a little while ago.\" I do not say this bound Cadw or required\nlisting. I ask:\n(a) was Cadw aware of it in December 1988;\n(b) did the planning authority draw it to Cadw's attention; and\n(c) if neither can be established, will Cadw say so expressly.\n\n2. The search undertaken on 21 May (matters D and F)\n\nMr Weatherhead wrote that he had asked a colleague to search other paper\nfiles, \"including the Welsh Office files\", and would write again with the\nresult, while fairly warning that some files had quite likely been\ndestroyed. I have not received that letter. Please confirm whether the\nsearch was carried out, which series were searched, what was found, and\nwhether Cadw regards the ATISN 27021 response of 11 June as its report of\nthat search.\n\n3. The meaning of \"YYY\" (matter J)\n\nCadw has given two accounts of the marking — that the buildings were\n\"marginally below the bar for listing\" (21 May) and that the building did\nnot meet the criteria (10 July) — and one inference, that it did not meet\nthe criteria \"as it was not included on the list\" (29 July). I ask for one\nsettled meaning, the documentary source for it, and, if there is none, a\nstatement to that effect.\n\n4. The retention period (matter J)\n\nYour email of 27 August said that no records of the historic retention\narrangements are available, and that the three-year period is what current\ncolleagues advised from their knowledge of the systems then in place. I ask\nonly that the complaint response confirm expressly that this is an account\nof general practice, not evidence that the Great House Farm file was in\nfact destroyed after three years.\n\n5. Professional archaeological knowledge of this site before December 1988\n(matter C)\n\nOn 22 September 2026 Amgueddfa Cymru disclosed to me, under FOI 2026-032,\nits collections records for two accessions from this site. The first,\n82.44H, is the material from the Glamorgan-Gwent Archaeological Trust's\nexcavation of 1979 on land formerly part of Great House Farm, carried out\nin advance of housing development. The museum's records give the findspot\nas ST 168 733, the donor as Wales & West Housing Association Ltd, and the\npublished report as Owen-John 1988. The accession runs to several hundred\nentries and includes Roman coins, samian, glass, a bone scabbard slide and\nquerns, together with medieval pottery and ironwork.\n\nThis is not archaeology discovered after the event. By the time Cadw\nconsidered this building, the site had been professionally excavated, the\nfinds had been accessioned by the national museum under a 1982 accession\nnumber, and the excavation report had been published. I therefore ask, as\nan addition to matter C:\n(a) whether Cadw held, or had access to, the 1979 excavation record or its\npublished report when it considered the buildings in 1988;\n(b) whether that material was drawn to its attention by GGAT, the planning\nauthority or the Royal Commission; and\n(c) if neither can now be established, that Cadw say so in terms.\n\nI do not say this material required the building to be listed. I say that\nthe conclusion recorded in December 1988, that the site was not of\nsufficient archaeological interest, was reached against a professional\nrecord that already existed and is still held by the national museum today.\n\n6. The National Museum attribution (matter H)\n\nThe GGAT assessment of August 1990, which Cadw supplied to me on 21 May,\nstates that \"it is likely that some medieval ironwork which is held by the\nNational Museum of Wales was discovered under the rear wing c 1880 (RCAHMW\n1988).\"\n\nThe museum's disclosure allows that sentence to be tested, and the position\nis more precise than either of us has previously put it. The museum does\nhold medieval ironwork from this site — knife blades, arrowheads, a buckle\nand a pair of compasses within accession 82.44H — but as excavated material\nfrom 1979, not as an 1880 discovery beneath the rear wing. Its searches\nfound no accession, donation or deposit answering the c.1880 provenance at\nall. The museum's own explanation, given in its disclosure, is that objects\nrecorded as found at Church Farm, Llandough in 1858 and donated in 1963\nunder accession 63.24 \"could well be the basis for such a local\nstory/tradition\".\n\nI therefore no longer ask Cadw to accept that the attribution is\nuncorroborated in the round. I ask, more narrowly, whether Cadw holds\nanything supporting the c.1880 rear-wing provenance beyond the GGAT\nsentence it forwarded, and whether it will ask that the derived record note\nthe museum's findings rather than continue to carry the 1880 attribution\nunqualified.\n\n7. Correcting the record (matter H)\n\nYou directed me to Heneb as manager of the HER, which was reasonable. The\nonly substantive reply I have had from Heneb is its statement of 22 May\nthat it is not subject to FOI as a limited company; a schedule I sent on 19\nSeptember remains outstanding.\n\nThe museum's disclosure now gives a firm anchor for what the record should\nshow. Accession 95.56H comprises the material from the Cotswold\nArchaeological Trust excavation of 1994, donated by Ideal Homes Wales Ltd,\nand its locality is recorded in the museum's own collections database as\n\"Great House Farm Llandough\" at ST 1681 7331 — the grid reference carried\nby the historic environment record for this site. That accession runs to\nseveral hundred sets of early medieval human remains from this ground.\n\nAs the statutory duty to keep historic environment records rests with the\nWelsh Ministers, I ask:\n(a) which body is responsible for correcting an inaccurate or incomplete\nhistoric environment record;\n(b) what Cadw's own role is where the record is managed by Heneb; and\n(c) what Cadw will do if the entry for this site (GGAT02038s) is not\ncorrected to reflect the 1979 and 1994 excavations and the accessions\narising from them.\n\nCorrection to my letter of 20 September\n\nI said [email redacted] had rejected my correspondence on three occasions.\nHaving checked properly, it rejected messages on at least eight occasions\nbetween January and September 2026: 23 January; 9 May (twice); 17 May; 10\nAugust; 11 August; 29 August; and 17 September. Separate failures on 29\nAugust and 17 September came from the retired address [email redacted].\nI correct this because I have asked Cadw to distinguish what is established\nfrom what is inferred, and the same standard applies to me.\n\nAddition to the outcomes at section 9\n\n6. A written acknowledgement that the 21 May undertaking was not reported\non, and that Cadw's accounts of the 1988 assessment have varied.\n7. A single statement of what can and cannot now be established about the\n1988 decisions, which my family can rely on and cite.\n8. That Cadw write to Heneb and the Royal Commission asking that their\nrecords for this site reflect that statement and the museum's accession\nrecords.\n9. Consideration, under the Welsh Government complaints policy, of redress\nfor the time and trouble my family has spent since May pursuing questions\nthat Cadw's own records could not answer.\n\nI am content to supply the museum's disclosure to Cadw if that would\nassist, and my offer of 20 September stands: any 1988 Cadw correspondence\nrecovered from the Royal Commission or Heneb will be sent to you. I remain\ngrateful for the courtesy you and your colleagues have shown me.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nRequester \n"},{"id":"gm-1a0c8b62d56ff57c","subject":"FOI - 2026-032 - Great House Farm, Llandough","name":"FOI - 2026-032 - Great House Farm, Llandough","date":"2026-09-22T10:42:14Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0c8b62d56ff57c.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0c8b62d56ff57c-foi-2026-032-great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":["museum"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["museum"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0c8b62d56ff57c","direction":"received","from":"FOI Requests","institutions":[{"id":"museum","name":"Amgueddfa Cymru - Museum Wales"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0c8b62d56ff57c.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester\n\nThank you for your request for information, registered as FOI 2026-032.\n\nAs explained in our previous correspondence, this request has been considered separately from the internal review relating to FOI 2026-024.\n\nWe have identified a number of records relating to archaeological discoveries recorded at Great House Farm and Church Farm, both at Llandough. These records were identified during searches undertaken in connection with the earlier request but were not considered to fall within the scope of that request, which concerned a specific reported discovery.\n\nWe have now reviewed those records for the purposes of your new request.\n\nAttached are copies of the relevant records. Certain information has been redacted from the disclosed records under section 40(2) of the Freedom of Information Act 2000, where it comprises the personal data of third parties. Disclosure of that information would contravene the data protection principles set out in UK data protection legislation.\n\nTo assist interpretation, we have also included a brief descriptor for each record, together with any available reference or catalogue information held within the Museum's records.\n\nFurther to these documents, we have also identified some publicly available records which I have referenced below.\n\nAn Early-medieval Monastic Cemetery at Llandough, Glamorgan: Excavations in 1994: Medieval Archaeology: Vol 49 , No 1 - Get Access<https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/epdf/10.1179/007660905x54044?needAccess=true>\n\nHighlight 5: Llandough early medieval cemetery, Glamorgan (1994) - Cotswold Archaeology<https://cotswoldarchaeology.co.uk/highlight-5-llandough-early-medieval-cemetery-glamorgan-1994/>\n\nPlease note that the provision of these records should not be taken as indicating any association between them and the discovery you have described in FOI 2026-024. The records are being disclosed in response to your separate request for information relating to other archaeological discoveries recorded at the site.\n\nIf you are dissatisfied with the handling of this request, you may request an internal review within 40 working days of this response.\n\nCofion cynnes,\n\n[A picture containing text, tableware, plate, dishware  Description automatically generated]\nFOI Team\n[cid:image002.png@01DD4A87.0EE79000]\n[email redacted]<[email redacted]>\namgueddfa.cymru<applewebdata://288FE56C-5BFC-474C-ADB3-A1017EF25E69/amgueddfa.cymru> | museum.wales<applewebdata://288FE56C-5BFC-474C-ADB3-A1017EF25E69/museum.wales>\n\nRhif elusen | Charity number 525774\n\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]>\nSent: 26 August 2026 07:24\nTo: FOI Requests <[email redacted]>\nSubject: Re: FOI - 2026-024 - Great House Farm, Llandough\n\n\nThis email comes from outside the organisation.\n\nDo not click links or open attachments unless it is an email you expected to receive.\nDear FOI Team,\n\nThank you for your response.\n\nI am requesting an internal review of the handling of my request. I do not consider the response sufficient because it appears that the searches were conducted too narrowly around the later description of a \"medieval visor, lance, helmet or other item of armour\", rather than around the underlying historical discovery at Great House Farm itself.\n\nThe issue I am investigating is a reported nineteenth-century discovery at Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough. My understanding from contemporary or near-contemporary newspaper and historical accounts is that the discovery was described as involving the remains of a Roman soldier, with associated remains and objects. The dating and description of the individual objects as \"medieval\" may therefore itself be inaccurate, or may represent a later conflation or retelling of the original discovery.\n\nYour response is particularly significant because it states:\n\n<\"Our searches identified records relating to ... other archaeological discoveries recorded at Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough. These records do not relate to, nor are they otherwise associated with, the discovery described in your request. We have therefore not considered them to fall within the scope of this request.\">\n\nI respectfully challenge that approach.\n\nThe central question is precisely whether the discovery described in later historical accounts can be identified, corroborated, corrected or explained through the Museum's records concerning archaeological discoveries at Great House Farm. If the Museum has identified records relating to other discoveries at the same historically significant site, it is not sufficient simply to conclude internally that they are unrelated without disclosing what those discoveries are, when they were recorded, and the basis upon which that conclusion was reached.\n\nI therefore request that the internal review reconsider the scope of my request and undertake searches using, at minimum, the following terms and concepts:\n\n- Great House Farm\n- Ty Mawr\n- Llandough\n- Llandough, Cardiff\n- Roman soldier\n- Roman burial\n- Roman grave\n- Roman remains\n- human remains\n- skeleton\n- horse remains\n- equine remains\n- soldier and horse\n- cavalry burial\n- military burial\n- grave goods\n- Roman military equipment\n- visor\n- helmet\n- lance\n- spear\n- armour\n- archaeological discovery\n- nineteenth-century discovery\n- 1870 and adjacent years\n\nPlease also search for relevant records under alternative historical place-name indexing, donor names, findspot descriptions, parish names and catalogue terminology where the precise modern name \"Great House Farm\" may not have been used.\n\nMost importantly, I ask that you disclose sufficient information about the \"other archaeological discoveries\" which your searches identified at Great House Farm to enable me to understand and assess the Museum's conclusion that they are unrelated. This should include, so far as held:\n\n1. A description or title of each relevant discovery or record.\n2. The approximate date of the discovery or record.\n3. The nature of the material or remains involved.\n4. Any accession number, catalogue number or archival reference.\n5. The recorded provenance or findspot.\n6. Any dating attributed to the discovery.\n7. The basis upon which Amgueddfa Cymru concluded that each record was unrelated to the nineteenth-century discovery described in my request.\n\nI am not asking the Museum to create new archaeological opinions or undertake speculative research. I am asking for recorded information which has already been identified during the searches and which directly concerns archaeological discoveries at the specific site named in my request.\n\nIf the Museum maintains that any of those records fall outside the original scope, then please treat this email additionally as a refined request for the recorded information identified above.\n\nI would also ask the reviewer to consider whether the original response gave insufficient weight to the possibility that the historical account may have inaccurately dated or described the find. A search which excludes Roman, nineteenth-century, burial and equine terminology risks missing the very records capable of establishing what was actually discovered beneath Great House Farm.\n\nGiven that your searches have already identified records concerning archaeological discoveries at Great House Farm itself, I would be grateful for a full reconsideration and disclosure of the recorded information requested above.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\nRequester \n\n\nOn Tue, 25 Aug 2026, 04:25 FOI Requests, <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>> wrote:\nDear Requester,\n\nThank you for your request for information received on 27 July 2026 concerning records relating to a reported discovery at Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough, including a soldier, horse remains and items described as a visor and lance.\n\nAmgueddfa Cymru has undertaken searches of its collections management systems, accession records and associated documentation.\n\nWe have not identified any records relating to the acquisition, accession, donation, transfer or deposit of a medieval visor, lance, helmet or other item of armour associated with Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough. We have also not identified records relating to the specific discovery described in your request, including records of associated human or animal remains, reburial records, excavation reports, watching briefs or discovery reports.\n\nAccordingly, under section 1(1)(a) of the Freedom of Information Act 2000, our response is that Amgueddfa Cymru does not hold the requested information.\n\nFor completeness, our searches identified records relating to other archaeological finds from Church Farm, Llandough and to other archaeological discoveries recorded at Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough. These records do not relate to, nor are they otherwise associated with, the discovery described in your request. We have therefore not considered them to fall within the scope of this request.\n\nIf you are dissatisfied with this response, you may request an internal review by writing to [email redacted]<[email redacted]> within 40 working days of the date of this letter.\n\n\n\nCofion cynnes,\n\n[A picture containing text, tableware, plate, dishware    Description automatically generated]\nFOI Team\n[cid:image002.png@01DD4A87.0EE79000]\n[email redacted]<[email redacted]>\namgueddfa.cymru | museum.wales\n\nRhif elusen | Charity number 525774\n\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSent: 27 July 2026 02:22\nTo: FOI Requests <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSubject: ALLANOL/EXTERNAL - FOI - Great House Farm, Llandough\n\n\nThis email comes from outside the organisation.\n\nDo not click links or open attachments unless it is an email you expected to receive.\nYou don't often get email from [email redacted]<[email redacted]>. Learn why this is important<https://aka.ms/LearnAboutSenderIdentification>\nDear Freedom of Information Team,\n\nI am writing to request information under the Freedom of Information Act 2000 and, where applicable, the Environmental Information Regulations 2004.\n\nA published local history account states that, during works to replace the stone floor of the dining room at Great House Farm (also known as Ty Mawr), Llandough, Cardiff, the remains of a soldier and his horse were discovered beneath the floor.\n\n The remains were subsequently re-buried in the churchyard, and the soldier's visor and lance were given to the National Museum of Wales (Amgueddfa Cymru).\n\nI therefore request the following recorded information held by Amgueddfa Cymru:\n\nAny records relating to the acquisition, accession, donation, transfer or deposit of a medieval visor, lance, helmet, or other items of armour associated with Great House Farm / Ty Mawr / Llandough.\n\nThe accession number(s), date of acquisition or transfer, name of the donor or transferring body, and any provenance or discovery notes linked to these objects.\n\nAny catalogue entries, object records, or internal correspondence concerning the above items.\n\nAny records of associated human or animal remains from the same discovery, including notes on reburial.\n\nAny related excavation, watching-brief, or discovery reports held by the Museum.\n\nIf any of the requested information is already publicly available (for example via the Museum's online collections database), I would be grateful if you could simply provide the relevant accession numbers or catalogue links.\n\nPlease treat this as a request for recorded information only. I am not seeking advice or opinion.\n\nI look forward to your response within the statutory timescale.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\nRequester \n[cid:image003.jpg@01DD4A87.0EE79000]"},{"id":"gm-1a0c86e31d8df932","subject":"FOI Internal Review - 2026-024 - Great House Farm, Llandough","name":"FOI Internal Review - 2026-024 - Great House Farm, Llandough","date":"2026-09-22T09:24:10Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0c86e31d8df932.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0c86e31d8df932-foi-internal-review-2026-024-great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":["museum"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["museum"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0c86e31d8df932","direction":"received","from":"FOI Requests","institutions":[{"id":"museum","name":"Amgueddfa Cymru - Museum Wales"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0c86e31d8df932.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester\n\nThank you for your request for an internal review of Amgueddfa Cymru's response to your Freedom of Information request concerning a reported archaeological discovery at Great House Farm, Llandough.\n\nI have now completed my review of the handling of your request, including the searches undertaken and the concerns raised in your request for review.\n\nHaving reviewed the original request, the searches conducted and the response provided, I am satisfied that the request was handled appropriately and that reasonable and proportionate searches were undertaken in those locations where information falling within the scope of the request would reasonably be expected to be held.\n\nIn your request for review, you suggest that additional searches should have been undertaken using a wider range of terms and concepts, including references to Roman burials, military burials, cavalry burials, human remains, horse remains and other archaeological terminology. I have carefully considered this point. However, when assessing whether information is held for the purposes of the Freedom of Information Act 2000, the relevant issue is not whether every conceivable search term has been used, but whether the authority has undertaken searches that are reasonable and proportionate in the circumstances and reasonably likely to identify relevant information if held.\n\nYour request concerned a specific reported discovery at a named location. Accordingly, the location identified in the request was used as the primary search criterion. Searches were undertaken across Amgueddfa Cymru's information holdings where records relating to acquisitions, accessions, archaeological finds, provenance information and associated correspondence would reasonably be expected to be held. This included collections management systems, accession records, archived documentation, shared electronic records and other organisational record repositories.\n\nRecords identified through those searches were reviewed by collections staff and the FOI team to determine whether they related to the reported discovery described in your request. As the search methodology was location-based rather than focused on a particular object type, archaeological period or interpretation, it was reasonably likely of identifying relevant records regardless of whether associated material had been recorded as Roman, medieval or otherwise.  I have therefore considered your suggestion that later historical accounts may have inaccurately described the nature, date or attribution of the reported discovery. I am satisfied that this possibility would not have affected the outcome of the searches undertaken.\n\nI have also considered whether broader keyword searches would have been appropriate. Given the scale and diversity of Amgueddfa Cymru's collections and records, generic terms such as \"horse\", \"burial\" or \"skeleton\" would retrieve large volumes of records relating to many different sites and periods and would not provide a reliable or proportionate means of locating records relating to a specific reported discovery at a named location. I am satisfied that the location-based search strategy adopted was the most appropriate and proportionate approach.\n\nThe searches undertaken did not identify any records relating to:\n* the acquisition, accession, donation, transfer or deposit of a visor, lance, helmet, armour or related objects associated with the discovery described in your request;\n* associated human or animal remains connected with that discovery;\n* reburial records relating to that discovery; or\n* excavation reports, discovery reports or watching-brief records relating to the discovery described in your request.\n\nHaving reviewed both the scope of the original request and the searches undertaken, I am satisfied on the balance of probabilities that, if information relating to the reported discovery were held by Amgueddfa Cymru, those searches would have been likely to identify it.\n\nYou also raised concerns regarding the reference in the original response to other archaeological records identified during the course of the searches. Those records were reviewed and were not considered to relate to, or otherwise be associated with, the reported discovery described in your request. However, I can confirm that this aspect has been subject to further consideration and a separate response will be issued in relation to those records.\n\nI therefore uphold the original conclusion that Amgueddfa Cymru does not hold recorded information falling within the scope of your request.\n\nThis concludes Amgueddfa Cymru's internal review process. If you remain dissatisfied, you may complain to the Information Commissioner's Office under section 50 of the Freedom of Information Act 2000.\n\n\nCofion cynnes,\n\n[A picture containing text, tableware, plate, dishware  Description automatically generated]\nFOI Team\n[cid:image002.png@01DD4A6D.9E375520]\n[email redacted]<[email redacted]>\namgueddfa.cymru<applewebdata://288FE56C-5BFC-474C-ADB3-A1017EF25E69/amgueddfa.cymru> | museum.wales<applewebdata://288FE56C-5BFC-474C-ADB3-A1017EF25E69/museum.wales>\n\nRhif elusen | Charity number 525774\n\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]>\nSent: 26 August 2026 07:24\nTo: FOI Requests <[email redacted]>\nSubject: Re: FOI - 2026-024 - Great House Farm, Llandough\n\n\nThis email comes from outside the organisation.\n\nDo not click links or open attachments unless it is an email you expected to receive.\nDear FOI Team,\n\nThank you for your response.\n\nI am requesting an internal review of the handling of my request. I 