Cardiff\n- Roman soldier\n- Roman burial\n- Roman grave\n- Roman remains\n- human remains\n- skeleton\n- horse remains\n- equine remains\n- soldier and horse\n- cavalry burial\n- military burial\n- grave goods\n- Roman military equipment\n- visor\n- helmet\n- lance\n- spear\n- armour\n- archaeological discovery\n- nineteenth-century discovery\n- 1870 and adjacent years\n\nPlease also search for relevant records under alternative historical place-name indexing, donor names, findspot descriptions, parish names and catalogue terminology where the precise modern name \"Great House Farm\" may not have been used.\n\nMost importantly, I ask that you disclose sufficient information about the \"other archaeological discoveries\" which your searches identified at Great House Farm to enable me to understand and assess the Museum's conclusion that they are unrelated. This should include, so far as held:\n\n1. A description or title of each relevant discovery or record.\n2. The approximate date of the discovery or record.\n3. The nature of the material or remains involved.\n4. Any accession number, catalogue number or archival reference.\n5. The recorded provenance or findspot.\n6. Any dating attributed to the discovery.\n7. The basis upon which Amgueddfa Cymru concluded that each record was unrelated to the nineteenth-century discovery described in my request.\n\nI am not asking the Museum to create new archaeological opinions or undertake speculative research. I am asking for recorded information which has already been identified during the searches and which directly concerns archaeological discoveries at the specific site named in my request.\n\nIf the Museum maintains that any of those records fall outside the original scope, then please treat this email additionally as a refined request for the recorded information identified above.\n\nI would also ask the reviewer to consider whether the original response gave insufficient weight to the possibility that the historical account may have inaccurately dated or described the find. A search which excludes Roman, nineteenth-century, burial and equine terminology risks missing the very records capable of establishing what was actually discovered beneath Great House Farm.\n\nGiven that your searches have already identified records concerning archaeological discoveries at Great House Farm itself, I would be grateful for a full reconsideration and disclosure of the recorded information requested above.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\nRequester \n\n\nOn Tue, 25 Aug 2026, 04:25 FOI Requests, <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>> wrote:\nDear Requester,\n\nThank you for your request for information received on 27 July 2026 concerning records relating to a reported discovery at Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough, including a soldier, horse remains and items described as a visor and lance.\n\nAmgueddfa Cymru has undertaken searches of its collections management systems, accession records and associated documentation.\n\nWe have not identified any records relating to the acquisition, accession, donation, transfer or deposit of a medieval visor, lance, helmet or other item of armour associated with Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough. We have also not identified records relating to the specific discovery described in your request, including records of associated human or animal remains, reburial records, excavation reports, watching briefs or discovery reports.\n\nAccordingly, under section 1(1)(a) of the Freedom of Information Act 2000, our response is that Amgueddfa Cymru does not hold the requested information.\n\nFor completeness, our searches identified records relating to other archaeological finds from Church Farm, Llandough and to other archaeological discoveries recorded at Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough. These records do not relate to, nor are they otherwise associated with, the discovery described in your request. We have therefore not considered them to fall within the scope of this request.\n\nIf you are dissatisfied with this response, you may request an internal review by writing to [email redacted]<[email redacted]> within 40 working days of the date of this letter.\n\n\n\nCofion cynnes,\n\n[A picture containing text, tableware, plate, dishware    Description automatically generated]\nFOI Team\n[cid:image002.png@01DD4A6D.9E375520]\n[email redacted]<[email redacted]>\namgueddfa.cymru | museum.wales\n\nRhif elusen | Charity number 525774\n\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSent: 27 July 2026 02:22\nTo: FOI Requests <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSubject: ALLANOL/EXTERNAL - FOI - Great House Farm, Llandough\n\n\nThis email comes from outside the organisation.\n\nDo not click links or open attachments unless it is an email you expected to receive.\nYou don't often get email from [email redacted]<[email redacted]>. Learn why this is important<https://aka.ms/LearnAboutSenderIdentification>\nDear Freedom of Information Team,\n\nI am writing to request information under the Freedom of Information Act 2000 and, where applicable, the Environmental Information Regulations 2004.\n\nA published local history account states that, during works to replace the stone floor of the dining room at Great House Farm (also known as Ty Mawr), Llandough, Cardiff, the remains of a soldier and his horse were discovered beneath the floor.\n\n The remains were subsequently re-buried in the churchyard, and the soldier's visor and lance were given to the National Museum of Wales (Amgueddfa Cymru).\n\nI therefore request the following recorded information held by Amgueddfa Cymru:\n\nAny records relating to the acquisition, accession, donation, transfer or deposit of a medieval visor, lance, helmet, or other items of armour associated with Great House Farm / Ty Mawr / Llandough.\n\nThe accession number(s), date of acquisition or transfer, name of the donor or transferring body, and any provenance or discovery notes linked to these objects.\n\nAny catalogue entries, object records, or internal correspondence concerning the above items.\n\nAny records of associated human or animal remains from the same discovery, including notes on reburial.\n\nAny related excavation, watching-brief, or discovery reports held by the Museum.\n\nIf any of the requested information is already publicly available (for example via the Museum's online collections database), I would be grateful if you could simply provide the relevant accession numbers or catalogue links.\n\nPlease treat this as a request for recorded information only. I am not seeking advice or opinion.\n\nI look forward to your response within the statutory timescale.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\nRequester \n[cid:image003.jpg@01DD4A6D.9E375520]"},{"id":"gm-1a0c851592923ec2","subject":"26FOI 103 - Buckler - Response","name":"26FOI 103 - Buckler - Response","date":"2026-09-22T08:52:11Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0c851592923ec2.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0c851592923ec2-26foi-103-buckler-response","scopeTopics":["nio"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["nio"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0c851592923ec2","direction":"received","from":"NIO FOI","institutions":[{"id":"nio","name":"Northern Ireland Office"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0c851592923ec2.json","attachments":[{"name":"26FOI 103 - Buckler - Response.pdf","title":"26FOI 103 - Buckler - Response.pdf","url":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/pdf/foi/2026-09-22_wales-office_26foi-103_response.pdf","mime":"application/pdf","bytes":166430,"sha256":"d92caba80e4cea68845fa8b48048cb4703d2701b248cb6667ad25b26cf101b8d"}],"textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester,\n\n\nPlease see the attached response in respect of your FOI request to the\nWales Office, reference 26FOI 103.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nFreedom of information Team\n\n\n[image: image.png]"},{"id":"gm-1a0c7d0f680dc3c6","subject":"Please update the status of your request - BP vs Buckler 1987","name":"Please update the status of your request - BP vs Buckler 1987","date":"2026-09-22T06:32:35Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0c7d0f680dc3c6.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0c7d0f680dc3c6-please-update-the-status-of-your-request-bp-vs-buckler-1987","scopeTopics":["nactso"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["nactso"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0c7d0f680dc3c6","direction":"received","from":"(name not shown; whatdotheyknow.com)","institutions":[{"id":"nactso","name":"Counter Terrorism Policing (NaCTSO)"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0c7d0f680dc3c6.json","textStatus":"available","text":"To let everyone know, follow this link and then select the appropriate box.\n\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/profile/sign_in?r=%2Frequest%2Fbp_vs_buckler_1987_1988%23describe_state_form_1\n\nYour request was called 'BP vs Buckler 1987'. Letting everyone know whether you got the information will help us keep tabs on\nNational Counter Terrorism Security Office.\n\n-- the WhatDoTheyKnow team\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nWhatDoTheyKnow is a project of mySociety run by a small team of staff and\ndedicated volunteers.\n\nmySociety is a charity that believes that information should be accessible to everyone.\nIf this is important to you, please consider donating to help us do more:\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/donate\n\nFind out more about how we handle your data at:\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/help/privacy\n\nmySociety is a registered charity in England and Wales (1076346), and a limited\ncompany (03277032)"},{"id":"gm-1a0c7875d844914a","subject":"Delayed response to your FOI request - 2020 01590 HYB","name":"Delayed response to your FOI request - 2020 01590 HYB","date":"2026-09-22T05:12:11Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0c7875d844914a.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0c7875d844914a-delayed-response-to-your-foi-request-2020-01590-hyb","scopeTopics":["vale-foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["vale-foi"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0c7875d844914a","direction":"received","from":"(name not shown; whatdotheyknow.com)","institutions":[{"id":"vale-foi","name":"Vale of Glamorgan Council"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0c7875d844914a.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Vale of Glamorgan Council have delayed.\n\nThey have not replied to your FOI request '2020 01590 HYB' promptly, as normally required by law.\n\nClick on the link below to send a message to Vale of Glamorgan Council reminding them to reply to your request. You can also refer your request to the Information Commissioner once the statutory deadline has passed.\n\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/request/2020_01590_hyb/followups/new\n\n\n-- the WhatDoTheyKnow team\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nWhatDoTheyKnow is a project of mySociety run by a small team of staff and\ndedicated volunteers.\n\nmySociety is a charity that believes that information should be accessible to everyone.\nIf this is important to you, please consider donating to help us do more:\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/donate\n\nFind out more about how we handle your data at:\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/help/privacy\n\nmySociety is a registered charity in England and Wales (1076346), and a limited\ncompany (03277032)"},{"id":"gm-1a0c74ecb7fd9dcb","subject":"N610CGV — request for electronic copies of listed first-registration documents (WA240304 / WA231076)","name":"N610CGV — request for electronic copies of listed first-registration documents (WA240304 / WA231076)","date":"2026-09-22T04:10:24Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0c74ecb7fd9dcb.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0c72f4d407f4e5-n610cgv-request-for-electronic-copies-of-listed-first-registration-documents-wa240304-wa231076","scopeTopics":["hmlr"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["hmlr"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0c72f4d407f4e5","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"hmlr","name":"HM Land Registry"}],"refs":["N610CGV"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0c74ecb7fd9dcb.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Mr Haste\n\nReference: N610CGV\nTitles: WA231076 and WA240304 (closed)\nProperty: Land on the north side of Leckwith Road, Llandough (Great House\nFarm / Tÿ Mawr)\nYour letter: 30 July 2026\nAlso: customer-support refs 260918-6720625 and 260919-6726246;\ncorrespondence via Stephen Doughty MP and Mr Iain Banfield\n\nI write further to your letter of 30 July 2026, in which you listed the\nsurviving first-registration documents for both titles and stated that they\nremain on file and are open to inspection.\n\nI cannot obtain a current official copy of WA240304 through the public\nSearch for land and property service. Find a Property Support (Sandra, 21\nSeptember 2026, ref 260919-6726246) has confirmed that WA240304 is not a\ncurrent title number and that no copy of that register is available on that\nservice. That is why I am writing to you, as the officer who has already\nidentified the historic file.\n\nI have also already paid for a hand full of official copies and related\nland documents in this matter. I am asking you to issue, by email to\n[email redacted], unofficial or official copies of the below items\nfrom your 30 July schedule, and to waive the information-services fee for\nthis request, more so to make this all less problematic and cumberson.\nJustice delayed is justice denied. I've experienced months of non\nco-operetion and rhetoric and I'd like some good faith restored.\n\nFrom title WA240304\n1. Plan to Conveyance dated 23 May 1975\n7. Application form D23 (inside exam) and letter dated 8 April 1983 from\nLinklaters & Paines\n9. Old title plan for WA240304\n10. Copy of register edition dated 23 February 1983\n11. Form 1C application form from Linklaters & Paines\n12. Conveyance 23 May 1975\n13. Plan to conveyance dated 23 February 1975\n\nFrom title WA231076\n4. Conveyance dated 19 November 1982\n13. Application form 1E dated 30 November 1982\n16. Customer correspondence dated 12 May 1983 — Linklaters & Paines and\nLand Registry\n17. A13-DL list of documents lodged with first registration\n18. Letter from Linklaters & Paines (undated) to Land Registry\n\nPlease also send the 1971 abstract of title if it survives, whether as a\nseparate document or as part of item 17.\n\nI ask for the fee to be waived because:\n- these documents cannot be bought on the public website for the closed\ntitle;\n- you have already retrieved and, as of February 2026, scanned the historic\npaper files;\n- I have already incurred repeated official-copy fees on the live title and\nrelated records;\n- Stephen Doughty MP and the Chief Land Registrar are already seized of\nthis file;\n- the request is confined to documents you have listed as still held.\n\nThis is not a request to reopen the stage 2 complaint concluded by Mr Gill\non 25 May 2026. I am not asking you to determine ownership. I am asking for\ncopies of records you have already said exist.\n\nIf you will not waive the fee or issue the copies by email, please say so\nin terms, confirm that each numbered item above is still held, and state\nthe exact OC2 wording and address so the application can go in the post\nwithout a further inventory letter.\n\nPlease reply to this email.\n\nIn light of land registry maintaining its position no mistake was made. And\nyour confidence in the 1987 ruling. I'm trying my best to deduce if either\nI am mistaken or BP commited purgergy and/or concealment when registering\nour land parcel in their names and registering.\n\nI'm 3 decades into my career in defence cyber and electronic warfare and\nI'm the son of Buckler in BP vs Buckler 1987. This is also my birthland.\nAnd I have 2 decades of evidence collected. All of which I've had A.I\nstudy.\n\nThe truth is always somewhere in the middle I guess. So perhaps yourselves,\nBP and my family are all in some ways correct and I other ways incorrect.\nEstablishing that is a key point of this whole excercise.\n\nI also suspect fraud, concealment and Government involvement. GGAT actually\nworked for both the government and BP in this case. UK Gov were also a\nmajor shareholder in BP until 1987 too. It's not such an outlandish\nallegation to make. The question is more to what extent the injustice\nagainst my family goes.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester \n"},{"id":"gm-1a0c4f015168ab78","subject":"RE: Ty Mawr/ Great House, Llandough, Cardiff","name":"RE: Ty Mawr/ Great House, Llandough, Cardiff","date":"2026-09-21T17:07:34Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0c4f015168ab78.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0bda2634ae66e1-ty-mawr-great-house-llandough-cardiff","scopeTopics":["rcahmw"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["rcahmw"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0bda2634ae66e1","direction":"received","from":"NMR Enquiries","institutions":[{"id":"rcahmw","name":"RCAHMW (Royal Commission)"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0c4f015168ab78.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Prynhawn da.\n\nDiolch am eich ymholiad rhyddid gwybodaeth.  Byddwn yn ymateb o fewn 20 diwrnod gwaith.\n\nThank you for your FoI enquiry.  We will respond within 20 working days.\n\n\n\n\nNMR Enquiries\nYmholiadau Cyhoeddus I Public Enquiries\nFfordd Penglais | Penglais Road, Aberystwyth, [postcode redacted]\n[phone redacted]\n[email redacted] | [email redacted]\nwww.cbhc.gov.uk | www.rcahmw.gov.uk\nNoddir gan Lywodraeth Cymru | Sponsored by Welsh Government\nRydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg a Saesneg. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn peri oedi.\nWe welcome correspondence in Welsh and English. Corresponding in Welsh will not lead to any delay.\n\nYn unol â Rheoliadau Safonau'r Gymraeg (Rhif 2) 2016, mae gennych hawl i gyfathrebu a gohebu â Chomisiwn Brenhinol Henebion Cymru yn eich dewis iaith. Er mwyn sicrhau ein bod yn cyflawni’r hawl, rhowch wybod i ni a ydych yn dymuno derbyn gohebiaeth a/neu alwadau ffôn oddi wrthym yn y Gymraeg. Bydd yr wybodaeth hon yn cael ei chofnodi gennym ni, a byddwn yn defnyddio'r iaith o’ch dewis ym mhob cyfathrebu yn y dyfodol. Diolch.\nUnder the Welsh Language Standards (No. 2) Regulations 2016, you have the right to communicate and correspond with the Royal Commission on the Ancient and Historical Monuments of Wales in your preferred language. To ensure we uphold this right, please let us know whether you wish to receive correspondence and/or telephone calls from us in Welsh. This information will be recorded by us, and we will use your preferred language in all future communication. Thank you.\n​\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]>\nSent: 20 September 2026 08:07\nTo: NMR Enquiries <[email redacted]>\nSubject: Ty Mawr/ Great House, Llandough, Cardiff\n\nDear Rhodri, dear colleagues,\n>\n> Diolch am eich cydnabyddiaeth ar 8 Medi. On the Welsh Language Standards point raised in your signature — I am Welsh and I would be glad to receive correspondence in Welsh, but please use whichever language is quicker for you. I do not want language to delay anything, and I will not treat a Welsh reply as needing translation.\n>\n> This is a request under section 1 of the Freedom of Information Act 2000 and, so far as the records concern the historic environment, under the Environmental Information Regulations 2004.\n>\n> **First, a point of housekeeping. Nothing in this email amends, clarifies, narrows or withdraws my requests of 8, 15 or 17 September.** Those stand exactly as made and their statutory periods run from their own dates. This is an additional request, and I would not want it to be treated as restarting any clock. On my reckoning:\n>\n> - **8 September 2026** — FOI/EIR on R. F. Suggett's December 1988 unpublished records, H. J. Thomas's 1974 survey, the source of the \"held by the National Museum of Wales\" attribution, and copy correspondence with Amgueddfa Cymru, GGAT, Cadw and the Vale. Acknowledged by you the same day, twenty working days. **Due Tuesday 6 October 2026.** No reference number issued; nothing yet supplied.\n> - **15 September 2026** — catalogue check on Emergency Recording case **RCS2/1/1777**, filed under Great House (Ty Mawr), Bonvilston, where the material described is the pattern one would expect for the Llandough demolition. **Due Tuesday 13 October 2026.**\n> - **17 September 2026** — whether the Commission was notified, consulted or asked to record the farmhouse before its demolition on 6 December 1988. **Due Thursday 15 October 2026.**\n>\n> The second and third received only the automated enquiry acknowledgement, which cites fifteen working days and notes that the priority search service is suspended. I understand the staffing position and I am not complaining about it. But both emails ask for recorded information, and a request for recorded information is a request under section 1 whether or not it is labelled as one. **Please confirm that all three are logged as FOIA / EIR requests and let me have the reference numbers.** If they are currently sitting in the research-enquiry queue rather than the information-rights queue, that is easily fixed now and much harder to fix later.\n>\n> **On the number of requests.** I am conscious that this is my fifth email in twelve days. It is not an attempt to burden the Commission. Each request arose from a document I only found after sending the last one — the GGAT 1990 assessment led me to Suggett, Suggett's own note led me to the lost correspondence, and a Coflein search led me to RCS2/1/1777. I am content for all of them to be answered together in a single response if that is more efficient for you, and if the whole exceeds the appropriate limit under section 12 I will happily tell you which items to do first rather than have anything refused outright. Please just ask.\n>\n> **The site.** Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr, Llandough (Llandochau) juxta Penarth, Vale of Glamorgan — the community of St Dochdwy's Church and Llandough Hospital. **Not** Llandough near Cowbridge, and **not** Ty Mawr, Bonvilston (Cadw 13603), which is a different and still-standing house. NPRN 20182. ST 1681 7331. HER GGAT02038s. Now Church View Close. Outbuildings demolished 30 November 1988; farmhouse 6 December 1988.\n>\n> Holdings I am aware of include file references **NA/GL/88/49** and **NA/GL/89/208**, NMR site file material catalogued on Coflein (a plan with additions dated 12 December 1988, a descriptive account, a press cutting), Inventory working papers for Glamorgan Inventory site 425, and the disputed ERC bundle RCS2/1/1777.\n>\n> **Why this matters to me.** The farm was my family's. The immediate reason for this request is a line in your own records: R. F. Suggett's December 1988 assessment notes that *\"Correspondence between C. N. Johns and Mrs Buckler, Great House Farm, has been lost from the file since 1986/87\"*, and that *\"There is apparently no photographic record of the site.\"* Mrs Buckler was my relative. I want the correspondence if it survives, the record of how it came to be lost if it does not, and the rest of the file either way. I am not asking the Commission for a view on title or on the legality of the demolition, and I would not expect one.\n>\n> **Information requested**\n>\n> Please supply copies, so far as held:\n>\n> 1. A full contents list or file schedule for **NA/GL/88/49** and **NA/GL/89/208**, itemising every document on each file, whether or not the documents themselves are disclosed.\n> 2. Copies of all documents on those two files.\n> 3. **The file cover, front sheet or wrapper of each file**, and any enclosure list, loans register, issue slip, reader-request record or file-tracking entry attached to or kept for them.\n> 4. Every record concerning the **C. N. Johns / Mrs Buckler correspondence** recorded as lost since 1986/87: any surviving copy, draft, extract, summary, index entry or catalogue stub; any contemporaneous or later note of its loss; any record of a search for it; any record of who consulted, borrowed or handled the file between 1 January 1985 and 31 December 1988; and any internal or external correspondence about its absence. If no such record exists, please say so expressly rather than leaving it unaddressed.\n> 5. The Commission's **retention and disposal schedule** applicable to site files and correspondence files in the 1980s and today, and confirmation whether any records relating to this site have been destroyed under it, and if so when and under what authority.\n> 6. Whether the files listed above have been **reviewed for transfer to, or retention from, The National Archives or the Glamorgan Archives**, and the outcome of any such review.\n> 7. All **Inventory working papers, drafts, card-index entries and record cards** for the site, including those for Glamorgan Inventory site 425.\n> 8. All **photographs** of the site in any format — prints, negatives, transparencies, digital images, and any oblique or vertical aerial coverage of Llandough showing the farm at any date — whether or not catalogued, with their catalogue entries. Given Suggett's 1988 note that there is apparently no photographic record, **please state expressly whether the Commission holds none**, and whether any images sit within RCS2/1/1777 or in uncatalogued Emergency Recording material.\n> 9. Confirmation, from the location plan, map copies and photographs inside **RCS2/1/1777**, whether that bundle is Bonvilston or Llandough. If Llandough, please re-link it to NPRN 20182 and supply a contents list and copies. Please also check adjacent case **RCS2/1/1776**. *(This is my request of 15 September, restated here for completeness only — it stands on its own date.)*\n> 10. Any record of a site visit, watching brief, recording exercise or emergency recording carried out or considered before, during or after the demolition; any notification of the proposed demolition, from whom and on what date; and any decision **not** to record the building together with the reasons recorded for it. The building was not listed — Cadw inspected on 29 July 1988 and did not list it — so I make no assumption that the Commission was entitled to notice. What I would like to know is what the Commission's own criteria for initiating Emergency Recording were at the time, and whether this site was considered against them. Contemporary reports of 3 December 1988 stated that Cadw was considering emergency listing and that discussion with the Commission was being arranged. The house came down three days later.\n> 11. The **1974 survey by H. J. Thomas**, his site notes of 26 April 1974 and 12 December 1988, and any measured drawings, sketches, plans or elevations, published or unpublished.\n> 12. **R. F. Suggett's December 1988 records in full** — every page, card, continuation sheet, annotation and enclosure — not only the passages concerning the c.1880 find.\n> 13. Any record of the source of the statement that the c.1880 ironwork \"is held by the National Museum of Wales\", and any later file that confirms, corrects or withdraws that attribution.\n> 14. All correspondence between the Commission and any of the following concerning this site, 1970–1995: Cadw or the Welsh Office; the Glamorgan-Gwent Archaeological Trust; Vale of Glamorgan Borough Council; BP Properties Limited or its agents, including Debenham Tewson & Chinnocks; Persimmon Homes (Wales) Limited; Ideal Homes Wales Limited; Amgueddfa Cymru; any Member of Parliament; and any member of my family.\n> 15. Any accession records for material deposited with the Commission by GGAT, Cadw, Cotswold Archaeology or any other body relating to this site.\n>\n> **Practicalities.** Please reply by email to this address. I cannot attend in person and I am not seeking anything that attracts a charge. Scans are fine. Where an item is already on Coflein, a stable reference and link is enough **provided** you confirm the online record is complete. If any item is held elsewhere — The National Archives, Glamorgan Archives, Cadw, Heneb or Amgueddfa Cymru — please name the body and reference so I can apply there directly, which I will.\n>\n> Finally, so that it is on record from the outset rather than raised later: please let me have the name and contact details of the officer responsible for information rights at the Commission, and confirmation of the internal review procedure, so that I know where to direct any review request if one becomes necessary.\n>\n> With thanks for your patience with a long list.\n>\n> Yn gywir / Yours faithfully,\n>\n> Requester\n> [email redacted]<[email redacted]>\n\n--"},{"id":"gm-1a0c1e743d10b2b2","subject":"Re: Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough — request for information under FOIA 2000 / EIR 2004","name":"Re: Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough — request for information under FOIA 2000 / EIR 2004","date":"2026-09-21T02:59:13Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0c1e743d10b2b2.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0c1e36871ebfd1-great-house-farm-t-mawr-llandough-request-for-information-under-foia-2000-eir-2004","scopeTopics":["llandough"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["llandough"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0c1e36871ebfd1","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"llandough","name":"Llandough Community Council"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0c1e743d10b2b2.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dr Misra,\n\nThank you for replying, and for doing so promptly and at a weekend. Good\ndrills Sir. What an honour. I know you and the Council serve as volunteers,\nand I am grateful for the time.\n\nLet me set out plainly what I am asking of the Council, which is narrow. If\nyou'll allow me?\n\nI am not asking your Council to revisit Mr Egan's answer of 14 June 2026.\nHe explained that the minute books from 1982 onwards were deposited with\nGlamorgan Archives in 2002, and I am pursuing those there.\n\nWhat I am asking about is what that answer did not cover. Llandough\nCommunity Council is a public authority under Schedule 1, Part II,\nparagraph 7(b) of the Freedom of Information Act 2000. The Act applies to\nthe recorded information set out below generally; the Environmental\nInformation Regulations 2004 apply in addition to any part of it that is\nenvironmental information.\n\nPART ONE — REQUEST FOR RECORDED INFORMATION\n\n1. Whether the Council holds any recorded information created after the\n2002 deposit — minutes, correspondence, reports, emails, photographs or\nfiles of any kind — concerning Great House Farm (Ty Mawr) / Church View\nClose, the village green in front of St Dochdwy's including registered\ngreen VG 41, or the roadside verge by the churchyard wall.\n\n2. Whether the Council holds any photographs, postcards or images of the\nfarm, the barn, the green or the church surroundings, of any date.\n\n3. Whether the Council holds any recorded information showing what, if\nanything, transferred to it from Penarth Town Council at the 1982\nreorganisation, and any recorded information identifying where the pre-1982\nLlandough community records were deposited.\n\nIf the answer to any of these is that no such information is held, I would\nbe content with a short statement to that effect — but I do need it stated.\nSection 1(1)(a) of the Act requires the Council to confirm or deny whether\nthe information is held.\n\nSection 10 (and regulation 5 of the EIR) allows twenty working days from\nreceipt, which on my request of 20 September 2026 I calculate as 16 October\n2026.\n\nIf the Council is refusing, section 17 (and regulation 14) requires a\nwritten refusal notice identifying the exemption or exception relied on;\nsection 17(7) requires the Council to give particulars of any complaints\nprocedure it operates, or to state that it has none, and to notify me of my\nright to apply to the Information Commissioner; and regulation 11 gives a\nright to an internal review of any refusal under the EIR.\n\n\"We have nothing more to add\" does not meet those requirements. If the\nperiod passes without a compliant response I will have no option but to\ncomplain to the Information Commissioner.\n\nPART TWO — MATTERS FOR THE COUNCIL'S DISCRETION\n\nThese are not part of the request above and carry no deadline. They are\nsimply things I would be grateful for.\n\nA. I am not asking for the name or contact details of any individual. I am\nasking whether the Council would be willing to pass my details to any\nformer member, former clerk or long-standing resident who might remember\nthe village between the 1950s and the 1990s, leaving it entirely to them\nwhether to contact me. I appreciate the Council may have data protection or\nadministrative reasons for declining, and I would not press it.\n\nB. Whether the Council would be willing to table this correspondence at a\nmeeting and minute it. I am content for it to be tabled in full.\n\nC. Whether the Council would consider, in due course, whether an\ninterpretation board or a plaque near the green would be appropriate. The\nexcavation on the land beneath Church View Close recorded 1,026 burials,\ndescribed in the published report as by far the largest early medieval\nburial population so far recovered from Wales, above a Roman villa and an\nIron Age roundhouse. That history belongs to Llandough as much as to my\nfamily, and there is nothing on the ground that records it. I would be glad\nto give the Council or the community a short talk on the documentary\nrecord, without charge, if that would be useful.\n\nI should be grateful for a response to items 1 to 3 by 16 October 2026.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester \n\n\n\nOn Sun, 20 Sept 2026, 18:31 Mo Misra, <[email redacted]> wrote:\n\n> Dear Requester,\n>\n> Thanks for your email of 20th September 2026.\n>\n> I note that our Clerk, Paul Egan, has already directed you to all the\n> information we have on the matter as indicated in paragraph 6 of your\n> email.\n>\n> We have nothing more to add.\n>\n> Best regards\n>\n> Dr M C Misra,\n> Chair Llandough Community Council"},{"id":"gm-1a0c1cb194cd5fc4","subject":"Re: FOI/EIR request - Case Ref/ 00211463 — clarification and refinement of request","name":"Re: FOI/EIR request - Case Ref/ 00211463 — clarification and refinement of request","date":"2026-09-21T02:28:27Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0c1cb194cd5fc4.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0c1c87a6f1717d-foi-eir-request-case-ref-00211463-clarification-and-refinement-of-request","scopeTopics":["vale-foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["vale-foi"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0c1c87a6f1717d","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"vale-foi","name":"Vale of Glamorgan Council"}],"refs":["00211463"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0c1cb194cd5fc4.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Freedom of Information Officer,\n\nCase Ref: 00211463 — request received 17 September 2026\n\nI write to clarify and refine the above request while it remains open,\nhaving now examined the original manuscript of the planning officer's\nreport rather than a transcription. My earlier request named two\norganisations; the document in fact identifies the correspondents\ndifferently, and I ask that the request be read as follows.\n\nThe passage relied upon\nThe planning officer's report on application 89/01396/OUT (residential\ndevelopment including new access road, land at Great House Farm, Leckwith\nRoad, Llandough, Penarth; outline permission granted 13 March 1990) states,\nat page 2:\n\n\"Two letters have been received from the Barry Preservation Society and\nCivic Trust and a local County Councillor (who is a member of the Glamorgan\nGwent Archaeological Trust) making representations in respect of this\napplication and a copy of both these letters are attached to this report as\nAppendices 'C' and 'D' respectively.\"\n\nThe following paragraph of the same report states:\n\n\"The Applicants' Agents have written in response to the report of the\nGlamorgan Gwent Archaeological Trust Ltd. on the site and a copy of this\nletter is attached to this report as Appendix 'E'.\"\n\nWhat I request\nAppendix \"C\" to the officer's report on 89/01396/OUT — the letter from the\nBarry Preservation Society and Civic Trust.\n\nAppendix \"D\" — the letter from the local County Councillor referred to,\ntogether with that Councillor's name.\n\nAppendix \"E\" — the letter from the Applicants' Agents responding to the\nreport of the Glamorgan-Gwent Archaeological Trust Ltd.\n\nA list of all appendices (\"A\" to \"E\" or further) to that officer's report,\nso that the complete annex set is identified.\n\nAny register, index, schedule or file list for application 89/01396/OUT\nrecording the documents originally held on the file.\nA further document referred to in the same report\n\nThe officer's report also records, of the East Vale Local Plan:\n\n\"The greater part of the land is allocated for new residential development\nin the East Vale Local Plan (Policy No. H.2.) The information supporting\nthe policy states that buildings on the site should be retained, however\nwere unfortunately demolished a little while ago.\"\n\nI therefore also request:\nThe text of Policy H.2 of the East Vale Local Plan and the supporting\ninformation to that policy referred to above, in the version current in\n1989–1990.\n\nPoints on handling\nThese are documents the Council's own report states were attached to it. If\nany of them is not held, I ask the Council to say so expressly, and to\nstate (a) when it ceased to be held, (b) under what retention schedule or\ndisposal decision, and (c) whether any record of that disposal exists. A\nresponse that \"no additional information has been identified\" would not\naddress a request for annexes that the Council's own report records as\nattached.\n\nI note the Council's letter of 11 September 2026 in a related matter, which\nstated that \"further historical documentation has been found... not\nnecessarily Council documentation but is nonetheless in the Council's\npossession\". I ask that this material be searched in responding to the\npresent request.\n\nI intend this as a clarification and refinement of case 00211463 under the\nCouncil's duty to advise and assist (s.16 FOIA 2000 / reg. 9 EIR 2004), and\nnot as a new request. If the Council takes the view that any part of it\nmust be treated as a fresh request, please confirm that in writing,\nidentify which parts, and give the revised due date, so that I am not left\nuncertain which deadline applies to which element.\nI should be grateful for confirmation of receipt.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester \n"},{"id":"gm-1a0bff2e07c1cfc3","subject":"In Touch with Llandough!","name":"In Touch with Llandough!","date":"2026-09-20T17:52:37Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0bff2e07c1cfc3.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0bff2e07c1cfc3-in-touch-with-llandough","scopeTopics":["vale-scrutiny"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["vale-scrutiny"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0bff2e07c1cfc3","direction":"received","from":"Cllr George Carroll","institutions":[{"id":"vale-scrutiny","name":"Vale of Glamorgan Council (Scrutiny)"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0bff2e07c1cfc3.json","textStatus":"available","text":"View this email in your browser (https://mailchi.mp/598b71eb066b/in-touch-with-llandough-17666028?e=1429c2c1bb)\nLLANDOUGH HILL DRAINAGE ISSUES\n\n\n**\n------------------------------------------------------------\nDear Requester\n\nWater leaks on and around Llandough Hill continue to pose a significant problem.\n\nAs you'll recall, our community's pressure secured a subsurface drain to help prevent water emerging from the road surface.\n\nThe drain isn't visible from the road surface, so you may not have seen it. I've therefore included a photo below.\nWhile the drain has helped reduce the problem significantly, issues remain.\n\nFor example, a leak on the Brook Green footpath has forced the Community Council to close the route.\n\nThis is problematic, as many residents of Willowmere and Llandough Hill who do not drive rely on the path to access bus stops on Penlan Road.\n\nWhile the Community Council are responsible for this stretch of land, it's clear the Vale Highways Department's works may well have diverted the water leak onto Brook Green.\n\nThey must therefore play their part in resolving the situation.\nhttps://www.facebook.com/share/r/1GH3Nzx1q3/\nI raised the Llandough Hill water leaks again this week at the Council's Resources Scrutiny Committee.\n\nAn extra £17,000 has recently been allocated to flood risk management on Llandough Hill in the Council's capital budget.\n\nThe pressure our community placed on the Council resulted in this money being allocated.\n\nBut given the extent of the problem, I question if it is enough. You can view my comments in the meeting below.\n\nhttps://www.facebook.com/share/v/1BwTiNDGbZ/\n\nPreventing water leaks, and their associated impacts such as damaging road surfaces and posing an ice risk, is a key part of Our Plan for Llandough.\n\nOur efforts have secured investment that has helped reduce the problem. It would not have happened without us.\n\nBut there is still much more to do.\n\nWe must double down our efforts and work together to resolve the problem once and for all.\n\n\n**\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nCommunity Hero: Rachel Green\n\nYou may have seen Llandough resident Rachel Green in the news this week, following her life saving heroics.\n\nRachel was on her way to the gym when she saw a man, Alan Hale, collapsed on the side of the road.\n\nShe immediately stopped her car and performed CPR, saving Alan's life.\n\nRachel has now been honoured by the Royal Humane Society with its Resuscitation Certificate.\n\nIt goes without saying her award is fully deserved and our whole community is exceptionally proud.\n\nYou can read more about Rachel's story by clicking the link below.\nRead Rachel's story (https://www.penarthtimes.co.uk/news/26535649.woman-honoured-saving-mans-life-cpr-shock/)\n\nPenarth Road slip hazard\n\nEarlier this week, I received another complaint from a resident about the leak on Penarth Road.\n\nAlgae growing in the discharge is causing a slip hazard, posing a danger to pedestrians.\n\nAs you'll recall, this is not a Council manhole. It's the responsibility of BT.\n\nI raised the slip hazard with the Council, who confirmed they're continuing to push BT to resolve it.\n\nThe Council have also provided assurances they'll carry out regular sweeping to clear the algae.\n\nThis is not sustainable in the long term, and the leak must be repaired.\n\nI'll keep pushing all responsible authorities on this.\n\nCommunity drop-in event:\n20th October\n\nOur next community drop-in event takes place at the Merrie Harrier on 20th October.\n\nLike the last one, it'll start at 7.00pm.\n\nThe pub is a key asset to our community.\n\nTracey and the team do a lot for us - help return the favour by lending them your support.\n\nYou can see some of their midweek offers below!\n\nhttps://www.facebook.com/share/r/1EM9z5wbMs/\n\nOur Plan for Llandough!\n\n\nAs your councillor, I'm working with you all to secure a better future for Llandough. Together, we have a plan to deliver for the village.\n\nOur plan is making Llandough better by:\n\n1. Sorting out the parking problems\n2. Stamping out dog fouling\n3. Repairing our roads\n4. Stopping littering and flytipping\n5. Delivering better sports facilities\n6. Keeping the whole community involved\n\nIf you have any suggestions of what you'd like to see in Our Plan, please get in touch!\n\nhttp://www.facebook.com/sharer/sharer.php?u=https%3A%2F%2Fmailchi.mp%2F598b71eb066b%2Fin-touch-with-llandough-17666028 Share (http://www.facebook.com/sharer/sharer.php?u=https%3A%2F%2Fmailchi.mp%2F598b71eb066b%2Fin-touch-with-llandough-17666028)\nhttps://us19.forward-to-friend.com/forward?u=7b7a08284cdbc1f69f5b0afd8&id=d76642f53e&e=1429c2c1bb Forward (https://us19.forward-to-friend.com/forward?u=7b7a08284cdbc1f69f5b0afd8&id=d76642f53e&e=1429c2c1bb)\n\n============================================================\nI'm hoping to reach as many people as possible with this newsletter, so please feel free to forward it to friends and neighbours.\n\nIf this email has been forwarded to you, and you would like to sign up to my newsletter, please click ** here ([email redacted]\n.\nThis email was sent to you because your email address is on my subscribers list. If you don't want to receive any more messages from me, please click below to unsubscribe.\n\nYour consent was previously given during an online or offline interaction with me or the Conservatives. If you feel this is incorrect or would like to comment, please contact me. I do not buy email lists.\n\nPromoted by Chris Kirkham on behalf of George Carroll, both of Vale of Glamorgan Conservatives, Holton House, 50 Holton Road, Barry [postcode redacted].\n\nThis email was sent to [email redacted] ([email redacted]\nwhy did I get this? (https://facebook.us19.list-manage.com/about?u=7b7a08284cdbc1f69f5b0afd8&id=daad00f7e3&e=1429c2c1bb&c=d76642f53e)     unsubscribe from this list (***REDACTED***     update subscription preferences (https://facebook.us19.list-manage.com/profile?u=7b7a08284cdbc1f69f5b0afd8&id=daad00f7e3&e=1429c2c1bb&c=d76642f53e)\nPolitician . Unit 5 Pro Copy Business Centre . Parc Ty Glas . Cardiff, Crf [postcode redacted] . USA"},{"id":"gm-1a0bfdf0edae9468","subject":"Re: Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr) — heritage research and a Welsh museum project in Patagonia","name":"Re: Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr) — heritage research and a Welsh museum project in Patagonia","date":"2026-09-20T17:31:00Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0bfdf0edae9468.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0bcd6554c4ebec-great-house-farm-t-mawr-heritage-research-and-a-welsh-museum-project-in-patagonia","scopeTopics":["llandough"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["llandough"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0bcd6554c4ebec","direction":"received","from":"Mo Misra","institutions":[{"id":"llandough","name":"Llandough Community Council"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0bfdf0edae9468.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester,\n\nThanks for your email of 20th September 2026.\n\nI note that our Clerk, Paul Egan, has already directed you to all the \ninformation we have on the matter as indicated in paragraph 6 of your \nemail.\n\nWe have nothing more to add.\n\nBest regards\n\nDr M C Misra,\n\nChair Llandough Community Council\n\nOn 2026-09-20 07:29, Requester wrote:\n\n> Councillor Misra & Co,\n> \n> My family, the Williams and later Buckler family, lived at Great House \n> Farm (Tŷ Mawr) beside St Dochdwy's for almost 4 consecutive centuries \n> until 1988, when our 800 year old 10 bed manorial home was subject to \n> an SAS style siege and demolished. I was born there. The site is now \n> Church View Close.\n> \n> That whole incident appears to stem from a 1955 possession order for a \n> parcel of land east of the parcel we held freehold of. The order \n> contained ambiguity that was later misinterprited to make our occupancy \n> of our parcel appear permissive, following our paper title copy being \n> stolen. A 1962 possession order for our parcel was created based on \n> that but not enforced on my disabled, widow grandmother. Who just had a \n> leg amputated in 1955. Instead the interest in the 1962 order was \n> conveyed from Western Ground Rents to BP in 1969. BP altered the \n> ordinance survey, shifting the descriptor from their parcel onto my \n> nans. And appending \"farm' to it. They again made her occupancy appear \n> pernissive in 1974 with their famous licence letters. Then conveyed \n> interest in that, again, to their own sister company in 1975. Not \n> withstanding the possession of the 1962 order was quashed in 1974. Also \n> in 1974 my nans ownership claim was not legally exstiguished and \n> instead the court ajourned. In 1983 BP then quietly registered our land \n> in their names at the land registry, failing to disclose my nans \n> ownership claim, she also died in the 2 weeks she will have had to \n> challenge them. She had not been known to not challenge them in decades \n> of her campaigning. Land registry also didn't notify her/ us. BP then \n> merged our parcel into theirs, as my nan fought for her life and died. \n> The land registry wrote to the MP this week claiming not find anything \n> wrong with the role they played in 1982/1983. .\n> \n> In 1877 title became our families from Daniel Thomas & Son. But not \n> until 1928 when quarrying completed on the land. In 1916 Bute produced \n> an agrictural tenancy for our family featuring our occupied parcel and \n> a parcel to the east. The interest in this is what conveyed to Western \n> Ground Rents in 1938. And in 1939 they revised the agreement for the \n> parcel east of ours. And excluded ours parcel from any tenancy due to \n> the 1928 event and my great grandads registration of the freehold.\n> \n> The 1877 - 1928 agreement and title deed was stolen from our home \n> according to my late nans statement in these proceedings.\n> \n> When WGR saught possession of their parcel to the east of ours in 1955, \n> they exercised moral hazard to introduce ambiguity into the order, \n> considering my nans vulnerable state (amputee, widow, pensioner, stolen \n> title deed), which could have and did get later misinterprited to make \n> my nans occupancy appear permissive. And Western Ground Rents the \n> owner. Thus fabricating a defective title with the 1962 possession \n> order for my nans parcel and through conveying that unenforced \n> possession order to BP in 1969. BPs permissive occupancy licence in \n> 1974 and 1975 conveyance repeated that process to make interest \n> transfer and a title chain emerge, with no root title.\n> \n> Mr Paul Egan answered a request from me on 14 June this year. He \n> searched the Council's records, explained that the minute books from \n> 1982 onward were deposited with Glamorgan Archives in 2002, set out the \n> section 21 position correctly, and pointed me to the Council's own \n> pages on buildings of significance in the community. He also told me \n> something I had not known -- that before 1982 Llandough came under \n> Penarth Town Council -- which has opened a line of research I would \n> otherwise have missed. I had written to him in strong terms and he \n> replied with courtesy and care anyway. I understand he has since \n> retired after long service to the village. Please pass on my thanks if \n> you are able to. I am not asking the Council to revisit his answer; it \n> was a proper one.\n> \n> I am assembling a complete documentary and photographic record of my \n> family's old manorial 10-bedroom birth and family home since 1667, at \n> the centre of that church you British built in the 1800s -- I am \n> interested in everything: planning, archaeology, land registration, \n> family papers, oral memory -- all into a single, centralised searchable \n> archive. So the actual full story can be retold. Warts and all.\n> \n> Please bear with me for the rest of my email. I was traumatised by our \n> experience being dispossessed in the 80's. We've seen no justice. My \n> family died challenging any of the false narrative. And the remaining \n> alive won't speak up. The State have tried covering this up and \n> continue to do so for decades too. I'm the first to try to bring this \n> all to light.\n> \n> My intent with all this is a Welsh heritage project I have underway in \n> Patagonia, Argentina, where a replica of my old farm is to be \n> constructed as a museum.\n> \n> In Argentina they are different from you British currently occupying us \n> Welsh in Wales, in that they celebrate and don't supress or conceal our \n> Welsh heritage, culture, language, history. It is very refreshing for \n> me there, as an indigenous Briton who was brutally torn from my \n> ancestral birthland in 1988, in what can only be described as ethnic \n> cleansing. I am not even exaggerating; the particulars of the horrors \n> done to my family there make reconstructing the full chronology very \n> challenging. It's not new what Brit'ish do to indigenous people \n> globally, or the Welsh historically. But we were maybe the last to be \n> annexed, colonised and looted in such a systematic and brutal way.\n> \n> It would be passable if we were sincerely squatters being evicted as \n> the press reported. Or even courts believed. But the land BP possessed \n> they had. We lost the parcel east of ours fair and square. They came to \n> occupy our neighbouring land and home too out of greed, to build Church \n> View Close. Using force, stealth/ secrecy and license. The destinction \n> would have been clear had BP not registered our land in their names and \n> merged ours into theirs, in such a clandestine way, in 1982/1983.\n> \n> So much of my family's and people's history and heritage in Llandough \n> continues to be completely covered up in lieu of those 20 characterless \n> (Church View Close) concentration work-camp houses, without so much as \n> a park-bench plaque to commemorate any of the following:\n> \n> - the 1,026 medieval burials\n> - Marconi staying with us while inventing radio\n> - Peerless Joe Driscoll fighting his first fight in our barn\n> - the Roman villa\n> - Christian monastery\n> - 2,200-year-old Celtic roundhouse\n> \n> the list goes on. The LiDAR scans etc.\n> \n> I find it abhorrent that the Llandough website history page does not \n> mention any of this either. I know the British are proud of their No.1 \n> invention: the concentration work camp -- but it is sad witnessing all \n> our towns and cities being turned into them. Not that I am on the \n> British Isles much, except occasionally as a sort of autochthonous \n> Django Unchained. Seeing all the voters being imported to keep the \n> kinds of governors, Princess of Darkness and demons the Bible warns us \n> about in power does not even sadden me anymore. I think crusades may \n> happen sooner as a result, and therein resides my hope for my small \n> pale island.\n> \n> It was not always such a demonic realm for us natives, nor our \n> non-indigenous actual guests, friends and family in Llandough. My \n> family lived in the main manorial house in Llandough consecutively \n> since 1667 until the British's highly illegal, unlawful and \n> unconstitutional siege and theft of our stronghold in 1988. We still \n> appear to own the land under Church View Close. You British only \n> awarded yourselves possession of our Christian stronghold. Possession \n> being the weapon of choice of a demonic realm. When I was a soldier in \n> Cyprus I would enjoy watching the UN hand back stolen, occupied land to \n> the Greeks and Turks. I look forward to that occurring when Wales \n> literally, not figuratively, decolonises. Or Scotland renounces. Or \n> Ireland unites. I see the British State as more of a terrorist \n> organisation than the IRA, and I think it is the British State we need \n> to stop harbouring on our motherland.\n> \n> In the interim of revolution, I hold some faith in coalitions. Namely \n> thanks to Councillor Chris Franks, my grandmother's old good friend of \n> decades, who at least acknowledged my email. And that a grave injustice \n> does appear to have occurred to my family and, as a matter of public \n> interest, others too. To be brutally honest I think my case shows signs \n> we are in a landmark bubble and a land-market crash is coming. This \n> racket of converting amputee, disabled widow pensioners' occupancy of \n> our lands from freehold to permissive (with ambiguity and \n> misinterpretation and fraud and concealment) possession orders and \n> conveyance, stealth/secrecy etc. -- was not a sustainable viable way of \n> annexing. You can fool some of the people some of the time. But not all \n> of the people all of the time.\n> \n> But this is a request for co-operation with reconstructing history, not \n> a complaint. And history which has already been driven to lands many \n> thousands of miles away, so it can remain buried in the British's \n> wardrobe of skeletons as you so wish.\n> \n> With 99% of British MPs refusing to sign EDM 2025 condemning \n> decapitating Christians, I think Argentina is an apt location for my \n> replica manorial house, notwithstanding Michael Jones who founded the \n> settlement in Patagonia had followers who even nicknamed the reason for \n> it as being the \"decapitation committee\" here on the British Isles (a \n> century after we acquired my birth home in Llandough, so some time in \n> the late 1700s).\n> \n> In the interests of being straight with you: I had difficulty getting \n> replies from the Vale ward member, Councillor George Carroll, and \n> earlier this year I made a formal complaint to the Vale of Glamorgan \n> about that. Which I was surprised at after he got thrown out of a \n> council meeting for being against the City of Sanctuary scheme. My \n> motto is, if you are not on a government watchlist by now, you should \n> be ashamed of yourself.\n> \n> I am already resigned to flying in and out of this small formerly pale \n> island, like it is another military tour of the Middle East. I actually \n> do not harbour much ill will; I am just sad it has gone to the dogs and \n> half expect a nuclear strike, flood or crusade.\n> \n> My advice to my followers is not even to keep defending the \n> concentration work camps our hijacked towns and cities have been turned \n> into. Instead I encourage soldiers under my command to enjoy exile or \n> mass exodus. There is plenty in the British organisations' cells/camps \n> and networks' own rules which permits corps and exodusers. That way \n> everyone's happy.\n> \n> The British State is not the first regime to go rogue. Terrorist \n> organisations (now the official government) like in Afghanistan also \n> have hostages and prisoners of war who are masochist submissives, like \n> British/UK citizens. And it is a situationship that suits both parties. \n> But not something I would want to get in the middle of or encourage \n> others to get in the middle of. I do not know why so many do.\n> \n> I am persuaded to organise veterans to attend funerals of casualties of \n> the civil war our infiltrated, hijacked and traitor-filled regimes \n> waged on indigenous Britons, with its biological weapons, hybrid \n> warfare, ethnic cleansing and so forth. And we simply wear our uniforms \n> and salute the casualties. It is some remedy for the grieving families \n> who, even if speaking out from the decades of being terrorised into \n> silence, face decades of being stonewalled from justice from the State. \n> So I am all about the peaceful, legal and lawful paths forward -- as a \n> bona fide, certified, experienced military commander, leader and \n> warfare professional.\n> \n> I have seen Councillor Carroll's recent social media posts standing in \n> front of the photograph of my old farm's only public photo that hangs \n> in the Merry Harrier pub. If it is in good faith and support I am glad. \n> Anything less I would not be surprised. Only disappointed. But I extend \n> the benefit of the doubt with my farm case, because it is a complex \n> case. Even in my profession as a seasoned cyber/electronic military \n> warfare professional I find it colossal to navigate.\n> \n> What I would be grateful for from you who occupy and rule my land, in \n> place of your Pharisee predecessors, is a coalition with \n> counter-officers such as myself.\n> \n> Does your occupying forces/council hold any records that were created \n> after 2002 and therefore not part of the 2002 deposit, concerning our \n> stolen, occupied Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr/Cedfin)/Church View Close \n> land?\n> \n> And I should add: suspected, as I am still gathering evidence and \n> investigating. Albeit it all looks damning for the British and many \n> native traitors already, and I am only scratching the surface of what \n> happened in Llandough. My ancestral birthland would not be the first to \n> be looted by the British and I am doubtful it will be the last. Our \n> SAS-looted treasures are actually now all captive in British museums. \n> And our SAS-looted furniture, all from Cardiff Castle, are also held \n> hostage or in circulation too.\n> \n> My focus and what I require of your Council, as but a humble \n> (registered to vote) constituent of the home we got in Dinas Powys and \n> have lived in since our land became illegally occupied by the British \n> in 1988 -- is the village green in front of St Dochdwy's -- including \n> registered village green VG 41, which your Council raised formally with \n> the Vale when the Great House Farm site came forward for planning in \n> 1989.\n> \n> By constituent I do mean I am happy to play the role of a member of \n> society in a democracy. Albeit it is largely now pageantry and being \n> made a mockery of in my estimation. I see something very different \n> going on here, i.e. a terrorist/hostage situationship. And I do not \n> play the role of a hostage or prisoner of war well -- at all! Nor am I \n> a masochist submissive and much like kissing ring fingers or bending \n> knees. My 22 in Oath ended in 2024 (employed under the Vale of \n> Glamorgan Council too, by some freak coincidence). So I will thank you \n> in advance not to ask weird stuff of me. I am nice until it is time not \n> to be. And I believe we are all equal under law and God.\n> \n> My family have endured enough force, stealth/secrecy and licence from \n> the British in the 80s to last us a lifetime. And having been behind a \n> machine gun in a helicopter in the Middle East, and trained on \n> explosives and weapons and cyber/electronic warfare capabilities for my \n> military career, which I left with full honours, I am well aware of how \n> the British regime operates and human-farms and projects its force. I \n> once had Britain's very own woke mind virus walking through my mind \n> with its muddy boots. I hope you all get better too.\n> \n> Better your cell/network/camp just skip to bringing guns and trying to \n> force me at gunpoint if you demand anything of me that contrasts \n> co-operation or coalition. I have seen already a lot of local \n> authorities, even South Wales Police, opting for clandestine and \n> shadowy silence and violence, rather than just responding to public \n> FOIs. I think your regime's attempt to keep our Llandough farm story \n> from ever seeing the light of day is understandable. But I do find \n> people from time to time, even Iraqis in Iraq, even myself as a former \n> British soldier, wanting to choose the right side of history rather \n> than allegiance to a sinking rat ship.\n> \n> Any record of works carried out on the green and the roadside verge by \n> the churchyard wall in the mid-1980s is all I am asking for. I hold \n> colour photographs of trees being removed, spoil carted away and new \n> kerbstones laid; vehicle registrations in them date the works to \n> between about 1983 and 1990, on more than one occasion. (See attached.) \n> Councillor Carroll's months of non-response to this specific request to \n> find this information out was not going to stop me finding all this \n> out. Asking for transparency and co-operation is mainly a courtesy and \n> formality, seeing as I am a seasoned military cyber/electronic warfare \n> professional. Going on 30 years now in fact.\n> \n> My great-grandfather planted a tree on that green to mark my family's \n> claim to that land, and my family believe that tree was lost in the \n> work of making that green, i.e. possible further destruction of \n> evidence, possibly nothing. I should be clear that the green does not \n> even appear to fall inside the boundary of land we have the interest \n> in, in our ancestral town you currently occupy, so this is a question \n> about the village's history as much as ours.\n> \n> Any photographs, postcards or images of the farm, the barn, the green \n> or the church surroundings that the Council holds or knows of. Please \n> disclose. I have vacuumed most of Leckwith Archives already.\n> \n> Any guidance on where the pre-1982 community records now sit, given \n> that Llandough fell within Penarth Town Council before then, and \n> whether the Council holds anything that came across at the 1982 \n> reorganisation, I would like you to please disclose.\n> \n> The names of any former members, former clerks or long-standing \n> residents who might be willing to talk to me about the village between \n> the 1950s and the 1990s. I would approach anyone you suggest \n> courteously and would not press a refusal. Please disclose.\n> \n> I am content for this email to be tabled at a meeting and minuted if \n> the Council wishes.\n> I am also happy to give a short talk to the Council or the community, \n> without charge, on what the records have revealed about Llandough's \n> past -- the Roman villa, the early medieval cemetery beneath Church \n> View Close, the monastic history, and the farm itself.\n> \n> A good deal of what I have found belongs to the village as much as my \n> family. Llandough just appears more committed to its current trajectory \n> of becoming even more of a concentration work camp, having already \n> rejected much of this history and heritage and native people, in lieu \n> of whatever it is you have underway there. I am not going to burn \n> Llandough down to feel its warmth. I know demonic realms are inherently \n> ice cold. To give an example of what I speak of, I found the Llandough \n> Facebook page to contain only photos of exotic takeaway foods. Sad \n> considering its rich history and heritage.\n> \n> I am not about leading my men to spill their lifeblood on Home Office \n> Mahmoods and other British blades, over any of this demographic \n> replacement and ethnic cleansing. I am just trying to salvage, \n> reconstruct a part of my family's and Welsh history and heritage while \n> people still have heads on their shoulders here, to be able to \n> co-operate in this matter. Even without civil unrest, alleged \n> fiduciaries have not kept proper records of this case. Mine may be more \n> complete than the British Government's and EU's at this point. And even \n> the Internet's, [family detail redacted].\n> \n> Regards,\n> \n> Requester \n> \n> \n> P.S. Vehicle registrations visible in them (including A305 MKG, which \n> cannot predate August 1983, and plant registered MTX 936X and MTX 930X) \n> place the works in the mid-1980s, between 1983 and 1990, and not during \n> the later residential development."},{"id":"gm-1a0bdadccdea9723","subject":"Formal complaint of 17 September 2026 — Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough — non-delivery and re-service","name":"Formal complaint of 17 September 2026 — Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough — non-delivery and re-service","date":"2026-09-20T07:17:58Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0bdadccdea9723.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0bdab5e24ed797-formal-complaint-of-17-september-2026-great-house-farm-t-mawr-llandough-non-delivery-and-re-service","scopeTopics":["cadw"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["cadw"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0bdab5e24ed797","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"cadw","name":"Welsh Government / Cadw"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0bdadccdea9723.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Wigley,\n\nI sent you the formal complaint below on 17 September 2026. I have since\nestablished that it never reached you.\n\nIt was addressed to [email redacted], which rejected it — my mail server\nrecorded \"message blocked… the address couldn't be found or is unable to\nreceive email.\" A copy addressed to [email redacted] also failed, that\ndomain having been decommissioned. The only addresses that accepted it were\n[email redacted] and [email redacted].\n\nThe latter is Cadw's visitor-services contact centre operated by Transport\nfor Wales, which replied on 18 September under reference #628635887 to say\nit had been passed to their complaints manager. That is plainly not the\nright destination for a complaint about a 1988 listing assessment, and I do\nnot want it pursued there. I would be grateful if that reference could be\nclosed.\nI should add that [email redacted] has now rejected correspondence from me on\nthree separate occasions — 23 January, 29 August and 17 September 2026. If\nthat address is still published anywhere as a contact point for Cadw, it\nmay be worth someone looking at.\n\nI am therefore re-serving the complaint on you directly, unchanged. The\ntext below is exactly as sent on 17 September. Please treat it as received\ntoday, and please let me have a complaint reference.\n\nTwo points of clarification, so that nothing is misread.\n\nFirst, this is not a further Freedom of Information request. I accept your\nemail of 4 September, in which you confirmed that Cadw has released all the\nrecords it holds concerning the 1988 listing decision, and I am not asking\nyou to produce documents you have already said no longer survive. I am\nconscious that I have made requests to Cadw and to the Welsh Government\nInformation Rights Unit since May of this year and that they have been\nanswered. My complaint is not that records are being withheld. It is about\nwhat can properly be established from the evidence that does survive, and\nabout whether Cadw's account distinguishes clearly enough between \"we\ncannot now establish this\" and \"this did not happen.\"\n\nSecond, I am treating this as stage one of the Welsh Government complaints\nprocess, not stage two. You directed me to that process on 4 September. I\nhave copied the Complaints Advice Team so that the complaint is logged and\ndated, but I am not asking for a formal investigation yet. I would rather\nCadw had the first opportunity to answer it. I will only escalate if the\nstage one response does not engage with the ten matters set out at section\n7 below.\n\nOne further matter, which I raise in the hope it is useful rather than as\npart of the complaint. Cadw's 1988 file is lost, but Cadw did not\ncorrespond with itself. Any letters Cadw wrote in 1988 about this building\nwere received by the Royal Commission, by Vale of Glamorgan Borough\nCouncil, and by the Glamorgan-Gwent Archaeological Trust, and copies may\nsurvive in their files even though yours have gone. I have requests with\nthe Royal Commission and with Heneb which cover exactly that. If either\nproduces Cadw correspondence you no longer hold, I will send it to you, as\nI said I would in August. I would rather Cadw's own record of this was\ncomplete than score a point off its being incomplete.\n\nI look forward to a complaint reference and to a substantive reply.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nRequester \n"},{"id":"gm-1a0bda407bdaa9ab","subject":"Ty Mawr/ Great House, Llandough, Cardiff","name":"Ty Mawr/ Great House, Llandough, Cardiff","date":"2026-09-20T07:07:18Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0bda407bdaa9ab.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0bda2634ae66e1-ty-mawr-great-house-llandough-cardiff","scopeTopics":["rcahmw"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["rcahmw"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0bda2634ae66e1","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"rcahmw","name":"RCAHMW (Royal Commission)"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0bda407bdaa9ab.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Rhodri, dear colleagues,\n>\n> Diolch am eich cydnabyddiaeth ar 8 Medi. On the Welsh Language Standards\npoint raised in your signature — I am Welsh and I would be glad to receive\ncorrespondence in Welsh, but please use whichever language is quicker for\nyou. I do not want language to delay anything, and I will not treat a Welsh\nreply as needing translation.\n>\n> This is a request under section 1 of the Freedom of Information Act 2000\nand, so far as the records concern the historic environment, under the\nEnvironmental Information Regulations 2004.\n>\n> **First, a point of housekeeping. Nothing in this email amends,\nclarifies, narrows or withdraws my requests of 8, 15 or 17 September.**\nThose stand exactly as made and their statutory periods run from their own\ndates. This is an additional request, and I would not want it to be treated\nas restarting any clock. On my reckoning:\n>\n> - **8 September 2026** — FOI/EIR on R. F. Suggett's December 1988\nunpublished records, H. J. Thomas's 1974 survey, the source of the \"held by\nthe National Museum of Wales\" attribution, and copy correspondence with\nAmgueddfa Cymru, GGAT, Cadw and the Vale. Acknowledged by you the same day,\ntwenty working days. **Due Tuesday 6 October 2026.** No reference number\nissued; nothing yet supplied.\n> - **15 September 2026** — catalogue check on Emergency Recording case\n**RCS2/1/1777**, filed under Great House (Ty Mawr), Bonvilston, where the\nmaterial described is the pattern one would expect for the Llandough\ndemolition. **Due Tuesday 13 October 2026.**\n> - **17 September 2026** — whether the Commission was notified, consulted\nor asked to record the farmhouse before its demolition on 6 December 1988.\n**Due Thursday 15 October 2026.**\n>\n> The second and third received only the automated enquiry acknowledgement,\nwhich cites fifteen working days and notes that the priority search service\nis suspended. I understand the staffing position and I am not complaining\nabout it. But both emails ask for recorded information, and a request for\nrecorded information is a request under section 1 whether or not it is\nlabelled as one. **Please confirm that all three are logged as FOIA / EIR\nrequests and let me have the reference numbers.** If they are currently\nsitting in the research-enquiry queue rather than the information-rights\nqueue, that is easily fixed now and much harder to fix later.\n>\n> **On the number of requests.** I am conscious that this is my fifth email\nin twelve days. It is not an attempt to burden the Commission. Each request\narose from a document I only found after sending the last one — the GGAT\n1990 assessment led me to Suggett, Suggett's own note led me to the lost\ncorrespondence, and a Coflein search led me to RCS2/1/1777. I am content\nfor all of them to be answered together in a single response if that is\nmore efficient for you, and if the whole exceeds the appropriate limit\nunder section 12 I will happily tell you which items to do first rather\nthan have anything refused outright. Please just ask.\n>\n> **The site.** Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr, Llandough (Llandochau) juxta\nPenarth, Vale of Glamorgan — the community of St Dochdwy's Church and\nLlandough Hospital. **Not** Llandough near Cowbridge, and **not** Ty Mawr,\nBonvilston (Cadw 13603), which is a different and still-standing house.\nNPRN 20182. ST 1681 7331. HER GGAT02038s. Now Church View Close.\nOutbuildings demolished 30 November 1988; farmhouse 6 December 1988.\n>\n> Holdings I am aware of include file references **NA/GL/88/49** and\n**NA/GL/89/208**, NMR site file material catalogued on Coflein (a plan with\nadditions dated 12 December 1988, a descriptive account, a press cutting),\nInventory working papers for Glamorgan Inventory site 425, and the disputed\nERC bundle RCS2/1/1777.\n>\n> **Why this matters to me.** The farm was my family's. The immediate\nreason for this request is a line in your own records: R. F. Suggett's\nDecember 1988 assessment notes that *\"Correspondence between C. N. Johns\nand Mrs Buckler, Great House Farm, has been lost from the file since\n1986/87\"*, and that *\"There is apparently no photographic record of the\nsite.\"* Mrs Buckler was my relative. I want the correspondence if it\nsurvives, the record of how it came to be lost if it does not, and the rest\nof the file either way. I am not asking the Commission for a view on title\nor on the legality of the demolition, and I would not expect one.\n>\n> **Information requested**\n>\n> Please supply copies, so far as held:\n>\n> 1. A full contents list or file schedule for **NA/GL/88/49** and\n**NA/GL/89/208**, itemising every document on each file, whether or not the\ndocuments themselves are disclosed.\n> 2. Copies of all documents on those two files.\n> 3. **The file cover, front sheet or wrapper of each file**, and any\nenclosure list, loans register, issue slip, reader-request record or\nfile-tracking entry attached to or kept for them.\n> 4. Every record concerning the **C. N. Johns / Mrs Buckler\ncorrespondence** recorded as lost since 1986/87: any surviving copy, draft,\nextract, summary, index entry or catalogue stub; any contemporaneous or\nlater note of its loss; any record of a search for it; any record of who\nconsulted, borrowed or handled the file between 1 January 1985 and 31\nDecember 1988; and any internal or external correspondence about its\nabsence. If no such record exists, please say so expressly rather than\nleaving it unaddressed.\n> 5. The Commission's **retention and disposal schedule** applicable to\nsite files and correspondence files in the 1980s and today, and\nconfirmation whether any records relating to this site have been destroyed\nunder it, and if so when and under what authority.\n> 6. Whether the files listed above have been **reviewed for transfer to,\nor retention from, The National Archives or the Glamorgan Archives**, and\nthe outcome of any such review.\n> 7. All **Inventory working papers, drafts, card-index entries and record\ncards** for the site, including those for Glamorgan Inventory site 425.\n> 8. All **photographs** of the site in any format — prints, negatives,\ntransparencies, digital images, and any oblique or vertical aerial coverage\nof Llandough showing the farm at any date — whether or not catalogued, with\ntheir catalogue entries. Given Suggett's 1988 note that there is apparently\nno photographic record, **please state expressly whether the Commission\nholds none**, and whether any images sit within RCS2/1/1777 or in\nuncatalogued Emergency Recording material.\n> 9. Confirmation, from the location plan, map copies and photographs\ninside **RCS2/1/1777**, whether that bundle is Bonvilston or Llandough. If\nLlandough, please re-link it to NPRN 20182 and supply a contents list and\ncopies. Please also check adjacent case **RCS2/1/1776**. *(This is my\nrequest of 15 September, restated here for completeness only — it stands on\nits own date.)*\n> 10. Any record of a site visit, watching brief, recording exercise or\nemergency recording carried out or considered before, during or after the\ndemolition; any notification of the proposed demolition, from whom and on\nwhat date; and any decision **not** to record the building together with\nthe reasons recorded for it. The building was not listed — Cadw inspected\non 29 July 1988 and did not list it — so I make no assumption that the\nCommission was entitled to notice. What I would like to know is what the\nCommission's own criteria for initiating Emergency Recording were at the\ntime, and whether this site was considered against them. Contemporary\nreports of 3 December 1988 stated that Cadw was considering emergency\nlisting and that discussion with the Commission was being arranged. The\nhouse came down three days later.\n> 11. The **1974 survey by H. J. Thomas**, his site notes of 26 April 1974\nand 12 December 1988, and any measured drawings, sketches, plans or\nelevations, published or unpublished.\n> 12. **R. F. Suggett's December 1988 records in full** — every page, card,\ncontinuation sheet, annotation and enclosure — not only the passages\nconcerning the c.1880 find.\n> 13. Any record of the source of the statement that the c.1880 ironwork\n\"is held by the National Museum of Wales\", and any later file that\nconfirms, corrects or withdraws that attribution.\n> 14. All correspondence between the Commission and any of the following\nconcerning this site, 1970–1995: Cadw or the Welsh Office; the\nGlamorgan-Gwent Archaeological Trust; Vale of Glamorgan Borough Council; BP\nProperties Limited or its agents, including Debenham Tewson & Chinnocks;\nPersimmon Homes (Wales) Limited; Ideal Homes Wales Limited; Amgueddfa\nCymru; any Member of Parliament; and any member of my family.\n> 15. Any accession records for material deposited with the Commission by\nGGAT, Cadw, Cotswold Archaeology or any other body relating to this site.\n>\n> **Practicalities.** Please reply by email to this address. I cannot\nattend in person and I am not seeking anything that attracts a charge.\nScans are fine. Where an item is already on Coflein, a stable reference and\nlink is enough **provided** you confirm the online record is complete. If\nany item is held elsewhere — The National Archives, Glamorgan Archives,\nCadw, Heneb or Amgueddfa Cymru — please name the body and reference so I\ncan apply there directly, which I will.\n>\n> Finally, so that it is on record from the outset rather than raised\nlater: please let me have the name and contact details of the officer\nresponsible for information rights at the Commission, and confirmation of\nthe internal review procedure, so that I know where to direct any review\nrequest if one becomes necessary.\n>\n> With thanks for your patience with a long list.\n>\n> Yn gywir / Yours faithfully,\n>\n> Requester\n> [email redacted]\n\n--"},{"id":"gm-1a0bd8120efb077e","subject":"Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr) — heritage research and a Welsh museum project in Patagonia","name":"Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr) — heritage research and a Welsh museum project in Patagonia","date":"2026-09-20T06:29:10Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0bd8120efb077e.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0bcd6554c4ebec-great-house-farm-t-mawr-heritage-research-and-a-welsh-museum-project-in-patagonia","scopeTopics":["llandough"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["llandough"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0bcd6554c4ebec","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"llandough","name":"Llandough Community Council"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0bd8120efb077e.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Councillor Misra & Co,\n\nMy family, the Williams and later Buckler family, lived at Great House Farm\n(Tŷ Mawr) beside St Dochdwy's for almost 4 consecutive centuries until\n1988, when our 800 year old 10 bed manorial home was subject to an SAS\nstyle siege and demolished. I was born there. The site is now Church View\nClose.\n\nThat whole incident appears to stem from a 1955 possession order for a\nparcel of land east of the parcel we held freehold of. The order contained\nambiguity that was later misinterprited to make our occupancy of our parcel\nappear permissive, following our paper title copy being stolen. A 1962\npossession order for our parcel was created based on that but not enforced\non my disabled, widow grandmother. Who just had a leg amputated in 1955.\nInstead the interest in the 1962 order was conveyed from Western Ground\nRents to BP in 1969. BP altered the ordinance survey, shifting the\ndescriptor from their parcel onto my nans. And appending \"farm' to it. They\nagain made her occupancy appear pernissive in 1974 with their famous\nlicence letters. Then conveyed interest in that, again, to their own sister\ncompany in 1975. Not withstanding the possession of the 1962 order was\nquashed in 1974. Also in 1974 my nans ownership claim was not legally\nexstiguished and instead the court ajourned. In 1983 BP then quietly\nregistered our land in their names at the land registry, failing to\ndisclose my nans ownership claim, she also died in the 2 weeks she will\nhave had to challenge them. She had not been known to not challenge them in\ndecades of her campaigning. Land registry also didn't notify her/ us. BP\nthen merged our parcel into theirs, as my nan fought for her life and died.\nThe land registry wrote to the MP this week claiming not find anything\nwrong with the role they played in 1982/1983. .\n\nIn 1877 title became our families from Daniel Thomas & Son. But not until\n1928 when quarrying completed on the land. In 1916 Bute produced an\nagrictural tenancy for our family featuring our occupied parcel and a\nparcel to the east. The interest in this is what conveyed to Western Ground\nRents in 1938. And in 1939 they revised the agreement for the parcel east\nof ours. And excluded ours parcel from any tenancy due to the 1928 event\nand my great grandads registration of the freehold.\n\nThe 1877 - 1928 agreement and title deed was stolen from our home according\nto my late nans statement in these proceedings.\n\nWhen WGR saught possession of their parcel to the east of ours in 1955,\nthey exercised moral hazard to introduce ambiguity into the order,\nconsidering my nans vulnerable state (amputee, widow, pensioner, stolen\ntitle deed), which could have and did get later misinterprited to make my\nnans occupancy appear permissive. And Western Ground Rents the owner. Thus\nfabricating a defective title with the 1962 possession order for my nans\nparcel and through conveying that unenforced possession order to BP in\n1969. BPs permissive occupancy licence in 1974 and 1975 conveyance repeated\nthat process to make interest transfer and a title chain emerge, with no\nroot title.\n\nMr Paul Egan answered a request from me on 14 June this year. He searched\nthe Council's records, explained that the minute books from 1982 onward\nwere deposited with Glamorgan Archives in 2002, set out the section 21\nposition correctly, and pointed me to the Council's own pages on buildings\nof significance in the community. He also told me something I had not known\n— that before 1982 Llandough came under Penarth Town Council — which has\nopened a line of research I would otherwise have missed. I had written to\nhim in strong terms and he replied with courtesy and care anyway. I\nunderstand he has since retired after long service to the village. Please\npass on my thanks if you are able to. I am not asking the Council to\nrevisit his answer; it was a proper one.\n\nI am assembling a complete documentary and photographic record of my\nfamily's old manorial 10-bedroom birth and family home since 1667, at the\ncentre of that church you British built in the 1800s — I am interested in\neverything: planning, archaeology, land registration, family papers, oral\nmemory — all into a single, centralised searchable archive. So the actual\nfull story can be retold. Warts and all.\n\nPlease bear with me for the rest of my email. I was traumatised by our\nexperience being dispossessed in the 80's. We've seen no justice. My family\ndied challenging any of the false narrative. And the remaining alive won't\nspeak up. The State have tried covering this up and continue to do so for\ndecades too. I'm the first to try to bring this all to light.\n\nMy intent with all this is a Welsh heritage project I have underway in\nPatagonia, Argentina, where a replica of my old farm is to be constructed\nas a museum.\n\nIn Argentina they are different from you British currently occupying us\nWelsh in Wales, in that they celebrate and don't supress or conceal our\nWelsh heritage, culture, language, history. It is very refreshing for me\nthere, as an indigenous Briton who was brutally torn from my ancestral\nbirthland in 1988, in what can only be described as ethnic cleansing. I am\nnot even exaggerating; the particulars of the horrors done to my family\nthere make reconstructing the full chronology very challenging. It's not\nnew what Brit'ish do to indigenous people globally, or the Welsh\nhistorically. But we were maybe the last to be annexed, colonised and\nlooted in such a systematic and brutal way.\n\nIt would be passable if we were sincerely squatters being evicted as the\npress reported. Or even courts believed. But the land BP possessed they\nhad. We lost the parcel east of ours fair and square. They came to occupy\nour neighbouring land and home too out of greed, to build Church View\nClose. Using force, stealth/ secrecy and license. The destinction would\nhave been clear had BP not registered our land in their names and merged\nours into theirs, in such a clandestine way, in 1982/1983.\n\nSo much of my family's and people's history and heritage in Llandough\ncontinues to be completely covered up in lieu of those 20 characterless\n(Church View Close) concentration work-camp houses, without so much as a\npark-bench plaque to commemorate any of the following:\n\n- the 1,026 medieval burials\n- Marconi staying with us while inventing radio\n- Peerless Joe Driscoll fighting his first fight in our barn\n- the Roman villa\n- Christian monastery\n- 2,200-year-old Celtic roundhouse\n\nthe list goes on. The LiDAR scans etc.\n\nI find it abhorrent that the Llandough website history page does not\nmention any of this either. I know the British are proud of their No.1\ninvention: the concentration work camp — but it is sad witnessing all our\ntowns and cities being turned into them. Not that I am on the British Isles\nmuch, except occasionally as a sort of autochthonous Django Unchained.\nSeeing all the voters being imported to keep the kinds of governors,\nPrincess of Darkness and demons the Bible warns us about in power does not\neven sadden me anymore. I think crusades may happen sooner as a result, and\ntherein resides my hope for my small pale island.\n\nIt was not always such a demonic realm for us natives, nor our\nnon-indigenous actual guests, friends and family in Llandough. My family\nlived in the main manorial house in Llandough consecutively since 1667\nuntil the British's highly illegal, unlawful and unconstitutional siege and\ntheft of our stronghold in 1988. We still appear to own the land under\nChurch View Close. You British only awarded yourselves possession of our\nChristian stronghold. Possession being the weapon of choice of a demonic\nrealm. When I was a soldier in Cyprus I would enjoy watching the UN hand\nback stolen, occupied land to the Greeks and Turks. I look forward to that\noccurring when Wales literally, not figuratively, decolonises. Or Scotland\nrenounces. Or Ireland unites. I see the British State as more of a\nterrorist organisation than the IRA, and I think it is the British State we\nneed to stop harbouring on our motherland.\n\nIn the interim of revolution, I hold some faith in coalitions. Namely\nthanks to Councillor Chris Franks, my grandmother's old good friend of\ndecades, who at least acknowledged my email. And that a grave injustice\ndoes appear to have occurred to my family and, as a matter of public\ninterest, others too. To be brutally honest I think my case shows signs we\nare in a landmark bubble and a land-market crash is coming. This racket of\nconverting amputee, disabled widow pensioners' occupancy of our lands from\nfreehold to permissive (with ambiguity and misinterpretation and fraud and\nconcealment) possession orders and conveyance, stealth/secrecy etc. — was\nnot a sustainable viable way of annexing. You can fool some of the people\nsome of the time. But not all of the people all of the time.\n\nBut this is a request for co-operation with reconstructing history, not a\ncomplaint. And history which has already been driven to lands many\nthousands of miles away, so it can remain buried in the British's wardrobe\nof skeletons as you so wish.\n\nWith 99% of British MPs refusing to sign EDM 2025 condemning decapitating\nChristians, I think Argentina is an apt location for my replica manorial\nhouse, notwithstanding Michael Jones who founded the settlement in\nPatagonia had followers who even nicknamed the reason for it as being the\n\"decapitation committee\" here on the British Isles (a century after we\nacquired my birth home in Llandough, so some time in the late 1700s).\n\nIn the interests of being straight with you: I had difficulty getting\nreplies from the Vale ward member, Councillor George Carroll, and earlier\nthis year I made a formal complaint to the Vale of Glamorgan about that.\nWhich I was surprised at after he got thrown out of a council meeting for\nbeing against the City of Sanctuary scheme. My motto is, if you are not on\na government watchlist by now, you should be ashamed of yourself.\n\nI am already resigned to flying in and out of this small formerly pale\nisland, like it is another military tour of the Middle East. I actually do\nnot harbour much ill will; I am just sad it has gone to the dogs and half\nexpect a nuclear strike, flood or crusade.\n\nMy advice to my followers is not even to keep defending the concentration\nwork camps our hijacked towns and cities have been turned into. Instead I\nencourage soldiers under my command to enjoy exile or mass exodus. There is\nplenty in the British organisations' cells/camps and networks' own rules\nwhich permits corps and exodusers. That way everyone's happy.\n\nThe British State is not the first regime to go rogue. Terrorist\norganisations (now the official government) like in Afghanistan also have\nhostages and prisoners of war who are masochist submissives, like\nBritish/UK citizens. And it is a situationship that suits both parties. But\nnot something I would want to get in the middle of or encourage others to\nget in the middle of. I do not know why so many do.\n\nI am persuaded to organise veterans to attend funerals of casualties of the\ncivil war our infiltrated, hijacked and traitor-filled regimes waged on\nindigenous Britons, with its biological weapons, hybrid warfare, ethnic\ncleansing and so forth. And we simply wear our uniforms and salute the\ncasualties. It is some remedy for the grieving families who, even if\nspeaking out from the decades of being terrorised into silence, face\ndecades of being stonewalled from justice from the State. So I am all about\nthe peaceful, legal and lawful paths forward — as a bona fide, certified,\nexperienced military commander, leader and warfare professional.\n\nI have seen Councillor Carroll's recent social media posts standing in\nfront of the photograph of my old farm's only public photo that hangs in\nthe Merry Harrier pub. If it is in good faith and support I am glad.\nAnything less I would not be surprised. Only disappointed. But I extend the\nbenefit of the doubt with my farm case, because it is a complex case. Even\nin my profession as a seasoned cyber/electronic military warfare\nprofessional I find it colossal to navigate.\n\nWhat I would be grateful for from you who occupy and rule my land, in place\nof your Pharisee predecessors, is a coalition with counter-officers such as\nmyself.\n\nDoes your occupying forces/council hold any records that were created after\n2002 and therefore not part of the 2002 deposit, concerning our stolen,\noccupied Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr/Cedfin)/Church View Close land?\n\nAnd I should add: suspected, as I am still gathering evidence and\ninvestigating. Albeit it all looks damning for the British and many native\ntraitors already, and I am only scratching the surface of what happened in\nLlandough. My ancestral birthland would not be the first to be looted by\nthe British and I am doubtful it will be the last. Our SAS-looted treasures\nare actually now all captive in British museums. And our SAS-looted\nfurniture, all from Cardiff Castle, are also held hostage or in circulation\ntoo.\n\nMy focus and what I require of your Council, as but a humble (registered to\nvote) constituent of the home we got in Dinas Powys and have lived in since\nour land became illegally occupied by the British in 1988 — is the village\ngreen in front of St Dochdwy's — including registered village green VG 41,\nwhich your Council raised formally with the Vale when the Great House Farm\nsite came forward for planning in 1989.\n\nBy constituent I do mean I am happy to play the role of a member of society\nin a democracy. Albeit it is largely now pageantry and being made a mockery\nof in my estimation. I see something very different going on here, i.e. a\nterrorist/hostage situationship. And I do not play the role of a hostage or\nprisoner of war well — at all! Nor am I a masochist submissive and much\nlike kissing ring fingers or bending knees. My 22 in Oath ended in 2024\n(employed under the Vale of Glamorgan Council too, by some freak\ncoincidence). So I will thank you in advance not to ask weird stuff of me.\nI am nice until it is time not to be. And I believe we are all equal under\nlaw and God.\n\nMy family have endured enough force, stealth/secrecy and licence from the\nBritish in the 80s to last us a lifetime. And having been behind a machine\ngun in a helicopter in the Middle East, and trained on explosives and\nweapons and cyber/electronic warfare capabilities for my military career,\nwhich I left with full honours, I am well aware of how the British regime\noperates and human-farms and projects its force. I once had Britain's very\nown woke mind virus walking through my mind with its muddy boots. I hope\nyou all get better too.\n\nBetter your cell/network/camp just skip to bringing guns and trying to\nforce me at gunpoint if you demand anything of me that contrasts\nco-operation or coalition. I have seen already a lot of local authorities,\neven South Wales Police, opting for clandestine and shadowy silence and\nviolence, rather than just responding to public FOIs. I think your regime's\nattempt to keep our Llandough farm story from ever seeing the light of day\nis understandable. But I do find people from time to time, even Iraqis in\nIraq, even myself as a former British soldier, wanting to choose the right\nside of history rather than allegiance to a sinking rat ship.\n\nAny record of works carried out on the green and the roadside verge by the\nchurchyard wall in the mid-1980s is all I am asking for. I hold colour\nphotographs of trees being removed, spoil carted away and new kerbstones\nlaid; vehicle registrations in them date the works to between about 1983\nand 1990, on more than one occasion. (See attached.) Councillor Carroll's\nmonths of non-response to this specific request to find this information\nout was not going to stop me finding all this out. Asking for transparency\nand co-operation is mainly a courtesy and formality, seeing as I am a\nseasoned military cyber/electronic warfare professional. Going on 30 years\nnow in fact.\n\nMy great-grandfather planted a tree on that green to mark my family's claim\nto that land, and my family believe that tree was lost in the work of\nmaking that green, i.e. possible further destruction of evidence, possibly\nnothing. I should be clear that the green does not even appear to fall\ninside the boundary of land we have the interest in, in our ancestral town\nyou currently occupy, so this is a question about the village's history as\nmuch as ours.\n\nAny photographs, postcards or images of the farm, the barn, the green or\nthe church surroundings that the Council holds or knows of. Please\ndisclose. I have vacuumed most of Leckwith Archives already.\n\nAny guidance on where the pre-1982 community records now sit, given that\nLlandough fell within Penarth Town Council before then, and whether the\nCouncil holds anything that came across at the 1982 reorganisation, I would\nlike you to please disclose.\n\nThe names of any former members, former clerks or long-standing residents\nwho might be willing to talk to me about the village between the 1950s and\nthe 1990s. I would approach anyone you suggest courteously and would not\npress a refusal. Please disclose.\n\nI am content for this email to be tabled at a meeting and minuted if the\nCouncil wishes.\nI am also happy to give a short talk to the Council or the community,\nwithout charge, on what the records have revealed about Llandough's past —\nthe Roman villa, the early medieval cemetery beneath Church View Close, the\nmonastic history, and the farm itself.\n\nA good deal of what I have found belongs to the village as much as my\nfamily. Llandough just appears more committed to its current trajectory of\nbecoming even more of a concentration work camp, having already rejected\nmuch of this history and heritage and native people, in lieu of whatever it\nis you have underway there. I am not going to burn Llandough down to feel\nits warmth. I know demonic realms are inherently ice cold. To give an\nexample of what I speak of, I found the Llandough Facebook page to contain\nonly photos of exotic takeaway foods. Sad considering its rich history and\nheritage.\n\nI am not about leading my men to spill their lifeblood on Home Office\nMahmoods and other British blades, over any of this demographic replacement\nand ethnic cleansing. I am just trying to salvage, reconstruct a part of my\nfamily's and Welsh history and heritage while people still have heads on\ntheir shoulders here, to be able to co-operate in this matter. Even without\ncivil unrest, alleged fiduciaries have not kept proper records of this\ncase. Mine may be more complete than the British Government's and EU's at\nthis point. And even the Internet's, [family detail redacted].\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester \n\n\nP.S. Vehicle registrations visible in them (including A305 MKG, which\ncannot predate August 1983, and plant registered MTX 936X and MTX 930X)\nplace the works in the mid-1980s, between 1983 and 1990, and not during the\nlater residential development."},{"id":"gm-1a0bbe265cd51e5b","subject":"Automatic reply: Great House Farm, Llandough, Cardiff","name":"Automatic reply: Great House Farm, Llandough, Cardiff","date":"2026-09-19T22:56:08Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0bbe265cd51e5b.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0bbe265cd51e5b-automatic-reply-great-house-farm-llandough-cardiff","scopeTopics":["heneb"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["heneb"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0bbe265cd51e5b","direction":"received","from":"Barbara Rees","institutions":[{"id":"heneb","name":"Heneb (formerly GGAT) / Historic Environment Record"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0bbe265cd51e5b.json","textStatus":"available","text":"My next working day is Wednesday, 23rd September.\n\nDiolch."},{"id":"gm-1a0bbe1ba706aa71","subject":"Great House Farm, Llandough, Cardiff","name":"Great House Farm, Llandough, Cardiff","date":"2026-09-19T22:55:27Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0bbe1ba706aa71.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0bbdf18c9ea3b9-great-house-farm-llandough-cardiff","scopeTopics":["heneb"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["heneb"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0bbdf18c9ea3b9","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"heneb","name":"Heneb (formerly GGAT) / Historic Environment Record"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0bbe1ba706aa71.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Calli,\n\nFurther to my email of 8 September 2026 concerning Great House Farm / Tŷ\nMawr, Llandough, I attach a formal schedule of the records I am seeking, so\nthat the request is easier for Heneb to work through and to allocate\ninternally. I have also completed your online enquiry form, so the request\nis in your preferred channel.\n\nI appreciate a response would not fall due until 6 October, so this is not\na chaser. It is to put the detail in one place and to clarify the basis of\nthe request.\n\nOn your note of 22 May. You are right that Heneb, as a limited company, is\nnot a public authority under the Freedom of Information Act 2000, and I do\nnot pursue that. Two separate points remain, and I would welcome Heneb's\nposition on each.\n\nFirst, the Environmental Information Regulations 2004 apply on a different\ntest — regulation 2(2) reaches bodies carrying out functions of public\nadministration relating to the environment, or under the control of a\npublic authority. If Heneb considers it falls outside that, please say so\nexpressly with brief reasons, so that I can direct the request elsewhere.\n\nSecond, and independently, the Historic Environment Record is a statutory\nrecord: sections 194 and 195 of the Historic Environment (Wales) Act 2023\nrequire the Welsh Ministers to maintain an HER for every local authority\narea and to make it available for public inspection, and Heneb keeps the\nsouth-east Wales record on their behalf. I therefore ask in the alternative\nthat this be treated as a request for access under that regime and under\nHeneb's own policy.\n\nTwo things that will make this easier. If any item is too broad, please\ntell me which and I will narrow it rather than have the whole request\nrefused. And if Heneb can supply, as a first step, simply a schedule or\nindex of the records it holds for this site — without copying them — that\nwould let me target the rest precisely and save your staff considerable\ntime. I am content to inspect material by appointment at Baglan or at the\nHER, and any reasonable copying or staff-time charge can be notified to me\nin advance for approval.\n\nTwo small administrative points. Please confirm which legal entity now\nholds the records and functions formerly of Glamorgan-Gwent Archaeological\nTrust Ltd, and who within Heneb handles access requests of this kind.\n\nFinally, since my correspondence in May was framed more formally: my\npurpose is documentary. I am reconstructing the record of a farm my family\noccupied for generations, and I would far rather do that with Heneb's help\nthan around it.\n\nI should say plainly that I have read GGAT's December 1993 notification to\nthe Vale of Glamorgan that Condition 18 had not been complied with, and the\nrecommendation that a Breach of Condition Notice be issued. Whatever else\nis in dispute about this site, that was the Trust doing its job properly,\nand I am grateful for it.\n\nThank you in advance for your time in this matter,\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester \n\n\nEnc: Schedule of Records Requested, ref GHF/HENEB/2026-09-20"},{"id":"gm-1a0baa2acfb5f1ec","subject":"Automatic reply: Great House Farm, Llandough — two questions for HM Land Registry (ref N610CGV)","name":"Automatic reply: Great House Farm, Llandough — two questions for HM Land Registry (ref N610CGV)","date":"2026-09-19T17:06:47Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0baa2acfb5f1ec.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0baa2acfb5f1ec-automatic-reply-great-house-farm-llandough-two-questions-for-hm-land-registry-ref-n610cgv","scopeTopics":["mp"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["mp"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0baa2acfb5f1ec","direction":"received","from":"DOUGHTY, Stephen","institutions":[{"id":"mp","name":"Member of Parliament"}],"refs":["N610CGV"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0baa2acfb5f1ec.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Hello,\n\nThank you for your email. Please read this automatic response, which serves as notice that your message has been received.\n\nI receive hundreds of emails every week, and although I intend to reply as quickly as I can to all, I prioritise those that raise immediate problems and urgent cases.\n\n\nCasework\n\nThere is a strict protocol that means Members of Parliament may only provide assistance to their own constituents. This means you must live in the constituency of Cardiff South and Penarth in order for me to assist.\nPlease therefore ensure that you have included:\n\n• Your full name\n\n• Your full postal address\n\n• Your contact telephone number\n\n• Any relevant reference numbers\n\n\nParliamentary Business\n\nIf your email relates to my work in Parliament, it will be dealt with as appropriate.\n\n\nMinisterial Business\n\nIf you are contacting me in my capacity as a Minister at the FCDO, you will need to redirect your email to to [email redacted]<[email redacted]>, or alternatively contact your own MP – you can find who that is at https://members.parliament.uk/FindYourMP\n\n\nThank you for taking the time to contact me.\n\nFor information about how we use your personal data, see our Privacy Notice<https://www.stephendoughty.wales/privacynotice>.\n\n\nYours sincerely,\n\n\nStephen\n\n\nStephen Doughty MP / AS\nMember of Parliament for Cardiff South and Penarth // Aelod Seneddol dros Dde Caerdydd a Phenarth\nHouse of Commons, Westminster, London, [postcode redacted]\nTel: [phone redacted] | Email: [email redacted]\n\n\nUK Parliament Disclaimer: this e-mail is confidential to the intended recipient. If you have received it in error, please notify the sender and delete it from your system. Any unauthorised use, disclosure, or copying is not permitted. This e-mail has been checked for viruses, but no liability is accepted for any damage caused by any virus transmitted by this e-mail. This e-mail address is not secure, is not encrypted and should not be used for sensitive data."},{"id":"gm-1a0baa224660ca61","subject":"Great House Farm, Llandough — two questions for HM Land Registry (ref N610CGV)\r\n","name":"Great House Farm, Llandough — two questions for HM Land Registry (ref N610CGV)\r\n","date":"2026-09-19T17:06:23Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0baa224660ca61.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0ba9e77a49f085-great-house-farm-llandough-two-questions-for-hm-land-registry-ref-n610cgv","scopeTopics":["mp"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["mp"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0ba9e77a49f085","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"mp","name":"Member of Parliament"}],"refs":["N610CGV"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0baa224660ca61.json","textStatus":"available","text":"MP Doughty,\n\nFurther to my letter earlier, I would like to narrow what I am kindly\nasking of you, and to correct one other thing.\n\nMy letter asked which document brought the former farmhouse and garden at\nGreat House Farm into BP Properties Limited's registrable estate. HM Land\nRegistry has in fact already answered that, and I should have acknowledged\nit. Its letters of 23 March and 19 April 2026 identify a conveyance dated\n19 November 1982 for title WA231076, and a conveyance dated 23 May 1975 for\ntitle WA240304. I will apply to HM Land Registry directly, on form OC2, for\ncopies of those deeds and their plans. I am not asking you to obtain them\nfor me.\n\nTwo questions remain. Both concern inconsistencies within HM Land\nRegistry's own correspondence, rather than anything I am asserting. So that\nyou need not open any attachments, I have set out the relevant passages\nverbatim in the annex below, with dates and authors.\n\n1. The 1971 abstract of title. The letter of 19 April 2026 states twice\nthat an abstract of title dated 1971 was lodged with the applications. The\nletter of 30 July 2026 sets out a complete schedule of the documents held\non both first-registration files — thirty items for WA231076 and thirteen\nfor WA240304 — and no abstract of title appears anywhere on either list.\nWas the abstract lodged; does the list of documents lodged (item 17 on that\nschedule) record it; and if it was lodged, what became of it?\n\n2. The conveyance of 31 December 1969. Both operative conveyances recite\nthe same root of title: a conveyance dated 31 December 1969 between Western\nGround Rents Limited and The British Petroleum Pension Trust Limited. That\ndeed does not appear on the 30 July schedule either. Was it produced to the\nregistry at first registration; does the list of documents lodged record\nit; and if it was not produced, on what evidence was the recital accepted\nas establishing the root of title?\n\nBoth questions can be answered by examining a single document which HM Land\nRegistry confirms it holds and describes as open to public inspection. I am\nnot asking it to reopen BP Properties Ltd v Buckler [1987] EWCA Civ 2, to\ndetermine ownership, or to revisit any decision it has made. A written\nanswer to those two questions is all I am seeking.\n\nI can also withdraw one request from my earlier letter. I asked that the\nsurviving records be preserved; that is no longer necessary, as HM Land\nRegistry has confirmed the documents will remain on file.\n\nMy earlier request to meet you stands, but it is not pressing. These two\nquestions can be dealt with in writing.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nRequester\n\nPS - see attached images. I purchased all deeds I could, but the primary\none is inaccessible for purchase as of today. I have raised this issue with\nA.I.\n\nANNEX — VERBATIM EXTRACTS FROM HM LAND REGISTRY CORRESPONDENCE\nAll under reference N610CGV. Originals available on request.\n\n\nA. Letter of 23 March 2026 — Lisa Griffiths, HM Land Registry Wales Office\n\n\"It appears Ty Mawr Farm was originally registered under two different\ntitle numbers, WA231076 and WA240304. These two titles were amalgamated.\nTitle WA240304 was closed as a result of the amalgamation and the\nregistration continued under title WA231076.\"\n\n\"We received an application for registration on 30 Nov 1982. The\napplication was submitted by Linklaters & Paines of Barrington House, 59/67\nGresham Street, London, [postcode redacted], to register BP properties Limited of\nBritannic House, Moor Lane, London, EC2 as proprietor. The land was\nconveyed to BP Properties Limited by a Conveyance dated 19 Nov 1982 made\nbetween (1) The British Petroleum Pension Trust Limited, (2) BP Trustees\nLimited and (3) BP Properties Limited\"\n\n\"We received an application for registration on 23 February 1983… The land\nwas conveyed to BP Properties Limited by a Conveyance dated 23 May 1975\nmade between (1) The British Petroleum Pension Trust Limited, (2) BP\nTrustees Limited, (3) BP Properties Limited.\"\n\n\nB. Letter of 19 April 2026 — Charlotte Takeuchi, Assistant Land Registrar\n(stage one complaint response)\n\nOn the standard applied:\n\n\"In unregistered conveyancing, title is ultimately based on the estate\nowner's right to be in possession of the land or to receive the rents and\nprofits of it. Showing a full documentary title commencing with a good root\nof title that is at least 15 years old should normally establish this right\nand is sufficient for land registration purposes.\"\n\nOn the abstract of title, in relation to WA231076:\n\n\"Our files show an abstract of title listed dated 1971 was lodged with the\napplication for first registration. This is effectively a summary of deeds,\ndocuments and events which show a person's title to unregistered land.\"\n\nOn the abstract of title, in relation to WA240304:\n\n\"Again, our files indicate that we had sight of a copy of a 1971 abstract\nof title.\"\n\nOn the root of title recited in the 19 November 1982 conveyance:\n\n\"The recitals clause (preamble) to that Conveyance states 'The Vendors are\nseised in fee simple in the premises described in the First Schedule hereto\n(hereinafter called \"the premises\") upon the Trusts referred to in a\nConveyance dated 31 December 1969 and made between Western Ground Rents\nLimited of the one part and the Vendors of the other part..' It is clear\ntherefore that the applicant (BP Properties Limited) could show a good root\nof title and that The British Petroleum Pension Trust acquired the land in\n1969.\"\n\nOn the root of title recited in the 23 May 1975 conveyance:\n\n\"The recitals clause to that Conveyance states 'The Vendors are seised in\nfee simple upon the trusts referred to in a conveyance dated the thirty\nfirst day of December One thousand One hundred and sixty nine [sic] and\nmade between Western Ground Rents Limited of the one part and the vendors\nof the other part.'\"\n\n\nC. Letter of 25 May 2026 — Harbinder Gill, Land Registrar (stage two, final\nresponse)\n\n\"HM Land Registry operates a two-stage internal complaints procedure, and\nthis constitutes our second and final response. It is not possible to ask\nanyone else in HM Land Registry to review the matter again.\"\n\n\"We will not be commencing the process of rectification or formally\nreferring the dispute.\"\n\n\nD. Letter of 30 July 2026 — S. G. Haste, HM Land Registry Wales Office\n\n\"After an investigation of our files, we can confirm that the following\ndocuments are available.\"\n\nThe letter then lists thirty documents held for title WA231076 and thirteen\nfor title WA240304. Item 17 of the WA231076 list reads:\n\n\"A13- DL-List of documents lodged with first registration\"\n\nNeither list includes an abstract of title of any date, nor a conveyance\ndated 31 December 1969.\n\nThe letter concludes:\n\n\"We can confirm that all these documents are held on file and will remain\non file. They are all open to public inspection if require copies of them.\"\n[sic]"},{"id":"gm-1a0b7ad7c7c10d58","subject":"Re: From the Office of Stephen Doghty MP","name":"Re: From the Office of Stephen Doghty MP","date":"2026-09-19T03:19:53Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0b7ad7c7c10d58.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0b3cb33eb0553d-from-the-office-of-stephen-doghty-mp","scopeTopics":["mp"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["mp"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0b3cb33eb0553d","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"mp","name":"Member of Parliament"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0b7ad7c7c10d58.json","textStatus":"available","text":"*MP *Doughty,\n\nFurther to the correspondence from Mr Iain Banfield, and my earlier email,\nI am writing again to be sure, placed on record, is the central issue which\nHM Land Registry's response still does not answer.\n\nI appreciate that HM Land Registry is not a court and that it cannot simply\nreopen a Court of Appeal judgment. That, however, does not answer the\nseparate question of whether the land registered in 1982/83 was correctly\nidentified and supported by the documentary title then placed before the\nRegistrar.\n\nMr Banfield's response relies heavily upon *BP Properties Ltd v Buckler*\n[1987] EWCA Civ 2. That judgment post-dated the registration by\napproximately four years. It records BP Properties Ltd as having become\nregistered proprietor of the \"whole farm\" in November 1982 and then\ndetermines the later adverse-possession dispute concerning the farmhouse\nand garden. It does not, however, determine whether the 1982\nfirst-registration application had itself correctly included the farmhouse\nparcel in BP's documentary title, or whether the registration had\nmistakenly conflated two historically separate parcels.\n\nThat distinction is fundamental.\n\nMy family's parcel — the former farmhouse, garden and curtilage, which I\nrefer to as the *House Plot* — was historically distinct from the adjoining\neastern agricultural land. The documentary history I have obtained\nindicates that these areas were not simply one undifferentiated proprietary\nparcel.\n\nThe 1916 agricultural tenancy is one important document because it embraced\nboth areas. But that is not the end of the conveyancing history. The\nearlier 1877 Daniel Thomas & Son instrument, and the arrangement\nsubsequently acted upon when the quarrying ceased in 1928, point to a\nseparate proprietary interest in the House Plot.\n\nI have the 1938 conveyance from the Marquis of Bute to Western Ground\nRents, and the revised 1939 version of the 1916 tenancy, and it did not\nconvey or demise the House Plot at all: the actual 1938 conveyance, the\n1939 tenancy and their plans need to be examined by you lot too, not just\nme. Indeed, the 1987 judgment itself records that the alleged yearly\ntenancy commencing in 1949 has not been produced in the proceedings. Its\ngeographical extent therefore remains unproved. Not withstanding it may\npertain to the parcel to the east we leased and defaulted on, not our\nfreehold parcel. Furthermore my nan expressly rejected the 1949 tenancy and\nattempt to make her occupancy of our parcel permissive in such a way, but\nexpressly rejecting it while asserting her ownership claim of her parcel.\n\nIt's adolescent of everyone to keep having a meltdown and failing to\ncompute the simplicity of the fact Mary Williams asserted she owned her\nparcel. And WGR saught possession of theirs, east of it. Western Ground\nRents, already described in UK Parliament as praying on elderly and being\nabhorrent and scrupilous, clearly tried to make Mary's occupany appear\npermissive in 1949 and the 1955  proceedings.\n\nThe 1987 judgment records that and the 1962 proceeding, after the\nagricultural tenancy was terminated, as Western Ground Rents obtaining a\n1955 possession order for the \"whole of the farm\". Crucially, however, when\nthat order was enforced on 4 July 1955, possession was taken of all\nparts *except\nthe farmhouse and garden*. The judgment gives the immediate reason as Mrs\nMary Buckler's condition and her strong resistance to leaving the\nfarmhouse. Ambiguity which is later misinterprited and clearly an early\nattempt at making her occupancy of her parcel appear permissive.\n\nWGR subsequently brought a *fresh action in 1962 specifically against Mr\nand Mrs  Buckler for possession of the farmhouse and garden and mesne\nprofits*, resulting in the possession and mesne-profits order of 11\nDecember 1962. The judgment records that this order was not enforced before\n1974.\n\nThat sequence matters.\n\nIf the two areas had genuinely always constituted one undifferentiated\nproprietary estate subject to the same right of possession, it is difficult\nto understand why possession of the agricultural land was recovered in 1955\nwhile the farmhouse and garden were left occupied and a separate possession\naction had to be brought in 1962.\n\nI therefore ask that this fact not be retrospectively converted into an\nassumption that the 1955 \"whole farm\" wording necessarily comprised the\nHouse Plot in the same legal sense as the agricultural land.\n\nThe statutory effect attributed by the Court of Appeal to the 1955/1962\nproceedings cannot itself establish title to a different, historically\nseparate parcel which was never actually comprised in those proceedings.\n\nThe possession orders were orders for possession and associated relief.\nThey were not conveyances. They did not retrospectively create a\ndocumentary chain of title where none existed, nor did they by themselves\ndetermine the geographical extent of an underlying freehold documentary\ntitle.\n\nThat is especially important in light of the sequence of events recorded in\nthe 1987 judgment. WGR's own successive proceedings and selective\nenforcement distinguished the agricultural land from the farmhouse and\ngarden in practical terms, whatever the later interpretation of the\nexpression \"whole farm\".\n\nThere is a further point which I believe HM Land Registry has not addressed.\n\nIn 1949, according to the history now being assembled, Mrs Mary Williams\nwas offered a tenancy of the farmhouse and garden which she rejected\nbecause she asserted ownership. The 1987 judgment itself confirms that she\nwas offered a tenancy of the farmhouse and garden by Western Ground Rents\nand refused it. It also records that she had come to believe she had a\ntitle through her grandfather and that documents existed which supported\nthat claim.\n\nThus there was plainly a known dispute over the House Plot long before\nfirst registration.\n\nThe 1974 proceedings and letters must also be put in their proper place.\nThe Court of Appeal held that the 1974 letters operated as a licence,\nnotwithstanding that Mrs Buckler had never expressly accepted them, and\nheld that her possession thereafter was no longer adverse.\n\nI am not asking HM Land Registry to disregard that decision.\n\nDespite it being clear to any sane, reasonable person to see that, given\nthe evidence, the 1974 order was not truly quashed due to the licence\nletters, which came afterwards and were backdated. Made out to a non\nexistent Mrs Buckler. And quashed the case within minutes of Mary being\nnotified of them, with no ample opportunity to respond. Albeit she actually\ndid in writing. And asserted her ownership and challenged BP in a public\nnewspaper. Not withstanding BP couldn't give something they don't own i.e.\npermissive occupancy.\n\nThis permissive occupancy followed by a conversion is a racket. Look at the\npattern;\n\n1955 permissive occupancy ploy > 1969 conveyance (WGR to BP Pension Trust\nLtd)\n\n1974 permissive occupancy ploy > 1975 conveyance (BP Pension Trust to BP\nProperties Ltd)\n\nNor am I asking the Land Registry to even acknowledge the fact court\nclerked notified the press who all decended on the scene, covered the story\n(that Mary asserted ownership, family occupancy since 1667, lawyer confirms\nher ownership claim was valid etc).\n\nI am asking a different question: *what land did BP actually have title to\nin 1974, and what land was actually capable of being registered in BP's\nname in 1982?*\n\nIf the House Plot had never formed part of WGR's documentary estate (not in\nthe 1938 conveyance from Bute, as it went to Daniel Thomas & Son in 1876\nthen to my great grandad John Williams in 1928), then the 1974 letters and\nthe 1962 possession order cannot simply be assumed to cure that problem.\nThe 1987 judgment did not adjudicate that separate issue of\nfirst-registration procedure and documentary extent.\n\nBP never had ownership. Ours ownership claim was never legally\nexstiguished. BP never even saught ownership, only possession. And with no\ntitle chain, only their own issuance of permissive occupancy, based on\ninterest they'd purchased in a possession order, which itself was also\nbased on issuance of permissive occupancy. And with little to no meeting of\nthe minds, but instead stealth/ secrecy. And the registration itself in\n1983 was secret and undisclosed to Mary Williams and us living heirs and\nsuccessors.\nThe Land Registry issue\n\nThe applicable statutory framework in 1982/83 was the Land Registration Act\n1925 and the Land Registration Rules 1925.\n\nThe Rules expressly contained provisions dealing with first registration,\nincluding *rule 20, requiring the applicant to deliver the original deeds\nand documents relating to the title which were in its possession or control*.\nThe operation of that rule in the first-registration context is confirmed\nin *Walker v Burton*, where the High Court described the requirement in\nthose terms.\n\nThe Rules also contained a specific provision headed *\"Examination of title\nby Registrar\"* and provisions concerning searches and enquiries by the\nRegistrar.\n\nThis means that the question cannot simply be reduced to \"Land Registry\nregisters what it is given\".\n\nThe relevant question is whether, in this case:\n\n   1.\n\n   the applicant supplied the material title documents in its possession or\n   control;\n   2.\n\n   the documents supplied actually demonstrated that the House Plot formed\n   part of the estate being registered;\n   3.\n\n   the Registrar examined the documentary chain sufficiently to identify\n   any inconsistency between the deeds and the land claimed for registration;\n   4.\n\n   any discrepancy between the deeds, plans, possession orders and the\n   proposed registration plan generated a requisition or further enquiry;\n   5.\n\n   the registration was completed as an absolute title despite a material\n   uncertainty as to the extent of the documentary title; and\n   6.\n\n   the Registrar's own records contain any warning, requisition, note,\n   correspondence or other indication that the separate House Plot issue was\n   considered.\n\nThere is a particularly important point concerning *registration in error*.\n\nThe Law Commission's review of the 1925 system records that the Land\nRegistration Rules 1925 contained a specific power in *rule 14* dealing\nwith \"registration in error\", including registration of too large a part of\na title, and explained that the registrar had an express power to rectify\nregistration arising from mistake.\n\nThat is significant because HM Land Registry is currently saying, in\neffect, that there was no mistake. That conclusion should not be treated as\nestablished unless HM Land Registry has actually examined the surviving\n1982/83 registration file and established what documentary evidence\njustified the extent registered.\n\nHM Land Registry's present published guidance also expressly recognises\nthat *including too much land in a registered title can constitute a\nmistake in the register*, and gives as an example a title being mapped to\ninclude land for which the registered proprietor has no documentary title.\nIt also recognises that failure by a solicitor to lodge documents in their\npossession can amount to lack of proper care. I appreciate that this is\nmodern guidance and I do not suggest that it can simply be imposed\nretrospectively as the law of 1983. It is nevertheless directly relevant to\nthe distinction HM Land Registry now draws between a genuine registration\nmistake and a mere disagreement about the title boundary.\n\nLikewise, HM Land Registry's current guidance states that a title plan\ncannot describe the land more accurately than the pre-registration deeds\nallow, and that the pre-registration deeds are crucial to resolving\nquestions about the extent of a registered title. Again, I cite this as an\nexpression of the documentary basis of registration, not as a claim that a\n2025/26 practice guide was itself the governing rule in 1983.\nThe present register raises further questions\nIn addition to reperations for the demolition of our 800 year old home with\nus practically still inside at the time, inhumane abuse of my pregnant mum,\nterminally ill father and amputee grandmother, not withstanding 3 toddlers:\nmyself included. I seek restoration of property in that I seek possession\nof the undeveloped woodland half of our land parcel. Which is virgin land,\nhaving not been touched since we were forced from it in 1988.\n\nThe current title material itself refers separately to:\n\n   - the *23 May 1975 conveyance of land tinted blue*, with the copy plan\n   filed under *WA240304*;\n   - the *19 November 1982 conveyance of land tinted pink*, with the\n   original filed under *WA231076*;\n   - rights and retained land associated with those separate conveyances;\n   and\n   - the vendors' adjoining or retained land.\n\nThose entries require the original plans to be compared with the House Plot.\n\nI am particularly concerned that two separate conveyancing areas were\nsubsequently presented as part of a single \"whole farm\" registered estate.\nIf the House Plot was not actually comprised in the conveyances relied\nupon, the relevant question is not whether the modern red edging can be\nmade to fit an Ordnance Survey map. The question is *what deed, order or\nother legally sufficient evidence put that specific land into BP's\nregistrable estate in the first place*.\n\nAn Ordnance Survey description cannot by itself create a conveyance. Much\nlike BPs 1971 alteration of the descriptor of our parcel, shifting \"ty\nmawr\" from theirs to ours and appending \"farm\" to the end of it.\n\nNor can a later registration description retrospectively supply a missing\nlink in the pre-registration conveyancing chain.\n\nI'll repeat this as its important;\n\nNor can a later registration description retrospectively supply a missing\nlink in the pre-registration conveyancing chain.\nThe 1982/83 registration file\n\nHM Land Registry's letter of *30 July 2026, reference N610CGV*, has already\nconfirmed that surviving first-registration material relating to the\nrelevant titles exists and is open to inspection.\n\nI therefore ask you to write again to HM Land Registry, not asking it to\ndecide the family title, but asking it to answer the following precise\nadministrative questions:\n\n*1.* What exact first-registration applications were made for *WA231076*\nand *WA240304*, and what deeds, abstracts, declarations, plans, court\norders and other documents accompanied each application?\n\n*2.* Did the application files contain the *1938 Bute–Western Ground Rents\nconveyance*, the *1939 revised tenancy and plan*, *1877 Daniel Thomas & Son\ninstrument*, or any other document showing that the House Plot was\nhistorically separate? What was included and not included.\n\n*3.* What document was relied upon to establish that the House Plot formed\npart of BP Properties Limited's documentary estate? How was Land Registry\nnot able to see only a plaintiff on the court paperwork preceeding 1982,\nand no defendant?\n\n*4.* What was the precise geographical extent attributed to the 1955\npossession order and the 1962 possession order by the Registrar, and were\nthe original orders or plans ever examined?\n\n*5.* Were the Registrar's records aware of the 1974 proceedings and Mrs\nWilliams  asserted ownership claim when the first-registration applications\nwere processed?\n\nThe only way they could have not been is if they willfully ignorantly and\nselective vision didn't see there was another party to the court\nproceedings, not just a claimant but also a defendant. They/ BP will have\nhad to have fished out a 1935 marriage certificate to find anything to\nmatch the name Mrs Buckler, on the licence letters. A deperate measure to\navoid any of this clandestine registration reaching the families knowledge,\nwhich we would have been very quick to correct all this. Not withstanding\nmy nan will have had to have died to not challenge this, having\nrelentlessly and unwaveringly challenged this for decades already. And low\nand behold she did conveniently die in the two weeks she will have had to\nchallenge this in 1983. I guess someone murdered her, just to be absolutely\nsure all this went ahead without any issues.\n\n*6.* Did the Registrar raise any requisition because the possession\nhistory, the separate 1955/1962 proceedings, the 1975 blue conveyance, the\n1982 pink conveyance and the proposed registered extent did not correspond\nstraightforwardly?\n\n*7.* On what evidence was the House Plot incorporated into the land\nregistered to BP, rather than being left outside the registration pending\nproof of title?\n\n*8.* Was the title registered with an absolute title, qualified title,\npossessory title or another class, and what was the evidential basis for\nthe class of title granted?\n\n*9.* Does HM Land Registry's surviving file contain any memorandum,\nexaminer's note, requisition, map correspondence or other record explaining\nhow the apparently separate areas were reconciled into the registered\nextent?\nWhat I am asking you to investigate\n\nI am not asking you, or HM Land Registry, to determine that my family is\npresently entitled to reporations of land now occupied by others. Or\npossession of the unoccupied woodlands north of church view close.\n\nI am asking that the Department distinguish between two entirely different\npropositions:\n\n*First:* the Court of Appeal decided the legal effect of the 1962\npossession order and the 1974 letters in the particular litigation that\ncame before it.\n\n*Second:* HM Land Registry registered land in BP's name in 1982/83. That\nadministrative act necessarily depended upon a first-registration\napplication, documentary evidence and a determination of the land capable\nof registration.\n\nThe second proposition cannot simply be proved by citing the first.\n\nIf HM Land Registry now asserts that there was no mistake in registration,\nit should be able to identify the documentary chain and the registration\nevidence which led it to that conclusion.\n\nIs land registry claiming my great grandad John Williams DID NOT gain and\nregister title, in 1928, when the 1876/77 Marquess of Bute to Daniel Thomas\nand Son agreement enacted, thus title chain/ freehold transferred from DT&S\nto us? Is their understanding it conveyed from Bute/ DT&S to Western Ground\nRents in 1938? Or even Williams Title ended up being conveyed to Western\nGround Rents prior to 1955. And then to BP in 1969?  Because both my nans\nstatement alleges her paper title was stolen in the 40's. And some family\nmembers rumour her own husband Fredrick, pawned it, possible to settle Mary\nWilliams fathers debts. That might explain Fredricks interest in the 1949\ntenancy offer from WGR, if it indeed compromised the House Plot.\n\nIf the surviving file shows that the House Plot was supported by a valid\nconveyance or other legally sufficient title, that would answer these\nquestions.\n\nIf, however, the file shows that the House Plot was included because an\nambiguous \"whole farm\" permissive occupancy/  possession history was\ntreated as equivalent to documentary title, or because two separate parcels\nwere inadvertently conflated, that would be a materially different\nsituation. It would raise an obvious question as to whether the\nregistration process contained an error, an omission, inadequate\nexamination, or reliance upon an incomplete or inaccurate presentation of\nthe title.\n\nI am deliberately not asserting fraud or negligence as an established fact\nat this stage. I'm just determined to make sense of what happened so that I\ncan make the case and get this matter and evidence adjudicated.\n\nEvidence must establish what happened. But the possibility of *administrative\nerror, inadequate examination, lack of proper care, or mistaken inclusion\nof land* cannot sensibly be dismissed until the original registration\nmaterial has been examined.\n\nThat is precisely why I am asking for the surviving file to be reviewed.\n\nI remain willing to provide the title extracts, the 30 July 2026 HM Land\nRegistry letter, the relevant historical documents and my chronology. I\nwould also be grateful for your assistance in obtaining a substantive\nanswer from HM Land Registry rather than a further general statement that\nit cannot reopen *BP Properties Ltd v Buckler*.\n\nI just bought some records from them ref the forest half of our land\nparcel. To begin seeking possession of that. But I don't want to be\nextorted by Land Registry and have yet another local authority gatekeep\ninformation in some ploy to mitigate their legal liability exposure if it\nshows any negligence or incompetence un 1982/83. I was born on this land,\nit seems to be my land. I could just as easily do GDPR and SAR and not just\nFOI's.\n\nI also resent Land Registry’s initial response to my intial enquiry was to\nconvert my enquriy into a complaint, only to shut it down. And say I could\nnever ask the same question again. This is the kind of behaviour that\nsignals guilty conscience and bad faith. It was very hostile. Much like\ntaking the maximum time to respond, is that really neccesary?\n\nI have allocated at least 6 years to gathering evidence and making my case\non this. It only looks damming for local authories when I visualise all\nthis back and forth for months just to get to the information I should have\nbeen presented with in the first instance. I've barely if at all paid for\nevidence or had my lawyer demand/ summon evidence be disclosed yet. I'm\njust finding out who will co-operate at this stage. I'm also perfectly\nwilling, like with CADW and Llandough councillor, to file complaints so\nthat each party has ample opportunity and due procedure to try to satisfy\nour grievances directly. Dwr Cymru already posted me a cheque for ignoring\nmy initial FOI. Albeit minor.\n\nI have travelled the world. My wife and daughter and dog from Swansea live\nin the Caribbean. I'm mainly in Argentina as of 2025 constructing a replica\nGreat House Farm Museum. What happens in this small county and country\ndoesn't concern me much anymore. I think its mostly a lot of massachrist\nsubmissive hostages in concentration work camps under plastic terrorists\nnow. And its a situationship that suits both parties and I don't intend on\ngetting in the way of either.\n\nI am more concerned to get the story of what happened in Llandough correct,\nso it can be accurately retold at the museum in Argentina. And gather\nevidence before any more goes missing or is destroyed. If it transpires the\ncase can be reopened and reporations are due and/or possession of some/ any\nof our ancestral birthland can be returned to us, then all the better.\n\nI'm also sure this what happened to us was an epademic racket/ playbook\nnationwide and if so, we're likely in a land market bubble and so a land\nmarket crash may come. Which would be literal, actionable and logistical\ndecolonisation, like the way occupying UN issued land in Nicosia back to\nGreeks/ Turks. Not just political and figurative decolonisation. Not\nwithstanding civil war brewing on British Isles, which is why having family\nestate records in order matters. I've already lost any confidence self\nproclaimed fudiciaries actually preserve records.\n\nI've got herlooms going back centuries. Which myself and my family have\nsomehow managed to preserve. How CADW and National Archieves and the Vale\nCouncil and even EU and Parliment don't have  records of this case anymore,\nbrings into question the point of their very existence.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\n*Requester *\n**\n\nOn Fri, 18 Sept 2026, 10:13 DOUGHTY, Stephen, <\n[email redacted]> wrote:\n\n> Good morning Requester\n>\n>\n>\n> Following enquiries on your behalf, please see the attached response from\n> Chief Executive and Chief Land Registrar at HM Land Registry.\n>\n> We are sorry to provide what we understand may be a disappointing reply\n> but trust that the information is accurate and goes someway to clarify\n> their position.\n>\n> We are still waiting for a full response from the Ministry of Justice but\n> will of course let you know as soon as we hear back.\n>\n>\n>\n> Kind regards,\n>\n>\n>\n> *Office of Stephen Doughty MP / AS*\n> *Labour and Co-operative Member of Parliament for Cardiff South and\n> Penarth*\n>\n> *Llafur a Cydweithredol Aelod Seneddol dros Dde Caerdydd a Phenarth*\n>\n> *Westminster / San Steffan *| [phone redacted] | House of Commons, London,\n> [postcode redacted]\n>\n> *Email / E-bost                      *| [email redacted]\n>\n> *Website / Gwefan      *          | www.stephendoughty.wales\n>\n>\n>\n> For information about how we use your personal data, see our Privacy\n> Notice <https://www.stephendoughty.wales/privacynotice>.\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> UK Parliament Disclaimer: this e-mail is confidential to the intended\n> recipient. If you have received it in error, please notify the sender and\n> delete it from your system. Any unauthorised use, disclosure, or copying is\n> not permitted. This e-mail has been checked for viruses, but no liability\n> is accepted for any damage caused by any virus transmitted by this e-mail.\n> This e-mail address is not secure, is not encrypted and should not be used\n> for sensitive data.\n>"},{"id":"gm-1a0b55432e14a224","subject":"Re: Escalation — Llandough ward member silence: Great House Farm / Church View Close and removal of commemorative tree","name":"Re: Escalation — Llandough ward member silence: Great House Farm / Church View Close and removal of commemorative tree","date":"2026-09-18T16:23:08Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0b55432e14a224.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a08ba0ae4dec885-escalation-llandough-ward-member-silence-great-house-farm-church-view-close-and-removal-of-commemorative-tree","scopeTopics":["vale-foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["vale-foi"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a08ba0ae4dec885","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"vale-foi","name":"Vale of Glamorgan Council"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0b55432e14a224.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Ms Davidson,\n\nThanks for your reply. Firstly see attached an actual image of my amputee,\nwidow pensioner grandmother Mrs Williams, who's land I believe was stolen.\nResulting in mine and my families inhumane dispossession by BP and local\nauthories here, in 1988. I will save you images of the small army of her\nheirs and successors which have the interest in this land i.e. her 7\nchildren and their many surviving children each. My grandmother died\nasserting her ownership. My father died asserting his ownership.\n\nWe have never surrendered or conveyed or sold our ownership. Ignoring us,\nkilling us, supressing/ concealing even destroying evidence has never\nchanged the fabric of reality or changed our position on this matter. The\nthing with twisting reality to suite, in this case more concentration work\ncamp houses, by stealing an indigenous disabled, widow pensioners family\nland and home of almost 4 centuries, is that reality always snaps back.\nTruth takes longer, but it does always come out. And you can fool some of\nthe people some of the time, but not all of the people all of the time.\n\nThe land registry even explicitly wrote to me, not just the MP today, to me\nthey stated they have never claimed we do not own our land parcel. Nor that\nevidence existed we don't own it. Nor has any court ever ruled we don't.\nPossession fell to BP due to their presentation to the court and lawfare.\nOur family fell for their theatrics as much as local authories. It doesn't\nchange the fact the title chain of the possessors is defective and is based\non a falsehood that our occupancy of our parcel was permissive. When in\nfact our undefeated title chain of our land parcel from 1928, is proceeded\nby Daniel Thomas & Son, before that Marquess of Bute, before that Vaughan\nfamily and the origin is a 1000 year freehold from Knight Sir Herbert of\nSwansea to Vaughan in the 1500's.\n\nBPs interest in our parcel was a 1969 purchase of interest in an unenforced\n(possible undeclaired) possession order, by Western Ground Rents, based on\na 1955 order that, like in 1974, attempted to make our occupancy of our\nland parcel permissive. It said the order was for the \"whole farm\" and only\nexcluded our parcel because of my nans \"condition and resistence\". Having\njust had her leg amputated.\n\nFirstly the whole farm was defined as the parcel they'd possessed to the\neast of ours, as per the 1939 agreement (see attached). It no longer\nincluded our parcel from the 1916 agricultral tenancy, because in 1928 our\n1877 agreement with Daniel Thomas enacted when the last quarrying machine\nwas removed. Our grandad even registered the land in our names in and\naround 1928 too. The 1939 revisal of the 1916 agricultural tenancy\nexplicitly didn't include our land parcel. So the whole farm was not the\nsame definition as the 1916 agricutural tenancy which was conveyed to\nWestern Ground Rents in 1938. Secondly my nan wasn't just resisting moving\ndue to having her leg amputated. She was asserting ownership. She also\nrefused a previous attempt to make her occupancy permissive in 1949, by\nrejecting a WGR attempt to get her into a tenancy. Which she explicitly\nrejected due to asserting ownership.\n\nSo you see my disabled, widow, pensioner grandmother, even until her\nconveniently timed death in 1983 , in the two week window she will have had\nto challenge BP registering our land in their names at the land registry\n(which she was never even made aware of, nor did BP disclose to land\nregistry her ownership claim), even unto her dying breath she challenged\npermissive occupancy and asserted ownership.\n\nThe land registry wrote back to the MP actually and is still trying to worm\ntheir way out of this. Them not admitting a mistake is even an audacity as\nit was clearly more than a mistake at the land registry in my estimation.\nThey will have had to look at the 1974 court proceedings to see BP seeking\npossession, yet ownership wasn't determined in case 1 of 1974, it was\najourned. And the quashing of the posssession order in case 2 in 1974 is\nalso dubious, as the case was quashed the same day my nan was notified of\nBPs offer. So she wasn't actually given ample time to respond. Furthermore\nthe license letters were to a mrs buckler, my nan was a Mrs Williams. The\nland registry admitted to me in writing they will have used my nans 1935\nmarriage certificate to officiate BPs 1974 license letters to a Mrs\nBuckler. Furthemore the land registry cite the 1983 decision they made was\nfine as the 1987 court paperwork says so. Not that they could have known\nits outcome in 1983. And the 1987 case is based on the 1983 land registry.\nAnd they both depended on BPs attempt to make our occupancy of our 800 year\nhome, and family home since 1667 - permissive, when we owned it since 1928.\nFurthmore, having never paid anything on our land since 1928, we will have\nqualified for adverse possession by 1940 in any case. The interruption of\nour adverse possession which is alleged to have begun in 1955, and was\ninterrupted in 1962 and 1974, will have only been for the land parcel to\nthe east of ours. Not the land parcel we were on.\n\nThis is the problem with theatre, fraud, concentalment, government\ninvolvement (force), corporate manslaughter, suppression, lawfare etc. It\nis all short term gains. But without legitimacy, local authorities lack\nactual authority. The authority must derive from legitimacy. What I am\nalluding to may be rough edged and not meticulous, but its not before a\ncourt yet. Also what I alledge isn't entirely contrary to the official\nnarrative. Both can be true simultaneously is the wild part of all this.\n\nThe concern I raise, is more for local authories sakes, because a lot of\nactions were taken by the council, CADW, Police, which were based on fraud\nand BPs defective title. My mum was physically thrown over our garden gate\nto get her off the property, while pregnant,  dislocating her collar bone\nin that process too. All she was doing was trying to get medicine from our\noccupied/ stolen home (since the 16th century), for my terminally ill dad\nwho was in hospital. The press all labelled us \"squatters being evicted\".\n\nI am horrified at the local authories here who insist on my family of\nmostly women being a hostage prisoner of war class under terrorists.\nThey're all afraid to even speak out or challenge the narrative as anyone\nin our family who has conveniently died. Myself, as a decorated war veteran\nwarfare peofessional with tens of soldiers I've served with, do not\nnegotatiate with terrorists as it is. We don't stand for this terroist/\nhostage situationship. Much like us indigenous Briton welsh nationals on\nour ancestral birthland, accept our towns and cities being turned into\nethnic cleansing concentration work camps.\n\nMarconi's stay with us is covered up. Even unto this day. Along with the\nRoman Villa, Monestry, Peerless Jims first fight in our barns. Even the\n2,200 year old round house isn't mentioned. Why? Llandough has been turned\ninto a concentration work camp. The town website history page doesn't even\nmention the half of this. Also all the campaigning to petition the housing\ndevelopment (on our land) in 1974 and affirm our home was a county\ntreasure, was suppressed. When BP demolished our home in 1988 and went back\nto the Vale council for planning approval. The council never held BP to\naccount for just demolishing our country treasure home. And even suppressed\nor down played any record it was ever a county treasure property. Or site\nof historic and archelogical signifcants.\n\nI am not just writing about my disappointment in the councillor. I know\nhe's likely already been scolded by the aforementioned terrorists that\noutrank him, in the aftermath of rejecting being replaced and ethnically\ncleansed which resulted in him being ejected from a council meeting for\nchallenging Cardiff being a city of Sanctuary.  My motto is different in\nthat I say \"Bring Guns\" and am fully aware everything comes down to guns\nand fire fights. So I will always need an actual gun to be fired at me or\nheld to my head to make me even consider standing down. And even then the\nlast 3 times firearms have been drawn on me I ended up disarming the\naggressor. So I'm perhaps a different breed to the councillor or civilians\nalike.\n\nBut I am bona fide. I left my military career with full honours.\n\nIn any case, separately from the Councillor Carroll complaint which you\nclosed today. This complaint concerns two connected matters: the custody\nand handling of Llandough Community Council records concerning Great House\nFarm / Tŷ Mawr, Llandough, and the completeness and treatment of Vale of\nGlamorgan Council's planning and heritage records relating to application\n89/01396/OUT and the subsequent reserved-matters application.\n\nI appreciate that you do not employ the former Clerk, Mr Paul R. Egan. I am\nnevertheless raising the matter with you as Monitoring Officer because the\nunderlying planning file is a Vale file, the Community Council's records\nform part of the local documentary record, and I need a clear written\naccount of who now has responsibility for those records and what has\nhappened to them.\n\nEveryone's also got a boss. Mine being God and my 56 British Soldier\nshareholders in my US based company private security group. So feel free to\npass this up your ranks to your overlords if you can't or won't make sense\nof it or take any action.\n\nI also want to be precise about what I am, and am not, alleging. I do not\nhave evidence that Mr Egan removed, altered or destroyed any document. Nor\ncan I establish from the present evidence why he retired when he did. The\nfact that a long-serving Clerk retired during the period in which I was\nseeking these records is, however, a material chronology, particularly\nbecause he had initially adopted a position of not engaging beyond a\nstatutory search. I therefore ask the Council to establish the facts rather\nthan assume either that the matter is innocuous or that any wrongdoing\noccurred.\n\n   1. Llandough Community Council records\n\nIn May and June 2026 I asked Mr Egan for Community Council records\nconcerning Great House Farm, including material concerning the events\nsurrounding my late father's involvement with the property in 1988.\n\nOn 14 June 2026 Mr Egan stated that minute books from 1982 onwards had been\ndeposited with Glamorgan Archives in 2002 and relied on section 21 FOIA. He\nsubsequently retired. On the day of his retirement he directed me towards\nLeckwith records and the 1939 Western Ground Rents tenancy with my\ngreat-grandfather.\n\nThat tenancy is significant because, on the documents I have examined, it\nappears to concern the eastern field rather than the House Plot containing\nthe farmhouse and associated buildings. I therefore do not regard that\nreference as resolving the documentary position concerning Great House Farm\nitself.\n\nI need the Council to establish:\n\n   - who is now Clerk and Proper Officer of Llandough Community Council;\n   - who currently has custody of its historic minute books, correspondence\n   and other records;\n   - what records were handed over or inventoried when Mr Egan retired;\n   - whether any search has been made specifically for Great House Farm /\n   Tŷ Mawr records, rather than relying solely on the general statement that\n   minute books were deposited in 2002; and\n   - whether any Community Council correspondence, consultation response or\n   minute relating to 89/01396/OUT or the later reserved matters survives.\n\nIf any part of this falls solely within the responsibility of Llandough\nCommunity Council or the Public Services Ombudsman for Wales, please\nidentify the appropriate authority and the person to whom I should address\nthat part of the complaint.\n\n   2. What the existing evidence establishes\n\nThis section turns from Community Council custody to the Vale's own\nplanning and archaeological file for 89/01396/OUT. The points below are\nbased on documents already in my possession and published archaeological\nmaterial, and are separate from the matters in Section 1, where I am asking\nthe Council to search its records.\n\n   1.\n\n   Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr is recorded in the historic environment\n   record as County Treasure, reference SMR/HER 2038s / GGAT02038s, at\n   approximately ST 1681 7331. The 1990 GGAT assessment records that, because\n   of an ownership dispute and the subsequent sudden destruction of the\n   property in 1988, the building was incompletely understood.\n   2.\n\n   Outline planning application 89/01396/OUT, submitted by BP Properties\n   Ltd, followed the 1988 demolition by approximately a year. The application\n   answers the relevant questions as follows: buildings to be demolished —\n   \"None\"; site — \"Disused\"; previous use — \"Not known\".\n   3.\n\n   The Vale officer material in my possession refers to an outline-stage\n   indicative scheme of 18 dwellings. The subsequent reserved-matters scheme\n   approved on 3 September 1992 is for 20 dwellings.\n   4.\n\n   The 1990 evaluation recorded archaeological potential to the north of\n   the churchyard and identified a medium-to-high potential area. What it does\n   not establish by itself is the full extent of the archaeological remains\n   subsequently encountered.\n   5.\n\n   Cotswold Archaeology excavated at the same general location in 1994,\n   immediately adjacent to St Dochdwy's Church. The published archaeological\n   account records 1,026 burials from an excavated area of 0.22 hectares. I am\n   relying on the excavators' published figure rather than the lower secondary\n   figure of 858, which appears to conflate different phases of investigation.\n   6.\n\n   PPG16 was issued in November 1990 and therefore post-dates the March\n   1990 outline decision, but was applicable by the time of the September 1992\n   reserved-matters decision. I am not presently asserting what archaeological\n   conditions were imposed in 1992. The drawings I hold are stamped as\n   approved on 3 September 1992, subject to compliance with conditions if any,\n   but I do not hold the decision notice setting out the actual conditions.\n   That decision notice is therefore a critical missing document.\n   7.\n\n   I am also not treating the handwritten numbering on the surviving\n   consultation material as conclusively establishing which sheet relates to\n   which responding body. The underlying consultation responses remain\n   relevant and should be located irrespective of that numbering issue.\n   8.\n\n   The documentary gap that now needs explaining\n\nThe sequence is straightforward.\n\nThe historic farmhouse and associated buildings were demolished in December\n1988.\n\nThe subsequent 1989 outline application described the site as \"Disused\",\nstated that there were \"None\" buildings to be demolished, and gave the\nprevious use as \"Not known\".\n\nThe 1990 archaeological evaluation had already identified significant\narchaeological potential near the churchyard.\n\nThe 1992 reserved-matters approval provided for 20 dwellings.\n\nThe 1994 excavation subsequently recorded 1,026 burials from 0.22 hectares\nimmediately adjacent to the church.\n\nWhat I cannot establish from the records presently available to me is how\nthe archaeological excavation area relates spatially to the approved\nhousing layout and retained areas. In particular, I need the 1994\nexcavation plan and the corresponding Ideal Homes layout so that the two\ncan be compared accurately.\n\nI also need to understand how the 1989 application descriptions came to be\naccepted against the documentary and historic-environment information\nalready available concerning the demolished farmhouse.\n\nI am not asking the Council to adopt my interpretation of these matters. I\nam asking it to establish what was known, what was checked, what was\nrecorded, and what records still exist.\n\n   4. Specific information and records requested\n\nPlease provide a substantive written response to each of the following:\n\n   1.\n\n   Who is currently Clerk and Proper Officer of Llandough Community\n   Council, and who has custody of its historic records?\n   2.\n\n   Was Llandough Community Council consulted on application 89/01396/OUT\n   and/or the subsequent reserved-matters application? If so, please provide\n   the consultation requests, replies, minutes and associated correspondence,\n   or confirm specifically that no such material is held.\n   3.\n\n   Please provide the full planning decision notice dated 3 September 1992,\n   including all conditions, rather than the approved drawings alone.\n   4.\n\n   Please confirm whether Vale holds the 1994 Cotswold Archaeology\n   site/excavation plans and the relevant Ideal Homes development layout\n   plans. Please also confirm whether the Council has ever undertaken or\n   commissioned a plan-to-plan comparison showing the excavation area against\n   the approved development footprint.\n   5.\n\n   Please identify the officer or officers responsible for assessing the\n   1989 application against the information then available concerning the 1988\n   demolition and HER/SMR record 2038s, including the entries \"None\",\n   \"Disused\" and \"Not known\".\n   6.\n\n   Please confirm what planning, enforcement, conservation/heritage,\n   archaeological, consultation, photographic and correspondence records\n   remain for the site and where they are presently held.\n   7.\n\n   Given the issues raised by my FOI/EIR correspondence and the corporate\n   complaint of 17 September 2026, please ensure that surviving records\n   concerning Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr, including Community Council records\n   where they remain identifiable, are preserved pending resolution of these\n   matters. I am particularly concerned that records should not disappear\n   simply because responsibility has passed from one office-holder to another.\n   8.\n\n   Please confirm whether there was a formal handover, inventory or records\n   transfer when Mr Egan retired, and whether any such documentation records\n   the location of material concerning Great House Farm, planning\n   consultations, or the 1988 demolition.\n   9.\n\n   Finally, if the Council considers that any aspect concerning Mr Egan's\n   conduct as Community Council Clerk falls outside the Vale's jurisdiction,\n   please identify the proper route and responsible body rather than simply\n   closing that part of the complaint.\n\nI am deliberately separating evidence from inference. I am not alleging\nthat Mr Egan's retirement was caused by my FOI requests, nor am I alleging\nthat any individual deliberately concealed records. The timing, however, is\nsufficiently relevant that the Council should establish what happened to\nthe records at the point responsibility changed. The immediate issue is\ncapable of being answered objectively: what records exist, what was known\nto the authority, what checks were carried out, what decisions were made,\nand where the missing documents are now.\n\nPlease treat this as a formal request for a written governance/records\nresponse and address each numbered question individually. It should be read\nalongside FOI/EIR reference 00210772 and my correspondence of 17–18\nSeptember 2026.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester \n\n------------------------------\n\nChanges made: added the Re: line, opened Section 2 with a sentence flagging\nthe shift from Community Council custody to the Vale's own file, and merged\nthe two closing paragraphs to drop the repeated point. Everything else —\nincluding all the evidence/inference hedging — is untouched. Want it as a\nWord doc or PDF instead, or any further tweaks?\n\n\n\nOn Fri, 18 Sept 2026, 15:01 Davidson, Victoria, <\n[email redacted]> wrote:\n\n> Dear Requester\n>\n> Thank you for your recent correspondence which I am responding to on\n> behalf of the Leader, concerning Great House Farm/Church View Close, the\n> removal of a commemorative tree and your communications with Councillor\n> Carroll.\n>\n> I have considered the matters you have raised.\n>\n> As you are aware, I have previously responded to your concerns regarding\n> Councillor Carroll's lack of response to your correspondence. Having\n> reviewed those concerns, I concluded that the matter did not fall within\n> the Council's Corporate Complaints Policy and I did not identify any basis\n> for further action under the Members' Code of Conduct process. The Council\n> has no mechanism by which it can compel an elected member to respond to\n> correspondence from a constituent and, accordingly, the Council's\n> consideration of that aspect of the matter is concluded.\n>\n> Please note that the Leader is not responsible for the conduct of Members\n> other than those with her own political group\n>\n> I note that your recent correspondence extends beyond the issue of\n> Councillor Carroll's responsiveness and raises a broader range of concerns\n> relating to historical land ownership, planning and heritage matters\n> associated with Great House Farm/Church View Close, together with a request\n> for information regarding the removal of a commemorative tree.\n>\n> In relation to the tree, I am making enquiries to establish whether the\n> Council holds any records which may assist in answering your question\n> regarding its removal. This is being dealt with by the Information\n> Governance Team, I have copied in the FOI unit email domain. If relevant\n> information is held by the Council, I will advise you accordingly. Equally,\n> if no such records are held, I will confirm that position.\n>\n> In respect of the wider matters raised in your correspondence, it is\n> important that I clarify the scope of the Council's role. The Council is\n> not able to determine disputed ownership of land, revisit historical legal\n> proceedings, adjudicate upon competing title claims, or investigate\n> allegations of fraud or other criminal conduct. Those matters fall within\n> the jurisdiction of the courts, HM Land Registry, the police and other\n> external bodies as appropriate.\n>\n> Whilst I appreciate the strength of feeling reflected in your\n> correspondence and recognise the significance that these matters have for\n> you and your family, the Council must limit its involvement to those issues\n> which fall within its statutory functions and responsibilities. Any\n> response provided by the Council will therefore be confined to matters upon\n> which it may properly comment and any information which it may hold.\n>\n> The FOI team will write to you further once enquiries regarding the tree\n> have been completed.\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> Victoria Davidson\n>\n> Monitoring Officer/Head of Legal and Democratic Services / Swyddog\n> Monitro / Pennaeth Gwasanaethau Cyfreithiol a Democrataidd\n>\n> Director's Office - Resources / Swyddfa’r Cyfarwyddwr - Adnoddau\n>\n> Vale of Glamorgan Council / Cyngor Bro Morgannwg\n>\n> tel / ffôn: [phone redacted]\n>\n> mob / sym: [phone redacted]\n>\n> e-mail / e-bost: [email redacted]\n>\n>\n>\n> *Consider the environment. Please don't print this e-mail unless you\n> really need to.*\n>\n> *Ystyriwch yr amgylchedd. Peidiwch ag argraffu'r neges hon oni bai fod\n> gwir angen.*\n>\n>\n>\n> Visit our Website at www.valeofglamorgan.gov.uk\n>\n> Ewch i'n gwefan yn www.bromorgannwg.gov.uk\n>\n>\n>\n> Find us on Facebook / Cewch ddod o hyd i ni ar Facebook\n> <https://www.facebook.com/valeofglamorgancouncil>\n>\n> Follow us on Twitter / Dilynwch ni ar Twitter\n> <http://twitter.com/vogcouncil>\n>\n>\n>\n> Correspondence is welcomed in Welsh or English / Croesewir Gohebiaeth yn y\n> Gymraeg neu yn Saesneg.\n>\n>\n>\n> *From:* Requester <[email redacted]>\n> *Sent:* 10 September 2026 15:23\n> *To:* Burnett, Lis (Cllr) <[email redacted]>; Leader <\n> [email redacted]>\n> *Cc:* Carroll, George D (Cllr) <[email redacted]>;\n> Democratic <[email redacted]>; Davidson, Victoria <\n> [email redacted]>\n> *Subject:* Escalation — Llandough ward member silence: Great House Farm /\n> Church View Close and removal of commemorative tree\n>\n>\n>\n> Councillor Burnett,\n>\n> I write to you as Leader of the Vale of Glamorgan Council because the\n> elected member for Llandough, Councillor George D. D. Carroll, has not\n> answered a sequence of written enquiries spanning more than six months. I\n> am going above him for that reason alone. I am not asking you to adjudicate\n> the historic title dispute. I am asking that a constituent with a\n> documented family interest in the ward receive a reply that the ward member\n> has declined to give.\n>\n> I understand that elected members do not have a line manager in the\n> employment sense. Councillor Carroll is Leader of the Conservative Group.\n> You are the political head of the authority. After the ward member and the\n> Monitoring Officer, you are the proper next addressee.\n>\n> Why I am writing to you rather than waiting further\n>\n> 1. Councillor Carroll has not sent a single substantive reply to me at\n> [email redacted].\n> 2. On 27 July 2026 I filed a formal complaint about that silence (copied\n> to him, Democratic Services and the Monitoring Officer).\n> 3. On 12 August 2026 Victoria Davidson, Monitoring Officer, wrote that the\n> matter fell outside the Corporate Concerns and Complaints Policy and that\n> she had not identified a Code of Conduct breach. That determination did not\n> require him to respond. It left the original questions unanswered.\n> 4. I followed up with the Monitoring Officer on 12 August, 21 August and\n> 24 August 2026. The position did not change.\n> 5. I copied Councillor Carroll again on 28 August 2026 (joint letter with\n> Stephen Doughty MP). Still no reply from him.\n>\n> The questions he has not answered include a simple, current, ward-level\n> one: who removed the tree my great-grandfather planted to mark the family’s\n> claim, as recorded in the Collection of Wales / archival image. That is a\n> local enquiry a ward member can signpost to Parks, Highways, Planning or\n> the landowner. It is not a request that he retry BP Properties Ltd v\n> Buckler.\n>\n> From his public output, Councillor Carroll appears to concentrate on\n> blocked drains, potholes and similar highway defects. Those matters matter.\n> The correspondence I sent him is of a different character: heritage,\n> planning and enforcement records, the 1988 demolition of Great House Farm\n> (Ty Mawr), now Church View Close, and the removal of a commemorative tree.\n> I accept that may be outside the type of casework he usually handles. That\n> is why a short, honest reply — even if only to say the questions sit with\n> named officers, and to pass the tree query to the correct department —\n> would have been sufficient. Silence is not.\n>\n> Recorded approaches to Councillor Carroll (\n> [email redacted])\n>\n> All sent. No substantive reply on any of them.\n>\n> - 16 February 2026 — “Title Fraud and Procedural Concealment – Church View\n> Close, Llandough (formerly Great House Farm / Ty Mawr)”. Planning and\n> record-keeping gaps; 1988 demolition.\n> - 4 May 2026 — follow-up on BP v Buckler 1987; ITV footage; request for\n> attention as Llandough councillor.\n> - 3 May 2026 — joined the public “George Carroll for Llandough” Facebook\n> group (approved). Not a substitute for a council-email reply.\n> - 9 May 2026 — a wider formal notice also addressed to\n> [email redacted]. That address bounced. Subsequent mail\n> used the correct gdcarroll@ address.\n> - 7 June 2026 — “Church View Close, Llandough”. 1988 events from public\n> records, family testimony and media.\n> - 25 June 2026 — “Great House Farm, Llandough”. Asked whether he had\n> reviewed the earlier concerns and whether any discussion was possible.\n> - 13 July 2026 — “Great House Farm / Church View Close”. Full timeline\n> restated. Ten-working-day request. Explicit question: who removed the tree\n> my great-grandfather planted to commemorate the ownership claim, as shown\n> in the Collection of Wales archive image.\n> - 27 July 2026 — formal complaint to [email redacted],\n> copied to Councillor Carroll, Democratic Services and the Monitoring\n> Officer.\n> - 28 August 2026 — further letter to Councillor Carroll and Stephen\n> Doughty MP on the same site.\n>\n> What I am asking you to do\n>\n> 1. Note that the ward member has not answered, and that the complaint\n> process produced no requirement that he do so.\n>\n>\n> 2. Ask Councillor Carroll, or the relevant Director, to provide a short\n> written reply on the tree: which department or landowner authorised or\n> carried out its removal, on what date, and under what power or works order\n> — or to confirm that the Council holds no such record.\n>\n>\n> 3. Identify the correct officer contact for heritage, trees and historic\n> planning records at Church View Close / former Great House Farm, so I am\n> not left circling a silent ward inbox.\n>\n>\n> 4. Confirm whether the Council will treat a documented family connection\n> to a demolished historic farmstead in the ward as a legitimate basis for a\n> reply, even where the correspondent is not presently resident in Llandough.\n>\n>\n>\n> The thesis of my concern is that the lawfare/ warfare that systematically\n> attempted to make our occupancy permissive as early as the 40's (with my\n> grandmothers statement of title being stolen, a failed attempt to get us\n> into a tenancy afterwards, and ambiguity in a 1955 order for another parcel\n> we leased and defaulted on, which later meant it was able to be\n> misinterpreted to make it appear our parcel of land (now Church View Close)\n> was part of theirs, like it was in 1916 - 1928) is a racket. The ownership\n> dispute was a problem created. The reaction followed and the solution was\n> to focus on possession. And so in 1987 BP were awarded possession with what\n> was a defective title chain.\n>\n> I do not expect you to reopen 1987 litigation. I do expect the authority,\n> once the ward member has been given months and a formal complaint, to\n> answer a factual question about a tree on land in that ward. Because that\n> tree was part of a long list of evidence supporting our ownership claim.\n>\n> I can supply the original emails and the 27 July complaint in full if\n> Democratic Services does not already have them. See attached the\n> aforementioned tree planting ceremy image. I also enclose a draft chancery\n> court allocation so you can understand the legal arguments.\n>\n>\n>\n> Due to a lot of suspicious and conveniently timed deaths in our family,\n> surrounding this case, like my grandmother dying in the 2 weeks she will\n> have had to challenge BP quietly registering our land parcel in their names\n> in 1983, without declaring our ownership claim, which was never\n> exstinguished as the first 1974 court case was ajourned. Or my fathers\n> death after his appeal of the 1987 ruling was dismissed on grounds of\n> \"heads BP win, tails we lose\". Followed by ECHR rejection citing BP own the\n> land, despite BP only seeking possession and never seeking or gaining\n> ownership.\n>\n>\n>\n> I have a trove of evidence and I am collecting more and investigating and\n> in talks with CPS and Fraud Police and MoJ and Land Registry. The initial\n> thing I would like from you is the record of the removal of the tree we\n> planted. The picture attached depicts its location. I'd like to know who\n> removed it and when it was removed?\n>\n>\n> Regards,\n>\n> Requester \n>\n> \n>\n> [address redacted], [postcode redacted]\n> [map link redacted]\n>\n>\n>"},{"id":"gm-1a0b534a66786f28","subject":"FOI/EIR request - Case Ref/ 00211484","name":"FOI/EIR request - Case Ref/ 00211484","date":"2026-09-18T15:48:34Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0b534a66786f28.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0b534a66786f28-foi-eir-request-case-ref-00211484","scopeTopics":["vale-foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["vale-foi"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0b534a66786f28","direction":"received","from":"(name not shown; valeofglamorgan.gov.uk)","institutions":[{"id":"vale-foi","name":"Vale of Glamorgan Council"}],"refs":["00211484"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0b534a66786f28.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester\n\nFREEDOM OF INFORMATION ACT 2000/ENVIRONMENTAL INFORMATION REGULATIONS 2004 - INFORMATION REQUEST\n\nThank you for your request for information about: Removal of a commemorative tree\n\nYour request was received on 18/09/2026 and is being considered under the terms of the Freedom of Information Act 2000 and the Environmental Information Regulations 2004. Your query has been given the reference 00211484.\n\nIn some circumstances a fee may be payable and if that is the case we will let you know. A fees notice will be issued to you, and you will be required to pay before we will proceed to deal with your request.\n\n\n\n\nYou should receive the information requested within 20 working days unless the Council does not hold the information or there is a reason for it to be withheld. We will write to you in any event.\n\nIf you have any requirements regarding the format any information should be supplied in, e.g. the language to be used, audio, large print and so on, then if you have not already done so please let us know. \n\nIf you have any queries or concerns then please do not hesitate to contact us. Please remember to quote the reference number above in any future communications.\n\n\n\nFurther information about your rights is also available from the Information\nCommissioner at:\n\n\nInformation Commissioner's Office - Wales\n\nSecond Floor, Churchill House\n\nChurchill Way,\n\nCardiff\n\n[postcode redacted]\n\n[phone redacted] \n\n[email redacted] \n\n\n\nYours sincerely,\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nInformation Governance Officer/ Swyddog Llywodraethu Gwybodaeth\n\nDirector's Office - Resources / Swyddfa'r Cyfarwyddwr - Adnoddau\n\nVale of Glamorgan Council / Cyngor Bro Morgannwg\n\ntel / ffôn: [phone redacted]\n\ne-mail / e-bost: [email redacted] or [email redacted]\n\n \n\nConsider the environment. Please don't print this e-mail unless you really need to.\n\nYstyriwch yr amgylchedd. Peidiwch ag argraffu'r neges hon oni bai fod gwir angen.\n\n \n\nVisit our Website at www.valeofglamorgan.gov.uk\n\nEwch i'n gwefan yn www.bromorgannwg.gov.uk\n\n \n\nFind us on Facebook / Cewch ddod o hyd i ni ar Facebook\n\nFollow us on Twitter /Dilynwch ni ar Twitter\n\n \n\nCorrespondence is welcomed in Welsh or English / Croesewir Gohebiaeth yn y Gymraeg neu yn Saesneg."},{"id":"gm-1a0b52e23d30651d","subject":"FOI/EIR request - Case Ref/ 00211483","name":"FOI/EIR request - Case Ref/ 00211483","date":"2026-09-18T15:41:31Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0b52e23d30651d.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0b52e23d30651d-foi-eir-request-case-ref-00211483","scopeTopics":["vale-foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["vale-foi"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0b52e23d30651d","direction":"received","from":"(name not shown; valeofglamorgan.gov.uk)","institutions":[{"id":"vale-foi","name":"Vale of Glamorgan Council"}],"refs":["00211483"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0b52e23d30651d.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester\n\nFREEDOM OF INFORMATION ACT 2000/ENVIRONMENTAL INFORMATION REGULATIONS 2004 - INFORMATION REQUEST\n\nThank you for your request for information about: Great House Farm, Llandough (89/01396/OUT)\n\nYour request was received on 18/09/2026 and is being considered under the terms of the Freedom of Information Act 2000 and the Environmental Information Regulations 2004. Your query has been given the reference 00211483.\n\nIn some circumstances a fee may be payable and if that is the case we will let you know. A fees notice will be issued to you, and you will be required to pay before we will proceed to deal with your request.\n\n\n\n\nYou should receive the information requested within 20 working days unless the Council does not hold the information or there is a reason for it to be withheld. We will write to you in any event.\n\nIf you have any requirements regarding the format any information should be supplied in, e.g. the language to be used, audio, large print and so on, then if you have not already done so please let us know. \n\nIf you have any queries or concerns then please do not hesitate to contact us. Please remember to quote the reference number above in any future communications.\n\n\n\nFurther information about your rights is also available from the Information\nCommissioner at:\n\n\nInformation Commissioner's Office - Wales\n\nSecond Floor, Churchill House\n\nChurchill Way,\n\nCardiff\n\n[postcode redacted]\n\n[phone redacted] \n\n[email redacted] \n\n\n\nYours sincerely,\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nInformation Governance Officer/ Swyddog Llywodraethu Gwybodaeth\n\nDirector's Office - Resources / Swyddfa'r Cyfarwyddwr - Adnoddau\n\nVale of Glamorgan Council / Cyngor Bro Morgannwg\n\ntel / ffôn: [phone redacted]\n\ne-mail / e-bost: [email redacted] or [email redacted]\n\n \n\nConsider the environment. Please don't print this e-mail unless you really need to.\n\nYstyriwch yr amgylchedd. Peidiwch ag argraffu'r neges hon oni bai fod gwir angen.\n\n \n\nVisit our Website at www.valeofglamorgan.gov.uk\n\nEwch i'n gwefan yn www.bromorgannwg.gov.uk\n\n \n\nFind us on Facebook / Cewch ddod o hyd i ni ar Facebook\n\nFollow us on Twitter /Dilynwch ni ar Twitter\n\n \n\nCorrespondence is welcomed in Welsh or English / Croesewir Gohebiaeth yn y Gymraeg neu yn Saesneg."},{"id":"gm-1a0b4d2ccd7bfc79","subject":"RE: Escalation — Llandough ward member silence: Great House Farm / Church View Close and removal of commemorative tree","name":"RE: Escalation — Llandough ward member silence: Great House Farm / Church View Close and removal of commemorative tree","date":"2026-09-18T14:01:42Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0b4d2ccd7bfc79.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a08ba0ae4dec885-escalation-llandough-ward-member-silence-great-house-farm-church-view-close-and-removal-of-commemorative-tree","scopeTopics":["vale-foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["vale-foi"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a08ba0ae4dec885","direction":"received","from":"Davidson, Victoria","institutions":[{"id":"vale-foi","name":"Vale of Glamorgan Council"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0b4d2ccd7bfc79.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester\nThank you for your recent correspondence which I am responding to on behalf of the Leader, concerning Great House Farm/Church View Close, the removal of a commemorative tree and your communications with Councillor Carroll.\nI have considered the matters you have raised.\nAs you are aware, I have previously responded to your concerns regarding Councillor Carroll's lack of response to your correspondence. Having reviewed those concerns, I concluded that the matter did not fall within the Council's Corporate Complaints Policy and I did not identify any basis for further action under the Members' Code of Conduct process. The Council has no mechanism by which it can compel an elected member to respond to correspondence from a constituent and, accordingly, the Council's consideration of that aspect of the matter is concluded.\nPlease note that the Leader is not responsible for the conduct of Members other than those with her own political group\nI note that your recent correspondence extends beyond the issue of Councillor Carroll's responsiveness and raises a broader range of concerns relating to historical land ownership, planning and heritage matters associated with Great House Farm/Church View Close, together with a request for information regarding the removal of a commemorative tree.\nIn relation to the tree, I am making enquiries to establish whether the Council holds any records which may assist in answering your question regarding its removal. This is being dealt with by the Information Governance Team, I have copied in the FOI unit email domain. If relevant information is held by the Council, I will advise you accordingly. Equally, if no such records are held, I will confirm that position.\nIn respect of the wider matters raised in your correspondence, it is important that I clarify the scope of the Council's role. The Council is not able to determine disputed ownership of land, revisit historical legal proceedings, adjudicate upon competing title claims, or investigate allegations of fraud or other criminal conduct. Those matters fall within the jurisdiction of the courts, HM Land Registry, the police and other external bodies as appropriate.\nWhilst I appreciate the strength of feeling reflected in your correspondence and recognise the significance that these matters have for you and your family, the Council must limit its involvement to those issues which fall within its statutory functions and responsibilities. Any response provided by the Council will therefore be confined to matters upon which it may properly comment and any information which it may hold.\nThe FOI team will write to you further once enquiries regarding the tree have been completed.\nYours sincerely,\n\n\nVictoria Davidson\nMonitoring Officer/Head of Legal and Democratic Services / Swyddog Monitro / Pennaeth Gwasanaethau Cyfreithiol a Democrataidd\nDirector's Office - Resources / Swyddfa’r Cyfarwyddwr - Adnoddau\nVale of Glamorgan Council / Cyngor Bro Morgannwg\ntel / ffôn: [phone redacted]\nmob / sym: [phone redacted]\ne-mail / e-bost: [email redacted]<[email redacted]>\n\nConsider the environment. Please don't print this e-mail unless you really need to.\nYstyriwch yr amgylchedd. Peidiwch ag argraffu'r neges hon oni bai fod gwir angen.\n\nVisit our Website at www.valeofglamorgan.gov.uk<http://www.valeofglamorgan.gov.uk/>\nEwch i'n gwefan yn www.bromorgannwg.gov.uk<http://www.bromorgannwg.gov.uk/>\n\nFind us on Facebook / Cewch ddod o hyd i ni ar Facebook<https://www.facebook.com/valeofglamorgancouncil>\nFollow us on Twitter / Dilynwch ni ar Twitter<http://twitter.com/vogcouncil>\n\nCorrespondence is welcomed in Welsh or English / Croesewir Gohebiaeth yn y Gymraeg neu yn Saesneg.\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]>\nSent: 10 September 2026 15:23\nTo: Burnett, Lis (Cllr) <[email redacted]>; Leader <[email redacted]>\nCc: Carroll, George D (Cllr) <[email redacted]>; Democratic <[email redacted]>; Davidson, Victoria <[email redacted]>\nSubject: Escalation — Llandough ward member silence: Great House Farm / Church View Close and removal of commemorative tree\n\nCouncillor Burnett,\n\nI write to you as Leader of the Vale of Glamorgan Council because the elected member for Llandough, Councillor George D. D. Carroll, has not answered a sequence of written enquiries spanning more than six months. I am going above him for that reason alone. I am not asking you to adjudicate the historic title dispute. I am asking that a constituent with a documented family interest in the ward receive a reply that the ward member has declined to give.\n\nI understand that elected members do not have a line manager in the employment sense. Councillor Carroll is Leader of the Conservative Group. You are the political head of the authority. After the ward member and the Monitoring Officer, you are the proper next addressee.\n\nWhy I am writing to you rather than waiting further\n\n1. Councillor Carroll has not sent a single substantive reply to me at [email redacted]<[email redacted]>.\n2. On 27 July 2026 I filed a formal complaint about that silence (copied to him, Democratic Services and the Monitoring Officer).\n3. On 12 August 2026 Victoria Davidson, Monitoring Officer, wrote that the matter fell outside the Corporate Concerns and Complaints Policy and that she had not identified a Code of Conduct breach. That determination did not require him to respond. It left the original questions unanswered.\n4. I followed up with the Monitoring Officer on 12 August, 21 August and 24 August 2026. The position did not change.\n5. I copied Councillor Carroll again on 28 August 2026 (joint letter with Stephen Doughty MP). Still no reply from him.\n\nThe questions he has not answered include a simple, current, ward-level one: who removed the tree my great-grandfather planted to mark the family’s claim, as recorded in the Collection of Wales / archival image. That is a local enquiry a ward member can signpost to Parks, Highways, Planning or the landowner. It is not a request that he retry BP Properties Ltd v Buckler.\n\nFrom his public output, Councillor Carroll appears to concentrate on blocked drains, potholes and similar highway defects. Those matters matter. The correspondence I sent him is of a different character: heritage, planning and enforcement records, the 1988 demolition of Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), now Church View Close, and the removal of a commemorative tree. I accept that may be outside the type of casework he usually handles. That is why a short, honest reply — even if only to say the questions sit with named officers, and to pass the tree query to the correct department — would have been sufficient. Silence is not.\n\nRecorded approaches to Councillor Carroll ([email redacted]<[email redacted]>)\n\nAll sent. No substantive reply on any of them.\n\n- 16 February 2026 — “Title Fraud and Procedural Concealment – Church View Close, Llandough (formerly Great House Farm / Ty Mawr)”. Planning and record-keeping gaps; 1988 demolition.\n- 4 May 2026 — follow-up on BP v Buckler 1987; ITV footage; request for attention as Llandough councillor.\n- 3 May 2026 — joined the public “George Carroll for Llandough” Facebook group (approved). Not a substitute for a council-email reply.\n- 9 May 2026 — a wider formal notice also addressed to [email redacted]<[email redacted]>. That address bounced. Subsequent mail used the correct gdcarroll@ address.\n- 7 June 2026 — “Church View Close, Llandough”. 1988 events from public records, family testimony and media.\n- 25 June 2026 — “Great House Farm, Llandough”. Asked whether he had reviewed the earlier concerns and whether any discussion was possible.\n- 13 July 2026 — “Great House Farm / Church View Close”. Full timeline restated. Ten-working-day request. Explicit question: who removed the tree my great-grandfather planted to commemorate the ownership claim, as shown in the Collection of Wales archive image.\n- 27 July 2026 — formal complaint to [email redacted]<[email redacted]>, copied to Councillor Carroll, Democratic Services and the Monitoring Officer.\n- 28 August 2026 — further letter to Councillor Carroll and Stephen Doughty MP on the same site.\n\nWhat I am asking you to do\n\n1. Note that the ward member has not answered, and that the complaint process produced no requirement that he do so.\n\n2. Ask Councillor Carroll, or the relevant Director, to provide a short written reply on the tree: which department or landowner authorised or carried out its removal, on what date, and under what power or works order — or to confirm that the Council holds no such record.\n\n3. Identify the correct officer contact for heritage, trees and historic planning records at Church View Close / former Great House Farm, so I am not left circling a silent ward inbox.\n\n4. Confirm whether the Council will treat a documented family connection to a demolished historic farmstead in the ward as a legitimate basis for a reply, even where the correspondent is not presently resident in Llandough.\n\nThe thesis of my concern is that the lawfare/ warfare that systematically attempted to make our occupancy permissive as early as the 40's (with my grandmothers statement of title being stolen, a failed attempt to get us into a tenancy afterwards, and ambiguity in a 1955 order for another parcel we leased and defaulted on, which later meant it was able to be misinterpreted to make it appear our parcel of land (now Church View Close) was part of theirs, like it was in 1916 - 1928) is a racket. The ownership dispute was a problem created. The reaction followed and the solution was to focus on possession. And so in 1987 BP were awarded possession with what was a defective title chain.\n\nI do not expect you to reopen 1987 litigation. I do expect the authority, once the ward member has been given months and a formal complaint, to answer a factual question about a tree on land in that ward. Because that tree was part of a long list of evidence supporting our ownership claim.\n\nI can supply the original emails and the 27 July complaint in full if Democratic Services does not already have them. See attached the aforementioned tree planting ceremy image. I also enclose a draft chancery court allocation so you can understand the legal arguments.\n\nDue to a lot of suspicious and conveniently timed deaths in our family, surrounding this case, like my grandmother dying in the 2 weeks she will have had to challenge BP quietly registering our land parcel in their names in 1983, without declaring our ownership claim, which was never exstinguished as the first 1974 court case was ajourned. Or my fathers death after his appeal of the 1987 ruling was dismissed on grounds of \"heads BP win, tails we lose\". Followed by ECHR rejection citing BP own the land, despite BP only seeking possession and never seeking or gaining ownership.\n\nI have a trove of evidence and I am collecting more and investigating and in talks with CPS and Fraud Police and MoJ and Land Registry. The initial thing I would like from you is the record of the removal of the tree we planted. The picture attached depicts its location. I'd like to know who removed it and when it was removed?\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester \n\n[address redacted], [postcode redacted]\n\n[cid:image001.jpg@01DD477E.9C9D0350]"},{"id":"gm-1a0b4a2578a1c042","subject":"Re: From the Office of Stephen Doghty MP","name":"Re: From the Office of Stephen Doghty MP","date":"2026-09-18T13:08:52Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0b4a2578a1c042.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0b3cb33eb0553d-from-the-office-of-stephen-doghty-mp","scopeTopics":["mp"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["mp"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0b3cb33eb0553d","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"mp","name":"Member of Parliament"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0b4a2578a1c042.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Thank you.\n\nCould we meet so that I may explain this to you in person? I flew back from\nArgentina for this. I'm at my house in [location redacted], which has been my home\nsince, Great House Farm, Llandough is my previous address. I'm registered\nto vote, I am a constituent. And my last employer was the Vale Council.\n\nI left my military career with full honours. I'm a decorated war veteran.\nI'm in full mental and physical health. I just wholeheartedly reject a\nterrorist/ hostage situationship/ relationship. I don't play the role of\nanyone's prisoner of war or hostage well.\n\nThe land registry didn't appear to have notified my grandmother. She also\nconveniently died in the 2 weeks she will have had to challenge this had\nshe known. Land registry also seem to have somehow looked at the 1974 court\nproceedings between BP and my disabled, widow, pensioner grandmother Mary\nWilliams and selectively not seen her on there. The licence letters BP\npresented also said Mrs Buckler, her name was Mrs Williams. She was only a\nBuckler from marriage in 1935 for a brief period afterwards. In that period\nshe was married to her husband Fredrick Buckler,  they were presented a\ntenancy for the parcel of land they lived on and claimed to own, which my\ngrandmother rejected specifically asserting ownership. Despite reporting\n(as per her statement) her paper title deeds were stolen shortly\nbeforehand. Therfore the land registry didn't consult Mrs Williams, and\ndidn't challenge the discrepancy with the name difference in 1982/1983,\nthey instead fished out my 1935 marriage certificate with Requester, to\nofficiate and provide supporting evidence of a Mrs Buckler. So much of all\nof this was done by force/ stealth, secrecy and license. BP also moved the\ndescriptor from their parcel to our parcel in 1971 on the ordinance survey.\nMaking it more neatly appear to be the subject of thr 1962 possession order\nfor our parcel. Which was itself possible generated in secrecy, as it was\nrecorded as not being enforced, only the interest in it conveyed to BP in\n1969. But that 1962 order, is based on misinterpretation of the ambiguity\nin the 1955 order.\n\nThe 1955 order is for the \"whole farm\" and excluded our parcel because of\nmy nans \"condition and resistenance\" having just had her leg amputated. But\nthe definition of whole farm had changed from the 1916 agricultural tenancy\nit was based on, from 2 parcels to 1 parcel. Our parcel wasn't in what was\nconveyed from marquess of bute to western ground rents in 1938. Because\nthe 1977 Daniel Thomas & Son agreement enacted in 1928 when the last quarry\nmachine left. Nor was our parcel in the revised tenancy between Western\nGround Rents and my great grandfather in 1939. So the definition \"whole\nfarm\" in the 1955 order didn't include our parcel of land but was treated\nas though it was, just temporarily excluded due to my nans \"condition and\nresistenance\". And her condition and resistance was, like in 1949's\nrejection of WGR's attempt to offer her and her husband a tenancy on her\nown land, rejected due to her ownership claim.\n\nThere's a rumour her own husband stole her title deed and pawned it, but\nthat would still be theft and my grandmothers and us heirs and successors\ninterest would remain in the land. But keeping her as Mrs Buckler when she\nwasn't appeared to be important to BP. Even in the 1987 case the judge\ndefended our family when BP tried to claim Mr Fredrick Buckler (my grandad)\nexpressed interest in the 1949 tenancy offer, and in doing so waivered any\nownership claim in any case, which the judge actually rejected.\n\nIrrespective of if Frederick Buckler was or was not involved in my\ngrandmothers and dad's and our current generations highly unlawful,\nunconstitutional, inhumane and illegal dispossession of our land and home\nis largely inconsequential. The judge even said that would have died when\nhe died. And the conveniently timed deaths of my grandad, nan, father also\nraise bigger concerns here.\n\nThe core issue is the 1916 agricultural tenancy from Bute to our family was\n2 parcels. Our house and farm north of the church, with woodland north of\nthat. And the land to the east of this parcel (see attached) we leased,\ndefaulted on and was repossessed. But merging our parcel into the parcel\npossessed, and ignoring the split so that from 1916 to 1983 the \"whole\nfarm\" can appear and be treated as one parcel, with WGR and later BP\nappearing to own both parcels during that split is fraud. Fraud unravels\nall. Not withstanding the Government involvement and concealment. And so\nthe statue of limitation doesn't apply. I am seeking to reopen this case.\nAnd have ownership adjuctated, as the ownership ruling was adjourned and\nonly possession was awarded and final.\n\nWe also don't seek possession of the 13 houses, which were being planned to\ngo on our land long before ownership was even tried in 1974 (and\npetitioned) and long before even possession was awarded to BP in 1987. They\ndidn't even have the land to give, in 1974, when they gave their licence\nletters. Their title for our parcel was defective all along.\n\nLand registry took the ploy by WGR and BP to make our occupancy permissive,\nwhich wasn't just in 1974 with the licence letters, but the ambiguity\ninjected into the 1955 order so it could later be misinterpreted, like with\nthe stealthful 1962 (unenforced/ possibly undeclared) possession order for\nour parcel based on misinterpretation of the ambiguity inserted into the\n1955 order. Not withstanding the 1949 and other 40's attempt to make our\nownership claim difficult to prove and our occupancy permissive.\n\nI've even had archives check the content of packets to see if their titles\nhad been tampered with and they confirm there was errors. Much like records\nare missing or alleged to have been destroyed with no authorised record\ndisposal registery entry. I also believe I may have located other records\nwhich support our ownership claim but the titles have been altered so as\nthey don't appear in searched.\n\nI have only 6 years from discovery of fraud to reopen this case, and I'm\nconcerned the local authories months of lies, negligence, incompetence,\nrail roading, obstructing, time waiting, refusals to co-operate is an\noutrage. I'm furious at this. Surely local authories should want correct\nrecords as fudiaries. Surely concealment of the sites historic and\narcheligical significance needs to end at some point.\n\nAs stated, I'm having to reconstruct a replica of our former 800 year old\nhome as a museum in patagonia, just to tell the story of the Roman Villa\nunder it, the largest most significant medieval burials site, Cedwyn,\nPeerless Jim fighting his first fight in our barn. Because everyone here is\nbeing as dead as a door nail about this scandal. Which isn't just upon my\nfamily but it is and always was a matter of public interest. 2,200 year old\nceltic round houses also we're discovered under our farm house. The church\nbuilt in 1840 also got grade II listing with ease in 1989, after our 1200's\nhome was denied it in 1988. Despite a huge huge pile of advice to\nacknowledge it's historic significants. Much evidence was ignored, IS\nMISSING, was downplayed etc.\n\nSorry but people got bought. The council, CADW, the list goes on and on. I\nam a cyber and electronic warfare professional and I have AI and it's not\njust I have the intelligence to make sense of this. But the responses I get\nto even FOI's the last year make it very damming for all parties involved.\nMore damming that things already are for local authories.\n\nSouth Wales police first said they held no records. Then I cited collar\nnumbers of officers and some info I have from my father's diaries and\ndeclassified footage. Then they said they'd only speak by phone not in\nwriting. Always taking the maximum time to respond. So I provided my\nnumber. Then got no call for weeks. So I followed up. They then said I\ndidn't provide my number, when the thread clearly shows my phone number\nprovided in my previous email under their statement fasly alledging I\ndidn't provide a contact number.\n\nI'm a military warfare professional. I have 56 British soldiers in my close\ncommunity. I don't appreciate the effort to drag this matter into the\nshadows, buy time to get to me before I get to the truth and evidence. I\ndon't appreciate clandestine warfare because it will become a case of\nsilence and violence and bloodshed. And this isn't me creating that\nenvironment. I'm insisting on open/ transparent handling of this. Not more\nfighting and more loss of liberty and life than has already been the case.\n\nI also wish to state that this modern land annex racketing (making\noccupancy permissive > ceizing possession) , may extend beyond our sole\nparcel, to other parcels we held before. Ty Mawr was actually the north\nhalf of Llandough and Leckwith up to Ely river. Some 40 parcels. I believe\nCedwyn Estate. Our manorial house and home since 1667 was the manner house.\nAnd many of our dealings with Bute were that of equals and minor gentry. So\nthis unravelling of fraud is only for one land parcel, but more appear to\nexist.\n\nNot withstanding nationwide. We may have identified we are in a land market\nbubble. And so a land market crash may come from this. So I would encourage\nall parties to take this case more seriously as every aspect of it will be\nscrutinised later. And more likely by AI and so with relative ease.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester \n\n\n\n\n\nOn Fri, 18 Sept 2026, 10:13 DOUGHTY, Stephen, <\n[email redacted]> wrote:\n\n> Good morning Requester\n>\n>\n>\n> Following enquiries on your behalf, please see the attached response from\n> Chief Executive and Chief Land Registrar at HM Land Registry.\n>\n> We are sorry to provide what we understand may be a disappointing reply\n> but trust that the information is accurate and goes someway to clarify\n> their position.\n>\n> We are still waiting for a full response from the Ministry of Justice but\n> will of course let you know as soon as we hear back.\n>\n>\n>\n> Kind regards,\n>\n>\n>\n> *Office of Stephen Doughty MP / AS*\n> *Labour and Co-operative Member of Parliament for Cardiff South and\n> Penarth*\n>\n> *Llafur a Cydweithredol Aelod Seneddol dros Dde Caerdydd a Phenarth*\n>\n> *Westminster / San Steffan *| [phone redacted] | House of Commons, London,\n> [postcode redacted]\n>\n> *Email / E-bost                      *| [email redacted]\n>\n> *Website / Gwefan      *          | www.stephendoughty.wales\n>\n>\n>\n> For information about how we use your personal data, see our Privacy\n> Notice <https://www.stephendoughty.wales/privacynotice>.\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> UK Parliament Disclaimer: this e-mail is confidential to the intended\n> recipient. If you have received it in error, please notify the sender and\n> delete it from your system. Any unauthorised use, disclosure, or copying is\n> not permitted. This e-mail has been checked for viruses, but no liability\n> is accepted for any damage caused by any virus transmitted by this e-mail.\n> This e-mail address is not secure, is not encrypted and should not be used\n> for sensitive data.\n>"},{"id":"gm-1a0b3cb33eb0553d","subject":"From the Office of Stephen Doghty MP","name":"From the Office of Stephen Doghty MP","date":"2026-09-18T09:13:47Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0b3cb33eb0553d.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0b3cb33eb0553d-from-the-office-of-stephen-doghty-mp","scopeTopics":["mp"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["mp"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0b3cb33eb0553d","direction":"received","from":"DOUGHTY, Stephen","institutions":[{"id":"mp","name":"Member of Parliament"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0b3cb33eb0553d.json","attachments":[{"name":"260900 - Letter to Stephen Doughty MP.pdf","title":"260900 - Letter to Stephen Doughty MP.pdf","url":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/pdf/evidence/2026-09-09_hmlr_chief-land-registrar-letter-to-mp.pdf","mime":"application/pdf","bytes":116171,"sha256":"667289b9f3fb8d4f90a4847b7b6b3b35d8c53665f8d3eb6ed0530661128cf99f"}],"textStatus":"available","text":"Good morning Requester\n\nFollowing enquiries on your behalf, please see the attached response from Chief Executive and Chief Land Registrar at HM Land Registry.\nWe are sorry to provide what we understand may be a disappointing reply but trust that the information is accurate and goes someway to clarify their position.\nWe are still waiting for a full response from the Ministry of Justic