ion":"received","from":"(name not shown; whatdotheyknow.com)","institutions":[{"id":"nactso","name":"Counter Terrorism Policing (NaCTSO)"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0b3ad95bcb41e9.json","textStatus":"available","text":"You have a new response to the Freedom of Information request 'BP vs Buckler 1987' that you made to National Counter Terrorism Security Office.\n\nTo view the response, click on the link below.\n\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/request/bp_vs_buckler_1987_1988?nocache=incoming-3558417#incoming-3558417\n\nWhen you get there, please update the status to say if the response contains any useful information.\n\nAlthough all responses are automatically published, we depend on you, the original requester, to evaluate them.\n\n-- the WhatDoTheyKnow team\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nWhatDoTheyKnow is a project of mySociety run by a small team of staff and\ndedicated volunteers.\n\nmySociety is a charity that believes that information should be accessible to everyone.\nIf this is important to you, please consider donating to help us do more:\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/donate\n\nFind out more about how we handle your data at:\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/help/privacy\n\nmySociety is a registered charity in England and Wales (1076346), and a limited\ncompany (03277032)"},{"id":"gm-1a0b38fe35cc0d58","subject":"Automated Response: Email address not supported","name":"Automated Response: Email address not supported","date":"2026-09-18T08:09:03Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0b38fe35cc0d58.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0b38fe35cc0d58-automated-response-email-address-not-supported","scopeTopics":["hmlr"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["hmlr"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0b38fe35cc0d58","direction":"received","from":"HM Land Registry - Do Not Reply","institutions":[{"id":"hmlr","name":"HM Land Registry"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0b38fe35cc0d58.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Enquiry Reference: 260918-6720624 Title Number: WA231076\n\nDear Customer\n\nThis email address is no longer supported.\n\nOur Contact HM Land Registry  page has comprehensive help pages for most enquiries.\n\nThis is an automated response and no action will be taken with your enquiry.\n\nPlease do not reply to this email. This mailbox is not monitored.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nNid yw’r cyfeiriad ebost hwn yn cael ei gynnal mwyach.\n\nMae gan ein tudalen Cysylltu â Chofrestrfa Tir EF  dudalennau cymorth cynhwysfawr ar gyfer y rhan fwyaf o ymholiadau.\n\nYmateb awtomataidd yw hwn ac ni chymerir unrhyw gamau gyda’ch ymholiad.\n\nPeidiwch ag ateb yr ebost hwn. Nid yw’r blwch negeseuon ebost hwn yn cael ei fonitro.\n\n\n\nHM Land Registry’s purpose is to protect your land ownership and provide services and data that underpin an efficient and informed property market. Our vision is: “A world-leading property market as part of a thriving economy and a sustainable future”.\n\nWe check all mail and attachments for known viruses. However, you are advised that you open any attachments at your own risk. If you have received this email and it was not intended for you, please let us know, then delete it.\n\nWe welcome correspondence in English and Welsh.\n\nTo see how HM Land Registry treats your personal information, read our Personal information charter: www.gov.uk/government/organisations/land-registry/about/personal-information-charter."},{"id":"gm-1a0b32a056d8b1b1","subject":"Re: Great House Farm, Llandough #628635887","name":"Re: Great House Farm, Llandough #628635887","date":"2026-09-18T06:17:49Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0b32a056d8b1b1.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0b32a056d8b1b1-great-house-farm-llandough-628635887","scopeTopics":["tfw"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["tfw"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0b32a056d8b1b1","direction":"received","from":"(name not shown; customersupport.tfw.wales)","institutions":[{"id":"tfw","name":"Transport for Wales"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0b32a056d8b1b1.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Hi Requester Thank you for your email.We have forwarded your complaint to our complaints manager.Kind regardsColin.\n\nOn Thu, Sep 17, 2026 at 8:32 PM, Requester   wrote:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nYou don't often get email from [email redacted]. \nLearn why this is important \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nEXTERNAL EMAIL WARNING\n\nThis email originated from outside of the organisation. You should take extra care and make sure this email is genuine before taking any action. If this email appears suspicious, use the\nReport button function in Outlook\nIf you've already interacted with a suspicious or malicious email, please contact\nService Desk on [phone redacted] or [email redacted] \n\n\n\nMr Wigley,\nI am writing to make a formal complaint to Cadw concerning the handling and substantive response to my correspondence regarding Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough, and particularly Cadw's 1988 consideration of whether the building should\n be protected by listing.\nI am making this complaint directly to Cadw at this stage because I consider that Cadw should be given a proper opportunity to review and address the matters before any escalation to the wider Welsh Government complaints process.\nFor the avoidance of doubt, I am not making a further Freedom of Information request and I am not simply asking Cadw to produce records which it has already said no longer exist. I accept that Cadw has stated that all records it currently\n holds concerning the 1988 listing decision have been released.\nMy complaint concerns what can properly be established from the surviving evidence, whether the responses I have received have adequately addressed that evidence, and whether the historical record can now be described accurately where the underlying file\n has been lost.\n1. The newly identified 1982 evidence\nI have now identified a 1 October 1982 consultation letter from the Glamorgan-Gwent Archaeological Trust concerning the East Vale Local Plan and the proposed Ty Maw Farm site, identified as\nH2.1.\nThe letter states:\n\n“Ty Maw Farm (H2.1) is designated as a three-acre residential site. As you will see from the enclosed information the settlement at Llandough developed considerably from farming and fishing... Recent excavations at Llandough by the Trust revealed early occupation,\n part of the villa and medieval grange buildings. There remains a possibility that occupation continues to the north of the church; provision should be made, therefore, for thorough investigative work prior to the development.”\n\nI do not suggest that this document, by itself, establishes that the standing farmhouse was required to be listed.\nIts significance is narrower and more demonstrable: six years before the 1988 demolition, the archaeological and planning system already contained site-specific information identifying Ty Maw Farm as a proposed development site and recording substantial\n archaeological sensitivity in the immediate Llandough area, including possible continued occupation north of the church.\nThat materially affects the historical context in which Cadw's 1988 assessment needs to be understood.\nI therefore ask Cadw to address:\n\n\nWhether this 1982 information was held by, available to, or capable of being obtained by Cadw in the period leading to the 1988 decision.\n\nWhether Cadw has any surviving evidence showing what archaeological, planning or historic-building information was available to the inspectors or decision-makers in 1988.\n\nWhether there was communication between Cadw and the relevant planning/archaeological authorities concerning the proposed development of Ty Maw Farm and the need for investigation or protection.\n\nIf it is no longer possible to establish whether this evidence was actually considered, whether Cadw will expressly acknowledge that uncertainty rather than treating the absence of the historic file as evidence that the relevant information was absent.\n\n2. The 1974 RCAHMW survey\nI have previously drawn Cadw's attention to the 26 April 1974 Royal Commission survey by H.J. Thomas at ST16817331.\nThat survey recorded Great House Farm as a historic building, including features such as the late seventeenth-century character of the house, its cross-passage arrangement, adze-worked beams, ogee stops and barrel-vaulted cellar.\nCadw has stated that only two photographs from its relevant historic material survive and that it cannot now establish whether the 1974 survey was taken into account in the 1988 listing consideration.\nI accept that this may now be incapable of being proved from the surviving file.\nHowever, there is an important distinction between:\n“Cadw cannot now establish whether the information was considered”\nand\n“there is no evidence that the information was available or relevant.”\nThe surviving RCAHMW material demonstrates that professional historic-building recording existed well before the 1988 decision.\nI therefore ask Cadw to address specifically whether that material was available to Cadw through the inter-agency heritage system at the time and, if so, what can now be established about its availability.\n3. Cadw's own evidence that listing was actively being considered in 1988\nThe surviving contemporary material indicates that in December 1988 Cadw was actively considering emergency protection/spot-listing of Great House Farm, including inspection and consultation concerning the building's historic significance.\nThat is important because it demonstrates that this was not retrospectively invented as a heritage issue decades later. Cadw itself was considering protection immediately before the building was demolished.\nCadw's present position is that the historical listing file no longer survives and that its retention arrangements at the time cannot now be established with certainty.\nI am not asking Cadw to speculate about what a destroyed file contained.\nI am asking Cadw to distinguish clearly between:\n\nwhat the surviving evidence positively establishes;what information was demonstrably available by 1988;what actions by Cadw in 1988 can still be established;what cannot now be established because the historic record has been lost; andany conclusions Cadw considers cannot responsibly be drawn.\nThat distinction is particularly important when assessing whether the surviving record provides a sufficiently reliable account of what happened.\n4. The planning authority and temporary protection\nI also wish to clarify the position concerning temporary protection.\nI am not suggesting that Cadw itself necessarily possessed the statutory power to serve a Building Preservation Notice. I understand that such protection was a matter for the relevant local planning authority under the legislation then in force.\nThe question for Cadw is therefore whether, during the 1988 consideration, \nCadw advised, discussed or communicated with the relevant planning authority concerning any available temporary protection mechanism or the need to prevent demolition pending consideration of listing.\nIf Cadw cannot establish what was discussed because the records no longer survive, that should be stated expressly.\n5. The later description of Great House Farm as “a listed building”\nI have also identified later archaeological/planning documentation in which the former site is described in terms including:\n\n“The site formerly contained Great House Farm (a listed building).”\n\nI am not asserting that this wording conclusively proves that Great House Farm was formally listed at that time.\nI am asking Cadw to explain, insofar as the surviving evidence permits, whether there is any known provenance for that description and whether Cadw considers it accurate, erroneous, or simply impossible now to determine.\nThis is relevant because it illustrates the existence of potentially conflicting historical accounts concerning the building's protection status.\n6. The significance of the loss of the 1988 record\nCadw has understandably stated that the historic file cannot now be produced.\nHowever, the loss of that file creates a historical evidential limitation; it should not automatically resolve questions arising from the surviving evidence in Cadw's favour.\nThe appropriate conclusion may simply be that some aspects of the 1988 decision\ncannot now be reconstructed with certainty.\nI would therefore like Cadw to consider whether its previous responses have drawn that distinction sufficiently clearly.\n7. What I am asking Cadw to do\nI am asking Cadw to treat this as a formal complaint and provide a substantive response addressing the following:\nA. Whether the newly identified 1982 GGAT evidence materially changes Cadw's understanding of the information environment surrounding Ty Maw Farm before the 1988 demolition.\nB. Whether Cadw can establish that the 1974 RCAHMW survey was available to it in 1988.\nC. Whether Cadw can establish what archaeological, planning and historic-building information was available to its officers when the building was being considered for protection.\nD. What can now be established about Cadw's communications with the local planning authority and other heritage bodies during the 1988 listing consideration.\nE. Whether temporary protection was discussed or advised upon and, if not capable of being established, whether Cadw will state that plainly.\nF. What can be established about the inspection of Great House Farm and the ultimate listing decision.\nG. Whether Cadw accepts that the loss of the historic file means that some aspects of the 1988 decision can no longer be reconstructed reliably.\nH. Whether Cadw considers the surviving documentary evidence, including the 1974 survey and 1982 archaeological/planning evidence, should be reflected in any relevant contemporary heritage record or historical account.\nI. Whether Cadw can explain the provenance, insofar as it is capable of doing so, of the later description of the former site as containing “a listed building.”\nJ. Whether Cadw considers that its previous responses have adequately distinguished between the absence of surviving evidence and evidence that something did not happen.\n8. Scope of this complaint\nI want to be clear that I am not asking Cadw to determine my family's wider property or title claims, nor am I asking Cadw to reach a predetermined conclusion about the historical ownership of the land.\nThose matters are separate.\nThis complaint is confined to Cadw's own heritage-related role, the 1988 protection/listing process, the information that can now be established as having existed, and the adequacy and accuracy of Cadw's present explanation of that history.\nI am also not asking Cadw to speculate about missing documents. On the contrary, I ask that the absence of those documents be treated as an evidential limitation and expressly identified as such.\n9. Requested outcome\nI would like Cadw to:\n\n\nRegister this as a formal complaint and provide a complaint reference.\n\nHave the matter reviewed by an appropriate person who has not simply repeated the previous FOI position.\n\nProvide a point-by-point response to the matters above, taking account of the newly identified 1982 evidence.\n\nCorrect or clarify any part of Cadw's previous correspondence that is found to have been incomplete, inaccurate or insufficiently qualified.\n\nState clearly which matters can now be established, which cannot, and which remain uncertain because the historical records no longer survive.\n\nI regard that as a proportionate and reasonable opportunity for Cadw to address the matter directly before I consider whether escalation through the Welsh Government complaints process is necessary.\nYours sincerely,\n\nRequester \n"},{"id":"gm-1a0b2521a96c9c04","subject":"Further to Your Enquiry:  Enquiry Ref: 260918-6720625 Title No: WA240304","name":"Further to Your Enquiry:  Enquiry Ref: 260918-6720625 Title No: WA240304","date":"2026-09-18T02:21:55Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0b2521a96c9c04.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0b2521a96c9c04-further-to-your-enquiry-enquiry-ref-260918-6720625-title-no-wa240304","scopeTopics":["hmlr"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["hmlr"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0b2521a96c9c04","direction":"received","from":"Customer Support","institutions":[{"id":"hmlr","name":"HM Land Registry"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0b2521a96c9c04.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Enquiry Reference: 260918-6720625 Title Number: WA240304\n\nDear Customer\n\nWe've received your enquiry.\n\nWe aim to deal with enquiries as quickly as possible but there may be some delay before we can respond.\n\nPlease bear with us and we will get back to you as soon as we can.\n\nIf you do need to contact us about this enquiry, reply to this email to ensure the correct team can help you.\n\n\nYours faithfully\nHMLR Customer Support\n\n\n\nHM Land Registry's purpose is to protect your land ownership and provide services and data that underpin an efficient and informed property market. Our vision is: \"A world-leading property market as part of a thriving economy and a sustainable future\".\n\nWe check all mail and attachments for known viruses. However, you are advised that you open any attachments at your own risk. If you have received this email and it was not intended for you, please let us know, then delete it.\n\nWe welcome correspondence in English and Welsh.\n\nTo see how HM Land Registry treats your personal information, read our Personal information charter: www.gov.uk/government/organisations/land-registry/about/personal-information-charter."},{"id":"gm-1a0b24c6520eebce","subject":"N610CGV — WA231076 / WA240304","name":"N610CGV — WA231076 / WA240304","date":"2026-09-18T02:15:45Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0b24c6520eebce.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0b24c023bcbc52-n610cgv-wa231076-wa240304","scopeTopics":["hmlr"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["hmlr"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0b24c023bcbc52","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"hmlr","name":"HM Land Registry"}],"refs":["N610CGV"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0b24c6520eebce.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Haste,\n\nThis supplements my emails of 16 September 2026 (points 1–14) on N610CGV /\nWA231076 and WA240304. It is not a complaint, not a request to reopen the\nstage 2 review of 25 May 2026, and not a FOIA request.\nI have not previously asked HM Land Registry to record the following\nconstruction of the title chain. I do so now as a factual statement of the\ncase I put on the instruments and their plans. I am not asking you to\ndetermine ownership. I am asking you to confirm what the surviving\nfirst-registration records show about which parcel was lodged.\nThe land I refer to as the House Plot is the former farmhouse, garden and\ncurtilage of Great House Farm, Llandough, immediately west / north-west of\nSt Dochdwy’s Church, later part of Church View Close, [postcode redacted].\nMy case on the chain is:\nThe holding was two parcels. The 1916 Bute agricultural tenancy (John\nWilliams) covered both. Farm rents and cottage rents were accounted\nseparately on the Bute rental listings.\nAfter quarrying ceased in or about 1928, the House Plot was treated as\nsevered from the agricultural tenancy and held as a separate proprietary\nparcel by those through whom I claim.\nThe 1938 conveyance from the Bute interest to Western Ground Rents Limited,\nconstrued against its plan, conveyed the eastern agricultural parcel. It\ndid not convey the House Plot.\nThe May 1939 agricultural tenancy between WGR and John Williams concerned\nthat eastern parcel. It did not comprise the House Plot.\nThe 1955 enforcement papers and the 11 December 1962 Cardiff County Court\npossession order against Frederick Buckler and Mary Williams (sued as Mrs\nBuckler) were directed at the farmhouse and garden. Those proceedings\nasserted a right to possession. They did not construe the 1877, 1938 or\nlater deeds and were not a conveyance of the House Plot into the WGR estate.\nThe 31 December 1969 conveyance (WGR to the BP Pension Trust parties), the\n23 May 1975 conveyance (BP Pension Trust Limited to BP Properties Limited),\nand the 1982 first registration of WA231076 could only pass such land as\nthen stood in that documentary estate. On the construction above, that\nestate did not include the House Plot.\nTitle WA231076 (and WA240304, later amalgamated) nevertheless included the\nHouse Plot. That is the alleged mistake as to extent.\nPlease therefore add these numbered questions to the live enquiry (also\ncustomer-support refs 260812-6561894, 260812-6565153, 260917-6714794,\n260917-6714796 and 260917-6718215):\nConfirm whether any surviving first-registration document for WA231076 or\nWA240304 identifies, by plan, OS parcel, colouring, exception, reservation\nor recital, a distinction between (a) the farmhouse, garden and curtilage\nand (b) the eastern agricultural / quarrying parcel.\nConfirm whether the 1938 Bute–WGR conveyance and plan, or the 1939 tenancy\nand plan, were lodged on either first-registration file. If held, give\ntitle and item number. If only recited, say so. If neither lodged nor\nrecited, say so.\nConfirm whether the 23 May 1975 conveyance and plan (WA240304 items 11 and\n12 on your 30 July 2026 schedule) describe or colour the House Plot as\nincluded, excluded, excepted or reserved. If you will not read the deed,\nidentify it as an OC2 item and say so.\nConfirm whether the 31 December 1969 WGR conveyance (point 10 of my 16\nSeptember email) describes or colours the House Plot as included. Same\noptions as 17.\nConfirm whether any examiner note, requisition or Linklaters & Paines\nletter on the surviving files addresses the geographic extent of the land\napplied for, as distinct from the applicant’s paper title generally.\nI do not ask you to accept this construction. I ask you to say what the\nlisted records contain about parcel identity, or to state in writing that\nyou will not examine them and that the questions should be answered by Form\nOC2.\nYours sincerely,\nRequester\nN610CGV — WA231076 / WA240304"},{"id":"gm-1a0b20ca8346b3e8","subject":"Great House Farm, Llandough (89/01396/OUT)","name":"Great House Farm, Llandough (89/01396/OUT)","date":"2026-09-18T01:06:08Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0b20ca8346b3e8.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0b1f3976299c3f-great-house-farm-llandough-89-01396-out","scopeTopics":["vale-foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["vale-foi"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0b1f3976299c3f","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"vale-foi","name":"Vale of Glamorgan Council"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0b20ca8346b3e8.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear FOI Unit,\n\nUnder the Freedom of Information Act 2000 and/or the Environmental\nInformation Regulations 2004 (whichever regime the Council considers\napplicable to the material), I am requesting information held in relation\nto:\n\n   - Planning application *89/01396/OUT* (outline permission granted 13\n   March 1990); and\n   - The subsequent reserved matters application *92/00671/RES*,\n\nboth concerning land at Great House Farm, Leckwith Road, Llandough,\nsubsequently developed as Church View Close.\n\nI am seeking to reconstruct the documentary planning and consultation\nrecord between the outline permission and the subsequent\ndetailed/reserved-matters approval.\n1. Consultation responses to application 89/01396/OUT\n\nThe Council's own case officer's report on application 89/01396/OUT\nrecords, in its consultations section:\n\n\"Cardiff Bay Development Corporation: No objections – however, the\nCorporation would hope that the trees on the slope of the site facing\nnorth-west are retained and that the public right of way which runs through\nthe site will be retained.\"\n\nThe same report refers to further consultation responses from the *National\nRivers Authority* and the *County Engineer* by reference to attached typed\nsheets (\"please type attached sh②\" and \"please type attached sh③\"), without\nreproducing their substantive contents in the disclosed copy.\n\nPlease therefore provide:\n\n   1.\n\n   The *complete original written response from the Cardiff Bay Development\n   Corporation* concerning application 89/01396/OUT, including any\n   accompanying letter, report, plan, attachment or other document forming\n   part of that consultation response.\n   2.\n\n   The *complete National Rivers Authority consultation response* referred\n   to in the report as one of the attached sheets, including the original\n   document and any accompanying material.\n   3.\n\n   The *complete County Engineer consultation response* referred to in the\n   report as one of the attached sheets, including the original document and\n   any accompanying material.\n\nI am deliberately not relying upon the handwritten numbering of sheets ②\nand ③ to identify which sheet belongs to which consultee, as the numerals\nare difficult to distinguish in the surviving copy. Please identify the\ncorresponding sheet/document by reference to its contents or file\nprovenance.\n\n   4. Any *covering correspondence, transmission record or internal record*\n   showing when the NRA and County Engineer responses were received by the\n   Council and how those responses were placed on, incorporated into or\n   considered as part of the planning file.\n\n2. Archaeological assessment and discharge of outline conditions\n\nPlease provide:\n\n   5.\n\n   The *formal decision notice and complete schedule of conditions* issued\n   in connection with planning application 89/01396/OUT, including any\n   condition concerning archaeological investigation, evaluation, assessment,\n   excavation, recording, preservation or protection.\n   6.\n\n   All records held by the Council demonstrating the *discharge,\n   implementation or consideration of any archaeological condition*\n   attached to the 1990 outline permission, including any archaeological\n   evaluation, field assessment, report, correspondence, approval, discharge\n   notice, consultation response or internal officer record.\n   7.\n\n   Any correspondence or documentation showing how the Council considered\n   the findings of that archaeological work in determining the subsequent\n   reserved matters application 92/00671/RES.\n\n3. Reserved matters application 92/00671/RES\n\nPlease provide:\n\n   8.\n\n   The *formal decision notice* for application 92/00671/RES, including the\n   complete schedule of any conditions attached to the approval.\n   9.\n\n   The consultation responses and representations received in connection\n   with 92/00671/RES, including any response or representation from\nthe *Cardiff\n   Bay Development Corporation*, if one exists.\n   10.\n\n   Any officer report, committee report, delegated report, recommendation\n   or internal planning assessment relating to the determination of\n   92/00671/RES.\n   11.\n\n   Any document recording the Council's consideration of the relationship\n   between the reserved-matters proposal and the parameters established by the\n   outline permission 89/01396/OUT.\n\n4. Change from the outline scheme to the reserved-matters scheme\n\nThe officer's report for the outline application describes the indicative\nscheme as *18 dwellings*, whereas the subsequent reserved-matters scheme\nappears to comprise *20 dwellings*.\n\nPlease therefore provide:\n\n   12.\n\n   Any document held by the Council recording or explaining the change from\n   the indicative *18-dwelling* scheme at outline stage to the *20-dwelling*\n   scheme submitted/approved at reserved-matters stage.\n   13.\n\n   Any planning assessment, officer report, correspondence,\n   committee/delegated report or other record explaining the planning basis\n   upon which the additional two dwellings were considered acceptable under\n   the outline permission.\n   14.\n\n   Any document identifying the relevant condition, parameter, planning\n   provision or other mechanism under application 89/01396/OUT under which the\n   reserved-matters scheme was considered capable of differing from the\n   indicative 18-dwelling layout.\n\n5. Scope and record availability\n\nFor the avoidance of doubt, this is *not a request for a general search of\nall historic records concerning Great House Farm*. It is specifically\ndirected to the planning records and decision-making documents identified\nabove for applications *89/01396/OUT and 92/00671/RES*.\n\nPlease search the relevant planning application files and associated\narchived/scanned material, including attachments and documents stored\nseparately from the principal application file where those documents form\npart of the application or consultation record.\n\nPlease supply the information electronically, such as scanned copies, where\npracticable.\n\nIf any requested record is *not held*, please confirm this specifically for\nthe relevant item rather than leaving the point unanswered.\n\nIf the Council considers that any information is exempt from disclosure,\nplease identify the *specific exemption or exception relied upon* and\nexplain its application.\n\nIf any of the identified historical records were previously held but are no\nlonger available, please confirm, where recorded:\n\n   - whether the record was destroyed, transferred, archived elsewhere or\n   otherwise disposed of;\n   - the date or approximate date of that action, if recorded;\n   - the authority or process under which it occurred; and\n   - whether any surviving catalogue, disposal schedule, transfer record or\n   other audit trail exists.\n\nI also understand that the application for housing began much further back\nas a petition was made in 1974 for 13 houses. Please could you give me a\nreference or link or that entire planning application record too.\n\n Please acknowledge receipt of this request and provide a reference number.\n\nI understand that the statutory response period is 20 working days.\n\nRegards,\n\n\nRequester \n\n"},{"id":"gm-1a0b0dac48ca3d12","subject":"Great House Farm, Llandough","name":"Great House Farm, Llandough","date":"2026-09-17T19:32:02Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0b0dac48ca3d12.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0b0a30bb4dd216-great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":["cadw"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["cadw"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0b0a30bb4dd216","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"cadw","name":"Welsh Government / Cadw"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0b0dac48ca3d12.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Mr Wigley,\n\nI am writing to make a *formal complaint to Cadw* concerning the handling\nand substantive response to my correspondence regarding Great House Farm\n(Tŷ Mawr), Llandough, and particularly Cadw's 1988 consideration of whether\nthe building should be protected by listing.\n\nI am making this complaint directly to Cadw at this stage because I\nconsider that Cadw should be given a proper opportunity to review and\naddress the matters before any escalation to the wider Welsh Government\ncomplaints process.\n\nFor the avoidance of doubt, I am *not making a further Freedom of\nInformation request* and I am not simply asking Cadw to produce records\nwhich it has already said no longer exist. I accept that Cadw has stated\nthat all records it currently holds concerning the 1988 listing decision\nhave been released.\n\nMy complaint concerns what can properly be established from the surviving\nevidence, whether the responses I have received have adequately addressed\nthat evidence, and whether the historical record can now be described\naccurately where the underlying file has been lost.\n1. The newly identified 1982 evidence\n\nI have now identified a 1 October 1982 consultation letter from the\nGlamorgan-Gwent Archaeological Trust concerning the East Vale Local Plan\nand the proposed Ty Maw Farm site, identified as *H2.1*.\n\nThe letter states:\n\n“Ty Maw Farm (H2.1) is designated as a three-acre residential site. As you\nwill see from the enclosed information the settlement at Llandough\ndeveloped considerably from farming and fishing... Recent excavations at\nLlandough by the Trust revealed early occupation, part of the villa and\nmedieval grange buildings. There remains a possibility that occupation\ncontinues to the north of the church; provision should be made, therefore,\nfor thorough investigative work prior to the development.”\n\nI do not suggest that this document, by itself, establishes that the\nstanding farmhouse was required to be listed.\n\nIts significance is narrower and more demonstrable: *six years before the\n1988 demolition, the archaeological and planning system already contained\nsite-specific information identifying Ty Maw Farm as a proposed development\nsite and recording substantial archaeological sensitivity in the immediate\nLlandough area, including possible continued occupation north of the\nchurch.*\n\nThat materially affects the historical context in which Cadw's 1988\nassessment needs to be understood.\n\nI therefore ask Cadw to address:\n\n   1.\n\n   Whether this 1982 information was held by, available to, or capable of\n   being obtained by Cadw in the period leading to the 1988 decision.\n   2.\n\n   Whether Cadw has any surviving evidence showing what archaeological,\n   planning or historic-building information was available to the inspectors\n   or decision-makers in 1988.\n   3.\n\n   Whether there was communication between Cadw and the relevant\n   planning/archaeological authorities concerning the proposed development of\n   Ty Maw Farm and the need for investigation or protection.\n   4.\n\n   If it is no longer possible to establish whether this evidence was\n   actually considered, whether Cadw will expressly acknowledge that\n   uncertainty rather than treating the absence of the historic file as\n   evidence that the relevant information was absent.\n\n2. The 1974 RCAHMW survey\n\nI have previously drawn Cadw's attention to the 26 April 1974 Royal\nCommission survey by H.J. Thomas at ST16817331.\n\nThat survey recorded Great House Farm as a historic building, including\nfeatures such as the late seventeenth-century character of the house, its\ncross-passage arrangement, adze-worked beams, ogee stops and barrel-vaulted\ncellar.\n\nCadw has stated that only two photographs from its relevant historic\nmaterial survive and that it cannot now establish whether the 1974 survey\nwas taken into account in the 1988 listing consideration.\n\nI accept that this may now be incapable of being proved from the surviving\nfile.\n\nHowever, there is an important distinction between:\n\n*“Cadw cannot now establish whether the information was considered”*\n\nand\n\n*“there is no evidence that the information was available or relevant.”*\n\nThe surviving RCAHMW material demonstrates that professional\nhistoric-building recording existed well before the 1988 decision.\n\nI therefore ask Cadw to address specifically whether that material was\navailable to Cadw through the inter-agency heritage system at the time and,\nif so, what can now be established about its availability.\n3. Cadw's own evidence that listing was actively being considered in 1988\n\nThe surviving contemporary material indicates that in December 1988 Cadw\nwas actively considering emergency protection/spot-listing of Great House\nFarm, including inspection and consultation concerning the building's\nhistoric significance.\n\nThat is important because it demonstrates that this was not retrospectively\ninvented as a heritage issue decades later. Cadw itself was considering\nprotection immediately before the building was demolished.\n\nCadw's present position is that the historical listing file no longer\nsurvives and that its retention arrangements at the time cannot now be\nestablished with certainty.\n\nI am not asking Cadw to speculate about what a destroyed file contained.\n\nI am asking Cadw to distinguish clearly between:\n\n   - what the surviving evidence positively establishes;\n   - what information was demonstrably available by 1988;\n   - what actions by Cadw in 1988 can still be established;\n   - what cannot now be established because the historic record has been\n   lost; and\n   - any conclusions Cadw considers cannot responsibly be drawn.\n\nThat distinction is particularly important when assessing whether the\nsurviving record provides a sufficiently reliable account of what happened.\n4. The planning authority and temporary protection\n\nI also wish to clarify the position concerning temporary protection.\n\nI am not suggesting that Cadw itself necessarily possessed the statutory\npower to serve a Building Preservation Notice. I understand that such\nprotection was a matter for the relevant local planning authority under the\nlegislation then in force.\n\nThe question for Cadw is therefore whether, during the 1988\nconsideration, *Cadw\nadvised, discussed or communicated with the relevant planning authority\nconcerning any available temporary protection mechanism or the need to\nprevent demolition pending consideration of listing.*\n\nIf Cadw cannot establish what was discussed because the records no longer\nsurvive, that should be stated expressly.\n5. The later description of Great House Farm as “a listed building”\n\nI have also identified later archaeological/planning documentation in which\nthe former site is described in terms including:\n\n“The site formerly contained Great House Farm (a listed building).”\n\nI am not asserting that this wording conclusively proves that Great House\nFarm was formally listed at that time.\n\nI am asking Cadw to explain, insofar as the surviving evidence permits,\nwhether there is any known provenance for that description and whether Cadw\nconsiders it accurate, erroneous, or simply impossible now to determine.\n\nThis is relevant because it illustrates the existence of potentially\nconflicting historical accounts concerning the building's protection status.\n6. The significance of the loss of the 1988 record\n\nCadw has understandably stated that the historic file cannot now be\nproduced.\n\nHowever, the loss of that file creates a historical evidential limitation;\nit should not automatically resolve questions arising from the surviving\nevidence in Cadw's favour.\n\nThe appropriate conclusion may simply be that some aspects of the 1988\ndecision *cannot now be reconstructed with certainty*.\n\nI would therefore like Cadw to consider whether its previous responses have\ndrawn that distinction sufficiently clearly.\n7. What I am asking Cadw to do\n\nI am asking Cadw to treat this as a formal complaint and provide a\nsubstantive response addressing the following:\n\n*A.* Whether the newly identified 1982 GGAT evidence materially changes\nCadw's understanding of the information environment surrounding Ty Maw Farm\nbefore the 1988 demolition.\n\n*B.* Whether Cadw can establish that the 1974 RCAHMW survey was available\nto it in 1988.\n\n*C.* Whether Cadw can establish what archaeological, planning and\nhistoric-building information was available to its officers when the\nbuilding was being considered for protection.\n\n*D.* What can now be established about Cadw's communications with the local\nplanning authority and other heritage bodies during the 1988 listing\nconsideration.\n\n*E.* Whether temporary protection was discussed or advised upon and, if not\ncapable of being established, whether Cadw will state that plainly.\n\n*F.* What can be established about the inspection of Great House Farm and\nthe ultimate listing decision.\n\n*G.* Whether Cadw accepts that the loss of the historic file means that\nsome aspects of the 1988 decision can no longer be reconstructed reliably.\n\n*H.* Whether Cadw considers the surviving documentary evidence, including\nthe 1974 survey and 1982 archaeological/planning evidence, should be\nreflected in any relevant contemporary heritage record or historical\naccount.\n\n*I.* Whether Cadw can explain the provenance, insofar as it is capable of\ndoing so, of the later description of the former site as containing “a\nlisted building.”\n\n*J.* Whether Cadw considers that its previous responses have adequately\ndistinguished between the absence of surviving evidence and evidence that\nsomething did not happen.\n8. Scope of this complaint\n\nI want to be clear that I am not asking Cadw to determine my family's wider\nproperty or title claims, nor am I asking Cadw to reach a predetermined\nconclusion about the historical ownership of the land.\n\nThose matters are separate.\n\nThis complaint is confined to *Cadw's own heritage-related role, the 1988\nprotection/listing process, the information that can now be established as\nhaving existed, and the adequacy and accuracy of Cadw's present explanation\nof that history.*\n\nI am also not asking Cadw to speculate about missing documents. On the\ncontrary, I ask that the absence of those documents be treated as an\nevidential limitation and expressly identified as such.\n9. Requested outcome\n\nI would like Cadw to:\n\n   1.\n\n   Register this as a formal complaint and provide a complaint reference.\n   2.\n\n   Have the matter reviewed by an appropriate person who has not simply\n   repeated the previous FOI position.\n   3.\n\n   Provide a point-by-point response to the matters above, taking account\n   of the newly identified 1982 evidence.\n   4.\n\n   Correct or clarify any part of Cadw's previous correspondence that is\n   found to have been incomplete, inaccurate or insufficiently qualified.\n   5.\n\n   State clearly which matters can now be established, which cannot, and\n   which remain uncertain because the historical records no longer survive.\n\nI regard that as a proportionate and reasonable opportunity for Cadw to\naddress the matter directly before I consider whether escalation through\nthe Welsh Government complaints process is necessary.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\n*Requester *\n"},{"id":"gm-1a0b031458eb1b88","subject":"Re: Welsh Water - Mail reference: 93027237/01","name":"Re: Welsh Water - Mail reference: 93027237/01","date":"2026-09-17T16:26:53Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0b031458eb1b88.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0afe15770e3994-welsh-water-mail-reference-93027237-01","scopeTopics":["dwrcymru"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["dwrcymru"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0afe15770e3994","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"dwrcymru","name":"Dwr Cymru Welsh Water"}],"refs":["93027237/01"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0b031458eb1b88.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Daniel,\n\nThank you for your response concerning mail reference 93027237/01.\n\nI note that Welsh Water's position is that no current records relating to\nGreat House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough have been located, and that Welsh\nWater is unable to establish whether historical records existed, when they\nceased to be held, or whether any records were removed during retention\nprocesses or migration from legacy systems.\n\nFor the purposes of my ongoing investigation, please clarify the following\npoints.\n\n   1.\n\n   You state that your enquiries “indicate that Great House Farm was\n   demolished during the 1980s”. Please identify the source or record on which\n   that statement is based, including any internal system, historical account,\n   property record, mapping record or other source used to reach that\n   conclusion.\n   2.\n\n   Please confirm whether Welsh Water searched any legacy systems, archived\n   customer-account systems, paper archives, scanned records, historical\n   billing systems, property databases, sewerage/water connection records, or\n   other records dating from the period before the present operating systems.\n   3.\n\n   Please confirm the applicable retention periods relied upon in\n   concluding that records from the relevant period would no longer be\n   expected to remain available.\n   4.\n\n   Please confirm whether the statement that “not all historic information\n   was transferred from legacy systems” applies to records of former\n   properties and customer accounts generally, and whether Welsh Water has any\n   documented migration or data-transfer policy governing the omission or\n   destruction of such historic information.\n   5.\n\n   Please confirm whether Welsh Water holds any records identifying\n   categories of information that were not migrated from the relevant legacy\n   systems, even if the individual Great House Farm records themselves are no\n   longer available.\n\nI am not asking Welsh Water to recreate records which it does not hold. I\nam asking for clarification of the evidential basis for the conclusions set\nout in your response, particularly where your email confirms that Welsh\nWater is unable to establish whether the historic records once existed or\nhow they ceased to be available.\n\nPlease retain this correspondence under mail reference 93027237/01.\n\nKind regards,\n\nRequester\n\nOn Thu, 17 Sept 2026, 15:59 CustomerCareTeam, <[email redacted]>\nwrote:\n\n> Mail reference: 93027237/01\n>\n>\n>\n> Your emails of: 3rd September 2026\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> Hello Requester,\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you for your email.\n>\n>\n>\n> I appreciate that you are seeking further clarification regarding the\n> records associated with Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough, and whether\n> any evidence exists to demonstrate what may have happened to historical\n> records relating to the property.\n>\n>\n>\n> As outlined in our previous correspondence, we have undertaken searches\n> using the information you have provided. Despite these searches, we have\n> been unable to locate any records relating to either the property or\n> yourself on our current systems. Based on the information available, our\n> position remains that we do not hold any records relating to Great House\n> Farm (Ty Mawr).\n>\n>\n>\n> You have asked whether records may have existed historically and whether\n> we can provide evidence showing if and when such records were deleted.\n> Unfortunately, as no records relating to the property have been located, we\n> do not hold any corresponding account information, customer records,\n> deletion logs, or audit records that would enable us to confirm whether\n> particular documents once existed or the circumstances in which they may\n> have been removed.\n>\n>\n>\n> As previously explained, our enquiries indicate that Great House Farm was\n> demolished during the 1980s. Any customer or property records from that\n> period would fall well outside our applicable retention periods and,\n> consequently, would not be expected to remain available today. Furthermore,\n> during the migration to our current operating systems, not all historic\n> information was transferred from legacy systems. As a result, we are unable\n> to access or recover records that are no longer held.\n>\n>\n>\n> Whilst I appreciate that this may be frustrating, the absence of records\n> means we are unable to verify whether specific documents once existed,\n> whether they were subsequently removed in accordance with retention\n> requirements, or provide documentary evidence of their deletion. We can\n> only disclose information that is currently held by the company and,\n> following the searches completed, no information relating to the property\n> has been identified.\n>\n>\n>\n> For clarity, our position is not that records have been located and are\n> being withheld. Rather, despite conducting further searches using the\n> information available, we have been unable to identify any records relating\n> to the property within the scope of your request.\n>\n>\n>\n> Consequently, there is no further information that we are able to provide\n> in relation to Great House Farm (Ty Mawr).\n>\n>\n>\n> *Outcome*\n>\n>\n>\n> I appreciate that this may not be the outcome you were hoping for.\n> However, our position remains unchanged, as we do not hold any information\n> relating to the property and are therefore unable to provide any further\n> details regarding its historical records.\n>\n>\n>\n> Lastly, using the information you have provided, my colleague arranged for\n> the £20.00 GSS payment to be issued to your address at [address redacted], CF64\n> 4TF\n> [map link redacted].\n> The payment should be delivered within 7 days.\n>\n>\n>\n> If you have any questions or concerns, please do not hesitate to get in\n> touch by replying to this email, or by calling Customer Care on 0330 041\n> 3307, available Monday to Friday between 9:00am and 4:00pm. Alternatively,\n> you can contact our Billing Department on [phone redacted], available Monday\n> to Friday between 8:00am and 6:00pm, and Saturday between 9:00am and 1:00pm.\n>\n>\n>\n> Kind regards\n>\n>\n>\n> Daniel\n>\n> Customer Services\n>\n> Web: www.dwrcymru.com\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> We take compliance with Data Protection seriously at Welsh Water.   For\n> more information on how we use your Personal Information please click here\n> for our Privacy Notice www.dwrcymru.com/legal-privacy or call us on 0800\n> 052 0145 to ask us to send you a copy in the post.\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> _______________________________________________________ Dwr Cymru Welsh\n> Water is firmly committed to water conservation and promoting water\n> efficiency. Please log on to our website www.dwrcymru.com/waterefficiency\n> to find out how you can become water wise. Mae Dwr Cymru Welsh Water wedi\n> ymrwymo i warchod adnoddau dwr a hyrwyddo defnydd dwr effeithiol. Mae\n> cyngor i' ch helpu i ddefnyddio dwr yn ddoeth yn\n> www.dwrcymru.com/waterefficiency\n> ________________________________________________________\n> ********************************************************************** This\n> email and any file attached is confidential. If you are not a named\n> recipient or believe you may have received this email in error please\n> delete from your system and promptly inform the sender. Dwr Cymru Cyf\n> (trading as Welsh Water) is a company registered in England and Wales,\n> number 02366777, registered office Linea, Fortran Road, St Mellons,\n> Cardiff [postcode redacted]\n> [map link redacted].\n> Mae’r neges e-bost yma ac unrhyw ffeil sydd ynghlwm wrthi'n gyfrinachol. Os\n> nad chi yw’r derbynnydd a enwir, neu os ydych chi’n credu eich bod wedi\n> derbyn y neges yma ar gam, dylech ei dileu o’ch system ar unwaith a\n> hysbysu’r anfonwr. Cwmni sydd wedi ei gofrestru yng Nghymru yw Dwr Cymru\n> Cyf (yn masnachu fel Dwr Cymru), ei rif cofrestredig yw 02366777, ,, ac mae\n> ei swyddfa gofrestredig yn Linea, Heol Fortran, Llaneirwg, Caerdydd, CF3\n> 0LT. **********************************************************************\n>\n>\n> _____