tps://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0b02f7e35332ab.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19ff079027ee0614-acknowledgement-from-the-culture-communications-cymraeg-and-sport-committee","scopeTopics":["senedd"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["senedd"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19ff079027ee0614","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"senedd","name":"Senedd"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0b02f7e35332ab.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Haidee,\n\nThanks.\n\nI am also asking the Senedd to consider whether the circumstances\nsurrounding the demolition of Great House Farm, Llandough-juxta-Penarth,\nand the subsequent planning and heritage history of the site warrant\nindependent scrutiny and, ultimately, a public inquiry or Senedd committee\ninquiry.\n\nI am not asking the Senedd to determine a private property dispute or to\ndecide legal questions that properly belong before the courts. My concern\nis the conduct, decision-making, record-keeping and accountability of\npublic bodies responsible for heritage protection, planning and local\ngovernment.\n\nThe surviving evidence presents a series of unusually important questions.\n\nGreat House Farm was professionally surveyed by the Royal Commission on the\nAncient and Historical Monuments of Wales in 1974. In December 1988, Cadw\nwas considering the building for emergency spot-listing, with a\ncontemporary report stating that inspectors were due to visit and consider\nthe case. The European Commission of Human Rights records that the\nConvention application had been introduced on 3 May 1988; BP obtained\npossession on 29 November 1988; demolition began on 6 December 1988; and\nthe application was not registered by the Commission until 12 December 1988.\n\nThe evidence therefore establishes a very compressed sequence in which\nemergency heritage protection was being considered, domestic interim relief\nwas refused, possession was obtained and the building was then destroyed\nwithin days.\n\nThat chronology does not, by itself, establish coordination, deliberate\nsuppression or unlawful conduct. It does, however, warrant investigation.\n\nThere are several specific questions which I believe require answers from\nan authority independent of the present local authority:\n\n   1.\n\n   What information and records were actually considered by Cadw when the\n   1988 spot-listing decision was made?\n   2.\n\n   Was the 1974 RCAHMW architectural survey considered, and if not, why not?\n   3.\n\n   What communications took place between Cadw, South Glamorgan County\n   Council, BP Properties Ltd, the courts and any other public bodies during\n   the critical period immediately preceding demolition?\n   4.\n\n   What consideration was given by the local planning authority to the\n   available statutory powers of preservation in circumstances where\n   demolition was imminent?\n   5.\n\n   Why was the building destroyed before there could be an independent\n   retrospective architectural examination of the fabric?\n   6.\n\n   How should the later archaeological evidence, including the discovery of\n   approximately 1,026 burials in 1994, be understood in assessing the\n   historic significance of the site and the adequacy of the earlier\n   decision-making? I recognise that this later discovery cannot\n   retrospectively prove that the 1988 listing decision was unlawful.\n   7.\n\n   A 1989 planning document appears to describe the site as having formerly\n   contained Great House Farm “(a listed building)”. I ask that this apparent\n   discrepancy be investigated and reconciled with the 1988 Cadw decision.\n   8.\n\n   What records relating to these decisions still exist, where are they\n   held, and have reasonable steps been taken to preserve and disclose them?\n\nI am particularly concerned that the destruction of the physical building\nin December 1988 permanently removed primary architectural evidence which\ncould otherwise have been examined independently.\n\nI also ask the Senedd to consider the wider public-interest question:\nwhether the present mechanisms for accountability are adequate where a\nhistoric building is destroyed during a period of rapidly developing legal,\nplanning and heritage proceedings and significant questions remain about\nwhat information was available to decision-makers.\n\nI would therefore ask the Petitions Committee to consider accepting a\npetition on this subject and, where appropriate, referring the matter to\nthe relevant Senedd committee or committees for investigation. I would also\nwelcome consideration of whether an independent public inquiry, committee\ninquiry or other form of external investigation would be appropriate.\n\nI am prepared to provide the underlying documents, chronology, court\nmaterial, heritage records, planning documents and correspondence\nsupporting these questions.\n\nI am not asking the Senedd to adopt my conclusions. I am asking that the\nevidence and unanswered questions be examined independently and that the\nrelevant public bodies be required to account for what they did, what they\nknew, and what records exist.\n\nPlease confirm the appropriate route for submitting this formally to the\nSenedd and whether the material can be referred to the Petitions Committee\nfor consideration.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\nRequester \n\n\n\n\n\nOn Wed, 12 Aug 2026, 10:13 Pwyllgor Diwylliant, Cyfathrebu, y Gymraeg a\nChwaraeon - Culture, Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee,\n<[email redacted]> wrote:\n\n> Dear Requester,\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you for your further correspondence and for clarifying the purpose\n> of your submission.\n>\n>\n>\n> I understand the distinction you draw between seeking a review of the\n> original 1988 decision and raising broader concerns about record-keeping,\n> archival practice, and public accountability. However, the Committee's role\n> is to scrutinise and hold the Welsh Government and associated public bodies\n> to account in relation to policy, governance, and the use of public\n> resources.\n>\n>\n>\n> While I appreciate the broader administrative concerns you have\n> identified, the issues raised in your correspondence arise from a specific\n> historical case. The Committee does not have a role in reviewing or\n> overseeing day-to-day operational decisions taken in individual cases.\n> Accordingly, the Committee is unable to take forward the matters raised in\n> your correspondence.\n>\n>\n>\n> If you wish to pursue concerns regarding the management, retention or\n> preservation of historical records, these matters are most appropriately\n> raised with the relevant public authority or archival body responsible for\n> those records. Similarly, concerns regarding the administration of a public\n> body should be pursued through that body's complaints procedure and, where\n> appropriate, with the Public Services Ombudsman for Wales.\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you for taking the time to write to the Committee and to set out\n> your concerns in detail.\n>\n>\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n>\n>\n> Haidee\n>\n>\n>\n> *Haidee James*\n>\n> *Ail Glerc, Pwyllgor Diwylliant, Cyfathrebu, y Gymraeg a Chwaraeon,*\n> Senedd Cymru\n>\n> *Second Clerk, Culture, Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee,*\n> Welsh Parliament\n>\n> [phone redacted] *Rhagenwau|Pronouns:* hi – she/her\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> *From:* Requester <[email redacted]>\n> *Sent:* 11 August 2026 16:57\n> *To:* Pwyllgor Diwylliant, Cyfathrebu, y Gymraeg a Chwaraeon - Culture,\n> Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee <[email redacted]\n> >\n> *Subject:* Re: Acknowledgement from the Culture, Communications, Cymraeg\n> and Sport Committee\n>\n>\n>\n> Dear Ms James,\n>\n> Thank you for your considered response.\n>\n> I understand and accept that the Committee does not have a remit to\n> adjudicate upon an individual dispute, determine whether a historical\n> decision was lawful, or direct Cadw to reopen a particular case. My\n> submission was deliberately framed so as not to ask the Committee to do any\n> of those things.\n>\n> I would, however, respectfully clarify one point, because I am concerned\n> that the nature of the request may have been characterised more narrowly\n> than intended.\n>\n> The attached report expressly states that it is *not a legal challenge, a\n> forensic proof of wrongdoing, a complaint seeking adjudication, or a demand\n> for reversal of the 1988 decision*. Its stated purpose is historical\n> record correction, archival explanation and administrative acknowledgement.\n> In particular, it asks for an explanation of why almost none of the records\n> concerning the 1988 assessment survive, whether that reflects the\n> applicable record-retention arrangements, and whether the surviving\n> independent evidence should be properly reflected in the permanent public\n> record.\n>\n> The central issue therefore extends beyond the merits of the individual\n> 1988 listing assessment.\n>\n> Cadw's 2026 FOI response confirms that an assessment took place, but that\n> only two exterior photographs now survive; the assessment report, reasons\n> for refusal, site notes, internal recommendations and consultation\n> correspondence are no longer available. The report does not assert that\n> this proves wrongdoing. Rather, it identifies a straightforward\n> public-administration question: *how can the adequacy and evidential\n> basis of an irreversible public heritage decision be understood or\n> scrutinised when the underlying administrative record no longer survives?*\n>\n> That seems potentially relevant to the Committee's stated function of\n> scrutinising the administration and accountability of Welsh Government and\n> associated public bodies, even if the Committee cannot itself determine\n> whether the 1988 decision was correct.\n>\n> I am therefore not asking the Committee to investigate the Buckler\n> family's historical dispute with BP, adjudicate upon the 1988 decision, or\n> direct Cadw to reverse anything. The specific administrative questions\n> raised in the report are instead:\n>\n>    1. What record-retention arrangements applied to the 1988 assessment?\n>    2. Why do the assessment report, reasons, site notes and consultation\n>    records no longer survive?\n>    3. Were the records destroyed under an applicable retention policy or\n>    other authority, and were they considered for transfer to the appropriate\n>    archival institution?\n>    4. Is the present position consistent with Welsh Government/Cadw\n>    expectations for the preservation of records concerning significant\n>    heritage decisions?\n>    5. Are current arrangements sufficient to ensure that comparable\n>    heritage assessments, particularly where a building is under imminent\n>    threat of demolition, are properly documented and retained?\n>\n> These are not questions that require the Committee to determine the rights\n> of either party to the original dispute. They concern *administrative\n> practice, institutional record-keeping and the ability of the Senedd and\n> the public to scrutinise decisions taken by public bodies*. The report\n> specifically asks Cadw to explain the archival position and review its\n> current record-keeping practices for this reason.\n>\n> I would therefore be grateful if the Committee could reconsider whether\n> the broader administrative and accountability issue falls within its\n> scrutiny function, even if the Committee cannot examine the underlying\n> individual dispute.\n>\n> If the Committee considers that it nevertheless cannot engage with the\n> matter, I would appreciate clarification as to *which Senedd or Welsh\n> Government scrutiny, oversight, archival or complaints mechanism is\n> considered appropriate for raising a documented concern about the absence\n> of the administrative record of a historical Cadw decision and the\n> implications this has for public accountability*.\n>\n> For completeness, I should also emphasise that the report itself\n> deliberately recognises the limits of the surviving evidence. It does not\n> claim that Cadw acted unlawfully or in bad faith, and it expressly\n> acknowledges that the absence of the 1988 working papers means that the\n> assessment cannot now be reconstructed definitively.\n>\n> My objective is therefore quite limited: *to ensure that the surviving\n> evidence is preserved, that the archival gap is properly explained, and\n> that the public record accurately distinguishes what is established from\n> what can no longer be verified.*\n>\n> I hope that clarification assists, and I would be grateful if you could\n> confirm whether the Committee is able to consider the matter from that\n> wider public-administration and accountability perspective.\n>\n> Kind regards,\n>\n> Requester\n> \n>\n>\n>\n> On Tue, 11 Aug 2026, 07:58 Pwyllgor Diwylliant, Cyfathrebu, y Gymraeg a\n> Chwaraeon - Culture, Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee, <\n> [email redacted]> wrote:\n>\n> Dear Requester,\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you for your correspondence and for sharing the forensic review of\n> Cadw's 1988 assessment of Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough. I\n> acknowledge the strength of feeling expressed in your letter and recognise\n> the longstanding concerns that you and your family have raised.\n>\n>\n>\n> The Committee’s role is to scrutinise the work of the Welsh Government and\n> associated public bodies, including holding ministers and public\n> authorities to account for their policies, administration and use of public\n> resources. However, the Committee does not have a remit to investigate,\n> adjudicate upon, or oversee individual complaints or dispute. Nor does it\n> have the powers to reopen historical decisions or direct public bodies to\n> undertake investigations into specific matters.\n>\n>\n>\n> The Committee is therefore unable to take on an oversight role in relation\n> to the issues you raise or to intervene in these matters.\n>\n>\n>\n> The matters you raise appear principally to concern the actions and\n> decision-making processes of Cadw in relation to a specific historical\n> assessment. Cadw are responsible for responding to requests for information\n> about their records and decision-making processes, and for considering any\n> representations made regarding the accuracy or completeness of the\n> historical record.\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you again for writing to the Committee.\n>\n>\n>\n> Kind regards,\n>\n>\n>\n> Haidee\n>\n>\n>\n> *Haidee James*\n>\n> *Ail Glerc, Pwyllgor Diwylliant, Cyfathrebu, y Gymraeg a Chwaraeon,*\n> Senedd Cymru\n>\n> *Second Clerk, Culture, Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee,*\n> Welsh Parliament\n>\n> [phone redacted] *Rhagenwau|Pronouns:* hi – she/her\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> *From:* Requester <[email redacted]>\n> *Sent:* 10 August 2026 22:46\n> *To:* [email redacted]\n> *Cc:* [email redacted]; [email redacted]; DOUGHTY,\n> Stephen <[email redacted]>\n> *Subject:* Re: CADW failures – Revised Report (10 August 2026)\n>\n>\n>\n> Sirs/Ma'ams,\n>\n> I write further to my email of 9 August 2026 submitting a review\n> concerning Cadw’s 1988 assessment of Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough.\n>\n> Please find attached (and linked below) a revised edition of the report,\n> dated 10 August 2026. This version has been carefully restructured to\n> present the same evidence in a more measured and administratively focused\n> form. It is offered as a request for historical record correction, archival\n> explanation, and administrative acknowledgement rather than as a forensic\n> challenge.\n>\n> The revised document:\n>\n> • Clearly states what it is and is not;\n> • Sets out the surviving independent evidence (principally the H.J. Thomas\n> RCAHMW survey of 1974);\n> • Notes the near-total absence of Cadw’s 1988 working papers confirmed in\n> ATISN 27021;\n> • Asks only for realistic, achievable administrative steps: explanation of\n> the archival position, reflection of the surviving evidence in the Historic\n> Environment Record, acknowledgement of the records gap, and consideration\n> of a local heritage marker.\n>\n> It does not claim unlawfulness, seek compensation from Cadw, or demand a\n> public inquiry.\n>\n> I hope this revised presentation is more palatable and will assist the\n> relevant bodies in providing a constructive response.\n>\n> I look forward to your substantive response.\n>\n> Regards,\n>\n> Requester\n> \n> [email redacted]\n>\n> [phone redacted]\n>\n>"},{"id":"gm-1a0aff0b9f844710","subject":"New response to your FOI request - BP vs Buckler 1987","name":"New response to your FOI request - BP vs Buckler 1987","date":"2026-09-17T15:16:23Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0aff0b9f844710.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0aff0b9f844710-new-response-to-your-foi-request-bp-vs-buckler-1987","scopeTopics":["nactso"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["nactso"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0aff0b9f844710","direction":"received","from":"(name not shown; whatdotheyknow.com)","institutions":[{"id":"nactso","name":"Counter Terrorism Policing (NaCTSO)"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0aff0b9f844710.json","textStatus":"available","text":"You have a new response to the Freedom of Information request 'BP vs Buckler 1987' that you made to National Counter Terrorism Security Office.\n\nTo view the response, click on the link below.\n\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/request/bp_vs_buckler_1987_1988?nocache=incoming-3558044#incoming-3558044\n\nWhen you get there, please update the status to say if the response contains any useful information.\n\nAlthough all responses are automatically published, we depend on you, the original requester, to evaluate them.\n\n-- the WhatDoTheyKnow team\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nWhatDoTheyKnow is a project of mySociety run by a small team of staff and\ndedicated volunteers.\n\nmySociety is a charity that believes that information should be accessible to everyone.\nIf this is important to you, please consider donating to help us do more:\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/donate\n\nFind out more about how we handle your data at:\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/help/privacy\n\nmySociety is a registered charity in England and Wales (1076346), and a limited\ncompany (03277032)"},{"id":"gm-1a0afe15770e3994","subject":"Welsh Water - Mail reference: 93027237/01","name":"Welsh Water - Mail reference: 93027237/01","date":"2026-09-17T14:59:29Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0afe15770e3994.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0afe15770e3994-welsh-water-mail-reference-93027237-01","scopeTopics":["dwrcymru"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["dwrcymru"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0afe15770e3994","direction":"received","from":"CustomerCareTeam","institutions":[{"id":"dwrcymru","name":"Dwr Cymru Welsh Water"}],"refs":["93027237/01"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0afe15770e3994.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Mail reference: 93027237/01\n\nYour emails of: 3rd September 2026\n\n\nHello Mr Buckler,\n\n\nThank you for your email.\n\nI appreciate that you are seeking further clarification regarding the records associated with Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough, and whether any evidence exists to demonstrate what may have happened to historical records relating to the property.\n\nAs outlined in our previous correspondence, we have undertaken searches using the information you have provided. Despite these searches, we have been unable to locate any records relating to either the property or yourself on our current systems. Based on the information available, our position remains that we do not hold any records relating to Great House Farm (Ty Mawr).\n\nYou have asked whether records may have existed historically and whether we can provide evidence showing if and when such records were deleted. Unfortunately, as no records relating to the property have been located, we do not hold any corresponding account information, customer records, deletion logs, or audit records that would enable us to confirm whether particular documents once existed or the circumstances in which they may have been removed.\n\nAs previously explained, our enquiries indicate that Great House Farm was demolished during the 1980s. Any customer or property records from that period would fall well outside our applicable retention periods and, consequently, would not be expected to remain available today. Furthermore, during the migration to our current operating systems, not all historic information was transferred from legacy systems. As a result, we are unable to access or recover records that are no longer held.\n\nWhilst I appreciate that this may be frustrating, the absence of records means we are unable to verify whether specific documents once existed, whether they were subsequently removed in accordance with retention requirements, or provide documentary evidence of their deletion. We can only disclose information that is currently held by the company and, following the searches completed, no information relating to the property has been identified.\n\nFor clarity, our position is not that records have been located and are being withheld. Rather, despite conducting further searches using the information available, we have been unable to identify any records relating to the property within the scope of your request.\n\nConsequently, there is no further information that we are able to provide in relation to Great House Farm (Ty Mawr).\n\nOutcome\n\nI appreciate that this may not be the outcome you were hoping for. However, our position remains unchanged, as we do not hold any information relating to the property and are therefore unable to provide any further details regarding its historical records.\n\nLastly, using the information you have provided, my colleague arranged for the £20.00 GSS payment to be issued to your address at [address redacted], [postcode redacted]. The payment should be delivered within 7 days.\n\nIf you have any questions or concerns, please do not hesitate to get in touch by replying to this email, or by calling Customer Care on [phone redacted], available Monday to Friday between 9:00am and 4:00pm. Alternatively, you can contact our Billing Department on [phone redacted], available Monday to Friday between 8:00am and 6:00pm, and Saturday between 9:00am and 1:00pm.\n\nKind regards\n\nDaniel\nCustomer Services\nWeb: www.dwrcymru.com<http://www.dwrcymru.com/>\n\n[cid:[email redacted]71D50]\n\nWe take compliance with Data Protection seriously at Welsh Water.   For more information on how we use your Personal Information please click here for our Privacy Notice www.dwrcymru.com/legal-privacy<http://www.dwrcymru.com/legal-privacy> or call us on [phone redacted] to ask us to send you a copy in the post.\n\n\n_______________________________________________________ Dwr Cymru Welsh Water is firmly committed to water conservation and promoting 