NFORMATION ACT 2000/ENVIRONMENTAL INFORMATION REGULATIONS 2004 - INFORMATION REQUEST\n\nThank you for your request for information about: Barry Preservation Society / the Civic Trust\n\nYour request was received on 17/09/2026 and is being considered under the terms of the Freedom of Information Act 2000 and the Environmental Information Regulations 2004. Your query has been given the reference 00211463.\n\nIn some circumstances a fee may be payable and if that is the case we will let you know. A fees notice will be issued to you, and you will be required to pay before we will proceed to deal with your request.\n\n\n\n\nYou should receive the information requested within 20 working days unless the Council does not hold the information or there is a reason for it to be withheld. We will write to you in any event.\n\nIf you have any requirements regarding the format any information should be supplied in, e.g. the language to be used, audio, large print and so on, then if you have not already done so please let us know. \n\nIf you have any queries or concerns then please do not hesitate to contact us. Please remember to quote the reference number above in any future communications.\n\n\n\nFurther information about your rights is also available from the Information\nCommissioner at:\n\n\nInformation Commissioner's Office - Wales\n\nSecond Floor, Churchill House\n\nChurchill Way,\n\nCardiff\n\n[postcode redacted]\n\n[phone redacted] \n\n[email redacted] \n\n\n\nYours sincerely,\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nInformation Governance Officer/ Swyddog Llywodraethu Gwybodaeth\n\nDirector's Office - Resources / Swyddfa'r Cyfarwyddwr - Adnoddau\n\nVale of Glamorgan Council / Cyngor Bro Morgannwg\n\ntel / ffôn: [phone redacted]\n\ne-mail / e-bost: [email redacted] or [email redacted]\n\n \n\nConsider the environment. Please don't print this e-mail unless you really need to.\n\nYstyriwch yr amgylchedd. Peidiwch ag argraffu'r neges hon oni bai fod gwir angen.\n\n \n\nVisit our Website at www.valeofglamorgan.gov.uk\n\nEwch i'n gwefan yn www.bromorgannwg.gov.uk\n\n \n\nFind us on Facebook / Cewch ddod o hyd i ni ar Facebook\n\nFollow us on Twitter /Dilynwch ni ar Twitter\n\n \n\nCorrespondence is welcomed in Welsh or English / Croesewir Gohebiaeth yn y Gymraeg neu yn Saesneg."},{"id":"gm-1a0aea300b975c27","subject":"RE: Great House Farm, Llandough","name":"RE: Great House Farm, Llandough","date":"2026-09-17T09:10:29Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0aea300b975c27.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0ad615441a4131-great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":["rcahmw"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["rcahmw"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0ad615441a4131","direction":"received","from":"NMR Enquiries","institutions":[{"id":"rcahmw","name":"RCAHMW (Royal Commission)"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0aea300b975c27.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Diolch am eich ymholiad. Byddwn yn ceisio ymateb cyn pen 15 diwrnod gwaith. Rydym wedi gohirio ein gwasanaeth chwilio a blaenoriaeth ar hyn o bryd oherwydd prinder staff. Ymddiheuriadau am unrhyw anghyfleustra a achosir.\n\nYn y cyfamser, gallai’r wybodaeth isod fod yn ddefnyddiol i chi:\n\nI gael gwybodaeth am safleoedd hanesyddol yng Nghymru a chael manylion am yr hyn sydd yn ein harchifau, ewch i’n cronfa ddata a’n catalog, https://coflein.gov.uk/cy/\n\nOs yn bosibl, rydym yn argymell eich bod yn trefnu apwyntiad i ymweld â’n llyfrgell ac ystafell chwilio<https://cbhc.gov.uk/gwasanaethau/> ein harchif, os hoffech weld deunydd yr archif â’ch llygaid eich hun a thynnu lluniau heb orfod talu amdanynt. Yn gyffredinol, mae gwneud hynny’n gynt ac yn rhatach o lawer nag archebu sganiau o bell.\n\nMae llawer o’n delweddau digidol ar gael i’w lawrlwytho, eu prynu a’u trwyddedu yn syth o Coflein. Mae canllawiau ynghylch sut mae gwneud hynny i’w gweld yn y fideo byr hwn: Lawrlwytho delweddau o Coflein<https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JYJmCNrRFqY> Os nad yw’r ddelwedd yr ydych yn chwilio amdani ar gael drwy’r system hon, y rheswm am hynny’n aml fydd cyfyngiadau hawlfraint, ac efallai y byddwch am chwilio am ddelwedd amgen sydd ar gael, os yw hynny’n bosibl.\n\n\nThank you for your enquiry. We will endeavour to respond within 15 working days. Please note, we have currently suspended our Priority Enquiry Service due to staff shortage. We apologise for any inconvenience caused.\n\nIn the meantime, you may find the information below helpful:\n\nFor information on historic sites in Wales and details of archive holdings see our database and catalogue, https://coflein.gov.uk/en/\n\nWhere possible we recommend that you make an appointment to visit our archive search room and library<https://rcahmw.gov.uk/services/> to view archive material in person and take photos without charge.  This is generally much quicker and cheaper than ordering scans remotely.\n\nMany of our digital images are available to download, purchase and licence directly from Coflein.  Guidance on how to do this can be seen in this short video: Downloading images from Coflein (youtube.com)<https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gKpL61F1M2w&t=3s>  If the image which you want is not available via this system, this is often due to copyright restrictions and you may wish to identify an alternative image which is available, if possible.\n\n\n\nNMR Enquiries\nYmholiadau Cyhoeddus I Public Enquiries\nFfordd Penglais | Penglais Road, Aberystwyth, [postcode redacted]\n[phone redacted]\n[email redacted] | [email redacted]\nwww.cbhc.gov.uk | www.rcahmw.gov.uk\nNoddir gan Lywodraeth Cymru | Sponsored by Welsh Government\nRydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg a Saesneg. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn peri oedi.\nWe welcome correspondence in Welsh and English. Corresponding in Welsh will not lead to any delay.\n\nYn unol â Rheoliadau Safonau'r Gymraeg (Rhif 2) 2016, mae gennych hawl i gyfathrebu a gohebu â Chomisiwn Brenhinol Henebion Cymru yn eich dewis iaith. Er mwyn sicrhau ein bod yn cyflawni’r hawl, rhowch wybod i ni a ydych yn dymuno derbyn gohebiaeth a/neu alwadau ffôn oddi wrthym yn y Gymraeg. Bydd yr wybodaeth hon yn cael ei chofnodi gennym ni, a byddwn yn defnyddio'r iaith o’ch dewis ym mhob cyfathrebu yn y dyfodol. Diolch.\nUnder the Welsh Language Standards (No. 2) Regulations 2016, you have the right to communicate and correspond with the Royal Commission on the Ancient and Historical Monuments of Wales in your preferred language. To ensure we uphold this right, please let us know whether you wish to receive correspondence and/or telephone calls from us in Welsh. This information will be recorded by us, and we will use your preferred language in all future communication. Thank you.\n​\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]>\nSent: 17 September 2026 04:24\nTo: NMR Enquiries <[email redacted]>\nSubject: Great House Farm, Llandough\n\n\nRoyal Commission,\n\nI am researching the demolition of Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough-juxta-Penarth, in December 1988 (HER reference GGAT02038s / ST 1681 7331).\n\nI am seeking to establish whether the Royal Commission was consulted or notified before the farmhouse was demolished.\n\nContemporary reports from 3 December 1988 state that Cadw was considering emergency protection/listing of Great House Farm and that a Cadw inspection was being arranged, including discussion with the Royal Commission for Ancient and Historical Monuments.\n\nThe farmhouse was then demolished on 6 December 1988.\n\nI understand that the statutory framework at the time included provisions concerning the recording of historic buildings before authorised demolition. I would therefore be grateful if you could check your records and confirm:\n\n  1.  Whether the Royal Commission received any notification, consultation, referral or request for advice concerning Great House Farm before its demolition in December 1988.\n  2.  Whether the Royal Commission was asked to inspect, photograph, record or otherwise document the building before demolition.\n  3.  Whether any correspondence exists between the Royal Commission, Cadw, the local planning authority, BP Properties Ltd, or their representatives concerning the proposed demolition.\n  4.  Whether the Royal Commission was given an opportunity to complete or supplement its earlier recording of the farmhouse before demolition.\n  5.  Whether your records contain any photographs, measured drawings, reports, field notes or other documentation relating specifically to the farmhouse immediately before its demolition.\n  6.  If records exist, I would be grateful for the relevant reference numbers and, where possible, copies.\n\nFor context, the Royal Commission had already recorded Great House Farm architecturally in 1974. Contemporary material indicates that the building was subsequently under consideration for emergency listing shortly before its destruction.\n\nThere is also an important chronology concerning the occupation and possession of the property: my late father William Buckler had lodged European Commission of Human Rights application 14464/88 on 3 May 1988; BP obtained possession on 29 November 1988; demolition began on 6 December 1988; and the Strasbourg application was registered on 12 December 1988.\n\nI am not asking the Royal Commission to express an opinion on the ownership dispute or the legality of the demolition. I am simply seeking to establish, from your records, whether the Commission was notified or consulted and what opportunity, if any, it was given to record the building (our home since 1667) before it was destroyed?\n\nGiven the historical and archaeological significance subsequently identified at the site, I would particularly appreciate confirmation of whether any pre-demolition recording was requested or undertaken.\n\nRegards\n[cid:image001.jpg@01DD468C.C3547200]\n\n,\n\n\n\nRequester \n\n"},{"id":"gm-1a0ae09b93ceae38","subject":"Further to Your Enquiry: Ref: 260917-6714796 Title No: WA231076","name":"Further to Your Enquiry: Ref: 260917-6714796 Title No: WA231076","date":"2026-09-17T06:24:26Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0ae09b93ceae38.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0ae09b93ceae38-further-to-your-enquiry-ref-260917-6714796-title-no-wa231076","scopeTopics":["hmlr"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["hmlr"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0ae09b93ceae38","direction":"received","from":"_Central","institutions":[{"id":"hmlr","name":"HM Land Registry"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0ae09b93ceae38.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Enquiry Reference: 260917-6714796 Title Number: WA231076\n\nDear Customer\n\nI am sorry that the wrong contact information was shown on our letter to you. We are directing our customers to contact us via our contact form .\n\n\n\nBackground:\n\nHM Land Registry has created many email addresses over the years. We can no longer service the incoming emails or route such emails into our current system.\n\n\n\nPlanned system improvements:\n\nWe plan to remove all reference to individual HM Land Registry email addresses. This process was more complex than expected and some templates still need updating.\n\n\n\nWorking with our Customers:\n\nWe want to ensure that correspondence goes direct to our customer service team. To do this, we now ask customers to use our online contact form. This is why we put an auto-response in place.\n\n\n\n\n\nYours faithfully\n\nLaura Cotton\nHM Land Registry\n\nWe only provide factual information and impartial advice about our procedures. Read more about the advice we give. \n\nEasy and free support to help you work with HM Land Registry:\nGuidance for members of the public - GOV.UK \nGuidance for property professionals - GOV.UK \n\n\n\nHM Land Registry's purpose is to protect your land ownership and provide services and data that underpin an efficient and informed property market. Our vision is: \"A world-leading property market as part of a thriving economy and a sustainable future\".\n\nWe check all mail and attachments for known viruses. However, you are advised that you open any attachments at your own risk. If you have received this email and it was not intended for you, please let us know, then delete it.\n\nWe welcome correspondence in English and Welsh.\n\nTo see how HM Land Registry treats your personal information, read our Personal information charter: www.gov.uk/government/organisations/land-registry/about/personal-information-charter."},{"id":"gm-1a0ae07c905b2060","subject":"Further to Your Enquiry: Ref: 260917-6714794 Title No: WA231076","name":"Further to Your Enquiry: Ref: 260917-6714794 Title No: WA231076","date":"2026-09-17T06:22:17Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0ae07c905b2060.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0ae07c905b2060-further-to-your-enquiry-ref-260917-6714794-title-no-wa231076","scopeTopics":["hmlr"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["hmlr"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0ae07c905b2060","direction":"received","from":"_Central","institutions":[{"id":"hmlr","name":"HM Land Registry"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0ae07c905b2060.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Enquiry Reference: 260917-6714794 Title Number: WA231076\n\nDear Customer\n\nI am sorry that the wrong contact information was shown on our letter to you. We are directing our customers to contact us via our contact form .\n\n\n\nBackground:\n\nHM Land Registry has created many email addresses over the years. We can no longer service the incoming emails or route such emails into our current system.\n\n\n\nPlanned system improvements:\n\nWe plan to remove all reference to individual HM Land Registry email addresses. This process was more complex than expected and some templates still need updating.\n\n\n\nWorking with our Customers:\n\nWe want to ensure that correspondence goes direct to our customer service team. To do this, we now ask customers to use our online contact form. This is why we put an auto-response in place.\n\n\n\n\n\nYours faithfully\n\nLaura Cotton\nHM Land Registry\n\nWe only provide factual information and impartial advice about our procedures. Read more about the advice we give. \n\nEasy and free support to help you work with HM Land Registry:\nGuidance for members of the public - GOV.UK \nGuidance for property professionals - GOV.UK \n\n\n\nHM Land Registry's purpose is to protect your land ownership and provide services and data that underpin an efficient and informed property market. Our vision is: \"A world-leading property market as part of a thriving economy and a sustainable future\".\n\nWe check all mail and attachments for known viruses. However, you are advised that you open any attachments at your own risk. If you have received this email and it was not intended for you, please let us know, then delete it.\n\nWe welcome correspondence in English and Welsh.\n\nTo see how HM Land Registry treats your personal information, read our Personal information charter: www.gov.uk/government/organisations/land-registry/about/personal-information-charter."},{"id":"gm-1a0ad9704a203b54","subject":"Great House Farm, Llandough","name":"Great House Farm, Llandough","date":"2026-09-17T04:19:10Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0ad9704a203b54.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0ad93b4ce3942b-great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":["vale-foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["vale-foi"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0ad93b4ce3942b","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"vale-foi","name":"Vale of Glamorgan Council"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0ad9704a203b54.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Customer Relations / Planning Complaints Officer,\n\nI wish to make a formal complaint concerning the Vale of Glamorgan\nCouncil's Dear Customer Relations / Planning Complaints Officer,\n\nI wish to make a formal complaint concerning the Vale of Glamorgan\nCouncil's handling of Great House Farm, Llandough-juxta-Penarth,\nparticularly the circumstances surrounding its demolition in December 1988\nand the subsequent planning process.\n\n\nPlease register this as a formal complaint under the Council's corporate\ncomplaints procedure and provide me with a complaint reference number.\n\n\nThis complaint concerns the Council's administration and exercise of its\nplanning and heritage responsibilities. It is not intended to constitute an\nappeal against a properly made historic planning decision.\n\n\n1. Great House Farm, Llandough (Cardiff not Cowbridge) was recognised as a\nbuilding of historic importance\n\n\nThe planning and archaeological documentation concerning the site\nidentifies Great House Farm as a building of heritage significance.\n\n\nIn particular, the archaeological material identifies the former building\nas a \"county treasure\" associated with SMR 2038s, while earlier\narchitectural recording by RCAHMW describes a substantial historic\nfarmhouse with surviving historic fabric.\n\n\nThe later 1990 archaeological assessment states:\n\n\n«\"Owing to an ownership dispute and the subsequent sudden destruction of\nthe property in 1988 the building was incompletely understood.\"»\n\n\nI regard that statement as highly significant because it records,\ncontemporaneously with the subsequent planning process, that the building\nhad been destroyed suddenly and that its archaeological and architectural\nhistory had therefore not been adequately understood or recorded.\n\n\n2. The Council's apparent failure to take protective action before the 1988\ndemolition\n\n\nI ask the Council to investigate what action, if any, its planning and\nheritage officers took when Great House Farm was at risk of demolition.\n\n\nIn particular, I ask the Council to establish:\n\n\n1. Whether the Council knew that Great House Farm was at risk of demolition\nbefore December 1988.\n\n2. Whether the Council received any notification, complaint, application,\ncorrespondence or other warning concerning the proposed demolition.\n\n3. Whether the building had been considered for statutory listing or other\nstatutory protection.\n\n4. Whether consideration was given to the use of a Building Preservation\nNotice or any other available statutory or administrative mechanism capable\nof preventing or delaying demolition pending assessment.\n\n5. Whether the Council's heritage officers, planning officers or\narchaeological advisers were consulted.\n\n6. Whether any inspection or photographic/architectural record was\nundertaken immediately before or after the demolition.\n\n7. Why, given the building's recognised historic significance, the Council\ndid not secure a comprehensive standing-building record before the building\nwas lost.\n\n\nI am not asserting at this stage that a particular statutory power\nnecessarily had to be exercised. I am asking the Council to investigate\nwhat powers and procedures were available in 1988 and why they were, or\nwere not, used in this case.\n\n\n3. The \"sudden destruction\" of the building requires investigation\n\n\nThe 1990 archaeological assessment expressly attributes the incomplete\nunderstanding of the building to its \"subsequent sudden destruction ... in\n1988\".\n\n\nI therefore ask the Council to establish from its records:\n\n\n- the date on which it first became aware of the proposed or actual\ndemolition;\n\n- the identity of the relevant officers;\n\n- whether the Council received complaints or reports concerning demolition\nactivity;\n\n- whether any site visit took place;\n\n- whether any notice, warning, enforcement action, preservation action or\nother intervention was considered;\n\n- and whether the Council has retained records explaining why the\ndemolition was allowed to proceed.\n\n\nThis is particularly important because the building was subsequently\ntreated within the planning process as an already-cleared site.\n\n\n4. The 1989 planning application appears to contain a significant\nhistorical discrepancy\n\n\nThe outline planning application submitted by BP Properties Ltd in 1989\nstates, in the section asking which buildings were to be demolished:\n\n\n«\"None.\"»\n\n\nThe same application describes the site as \"Disused\" and the previous use\nas \"Not known\".\n\n\nThis requires explanation because the application followed the demolition\nof Great House Farm in December 1988 by approximately one year.\n\n\nI ask the Council to investigate whether its planning officers:\n\n\n- were aware of the former Great House Farm building when the 1989\napplication was processed;\n\n- knew when and how it had been demolished;\n\n- checked the accuracy of the applicant's statement that no buildings were\nto be demolished;\n\n- identified the former farmhouse in the planning history and supporting\ndocumentation;\n\n- and considered whether the demolition itself raised any planning,\nheritage or enforcement issue requiring investigation.\n\n\nI am particularly concerned that the planning process appears to have\nproceeded on the basis of a cleared site without first establishing the\ncircumstances in which the historic building had disappeared.\n\n\n5. Failure to create an adequate record of the lost building\n\n\nThe subsequent archaeological documentation states that the building was\n\"incompletely understood\".\n\n\nI ask the Council to explain what mitigation, recording or investigation it\nrequired in consequence.\n\n\nThe documentary evidence presently available indicates that substantial\nhistoric fabric and multiple phases of the building existed, yet the\nbuilding was destroyed without a complete standing-building survey having\nbeen secured.\n\n\nI therefore ask the Council to identify:\n\n\n- all surveys undertaken before demolition;\n\n- all surveys undertaken after demolition;\n\n- all archaeological or architectural advice obtained by the Council;\n\n- all photographs held by the Council;\n\n- all correspondence concerning the building's architectural or historic\nimportance;\n\n- and the Council's policy or practice in 1988 concerning the recording of\nsignificant unlisted historic buildings at risk.\n\n\n6. Subsequent planning treatment of the site\n\n\nFollowing demolition, the planning process concentrated heavily on\nbelow-ground archaeology, ultimately resulting in archaeological\ninvestigation of the cemetery and associated remains.\n\n\nI do not suggest that archaeological excavation was inappropriate. My\nconcern is that the destruction of the standing historic building appears\nto have been treated as a fait accompli rather than as an event requiring\naccountability and an explanation of how the Council's heritage\nresponsibilities had been discharged.\n\n\nThe later planning documentation should therefore be reviewed alongside the\nrecords relating to the 1988 demolition.\n\n\nIn particular, I ask the Council to identify precisely when the planning\nauthority first recorded the former farmhouse as demolished and what\nexplanation was placed on the planning file for its loss.\n\n\n7. Matters I ask the Council to investigate\n\n\nPlease investigate and provide a substantive response to the following\nquestions:\n\n\nA. What did Vale of Glamorgan Council know about the impending demolition\nof Great House Farm before December 1988?\n\n\nB. What statutory and non-statutory heritage protections were available to\nthe Council at the time?\n\n\nC. Were any of those mechanisms considered or discussed?\n\n\nD. Were any Building Preservation Notice, listing, enforcement, urgent\nworks, archaeological recording or other protective measures considered?\n\n\nE. Why was the building apparently destroyed without a comprehensive\nstanding-building survey?\n\n\nF. What records does the Council hold concerning the demolition itself?\n\n\nG. Why did the 1989 planning application proceed despite the recent\ndemolition of a building identified in the archaeological record as a\n\"county treasure\"?\n\n\nH. Why was the applicant's answer \"None\" given to the question concerning\nbuildings to be demolished, and did the Council investigate the apparent\ndiscrepancy?\n\n\nI. Did any Council officer consider whether the 1988 demolition constituted\na breach of planning control or otherwise required investigation?\n\n\nJ. Did the Council investigate whether the building had been demolished\nlawfully?\n\n\nK. What heritage assessment was undertaken before the subsequent\ndevelopment permissions were processed?\n\n\nL. What lessons has the Council subsequently incorporated into its\nprocedures for County Treasures and other locally significant historic\nbuildings?\n\n\n8. Records requested as part of the investigation\n\n\nPlease preserve and review all relevant records, including planning files,\nenforcement records, heritage files, committee papers, officer reports,\ninternal correspondence, site inspection notes, photographs, archaeological\ncorrespondence and consultation records concerning:\n\n\n- Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr / Cedfin / Cydfin;\n\n- SMR/HER reference 2038s / GGAT02038s;\n\n- the December 1988 demolition;\n\n- the 1989 outline planning application;\n\n- the 1990 archaeological assessment;\n\n- and the subsequent reserved matters/development applications.\n\n\nI would also ask that the Council identify any material which has been\ndestroyed or cannot now be located, together with the applicable\nretention/disposal record where one exists.\n\n\n9. Outcome sought\n\n\nI am seeking a formal investigation and a reasoned written explanation of\nthe Council's actions and omissions.\n\n\nIn particular, I ask the Council to determine:\n\n\n1. Whether its officers discharged the heritage and planning\nresponsibilities applicable at the time.\n\n2. Whether any failure occurred in relation to the identification,\nprotection, recording or investigation of Great House Farm.\n\n3. Whether the Council's handling of the 1989 planning application\nappropriately addressed the recent demolition of the building.\n\n4. Whether any relevant records have been lost or destroyed and, if so,\nwhen and under what retention arrangements.\n\n5. What procedural lessons arise from the case.\n\n\nI also request disclosure of the relevant non-exempt records identified\nduring the complaint investigation, where these are not otherwise\navailable. I recognise that requests for specific recorded information may\nneed to be dealt with separately under the appropriate information-access\nregime.\n\n\nI have only recently been able to examine and correlate the surviving\nplanning, archaeological and heritage documentation sufficiently to\nidentify these issues. I therefore ask that the Council accepts the\ncomplaint for investigation notwithstanding the age of the events concerned.\n\n\nPlease acknowledge receipt and confirm the complaint reference and the\nofficer responsible for its investigation.\n\n\nYours faithfully,\n\n\nRequester of Great House Farm, Llandough-juxta-Penarth, particularly the\ncircumstances surrounding its demolition in December 1988 and the\nsubsequent planning process.\n\nPlease register this as a formal complaint under the Council's corporate\ncomplaints procedure and provide me with a complaint reference number.\n\nThis complaint concerns the Council's administration and exercise of its\nplanning and heritage responsibilities. It is not intended to constitute an\nappeal against a properly made historic planning decision.\n1. Great House Farm was recognised as a building of historic importance\n\nThe planning and archaeological documentation concerning the site\nidentifies Great House Farm as a building of heritage significance.\n\nIn particular, the archaeological material identifies the former building\nas a \"county treasure\" associated with SMR 2038s, while earlier\narchitectural recording by RCAHMW describes a substantial historic\nfarmhouse with surviving historic fabric.\n\nThe later 1990 archaeological assessment states:\n\n\"Owing to an ownership dispute and the subsequent sudden destruction of the\nproperty in 1988 the building was incompletely understood.\"\n\nI regard that statement as highly significant because it records,\ncontemporaneously with the subsequent planning process, that the building\nhad been destroyed suddenly and that its archaeological and architectural\nhistory had therefore not been adequately understood or recorded.\n2. The Council's apparent failure to take protective acti