 and can be identified without\n> a new full trawl.\n>\n>\n>\n> 3. Any accession, register entry, day-book, donations book or\n> correspondence in the collections of Amgueddfa Cymru or of its predecessor\n> Cardiff Museum / Welsh Museum of Natural History / National Museum of\n> Wales, dated 1875–1915, recording ironwork, armour, a visor, a lance, a\n> helmet, “armour from Llandough”, or a find from Great House / Llandough\n> Farm.\n>\n>\n>\n>    Published sources which state that such ironwork is in the National\n> Museum, and which therefore make a search of predecessor registers\n> reasonable, are:\n>\n>\n>\n>    • GGAT, Land at Great House Farm, Llandough: An Archaeological\n> Assessment, August 1990 (planning file 89/01396/OUT and 92/00671/RES): “it\n> is likely that some medieval ironwork which is held by the National Museum\n> of Wales was discovered under the rear wing c 1880 (RCAHMW 1988).”\n>\n>\n>\n>    • Holbrook & Thomas, Medieval Archaeology 49 (2005), p. 70 n. 142:\n> “medieval ironwork was discovered under the rear wing of Great House Farm\n> c. 1880. It is now in the collections of the National Museums & Galleries\n> of Wales.”\n>\n>\n>\n>    I note that the National Museum of Wales was not founded until 1907. If\n> a find of c.1880 entered a public collection in Cardiff it would have done\n> so through the earlier municipal museum. Please search those predecessor\n> accession registers, not only the post-1907 NMW database.\n>\n>\n>\n> 4. Any object record for Llandough ironwork that is not labelled “visor”\n> or “lance” but is or was associated with the farmhouse floor or rear wing /\n> dairy (the room identified on H. J. Thomas’s 1974 RCAHMW plan).\n>\n>\n>\n> Please treat this as a request for recorded information only.\n>\n>\n>\n> Regards,\n>\n>\n>\n> Requester\n>\n> [email redacted]\n>"},{"id":"gm-1a080f30175eb85a","subject":"Re: Internal Review: FOI 2026-020 - Great House Farm, Llandough | Cardiff Castle Furniture","name":"Re: Internal Review: FOI 2026-020 - Great House Farm, Llandough | Cardiff Castle Furniture","date":"2026-09-08T12:16:43Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a080f30175eb85a.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a080efa55274a93-internal-review-foi-2026-020-great-house-farm-llandough-cardiff-castle-furniture","scopeTopics":["museum"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["museum"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a080efa55274a93","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"museum","name":"Amgueddfa Cymru - Museum Wales"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a080f30175eb85a.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear FOI Team,\n\nThank you for the internal review outcome dated 8 September 2026.\n\nI note that the review upholds the original decision. I do not, however,\nconsider that the review has answered the substantive questions raised in\nmy request for internal review.\n\nI am therefore putting the position on record, while also making a separate\nnew request for recorded information which your review confirms already\nexists.\n\n1. Records identified during the additional searches\n\nOn 14 August 2026, Amgueddfa Cymru stated that the additional enquiries had\nidentified:\n\n\"some information relating generally to Great House Farm and the\nsurrounding area\".\n\nThe internal review now describes these as:\n\n\"a small number of records referring generally to Great House Farm,\nLlandough\".\n\nThe wording has therefore changed slightly, but the material fact has not:\nthe Museum confirms that records referring to Great House Farm were\nidentified.\n\nMy internal-review request specifically asked for those records to be\nidentified and, where not exempt, provided.\n\nThe review does not do so.\n\nSimply stating that the records are considered to fall outside categories 1\nto 9 does not identify what they are, where they are held, when they were\ncreated, or their reference numbers.\n\n2. Archived electronic systems\n\nMy request of 14 August specifically asked:\n\n- which archived systems were searched;\n- the approximate date ranges covered;\n- whether historical email or correspondence archives were searched;\n- whether legacy collections-management databases were searched; and\n- whether any archived systems were unavailable, incomplete or incapable of\nbeing searched.\n\nThe review answers none of these questions individually.\n\nInstead, it states:\n\n\"archived electronic records reasonably capable of containing information\nwithin scope were considered\"\n\nand:\n\n\"Any data stored within a system or database pre-dating the existing\nCollections Management Systems, would have been migrated into the incumbent\nsolution, and therefore included in the search.\"\n\nThe first statement describes a conclusion, rather than identifying the\nsystems actually searched.\n\nThe second is an assumption about migration completeness. \"Would have been\nmigrated\" is not a search result and does not establish that the relevant\nhistorical data was in fact migrated, completely migrated, retained,\nsearchable and included in the particular search undertaken.\n\n3. Correspondence independent of accessioned objects\n\nThis is the most significant issue.\n\nThe original request expressly sought correspondence and administrative\nrecords concerning, among other things:\n\n- identification;\n- recovery;\n- ownership;\n- location;\n- the Buckler and Williams families;\n- Cardiff Council;\n- Cardiff Castle;\n- the Bute Estate;\n- museums; and\n- other public authorities.\n\nThose categories were deliberately not restricted to objects which had\nsubsequently been accessioned by the Museum.\n\nThe review states:\n\n\"As no such objects were identified as having been acquired by the Museum,\nno associated acquisition, provenance, ownership, loan, disposal or related\ncorrespondence concerning such objects was identified.\"\n\nThat reasoning addresses correspondence attached to an identified\naccessioned object. It does not establish that correspondence was searched\nfor independently of an accession record.\n\nFor example, an enquiry concerning an item, a communication about possible\nprovenance, a request to identify an object, a discussion concerning\ntemporary custody, a disposal enquiry, a communication with another\ninstitution, or correspondence about the contents of a property could exist\nwithout the item ever becoming an accessioned Museum object.\n\nAccordingly, the fact that no accessioned object was identified does not\nitself establish that no such correspondence existed or was held.\n\nThe subsequent statement:\n\n\"The review did not identify evidence that the Museum holds correspondence\nconcerning the identification, ownership, recovery or location of Great\nHouse Farm furniture or household effects.\"\n\nstill does not establish whether the relevant correspondence repositories\nwere actually searched.\n\n4. Search terms\n\nMy internal-review request set out specific terms and variants and asked\nthe Museum to confirm whether those terms had been used.\n\nThe review now states only:\n\n\"appropriate search terms and variants were used\"\n\nand refers generally to:\n\n\"location names, property names, family names and associated variants\".\n\nThat does not confirm whether the specific terms identified in my request\nwere searched.\n\nIn particular, the review does not say whether searches included\ncombinations or variants involving:\n\n\"Bute Estate\", \"Bute\", \"Cardiff Castle\", \"Cardiff Council\", \"household\neffects\", \"contents\", \"dispersal\", \"recovery\", \"transfer\", \"demolition\",\n\"provenance\", \"ownership\"\n\nand the other terms specifically identified in my 14 August correspondence.\n\nThe distinction matters because a search for a property name or family name\nis not necessarily equivalent to a search for correspondence concerning the\ndispersal, recovery, transfer or ownership of its contents.\n\n5. Transfer, disposal and destruction\n\nMy request also asked what evidential basis existed for the earlier\nstatement that the Museum was not aware of relevant records having been\ntransferred to another institution or destroyed.\n\nThe review now says:\n\n\"The statement that the Museum is not aware of relevant records having been\ntransferred or destroyed reflects the outcome of the searches undertaken\nand the knowledge of relevant collections staff.\"\n\nThis combines two different things: documentary searches and institutional\nrecollection.\n\nIt does not tell me whether the Museum searched:\n\n- transfer or accession-out records;\n- disposal records;\n- retention and destruction records;\n- archival transfer documentation; or\n- other records capable of establishing whether material had previously\nbeen transferred or destroyed.\n\nThere is an important distinction between:\n\n\"no record of transfer or destruction was identified in the records\nsearched\"\n\nand:\n\n\"there is no evidence that any transfer or destruction occurred\".\n\nThe review does not make that distinction sufficiently clear.\n\n6. Searches concerning external institutions\n\nThe original request expressly contemplated records concerning Cardiff\nCouncil, Cardiff Castle, the Bute Estate and other external bodies.\n\nThe review states:\n\n\"Searches were not limited solely to object catalogue records.\"\n\nThat is helpful, but it still does not identify what non-catalogue\nrepositories were searched.\n\nFor example, it does not identify whether searches were made of curator\ncorrespondence, departmental files, donor correspondence,\nacquisition/disposal correspondence, provenance files, loan files,\ninstitutional correspondence indexes, or other historical administrative\nrecords in which communications with external institutions might have been\nrecorded.\n\nAgain, the issue is not whether the Museum considers the search reasonable.\nIt is whether the relevant classes of records were actually searched.\n\n7. The significance of the \"balance of probabilities\" conclusion\n\nI note the review's conclusion that, \"on the balance of probabilities\", the\nMuseum does not hold recorded information within the scope of the request.\n\nI do not dispute that a negative FOI determination may ultimately be\nreached on that basis.\n\nHowever, the balance-of-probabilities conclusion necessarily depends upon\nthe quality, scope and rigour of the searches on which it is based. The\nICO's guidance states that, where a requester disputes whether relevant\ninformation has been located, the authority should be able to provide a\nreasoned and detailed account of how it determined that further information\nwas not held, including the areas searched, staff consulted and, for\nelectronic searches, the keywords, accounts and time parameters used.\n\nMy concern remains that the review repeatedly substitutes statements such\nas \"we are satisfied\", \"appropriate\", \"reasonably capable\" and \"on the\nbalance of probabilities\" for the underlying factual information about what\nwas actually searched.\n\nI therefore consider the review outcome insufficiently informative on the\nspecific search methodology raised in my review request.\n\nI reserve my right to refer the handling of FOI 2026-020 and the internal\nreview to the Information Commissioner's Office.\n\nSeparate new FOI request\n\nSeparately from the above, I am now making a new request under the Freedom\nof Information Act 2000.\n\nThis new request is deliberately limited to recorded information which your\nown correspondence confirms was identified or generated in connection with\nthe searches already undertaken.\n\nPlease provide:\n\nA. Records identified\n\nThe \"small number of records referring generally to Great House Farm,\nLlandough\" identified during the additional enquiries referred to in your\nemail of 14 August 2026 and the internal review outcome of 8 September 2026.\n\nFor each record, please provide, where recorded:\n\n- title or description;\n- date;\n- collection, series or department;\n- system or repository;\n- reference, accession or record number; and\n- a copy of the record, subject to any applicable exemption.\n\nIf any part of a record is exempt, please identify the exemption relied\nupon and provide the remainder where reasonably practicable.\n\nLimb A is confined to the records your 14 August and 8 September letters\nsay were identified in the FOI 2026-020 additional enquiries. It is not a\nnew collection-wide search. If any of those records are also in scope of\nFOI 2026-032 or FOI 2026-036, please schedule them once and cross-refer.\nPlease do not refuse this request as a duplicate.\n\nB. Existing search records\n\nAny recorded information already held concerning the searches undertaken\nfor FOI 2026-020 or its internal review, including any existing:\n\n- search notes;\n- search logs;\n- search checklists;\n- system lists;\n- search-term lists;\n- IT tickets or instructions;\n- archived-system enquiries;\n- staff search records; or\n- other contemporaneous records documenting the searches undertaken.\n\nI am asking only for recorded information already held. I am not asking the\nMuseum to create a new narrative or retrospective report.\n\nIf no such search records are held, please state that expressly.\n\nPlease register this as a new FOI request and provide it with its own\nreference number. It is not a further internal review of FOI 2026-020, nor\nis it a request to reopen or amend FOI 2026-032 or FOI 2026-036.\n\nI would therefore be grateful if this request could be processed separately\nand not treated as a clarification or continuation of those matters.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester \n"},{"id":"gm-1a080b3334e0eb9f","subject":"26FOI 103 - Buckler - Acknowledgement","name":"26FOI 103 - Buckler - Acknowledgement","date":"2026-09-08T11:06:22Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a080b3334e0eb9f.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a080b3334e0eb9f-26foi-103-buckler-acknowledgement","scopeTopics":["nio"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["nio"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a080b3334e0eb9f","direction":"received","from":"NIO FOI","institutions":[{"id":"nio","name":"Northern Ireland Office"}],"refs":["26FOI103"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a080b3334e0eb9f.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester,\n\n\nThank you for your enquiry of 29/08/26 to the Wales Office.\n\n\nYour request is being handled under the Freedom of Information Act 2000\n(FOIA). We will endeavour to provide you with a response within the\ntwenty-working day deadline provided by section 10(1) of the Act and no\nlater than 28/09/26.\n\n\n\nIf you have any queries or concerns about the FOI process please do not\nhesitate to contact us, remembering to quote the reference number above in\nany correspondence.\n\n\nYours sincerely,\n\n\nFreedom of Information Team\n\n\n[image: image.png]\n\n\n\n*From:* Requester <[email redacted]>\n*Sent:* 29 August 2026 21:12\n*To:* Correspondence, Wales Office <[email redacted]>\n*Subject:* Williams Heirs, Great House Farm, Llandough (Est. 1667)\n\n\n\nSecretary of State,\n\nI write as the representative of my family, the House of Williams/Buckler,\nconcerning Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough — an approximately\n800-year-old manorial house which we occupied continuously from 1667 until\nits destruction in 1988, with me as an infant at the time, practically\nstill inside the house, with my pregnant mum.\n\nIt was my birthplace and our family’s home and land. And it still appears\nto be our land, despite BP being awarded possession in 1987. I discovered\nthat the final ruling (for possession, not determination of ownership) was\nbased on a quiet 1983 land registry by BP of our land, in their names,\nwithout disclosing my disabled widow pensioner’s ownership claim, who also\nconveniently died in the two weeks she would have had to challenge this\nregistration, not that she was even made aware. It also states our\npossession was adverse from 1955. But that was on a land parcel we rented\nand defaulted on, to the east of ours, not the parcel our home and garden\nand farm was on. Our parcel we occupied since 1667; we owned from 1928 when\nwe made our last payment on it. Originally an 1877 agreement which went\nmissing from our home in the 1940s, but even without that paper title,\nhaving not paid since 1928, our possession and ownership would have\nqualified as adverse by 1940.\n\nFurthermore, this 1983 land registry non-contest by my nan was after 55\nconsecutive years of her asserting ownership, after the 1877 agreement\nbetween Bute quarryman Daniel Thomas & Son enacted in 1928, when quarrying\ncompleted. An agreement her statements report was stolen from our home in\nthe mid-1940s. Providing both the name of the thief who confessed to\nstealing it and the estate agent who contracted him to do so.\n\nIt appears from my research so far that while a field we rented to the east\nof our land was repossessed, for which our possession might very well have\nbeen adverse from 1955, our land parcel was falsely and wrongfully treated\nas being part of the systematic repossession of the adjacent land parcel,\nwhich BP merged into theirs in 1983 from what I can gather.\n\nI believe this confusion stems from the definition of “whole farm” (as\ncited in the 1955 court order) which changed from two parcels of land in\nthe 1916 agricultural tenancy (conveyed to Western Ground Rents by the\nMarquess of Bute in 1938) to one parcel of land following the enactment of\nthe 1877 agreement in 1928 (after 51 years of our payments, reportedly £4 a\nyear). Furthermore, the modern tenancy/agricultural tenancy Western Ground\nRents placed my great-grandfather into in 1939 for the land parcel to the\neast of ours was, according to Leckwith archived records, only for the one\nparcel to the east of ours and not both parcels.\n\nIn addition to the definition of “whole farm” being misunderstood to still\ninclude our parcel after 1928, introducing ambiguity in the 1955 order, the\norder of possession for Western Ground Rents’ land parcel to the east of\nthe one we owned and occupied (since 1667) also says our land parcel (home\nand garden) was excluded from the order, but not because we owned it, but\nbecause of my grandmother’s “condition (having just had a leg amputated)\nand her resistance”. It does not mention her assertion of ownership. This\nmade our occupancy appear permissive and is what BP later tried in 1974\nwith their permissive occupancy licence letters. The basis of the pinnacle\nof this dispute: the landmark legal case BP v Buckler 1987.\n\nI say all this at least to defend my grandmother’s honour. After her\nconveniently timed death in 1983, during the 1987 hearing in which she was\nunable to defend herself, the judge took it upon himself to enter onto the\nrecord that my grandmother “conceived the notion” she owned the farm. He\nalso dismissed my father’s appeal, citing that both BP companies were all\nthe same. But in the ruling in BP’s favour prior, the judge said “had my\nnan challenged BP Pension Trust, it wouldn’t have counted as BP Properties,\nthe plaintiff, were a different company”. Which is not just a “heads they\nwin, tails we lose” procedural unfairness matter. But the legal precedent\nthat landmark legal case set by ruling my grandmother “accepted the\npermissive occupancy and offer from BP and their licence letters in 1974,\nbecause she didn’t expressly reject them” is firstly absurd by any sane,\nreasonable person’s standards. Secondly, that 1987 case excluded a\nnewspaper article from 1974 whereby my nan expressly rejects, even got\n1,000+ people to sign a petition against their housing development plan of\n13 houses (likely on their parcel to the east, not ours as they never owned\nours) and the article reports her stating her lawyer had assured her her\nownership claim was valid. And she asserts “it’s my home”.\n\nAnd further to all that, it is documented the court never ruled in 1974.\nNot adjudicating ownership in the first hearing, which was adjourned. And\nnot ruling for possession in the second hearing in 1974, which was quashed.\nFirstly because the clerk called them press when my disabled nan crawled to\ncourt begging for an audience with the judge after forced attempts to\nremove her. Including water being shut off for months, resulting in my\ninfant aunt having to go live with another family. And all our cows\ndrinking from the river and dying. The other factor is while the public and\npress and family all had a victory, my nan’s lawyers, BP and the courts\nquashed the case. But what they did was call to mention the offer to my\nnan, and quashing it instantly before she had opportunity to respond. We\nmay still have the tape recording.\n\nSo it is not a case of her not responding and so she accepted. It is a case\nthat the decades of rejection and petition and press reports were concealed\nand suppressed, along with these important facts, and the backdated licence\nletters BP produced afterwards in 1974 and clandestine 1971 alteration of\nour land parcel on the Ordnance Survey, and conveyance from BP Pension\nTrust to Properties in 1975, and land registration in 1983, unbeknown to us\nor anyone I suspect, was not meeting of the minds by a long shot. Yet was\nall that was heard or considered in 1987. The 1987 case was not judged on\nthe merits of the case as it should not even have been held in the court it\nwas in. The court it was decided in was the least applicable court of them\nall. A Chancery/Queen’s Bench i.e. equity court would have been the\nappropriate court.\n\nFurther to even all this, an attempt was made by Western Ground Rents to\nget my nan into a tenancy in 1949 too, for our plot. Which my nan expressly\nrejected in lieu of her assertion of ownership. Even with our paper title\nstolen prior to that attempt, having not paid since 1928 our possession,\neven if adverse, would have qualified us as owners through adverse\npossession by 1940 in any case.\n\nWGR generated a possession order for our land, based on the ambiguity they\nintroduced into the 1955 order, which was never enforced and is the\ninterest they conveyed to BP in 1969, when conveying the land they\npossessed east of ours. BP then altered the Ordnance Survey in 1971,\nshifting the title “Ty Mawr” from the land parcel to the east onto our land\nparcel. And appending “Farm” to the descriptor. Making our parcel better\nfit the defective title chain being forged, in lieu of my nan’s missing\ntitle chain. The 133 houses they are seeking to build on that land parcel,\nours (now Church View Close) was merged into, is defective. The title chain\ntraces back to and through us to what may be the 1,000 freehold issued by\nKnight Sir Herbert to the Vaughan family in the 1500s. It is even possible\nwe have been playing the role of virtual freeholder on our land parcel, or\neven tenant on the land east of ours. When all things considered, like the\nfact we occupied the manorial house since 1667, may even make it possible\nthat we were and still are the rightful freeholders of Llandough Town\n(formerly Cedwyn Estate), with 640 years left on that freehold. Because\nBute’s purchase of the last Vaughan heirs’ estate reportedly did not\ninclude our Ty Mawr.\n\nAnd Ty Mawr (alias Cefin (a possible attempt at Cedwyn) according to an\n1824 David Stewart survey) was not just our home or land parcel, but enough\nparcels to conclude the estate being half the town of Llandough. So this\nall may be a lot bigger and run a lot deeper than even what I am bringing\nto your attention. Our family also have bloodline connections to the\nVaughan, and so it is not just title, estate and deeds. I only focus on the\nparcel we own, for which possession was awarded to BP, to begin unravelling\ntruths long suppressed and concealed.\n\nIn 1974 an ownership hearing did occur to adjudicate the ownership dispute,\nbut seeing as my nan could not present her title as it was stolen, the\nhearing was adjourned. BP then tried to enforce the 1962 possession order.\n\nSo in summary: July 1974 = possession case, adjourned, never finally\ndecided.\n\nSeptember 1974 = old 1962 possession order revived.\n\nOctober/November 1974 = threatened eviction stopped and warrant withdrawn.\nNot licence letters, quashed hearing, but in fact because of overwhelming\ncourt clerk, press and public support.\n\nAnother dimension of this, of which there are many, including BP Bailiffs\nbeing undercover soldiers and/or accompanied by soldiers, allegedly even\nSAS, is the abrupt demolition of our 800-year-old “county treasure” home.\n4am the morning after, almost simultaneously, CADW refused to spot-list it\nas Grade II, the council refused to issue a demolition stop order and our\nHigh Court injunction preventing demolition or removal of our property (all\nof our furniture from Cardiff Castle etc.) got overturned.\n\nThis surely should not have happened prior to the appeal procedure of those\ndecisions concluding, to say the least. More indications of Government\ninvolvement/military operation. Which I do not just say as a military\nwarfare professional, but because other evidence supports it (including\nrecently declassified ITV coverage which may not even have aired). Along\nwith fraud and all the concealment, there are grounds to exercise the\nclause in the Limitation Act and reopen this case.\n\nI formally submit the accompanying CADW — Forensic Critique, Great House\nFarm (Ty Mawr), Llandough – Version 5.0.0 (August 2026).\n\nI also enclose a mock application to an AI-assisted mock Chancery Court I\nam working with. I have been using iterative research and legal-analysis\nprocesses to test and refine the arguments arising from the historical\nevidence, pending any formal legal proceedings on behalf of my family. I\ninclude it not as a substitute for legal proceedings, but to give your\nOffice the wider context in which I am examining what happened. And an\nillustration of a final single legal case which articulates my family’s\ngrievances. If they were not all so terrified to speak out, still now, even\nafter 38 years.\n\nThe CADW Forensic Critique is deliberately evidence-led. It distinguishes\nbetween established facts, reasonable inferences and matters which cannot\npresently be established because the underlying records no longer appear to\nbe available.\n\nIts central concern is straightforward:\n\nCadw cannot presently produce sufficient surviving evidence to explain how\nits December 1988 decision was reached, nor can it presently explain what\nhappened to the records documenting that decision.\n\nAlbeit we are still corresponding to establish the facts. In any case what\nhas been established thus far is particularly troubling given what happened\nimmediately afterwards.\n\nIn 1988, Cadw publicly stated that it was considering an appeal to the\nSecretary of State for Wales to protect the building. The South Wales Echo\nreported on 3 December 1988 that an inspector would visit “this weekend”\nand liaise with RCAHMW.\n\nThe building was then demolished overnight on 5/6 December 1988, within\nhours of the refusal and the simultaneous dismissal of a High Court\ninjunction.\n\nI am therefore asking your Office to help, at the very least in this\ncorrespondence with CADW, to establish as precisely as possible what\nhappened.\n\n1. Contact between Cadw and the Welsh Office / Secretary of State\n\nPlease confirm, deny or clarify each of the following:\n\nWhether any communication or outreach took place between Cadw, or its\npredecessor functions, and the Office of the Secretary of State for Wales /\nWelsh Office concerning Great House Farm during the period July–December\n1988.\n\nWhether any recommendation, briefing, submission, notification or formal\nappeal concerning the protection or listing of Great House Farm was\nsubmitted to the Secretary of State.\n\nWhether my family, as the occupiers and asserted owners of the property,\nwas ever consulted, contacted or notified by Cadw or the Welsh Office\nbefore the refusal decision of 5 December 1988.\n\nIf such communications occurred, I ask that the surviving records be\nidentified and disclosed.\n\nIf they did not occur, I ask that this be stated clearly.\n\n2. What happened to the 1988 assessment records?\n\nCadw’s responses, including FOI ATISN 27021, the response from Amy Longford\ndated 10 July 2026, the subsequent internal review, and the response from\nDr Andy Wigley dated 27 August 2026, indicate that:\n\nonly the two July 1988 photographs have been located;\n\nno assessment file, inspector’s notes, decision record or internal\ncorrespondence has been located;\n\nno destruction record, transfer list, accession record or archive schedule\nhas been located; and\n\nCadw has stated that unsuccessful listing assessments were historically\nretained for approximately three years before transfer or destruction under\nthe Public Records Act 1958.\n\nThis raises an obvious question which I hope your Office will appreciate is\nimportant to my family:\n\nIf the records were destroyed, transferred or otherwise disposed of, where\nis the record showing that this happened and under whose authority?\n\nI therefore request the immediate production of any surviving destruction,\ndisposal, transfer or accession documentation relating to the Great House\nFarm 1988 assessment file, including certificates, dockets, lists, database\nentries, retention schedules, disposal authorities or other contemporaneous\nrecords held by the Welsh Office, its predecessors, Cadw, or any repository\nto which relevant records were transferred.\n\nWhere such a record exists, please provide its exact wording and identify,\ninsofar as recorded:\n\nthe file reference;\n\nthe disposal or transfer authority;\n\nthe date of the action;\n\nthe relevant retention period;\n\nand the officer or authority responsible for authorising it.\n\nIf any residual material remains in existence, I ask that it be preserved\nimmediately and not destroyed, transferred or otherwise disposed of while\nthese matters remain under examination.\n\nIf no destruction, disposal or transfer documentation exists, I ask that\nyour Office confirm that expressly.\n\n3. The unanswered questions surrounding the decision itself\n\nI also ask your Office to address, or to require Cadw to address, the\noutstanding questions identified in Part XII of the accompanying Forensic\nCritique.\n\nIn particular:\n\nWho was the decision-maker responsible for the refusal on 5 December 1988?\n\nWhat was the registered file reference, title and record series?\n\nWhat evidence was actually considered?\n\nWas the 1974 RCAHMW survey (ST 16817331) considered?\n\nWere SMR 2038/2038s and the relevant 1978–1980 GGAT reports considered?\n\nWhat was the basis, authorship and status of the July 1988 “YYY” coding?\n\nWhat communications took place with BP Properties or its agents, the local\nplanning authority, RCAHMW, GGAT or the High Court during the critical\nperiod?\n\nWas a Building Preservation Notice considered?\n\nWhat retention and disposal schedule applied to the assessment?\n\nWhat was the scheduled disposal/review date?\n\nWho authorised disposal, if disposal occurred?\n\nWhich repositories and catalogues have been searched, including The\nNational Archives and historic Welsh Office records?\n\nDo comparable unsuccessful listing assessments from 1988–1991 survive, and\nif so, how were they retained?\n\n\n\nThese are not questions I ask simply because I disagree with the historical\ndecision.\n\n\n\nI am trying to establish what evidence was before the decision-maker, what\nprocess was followed, and what happened to the official record afterwards.\n\nThat distinction matters.\n\n\n\n4. Independent verification\n\nI therefore ask your Office to confirm whether it has searched, or will now\nsearch, The National Archives catalogue and relevant historic Welsh Office\ntransfer documentation for records concerning Great House Farm.\n\nIf this has not already been done comprehensively, I ask that it be\nundertaken and that Cadw be asked to undertake any corresponding searches\nnecessary to establish whether the missing assessment material was ever\ntransferred elsewhere.\n\nI would also ask that any surviving records relating to the case be\npreserved while these enquiries are ongoing.\n\n5. Why I am asking\n\nI appreciate that this is a historic matter.\n\nBut for my family it is not simply history.\n\nThis was our home. It was the place into which I was born, and the\ndestruction of the building came at the end of a much longer dispute\nconcerning our family’s occupation, land and property rights.\n\nI have spent considerable time reconstructing what happened from surviving\ncourt records, land records, archaeological evidence, newspapers, archival\nmaterial, FOI responses and contemporary documentation. I am not asking\nyour Office to accept every conclusion I have drawn.\n\nI am asking for something more fundamental:\n\nwhere the Government and its agencies can still establish what happened, I\nask that they do so; where the evidence no longer exists, I ask that they\nsay so plainly; and where the record of its disappearance should exist, I\nask that it be identified.\n\nMy family has lived with the consequences of these events for decades. I\nbelieve that, at this stage, the least that can reasonably be expected is\nopenness, proper record-keeping and an honest examination of what happened.\n\nI therefore ask that this correspondence, together with the attached CADW —\nForensic Critique v5.0.0, be treated as a formal request for recorded\ninformation under the Freedom of Information Act 2000, as well as a request\nfor clarification of the records-management position concerning Cadw and\nthe historic Welsh Office functions now falling within the responsibility\nof your Office.\n\nI would be grateful for a substantive response addressing each of the\nmatters above individually.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\n\n\nRequester\n\n\n------------------------------\nThis e-mail and any attachments is intended only for the attention of the\naddressee(s). Its unauthorised use, disclosure, storage or copying is not\npermitted. If you are not the intended recipient, please destroy all copies\nand inform the sender by return e-mail. Internet e-mail is not a secure\nmedium. Any reply to this message could be intercepted and read by someone\nelse. Please bear that in mind when deciding whether to send material in\nresponse to this message by e-mail. This e-mail (whether you are the sender\nor the recipient) may be monitored, recorded and retained by the Ministry\nof Justice. Monitoring / blocking software may be used, and e-mail content\nmay be read at any time. You have a responsibility to ensure laws are not\nbroken when composing or forwarding e-mails and their contents.\n------------------------------\nMae’r e-bost hwn (ac unrhyw atodiad) wedi’i fwriadu at sylw’r derbynnydd\n(derbynwyr) yn unig. Ni chaniateir i unrhyw un ei ddefnyddio, ei ddatgelu,\nei gadw na gwneud copi ohono heb awdurdod. Os nad chi yw’r unigolyn y mae’r\ne-bost wedi’i fwriadu ar ei gyfer, dilëwch bob copi, os gwelwch yn dda a\nrhowch wybod i’r anfonwr drwy anfon e-bost yn ôl. Nid yw anfon negeseuon\ne-bost ar y rhyngrwyd yn ddull diogel o gyfathrebu. Gallai rhywun arall\nagor a darllen unrhyw ymateb i’r neges hon. Cofiwch hyn wrth benderfynu\nanfon deunydd mewn ymateb i'r neges hon drwy e-bost. Gall y Weinyddiaeth\nCyfiawnder fonitro, cofnodi a chadw'r e-bost hwn (pa un ai chi yw'r anfonwr\nynteu’r derbynnydd). Mae’n bosib y defnyddir meddalwedd monitro / atal\nnegeseuon e-bost, a gellir darllen cynnwys yr e-bost ar unrhyw adeg. Mae\ngennych gyfrifoldeb i sicrhau nad ydych yn torri unrhyw gyfreithiau wrth\ngreu neu anfon negeseuon e-bost a’u cynnwys ymlaen. Wrth adael Mewnrwyd\nDdiogel y Llywodraeth, nid oedd unrhyw feirws yn gysylltiedig â’r e-bost\nhwn. Mae’n ddigon posibl y bydd unrhyw ohebiaeth drwy’r GSi yn cael ei\nlogio, ei monitro a/neu ei chofnodi yn awtomatig am resymau cyfreithiol."},{"id":"gm-1a08046912368e30","subject":"FOI: 2026-036 - Great House Farm, Llandough, Cardiff","name":"FOI: 2026-036 - Great House Farm, Llandough, Cardiff","date":"2026-09-08T09:08:16Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a08046912368e30.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a08046912368e30-foi-2026-036-great-house-farm-llandough-cardiff","scopeTopics":["museum"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["museum"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a08046912368e30","direction":"received","from":"FOI Requests","institutions":[{"id":"museum","name":"Amgueddfa Cymru - Museum Wales"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a08046912368e30.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester\n\nThank you for your email.\n\nI note that this request covers the same subject matter as FOI:2026-032 and extends the scope of the information requested in that case.\n\nIn order to avoid duplication and ensure that we provide a single, comprehensive response, our intention is to treat FOI:2026-036 as replacing FOI:2026-032 and to close FOI:2026-032 accordingly.\n\nUnless we hear from you to the contrary within the next few working days, we will proceed on that basis and respond to the broader request contained in FOI:2026-036.\n\nPlease note that your request for an internal review of FOI:2026-024 remains a separate matter and will continue to be considered independently.\n\nCofion cynnes,\n\n[A picture containing text, tableware, plate, dishware  Description automatically generated]\nFOI Team\n[cid:[email redacted]24F960]\n[email redacted]<[email redacted]>\namgueddfa.cymru<applewebdata://288FE56C-5BFC-474C-ADB3-A1017EF25E69/amgueddfa.cymru> | museum.wales<applewebdata://288FE56C-5BFC-474C-ADB3-A1017EF25E69/museum.wales>\n\nRhif elusen | Charity number 525774\n\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]>\nSent: 08 September 2026 04:05\nTo: FOI Requests <[email redacted]>\nSubject: ALLANOL/EXTERNAL - Great House Farm, Llandough, Cardiff\n\n\nThis email comes from outside the organisation.\n\nDo not click links or open attachments unless it is an email you expected to receive.\nFOI Team,\n\nThis is a new request under the Freedom of Information Act 2000. It is not a repeat of FOI 2026-024 and it is not a comment on the internal review of that request.\n\nIn your letter of 25 August 2026 (FOI 2026-024) you stated:\n\n  \"For completeness, our searches identified records relating to other archaeological finds from Church Farm, Llandough and to other archaeological discoveries recorded at Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough. These records do not relate to, nor are they otherwise associated with, the discovery described in your request. We have therefore not considered them to fall within the scope of this request.\"\n\nI now request those records.\n\nPlease provide:\n\n1. A schedule of every object, archive file, accession, loan, enquiry or correspondence record that Amgueddfa Cymru holds which is indexed, provenanced or otherwise recorded as from Great House Farm / Ty Mawr / Llandough Farm, Llandough (Penarth / Vale of Glamorgan). For each entry: accession or archive number, object or file title, date of acquisition or creation, donor or excavator if recorded, and a one-line description.\n\n2. The same schedule for Church Farm, Llandough, but only so far as the records were retrieved in the 2026-024 search and can be identified without a new full trawl.\n\n3. Any accession, register entry, day-book, donations book or correspondence in the collections of Amgueddfa Cymru or of its predecessor Cardiff Museum / Welsh Museum of Natural History / National Museum of Wales, dated 1875-1915, recording ironwork, armour, a visor, a lance, a helmet, \"armour from Llandough\", or a find from Great House / Llandough Farm.\n\n   Published sources which state that such ironwork is in the National Museum, and which therefore make a search of predecessor registers reasonable, are:\n\n   * GGAT, Land at Great House Farm, Llandough: An Archaeological Assessment, August 1990 (planning file 89/01396/OUT and 92/00671/RES): \"it is likely that some medieval ironwork which is held by the National Museum of Wales was discovered under the rear wing c 1880 (RCAHMW 1988).\"\n\n   * Holbrook & Thomas, Medieval Archaeology 49 (2005), p. 70 n. 142: \"medieval ironwork was discovered under the rear wing of Great House Farm c. 1880. It is now in the collections of the National Museums & Galleries of Wales.\"\n\n   I note that the National Museum of Wales was not founded until 1907. If a find of c.1880 entered a public collection in Cardiff it would have done so through the earlier municipal museum. Please search those predecessor accession registers, not only the post-1907 NMW database.\n\n4. Any object record for Llandough ironwork that is not labelled \"visor\" or \"lance\" but is or was associated with the farmhouse floor or rear wing / dairy (the room identified on H. J. Thomas's 1974 RCAHMW plan).\n\nPlease treat this as a request for recorded information only.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester\n[email redacted]<[email redacted]>"},{"id":"gm-1a080232600c3e70","subject":"Internal Review: FOI 2026-020 - Great House Farm, Llandough | Cardiff Castle Furniture","name":"Internal Review: FOI 2026-020 - Great House Farm, Llandough | Cardiff Castle Furniture","date":"2026-09-08T08:29:36Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a080232600c3e70.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a080232600c3e70-internal-review-foi-2026-020-great-house-farm-llandough-cardiff-castle-furniture","scopeTopics":["museum"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["museum"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a080232600c3e70","direction":"received","from":"FOI Requests","institutions":[{"id":"museum","name":"Amgueddfa Cymru - Museum Wales"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a080232600c3e70.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester\n\nThank you for your request for an internal review of FOI 2026-020 concerning furniture, furnishings, antiques, fixtures, heirlooms and other movable property originating from Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough.\n\nThe reviewer has considered your original request, the responses provided by the Museum, the additional enquiries undertaken following your request for clarification on 3rd August 2026, and the concerns raised in your email requesting an internal review.\n\nWe are satisfied that the request was interpreted correctly and that reasonable and proportionate searches were undertaken to identify information falling within the scope of the request. Following the original response, the Museum undertook additional enquiries across relevant collections records and archived electronic records in order to satisfy itself that no information had been overlooked.\n\nWe are satisfied that the searches undertaken were appropriate to the nature and scope of the request and that no further searches are required in order to determine whether Amgueddfa Cymru holds information within scope.  The review therefore upholds the original decision that Amgueddfa Cymru does not hold recorded information concerning furniture, furnishings, antiques, fixtures, heirlooms or other movable property originating from Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough falling within categories 1 to 9 of your request.\n\nResponse to the points raised\n\n1. Information identified concerning Great House Farm\nDuring the additional enquiries, a small number of records referring generally to Great House Farm, Llandough were identified. These records do not relate to furniture, furnishings, antiques, fixtures, heirlooms or other movable property originating from Great House Farm and do not fall within categories 1 to 9 of your original request. The reviewer is therefore satisfied that they fall outside the scope of the request.\n\n2. Scope of the archived electronic search\n\nWe are satisfied that archived electronic records reasonably capable of containing information within scope were considered as part of the searches undertaken. Any data stored within a system or database pre-dating the existing Collections Management Systems, would have been migrated into the incumbent solution, and therefore included in the search.\n\n3. Correspondence and administrative records\nThe review considered whether correspondence or administrative records relating to the requested items were held.\n\nThe Museum's collections records include accession documentation and associated collection records relating to objects acquired by the Museum. Searches of those records did not identify any furniture, furnishings, antiques, fixtures, heirlooms or other movable property originating from Great House Farm.   As no such objects were identified as having been acquired by the Museum, no associated acquisition, provenance, ownership, loan, disposal or related correspondence concerning such objects was identified.\n\nThe review did not identify evidence that the Museum holds correspondence concerning the identification, ownership, recovery or location of Great House Farm furniture or household effects.\n\n4. Search terms\nWe are satisfied that appropriate search terms and variants were used across the relevant collections management systems, collection records and associated documentation. These included relevant location names, property names, family names and associated variants reasonably capable of identifying records within scope.\n\nThe review is satisfied that the search methodology was reasonable and proportionate in the circumstances.\n\n5. Transfer, disposal and destruction\nThe statement that the Museum is not aware of relevant records having been transferred or destroyed reflects the outcome of the searches undertaken and the knowledge of relevant collections staff.  The review did not identify any records showing that information within the scope of your request had been transferred to another institution or destroyed.\n\nFor clarity, the Museum's position is that no relevant records were identified.\n\n6. Records concerning external institutions\nWe note that your request sought records concerning communications with Cardiff Council, Cardiff Castle, the Bute Estate and other bodies regarding objects originating from Great House Farm. Searches were not limited solely to object catalogue records. Relevant collection documentation and associated records were also considered.\n\nNo records were identified concerning furniture, furnishings, antiques, fixtures, heirlooms, or other movable property originating from Great House Farm, including correspondence with the organisations referred to in your request.\n\n7. Advice and assistance\nThe Museum does not hold information identifying a specific department, collection, archive or record series within Cardiff Council or Cardiff Castle that is likely to contain the information you seek. The advice previously provided reflected our general view that those organisations would be more likely than Amgueddfa Cymru to hold records relating to the ownership, management or disposal of property associated with Great House Farm.\n\nConclusion\nHaving considered all of the information available, we are satisfied that Amgueddfa Cymru undertook reasonable and proportionate searches and that, on the balance of probabilities, the Museum does not hold recorded information falling within the scope of your request. The original decision is therefore upheld.\n\nIf you remain dissatisfied, you have the right to complain to the Information Commissioner's Office.\n\n\n\nCofion cynnes,\n\n[A picture containing text, tableware, plate, dishware  Description automatically generated]\nFOI Team\n[cid:image002.png@01DD3F74.8F8D9600]\n[email redacted]<[email redacted]>\namgueddfa.cymru<applewebdata://288FE56C-5BFC-474C-ADB3-A1017EF25E69/amgueddfa.cymru> | museum.wales<applewebdata://288FE56C-5BFC-474C-ADB3-A1017EF25E69/museum.wales>\n\nRhif elusen | Charity number 525774\n\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]>\nSent: 14 August 2026 18:40\nTo: FOI Requests <[email redacted]>\nSubject: Re: FOI 2026-020 - Great House Farm, Llandough | Cardiff Castle Furniture\n\n\nThis email comes from outside the organisation.\n\nDo not click links or open attachments unless it is an email you expected to receive.\n\nFOI Team,\n\nThank you for your detailed response and for undertaking the additional enquiries.\n\nI appreciate the further information you have provided concerning the searches undertaken. However, I would like to request an internal review under the Freedom of Information Act 2000.\n\nMy concern is not simply that no objects have been identified. It is whether the searches undertaken have fully addressed the scope of my original request, particularly the documentary evidence concerning the provenance, acquisition, transfer, ownership, identification and dispersal of material originating from Great House Farm (Ty Mawr).\n\nI would be grateful if the internal review could consider the following points.\n\n1. Information identified concerning Great House Farm\n\nYour response states that the additional enquiries identified \"some information relating generally to Great House Farm and the surrounding area\".\n\nPlease could you identify what categories of information this refers to and, where it is not exempt, provide the relevant recorded information or explain where it can be accessed?\n\nThis appears potentially relevant to the original request even if it does not itself identify furniture or household objects.\n\n2. Scope of the archived electronic search\n\nYou state that the Information Technology team reviewed \"archived electronic systems and records retained by the Museum\".\n\nPlease could the review clarify:\n\n  *   which archived systems were searched;\n  *   the approximate date ranges covered;\n  *   whether historical email or correspondence archives were searched;\n  *   whether legacy collections-management databases were searched;\n  *   whether databases or systems preceding the current CMS were searched; and\n  *   whether any archived systems were unavailable, incomplete or incapable of being searched.\n\n3. Correspondence and administrative records\n\nMy original request expressly sought correspondence concerning the identification, recovery, ownership or location of objects, as well as correspondence with the Bute Estate, Cardiff Council, Cardiff Castle and other public authorities.\n\nYour response refers generally to \"associated collection documentation\" and \"administrative records\", but does not make clear whether historical correspondence files were specifically searched.\n\nPlease could the internal review establish whether searches were made of:\n\n  *   curator correspondence;\n  *   departmental correspondence files;\n  *   donor correspondence;\n  *   acquisition correspondence;\n  *   disposal correspondence;\n  *   loan correspondence;\n  *   provenance correspondence;\n  *   correspondence with Cardiff Council;\n  *   correspondence with Cardiff Castle;\n  *   correspondence with the Bute Estate;\n  *   correspondence with other museums or public authorities; and\n  *   any relevant institutional or external correspondence indexes.\n\n4. Search terms\n\nThank you for identifying many of the search terms used.\n\nPlease could the internal review confirm whether the search also included combinations and variants involving:\n\n  *   \"Bute Estate\"\n  *   \"Bute\"\n  *   \"Cardiff Castle\"\n  *   \"Cardiff Council\"\n  *   \"Great House Farm\"\n  *   \"Ty Mawr\"\n  *   \"Great House\"\n  *   \"Llandough\"\n  *   \"Buckler\"\n  *   \"Williams\"\n  *   \"Mary Williams\"\n  *   \"Mary Doreen Williams\"\n  *   \"household effects\"\n  *   \"contents\"\n  *   \"furniture\"\n  *   \"furnishings\"\n  *   \"heirlooms\"\n  *   \"fixtures\"\n  *   \"estate\"\n  *   \"dispersal\"\n  *   \"recovery\"\n  *   \"provenance\"\n  *   \"ownership\"\n  *   \"transfer\"\n  *   \"demolition\"\n\nI appreciate that historical records may not have been consistently indexed, which is why I would be grateful for confirmation of the methodology used.\n\n5. Transfer, disposal and destruction\n\nYour response states:\n\n\"We are also not aware of any relevant collection records relating to such material having been transferred to another institution or destroyed.\"\n\nPlease could the internal review clarify whether this conclusion is based upon:\n\na. a search of transfer/accession-out records;\n\nb. a review of retention and disposal records;\n\nc. enquiries with relevant collections staff;\n\nd. review of archival transfer documentation; or\n\ne. institutional knowledge rather than a documentary search.\n\nI am particularly interested in distinguishing between \"no record exists\" and \"no record was identified in the systems searched\".\n\n6. The original request concerning external institutions\n\nThe original request was deliberately framed broadly because the question concerns possible dispersal of the contents of a demolished historic property.\n\nIt expressly included records concerning Cardiff Council, Cardiff Castle, the Bute Estate, museums, Arts Council England, DCMS, RCEWA and other public authorities.\n\nI would therefore be grateful if the review could confirm whether records of communications or transactions involving those bodies were searched within Amgueddfa Cymru's records, rather than simply searching the Museum's object catalogue for Great House Farm objects.\n\n7. Advice and assistance\n\nYou have stated that Cardiff Council and Cardiff Castle \"would appear to be the organisations most likely to hold records relating to furniture, furnishings or other movable property associated with Great House Farm\".\n\nI appreciate that you cannot transfer another authority's records to me. However, could you please provide any further information available to the Museum as to which particular department, collection, archive or record series at Cardiff Council or Cardiff Castle is most likely to contain such material?\n\nThat would materially assist me in directing subsequent FOI and archival enquiries.\n\nI am grateful for the additional work already undertaken. I am requesting the internal review because I would like the record to be clear as to precisely what was searched, what information was identified, and what the negative result does and does not establish.\n\nI am not asking Amgueddfa Cymru to determine any allegation concerning the historical ownership of Great House Farm. The purpose of the review is simply to ensure that the documentary search corresponds fully with the scope of the original FOI request.\n\nPlease treat this email as my formal request for an internal review of FOI 2026-020.\n\nRegards,\n\n\n\nRequester \n\n\n\nWilliams / Buckler Family Estate\n\nOn Fri, 14 Aug 2026, 05:43 FOI Requests, <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>> wrote:\nDear Requester\n\nThank you for your email.\n\nFollowing receipt of your query, Amgueddfa Cymru undertook further enquiries to satisfy itself that no information had been overlooked when responding to your request.\n\nThese enquiries included a review of records held across the Museum's collections and a review of archived electronic systems and records by the Museum's Information Technology team. The review considered all collections management systems used by the Museum, including those most likely to contain information falling within the scope of your request.\n\nFor the History collections, searches were undertaken of the Collections Management System (CMS), accession files, donor indexes, location indexes and associated collection documentation. Searches were carried out using terms including \"Great House Farm\", \"Ty Mawr\", \"Llandough\", \"Buckler\" and \"Bute\". Additional consideration was given to acquisition records, provenance information and associated administrative records.\n\nFor the Art collections, searches were undertaken within the collections management system across description, comments, acquisition notes and place fields using the terms \"Ty Mawr\", \"Great House Farm\", \"Great House\", \"Williams\", \"Buckler\", \"Llandough\" and combinations of those terms. No additional paper archives relating to Great House Farm, Ty Mawr, Great House or Buckler were identified.\n\nThe Museum's Information Technology team also reviewed archived electronic systems and records retained by the Museum.\n\nThese enquiries identified some information relating generally to Great House Farm and the surrounding area. However, no information was identified concerning furniture, furnishings, antiques, fixtures, heirlooms or other movable property originating from Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough. We did not identify any inventories, catalogues, acquisition records, disposal records, loan records, provenance files, ownership records, photographs, correspondence or other records falling within categories 1 to 9 of your request.\n\nWe are also not aware of any relevant collection records relating to such material having been transferred to another institution or destroyed.\n\nAccordingly, having completed these additional enquiries, Amgueddfa Cymru remains satisfied that it does not hold recorded information within the scope of your request and our response under section 1(1)(a) of the Freedom of Information Act 2000 remains unchanged.\nIn relation to your request for advice and assistance, Cardiff Council and Cardiff Castle would appear to be the organisations most likely to hold records relating to furniture, furnishings or other movable property associated with Great House Farm.\n\nIf you remain dissatisfied with this response, you may request an internal review within 40 working days of the date of this email.\n\n\nCofion cynnes,\n\n[A picture containing text, tableware, plate, dishware    Description automatically generated]\nFOI Team\n[cid:image002.png@01DD3F74.8F8D9600]\n[email redacted]<[email redacted]>\namgueddfa.cymru | museum.wales\n\nRhif elusen | Charity number 525774\n\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSent: 03 August 2026 17:29\nTo: FOI Requests <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSubject: Re: FOI 2026-020 - Great House Farm, Llandough | Cardiff Castle Furniture\n\n\nThis email comes from outside the organisation.\n\nDo not click links or open attachments unless it is an email you expected to receive.\n\nDear FOI Team,\n\nThank you for your response.\n\nI note your position that Amgueddfa Cymru does not hold recorded information within the scope of my request.\n\nHowever, I would be grateful if you could clarify the searches undertaken to reach that conclusion.\n\nIn particular, please confirm:\n\n  *   Which collections management systems, databases and \"associated records\" were searched.\n  *   Whether searches extended beyond accessioned objects to include acquisition files, provenance files, curator correspondence, donor files, temporary custody records, unaccessioned material, photographs and administrative records.\n  *   The search terms used (for example: \"Great House Farm\", \"Ty Mawr\", \"Llandough\", \"Williams\", \"Buckler\", \"Bute Estate\" and any other relevant variants).\n  *   Whether any potentially relevant records have been transferred to another institution or destroyed in accordance with a retention policy.\n\nMy original request was not limited to identifying surviving objects. It also sought records relating to their provenance, acquisition, transfer, ownership, photographs and correspondence. This reflects the fact that the contents of Great House Farm may have been dispersed following the property's demolition, meaning relevant records could exist even if no objects were ultimately accessioned by the Museum.\n\nIf, following those searches, Amgueddfa Cymru remains satisfied that no recorded information is held, I would be grateful for confirmation of that position.\n\nFinally, if you are aware of another public authority that is more likely to hold records falling within the scope of my request, I would appreciate your advice in accordance with your duty to provide advice and assistance.\n\nThank you for your assistance.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\nRequester\n\nOn Mon, 3 Aug 2026, 10:39 FOI Requests, <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>> wrote:\nDear Requester\n\nThank you for your request for information received on 2nd July 2026, concerning furniture, furnishings, antiques, fixtures, heirlooms and other movable property originating from Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough.\n\nFollowing searches of the Museum's collections management systems and associated records, Amgueddfa Cymru has not identified any furniture, furnishings, antiques, fixtures, heirlooms or other movable property recorded as originating from Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough.\n\nAccordingly, Amgueddfa Cymru does not hold recorded information falling within the scope of items 1 to 9 of your request relating to such objects.\nFor clarity:\n\n  *   We have not identified any objects of the type described in your request as originating from Great House Farm.\n  *   We have not identified inventories, catalogues, acquisition records, disposal records, loan records, provenance files, ownership records, photographs, correspondence, reports, memoranda or related records concerning such objects within the scope of your request.\n  *   We have not identified any records relating to the identification, recovery, ownership or location of furniture or household effects from Great House Farm.\n\nWe note your references to archaeological material from land associated with Great House Farm. However, archaeological finds are outside the scope of this response because they do not fall within your request for furniture, furnishings, antiques, fixtures, heirlooms and other movable property. We understand that archaeological material is the subject of a separate information request and will be dealt with separately.\n\nUnder section 1(1)(a) of the Freedom of Information Act 2000, our response is therefore that Amgueddfa Cymru does not hold the requested information.\n\nCofion cynnes,\n\n[A picture containing text, tableware, plate, dishware    Description automatically generated]\nFOI Team\n[cid:image002.png@01DD3F74.8F8D9600]\n[email redacted]<[email redacted]>\namgueddfa.cymru | museum.wales\n\nRhif elusen | Charity number 525774\n\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSent: 02 July 2026 20:51\nTo: [email redacted]<[email redacted]>\nCc: [email redacted]<[email redacted]>; [email redacted]<[email redacted]>; [email redacted]<[email redacted]>; [email redacted]<[email redacted]>; FOI Requests <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>; [email redacted]<[email redacted]>; [email redacted]<[email redacted]>; National Library of Wales <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSubject: ALLANOL/EXTERNAL - Great House Farm, Llandough | Cardiff Castle Furniture\n\nYou don't often get email from [email redacted]<[email redacted]>. Learn why this is important<https://aka.ms/LearnAboutSenderIdentification>\n\nFreedom of Information Act 2000 Request\n\nSir/Madam,\n\nI am making this request under the Freedom of Information Act 2000.\n\nI seek all recorded information held concerning furniture, furnishings, antiques, fixtures, heirlooms and other movable property originating from Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough, including any and all items we received from Bute Estate, Cardiff Castle, Cardiff Council.  Weather it be museums, archives or any other public body. I know museums have artifacts from our land in Llandough.\n\nSpecifically, I request copies of all:\n\n  1.  Inventories, catalogues and asset registers.\n  2.  Acquisition, disposal, loan and transfer records.\n  3.  Provenance files and ownership records.\n  4.  Photographs of items identified as originating from Great House Farm.\n  5.  Correspondence concerning the identification, recovery, ownership or location of such items.\n  6.  Internal reports, legal advice and memoranda relating to Great House Farm furniture or household effects.\n  7.  Correspondence mentioning the Buckler or Williams families in connection with these items.\n  8.  Records concerning any efforts by Cardiff Council or Cardiff Castle to trace, recover or secure items believed to have originated from Great House Farm.\n  9.  Correspondence with the Bute Estate, museums, the Department for Culture, Media and Sport, the Reviewing Committee on the Export of Works of Art and Objects of Cultural Interest (RCEWA), Arts Council England or any other public authority regarding objects from Great House Farm.\n\nFor context, I am allegeing that my family was unlawfully dispossessed of Great House Farm through fraud, and that furniture and other historic possessions belonging to the family were consequently dispersed. It's possible the bailiffs from BP were also SAS soldiers. We are still gathering actionable intelligence.\n\nI appreciate that the Freedom of Information Act does not require the authority to determine the merits of that allegation. However, it is directly relevant to understanding why I seek the recorded information.\n\nI am currently developing a full-scale replica of Great House Farm in Patagonia, Argentina as a museum dedicated to preserving the history of the house, its occupants and its contents. Should records identify surviving items from the farm, whether held by Cardiff Council, Cardiff Castle, museums or other public institutions, I intend to pursue their lawful return or transfer so they may ultimately be reunited with the reconstructed Great House Farm museum. This statement is provided solely as context for my request and does not seek a determination under the Freedom of Information Act.\n\nIf any information is withheld, please identify each exemption relied upon and explain why it applies, including the outcome of any public interest test where required.\n\nIf another public authority is more likely to hold the requested information, I would be grateful if you would transfer the request or advise me accordingly.\n\nI look forward to your response within the statutory time limit.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\nRequester "},{"id":"gm-1a07efaa39026122","subject":"Great House Farm, Llandough, Cardiff","name":"Great House Farm, Llandough, Cardiff","date":"2026-09-08T03:05:49Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a07efaa39026122.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a07efa0b6b10900-great-house-farm-llandough-cardiff","scopeTopics":["heneb"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["heneb"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a07efa0b6b10900","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"heneb","name":"Heneb (formerly GGAT) / Historic Environment Record"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a07efaa39026122.json","textStatus":"available","text":"HER Officer (Heneb: Glamorgan-Gwent),\n\nThis is a request for recorded information under the Environmental\nInformation Regulations 2004 and, so far as applicable, the Freedom of\nInformation Act 2000, and under the public-access duty to the historic\nenvironment record.\n\nI request the full HER source file for Great House Farm, Llandough\n(GGAT02038s and any linked event or find records), specifically:\n\n1. The complete text of the entry or entries that refer to armour or\nmedieval ironwork found under the rear wing / floor c.1880, including every\nsource code and footnote.\n\n2. A copy or transcript of the RCAHMW / Suggett 1988 material held on the\nHER file (GGAT’s 1990 assessment cites “RCAHMW 1988” for the museum\nattribution).\n\n3. Any museum accession number, letter, or catalogue slip on the file that\nidentifies where the ironwork went.\n\n4. Any distinction on the file between:\n   (a) the c.1880 rear-wing ironwork;\n   (b) iron from the 1979 villa excavation south of the church;\n   (c) the 1994 cemetery iron girdle (Burial 631) later examined by the\nNational Museum of Wales.\n\nPlease send electronic copies. I am an interested person researching the\ndocumented history of this site, not a commercial applicant.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester\n[email redacted]"},{"id":"gm-1a07ef9bc9c8d2cd","subject":"Great House Farm, Llandough, Cardiff","name":"Great House Farm, Llandough, Cardiff","date":"2026-09-08T03:04:50Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a07ef9bc9c8d2cd.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a07ef81245f400b-great-house-farm-llandough-cardiff","scopeTopics":["museum"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["museum"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a07ef81245f400b","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"museum","name":"Amgueddfa Cymru - Museum Wales"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a07ef9bc9c8d2cd.json","textStatus":"available","text":"FOI Team,\n\nThis is a new request under the Freedom of Information Act 2000. It is not\na repeat of FOI 2026-024 and it is not a comment on the internal review of\nthat request.\n\nIn your letter of 25 August 2026 (FOI 2026-024) you stated:\n\n  “For completeness, our searches identified records relating to other\narchaeological finds from Church Farm, Llandough and to other\narchaeological discoveries recorded at Great House Farm (Ty Mawr),\nLlandough. These records do not relate to, nor are they otherwise\nassociated with, the discovery described in your request. We have therefore\nnot considered them to fall within the scope of this request.”\n\nI now request those records.\n\nPlease provide:\n\n1. A schedule of every object, archive file, accession, loan, enquiry or\ncorrespondence record that Amgueddfa Cymru holds which is indexed,\nprovenanced or otherwise recorded as from Great House Farm / Ty Mawr /\nLlandough Farm, Llandough (Penarth / Vale of Glamorgan). For each entry:\naccession or archive number, object or file title, date of acquisition or\ncreation, donor or excavator if recorded, and a one-line description.\n\n2. The same schedule for Church Farm, Llandough, but only so far as the\nrecords were retrieved in the 2026-024 search and can be identified without\na new full trawl.\n\n3. Any accession, register entry, day-book, donations book or\ncorrespondence in the collections of Amgueddfa Cymru or of its predecessor\nCardiff Museum / Welsh Museum of Natural History / National Museum of\nWales, dated 1875–1915, recording ironwork, armour, a visor, a lance, a\nhelmet, “armour from Llandough”, or a find from Great House / Llandough\nFarm.\n\n   Published sources which state that such ironwork is in the National\nMuseum, and which therefore make a search of predecessor registers\nreasonable, are:\n\n   • GGAT, Land at Great House Farm, Llandough: An Archaeological\nAssessment, August 1990 (planning file 89/01396/OUT and 92/00671/RES): “it\nis likely that some medieval ironwork which is held by the National Museum\nof Wales was discovered under the rear wing c 1880 (RCAHMW 1988).”\n\n   • Holbrook & Thomas, Medieval Archaeology 49 (2005), p. 70 n. 142:\n“medieval ironwork was discovered under the rear wing of Great House Farm\nc. 1880. It is now in the collections of the National Museums & Galleries\nof Wales.”\n\n   I note that the National Museum of Wales was not founded until 1907. If\na find of c.1880 entered a public collection in Cardiff it would have done\nso through the earlier municipal museum. Please search those predecessor\naccession registers, not only the post-1907 NMW database.\n\n4. Any object record for Llandough ironwork that is not labelled “visor” or\n“lance” but is or was associated with the farmhouse floor or rear wing /\ndairy (the room identified on H. J. Thomas’s 1974 RCAHMW plan).\n\nPlease treat this as a request for recorded information only.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester\n[email redacted]"},{"id":"gm-1a07ef79c476e63d","subject":"Great House Farm, Llandough, Cardiff","name":"Great House Farm, Llandough, Cardiff","date":"2026-09-08T03:02:31Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a07ef79c476e63d.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a07ef67ca845e50-great-house-farm-llandough-cardiff","scopeTopics":["rcahmw"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["rcahmw"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a07ef67ca845e50","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"rcahmw","name":"RCAHMW (Royal Commission)"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a07ef79c476e63d.json","textStatus":"available","text":"National Monuments Record / FOI Officer,\n\nThis is a request under the Freedom of Information Act 2000 and, so far as\nthe records concern the historic environment, the Environmental Information\nRegulations 2004.\n\nI request copies of recorded information held by RCAHMW / the National\nMonuments Record of Wales relating to a reported discovery of ironwork or\n“armour” under the rear wing of Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough, Vale\nof Glamorgan (approx. ST 1681 7331; SMR / HER GGAT02038s; NMR NPRN\nassociated with Great House Farm).\n\nThe published citations which identify RCAHMW as the source, and which\nstate that the objects are at the National Museum of Wales, are:\n\n1. RCAHMW, December 1988 — “Unpublished Records relating to Great House\nFarm prepared by R F Suggett, December 1988.” This is the source cited in\nthe Glamorgan-Gwent Archaeological Trust assessment lodged on planning\napplication 89/01396/OUT (and carried into 92/00671/RES):\n\n   “it is likely that some medieval ironwork which is held by the National\nMuseum of Wales was discovered under the rear wing c 1880 (RCAHMW 1988).”\n\n   Bibliography: RCAHMW 1988 = Unpublished Records relating to Great House\nFarm prepared by R F Suggett, December 1988.\n   (GGAT, Land at Great House Farm, Llandough: An Archaeological\nAssessment, August 1990.)\n\n2. The same statement is repeated in Holbrook & Thomas, “An Early-medieval\nMonastic Cemetery at Llandough, Glamorgan”, Medieval Archaeology 49 (2005),\np. 70 n. 142:\n\n   “Mention should also be made of the fact that medieval ironwork was\ndiscovered under the rear wing of Great House Farm c. 1880. It is now in\nthe collections of the National Museums & Galleries of Wales.”\n\n3. The Heneb / Archwilio HER entry for Great House Farm (GGAT02038s)\nrecords armour / medieval ironwork found under the rear-wing floor c.1880\nand attributes the note to RCAHMW.\n\nPlease therefore provide:\n\nA. The full text of R F Suggett’s December 1988 unpublished records\nrelating to Great House Farm, including every page or card that mentions\nironwork, armour, a visor, a lance, a helmet, a burial, a soldier, a horse,\na museum, or a find under the floor or rear wing / dairy.\n\nB. Any earlier NMR / RCAHMW survey notes, correspondence or drawings that\nSuggett was using (including H. J. Thomas’s 1974 survey of the house,\nupdated December 1988), insofar as they mention the c.1880 find or the\ndestination of the objects.\n\nC. Any record of the source of Suggett’s statement that the ironwork “is\nheld by the National Museum of Wales” — for example a letter, accession\nnote, conversation record, or museum catalogue reference.\n\nD. Any later NMR / Coflein / correspondence file that confirms, corrects or\nwithdraws that museum attribution.\n\nE. File references, NPRNs and any copy letters sent to or received from the\nNational Museum of Wales / Amgueddfa Cymru, GGAT, Cadw or the Vale of\nGlamorgan concerning this find.\n\nI am not asking for advice or for a new interpretation. I am asking for the\nrecorded information that underpins the 1988 sentence now cited as fact in\nthe planning file and in Medieval Archaeology.\n\nPlease supply electronic copies where held. If any item is already\npublished on Coflein, a catalogue link and reference is enough.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester\n[email redacted]"},{"id":"gm-1a07d705f291c33b","subject":"Re: Ty Mawr, Llandough, Cardiff","name":"Re: Ty Mawr, Llandough, Cardiff","date":"2026-09-07T19:55:07Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a07d705f291c33b.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a02ad4c2f0fd6e3-ty-mawr-llandough-cardiff","scopeTopics":["glamorgan"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["glamorgan"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a02ad4c2f0fd6e3","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"glamorgan","name":"Glamorgan Archives"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a07d705f291c33b.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Louise,\n\nThanks for your detailed and prompt reply and for the additional\ninformation concerning the Bute Estate records and D696.\n\nI just want to clarify one important point in relation to my original\nenquiry, as I think I may have caused a slight misunderstanding about the\nparticular transaction I am trying to establish.\n\nMy principal question is *not whether there are deeds between the Bute\nEstate and Alfred Thomas*. The key transaction I am trying to locate and\nverify is the *1877 transaction concerning Daniel Thomas and the Bute\nEstate*, which I understand from the surviving family evidence to have\nconcerned a defined portion of Great House Farm, including the farmhouse\nand surrounding land. I see an 1877 agreement between both parties, for\nthat year, but for a different property in Cardiff. I wondered if it was\nmis labelled and if you could validify its content, particularly any map\ndata. So as the integrity of the descriptor of the record is assured or\nrevisited.\n\nThe issue is whether the Bute Estate *conveyed, granted or otherwise dealt\nwith that defined parcel in favour of Daniel Thomas*, and, crucially, what\nthe instrument and its plan actually show about the boundaries and the\nnature of the interest created. In 1877.\n\nThis is important because the present documentary reconstruction I am\npreparing treats the 1877 instrument as the potential starting point for\nthe separate House Plot chain. The working case is that the instrument\ndealt with the farmhouse land separately from the eastern\nagricultural/quarrying parcel, with provisions connected to quarrying and\nthe subsequent passing of the house land to the occupying Williams family\nwhen quarrying ceased. That proposition is expressly being treated as a\nmatter requiring verification against the original instrument and plan,\nrather than as an established fact.\n\nI therefore wonder whether the catalogue search could be reconsidered\nspecifically on the following basis:\n\n*Bute Estate → Daniel Thomas → defined land at Llandough / Great House\nFarm, approximately 1876–1878*\n\nincluding instruments which may not themselves be catalogued under “Great\nHouse Farm” or “Daniel Thomas”, but which might appear under:\n\n   - the name of the property or former field name;\n   - the Marquess of Bute or his trustees as grantor;\n   - Daniel Thomas as builder, quarryman, lime-burner or occupier;\n   - a lease, conveyance, agreement, surrender or counterpart;\n   - quarrying or mineral rights;\n   - a plan accompanying an instrument;\n   - or a transaction recorded in a Bute Estate rental, ledger or estate\n   correspondence rather than in the deed catalogue.\n\nI appreciate that your catalogue may simply contain no surviving record of\nthis transaction. If that is the position, that is useful information in\nitself. What I am particularly trying to establish is whether *the 1877\ntransaction has been positively searched for and not found*, as opposed to\nthere being no later catalogue entry linking Daniel or Alfred Thomas to the\nBute Estate.\nD696 and the later Western Ground Rents material\n\nThank you also for pointing me towards D696. Please do let me know which\nitem(s) within D696 contain the *book plans and rental books for Western\nGround Rents Ltd c.1938–1944*, particularly anything relating to Great\nHouse Farm, Llandough or the land occupied by John Williams.\n\nThis is potentially very relevant because the documentary issue I am\ninvestigating is whether the later WGR estate corresponded geographically\nwith the parcel dealt with by the Bute Estate in 1877, or instead with the\nseparate agricultural/quarrying parcel.\nBute Estate finding aid\n\nI would also be grateful if you could retain my enquiry against the Bute\nEstate material being transferred from Cardiff Library. In particular, I\nwould be interested in any items concerning:\n\n   - Llandough / Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr;\n   - Daniel Thomas;\n   - the 1870s Bute Estate conveyances or leases;\n   - estate plans showing parcel boundaries;\n   - quarrying or lime works;\n   - rentals distinguishing the farm from the farmhouse/cottages; and\n   - subsequent dealings with the relevant parcels.\n\n1910 Land Valuation map\n\nFinally, thank you for identifying DVAL/1/33. If Glamorgan Archives has any\ninformation enabling me to identify the corresponding *1910 valuation map\nheld by The National Archives*, I would be grateful for the relevant\nreference. I am trying to make a targeted request rather than simply asking\nThe National Archives to search broadly for a Llandough map.\n\nThank you again for your assistance. The clarification regarding what is\nand is not held at Glamorgan Archives is useful, and I appreciate your help\nin narrowing down the surviving documentary trail.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\n*Requester *\n**\n\n\nOn Mon, 7 Sept 2026, 13:46 Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg, <\n[email redacted]> wrote:\n\n> *Ref: 2026/3793*\n>\n>\n>\n> Dear Requester,\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you for your email of 22nd August, received into our enquiries\n> system 24th August.\n>\n>\n>\n> To answer your points in turn:\n>\n>\n>\n>    1. DBDT/10/19 describes Daniel Thomas as ‘of the Town of Cardiff,\n>    Builder’.  There are no further identifying details for Daniel Thomas in\n>    the documents.  We can provide copies of the documents at a charge or they\n>    can be accessed for free in our public searchroom.  Please let us know if\n>    you would like a quote for digital copies.\n>    2. There are no additional deeds described on our catalogue between\n>    Daniel or Alfred Thomas and the Bute Estate.\n>    3. There are no additional records relating to quarrying in Llandough\n>    or around Ty Mawr / Great House Farm.\n>    4. If you have not already got a copy of Daniel Thomas’ will, you can\n>    apply for a copy of probate from the Probate Office: Search probate\n>    records for documents and wills (England and Wales) - GOV.UK\n>    <https://www.gov.uk/search-will-probate>.\n>    5. We think you have probably already covered all the material we have\n>    on this matter but…\n>\n> 1-3)  We do not hold the majority of the Bute Estate records.  I attach a\n> finding aid to the Bute Estate records that are currently held by Cardiff\n> Library. These records are soon to be transferred to us so please let us\n> know if there is anything on this list that you are interested in.  Other\n> records of the Bute Estate can be found at the National Library of Wales.\n>\n> 4)  We are not sure if you have consulted D696 which includes a book plans\n> and rental books for land owned by Western Ground Rents Ltd c1938-1944.\n>\n> 6. Have you looked at historic Ordnance Survey plans to show boundaries?\n> Some of these can be viewed online e.g. Map Finder - with Outlines -\n> National Library of Scotland\n> <https://maps.nls.uk/geo/find/#zoom=15.0&lat=51.44833&lon=-3.19885&layers=101&b=1&z=0&point=51.45192,-3.19801&i=135196558>.\n> Mary consulted the 1910 Land Valuation book for Llandough (DVAL/1/33) but\n> we do not have the corresponding maps to go with this – you would need to\n> contact the National Archives for a copy of this.\n>\n>\n>\n> We hope that this information is of use to you.  Please note that we aim\n> to answer all emails in order but the longer more complicated ones may take\n> up to 15 working days to respond to.\n>\n>\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n> [map link redacted]\n>\n>\n>\n> [map link redacted]\n>\n> Louise Hunt\n>\n> [map link redacted]\n>\n> *Archifydd / Archivist*\n>\n>\n>\n> *Archifau Morgannwg / Glamorgan Archives*\n>\n> *Clos Parc Morgannwg\n> [map link redacted]*\n>\n> *Lecwydd / Leckwith\n> [map link redacted]*\n>\n> *Caerdydd / Cardiff\n> [map link redacted]*\n>\n> *[postcode redacted]\n> [map link redacted]*\n>\n>\n>\n> [phone redacted]\n>\n> *[email redacted]* <[email redacted]>\n>\n>\n>\n> *www.archifaumorgannwg.gov.uk/* <http://www.archifaumorgannwg.gov.uk/>\n> *www.glamarchives.gov.uk* <http://www.glamarchives.gov.uk/>\n>\n> www.facebook.com/glamarchives\n>\n> www.instagram.com/glamarchives\n>\n>\n>\n> *From:* Requester <[email redacted]>\n> *Sent:* 22 August 2026 19:57\n> *To:* Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg <[email redacted]>\n> *Subject:* Ty Mawr, Llandough, Cardiff\n>\n>\n>\n> *EXTERNAL:* This email originated from outside Cardiff Council, take care\n> when clicking links.\n> *ALLANOL:* Daw'r e-bost hwn o'r tu allan i Gyngor Caerdydd, cymerwch ofal\n> wrth glicio ar ddolenni.\n>\n>\n>\n> Glamorgan Archives Team,\n>\n> I am writing to make a targeted archive research enquiry concerning the\n> DBDT collection and any related Bute, Mountjoy or Western Ground Rents\n> material held by Glamorgan Archives.\n>\n> I am investigating the historic title, occupation and parcel boundaries of *\n> Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough*, particularly evidence concerning\n> a possible historical division of the farm between land retained by the\n> Bute Estate and land allegedly sold or transferred to *Daniel Thomas*.\n>\n> A family witness account describes a specific arrangement under which\n> approximately ten acres, including the farmhouse, were allegedly sold by\n> the Bute Estate to Daniel Thomas, while a separate portion of approximately\n> nine acres remained under the Bute Estate. The account further describes\n> quarrying on the Thomas-held land and an agreement under which the freehold\n> would eventually pass to the occupying family when quarrying ceased.\n>\n> I appreciate that this account requires verification against the original\n> documentary record. I am therefore seeking to identify the relevant deeds,\n> agreements, plans and subsequent title documents.\n> 1. Verification of specific DBDT catalogue entries\n>\n> Please confirm the exact property description, parties and date of the\n> following catalogue entries, and, if possible, provide a digital copy or\n> scan of the opening parties clause and any attached or marginal plan:\n>\n>    - *DBDT10/19* – Lease (counterpart), described in the catalogue as\n>    concerning land and messuages in Bute Street, Cardiff, from trustees of the\n>    late Marquess of Bute to Mr Daniel Thomas, October 1850.\n>    - *DBDT10/20* – Agreement for a building lease of premises in Bute\n>    Town, Cardiff, involving the same parties, June 1849.\n>\n> I understand from the catalogue descriptions that these items concern\n> Cardiff rather than Llandough. My purpose is to establish whether the *Daniel\n> Thomas named in these instruments can be identified sufficiently to\n> determine whether he is the same individual later connected with the\n> Llandough lime works and land interests*, or whether these are unrelated\n> transactions involving another person of the same name.\n>\n> Please also advise whether either document contains information\n> identifying Daniel Thomas by residence, occupation, estate, trusteeship or\n> other distinguishing details.\n> 2. Targeted search for a Bute–Daniel Thomas transaction at or near\n> Llandough\n>\n> Please search the catalogue descriptions and indexes available to\n> Glamorgan Archives for any deed, conveyance, exchange, sale, transfer,\n> lease, agreement, plan or related instrument involving:\n>\n>    - *Daniel Thomas*;\n>    - *Alfred Thomas*;\n>    - the *Marquess of Bute*;\n>    - trustees of the Marquess of Bute;\n>    - the *Bute Estate*;\n>\n> and land at or near:\n>\n>    - *Llandough*;\n>    - *Great House Farm / Great House / Tŷ Mawr*;\n>    - adjoining land or parcels that may subsequently have formed part of\n>    the farm.\n>\n> I am particularly interested in transactions dating from approximately *\n> 1876 to 1878*, but would be grateful for references to earlier or later\n> documents where they form part of the same chain of title.\n>\n> In particular, I am seeking evidence capable of confirming or disproving\n> whether any defined parcel of land was conveyed by the Bute Estate to\n> Daniel Thomas.\n> 3. Quarrying, lime works and any conditional reversion or transfer\n>\n> Please identify any relevant records concerning land at Llandough or Great\n> House Farm that refer to:\n>\n>    - quarrying;\n>    - quarries or quarry land;\n>    - lime works or lime kilns;\n>    - mineral rights;\n>    - removal of quarrying machinery;\n>    - reduction or cessation of rent as land was quarried;\n>    - termination of quarrying;\n>    - a future conveyance, reversion, surrender or transfer of a freehold\n>    interest when quarrying ceased.\n>\n> The particular issue I am investigating is whether an agreement existed\n> under which occupation or ownership of a defined parcel was intended to\n> change when quarrying operations came to an end.\n> 4. Daniel Thomas, Alfred Thomas and the Llandough lime works\n>\n> Please identify any documents linking *Daniel Thomas*, or his son *Alfred\n> Thomas (later Lord Pontypridd)*, with:\n>\n>    - land at Llandough;\n>    - the Llandough lime works or lime kilns;\n>    - quarrying operations;\n>    - Great House Farm or adjoining agricultural land;\n>    - any freehold or leasehold interest in the area.\n>\n> I would be particularly interested in estate papers, correspondence,\n> conveyances, wills, probate-related estate records, rentals, rent books,\n> abstracts of title and plans that may trace what became of any Daniel\n> Thomas interest after his death or thereafter.\n> 5. Subsequent title and occupation\n>\n> Please identify any abstracts of title, estate maps, conveyances,\n> correspondence, tenancy records or other material that may assist in\n> establishing:\n>\n>    1. what happened to any freehold interest held by Daniel Thomas or his\n>    estate;\n>    2. whether such an interest was ever conveyed, surrendered or\n>    transferred back to the Bute Estate or another party;\n>    3. whether the Bute Estate later reacquired any relevant parcel;\n>    4. whether later parties, including *Mountjoy Ltd* or *Western Ground\n>    Rents Ltd*, acquired title to land previously held by Daniel Thomas or\n>    his successors;\n>    5. the basis on which later tenancies described as covering the\n>    \"whole\" of Great House Farm were granted.\n>\n> I am also interested in any material linking these title questions with\n> the occupation of the farm by the *Williams family*.\n> 6. Plans and parcel boundaries\n>\n> A separate line of research concerns the possibility that historic legal\n> boundaries passed through or between different parts of the Great House\n> Farm complex.\n>\n> Accordingly, please identify any:\n>\n>    - estate maps;\n>    - conveyance plans;\n>    - tenancy plans;\n>    - quarry plans;\n>    - large-scale plans;\n>    - boundary plans;\n>    - valuation plans;\n>\n> that show Great House Farm, the farmhouse, garden, stables, outbuildings\n> or adjoining parcels.\n>\n> I am particularly interested in documents that could establish whether\n> different parts of the historic building complex fell within different\n> legal parcels.\n>\n> I appreciate that some of these requests may require staff research beyond\n> a standard enquiry. My immediate priority is to obtain *the relevant\n> catalogue references and confirmation of what material exists*. If more\n> detailed searching or document retrieval is necessary, please let me know\n> the applicable procedure, estimated cost and whether a formal paid research\n> request would be required.\n>\n> If relevant documents have been digitised, I would be grateful for details\n> of the cost of obtaining copies. Otherwise, please advise whether they can\n> be consulted in person and whether any restrictions or advance ordering\n> requirements apply.\n>\n> Thank you for your assistance. The documents identified through this\n> enquiry may be important to reconstructing the historical chain of title\n> and occupation of Great House Farm, Llandough from the nineteenth century\n> onwards.\n>\n> Yours faithfully,\n>\n> *Requester *\n>\n> **********************************************************************\n>\n> The Council welcomes correspondence in Welsh, English or bilingually. We\n> will ensure that we communicate with you in the language of your choice, as\n> long as you let us know which you prefer. Corresponding in Welsh will not\n> lead to delay.\n>\n> Privileged/Confidential Information may be contained in this message. If\n> you are not the addressee indicated in this message (or responsible for\n> delivery of the message to such person), you may not copy or deliver this\n> message to anyone. In such case, you should destroy this message and kindly\n> notify the sender by reply email. Please advise immediately if you or your\n> employer does not consent to Internet email for messages of this kind.\n> Opinions, conclusions and other information in this message that do not\n> relate to the official business of the Council of the City and County of\n> Cardiff shall be understood as neither given nor endorsed by it. All e-mail\n> sent to or from this address will be processed by Cardiff County Councils\n> Corporate E-mail system and may be subject to scrutiny by someone other\n> than the addressee.\n>\n> **********************************************************************\n>\n> Mae’r Cyngor yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg, Saesneg neu’n ddwyieithog.\n> Byddwn yn cyfathrebu â chi yn ôl eich dewis, dim ond i chi roi gwybod i ni\n> pa un sydd well gennych. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi.\n>\n> Mae'n bosibl bod gwybodaeth gyfrinachol yn y neges hon. Os na chyfeirir y\n> neges atoch chi'n benodol (neu os nad ydych chi'n gyfrifol am drosglwyddo'r\n> neges i'r person a enwir), yna ni chewch gopio na throsglwyddo'r neges.\n> Mewn achos o'r fath, dylech ddinistrio'r neges a hysbysu'r anfonwr drwy\n> e-bost ar unwaith. Rhowch wybod i'r anfonydd ar unwaith os nad ydych chi\n> neu eich cyflogydd yn caniatau e-bost y Rhyngrwyd am negeseuon fel hon.\n> Rhaid deall nad yw'r safbwyntiau, y casgliadau a'r wybodaeth arall yn y\n> neges hon nad ydynt yn cyfeirio at fusnes swyddogol Cyngor Dinas a Sir\n> Caerdydd yn cynrychioli barn y Cyngor Sir nad yn cael sel ei fendith. Caiff\n> unrhyw negeseuon a anfonir at, neu o'r cyfeiriad e-bost hwn eu prosesu gan\n> system E-bost Gorfforaethol Cyngor Sir Caerdydd a gallant gael eu\n> harchwilio gan rywun heblaw'r person a enwir.\n>\n> **********************************************************************\n>"},{"id":"gm-1a07cc3e251e0967","subject":"RE: Ty Mawr, Llandough, Cardiff","name":"RE: Ty Mawr, Llandough, Cardiff","date":"2026-09-07T16:46:31Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a07cc3e251e0967.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a02ad4c2f0fd6e3-ty-mawr-llandough-cardiff","scopeTopics":["glamorgan"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["glamorgan"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a02ad4c2f0fd6e3","direction":"received","from":"Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg","institutions":[{"id":"glamorgan","name":"Glamorgan Archives"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a07cc3e251e0967.json","attachments":[{"name":"Bute zipped.zip","title":"Bute catalogue documents (41 Word files, zipped)","url":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/pdf/foi/2026-09-07_glamorgan-archives_bute-catalogue-documents.zip","mime":"application/zip","bytes":1058901,"sha256":"892e94db5ef55484d068c7428af6909bdb6e5947fa4e7d28f4285779e53a3893"}],"textStatus":"available","text":"Ref: 2026/3793\n\nDear Requester,\n\nThank you for your email of 22nd August, received into our enquiries system 24th August.\n\nTo answer your points in turn:\n\n\n  1.  DBDT/10/19 describes Daniel Thomas as ‘of the Town of Cardiff, Builder’.  There are no further identifying details for Daniel Thomas in the documents.  We can provide copies of the documents at a charge or they can be accessed for free in our public searchroom.  Please let us know if you would like a quote for digital copies.\n  2.  There are no additional deeds described on our catalogue between Daniel or Alfred Thomas and the Bute Estate.\n  3.  There are no additional records relating to quarrying in Llandough or around Ty Mawr / Great House Farm.\n  4.  If you have not already got a copy of Daniel Thomas’ will, you can apply for a copy of probate from the Probate Office: Search probate records for documents and wills (England and Wales) - GOV.UK<https://www.gov.uk/search-will-probate>.\n  5.  We think you have probably already covered all the material we have on this matter but…\n1-3)  We do not hold the majority of the Bute Estate records.  I attach a finding aid to the Bute Estate records that are currently held by Cardiff Library. These records are soon to be transferred to us so please let us know if there is anything on this list that you are interested in.  Other records of the Bute Estate can be found at the National Library of Wales.\n4)  We are not sure if you have consulted D696 which includes a book plans and rental books for land owned by Western Ground Rents Ltd c1938-1944.\n6. Have you looked at historic Ordnance Survey plans to show boundaries?  Some of these can be viewed online e.g. Map Finder - with Outlines - National Library of Scotland<https://maps.nls.uk/geo/find/#zoom=15.0&lat=51.44833&lon=-3.19885&layers=101&b=1&z=0&point=51.45192,-3.19801&i=135196558>.  Mary consulted the 1910 Land Valuation book for Llandough (DVAL/1/33) but we do not have the corresponding maps to go with this – you would need to contact the National Archives for a copy of this.\n\nWe hope that this information is of use to you.  Please note that we aim to answer all emails in order but the longer more complicated ones may take up to 15 working days to respond to.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nLouise Hunt\nArchifydd / Archivist\n\nArchifau Morgannwg / Glamorgan Archives\nClos Parc Morgannwg\nLecwydd / Leckwith\nCaerdydd / Cardiff\n[postcode redacted]\n\n[phone redacted]\n[email redacted]<[email redacted]>\n\nwww.archifaumorgannwg.gov.uk/<http://www.archifaumorgannwg.gov.uk/> www.glamarchives.gov.uk<http://www.glamarchives.gov.uk/>\nwww.facebook.com/glamarchives<http://www.facebook.com/glamarchives>\nwww.instagram.com/glamarchives<https://www.instagram.com/glamarchives/>\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]>\nSent: 22 August 2026 19:57\nTo: Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg <[email redacted]>\nSubject: Ty Mawr, Llandough, Cardiff\n\nEXTERNAL: This email originated from outside Cardiff Council, take care when clicking links.\nALLANOL: Daw'r e-bost hwn o'r tu allan i Gyngor Caerdydd, cymerwch ofal wrth glicio ar ddolenni.\n\n\nGlamorgan Archives Team,\n\nI am writing to make a targeted archive research enquiry concerning the DBDT collection and any related Bute, Mountjoy or Western Ground Rents material held by Glamorgan Archives.\n\nI am investigating the historic title, occupation and parcel boundaries of Great House Farm (Tŷ Mawr), Llandough, particularly evidence concerning a possible historical division of the farm between land retained by the Bute Estate and land allegedly sold or transferred to Daniel Thomas.\n\nA family witness account describes a specific arrangement under which approximately ten acres, including the farmhouse, were allegedly sold by the Bute Estate to Daniel Thomas, while a separate portion of approximately nine acres remained under the Bute Estate. The account further describes quarrying on the Thomas-held land and an agreement under which the freehold would eventually pass to the occupying family when quarrying ceased.\n\nI appreciate that this account requires verification against the original documentary record. I am therefore seeking to identify the relevant deeds, agreements, plans and subsequent title documents.\n\n1. Verification of specific DBDT catalogue entries\n\nPlease confirm the exact property description, parties and date of the following catalogue entries, and, if possible, provide a digital copy or scan of the opening parties clause and any attached or marginal plan:\n\n  *   DBDT10/19 – Lease (counterpart), described in the catalogue as concerning land and messuages in Bute Street, Cardiff, from trustees of the late Marquess of Bute to Mr Daniel Thomas, October 1850.\n  *   DBDT10/20 – Agreement for a building lease of premises in Bute Town, Cardiff, involving the same parties, June 1849.\n\nI understand from the catalogue descriptions that these items concern Cardiff rather than Llandough. My purpose is to establish whether the Daniel Thomas named in these instruments can be identified sufficiently to determine whether he is the same individual later connected with the Llandough lime works and land interests, or whether these are unrelated transactions involving another person of the same name.\n\nPlease also advise whether either document contains information identifying Daniel Thomas by residence, occupation, estate, trusteeship or other distinguishing details.\n\n2. Targeted search for a Bute–Daniel Thomas transaction at or near Llandough\n\nPlease search the catalogue descriptions and indexes available to Glamorgan Archives for any deed, conveyance, exchange, sale, transfer, lease, agreement, plan or related instrument involving:\n\n  *   Daniel Thomas;\n  *   Alfred Thomas;\n  *   the Marquess of Bute;\n  *   trustees of the Marquess of Bute;\n  *   the Bute Estate;\n\nand land at or near:\n\n  *   Llandough;\n  *   Great House Farm / Great House / Tŷ Mawr;\n  *   adjoining land or parcels that may subsequently have formed part of the farm.\n\nI am particularly interested in transactions dating from approximately 1876 to 1878, but would be grateful for references to earlier or later documents where they form part of the same chain of title.\n\nIn particular, I am seeking evidence capable of confirming or disproving whether any defined parcel of land was conveyed by the Bute Estate to Daniel Thomas.\n\n3. Quarrying, lime works and any conditional reversion or transfer\n\nPlease identify any relevant records concerning land at Llandough or Great House Farm that refer to:\n\n  *   quarrying;\n  *   quarries or quarry land;\n  *   lime works or lime kilns;\n  *   mineral rights;\n  *   removal of quarrying machinery;\n  *   reduction or cessation of rent as land was quarried;\n  *   termination of quarrying;\n  *   a future conveyance, reversion, surrender or transfer of a freehold interest when quarrying ceased.\n\nThe particular issue I am investigating is whether an agreement existed under which occupation or ownership of a defined parcel was intended to change when quarrying operations came to an end.\n\n4. Daniel Thomas, Alfred Thomas and the Llandough lime works\n\nPlease identify any documents linking Daniel Thomas, or his son Alfred Thomas (later Lord Pontypridd), with:\n\n  *   land at Llandough;\n  *   the Llandough lime works or lime kilns;\n  *   quarrying operations;\n  *   Great House Farm or adjoining agricultural land;\n  *   any freehold or leasehold interest in the area.\n\nI would be particularly interested in estate papers, correspondence, conveyances, wills, probate-related estate records, rentals, rent books, abstracts of title and plans that may trace what became of any Daniel Thomas interest after his death or thereafter.\n\n5. Subsequent title and occupation\n\nPlease identify any abstracts of title, estate maps, conveyances, correspondence, tenancy records or other material that may assist in establishing:\n\n  1.  what happened to any freehold interest held by Daniel Thomas or his estate;\n  2.  whether such an interest was ever conveyed, surrendered or transferred back to the Bute Estate or another party;\n  3.  whether the Bute Estate later reacquired any relevant parcel;\n  4.  whether later parties, including Mountjoy Ltd or Western Ground Rents Ltd, acquired title to land previously held by Daniel Thomas or his successors;\n  5.  the basis on which later tenancies described as covering the \"whole\" of Great House Farm were granted.\n\nI am also interested in any material linking these title questions with the occupation of the farm by the Williams family.\n\n6. Plans and parcel boundaries\n\nA separate line of research concerns the possibility that historic legal boundaries passed through or between different parts of the Great House Farm complex.\n\nAccordingly, please identify any:\n\n  *   estate maps;\n  *   conveyance plans;\n  *   tenancy plans;\n  *   quarry plans;\n  *   large-scale plans;\n  *   boundary plans;\n  *   valuation plans;\n\nthat show Great House Farm, the farmhouse, garden, stables, outbuildings or adjoining parcels.\n\nI am particularly interested in documents that could establish whether different parts of the historic building complex fell within different legal parcels.\n\nI appreciate that some of these requests may require staff research beyond a standard enquiry. My immediate priority is to obtain the relevant catalogue references and confirmation of what material exists. If more detailed searching or document retrieval is necessary, please let me know the applicable procedure, estimated cost and whether a formal paid research request would be required.\n\nIf relevant documents have been digitised, I would be grateful for details of the cost of obtaining copies. Otherwise, please advise whether they can be consulted in person and whether any restrictions or advance ordering requirements apply.\n\nThank you for your assistance. The documents identified through this enquiry may be important to reconstructing the historical chain of title and occupation of Great House Farm, Llandough from the nineteenth century onwards.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\nRequester \n\n**********************************************************************\nThe Council welcomes correspondence in Welsh, English or bilingually. We will ensure that we communicate with you in the language of your choice, as long as you let us know which you prefer. Corresponding in Welsh will not lead to delay.\n\nPrivileged/Confidential Information may be contained in this message. If you are not the addressee indicated in this message (or responsible for delivery of the message to such person), you may not copy or deliver this message to anyone. In such case, you should destroy this message and kindly notify the sender by reply email. Please advise immediately if you or your employer does not consent to Internet email for messages of this kind.  Opinions, conclusions and other information in this message that do not relate to the official business of the Council of the City and County of Cardiff shall be understood as neither given nor endorsed by it.  All e-mail sent to or from this address will be processed by Cardiff County Councils Corporate E-mail system and may be subject to scrutiny by someone other than the addressee.\n**********************************************************************\nMae’r Cyngor yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg, Saesneg neu’n ddwyieithog. Byddwn yn cyfathrebu â chi yn ôl eich dewis, dim ond i chi roi gwybod i ni pa un sydd well gennych. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi.\n\nMae'n bosibl bod gwybodaeth gyfrinachol yn y neges hon. Os na chyfeirir y neges atoch chi'n benodol (neu os nad ydych chi'n gyfrifol am drosglwyddo'r neges i'r person a enwir), yna ni chewch gopio na throsglwyddo'r neges. Mewn achos o'r fath, dylech ddinistrio'r neges a hysbysu'r anfonwr drwy e-bost ar unwaith. Rhowch wybod i'r anfonydd ar unwaith os nad ydych chi neu eich cyflogydd yn caniatau e-bost y Rhyngrwyd am negeseuon fel hon. Rhaid deall nad yw'r safbwyntiau, y casgliadau a'r wybodaeth arall yn y neges hon nad ydynt yn cyfeirio at fusnes swyddogol Cyngor Dinas a Sir Caerdydd yn cynrychioli barn y Cyngor Sir nad yn cael sel ei fendith. Caiff unrhyw negeseuon a anfonir at, neu o'r cyfeiriad e-bost hwn eu prosesu gan system E-bost Gorfforaethol Cyngor Sir Caerdydd a gallant gael eu harchwilio gan rywun heblaw'r person a enwir.\n**********************************************************************\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\nAttachments (1)\n- Bute zipped.zip (application/zip, 1034 KB): https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/pdf/foi/2026-09-07_glamorgan-archives_bute-catalogue-documents.zip"},{"id":"gm-1a07c8b70851bfd9","subject":"Re: Epexio contact us form submission","name":"Re: Epexio contact us form submission","date":"2026-09-07T15:45:07Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a07c8b70851bfd9.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a07b3bf0b65e56c-epexio-contact-us-form-submission","scopeTopics":["glamorgan"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["glamorgan"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a07b3bf0b65e56c","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"glamorgan","name":"Glamorgan Archives"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a07c8b70851bfd9.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Alexis Brito / Glamorgan Archives Team,\n\nThank you for your email (ref 2026–3941) on ACA/8/2. I note the £60 quote\nfor 53 images and will revert separately on that copy.\n\nThis email lists only questions I have already asked which remain\nunanswered. It excludes records a family researcher has already seen by\nfamily members at Leckwith.\n\nAlready seen, and not requested again:\n- D160/1–4 as produced (Neath/Aberdare tithe maps — not the Lambert\nWilliams–Bute agreements)\n- DSA/6/701, DSA/6/1158, booklet marked “255” / GD/LA/15/255 (Cowbridge\nfarm)\n- DCO/WHS/2 (Cowbridge memoir)\n- 6 November 1877 counterpart, Bute trustees to John Williams, Plan N5,\nplus the proposed-houses plans\n- Penarth poor-rate 1872–77, history-society calendars, Nancy Buckler\nmanuscript notes\n\nFor each item: held (ref) / not held / held elsewhere / refused. I am in\nPatagonia. If held, say whether a digital copy can be supplied and at what\ncost.\n\n**26 May 2026 (2026/2370)**\n1. 1974 demolition petition, Llandough juxta Penarth — ref or not held.\n\n**29 May 2026 FOIA/EIR (2026/2372)**\n\n2. Williams/Buckler as owners, tenants, or reporters of the 1880 floor\ndiscovery.\n\n3. Gaps / missing volumes / withdrawals 1950–1989.\n\n4. Why the fabric was not scheduled before 1988 (or “Cadw holds this; we\nhold nothing”).\n\n5. Internal preservation assessments.\n6. Planning files for this acreage / demolition / Church View Close — file\nrefs, not class names.\n\n7. When the known archaeology was raised in planning.\n\n8. EIAs / HER / developer briefs 1980–1990.\n\n**27 July / 5 August 2026 (2026/3309)**\n9. DB/E/1–2 Stewart survey / Cedfin.\n\n10. Lambert Williams–Bute agreements 1818–1835 (not the D160 maps already\nseen).\n\n11. Material related to NLW D219.\n\n12. D153/4–8 1876 limeworks severance — not seen.\n\n13. DXJF/64/27–32 full sketch bundle — notes seen; confirm sketches and\nrefs.\n\n14. DCOWHS on Buckler of Penarth (not DCO/WHS/2).\n\n15. Exact ref of the Nov 1877 counterpart already photographed; separate\nJuly 1877 counterpart?\n\n16. Cardiff RDC records for this farm.\n\n17. DRA 418–420 / DRA 5/31 if this site.\n\n18. UDPE/S/1/68.\n\n19. Penarth estate/tithe/OS/boundary plans of the house plot (not Cowbridge\n1924/38).\n\n20. WGR / Mountjoy / BP Pension Trust / BP Properties papers.\n\n21. Associated bundles stored separately.\n\n22. Uncatalogued / recent deposits 1875–1988.\n\n23. Photography for copies to Patagonia — rules.\n\n**22 August 2026 — no reply**\n\n24–29. DBDT10/19 and 10/20 identification of Daniel Thomas; Bute–Thomas\nsearch at Llandough juxta Penarth; quarry/lime/reversion instruments\n(poor-rate occupation already seen); later title; research cost.\n\n**26 August 2026 — no reply**\n30–35. Physical examination of original **DBDT79/1** (catalogued May 1877,\nPembroke Terrace). Treat as a different instrument from the Nov 1877\nLlandough counterpart unless you confirm they are the same.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester\nReader's ticket 2004093\n\nOpen Drafts and send when ready.\nOn Mon, 7 Sept 2026, 06:38 Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg, <\n[email redacted]> wrote:\n\n> Ref: 2026 – 3941\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> Dear Requester,\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you for your e-mail received 29th August regarding obtaining a copy\n> of document reference ACA/8/2.\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> Alternatively, we offer a “copying” service\n> <https://glamarchives.gov.uk/the-shop/copying/> at Glamorgan Archives. We\n> have listed the costs below for your viewing.\n>\n>\n>\n> *Charges*\n>\n> Single image (digital copy)\n>\n> £15.60\n>\n> Subsequent images (per image, from same document)\n>\n> £4.80\n>\n> Multiple images from multiple documents (per hour)\n>\n> £60.00\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> We can confirm that the volume contains 106 pages so would require 53\n> images – total cost *£60* (for one hour work)\n>\n>\n>\n> If you would like to use this service please complete and return the\n> attached form. Once received we will send instructions on how to pay for\n> the service.\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> Please let us know if you have additional queries.\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> Yours sincerely\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> [map link redacted]\n>\n> Alexis Brito\n>\n> [map link redacted]\n>\n> Cynorthwyydd Cofnodion/Records Assistant\n>\n> [map link redacted]\n>\n>\n>\n> Archifau Morgannwg / Glamorgan Archives\n>\n> Clos Parc Morgannwg\n> [map link redacted]\n>\n> Lecwydd / Leckwith\n> [map link redacted]\n>\n> Caerdydd / Cardiff\n> [map link redacted]\n>\n> [postcode redacted]\n> [map link redacted]\n>\n>\n>\n> [phone redacted]\n>\n> www.archifaumorgannwg.gov.uk / www.glamarchives.gov.uk\n>\n> www.facebook.com/glamarchives\n>\n> Tweet@glamarchives\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>