ase correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a06d4518cc4ae45.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a04e2b752f50b6f-great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":["cadw"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["cadw"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a04e2b752f50b6f","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"cadw","name":"Welsh Government / Cadw"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a06d4518cc4ae45.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Thank you Andy,\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester\n\nOn Fri, 4 Sept 2026, 12:49 ,   wrote:\n\n> Dear Requester,\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you for your further emails of 27, 28 and 29 August.  I am sending\n> this email as a single reply to all three of these emails.\n>\n>\n>\n> I appreciate the importance of this issue to you and your family, and we\n> have therefore provided all the records that we hold regarding the 1988\n> listing decision to assist with your research.  However, we have now\n> provided all the information that we hold, which taken together with our\n> previous emails, I consider to be self-explanatory.  There is nothing\n> further that I can add to our previous replies, and it would be\n> inappropriate for me to speculate on the events around the listing\n> assessment without our own contemporary evidence being available to me to\n> substantiate precisely what occurred.  I am sorry that this final reply\n> could not be more helpful to you.\n>\n>\n>\n> If you remain dissatisfied with the way Cadw has dealt with this matter\n> following this email please find a link to the WG Complaints process How\n> to make a complaint about Welsh Government [HTML] | GOV.WALES\n>  \n>\n>\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n>\n>\n> Andy Wigley\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> Dr Andy Wigley\n>\n> Prif Arolygydd – Pennaeth Arolygiaeth yr Amgylchedd Hanesyddol / Chief\n> Inspector - Head of Historic Environment Inspectorate\n>\n> Cadw\n>\n> Dysgu, Cymunedau a Diwylliant / Learning, Communities and Culture\n>\n> Llywodraeth Cymru / Welsh Government\n>\n> Tŷ Ladywell / Ladywell House\n> Y Drenewydd / Newtown\n> Powys\n> [postcode redacted]\n>\n>\n>\n> Ffôn / Tel: [phone redacted]\n> Ffôn symudol / Mobile: [phone redacted]\n>\n> E-bost/ Email: [email redacted] / [email redacted]\n>\n> P Helpwch yr amgylchedd - peidiwch ag argraffu hon os nad oes gwir\n> raid   Help our environment - only print this if really necessary\n>\n> Rydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth Gymraeg. Cewch ateb Cymraeg i bob gohebiaeth\n> Gymraeg ac ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi.\n>\n> We welcome correspondence in Welsh. Correspondence received in Welsh will\n> be answered in Welsh and corresponding in Welsh will not involve any delay.\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> *From:* Requester  \n> *Sent:* 29 August 2026 18:15\n> *To:* Wigley, Andy (LCC - Cadw)  \n> *Cc:* Cadw Planning  ; Longford, Amy (LCC - Cadw)\n>  ; Weatherhead, Michael (LCC - Cadw)\n>  \n> *Subject:* Great House Farm, Llandough\n>\n>\n>\n> Sirs/Ma'am,\n>\n> Further to my last two emails this week, and in response to yours, which\n> is appreciated, please see the attached documents.\n>\n> My latest version file on yourselves; CADW. And a mock application to a\n> mock AI chancery court; a simulation I'm running in a karpathy autoresearch\n> loop as a long horizon agent to refine the arguments my family made for the\n> last century before being silenced. And spending the last 4 decades like\n> hostage prisoners of war.\n>\n> While CADW may ultimately have played what could appear to be a relatively\n> small part in the wider circumstances surrounding my family's dispossession\n> of our home and land, I want that part to be fully understood, accurately\n> recorded and, where necessary, properly scrutinised. Any possible\n> institutional failure you might have identified isn't the ice berge here, I\n> assure you. Not even the tip of it.\n>\n> I am not approaching this simply to apportion blame, although I am both\n> horrified and furious reading into all this. I am trying to establish, as\n> honestly and completely as I can, what happened, what was known at the\n> time, what was recorded, what decisions were made, and whether proper care\n> was exercised when it mattered.\n>\n> Thank you in advance for your cooperation. This may ultimately develop\n> into a formal complaint, and if it does, CADW will of course be given a\n> fair opportunity to respond to the concerns we identify. At this stage,\n> however, I am trying to understand the full scope of what happened before\n> reaching those conclusions.\n>\n> As my research continues, I may also be in a position to provide CADW with\n> records concerning your own involvement which may not presently be readily\n> apparent from your current records or understanding of the matter. I hope\n> that, if that happens, they will be received in the same spirit in which I\n> am providing them: to establish the historical truth.\n>\n> In the much wider context of my family's grievances concerning BP and the\n> local authorities, and the eventual destruction of what had been described\n> as a \"county treasure\", CADW may prove to be only a small part of the\n> overall story. Nevertheless, I have also attached a draft Chancery Court\n> application so that you can see the wider context of our grievance and\n> understand where CADW's involvement sits within it.\n>\n> I believe that context is important.\n>\n> For 38 years, my family has lived with the consequences of what happened\n> in 1988. We were portrayed publicly as little more than \"squatters being\n> evicted\", and that description has stayed with us and shamed us ever since.\n> The reality was considerably more complicated and, for my family,\n> profoundly distressing. It was, in fact, the first time we had been off our\n> land — land which we may even still own — since the 16th century, having\n> acquired the estate in 1667.\n>\n> It remains a theory, but it is possible that an alternate, untold story is\n> yet to be told, including that Llandough was once called Cedwyn Estate, and\n> that the 1,000-year freehold for it, with 640 years left on that agreement,\n> passed from Sir Herbert of Swansea to the Vaughan family and onto us, not\n> Bute.\n>\n> Given that we occupied the manorial house for so many centuries, it is\n> entirely plausible that we are the rightful holders of the freehold — and\n> that the heirlooms Bute purchased from the last heirs of the Vaughan family\n> did not actually include Cedwyn Estate. He nevertheless acted as though he\n> did, dividing the land parcels. Developing his houses and the town etc. And\n> I note things like the 1824 David Stewart survey in which our estate, being\n> reduced to a home on the parcel it occupied, was recorded as Great House\n> alias \"Cedfin\". Elsewhere Ked fin. And other variations.\n>\n> The same method used in 1974 with BP's licence letters was also used in\n> 1955 (rendering our owner/occupier status merely permissive), and this may\n> have occurred on multiple other land parcels prior to this — potentially in\n> clandestine collaborations with the British State, given that 33 acres were\n> carved out for the limeworks in the WWI effort, and War Ag occupied the\n> land adjacent to ours in WWII. The council also absorbed our \"playing\n> fields\" and so forth: a sort of eminent domain without our family receiving\n> proper reparations (except once, in 1878). Llandough may have been the last\n> of Wales to be colonised.\n>\n> I can tell you as a soldier. In Nicosia the indigenous Greeks and many\n> Turks too, would approach the green line with old records of their land\n> holding the UN occupied, even 4 decades later, and the UN would have to\n> surrender those lands back to the indigenous. I draw a lot of parallels\n> between this, our case and Wales' dream of devolution/ decolonisation. This\n> case also might show we've been in a land market bubble and so a land\n> market crash is coming. Our case isn't isolated or unique.\n>\n> I was a child in that house. My mother was pregnant, and the three of us\n> were still young children. The people who removed us by force — following\n> BP and the UK Government (synonymous at the time) after awarding themselves\n> victory in their courts, (Magistrates as oppose to Chancery or before the\n> Bench as a matter of equity, as should have happened on the merits of this\n> case) — caused my mother's shoulder to become dislocated when she attempted\n> to serve them with a High Court injunction ordering the occupiers not to\n> destroy our home or remove our belongings. Despite that injunction, the\n> house was ultimately destroyed and our possessions removed, including all\n> our furniture, which came from Cardiff Castle, £30,000 of goods, a stone\n> Roman-villa-type pillar, and so forth.\n>\n> The ITV coverage at the time showed some of what happened, including the\n> injury to my mother, but not that she was pregnant and that three young\n> children were inside. ITV, it seems, never aired that footage, and instead\n> of responding to my FOI request, they quietly published it to a YouTube\n> archive last year — apparently hoping that I, or my AI agents, or anyone\n> else, would not notice this suppression. I also hold other ITV footage\n> which they have not admitted to or disclosed, showing my younger baby\n> sister held to watch the looting of our home.\n>\n> The MP is hesitant to respond, the Llandough councillor will not respond,\n> and the longstanding Llandough clerk retired after initially trying, and\n> failing, to dismiss me. I am ashamed of my nation. I served in war too, and\n> left my military career with honours; I am bona fide, having also completed\n> 22 years in Oath in 2024, the last 2 under the employ of the Vale of\n> Glamorgan Council in adult care. The Land Registry also tried everything to\n> steer me away from identifying their mistake in allowing BP to register our\n> land in their name, without our ownership claim being lodged, in 1983. The\n> fact that my nan — who had already spent 40 years challenging her ownership\n> claim by 1983 — died within the two weeks she would have had to challenge\n> BP's registration of our land in their name, is another matter requiring\n> investigation.\n>\n> Much like my late father's sudden death at age 41, between BP's\n> application and approval of housing development, his ECHR claim was\n> rejected, and his appeal of the 1987 ruling was dismissed — on the grounds\n> that BP's companies were treated as one and the same. Yet in the ruling\n> itself, BP's companies were considered separately, meaning our challenge to\n> one would not have affected the other. Outrageous. The same judge also went\n> out of his way to say my nan \"conceived the notion she owned\" and said the\n> unajudicated ownership dispute, set aside to award BP possession of our\n> land, was final. Very suspect and nobody raised questions or challenged\n> except our family i.e. 'Buckler vs United Kingdom' at the ECHR in 1988/89.\n> Who also ruled in lieu of BP rights over ours as the owners. In 1974 the\n> ownership hearing was ajourned. Ownership to this day has never been\n> properly adjudicated.\n>\n> For us, this was not simply a \"squatters being evicted\" case, as was\n> falsely reported in the news. It was the loss of our family home since\n> 1667, and the forceful and prolonged seperation and severing of our\n> connection to that land, which our family had maintained for generations\n> and owned, and still appear to own in spite of who awarded themselves\n> possession of it or occupy it — and perhaps not just the parcel we were on,\n> but half the town of Llandough, by birthright. With 6 centuries left on\n> that freehold from the Knight in 1500, to a military commander and leader\n> like myself. The true title chain. All the while the occupiers operate from\n> defective title. It's possible getting to me may prove more difficult than\n> how my dad before me or nan before him were gotten to, as I am a military\n> warfare professional of almost 3 decades now.\n>\n> It is otherwise difficult to explain what all we went through does to a\n> family, particularly when the public account of what happened does not\n> reflect the reality experienced by those of us who were inside the house at\n> the time. My younger sister had nightmares when I brought this up with her.\n> It's subsequently cruel to see her having to visit archieves for a 4th\n> time, to try to see again what we are there to see.\n>\n> I therefore hope that CADW will understand why I am asking you to approach\n> this matter with some care and humanity, as well as with the proper\n> historical and professional scrutiny that it deserves. The non response\n> from the Llandough councillor, classification of the 133 house plans on the\n> council planning portal, delay from MP responding, is also an outrage. And\n> clear bias towards the houses being developed on our stolen occupied land,\n> by the holders of the defective title. No wonder my surviving family, of\n> mainly women, aren't speaking up or out. It just horrifies me how they have\n> had to live like such hostages under such terrorists. After a literal act\n> of terrorism against us.\n>\n> I was involved in the effort in Iraq to break two SAS soldiers out of an\n> Iraq prison under police guard, days before their execution. Me and a lot\n> of the squadrons of soldiers I have around me to this day. I see my family\n> in a similar light there, under the tyranny of this. And I am not standing\n> for it. I'm only 41, I'm in full mental and physical health. I may have a\n> lot of years left in my life which I'm able to dedicate to this.\n>\n> I have also passed this matter to my Cardiff based BBC producer friend,\n> who also confirmed they'll be looking into this too. And Gareth Wyn Jones\n> and others will a platform. This is a matter of public and national\n> interest. It should not be drawn into clandestine warfare, with silence and\n> violence, albeit that is my domain. Because there's already been plenty of\n> it by the looks of it. I want this to remain in the open. Emails, open\n> letters, courts and Senedd public enquiry etc.\n>\n> Application of Property Law, did in this case, result in an inhumane\n> outcome in my estimation. It may need to be petitioned to be rectified.\n>\n> I am not asking CADW to accept my conclusions simply because I have\n> reached them. I am asking you to look at the evidence, to consider what\n> your own records show, and to be willing to acknowledge anything that may\n> have been overlooked, misunderstood, or inadequately dealt with at the time.\n>\n> If mistakes were made, I would much rather see them acknowledged and\n> properly understood than defended simply because they are now part of an\n> institutional record.\n>\n> I trust you will now appreciate the importance, significance and magnitude\n> of this matter for my family. I will continue to provide the material I\n> uncover, and I sincerely hope that CADW will continue to give it the proper\n> care and attention it deserves.\n>\n> Regards,\n>\n>\n>\n> Requester \n>\n> \n> Sganiwyd y neges hon am bob feirws hysbys wrth iddi adael Llywodraeth\n> Cymru. Mae Llywodraeth Cymru yn cymryd o ddifrif yr angen i ddiogelu eich\n> data. Os cysylltwch â Llywodraeth Cymru, mae ein hysbysiad preifatrwydd\n> yn esbonio sut rydym yn defnyddio eich gwybodaeth a sut rydym yn diogelu\n> eich preifatrwydd. Rydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg. Byddwn yn anfon\n> ateb yn Gymraeg i ohebiaeth a dderbynnir yn Gymraeg ac ni fydd gohebu yn\n> Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi. ----------------- On leaving the Welsh\n> Government this email was scanned for all known viruses. The Welsh\n> Government takes the protection of your data seriously. If you contact the\n> Welsh Government then our Privacy Notice\n>  \n> explains how we use your information and the ways in which we protect your\n> privacy. We welcome receiving correspondence in Welsh. Any correspondence\n> received in Welsh will be answered in Welsh and corresponding in Welsh will\n> not lead to a delay in responding.\n>"},{"id":"gm-1a06d1c7a5b0af60","subject":"RE: Great House Farm, Llandough","name":"RE: Great House Farm, Llandough","date":"2026-09-04T15:49:34Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a06d1c7a5b0af60.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a04e2b752f50b6f-great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":["cadw"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["cadw"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a04e2b752f50b6f","direction":"received","from":"(name not shown; gov.wales)","institutions":[{"id":"cadw","name":"Welsh Government / Cadw"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a06d1c7a5b0af60.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester,\n\nThank you for your further emails of 27, 28 and 29 August.  I am sending this email as a single reply to all three of these emails.\n\nI appreciate the importance of this issue to you and your family, and we have therefore provided all the records that we hold regarding the 1988 listing decision to assist with your research.  However, we have now provided all the information that we hold, which taken together with our previous emails, I consider to be self-explanatory.  There is nothing further that I can add to our previous replies, and it would be inappropriate for me to speculate on the events around the listing assessment without our own contemporary evidence being available to me to substantiate precisely what occurred.  I am sorry that this final reply could not be more helpful to you.\n\nIf you remain dissatisfied with the way Cadw has dealt with this matter following this email please find a link to the WG Complaints process How to make a complaint about Welsh Government [HTML] | GOV.WALES<https://www.gov.wales/how-make-complaint-about-welsh-government-html>\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nAndy Wigley\n\n[cid:image001.png@01DD3C89.719529D0]\n\nDr Andy Wigley\nPrif Arolygydd – Pennaeth Arolygiaeth yr Amgylchedd Hanesyddol / Chief Inspector - Head of Historic Environment Inspectorate\nCadw\nDysgu, Cymunedau a Diwylliant / Learning, Communities and Culture\nLlywodraeth Cymru / Welsh Government\nTŷ Ladywell / Ladywell House\nY Drenewydd / Newtown\nPowys\n[postcode redacted]\n\nFfôn / Tel: [phone redacted]\nFfôn symudol / Mobile: [phone redacted]\nE-bost/ Email: [email redacted]<[email redacted]> / [email redacted]<[email redacted]>\n[cid:image002.png@01DD3C89.719529D0][cid:image003.png@01DD3C89.719529D0]\nP Helpwch yr amgylchedd - peidiwch ag argraffu hon os nad oes gwir raid   Help our environment - only print this if really necessary\nRydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth Gymraeg. Cewch ateb Cymraeg i bob gohebiaeth Gymraeg ac ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi.\nWe welcome correspondence in Welsh. Correspondence received in Welsh will be answered in Welsh and corresponding in Welsh will not involve any delay.\n\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]>\nSent: 29 August 2026 18:15\nTo: Wigley, Andy (LCC - Cadw) <[email redacted]>\nCc: Cadw Planning <[email redacted]>; Longford, Amy (LCC - Cadw) <[email redacted]>; Weatherhead, Michael (LCC - Cadw) <[email redacted]>\nSubject: Great House Farm, Llandough\n\n\nSirs/Ma'am,\n\nFurther to my last two emails this week, and in response to yours, which is appreciated, please see the attached documents.\n\nMy latest version file on yourselves; CADW. And a mock application to a mock AI chancery court; a simulation I'm running in a karpathy autoresearch loop as a long horizon agent to refine the arguments my family made for the last century before being silenced. And spending the last 4 decades like hostage prisoners of war.\n\nWhile CADW may ultimately have played what could appear to be a relatively small part in the wider circumstances surrounding my family's dispossession of our home and land, I want that part to be fully understood, accurately recorded and, where necessary, properly scrutinised. Any possible institutional failure you might have identified isn't the ice berge here, I assure you. Not even the tip of it.\n\nI am not approaching this simply to apportion blame, although I am both horrified and furious reading into all this. I am trying to establish, as honestly and completely as I can, what happened, what was known at the time, what was recorded, what decisions were made, and whether proper care was exercised when it mattered.\n\nThank you in advance for your cooperation. This may ultimately develop into a formal complaint, and if it does, CADW will of course be given a fair opportunity to respond to the concerns we identify. At this stage, however, I am trying to understand the full scope of what happened before reaching those conclusions.\n\nAs my research continues, I may also be in a position to provide CADW with records concerning your own involvement which may not presently be readily apparent from your current records or understanding of the matter. I hope that, if that happens, they will be received in the same spirit in which I am providing them: to establish the historical truth.\n\nIn the much wider context of my family's grievances concerning BP and the local authorities, and the eventual destruction of what had been described as a \"county treasure\", CADW may prove to be only a small part of the overall story. Nevertheless, I have also attached a draft Chancery Court application so that you can see the wider context of our grievance and understand where CADW's involvement sits within it.\n\nI believe that context is important.\n\nFor 38 years, my family has lived with the consequences of what happened in 1988. We were portrayed publicly as little more than \"squatters being evicted\", and that description has stayed with us and shamed us ever since. The reality was considerably more complicated and, for my family, profoundly distressing. It was, in fact, the first time we had been off our land — land which we may even still own — since the 16th century, having acquired the estate in 1667.\n\nIt remains a theory, but it is possible that an alternate, untold story is yet to be told, including that Llandough was once called Cedwyn Estate, and that the 1,000-year freehold for it, with 640 years left on that agreement, passed from Sir Herbert of Swansea to the Vaughan family and onto us, not Bute.\n\nGiven that we occupied the manorial house for so many centuries, it is entirely plausible that we are the rightful holders of the freehold — and that the heirlooms Bute purchased from the last heirs of the Vaughan family did not actually include Cedwyn Estate. He nevertheless acted as though he did, dividing the land parcels. Developing his houses and the town etc. And I note things like the 1824 David Stewart survey in which our estate, being reduced to a home on the parcel it occupied, was recorded as Great House alias \"Cedfin\". Elsewhere Ked fin. And other variations.\n\nThe same method used in 1974 with BP's licence letters was also used in 1955 (rendering our owner/occupier status merely permissive), and this may have occurred on multiple other land parcels prior to this — potentially in clandestine collaborations with the British State, given that 33 acres were carved out for the limeworks in the WWI effort, and War Ag occupied the land adjacent to ours in WWII. The council also absorbed our \"playing fields\" and so forth: a sort of eminent domain without our family receiving proper reparations (except once, in 1878). Llandough may have been the last of Wales to be colonised.\n\nI can tell you as a soldier. In Nicosia the indigenous Greeks and many Turks too, would approach the green line with old records of their land holding the UN occupied, even 4 decades later, and the UN would have to surrender those lands back to the indigenous. I draw a lot of parallels between this, our case and Wales' dream of devolution/ decolonisation. This case also might show we've been in a land market bubble and so a land market crash is coming. Our case isn't isolated or unique.\n\nI was a child in that house. My mother was pregnant, and the three of us were still young children. The people who removed us by force — following BP and the UK Government (synonymous at the time) after awarding themselves victory in their courts, (Magistrates as oppose to Chancery or before the Bench as a matter of equity, as should have happened on the merits of this case) — caused my mother's shoulder to become dislocated when she attempted to serve them with a High Court injunction ordering the occupiers not to destroy our home or remove our belongings. Despite that injunction, the house was ultimately destroyed and our possessions removed, including all our furniture, which came from Cardiff Castle, £30,000 of goods, a stone Roman-villa-type pillar, and so forth.\n\nThe ITV coverage at the time showed some of what happened, including the injury to my mother, but not that she was pregnant and that three young children were inside. ITV, it seems, never aired that footage, and instead of responding to my FOI request, they quietly published it to a YouTube archive last year — apparently hoping that I, or my AI agents, or anyone else, would not notice this suppression. I also hold other ITV footage which they have not admitted to or disclosed, showing my younger baby sister held to watch the looting of our home.\n\nThe MP is hesitant to respond, the Llandough councillor will not respond, and the longstanding Llandough clerk retired after initially trying, and failing, to dismiss me. I am ashamed of my nation. I served in war too, and left my military career with honours; I am bona fide, having also completed 22 years in Oath in 2024, the last 2 under the employ of the Vale of Glamorgan Council in adult care. The Land Registry also tried everything to steer me away from identifying their mistake in allowing BP to register our land in their name, without our ownership claim being lodged, in 1983. The fact that my nan — who had already spent 40 years challenging her ownership claim by 1983 — died within the two weeks she would have had to challenge BP's registration of our land in their name, is another matter requiring investigation.\n\nMuch like my late father's sudden death at age 41, between BP's application and approval of housing development, his ECHR claim was rejected, and his appeal of the 1987 ruling was dismissed — on the grounds that BP's companies were treated as one and the same. Yet in the ruling itself, BP's companies were considered separately, meaning our challenge to one would not have affected the other. Outrageous. The same judge also went out of his way to say my nan \"conceived the notion she owned\" and said the unajudicated ownership dispute, set aside to award BP possession of our land, was final. Very suspect and nobody raised questions or challenged except our family i.e. 'Buckler vs United Kingdom' at the ECHR in 1988/89. Who also ruled in lieu of BP rights over ours as the owners. In 1974 the ownership hearing was ajourned. Ownership to this day has never been properly adjudicated.\n\nFor us, this was not simply a \"squatters being evicted\" case, as was falsely reported in the news. It was the loss of our family home since 1667, and the forceful and prolonged seperation and severing of our connection to that land, which our family had maintained for generations and owned, and still appear to own in spite of who awarded themselves possession of it or occupy it — and perhaps not just the parcel we were on, but half the town of Llandough, by birthright. With 6 centuries left on that freehold from the Knight in 1500, to a military commander and leader like myself. The true title chain. All the while the occupiers operate from defective title. It's possible getting to me may prove more difficult than how my dad before me or nan before him were gotten to, as I am a military warfare professional of almost 3 decades now.\n\nIt is otherwise difficult to explain what all we went through does to a family, particularly when the public account of what happened does not reflect the reality experienced by those of us who were inside the house at the time. My younger sister had nightmares when I brought this up with her. It's subsequently cruel to see her having to visit archieves for a 4th time, to try to see again what we are there to see.\n\nI therefore hope that CADW will understand why I am asking you to approach this matter with some care and humanity, as well as with the proper historical and professional scrutiny that it deserves. The non response from the Llandough councillor, classification of the 133 house plans on the council planning portal, delay from MP responding, is also an outrage. And clear bias towards the houses being developed on our stolen occupied land, by the holders of the defective title. No wonder my surviving family, of mainly women, aren't speaking up or out. It just horrifies me how they have had to live like such hostages under such terrorists. After a literal act of terrorism against us.\n\nI was involved in the effort in Iraq to break two SAS soldiers out of an Iraq prison under police guard, days before their execution. Me and a lot of the squadrons of soldiers I have around me to this day. I see my family in a similar light there, under the tyranny of this. And I am not standing for it. I'm only 41, I'm in full mental and physical health. I may have a lot of years left in my life which I'm able to dedicate to this.\n\nI have also passed this matter to my Cardiff based BBC producer friend, who also confirmed they'll be looking into this too. And Gareth Wyn Jones and others will a platform. This is a matter of public and national interest. It should not be drawn into clandestine warfare, with silence and violence, albeit that is my domain. Because there's already been plenty of it by the looks of it. I want this to remain in the open. Emails, open letters, courts and Senedd public enquiry etc.\n\nApplication of Property Law, did in this case, result in an inhumane outcome in my estimation. It may need to be petitioned to be rectified.\n\nI am not asking CADW to accept my conclusions simply because I have reached them. I am asking you to look at the evidence, to consider what your own records show, and to be willing to acknowledge anything that may have been overlooked, misunderstood, or inadequately dealt with at the time.\n\nIf mistakes were made, I would much rather see them acknowledged and properly understood than defended simply because they are now part of an institutional record.\n\nI trust you will now appreciate the importance, significance and magnitude of this matter for my family. I will continue to provide the material I uncover, and I sincerely hope that CADW will continue to give it the proper care and attention it deserves.\n\nRegards,\n\n\n\nRequester \n\n\n\nSganiwyd y neges hon am bob feirws hysbys wrth iddi adael Llywodraeth Cymru. Mae Llywodraeth Cymru yn cymryd o ddifrif yr angen i ddiogelu eich data. Os cysylltwch â Llywodraeth Cymru, mae ein hysbysiad preifatrwydd<”https://gov.wales/about/welsh-government-privacy-notice/?lang=cy”> yn esbonio sut rydym yn defnyddio eich gwybodaeth a sut rydym yn diogelu eich preifatrwydd. Rydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg. Byddwn yn anfon ateb yn Gymraeg i ohebiaeth a dderbynnir yn Gymraeg ac ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi. ----------------- On leaving the Welsh Government this email was scanned for all known viruses. The Welsh Government takes the protection of your data seriously. If you contact the Welsh Government then our Privacy Notice<https://gov.wales/about/welsh-government-privacy-notice/?lang=en> explains how we use your information and the ways in which we protect your privacy. We welcome receiving correspondence in Welsh. Any correspondence received in Welsh will be answered in Welsh and corresponding in Welsh will not lead to a delay in responding."},{"id":"gm-1a06946d28a86137","subject":"Automatic reply: External Email - Re-Opening of BP vs Buckler 1987","name":"Automatic reply: External Email - Re-Opening of BP vs Buckler 1987","date":"2026-09-03T21:57:01Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a06946d28a86137.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a06946d28a86137-automatic-reply-external-email-re-opening-of-bp-vs-buckler-1987","scopeTopics":["cps"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["cps"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a06946d28a86137","direction":"received","from":"Victim Liaison Cymru-Wales","institutions":[{"id":"cps","name":"Crown Prosecution Service"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a06946d28a86137.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Diolch am e-bostio Uned Cyswllt Dioddefwyr CPS Cymru.\n\nEin nod yw cydnabod eich ymholiad o fewn 3 diwrnod gwaith. Byddwn yn cysylltu â chi os bydd angen rhagor o wybodaeth arnom.\n\nYn y cyfamser, os hoffech gysylltu i drafod eich mater, ffoniwch ni ar [phone redacted]\n\nSylwch, os ydych wedi cael gwybod yn flaenorol na all y CPS roi cymorth i chi ac nad yw eich ymholiad yn codi unrhyw faterion newydd, ni fyddwn yn gallu darparu unrhyw ymateb pellach.\n\nOs nad yw'ch ymholiad yn ymwneud â'r CPS ni fyddwn yn gallu darparu ymateb, ond efallai y gallwn ddarparu manylion cyswllt ar gyfer yr asiantaeth neu'r sefydliad perthnasol.\n\n\nThank you for e-mailing CPS Cymru-Wales Victim Liaison Unit.\n\nWe aim to acknowledge your query within 3 working days. We will contact you if we require any further information.\n\nIn the meantime, if you would like to get in touch to discuss your issue please call us on [phone redacted]\n\nPlease note that if you have previously been advised that the CPS cannot provide assistance to you and your query does not raise any new issues we will not be able to provide any further response.\n\nIf your enquiry does not relate to the CPS we will be unable to provide a response, but may be able to provide contact details for the relevant agency or organisation."},{"id":"gm-1a06945131f0d7b3","subject":"Re-Opening of BP vs Buckler 1987","name":"Re-Opening of BP vs Buckler 1987","date":"2026-09-03T21:55:28Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a06945131f0d7b3.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0693988a9de5f0-opening-of-bp-vs-buckler-1987","scopeTopics":["cps"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["cps"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0693988a9de5f0","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"cps","name":"Crown Prosecution Service"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a06945131f0d7b3.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Sir/Madam,\n\nI write to formally place before the Crown Prosecution Service evidence\nconcerning suspected historical fraud and related criminal conduct arising\nfrom the dispossession and subsequent treatment of the former Great House\nFarm, Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan.\n\nI am currently investigating the matter and preparing proceedings\nconcerning the underlying proprietary dispute. I am also considering the\ninstitution of a *private prosecution* in respect of any criminal offences\ndisclosed by the evidence.\n\nI specifically invite the CPS to consider, at this stage, whether this is a\ncase in which the Director of Public Prosecutions should exercise the power\nunder *section 6(2) of the Prosecution of Offences Act 1985 to take over\nthe conduct of a private prosecution*, should proceedings be instituted.\n\nThe principal concern is not simply the disputed ownership of land. The\nhistorical evidence appears to disclose a possible pattern in which the\nfamily's established occupation and proprietary position were progressively\ncharacterised as merely permissive.\n\nOf particular significance is the apparent treatment of the occupation in\nthe *1955 proceedings*, followed by the more explicit attempt in *1974* to\nestablish a rent-free licence arrangement concerning the property. I am\ninvestigating whether these were independent legal positions or whether\nthey formed part of a longer and coordinated course of conduct intended to\ncreate or manufacture a documentary/legal basis for treating an established\nproprietary occupation as permissive, thereby facilitating the acquisition\nor retention of the land.\n\nThe subsequent involvement of *BP Properties Ltd and BP Pension Trust*, the\nregistration of title WA231076 in 1982, and the litigation culminating in\nthe 1987 Court of Appeal proceedings are material to that investigation.\n\nI do not ask the CPS to determine the civil title dispute. The question I\nask is whether the documentary sequence discloses evidence capable of\nsupporting criminal offences involving fraud, conspiracy, false or\nmisleading representations, concealment of material information, or other\noffences arising from the alleged acquisition or preservation of property\nrights.\n\nI am particularly concerned that conduct occurring at different stages may\nonly become intelligible when examined as a single historical sequence\nrather than as isolated civil proceedings. The apparent attempt to\nestablish permissive occupation in 1955, the subsequent licence proposal in\n1974, the later registration of title, and the representations made in the\nlitigation may therefore warrant consideration together.\n\nI recognise that the CPS will need sufficient evidence to apply the Full\nCode Test and that the existence of a civil dispute does not itself\nestablish criminal conduct. I am not presenting the allegation as proved. I\nam presenting the documentary evidence and asking the prosecuting authority\nto determine whether it warrants criminal investigation and/or prosecution.\n\nThe CPS's published guidance confirms that a private prosecutor may ask the\nCPS to take over a private prosecution and that, when specifically\nrequested to intervene, the CPS must make a decision whether or not to take\nit over. The guidance further recognises that the CPS may take over and\ncontinue a private prosecution where the Full Code Test is satisfied and\nthere is a particular need for the CPS to conduct the prosecution,\nincluding in serious or complex cases or cases involving substantial\ndisclosure issues.\n\nI therefore expressly request that the CPS:\n\n   1.\n\n   *Consider whether it would be prepared to take over a private\n   prosecution under section 6(2) of the Prosecution of Offences Act 1985 if\n   proceedings are instituted;*\n   2.\n\n   *Confirm whether the matter should instead be referred to an\n   investigative authority for investigation before the CPS considers taking\n   over proceedings;*\n   3.\n\n   *Identify the appropriate CPS division or office to which the evidence\n   should be submitted; and*\n   4.\n\n   *If the CPS is not prepared to consider taking over such proceedings,\n   expressly confirm that position and the reason for it.*\n\nI am able to provide an indexed evidential bundle containing the relevant\n1955 and 1974 material, historical conveyances and plans, Land Registry\ndocumentation concerning WA231076, relevant court records and a chronology\nidentifying the specific documentary inconsistencies and matters which I\nconsider require investigation.\n\nI would prefer the CPS to have the opportunity to consider the matter at\nthis stage rather than for a private prosecution to be commenced without\nfirst giving the public prosecuting authority an opportunity to determine\nwhether it considers the case sufficiently serious, complex or otherwise\nappropriate for public prosecution.\n\nPlease therefore treat this correspondence as a *formal request for\nconsideration of CPS involvement and potential exercise of the DPP's\nsection 6(2) power*, and confirm the appropriate procedure for submitting\nthe evidence for that purpose.\n\nThere's 133 houses approved for development on land that I believe to be\nconcealed sacred even holy ancient welsh woodland. And my land as\nsurviving, unestiguished claim and heir and successor of that claim has\nbeen a position our family have maintain unto our suspicious and\nconveniently timed deaths (my father while planning was approved for 20\nhouses, my nan before him in the 2 weeks she will have had to challenge BP\nregistering and merging our land as theirs at the land registry in 1983).\nThe current possessor holds defective title, in my humble estimation. And I\nhold interest in the land, which is being ignored along with my concerns,\nby local authories who have vested interest in the development of the\nhouses. The councillor for Llandough and MP both will not reply. The\ncouncil clerk retired. This is being dragged into the shadows and I suspect\nclandestine warfare, silence and violence. Whereas I am trying to keep the\nmatter open, transparent and in the public eye as it is a matter of public\ninterest being a site of historic and archeological signficants I.e.\nmonestry, roman villa, county treasure home, medieval burials etc.\n\nMy investigation is ongoing. But I do see a  multi generational racket was\nused for a land grab. And I am a military warfare professional, not just\nthe son of William Buckler.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\nRequester \n"},{"id":"gm-1a069003af48d247","subject":"Re: Welsh Water - Mail reference: 92994223/01","name":"Re: Welsh Water - Mail reference: 92994223/01","date":"2026-09-03T20:40:17Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a069003af48d247.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a067c8479753b0e-welsh-water-mail-reference-92994223-01","scopeTopics":["dwrcymru"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["dwrcymru"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a067c8479753b0e","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"dwrcymru","name":"Dwr Cymru Welsh Water"}],"refs":["92994223/01"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a069003af48d247.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Daniel,\n\nFurther to your email, please use the following forwarding address for the\n£20.00 Guaranteed Standards Scheme payment:\n\n[address redacted]\n[location redacted]\nVale of Glamorgan\n[postcode redacted]\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester \n\n\nOn Thu, 3 Sept 2026, 11:59 CustomerCareTeam,  \nwrote:\n\n> Mail reference: 92994223/01\n>\n>\n>\n> Your email of: 13th August 2026\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> Hello Requester,\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you for your email in which you request a further review of any\n> records relating to Great House Farm (Ty Mawr) and the disconnection which\n> you believe took place during the 1980s.\n>\n>\n>\n> I can see that our Data Subject Access Request (DSAR) team responded to\n> your previous email on 13 August 2026 and advised that we hold no records\n> relating either to yourself or the property in question.\n>\n>\n>\n> *My Review*\n>\n>\n>\n> I appreciate that your latest enquiry is intended to establish the\n> circumstances surrounding the water supply to Great House Farm (Ty Mawr) in\n> 1988, rather than seeking access to your personal data.\n>\n>\n>\n> As previously advised, we have been unable to locate any records relating\n> to the property following the searches undertaken. Our enquiries indicate\n> that the property was demolished during the 1980s and any operational,\n> customer or property records that may once have existed would fall well\n> outside our retention periods and are no longer held.\n>\n>\n>\n> As we do not hold any surviving records relating to the property, we are\n> unable to confirm whether any request was made to disconnect the supply,\n> who may have made such a request, whether supporting authority was\n> provided, whether works were undertaken, or whether any associated\n> correspondence or operational records were created.\n>\n>\n>\n> Equally, we have been unable to identify any surviving archived indexes,\n> work orders, engineer reports or other records relating to the property. As\n> no records have been located, we cannot provide further information\n> regarding the circumstances of any destruction beyond confirming that\n> records are retained in accordance with applicable retention schedules and\n> legal requirements.\n>\n>\n>\n> Whilst I appreciate this may be disappointing; Welsh Water can only\n> provide information that is currently held. We are unable to speculate on\n> events or actions that may have occurred where no records remain available\n> to verify them.\n>\n>\n>\n> In response to your request for clarification regarding our retention\n> periods, these vary depending on the type of data or information held. For\n> example, telephone recordings are retained for 12 months, whereas account\n> notes may be retained for significantly longer periods depending on the\n> nature of the information.\n>\n>\n>\n> We are generally required to retain customer correspondence for between\n> six and seven years. During this period, correspondence may remain\n> accessible through a customer's online account before it expires and is\n> removed in line with the relevant retention schedule.\n>\n>\n>\n> That said, following the migration to our current billing systems in 2015,\n> historical call notes, emails, webforms and other communications that may\n> previously have been associated with a customer or property account, were\n> not transferred and are therefore no longer available through our current\n> operating systems.\n>\n>\n>\n> *Outcome*\n>\n>\n>\n> I am sorry that we have been unable to provide the information you\n> requested regarding the property and any actions that may have been taken\n> in 1988. However, using the details provided, our DSAR team has been unable\n> to locate any records relating to either the property or yourself.\n>\n>\n>\n> For clarity, this has included searches across our available operating\n> systems, including customer registration and billing records, as well as\n> records relating to site visits and supply adjustments or alterations.\n>\n>\n>\n> Lastly, I have identified that our response falls outside our 10-working\n> day target for responding to written communications, and I would like to\n> apologise for any inconvenience this may have caused.\n>\n>\n>\n> To address this, a £20.00 Guaranteed Standards Scheme (GSS) payment can be\n> paid to yo