ere not migrated to the\ncurrent systems.\n\nI am therefore not asking Welsh Water to speculate about what happened in\n1988. I am asking whether there is any surviving documentary record\nconcerning the historical records themselves and their subsequent disposal\nor migration.\n\nCould you please clarify the following:\n\n   1. *Pre-2015 records*\n\nWere records relating to Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough held on any\nlegacy customer, property, works, engineering or billing system before the\n2015 migration?\n\n   2. *2015 migration*\n\nWere historical records excluded from migration because they had already\nreached their retention period, or were they excluded irrespective of their\nage?\n\nIn particular, was there a defined cut-off date or retention rule\ndetermining which historic property/account records were migrated?\n\n   3. *Disposal*\n\nIf historic records relating to the property were destroyed or deleted\nrather than migrated, is there any surviving disposal schedule, destruction\nlog, system-retirement record, data migration specification, audit record\nor other documentation showing what categories of historical records were\nremoved?\n\n   4. *Property records*\n\nYou state that you have been unable to identify surviving archived indexes,\nwork orders, engineer reports or other records relating to the property.\n\nCould you please confirm whether these categories of records were\nhistorically maintained separately from the customer billing/account\nsystem, and whether searches were made for legacy engineering or works\nrecords using the property name, rather than solely a modern customer\naccount number?\n\n   5. *1988 supply alteration/disconnection*\n\nI am particularly interested in whether the records show any historic\nsupply alteration, disconnection, meter removal, site visit, engineer\nattendance, works order or instruction concerning Great House Farm during\n1987–1989.\n\nIf no such records survive, please confirm whether this means:\n\na) no such record was ever created;\nb) such records were created but subsequently destroyed under the\napplicable retention policy; or\nc) it is impossible to determine which of those possibilities applies\nbecause the relevant historic records are no longer available.\n\n   6. *Historical property/account identifiers*\n\nIf your systems retain any historic property reference, account number,\nsupply point reference, meter reference, works reference or other\nidentifier associated with Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), I would be grateful\nif you could provide it. Even if the underlying records no longer survive,\nsuch an identifier could assist in tracing the historic record elsewhere.\n\n   7. *Records predating the current systems*\n\nFinally, could you please confirm whether Dŵr Cymru/Welsh Water has any\ncentral archive, historical records repository or retained legacy data from\nbefore the 2015 system migration, and whether the searches undertaken\nincluded those holdings?\n\nI appreciate your statement that you cannot confirm what happened in 1988\nwhere no records remain. I am not asking you to make such a determination\nwithout evidence.\n\nRather, I am trying to establish the documentary chain: whether a record\nexisted, where it was held, whether it survived the transition to the\ncurrent systems, and, if not, what happened to it.\n\nYour response has provided useful information in that respect, particularly\nthe confirmation that historical communications associated with\nproperty/customer accounts were not transferred during the 2015 migration.\n\nI would therefore be grateful if the above points could be answered so that\nI can accurately record the extent and limitations of the surviving Welsh\nWater records.\n\nWith regard to the £20 Guaranteed Standards Scheme payment, thank you. I am\nhappy to provide a forwarding address for that separately if required;\nacceptance of that payment should not be taken as resolving or withdrawing\nmy substantive reques