l":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a05eabf1010e3a9.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a05eaa94c597908-great-house-farm-llandough-request-00210772-internal-review-formal-chase","scopeTopics":["vale-foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["vale-foi"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a05eaa94c597908","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"vale-foi","name":"Vale of Glamorgan Council"}],"refs":["00210772"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a05eabf1010e3a9.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Information Governance Team,\n\nI refer again to the internal review of Case Ref 00210772 (Great House Farm\n/ Ty Mawr, Llandough), requested on 7 July 2026 and acknowledged by you on\n10 July 2026.\nOn 5 August 2026 you wrote that further enquiries were ongoing and that you\nwould provide a further update by Friday 14 August 2026.\n\nOn 14 August 2026 I chased and requested the completed internal review\ndecision by close of business on 24 August 2026.\nNo response or update has been received.\n\nThe Environmental Information Regulations 2004 require that internal\nreviews be completed promptly and within a reasonable time. The delay is\nnow substantial and exceeds both the statutory expectation and the\ndeadlines you yourselves set.\n\nPlease issue the completed internal review decision within 5 working days\nof this email (i.e. by close of business on Tuesday 8 September 2026).\n\nIf a substantive response is not received by that date I will escalate the\nmatter to the Information Commissioner’s Office for non-compliance with the\nEIR timescales.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester"},{"id":"gm-1a051fc0dc14d968","subject":"Great House Farm, Llandough","name":"Great House Farm, Llandough","date":"2026-08-30T09:24:27Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a051fc0dc14d968.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a051f931389320e-great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":["vale-foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["vale-foi"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a051f931389320e","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"vale-foi","name":"Vale of Glamorgan Council"}],"refs":["00210772"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a051fc0dc14d968.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Information Governance Team,\n\nI refer again to the internal review of Case Ref 00210772 (Great House Farm\n/ Ty Mawr, Llandough), requested on 7 July 2026.\n\nOn 5 August 2026 you stated that further enquiries were ongoing and that\nyou would write further by 14 August 2026.\n\nOn 14 August 2026 I chased and required the completed internal review\ndecision by close of business on 24 August 2026.\nNo substantive response or decision has been received.\n\nThis is a further formal chase. The delay is now substantial and continues\nto breach the requirement under the Environmental Information Regulations\n2004 that internal reviews be completed promptly.\n\nPlease provide the completed internal review decision within 5 working days\nof this email (i.e. by close of business on Friday 4 September 2026).\n\nIf a full response is not received by that date I will need to escalate the\nmatter to the Information Commissioner’s Office for non-compliance with the\nstatutory timescales under the EIR.\n\nIn addition to the outstanding review, and under the same reference, please\nconfirm the following:\n\nWhether the Council (as local planning authority) considered issuing a\nBuilding Preservation Notice under section 58 of the Town and Country\nPlanning Act 1971 (or any equivalent provision), or a demolition stop order\n/ equivalent interim protection, in respect of Great House Farm at any time\nbetween July and December 1988.\n\nIf considered, who decided against it and on what grounds.\n\nIf not considered, why not, given that the building was under active\nbailiff enforcement, partial demolition of outbuildings had already\noccurred, and Cadw had publicly stated on 3 December 1988 that listing was\nunder active consideration.\n\nWhether the 1984 Council meetings that raised the “county treasure”\nsignificance of the house were considered in any relevant planning process,\nincluding any BP application and any consideration of a demolition stop\norder or Building Preservation Notice.\n\nWhether the 1974 inspection by historians that noted the architectural\nsignificance of the house was considered in any relevant planning process,\nincluding any BP application and any consideration of a demolition stop\norder or Building Preservation Notice.\n\nWhether the 1974 planning application by BP for 13 houses is held and\navailable for disclosure.\n\nWhy the current planning permission for the 133 houses is not downloadable\non the planning portal, and whether the full documentation can be provided.\n\nWhether any report or allegation of fraud / concerns raised by my late\nfather (William “Billy” Buckler) was received and documented by the\nCouncil. If so, please provide the relevant records or confirm their\ndestruction / non-retention and the date of any such action.\n\nPlease also supply a copy of the Council’s current complaints procedure (or\na direct link to it).\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester\n"},{"id":"gm-1a04f26a05b1f9bb","subject":"Williams Heirs, Great House Farm, Llandough (Est. 1667)","name":"Williams Heirs, Great House Farm, Llandough (Est. 1667)","date":"2026-08-29T20:12:06Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a04f26a05b1f9bb.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a04ac6f688b848e-williams-heirs-great-house-farm-llandough-est-1667","scopeTopics":["wales-office"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["wales-office"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a04ac6f688b848e","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"wales-office","name":"Wales Office"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a04f26a05b1f9bb.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Secretary of State,\n\nI write as the representative of my family, the House of Williams/Buckler,\nconcerning Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough — an approximately\n800-year-old manorial house which we occupied continuously from 1667 until\nits destruction in 1988, with me as an infant at the time, practically\nstill inside the house, with my pregnant mum.\n\nIt was my birthplace and our family’s home and land. And it still appears\nto be our land, despite BP being awarded possession in 1987. I discovered\nthat the final ruling (for possession, not determination of ownership) was\nbased on a quiet 1983 land registry by BP of our land, in their names,\nwithout disclosing my disabled widow pensioner’s ownership claim, who also\nconveniently died in the two weeks she would have had to challenge this\nregistration, not that she was even made aware. It also states our\npossession was adverse from 1955. But that was on a land parcel we rented\nand defaulted on, to the east of ours, not the parcel our home and garden\nand farm was on. Our parcel we occupied since 1667; we owned from 1928 when\nwe made our last payment on it. Originally an 1877 agreement which went\nmissing from our home in the 1940s, but even without that paper title,\nhaving not paid since 1928, our possession and ownership would have\nqualified as adverse by 1940.\n\nFurthermore, this 1983 land registry non-contest by my nan was after 55\nconsecutive years of her asserting ownership, after the 1877 agreement\nbetween Bute quarryman Daniel Thomas & Son enacted in 1928, when quarrying\ncompleted. An agreement her statements report was stolen from our home in\nthe mid-1940s. Providing both the name of the thief who confessed to\nstealing it and the estate agent who contracted him to do so.\n\nIt appears from my research so far that while a field we rented to the east\nof our land was repossessed, for which our possession might very well have\nbeen adverse from 1955, our land parcel was falsely and wrongfully treated\nas being part of the systematic repossession of the adjacent land parcel,\nwhich BP merged into theirs in 1983 from what I can gather.\n\nI believe this confusion stems from the definition of “whole farm” (as\ncited in the 1955 court order) which changed from two parcels of land in\nthe 1916 agricultural tenancy (conveyed to Western Ground Rents by the\nMarquess of Bute in 1938) to one parcel of land following the enactment of\nthe 1877 agreement in 1928 (after 51 years of our payments, reportedly £4 a\nyear). Furthermore, the modern tenancy/agricultural tenancy Western Ground\nRents placed my great-grandfather into in 1939 for the land parcel to the\neast of ours was, according to Leckwith archived records, only for the one\nparcel to the east of ours and not both parcels.\n\nIn addition to the definition of “whole farm” being misunderstood to still\ninclude our parcel after 1928, introducing ambiguity in the 1955 order, the\norder of possession for Western Ground Rents’ land parcel to the east of\nthe one we owned and occupied (since 1667) also says our land parcel (home\nand garden) was excluded from the order, but not because we owned it, but\nbecause of my grandmother’s “condition (having just had a leg amputated)\nand her resistance”. It does not mention her assertion of ownership. This\nmade our occupancy appear permissive and is what BP later tried in 1974\nwith their permissive occupancy licence letters. The basis of the pinnacle\nof this dispute: the landmark legal case BP v Buckler 1987.\n\nI say all this at least to defend my grandmother’s honour. After her\nconveniently timed death in 1983, during the 1987 hearing in which she was\nunable to defend herself, the judge took it upon himself to enter onto the\nrecord that my grandmother “conceived the notion” she owned the farm. He\nalso dismissed my father’s appeal, citing that both BP companies were all\nthe same. But in the ruling in BP’s favour prior, the judge said “had my\nnan challenged BP Pension Trust, it wouldn’t have counted as BP Properties,\nthe plaintiff, were a different company”. Which is not just a “heads they\nwin, tails we lose” procedural unfairness matter. But the legal precedent\nthat landmark legal case set by ruling my grandmother “accepted the\npermissive occupancy and offer from BP and their licence letters in 1974,\nbecause she didn’t expressly reject them” is firstly absurd by any sane,\nreasonable person’s standards. Secondly, that 1987 case excluded a\nnewspaper article from 1974 whereby my nan expressly rejects, even got\n1,000+ people to sign a petition against their housing development plan of\n13 houses (likely on their parcel to the east, not ours as they never owned\nours) and the article reports her stating her lawyer had assured her her\nownership claim was valid. And she asserts “it’s my home”.\n\nAnd further to all that, it is documented the court never ruled in 1974.\nNot adjudicating ownership in the first hearing, which was adjourned. And\nnot ruling for possession in the second hearing in 1974, which was quashed.\nFirstly because the clerk called them press when my disabled nan crawled to\ncourt begging for an audience with the judge after forced attempts to\nremove her. Including water being shut off for months, resulting in my\ninfant aunt having to go live with another family. And all our cows\ndrinking from the river and dying. The other factor is while the public and\npress and family all had a victory, my nan’s lawyers, BP and the courts\nquashed the case. But what they did was call to mention the offer to my\nnan, and quashing it instantly before she had opportunity to respond. We\nmay still have the tape recording.\n\nSo it is not a case of her not responding and so she accepted. It is a case\nthat the decades of rejection and petition and press reports were concealed\nand suppressed, along with these important facts, and the backdated licence\nletters BP produced afterwards in 1974 and clandestine 1971 alteration of\nour land parcel on the Ordnance Survey, and conveyance from BP Pension\nTrust to Properties in 1975, and land registration in 1983, unbeknown to us\nor anyone I suspect, was not meeting of the minds by a long shot. Yet was\nall that was heard or considered in 1987. The 1987 case was not judged on\nthe merits of the case as it should not even have been held in the court it\nwas in. The court it was decided in was the least applicable court of them\nall. A Chancery/Queen’s Bench i.e. equity court would have been the\nappropriate court.\n\nFurther to even all this, an attempt was made by Western Ground Rents to\nget my nan into a tenancy in 1949 too, for our plot. Which my nan expressly\nrejected in lieu of her assertion of ownership. Even with our paper title\nstolen prior to that attempt, having not paid since 1928 our possession,\neven if adverse, would have qualified us as owners through adverse\npossession by 1940 in any case.\n\nWGR generated a possession order for our land, based on the ambiguity they\nintroduced into the 1955 order, which was never enforced and is the\ninterest they conveyed to BP in 1969, when conveying the land they\npossessed east of ours. BP then altered the Ordnance Survey in 1971,\nshifting the title “Ty Mawr” from the land parcel to the east onto our land\nparcel. And appending “Farm” to the descriptor. Making our parcel better\nfit the defective title chain being forged, in lieu of my nan’s missing\ntitle chain. The 133 houses they are seeking to build on that land parcel,\nours (now Church View Close) was merged into, is defective. The title chain\ntraces back to and through us to what may be the 1,000 freehold issued by\nKnight Sir Herbert to the Vaughan family in the 1500s. It is even possible\nwe have been playing the role of virtual freeholder on our land parcel, or\neven tenant on the land east of ours. When all things considered, like the\nfact we occupied the manorial house since 1667, may even make it possible\nthat we were and still are the rightful freeholders of Llandough Town\n(formerly Cedwyn Estate), with 640 years left on that freehold. Because\nBute’s purchase of the last Vaughan heirs’ estate reportedly did not\ninclude our Ty Mawr.\n\nAnd Ty Mawr (alias Cefin (a possible attempt at Cedwyn) according to an\n1824 David Stewart survey) was not just our home or land parcel, but enough\nparcels to conclude the estate being half the town of Llandough. So this\nall may be a lot bigger and run a lot deeper than even what I am bringing\nto your attention. Our family also have bloodline connections to the\nVaughan, and so it is not just title, estate and deeds. I only focus on the\nparcel we own, for which possession was awarded to BP, to begin unravelling\ntruths long suppressed and concealed.\n\nIn 1974 an ownership hearing did occur to adjudicate the ownership dispute,\nbut seeing as my nan could not present her title as it was stolen, the\nhearing was adjourned. BP then tried to enforce the 1962 possession order.\n\nSo in summary: July 1974 = possession case, adjourned, never finally\ndecided.\n\nSeptember 1974 = old 1962 possession order revived.\n\nOctober/November 1974 = threatened eviction stopped and warrant withdrawn.\nNot licence letters, quashed hearing, but in fact because of overwhelming\ncourt clerk, press and public support.\n\nAnother dimension of this, of which there are many, including BP Bailiffs\nbeing undercover soldiers and/or accompanied by soldiers, allegedly even\nSAS, is the abrupt demolition of our 800-year-old “county treasure” home.\n4am the morning after, almost simultaneously, CADW refused to spot-list it\nas Grade II, the council refused to issue a demolition stop order and our\nHigh Court injunction preventing demolition or removal of our property (all\nof our furniture from Cardiff Castle etc.) got overturned.\n\nThis surely should not have happened prior to the appeal procedure of those\ndecisions concluding, to say the least. More indications of Government\ninvolvement/military operation. Which I do not just say as a military\nwarfare professional, but because other evidence supports it (including\nrecently declassified ITV coverage which may not even have aired). Along\nwith fraud and all the concealment, there are grounds to exercise the\nclause in the Limitation Act and reopen this case.\n\nI formally submit the accompanying CADW — Forensic Critique, Great House\nFarm (Ty Mawr), Llandough – Version 5.0.0 (August 2026).\n\nI also enclose a mock application to an AI-assisted mock Chancery Court I\nam working with. I have been using iterative research and legal-analysis\nprocesses to test and refine the arguments arising from the historical\nevidence, pending any formal legal proceedings on behalf of my family. I\ninclude it not as a substitute for legal proceedings, but to give your\nOffice the wider context in which I am examining what happened. And an\nillustration of a final single legal case which articulates my family’s\ngrievances. If they were not all so terrified to speak out, still now, even\nafter 38 years.\n\nThe CADW Forensic Critique is deliberately evidence-led. It distinguishes\nbetween established facts, reasonable inferences and matters which cannot\npresently be established because the underlying records no longer appear to\nbe available.\n\nIts central concern is straightforward:\n\nCadw cannot presently produce sufficient surviving evidence to explain how\nits December 1988 decision was reached, nor can it presently explain what\nhappened to the records documenting that decision.\n\nAlbeit we are still corresponding to establish the facts. In any case what\nhas been established thus far is particularly troubling given what happened\nimmediately afterwards.\n\nIn 1988, Cadw publicly stated that it was considering an appeal to the\nSecretary of State for Wales to protect the building. The South Wales Echo\nreported on 3 December 1988 that an inspector would visit “this weekend”\nand liaise with RCAHMW.\n\nThe building was then demolished overnight on 5/6 December 1988, within\nhours of the refusal and the simultaneous dismissal of a High Court\ninjunction.\n\nI am therefore asking your Office to help, at the very least in this\ncorrespondence with CADW, to establish as precisely as possible what\nhappened.\n\n1. Contact between Cadw and the Welsh Office / Secretary of State\n\nPlease confirm, deny or clarify each of the following:\n\nWhether any communication or outreach took place between Cadw, or its\npredecessor functions, and the Office of the Secretary of State for Wales /\nWelsh Office concerning Great House Farm during the period July–December\n1988.\n\nWhether any recommendation, briefing, submission, notification or formal\nappeal concerning the protection or listing of Great House Farm was\nsubmitted to the Secretary of State.\n\nWhether my family, as the occupiers and asserted owners of the property,\nwas ever consulted, contacted or notified by Cadw or the Welsh Office\nbefore the refusal decision of 5 December 1988.\n\nIf such communications occurred, I ask that the surviving records be\nidentified and disclosed.\n\nIf they did not occur, I ask that this be stated clearly.\n\n2. What happened to the 1988 assessment records?\n\nCadw’s responses, including FOI ATISN 27021, the response from Amy Longford\ndated 10 July 2026, the subsequent internal review, and the response from\nDr Andy Wigley dated 27 August 2026, indicate that:\n\nonly the two July 1988 photographs have been located;\n\nno assessment file, inspector’s notes, decision record or internal\ncorrespondence has been located;\n\nno destruction record, transfer list, accession record or archive schedule\nhas been located; and\n\nCadw has stated that unsuccessful listing assessments were historically\nretained for approximately three years before transfer or destruction under\nthe Public Records Act 1958.\n\nThis raises an obvious question which I hope your Office will appreciate is\nimportant to my family:\n\nIf the records were destroyed, transferred or otherwise disposed of, where\nis the record showing that this happened and under whose authority?\n\nI therefore request the immediate production of any surviving destruction,\ndisposal, transfer or accession documentation relating to the Great House\nFarm 1988 assessment file, including certificates, dockets, lists, database\nentries, retention schedules, disposal authorities or other contemporaneous\nrecords held by the Welsh Office, its predecessors, Cadw, or any repository\nto which relevant records were transferred.\n\nWhere such a record exists, please provide its exact wording and identify,\ninsofar as recorded:\n\nthe file reference;\n\nthe disposal or transfer authority;\n\nthe date of the action;\n\nthe relevant retention period;\n\nand the officer or authority responsible for authorising it.\n\nIf any residual material remains in existence, I ask that it be preserved\nimmediately and not destroyed, transferred or otherwise disposed of while\nthese matters remain under examination.\n\nIf no destruction, disposal or transfer documentation exists, I ask that\nyour Office confirm that expressly.\n\n3. The unanswered questions surrounding the decision itself\n\nI also ask your Office to address, or to require Cadw to address, the\noutstanding questions identified in Part XII of the accompanying Forensic\nCritique.\n\nIn particular:\n\nWho was the decision-maker responsible for the refusal on 5 December 1988?\n\nWhat was the registered file reference, title and record series?\n\nWhat evidence was actually considered?\n\nWas the 1974 RCAHMW survey (ST 16817331) considered?\n\nWere SMR 2038/2038s and the relevant 1978–1980 GGAT reports considered?\n\nWhat was the basis, authorship and status of the July 1988 “YYY” coding?\n\nWhat communications took place with BP Properties or its agents, the local\nplanning authority, RCAHMW, GGAT or the High Court during the critical\nperiod?\n\nWas a Building Preservation Notice considered?\n\nWhat retention and disposal schedule applied to the assessment?\n\nWhat was the scheduled disposal/review date?\n\nWho authorised disposal, if disposal occurred?\n\nWhich repositories and catalogues have been searched, including The\nNational Archives and historic Welsh Office records?\n\nDo comparable unsuccessful listing assessments from 1988–1991 survive, and\nif so, how were they retained?\n\n\nThese are not questions I ask simply because I disagree with the historical\ndecision.\n\n\nI am trying to establish what evidence was before the decision-maker, what\nprocess was followed, and what happened to the official record afterwards.\n\nThat distinction matters.\n\n\n4. Independent verification\n\nI therefore ask your Office to confirm whether it has searched, or will now\nsearch, The National Archives catalogue and relevant historic Welsh Office\ntransfer documentation for records concerning Great House Farm.\n\nIf this has not already been done comprehensively, I ask that it be\nundertaken and that Cadw be asked to undertake any corresponding searches\nnecessary to establish whether the missing assessment material was ever\ntransferred elsewhere.\n\nI would also ask that any surviving records relating to the case be\npreserved while these enquiries are ongoing.\n\n5. Why I am asking\n\nI appreciate that this is a historic matter.\n\nBut for my family it is not simply history.\n\nThis was our home. It was the place into which I was born, and the\ndestruction of the building came at the end of a much longer dispute\nconcerning our family’s occupation, land and property rights.\n\nI have spent considerable time reconstructing what happened from surviving\ncourt records, land records, archaeological evidence, newspapers, archival\nmaterial, FOI responses and contemporary documentation. I am not asking\nyour Office to accept every conclusion I have drawn.\n\nI am asking for something more fundamental:\n\nwhere the Government and its agencies can still establish what happened, I\nask that they do so; where the evidence no longer exists, I ask that they\nsay so plainly; and where the record of its disappearance should exist, I\nask that it be identified.\n\nMy family has lived with the consequences of these events for decades. I\nbelieve that, at this stage, the least that can reasonably be expected is\nopenness, proper record-keeping and an honest examination of what happened.\n\nI therefore ask that this correspondence, together with the attached CADW —\nForensic Critique v5.0.0, be treated as a formal request for recorded\ninformation under the Freedom of Information Act 2000, as well as a request\nfor clarification of the records-management position concerning Cadw and\nthe historic Welsh Office functions now falling within the responsibility\nof your Office.\n\nI would be grateful for a substantive response addressing each of the\nmatters above individually.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\n\nRequester\n\n"},{"id":"gm-1a04e974b1318169","subject":"Re: Great House Farm, Llandough #611801922","name":"Re: Great House Farm, Llandough #611801922","date":"2026-08-29T17:35:32Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a04e974b1318169.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a04e974b1318169-great-house-farm-llandough-611801922","scopeTopics":["tfw"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["tfw"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a04e974b1318169","direction":"received","from":"(name not shown; customersupport.tfw.wales)","institutions":[{"id":"tfw","name":"Transport for Wales"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a04e974b1318169.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Good evening Requester,Thank you for contacting Cadw. I have passed your information on to the relevant team, who will be in touch.Kind regards,Sara\n\nOn Sat, Aug 29, 2026 at 6:15 PM, Requester   wrote:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nYou don't often get email from [email redacted]. \nLearn why this is important \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nEXTERNAL EMAIL WARNING\n\nThis email originated from outside of the organisation. You should take extra care and make sure this email is genuine before taking any action. If this email appears suspicious, use the\nReport button function in Outlook\nIf you've already interacted with a suspicious or malicious email, please contact\nService Desk on [phone redacted] or [email redacted] \n\n\n\nSirs/Ma'am,\nFurther to my last two emails this week, and in response to yours, which is appreciated, please see the attached documents.\nMy latest version file on yourselves; CADW. And a mock application to a mock AI chancery court; a simulation I'm running in a karpathy autoresearch loop as a long horizon agent to refine the arguments my family made for the last century before being silenced.\n And spending the last 4 decades like hostage prisoners of war. \nWhile CADW may ultimately have played what could appear to be a relatively small part in the wider circumstances surrounding my family's dispossession of our home and land, I want that part to be fully understood, accurately recorded and, where necessary,\n properly scrutinised. Any possible institutional failure you might have identified isn't the ice berge here, I assure you. Not even the tip of it. \nI am not approaching this simply to apportion blame, although I am both horrified and furious reading into all this. I am trying to establish, as honestly and completely as I can, what happened, what was known at the time, what was recorded, what decisions\n were made, and whether proper care was exercised when it mattered.\nThank you in advance for your cooperation. This may ultimately develop into a formal complaint, and if it does, CADW will of course be given a fair opportunity to respond to the concerns we identify. At this stage, however, I am trying to understand the\n full scope of what happened before reaching those conclusions.\nAs my research continues, I may also be in a position to provide CADW with records concerning your own involvement which may not presently be readily apparent from your current records or understanding of the matter. I hope that, if that happens, they will\n be received in the same spirit in which I am providing them: to establish the historical truth.\nIn the much wider context of my family's grievances concerning BP and the local authorities, and the eventual destruction of what had been described as a \"county treasure\", CADW may prove to be only a small part of the overall story. Nevertheless, I have\n also attached a draft Chancery Court application so that you can see the wider context of our grievance and understand where CADW's involvement sits within it.\nI believe that context is important.\nFor 38 years, my family has lived with the consequences of what happened in 1988. We were portrayed publicly as little more than \"squatters being evicted\", and that description has stayed with us and shamed us ever since. The reality was considerably more\n complicated and, for my family, profoundly distressing. It was, in fact, the first time we had been off our land — land which we may even still own — since the 16th century, having acquired the estate in 1667.\nIt remains a theory, but it is possible that an alternate, untold story is yet to be told, including that Llandough was once called Cedwyn Estate, and that the 1,000-year freehold for it, with 640 years left on that agreement, passed from Sir Herbert of\n Swansea to the Vaughan family and onto us, not Bute.\nGiven that we occupied the manorial house for so many centuries, it is entirely plausible that we are the rightful holders of the freehold — and that the heirlooms Bute purchased from the last heirs of the Vaughan family did not actually include Cedwyn Estate.\n He nevertheless acted as though he did, dividing the land parcels. Developing his houses and the town etc. And I note things like the 1824 David Stewart survey in which our estate, being reduced to a home on the parcel it occupied, was recorded as Great House\n alias \"Cedfin\". Elsewhere Ked fin. And other variations. \nThe same method used in 1974 with BP's licence letters was also used in 1955 (rendering our owner/occupier status merely permissive), and this may have occurred on multiple other land parcels prior to this — potentially in clandestine collaborations with\n the British State, given that 33 acres were carved out for the limeworks in the WWI effort, and War Ag occupied the land adjacent to ours in WWII. The council also absorbed our \"playing fields\" and so forth: a sort of eminent domain without our family receiving\n proper reparations (except once, in 1878). Llandough may have been the last of Wales to be colonised. \nI can tell you as a soldier. In Nicosia the indigenous Greeks and many Turks too, would approach the green line with old records of their land holding the UN occupied, even 4 decades later, and the UN would have to surrender those lands back to the indigenous.\n I draw a lot of parallels between this, our case and Wales' dream of devolution/ decolonisation. This case also might show we've been in a land market bubble and so a land market crash is coming. Our case isn't isolated or unique. \nI was a child in that house. My mother was pregnant, and the three of us were still young children. The people who removed us by force — following BP and the UK Government (synonymous at the time) after awarding themselves victory in their courts, (Magistrates\n as oppose to Chancery or before the Bench as a matter of equity, as should have happened on the merits of this case) — caused my mother's shoulder to become dislocated when she attempted to serve them with a High Court injunction ordering the occupiers not\n to destroy our home or remove our belongings. Despite that injunction, the house was ultimately destroyed and our possessions removed, including all our furniture, which came from Cardiff Castle, £30,000 of goods, a stone Roman-villa-type pillar, and so forth.\nThe ITV coverage at the time showed some of what happened, including the injury to my mother, but not that she was pregnant and that three young children were inside. ITV, it seems, never aired that footage, and instead of responding to my FOI request, they\n quietly published it to a YouTube archive last year — apparently hoping that I, or my AI agents, or anyone else, would not notice this suppression. I also hold other ITV footage which they have not admitted to or disclosed, showing my younger baby sister held\n to watch the looting of our home.\nThe MP is hesitant to respond, the Llandough councillor will not respond, and the longstanding Llandough clerk retired after initially trying, and failing, to dismiss me. I am ashamed of my nation. I served in war too, and left my military career with honours;\n I am bona fide, having also completed 22 years in Oath in 2024, the last 2 under the employ of the Vale of Glamorgan Council in adult care. The Land Registry also tried everything to steer me away from identifying their mistake in allowing BP to register our\n land in their name, without our ownership claim being lodged, in 1983. The fact that my nan — who had already spent 40 years challenging her ownership claim by 1983 — died within the two weeks she would have had to challenge BP's registration of our land in\n their name, is another matter requiring investigation. \nMuch like my late father's sudden death at age 41, between BP's application and approval of housing development, his ECHR claim was rejected, and his appeal of the 1987 ruling was dismissed — on the grounds that BP's companies were treated as one and the\n same. Yet in the ruling itself, BP's companies were considered separately, meaning our challenge to one would not have affected the other. Outrageous. The same judge also went out of his way to say my nan \"conceived the notion she owned\" and said the unajudicated\n ownership dispute, set aside to award BP possession of our land, was final. Very suspect and nobody raised questions or challenged except our family i.e. 'Buckler vs United Kingdom' at the ECHR in 1988/89. Who also ruled in lieu of BP rights over ours as the\n owners. In 1974 the ownership hearing was ajourned. Ownership to this day has never been properly adjudicated. \nFor us, this was not simply a \"squatters being evicted\" case, as was falsely reported in the news. It was the loss of our family home since 1667, and the forceful and prolonged seperation and severing of our connection to that land, which our family had\n maintained for generations and owned, and still appear to own in spite of who awarded themselves possession of it or occupy it — and perhaps not just the parcel we were on, but half the town of Llandough, by birthright. With 6 centuries left on that freehold\n from the Knight in 1500, to a military commander and leader like myself. The true title chain. All the while the occupiers operate from defective title. It's possible getting to me may prove more difficult than how my dad before me or nan before him were gotten\n to, as I am a military warfare professional of almost 3 decades now. \nIt is otherwise difficult to explain what all we went through does to a family, particularly when the public account of what happened does not reflect the reality experienced by those of us who were inside the house at the time. My younger sister had nightmares\n when I brought this up with her. It's subsequently cruel to see her having to visit archieves for a 4th time, to try to see again what we are there to see. \nI therefore hope that CADW will understand why I am asking you to approach this matter with some care and humanity, as well as with the proper historical and professional scrutiny that it deserves. The non response from the Llandough councillor, classification\n of the 133 house plans on the council planning portal, delay from MP responding, is also an outrage. And clear bias towards the houses being developed on our stolen occupied land, by the holders of the defective title. No wonder my surviving family, of mainly\n women, aren't speaking up or out. It just horrifies me how they have had to live like such hostages under such terrorists. After a literal act of terrorism against us. \nI was involved in the effort in Iraq to break two SAS soldiers out of an Iraq prison under police guard, days before their execution. Me and a lot of the squadrons of soldiers I have around me to this day. I see my family in a similar light there, under\n the tyranny of this. And I am not standing for it. I'm only 41, I'm in full mental and physical health. I may have a lot of years left in my life which I'm able to dedicate to this. \nI have also passed this matter to my Cardiff based BBC producer friend, who also confirmed they'll be looking into this too. And Gareth Wyn Jones and others will a platform. This is a matter of public and national interest. It should not be drawn into clandestine\n warfare, with silence and violence, albeit that is my domain. Because there's already been plenty of it by the looks of it. I want this to remain in the open. Emails, open letters, courts and Senedd public enquiry etc. \nApplication of Property Law, did in this case, result in an inhumane outcome in my estimation. It may need to be petitioned to be rectified. \nI am not asking CADW to accept my conclusions simply because I have reached them. I am asking you to look at the evidence, to consider what your own records show, and to be willing to acknowledge anything that may have been overlooked, misunderstood, or\n inadequately dealt with at the time.\nIf mistakes were made, I would much rather see them acknowledged and properly understood than defended simply because they are now part of an institutional record.\nI trust you will now appreciate the importance, significance and magnitude of this matter for my family. I will continue to provide the material I uncover, and I sincerely hope that CADW will continue to give it the proper care and attention it deserves.\nRegards,\n\n\nRequester \n"},{"id":"gm-1a04e84719804232","subject":"Great House Farm, Llandough","name":"Great House Farm, Llandough","date":"2026-08-29T17:14:47Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a04e84719804232.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a04e2b752f50b6f-great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":["cadw"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["cadw"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a04e2b752f50b6f","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"cadw","name":"Welsh Government / Cadw"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a04e84719804232.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Sirs/Ma'am,\n\nFurther to my last two emails this week, and in response to yours, which is\nappreciated, please see the attached documents.\n\nMy latest version file on yourselves; CADW. And a mock application to a\nmock AI chancery court; a simulation I'm running in a karpathy autoresearch\nloop as a long horizon agent to refine the arguments my family made for the\nlast century before being silenced. And spending the last 4 decades like\nhostage prisoners of war.\n\nWhile CADW may ultimately have played what could appear to be a relatively\nsmall part in the wider circumstances surrounding my family's dispossession\nof our home and land, I want that part to be fully understood, accurately\nrecorded and, where necessary, properly scrutinised. Any possible\ninstitutional failure you might have identified isn't the ice berge here, I\nassure you. Not even the tip of it.\n\nI am not approaching this simply to apportion blame, although I am both\nhorrified and furious reading into all this. I am trying to establish, as\nhonestly and completely as I can, what happened, what was known at the\ntime, what was recorded, what decisions were made, and whether proper care\nwas exercised when it mattered.\n\nThank you in advance for your cooperation. This may ultimately develop into\na formal complaint, and if it does, CADW will of course be given a fair\nopportunity to respond to the concerns we identify. At this stage, however,\nI am trying to understand the full scope of what happened before reaching\nthose conclusions.\n\nAs my research continues, I may also be in a position to provide CADW with\nrecords concerning your own involvement which may not presently be readily\napparent from your current records or understanding of the matter. I hope\nthat, if that happens, they will be received in the same spirit in which I\nam providing them: to establish the historical truth.\n\nIn the much wider context of my family's grievances concerning BP and the\nlocal authorities, and the eventual destruction of what had been described\nas a \"county treasure\", CADW may prove to be only a small part of the\noverall story. Nevertheless, I have also attached a draft Chancery Court\napplication so that you can see the wider context of our grievance and\nunderstand where CADW's involvement sits within it.\n\nI believe that context is important.\n\nFor 38 years, my family has lived with the consequences of what happened in\n1988. We were portrayed publicly as little more than \"squatters being\nevicted\", and that description has stayed with us and shamed us ever since.\nThe reality was considerably more complicated and, for my family,\nprofoundly distressing. It was, in fact, the first time we had been off our\nland — land which we may even still own — since the 16th century, having\nacquired the estate in 1667.\n\nIt remains a theory, but it is possible that an alternate, untold story is\nyet to be told, including that Llandough was once called Cedwyn Estate, and\nthat the 1,000-year freehold for it, with 640 years left on that agreement,\npassed from Sir Herbert of Swansea to the Vaughan family and onto us, not\nBute.\n\nGiven that we occupied the manorial house for so many centuries, it is\nentirely plausible that we are the rightful holders of the freehold — and\nthat the heirlooms Bute purchased from the last heirs of the Vaughan family\ndid not actually include Cedwyn Estate. He nevertheless acted as though he\ndid, dividing the land parcels. Developing his houses and the town etc. And\nI note things like the 1824 David Stewart survey in which our estate, being\nreduced to a home on the parcel it occupied, was recorded as Great House\nalias \"Cedfin\". Elsewhere Ked fin. And other variations.\n\nThe same method used in 1974 with BP's licence letters was also used in\n1955 (rendering our owner/occupier status merely permissive), and this may\nhave occurred on multiple other land parcels prior to this — potentially in\nclandestine collaborations with the British State, given that 33 acres were\ncarved out for the limeworks in the WWI effort, and War Ag occupied the\nland adjacent to ours in WWII. The council also absorbed our \"playing\nfields\" and so forth: a sort of eminent domain without our family receiving\nproper reparations (except once, in 1878). Llandough may have been the last\nof Wales to be colonised.\n\nI can tell you as a soldier. In Nicosia the indigenous Greeks and many\nTurks too, would approach the green line with old records of their land\nholding the UN occupied, even 4 decades later, and the UN would have to\nsurrender those lands back to the indigenous. I draw a lot of parallels\nbetween this, our case and Wales' dream of devolution/ decolonisation. This\ncase also might show we've been in a land market bubble and so a land\nmarket crash is coming. Our case isn't isolated or unique.\n\nI was a child in that house. My mother was pregnant, and the three of us\nwere still young children. The people who removed us by force — following\nBP and the UK Government (synonymous at the time) after awarding themselves\nvictory in their courts, (Magistrates as oppose to Chancery or before the\nBench as a matter of equity, as should have happened on the merits of this\ncase) — caused my mother's shoulder to become dislocated when she attempted\nto serve them with a High Court injunction ordering the occupiers not to\ndestroy our home or remove our belongings. Despite that injunction, the\nhouse was ultimately destroyed and our possessions removed, including all\nour furniture, which came from Cardiff Castle, £30,000 of goods, a stone\nRoman-villa-type pillar, and so forth.\n\nThe ITV coverage at the time showed some of what happened, including the\ninjury to my mother, but not that she was pregnant and that three young\nchildren were inside. ITV, it seems, never aired that footage, and instead\nof responding to my FOI request, they quietly published it to a YouTube\narchive last year — apparently hoping that I, or my AI agents, or anyone\nelse, would not notice this suppression. I also hold other ITV footage\nwhich they have not admitted to or disclosed, showing my younger baby\nsister held to watch the looting of our home.\n\nThe MP is hesitant to respond, the Llandough councillor will not respond,\nand the longstanding Llandough clerk retired after initially trying, and\nfailing, to dismiss me. I am ashamed of my nation. I served in war too, and\nleft my military career with honours; I am bona fide, having also completed\n22 years in Oath in 2024, the last 2 under the employ of the Vale of\nGlamorgan Council in adult care. The Land Registry also tried everything to\nsteer me away from identifying their mistake in allowing BP to register our\nland in their name, without our ownership claim being lodged, in 1983. The\nfact that my nan — who had already spent 40 years challenging her ownership\nclaim by 1983 — died within the two weeks she would have had to challenge\nBP's registration of our land in their name, is another matter requiring\ninvestigation.\n\nMuch like my late father's sudden death at age 41, between BP's application\nand approval of housing development, his ECHR claim was rejected, and his\nappeal of the 1987 ruling was dismissed — on the grounds that BP's\ncompanies were treated as one and the same. Yet in the ruling itself, BP's\ncompanies were considered separately, meaning our challenge to one would\nnot have affected the other. Outrageous. The same judge also went out of\nhis way to say my nan \"conceived the notion she owned\" and said the\nunajudicated ownership dispute, set aside to award BP possession of our\nland, was final. Very suspect and nobody raised questions or challenged\nexcept our family i.e. 'Buckler vs United Kingdom' at the ECHR in 1988/89.\nWho also ruled in lieu of BP rights over ours as the owners. In 1974 the\nownership hearing was ajourned. Ownership to this day has never been\nproperly adjudicated.\n\nFor us, this was not simply a \"squatters being evicted\" case, as was\nfalsely reported in the news. It was the loss of our family home since\n1667, and the forceful and prolonged seperation and severing of our\nconnection to that land, which our family had maintained for generations\nand owned, and still appear to own in spite of who awarded themselves\npossession of it or occupy it — and perhaps not just the parcel we were on,\nbut half the town of Llandough, by birthright. With 6 centuries left on\nthat freehold from the Knight in 1500, to a military commander and leader\nlike myself. The true title chain. All the while the occupiers operate from\ndefective title. It's possible getting to me may prove more difficult than\nhow my dad before me or nan before him were gotten to, as I am a military\nwarfare professional of almost 3 decades now.\n\nIt is otherwise difficult to explain what all we went through does to a\nfamily, particularly when the public account of what happened does not\nreflect the reality experienced by those of us who were inside the house at\nthe time. My younger sister had nightmares when I brought this up with her.\nIt's subsequently cruel to see her having to visit archieves for a 4th\ntime, to try to see again what we are there to see.\n\nI therefore hope that CADW will understand why I am asking you to approach\nthis matter with some care and humanity, as well as with the proper\nhistorical and professional scrutiny that it deserves. The non response\nfrom the Llandough councillor, classification of the 133 house plans on the\ncouncil planning portal, delay from MP responding, is also an outrage. And\nclear bias towards the houses being developed on our stolen occupied land,\nby the holders of the defective title. No wonder my surviving family, of\nmainly women, aren't speaking up or out. It just horrifies me how they have\nhad to live like such hostages under such terrorists. After a literal act\nof terrorism against us.\n\nI was involved in the effort in Iraq to break two SAS soldiers out of an\nIraq prison under police guard, days before their execution. Me and a lot\nof the squadrons of soldiers I have around me to this day. I see my family\nin a similar light there, under the tyranny of this. And I am not standing\nfor it. I'm only 41, I'm in full mental and physical health. I may have a\nlot of years left in my life which I'm able to dedicate to this.\n\nI have also passed this matter to my Cardiff based BBC producer friend, who\nalso confirmed they'll be looking into this too. And Gareth Wyn Jones and\nothers will a platform. This is a matter of public and national interest.\nIt should not be drawn into clandestine warfare, with silence and violence,\nalbeit that is my domain. Because there's already been plenty of it by the\nlooks of it. I want this to remain in the open. Emails, open letters,\ncourts and Senedd public enquiry etc.\n\nApplication of Property Law, did in this case, result in an inhumane\noutcome in my estimation. It may need to be petitioned to be rectified.\n\nI am not asking CADW to accept my conclusions simply because I have reached\nthem. I am asking you to look at the evidence, to consider what your own\nrecords show, and to be willing to acknowledge anything that may have been\noverlooked, misunderstood, or inadequately dealt with at the time.\n\nIf mistakes were made, I would much rather see them acknowledged and\nproperly understood than defended simply because they are now part of an\ninstitutional record.\n\nI trust you will now appreciate the importance, significance and magnitude\nof this matter for my family. I will continue to provide the material I\nuncover, and I sincerely hope that CADW will continue to give it the proper\ncare and attention it deserves.\n\nRegards,\n\n\nRequester \n\n"},{"id":"gm-1a04c5d2633fe7de","subject":"Please update the status of your request - 1988 Dispossession of Great House Farm, Llandough from the Williams/ Buckler Family","name":"Please update the status of your request - 1988 Dispossession of Great House Farm, Llandough from the Williams/ Buckler Family","date":"2026-08-29T07:12:48Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a04c5d2633fe7de.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a04c5d2633fe7de-please-update-the-status-of-your-request-1988-dispossession-of-great-house-farm-llandough-from-the-williams-buckler-fami","scopeTopics":["mod"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["mod"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a04c5d2633fe7de","direction":"received","from":"(name not shown; whatdotheyknow.com)","institutions":[{"id":"mod","name":"Ministry of Defence"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a04c5d2633fe7de.json","textStatus":"available","text":"To let everyone know, follow this link and then select the appropriate box.\n\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/profile/sign_in?r=%2Frequest%2F1988_dispossession_of_great_hous%23describe_state_form_1\n\nYour request was called '1988 Dispossession of Great House Farm, Llandough from the Williams/ Buckler Family'. Letting everyone know whether you got the information will help us keep tabs on\nMinistry of Defence.\n\n-- the WhatDoTheyKnow team\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nWhatDoTheyKnow is a project of mySociety run by a small team of staff and\ndedicated volunteers.\n\nmySociety is a charity that believes that information should be accessible to everyone.\nIf this is important to you, please consider donating to help us do more:\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/donate\n\nFind out more about how we handle your data at:\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/help/privacy\n\nmySociety is a registered charity in England and Wales (1076346), and a limited\ncompany (03277032)"},{"id":"gm-1a04a988bb1060e4","subject":"Automatic reply: Great House Farm, Llandough","name":"Automatic reply: Great House Farm, Llandough","date":"2026-08-28T22:58:19Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a04a988bb1060e4.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a04a988bb1060e4-automatic-reply-great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":["mp"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["mp"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a04a988bb1060e4","direction":"received","from":"DOUGHTY, Stephen","institutions":[{"id":"mp","name":"Member of Parliament"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a04a988bb1060e4.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Hello,\n\nThank you for your email. Please read this automatic response, which serves as notice that your message has been received.\n\nI receive hundreds of emails every week, and although I intend to reply as quickly as I can to all, I prioritise those that raise immediate problems and urgent cases.\n\n\nCasework\n\nThere is a strict protocol that means Members of Parliament may only provide assistance to their own constituents. This means you must live in the constituency of Cardiff South and Penarth in order for me to assist.\nPlease therefore ensure that you have included:\n\n• Your full name\n\n• Your full postal address\n\n• Your contact telephone number\n\n• Any relevant reference numbers\n\n\nParliamentary Business\n\nIf your email relates to my work in Parliament, it will be dealt with as appropriate.\n\n\nMinisterial Business\n\nIf you are contacting me in my capacity as a Minister at the FCDO, you will need to redirect your email to to [email redacted]<[email redacted]>, or alternatively contact your own MP – you can find who that is at https://members.parliament.uk/FindYourMP\n\n\nThank you for taking the time to contact me.\n\nFor information about how we use your personal data, see our Privacy Notice<https://www.stephendoughty.wales/privacynotice>.\n\n\nYours sincerely,\n\n\nStephen\n\n\nStephen Doughty MP / AS\nMember of Parliament for Cardiff South and Penarth // Aelod Seneddol dros Dde Caerdydd a Phenarth\nHouse of Commons, Westminster, London, [postcode redacted]\nTel: [phone redacted] | Email: [email redacted]\n\n\nUK Parliament Disclaimer: this e-mail is confidential to the intended recipient. If you have received it in error, please notify the sender and delete it from your system. Any unauthorised use, disclosure, or copying is not permitted. This e-mail has been checked for viruses, but no liability is accepted for any damage caused by any virus transmitted by this e-mail. This e-mail address is not secure, is not encrypted and should not be used for sensitive data."},{"id":"gm-1a04a9786334d740","subject":"Great House Farm, Llandough","name":"Great House Farm, Llandough","date":"2026-08-28T22:57:18Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a04a9786334d740.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a04a61cde22259f-great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":["mp"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["mp"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a04a61cde22259f","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"mp","name":"Member of Parliament"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a04a9786334d740.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Good Sirs,\n\nI'm writing to you both as elected representatives because, frankly, the\ninstitutional dysfunction I am uncovering is becoming quite extraordinary.\n\nMy family — including the last remaining members of my dispossessed Welsh\nfamily in South Wales — have spent 38 years living with the consequences of\nwhat happened to our home and land. They have been frightened into silence,\nwhile the institutions responsible for safeguarding justice, heritage and\npublic accountability appear, time and again, to have preferred discretion,\nobfuscation and exquisitely worded emails to the rather unfashionable\nconcept of actually answering the questions.\n\nI have now spent considerable time reconstructing what happened. The deeper\nI dig, the more the story appears to unravel.\n\nWe were not merely separated from a house. We were separated from ancestral\nland, family history and heritage. And now the woodland on that land is\nbeing cleared for a development of 133 homes.\n\nOne might reasonably ask: *whose history is being developed over, and whose\nhistory is being erased?*\n\nWhatever one's political position, the fact remains that possession being\nawarded through historical court proceedings does not magically make every\nunderlying question of title, evidence, procedure or institutional conduct\ndisappear. A court order is not an archaeological dig, a title\ninvestigation, or a certificate of immaculate institutional behaviour.\n\nI attach a summary of my grievances concerning CADW, particularly its\nhistoric involvement in this matter. I also attach a mock court application\nwhich I have been developing alongside AI-assisted legal research, not as a\nsubstitute for legal advice, but as a means of stress-testing the evidence\nand identifying the questions that require proper legal determination.\n\nI hope you will read them carefully.\nThis is considerably larger than one parcel of land\n\nThe parcel my family occupied — Church View Close — may ultimately prove to\nbe only one piece of a much larger story.\n\nThe same apparent pattern of irregularity that I am investigating in\nrelation to our parcel appears potentially relevant to:\n\n   - the parcel we leased immediately to the east, now proposed for the\n   133-home development;\n   - the playing fields subsequently acquired by the council;\n   - the 33 acres associated with the limeworks; and\n   - potentially the underlying question of whether the Marquess of Bute\n   possessed the freehold interest that has subsequently been assumed.\n\nThat last question is obviously not something I intend to settle by\nrhetorical flourish. It is precisely the sort of question that should be\nsettled by *documents, title records and evidence*.\n\nOne relevant public-record reference is here:\n\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/request/2020_01590_hyb\n<https://reference-url-citation.invalid/0>\n\nThat is the point of my investigation.\n\nI am not asking anybody to accept my conclusions simply because I have\nreached them.\n\n*I am asking institutions to produce the evidence.*\nWhat happened to my family cannot simply be filed away\n\nWhat happened to my family and our home in the 1980s was, in its human\nconsequences, devastating. From my perspective, it amounted to an assault\non our family itself. And decades of it. At least mid 40's by Western\nGround Rents and onwards.\n\nFor decades there has been no meaningful after-support, no meaningful\nattempt at reconciliation, no reparative process and, above all, no\nsatisfactory account of what actually happened.\n\nInstead, the historical record appears fragmented, inaccessible and in\nplaces remarkably difficult to obtain.\n\nCADW is only one of numerous agencies about which my representatives are\nnow compiling material.\n\nThe emerging picture includes questions concerning procedural fairness,\ncourt proceedings, evidential failures, institutional decision-making,\nheritage protection and the apparent absence of effective oversight.\n\nThe more documents I obtain, the more questions arise.\n\nAnd I am increasingly uncomfortable with the proposition that *the\ndescendants of the people affected should have to conduct the investigation\nthemselves simply to discover what the institutions already ought to know.*\n\nIt is particularly distressing that I am having to investigate matters\nsurrounding my own father's and grandmother's lives and deaths in this way.\nI am asking for accountability, not theatre\n\nI appreciate that public bodies have procedures.\n\nI appreciate that officials have workloads.\n\nI appreciate that nobody likes receiving an inconvenient historical file\nwith several decades' worth of questions attached to it.\n\nBut carefully crafted sentences, selective disclosure, unexplained\nomissions and silence are not substitutes for accountability.\n\nI have repeatedly encountered what appears to be a culture of concealment,\nstealth and institutional defensiveness. Sometimes I receive an answer.\nSometimes I receive an answer to a question I did not ask. Sometimes I\nreceive an exquisitely polished paragraph which manages, with almost\nbreathtaking elegance, to avoid answering the actual question.\n\nIt is rather like asking for the keys to the filing cabinet and being\nhanded a brochure about the importance of filing cabinets.\n\nThat may be administratively impressive.\n\nIt is not an answer.\nI am asking you to intervene\n\nI do not expect either of you to determine the merits of my family's\nhistorical or legal claims yourselves.\n\nI am asking you to ensure that the matter receives *proper independent\nscrutiny*.\n\nSpecifically, I am asking you to assist in ensuring that:\n\n   1. relevant surviving records are identified and preserved;\n   2. agencies holding potentially material records provide substantive\n   answers rather than procedural evasions;\n   3. the historical involvement of CADW and other public bodies is\n   properly examined;\n   4. the underlying title, conveyancing and land-history evidence is\n   obtained wherever it survives;\n   5. any allegations of procedural unfairness or institutional failure are\n   referred to the appropriate independent bodies where necessary; and\n   6. my family is finally given a meaningful opportunity to establish,\n   through evidence, what actually happened.\n\nI have no interest in mythology.\n\nI want the documents.\n\nI want the chronology.\n\nI want the title deeds.\n\nI want the correspondence.\n\nI want the decisions.\n\nI want to know who knew what, when they knew it, what they did with that\ninformation, and why.\n\nIf the evidence ultimately disproves some of my suspicions, so be it.\n\n*But the evidence must first be allowed to speak.*\nAnd yes, I am angry\n\nI make no apology for that.\n\nI served this country. I spent years working in public service. I have\nworked for the Vale Council. I have served in the military and operated in\ncircumstances considerably more dangerous than sending an email to a public\nauthority.\n\nI am therefore perfectly capable of distinguishing between legitimate\nadministration and institutional stonewalling.\n\nI am no longer asking to be placated.\n\nI am asking for cooperation in recovering whatever evidence remains and\nallowing it to be examined properly.\n\nThe local community members who have contacted me have long memories. Many\nremain convinced that something about this episode was profoundly wrong. I\nintend to establish exactly what happened — not through rumour, grievance\nor inherited mythology, but through the surviving documentary record.\n\nAnd if the record contradicts me, I will accept that.\n\nBut if the record supports my family, then somebody is eventually going to\nhave to explain why it took a descendant *38 years, hundreds of enquiries\nand an obscene amount of archival digging* to uncover it.\n\nThat is not an acceptable model of public accountability.\nSo, gentlemen — enough of the situationship.\n\nWhatever happened to:\n\n*“We don't negotiate with terrorists”?*\n\nIs there peace?\n\n*No.*\n\nIs there peace?\n\n*No.*\n\nIs there peace?\n\n*No.*\n\nThere is, however, apparently an absolutely fabulous quantity of\nbureaucracy.\n\nWhat there has not yet been is accountability.\n\nI therefore ask you, gentlemen, to address this matter with the seriousness\nit deserves and to assist my family in obtaining the answers that have been\ndenied to us for far too long.\n\nI am not asking for special treatment.\n\nI am asking for the truth.\n\nAnd if the institutions involved have nothing to hide, then surely they\nshould have nothing to fear from producing the documents.\n\nRegards,\n\n\nRequester "},{"id":"gm-1a046b9c74c62b02","subject":"Re: CADW failures – Revised Report (10 August 2026)","name":"Re: CADW failures – Revised Report (10 August 2026)","date":"2026-08-28T04:56:14Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a046b9c74c62b02.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19fe7c63edda5834-cadw-failures-revised-report-10-august-2026","scopeTopics":["cadw"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["cadw"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19fe7c63edda5834","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"cadw","name":"Welsh Government / Cadw"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a046b9c74c62b02.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dr Wigley,\n\nOne further point has arisen from my continuing examination of the archival\nmaterial which I would like to place on the record before you respond to my\nprevious correspondence.\n\nI was recently directed to material held at Glamorgan Archives to\n*Glamorgan-Gwent\nArchaeological Trust (GGAT) planning papers concerning Great House Farm*.\nIf I recall by the longstanding Llandough Council Clerk, who retired the\nday he finally replied to my FOI's. The references state that the property\nhad previously been described as a *“county treasure”* and that this was\nrecorded in GGAT documentation in connection with the development process.\n\nI am presently tracing the underlying GGAT document so that I can establish\nits precise file reference, author and date before relying upon the wording\nas a primary citation. I therefore do *not* ask Cadw to accept the “county\ntreasure” description as an established fact at this stage.\n\nThe significance of the material is instead that it identifies another\npotentially important body of *professional archaeological documentation\nconcerning Great House Farm which appears to pre-date, or be\ncontemporaneous with, the demolition and development process*.\n\nThis is relevant to the question I raised in my previous email:\n\n*Can Cadw establish from its surviving records what pre-existing\narchaeological information concerning Great House Farm was actually\nconsidered in reaching the December 1988 conclusion that the site was “not\nof sufficient archaeological interest”?*\n\nI appreciate that Cadw may now be unable to answer that question. If so,\nthat inability should be distinguished from evidence that the information\nwas not available or was not considered.\n\nI also wish to make one clarification concerning the additional Llandough\nmaterial I mentioned previously.\n\nI am *not* seeking to bring the architectural or listing status of St\nDochdwy’s Church within the scope of my Great House Farm enquiry. The\nchurch is relevant only insofar as the archaeological evidence concerning\nthe wider Llandough site may assist in understanding the archaeological\ncontext in which Great House Farm was assessed.\n\nThe focus remains exclusively on *Great House Farm and the December 1988\nspot-listing decision concerning that building*.\n\nThe evidential position I am attempting to establish is therefore\nincreasingly precise:\n\n   1. What professional architectural and archaeological evidence\n   concerning Great House Farm existed before December 1988?\n   2. What of that evidence can Cadw establish was considered by the 1988\n   inspector or decision-maker?\n   3. Where Cadw cannot establish this because the assessment records no\n   longer survive, can that evidential limitation be expressly acknowledged?\n\nI will continue separately with Glamorgan Archives/Heneb to identify the\nunderlying GGAT “county treasure” reference and will provide the precise\ncitation once established.\n\nPlease treat this email as a further supplementary note to my existing\nsubmission rather than as a new FOI request.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nRequester \nOn Thu, 27 Aug 2026, 15:52 Requester, <[email redacted]> wrote:\n\n> Dear Dr Wigley,\n>\n> Thank you for your response and for the further clarification concerning\n> the surviving Cadw records and the historic retention arrangements.\n>\n> Having considered your response alongside the evidence set out in my\n> report of 10 August, I think it is important to distinguish between two\n> quite separate propositions:\n>\n>    1. *Cadw has released all records concerning the 1988 assessment which\n>    it can now locate*; and\n>    2. *the surviving records establish the evidential and administrative\n>    basis upon which the December 1988 decision was made.*\n>\n> Your response appears to establish the first, but not the second.\n>\n> That distinction is important because the surviving evidence is\n> exceptionally limited: two photographs. The absence of the underlying\n> assessment papers means that a number of historically significant questions\n> cannot now be answered from Cadw's records.\n>\n> I am therefore asking for clarification of the position rather than asking\n> Cadw to recreate records which no longer exist.\n> 1. What the surviving record establishes\n>\n> Could Cadw please confirm whether the following is an accurate description\n> of the present evidential position:\n>\n> *Cadw's surviving records do not establish the evidential or\n> administrative basis of the December 1988 decision not to list Great House\n> Farm. In particular, the surviving records do not establish what evidence\n> was considered by the inspector or decision-maker, whether the H.J. Thomas\n> RCAHMW survey of 26 April 1974 was considered, whether the relevant\n> SMR/GGAT archaeological material was considered, what inspection findings\n> were recorded in December 1988, or whether any internal recommendation or\n> consultation took place.*\n>\n> If that is correct, I would be grateful if Cadw would confirm it expressly.\n>\n> I am not suggesting that the absence of records proves that those matters\n> did not occur. Equally, however, the absence of records means that Cadw\n> cannot now establish that they did occur.\n>\n> That is the central evidential limitation created by the loss of the 1988\n> working papers.\n> 2. The 1974 H.J. Thomas RCAHMW survey\n>\n> Your response says:\n>\n> \"we are unable to determine whether or not the 1974 survey was taken into\n> account at the time.\"\n>\n> That is a particularly important clarification.\n>\n> The RCAHMW survey was not a document subsequently created in response to\n> the dispute. It was a professional survey undertaken on 26 April 1974 and\n> recorded architectural features including the late-17th-century\n> construction, Type A sub-medieval cross-passage plan, substantial timber\n> framing and the barrel-vaulted cellar.\n>\n> I therefore ask Cadw to confirm simply:\n>\n> *Cadw cannot now establish from its surviving records whether the\n> inspector or decision-maker responsible for the December 1988 assessment\n> had considered the H.J. Thomas survey before concluding that Great House\n> Farm was \"not of sufficient architectural merit\".*\n>\n> If that is Cadw's position, I will record it as such.\n> 3. The archaeological evidence\n>\n> The same issue appears to arise in relation to the archaeological\n> assessment.\n>\n> My report identifies evidence predating December 1988 concerning Roman,\n> Iron Age and medieval activity at the site, including the recorded SMR\n> material and the 1978–80 archaeological investigations.\n>\n> I appreciate that the later 1994 excavation cannot simply be projected\n> backwards and treated as evidence which Cadw necessarily possessed in 1988.\n>\n> The relevant question is narrower:\n>\n> *Can Cadw now establish, from any surviving record, what archaeological\n> information was actually considered when the December 1988 conclusion was\n> reached that the site was \"not of sufficient archaeological interest\"?*\n>\n> If it cannot, please confirm that this cannot now be established.\n>\n> This would avoid conflating the later archaeological discoveries with the\n> separate question of what information was available to Cadw in 1988.\n> 4. The July \"YYY\" coding\n>\n> There remains an important unresolved point concerning the July 1988\n> photographs.\n>\n> Cadw has previously explained that the \"YYY\" coding indicated that the\n> building did not meet the listing criteria. The photographs were taken on\n> 29 July 1988.\n>\n> The contemporary press report of 3 December 1988, however, records Cadw as\n> saying that it was then \"considering\" asking the Secretary of State to\n> protect Great House Farm as a listed building and that an inspector would\n> visit that weekend.\n>\n> I appreciate that the records necessary to resolve this may no longer\n> exist.\n>\n> Nevertheless, could Cadw confirm whether it can establish:\n>\n>    - what \"YYY\" specifically represented within the July 1988 process;\n>    - whether it was a final assessment or provisional coding;\n>    - whether it was applied by the July inspector;\n>    - whether the July assessment was subsequently reviewed in December;\n>    and\n>    - whether the December inspector had access to the July photographs or\n>    any associated assessment.\n>\n> If none of these matters can now be established, I would be grateful for\n> that to be confirmed rather than inferred.\n> 5. Identity of the December decision-maker\n>\n> One further factual gap remains.\n>\n> Can Cadw establish the identity of:\n>\n>    - the inspector who undertook the December 1988 inspection; and\n>    - the person who made or authorised the decision communicated on 5\n>    December 1988 that Great House Farm was not of sufficient architectural\n>    merit and archaeological interest?\n>\n> If the relevant individual(s) cannot now be identified from surviving\n> records, please confirm that.\n>\n> This is not a request for personal information beyond what would\n> ordinarily form part of the historical administrative record. It is simply\n> an attempt to establish whether the decision itself can still be attributed\n> to an identifiable office-holder.\n> 6. The retention explanation\n>\n> I also wish to clarify an important qualification in your latest response.\n>\n> You state that current colleagues have advised that, based on their\n> knowledge of the systems then in place, unsuccessful listing files were\n> *\"likely\"* to have been retained for approximately three years.\n>\n> I understand that this is necessarily a reconstruction of historic\n> practice because the relevant records no longer exist.\n>\n> Could you therefore confirm whether Cadw has located:\n>\n>    - a 1980s retention schedule;\n>    - a disposal or destruction policy applicable to the relevant records;\n>    - a disposal schedule;\n>    - a destruction certificate or log;\n>    - a transfer/accession record to RCAHMW or another repository; or\n>    - any other contemporaneous record showing what happened to the Great\n>    House Farm material.\n>\n> If none has been located, I would be grateful if Cadw could state\n> expressly that *the approximately three-year retention period is an\n> account of the general historic practice as presently understood by current\n> staff, and not documentary evidence establishing that the Great House Farm\n> records were actually destroyed after three years.*\n>\n> That is an important distinction for the historical record.\n> 7. What I am seeking from Cadw\n>\n> I am not asking Cadw to speculate about matters for which the documentary\n> evidence has disappeared.\n>\n> Indeed, the purpose of my report was deliberately to separate:\n>\n>\n>\n> *what is established; what is a reasonable inference; and what can no\n> longer be verified because the records do not survive.*\n>\n> Your latest response is helpful in establishing the third category.\n>\n> I would therefore be grateful if Cadw could confirm, insofar as its\n> records permit, the following concise position:\n>\n> *The surviving Cadw records relating to the December 1988 Great House Farm\n> assessment consist of two photographs. Cadw cannot now establish from\n> surviving records the detailed evidential or administrative basis of the\n> decision, including whether the 1974 H.J. Thomas RCAHMW survey or relevant\n> archaeological records were considered. Cadw has not located records\n> establishing the disposal or transfer of the missing assessment material,\n> and the approximately three-year retention period represents the best\n> present understanding of historic practice rather than documentary evidence\n> concerning the disposal of this particular file.*\n>\n> If that accurately reflects Cadw's position, I would regard that\n> clarification as extremely useful.\n> 8. Historical record and next steps\n>\n> I will separately approach Heneb concerning the Historic Environment\n> Record, as you have advised, and will pursue the question of any\n> appropriate heritage interpretation or marker with the relevant local\n> authority.\n>\n> I also appreciate your clarification that Cadw cannot require or\n> facilitate installation of a marker.\n>\n> My purpose in continuing this correspondence is therefore narrower than\n> seeking to reopen the FOI request. I am seeking to ensure that the\n> historical record does not inadvertently suggest that the absence of\n> surviving records means that the basis of the 1988 decision has been\n> established.\n>\n> On the evidence presently available, the more accurate position appears to\n> be that *the decision is documented, but its underlying evidential and\n> administrative reasoning is no longer recoverable from Cadw's surviving\n> records.*\n>\n> That is a significant historical finding in its own right.\n>\n> Thank you again for your assistance. I would be grateful for confirmation\n> of the above points, particularly the status of the 1974 survey, the July\n> \"YYY\" coding, the identity of the December decision-maker, and the\n> evidential basis (if any) for the disposal of the missing records.\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n> Requester \n>\n> On Thu, 27 Aug 2026, 13:21 , <[email redacted]> wrote:\n>\n>> Dear Requester\n>>\n>>\n>>\n>> Thank you for your latest correspondence dated 10th August in relation\n>> to your initial freedom of Information request (ATISN 27021) submitted in\n>> June 2026, which has been escalated to me for response.\n>>\n>>\n>>\n>> As stated by my colleagues, I can confirm that all records we hold have\n>> been released to you. We do not hold any further records relating to the\n>> content or disposal of the historic records you have requested.\n>>\n>>\n>>\n>> To confirm, colleagues in the records office have clarified the historic\n>> retention position to the best of their knowledge. Given that the issue\n>> relates to an historic record retention policy, and that no records\n>> regarding this are available, it is not possible to say exactly what CADW’s\n>> retention arrangements were over 38 years ago. However, based on their\n>> knowledge of the systems in place at the time, current colleagues have\n>> advised that it is likely that records of unsuccessful listings were only\n>> retained for a short period, usually about 3 years. This has since changed\n>> and current retention policy in relation to unsuccessful listings is to\n>> retain the files indefinitely, reviewed every 5 years.\n>>\n>>\n>>\n>> In relation to your question *‘Did Cadw’s 1988 assessment of Great House\n>> Farm fully account for the independent professional evidence then\n>> available- particularly the H.J. Thomas RCAHMW survey of 1974- and if not\n>> why not?’*. As stated, all of the records we hold have been released to\n>> you under the FOI and are now a public record. As the release consists of\n>> only two photographs we are unable to determine whether or not the 1974\n>> survey was taken into account at the time.\n>>\n>>\n>>\n>> In relation to question A, please see our previous emails of 2nd, 3rd, 10\n>> th and 29th July, together with the above explanation of the historic\n>> and current retention policies.\n>>\n>>\n>>\n>> In relation to question B, content of the HER is managed by Heneb. Please\n>> contact www.heneb.org.uk in relation to adding information to the HER.\n>>\n>>\n>>\n>> In relation to question C, please refer to the FOI response provided on\n>> the 11th June 2026 which is a matter of public record and confirms\n>> Cadw’s position.\n>>\n>>\n>>\n>> In relation to question D, noted above.\n>>\n>>\n>>\n>> In relation to question E, Cadw are unable to require or facilitate the\n>> installation of a heritage marker at the site.\n>>\n>>\n>>\n>> Yours sincerely,\n>>\n>>\n>>\n>> Andy Wigley\n>>\n>>\n>>\n>>\n>>\n>>\n>>\n>> Dr Andy Wigley\n>>\n>> Prif Arolygydd – Pennaeth Arolygiaeth yr Amgylchedd Hanesyddol / Chief\n>> Inspector - Head of Historic Environment Inspectorate\n>>\n>> Cadw\n>>\n>> Dysgu, Cymunedau a Diwylliant / Learning, Communities and Culture\n>>\n>> Llywodraeth Cymru / Welsh Government\n>>\n>> Tŷ Ladywell / Ladywell House\n>> Y Drenewydd / Newtown\n>> Powys\n>> [postcode redacted]\n>>\n>>\n>>\n>> Ffôn / Tel: [phone redacted]\n>> Ffôn symudol / Mobile: [phone redacted]\n>>\n>> E-bost/ Email: [email redacted] / [email redacted]\n>>\n>> P Helpwch yr amgylchedd - peidiwch ag argraffu hon os nad oes gwir\n>> raid   Help our environment - only print this if really necessary\n>>\n>> Rydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth Gymraeg. Cewch ateb Cymraeg i bob gohebiaeth\n>> Gymraeg ac ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi.\n>>\n>> We welcome correspondence in Welsh. Correspondence received in Welsh will\n>> be answered in Welsh and corresponding in Welsh will not involve any delay.\n>>\n>>\n>>\n>>\n>>\n>> *From:* Requester <[email redacted]>\n>> *Sent:* 10 August 2026 22:46\n>> *To:* Longford, Amy (LCC - Cadw) <[email redacted]>\n>> *Cc:* Cadw Planning <[email redacted]>;\n>> [email redacted]; DOUGHTY, Stephen <\n>> [email redacted]>\n>> *Subject:* Re: CADW failures – Revised Report (10 August 2026)\n>>\n>>\n>>\n>> Sirs/Ma'ams,\n>>\n>> I write further to my email of 9 August 2026 submitting a review\n>> concerning Cadw’s 1988 assessment of Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough.\n>>\n>> Please find attached (and linked below) a revised edition of the report,\n>> dated 10 August 2026. This version has been carefully restructured to\n>> present the same evidence in a more measured and administratively focused\n>> form. It is offered as a request for historical record correction, archival\n>> explanation, and administrative acknowledgement rather than as a forensic\n>> challenge.\n>>\n>> The revised document:\n>>\n>> • Clearly states what it is and is not;\n>> • Sets out the surviving independent evidence (principally the H.J.\n>> Thomas RCAHMW survey of 1974);\n>> • Notes the near-total absence of Cadw’s 1988 working papers confirmed in\n>> ATISN 27021;\n>> • Asks only for realistic, achievable administrative steps: explanation\n>> of the archival position, reflection of the surviving evidence in the\n>> Historic Environment Record, acknowledgement of the records gap, and\n>> consideration of a local heritage marker.\n>>\n>> It does not claim unlawfulness, seek compensation from Cadw, or demand a\n>> public inquiry.\n>>\n>> I hope this revised presentation is more palatable and will assist the\n>> relevant bodies in providing a constructive response.\n>>\n>> I look forward to your substantive response.\n>>\n>> Regards,\n>>\n>> Requester\n>> \n>> [email redacted]\n>>\n>> [phone redacted]\n>> Sganiwyd y neges hon am bob feirws hysbys wrth iddi adael Llywodraeth\n>> Cymru. Mae Llywodraeth Cymru yn cymryd o ddifrif yr angen i ddiogelu eich\n>> data. Os cysylltwch â Llywodraeth Cymru, mae ein hysbysiad preifatrwydd\n>> yn esbonio sut rydym yn defnyddio eich gwybodaeth a sut rydym yn diogelu\n>> eich preifatrwydd. Rydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg. Byddwn yn anfon\n>> ateb yn Gymraeg i ohebiaeth a dderbynnir yn Gymraeg ac ni fydd gohebu yn\n>> Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi. ----------------- On leaving the Welsh\n>> Government this email was scanned for all known viruses. The Welsh\n>> Government takes the protection of your data seriously. If you contact the\n>> Welsh Government then our Privacy Notice\n>> <https://gov.wales/about/welsh-government-privacy-notice/?lang=en>\n>> explains how we use your information and the ways in which we protect your\n>> privacy. We welcome receiving correspondence in Welsh. Any correspondence\n>> received in Welsh will be answered in Welsh and corresponding in Welsh will\n>> not lead to a delay in responding.\n>>\n>"},{"id":"gm-1a04490ff1a7a310","subject":"Re: CADW failures – Revised Report (10 August 2026)","name":"Re: CADW failures – Revised Report (10 August 2026)","date":"2026-08-27T18:52:28Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a04490ff1a7a310.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19fe7c63edda5834-cadw-failures-revised-report-10-august-2026","scopeTopics":["cadw"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["cadw"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19fe7c63edda5834","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"cadw","name":"Welsh Government / Cadw"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a04490ff1a7a310.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Dr Wigley,\n\nThank you for your response and for the further clarification concerning\nthe surviving Cadw records and the historic retention arrangements.\n\nHaving considered your response alongside the evidence set out in my report\nof 10 August, I think it is important to distinguish between two quite\nseparate propositions:\n\n   1. *Cadw has released all records concerning the 1988 assessment which\n   it can now locate*; and\n   2. *the surviving records establish the evidential and administrative\n   basis upon which the December 1988 decision was made.*\n\nYour response appears to establish the first, but not the second.\n\nThat distinction is important because the surviving evidence is\nexceptionally limited: two photographs. The absence of the underlying\nassessment papers means that a number of historically significant questions\ncannot now be answered from Cadw's records.\n\nI am therefore asking for clarification of the position rather than asking\nCadw to recreate records which no longer exist.\n1. What the surviving record establishes\n\nCould Cadw please confirm whether the following is an accurate description\nof the present evidential position:\n\n*Cadw's surviving records do not establish the evidential or administrative\nbasis of the December 1988 decision not to list Great House Farm. In\nparticular, the surviving records do not establish what evidence was\nconsidered by the inspector or decision-maker, whether the H.J. Thomas\nRCAHMW survey of 26 April 1974 was considered, whether the relevant\nSMR/GGAT archaeological material was considered, what inspection findings\nwere recorded in December 1988, or whether any internal recommendation or\nconsultation took place.*\n\nIf that is correct, I would be grateful if Cadw would confirm it expressly.\n\nI am not suggesting that the absence of records proves that those matters\ndid not occur. Equally, however, the absence of records means that Cadw\ncannot now establish that they did occur.\n\nThat is the central evidential limitation created by the loss of the 1988\nworking papers.\n2. The 1974 H.J. Thomas RCAHMW survey\n\nYour response says:\n\n\"we are unable to determine whether or not the 1974 survey was taken into\naccount at the time.\"\n\nThat is a particularly important clarification.\n\nThe RCAHMW survey was not a document subsequently created in response to\nthe dispute. It was a professional survey undertaken on 26 April 1974 and\nrecorded architectural features including the late-17th-century\nconstruction, Type A sub-medieval cross-passage plan, substantial timber\nframing and the barrel-vaulted cellar.\n\nI therefore ask Cadw to confirm simply:\n\n*Cadw cannot now establish from its surviving records whether the inspector\nor decision-maker responsible for the December 1988 assessment had\nconsidered the H.J. Thomas survey before concluding that Great House Farm\nwas \"not of sufficient architectural merit\".*\n\nIf that is Cadw's position, I will record it as such.\n3. The archaeological evidence\n\nThe same issue appears to arise in relation to the archaeological\nassessment.\n\nMy report identifies evidence predating December 1988 concerning Roman,\nIron Age and medieval activity at the site, including the recorded SMR\nmaterial and the 1978–80 archaeological investigations.\n\nI appreciate that the later 1994 excavation cannot simply be projected\nbackwards and treated as evidence which Cadw necessarily possessed in 1988.\n\nThe relevant question is narrower:\n\n*Can Cadw now establish, from any surviving record, what archaeological\ninformation was actually considered when the December 1988 conclusion was\nreached that the site was \"not of sufficient archaeological interest\"?*\n\nIf it cannot, please confirm that this cannot now be established.\n\nThis would avoid conflating the later archaeological discoveries with the\nseparate question of what information was available to Cadw in 1988.\n4. The July \"YYY\" coding\n\nThere remains an important unresolved point concerning the July 1988\nphotographs.\n\nCadw has previously explained that the \"YYY\" coding indicated that the\nbuilding did not meet the listing criteria. The photographs were taken on\n29 July 1988.\n\nThe contemporary press report of 3 December 1988, however, records Cadw as\nsaying that it was then \"considering\" asking the Secretary of State to\nprotect Great House Farm as a listed building and that an inspector would\nvisit that weekend.\n\nI appreciate that the records necessary to resolve this may no longer exist.\n\nNevertheless, could Cadw confirm whether it can establish:\n\n   - what \"YYY\" specifically represented within the July 1988 process;\n   - whether it was a final assessment or provisional coding;\n   - whether it was applied by the July inspector;\n   - whether the July assessment was subsequently reviewed in December; and\n   - whether the December inspector had access to the July photographs or\n   any associated assessment.\n\nIf none of these matters can now be established, I would be grateful for\nthat to be confirmed rather than inferred.\n5. Identity of the December decision-maker\n\nOne further factual gap remains.\n\nCan Cadw establish the identity of:\n\n   - the inspector who undertook the December 1988 inspection; and\n   - the person who made or authorised the decision communicated on 5\n   December 1988 that Great House Farm was not of sufficient architectural\n   merit and archaeological interest?\n\nIf the relevant individual(s) cannot now be identified from surviving\nrecords, please confirm that.\n\nThis is not a request for personal information beyond what would ordinarily\nform part of the historical administrative record. It is simply an attempt\nto establish whether the decision itself can still be attributed to an\nidentifiable office-holder.\n6. The retention explanation\n\nI also wish to clarify an important qualification in your latest response.\n\nYou state that current colleagues have advised that, based on their\nknowledge of the systems then in place, unsuccessful listing files were\n*\"likely\"* to have been retained for approximately three years.\n\nI understand that this is necessarily a reconstruction of historic practice\nbecause the relevant records no longer exist.\n\nCould you therefore confirm whether Cadw has located:\n\n   - a 1980s retention schedule;\n   - a disposal or destruction policy applicable to the relevant records;\n   - a disposal schedule;\n   - a destruction certificate or log;\n   - a transfer/accession record to RCAHMW or another repository; or\n   - any other contemporaneous record showing what happened to the Great\n   House Farm material.\n\nIf none has been located, I would be grateful if Cadw could state expressly\nthat *the approximately three-year retention period is an account of the\ngeneral historic practice as presently understood by current staff, and not\ndocumentary evidence establishing that the Great House Farm records were\nactually destroyed after three years.*\n\nThat is an important distinction for the historical record.\n7. What I am seeking from Cadw\n\nI am not asking Cadw to speculate about matters for which the documentary\nevidence has disappeared.\n\nIndeed, the purpose of my report was deliberately to separate:\n\n\n\n*what is established; what is a reasonable inference; and what can no\nlonger be verified because the records do not survive.*\n\nYour latest response is helpful in establishing the third category.\n\nI would therefore be grateful if Cadw could confirm, insofar as its records\npermit, the following concise position:\n\n*The surviving Cadw records relating to the December 1988 Great House Farm\nassessment consist of two photographs. Cadw cannot now establish from\nsurviving records the detailed evidential or administrative basis of the\ndecision, including whether the 1974 H.J. Thomas RCAHMW survey or relevant\narchaeological records were considered. Cadw has not located records\nestablishing the disposal or transfer of the missing assessment material,\nand the approximately three-year retention period represents the best\npresent understanding of historic practice rather than documentary evidence\nconcerning the disposal of this particular file.*\n\nIf that accurately reflects Cadw's position, I would regard that\nclarification as extremely useful.\n8. Historical record and next steps\n\nI will separately approach Heneb concerning the Historic Environment\nRecord, as you have advised, and will pursue the question of any\nappropriate heritage interpretation or marker with the relevant local\nauthority.\n\nI also appreciate your clarification that Cadw cannot require or facilitate\ninstallation of a marker.\n\nMy purpose in continuing this correspondence is therefore narrower than\nseeking to reopen the FOI request. I am seeking to ensure that the\nhistorical record does not inadvertently suggest that the absence of\nsurviving records means that the basis of the 1988 decision has been\nestablished.\n\nOn the evidence presently available, the more accurate position appears to\nbe that *the decision is documented, but its underlying evidential and\nadministrative reasoning is no longer recoverable from Cadw's surviving\nrecords.*\n\nThat is a significant historical finding in its own right.\n\nThank you again for your assistance. I would be grateful for confirmation\nof the above points, particularly the status of the 1974 survey, the July\n\"YYY\" coding, the identity of the December decision-maker, and the\nevidential basis (if any) for the disposal of the missing records.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nRequester \n\nOn Thu, 27 Aug 2026, 13:21 , <[email redacted]> wrote:\n\n> Dear Requester\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you for your latest correspondence dated 10th August in relation to\n> your initial freedom of Information request (ATISN 27021) submitted in June\n> 2026, which has been escalated to me for response.\n>\n>\n>\n> As stated by my colleagues, I can confirm that all records we hold have\n> been released to you. We do not hold any further records relating to the\n> content or disposal of the historic records you have requested.\n>\n>\n>\n> To confirm, colleagues in the records office have clarified the historic\n> retention position to the best of their knowledge. Given that the issue\n> relates to an historic record retention policy, and that no records\n> regarding this are available, it is not possible to say exactly what CADW’s\n> retention arrangements were over 38 years ago. However, based on their\n> knowledge of the systems in place at the time, current colleagues have\n> advised that it is likely that records of unsuccessful listings were only\n> retained for a short period, usually about 3 years. This has since changed\n> and current retention policy in relation to unsuccessful listings is to\n> retain the files indefinitely, reviewed every 5 years.\n>\n>\n>\n> In relation to your question *‘Did Cadw’s 1988 assessment of Great House\n> Farm fully account for the independent professional evidence then\n> available- particularly the H.J. Thomas RCAHMW survey of 1974- and if not\n> why not?’*. As stated, all of the records we hold have been released to\n> you under the FOI and are now a public record. As the release consists of\n> only two photographs we are unable to determine whether or not the 1974\n> survey was taken into account at the time.\n>\n>\n>\n> In relation to question A, please see our previous emails of 2nd, 3rd, 10\n> th and 29th July, together with the above explanation of the historic and\n> current retention policies.\n>\n>\n>\n> In relation to question B, content of the HER is managed by Heneb. Please\n> contact www.heneb.org.uk in relation to adding information to the HER.\n>\n>\n>\n> In relation to question C, please refer to the FOI response provided on\n> the 11th June 2026 which is a matter of public record and confirms Cadw’s\n> position.\n>\n>\n>\n> In relation to question D, noted above.\n>\n>\n>\n> In relation to question E, Cadw are unable to require or facilitate the\n> installation of a heritage marker at the site.\n>\n>\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n>\n>\n> Andy Wigley\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> Dr Andy Wigley\n>\n> Prif Arolygydd – Pennaeth Arolygiaeth yr Amgylchedd Hanesyddol / Chief\n> Inspector - Head of Historic Environment Inspectorate\n>\n> Cadw\n>\n> Dysgu, Cymunedau a Diwylliant / Learning, Communities and Culture\n>\n> Llywodraeth Cymru / Welsh Government\n>\n> Tŷ Ladywell / Ladywell House\n> Y Drenewydd / Newtown\n> Powys\n> [postcode redacted]\n>\n>\n>\n> Ffôn / Tel: [phone redacted]\n> Ffôn symudol / Mobile: [phone redacted]\n>\n> E-bost/ Email: [email redacted] / [email redacted]\n>\n> P Helpwch yr amgylchedd - peidiwch ag argraffu hon os nad oes gwir\n> raid   Help our environment - only print this if really necessary\n>\n> Rydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth Gymraeg. Cewch ateb Cymraeg i bob gohebiaeth\n> Gymraeg ac ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi.\n>\n> We welcome correspondence in Welsh. Correspondence received in Welsh will\n> be answered in Welsh and corresponding in Welsh will not involve any delay.\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> *From:* Requester <[email redacted]>\n> *Sent:* 10 August 2026 22:46\n> *To:* Longford, Amy (LCC - Cadw) <[email redacted]>\n> *Cc:* Cadw Planning <[email redacted]>;\n> [email redacted]; DOUGHTY, Stephen <\n> [email redacted]>\n> *Subject:* Re: CADW failures – Revised Report (10 August 2026)\n>\n>\n>\n> Sirs/Ma'ams,\n>\n> I write further to my email of 9 August 2026 submitting a review\n> concerning Cadw’s 1988 assessment of Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough.\n>\n> Please find attached (and linked below) a revised edition of the report,\n> dated 10 August 2026. This version has been carefully restructured to\n> present the same evidence in a more measured and administratively focused\n> form. It is offered as a request for historical record correction, archival\n> explanation, and administrative acknowledgement rather than as a forensic\n> challenge.\n>\n> The revised document:\n>\n> • Clearly states what it is and is not;\n> • Sets out the surviving independent evidence (principally the H.J. Thomas\n> RCAHMW survey of 1974);\n> • Notes the near-total absence of Cadw’s 1988 working papers confirmed in\n> ATISN 27021;\n> • Asks only for realistic, achievable administrative steps: explanation of\n> the archival position, reflection of the surviving evidence in the Historic\n> Environment Record, acknowledgement of the records gap, and consideration\n> of a local heritage marker.\n>\n> It does not claim unlawfulness, seek compensation from Cadw, or demand a\n> public inquiry.\n>\n> I hope this revised presentation is more palatable and will assist the\n> relevant bodies in providing a constructive response.\n>\n> I look forward to your substantive response.\n>\n> Regards,\n>\n> Requester\n> \n> [email redacted]\n>\n> [phone redacted]\n> Sganiwyd y neges hon am bob feirws hysbys wrth iddi adael Llywodraeth\n> Cymru. Mae Llywodraeth Cymru yn cymryd o ddifrif yr angen i ddiogelu eich\n> data. Os cysylltwch â Llywodraeth Cymru, mae ein hysbysiad preifatrwydd\n> yn esbonio sut rydym yn defnyddio eich gwybodaeth a sut rydym yn diogelu\n> eich preifatrwydd. Rydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg. Byddwn yn anfon\n> ateb yn Gymraeg i ohebiaeth a dderbynnir yn Gymraeg ac ni fydd gohebu yn\n> Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi. ----------------- On leaving the Welsh\n> Government this email was scanned for all known viruses. The Welsh\n> Government takes the protection of your data seriously. If you contact the\n> Welsh Government then our Privacy Notice\n> <https://gov.wales/about/welsh-government-privacy-notice/?lang=en>\n> explains how we use your information and the ways in which we protect your\n> privacy. We welcome receiving correspondence in Welsh. Any correspondence\n> received in Welsh will be answered in Welsh and corresponding in Welsh will\n> not lead to a delay in responding.\n>"},{"id":"gm-1a04406c83911cd3","subject":"RE: CADW failures – Revised Report (10 August 2026)","name":"RE: CADW failures – Revised Report (10 August 2026)","date":"2026-08-27T16:21:26Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a04406c83911cd3.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19fe7c63edda5834-cadw-failures-revised-report-10-august-2026","scopeTopics":["cadw"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["cadw"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19fe7c63edda5834","direction":"received","from":"(name not shown; gov.wales)","institutions":[{"id":"cadw","name":"Welsh Government / Cadw"}],"refs":["ATISN27021"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a04406c83911cd3.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester\n\nThank you for your latest correspondence dated 10th August in relation to your initial freedom of Information request (ATISN 27021) submitted in June 2026, which has been escalated to me for response.\n\nAs stated by my colleagues, I can confirm that all records we hold have been released to you. We do not hold any further records relating to the content or disposal of the historic records you have requested.\n\nTo confirm, colleagues in the records office have clarified the historic retention position to the best of their knowledge. Given that the issue relates to an historic record retention policy, and that no records regarding this are available, it is not possible to say exactly what CADW’s retention arrangements were over 38 years ago. However, based on their knowledge of the systems in place at the time, current colleagues have advised that it is likely that records of unsuccessful listings were only retained for a short period, usually about 3 years. This has since changed and current retention policy in relation to unsuccessful listings is to retain the files indefinitely, reviewed every 5 years.\n\nIn relation to your question ‘Did Cadw’s 1988 assessment of Great House Farm fully account for the independent professional evidence then available- particularly the H.J. Thomas RCAHMW survey of 1974- and if not why not?’. As stated, all of the records we hold have been released to you under the FOI and are now a public record. As the release consists of only two photographs we are unable to determine whether or not the 1974 survey was taken into account at the time.\n\nIn relation to question A, please see our previous emails of 2nd, 3rd, 10th and 29th July, together with the above explanation of the historic and current retention policies.\n\nIn relation to question B, content of the HER is managed by Heneb. Please contact www.heneb.org.uk<http://www.heneb.org.uk/> in relation to adding information to the HER.\n\nIn relation to question C, please refer to the FOI response provided on the 11th June 2026 which is a matter of public record and confirms Cadw’s position.\n\nIn relation to question D, noted above.\n\nIn relation to question E, Cadw are unable to require or facilitate the installation of a heritage marker at the site.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nAndy Wigley\n\n\n[cid:[email redacted]1F18F0]\n\nDr Andy Wigley\nPrif Arolygydd – Pennaeth Arolygiaeth yr Amgylchedd Hanesyddol / Chief Inspector - Head of Historic Environment Inspectorate\nCadw\nDysgu, Cymunedau a Diwylliant / Learning, Communities and Culture\nLlywodraeth Cymru / Welsh Government\nTŷ Ladywell / Ladywell House\nY Drenewydd / Newtown\nPowys\n[postcode redacted]\n\nFfôn / Tel: [phone redacted]\nFfôn symudol / Mobile: [phone redacted]\nE-bost/ Email: [email redacted]<[email redacted]> / [email redacted]<[email redacted]>\n[cid:[email redacted]1F18F0][cid:[email redacted]1F18F0]\nP Helpwch yr amgylchedd - peidiwch ag argraffu hon os nad oes gwir raid   Help our environment - only print this if really necessary\nRydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth Gymraeg. Cewch ateb Cymraeg i bob gohebiaeth Gymraeg ac ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi.\nWe welcome correspondence in Welsh. Correspondence received in Welsh will be answered in Welsh and corresponding in Welsh will not involve any delay.\n\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSent: 10 August 2026 22:46\nTo: Longford, Amy (LCC - Cadw) <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nCc: Cadw Planning <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>; [email redacted]<[email redacted]>; DOUGHTY, Stephen <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSubject: Re: CADW failures – Revised Report (10 August 2026)\n\nSirs/Ma'ams,\n\nI write further to my email of 9 August 2026 submitting a review concerning Cadw’s 1988 assessment of Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough.\n\nPlease find attached (and linked below) a revised edition of the report, dated 10 August 2026. This version has been carefully restructured to present the same evidence in a more measured and administratively focused form. It is offered as a request for historical record correction, archival explanation, and administrative acknowledgement rather than as a forensic challenge.\n\nThe revised document:\n\n• Clearly states what it is and is not;\n• Sets out the surviving independent evidence (principally the H.J. Thomas RCAHMW survey of 1974);\n• Notes the near-total absence of Cadw’s 1988 working papers confirmed in ATISN 27021;\n• Asks only for realistic, achievable administrative steps: explanation of the archival position, reflection of the surviving evidence in the Historic Environment Record, acknowledgement of the records gap, and consideration of a local heritage marker.\n\nIt does not claim unlawfulness, seek compensation from Cadw, or demand a public inquiry.\n\nI hope this revised presentation is more palatable and will assist the relevant bodies in providing a constructive response.\n\nI look forward to your substantive response.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester\n\n[email redacted]<[email redacted]>\n[phone redacted]\nSganiwyd y neges hon am bob feirws hysbys wrth iddi adael Llywodraeth Cymru. Mae Llywodraeth Cymru yn cymryd o ddifrif yr angen i ddiogelu eich data. Os cysylltwch â Llywodraeth Cymru, mae ein hysbysiad preifatrwydd<”https://gov.wales/about/welsh-government-privacy-notice/?lang=cy”> yn esbonio sut rydym yn defnyddio eich gwybodaeth a sut rydym yn diogelu eich preifatrwydd. Rydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg. Byddwn yn anfon ateb yn Gymraeg i ohebiaeth a dderbynnir yn Gymraeg ac ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi. ----------------- On leaving the Welsh Government this email was scanned for all known viruses. The Welsh Government takes the protection of your data seriously. If you contact the Welsh Government then our Privacy Notice<https://gov.wales/about/welsh-government-privacy-notice/?lang=en> explains how we use your information and the ways in which we protect your privacy. We welcome receiving correspondence in Welsh. Any correspondence received in Welsh will be answered in Welsh and corresponding in Welsh will not lead to a delay in responding."},{"id":"gm-1a03f6bf8659fbd6","subject":"DBDT79/1 – physical examination of the original","name":"DBDT79/1 – physical examination of the original","date":"2026-08-26T18:53:54Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a03f6bf8659fbd6.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a03f5d330ba15ad-dbdt79-1-physical-examination-of-the-original","scopeTopics":["glamorgan"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["glamorgan"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a03f5d330ba15ad","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"glamorgan","name":"Glamorgan Archives"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a03f6bf8659fbd6.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Glamorgan Archives Team,\n\nFurther to my email of 22 August 2026 concerning the DBDT collection and\nrelated Bute, Mountjoy and Western Ground Rents material (particularly\nGreat House Farm / Tŷ Mawr, Llandough), please treat this as an additional\nrequest.\n\n\n\n\nI have attached an image of the land parcel in question, believed to be in\nthe mislabelled record in question.\n\n**Request: physical examination of DBDT79/1**\n\nCatalogue entry:\n\nLease (counterpart) of land and premises situate in Pembroke Terrace,\nCardiff, from the Marquess of Bute to Mr John Williams, May 1877 [1\nparchment, plan in margin].\n\nPlease examine the original document (not the catalogue description alone)\nand confirm:\n\n1. Parties and date – Does the original name the Marquess of Bute and Mr\nJohn Williams, and is it dated May 1877?\n\n2. Property description – Exact wording of the land and premises. Does it\nexpressly locate the property in Pembroke Terrace, Cardiff?\n\n3. Llandough / other references – Any mention (body text, plan, schedule,\nendorsements or annotations) of Llandough, Great House Farm / Tŷ Mawr,\nWestern Ground Rents, or any locality outside Pembroke Terrace?\n\n4. Marginal plan – Does the plan identify the location sufficiently? If\nprocedures allow, please supply a digital image or scan of the plan.\n\n5. Further identifying details – Any adjoining owners, plot numbers,\nparish, street numbers, measurements, previous ownership, rent,\nconsideration, or cross-references to other instruments.\n\nIf scanning is permitted, images of the parties clause, property\ndescription and marginal plan would be ideal. Otherwise a transcription or\ndetailed description of those sections will suffice.\n\n\n**Catalogue wording discrepancy**\n\nMost other 1877 entries in the series describe the grantor as the “Trustees\nof the Will of the late Marquess of Bute”. DBDT79/1 is catalogued simply as\n“from the Marquess of Bute”.\n\nFrom the original, please confirm:\n\n- who executed the lease;\n\n- the capacity in which the grantor is described;\n\n- whether the Marquess is shown acting personally or through trustees;\n\n- any evident reason for the difference from the other 1877 entries.\n\n(I note the 3rd Marquess attained majority around this period, but I seek\nthe Archives’ evidence-based confirmation rather than assumption.)\n\n\n**Purpose**\n\nI am reconstructing the historical title position for Great House Farm / Tŷ\nMawr, Llandough, and need to distinguish any Cardiff property records from\nthose relating to the Llandough estate. Please report the physical contents\nof DBDT79/1 separately from the catalogue description, noting any\ndifferences.\n\nThank you for your assistance. I look forward to your response to both the\noriginal enquiry and this request.\n\n\nAlso see attached a letter to my friend earlier today, which puts the\npurpose of this enquiry and search and record and its importance into some\ncontext for you. If you're at all interested. It might help you to unearth\nrecords I'm unaware of.\n\nThere's 133 houses about to be developed on this land and their title maybe\ndefective. So please be diligent so that an egregious irreversible mistake\nor injustice isn't made.\n\nRegards,\n\n\nRequester "},{"id":"gm-1a03ec02083c07d9","subject":"Re: FOI / Records Request – Deaths of William (Billy) Buckler (1990) and Mary Doreen Williams (1983)","name":"Re: FOI / Records Request – Deaths of William (Billy) Buckler (1990) and Mary Doreen Williams (1983)","date":"2026-08-26T15:46:13Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a03ec02083c07d9.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0165faa913f91d-foi-records-request-deaths-of-william-billy-buckler-1990-and-mary-doreen-williams-1983","scopeTopics":["rct"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["rct"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0165faa913f91d","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"rct","name":"Coroner's Service (RCT Council)"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a03ec02083c07d9.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Bev,\n\nThank you for the clarification. The distinction is important to my enquiry.\n\nI am conducting a forensic reconstruction of the historical circumstances\nsurrounding the *BP Properties Ltd v Buckler* dispute, including the events\nsurrounding Mary Doreen Williams, and her death in March 1983.\n\nMary died during the very short period in which she would have had an\nopportunity to challenge the registration of her land in BP’s name. Her\nownership claim was never adjudicated or exstinguished. BP's award of\npossession of her land (not ownership) resulted in a brutal dispossession\nof her children and grandchildren resulting in a ECHR case in 1989, which\nalso prioritised BP's rights over the families.\n\nI am therefore examining the documentary evidence concerning both the\nland-registration dispute and the circumstances surrounding her death. I am\nnot suggesting from the timing alone that the two matters were connected;\nrather, I am seeking to establish the underlying facts and ensure that the\nsurviving official record has been properly accounted for.\n\nFor that reason, I need to distinguish between *a historical absence of a\ncoronial record* and *the absence of a record from a modern computer system\nwhich does not contain that historical material*.\n\nI understand from your latest email that your computer system does not\nstore information concerning referrals received in 1983 or before. On that\nbasis, I understand that the search of your present system cannot establish\nwhether Mary Doreen Williams was or was not referred to the coroner.\n\nCould I therefore clarify two points separately?\n\n*1. Mary Doreen Williams — March 1983*\n\nDoes your office have any information concerning where the historical\nrecords of the coroner responsible for Llandough Hospital in March 1983 are\nnow held, even if those records are no longer searchable by your office?\n\nI am trying to establish the documentary chain:\n\n*responsible coroner/jurisdiction → historical registers or case files →\ntransfer to archive or successor authority → present custodian, or\ndocumented destruction/retention history.*\n\nIf you do not know the name of the coroner, could you identify the former\ncoroner’s jurisdiction or office responsible for Llandough Hospital at that\ntime? That would enable me to make a properly targeted request to the\nrelevant archive or records custodian.\n\nYou previously indicated that Glamorgan Archives may hold paper copies of\nreports and post-mortem examination details. Could you clarify whether you\nknow that the historical Cardiff and Vale coronial records were actually\ntransferred to Glamorgan Archives, or whether Glamorgan Archives is being\nsuggested simply as the most likely repository?\n\nIf there is a known archive series, accession, register, transfer record or\nother indication of where the former coroner’s records were deposited, that\ninformation would be particularly helpful.\n\n*2. William (Billy) Buckler — May 1990*\n\nThere is a separate point concerning William (Billy) Buckler, who died in\n1990.\n\nYour latest clarification refers specifically to information for referrals\nreceived in *1983 or before*. As Billy died in 1990, could you please\nclarify whether his case was capable of being searched in the computer\nsystem you checked?\n\nIf it was searchable, I would be grateful if you could explain what the\nnegative result means — for example, whether no electronic record exists,\nor whether the relevant Cardiff and Vale coronial records for that period\nare held elsewhere.\n\nYour earlier email stated:\n\n“Cardiff and Vale cases prior to 2002 should be with the Glamorgan\nArchivist.”\n\nCould you please confirm whether that applies to Billy’s 1990 case as well?\n\nMore generally, I am trying to establish whether either death generated,\nand whether there may now survive:\n\n   - a referral to the coroner;\n   - a coroner’s register entry or index;\n   - a preliminary investigation or enquiry;\n   - a post-mortem examination;\n   - an inquest;\n   - police, hospital or other official correspondence;\n   - cause-of-death documentation;\n   - coroner’s case papers; or\n   - an archival transfer, retention or destruction record.\n\nI appreciate that some underlying records may be subject to access\nrestrictions, and I am not asking your office to disclose confidential\nmedical information at this stage. My immediate objective is more\nfundamental: *to establish whether a coronial record was created, whether\nit survives, and what happened to it.*\n\nThat distinction is important to the historical investigation. A\npresent-day search producing no result is evidentially quite different from\na historical record establishing that no referral, investigation,\npost-mortem or inquest ever took place.\n\nI will now approach Glamorgan Archives, but any information you can provide\nregarding the former coroner’s jurisdiction, the historical records held by\nthat jurisdiction, and their subsequent transfer or disposal would allow me\nto make that request considerably more precise.\n\nThank you again for taking the time to clarify the position. Your latest\nexplanation has been genuinely helpful in establishing the limitations of\nthe present electronic search, and I appreciate your assistance in\nidentifying the surviving historical record.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester\n\n\nOn Tue, 25 Aug 2026, 06:43 Morgan, Beverley, <[email redacted]>\nwrote:\n\n> Good morning, Requester,\n>\n> Sorry for the delay in my reply.\n>\n> Confirming my statement  “Unable to locate any details”- My computer\n> system does not store any information for referrals received in 1983 or\n> before, I am unable to confirm if any referrals were ever received for the\n> late Mary Doreen Williams or the late Mr William (Billy) Buckler. I am\n> unable to give details of the coroner at that time for Llandough Hospital.\n>\n> Glamorgan Archives will hopefully have the information you requested, and\n> they may hold paper copies of reports and postmortem examinations details\n> that you require.\n>\n> Wishing you well in your search\n>\n> Kind Regards\n>\n> Bev Morgan\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> *From:* Requester <[email redacted]>\n> *Sent:* 21 August 2026 17:43\n> *To:* Coroner Admin <[email redacted]>\n> *Subject:* Re: FOI / Records Request – Deaths of William (Billy) Buckler\n> (1990) and Mary Doreen Williams (1983)\n>\n>\n>\n> Dear Bev,\n>\n> Thank you for checking the system and for your prompt and helpful reply.\n>\n> I am grateful for the clarification regarding the historical position of\n> Cardiff and Vale cases prior to 2002, and I will now pursue enquiries with\n> Glamorgan Archives.\n>\n> Before I do so, I would be grateful if I could clarify one point\n> concerning the result of your search, particularly in relation to my\n> grandmother, Mary Doreen Williams.\n>\n> My purpose is not simply genealogical. I am attempting to reconstruct and\n> forensically assess the historical circumstances surrounding her death in\n> 1983, and there is information concerning the circumstances before and\n> around her death which I am seeking to reconcile against the surviving\n> official documentary record.\n>\n> Could you therefore please confirm whether your statement that you are\n> \"unable to locate any details\" means only that neither individual appears\n> in the records or computerised/indexed system currently available to your\n> office, or whether your office is able positively to state that neither\n> death was ever referred to the coroner?\n>\n> In particular, if you are able to clarify, I would be grateful to know\n> whether the absence of a record in your system excludes, or does not\n> exclude, the historical existence of:\n>\n>    - a report of the death to a coroner;\n>    - a preliminary coroner's enquiry;\n>    - a post-mortem examination;\n>    - an inquest;\n>    - correspondence with the police, hospital or other authority;\n>    - a case file or register subsequently transferred to an archive or\n>    destroyed in accordance with an applicable retention policy.\n>\n> I appreciate that you may not hold the historical records themselves. I am\n> not asking you to speculate about the circumstances of either death. I am\n> simply seeking to ensure that I correctly understand the evidential\n> significance of the negative search result before proceeding to the archive.\n>\n> If possible, I would also be grateful if you could confirm the name of the\n> coroner jurisdiction or office responsible for Llandough/Llandough Hospital\n> in March 1983, when Mary Doreen Williams died, and whether you know which\n> body would have inherited or transferred that jurisdiction's surviving\n> records.\n>\n> For completeness, if there are any records held off-system, legacy\n> indexes, paper registers, accession records or transfer information which\n> are not searchable through the system you checked, I would be grateful if\n> you could let me know whether those exist and who now holds them.\n>\n> I appreciate your assistance. I am trying to establish the documentary\n> facts carefully and avoid drawing conclusions before the relevant records\n> have been located or ruled out.\n>\n> Kind regards,\n>\n> Requester \n>\n>\n>\n> On Fri, 21 Aug 2026, 05:14 Coroner Admin, <[email redacted]>\n> wrote:\n>\n> Good morning, Requester\n>\n> Thank you for your prompt reply.\n>\n> I have checked our system for any records relating to the two cases you\n> have asked information for.\n>\n> Unfortunately, I am unable to locate any details on the Late Mr William\n> Buckler or the late Mary Doreen Williams.\n>\n> Cardiff and Vale cases prior to 2002 should be with the Glamorgan\n> Archivist, you can contact them on Tel; [phone redacted] or\n> [email redacted]\n>\n> I hope this information helps and I wish you the best in your search.\n>\n> Kind Regards\n>\n> Bev\n>\n>\n>\n> *From:* Requester <[email redacted]>\n> *Sent:* 21 August 2026 08:47\n> *To:* Coroner Admin <[email redacted]>\n> *Subject:* Re: FOI / Records Request – Deaths of William (Billy) Buckler\n> (1990) and Mary Doreen Williams (1983)\n>\n>\n>\n> Bev,\n>\n> Thank you for clarifying the position regarding the Freedom of Information\n> Act.\n>\n> I understand that coroners and their records are not subject to the FOI\n> access regime. I am therefore not seeking to pursue the matter as an FOI\n> request if that is not the appropriate route.\n>\n> My primary purpose is to establish whether either of these deaths was ever\n> referred to the coroner and, if so, whether any surviving record exists and\n> what lawful route is available for an interested family member to obtain\n> information from it.\n>\n> I would therefore be grateful if you could clarify the following, insofar\n> as you are able:\n>\n>    1. Whether your office has any record or index indicating that either\n>    William (Billy) Buckler, who died at Llandough Hospital on or about 29 May\n>    1990, or Mary Doreen Williams, whose death was registered in March 1983 in\n>    South Glamorgan (Vol. 28, p. 3029), was reported to or dealt with by the\n>    coroner;\n>    2. If either death was the subject of a coroner's investigation,\n>    whether an inquest, post-mortem examination or other coroner's\n>    investigation took place;\n>    3. Whether any historical record relating to either death remains in\n>    the custody of the coroner's service, or whether such records have been\n>    transferred to another archive, authority or successor body;\n>    4. If records exist but cannot be disclosed directly, what procedure\n>    is available for a descendant or other interested person to establish the\n>    basic factual information concerning the case;\n>    5. If your office has no record whatsoever of either death, whether\n>    that means that no coroner's record exists, or simply that the surviving\n>    records are no longer held by your office.\n>\n> I am particularly interested in establishing the documentary chain rather\n> than necessarily seeking disclosure of confidential medical information. A\n> confirmation that a death was, or was not, referred to the coroner would\n> itself be useful.\n>\n> If you are not the appropriate office to answer these historical-record\n> questions, I would be grateful if you could identify the relevant archive,\n> successor body or other custodian to whom I should direct the enquiry.\n>\n> Thank you again for your assistance.\n>\n> Regards,\n>\n>\n>\n> Requester \n>\n> \n>\n>\n>\n> On Fri, 21 Aug 2026, 04:33 Coroner Admin, <[email redacted]>\n> wrote:\n>\n> Good morning, Requester\n>\n> Thank you for your e-mail dated 20/08/2026.\n>\n>\n>\n> Although appointed and paid for by local councils, the coroner is not a\n> local government officer but holds office under the Crown.\n>\n>\n>\n> Coroners are not designated as public authorities under the Freedom of\n> Information Act 2000 and therefore their records are not subject to the\n> information access regime of this Act.\n>\n>\n>\n> Please let me know if I can be of further assistance.\n>\n> Kind Regards\n>\n> Bev Morgan\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> *From:* Requester <[email redacted]>\n> *Sent:* 20 August 2026 20:34\n> *To:* Coroner Admin <[email redacted]>\n> *Subject:* FOI / Records Request – Deaths of William (Billy) Buckler\n> (1990) and Mary Doreen Williams (1983)\n>\n>\n>\n> Dear Coroner’s Office,\n>\n> I am writing under the Freedom of Information Act 2000 and as an\n> interested person to request any records held by the South Wales Central\n> Coroner’s Service relating to the following two deaths:\n>\n> 1. William (Billy) Buckler\n>    - Late of Great House Farm, Llandough\n>    - Died at Llandough Hospital on or about 29 May 1990, aged 41\n>    - Reported cause: heart attack\n>    - Funeral 5 June 1990, Bethesda Chapel, Dinas Powys; interment St\n> Michael’s Church, Michaelston-le-Pit\n>\n> 2. Mary Doreen Williams\n>    - Born 10 August 1913\n>    - Death registered March 1983, South Glamorgan (Vol 28, Page 3029)\n>\n> Please advise whether either death was reported to the Coroner, whether a\n> post-mortem or inquest was held, and what documentati