"text":"Dear Requester\n\nThank you for your email requesting an internal review of our response to FOI 2026-024.\n\nInternal Review\nWe have accepted your request for an internal review of FOI 2026-024.\n\nThe purpose of an internal review is to consider whether Amgueddfa Cymru handled the original request appropriately, including whether:\n\n  *   the request was interpreted correctly;\n  *   reasonable and proportionate searches were undertaken;\n  *   relevant information held by the Museum was identified and considered; and\n  *   the response complied with the requirements of the Freedom of Information Act 2000.\n\nThe internal review process is not ordinarily intended to redefine or broaden the scope of the original request, nor to conduct new searches solely on the basis of additional search terms or lines of enquiry proposed after the original response has been issued.\nNevertheless, the reviewer will consider the points raised in your correspondence when assessing whether the original request was interpreted appropriately and whether the searches undertaken were reasonable in the circumstances.\n\nWe aim to provide the outcome of the internal review within 20 working days of receipt of your request. If, exceptionally, we are unable to conclude the review within that period, we will write to you again explaining the position.\n\nAdditional Information Request\nYour email also requests information concerning other archaeological discoveries recorded at Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough, including details of those discoveries and the records identified during our searches. We consider this element of your correspondence to be a new request for recorded information, distinct from FOI 2026-024.\n\nWe have therefore registered this as a separate Freedom of Information request, ref FOI 2026-32.\n\nUnder section 10 of the Freedom of Information Act 2000, we will respond to that request within 20 working days of receipt.   Based on the date your correspondence was received, we aim to respond to the new request by 23 September 2026.\n\nThe internal review and the new request will be considered separately and may result in separate responses.\n\n\nCofion cynnes,\n\n[A picture containing text, tableware, plate, dishware  Description automatically generated]\nFOI Team\n[cid:image002.png@01DD3548.863D3AA0]\n[email redacted]<[email redacted]>\namgueddfa.cymru<applewebdata://288FE56C-5BFC-474C-ADB3-A1017EF25E69/amgueddfa.cymru> | museum.wales<applewebdata://288FE56C-5BFC-474C-ADB3-A1017EF25E69/museum.wales>\n\nRhif elusen | Charity number 525774\n\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]>\nSent: 26 August 2026 07:24\nTo: FOI Requests <[email redacted]>\nSubject: Re: FOI - 2026-024 - Great House Farm, Llandough\n\n\nThis email comes from outside the organisation.\n\nDo not click links or open attachments unless it is an email you expected to receive.\nDear FOI Team,\n\nThank you for your response.\n\nI am requesting an internal review of the handling of my request. I do not consider the response sufficient because it appears that the searches were conducted too narrowly around the later description of a \"medieval visor, lance, helmet or other item of armour\", rather than around the underlying historical discovery at Great House Farm itself.\n\nThe issue I am investigating is a reported nineteenth-century discovery at Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough. My understanding from contemporary or near-contemporary newspaper and historical accounts is that the discovery was described as involving the remains of a Roman soldier, with associated remains and objects. The dating and description of the individual objects as \"medieval\" may therefore itself be inaccurate, or may represent a later conflation or retelling of the original discovery.\n\nYour response is particularly significant because it states:\n\n<\"Our searches identified records relating to ... other archaeological discoveries recorded at Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough. These records do not relate to, nor are they otherwise associated with, the discovery described in your request. We have therefore not considered them to fall within the scope of this request.\">\n\nI respectfully challenge that approach.\n\nThe central question is precisely whether the discovery described in later historical accounts can be identified, corroborated, corrected or explained through the Museum's records concerning archaeological discoveries at Great House Farm. If the Museum has identified records relating to other discoveries at the same historically significant site, it is not sufficient simply to conclude internally that they are unrelated without disclosing what those discoveries are, when they were recorded, and the basis upon which that conclusion was reached.\n\nI therefore request that the internal review reconsider the scope of my request and undertake searches using, at minimum, the following terms and concepts:\n\n- Great House Farm\n- Ty Mawr\n- Llandough\n- Llandough, Cardiff\n- Roman soldier\n- Roman burial\n- Roman grave\n- Roman remains\n- human remains\n- skeleton\n- horse remains\n- equine remains\n- soldier and horse\n- cavalry burial\n- military burial\n- grave goods\n- Roman military equipment\n- visor\n- helmet\n- lance\n- spear\n- armour\n- archaeological discovery\n- nineteenth-century discovery\n- 1870 and adjacent years\n\nPlease also search for relevant records under alternative historical place-name indexing, donor names, findspot descriptions, parish names and catalogue terminology where the precise modern name \"Great House Farm\" may not have been used.\n\nMost importantly, I ask that you disclose sufficient information about the \"other archaeological discoveries\" which your searches identified at Great House Farm to enable me to understand and assess the Museum's conclusion that they are unrelated. This should include, so far as held:\n\n1. A description or title of each relevant discovery or record.\n2. The approximate date of the discovery or record.\n3. The nature of the material or remains involved.\n4. Any accession number, catalogue number or archival reference.\n5. The recorded provenance or findspot.\n6. Any dating attributed to the discovery.\n7. The basis upon which Amgueddfa Cymru concluded that each record was unrelated to the nineteenth-century discovery described in my request.\n\nI am not asking the Museum to create new archaeological opinions or undertake speculative research. I am asking for recorded information which has already been identified during the searches and which directly concerns archaeological discoveries at the specific site named in my request.\n\nIf the Museum maintains that any of those records fall outside the original scope, then please treat this email additionally as a refined request for the recorded information identified above.\n\nI would also ask the reviewer to consider whether the original response gave insufficient weight to the possibility that the historical account may have inaccurately dated or described the find. A search which excludes Roman, nineteenth-century, burial and equine terminology risks missing the very records capable of establishing what was actually discovered beneath Great House Farm.\n\nGiven that your searches have already identified records concerning archaeological discoveries at Great House Farm itself, I would be grateful for a full reconsideration and disclosure of the recorded information requested above.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\nRequester \n\n\nOn Tue, 25 Aug 2026, 04:25 FOI Requests, <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>> wrote:\nDear Requester,\n\nThank you for your request for information received on 27 July 2026 concerning records relating to a reported discovery at Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough, including a soldier, horse remains and items described as a visor and lance.\n\nAmgueddfa Cymru has undertaken searches of its collections management systems, accession records and associated documentation.\n\nWe have not identified any records relating to the acquisition, accession, donation, transfer or deposit of a medieval visor, lance, helmet or other item of armour associated with Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough. We have also not identified records relating to the specific discovery described in your request, including records of associated human or animal remains, reburial records, excavation reports, watching briefs or discovery reports.\n\nAccordingly, under section 1(1)(a) of the Freedom of Information Act 2000, our response is that Amgueddfa Cymru does not hold the requested information.\n\nFor completeness, our searches identified records relating to other archaeological finds from Church Farm, Llandough and to other archaeological discoveries recorded at Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough. These records do not relate to, nor are they otherwise associated with, the discovery described in your request. We have therefore not considered them to fall within the scope of this request.\n\nIf you are dissatisfied with this response, you may request an internal review by writing to [email redacted]<[email redacted]> within 40 working days of the date of this letter.\n\n\n\nCofion cynnes,\n\n[A picture containing text, tableware, plate, dishware    Description automatically generated]\nFOI Team\n[cid:image002.png@01DD3548.863D3AA0]\n[email redacted]<[email redacted]>\namgueddfa.cymru | museum.wales\n\nRhif elusen | Charity number 525774\n\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSent: 27 July 2026 02:22\nTo: FOI Requests <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSubject: ALLANOL/EXTERNAL - FOI - Great House Farm, Llandough\n\n\nThis email comes from outside the organisation.\n\nDo not click links or open attachments unless it is an email you expected to receive.\nYou don't often get email from [email redacted]<[email redacted]>. Learn why this is important<https://aka.ms/LearnAboutSenderIdentification>\nDear Freedom of Information Team,\n\nI am writing to request information under the Freedom of Information Act 2000 and, where applicable, the Environmental Information Regulations 2004.\n\nA published local history account states that, during works to replace the stone floor of the dining room at Great House Farm (also known as Ty Mawr), Llandough, Cardiff, the remains of a soldier and his horse were discovered beneath the floor.\n\n The remains were subsequently re-buried in the churchyard, and the soldier's visor and lance were given to the National Museum of Wales (Amgueddfa Cymru).\n\nI therefore request the following recorded information held by Amgueddfa Cymru:\n\nAny records relating to the acquisition, accession, donation, transfer or deposit of a medieval visor, lance, helmet, or other items of armour associated with Great House Farm / Ty Mawr / Llandough.\n\nThe accession number(s), date of acquisition or transfer, name of the donor or transferring body, and any provenance or discovery notes linked to these objects.\n\nAny catalogue entries, object records, or internal correspondence concerning the above items.\n\nAny records of associated human or animal remains from the same discovery, including notes on reburial.\n\nAny related excavation, watching-brief, or discovery reports held by the Museum.\n\nIf any of the requested information is already publicly available (for example via the Museum's online collections database), I would be grateful if you could simply provide the relevant accession numbers or catalogue links.\n\nPlease treat this as a request for recorded information only. I am not seeking advice or opinion.\n\nI look forward to your response within the statutory timescale.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\nRequester \n[cid:image003.jpg@01DD3548.863D3AA0]"},{"id":"gm-1a03cbd6af82d931","subject":"Re: FOI - 2026-024 - Great House Farm, Llandough","name":"Re: FOI - 2026-024 - Great House Farm, Llandough","date":"2026-08-26T06:24:01Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a03cbd6af82d931.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a037cf4054f6219-foi-2026-024-great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":["museum"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["museum"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a037cf4054f6219","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"museum","name":"Amgueddfa Cymru - Museum Wales"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a03cbd6af82d931.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear FOI Team,\n\nThank you for your response.\n\nI am requesting an internal review of the handling of my request. I do not\nconsider the response sufficient because it appears that the searches were\nconducted too narrowly around the later description of a \"medieval visor,\nlance, helmet or other item of armour\", rather than around the underlying\nhistorical discovery at Great House Farm itself.\n\nThe issue I am investigating is a reported nineteenth-century discovery at\nGreat House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough. My understanding from contemporary\nor near-contemporary newspaper and historical accounts is that the\ndiscovery was described as involving the remains of a Roman soldier, with\nassociated remains and objects. The dating and description of the\nindividual objects as \"medieval\" may therefore itself be inaccurate, or may\nrepresent a later conflation or retelling of the original discovery.\n\nYour response is particularly significant because it states:\n\n«\"Our searches identified records relating to ... other archaeological\ndiscoveries recorded at Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough. These\nrecords do not relate to, nor are they otherwise associated with, the\ndiscovery described in your request. We have therefore not considered them\nto fall within the scope of this request.\"»\n\nI respectfully challenge that approach.\n\nThe central question is precisely whether the discovery described in later\nhistorical accounts can be identified, corroborated, corrected or explained\nthrough the Museum's records concerning archaeological discoveries at Great\nHouse Farm. If the Museum has identified records relating to other\ndiscoveries at the same historically significant site, it is not sufficient\nsimply to conclude internally that they are unrelated without disclosing\nwhat those discoveries are, when they were recorded, and the basis upon\nwhich that conclusion was reached.\n\nI therefore request that the internal review reconsider the scope of my\nrequest and undertake searches using, at minimum, the following terms and\nconcepts:\n\n- Great House Farm\n- Ty Mawr\n- Llandough\n- Llandough, Cardiff\n- Roman soldier\n- Roman burial\n- Roman grave\n- Roman remains\n- human remains\n- skeleton\n- horse remains\n- equine remains\n- soldier and horse\n- cavalry burial\n- military burial\n- grave goods\n- Roman military equipment\n- visor\n- helmet\n- lance\n- spear\n- armour\n- archaeological discovery\n- nineteenth-century discovery\n- 1870 and adjacent years\n\nPlease also search for relevant records under alternative historical\nplace-name indexing, donor names, findspot descriptions, parish names and\ncatalogue terminology where the precise modern name \"Great House Farm\" may\nnot have been used.\n\nMost importantly, I ask that you disclose sufficient information about the\n\"other archaeological discoveries\" which your searches identified at Great\nHouse Farm to enable me to understand and assess the Museum's conclusion\nthat they are unrelated. This should include, so far as held:\n\n1. A description or title of each relevant discovery or record.\n2. The approximate date of the discovery or record.\n3. The nature of the material or remains involved.\n4. Any accession number, catalogue number or archival reference.\n5. The recorded provenance or findspot.\n6. Any dating attributed to the discovery.\n7. The basis upon which Amgueddfa Cymru concluded that each record was\nunrelated to the nineteenth-century discovery described in my request.\n\nI am not asking the Museum to create new archaeological opinions or\nundertake speculative research. I am asking for recorded information which\nhas already been identified during the searches and which directly concerns\narchaeological discoveries at the specific site named in my request.\n\nIf the Museum maintains that any of those records fall outside the original\nscope, then please treat this email additionally as a refined request for\nthe recorded information identified above.\n\nI would also ask the reviewer to consider whether the original response\ngave insufficient weight to the possibility that the historical account may\nhave inaccurately dated or described the find. A search which excludes\nRoman, nineteenth-century, burial and equine terminology risks missing the\nvery records capable of establishing what was actually discovered beneath\nGreat House Farm.\n\nGiven that your searches have already identified records concerning\narchaeological discoveries at Great House Farm itself, I would be grateful\nfor a full reconsideration and disclosure of the recorded information\nrequested above.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\nRequester \n\n\nOn Tue, 25 Aug 2026, 04:25 FOI Requests, <[email redacted]>\nwrote:\n\n> Dear Requester,\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you for your request for information received on 27 July 2026\n> concerning records relating to a reported discovery at Great House Farm (Ty\n> Mawr), Llandough, including a soldier, horse remains and items described as\n> a visor and lance.\n>\n>\n>\n> Amgueddfa Cymru has undertaken searches of its collections management\n> systems, accession records and associated documentation.\n>\n>\n>\n> We have not identified any records relating to the acquisition, accession,\n> donation, transfer or deposit of a medieval visor, lance, helmet or other\n> item of armour associated with Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough. We\n> have also not identified records relating to the specific discovery\n> described in your request, including records of associated human or animal\n> remains, reburial records, excavation reports, watching briefs or discovery\n> reports.\n>\n>\n>\n> Accordingly, under section 1(1)(a) of the Freedom of Information Act 2000,\n> our response is that Amgueddfa Cymru does not hold the requested\n> information.\n>\n>\n>\n> For completeness, our searches identified records relating to other\n> archaeological finds from Church Farm, Llandough and to other\n> archaeological discoveries recorded at Great House Farm (Ty Mawr),\n> Llandough. These records do not relate to, nor are they otherwise\n> associated with, the discovery described in your request. We have therefore\n> not considered them to fall within the scope of this request.\n>\n>\n>\n> If you are dissatisfied with this response, you may request an internal\n> review by writing to [email redacted] within 40 working\n> days of the date of this letter.\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> Cofion cynnes,\n>\n>\n>\n> [image: A picture containing text, tableware, plate, dishware Description\n> automatically generated]\n>\n> *FOI Team*\n>\n> [email redacted] <[email redacted]>\n>\n> amgueddfa.cymru | museum.wales\n>\n>\n>\n> *Rhif elusen* | Charity number 525774\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> *From:* Requester <[email redacted]>\n> *Sent:* 27 July 2026 02:22\n> *To:* FOI Requests <[email redacted]>\n> *Subject:* ALLANOL/EXTERNAL - FOI - Great House Farm, Llandough\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> This email comes from outside the organisation.\n>\n> Do not click links or open attachments unless it is an email you expected\n> to receive.\n>\n> You don't often get email from [email redacted]. Learn why this\n> is important <https://aka.ms/LearnAboutSenderIdentification>\n>\n> Dear Freedom of Information Team,\n>\n>\n>\n> I am writing to request information under the Freedom of Information Act\n> 2000 and, where applicable, the Environmental Information Regulations 2004.\n>\n>\n>\n> A published local history account states that, during works to replace the\n> stone floor of the dining room at Great House Farm (also known as Ty Mawr),\n> Llandough, Cardiff, the remains of a soldier and his horse were discovered\n> beneath the floor.\n>\n>\n>\n>  The remains were subsequently re-buried in the churchyard, and the\n> soldier’s visor and lance were given to the National Museum of Wales\n> (Amgueddfa Cymru).\n>\n>\n>\n> I therefore request the following recorded information held by Amgueddfa\n> Cymru:\n>\n>\n>\n> Any records relating to the acquisition, accession, donation, transfer or\n> deposit of a medieval visor, lance, helmet, or other items of armour\n> associated with Great House Farm / Ty Mawr / Llandough.\n>\n>\n>\n> The accession number(s), date of acquisition or transfer, name of the\n> donor or transferring body, and any provenance or discovery notes linked to\n> these objects.\n>\n>\n>\n> Any catalogue entries, object records, or internal correspondence\n> concerning the above items.\n>\n>\n>\n> Any records of associated human or animal remains from the same discovery,\n> including notes on reburial.\n>\n>\n>\n> Any related excavation, watching-brief, or discovery reports held by the\n> Museum.\n>\n>\n>\n> If any of the requested information is already publicly available (for\n> example via the Museum’s online collections database), I would be grateful\n> if you could simply provide the relevant accession numbers or catalogue\n> links.\n>\n>\n>\n> Please treat this as a request for recorded information only. I am not\n> seeking advice or opinion.\n>\n>\n>\n> I look forward to your response within the statutory timescale.\n>\n>\n>\n> Yours faithfully,\n>\n>\n>\n> Requester \n>"},{"id":"gm-1a0389c4e9493c7a","subject":"RE: Reading Room request","name":"RE: Reading Room request","date":"2026-08-25T11:09:19Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0389c4e9493c7a.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0073ad95b3bb32-ading-room-request","scopeTopics":["bp"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["bp"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0073ad95b3bb32","direction":"received","from":"BP Archive","institutions":[{"id":"bp","name":"BP"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0389c4e9493c7a.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester,\n\nI have checked our records in relation to your request and have been unable to locate any documents concerning the matter prior to 1955. As explained in your email, this is because bp did not acquire the property until the 1960s.\n\nIf you have any other questions do let me know.\n\nThanks,\n\nBethany Jones\narchive assistant\nbp archive\n\nTel: [phone redacted]\n[email redacted] \n\nBP International Limited. Registered office: Chertsey Road, Sunbury on Thames, Middlesex, [postcode redacted]. Registered in England and Wales, number 542515.\n\n\nThe information in this e-mail is confidential and may be legally privileged. It is intended solely for the addressee(s) only. Access to this e-mail by anyone else is unauthorised. If you are not the intended recipient, any disclosure, copying, distribution or an action taken or omitted to be taken in reliance on it, is prohibited and may be unlawful. Within the bounds of the law, electronic transmissions through internal and external networks may be monitored to ensure compliance with internal policies and legitimate business purposes.\n\nFrom: Requester  \nSent: 20 August 2026 07:51\nTo: BP Archive  \nSubject: Re: Reading Room request\n\nYou don't often get email from [email redacted] . Learn why this is important \n\nBethany,\n\nThank you for your reply.\n\nI understand that the BP Archive contains material up to the end of 1954, but that post-1954 material is currently unavailable for external access. My original enquiry did include the later BP acquisition, litigation and registration records, so I understand that those parts of my request cannot currently be accessed.\n\nI would therefore like to clarify what pre-1955 material concerning Great House Farm / Ty Mawr is held within the accessible BP Archive.\n\nThe reason this is particularly relevant is that Great House Farm was subsequently acquired by BP Pension Trust Ltd from Western Ground Rents in 1969, and became the subject of BP Properties Ltd v Buckler [1987] EWCA Civ 2. I am researching the underlying root of title and the history of the family's occupation before 2 February 1955, which is a critical date in the subsequent dispute.\n\nCould you please search the pre-1955 BP Archive holdings for Great House Farm / Ty Mawr, Llandough, including references under:\n\n  *   Great House Farm / Great House / Ty Mawr\n  *   Llandough\n  *   Western Ground Rents Ltd\n  *   Mountjoy Ltd\n  *   Bute Estate / Llandough Estate\n  *   Williams\n  *   Buckler\n\nIn particular, I am looking for any surviving copies or references to:\n\n  *   the 1916 yearly tenancy agreement between the Bute Estate and John Williams;\n  *   the 1924 Llandough Estate sale particulars - DSA/6/701;\n  *   the 1938 Great House Farm auction particulars - GD/LA/15/255 and DSA/6/1158;\n  *   the 1938 conveyance from Mountjoy Ltd to Western Ground Rents;\n  *   the 1949 agreement or correspondence concerning Frederick Buckler and Mary Williams taking over the occupation;\n  *   the 1953 judgment concerning rent arrears at Great House Farm; and\n  *   any notice to quit issued before 2 February 1955.\n\nI would also be interested in any pre-1955 plans, title descriptions, schedules of deeds, abstracts of title or other records showing how the farmhouse, garden, farmyard, cottages and agricultural land were described or treated.\n\nI appreciate that some of these documents may have originated with the Bute Estate, Mountjoy or Western Ground Rents rather than BP itself. What I am trying to establish is whether any such historical material is present within the BP Archive, or whether the BP Archive has catalogue references identifying material which may have subsequently formed part of the title history of the property.\n\nIf the answer is that the BP Archive contains no pre-1955 material relating to Great House Farm / Ty Mawr, I would be grateful if you could confirm that explicitly. If you can identify relevant catalogue references even where the underlying document is not held, those references would also be very useful.\n\nThank you again for your assistance.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester \n\n\n\n\n\nOn Wed, 19 Aug 2026, 08:06 BP Archive,  > wrote:\nDear Requester,\n\nThank you for your interest in BP and our archived records. The materials available are from the organisation's early beginnings up to the end of 1954. Unfortunately, due to the increasing complexity, resource implications of reviewing and recording, and dealing with external access requests, access to post-1954 materials held in the BP Archive is not available.\n\nPlease let me know if you are still interested in accessing any pre-1955 materials for your research.\n\nKind regards,\n\nBethany Jones\narchive assistant\nbp archive\n\nTel: [phone redacted]\n[email redacted] \n\nBP International Limited. Registered office: Chertsey Road, Sunbury on Thames, Middlesex, [postcode redacted]. Registered in England and Wales, number 542515.\n\n\nThe information in this e-mail is confidential and may be legally privileged. It is intended solely for the addressee(s) only. Access to this e-mail by anyone else is unauthorised. If you are not the intended recipient, any disclosure, copying, distribution or an action taken or omitted to be taken in reliance on it, is prohibited and may be unlawful. Within the bounds of the law, electronic transmissions through internal and external networks may be monitored to ensure compliance with internal policies and legitimate business purposes.\n\nFrom: Requester  >\nSent: 15 August 2026 22:03\nTo: BP Archive  >\nSubject: Reading Room request\n\nYou don't often get email from [email redacted] . Learn why this is important \n\nBP Archive Team,\n\nI am writing to enquire about records held within the BP corporate archive relating to the former Great House Farm / Ty Mawr property at Llandough, near Penarth, Glamorgan, South Wales.\n\nI am conducting historical and legal research into the property, which was acquired by BP Pension Trust Ltd in December 1969 and subsequently administered by BP Properties Ltd. The property was the subject of extensive litigation, including BP Properties Ltd v Buckler [1987] EWCA Civ 2, followed by the eviction of the occupying family in 1988 and demolition of the farmhouse later that year.\n\nResearch context\n\nThe central issue of my research is the identification of the individual parcels comprising the historic property.\n\nThe evidence currently being examined indicates that the historic complex may have comprised separately identifiable:\n\n  *   a house-and-garden plot;\n  *   a western agricultural/farm plot containing the barn, stables and courtyard; and\n  *   an eastern cottages plot.\n\nLater mapping and Land Registry records appear to have treated some of these areas as a combined property.\n\nI am therefore particularly interested in establishing what BP itself understood the boundaries and constituent parcels of the property to be, and when any change or consolidation of those parcels occurred.\n\nRecords sought\n\nI would be grateful if the BP Archive could search its holdings for records concerning:\n\n  *   BP Properties Ltd;\n  *   BP Pension Trust Ltd;\n  *   British Petroleum and relevant predecessor/subsidiary property records;\n  *   Great House Farm / Great House / Ty Mawr, Llandough;\n  *   Llandough-juxta-Penarth / Cogan / Penarth;\n  *   the Williams and Buckler families;\n  *   possession proceedings and enforcement, particularly 1955, 1962 and 1974-1988;\n  *   property acquisition, title investigation and disposal;\n  *   conveyances, title deeds, abstracts of title and schedules of deeds;\n  *   estate-management correspondence;\n  *   property, site and boundary plans;\n  *   Land Registry correspondence and registration, particularly 1982-1983.\n\nThe relevant period is approximately 1955-1988, with particular emphasis on 1962-1983.\n\nThree priority categories\n\nIf relevant material survives, I would particularly like to identify the following, in this order:\n\n1. The BP Properties Ltd / BP Pension Trust Ltd property file for Great House Farm / Ty Mawr, Llandough\n\nIn particular, any acquisition/title file containing the original property description, conveyances, title investigation, schedules of deeds, property plans, boundary plans or correspondence identifying exactly which land and buildings BP considered to form the property.\n\n2. The legal/property file relating to the 1962 possession proceedings and subsequent enforcement/re-entry\n\nIn particular, any BP legal correspondence, instructions to solicitors, plans, property descriptions or internal documents identifying the land and buildings to which the possession order was understood to apply.\n\n3. The 1982-1983 title/registration/consolidation file\n\nIn particular, correspondence, plans, conveyances, title investigation and Land Registry material concerning the registration or amalgamation of the relevant parcels, including any documentation showing how the house-and-garden, eastern cottages and western farm/barn/stables/courtyard areas were treated.\n\nIf these exact files do not survive, I would be very grateful for the nearest surviving files, alternative references or catalogue entries covering the same transactions.\n\nAccess\n\nI would also appreciate advice on:\n\n  *   whether the relevant records are available for public research;\n  *   whether they can be viewed in person at the BP Archive at the University of Warwick;\n  *   whether advance appointment or reader registration is required;\n  *   whether particular records require advance retrieval;\n  *   whether copies or digital reproductions can be ordered; and\n  *   whether any relevant records are subject to closure or other access restrictions.\n\nI am happy to provide further information, including the relevant court case reference, property names, dates and Land Registry title numbers, if this would assist the archivist in identifying the records.\n\nResearch purpose\n\nI am particularly interested in contemporary BP documentation rather than later summaries, because the central research question is what BP itself understood the property's legal identity, boundaries and constituent parcels to be at the relevant dates.\n\nThank you very much for any assistance you can provide.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nRequester"},{"id":"gm-1a0384dc67157dc9","subject":"RE: FOI / Records Request – Deaths of William (Billy) Buckler (1990) and Mary Doreen Williams (1983)","name":"RE: FOI / Records Request – Deaths of William (Billy) Buckler (1990) and Mary Doreen Williams (1983)","date":"2026-08-25T09:43:30Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0384dc67157dc9.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a0165faa913f91d-foi-records-request-deaths-of-william-billy-buckler-1990-and-mary-doreen-williams-1983","scopeTopics":["rct"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["rct"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a0165faa913f91d","direction":"received","from":"Morgan, Beverley","institutions":[{"id":"rct","name":"Coroner's Service (RCT Council)"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0384dc67157dc9.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Good morning, Requester,\nSorry for the delay in my reply.\nConfirming my statement  “Unable to locate any details”- My computer system does not store any information for referrals received in 1983 or before, I am unable to confirm if any referrals were ever received for the late Mary Doreen Williams or the late Mr William (Billy) Buckler. I am unable to give details of the coroner at that time for Llandough Hospital.\nGlamorgan Archives will hopefully have the information you requested, and they may hold paper copies of reports and postmortem examinations details that you require.\nWishing you well in your search\nKind Regards\nBev Morgan\n\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]>\nSent: 21 August 2026 17:43\nTo: Coroner Admin <[email redacted]>\nSubject: Re: FOI / Records Request – Deaths of William (Billy) Buckler (1990) and Mary Doreen Williams (1983)\n\n\nDear Bev,\n\nThank you for checking the system and for your prompt and helpful reply.\n\nI am grateful for the clarification regarding the historical position of Cardiff and Vale cases prior to 2002, and I will now pursue enquiries with Glamorgan Archives.\n\nBefore I do so, I would be grateful if I could clarify one point concerning the result of your search, particularly in relation to my grandmother, Mary Doreen Williams.\n\nMy purpose is not simply genealogical. I am attempting to reconstruct and forensically assess the historical circumstances surrounding her death in 1983, and there is information concerning the circumstances before and around her death which I am seeking to reconcile against the surviving official documentary record.\n\nCould you therefore please confirm whether your statement that you are \"unable to locate any details\" means only that neither individual appears in the records or computerised/indexed system currently available to your office, or whether your office is able positively to state that neither death was ever referred to the coroner?\n\nIn particular, if you are able to clarify, I would be grateful to know whether the absence of a record in your system excludes, or does not exclude, the historical existence of:\n\n  *   a report of the death to a coroner;\n  *   a preliminary coroner's enquiry;\n  *   a post-mortem examination;\n  *   an inquest;\n  *   correspondence with the police, hospital or other authority;\n  *   a case file or register subsequently transferred to an archive or destroyed in accordance with an applicable retention policy.\n\nI appreciate that you may not hold the historical records themselves. I am not asking you to speculate about the circumstances of either death. I am simply seeking to ensure that I correctly understand the evidential significance of the negative search result before proceeding to the archive.\n\nIf possible, I would also be grateful if you could confirm the name of the coroner jurisdiction or office responsible for Llandough/Llandough Hospital in March 1983, when Mary Doreen Williams died, and whether you know which body would have inherited or transferred that jurisdiction's surviving records.\n\nFor completeness, if there are any records held off-system, legacy indexes, paper registers, accession records or transfer information which are not searchable through the system you checked, I would be grateful if you could let me know whether those exist and who now holds them.\n\nI appreciate your assistance. I am trying to establish the documentary facts carefully and avoid drawing conclusions before the relevant records have been located or ruled out.\n\nKind regards,\n\nRequester \n\nOn Fri, 21 Aug 2026, 05:14 Coroner Admin, <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>> wrote:\nGood morning, Requester\nThank you for your prompt reply.\nI have checked our system for any records relating to the two cases you have asked information for.\nUnfortunately, I am unable to locate any details on the Late Mr William Buckler or the late Mary Doreen Williams.\nCardiff and Vale cases prior to 2002 should be with the Glamorgan Archivist, you can contact them on Tel; [phone redacted] or [email redacted]<[email redacted]>\nI hope this information helps and I wish you the best in your search.\nKind Regards\nBev\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSent: 21 August 2026 08:47\nTo: Coroner Admin <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSubject: Re: FOI / Records Request – Deaths of William (Billy) Buckler (1990) and Mary Doreen Williams (1983)\n\n\nBev,\n\nThank you for clarifying the position regarding the Freedom of Information Act.\n\nI understand that coroners and their records are not subject to the FOI access regime. I am therefore not seeking to pursue the matter as an FOI request if that is not the appropriate route.\n\nMy primary purpose is to establish whether either of these deaths was ever referred to the coroner and, if so, whether any surviving record exists and what lawful route is available for an interested family member to obtain information from it.\n\nI would therefore be grateful if you could clarify the following, insofar as you are able:\n\n  1.  Whether your office has any record or index indicating that either William (Billy) Buckler, who died at Llandough Hospital on or about 29 May 1990, or Mary Doreen Williams, whose death was registered in March 1983 in South Glamorgan (Vol. 28, p. 3029), was reported to or dealt with by the coroner;\n  2.  If either death was the subject of a coroner's investigation, whether an inquest, post-mortem examination or other coroner's investigation took place;\n  3.  Whether any historical record relating to either death remains in the custody of the coroner's service, or whether such records have been transferred to another archive, authority or successor body;\n  4.  If records exist but cannot be disclosed directly, what procedure is available for a descendant or other interested person to establish the basic factual information concerning the case;\n  5.  If your office has no record whatsoever of either death, whether that means that no coroner's record exists, or simply that the surviving records are no longer held by your office.\n\nI am particularly interested in establishing the documentary chain rather than necessarily seeking disclosure of confidential medical information. A confirmation that a death was, or was not, referred to the coroner would itself be useful.\n\nIf you are not the appropriate office to answer these historical-record questions, I would be grateful if you could identify the relevant archive, successor body or other custodian to whom I should direct the enquiry.\n\nThank you again for your assistance.\n\nRegards,\n\n\n\nRequester \n\n\n\nOn Fri, 21 Aug 2026, 04:33 Coroner Admin, <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>> wrote:\nGood morning, Requester\nThank you for your e-mail dated 20/08/2026.\n\nAlthough appointed and paid for by local councils, the coroner is not a local government officer but holds office under the Crown.\n\nCoroners are not designated as public authorities under the Freedom of Information Act 2000 and therefore their records are not subject to the information access regime of this Act.\n\nPlease let me know if I can be of further assistance.\nKind Regards\nBev Morgan\n\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSent: 20 August 2026 20:34\nTo: Coroner Admin <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSubject: FOI / Records Request – Deaths of William (Billy) Buckler (1990) and Mary Doreen Williams (1983)\n\nDear Coroner’s Office,\n\nI am writing under the Freedom of Information Act 2000 and as an interested person to request any records held by the South Wales Central Coroner’s Service relating to the following two deaths:\n\n1. William (Billy) Buckler\n   - Late of Great House Farm, Llandough\n   - Died at Llandough Hospital on or about 29 May 1990, aged 41\n   - Reported cause: heart attack\n   - Funeral 5 June 1990, Bethesda Chapel, Dinas Powys; interment St Michael’s Church, Michaelston-le-Pit\n\n2. Mary Doreen Williams\n   - Born 10 August 1913\n   - Death registered March 1983, South Glamorgan (Vol 28, Page 3029)\n\nPlease advise whether either death was reported to the Coroner, whether a post-mortem or inquest was held, and what documentation (reports, police referrals, hospital communications, etc.) remains available. Please also confirm the procedure, identification required, any fees, and timescales for release to next-of-kin or interested persons.\n\nI can supply further identifying details, proof of interest, and contemporary newspaper cuttings / family notes upon request.\n\nThank you for your assistance.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\nRequester \n\n\n\nCroesawn ohebu yn Gymraeg a fydd gohebu yn y Gymraeg ddim yn arwain at oedi. Rhowch wybod inni beth yw'ch dewis iaith e.e. Cymraeg neu'n ddwyieithog\n\nMae'r neges ar gyfer y person / pobl enwedig yn unig. Gall gynnwys gwybodaeth bersonol, sensitif neu gyfrinachol. Os nad chi yw'r person a enwyd (neu os nad oes gyda chi’r awdurdod i'w derbyn ar ran y person a enwyd) chewch chi ddim ei chopïo neu’i defnyddio, neu'i datgelu i berson arall. Os ydych chi wedi derbyn y neges ar gam, rhowch wybod i'r sawl sy wedi anfon y neges ar unwaith. Mae'n bosibl y bydd holl negeseuon yn cael eu cofnodi a/neu fonitro unol â’r ddeddfwriaeth berthnasol. I ddarllen yr ymwadiad llawn, ewch i http://www.rctcbc.gov.uk/ymwadiad<http://www.rctcbc.gov.uk/ymwadiad>\n\nWe welcome correspondence in Welsh and corresponding with us in Welsh will not lead to a d