> Dear Medical Examiner Service (South Wales Central),\n>\n> I am writing to enquire whether any records or references are held in\n> relation to the following historical deaths that occurred in the Cardiff &\n> Vale / Llandough area:\n>\n> 1. William (Billy) Buckler\n>    - Died at Llandough Hospital on or about 29 May 1990, aged 41\n>    - Reported cause: heart attack\n>\n> 2. Mary Doreen Williams\n>    - Born 10 August 1913\n>    - Death registered March 1983, South Glamorgan (Vol 28, Page 3029)\n>\n> Although these pre-date the current Medical Examiner Service, I would be\n> grateful if you could advise whether any legacy records, referrals, or\n> related documentation exist, or direct me to the appropriate archive or\n> department that may hold such information.\n>\n> Thank you for your assistance.\n>\n> Yours faithfully,\n>\n>\n> Requester \n> \n>\n>"},{"id":"gm-1a035c288e566f41","subject":"RE: Request for burial / parish register entries – William Buckler (1990) and related records (Mary Doreen Williams)","name":"RE: Request for burial / parish register entries – William Buckler (1990) and related records (Mary Doreen Williams)","date":"2026-08-24T21:52:14Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a035c288e566f41.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a016600b59eb32a-quest-for-burial-parish-register-entries-william-buckler-1990-and-related-records-mary-doreen-williams","scopeTopics":["glamorgan"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["glamorgan"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a016600b59eb32a","direction":"received","from":"Brito, Alexis","institutions":[{"id":"glamorgan","name":"Glamorgan Archives"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a035c288e566f41.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Ref: 2026 – 3769\n\n\n\nDear Requester,\n\n\n\nThank you for your e-mail received 20th August regarding your research.\n\n\nStaff at Glamorgan Archives can confirm that the burial register we have for Michaelston-le-pit only goes up to 1980 so you would need to contact the parish about more recent burials.\n\nWe do not have any registers for the relevant dates for Dinas Powys either but we do have a register that goes up to 2009 for Llandough that could be searched: [postcode redacted]/3/3 .\n\n\nYou are welcome to visit  us to view any documents of interest in person at Glamorgan Archives. Due to limited availability in our research room, you will need to book in advance via e-mail (please provide references of documents that you would like to access on your visit). Also, if you have not visited the archives previously you will need to register with us. So please bring identification with you in the form of a drivers license/passport and utility bill. Our opening times are as follows:\n\nTuesday 09.30 – 12.30 and 1.30 – 4.30\nWednesday 09.30 – 12.30 and 1.30 – 4.30\nThursday 09.30 – 12.30 and 1.30 – 4.30\n\nAlternatively, staff at Glamorgan Archives would be happy to carry out research on your behalf, for which we make a charge of £60 (including VAT) for up to one hour’s work, payable in advance.\nThe following are accepted payment methods:\n\n\n  *   Online payment – details on request\n  *   Cheque, in sterling and drawn on a British bank, made payable to Cardiff Council\n  *   Postal Order\n\nIt will help us in answering your research enquiry if you can be concise in identifying exactly what you want. Keeping it short and accurate will save us time and avoid the risk of duplicating research. State exactly what you want us to do and identify specifics such as names, dates, and places. But only supply information that is relevant to your enquiry. You can complete the necessary form here: research-application-form-2025.doc \n\nWe would also suggest contacting municipal cemeteries for the areas.\n\n\nPlease let us know if you have additional queries or would like to use any of our services.\n\n\n\n\nYours sincerely\n\n\n\nAlexis Brito\nCynorthwyydd Cofnodion/Records Assistant\n\nArchifau Morgannwg / Glamorgan Archives\nClos Parc Morgannwg\nLecwydd / Leckwith\nCaerdydd / Cardiff\n[postcode redacted]\n\n[phone redacted]\nwww.archifaumorgannwg.gov.uk  / www.glamarchives.gov.uk \nwww.facebook.com/glamarchives \nTweet@glamarchives\n\n\n\n\n\nFrom: Requester  \nSent: 20 August 2026 20:34\nTo: Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg  \nSubject: Request for burial / parish register entries – William Buckler (1990) and related records (Mary Doreen Williams)\n\nEXTERNAL: This email originated from outside Cardiff Council, take care when clicking links.\nALLANOL: Daw'r e-bost hwn o'r tu allan i Gyngor Caerdydd, cymerwch ofal wrth glicio ar ddolenni.\n\nDear Glamorgan Archives,\n\nI am researching the deaths and burial arrangements of the following individuals and would be grateful for any assistance with parish / burial registers or related records in your custody:\n\n1. William (Billy) Buckler\n- Died on or about 29 May 1990 at Llandough Hospital, aged 41\n- Interred at St Michael’s Church, Michaelston-le-Pit (funeral service at Bethesda Chapel, Dinas Powys on 5 June 1990)\n- Contemporary reports confirm burial at St Michael’s rather than the family resting place at Llandough.\n\n2. Mary Doreen Williams\n- Born 10 August 1913\n- Death registered March 1983, South Glamorgan (Vol 28, Page 3029)\n- Any burial or related parish entries that may connect her to the Llandough / Michaelston / Dinas Powys area or the Buckler family would be of interest.\n\nPlease advise what registers or indexes are held for St Michael’s, Michaelston-le-Pit and nearby parishes for the relevant periods, the procedure for obtaining copies or transcripts, any research service fees, and how best to proceed.\n\nThank you for your help.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\nRequester \n\n\n**********************************************************************\nThe Council welcomes correspondence in Welsh, English or bilingually. We will ensure that we communicate with you in the language of your choice, as long as you let us know which you prefer. Corresponding in Welsh will not lead to delay.\n\nPrivileged/Confidential Information may be contained in this message. If you are not the addressee indicated in this message (or responsible for delivery of the message to such person), you may not copy or deliver this message to anyone. In such case, you should destroy this message and kindly notify the sender by reply email. Please advise immediately if you or your employer does not consent to Internet email for messages of this kind.  Opinions, conclusions and other information in this message that do not relate to the official business of the Council of the City and County of Cardiff shall be understood as neither given nor endorsed by it.  All e-mail sent to or from this address will be processed by Cardiff County Councils Corporate E-mail system and may be subject to scrutiny by someone other than the addressee.\n**********************************************************************\nMae’r Cyngor yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg, Saesneg neu’n ddwyieithog. Byddwn yn cyfathrebu â chi yn ôl eich dewis, dim ond i chi roi gwybod i ni pa un sydd well gennych. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi.\n\nMae'n bosibl bod gwybodaeth gyfrinachol yn y neges hon. Os na chyfeirir y neges atoch chi'n benodol (neu os nad ydych chi'n gyfrifol am drosglwyddo'r neges i'r person a enwir), yna ni chewch gopio na throsglwyddo'r neges. Mewn achos o'r fath, dylech ddinistrio'r neges a hysbysu'r anfonwr drwy e-bost ar unwaith. Rhowch wybod i'r anfonydd ar unwaith os nad ydych chi neu eich cyflogydd yn caniatau e-bost y Rhyngrwyd am negeseuon fel hon. Rhaid deall nad yw'r safbwyntiau, y casgliadau a'r wybodaeth arall yn y neges hon nad ydynt yn cyfeirio at fusnes swyddogol Cyngor Dinas a Sir Caerdydd yn cynrychioli barn y Cyngor Sir nad yn cael sel ei fendith. Caiff unrhyw negeseuon a anfonir at, neu o'r cyfeiriad e-bost hwn eu prosesu gan system E-bost Gorfforaethol Cyngor Sir Caerdydd a gallant gael eu harchwilio gan rywun heblaw'r person a enwir.\n**********************************************************************"},{"id":"gm-1a03541b39b9a3ee","subject":"Re: Complaint – Failure to Respond / Failure to Deal Properly with Case – Cllr George Carroll (Llandough) – Great House Farm / Church View Close","name":"Re: Complaint – Failure to Respond / Failure to Deal Properly with Case – Cllr George Carroll (Llandough) – Great House Farm / Church View Close","date":"2026-08-24T19:31:32Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a03541b39b9a3ee.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19fa56f91e54c219-complaint-failure-to-respond-failure-to-deal-properly-with-case-cllr-george-carroll-llandough-great-house-farm-church-vi","scopeTopics":["vale-foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["vale-foi"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19fa56f91e54c219","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"vale-foi","name":"Vale of Glamorgan Council"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a03541b39b9a3ee.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Ms Davidson,\n\nThank you for your further response. Very much appreciate your time in this\nmatter.\n\nI have considered it carefully. I now need to make one fundamental point\nclear. This is not any animosity or prejudice but a matter of upmost\nimportance to my family, many of whome are women and too frightened to\nspeak out. I must do my ahsolute best and part here and take the following\nposition on this matter.\n\nI do not accept the characterisation of my complaint as being simply that a\ncouncillor failed to answer a number of emails.\n\nThat formulation removes the very circumstances which made the conduct\ncomplained of potentially relevant to the standards expected of an elected\nmember.\n\nCouncillor Carroll initially responded to me and asked how he could help. I\nthen provided the information and evidence requested. What followed was not\nan isolated unanswered email, but a documented pattern of non-engagement\nover approximately five months, notwithstanding repeated approaches\nconcerning matters affecting his own ward, including planning, development,\nhistorical records and a serious concern that the title upon which current\ndevelopment is proceeding may itself require examination.\n\nI am not asking the Monitoring Officer to determine the ownership of Great\nHouse Farm by correspondence. Nor am I asking the Council to accept my\nallegations as established fact.\n\nI am asking whether the conduct of an elected member, in the circumstances\nactually presented, was properly assessed against the full range of\nstandards applicable to that member.\n1. The Code cannot be reduced to an obligation to answer emails\n\nI accept the proposition that the Members' Code does not create a general\nrule requiring a councillor to answer every email received from a member of\nthe public within a prescribed period.\n\nThat is not the proposition I have advanced.\n\nThe question is whether a sustained course of conduct can engage provisions\nof the Code concerning respect and consideration, conduct capable of\nbringing the member's office or authority into disrepute, misuse of\nposition, and the broader principles governing the conduct of elected\nrepresentatives.\n\nThe Council's current Constitution places the Members' Code within Section\n19 and separately identifies the principles governing members' conduct.\n\nThe Public Services Ombudsman for Wales itself describes the Code as\nimposing enforceable minimum standards on councillors and identifies,\namongst other matters, conduct which negatively affects an authority's\nreputation, unfair use of position, bullying, and failures to treat people\nequally as matters which can potentially constitute breaches.\n\nI therefore ask you to identify precisely which provisions you considered\nand, more importantly, why the particular factual circumstances I supplied\nwere considered incapable of engaging any of them.\n\nA conclusion that \"failure to respond is not itself a breach\" does not\nanswer that question.\n2. The actual chronology matters\n\nPlease record the chronology accurately.\n\nThis was not:\n\nfive emails were sent and a councillor did not reply.\n\nIt was:\n\n   - I approached my elected ward councillor concerning matters affecting\n   his ward;\n   - Councillor Carroll responded and asked how he could help;\n   - I then supplied substantive information and evidence;\n   - further correspondence followed on 16 February, 4 May, 7 June, 25 June\n   and 13 July 2026;\n   - the correspondence concerned, amongst other things, the historical\n   planning and development of Great House Farm / Church View Close, access to\n   relevant public records, and an apparent defect or inconsistency in the\n   historical title chain;\n   - despite the initial invitation to explain how he could assist, there\n   was subsequently no substantive engagement with the matters raised.\n\nThat is the conduct I asked you to assess.\n\nI therefore ask you to confirm whether you considered the actual underlying\nemails and attachments, rather than merely the summary chronology.\n3. Was Councillor Carroll's account obtained?\n\nYou have now confirmed that you did not consider it necessary to seek\ncomments from Councillor Carroll.\n\nI appreciate that you do not have to conduct a full investigation at this\nstage.\n\nNevertheless, that makes the precise nature of your decision particularly\nimportant.\n\nYou have reached a conclusion that the information provided did not\ndisclose evidence capable of amounting to a breach, whilst apparently not\nobtaining the explanation of the person whose conduct was complained of.\n\nThat does not necessarily make the decision unlawful. But it does mean that\nI am entitled to understand precisely what factual inference you drew from\nthe material before you.\n\nIn particular, if there was a reason why Councillor Carroll did not respond\nafter initially offering to help, I do not know what that reason was.\n\nIf the reason was that he considered the matter outside his role, please\nsay so.\n\nIf the reason was that he considered the correspondence excessive,\nvexatious or otherwise inappropriate, please say so.\n\nIf the reason was connected with material I had published or discussed on\nsocial media, please say so.\n\nIf there was another reason, please identify it.\n\nI am not alleging any particular explanation. I am asking that the factual\nbasis of the conduct not simply be assumed.\n4. Social media and equal treatment\n\nI also wish to place one specific issue on record.\n\nI understand that the Code does not simply regulate everything a member or\nmember of the public says on social media. The Ombudsman itself has\nseparate guidance concerning social media and the Code.\n\nAccordingly, if social-media material has influenced Councillor Carroll's\ndecision to disengage from me, I would ask that this be disclosed rather\nthan silently becoming the background justification for his conduct.\n\nIt would be an obvious inconsistency if material said or published by a\nconstituent outside the relevant Code framework were treated as a reason\nfor refusing to engage with that constituent, while the resulting conduct\nof the elected member were then assessed in isolation as merely \"a failure\nto respond\".\n\nAgain, I am not asserting that this occurred. I am asking that it not be\nassumed away.\n5. This is also a question of democratic accountability\n\nThere is a much wider issue here which I do not believe has been addressed.\n\nI am raising a documented public-interest concern concerning land within\nthe Council's area, historical planning and enforcement records, the\ndemolition and subsequent development of Great House Farm / Church View\nClose, and an apparent problem in the title history of land now associated\nwith substantial residential development.\n\nI am not asking the Council to decide my private ownership claim.\n\nI am asking that the concern be capable of being placed before the\ndemocratic institutions of the Council and scrutinised by the appropriate\npeople.\n\nThe Council itself expressly operates mechanisms for public questions at\nFull Council, and its published meeting material demonstrates that\nquestions from the public are part of its democratic process. The Council\nalso states that its meetings are conducted to support and promote\ndemocratic engagement and public interest.\n\nYour response says that no further action will be taken by you. It does\nnot, however, tell me what democratic route remains available for me to\nplace the underlying public-interest concern before elected members.\n\nI therefore ask you to identify that route.\n\nSpecifically, please confirm how I may:\n\n   1.\n\n   submit a public question to Full Council concerning the Council's\n   records and handling of this matter;\n   2.\n\n   submit a petition or e-petition;\n   3.\n\n   seek consideration by the appropriate Scrutiny Committee;\n   4.\n\n   raise the adequacy of the Council's handling of the matter with the\n   Governance and Audit Committee or other appropriate constitutional body; and\n   5.\n\n   request inspection of relevant historical background papers associated\n   with the planning and demolition history of the site.\n\nThe answer cannot simply be that the Monitoring Officer has decided not to\ntake further action. That determines one particular route. It does not\nextinguish democratic accountability.\n6. The public-record issue needs to be separated from the ownership dispute\n\nI also want to avoid any further misunderstanding concerning the planning\nrecords.\n\nI have repeatedly encountered historical material which is not presently\naccessible to me through the Council's online planning system.\n\nI am not presently asserting that this proves deliberate concealment.\n\nThere may be perfectly legitimate explanations: age of the records,\ndigitisation, migration between systems, retention arrangements, technical\nproblems, or the fact that historical material was never placed online.\n\nBut the important point is that the Council's own planning-register\narrangements acknowledge that the online register is not necessarily a\ncomplete database of everything held by the Council.\n\nThat makes the appropriate question one of transparency:\n\n*What records does the Council hold, what records does it no longer hold,\nwhere are the surviving records, and how can they be inspected?*\n\nThat is a straightforward public-record question.\n\nIt should not depend upon the Council first accepting my interpretation of\nthe title history.\n\nI therefore intend to pursue the records question separately and formally,\nincluding under the applicable information-access legislation where\nappropriate.\n7. The historical planning record is itself relevant\n\nThe historical demolition of Great House Farm is not an invented issue\narising solely from my family account.\n\nThe historic environment record identifies Great House Farm as a\nhistorically significant site and records its sudden and complete\ndemolition in December 1988 amid considerable local controversy.\n\nWhatever conclusion is ultimately reached concerning ownership, title or\nthe historical allegations, the existence of a significant historical\nrecord means that it is reasonable to ask what planning, enforcement,\ndemolition, committee and officer records survive and what decisions were\nmade.\n\nI am therefore not asking the Council to rewrite history in accordance with\nmy family's interpretation.\n\nI am asking the Council to disclose its own documentary history.\n8. Monitoring Officer responsibilities\n\nI also ask you to clarify the extent to which you have considered the\nCouncil's own potential obligations as an authority, as distinct from your\nassessment of Councillor Carroll's individual conduct.\n\nSection 5 of the Local Government and Housing Act 1989 places a statutory\nreporting function upon a Monitoring Officer where it appears that a\nproposal, decision or omission by the authority, its committees,\nsub-committees, officers or other specified authority actors gives rise to\nthe statutory circumstances identified in that provision.\n\nI am not asserting that section 5 automatically requires you to issue a\nreport merely because I have asked you to do so.\n\nI am asking a narrower and more reasonable question:\n\n*Have you considered whether any separate omission or decision by the\nCouncil itself — particularly concerning access to public records,\ndemocratic engagement or the handling of this matter — engages your\nstatutory Monitoring Officer responsibilities?*\n\nIf you have considered that question and concluded that section 5 is not\nengaged, please say so and give the reason.\n\nIf you have not considered it, please confirm that.\n9. The Ombudsman\n\nYour response expressly directs me to the Public Services Ombudsman for\nWales.\n\nI will therefore consider making a direct Code of Conduct complaint\nsupported by the underlying correspondence rather than relying upon the\nCouncil's characterisation of the matter.\n\nThat is particularly appropriate because the Ombudsman states that its\ninitial assessment considers whether evidence suggests that a Code breach\nmay have occurred and, separately, whether investigation would be\nsufficiently serious and in the public interest. The Ombudsman specifically\nidentifies maintaining public confidence in local councillors as one factor\nrelevant to that assessment.\n\nI will therefore allow the Ombudsman to make its own assessment of the\nevidence.\n\nI will provide it with the actual chronology, the original correspondence,\nthe initial response from Councillor Carroll asking how he could assist,\nthe subsequent correspondence, and your decision.\n10. One final point about the characterisation of my conduct\n\nI have been persistent because I consider the underlying matter important.\n\nPersistence should not be confused with misconduct.\n\nThe Ombudsman itself expressly states that it does not regard behaviour as\nunacceptable merely because a complainant is forceful or determined, while\nalso making clear that correspondence should remain polite and courteous.\n\nI accept the latter completely.\n\nI am therefore going to continue to pursue these matters firmly, but\nthrough the proper channels and on the evidence.\n\nWhat I will not accept is the repeated administrative reframing of a\nsubstantive public-interest concern as nothing more than an unhappy\nconstituent sending too many emails.\nAccordingly, I ask you to confirm:\n\n   1.\n\n   The exact provisions of the Members' Code of Conduct which you\n   considered.\n   2.\n\n   Whether you considered the five original emails and their attachments in\n   full.\n   3.\n\n   Whether you considered Councillor Carroll's initial response asking how\n   he could assist.\n   4.\n\n   Whether Councillor Carroll was asked for an explanation. If not, please\n   confirm that.\n   5.\n\n   Whether you are aware of any reason why he subsequently ceased\n   substantive engagement.\n   6.\n\n   Whether social-media activity formed any part of the reason for his\n   non-engagement.\n   7.\n\n   Whether you have considered any potential omission or decision by the\n   Council itself under your statutory Monitoring Officer responsibilities.\n   8.\n\n   What democratic mechanism you say is available for me to place the\n   underlying public-interest concern before elected members.\n   9.\n\n   How I can obtain or inspect the surviving planning, enforcement,\n   demolition, committee and officer records relating to Great House Farm /\n   Church View Close which are not available through the online planning\n   system.\n   10.\n\n   Whether any relevant records are known to have been destroyed, disposed\n   of, transferred, archived elsewhere or otherwise become unavailable, and,\n   if so, under what records-management arrangements.\n   11.\n\n   Whether the historical demolition and subsequent development were\n   considered by a Council committee and, if so, where the relevant agendas,\n   reports, minutes and background papers can be inspected.\n\nI am deliberately keeping these questions precise because I want a proper\nevidential record rather than another exchange in which the substance of\nthe issue is lost in administrative categorisation.\n\nI am also not asking you to endorse my conclusions concerning title.\n\nThe democratic question is much simpler:\n\n*Where a constituent places before his elected representative a documented\nconcern that land within the ward may be affected by a defective or\nunresolved title history, and where the historical public records needed to\nexamine that concern are not readily accessible, what mechanism exists for\nthat concern to be transparently examined rather than simply closed\nadministratively?*\n\nThat question remains unanswered.\n\nI would therefore be grateful for a substantive response to the points\nabove.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\n*Requester *\n\n\nOn Mon, 24 Aug 2026, 10:59 Davidson, Victoria, <\n[email redacted]> wrote:\n\n> Dear Requester,\n>\n> Thank you for your email.\n>\n> I note your request for further clarification regarding my decision in my\n> capacity as Monitoring Officer.\n>\n> The assessment I undertook was limited to considering whether the\n> information provided disclosed evidence which, if established, could\n> indicate a failure by the member to comply with the statutory Member’s Code\n> of Conduct. As Monitoring Officer, I do not have formal investigatory\n> powers equivalent to those of the Public Services Ombudsman for Wales and I\n> am not required to undertake an investigation or obtain representations\n> from a member before deciding whether there is sufficient basis for a\n> referral to be made. My role at this stage is to consider whether the\n> information presented discloses evidence of conduct that appears capable of\n> amounting to a breach of the Code.\n>\n> In reaching my view, I considered the information provided within your\n> complaint, including the chronology you supplied regarding correspondence\n> sent to Councillor Carroll between February and July 2026. The substance of\n> the complaint was that you had not received substantive responses to a\n> number of emails sent over that period.\n>\n> For completeness, I considered whether the conduct alleged could\n> potentially engage any of the principal obligations contained within the\n> Member's Code of Conduct. These include requirements that members show\n> respect and consideration for others, do not engage in bullying, harassment\n> or discriminatory behaviour, do not bring their office or authority into\n> disrepute, do not improperly use their position to confer an advantage or\n> disadvantage on any person, and do not misuse Council resources or\n> confidential information.\n>\n> Whilst I appreciate that the absence of a response to correspondence may\n> be frustrating, the information provided did not disclose evidence of\n> disrespectful behaviour, bullying, harassment, misuse of position, misuse\n> of Council resources, disclosure of confidential information, conduct\n> bringing the office of member or the authority into disrepute, or any other\n> behaviour which appeared capable of amounting to a breach of the Code. In\n> reaching that view, I considered the nature of the conduct alleged, namely\n> a failure to respond to correspondence, and whether that conduct engaged\n> any provision of the Code.\n>\n> It is important to note that the Member’s Code of Conduct does not impose\n> a general obligation on elected members to respond to all correspondence\n> received from members of the public, nor does it prescribe timescales\n> within which responses must be provided. The Code is concerned with\n> standards of conduct rather than the quality, adequacy or timeliness of\n> constituency casework. Consequently, a decision not to respond, or not to\n> respond in the manner sought by a correspondent, does not of itself\n> constitute evidence of a breach of the Code.\n>\n> Given that the information provided did not disclose evidence which\n> appeared capable of amounting to a breach of the Code, I did not consider\n> it necessary or proportionate to seek comments from Councillor Carroll\n> before reaching that conclusion. My decision was therefore based upon the\n> information contained within your complaint and whether that information,\n> taken at its highest, indicated a potential breach of the Code warranting\n> further action.\n>\n> For completeness, any person is entitled to submit a complaint directly to\n> the Public Services Ombudsman for Wales if they believe a member has failed\n> to comply with the Member’s Code of Conduct. The Ombudsman is independent\n> of the Council and is responsible for determining whether a complaint\n> should be investigated and, if so, whether any further action is\n> appropriate.\n>\n> I trust this clarifies the basis of my decision. Having reviewed the\n> information provided, my position remains that I have not identified\n> evidence which would justify further action by me in my capacity as\n> Monitoring Officer.\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> Victoria Davidson\n>\n> Monitoring Officer/Head of Legal and Democratic Services / Swyddog\n> Monitro / Pennaeth Gwasanaethau Cyfreithiol a Democrataidd\n>\n> Director's Office - Resources / Swyddfa’r Cyfarwyddwr - Adnoddau\n>\n> Vale of Glamorgan Council / Cyngor Bro Morgannwg\n>\n> tel / ffôn: [phone redacted]\n>\n> mob / sym: [phone redacted]\n>\n> e-mail / e-bost: [email redacted]\n>\n>\n>\n> *Consider the environment. Please don't print this e-mail unless you\n> really need to.*\n>\n> *Ystyriwch yr amgylchedd. Peidiwch ag argraffu'r neges hon oni bai fod\n> gwir angen.*\n>\n>\n>\n> Visit our Website at www.valeofglamorgan.gov.uk\n>\n> Ewch i'n gwefan yn www.bromorgannwg.gov.uk\n>\n>\n>\n> Find us on Facebook / Cewch ddod o hyd i ni ar Facebook\n> <https://www.facebook.com/valeofglamorgancouncil>\n>\n> Follow us on Twitter / Dilynwch ni ar Twitter\n> <http://twitter.com/vogcouncil>\n>\n>\n>\n> Correspondence is welcomed in Welsh or English / Croesewir Gohebiaeth yn y\n> Gymraeg neu yn Saesneg.\n>\n>\n>\n> *From:* Requester <[email redacted]>\n> *Sent:* 13 August 2026 00:47\n> *To:* Davidson, Victoria <[email redacted]>\n> *Subject:* Re: Complaint – Failure to Respond / Failure to Deal Properly\n> with Case – Cllr George Carroll (Llandough) – Great House Farm / Church\n> View Close\n>\n>\n>\n> You don't often get email from [email redacted]. Learn why this\n> is important <https://aka.ms/LearnAboutSenderIdentification>\n>\n> Ms Davidson,\n>\n> Thank you for your response.\n>\n> I accept the distinction you draw between the Council's Corporate Concerns\n> and Complaints Policy and the separate statutory framework governing the\n> conduct of elected members. I am not seeking to use the corporate\n> complaints procedure as an appeal against historic planning, enforcement,\n> land or judicial decisions.\n>\n> However, I do wish to clarify one important point concerning your decision\n> in your capacity as Monitoring Officer.\n>\n> My complaint about Councillor George Carroll was not simply that the\n> Council should revisit historic decisions concerning Great House Farm. The\n> specific concern was his repeated failure to provide any substantive\n> response to correspondence sent to him in his capacity as the elected\n> councillor for Llandough, despite repeated approaches over a period of\n> approximately five months and despite the correspondence setting out\n> specific matters concerning his ward.\n>\n> The chronology supplied with my complaint records correspondence on:\n>\n>    - 16 February 2026;\n>    - 4 May 2026;\n>    - 7 June 2026;\n>    - 25 June 2026; and\n>    - 13 July 2026.\n>\n> My complaint therefore concerned the conduct and handling of that\n> correspondence by an elected member, rather than asking the Monitoring\n> Officer to determine the underlying historical ownership or planning\n> dispute.\n>\n> You state:\n>\n> “Having reviewed the information you have provided, I have not identified\n> evidence which would indicate a breach of the Members Code of Conduct.”\n>\n> I would therefore be grateful for clarification of the basis upon which\n> that conclusion was reached.\n>\n> In particular, please confirm:\n>\n>    1. *Which specific provisions of the Members' Code of Conduct were\n>    considered* in assessing my complaint.\n>    2. *Whether the complete correspondence chronology and the underlying\n>    emails and attachments were considered*, rather than merely my summary\n>    of them.\n>    3. *Whether Councillor Carroll was invited to provide an explanation\n>    or response* concerning the repeated absence of any substantive\n>    response to the correspondence.\n>    4. If so, whether his response was taken into account in reaching your\n>    conclusion.\n>    5. If not, please explain how the Monitoring Officer was able to\n>    determine that there was no evidence indicating a possible breach without\n>    obtaining the member's account of the conduct complained of.\n>    6. Please explain, in sufficient detail to enable me to understand the\n>    decision, *why the documented pattern of conduct was considered\n>    incapable of engaging any provision of the Members' Code of Conduct*.\n>\n> I appreciate that failure to answer correspondence does not, by itself,\n> necessarily constitute a breach of the Code. I am not asserting that\n> proposition. My concern is that the Monitoring Officer appears to have\n> reached a definitive conclusion without identifying the relevant Code\n> provisions, the evidence considered, or the reasoning by which that\n> evidence was found insufficient.\n>\n> I would also ask you to clarify whether you consider that the matter\n> should instead be referred to the *Public Services Ombudsman for Wales*\n> for consideration under its jurisdiction concerning complaints about the\n> conduct of elected members.\n>\n> I note that your response states that no further action will be taken by\n> you as Monitoring Officer. I will therefore retain your letter, together\n> with my original complaint and the complete correspondence record, for\n> consideration of whether a complaint should be submitted to the Public\n> Services Ombudsman for Wales.\n>\n> For the avoidance of doubt, I am not asking you to determine the\n> historical merits of the Great House Farm dispute through the corporate\n> complaints procedure. I am asking for the reasoning and evidential basis\n> for the separate determination you have made concerning the conduct of an\n> elected member.\n>\n> I would be grateful if you could therefore provide the above clarification\n> and confirm the relevant Code provisions considered.\n>\n> Regards,\n>\n> Requester\n>\n> \n>\n>\n>\n> On Wed, 12 Aug 2026, 11:24 Davidson, Victoria, <\n> [email redacted]> wrote:\n>\n> Dear Requester,\n>\n> Thank you for your email regarding Councillor George Carroll and for\n> setting out the background to your concerns relating to Great House Farm\n> (Ty Mawr), Llandough, now Church View Close.\n>\n> I acknowledge receipt of your correspondence and have carefully considered\n> the matters you have raised.\n>\n> Having reviewed your complaint, I do not consider that it falls within the\n> scope of the Vale of Glamorgan Council's Corporate Concerns and Complaints\n> Policy. The substance of your correspondence relates to historic planning,\n> enforcement, land ownership, heritage and record keeping matters dating\n> back several decades, together with requests for review, explanation and\n> further investigation of those matters.\n>\n> The Corporate Concerns and Complaints Policy is not intended to provide a\n> mechanism for challenging or revisiting decisions where there is a separate\n> statutory process or right of appeal; seeking changes to properly made\n> decisions, policies or legislation; pursuing matters which are not the\n> responsibility of the Council; promoting a particular cause or campaign; or\n> addressing matters for which alternative legal or regulatory processes\n> exist.\n>\n> You have also requested that the matter be considered by me as the\n> Monitoring Officer as a potential breach of the Member’s Code of Conduct by\n> Councillor Carroll.\n>\n> The role of the Monitoring Officer in this context is limited to\n> considering whether there is evidence that a member has failed to comply\n> with the statutory Members’s Code of Conduct. Having reviewed the\n> information you have provided, I have not identified evidence which would\n> indicate a breach of the Members Code of Conduct.\n>\n> Accordingly, the issues raised will not be progressed under the Council's\n> Corporate Concerns and Complaints Policy as they fall outside the scope of\n> that procedure. I do not consider there is sufficient evidence to justify\n> referral of the matter for consideration under the Members’s Code of\n> Conduct. No further action will therefore be taken by me in my capacity as\n> the Council’s Monitoring Officer in relation to this complaint.\n>\n> I appreciate that this response may be disappointing. However, I trust it\n> clarifies the Council's position and the limits of the Corporate Complaints\n> Procedure and the Monitoring Officer's role in the context of the matters\n> you raise.\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n>\n>\n> Victoria Davidson\n>\n> Monitoring Officer/Head of Legal & Democratic Services / Rheolydd\n> Gweithredol, Gwasanaethau Cyfreithiol\n>\n> Management Team - Resources / Swyddfa’r Cyfarwyddwr - Adnoddau\n>\n> Vale of Glamorgan Council / Cyngor Bro Morgannwg\n>\n> tel / ffôn:\n>\n> mob / sym: [phone redacted]\n>\n> e-mail / e-bost: [email redacted]\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> *Consider the environment.. Please don't print this e-mail unless you\n> really need to.*\n>\n> *Ystyriwch yr amgylchedd. Peidiwch ag argraffu'r neges hon oni bai fod\n> gwir angen.*\n>\n>\n>\n> Visit our Website at www.valeofglamorgan.gov.uk\n>\n> Ewch i'n gwefan yn www.bromorgannwg.gov.uk\n>\n>\n>\n> Find us on Facebook / Cewch ddod o hyd i ni ar Facebook\n> <http://en-gb.facebook.com/valeofglamorganlife>\n>\n> Follow us on Instagram / Dilynwch ni ar Instagram\n> <https://www.instagram.com/vog_council>\n>\n> Follow us on LinkedIn / Dilynwch ni ar LinkedIn\n> <https://www.linkedin.com/company/vale-of-glamorgan-council>\n>\n>\n>\n> Correspondence is welcomed in Welsh or English / Croesewir Gohebiaeth yn y\n> Gymraeg neu yn Saesneg.\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> <https://www.valeofglamorgan.gov.uk/en/our_council/achieving_our_vision/Corporate-Plan.aspx>\n>\n>\n>\n> *From:* Requester <[email redacted]>\n> *Sent:* 27 July 2026 22:22\n> *To:* [email redacted]\n> *Cc:* [email redacted]; [email redacted];\n> Carroll, George D (Cllr) <[email redacted]>\n> *Subject:* Complaint – Failure to Respond / Failure to Deal Properly with\n> Case – Cllr George Carroll (Llandough) – Great House Farm / Church View\n> Close\n>\n>\n>\n> You don't often get email from [email redacted]. Learn why this\n> is important <https://aka.ms/LearnAboutSenderIdentification>\n>\n> Dear Monitoring Officer / Corporate Complaints Team,\n>\n>\n>\n> With great regret I am writing to make a complaint about the persistent\n> failure of Councillor George D. D. Carroll (Llandough ward) to respond\n> substantively to my repeated and reasonable correspondence concerning\n> historic land, planning, enforcement, heritage and related public-interest\n> matters at Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough (now Church View Close),\n> Vale of Glamorgan.\n>\n>\n>\n> I have a legitimate and long-standing family interest in the site\n> (Williams/Buckler family connection documented from the 17th century). See\n> attached.\n>\n>\n>\n> The issues raised include alleged defective notices, loss of opportunity\n> to contest enforcement leading to demolition in 1988, heritage/regulatory\n> oversight, planning and title records, and related transparency failures.\n>\n>\n>\n> Despite multiple contacts over several months, I have received no\n> substantive replies from Cllr Carroll. This constitutes a failure to\n> respond and a failure to deal with the case properly.\n>\n>\n>\n> ### Full correspondence log with Cllr George Carroll (\n> [email redacted])\n>\n>\n>\n> | Date       | Subject / Key content\n>                            | Response received |\n>\n>\n> |------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------|\n>\n> | 16 Feb 2026 | Potential gaps in Council planning/record-keeping for\n> Great House Farm (demolished 1988); title fraud and procedural issues\n> relating to Church View Close | None |\n>\n>\n>\n> | 4 May 2026  | Request regarding BP vs Buckler 1987; shared\n> media/evidence; request for attention as Llandough councillor | None |\n>\n>\n>\n> | 7 Jun 2026  | “Church View Close, Llandough” – piecing together 1988\n> events from public records, family testimony and media | None |\n>\n>\n>\n> | 25 Jun 2026 | “Great House Farm, Llandough” – enquiry whether prior\n> concerns on family dispossession had been reviewed; request for any\n> progress or discussion | None |\n>\n>\n>\n> | 13 Jul 2026 | “Great House Farm/ Church View Close” – formal follow-up\n> listing the above timeline, restating legitimate interest (family roots\n> since 1667), noting absence of replies, requesting detailed response within\n> 10 working days, and raising the removed commemorative tree | None |\n>\n>\n>\n> ### Remedy sought\n>\n>\n>\n> 1. A full written response from Cllr Carroll addressing the points raised\n> in the logged emails, within 10–15 working days.\n>\n>\n>\n> 2. Confirmation that this complaint has been logged under the Council’s\n> corporate complaints procedure and/or referred to the Monitoring Officer /\n> Standards Committee as appropriate for any potential breach of the\n> councillor Code of Conduct (failure to respond / failure to deal properly\n> with a case).\n>\n>\n>\n> 3. Written confirmation of the process and timescale for investigation and\n> response.\n>\n>\n>\n> I have retained the full original emails and attachments and can supply\n> them on request. Please acknowledge receipt of this formal complaint.\n>\n>\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n>\n>\n> Requester\n>\n> [address redacted], [postcode redacted]\n> [map link redacted]\n>\n>\n> [email redacted]\n>\n> [phone redacted]\n>\n>"},{"id":"gm-1a035340b50bf419","subject":"Re: Enquiry regarding historical deaths – William Buckler (1990) and Mary Doreen Williams (1983)","name":"Re: Enquiry regarding historical deaths – William Buckler (1990) and Mary Doreen Williams (1983)","date":"2026-08-24T19:16:33Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a035340b50bf419.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a016607283cece4-enquiry-regarding-historical-deaths-william-buckler-1990-and-mary-doreen-williams-1983","scopeTopics":["nhs-cav"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["nhs-cav"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a016607283cece4","direction":"received","from":"Tim Knifton (NWSSP - Information Governance)","institutions":[{"id":"nhs-cav","name":"Cardiff and Vale University Health Board"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a035340b50bf419.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Glad I could help you, best of luck with your further enquiries.\n\nTim\n\nSent from Outlook for iOS \n________________________________\nFrom: Requester  \nSent: Monday, 24 August 2026 19:20:53\nTo: Tim Knifton (NWSSP - Information Governance)  \nSubject: Re: FW: Enquiry regarding historical deaths – William Buckler (1990) and Mary Doreen Williams (1983)\n\nWARNING: External email – take care before clicking links or opening attachments.\n\nTim,\n\nIt's been pleasure.\nGod bless you.\nGood drills.\n\nRequester \n[phone redacted]\n\nOn Mon, 24 Aug 2026, 06:16 Tim Knifton (NWSSP - Information Governance),  > wrote:\n\nDear Requester,\n\n\n\nMy responses in red below.\n\n\n\nTim\n\n\n\nFrom: Requester  >\nSent: 24 August 2026 10:11\nTo: Tim Knifton (NWSSP - Information Governance)  >\nSubject: Re: FW: Enquiry regarding historical deaths – William Buckler (1990) and Mary Doreen Williams (1983)\n\n\n\nWARNING: External email – take care before clicking links or opening attachments.\n\n\n\nMr Knifton,\n\nThank you for confirming that, having read the points, you confirm that this is the correct position.\n\nBefore the matter is closed, I would be grateful if I could clarify one important point about the scope of that confirmation, as I do not wish the correspondence to be capable of being interpreted more broadly than the searches actually undertaken.\n\nMy understanding is that your confirmation means that no records were found for William Buckler or Mary Doreen Williams on the specific current registration systems searched by your registration team, and that the Medical Examiner Service itself has no predecessor records because that service did not exist at the time of either death. Correct as explained in my original email.\n\nIt does not, however, mean that searches have established that no historical records ever existed in relation to either death, nor that all records formerly held by the South Glamorgan Health Authority or its constituent hospitals and services have been searched and found not to exist. Unless there is something I am misunderstanding? I want to be sure I don't overlook any traces of evidence.  Correct, this would be the Health Board (Cardiff & Vale or C&V) to determine if records still exist.\n\nIn particular, as I understand the position you have confirmed, the following remain separate questions outside the scope of the current systems search:\n\n  *   whether hospital records relating to William Buckler's admission and death at Llandough Hospital on or about 29 May 1990 were created and, if so, whether any survive with Cardiff and Vale University Health Board or an archive; For C&V to confirm\n  *   whether equivalent medical, hospital or health-authority records relating to Mary Doreen Williams were created and, if so, where any surviving records are held; For C&V to confirm\n  *   whether either death was referred to a coroner or involved a post-mortem, inquest or other separate investigation; For coroner’s office to confirm\n  *   whether any relevant records formerly held by the South Glamorgan Health Authority were transferred to a successor body or archive; and For C&V to confirm\n  *   where records no longer survive, whether they were destroyed under an applicable retention and disposal process. They would have been completed many years ago but this would be for the responsible Health Body to confirm.\n\nI appreciate that these matters may fall outside your office's remit and I am not asking you to undertake those searches yourself. I simply want to ensure that the documentary record accurately distinguishes \"no records found on the systems searched\" from \"no historical records ever existed or survive anywhere within the former health-authority system.\"\n\nIf that distinction is correct, I would be grateful for your confirmation. I will then pursue the surviving-record and archival questions with the appropriate successor bodies.\n\nThank you again for your assistance.\n\nKind regards,\n\nRequester  \n\n\n\nOn Mon, 24 Aug 2026, 05:42 Tim Knifton (NWSSP - Information Governance),  > wrote:\n\nDear Requester,\n\n\n\nYou’re very welcome and glad I could be of some further assistance.\n\n\n\nAs requested, reading the points as set out in your email below, this is the correct position.\n\n\n\nYours sincerely,\n\n\n\nTim Knifton, MSc Gwybodeg Iechyd/Health Informatics\n\nRheolwr Rheoli Gwybodaeth/Information Governance Manager\n\nPartneriaeth Cydwasanaethau GIG Cymru/NHS Wales Shared Services Partnership\n\ne-bost/ email: [email redacted] \n\nRhyngrwyd • Internet: https://nwssp.nhs.wales/\n\nTwitter: @nwssp \n\n\n\nMae PCGC yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn y Gymraeg neu Saesneg.\n\nNWSSP welcomes correspondence in Welsh or English.\n\n\n\n'Mae'r e-bost hwn ac unrhyw ffeiliau sy'n cyd-fynd gyda’r e-bost hwn, ddim ond ar gyfer defnydd priodol y derbynnydd/ion, a gall gynnwys gwybodaeth sy'n freintiedig, yn gyfrinachol neu eithriedig rhag cael ei ddatgelu. Os nad yw'r darllenydd yn dderbynnydd a fwriadwyd i dderbyn yr e-bost, gwaherddir unrhyw ddatgeliad, dosbarthiad neu unrhyw gamau eraill y cymerwyd neu sydd heb gael eu cymryd gan ddibynnu arno, a gallai fod yn anghyfreithlon. Os ydych wedi derbyn yr e-bost hwn trwy gamgymeriad, dilewch o'ch system ac yn rhowch wybod i'r anfonwr ar unwaith. Bydd y sawl sy’n datgelu unrhyw wybodaeth heb awdurdod i ddatgelu’r wybodaeth a gynhwysir yn yr e-bost yn cael eu gwahardd yn llym.’\n\n\n\n‘This e-mail and any files that accompany it are intended only for the appropriate use of the addressee/s, and may contain information that is privileged, confidential or exempt from disclosure. If the reader is not an intended recipient, any disclosure, distribution or any action taken or omitted to be taken in reliance on it, is prohibited and may be unlawful. If you have received this e-mail in error, please delete it from your system and notify the sender immediately. Any unauthorised disclosure of the information contained in this e-mail is strictly prohibited, as is use or application of its contents other than for its intended purpose’\n\n\n\nFrom: Requester  >\nSent: 24 August 2026 09:39\nTo: Tim Knifton (NWSSP - Information Governance)  >\nSubject: Re: FW: Enquiry regarding historical deaths – William Buckler (1990) and Mary Doreen Williams (1983)\n\n\n\nWARNING: External email – take care before clicking links or opening attachments.\n\n\n\nMr Knifton,\n\nVery good of you. Thank you for your detailed response and for taking the time to clarify the position. I have passed my concerned to the National Crime Agency too.\n\nI appreciate the confirmation that the All Wales Medical Examiner Service did not exist at the time of either death and that, consequently, it would not hold predecessor records relating to deaths in 1983 or 1990.\n\nI am also grateful that you asked the registration team to conduct a search and confirmed that no records were found for either individual on the systems searched.\n\nFor the purposes of maintaining an accurate documentary record, I would be grateful if you could clarify one final point. My objective is to distinguish clearly between records which were never held by the present organisation, records which once existed but have since been destroyed, and records which may survive with a successor body or archive.\n\nAs I understand your response, the position is as follows:\n\n  *   the Medical Examiner Service holds no relevant historical records because the service was not established until 2019;\n  *   the primary care registration system and the records searched by your registration team contain no records for either William Buckler or Mary Doreen Williams;\n  *   any original hospital or health-authority records relating to these deaths would have fallen under the former South Glamorgan Health Authority and its applicable records-management arrangements;\n  *   surviving hospital records relating to William Buckler's death at Llandough Hospital may therefore be held by, or have passed to, Cardiff and Vale University Health Board or another successor/archive repository;\n  *   records relating to any coroner referral, post-mortem or inquest, if such events occurred, may have been held separately; and\n  *   GP records may have been destroyed under applicable retention arrangements, although the present search has not identified a specific destruction record for either individual.\n\nIf that is an accurate summary of your position, no further action is required from your office. I would simply be grateful for confirmation, particularly that the statement that no records were found does not amount to confirmation that no historical records ever existed elsewhere within the former South Glamorgan Health Authority system.\n\nThank you again for clarifying the historical position and for identifying Cardiff and Vale University Health Board, Glamorgan Archives and the coroner's records as the more appropriate avenues for further enquiry.\n\nKind regards,\n\nRequester \n\n\n\n\nOn Mon, 24 Aug 2026, 04:56 Tim Knifton (NWSSP - Information Governance),  > wrote:\n\nDear Requester\n\n\n\nThank you for your email. As the main contact for Freedom of Information, I have not seen your request previously but I will explain what I can in regards to your original request:\n\n\n\nI am writing to enquire whether any records or references are held in relation to the following historical deaths that occurred in the Cardiff & Vale / Llandough area:\n\n1. William (Billy) Buckler\n   - Died at Llandough Hospital on or about 29 May 1990, aged 41\n   - Reported cause: heart attack\n\n2. Mary Doreen Williams\n   - Born 10 August 1913\n   - Death registered March 1983, South Glamorgan (Vol 28, Page 3029)\n\nAlthough these pre-date the current Medical Examiner Service, I would be grateful if you could advise whether any legacy records, referrals, or related documentation exist, or direct me to the appropriate archive or department that may hold such information.\n\n\n\nThe All Wales Medical Examiner Service was introduced on a non-statutory basis in 2019. It operated across Wales to provide independent scrutiny of deaths that were not referred to a coroner so deaths in 1983 and 1990 would not apply in this case. The service became statutory on 9 September 2024, when the death certification reforms came into force in England and Wales. From that date, independent scrutiny by a Medical Examiner became a legal requirement before registration of all non-coronial deaths. Therefore, for deaths in 1983/1990, there would have been no Medical Examiner Service. Death certification would have been carried out by the attending doctor, with referrals made to the coroner where required under the rules that existed at the time. The Medical Examiner system only arrived many decades later.\n\n\n\nIf both deaths occurred in Secondary Care, Hospitals do maintain records of deaths that occurred in their facilities, but these are not generally public records.\n\n\n\nThe official legal record of a death is the death certificate registered with the local registrar and held by the General Register Office (GRO). In Wales, you can obtain copies of death certificates dating back before 1983 if the death was registered.  Hospital medical records are usually retained under NHS records management policies and may still exist for deaths in 1983, but access is restricted because they contain confidential medical information.\n\n\n\nIf you are researching a specific death from 1990 at Llandough Hospital, possible sources are:\n\n\n\n  1.  Death certificate from the General Register Office.\n  2.  Glamorgan Archives for historical NHS administrative records.\n  3.  The relevant NHS body that inherited Llandough Hospital records, now part of Cardiff and Vale University Health Board.\n  4.  Coroner's records, if an inquest was held.\n\n\n\nIn 1990, Llandough Hospital was still an NHS hospital managed through the local health authority system. This was under the South Glamorgan Health Authority, which was responsible for hospitals in the Cardiff and Vale area before NHS trusts were created in the early 1990s and now the Cardiff & Vale University Health Board.\n\n\n\nThey can be contacted here:\n\n\n\nHealth Records - Cardiff and Vale University Health Board \n\n\n\nGP records are generally retained for a long period. Under current NHS records management guidance, GP records are typically kept for 10 years after a patient's death before being destroyed so these records would be out of scope.\n\n\n\nI have asked our registration team to search both patients and there are no records found for either patients due to the time elapsed.\n\n\n\nFurther comments have been provided below in red for reference.\n\n\n\nI hope that this is an acceptable response.\n\n\n\nYours sincerely,\n\n\n\nTim Knifton, MSc Gwybodeg Iechyd/Health Informatics\n\nRheolwr Rheoli Gwybodaeth/Information Governance Manager\n\nPartneriaeth Cydwasanaethau GIG Cymru/NHS Wales Shared Services Partnership\n\ne-bost/ email: [email redacted] \n\nRhyngrwyd • Internet: https://nwssp.nhs.wales/\n\nTwitter: @nwssp \n\n\n\nMae PCGC yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn y Gymraeg neu Saesneg.\n\nNWSSP welcomes correspondence in Welsh or English.\n\n\n\n'Mae'r e-bost hwn ac unrhyw ffeiliau sy'n cyd-fynd gyda’r e-bost hwn, ddim ond ar gyfer defnydd priodol y derbynnydd