e – Cllr George Carroll (Llandough) – Great House Farm / Church View Close","name":"RE: Complaint – Failure to Respond / Failure to Deal Properly with Case – Cllr George Carroll (Llandough) – Great House Farm / Church View Close","date":"2026-08-24T13:59:34Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a03411e9c3eadf2.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19fa56f91e54c219-complaint-failure-to-respond-failure-to-deal-properly-with-case-cllr-george-carroll-llandough-great-house-farm-church-vi","scopeTopics":["vale-foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["vale-foi"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19fa56f91e54c219","direction":"received","from":"Davidson, Victoria","institutions":[{"id":"vale-foi","name":"Vale of Glamorgan Council"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a03411e9c3eadf2.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester,\nThank you for your email.\nI note your request for further clarification regarding my decision in my capacity as Monitoring Officer.\nThe assessment I undertook was limited to considering whether the information provided disclosed evidence which, if established, could indicate a failure by the member to comply with the statutory Member’s Code of Conduct. As Monitoring Officer, I do not have formal investigatory powers equivalent to those of the Public Services Ombudsman for Wales and I am not required to undertake an investigation or obtain representations from a member before deciding whether there is sufficient basis for a referral to be made. My role at this stage is to consider whether the information presented discloses evidence of conduct that appears capable of amounting to a breach of the Code.\nIn reaching my view, I considered the information provided within your complaint, including the chronology you supplied regarding correspondence sent to Councillor Carroll between February and July 2026. The substance of the complaint was that you had not received substantive responses to a number of emails sent over that period.\nFor completeness, I considered whether the conduct alleged could potentially engage any of the principal obligations contained within the Member's Code of Conduct. These include requirements that members show respect and consideration for others, do not engage in bullying, harassment or discriminatory behaviour, do not bring their office or authority into disrepute, do not improperly use their position to confer an advantage or disadvantage on any person, and do not misuse Council resources or confidential information.\nWhilst I appreciate that the absence of a response to correspondence may be frustrating, the information provided did not disclose evidence of disrespectful behaviour, bullying, harassment, misuse of position, misuse of Council resources, disclosure of confidential information, conduct bringing the office of member or the authority into disrepute, or any other behaviour which appeared capable of amounting to a breach of the Code. In reaching that view, I considered the nature of the conduct alleged, namely a failure to respond to correspondence, and whether that conduct engaged any provision of the Code.\nIt is important to note that the Member’s Code of Conduct does not impose a general obligation on elected members to respond to all correspondence received from members of the public, nor does it prescribe timescales within which responses must be provided. The Code is concerned with standards of conduct rather than the quality, adequacy or timeliness of constituency casework. Consequently, a decision not to respond, or not to respond in the manner sought by a correspondent, does not of itself constitute evidence of a breach of the Code.\nGiven that the information provided did not disclose evidence which appeared capable of amounting to a breach of the Code, I did not consider it necessary or proportionate to seek comments from Councillor Carroll before reaching that conclusion. My decision was therefore based upon the information contained within your complaint and whether that information, taken at its highest, indicated a potential breach of the Code warranting further action.\nFor completeness, any person is entitled to submit a complaint directly to the Public Services Ombudsman for Wales if they believe a member has failed to comply with the Member’s Code of Conduct. The Ombudsman is independent of the Council and is responsible for determining whether a complaint should be investigated and, if so, whether any further action is appropriate.\nI trust this clarifies the basis of my decision. Having reviewed the information provided, my position remains that I have not identified evidence which would justify further action by me in my capacity as Monitoring Officer.\nYours sincerely,\n\n\nVictoria Davidson\nMonitoring Officer/Head of Legal and Democratic Services / Swyddog Monitro / Pennaeth Gwasanaethau Cyfreithiol a Democrataidd\nDirector's Office - Resources / Swyddfa’r Cyfarwyddwr - Adnoddau\nVale of Glamorgan Council / Cyngor Bro Morgannwg\ntel / ffôn: [phone redacted]\nmob / sym: [phone redacted]\ne-mail / e-bost: [email redacted]<[email redacted]>\n\nConsider the environment. Please don't print this e-mail unless you really need to.\nYstyriwch yr amgylchedd. Peidiwch ag argraffu'r neges hon oni bai fod gwir angen.\n\nVisit our Website at www.valeofglamorgan.gov.uk<http://www.valeofglamorgan.gov.uk/>\nEwch i'n gwefan yn www.bromorgannwg.gov.uk<http://www.bromorgannwg.gov.uk/>\n\nFind us on Facebook / Cewch ddod o hyd i ni ar Facebook<https://www.facebook.com/valeofglamorgancouncil>\nFollow us on Twitter / Dilynwch ni ar Twitter<http://twitter.com/vogcouncil>\n\nCorrespondence is welcomed in Welsh or English / Croesewir Gohebiaeth yn y Gymraeg neu yn Saesneg.\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]>\nSent: 13 August 2026 00:47\nTo: Davidson, Victoria <[email redacted]>\nSubject: Re: Complaint – Failure to Respond / Failure to Deal Properly with Case – Cllr George Carroll (Llandough) – Great House Farm / Church View Close\n\nYou don't often get email from [email redacted]<[email redacted]>. Learn why this is important<https://aka.ms/LearnAboutSenderIdentification>\n\nMs Davidson,\n\nThank you for your response.\n\nI accept the distinction you draw between the Council's Corporate Concerns and Complaints Policy and the separate statutory framework governing the conduct of elected members. I am not seeking to use the corporate complaints procedure as an appeal against historic planning, enforcement, land or judicial decisions.\n\nHowever, I do wish to clarify one important point concerning your decision in your capacity as Monitoring Officer.\n\nMy complaint about Councillor George Carroll was not simply that the Council should revisit historic decisions concerning Great House Farm. The specific concern was his repeated failure to provide any substantive response to correspondence sent to him in his capacity as the elected councillor for Llandough, despite repeated approaches over a period of approximately five months and despite the correspondence setting out specific matters concerning his ward.\n\nThe chronology supplied with my complaint records correspondence on:\n\n  *   16 February 2026;\n  *   4 May 2026;\n  *   7 June 2026;\n  *   25 June 2026; and\n  *   13 July 2026.\n\nMy complaint therefore concerned the conduct and handling of that correspondence by an elected member, rather than asking the Monitoring Officer to determine the underlying historical ownership or planning dispute.\n\nYou state:\n\n“Having reviewed the information you have provided, I have not identified evidence which would indicate a breach of the Members Code of Conduct.”\n\nI would therefore be grateful for clarification of the basis upon which that conclusion was reached.\n\nIn particular, please confirm:\n\n  1.  Which specific provisions of the Members' Code of Conduct were considered in assessing my complaint.\n  2.  Whether the complete correspondence chronology and the underlying emails and attachments were considered, rather than merely my summary of them.\n  3.  Whether Councillor Carroll was invited to provide an explanation or response concerning the repeated absence of any substantive response to the correspondence.\n  4.  If so, whether his response was taken into account in reaching your conclusion.\n  5.  If not, please explain how the Monitoring Officer was able to determine that there was no evidence indicating a possible breach without obtaining the member's account of the conduct complained of.\n  6.  Please explain, in sufficient detail to enable me to understand the decision, why the documented pattern of conduct was considered incapable of engaging any provision of the Members' Code of Conduct.\n\nI appreciate that failure to answer correspondence does not, by itself, necessarily constitute a breach of the Code. I am not asserting that proposition. My concern is that the Monitoring Officer appears to have reached a definitive conclusion without identifying the relevant Code provisions, the evidence considered, or the reasoning by which that evidence was found insufficient.\n\nI would also ask you to clarify whether you consider that the matter should instead be referred to the Public Services Ombudsman for Wales for consideration under its jurisdiction concerning complaints about the conduct of elected members.\n\nI note that your response states that no further action will be taken by you as Monitoring Officer. I will therefore retain your letter, together with my original complaint and the complete correspondence record, for consideration of whether a complaint should be submitted to the Public Services Ombudsman for Wales.\n\nFor the avoidance of doubt, I am not asking you to determine the historical merits of the Great House Farm dispute through the corporate complaints procedure. I am asking for the reasoning and evidential basis for the separate determination you have made concerning the conduct of an elected member.\n\nI would be grateful if you could therefore provide the above clarification and confirm the relevant Code provisions considered.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester\n\n\n\nOn Wed, 12 Aug 2026, 11:24 Davidson, Victoria, <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>> wrote:\nDear Requester,\nThank you for your email regarding Councillor George Carroll and for setting out the background to your concerns relating to Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough, now Church View Close.\nI acknowledge receipt of your correspondence and have carefully considered the matters you have raised.\nHaving reviewed your complaint, I do not consider that it falls within the scope of the Vale of Glamorgan Council's Corporate Concerns and Complaints Policy. The substance of your correspondence relates to historic planning, enforcement, land ownership, heritage and record keeping matters dating back several decades, together with requests for review, explanation and further investigation of those matters.\nThe Corporate Concerns and Complaints Policy is not intended to provide a mechanism for challenging or revisiting decisions where there is a separate statutory process or right of appeal; seeking changes to properly made decisions, policies or legislation; pursuing matters which are not the responsibility of the Council; promoting a particular cause or campaign; or addressing matters for which alternative legal or regulatory processes exist.\nYou have also requested that the matter be considered by me as the Monitoring Officer as a potential breach of the Member’s Code of Conduct by Councillor Carroll.\nThe role of the Monitoring Officer in this context is limited to considering whether there is evidence that a member has failed to comply with the statutory Members’s Code of Conduct. Having reviewed the information you have provided, I have not identified evidence which would indicate a breach of the Members Code of Conduct.\nAccordingly, the issues raised will not be progressed under the Council's Corporate Concerns and Complaints Policy as they fall outside the scope of that procedure. I do not consider there is sufficient evidence to justify referral of the matter for consideration under the Members’s Code of Conduct. No further action will therefore be taken by me in my capacity as the Council’s Monitoring Officer in relation to this complaint.\nI appreciate that this response may be disappointing. However, I trust it clarifies the Council's position and the limits of the Corporate Complaints Procedure and the Monitoring Officer's role in the context of the matters you raise.\nYours sincerely,\n\nVictoria Davidson\nMonitoring Officer/Head of Legal & Democratic Services / Rheolydd Gweithredol, Gwasanaethau Cyfreithiol\nManagement Team - Resources / Swyddfa’r Cyfarwyddwr - Adnoddau\nVale of Glamorgan Council / Cyngor Bro Morgannwg\ntel / ffôn:\nmob / sym: [phone redacted]\ne-mail / e-bost: [email redacted]<[email redacted]>\n\n\nConsider the environment.. Please don't print this e-mail unless you really need to.\nYstyriwch yr amgylchedd. Peidiwch ag argraffu'r neges hon oni bai fod gwir angen.\n\nVisit our Website at www.valeofglamorgan.gov.uk<http://www.valeofglamorgan.gov.uk/>\nEwch i'n gwefan yn www.bromorgannwg.gov.uk<http://www.bromorgannwg.gov.uk/>\n\nFind us on Facebook / Cewch ddod o hyd i ni ar Facebook<http://en-gb.facebook.com/valeofglamorganlife>\nFollow us on Instagram / Dilynwch ni ar Instagram<https://www.instagram.com/vog_council>\nFollow us on LinkedIn / Dilynwch ni ar LinkedIn<https://www.linkedin.com/company/vale-of-glamorgan-council>\n\nCorrespondence is welcomed in Welsh or English / Croesewir Gohebiaeth yn y Gymraeg neu yn Saesneg.\n\n[cid:image001.png@01DD33D9.2A379B30]<https://www.valeofglamorgan.gov.uk/en/our_council/achieving_our_vision/Corporate-Plan.aspx>\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSent: 27 July 2026 22:22\nTo: [email redacted]<[email redacted]>\nCc: [email redacted]<[email redacted]>; [email redacted]<[email redacted]>; Carroll, George D (Cllr) <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSubject: Complaint – Failure to Respond / Failure to Deal Properly with Case – Cllr George Carroll (Llandough) – Great House Farm / Church View Close\n\nYou don't often get email from [email redacted]<[email redacted]>. Learn why this is important<https://aka.ms/LearnAboutSenderIdentification>\nDear Monitoring Officer / Corporate Complaints Team,\n\nWith great regret I am writing to make a complaint about the persistent failure of Councillor George D. D. Carroll (Llandough ward) to respond substantively to my repeated and reasonable correspondence concerning historic land, planning, enforcement, heritage and related public-interest matters at Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough (now Church View Close), Vale of Glamorgan.\n\nI have a legitimate and long-standing family interest in the site (Williams/Buckler family connection documented from the 17th century). See attached.\n\nThe issues raised include alleged defective notices, loss of opportunity to contest enforcement leading to demolition in 1988, heritage/regulatory oversight, planning and title records, and related transparency failures.\n\nDespite multiple contacts over several months, I have received no substantive replies from Cllr Carroll. This constitutes a failure to respond and a failure to deal with the case properly.\n\n### Full correspondence log with Cllr George Carroll ([email redacted]<[email redacted]>)\n\n| Date       | Subject / Key content                                                                 | Response received |\n|------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------|\n| 16 Feb 2026 | Potential gaps in Council planning/record-keeping for Great House Farm (demolished 1988); title fraud and procedural issues relating to Church View Close | None |\n\n| 4 May 2026  | Request regarding BP vs Buckler 1987; shared media/evidence; request for attention as Llandough councillor | None |\n\n| 7 Jun 2026  | “Church View Close, Llandough” – piecing together 1988 events from public records, family testimony and media | None |\n\n| 25 Jun 2026 | “Great House Farm, Llandough” – enquiry whether prior concerns on family dispossession had been reviewed; request for any progress or discussion | None |\n\n| 13 Jul 2026 | “Great House Farm/ Church View Close” – formal follow-up listing the above timeline, restating legitimate interest (family roots since 1667), noting absence of replies, requesting detailed response within 10 working days, and raising the removed commemorative tree | None |\n\n### Remedy sought\n\n1. A full written response from Cllr Carroll addressing the points raised in the logged emails, within 10–15 working days.\n\n2. Confirmation that this complaint has been logged under the Council’s corporate complaints procedure and/or referred to the Monitoring Officer / Standards Committee as appropriate for any potential breach of the councillor Code of Conduct (failure to respond / failure to deal properly with a case).\n\n3. Written confirmation of the process and timescale for investigation and response.\n\nI have retained the full original emails and attachments and can supply them on request. Please acknowledge receipt of this formal complaint.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nRequester\n[address redacted], [postcode redacted][map link redacted]\n[email redacted]<[email redacted]>\n[phone redacted]"},{"id":"gm-1a0330e6aeeefb59","subject":"RE: FW: Enquiry regarding historical deaths – William Buckler (1990) and Mary Doreen Williams (1983)","name":"RE: FW: Enquiry regarding historical deaths – William Buckler (1990) and Mary Doreen Williams (1983)","date":"2026-08-24T09:16:13Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-1a0330e6aeeefb59.txt","threadSlug":"gt-1a016607283cece4-fw-enquiry-regarding-historical-deaths-william-buckler-1990-and-mary-doreen-williams-1983","scopeTopics":["nhs-cav"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["nhs-cav"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-1a016607283cece4","direction":"received","from":"Tim Knifton (NWSSP - Information Governance)","institutions":[{"id":"nhs-cav","name":"Cardiff and Vale University Health Board"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-1a0330e6aeeefb59.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester,\n\nMy responses in red below.\n\nTim\n\nFrom: Requester  \nSent: 24 August 2026 10:11\nTo: Tim Knifton (NWSSP - Information Governance)  \nSubject: Re: FW: Enquiry regarding historical deaths – William Buckler (1990) and Mary Doreen Williams (1983)\n\nWARNING: External email – take care before clicking links or opening attachments.\n\n\nMr Knifton,\n\nThank you for confirming that, having read the points, you confirm that this is the correct position.\n\nBefore the matter is closed, I would be grateful if I could clarify one important point about the scope of that confirmation, as I do not wish the correspondence to be capable of being interpreted more broadly than the searches actually undertaken.\n\nMy understanding is that your confirmation means that no records were found for William Buckler or Mary Doreen Williams on the specific current registration systems searched by your registration team, and that the Medical Examiner Service itself has no predecessor records because that service did not exist at the time of either death. Correct as explained in my original email.\n\nIt does not, however, mean that searches have established that no historical records ever existed in relation to either death, nor that all records formerly held by the South Glamorgan Health Authority or its constituent hospitals and services have been searched and found not to exist. Unless there is something I am misunderstanding? I want to be sure I don't overlook any traces of evidence.  Correct, this would be the Health Board (Cardiff & Vale or C&V) to determine if records still exist.\n\nIn particular, as I understand the position you have confirmed, the following remain separate questions outside the scope of the current systems search:\n\n  *   whether hospital records relating to William Buckler's admission and death at Llandough Hospital on or about 29 May 1990 were created and, if so, whether any survive with Cardiff and Vale University Health Board or an archive; For C&V to confirm\n  *   whether equivalent medical, hospital or health-authority records relating to Mary Doreen Williams were created and, if so, where any surviving records are held; For C&V to confirm\n  *   whether either death was referred to a coroner or involved a post-mortem, inquest or other separate investigation; For coroner’s office to confirm\n  *   whether any relevant records formerly held by the South Glamorgan Health Authority were transferred to a successor body or archive; and For C&V to confirm\n  *   where records no longer survive, whether they were destroyed under an applicable retention and disposal process. They would have been completed many years ago but this would be for the responsible Health Body to confirm.\n\nI appreciate that these matters may fall outside your office's remit and I am not asking you to undertake those searches yourself. I simply want to ensure that the documentary record accurately distinguishes \"no records found on the systems searched\" from \"no historical records ever existed or survive anywhere within the former health-authority system.\"\n\nIf that distinction is correct, I would be grateful for your confirmation. I will then pursue the surviving-record and archival questions with the appropriate successor bodies.\n\nThank you again for your assistance.\n\nKind regards,\n\nRequester  \n\nOn Mon, 24 Aug 2026, 05:42 Tim Knifton (NWSSP - Information Governance),  > wrote:\nDear Requester,\n\nYou’re very welcome and glad I could be of some further assistance.\n\nAs requested, reading the points as set out in your email below, this is the correct position.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nTim Knifton, MSc Gwybodeg Iechyd/Health Informatics\nRheolwr Rheoli Gwybodaeth/Information Governance Manager\nPartneriaeth Cydwasanaethau GIG Cymru/NHS Wales Shared Services Partnership\ne-bost/ email: [email redacted] \nRhyngrwyd • Internet: https://nwssp.nhs.wales/\nTwitter: @nwssp \n\nMae PCGC yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn y Gymraeg neu Saesneg.\nNWSSP welcomes correspondence in Welsh or English.\n\n'Mae'r e-bost hwn ac unrhyw ffeiliau sy'n cyd-fynd gyda’r e-bost hwn, ddim ond ar gyfer defnydd priodol y derbynnydd/ion, a gall gynnwys gwybodaeth sy'n freintiedig, yn gyfrinachol neu eithriedig rhag cael ei ddatgelu. Os nad yw'r darllenydd yn dderbynnydd a fwriadwyd i dderbyn yr e-bost, gwaherddir unrhyw ddatgeliad, dosbarthiad neu unrhyw gamau eraill y cymerwyd neu sydd heb gael eu cymryd gan ddibynnu arno, a gallai fod yn anghyfreithlon. Os ydych wedi derbyn yr e-bost hwn trwy gamgymeriad, dilewch o'ch system ac yn rhowch wybod i'r anfonwr ar unwaith. Bydd y sawl sy’n datgelu unrhyw wybodaeth heb awdurdod i ddatgelu’r wybodaeth a gynhwysir yn yr e-bost yn cael eu gwahardd yn llym.’\n\n‘This e-mail and any files that accompany it are intended only for the appropriate use of the addressee/s, and may contain information that is privileged, confidential or exempt from disclosure. If the reader is not an intended recipient, any disclosure, distribution or any action taken or omitted to be taken in reliance on it, is prohibited and may be unlawful. If you have received this e-mail in error, please delete it from your system and notify the sender immediately. Any unauthorised disclosure of the information contained in this e-mail is strictly prohibited, as is use or application of its contents other than for its intended purpose’\n\nFrom: Requester  >\nSent: 24 August 2026 09:39\nTo: Tim Knifton (NWSSP - Information Governance)  >\nSubject: Re: FW: Enquiry regarding historical deaths – William Buckler (1990) and Mary Doreen Williams (1983)\n\nWARNING: External email – take care before clicking links or opening attachments.\n\n\nMr Knifton,\n\nVery good of you. Thank you for your detailed response and for taking the time to clarify the position. I have passed my concerned to the National Crime Agency too.\n\nI appreciate the confirmation that the All Wales Medical Examiner Service did not exist at the time of either death and that, consequently, it would not hold predecessor records relating to deaths in 1983 or 1990.\n\nI am also grateful that you asked the registration team to conduct a search and confirmed that no records were found for either individual on the systems searched.\n\nFor the purposes of maintaining an accurate documentary record, I would be grateful if you could clarify one final point. My objective is to distinguish clearly between records which were never held by the present organisation, records which once existed but have since been destroyed, and records which may survive with a successor body or archive.\n\nAs I understand your response, the position is as follows:\n\n  *   the Medical Examiner Service holds no relevant historical records because the service was not established until 2019;\n  *   the primary care registration system and the records searched by your registration team contain no records for either William Buckler or Mary Doreen Williams;\n  *   any original hospital or health-authority records relating to these deaths would have fallen under the former South Glamorgan Health Authority and its applicable records-management arrangements;\n  *   surviving hospital records relating to William Buckler's death at Llandough Hospital may therefore be held by, or have passed to, Cardiff and Vale University Health Board or another successor/archive repository;\n  *   records relating to any coroner referral, post-mortem or inquest, if such events occurred, may have been held separately; and\n  *   GP records may have been destroyed under applicable retention arrangements, although the present search has not identified a specific destruction record for either individual.\n\nIf that is an accurate summary of your position, no further action is required from your office. I would simply be grateful for confirmation, particularly that the statement that no records were found does not amount to confirmation that no historical records ever existed elsewhere within the former South Glamorgan Health Authority system.\n\nThank you again for clarifying the historical position and for identifying Cardiff and Vale University Health Board, Glamorgan Archives and the coroner's records as the more appropriate avenues for further enquiry.\n\nKind regards,\n\nRequester \n\n\nOn Mon, 24 Aug 2026, 04:56 Tim Knifton (NWSSP - Information Governance),  > wrote:\nDear Requester\n\nThank you for your email. As the main contact for Freedom of Information, I have not seen your request previously but I will explain what I can in regards to your original request:\n\nI am writing to enquire whether any records or references are held in relation to the following historical deaths that occurred in the Cardiff & Vale / Llandough area:\n\n1. William (Billy) Buckler\n   - Died at Llandough Hospital on or about 29 May 1990, aged 41\n   - Reported cause: heart attack\n\n2. Mary Doreen Williams\n   - Born 10 August 1913\n   - Death registered March 1983, South Glamorgan (Vol 28, Page 3029)\n\nAlthough these pre-date the current Medical Examiner Service, I would be grateful if you could advise whether any legacy records, referrals, or related documentation exist, or direct me to the appropriate archive or department that may hold such information.\n\nThe All Wales Medical Examiner Service was introduced on a non-statutory basis in 2019. It operated across Wales to provide independent scrutiny of deaths that were not referred to a coroner so deaths in 1983 and 1990 would not apply in this case. The service became statutory on 9 September 2024, when the death certification reforms came into force in England and Wales. From that date, independent scrutiny by a Medical Examiner became a legal requirement before registration of all non-coronial deaths. Therefore, for deaths in 1983/1990, there would have been no Medical Examiner Service. Death certification would have been carried out by the attending doctor, with referrals made to the coroner where required under the rules that existed at the time. The Medical Examiner system only arrived many decades later.\n\nIf both deaths occurred in Secondary Care, Hospitals do maintain records of deaths that occurred in their facilities, but these are not generally public records.\n\nThe official legal record of a death is the death certificate registered with the local registrar and held by the General Register Office (GRO). In Wales, you can obtain copies of death certificates dating back before 1983 if the death was registered.  Hospital medical records are usually retained under NHS records management policies and may still exist for deaths in 1983, but access is restricted because they contain confidential medical information.\n\nIf you are researching a specific death from 1990 at Llandough Hospital, possible sources are:\n\n\n  1.  Death certificate from the General Register Office.\n  2.  Glamorgan Archives for historical NHS administrative records.\n  3.  The relevant NHS body that inherited Llandough Hospital records, now part of Cardiff and Vale University Health Board.\n  4.  Coroner's records, if an inquest was held.\n\nIn 1990, Llandough Hospital was still an NHS hospital managed through the local health authority system. This was under the South Glamorgan Health Authority, which was responsible for hospitals in the Cardiff and Vale area before NHS trusts were created in the early 1990s and now the Cardiff & Vale University Health Board.\n\nThey can be contacted here:\n\nHealth Records - Cardiff and Vale University Health Board \n\nGP records are generally retained for a long period. Under current NHS records management guidance, GP records are typically kept for 10 years after a patient's death before being destroyed so these records would be out of scope.\n\nI have asked our registration team to search both patients and there are no records found for either patients due to the time elapsed.\n\nFurther comments have been provided below in red for reference.\n\nI hope that this is an acceptable response.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nTim Knifton, MSc Gwybodeg Iechyd/Health Informatics\nRheolwr Rheoli Gwybodaeth/Information Governance Manager\nPartneriaeth Cydwasanaethau GIG Cymru/NHS Wales Shared Services Partnership\ne-bost/ email: [email redacted] \nRhyngrwyd • Internet: https://nwssp.nhs.wales/\nTwitter: @nwssp \n\nMae PCGC yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn y Gymraeg neu Saesneg.\nNWSSP welcomes correspondence in Welsh or English.\n\n'Mae'r e-bost hwn ac unrhyw ffeiliau sy'n cyd-fynd gyda’r e-bost hwn, ddim ond ar gyfer defnydd priodol y derbynnydd/ion, a gall gynnwys gwybodaeth sy'n freintiedig, yn gyfrinachol neu eithriedig rhag cael ei ddatgelu. Os nad yw'r darllenydd yn dderbynnydd a fwriadwyd i dderbyn yr e-bost, gwaherddir unrhyw ddatgeliad, dosbarthiad neu unrhyw gamau eraill y cymerwyd neu sydd heb gael eu cymryd gan ddibynnu arno, a gallai fod yn anghyfreithlon. Os ydych wedi derbyn yr e-bost hwn trwy gamgymeriad, dilewch o'ch system ac yn rhowch wybod i'r anfonwr ar unwaith. Bydd y sawl sy’n datgelu unrhyw wybodaeth heb awdurdod i ddatgelu’r wybodaeth a gynhwysir yn yr e-bost yn cael eu gwahardd yn llym.’\n\n‘This e-mail and any files that accompany it are intended only for the appropriate use of the addressee/s, and may contain information that is privileged, confidential or exempt from disclosure. If the reader is not an intended recipient, any disclosure, distribution or any action taken or omitted to be taken in reliance on it, is prohibited and may be unlawful. If you have received this e-mail in error, please delete it from your system and notify the sender immediately. Any unauthorised disclosure of the information contained in this e-mail is strictly prohibited, as is use or application of its contents other than for its intended purpose’\n\n________________________________\nFrom: Requester  >\nSent: 21 August 2026 08:44\nTo: SouthWalesCentral MedicalExaminersOffice (Gen126594)  >\nSubject: Re: Enquiry regarding historical deaths – William Buckler (1990) and Mary Doreen Williams (1983)\n\nWARNING: External email – take care before clicking links or opening attachments.\n\n\nMichelle,\n\nThanks for your prompt response.\n\nI'd like to request an internal review of the handling of my enquiry and, insofar as it constitutes a Freedom of Information request, of the conclusion that no relevant records are held.\n\nI am not challenging the conclusion simply because it is inconvenient to my enquiry. Rather, I would like to establish whether an adequate search was undertaken before the conclusion was reached.\n\nYour latest response states that the Medical Examiner Service would not hold “any records whatsoever” or have “any knowledge” of these patients. Given that the deaths occurred in 1983 and 1990, before the establishment of the current Medical Examiner Service, I would be grateful if the internal review could specifically establish whether the search extended beyond current Medical Examiner records to any relevant predecessor, legacy or transferred records. This would have not been something that the medical examiner would have been able to complete as explained above.\n\nIn particular, could the review please confirm:\n\n  1.  What records and information systems were searched; primary care registration system\n  2.  Whether any legacy or predecessor records relating to the medical examination or certification of deaths were considered; No legacy records going back to 1983/1990 would be considered as the service concentrates on independent scrutiny of deaths that were not referred to a coroner in the present day\n  3.  Whether records relating to Llandough Hospital, where William Buckler is recorded as having died in approximately May 1990, are held by the Medical Examiner Service, NHS Wales Shared Services Partnership, another NHS body, or an archive to which you have access or knowledge; As explained above\n  4.  Whether any records concerning referrals to a coroner, inquests, post-mortems or other investigations into these deaths would have been held separately from Medical Examiner records;\n  5.  Whether the possibility of records having been transferred to an archive or successor organisation was considered; This would have been completed under the old Health Authority and retained for the specific time period under records management policies.\n  6.  Whether the statement that no records are held means that no relevant records are held by the South Wales Central Medical Examiner Service specifically, or that searches have established that no such records are held anywhere within NHS Wales Shared Services Partnership; and Medical Examiner was not established until 2019, historic records going back to the 1980s and 1990s would not form part of the remit of the service.\n  7.  If the records are not held by your organisation, whether you can identify the appropriate current custodian or archive for any surviving historical records. Explained above\n\nFor clarity, the individuals concerned are:\n\n  *   William (Billy) Buckler – died at Llandough Hospital on or about 29 May 1990, aged 41; and\n  *   Mary Doreen Williams – born 10 August 1913; death registered in March 1983, South Glamorgan, Vol. 28, p. 3029.\n\nI would be grateful if this could be treated as a formal internal review rather than simply a further general enquiry.\n\nI would also ask that the review be undertaken by someone who was not responsible for the original response, where practicable.\n\nMy purpose is to establish a reliable documentary record and, in particular, to distinguish between:\n\n(a) records which do not exist;\n\n(b) records which once existed but have been destroyed;\n\n(c) records which exist but are held by another organisation; and\n\n(d) records which are held within NHS Wales or a predecessor organisation but were not identified by the original search.\n\nI would be grateful for confirmation of the review and, where applicable, the relevant FOI reference number.\n\nKind regards,\n\nRequester \n\n\n\nOn Fri, 21 Aug 2026, 04:33 SouthWalesCentral MedicalExaminersOffice (Gen126594),  > wrote:\nGood morning\n\nWe would not hold any records whatsoever or have any knowledge of these patients.\n\nMany thanks\n\nMichelle\n\n\n\n\n\nSwyddfa Ganolog De Cymru South Wales Central Office\n\nGwasanaeth Archwilio Meddygol Cymru Gyfan  All Wales Medical Examiners Service\n\nPartneriaeth Cydwasanaethau GIG Cymru  NHS Wales Shared Services Partnership\n\nFfon Telephone:  [phone redacted]\n\nE-bost  Email: [email redacted] \n\nGwefan Website:  https://nwssp.nhs.wales/ourservices/medical-examiner-service/\n\n\n\nMae Partneriaeth Cydwasanaethau GIG Cymru yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn y Gymraeg.\n\nNHS Wales Shared Services welcomes correspondence in Welsh.\n\nDw i ddim yn deall Cymraeg. Fodd bynnag mae PCGC yn croesawu gohebiaet