s relating to deaths in 1983 or 1990.\n>\n> I am also grateful that you asked the registration team to conduct a\n> search and confirmed that no records were found for either individual on\n> the systems searched.\n>\n> For the purposes of maintaining an accurate documentary record, I would be\n> grateful if you could clarify one final point. My objective is to\n> distinguish clearly between records which were never held by the present\n> organisation, records which once existed but have since been destroyed, and\n> records which may survive with a successor body or archive.\n>\n> As I understand your response, the position is as follows:\n>\n>    - the Medical Examiner Service holds no relevant historical records\n>    because the service was not established until 2019;\n>    - the primary care registration system and the records searched by\n>    your registration team contain no records for either William Buckler or\n>    Mary Doreen Williams;\n>    - any original hospital or health-authority records relating to these\n>    deaths would have fallen under the former South Glamorgan Health Authority\n>    and its applicable records-management arrangements;\n>    - surviving hospital records relating to William Buckler's death at\n>    Llandough Hospital may therefore be held by, or have passed to, Cardiff and\n>    Vale University Health Board or another successor/archive repository;\n>    - records relating to any coroner referral, post-mortem or inquest, if\n>    such events occurred, may have been held separately; and\n>    - GP records may have been destroyed under applicable retention\n>    arrangements, although the present search has not identified a specific\n>    destruction record for either individual.\n>\n> If that is an accurate summary of your position, no further action is\n> required from your office. I would simply be grateful for confirmation,\n> particularly that the statement that no records were found does not amount\n> to confirmation that no historical records ever existed elsewhere within\n> the former South Glamorgan Health Authority system.\n>\n> Thank you again for clarifying the historical position and for identifying\n> Cardiff and Vale University Health Board, Glamorgan Archives and the\n> coroner's records as the more appropriate avenues for further enquiry.\n>\n> Kind regards,\n>\n> *Requester *\n> \n>\n>\n>\n> On Mon, 24 Aug 2026, 04:56 Tim Knifton (NWSSP - Information Governance), <\n> [email redacted]> wrote:\n>\n> Dear Requester\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you for your email. As the main contact for Freedom of Information,\n> I have not seen your request previously but I will explain what I can in\n> regards to your original request:\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> *I am writing to enquire whether any records or references are held in\n> relation to the following historical deaths that occurred in the Cardiff &\n> Vale / Llandough area: 1. William (Billy) Buckler    - Died at Llandough\n> Hospital on or about 29 May 1990, aged 41    - Reported cause: heart attack\n> 2. Mary Doreen Williams    - Born 10 August 1913    - Death registered\n> March 1983, South Glamorgan (Vol 28, Page 3029) Although these pre-date the\n> current Medical Examiner Service, I would be grateful if you could advise\n> whether any legacy records, referrals, or related documentation exist, or\n> direct me to the appropriate archive or department that may hold such\n> information.*\n>\n>\n>\n> The All Wales Medical Examiner Service was introduced on a non-statutory\n> basis in 2019. It operated across Wales to provide independent scrutiny of\n> deaths that were not referred to a coroner so deaths in 1983 and 1990 would\n> not apply in this case. The service became statutory on 9 September 2024,\n> when the death certification reforms came into force in England and Wales.\n> From that date, independent scrutiny by a Medical Examiner became a legal\n> requirement before registration of all non-coronial deaths. Therefore, for\n> deaths in 1983/1990, there would have been no Medical Examiner Service.\n> Death certification would have been carried out by the attending doctor,\n> with referrals made to the coroner where required under the rules that\n> existed at the time. The Medical Examiner system only arrived many decades\n> later.\n>\n>\n>\n> If both deaths occurred in Secondary Care, Hospitals do maintain records\n> of deaths that occurred in their facilities, but these are not generally\n> public records.\n>\n>\n>\n> The official legal record of a death is the death certificate registered\n> with the local registrar and held by the General Register Office (GRO). In\n> Wales, you can obtain copies of death certificates dating back before 1983\n> if the death was registered.  Hospital medical records are usually retained\n> under NHS records management policies and may still exist for deaths in\n> 1983, but access is restricted because they contain confidential medical\n> information.\n>\n>\n>\n> If you are researching a specific death from 1990 at Llandough Hospital,\n> possible sources are:\n>\n>\n>\n>    1. Death certificate from the General Register Office.\n>    2. Glamorgan Archives for historical NHS administrative records.\n>    3. The relevant NHS body that inherited Llandough Hospital records,\n>    now part of Cardiff and Vale University Health Board.\n>    4. Coroner's records, if an inquest was held.\n>\n>\n>\n> In 1990, Llandough Hospital was still an NHS hospital managed through the\n> local health authority system. This was under the South Glamorgan Health\n> Authority, which was responsible for hospitals in the Cardiff and Vale area\n> before NHS trusts were created in the early 1990s and now the Cardiff &\n> Vale University Health Board.\n>\n>\n>\n> They can be contacted here:\n>\n>\n>\n> Health Records - Cardiff and Vale University Health Board\n> <https://cavuhb.nhs.wales/our-services/health-records/>\n>\n>\n>\n> GP records are generally retained for a long period. Under current NHS\n> records management guidance, GP records are typically kept for 10 years\n> after a patient's death before being destroyed so these records would be\n> out of scope.\n>\n>\n>\n> I have asked our registration team to search both patients and there are\n> no records found for either patients due to the time elapsed.\n>\n>\n>\n> Further comments have been provided below in red for reference.\n>\n>\n>\n> I hope that this is an acceptable response.\n>\n>\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n>\n>\n> *Tim Knifton, MSc Gwybodeg Iechyd/Health Informatics*\n>\n> *Rheolwr Rheoli Gwybodaeth/**Information Governance Manager*\n>\n> Partneriaeth Cydwasanaethau GIG Cymru/NHS Wales Shared Services\n> Partnership\n>\n> e-bost/ email: *[email redacted] <[email redacted]>*\n>\n> Rhyngrwyd • Internet:* https://nwssp.nhs.wales/\n> <https://nwssp.nhs.wales/>*\n>\n> Twitter: @nwssp\n>\n>\n>\n> Mae PCGC yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn y Gymraeg neu Saesneg.\n>\n> NWSSP welcomes correspondence in Welsh or English.\n>\n>\n>\n> *'Mae'r e-bost hwn ac unrhyw ffeiliau sy'n cyd-fynd gyda’r e-bost hwn,\n> ddim ond ar gyfer defnydd priodol y derbynnydd/ion, a gall gynnwys\n> gwybodaeth sy'n freintiedig, yn gyfrinachol neu eithriedig rhag cael ei\n> ddatgelu. Os nad yw'r darllenydd yn dderbynnydd a fwriadwyd i dderbyn yr\n> e-bost, gwaherddir unrhyw ddatgeliad, dosbarthiad neu unrhyw gamau eraill y\n> cymerwyd neu sydd heb gael eu cymryd gan ddibynnu arno, a gallai fod yn\n> anghyfreithlon. Os ydych wedi derbyn yr e-bost hwn trwy gamgymeriad,\n> dilewch o'ch system ac yn rhowch wybod i'r anfonwr ar unwaith. Bydd y sawl\n> sy’n datgelu unrhyw wybodaeth heb awdurdod i ddatgelu’r wybodaeth a\n> gynhwysir yn yr e-bost yn cael eu gwahardd yn llym.’*\n>\n>\n>\n> *‘**This e-mail and any files that accompany it are intended only for the\n> appropriate use of the addressee/s, and may contain information that is\n> privileged, confidential or exempt from disclosure. If the reader is not an\n> intended 