:"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19fff7114be8ace0.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19fc7d9d48f162aa-foi-2026-020-great-house-farm-llandough-cardiff-castle-furniture","scopeTopics":["museum"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["museum"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19fc7d9d48f162aa","direction":"received","from":"FOI Requests","institutions":[{"id":"museum","name":"Amgueddfa Cymru - Museum Wales"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19fff7114be8ace0.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester\n\nThank you for your email.\n\nFollowing receipt of your query, Amgueddfa Cymru undertook further enquiries to satisfy itself that no information had been overlooked when responding to your request.\n\nThese enquiries included a review of records held across the Museum's collections and a review of archived electronic systems and records by the Museum's Information Technology team. The review considered all collections management systems used by the Museum, including those most likely to contain information falling within the scope of your request.\n\nFor the History collections, searches were undertaken of the Collections Management System (CMS), accession files, donor indexes, location indexes and associated collection documentation. Searches were carried out using terms including \"Great House Farm\", \"Ty Mawr\", \"Llandough\", \"Buckler\" and \"Bute\". Additional consideration was given to acquisition records, provenance information and associated administrative records.\n\nFor the Art collections, searches were undertaken within the collections management system across description, comments, acquisition notes and place fields using the terms \"Ty Mawr\", \"Great House Farm\", \"Great House\", \"Williams\", \"Buckler\", \"Llandough\" and combinations of those terms. No additional paper archives relating to Great House Farm, Ty Mawr, Great House or Buckler were identified.\n\nThe Museum's Information Technology team also reviewed archived electronic systems and records retained by the Museum.\n\nThese enquiries identified some information relating generally to Great House Farm and the surrounding area. However, no information was identified concerning furniture, furnishings, antiques, fixtures, heirlooms or other movable property originating from Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough. We did not identify any inventories, catalogues, acquisition records, disposal records, loan records, provenance files, ownership records, photographs, correspondence or other records falling within categories 1 to 9 of your request.\n\nWe are also not aware of any relevant collection records relating to such material having been transferred to another institution or destroyed.\n\nAccordingly, having completed these additional enquiries, Amgueddfa Cymru remains satisfied that it does not hold recorded information within the scope of your request and our response under section 1(1)(a) of the Freedom of Information Act 2000 remains unchanged.\nIn relation to your request for advice and assistance, Cardiff Council and Cardiff Castle would appear to be the organisations most likely to hold records relating to furniture, furnishings or other movable property associated with Great House Farm.\n\nIf you remain dissatisfied with this response, you may request an internal review within 40 working days of the date of this email.\n\n\nCofion cynnes,\n\n[A picture containing text, tableware, plate, dishware  Description automatically generated]\nFOI Team\n[cid:image002.png@01DD2BCC.D7F93860]\n[email redacted]<[email redacted]>\namgueddfa.cymru<applewebdata://288FE56C-5BFC-474C-ADB3-A1017EF25E69/amgueddfa.cymru> | museum.wales<applewebdata://288FE56C-5BFC-474C-ADB3-A1017EF25E69/museum.wales>\n\nRhif elusen | Charity number 525774\n\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]>\nSent: 03 August 2026 17:29\nTo: FOI Requests <[email redacted]>\nSubject: Re: FOI 2026-020 - Great House Farm, Llandough | Cardiff Castle Furniture\n\n\nThis email comes from outside the organisation.\n\nDo not click links or open attachments unless it is an email you expected to receive.\n\nDear FOI Team,\n\nThank you for your response.\n\nI note your position that Amgueddfa Cymru does not hold recorded information within the scope of my request.\n\nHowever, I would be grateful if you could clarify the searches undertaken to reach that conclusion.\n\nIn particular, please confirm:\n\n  *   Which collections management systems, databases and \"associated records\" were searched.\n  *   Whether searches extended beyond accessioned objects to include acquisition files, provenance files, curator correspondence, donor files, temporary custody records, unaccessioned material, photographs and administrative records.\n  *   The search terms used (for example: \"Great House Farm\", \"Ty Mawr\", \"Llandough\", \"Williams\", \"Buckler\", \"Bute Estate\" and any other relevant variants).\n  *   Whether any potentially relevant records have been transferred to another institution or destroyed in accordance with a retention policy.\n\nMy original request was not limited to identifying surviving objects. It also sought records relating to their provenance, acquisition, transfer, ownership, photographs and correspondence. This reflects the fact that the contents of Great House Farm may have been dispersed following the property's demolition, meaning relevant records could exist even if no objects were ultimately accessioned by the Museum.\n\nIf, following those searches, Amgueddfa Cymru remains satisfied that no recorded information is held, I would be grateful for confirmation of that position.\n\nFinally, if you are aware of another public authority that is more likely to hold records falling within the scope of my request, I would appreciate your advice in accordance with your duty to provide advice and assistance.\n\nThank you for your assistance.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\nRequester\n\nOn Mon, 3 Aug 2026, 10:39 FOI Requests, <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>> wrote:\nDear Requester\n\nThank you for your request for information received on 2nd July 2026, concerning furniture, furnishings, antiques, fixtures, heirlooms and other movable property originating from Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough.\n\nFollowing searches of the Museum's collections management systems and associated records, Amgueddfa Cymru has not identified any furniture, furnishings, antiques, fixtures, heirlooms or other movable property recorded as originating from Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough.\n\nAccordingly, Amgueddfa Cymru does not hold recorded information falling within the scope of items 1 to 9 of your request relating to such objects.\nFor clarity:\n\n  *   We have not identified any objects of the type described in your request as originating from Great House Farm.\n  *   We have not identified inventories, catalogues, acquisition records, disposal records, loan records, provenance files, ownership records, photographs, correspondence, reports, memoranda or related records concerning such objects within the scope of your request.\n  *   We have not identified any records relating to the identification, recovery, ownership or location of furniture or household effects from Great House Farm.\n\nWe note your references to archaeological material from land associated with Great House Farm. However, archaeological finds are outside the scope of this response because they do not fall within your request for furniture, furnishings, antiques, fixtures, heirlooms and other movable property. We understand that archaeological material is the subject of a separate information request and will be dealt with separately.\n\nUnder section 1(1)(a) of the Freedom of Information Act 2000, our response is therefore that Amgueddfa Cymru does not hold the requested information.\n\nCofion cynnes,\n\n[A picture containing text, tableware, plate, dishware    Description automatically generated]\nFOI Team\n[cid:image002.png@01DD2BCC.D7F93860]\n[email redacted]<[email redacted]>\namgueddfa.cymru | museum.wales\n\nRhif elusen | Charity number 525774\n\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSent: 02 July 2026 20:51\nTo: [email redacted]<[email redacted]>\nCc: [email redacted]<[email redacted]>; [email redacted]<[email redacted]>; [email redacted]<[email redacted]>; [email redacted]<[email redacted]>; FOI Requests <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>; [email redacted]<[email redacted]>; [email redacted]<[email redacted]>; National Library of Wales <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSubject: ALLANOL/EXTERNAL - Great House Farm, Llandough | Cardiff Castle Furniture\n\nYou don't often get email from [email redacted]<[email redacted]>. Learn why this is important<https://aka.ms/LearnAboutSenderIdentification>\n\nFreedom of Information Act 2000 Request\n\nSir/Madam,\n\nI am making this request under the Freedom of Information Act 2000.\n\nI seek all recorded information held concerning furniture, furnishings, antiques, fixtures, heirlooms and other movable property originating from Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough, including any and all items we received from Bute Estate, Cardiff Castle, Cardiff Council.  Weather it be museums, archives or any other public body. I know museums have artifacts from our land in Llandough.\n\nSpecifically, I request copies of all:\n\n  1.  Inventories, catalogues and asset registers.\n  2.  Acquisition, disposal, loan and transfer records.\n  3.  Provenance files and ownership records.\n  4.  Photographs of items identified as originating from Great House Farm.\n  5.  Correspondence concerning the identification, recovery, ownership or location of such items.\n  6.  Internal reports, legal advice and memoranda relating to Great House Farm furniture or household effects.\n  7.  Correspondence mentioning the Buckler or Williams families in connection with these items.\n  8.  Records concerning any efforts by Cardiff Council or Cardiff Castle to trace, recover or secure items believed to have originated from Great House Farm.\n  9.  Correspondence with the Bute Estate, museums, the Department for Culture, Media and Sport, the Reviewing Committee on the Export of Works of Art and Objects of Cultural Interest (RCEWA), Arts Council England or any other public authority regarding objects from Great House Farm.\n\nFor context, I am allegeing that my family was unlawfully dispossessed of Great House Farm through fraud, and that furniture and other historic possessions belonging to the family were consequently dispersed. It's possible the bailiffs from BP were also SAS soldiers. We are still gathering actionable intelligence.\n\nI appreciate that the Freedom of Information Act does not require the authority to determine the merits of that allegation. However, it is directly relevant to understanding why I seek the recorded information.\n\nI am currently developing a full-scale replica of Great House Farm in Patagonia, Argentina as a museum dedicated to preserving the history of the house, its occupants and its contents. Should records identify surviving items from the farm, whether held by Cardiff Council, Cardiff Castle, museums or other public institutions, I intend to pursue their lawful return or transfer so they may ultimately be reunited with the reconstructed Great House Farm museum. This statement is provided solely as context for my request and does not seek a determination under the Freedom of Information Act.\n\nIf any information is withheld, please identify each exemption relied upon and explain why it applies, including the outcome of any public interest test where required.\n\nIf another public authority is more likely to hold the requested information, I would be grateful if you would transfer the request or advise me accordingly.\n\nI look forward to your response within the statutory time limit.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\nRequester "},{"id":"gm-19ff85f848dede8e","subject":"Re: Complaint – Failure to Respond / Failure to Deal Properly with Case – Cllr George Carroll (Llandough) – Great House Farm / Church View Close","name":"Re: Complaint – Failure to Respond / Failure to Deal Properly with Case – Cllr George Carroll (Llandough) – Great House Farm / Church View Close","date":"2026-08-12T23:47:16Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19ff85f848dede8e.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19fa56f91e54c219-complaint-failure-to-respond-failure-to-deal-properly-with-case-cllr-george-carroll-llandough-great-house-farm-church-vi","scopeTopics":["vale-foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["vale-foi"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19fa56f91e54c219","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"vale-foi","name":"Vale of Glamorgan Council"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19ff85f848dede8e.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Ms Davidson,\n\nThank you for your response.\n\nI accept the distinction you draw between the Council's Corporate Concerns\nand Complaints Policy and the separate statutory framework governing the\nconduct of elected members. I am not seeking to use the corporate\ncomplaints procedure as an appeal against historic planning, enforcement,\nland or judicial decisions.\n\nHowever, I do wish to clarify one important point concerning your decision\nin your capacity as Monitoring Officer.\n\nMy complaint about Councillor George Carroll was not simply that the\nCouncil should revisit historic decisions concerning Great House Farm. The\nspecific concern was his repeated failure to provide any substantive\nresponse to correspondence sent to him in his capacity as the elected\ncouncillor for Llandough, despite repeated approaches over a period of\napproximately five months and despite the correspondence setting out\nspecific matters concerning his ward.\n\nThe chronology supplied with my complaint records correspondence on:\n\n   - 16 February 2026;\n   - 4 May 2026;\n   - 7 June 2026;\n   - 25 June 2026; and\n   - 13 July 2026.\n\nMy complaint therefore concerned the conduct and handling of that\ncorrespondence by an elected member, rather than asking the Monitoring\nOfficer to determine the underlying historical ownership or planning\ndispute.\n\nYou state:\n\n“Having reviewed the information you have provided, I have not identified\nevidence which would indicate a breach of the Members Code of Conduct.”\n\nI would therefore be grateful for clarification of the basis upon which\nthat conclusion was reached.\n\nIn particular, please confirm:\n\n   1.\n\n   *Which specific provisions of the Members' Code of Conduct were\n   considered* in assessing my complaint.\n   2.\n\n   *Whether the complete correspondence chronology and the underlying\n   emails and attachments were considered*, rather than merely my summary\n   of them.\n   3.\n\n   *Whether Councillor Carroll was invited to provide an explanation or\n   response* concerning the repeated absence of any substantive response to\n   the correspondence.\n   4.\n\n   If so, whether his response was taken into account in reaching your\n   conclusion.\n   5.\n\n   If not, please explain how the Monitoring Officer was able to determine\n   that there was no evidence indicating a possible breach without obtaining\n   the member's account of the conduct complained of.\n   6.\n\n   Please explain, in sufficient detail to enable me to understand the\n   decision, *why the documented pattern of conduct was considered\n   incapable of engaging any provision of the Members' Code of Conduct*.\n\nI appreciate that failure to answer correspondence does not, by itself,\nnecessarily constitute a breach of the Code. I am not asserting that\nproposition. My concern is that the Monitoring Officer appears to have\nreached a definitive conclusion without identifying the relevant Code\nprovisions, the evidence considered, or the reasoning by which that\nevidence was found insufficient.\n\nI would also ask you to clarify whether you consider that the matter should\ninstead be referred to the *Public Services Ombudsman for Wales* for\nconsideration under its jurisdiction concerning complaints about the\nconduct of elected members.\n\nI note that your response states that no further action will be taken by\nyou as Monitoring Officer. I will therefore retain your letter, together\nwith my original complaint and the complete correspondence record, for\nconsideration of whether a complaint should be submitted to the Public\nServices Ombudsman for Wales.\n\nFor the avoidance of doubt, I am not asking you to determine the historical\nmerits of the Great House Farm dispute through the corporate complaints\nprocedure. I am asking for the reasoning and evidential basis for the\nseparate determination you have made concerning the conduct of an elected\nmember.\n\nI would be grateful if you could therefore provide the above clarification\nand confirm the relevant Code provisions considered.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester\n\n\n\nOn Wed, 12 Aug 2026, 11:24 Davidson, Victoria, <\n[email redacted]> wrote:\n\n> Dear Requester,\n>\n> Thank you for your email regarding Councillor George Carroll and for\n> setting out the background to your concerns relating to Great House Farm\n> (Ty Mawr), Llandough, now Church View Close.\n>\n> I acknowledge receipt of your correspondence and have carefully considered\n> the matters you have raised.\n>\n> Having reviewed your complaint, I do not consider that it falls within the\n> scope of the Vale of Glamorgan Council's Corporate Concerns and Complaints\n> Policy. The substance of your correspondence relates to historic planning,\n> enforcement, land ownership, heritage and record keeping matters dating\n> back several decades, together with requests for review, explanation and\n> further investigation of those matters.\n>\n> The Corporate Concerns and Complaints Policy is not intended to provide a\n> mechanism for challenging or revisiting decisions where there is a separate\n> statutory process or right of appeal; seeking changes to properly made\n> decisions, policies or legislation; pursuing matters which are not the\n> responsibility of the Council; promoting a particular cause or campaign; or\n> addressing matters for which alternative legal or regulatory processes\n> exist.\n>\n> You have also requested that the matter be considered by me as the\n> Monitoring Officer as a potential breach of the Member’s Code of Conduct by\n> Councillor Carroll.\n>\n> The role of the Monitoring Officer in this context is limited to\n> considering whether there is evidence that a member has failed to comply\n> with the statutory Members’s Code of Conduct. Having reviewed the\n> information you have provided, I have not identified evidence which would\n> indicate a breach of the Members Code of Conduct.\n>\n> Accordingly, the issues raised will not be progressed under the Council's\n> Corporate Concerns and Complaints Policy as they fall outside the scope of\n> that procedure. I do not consider there is sufficient evidence to justify\n> referral of the matter for consideration under the Members’s Code of\n> Conduct. No further action will therefore be taken by me in my capacity as\n> the Council’s Monitoring Officer in relation to this complaint.\n>\n> I appreciate that this response may be disappointing. However, I trust it\n> clarifies the Council's position and the limits of the Corporate Complaints\n> Procedure and the Monitoring Officer's role in the context of the matters\n> you raise.\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n>\n>\n> Victoria Davidson\n>\n> Monitoring Officer/Head of Legal & Democratic Services / Rheolydd\n> Gweithredol, Gwasanaethau Cyfreithiol\n>\n> Management Team - Resources / Swyddfa’r Cyfarwyddwr - Adnoddau\n>\n> Vale of Glamorgan Council / Cyngor Bro Morgannwg\n>\n> tel / ffôn:\n>\n> mob / sym: [phone redacted]\n>\n> e-mail / e-bost: [email redacted]\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> *Consider the environment.. Please don't print this e-mail unless you\n> really need to.*\n>\n> *Ystyriwch yr amgylchedd. Peidiwch ag argraffu'r neges hon oni bai fod\n> gwir angen.*\n>\n>\n>\n> Visit our Website at www.valeofglamorgan.gov.uk\n>\n> Ewch i'n gwefan yn www.bromorgannwg.gov.uk\n>\n>\n>\n> Find us on Facebook / Cewch ddod o hyd i ni ar Facebook\n> <http://en-gb.facebook.com/valeofglamorganlife>\n>\n> Follow us on Instagram / Dilynwch ni ar Instagram\n> <https://www.instagram.com/vog_council>\n>\n> Follow us on LinkedIn / Dilynwch ni ar LinkedIn\n> <https://www.linkedin.com/company/vale-of-glamorgan-council>\n>\n>\n>\n> Correspondence is welcomed in Welsh or English / Croesewir Gohebiaeth yn y\n> Gymraeg neu yn Saesneg.\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> <https://www.valeofglamorgan.gov.uk/en/our_council/achieving_our_vision/Corporate-Plan.aspx>\n>\n>\n>\n> *From:* Requester <[email redacted]>\n> *Sent:* 27 July 2026 22:22\n> *To:* [email redacted]\n> *Cc:* [email redacted]; [email redacted];\n> Carroll, George D (Cllr) <[email redacted]>\n> *Subject:* Complaint – Failure to Respond / Failure to Deal Properly with\n> Case – Cllr George Carroll (Llandough) – Great House Farm / Church View\n> Close\n>\n>\n>\n> You don't often get email from [email redacted]. Learn why this\n> is important <https://aka.ms/LearnAboutSenderIdentification>\n>\n> Dear Monitoring Officer / Corporate Complaints Team,\n>\n>\n>\n> With great regret I am writing to make a complaint about the persistent\n> failure of Councillor George D. D. Carroll (Llandough ward) to respond\n> substantively to my repeated and reasonable correspondence concerning\n> historic land, planning, enforcement, heritage and related public-interest\n> matters at Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough (now Church View Close),\n> Vale of Glamorgan.\n>\n>\n>\n> I have a legitimate and long-standing family interest in the site\n> (Williams/Buckler family connection documented from the 17th century). See\n> attached.\n>\n>\n>\n> The issues raised include alleged defective notices, loss of opportunity\n> to contest enforcement leading to demolition in 1988, heritage/regulatory\n> oversight, planning and title records, and related transparency failures.\n>\n>\n>\n> Despite multiple contacts over several months, I have received no\n> substantive replies from Cllr Carroll. This constitutes a failure to\n> respond and a failure to deal with the case properly.\n>\n>\n>\n> ### Full correspondence log with Cllr George Carroll (\n> [email redacted])\n>\n>\n>\n> | Date       | Subject / Key content\n>                            | Response received |\n>\n>\n> |------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------|\n>\n> | 16 Feb 2026 | Potential gaps in Council planning/record-keeping for\n> Great House Farm (demolished 1988); title fraud and procedural issues\n> relating to Church View Close | None |\n>\n>\n>\n> | 4 May 2026  | Request regarding BP vs Buckler 1987; shared\n> media/evidence; request for attention as Llandough councillor | None |\n>\n>\n>\n> | 7 Jun 2026  | “Church View Close, Llandough” – piecing together 1988\n> events from public records, family testimony and media | None |\n>\n>\n>\n> | 25 Jun 2026 | “Great House Farm, Llandough” – enquiry whether prior\n> concerns on family dispossession had been reviewed; request for any\n> progress or discussion | None |\n>\n>\n>\n> | 13 Jul 2026 | “Great House Farm/ Church View Close” – formal follow-up\n> listing the above timeline, restating legitimate interest (family roots\n> since 1667), noting absence of replies, requesting detailed response within\n> 10 working days, and raising the removed commemorative tree | None |\n>\n>\n>\n> ### Remedy sought\n>\n>\n>\n> 1. A full written response from Cllr Carroll addressing the points raised\n> in the logged emails, within 10–15 working days.\n>\n>\n>\n> 2. Confirmation that this complaint has been logged under the Council’s\n> corporate complaints procedure and/or referred to the Monitoring Officer /\n> Standards Committee as appropriate for any potential breach of the\n> councillor Code of Conduct (failure to respond / failure to deal properly\n> with a case).\n>\n>\n>\n> 3. Written confirmation of the process and timescale for investigation and\n> response.\n>\n>\n>\n> I have retained the full original emails and attachments and can supply\n> them on request. Please acknowledge receipt of this formal complaint.\n>\n>\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n>\n>\n> Requester\n>\n> [address redacted], [postcode redacted]\n> [map link redacted]\n>\n>\n> [email redacted]\n>\n> [phone redacted]\n>"},{"id":"gm-19ff85c058b1ec5d","subject":"Re: Response to National Archives enquiry. Case number CAS-344046-K1S1R2 CRM:021100306","name":"Re: Response to National Archives enquiry. Case number CAS-344046-K1S1R2 CRM:021100306","date":"2026-08-12T23:43:26Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19ff85c058b1ec5d.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19ff5eec7294b507-sponse-to-national-archives-enquiry-case-number-cas-344046-k1s1r2-crm-021100306","scopeTopics":["tna"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["tna"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19ff5eec7294b507","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"tna","name":"The National Archives"}],"refs":["CAS-344046"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19ff85c058b1ec5d.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Sarah Abbott,\n\nThank you for your response.\n\nI have considered it carefully. Unfortunately, it does not appear to answer\nthe FOI enquiry I submitted.\n\nMy request was deliberately framed more broadly than a simple catalogue\nsearch for the words “Great House Farm”, “Ty Mawr”, “Llandough” or\n“Cedfin”. I specifically asked The National Archives to identify recorded\ninformation concerning the *existence, transfer, retention, destruction,\ndisposal or absence of records*, including records that might not be\ncatalogued under the property's later or local names.\n\nYour response confirms only that you searched the PRO and RW series using:\n\n“Great House Farm” OR “Ty Mawr” OR Llandough OR Cedfin\n\ncombined with “Glamorgan”, and that this produced no catalogue results.\n\nYou also correctly state that Discovery searches catalogue titles and\ndescriptions rather than the contents of records. That qualification is\nimportant, because it means the search described cannot establish that\nrelevant records did not exist, were never transferred, or were destroyed.\n\nIn particular, your response does not appear to address the other\ncategories expressly identified in my request, including searches or\nrecorded information relating to:\n\n   - Western Ground Rents Ltd;\n   - BP Pension Trust Ltd;\n   - BP Properties Ltd;\n   - Mountjoy Ltd;\n   - the proceedings in *BP Properties Ltd v Buckler* [1987] EWCA Civ 2;\n   - the 1982–1983 registration and associated government or administrative\n   records;\n   - the 1980–1988 public campaign and governmental correspondence;\n   - the 1988 eviction and demolition;\n   - Cadw or central-government communications concerning the property;\n   - European Commission of Human Rights Application 14464/88;\n   - records concerning transfer, destruction or disposal of potentially\n   relevant material; and\n   - HMC9/311 or related archival references.\n\nThese alternative lines of enquiry are intentional. The purpose is to\nestablish whether relevant information can be identified through the\n*government\nbodies, corporate entities, court proceedings, administrative processes and\narchival transfers associated with the property*, rather than relying\nsolely upon the modern name by which the property is known.\n\nI am conducting corresponding enquiries with other repositories and public\nbodies. This is specifically intended to triangulate the documentary\nhistory where records may have been catalogued under a different creator,\ndepartment, legal proceeding, estate, corporate body or administrative\nfunction.\n\nI therefore ask that you do not treat the absence of a Discovery keyword\nhit as an answer to the substantive questions raised.\n\nPlease clarify:\n\n   1.\n\n   *Whether the searches undertaken were limited to the PRO and RW\n   catalogue series and the four property/location search terms identified in\n   your email.*\n   2.\n\n   *Whether The National Archives searched for the named corporate bodies,\n   court proceedings and application references identified in my request*,\n   rather than only searching the property names.\n   3.\n\n   *Whether The National Archives holds any recorded transfer, accession,\n   disposal, destruction or retention information* indicating that records\n   concerning the relevant property, estate, companies, litigation or\n   government involvement were transferred elsewhere, destroyed, or otherwise\n   disposed of.\n   4.\n\n   If such information is not held, *whether this is because no such\n   records are held, because no relevant records were ever transferred to TNA,\n   or because TNA's catalogue/search systems contain no discoverable\n   description of them.*\n   5.\n\n   Whether *HMC9/311* or related series have been checked, or whether that\n   reference falls outside the search undertaken.\n   6.\n\n   Whether there are *central-government, court, departmental or\n   administrative records relating to the litigation and entities identified\n   above which may contain references to the property without using “Great\n   House Farm”, “Ty Mawr”, “Llandough” or “Cedfin” in the catalogue\n   description.*\n\nFor the avoidance of doubt, I am not asking The National Archives to infer\nthe contents of uncatalogued records or to undertake an unlimited research\nproject. I am asking for confirmation of the *recorded information held by\nTNA concerning the existence, provenance, transfer, retention, destruction\nor disposal of potentially relevant records*, and for the searches\nundertaken to locate that information.\n\nThat distinction is particularly important because a catalogue search\nreturning no result is not equivalent to a finding that no relevant\nhistorical, governmental or archival records existed.\n\nI would therefore be grateful if this could be reconsidered as a response\nto the scope of the original FOI enquiry rather than treated solely as a\nrequest for assistance with a Discovery keyword search.\n\nI am continuing parallel enquiries with the National Library of Wales,\nGlamorgan Archives, Cadw, RCAHMW, HM Land Registry and other relevant\nbodies. The purpose of these enquiries is to establish the documentary\nchain independently and to identify where records were created,\ntransferred, retained or potentially destroyed.\n\nI have had a lot of months, over a year in fact, of very carefully worded\nemails in response to my FOI's and GDPR's. It's possible I discovered a\nmajor mistake or oversight was made in our landmark legal case BP vs\nBuckler 1987, which resulted in our ownership claim of our 800 year old\nhome, on land we'd occupied since 1667, being ignored - And so my\nterminally ill father, pregnant mother and myself and infant sister and\nbrother were dragged from our home by force and it demolished within hours\nof our eviction, right in front of us. The first time we'd been out of our\nhouse since the 16th century.  20 houses were then built on land we appear\nto still own after my nan conveniently died in the two week window she had\nto challenge BP registering our land in their names at the land registry.\nAnd my father conveniently dying in between BP applying for and being\ngranted planning permission. It's very damning for the land registry and\ncourts if my theory is correct.\n\nI'm not just the child from this case. I am a military warfare\nprofessional. I have 2 decades of evidence collected. I have family\ntestimony ans heirlooms too. We are looking at an Erin Brockvick type movie\nsequeal from what I'm uncovering in this case.\n\nWhat has happened here is beyond belief and is a matter of public interest\nnot just a family matter, because of the historic and archelogical\nsignificants of our land and property, subsequently characterised by GGAT\nas a “county treasure”, which CADW, under very questionable and unanswered\ncircumstances refused to list to prevent its demolition, least of all while\nwe were still living in it as a family for the 3rd consecuting century. The\nplanning/development process did not correctly engaged with the\narchaeological significance and a significant site of national interest was\ndemolished. The long standing councillors clerk took his retirement when I\nchallenged his initial reply of electing not to assist. CADW have admitted\ndestroying records after not listing. The land registry sent me the most\ncryptic legal jargon making their \"mistake\" very evidence.\n\nMy nan was a disabled, widowed pensioner. My dad a terminally ill man. They\nendured decades of corporate pressure. My dads ECHR (Buckler vs United\nKingdom 1989) was dismissing in lieu of BP's rights, citing them as the\nowners - yet there has never a ruling of their ownership claim and our\nownership claim has never been estinguished.\n\nI really do not mean to burden you or your office with this matter more\nthan is welcomed by yourselves. I'm merely just emphasising the importance\nand significanfs of any and every crumb of information that can be located\nand disclosed. Every detail is instrumental in my investigation and\nresearch and quest for justice for my family. I did pass this case to\nPolice for investigation of the fraud and consealment I am seeing, and\nwithin just 3 hours of receipt of the evidence I spent 20 years collecting,\nthey decided against investigating.\n\nI am the only one in my family who dares even speak up about this, seeing\nas anyone in our family who has historically challenged the narrative, has\nabruptly died. I was already shot at last time I was back in Wales, this\nwhile I was employed under the Welsh Government/ Vale of Glamorgan Council\nin adult care. I am a war veteran, I served in the middle east. I left my\nmilitary career with full honours. My profession is cyber and electronic\nwarfare and this maybe the reason my dying aunties, sisters and mother,\nlate gran and father, confide so heavily in me. I am questioning a lot\nhaving just completed a bona fide 22 years in Oath as a public servant. The\nMP or Councillor for Llandough will not even reply to me or my family about\nthis matter. ITV declassified footage shows BP Bailiffs to be undercover\nSAS, which the MOD will neither confirm nor deny.\n\nAll I can do at this juncture is appeal to your offices and look forward to\nany clarification of the searches undertaken and the recorded information\nheld by The National Archives and your offices final position on this\nmatter, for the history books and record. And I thank you in advance and\nfor your co-operation.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester \n\n\nOn Wed, 12 Aug 2026, 09:24 RESO Records Enquiries, <\n[email redacted]> wrote:\n\n> Dear  Requester,\n>\n> Thank you for contacting The National Archives of the United Kingdom.\n>\n> Records relating to the transfer, retention or destruction of material can\n> be found on our catalogue within the record series created by the Public\n> Record Office (departmental code PRO) and The National Archives\n> (departmental code RW).\n>\n> I have searched our catalogue, via the advanced search page\n> <https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/advanced-search> for records\n> using (\"Great House Farm\" OR \"Ty Mawr\" OR Llandough OR Cedfin) AND\n> Glamorgan as search terms within the file series PRO and RW. As you can\n> see here, this search did not yield any results\n> <https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/results/r?_aq=%28%22Great%20House%20Farm%22%20OR%20%22Ty%20Mawr%22%20OR%20Llandough%20OR%20Cedfin%29%20AND%20Glamorgan&_cr=pro%7Crw&_dss=range&_ro=any&_st=adv>.\n> You can try some similar searches yourself.\n>\n> I recommend you consult our guides to searching for records relating land\n> and maps\n> <https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/help-with-your-research/research-guides/?research-category=land-and-maps>\n> and civil court records\n> <https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/help-with-your-research/research-guides/?research-category=criminals-courts-and-prisons&sub-category%5B%5D=civil-court-cases-and-litigation>\n> to assist with other aspects of your enquiry. These guides will help you to\n> use *our catalogue* <https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/>, or\n> other online sources, effectively and focus your search.\n>\n> Records are represented in our online catalogue by a title, a reference\n> and a brief description. When you search our catalogue using keywords you\n> are searching across these titles and descriptions, rather than the\n> contents of the records themselves.\n>\n> By clicking on any search result, you can see whether the record is\n> available online or if you need to visit us to view the original. You can\n> also request copies of records from their catalogue description page.\n>\n> If the record is held by another archive, you will need to contact them\n> directly to find out how you can access it.\n>\n> You can also watch this video\n> <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Tz4f--o3zUU> for more advice on using\n> our online catalogue.\n>\n> You are welcome to visit us\n> <https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/about/visit-us/> to continue your\n> research here in person, where access to our records is free of charge.\n> Before you visit, please check our website for information regarding our opening\n> hours <https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/about/visit-us/opening-times/>\n> and ID requirements\n> <https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/about/visit-us/researching-here/do-i-need-a-readers-ticket/>,\n> and videos with tips on how to handle and photograph original documents\n> <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DlJ1OKfXWOg&list=PLddhSH7bW0pVBzNFsPIaUVs9mMFsA0utJ>\n> .\n>\n> If you are unable to visit, or would like someone to pursue this research\n> on your behalf, please see the available paid search options\n> <https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/help-with-your-research/paying-for-research/>\n> on our website.\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n> Sarah Abbott\n>\n> Remote Enquiries Duty Officer\n> *www.nationalarchives.gov.uk* <http://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/>\n>\n> Please complete our brief, three question survey\n> <https://emea.dcv.ms/1PncPtnWn7> to let us know what you think about your\n> recent contact. Your views are important in helping us improve our service.\n>\n> *Enquiry:*\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> You don't often get email from [email redacted]. Learn why this\n> is important <https://aka.ms/LearnAboutSenderIdentification>\n>\n>\n> *EXTERNAL*: This email originates from outside of The National Archives.\n>\n> Dear Patricia Reynolds,\n>\n> Thank you for your respon