"},{"id":"gm-19ff59126fe47398","subject":"Re: Acknowledgement from the Culture, Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee","name":"Re: Acknowledgement from the Culture, Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee","date":"2026-08-12T10:42:38Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19ff59126fe47398.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19ff079027ee0614-acknowledgement-from-the-culture-communications-cymraeg-and-sport-committee","scopeTopics":["senedd"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["senedd"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19ff079027ee0614","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"senedd","name":"Senedd"}],"refs":["ATISN27021"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19ff59126fe47398.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Ms James,\n\nThank you for your response confirming the Committee’s position.\n\nI acknowledge the Committee’s decision not to take the matter forward. For\nthe record, however, my position remains that the issues raised concern the\nabsence, retention and preservation of the administrative record of Cadw’s\n1988 assessment, and the resulting implications for public accountability\nand the ability to scrutinise significant historical heritage decisions.\n\nMy correspondence was never intended to ask the Committee to adjudicate the\n1988 decision, reopen the original assessment, or intervene in the\nunderlying historical dispute. The specific concern was the near-total\nabsence of the administrative record, as confirmed by Cadw’s FOI response\nATISN 27021, and what that absence means for the integrity and scrutiny of\nthe historical public record.\n\nI note the routes identified in your correspondence and will now pursue\nthese governance and archival matters through the appropriate\nadministrative and complaints processes and, where appropriate, the Public\nServices Ombudsman for Wales and relevant archival authorities.\n\nI will retain this correspondence as part of the documentary record\ndemonstrating that these concerns were formally raised with the Senedd’s\nrelevant scrutiny committee and that the Committee declined to take the\nmatter forward.\n\nThank you for clarifying the Committee’s procedural position.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nRequester\n\n\nOn Wed, 12 Aug 2026, 06:13 Pwyllgor Diwylliant, Cyfathrebu, y Gymraeg a\nChwaraeon - Culture, Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee,\n<[email redacted]> wrote:\n\n> Dear Requester,\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you for your further correspondence and for clarifying the purpose\n> of your submission.\n>\n>\n>\n> I understand the distinction you draw between seeking a review of the\n> original 1988 decision and raising broader concerns about record-keeping,\n> archival practice, and public accountability. However, the Committee's role\n> is to scrutinise and hold the Welsh Government and associated public bodies\n> to account in relation to policy, governance, and the use of public\n> resources.\n>\n>\n>\n> While I appreciate the broader administrative concerns you have\n> identified, the issues raised in your correspondence arise from a specific\n> historical case. The Committee does not have a role in reviewing or\n> overseeing day-to-day operational decisions taken in individual cases.\n> Accordingly, the Committee is unable to take forward the matters raised in\n> your correspondence.\n>\n>\n>\n> If you wish to pursue concerns regarding the management, retention or\n> preservation of historical records, these matters are most appropriately\n> raised with the relevant public authority or archival body responsible for\n> those records. Similarly, concerns regarding the administration of a public\n> body should be pursued through that body's complaints procedure and, where\n> appropriate, with the Public Services Ombudsman for Wales.\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you for taking the time to write to the Committee and to set out\n> your concerns in detail.\n>\n>\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n>\n>\n> Haidee\n>\n>\n>\n> *Haidee James*\n>\n> *Ail Glerc, Pwyllgor Diwylliant, Cyfathrebu, y Gymraeg a Chwaraeon,*\n> Senedd Cymru\n>\n> *Second Clerk, Culture, Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee,*\n> Welsh Parliament\n>\n> [phone redacted] *Rhagenwau|Pronouns:* hi – she/her\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> *From:* Requester <[email redacted]>\n> *Sent:* 11 August 2026 16:57\n> *To:* Pwyllgor Diwylliant, Cyfathrebu, y Gymraeg a Chwaraeon - Culture,\n> Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee <[email redacted]\n> >\n> *Subject:* Re: Acknowledgement from the Culture, Communications, Cymraeg\n> and Sport Committee\n>\n>\n>\n> Dear Ms James,\n>\n> Thank you for your considered response.\n>\n> I understand and accept that the Committee does not have a remit to\n> adjudicate upon an individual dispute, determine whether a historical\n> decision was lawful, or direct Cadw to reopen a particular case. My\n> submission was deliberately framed so as not to ask the Committee to do any\n> of those things.\n>\n> I would, however, respectfully clarify one point, because I am concerned\n> that the nature of the request may have been characterised more narrowly\n> than intended.\n>\n> The attached report expressly states that it is *not a legal challenge, a\n> forensic proof of wrongdoing, a complaint seeking adjudication, or a demand\n> for reversal of the 1988 decision*. Its stated purpose is historical\n> record correction, archival explanation and administrative acknowledgement.\n> In particular, it asks for an explanation of why almost none of the records\n> concerning the 1988 assessment survive, whether that reflects the\n> applicable record-retention arrangements, and whether the surviving\n> independent evidence should be properly reflected in the permanent public\n> record.\n>\n> The central issue therefore extends beyond the merits of the individual\n> 1988 listing assessment.\n>\n> Cadw's 2026 FOI response confirms that an assessment took place, but that\n> only two exterior photographs now survive; the assessment report, reasons\n> for refusal, site notes, internal recommendations and consultation\n> correspondence are no longer available. The report does not assert that\n> this proves wrongdoing. Rather, it identifies a straightforward\n> public-administration question: *how can the adequacy and evidential\n> basis of an irreversible public heritage decision be understood or\n> scrutinised when the underlying administrative record no longer survives?*\n>\n> That seems potentially relevant to the Committee's stated function of\n> scrutinising the administration and accountability of Welsh Government and\n> associated public bodies, even if the Committee cannot itself determine\n> whether the 1988 decision was correct.\n>\n> I am therefore not asking the Committee to investigate the Buckler\n> family's historical dispute with BP, adjudicate upon the 1988 decision, or\n> direct Cadw to reverse anything. The specific administrative questions\n> raised in the report are instead:\n>\n>    1. What record-retention arrangements applied to the 1988 assessment?\n>    2. Why do the assessment report, reasons, site notes and consultation\n>    records no longer survive?\n>    3. Were the records destroyed under an applicable retention policy or\n>    other authority, and were they considered for transfer to the appropriate\n>    archival institution?\n>    4. Is the present position consistent with Welsh Government/Cadw\n>    expectations for the preservation of records concerning significant\n>    heritage decisions?\n>    5. Are current arrangements sufficient to ensure that comparable\n>    heritage assessments, particularly where a building is under imminent\n>    threat of demolition, are properly documented and retained?\n>\n> These are not questions that require the Committee to determine the rights\n> of either party to the original dispute. They concern *administrative\n> practice, institutional record-keeping and the ability of the Senedd and\n> the public to scrutinise decisions taken by public bodies*. The report\n> specifically asks Cadw to explain the archival position and review its\n> current record-keeping practices for this reason.\n>\n> I would therefore be grateful if the Committee could reconsider whether\n> the broader administrative and accountability issue falls within its\n> scrutiny function, even if the Committee cannot examine the underlying\n> individual dispute.\n>\n> If the Committee considers that it nevertheless cannot engage with the\n> matter, I would appreciate clarification as to *which Senedd or Welsh\n> Government scrutiny, oversight, archival or complaints mechanism is\n> considered appropriate for raising a documented concern about the absence\n> of the administrative record of a historical Cadw decision and the\n> implications this has for public accountability*.\n>\n> For completeness, I should also emphasise that the report itself\n> deliberately recognises the limits of the surviving evidence. It does not\n> claim that Cadw acted unlawfully or in bad faith, and it expressly\n> acknowledges that the absence of the 1988 working papers means that the\n> assessment cannot now be reconstructed definitively.\n>\n> My objective is therefore quite limited: *to ensure that the surviving\n> evidence is preserved, that the archival gap is properly explained, and\n> that the public record accurately distinguishes what is established from\n> what can no longer be verified.*\n>\n> I hope that clarification assists, and I would be grateful if you could\n> confirm whether the Committee is able to consider the matter from that\n> wider public-administration and accountability perspective.\n>\n> Kind regards,\n>\n> Requester\n> \n>\n>\n>\n> On Tue, 11 Aug 2026, 07:58 Pwyllgor Diwylliant, Cyfathrebu, y Gymraeg a\n> Chwaraeon - Culture, Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee, <\n> [email redacted]> wrote:\n>\n> Dear Requester,\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you for your correspondence and for sharing the forensic review of\n> Cadw's 1988 assessment of Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough. I\n> acknowledge the strength of feeling expressed in your letter and recognise\n> the longstanding concerns that you and your family have raised.\n>\n>\n>\n> The Committee’s role is to scrutinise the work of the Welsh Government and\n> associated public bodies, including holding ministers and public\n> authorities to account for their policies, administration and use of public\n> resources. However, the Committee does not have a remit to investigate,\n> adjudicate upon, or oversee individual complaints or dispute. Nor does it\n> have the powers to reopen historical decisions or direct public bodies to\n> undertake investigations into specific matters.\n>\n>\n>\n> The Committee is therefore unable to take on an oversight role in relation\n> to the issues you raise or to intervene in these matters.\n>\n>\n>\n> The matters you raise appear principally to concern the actions and\n> decision-making processes of Cadw in relation to a specific historical\n> assessment. Cadw are responsible for responding to requests for information\n> about their records and decision-making processes, and for considering any\n> representations made regarding the accuracy or completeness of the\n> historical record.\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you again for writing to the Committee.\n>\n>\n>\n> Kind regards,\n>\n>\n>\n> Haidee\n>\n>\n>\n> *Haidee James*\n>\n> *Ail Glerc, Pwyllgor Diwylliant, Cyfathrebu, y Gymraeg a Chwaraeon,*\n> Senedd Cymru\n>\n> *Second Clerk, Culture, Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee,*\n> Welsh Parliament\n>\n> [phone redacted] *Rhagenwau|Pronouns:* hi – she/her\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> *From:* Requester <[email redacted]>\n> *Sent:* 10 August 2026 22:46\n> *To:* [email redacted]\n> *Cc:* [email redacted]; [email redacted]; DOUGHTY,\n> Stephen <[email redacted]>\n> *Subject:* Re: CADW failures – Revised Report (10 August 2026)\n>\n>\n>\n> Sirs/Ma'ams,\n>\n> I write further to my email of 9 August 2026 submitting a review\n> concerning Cadw’s 1988 assessment of Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough.\n>\n> Please find attached (and linked below) a revised edition of the report,\n> dated 10 August 2026. This version has been carefully restructured to\n> present the same evidence in a more measured and administratively focused\n> form. It is offered as a request for historical record correction, archival\n> explanation, and administrative acknowledgement rather than as a forensic\n> challenge.\n>\n> The revised document:\n>\n> • Clearly states what it is and is not;\n> • Sets out the surviving independent evidence (principally the H.J. Thomas\n> RCAHMW survey of 1974);\n> • Notes the near-total absence of Cadw’s 1988 working papers confirmed in\n> ATISN 27021;\n> • Asks only for realistic, achievable administrative steps: explanation of\n> the archival position, reflection of the surviving evidence in the Historic\n> Environment Record, acknowledgement of the records gap, and consideration\n> of a local heritage marker.\n>\n> It does not claim unlawfulness, seek compensation from Cadw, or demand a\n> public inquiry.\n>\n> I hope this revised presentation is more palatable and will assist the\n> relevant bodies in providing a constructive response.\n>\n> I look forward to your substantive response.\n>\n> Regards,\n>\n> Requester\n> \n> [email redacted]\n>\n> [phone redacted]\n>\n>"},{"id":"gm-19ff53ffe0f61979","subject":"RE: Acknowledgement from the Culture, Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee","name":"RE: Acknowledgement from the Culture, Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee","date":"2026-08-12T09:13:52Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19ff53ffe0f61979.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19ff079027ee0614-acknowledgement-from-the-culture-communications-cymraeg-and-sport-committee","scopeTopics":["senedd"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["senedd"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19ff079027ee0614","direction":"received","from":"Pwyllgor Diwylliant, Cyfathrebu, y Gymraeg a Chwaraeon - Culture, Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee","institutions":[{"id":"senedd","name":"Senedd"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19ff53ffe0f61979.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester,\n\nThank you for your further correspondence and for clarifying the purpose of your submission.\n\nI understand the distinction you draw between seeking a review of the original 1988 decision and raising broader concerns about record-keeping, archival practice, and public accountability. However, the Committee's role is to scrutinise and hold the Welsh Government and associated public bodies to account in relation to policy, governance, and the use of public resources.\n\nWhile I appreciate the broader administrative concerns you have identified, the issues raised in your correspondence arise from a specific historical case. The Committee does not have a role in reviewing or overseeing day-to-day operational decisions taken in individual cases. Accordingly, the Committee is unable to take forward the matters raised in your correspondence.\n\nIf you wish to pursue concerns regarding the management, retention or preservation of historical records, these matters are most appropriately raised with the relevant public authority or archival body responsible for those records. Similarly, concerns regarding the administration of a public body should be pursued through that body's complaints procedure and, where appropriate, with the Public Services Ombudsman for Wales.\n\nThank you for taking the time to write to the Committee and to set out your concerns in detail.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nHaidee\n\n[cid:image001.png@01DD2A3D.D9F9C7A0]\nHaidee James[cid:image002.png@01DD2A3D.D9F9C7A0]\nAil Glerc, Pwyllgor Diwylliant, Cyfathrebu, y Gymraeg a Chwaraeon, Senedd Cymru\nSecond Clerk, Culture, Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee, Welsh Parliament\n[phone redacted] Rhagenwau|Pronouns: hi – she/her\n\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]>\nSent: 11 August 2026 16:57\nTo: Pwyllgor Diwylliant, Cyfathrebu, y Gymraeg a Chwaraeon - Culture, Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee <[email redacted]>\nSubject: Re: Acknowledgement from the Culture, Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee\n\n\nDear Ms James,\n\nThank you for your considered response.\n\nI understand and accept that the Committee does not have a remit to adjudicate upon an individual dispute, determine whether a historical decision was lawful, or direct Cadw to reopen a particular case. My submission was deliberately framed so as not to ask the Committee to do any of those things.\n\nI would, however, respectfully clarify one point, because I am concerned that the nature of the request may have been characterised more narrowly than intended.\n\nThe attached report expressly states that it is not a legal challenge, a forensic proof of wrongdoing, a complaint seeking adjudication, or a demand for reversal of the 1988 decision. Its stated purpose is historical record correction, archival explanation and administrative acknowledgement. In particular, it asks for an explanation of why almost none of the records concerning the 1988 assessment survive, whether that reflects the applicable record-retention arrangements, and whether the surviving independent evidence should be properly reflected in the permanent public record.\n\nThe central issue therefore extends beyond the merits of the individual 1988 listing assessment.\n\nCadw's 2026 FOI response confirms that an assessment took place, but that only two exterior photographs now survive; the assessment report, reasons for refusal, site notes, internal recommendations and consultation correspondence are no longer available. The report does not assert that this proves wrongdoing. Rather, it identifies a straightforward public-administration question: how can the adequacy and evidential basis of an irreversible public heritage decision be understood or scrutinised when the underlying administrative record no longer survives?\n\nThat seems potentially relevant to the Committee's stated function of scrutinising the administration and accountability of Welsh Government and associated public bodies, even if the Committee cannot itself determine whether the 1988 decision was correct.\n\nI am therefore not asking the Committee to investigate the Buckler family's historical dispute with BP, adjudicate upon the 1988 decision, or direct Cadw to reverse anything. The specific administrative questions raised in the report are instead:\n\n  1.  What record-retention arrangements applied to the 1988 assessment?\n  2.  Why do the assessment report, reasons, site notes and consultation records no longer survive?\n  3.  Were the records destroyed under an applicable retention policy or other authority, and were they considered for transfer to the appropriate archival institution?\n  4.  Is the present position consistent with Welsh Government/Cadw expectations for the preservation of records concerning significant heritage decisions?\n  5.  Are current arrangements sufficient to ensure that comparable heritage assessments, particularly where a building is under imminent threat of demolition, are properly documented and retained?\n\nThese are not questions that require the Committee to determine the rights of either party to the original dispute. They concern administrative practice, institutional record-keeping and the ability of the Senedd and the public to scrutinise decisions taken by public bodies. The report specifically asks Cadw to explain the archival position and review its current record-keeping practices for this reason.\n\nI would therefore be grateful if the Committee could reconsider whether the broader administrative and accountability issue falls within its scrutiny function, even if the Committee cannot examine the underlying individual dispute.\n\nIf the Committee considers that it nevertheless cannot engage with the matter, I would appreciate clarification as to which Senedd or Welsh Government scrutiny, oversight, archival or complaints mechanism is considered appropriate for raising a documented concern about the absence of the administrative record of a historical Cadw decision and the implications this has for public accountability.\n\nFor completeness, I should also emphasise that the report itself deliberately recognises the limits of the surviving evidence. It does not claim that Cadw acted unlawfully or in bad faith, and it expressly acknowledges that the absence of the 1988 working papers means that the assessment cannot now be reconstructed definitively.\n\nMy objective is therefore quite limited: to ensure that the surviving evidence is preserved, that the archival gap is properly explained, and that the public record accurately distinguishes what is established from what can no longer be verified.\n\nI hope that clarification assists, and I would be grateful if you could confirm whether the Committee is able to consider the matter from that wider public-administration and accountability perspective.\n\nKind regards,\n\nRequester\n\n\nOn Tue, 11 Aug 2026, 07:58 Pwyllgor Diwylliant, Cyfathrebu, y Gymraeg a Chwaraeon - Culture, Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee, <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>> wrote:\nDear Requester,\n\nThank you for your correspondence and for sharing the forensic review of Cadw's 1988 assessment of Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough. I acknowledge the strength of feeling expressed in your letter and recognise the longstanding concerns that you and your family have raised.\n\nThe Committee’s role is to scrutinise the work of the Welsh Government and associated public bodies, including holding ministers and public authorities to account for their policies, administration and use of public resources. However, the Committee does not have a remit to investigate, adjudicate upon, or oversee individual complaints or dispute. Nor does it have the powers to reopen historical decisions or direct public bodies to undertake investigations into specific matters.\n\nThe Committee is therefore unable to take on an oversight role in relation to the issues you raise or to intervene in these matters.\n\nThe matters you raise appear principally to concern the actions and decision-making processes of Cadw in relation to a specific historical assessment. Cadw are responsible for responding to requests for information about their records and decision-making processes, and for considering any representations made regarding the accuracy or completeness of the historical record.\n\nThank you again for writing to the Committee.\n\nKind regards,\n\nHaidee\n\n[cid:image001.png@01DD2A3D.D9F9C7A0]\nHaidee James[cid:image002.png@01DD2A3D.D9F9C7A0]\nAil Glerc, Pwyllgor Diwylliant, Cyfathrebu, y Gymraeg a Chwaraeon, Senedd Cymru\nSecond Clerk, Culture, Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee, Welsh Parliament\n[phone redacted] Rhagenwau|Pronouns: hi – she/her\n\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSent: 10 August 2026 22:46\nTo: [email redacted]<[email redacted]>\nCc: [email redacted]<[email redacted]>; [email redacted]<[email redacted]>; DOUGHTY, Stephen <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSubject: Re: CADW failures – Revised Report (10 August 2026)\n\nSirs/Ma'ams,\n\nI write further to my email of 9 August 2026 submitting a review concerning Cadw’s 1988 assessment of Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough.\n\nPlease find attached (and linked below) a revised edition of the report, dated 10 August 2026. This version has been carefully restructured to present the same evidence in a more measured and administratively focused form. It is offered as a request for historical record correction, archival explanation, and administrative acknowledgement rather than as a forensic challenge.\n\nThe revised document:\n\n• Clearly states what it is and is not;\n• Sets out the surviving independent evidence (principally the H.J. Thomas RCAHMW survey of 1974);\n• Notes the near-total absence of Cadw’s 1988 working papers confirmed in ATISN 27021;\n• Asks only for realistic, achievable administrative steps: explanation of the archival position, reflection of the surviving evidence in the Historic Environment Record, acknowledgement of the records gap, and consideration of a local heritage marker.\n\nIt does not claim unlawfulness, seek compensation from Cadw, or demand a public inquiry.\n\nI hope this revised presentation is more palatable and will assist the relevant bodies in providing a constructive response.\n\nI look forward to your substantive response.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester\n\n[email redacted]<[email redacted]>\n[phone redacted]"},{"id":"gm-19ff502babd77740","subject":"Further to Your Enquiry: Ref: 260812-6561894 Title No: WA231076","name":"Further to Your Enquiry: Ref: 260812-6561894 Title No: WA231076","date":"2026-08-12T08:07:01Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19ff502babd77740.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19ff502babd77740-further-to-your-enquiry-ref-260812-6561894-title-no-wa231076","scopeTopics":["hmlr"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["hmlr"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19ff502babd77740","direction":"received","from":"_Central","institutions":[{"id":"hmlr","name":"HM Land Registry"}],"refs":["N610CGV"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19ff502babd77740.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Enquiry Reference: 260812-6561894 Title Number: WA231076\n\nDear Customer\n\nI am sorry that the wrong contact information was shown on our letter to you. We are directing our customers to contact us via our contact form .\n\n\n\nBackground:\n\nHM Land Registry has created many email addresses over the years. We can no longer service the incoming emails or route such emails into our current system.\n\n\n\nPlanned system improvements:\n\nWe plan to remove all reference to individual HM Land Registry email addresses. This process was more complex than expected and some templates still need updating.\n\n\n\nWorking with our Customers:\n\nWe want to ensure that correspondence goes direct to our customer service team. To do this, we now ask customers to use our online contact form. This is why we put an auto-response in place.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nFrom: Requester  \nSent: Wednesday, August 12, 2026 8:18 AM\nTo: Wales.OfficeMail  \nSubject: Clarification of surviving first-registration documents N610CGV / WA231076 & WA240304\n\n\n\n\n\nDear Mr Haste,\n\nThank you for your letter of 30 July 2026 concerning reference N610CGV and the surviving records relating to the first registration of titles WA231076 and WA240304 at Great House Farm, Llandough.\n\nI am writing to clarify the factual contents of the surviving first-registration records and to identify the appropriate documents for inspection/copying. This is not a request to reopen my previous complaint, nor am I asking HM Land Registry to determine the historical ownership dispute.\n\nI would be grateful if you could clarify the following.\n\n1. The 1971 Abstract of Title\n\nYou previously confirmed that a 1971 Abstract of Title was seen in connection with this matter.\n\nPlease confirm:\n\n  *   whether it still survives;\n\n  *   which title/file it relates to;\n\n  *   its item or document reference within the surviving records; and\n\n  *   whether it is available for inspection or copying.\n\n2. The “question or doubt” certificate\n\nPlease confirm where in the surviving first-registration material the certificate/declaration appears in which the applicant's solicitors stated that they were “not aware of any question or doubt affecting the title.”\n\nIn particular, please confirm whether this forms part of the Form 1E dated 30 November 1982, or is a separate surviving document.\n\n3. What the first-registration file records about other interests\n\nPlease confirm whether any of the surviving first-registration documents contain any reference to:\n\n  *   Mary Williams;\n\n  *   Fred Buckler;\n\n  *   William (Billy) Buckler;\n\n  *   Great House Farm / Ty Mawr and its farmhouse, garden or curtilage;\n\n  *   any person other than the applicant asserting ownership or another interest in the land;\n\n  *   possession proceedings concerning the farmhouse or land; or\n\n  *   any objection, dispute, question or doubt concerning title.\n\nI am asking this solely as a factual question about the contents of the surviving records.\n\nIf no such reference appears in the surviving first-registration material, confirmation of that fact would be sufficient.\n\n4. Identification of the surviving documents\n\nYour 30 July letter identifies, amongst other things, the Form 1E, A13-DL list of documents lodged and correspondence from Linklaters & Paines.\n\nPlease identify the specific item numbers I should request by Form OC2 to obtain the fullest available surviving first-registration record, particularly:\n\n  *   the title evidence submitted by the applicant;\n\n  *   the 1971 Abstract of Title, if it survives;\n\n  *   the A13-DL list of documents lodged;\n\n  *   correspondence with Linklaters & Paines;\n\n  *   examiner's notes, requisitions, memoranda or file notes, if any survive; and\n\n  *   any document recording the basis upon which the Registrar accepted the applicant's title.\n\nIf any of these categories do not survive, please identify which ones do not.\n\n5. Historic records relevant to identifying the land\n\nI also wish to ensure that the correct physical parcel is being compared with the land registered in 1982–83.\n\nDuring recent research at the Glamorgan Archives, I have located historic material concerning Great House Farm/Ty Mawr, including:\n\n  *   the 6 November 1877 Bute Estate counterpart lease to John Williams;\n\n  *   the 1872 Llandough Rate Book;\n\n  *   the 1924 and 1938 Great House Farm sale particulars and associated OS parcel references; and\n\n  *   historic plans and notes concerning the farmhouse, gardens and associated boundaries.\n\nThese records appear potentially relevant to identifying the historic extent and boundaries of the farmhouse and curtilage.\n\nI am not asking HM Land Registry to determine the legal effect of those documents. I simply wish to establish whether the documents submitted during the 1982/83 first-registration process identify the same physical parcel and, if so, what documentary evidence was relied upon to establish that identity.\n\n6. Subsequent planning record\n\nFor completeness, the later planning record also contains a significant contemporaneous document: BP Properties Ltd's Certificate A dated 16 October 1989 in respect of outline application 89/01396/OUT, in which BP certified its position concerning ownership of the application land.\n\nAgain, I am not asking HM Land Registry to determine the effect of that document. I mention it only because it makes it particularly important for my research to establish precisely what documentary evidence was actually before the Registrar in 1982–83, several years before that planning certification.\n\n7. Form OC2\n\nI am particularly keen to avoid submitting an unnecessarily broad OC2 request.\n\nIf you could therefore identify the specific item numbers from your 30 July schedule which would provide the fullest surviving first-registration record, I can request those documents directly.\n\nIn particular, I would appreciate confirmation of the items corresponding to the Form 1E, A13-DL list, title evidence, Linklaters & Paines correspondence, the 1971 Abstract of Title, and any examiner/requisition material that survives.\n\nThank you for your assistance. I would be grateful for a substantive clarification of the above points so that I can proceed with a properly targeted inspection/copy request.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester\nSgt (Ret’d)\n\n\n\nHM Land Registry’s purpose is to protect your land ownership and provide services and data that underpin an efficient and informed property market. Our vision is: “A world-leading property market as part of a thriving economy and a sustainable future”.\n\nWe check all mail and attachments for known viruses. However, you are advised that you open any attachments at your own risk. If you have received this email and it was not intended for you, please let us know, then delete it.\n\nWe welcome correspondence in English and Welsh.\n\nTo see how HM Land Registry treats your personal information, read our Personal information charter: www.gov.uk/government/organisations/land-registry/about/personal-information-charter."},{"id":"gm-19ff4da656ed9ce9","subject":"Requester - 260805002 - FOIA - response","name":"Requester - 260805002 - FOIA - response","date":"2026-08-12T07:22:57Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wa