 disclosure, storage or copying is not permitted. If you are not the intended recipient, please destroy all copies and inform the sender by return e-mail. Internet e-mail is not a secure medium. Any reply to this message could be intercepted and read by someone else. Please bear that in mind when deciding whether to send material in response to this message by e-mail. This e-mail (whether you are the sender or the recipient) may be monitored, recorded and retained by the Ministry of Justice. Monitoring / blocking software may be used, and e-mail content may be read at any time. You have a responsibility to ensure laws are not broken when composing or forwarding e-mails and their contents.\n\n\nOFFICIAL - FOR PUBLIC RELEASE"},{"id":"gm-19ff4d63e990531d","subject":"Clarification of surviving first-registration documents — N610CGV / WA231076 & WA240304","name":"Clarification of surviving first-registration documents — N610CGV / WA231076 & WA240304","date":"2026-08-12T07:18:29Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19ff4d63e990531d.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19fb20f21f899949-clarification-of-surviving-first-registration-documents-n610cgv-wa231076-wa240304","scopeTopics":["hmlr"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["hmlr"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19fb20f21f899949","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"hmlr","name":"HM Land Registry"}],"refs":["N610CGV"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19ff4d63e990531d.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Mr Haste,\n\nThank you for your letter of *30 July 2026* concerning reference *N610CGV*\nand the surviving records relating to the first registration of titles\n*WA231076\nand WA240304* at Great House Farm, Llandough.\n\nI am writing to clarify the factual contents of the surviving\nfirst-registration records and to identify the appropriate documents for\ninspection/copying. *This is not a request to reopen my previous complaint,\nnor am I asking HM Land Registry to determine the historical ownership\ndispute.*\n\nI would be grateful if you could clarify the following.\n1. The 1971 Abstract of Title\n\nYou previously confirmed that a *1971 Abstract of Title* was seen in\nconnection with this matter.\n\nPlease confirm:\n\n   - whether it still survives;\n   - which title/file it relates to;\n   - its item or document reference within the surviving records; and\n   - whether it is available for inspection or copying.\n\n2. The “question or doubt” certificate\n\nPlease confirm where in the surviving first-registration material the\ncertificate/declaration appears in which the applicant's solicitors stated\nthat they were *“not aware of any question or doubt affecting the title.”*\n\nIn particular, please confirm whether this forms part of the *Form 1E dated\n30 November 1982*, or is a separate surviving document.\n3. What the first-registration file records about other interests\n\nPlease confirm whether any of the surviving first-registration documents\ncontain any reference to:\n\n   - *Mary Williams*;\n   - *Fred Buckler*;\n   - *William (Billy) Buckler*;\n   - *Great House Farm / Ty Mawr* and its farmhouse, garden or curtilage;\n   - any person other than the applicant asserting ownership or another\n   interest in the land;\n   - possession proceedings concerning the farmhouse or land; or\n   - any objection, dispute, question or doubt concerning title.\n\nI am asking this *solely as a factual question about the contents of the\nsurviving records*.\n\nIf no such reference appears in the surviving first-registration material,\nconfirmation of that fact would be sufficient.\n4. Identification of the surviving documents\n\nYour 30 July letter identifies, amongst other things, the *Form 1E, A13-DL\nlist of documents lodged and correspondence from Linklaters & Paines*.\n\nPlease identify the specific item numbers I should request by *Form OC2* to\nobtain the fullest available surviving first-registration record,\nparticularly:\n\n   - the title evidence submitted by the applicant;\n   - the 1971 Abstract of Title, if it survives;\n   - the A13-DL list of documents lodged;\n   - correspondence with Linklaters & Paines;\n   - examiner's notes, requisitions, memoranda or file notes, if any\n   survive; and\n   - any document recording the basis upon which the Registrar accepted the\n   applicant's title.\n\nIf any of these categories do not survive, please identify which ones do\nnot.\n5. Historic records relevant to identifying the land\n\nI also wish to ensure that the correct physical parcel is being compared\nwith the land registered in 1982–83.\n\nDuring recent research at the Glamorgan Archives, I have located historic\nmaterial concerning Great House Farm/Ty Mawr, including:\n\n   - the *6 November 1877 Bute Estate counterpart lease to John Williams*;\n   - the *1872 Llandough Rate Book*;\n   - the *1924 and 1938 Great House Farm sale particulars and associated OS\n   parcel references*; and\n   - historic plans and notes concerning the farmhouse, gardens and\n   associated boundaries.\n\nThese records appear potentially relevant to identifying the historic\nextent and boundaries of the farmhouse and curtilage.\n\nI am *not asking HM Land Registry to determine the legal effect of those\ndocuments*. I simply wish to establish whether the documents submitted\nduring the 1982/83 first-registration process identify the same physical\nparcel and, if so, what documentary evidence was relied upon to establish\nthat identity.\n6. Subsequent planning record\n\nFor completeness, the later planning record also contains a significant\ncontemporaneous document: *BP Properties Ltd's Certificate A dated 16\nOctober 1989 in respect of outline application 89/01396/OUT*, in which BP\ncertified its position concerning ownership of the application land.\n\nAgain, I am not asking HM Land Registry to determine the effect of that\ndocument. I mention it only because it makes it particularly important for\nmy research to establish precisely *what documentary evidence was actually\nbefore the Registrar in 1982–83*, several years before that planning\ncertification.\n7. Form OC2\n\nI am particularly keen to avoid submitting an unnecessarily broad OC2\nrequest.\n\nIf you could therefore identify the *specific item numbers* from your 30\nJuly schedule which would provide the fullest surviving first-registration\nrecord, I can request those documents directly.\n\nIn particular, I would appreciate confirmation of the items corresponding\nto the *Form 1E, A13-DL list, title evidence, Linklaters & Paines\ncorrespondence, the 1971 Abstract of Title, and any examiner/requisition\nmaterial that survives*.\n\nThank you for your assistance. I would be grateful for a substantive\nclarification of the above points so that I can proceed with a properly\ntargeted inspection/copy request.\n\nRegards,\n\n*Requester*\n*Sgt (Ret’d)*"},{"id":"gm-19ff18a9f8df46be","subject":"Re: Acknowledgement from the Culture, Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee","name":"Re: Acknowledgement from the Culture, Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee","date":"2026-08-11T15:57:00Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19ff18a9f8df46be.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19ff079027ee0614-acknowledgement-from-the-culture-communications-cymraeg-and-sport-committee","scopeTopics":["senedd"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["senedd"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19ff079027ee0614","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"senedd","name":"Senedd"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19ff18a9f8df46be.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Ms James,\n\nThank you for your considered response.\n\nI understand and accept that the Committee does not have a remit to\nadjudicate upon an individual dispute, determine whether a historical\ndecision was lawful, or direct Cadw to reopen a particular case. My\nsubmission was deliberately framed so as not to ask the Committee to do any\nof those things.\n\nI would, however, respectfully clarify one point, because I am concerned\nthat the nature of the request may have been characterised more narrowly\nthan intended.\n\nThe attached report expressly states that it is *not a legal challenge, a\nforensic proof of wrongdoing, a complaint seeking adjudication, or a demand\nfor reversal of the 1988 decision*. Its stated purpose is historical record\ncorrection, archival explanation and administrative acknowledgement. In\nparticular, it asks for an explanation of why almost none of the records\nconcerning the 1988 assessment survive, whether that reflects the\napplicable record-retention arrangements, and whether the surviving\nindependent evidence should be properly reflected in the permanent public\nrecord.\n\nThe central issue therefore extends beyond the merits of the individual\n1988 listing assessment.\n\nCadw's 2026 FOI response confirms that an assessment took place, but that\nonly two exterior photographs now survive; the assessment report, reasons\nfor refusal, site notes, internal recommendations and consultation\ncorrespondence are no longer available. The report does not assert that\nthis proves wrongdoing. Rather, it identifies a straightforward\npublic-administration question: *how can the adequacy and evidential basis\nof an irreversible public heritage decision be understood or scrutinised\nwhen the underlying administrative record no longer survives?*\n\nThat seems potentially relevant to the Committee's stated function of\nscrutinising the administration and accountability of Welsh Government and\nassociated public bodies, even if the Committee cannot itself determine\nwhether the 1988 decision was correct.\n\nI am therefore not asking the Committee to investigate the Buckler family's\nhistorical dispute with BP, adjudicate upon the 1988 decision, or direct\nCadw to reverse anything. The specific administrative questions raised in\nthe report are instead:\n\n   1. What record-retention arrangements applied to the 1988 assessment?\n   2. Why do the assessment report, reasons, site notes and consultation\n   records no longer survive?\n   3. Were the records destroyed under an applicable retention policy or\n   other authority, and were they considered for transfer to the appropriate\n   archival institution?\n   4. Is the present position consistent with Welsh Government/Cadw\n   expectations for the preservation of records concerning significant\n   heritage decisions?\n   5. Are current arrangements sufficient to ensure that comparable\n   heritage assessments, particularly where a building is under imminent\n   threat of demolition, are properly documented and retained?\n\nThese are not questions that require the Committee to determine the rights\nof either party to the original dispute. They concern *administrative\npractice, institutional record-keeping and the ability of the Senedd and\nthe public to scrutinise decisions taken by public bodies*. The report\nspecifically asks Cadw to explain the archival position and review its\ncurrent record-keeping practices for this reason.\n\nI would therefore be grateful if the Committee could reconsider whether the\nbroader administrative and accountability issue falls within its scrutiny\nfunction, even if the Committee cannot examine the underlying individual\ndispute.\n\nIf the Committee considers that it nevertheless cannot engage with the\nmatter, I would appreciate clarification as to *which Senedd or Welsh\nGovernment scrutiny, oversight, archival or complaints mechanism is\nconsidered appropriate for raising a documented concern about the absence\nof the administrative record of a historical Cadw decision and the\nimplications this has for public accountability*.\n\nFor completeness, I should also emphasise that the report itself\ndeliberately recognises the limits of the surviving evidence. It does not\nclaim that Cadw acted unlawfully or in bad faith, and it expressly\nacknowledges that the absence of the 1988 working papers means that the\nassessment cannot now be reconstructed definitively.\n\nMy objective is therefore quite limited: *to ensure that the surviving\nevidence is preserved, that the archival gap is properly explained, and\nthat the public record accurately distinguishes what is established from\nwhat can no longer be verified.*\n\nI hope that clarification assists, and I would be grateful if you could\nconfirm whether the Committee is able to consider the matter from that\nwider public-administration and accountability perspective.\n\nKind regards,\n\nRequester\n\n\nOn Tue, 11 Aug 2026, 07:58 Pwyllgor Diwylliant, Cyfathrebu, y Gymraeg a\nChwaraeon - Culture, Communications, Cymraeg and Sport Committee,\n<[email redacted]> wrote:\n\n> Dear Requester,\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you for your correspondence and for sharing the forensic review of\n> Cadw's 1988 assessment of Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough. I\n> acknowledge the strength of feeling expressed in your letter and recognise\n> the longstanding concerns that you and your family have raised.\n>\n>\n>\n> The Committee’s role is to scrutinise the work of the Welsh Government and\n> associated public bodies, including holding ministers 