c ni fydd gohebu yn\n> Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi. ----------------- On leaving the Welsh\n> Government this email was scanned for all known viruses. The Welsh\n> Government takes the protection of your data seriously. If you contact the\n> Welsh Government then our Privacy Notice\n> <https://gov.wales/about/welsh-government-privacy-notice/?lang=en>\n> explains how we use your information and the ways in which we protect your\n> privacy. We welcome receiving correspondence in Welsh. Any correspondence\n> received in Welsh will be answered in Welsh and corresponding in Welsh will\n> not lead to a delay in responding.\n>"},{"id":"gm-19fe010094d164d0","subject":"Please update the status of your request - 1988 Dispossession of Great House Farm, Llandough from the Williams/ Buckler Family","name":"Please update the status of your request - 1988 Dispossession of Great House Farm, Llandough from the Williams/ Buckler Family","date":"2026-08-08T06:29:34Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19fe010094d164d0.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19fe010094d164d0-please-update-the-status-of-your-request-1988-dispossession-of-great-house-farm-llandough-from-the-williams-buckler-fami","scopeTopics":["mod"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["mod"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19fe010094d164d0","direction":"received","from":"(name not shown; whatdotheyknow.com)","institutions":[{"id":"mod","name":"Ministry of Defence"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19fe010094d164d0.json","textStatus":"available","text":"To let everyone know, follow this link and then select the appropriate box.\n\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/profile/sign_in?r=%2Frequest%2F1988_dispossession_of_great_hous%23describe_state_form_1\n\nYour request was called '1988 Dispossession of Great House Farm, Llandough from the Williams/ Buckler Family'. Letting everyone know whether you got the information will help us keep tabs on\nMinistry of Defence.\n\n-- the WhatDoTheyKnow team\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nWhatDoTheyKnow is a project of mySociety run by a small team of staff and\ndedicated volunteers.\n\nmySociety is a charity that believes that information should be accessible to everyone.\nIf this is important to you, please consider donating to help us do more:\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/donate\n\nFind out more about how we handle your data at:\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/help/privacy\n\nmySociety is a registered charity in England and Wales (1076346), and a limited\ncompany (03277032)"},{"id":"gm-19fd74457e974ba4","subject":"RE: Marconi, Great House Farm, Llandough","name":"RE: Marconi, Great House Farm, Llandough","date":"2026-08-06T13:30:01Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19fd74457e974ba4.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19f94ad9e6597d2e-marconi-great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":["postal-museum"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["postal-museum"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19f94ad9e6597d2e","direction":"received","from":"Archives","institutions":[{"id":"postal-museum","name":"The Postal Museum"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19fd74457e974ba4.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester,\n\nFor your question on the date range, I looked at all the files we hold, including before and after the 1897 date. Our records go back to the 17th century and are up to the 21st century. So the dates you mentioned were checked.\n\n2. Catalogue results which were considered unrelated. Would it be possible to provide the catalogue reference numbers and descriptions of those records, even where you consider them unrelated, together with the reason they were excluded?\n\nIt would be a very large list of reference numbers to send you, but you can search those key words on the online catalogue here: https://catalogue.postalmuseum.org/\n\n3. Scope of the catalogue. Could you confirm whether this means that all existing Postal Museum archival holdings, including uncatalogued or incompletely catalogued material, are represented in the system searched, or whether there are categories of physical material which may not yet be represented?\n\nFor what you will find on the online catalogue: you will only find catalogued items. It includes the ones held at BT Archives. You can tell the difference on where the items are held by looking at the 'Held at' line. Here is an example (unrelated to your area of interest):\n\n[cid:image006.png@01DD25AF.B869F840]\n\nOn my system of archival catalogue: we can search catalogued and uncatalogued items. I also searched the uncatalogued items for your keywords, but only unrelated records came up. In addition to that, most uncatalogued items are records which are less than 20 years old. Any file from c.late 1890s or 1900s is already catalogued so you can't miss them.\n\n4. Restricted, closed or unprocessed material. Were any records identified which could potentially relate to the subject but which were unavailable because they were restricted, closed, uncatalogued, unprocessed or otherwise inaccessible?\n\nThe only material that would have restricted access would be records that hold individuals' private information. But considering all the people concerned were alive during the late 1800s and early 1900s, it's very unlikely anyone would still be alive and have any personal information that would still be up-to-date. The other type of records that are restricted are some philatelic items, which don't concern your research.\n\n5. Records transferred to BT Archives. You explained that relevant telecommunications material appearing in the catalogue is held at BT Archives. Could you confirm whether the Postal Museum retains any transfer records, accession records, finding aids or other information identifying the particular GPO/Telegraph/Engineering records which were transferred to BT Archives? If there are catalogue references which would help BT Archives locate the underlying material, I would be very grateful for those.\n\nWe don't have any transfer records any longer, it's been too long ago since the records were transferred to BT Archives. But you can easily find out which items are held at BT Archives when searching our online catalogue. See above the screenshot. Using the Finding Number mentioned on one of our online catalogue entries, you should be able to trace the item by contacting BT Archives directly. They are the only ones who have the most up-to-date information about the archival items that were transferred to them.\n\n6. The Williams connection\n\nI'm afraid that we don't hold any record that aren't about postal employees. So we wouldn't be able to find anything about this individual in our records. If there is any record of him in the records held at BT Archives, it's not mentioned in any of the file's descriptions.\n\n7. Preparatory and follow-up records\n\nConcerning all the terms you listed, the relevant records would be kept at BT Archives, so I can't know more about it.\n\nWe have some books about Marconi in our reference library, but they are books destined to a general audience and you could find those books in most libraries. It's likely you are already familiar with the titles! Here is the list of books we hold, Online catalogue search 'Marconi lib*'<https://catalogue.postalmuseum.org/collections/search?s=marconi+lib*&qa%5Bkeyword_reference_type%5D=0&qa%5Bpartner%5D=&qa%5Bidentifier%5D=&qa%5Bdate_from%5D=&qa%5Bdate_to%5D=&qa%5Btitle%5D=&qa%5Bperson%5D=&qa%5Bplace%5D=&qa%5Bsubject%5D=&qa%5Bformat%5D=&cbpt=0&cbav=2&cbadvsearchquery=>\n\nI hope those answers will help you in your future research.\n\nBest regards,\n\nMathilde\nMathilde Jourdan\nArchives Assistant\nThe Postal Museum | 15-20 Phoenix Place | London [postcode redacted]\nT: [phone redacted] | Archive enquiries: [phone redacted]  | E:[email redacted]<[email redacted]>\n[Facebook Icon - click to open a new browser window showing the Postal Museum Facebook channel]<https://www.facebook.com/thepostalmuseum/>[Twitter Icon - click to open a new browser window showing the Postal Museum Twitter channel]<https://twitter.com/thepostalmuseum/>[Instgram Icon - click to open a new browser window showing the Postal Museum Instragram channel]<https://www.instagram.com/thepostalmuseum/>[Youtube Icon - click to open a new browser window showing the Postal Museum Youtube channel]<https://www.youtube.com/thepostalmuseum>\n\n[cid:image005.png@01DD25AD.E041C700]\nThe Postal Museum is the public identity of the Postal Heritage Trust. Registered as a charity in England and Wales. Registered Charity Number 1102360. Company Number 4896056\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]>\nSent: 31 July 2026 17:27\nTo: Archives <[email redacted]>\nSubject: Re: Marconi, Great House Farm, Llandough\n\n\nMathilde,\n\nThank you for clarifying the search methodology. Your explanation is helpful, particularly the distinction between records appearing in the Postal Museum catalogue and records physically held at BT Archives.\n\nBefore I proceed to BT Archives, I would like to make sure I understand the limits of the search correctly and avoid unnecessarily duplicating it.\n\nThere are a few further points which I would be grateful if you could clarify.\n\n1. Date range\n\nWas the search for the various names and locations conducted across the surrounding period, or was material effectively assessed only against the 1897 demonstration?\n\nCould you confirm whether 1895-1900, including 1896, 1898, 1899 and 1900, was searched?\n\nThis is important because I am investigating not only the demonstration itself but also possible preparations beforehand and correspondence or reports afterwards.\n\n2. Catalogue results which were considered unrelated\n\nYou mentioned that some results appeared for terms including Penarth, wireless demonstrations, GPO Telegraph Department, Engineering Department and Williams, but that they were considered unrelated after examination of their catalogue descriptions.\n\nWould it be possible to provide the catalogue reference numbers and descriptions of those records, even where you consider them unrelated, together with the reason they were excluded?\n\nI would prefer to examine those references myself rather than inadvertently overlooking something that may become relevant when considered alongside other records.\n\n3. Scope of the catalogue\n\nYou have explained that the physical archives were not separately checked because the information is recorded on the archival cataloguing system.\n\nCould you confirm whether this means that all existing Postal Museum archival holdings, including uncatalogued or incompletely catalogued material, are represented in the system searched, or whether there are categories of physical material which may not yet be represented?\n\n4. Restricted, closed or unprocessed material\n\nWere any records identified which could potentially relate to the subject but which were unavailable because they were restricted, closed, uncatalogued, unprocessed or otherwise inaccessible?\n\nIf so, I would be grateful for the relevant reference numbers and any applicable access restrictions.\n\n5. Records transferred to BT Archives\n\nYou explained that relevant telecommunications material appearing in the catalogue is held at BT Archives.\n\nCould you confirm whether the Postal Museum retains any transfer records, accession records, finding aids or other information identifying the particular GPO/Telegraph/Engineering records which were transferred to BT Archives?\n\nIf there are catalogue references which would help BT Archives locate the underlying material, I would be very grateful for those.\n\n6. The Williams connection\n\nThe purpose of the search for Williams is not to establish that Mr Williams was a Post Office employee.\n\nThe family tradition concerns a local Mr Williams of Great House Farm, Llandough near Penarth, who may have assisted Marconi locally. Consequently, a relevant record might describe him as a local resident, landowner, farmer, host, assistant, carrier, messenger, proprietor or other private individual rather than as Post Office personnel.\n\nCould you therefore confirm whether the search for Williams included references of this kind, rather than only Post Office employees or personnel records?\n\nSimilarly, were variants such as Mr Williams, W. Williams, William Williams and Williams considered?\n\n7. Preparatory and follow-up records\n\nCould the search also be considered in terms of records concerning:\n\n  *   preparations for the South Wales experiments;\n  *   arrangements made with local residents or property owners;\n  *   visits to or use of private premises;\n  *   transport or movement of equipment;\n  *   correspondence immediately before or after the experiments;\n  *   follow-up reports;\n  *   subsequent demonstrations or experiments in the area;\n  *   photographs, plans, maps or other material which might identify locations or participants.\n\nI appreciate that you may ultimately conclude that none of these are held by the Postal Museum. I am simply trying to establish precisely what evidence has and has not survived, and where the relevant archival trail now leads.\n\nFinally, if the Postal Museum has any contemporary publications, newspaper-cutting collections, photographs, finding aids or other material concerning the South Wales Marconi experiments, even where they do not specifically mention Great House Farm or Mr Williams, I would be grateful for those references as well.\n\nThank you again for your assistance. Your clarification has helped considerably in narrowing the search, and I appreciate your taking the time to establish exactly what the Postal Museum catalogue can and cannot tell me.\n\nKind regards,\n\nRequester  \n\nOn Fri, 31 Jul 2026, 11:04 Archives, <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>> wrote:\nDear Requester,\n\nI searched our archive's catalogued, for all catalogued and uncatalogued items, using the following keywords:\n\n  *   Great House Farm\n  *   Llandough\n  *   Lavernock\nResult: no item is held in our archives using these words in the description.\nConcerning the below keywords:\n\n  *   Penarth\n  *   wireless demonstrations\n  *   GPO Telegraph Department\n  *   Engineering Department\n  *   Williams / Mr Williams\nResult: Some items held in our archives came up with those keywords, but when reading the description field on our catalogue, they turned out to be unrelated to the 1897 wireless telegraphy demonstration. Most were dated to before or after 1897. Many came up on our online catalogue, but they are held at BT Archives, therefore, we won't have any more information. This is what I meant by 'nothing relevant came up' - despite corresponding keyword, the dates or the content aren't related to your enquiry.\nThe physical archives were not checked as all information on our files are recorded on our archival cataloguing system, which is what I used to search information and keyword. Because the few records that seemed relevant are held at BT Archives, only they can inform you of the digitised or non-digitised status of the items.\nAlthough the items held at BT Archives come up in our archival cataloguing system, you would need to contact directly for more information.\nFor your questions in 4. On the link with Mr Williams, nothing came up about people who participated to the demonstrations.\nYou can search our online catalogue here: https://catalogue.postalmuseum.org/\nAnd here is the link to the catalogue of BT Archives: https://www.digitalarchives.bt.com/Calmview/\nI hope this helps,\nBest regards,\nMathilde\nMathilde Jourdan\nArchives Assistant\nThe Postal Museum | 15-20 Phoenix Place | London [postcode redacted][map link redacted]\nT: [phone redacted] | Archive enquiries: [phone redacted]  | E:[email redacted]<[email redacted]>\n[Facebook Icon - click to open a new browser window showing the Postal Museum Facebook channel]<https://www.facebook.com/thepostalmuseum/>[Twitter Icon - click to open a new browser window showing the Postal Museum Twitter channel]<https://twitter.com/thepostalmuseum/>[Instgram Icon - click to open a new browser window showing the Postal Museum Instragram channel]<https://www.instagram.com/thepostalmuseum/>[Youtube Icon - click to open a new browser window showing the Postal Museum Youtube channel]<https://www.youtube.com/thepostalmuseum>\n\n[cid:image005.png@01DD25AD.E041C700]\nThe Postal Museum is the public identity of the Postal Heritage Trust. Registered as a charity in England and Wales. Registered Charity Number 1102360. Company Number 4896056\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSent: 29 July 2026 14:41\nTo: Archives <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSubject: Re: Marconi, Great House Farm, Llandough\n\n\nDear Mathilde,\n\nThank you for your further reply and for carrying out the searches.\n\nI appreciate your assistance, but I would like to clarify the scope and conclusions of the search, as several points remain unresolved.\n\nFirstly, I should correct one detail: the individual I am researching is Mr Williams of Great House Farm, Llandough near Penarth, not \"Mr William\". The surname is significant because family history suggests that my great-grandfather, Mr Williams of Great House Farm, assisted Guglielmo Marconi during the 1897 South Wales wireless experiments.\n\nI fully understand that the absence of a result within the Postal Museum catalogue does not necessarily mean that no such person or record existed. However, I would be grateful if you could clarify the following:\n\n  1.  Search methodology\n\nCould you confirm exactly which catalogue collections, series, or record groups were searched for:\n\n  *   Great House Farm, Llandough (Penarth)\n  *   Llandough\n  *   Lavernock\n  *   Marconi\n  *   wireless telegraphy demonstrations (1897)\n  *   GPO Telegraph Department\n  *   Engineering Department\n  *   Williams / Mr Williams\n\n  1.  Scope of the negative result\n\nWhen you state that \"nothing relevant came up\", does this mean:\n\n  *   no indexed catalogue entries were found in Postal Museum collections;\n  *   no digitised records were found;\n  *   or that physical archival holdings were also checked?\n\n  1.  Records relating to GPO personnel\n\nYou mentioned that most Marconi and Telegraph Department records are held at BT Archives. Could you confirm whether the Postal Museum holds any catalogue references, finding aids, or transferred-record information indicating where the relevant GPO engineering correspondence, reports, notebooks, or staff records may have been moved?\n\n  1.  The Williams connection\n\nThe question I am trying to establish is not necessarily whether Mr Williams was a Post Office employee. He may instead have been the owner/occupier of Great House Farm who assisted Marconi locally.\n\nCould you therefore confirm whether your search included references to:\n\n  *   private individuals assisting demonstrations;\n  *   hosts or local contacts connected with Marconi's experiments;\n  *   correspondence mentioning residents of Llandough, Penarth, or Lavernock?\n\nI appreciate that the absence of a catalogue entry cannot prove that an event or person did not exist, particularly where local residents or private landowners were involved.\n\nMy aim is simply to establish the evidential trail: whether the Mr Williams mentioned in contemporary accounts can be identified, and whether surviving records connect him with Great House Farm and Marconi's experiments.\n\nThank you again for your assistance. I would be grateful for any further clarification on the above points before I proceed to BT Archives and Glamorgan Archives.\n\nKind regards,\n\nRequester\n\nOn Wed, 29 Jul 2026, 10:32 Archives, <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>> wrote:\nDear Requester,\n\nI searched for all files related to the key words Great House Farm, Llandough, Lavernock, wireless demonstration. Nothing relevant came up. For Marconi and the Telegraph Departments, all the files would be held at BT Archives.\n\nI hope this helps,\n\nBest regards,\n\nMathilde\n\nMathilde Jourdan\nArchives Assistant\nThe Postal Museum | 15-20 Phoenix Place | London [postcode redacted][map link redacted]\nT: [phone redacted] | Archive enquiries: [phone redacted]  | E:[email redacted]<[email redacted]>\n[Facebook Icon - click to open a new browser window showing the Postal Museum Facebook channel]<https://www.facebook.com/thepostalmuseum/>[Twitter Icon - click to open a new browser window showing the Postal Museum Twitter channel]<https://twitter.com/thepostalmuseum/>[Instgram Icon - click to open a new browser window showing the Postal Museum Instragram channel]<https://www.instagram.com/thepostalmuseum/>[Youtube Icon - click to open a new browser window showing the Postal Museum Youtube channel]<https://www.youtube.com/thepostalmuseum>\n\n[cid:image005.png@01DD25AD.E041C700]\nThe Postal Museum is the public identity of the Postal Heritage Trust. Registered as a charity in England and Wales. Registered Charity Number 1102360. Company Number 4896056\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSent: 29 July 2026 14:04\nTo: Archives <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSubject: Re: Marconi, Great House Farm, Llandough\n\n\nDear Mathilde,\n\nThank you very much for taking the time to carry out these searches and for your detailed response.\n\nI appreciate the clarification regarding the distinction between Great House Farm, Llandough near Penarth and the similarly named location near Cowbridge. The Penarth/Llandough/Lavernock area is the location of interest.\n\nYour advice regarding BT Archives is very helpful, and I will follow this up with them, as the surviving GPO Telegraph and wireless records appear likely to be held there.\n\nI am also grateful for the information about the Post Office family history records. My main interest is not that Mr Williams was necessarily a Post Office employee, but whether the individual mentioned in contemporary accounts as assisting Marconi at Great House Farm can be identified. Family history passed down through my great grandfather's line suggests that a Mr Williams of Great House Farm assisted Marconi during his South Wales experiments, and I am trying to establish whether this can be connected to my ancestor.\n\nIf possible, could you let me know whether the search you carried out included any of the following terms or collections, as this may help me understand the scope of the search:\n\n  *   Great House Farm (Llandough/Penarth)\n  *   Llandough\n  *   Lavernock\n  *   Marconi\n  *   wireless telegraphy demonstrations (1897)\n  *   GPO Telegraph Department / Engineering Department\n\nI will also contact Glamorgan Archives, as you suggest, since local newspapers, estate records, or correspondence may contain information not held in the national collections.\n\nThank you again for your assistance. Your guidance has helped narrow down where the relevant evidence is most likely to survive.\n\nKind regards,\n\nRequester\n\nOn Wed, 29 Jul 2026, 07:31 Archives, <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>> wrote:\nDear Requester,\n\nThank you for your enquiry. I will do my best to check if we have surviving records of this demonstration, although most of the records about telecommunications were transferred to BT Archives so they might have more information than us: https://www.bt.com/about/bt/our-history/bt-archives\n\n  *   Great House Farm, Llandough, near Penarth, Cardiff (not Great House Farm, Llandough near Cowbridge)\nI couldn't find any record mentioning Great House Farm, Llandough. For Penarth, there were some results but they were all dated much later than the demonstration. We have many records on Cardiff, which you can find on our online catalogue Here, but nothing relevant seemed to have come up.\n\n  *   Llandough, Penarth, Lavernock and the surrounding wireless demonstration sites.\nNothing came up for Lavernock either, the locations are too precise for this type of records to have reached our archives. I searched for 'wireless demonstration' and POST 88 Series titled 'Post Office: Wireless Telegraphy and Telephony' mentions such events, but all the files are kept in BT Archives.\n\n  *   Guglielmo Marconi's 1897 demonstrations.\nAlmost all the files related to Marconi are held in BT Archives.\n\n  *   GPO Telegraph or Engineering Department records concerning these demonstrations.\nSee above.\n\n  *   Correspondence, reports, notebooks, diaries, staff lists, expense claims, photographs or plans relating to engineers or officials present.\nSee above.\n\n  *   Any references to Mr Williams of Great House Farm, Llandough, who family history suggests assisted Marconi before, during or after his stay at the property.\nI couldn't find any specific file about a Mr William from Great House Farm, but the main records we have that are useful for family history research are the Appointment Books, and the pension and gratuity records. We also have Establishment Books and staff magazines that can be helpful too. You can find more information about what we hold here:  https://www.postalmuseum.org/collections/the-archive/family-history/  Please bear in mind that most villages, small towns, hamlets, etc. such as Llandough had business that ran a postal service, and these people were not directly employed by the Post Office, therefore, we won't have any information. If Mr Williams had another business, he was likely not employed by the Post Office. A local archive might hold newspaper cuttings or other information about this person: https://glamarchives.gov.uk/\nYou are welcome to visit our archives, but I believe most of the records and information you are looking for will be held at BT Archives. You can contact them directly to organise an appointment.\n\nI hope this helps,\n\nBest regards,\n\nMathilde\n\nMathilde Jourdan\nArchives Assistant\nThe Postal Museum | 15-20 Phoenix Place | London [postcode redacted][map link redacted]\nT: [phone redacted] | Archive enquiries: [phone redacted]  | E:[email redacted]<[email redacted]>\n[Facebook Icon - click to open a new browser window showing the Postal Museum Facebook channel]<https://www.facebook.com/thepostalmuseum/>[Twitter Icon - click to open a new browser window showing the Postal Museum Twitter channel]<https://twitter.com/thepostalmuseum/>[Instgram Icon - click to open a new browser window showing the Postal Museum Instragram channel]<https://www.instagram.com/thepostalmuseum/>[Youtube Icon - click to open a new browser window showing the Postal Museum Youtube channel]<https://www.youtube.com/thepostalmuseum>\n\n[cid:image005.png@01DD25AD.E041C700]\nThe Postal Museum is the public identity of the Postal Heritage Trust. Registered as a charity in England and Wales. Registered Charity Number 1102360. Company Number 4896056\n\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSent: 24 July 2026 16:17\nTo: Booking Support Team <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSubject: Marconi, Great House Farm, Llandough\n\nYou don't often get email from [email redacted]<[email redacted]>. Learn why this is important<https://aka.ms/LearnAboutSenderIdentification>\n\nDear Archive Team,\n\nI am conducting historical research into the General Post Office's involvement in Guglielmo Marconi's wireless demonstrations in South Wales during 1897.\n\nPost Office Ltd has advised that your archive is the most likely repository for surviving historic GPO records relating to this period.\n\nI would be grateful if you could search for any records relating to:\n\n  *   Great House Farm, Llandough, near Penarth, Cardiff (not Great House Farm, Llandough near Cowbridge).\n  *   Llandough, Penarth, Lavernock and the surrounding wireless demonstration sites.\n  *   Guglielmo Marconi's 1897 demonstrations.\n  *   GPO Telegraph or Engineering Department records concerning these demonstrations.\n  *   Correspondence, reports, notebooks, diaries, staff lists, expense claims, photographs or plans relating to engineers or officials present.\n  *   Any references to Mr Williams of Great House Farm, Llandough, who family history suggests assisted Marconi before, during or after his stay at the property.\n\nIf records relating specifically to Mr Williams cannot be located, I would also appreciate details of any records identifying GPO personnel, visitors or local residents connected with the demonstrations, as these may help establish the historical context.\n\nIf another archive holds any of these records, I would be grateful if you could advise where I should direct my enquiry.\n\nMr Williams of Great House Farm was my great grandad. I am trying to ascertain if the Mr Williams post office worker, mentioned helping Marconi in the article, was the same man as my great grandfather. I am also looking for any more connections between my family, my former home and Marconi and the radio experiments at lavernock point. Which I understand were already underway before Marconi arrived to South Wales.\n\nThank you for your time and assistance. I look forward to your response.\n\nKind regards,\n\n\n\nRequester\n\n"},{"id":"gm-19fd345e1123a2b4","subject":"Re: Great House Farm, Llandough, Cardiff","name":"Re: Great House Farm, Llandough, Cardiff","date":"2026-08-05T18:53:19Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19fd345e1123a2b4.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19fa4b5a70ee940a-great-house-farm-llandough-cardiff","scopeTopics":["glamorgan"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["glamorgan"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19fa4b5a70ee940a","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"glamorgan","name":"Glamorgan Archives"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19fd345e1123a2b4.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Diolch Rhian,\n\nI'm currently at the Welsh Settlement in South Argentina, otherwise I'd be\nthere myself. So for our private study and research of our family land,\nformer home and history, we require photos be permitted to be taken, of\nartifacts of interest, so they may be sent to me here.\n\nI'm attempting to reconstruct a replica of our old 800 year old farm house\nhere as a museum/ open house. We're also trying to understand the\nunresolved and ongoing ownership dispute between our family and BP, who\nwere awarded possession of our farm and home since 1667, which resulted in\nus being dispossessed of our ancestral birthland as children in 1988. It's\nall a very bizzar case.\n\nThe MP for Cardiff covering Llandough responded yesterday and is now aware\nof the queries and concerns I'm raising with the case and we appreciate\nyour cooperation and service and thank you in advance. Subject to me\nviewing the records, it's possible I may be warranted in proceeding with a\nSenedd public enquiry request, as this could be a matter of public\ninterest.\n\nNo single centralised chronology with all supporting evidence appears to\nexist for our land, family and former home and it's history and herritage.\nAnd so this is the purposeful and meaningful work I have chosen to\nundertake.\n\nFascinately world champion boxer Peerless Jim fought his first fight in our\nbarns. Marconi stayed with us while inventing radio. The house was a court\nhouse and stood on a Roman villa and monestry. It's a trove of hidden\nhistory.\n\nKind regards,\n\nRequester \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nOn Wed, 5 Aug 2026, 09:34 Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg, <\n[email redacted]> wrote:\n\n> Dear Requester.\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you for your email.\n>\n>\n>\n> Yes, we have a space reserved in our searchroom tomorrow for Mary\n> Buckler.  The list of documents supplied to us is with the searchroom team.\n>\n>\n>\n> We look forward to seeing Mary Buckler tomorrow.\n>\n>\n>\n> Your sincerely,\n>\n>\n>\n> Rhian Diggins\n>\n> Uwch Archifydd / Senior Archivist\n>\n>\n>\n> Archifau Morgannwg / Glamorgan Archives\n>\n> [phone redacted]\n>\n> [email redacted]\n>\n> https://glamarchives.gov.uk/\n>\n>\n>\n> *From:* Requester <[email redacted]>\n> *Sent:* 05 August 2026 11:58\n> *To:* Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg <[email redacted]>\n> *Subject:* Re: Great House Farm, Llandough, Cardiff\n>\n>\n>\n>  *EXTERNAL:* This email originated from outside Cardiff Council, take\n> care when clicking links.\n>  *ALLANOL:* Daw'r e-bost hwn o'r tu allan i Gyngor Caerdydd, cymerwch\n> ofal wrth glicio ar ddolenni.\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> Dear Ms Diggins,\n>\n> Thank you for your email.\n>\n> I realise that I made a mistake in my previous email. I inadvertently\n> referred to records \"held by the National Library of Wales\", when I\n> intended to enquire about material held by Glamorgan Archives.\n>\n> A member of my family has scheduled a visit to the Search Room tomorrow to\n> research Great House Farm (Ty Mawr/Cedfin/Cydfin), Llandough, and our\n> Williams and Buckler family history. If possible, I would be very grateful\n> if the following material could be prepared in advance.\n>\n> I appreciate that some references below may relate to connected\n> collections or require confirmation of the correct repository. Where a\n> reference is not held by Glamorgan Archives, we would be grateful if you\n> could advise whether you hold any related material, copies,\n> cross-references or catalogue information, or direct us to the appropriate\n> repository.\n>\n> The records requested are as follows:\n>\n>    - DB/E/1–2 – David Stewart survey of the Marquess of Bute's Glamorgan\n>    estates, including Great House Farm alias Cedfin, Llandough, together with\n>    any related maps or accompanying material.\n>    - D160/1–4 – Agreements between Lambert Williams and the Bute Estate\n>    (1818–1835).\n>    - Any copies, related material or cross-references connected with NLW\n>    D219 (Llandough manorial leases and agreements, including Ty Mawr/Cydfin\n>    references).\n>    - D153/4–8 – Records relating to the 1876 severance of land formerly\n>    part of Great House Farm for Llandough Limeworks, including associated\n>    plans.\n>    - DSA/6/701 – Parts of the Llandough Estate, 9 October 1924.\n>    - DSA/6/1158 – Great House Farm, Llandough, 15 November 1938,\n>    including sale particulars, plans and associated papers.\n>    - GD/LA/15/255 – Great House Farm sale particulars (1938), if held.\n>    - DXJF/64/27–32, including DXJF/64/30–32 – Nancy Buckler / Nancy\n>    Buckler Williams sketch and notes of the former layout of Llandough.\n>    - DCOWHS material containing references to the Buckler family.\n>    - DBDT catalogue reference relating to the July 1877 counterpart lease\n>    from the Trustees of the late Marquess of Bute to John Williams, if held.\n>    - Cardiff Rural District Council records relating to Great House Farm\n>    or Ty Mawr.\n>    - CVG/C/1/52.\n>