itutions":[{"id":"museum","name":"Amgueddfa Cymru - Museum Wales"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19fa4156c4788452.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester\n\nThank you for your Freedom of Information request, received on 27th July.\n\nWe are processing your request under the Freedom of Information Act 2000. The statutory deadline for responding is 20 working days from the date of receipt. We therefore aim to provide a response by 24th August 2026.\n\nIf we require any clarification in order to process your request, we will contact you.\n\nCofion cynnes,\n\n[A picture containing text, tableware, plate, dishware  Description automatically generated]\nFOI Team\n[cid:image002.png@01DD1DE0.B74E6170]\n[email redacted]<[email redacted]>\namgueddfa.cymru<applewebdata://288FE56C-5BFC-474C-ADB3-A1017EF25E69/amgueddfa.cymru> | museum.wales<applewebdata://288FE56C-5BFC-474C-ADB3-A1017EF25E69/museum.wales>\n\nRhif elusen | Charity number 525774\n\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]>\nSent: 27 July 2026 02:22\nTo: FOI Requests <[email redacted]>\nSubject: ALLANOL/EXTERNAL - FOI - Great House Farm, Llandough\n\n\nThis email comes from outside the organisation.\n\nDo not click links or open attachments unless it is an email you expected to receive.\nYou don't often get email from [email redacted]<[email redacted]>. Learn why this is important<https://aka.ms/LearnAboutSenderIdentification>\nDear Freedom of Information Team,\n\nI am writing to request information under the Freedom of Information Act 2000 and, where applicable, the Environmental Information Regulations 2004.\n\nA published local history account states that, during works to replace the stone floor of the dining room at Great House Farm (also known as Ty Mawr), Llandough, Cardiff, the remains of a soldier and his horse were discovered beneath the floor.\n\n The remains were subsequently re-buried in the churchyard, and the soldier's visor and lance were given to the National Museum of Wales (Amgueddfa Cymru).\n\nI therefore request the following recorded information held by Amgueddfa Cymru:\n\nAny records relating to the acquisition, accession, donation, transfer or deposit of a medieval visor, lance, helmet, or other items of armour associated with Great House Farm / Ty Mawr / Llandough.\n\nThe accession number(s), date of acquisition or transfer, name of the donor or transferring body, and any provenance or discovery notes linked to these objects.\n\nAny catalogue entries, object records, or internal correspondence concerning the above items.\n\nAny records of associated human or animal remains from the same discovery, including notes on reburial.\n\nAny related excavation, watching-brief, or discovery reports held by the Museum.\n\nIf any of the requested information is already publicly available (for example via the Museum's online collections database), I would be grateful if you could simply provide the relevant accession numbers or catalogue links.\n\nPlease treat this as a request for recorded information only. I am not seeking advice or opinion.\n\nI look forward to your response within the statutory timescale.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\nRequester \n[cid:image003.jpg@01DD1DE0.B74E6170]"},{"id":"gm-19fa129d5aaa48b2","subject":"FOI - Great House Farm, Llandough","name":"FOI - Great House Farm, Llandough","date":"2026-07-27T01:21:40Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19fa129d5aaa48b2.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19fa128887d5cab6-foi-great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":["museum"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["museum"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19fa128887d5cab6","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"museum","name":"Amgueddfa Cymru - Museum Wales"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19fa129d5aaa48b2.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Freedom of Information Team,\n\nI am writing to request information under the Freedom of Information Act\n2000 and, where applicable, the Environmental Information Regulations 2004.\n\nA published local history account states that, during works to replace the\nstone floor of the dining room at Great House Farm (also known as Ty Mawr),\nLlandough, Cardiff, the remains of a soldier and his horse were discovered\nbeneath the floor.\n\n The remains were subsequently re-buried in the churchyard, and the\nsoldier’s visor and lance were given to the National Museum of Wales\n(Amgueddfa Cymru).\n\nI therefore request the following recorded information held by Amgueddfa\nCymru:\n\nAny records relating to the acquisition, accession, donation, transfer or\ndeposit of a medieval visor, lance, helmet, or other items of armour\nassociated with Great House Farm / Ty Mawr / Llandough.\n\nThe accession number(s), date of acquisition or transfer, name of the donor\nor transferring body, and any provenance or discovery notes linked to these\nobjects.\n\nAny catalogue entries, object records, or internal correspondence\nconcerning the above items.\n\nAny records of associated human or animal remains from the same discovery,\nincluding notes on reburial.\n\nAny related excavation, watching-brief, or discovery reports held by the\nMuseum.\n\nIf any of the requested information is already publicly available (for\nexample via the Museum’s online collections database), I would be grateful\nif you could simply provide the relevant accession numbers or catalogue\nlinks.\n\nPlease treat this as a request for recorded information only. I am not\nseeking advice or opinion.\n\nI look forward to your response within the statutory timescale.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\nRequester "},{"id":"gm-19f94b41e012a821","subject":"Automatic reply: Marconi, Great House Farm, Llandough","name":"Automatic reply: Marconi, Great House Farm, Llandough","date":"2026-07-24T15:17:37Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19f94b41e012a821.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19f94b41e012a821-automatic-reply-marconi-great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":["postal-museum"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["postal-museum"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19f94b41e012a821","direction":"received","from":"Booking Support Team","institutions":[{"id":"postal-museum","name":"The Postal Museum"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19f94b41e012a821.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Thank you for getting in touch.\n\n\n\nIf you would like to book tickets or find out more information about visits, please visit our website: https://www.postalmuseum.org/visit-us/\n\n\n\nOur Booking Support team operates Monday - Saturday 9:00 - 17:00 and aims to respond to your email within 3 working days.\n\n\nKind regards,\n\nBooking Support team"},{"id":"gm-19f94b3c94c67f78","subject":"Marconi, Great House Farm, Llandough","name":"Marconi, Great House Farm, Llandough","date":"2026-07-24T15:17:17Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19f94b3c94c67f78.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19f94ad9e6597d2e-marconi-great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":["postal-museum"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["postal-museum"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19f94ad9e6597d2e","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"postal-museum","name":"The Postal Museum"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19f94b3c94c67f78.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Archive Team,\n\nI am conducting historical research into the General Post Office's\ninvolvement in Guglielmo Marconi's wireless demonstrations in South Wales\nduring 1897.\n\nPost Office Ltd has advised that your archive is the most likely repository\nfor surviving historic GPO records relating to this period.\n\nI would be grateful if you could search for any records relating to:\n\n   - Great House Farm, Llandough, near Penarth, Cardiff (not Great House\n   Farm, Llandough near Cowbridge).\n   - Llandough, Penarth, Lavernock and the surrounding wireless\n   demonstration sites.\n   - Guglielmo Marconi's 1897 demonstrations.\n   - GPO Telegraph or Engineering Department records concerning these\n   demonstrations.\n   - Correspondence, reports, notebooks, diaries, staff lists, expense\n   claims, photographs or plans relating to engineers or officials present.\n   - Any references to Mr Williams of Great House Farm, Llandough, who\n   family history suggests assisted Marconi before, during or after his stay\n   at the property.\n\nIf records relating specifically to Mr Williams cannot be located, I would\nalso appreciate details of any records identifying GPO personnel, visitors\nor local residents connected with the demonstrations, as these may help\nestablish the historical context.\n\nIf another archive holds any of these records, I would be grateful if you\ncould advise where I should direct my enquiry.\n\nMr Williams of Great House Farm was my great grandad. I am trying to\nascertain if the Mr Williams post office worker, mentioned helping Marconi\nin the article, was the same man as my great grandfather. I am also looking\nfor any more connections between my family, my former home and Marconi and\nthe radio experiments at lavernock point. Which I understand were already\nunderway before Marconi arrived to South Wales.\n\nThank you for your time and assistance. I look forward to your response.\n\nKind regards,\n\n\nRequester\n\n"},{"id":"gm-19f930e4c9ae3af6","subject":"RE: Freedom of Information / Historical Records Request - Mr. Williams, Cardiff Post Office Engineering Department, 1897 Marconi Lavernock Experiments","name":"RE: Freedom of Information / Historical Records Request - Mr. Williams, Cardiff Post Office Engineering Department, 1897 Marconi Lavernock Experiments","date":"2026-07-24T07:36:48Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19f930e4c9ae3af6.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19f682ed8be4fae0-freedom-of-information-historical-records-request-mr-williams-cardiff-post-office-engineering-department-1897-marconi-la","scopeTopics":["glamorgan"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["glamorgan"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19f682ed8be4fae0","direction":"received","from":"Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg","institutions":[{"id":"glamorgan","name":"Glamorgan Archives"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19f930e4c9ae3af6.json","textStatus":"available","text":"2026/3217\n\nDear Requester.\n\nThank you for your email, received into our post system on 23 July 2026.\n\nI can confirm that all the terms listed in your email have been used to search our catalogue.\n\nYou are welcome to replicate these searches yourself on our catalogue https://canfod.glamarchives.gov.uk/en/\n\nWe conducted our searches using the staff interface to our collections management system, which searches across our catalogue and our accessions database, which is in turn linked to our depositors database. It will therefore pick up any material received, but as yet uncatalogued, which could potentially be relevant.  This system includes all our collections including deposits from Cardiff Corporation, Cardiff Council, local organisations, engineering companies, and private estates.  As I noted, no relevant results were returned.\n\nOur searches were conducted using free text search of our descriptions. We do not have a subject index to our holdings.  We do not hold any other finding aids apart from our catalogue.\n\nWe do not have any record of any potentially relevant material having been transferred elsewhere.\n\nI hope this clarifies the situation for you.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nRhian Diggins [cid:image001.jpg@01DD1B47.8FCBD0B0]\nUwch Archifydd / Senior Archivist\n\nArchifau Morgannwg / Glamorgan Archives\n[phone redacted]\n[email redacted]<[email redacted]>\nhttps://glamarchives.gov.uk/\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]>\nSent: 22 July 2026 18:23\nTo: Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg <[email redacted]>\nSubject: Re: Freedom of Information / Historical Records Request - Mr. Williams, Cardiff Post Office Engineering Department, 1897 Marconi Lavernock Experiments\n\nEXTERNAL: This email originated from outside Cardiff Council, take care when clicking links.\nALLANOL: Daw'r e-bost hwn o'r tu allan i Gyngor Caerdydd, cymerwch ofal wrth glicio ar ddolenni.\n\n\nMs Diggins,\n\nThank you for your email and for clarifying that Glamorgan Archives does not hold records relating to engineering activities at Cardiff Post Office, Marconi's experiments at Lavernock Point, or connections between the Cardiff Post Office engineering team, Mr Williams and those events.\n\nHowever, I would be grateful for some further clarification regarding the searches undertaken, as I am trying to establish the extent of the records searched rather than simply whether records were found.\n\nYour response states that you searched \"our catalogue\". Could you please clarify:\n\n  1.  Does \"our catalogue\" refer only to publicly catalogued collections, or were uncatalogued, partially catalogued and recently accessioned collections also considered?\n  2.  Which search terms were used? For example, were searches undertaken for \"Marconi\", \"Lavernock\", \"Flat Holm\", \"Cardiff Post Office\", \"General Post Office\", \"wireless\", \"telegraph\", \"Williams\", \"Great House Farm\", \"Ty Mawr\", \"Tŷ Mawr\", \"Llandough\" and any obvious variants?\n  3.  Were deposited collections from Cardiff Corporation, Cardiff Council, local organisations, engineering companies, private estates, or other archival deposits searched where relevant?\n  4.  Were photographic collections, maps, correspondence files, accession records, depositor files, subject indexes or other finding aids examined, or was the search limited to catalogue descriptions?\n  5.  Are there any uncatalogued, restricted or unindexed collections that were not searched as part of responding to my enquiry?\n  6.  Has Glamorgan Archives ever held records within the scope of my request that have since been transferred to another repository, returned to a depositor or otherwise disposed of?\n\nI appreciate that an archive can only confirm what it currently holds. My intention is simply to understand the scope of the searches undertaken so that I can direct my research to the most appropriate repository if the material is not held by Glamorgan Archives.\n\nThank you for your time and assistance. I look forward to your clarification.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\n\n\nRequester \n\n\n\nOn Wed, 22 Jul 2026, 09:44 Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg, <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>> wrote:\n2026/3194\nDear Requester.\nThank you for your email.\nI have searched our catalogue and I can confirm that Glamorgan Archives does not hold any records relating to engineering activities at Cardiff Post Office, nor to Marconi’s experiments at Lavernock Point, or any material concerning connections between the Post Office’s engineering team / Mr Williams and these events.\nI’m sorry that I failed to clarify this fully in my previous email.\nYours sincerely,\nRhian Diggins [cid:image001.jpg@01DD1B47.8FCBD0B0]\nUwch Archifydd / Senior Archivist\nArchifau Morgannwg / Glamorgan Archives\n[phone redacted]\n[email redacted]<[email redacted]>\nhttps://glamarchives.gov.uk/\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSent: 21 July 2026 17:17\nTo: Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSubject: Re: Freedom of Information / Historical Records Request - Mr. Williams, Cardiff Post Office Engineering Department, 1897 Marconi Lavernock Experiments\nEXTERNAL: This email originated from outside Cardiff Council, take care when clicking links.\nALLANOL: Daw'r e-bost hwn o'r tu allan i Gyngor Caerdydd, cymerwch ofal wrth glicio ar ddolenni.\n\nMs Diggins,\n\nThank you for your reply.\n\nI appreciate your clarification that Glamorgan Archives does not hold records of past Post Office employees. However, my request was broader than personnel records alone.\n\nI was also seeking any historical records held by Glamorgan Archives relating to:\n\n  *   Cardiff Post Office / General Post Office engineering activities around 1897;\n  *   the Marconi wireless telegraphy experiments at Lavernock Point and tests to Flat Holm;\n  *   correspondence, reports, photographs, deposited collections or other references mentioning the Cardiff engineering team or “Mr Williams” in connection with these events.\n\nCould you please confirm whether Glamorgan Archives has searched its catalogues or collections for these broader categories of records, rather than only employee records?\n\nIf no relevant records are held, I would be grateful for confirmation of the searches undertaken and any catalogue references checked, so that I can direct my research appropriately.\n\nThank you for your assistance.\n\nKind regards,\n\nRequester \nOn Tue, 21 Jul 2026, 12:00 Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg, <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>> wrote:\n2026/3098\nDear Requester.\nThank you for your email, received into our postal system on 16 July 2026.\nGlamorgan Archives does not hold records of past employees of the Post Office. We suggest that you contact the Postal Museum and Archives<https://www.postalmuseum.org/collections/the-archive/> for further information on your grandfather’s time working at Cardiff Post Office in 1897.\nI’m sorry that we can’t be of any further help on this occasion.\nYours sincerely,\nRhian Diggins [cid:image001.jpg@01DD1B47.8FCBD0B0]\nUwch Archifydd / Senior Archivist\nArchifau Morgannwg / Glamorgan Archives\n[phone redacted]\n[email redacted]<[email redacted]>\nhttps://glamarchives.gov.uk/\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSent: 16 July 2026 01:45\nTo: [email redacted]<[email redacted]>; [email redacted]<[email redacted]>; [email redacted]<[email redacted]>; Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>; FOI / Rhyddid Gwybodaeth <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>; [email redacted]<[email redacted]>; [email redacted]<[email redacted]>; [email redacted]<[email redacted]>; [email redacted]<[email redacted]>\nSubject: Freedom of Information / Historical Records Request - Mr. Williams, Cardiff Post Office Engineering Department, 1897 Marconi Lavernock Experiments\nEXTERNAL: This email originated from outside Cardiff Council, take care when clicking links.\nALLANOL: Daw'r e-bost hwn o'r tu allan i Gyngor Caerdydd, cymerwch ofal wrth glicio ar ddolenni.\n\nDear Information Rights / Archives Team,\n\nI am writing to request information under the Freedom of Information Act 2000 (or equivalent records access) regarding my grandfather, Mr. Williams, who worked in the engineering department of the Cardiff Post Office / General Post Office around 1897.\n\nSpecifically, I seek any records, personnel files, correspondence, reports, or mentions confirming his involvement in Guglielmo Marconi’s wireless telegraphy experiments at Lavernock Point (including erecting the 120-foot pole and related tests to Flat Holm). This relates to contemporary newspaper reports from May 1897.\n\nPlease provide:\n\n  *   Any personnel or service records for Mr. Williams in the Cardiff engineering department circa 1897.\n  *   Documents referencing \"Mr. Williams\" in connection with Marconi’s demonstrations.\n  *   Any other relevant historical files from the GPO telegraph/engineering teams.\n\nMy full name: Requester Address: [address redacted], Vale of Glamorgan, [postcode redacted][map link redacted] Contact: [email redacted]<[email redacted]> Relationship: Great Grandson\n\nI am ultimately seeking any record about my great grandfather Mr Williams of Great House Farm, Llandough, Cardiff (formerly Vale of Glamorgan). Not to be confused with Great House Farm, Llandough, Cowbridge, Vale of Glamorgan. And his role in the Marconi experiment. And before and after Marconis stay with our family in our home in 1897.\n\nI am happy to provide further identification if required. Thank you for your assistance.\n\nBest regards,\n\nRequester \n\n & Life Member\n\n**********************************************************************\n\nThe Council welcomes correspondence in Welsh, English or bilingually. We will ensure that we communicate with you in the language of your choice, as long as you let us know which you prefer. Corresponding in Welsh will not lead to delay.\n\nPrivileged/Confidential Information may be contained in this message. If you are not the addressee indicated in this message (or responsible for delivery of the message to such person), you may not copy or deliver this message to anyone. In such case, you should destroy this message and kindly notify the sender by reply email. Please advise immediately if you or your employer does not consent to Internet email for messages of this kind. Opinions, conclusions and other information in this message that do not relate to the official business of the Council of the City and County of Cardiff shall be understood as neither given nor endorsed by it. All e-mail sent to or from this address will be processed by Cardiff County Councils Corporate E-mail system and may be subject to scrutiny by someone other than the addressee.\n\n**********************************************************************\n\nMae’r Cyngor yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg, Saesneg neu’n ddwyieithog. Byddwn yn cyfathrebu â chi yn ôl eich dewis, dim ond i chi roi gwybod i ni pa un sydd well gennych. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi.\n\nMae'n bosibl bod gwybodaeth gyfrinachol yn y neges hon. Os na chyfeirir y neges atoch chi'n benodol (neu os nad ydych chi'n gyfrifol am drosglwyddo'r neges i'r person a enwir), yna ni chewch gopio na throsglwyddo'r neges. Mewn achos o'r fath, dylech ddinistrio'r neges a hysbysu'r anfonwr drwy e-bost ar unwaith. Rhowch wybod i'r anfonydd ar unwaith os nad ydych chi neu eich cyflogydd yn caniatau e-bost y Rhyngrwyd am negeseuon fel hon. Rhaid deall nad yw'r safbwyntiau, y casgliadau a'r wybodaeth arall yn y neges hon nad ydynt yn cyfeirio at fusnes swyddogol Cyngor Dinas a Sir Caerdydd yn cynrychioli barn y Cyngor Sir nad yn cael sel ei fendith. Caiff unrhyw negeseuon a anfonir at, neu o'r cyfeiriad e-bost hwn eu prosesu gan system E-bost Gorfforaethol Cyngor Sir Caerdydd a gallant gael eu harchwilio gan rywun heblaw'r person a enwir.\n\n**********************************************************************"},{"id":"gm-19f8ada83c557a43","subject":"Re: Freedom of Information / Historical