ur data seriously. If you contact the Welsh Government then our Privacy Notice<https://gov.wales/about/welsh-government-privacy-notice/?lang=en> explains how we use your information and the ways in which we protect your privacy. We welcome receiving correspondence in Welsh. Any correspondence received in Welsh will be answered in Welsh and corresponding in Welsh will not lead to a delay in responding."},{"id":"gm-19f4b61fe25affd4","subject":"RE: EIR request - Case Ref/ 00210772","name":"RE: EIR request - Case Ref/ 00210772","date":"2026-07-10T09:35:35Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19f4b61fe25affd4.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19f3cd95eeea3854-eir-request-case-ref-00210772","scopeTopics":["vale-foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["vale-foi"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19f3cd95eeea3854","direction":"received","from":"FOI Unit","institutions":[{"id":"vale-foi","name":"Vale of Glamorgan Council"}],"refs":["00210772"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19f4b61fe25affd4.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester\n\nThank you for your email, we note your concerns as set out below. We are reviewing your concerns and will be in touch further as soon as possible.\n\nKind regards\n\nInformation Governance Officer / Swyddog Llywodraethu Gwybodaeth\nDirector's Office - Resources / Swyddfa'r Cyfarwyddwr - Adnoddau\nVale of Glamorgan Council / Cyngor Bro Morgannwg\ntel / ffôn: [phone redacted]\ne-mail / e-bost: [email redacted] or [email redacted] \n\nConsider the environment. Please don't print this e-mail unless you really need to.\nYstyriwch yr amgylchedd. Peidiwch ag argraffu'r neges hon oni bai fod gwir angen.\n\nVisit our Website at www.valeofglamorgan.gov.uk \nEwch i'n gwefan yn www.bromorgannwg.gov.uk \n\nFind us on Facebook / Cewch ddod o hyd i ni ar Facebook \nFollow us on Twitter / Dilynwch ni ar Twitter \n\nCorrespondence is welcomed in Welsh or English / Croesewir Gohebiaeth yn y Gymraeg neu yn Saesneg.\n\nFrom: Requester  \nSent: 07 July 2026 16:39\nTo: FOI Unit  \nSubject: Re: EIR request - Case Ref/ 00210772\n\nDear Monitoring Officer,\n\nI request an internal review of the Council's response to my Environmental Information Regulations 2004 request (reference 00210772).\n\nThank you for the response issued on 7 July 2026. I appreciate the time taken to consider my request and the searches undertaken. However, having carefully reviewed the response alongside the earlier correspondence, I do not believe my clarified request has been fully addressed, nor do I believe the response adequately explains the scope of the searches that were undertaken.\n\nClarification process\nOn 28 May 2026 the Council advised that the relevant service area required clarification regarding the nature and reason for my enquiry.\n\nI provided that clarification on 7 June 2026.\nOn 9 June 2026 I was informed that my clarification had been forwarded to the Planning Department for consideration.\nGiven that sequence, I reasonably expected the final response to address the full scope of my clarified request. Instead, the response appears to have focused primarily upon identifying two planning applications and referring me to the Planning Register.\nThose applications are relevant, but they represent only one aspect of the information requested.\n\nScope of my request\nMy request concerns environmental information relating to the historic Great House Farm (Ty Mawr) estate, including land now forming Church View Close and adjoining land on the north side of Leckwith Road.\n\nThe request extends from approximately 1875 onwards where records exist.\nIt includes, where held:\n\nplanning records;\nofficer reports;\ncommittee reports and minutes;\nplanning consultation responses;\nplanning correspondence;\nenvironmental reports;\narchaeological reports;\nheritage assessments;\ndemolition records;\nredevelopment records;\nengineering drawings;\nhighways information;\ndrainage and sewer records;\nGIS and mapping datasets;\naerial photography;\nenvironmental assessments;\nland-related correspondence;\nestate management records;\nproperty records;\narchived files;\n\nand any other environmental information falling within Regulation 2(1) of the Environmental Information Regulations 2004.\n\nMatters that remain unanswered\nAlthough the response identifies two planning applications, it does not explain whether information was sought or considered in relation to several parts of my request.\n\nIn particular, it does not explain whether searches were undertaken for:\n\narchived paper planning files;\ncommittee reports and minutes;\nofficer reports;\nconsultation responses;\nsupporting technical documents;\ndemolition records relating to Great House Farm;\narchaeological reports held by the Council;\nheritage assessments;\nenvironmental assessments;\nengineering reports;\nhighways records;\ndrainage records;\nGIS datasets;\ncorrespondence relating to redevelopment;\nrecords inherited from predecessor authorities;\nrecords transferred to Glamorgan Archives or any other repository.\n\nAdequacy of the searches\nTo assist my understanding of the Council's response, I would be grateful if the internal review could explain:\n\nWhich Council departments were asked to search.\nWhich electronic record systems were searched.\nWhich paper archives were searched.\nWhether off-site archived records were searched.\nWhether records inherited from predecessor authorities were searched.\nWhether any GIS databases were searched.\nWhether searches extended beyond the Planning Register itself.\nWhether search terms or keywords were used and, if so, what they were.\nWhether date restrictions were applied.\nWhether any information was identified but withheld.\n\nInformation not held\nWhere the Council concludes that information is not held, I respectfully request that the review explains:\nwhich reasonable searches were undertaken before reaching that conclusion;\nwhether the information never existed;\nwhether it was transferred elsewhere;\nwhether it was destroyed;\nif destroyed, the applicable retention schedule and date of destruction;\nif transferred, the destination repository.\nInformation withheld\n\nIf any information has been withheld under the Environmental Information Regulations, I request that the review identifies:\nthe precise Regulation relied upon;\nthe reasons why that exception applies;\nwhere required, the public interest considerations;\nwhether partial disclosure or redaction was considered.\n\nPlanning Register\nThe response relies upon Regulation 6(1)(b) on the basis that planning information is publicly available.\n\nI would therefore appreciate clarification as to whether the online Planning Register contains the complete planning files, including:\n\nofficer reports;\ncommittee reports;\nconsultation responses;\ninternal memoranda;\ncorrespondence;\namendments;\nsupporting plans;\ntechnical reports;\nconditions;\nenforcement material (if any);\narchived documents.\nIf it does not, I request disclosure of any remaining environmental information held by the Council.\nGIS information\n\nThe response states that my request for GIS information lacked sufficient detail.\nFor the avoidance of doubt, I seek copies of any GIS datasets or historic mapping held by the Council relating to Great House Farm/Ty Mawr, including where available:\n\nparcel boundaries;\nplanning polygons;\nhistoric base mapping;\naerial imagery;\nhighway extents;\nrights of way;\ndrainage assets;\nland referencing;\nscanned plans attached to GIS records.\nPersonal data\nSeparately, to the extent that any records contain my own personal data, I request that this element of my request also be treated as a Subject Access Request under Article 15 UK GDPR.\n\nClosing\nI have sought throughout this process to clarify and narrow my request wherever asked to do so. My intention is simply to understand the complete environmental and administrative record held by the Council concerning Great House Farm/Ty Mawr.\n\nIf the Council has conducted comprehensive searches, I would be grateful if the internal review could explain those searches in sufficient detail to demonstrate that all reasonable steps have been taken. If further information is held, I request that it be disclosed unless a specific statutory exception applies.\nThank you for undertaking the internal review. I look forward to receiving the outcome.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester\n & Life Member\n[phone redacted]\n\nP.S. For the avoidance of doubt, where the Council holds environmental information relating to the historic Great House Farm/Ty Mawr estate prior to the 1989-1992 redevelopment-including records connected with the 1938 auction of the Cowbridge and Llandough Great House Farm estate, subsequent ownership, occupation, boundary changes, estate management, landscaping, trees, rights of way, demolition, redevelopment or associated land management-I request that such information also be included within the scope of this review.\n\nIn particular, if the Council holds any recorded information concerning the removal of a commemorative tree formerly situated at or associated with Great House Farm, including work orders, contractor instructions, arboricultural reports, highways or grounds maintenance records, photographs, correspondence, GIS records or file notes, I request disclosure of those records. If no such information is held, I would be grateful if the Council could confirm whether it has ever held such records, whether they have been transferred or destroyed, or whether another public authority is likely to hold them.\n\nFinally, if, during the course of the internal review, it becomes apparent that any part of my request has been interpreted too narrowly, I ask that this letter be treated as further clarification rather than a new request, so that all relevant recorded information held by the Council can be identified and considered for disclosure under the Environmental Information Regulations 2004.\n\nOn Tue, 7 Jul 2026, 10:51 FOI Unit,  > wrote:\nDear Requester\n\nThank you for your request for information about Great House Farm, Williams/Buckler Family, Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan and the further clarification that was received on the 7th June. Your request has been processed under the Environmental Information Regulations 2004.\n\nThe Council's Planning records have been reviewed and the Planning department has provided the following response:\n\nIn line with your clarification that was received on the 7th June, searches have been undertaken by the Council's Planning department in relation to the historic Great House Farm / Ty Mawr estate in Llandough. This includes land adjacent to and including Church View Close and the north side of Leckwith Road, with particular reference to the area identified within Land Registry title WA231076 and the timeframe indicated from 1875 onwards, where records exist.\n\nWe have been advised that the links to the planning records relating to the redevelopment of the site during the 1980s-1990s have previously been provided by colleagues in the Planning department. These records, including associated documentation, are publicly available via the Council's Planning Register and can be accessed using the relevant application reference numbers at View or Comment on a Planning Application .\nThis information is therefore excepted under Regulation 6(1)(b) of the Environmental Information Regulations 2004 - information already publicly available and easily accessible by other means.\n\nWhilst planning files may contain some archaeological information, it is also noted that archaeological records for the Vale of Glamorgan are maintained by Heneb (formerly Glamorgan-Gwent Archaeological Trust). We suggest that you contact Heneb directly to access relevant records. Details on how to contact them can be found at Heneb - The Trust for Welsh Archaeology . To the extent that the Council does not hold further archaeological reports beyond those contained within planning files, Regulation 12(4)(a) of the Environmental Information Regulations 2004 (information not held) applies.\n\nFollowing searches of Planning records, no additional information has been identified within the scope of your request. You asked whether any records may have been transferred to Glamorgan Archives or otherwise. It is possible that historic material of this nature may be held externally, and you may therefore wish to contact Glamorgan Archives directly. Details on how to contact them can be found at https://glamarchives.gov.uk/contact/.\n\nFrom the clarification provided, the Council has been unable to identify which specific GIS datasets are being sought. Please provide further clarification regarding this and once received, we will consider this element further.\n\nAs previously advised, the Council does not hold records relating to the legal case of BP Properties Ltd v Buckler. Neither the Vale of Glamorgan Council nor its predecessor authorities were parties to that case, which appears to have been a private legal matter. Accordingly, information relating to those proceedings is not held by the Council.\n\nIf you are dissatisfied with the handling of your request, you have the right to ask for an internal review. Internal review requests should be made within 40 working days of the date of receipt of the response to your original letter and should be addressed to:\nc/o The Monitoring Officer - [email redacted] \nOr by post to The Monitoring Officer, Civic Offices, Holton Road, Barry, [postcode redacted].\n\nPlease remember to quote the reference number above in any future communications.\n\nIf you are not content with the outcome of the internal review, you have the right to apply directly to the Information Commissioner for a decision.\n\nThe Information Commissioner can be contacted at:\nInformation Commissioner's Office - Wales\nSecond Floor, Churchill House\nChurchill Way,\nCardiff\n[postcode redacted]\n\n[phone redacted] / Fax:[phone redacted]\n[email redacted] \n\nYours sincerely\n\nInformation Governance Officer / Swyddog Llywodraethu Gwybodaeth\nDirector's Office - Resources / Swyddfa'r Cyfarwyddwr - Adnoddau\nVale of Glamorgan Council / Cyngor Bro Morgannwg\ntel / ffôn: [phone redacted]\ne-mail / e-bost: [email redacted]  or [email redacted] \n\nConsider the environment. Please don't print this e-mail unless you really need to.\nYstyriwch yr amgylchedd. Peidiwch ag argraffu'r neges hon oni bai fod gwir angen.\n\nVisit our Website at www.valeofglamorgan.gov.uk \nEwch i'n gwefan yn www.bromorgannwg.gov.uk \n\nFind us on Facebook / Cewch ddod o hyd i ni ar Facebook \nFollow us on Twitter / Dilynwch ni ar Twitter \n\nCorrespondence is welcomed in Welsh or English / Croesewir Gohebiaeth yn y Gymraeg neu yn Saesneg."},{"id":"gm-19f406af09e13e91","subject":"Please update the status of your request - 1988 Dispossession of Great House Farm, Llandough from the Williams/ Buckler Family","name":"Please update the status of your request - 1988 Dispossession of Great House Farm, Llandough from the Williams/ Buckler Family","date":"2026-07-08T06:29:37Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19f406af09e13e91.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19f406af09e13e91-please-update-the-status-of-your-request-1988-dispossession-of-great-house-farm-llandough-from-the-williams-buckler-fami","scopeT