,"name":"RE: Buckler - 260607013 - FOI - response","date":"2026-07-01T10:30:56Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19f1d3b6ce31b0fe.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19f18a3ec528e53f-buckler-260607013-foi-response","scopeTopics":["moj"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["moj"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19f18a3ec528e53f","direction":"received","from":"Disclosure Team","institutions":[{"id":"moj","name":"Ministry of Justice / HMCTS"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19f1d3b6ce31b0fe.json","textStatus":"available","text":"OFFICIAL\n\nDear Requester\n\nThank you for your email.\n\nYour request has been handled in line with data protection legislation as a Subject Access Request (SAR) for your personal information. In order for us to process your request we require further information as detailed below.\n\nVerifying Identity\n\nThe Ministry of Justice (MoJ) is committed to protecting your personal data and keeping it secure. One of the key safeguards we use is verifying the identity of anyone who requests access to personal data. This ensures that only you can access your information through a SAR.\nTo process your request, we ask that you provide two forms of identification.\n\n  1.  Photo ID - This should clearly show your face, such as a passport or driving licence.\n\n  1.  Proof of Address - This should show your full name and current address, such as a utility bill (e.g. electricity, gas, water, telephone/broadband), council tax bill, or a bank/credit card statement. These documents must be dated within the last six months.\nYou can use your driving licence as proof of your current address, but you'll still need to provide an additional photo ID (such as your passport).\n\nWe do not use photo ID to confirm your likeness but because the process for obtaining photo ID involves additional safeguards that help us confirm your identity with greater confidence. This is a necessary and proportionate step to make sure your data remains secure.\nYou can find more information about how we handle personal data in our Personal Information Charter<https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/ministry-of-justice/about/personal-information-charter>\n\nOn receipt of this information, we will continue to process your request.\n\n\n\n[signature_1893814455]\n\nDisclosure Team\nSecurity and Information Directorate\nService Transformation Group\nMinistry of Justice\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nOFFICIAL\n\n________________________________\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSent: 30 June 2026 23:24\nTo: NSUKilo <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSubject: Re: Buckler - 260607013 - FOI - response\n\nThanks.\n\n\nAnd finally,\n\nI am writing now to make a Subject Access Request under Article 15 of the UK General Data Protection Regulation and the Data Protection Act 2018.\n\nI wish to obtain copies of any personal data that the Ministry of Justice, HM Courts & Tribunals Service, or any relevant department holds about me, Requester, whether in electronic or paper records.\n\nIn particular, I am seeking any personal data relating to the events surrounding the possession and eviction proceedings connected with Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan. As a young child, I was present in the farmhouse during the eviction and enforcement action involving BP and court bailiffs. I understand there were reports at the time that specialist security personnel may also have been involved. If any records identify me, refer to my presence, welfare, or involvement, I request copies of those records.\n\nI also request any personal data held in connection with proceedings before the European Court of Human Rights relating to my late father Buckler v United Kingdom (1989), where it concerns me, including any correspondence, memoranda, case notes, or other documents in which I am identified or discussed.\n\nFor the avoidance of doubt, this request is for my own personal data only. If information cannot be disclosed because it contains third-party personal data, I request that it be appropriately redacted rather than the document being withheld in its entirety where possible.\n\nPlease also advise whether any relevant records have been destroyed, transferred to another public authority or archive, or are otherwise no longer held.\n\nIf you require any further information to verify my identity or to process this request, please let me know.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\n\n\nRequester (Ret'd Sergenant)\n\nDOB: 24/10/1984\n\n[address redacted], Vale of Glamorgan, [postcode redacted]\n\nOn Tue, 30 Jun 2026, 10:07 NSUKilo, <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>> wrote:\n\nOFFICIAL - FOR PUBLIC RELEASE\n\n\nDear Requester\n\nPlease see the attached response to your request (our reference 260607013)\n\n\n\nRegards\n\nOperational Support - Knowledge and Information Liaison Officer\n\nNational Support Unit | HMCTS\n\ngov.uk/hmcts<http://www.gov.uk/hmcts>\n\n\n\n[HM Courts & Tribunals Service logo]\n\nHere is how HMCTS users your data<https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/hm-courts-and-tribunals-service/about/personal-information-charter>\n\n\n\n\n\n________________________________\nThis e-mail and any attachments is intended only for the attention of the addressee(s). Its unauthorised use, disclosure, storage or copying is not permitted. If you are not the intended recipient, please destroy all copies and inform the sender by return e-mail. Internet e-mail is not a secure medium. Any reply to this message could be intercepted and read by someone else. Please bear that in mind when deciding whether to send mat