enceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19eb8795275d1283.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19ea1524cf133911-great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":["glamorgan"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["glamorgan"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19ea1524cf133911","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"glamorgan","name":"Glamorgan Archives"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19eb8795275d1283.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Monika,\n\nThank you for taking the time to search the Cardiff Central Library\ncollections and for your helpful reply.\n\nI appreciate that \"Williams\" is far too common a surname to search\neffectively without further information.\n\nThe family associated with Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough, included\nMary Doreen Williams (died 1983), her father John Williams, and later her\nson, my father William Buckler and his wife, my mum; [member of the public]. The\nproperty was historically known as Great House Farm or Ty Mawr and stood in\nLlandough near Cardiff.\n\nThe reference to Daniel Thomas comes from historical accounts of the\nquarrying and land transactions affecting the estate. My understanding is\nthat there was a conveyance or land exchange involving Daniel Thomas and\nthe Bute Estate which has been referenced in later histories of the area.\nUnfortunately I do not yet have a deed reference number, which is one of\nthe reasons I am trying to trace the original records.\n\nI am particularly interested in:\n• Any references to Great House Farm / Ty Mawr, Llandough. • Estate records\nrelating to the Bute Estate and Daniel Thomas. • Local history files,\nnewspaper cuttings, photographs or manuscripts relating to the Williams\nfamily of Great House Farm. • Material relating to the historic house,\nfarm, manor, coach house, estate, quarrying activities, or later demolition.\n\nThank you also for directing me to Glamorgan Archives. I have been making\nenquiries there and will continue to do so.\nIf any estate maps, local history files, manuscript collections, newspaper\nindexes, parish material, or catalogues come to mind which may contain\nreferences to Great House Farm, I would be very grateful for any further\nsuggestions.\n\nThank you again for your assistance.\n\nKind regards,\n\nRequester \n\nOn Thu, 11 Jun 2026, 08:17 Library Central, <[email redacted]>\nwrote:\n\n> Hi Requester,\n>\n> based on the information you provided, I couldn't find any relevant\n> materials among the documents held at Cardiff Central Library. I searched\n> for Great Farm House, Llandough, and Daniel Thomas, but found no matches\n> with your query. I'm afraid last name Williams is too popular to conduct\n> any research without further details - first names, titles, etc.\n>\n> You mention that some files are \"referenced in quarry deal histories\" -\n> would you be able to send us the exact references? They could possibly\n> include a document/manuscript number that could help locating any materials\n> in question.\n>\n> Lastly, I had a brief look at materials held by our partner, Glamorgan\n> Archives. One of the records that came up is the below:\n> I think it would be worth contacting them (Contact - Glamorgan Archives\n> <https://glamarchives.gov.uk/contact/>) and asking if they have any more\n> documents relating to the house.\n>\n> Kind regards,\n>\n> *Monika Wroblewska*\n> *Rhagenwau/**Pronouns: **hi/ei / **she/her*\n>\n>\n> *Swyddog Hyb Uwch** Senior Hub Officer *\n>\n> *Hyb y Llyfrgell Ganolog* *Central Library Hub*\n>\n> *Yr Aes** The Hayes, **Caerdydd* *Cardiff [postcode redacted]*\n>\n> *Cyngor Dinas Caerdydd** City of Cardiff Council*\n>\n>\n>\n> ------------------------------\n> *From:* Requester <[email redacted]>\n> *Sent:* 07 June 2026 10:05\n> *To:* Libraries <[email redacted]>\n> *Subject:* Great House Farm, Llandough\n>\n>  *EXTERNAL:* This email originated from outside Cardiff Council, take\n> care when clicking links.\n>  *ALLANOL:* Daw'r e-bost hwn o'r tu allan i Gyngor Caerdydd, cymerwch\n> ofal wrth glicio ar ddolenni.\n>\n>\n> Sirs,\n>\n> Please help me locate a copy of Daniel Thomas/Bute Estates deed/swap for\n> Great House Farm (referenced in quarry deal histories) and any late 1800's\n> / early 1900s materials or Williams family files.\n>\n> Regards\n>\n>\n> Requester\n>\n> **********************************************************************\n>\n> The Council welcomes correspondence in Welsh, English or bilingually. We\n> will ensure that we communicate with you in the language of your choice, as\n> long as you let us know which you prefer. Corresponding in Welsh will not\n> lead to delay.\n>\n> Privileged/Confidential Information may be contained in this message. If\n> you are not the addressee indicated in this message (or responsible for\n> delivery of the message to such person), you may not copy or deliver this\n> message to anyone. In such case, you should destroy this message and kindly\n> notify the sender by reply email. Please advise immediately if you or your\n> employer does not consent to Internet email for messages of this kind.\n> Opinions, conclusions and other information in this message that do not\n> relate to the official business of the Council of the City and County of\n> Cardiff shall be understood as neither given nor endorsed by it. All e-mail\n> sent to or from this address will be processed by Cardiff County Councils\n> Corporate E-mail system and may be subject to scrutiny by someone other\n> than the addressee.\n>\n> **********************************************************************\n>\n> Mae’r Cyngor yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg, Saesneg neu’n ddwyieithog.\n> Byddwn yn cyfathrebu â chi yn ôl eich d