":"gt-19e83e88b2d43a0e","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"cadw","name":"Welsh Government / Cadw"}],"refs":["ATISN27021"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19eb848842881794.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Hi Mike,\n\nAppreciate the reply.\n\nI kindly request an internal review of the response provided under ATISN\n27021.\n\nI am grateful for the release of the two inspection photographs dated 29\nJuly 1988. However, I am not satisfied that the searches undertaken were\nsufficient to establish that no other relevant records are held or can be\naccounted for.\n\nThe response states that Great House Farm was visited and assessed for\nlisting by Cadw inspectors, but that the only surviving records are two\nphotographs. It further states that any associated records may have been\ntransferred to the Royal Commission on the Ancient and Historical Monuments\nof Wales or destroyed under the Public Records Act 1958.\n\nI respectfully submit that this conclusion appears difficult to reconcile\nwith the contemporary evidence.\n\nContemporary newspaper reports from December 1988 record that:\n\nCadw was actively considering emergency listing (\"spot-listing\") procedures\nin relation to Great House Farm;\n\nCadw inspectors visited the site;\nCadw assessed the architectural and archaeological significance of the\nproperty;\nCadw communicated a decision that the building did not warrant statutory\nprotection.\n\nThese activities would ordinarily be expected to generate records such as\ninspection notes, recommendations, assessments, correspondence, memoranda,\nlisting files, briefing notes, internal communications, or decision records.\n\nIn addition, a surviving family witness who was present during the\ninspection recalls that a written assessment was produced and supplied to\nthe family. The witness recalls that the assessment concluded that,\nalthough the site was old, the building had undergone substantial\nalteration over the centuries and was therefore not considered sufficiently\nsignificant for listing.\n\nThis recollection appears broadly consistent with the reasoning attributed\nto Cadw in contemporary press reports.\nI therefore request clarification of the following:\n\nWere searches undertaken for inspection notes, site notes, assessment\nreports, recommendations, listing files, briefing papers, internal\nmemoranda, correspondence, and decision records relating to Great House\nFarm?\nWere accession records, transfer schedules, disposal schedules, or archive\ncatalogues searched to determine whether such records once existed?\n\nIf records were transferred to RCAHMW, please identify any transfer\nreferences, accession numbers, file references, or other metadata recording\nthat transfer.\nIf records were destroyed, please identify the disposal authority,\nretention schedule, and date of destruction.\n\nIf no written assessment, recommendation, report, or decision record ever\nexisted, please explain how Cadw reached and communicated conclusions\nregarding the architectural and archaeological significance of the property.\n\nI am not seeking reconsideration of the 1988 listing decision itself.\nRather, I seek clarification regarding the apparent absence of records\nrelating to a matter that was publicly reported at the time as being under\nactive consideration by Cadw and which resulted in a communicated decision.\n\nThank you for undertaking an internal review.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\nRequester (Ret'd Sergenant)\n\n\n\n\nOn Thu, 11 Jun 2026, 12:54 , <[email redacted]> wrote:\n\n> Dear Requester,\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you for your request which I received on 1 June 2026, and your\n> follow-up request received on 8 June 2026.\n>\n>\n>\n> Please see attached response and documents.\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you,\n>\n>\n>\n> Mike Weatherhead\n>\n>\n> Arweinydd Tîm Gwaith Achosion / Casework Team Leader\n>\n> Polisi a Gwarchod / Policy and Protection\n>\n> Arolygiaeth yr Amgylchedd Hanesyddol / Historic Environment Inspectorate\n>\n> Cadw\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> *From:* Weatherhead, Michael (LCC - Cadw)\n> *Sent:* 01 June 2026 16:59\n> *To:* 'Requester' <[email redacted]>\n> *Subject:* RE: Great House Farm/ Church View Close, Llandough, nr.\n> Penarth, Vale of Glamorgan (ATISN 27021)\n>\n>\n>\n> Dear Requester,\n>\n>\n>\n> *Great House Farm, Llandough*\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you for your request which I received on 1 June 2026. You asked for:\n>\n>\n>\n>    1. Any consideration of the property for statutory listing.\n>    2. Any listing assessments, surveys, inspections, recommendations or\n>    reports concerning the building.\n>    3. Any correspondence between Cadw, Welsh Office officials, local\n>    authorities, conservation bodies, archaeological organisations or owners\n>    concerning the historic significance, preservation, demolition or\n>    protection of the property.\n>    4. Any records explaining why the building was not added to the\n>    statutory list of buildings of special architectural or historic interest.\n>    5. Any records discussing the age, architectural significance,\n>    archaeological significance or surviving historic fabric of the building.\n>    6. Any records concerning emergency listing, spot-listing,\n>    preservation notices or other forms of protection considered for the\n>    property.\n>    7. Any records held relating to public campaigns, petitions or\n>    representations seeking preservation of the building.\n>    8. Any photographs, plans, surveys or architectural records held by\n>    Cadw or the Welsh Government relating to Great House Farm.\n>\n>\n>\n> You will receive a response by 25 June 2026. If you have any queries\n> about this request, please do not hesitate to contact me at the address\n> below.  Please remember to quote the reference number above in any future\n> correspondence *(ATISN 27021).*\n>\n>\n>\n> The request you sent me contains personal information about you - for\n> exa