y from the office until 2 June 2026. I will answer e-mails on my return.\n\nIf urgent, please email [email redacted].\n\nCofion gorau / Best wishes,\nDr Adam N. Coward"},{"id":"gm-19e75f3fbc468d78","subject":"Re: RC26-0205 - Great House Farm, Williams/ Buckler Family, Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan","name":"Re: RC26-0205 - Great House Farm, Williams/ Buckler Family, Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan","date":"2026-05-29T22:56:22Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19e75f3fbc468d78.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19e6f0e4a747d5bc-rc26-0205-great-house-farm-williams-buckler-family-llandough-vale-of-glamorgan","scopeTopics":["rcahmw"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["rcahmw"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19e6f0e4a747d5bc","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"rcahmw","name":"RCAHMW (Royal Commission)"}],"refs":["RC26-0205"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19e75f3fbc468d78.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Thanks Adam.\n\nHaving recently completed my 2022 years in Oath as a public servant.\nEmployed under the welsh government/ Vale of Glamorgan for the last 2 of\nit. And left my military career with honours before that. I always prided\nmyself serving as a public servant.\n\nBut I am outraged at what occured to my family and its a huge scandal. And\nanyone in office should not be treating this like an us/them situation\nwhere I'm going up against my Welsh Govenrment and fudiciaries.\n\nThere's a lot of women in my family too terrified to even speak up against\nthis. My father alledged fraud. He died. My grandad died before being able\nto explain how BP got our title. My nan died in the window she had to\nchallenge BP's registration of our land in their names. I am the only one\nwho is willing to speak up and out about this.\n\nIts not I wasn't frightened like they were. Its that statute of limitation\napplied and there wasn't much that could be done. But I suspect fraud as of\nthis year and this means this can and should be reopened.\n\nI am horrified that the MP and Councillor for Llandough are remaining\nsilent. Not responding. But its possible they might need time to take a\nposition on this. So far as I see the British (Thatcher and BP) seem\nimplicated. And us indigenous welsh on our ancestral birthland home since\n1667, makes this very bad for them.\n\nSo if there's any Welsh still in Wales, that care that our herritage and\nhistory could be looted and history covered up as this appears to have, I\nhope someone else will make noise about this too. And not just let it rest.\n\nMarconi stayed with us while inventing radio and there's not even a plaque\nto commemorate Church View Close, Llandough as the birthplace of radio.\nThis is a huge scandal. I understand many Brits are liberal left and\nMarconi was right wing. Thats what Cardiff City Council say about why\nthere's no plaque to commemorate marconi on the statute of a radio at\nCardiff Bay. But this perversion and supression of history and facts is so\nunacceptable. I have nephews and children and the fact they can't see any\ntrace of our 320 years in Llandough , up until 1988 is an outrage. The fact\nthe press all labelled us squatters and thats what they will grow up\nreading, when it was our home since 1667 is also an outrage. They're\nclearly hostages under terrorists and there's nothing democratic about any\nof this.\n\nSo far as I see what happened to our family in 1988 , in how we were\ndisplaced, isn't just tyranny or british colonising. It's a literally act\nof terrorism. Which I reported to police yesterday as such. And I consider\nmy family there in Wales hostages under said terrorists. The situation is\nuntenable. We require remedy. Narrative justice. Correction of records.\nSome press release. Something to amend this situation.\n\nThe fact ITV quietly just declassified footage from 1988 after 38 years was\ncowardly. They cannot expect to get away with that.  The footage clearly\nsays neighbours report SAS used and the footage shows SAS and there's\nmention of the land being prime Real Estate overlooking cardiff bay. But\nmore so is the fact that my old best friends late mum, Nichola Hayward\nThomas, who is the news anchor, never mentioned this footage to me and took\nthis to her grave. And in the clip she fails to mention the assault on my\nmum, while serving the injuction to the illegal occupiers terrorists,\nproperly SAS. Didn't just dislocate her shoulder. But she was pregnant with\nthree toddlers. So even the declassified footage after 38 years supressed,\nis itself carefully scripted to show bias. And there's more on that. Like\nthe fact the edit/ cut is so abrupt when the SAS looking man or mention of\nreal estate value being prime.\n\nWe scattered to the wind, looted and half dead in 1988 after decades of\nlawfare and this terrorist attack and siege on our home. We want justice.\nBecause what we've said all along seems to have been the plausible story.\nAnd what the press, records and courts ruled seems to be the fraud.\n\nWithout legitimacy there's no authority here. It's important as fudiciaries\nyou be legitimate.\n\nI'm horrified at the replies I have received thus far. Like CADW saying\nthey had nothing to do with this or land registry converting my query into\na complaint only to squash is. And failing that some other cowardly reply.\n\nThere's institutional failure. There's court mistakes. Theres proceedural\nunfairness.\nPurgergy and fraud. I expect better from the Welsh Govenrment to fix this.\n\nPlease do everything in your power.\nThis is all I ask.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester\n[address redacted], Vale of Glamorgan\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nOn Thu, 28 May 2026, 11:47 Dr Adam Coward, <[email redacted]>\nwrote:\n\n> Good afternoon,\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you for your enquiry. As noted in my email of 26 November last,\n> while we cannot advise you on a legal case or undertake a review of\n> historical decisions, we do hold information and archives related to Great\n> House Farm, Llandough.\n>\n>\n>\n> The site entry and description for the site in the National Monuments\n> Record of Wales can be seen online on Coflein, here:\n> https://coflein.gov.uk/en/site/20182.\n>\n>\n>\n> We hold 14 archives related to the site, a list of which can be seen here:\n> https://coflein.gov.uk/en/site/20182/archives?size=25. These archives can\n> be viewed in our Search Room, located in the National Library of Wales,\n> which is open Tuesday through Thursday, 9:30-16:30. If you would like to\n> view the material in person, I would advise arranging with us beforehand so\n> that we can prepare the material ahead of your visit. Some of the material\n> is on microfiche and as we do not have a microfiche reader in the Search\n> Room, we will need to liaise with the National Library Reading Room staff\n> to try to negotiate the use of their machines ahead of your visit. However,\n> I have located an electronic copy of Archive Number 6524751, which can be\n> accessed here:\n> https://www.walesher1974.org/her/app/php/herumd.php?group=GGAT&level=3&docid=301361628\n>\n>\n>\n> While it is possible to order scans of material using the attached order\n> form, we may be unable to provide research-quality scans or photographs of\n> some of the material, such as the material on microfiche, which would\n> require high-resolution scanning at a cost of £20 per scan. Moreover,  many\n> of these records remain under third-party copyright, and we would only be\n> able to provide scans or copies for personal, non-commercial research.\n> Other uses, such as use in a legal case or report would likely require the\n> permission of the copyright holder(s). I would therefore advise arranging\n> to view the material in person rather than ordering scans, if possible.\n>\n>\n>\n> Finally, I have confirmed with our Records Manager that we do not hold any\n> correspondence related to the site.\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you again for your enquiry.\n>\n>\n>\n> Sincerely,\n>\n>\n>\n> Adam Coward\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> Dr Adam Coward\n>  Rheolwr Llyfrgell ac Ymholiadau | Library and Enquiries Manager\n> Ffordd Penglais | Penglais Road,  Aberystwyth,  [postcode redacted]\n> [phone redacted] <+44%20(0)%201970%20621%20216>\n> [email redacted]  |  [email redacted]\n> www.cbhc.gov.uk <https://cbhc.gov.uk/>  |  www.rcahmw.gov.uk\n> <https://rcahmw.gov.uk/>\n> Noddir gan Lywodraeth Cymru  |  Sponsored by Welsh Government\n> [image: Facebook]\n> <https://www.facebook.com/Royal-Commission-on-the-Ancient-and-Historical-Monuments-of-Wales-146120328739808>\n> [image: Twitter] <https://www.twitter.com/rcahmwales>\n>\n> Rydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg a Saesneg. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn peri oedi.\n>\n> We welcome correspondence in Welsh and English. Corresponding in Welsh will not lead to any delay.\n>\n>\n>\n> Yn unol â Rheoliadau Safonau'r Gymraeg (Rhif 2) 2016, mae gennych hawl i\n> gyfathrebu a gohebu â Chomisiwn Brenhinol Henebion Cymru yn eich dewis\n> iaith. Er mwyn sicrhau ein bod yn cyflawni’r hawl, rhowch wybod i ni a\n> ydych yn dymuno derbyn gohebiaeth a/neu alwadau ffôn oddi wrthym yn y\n> Gymraeg. Bydd yr wybodaeth hon yn cael ei chofnodi gennym ni, a byddwn yn\n> defnyddio'r iaith o’ch dewis ym mhob cyfathrebu yn y dyfodol. Diolch.\n> ​​​​\n> Under the Welsh Language Standards (No. 2) Regulations 2016, you have the\n> right to communicate and correspond with the Royal Commission on the\n> Ancient and Historical Monuments of Wales in your preferred language. To\n> ensure we uphold this right, please let us know whether you wish to receive\n> correspondence and/or telephone calls from us in Welsh. This information\n> will be recorded by us, and we will use your preferred language in all\n> future communication. Thank you.\n> ​\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> *From:* Requester <[email redacted]>\n> *Sent:* 20 May 2026 22:11\n> *To:* unclehowell <[email redacted]>\n> *Subject:* Great House Farm, Williams/ Buckler Family, Llandough, Vale of\n> Glamorgan\n>\n>\n>\n> Sir / Madam,\n>\n> To all those whom it concerns, BCC'd into this email.\n>\n> I write to request disclosure under the Freedom of Information Act 2000\n> and, where applicable, the Environmental Information Regulations 2004.\n>\n> This request concerns Great House Farm / Ty Mawr (Great House), Llandough,\n> associated estate lands, historic occupation and related heritage records.\n> My family is Buckler and before that Williams, and we occupied Great House\n> Farm from 1667 until our forceful dispossession of it in 1988.  Without our\n> ownership ever having been estiguished and without BP ever having a ruling\n> of ownership, only possession.\n>\n> It's alledged our fee simple absolute and manorial title was stolen. And\n> when BP registered our lands in their names in 1982, with our stolen title,\n> my nan who challenged this all her life, during the 2 week land registry\n> window to challenge BP's registration, which they achieved via purgergy and\n> document/ identity fraud, she conveniently died. As my father did after his\n> appeal of the 1987 mistake ruling was mistakely dismissed and his ECHR\n> request blocked and home abruptly demolished, which he was prevented from\n> stopping having been hospitalised and arrested without charges by armed\n> force officers and possible SAS.\n>\n> https://youtu.be/0eXOoj7eUCo?si=hcgSA2pFgEE943HG\n>\n> I understand records may exist within the National Monuments Record of\n> Wales, Historic Environment Record archives, archaeological consultations\n> and planning files, including references already identified as:\n>\n> • Great House Farm HER site entry – GGAT03586s\n> • Land at Great House Farm HER event entry – GGATE003872\n> • The 1990 archaeological assessment commissioned by BP Properties Ltd.\n>\n> Please provide copies of, or access to:\n>\n>    1. All records concerning Great House Farm / Ty Mawr / Great House\n>    Estate, Llandough including:\n>\n> • surveys\n> • reports\n> • site notes\n> • photographs\n> • maps\n> • aerial imagery\n> • GIS layers\n> • archive material\n> • annotations\n> • title references\n> • occupancy references\n> • landscape records\n> • archaeological observations.\n>\n>    2. All internal and external correspondence involving:\n>\n> • RCAHMW / NMRW\n> • Cadw\n> • GGAT / Heneb\n> • Vale of Glamorgan Council\n> • BP Properties Ltd\n> • developers\n> • planning authorities\n> • landowners, estate representatives or consultants.\n>\n>    3. Any documents discussing:\n>\n> • estate ownership\n> • occupation history\n> • family occupation or association\n> • agricultural use\n> • commemorative tree planting or memorial planting\n> • estate boundaries\n> • title history\n> • acquisition history\n> • disputes concerning ownership, heritage status or land use\n> • estate restructuring, extinguishment, compulsory acquisition or title\n> alteration.\n>\n>    4. Copies of all material linked to GGAT03586s and GGATE003872,\n>    including the archaeological assessment and associated correspondence.\n>    5. Any records identifying historic occupants, proprietors, tenants or\n>    family associations, including references to the Williams family where\n>    present within archives.\n>    6. Metadata identifying file creators, accession history, provenance,\n>    dates and retention schedules.\n>    7. File indexes, archive references or schedules identifying further\n>    related material not already listed.\n>\n> Please provide information electronically where possible.\n>\n> If any material is withheld, please identify the exemption relied upon and\n> provide the public interest assessment.\n>\n> Regards,\n>\n>\n>\n> Requester\n>\n> [address redacted]\n> [map link redacted]\n>"},{"id":"gm-19e7513aaa5f0339","subject":"RE: FORMAL DISCLOSURE NOTICE & LITIGATION HOLD: Historic Significance, Planning Approvals, and Displaced Family Tenancy Records for Great House Farm, Llandough (FOIA/EIR Request)","name":"RE: FORMAL DISCLOSURE NOTICE & LITIGATION HOLD: Historic Significance, Planning Approvals, and Displaced Family Tenancy Records for Great House Farm, Llandough (FOIA/EIR Request)","date":"2026-05-29T18:51:19Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19e7513aaa5f0339.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19e75029f91a5bd9-formal-disclosure-notice-litigation-hold-historic-significance-planning-approvals-and-displaced-family-tenancy-records-f","scopeTopics":["swp"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["swp"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19e75029f91a5bd9","direction":"received","from":"SWP101","institutions":[{"id":"swp","name":"South Wales Police"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19e7513aaa5f0339.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear member of the public.\nPlease note that South Wales Police no longer accepts reports via email. To make a report, we kindly request that you utilise one of the following options:\n1.           In case of an emergency, please call 999 immediately.\n2.           For non-emergency situations, please use our online Single Online Home (SOH) web form found in the reports section of at https://www.south-wales.police.uk/\nBy using the Single Online Home, you can provide us with accurate and comprehensive information, which will enable us to respond effectively and prioritise resources accordingly.\nWe appreciate your understanding and cooperation in adopting these preferred reporting channels.\nSincerely,\nSouth Wales Police\n[Welsh Language Translation]\nAnnwyl breswylydd,\nNoder nad yw Heddlu De Cymru yn derbyn adroddiadau drwy e-bost mwyach. Dylid gwneud pob adroddiad gan ddefnyddio un o'r opsiynau canlynol:\n1.           Mewn achos brys, ffoniwch 999 ar unwaith,\n2.           Mewn sefyllfaoedd nad ydynt yn rhai brys, defnyddiwch ein ffurfleni rhoi gwybod ar-lein sydd ar gael ar ein gwefan https://www.south-wales.police.uk/cy-GB/\nRydym yn gwerthfawrogi eich dealltwriaeth a'ch cydweithrediad wrth fabwysiadu'r sianeli rhoi gwybod hyn a ffefrir.\nYn gywir, Heddlu De Cymru\n\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]>\nSent: 29 May 2026 19:48\nTo: Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg <[email redacted]>\nSubject: FORMAL DISCLOSURE NOTICE & LITIGATION HOLD: Historic Significance, Planning Approvals, and Displaced Family Tenancy Records for Great House Farm, Llandough (FOIA/EIR Request)\n\nLEGAL NOTICE TO RECIPIENTS:\nTake notice that this request is issued in anticipation of formal legal proceedings and the filing of an official report regarding systemic institutional fraud, land misconduct, and the unlawful concealment of historic preservation data. You are under a strict legal duty to preserve all physical and electronic records, metadata, correspondence, maps, and erased files relating to this site. Any spoliation, deletion, or withholding of data will be cited in subsequent court filings.\nDear Freedom of Information Officer,\nRE: Information Request under the Freedom of Information Act 2000 and the Environmental Information Regulations 2004\nI am writing to request access to historical records, internal correspondence, planning briefs, and assessments held by your organization regarding Great House Farm, Llandough, near Penarth/Cardiff (demolished December 1988), and the historical occupancy of the Buckler family.\nStatutory Mandate on Accessibility & Charging Fees:\nI am aware that previous attempts to locate this data have resulted in directives to visit physical archives in person or use paywalled, commercial third-party web portals. Please be advised of the following statutory obligations:\n\n  1.  Under EIR Regulation 8, fees cannot act as an unreasonable barrier or deterrent to access. Because my family was historically displaced from this site and I am executing this request remotely, forcing physical attendance or demanding payment constitutes an unlawful obstacle.\n  2.  Public authorities are required to supply information in the format requested. I require all documents to be delivered digitally via email attachment or secure electronic download link, free of charge, as specified by the Information Commissioner’s Office (ICO) guidelines for data already held electronically.\nPlease provide all recorded information matching the following points:\n1. Tenant/Ownership Identification and the 1880 Report\n\n  *   Any documents, archival notes, or registry indexes citing the William/ Buckler family line (who occupied and farmed the house across four generations).\n  *   Clarification on whether my family members were formally cited as the legal owners, long-term tenants, or registered reporters of the 1880 discovery under the dining room floor.\n  *   Any explicit records indicating gaps, deleted metadata, or missing volumes in your authority’s archive concerning the tenancy, land-dispute maps, or agricultural heritage of Great House Farm between 1950 and 1989.\n2. Statutory Protection and Cadw’s Assessments\n\n  *   Given that the site's extreme archaeological value (a 1st-to-4th-century Roman precursor villa and a massive early medieval monastic settlement) was verified by rescue operations as early as 1979, what are the recorded reasons why Cadw failed to place a statutory Schedule or Protection Order on the standing fabric of Great House Farm before its demolition?\n  *   All internal correspondence, risk assessments, or ministerial briefs evaluating the preservation value of the sub-medieval direct-entry farmhouse core before it was levelled.\n3. Planning Approvals and Local Authority Governance\n\n  *   All minutes, planning committee files, and consultation responses from the local authority that granted planning permission and housing development rights on this specific acreage.\n  *   Direct information explaining how the council approved planning permission for a private housing development in light of the documented historical evidence surrounding the immediate parish boundary.\n4. Institutional Concealment and Historical \"Ignorance\"\n\n  *   Records showing exactly where and at what stage of the planning process this archaeological evidence should have been brought up but was not, which ultimately allowed developers and public bodies to claim they were unaware of the land's deep significance until after the farmhouse was destroyed and housing development began.\n  *   Any environmental impact assessments, historic environment register (HER) lookups, or developer briefs filed between 1980 and 1990 regarding the footprint of Great House Farm.\nIf any part of this request is refused under the guise of commercial sensitivity or archival limitations, you are required by law to state the exact statutory exemption applied and provide a formal internal review pathway.\nI expect your response within the statutory 20-working-day timeframe.\nKind regards,\nRequester \n[address redacted], Vale of Glamorgan\nYmateb nad yw'n Argyfwng\\ Non-Emergency Response\n\nOes angen i chi siarad â'r heddlu ond nid oes angen ymateb brys arnoch? Gallwch roi gwybod am fater drwy ein gwefan https://www.heddlu-de-cymru.police.uk, neu ffonio 101. Gallwch ddefnyddio 101 i roi gwybod am achosion nad ydynt yn rhai brys i unrhyw heddlu yng Nghymru a Lloegr. Mewn argyfwng, ffoniwch 999 bob amser.\n\nDo you need to speak to the police b