 provide the public interest assessment.\n>\n> Regards,\n>\n>\n>\n> Requester\n>\n> [address redacted]\n>\n>\n>\n> Flotek Group has scanned and approved this email for your delivery -\n> please note all website links have been replaced with a secure link for\n> your own safety and will be scanned again once clicked.\n>"},{"id":"gm-19e501c1312a8496","subject":"RE: Great House Farm, Williams/ Buckler Family, Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan","name":"RE: Great House Farm, Williams/ Buckler Family, Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan","date":"2026-05-22T14:34:29Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19e501c1312a8496.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19e46e010d552d92-great-house-farm-williams-buckler-family-llandough-vale-of-glamorgan","scopeTopics":["heneb"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["heneb"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19e46e010d552d92","direction":"received","from":"HER","institutions":[{"id":"heneb","name":"Heneb (formerly GGAT) / Historic Environment Record"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19e501c1312a8496.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester,\n\nThank you for your email.  Heneb is not subject to FOI requests as we are a Limited company.  However, if you fill out our Enquiry Form  then we will be happy to assist as best we can.\n\nKind regards,\n\nCalli\n\nFrom: Requester  \nSent: 20 May 2026 22:11\nTo: unclehowell  \nSubject: Great House Farm, Williams/ Buckler Family, Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan\n\n\nSir / Madam,\n\nTo all those whom it concerns, BCC'd into this email.\n\nI write to request disclosure under the Freedom of Information Act 2000 and, where applicable, the Environmental Information Regulations 2004.\n\nThis request concerns Great House Farm / Ty Mawr (Great House), Llandough, associated estate lands, historic occupation and related heritage records. My family is Buckler and before that Williams, and we occupied Great House Farm from 1667 until our forceful dispossession of it in 1988.  Without our ownership ever having been estiguished and without BP ever having a ruling of ownership, only possession.\n\nIt's alledged our fee simple absolute and manorial title was stolen. And when BP registered our lands in their names in 1982, with our stolen title, my nan who challenged this all her life, during the 2 week land registry window to challenge BP's registration, which they achieved via purgergy and document/ identity fraud, she conveniently died. As my father did after his appeal of the 1987 mistake ruling was mistakely dismissed and his ECHR request blocked and home abruptly demolished, which he was prevented from stopping having been hospitalised and arrested without charges by armed force officers and possible SAS.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/0eXOoj7eUCo?si=hcgSA2pFgEE943HG \n\nI understand records may exist within the National Monuments Record of Wales, Historic Environment Record archives, archaeological consultations and planning files, including references already identified as:\n\n• Great House Farm HER site entry – GGAT03586s\n• Land at Great House Farm HER event entry – GGATE003872\n• The 1990 archaeological assessment commissioned by BP Properties Ltd.\n\nPlease provide copies of, or access to:\n\n  1.  All records concerning Great House Farm / Ty Mawr / Great House Estate, Llandough including:\n\n• surveys\n• reports\n• site notes\n• photographs\n• maps\n• aerial imagery\n• GIS layers\n• archive material\n• annotations\n• title references\n• occupancy references\n• landscape records\n• archaeological observations.\n\n  1.  All internal and external correspondence involving:\n\n• RCAHMW / NMRW\n• Cadw\n• GGAT / Heneb\n• Vale of Glamorgan Council\n• BP Properties Ltd\n• developers\n• planning authorities\n• landowners, estate representatives or consultants.\n\n  1.  Any documents discussing:\n\n• estate ownership\n• occupation history\n• family occupation or association\n• agricultural use\n• commemorative tree planting or memorial planting\n• estate boundaries\n• title history\n• acquisition history\n• disputes concerning ownership, heritage status or land use\n• estate restructuring, extinguishment, compulsory acquisition or title alteration.\n\n  1.  Copies of all material linked to GGAT03586s and GGATE003872, including the archaeological assessment and associated correspondence.\n  2.  Any records identifying historic occupants, proprietors, tenants or family associations, including references to the Williams family where present within archives.\n  3.  Metadata identifying file creators, accession history, provenance, dates and retention schedules.\n  4.  File indexes, archive references or schedules identifying further related material not already listed.\n\nPlease provide information electronically where possible.\n\nIf any material is withheld, please identify the exemption relied upon and provide the public interest assessment.\n\nRegards,\n\n\n\nRequester\n\n[address redacted]\n\n\nFlotek Group has scanned and approved this email for your delivery - please note all website links have been replaced with a secure link for your own safety and will be scanned again once clicked."},{"id":"gm-19e500351c34e780","subject":"Re: FW: Great House Farm, Llandough (aka Ty Mawr)","name":"Re: FW: Great House Farm, Llandough (aka Ty Mawr)","date":"2026-05-22T14:07:32Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19e500351c34e780.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19e4b1bc46edc331-fw-great-house-farm-llandough-aka-ty-mawr","scopeTopics":["cadw"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["cadw"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19e4b1bc46edc331","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"cadw","name":"Welsh Government / Cadw"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19e500351c34e780.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Thank you Mike,\nVery decent reply.\nOn Thu, 21 May 2026 at 12:16,   wrote:\n\n> Good afternoon Requester,\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you for your email of 17 May regarding Great House Farm, Llandough,\n> and also for your email of 20 May.\n>\n>\n>\n> I have searched our electronic records relating to Great House Farm and\n> Barn and I can confirm they were assessed and rejected for listing in the\n> 1988 community survey of Llandough. I have attached two photos, dated 29\n> July 1988. The “YYY” marking on the rear of the photo indicates that the\n> buildings were considered marginally below the bar for listing. We hold no\n> other electronic records.\n>\n>\n>\n> I note the SW Echo report suggesting that Cadw inspectors would visit\n> again in December 1988, but I can find no record (as-yet) of this. The one\n> paper file we have located, dated 2002, makes no mention of Great House\n> Farm. I have asked a colleague to search for other related paper files,\n> including the Welsh Office files, and I will write to you again when I have\n> the result of the search. This may take a couple of weeks, and I must\n> advise that is quite likely that at least some of these files will have\n> been destroyed, but I will seek to disclose any records we recover to you.\n>\n>\n>\n> In the meantime, would suggest contacting the following archives services,\n> if you haven’t already done so, as they may hold relevant records or be\n> able to offer further assistance -\n>\n>\n>\n> Glamorgan Archives  \n>\n> National Library of Wales\n>  \n>\n> The Royal Commission on the Ancient & Historical Monuments of Wales\n>  , including their Coflein\n>   catalogue of historic buildings.\n>\n>\n>\n> These mapping services may also be of use to you:\n>\n> Tithe Maps of Wales   (1840s)\n>\n> Historic Ordnance Survey Maps\n>  \n> (1820s-1960s)\n>\n>\n>\n> The following research guide may too also be of interest - Land ownership\n> records and deeds: an overview - The National Archives\n>  \n> .\n>\n>\n>\n> And finally for your records, you may wish to note this record about Great\n> House Farm, held by the Royal Commission (Details | 20182 | Sites |\n> Coflein  ), this Historic\n> Environment Record (GGAT02038s\n>  ),\n> and the attached Welsh Archaeological Trust report.\n>\n>\n>\n> Yours Sincerely,\n>\n>\n>\n> Mike Weatherhead\n>\n>\n> Arweinydd Tîm Gwaith Achosion / Casework Team Leader\n>\n> Polisi a Gwarchod / Policy and Protection\n>\n> Arolygiaeth yr Amgylchedd Hanesyddol / Historic Environment Inspectorate\n>\n> Cadw\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> *From:* Requester  \n> *Sent:* 17 May 2026 05:11\n> *To:* Scheduled Monuments  \n> *Cc:* [email redacted]; Cadw Planning\n>  ; [email redacted]; Culture Division / Adran\n> Diwylliant  ; [email redacted]\n> *Subject:* BP vs Buckler, Great House Farm, Llandough\n>\n>\n>\n> To whom it may concern,\n>\n> I am writing as the child of [member of the public] and William (“Billy”) Buckler\n> regarding the destruction of Great House Farm, Llandough, and the\n> long-running dispute surrounding BP Properties Ltd v Buckler [1987].\n>\n> I have compiled a substantial historical archive, including newspaper\n> reports, court references, family testimony, archaeological material,\n> estate history, and public records relating to Great House Farm (“Ty\n> Mawr”), a site associated with medieval, monastic, and Roman occupation.\n>\n> The archive is currently being made into a website at:\n>\n> bpvsbuckler.datro.xyz\n>\n> The material raises serious unresolved questions regarding:\n>\n> • The treatment of us Williams/Buckler family’s longstanding occupation\n> and asserted ownership claims. • The repeated use of possession proceedings\n> without a full adjudication of superior title. • Missing or allegedly\n> suppressed title documentation connected to Bute estate transfers and later\n> transactions. • The demolition of an allegedly medieval structure during an\n> active heritage controversy. • Whether the archaeological significance of\n> the site was fully disclosed during planning and demolition processes. •\n> The wider public interest and cultural significance of the site and its\n> destruction.\n>\n> It's looking to me like my family actually did obtain fee simple absolute\n> and manorial title. Then it was stolen and attempts were made by the\n> possessor of the stolen title, to make my family lessee's / tenants as we\n> were when we were on the virtual freehold (999 year lease) aquired in 1667.\n> Thereafter it appears to be a death of a thousand cuts of proceedural\n> unfairness, audacity, fraud, court \"mistakes\" , purgery and more.\n>\n> https://youtu.be/4GXmR7mAas0?feature=shared\n>\n> Contemporaneous newspaper coverage from the 1970s and 1980s demonstrates\n> that these concerns were publicly known at the time, including heritage\n> objections, petitions, and public claims of ancestral ownership.\n>\n> I am requesting:\n>\n>    1. Review and preservation consideration of all surviving records\n>    connected to Great House Farm.\n>    2. Clarification as to whether any formal heritage review or\n>    investigation into the demolition process was ever completed.\n>    3. Guidance regarding access to archival and planning materials\n>    relevant to the site.\n>    4. Consideration of whether this matter warrants renewed historical or\n>    heritage examination.\n>\n> I appreciate that some claims remain disputed or unproven, and I am not\n> asking recipients to accept every allegation as established fact. However,\n> I believe the totality of the historical material warrants serious review\n> rather than dismissal.\n>\n> I would be grateful for acknowledgement of receipt and guidance on the\n> appropriate next steps.\n>\n> Kind regards,\n>\n>\n>\n> Requester Esq. \n>\n> Manor of Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan\n>\n>\n> Sganiwyd y neges hon am bob feirws hysbys wrth iddi adael Llywodraeth\n> Cymru. Mae Llywodraeth Cymru yn cymryd o ddifrif yr angen i ddiogelu eich\n> data. Os cysylltwch â Llywodraeth Cymru, mae ein hysbysiad preifatrwydd\n> yn esbonio sut rydym yn defnyddio eich gwybodaeth a sut rydym yn diogelu\n> eich preifatrwydd. Rydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg. Byddwn yn anfon\n> ateb yn Gymraeg i ohebiaeth a dderbynnir yn Gymraeg ac ni fydd gohebu yn\n> Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi. ----------------- On leaving the Welsh\n> Government this email was scanned for all known viruses. The Welsh\n> Government takes the protection of your data seriously. If you contact the\n> Welsh Government then our Privacy Notice\n>  \n> explains how we use your information and the ways in which we protect your\n> privacy. We welcome receiving correspondence in Welsh. Any correspondence\n> received in Welsh will be answered in Welsh and corresponding in Welsh will\n> not lead to a delay in responding.\n>"},{"id":"gm-19e4f946cd147d86","subject":"RE: Great House Farm, Williams/ Buckler Family, Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan","name":"RE: Great House Farm, Williams/ Buckler Family, Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan","date":"2026-05-22T12:06:13Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19e4f946cd147d86.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19e46e010d552d92-great-house-farm-williams-buckler-family-llandough-vale-of-glamorgan","scopeTopics":["rcahmw"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["rcahmw"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19e46e010d552d92","direction":"received","from":"NMR Enquiries","institutions":[{"id":"rcahmw","name":"RCAHMW (Royal Commission)"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19e4f946cd147d86.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Diolch am eich ymholiad. Byddwn yn ymateb cyn pen 20 diwrnod gwaith, oni bai bod chi wedi dewis ein Gwasanaeth Blaenoriaeth, wedyn byddwn mewn cysylltiad yn fuan gyda manylion pellach a gwybodaeth am dalu am y gwasanaeth.  Yn y cyfamser, gallai’r wybodaeth isod fod yn ddefnyddiol i chi:\nI gael gwybodaeth am safleoedd hanesyddol yng Nghymru a chael manylion am yr hyn sydd yn ein harchifau, ewch i’n cronfa ddata a’n catalog, https://coflein.gov.uk/cy/\nOs yn bosibl, rydym yn argymell eich bod yn trefnu apwyntiad i ymweld â’n llyfrgell ac ystafell chwilio<https://cbhc.gov.uk/gwasanaethau/> ein harchif, os hoffech weld deunydd yr archif â’ch llygaid eich hun a thynnu lluniau heb orfod talu amdanynt. Yn gyffredinol, mae gwneud hynny’n gynt ac yn rhatach o lawer nag archebu sganiau o bell.\nMae llawer o’n delweddau digidol ar gael i’w lawrlwytho, eu prynu a’u trwyddedu yn syth o Coflein. Mae canllawiau ynghylch sut mae gwneud hynny i’w gweld yn y fideo byr hwn: Lawrlwytho delweddau o Coflein<https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JYJmCNrRFqY> Os nad yw’r ddelwedd yr ydych yn chwilio amdani ar gael drwy’r system hon, y rheswm am hynny’n aml fydd cyfyngiadau hawlfraint, ac efallai y byddwch am chwilio am ddelwedd amgen sydd ar gael, os yw hynny’n bosibl.\nOs ydych yn gofyn am set ddata, cofiwch fod y data hwnnw ar gael am ddim yn awr dan y Drwydded Llywodraeth Agored<https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/doc/open-government-licence-cymraeg/version/3/>, ac y gallwch fynd ati eich hun i chwilio am ddata am y tir<https://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nmrw_jan2024_sites> a’r môr<https://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nmrw_jan2024_maritimesites> ar MapDataCymru neu Cymru Hanesyddol<https://historic-wales-rcahmw.hub.arcgis.com/#zoom=0&lat=300000&lon=258000&layers=BFFFFFTFFTTT>.\nOs ydych yn cysylltu â ni i ofyn am gael mynediad i safle hanesyddol, cofiwch nad yw’r Comisiwn Brenhinol yn berchen ar unrhyw un o’r safleoedd a gaiff eu rhestru ar Coflein, ac nad oes gennym unrhyw fanylion cyswllt ar gyfer perchnogion safleoedd. Bydd angen i chi gysylltu’n uniongyrchol â pherchnogion safleoedd i ofyn am gael mynediad.\nOs ydych yn poeni am gyflwr heneb neu safle hanesyddol, dylech gysylltu â Gwasanaeth Amgylchedd Hanesyddol Llywodraeth Cymru, sef Cadw, neu â’r awdurdod lleol perthnasol. Yn yr un modd, os oes gennych ymholiad am statws statudol safle hanesyddol neu am y broses gynllunio sy’n ymwneud ag Adeiladau Rhestredig neu Henebion Hynafol Cofrestredig, mae ystod o wybodaeth ar gael ar wefan Cadw: Cyngor a Chymorth | Cadw<https://cadw.llyw.cymru/cyngor-a-chymorth?_gl=1*15twz22*_ga*MjAwNzczNTg4MS4xNzExNjMwMjE1*_ga_B2BCVKM874*MTcxMzQ1MDIyNS43LjAuMTcxMzQ1MDIyNS42MC4wLjA.>.\nPrif rôl y Comisiwn Brenhinol yw creu, curadu a rhannu gwybodaeth am yr amgylchedd hanesyddol, ac rydym hefyd yn chwarae rhan fel ymgyngoreion yn y broses gynllunio ar gyfer yr amgylchedd hanesyddol ar sail y wybodaeth honno. Fodd bynnag, er bod yr amgylchedd hanesyddol yng Nghymru yn bwysig i’n staff, nid ydym ni fel corff yn gyfrifol am orfodi deddfwriaeth ym maes cynllunio a threftadaeth nac yn gyfrifol am ddiogelu safleoedd hanesyddol, ac nid ydym chwaith yn gweithredu fel grŵp lobïo.\n\n\nThank you for your enquiry.  We will respond within 20 working days, unless you have selected our Priority Service option in which case we will be in contact with further details and information on paying for this service soon.  In the meantime, you may find the information below helpful:\n\nFor information on historic sites in Wales and details of archive holdings see our database and catalogue, https://coflein.gov.uk/en/\n\nWhere possible we recommend that you make an appointment to visit our archive search room and library<https://rcahmw.gov.uk/services/> to view archive material in person and take photos without charge.  This is generally much quicker and cheaper than ordering scans remotely.\n\nMany of our digital images are available to download, purchase and licence directly from Coflein.  Guidance on how to do this can be seen in this short video: Downloading images from Coflein (youtube.com)<https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gKpL61F1M2w&t=3s>  If the image which you want is not available via this system, this is often due to copyright restrictions and you may wish to identify an alternative image which is available, if possible.\n\nIf you are requesting a dataset, please be advised that this data is now freely available under the Open Government Licence (nationalarchives.gov.uk)<https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/doc/open-government-licence/version/3/> and you can carry out the searches yourself for terrestrial<https://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nmrw_jan2024_sites> and maritime<https://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nmrw_jan2024_maritimesites> data on Data Map Wales and on Historic Wales (arcgis.com)<https://historic-wales-rcahmw.hub.arcgis.com/#zoom=0&lat=300000&lon=258000&layers=BFFFFFTFFTTT>.\n\nIf you are contacting us to request physical access to a historic site please note that the Royal Commission does not own any of the sites listed on Coflein, nor do we hold contact details for site owners.  You will need to contact the site owners directly with your request.\n\nIf you are concerned about the condition of a historic site or ancient monument please contact the Welsh Government’s Historic Environment Service, Cadw or the relevant local authority.  Similarly, if you have a query regarding the statutory status of a historic site or the planning process relating to Listed Buildings or Scheduled Ancient Monuments there is a range of information available on Cadw’s website: Advice & Support | Cadw (gov.wales)<https://cadw.gov.wales/advice-support>\nThe Royal Commission’s primary role is to create, curate and communicate information on the historic environment, we also play a role as consultees in the planning process for the historic environment on the basis of this information.  However, whilst our staff are passionate about the historic environment in Wales, as a body we are not responsible for enforcing planning or heritage legislation, safeguarding historic sites, nor do we act as a lobbying group.\n\n\n\n\n\nNMR Enquiries\nYmholiadau Cyhoeddus I Public Enquiries\nFfordd Penglais | Penglais Road, Aberystwyth, [postcode redacted]\n[phone redacted]\n[email redacted] | [email redacted]\nwww.cbhc.gov.uk | www.rcahmw.gov.uk\nNoddir gan Lywodraeth Cymru | Sponsored by Welsh Government\nRydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg a Saesneg. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn peri oedi.\nWe welcome correspondence in Welsh and English. Corresponding in Welsh will not lead to any delay.\n\nYn unol â Rheoliadau Safonau'r Gymraeg (Rhif 2) 2016, mae gennych hawl i gyfathrebu a gohebu â Chomisiwn Brenhinol Henebion Cymru yn eich dewis iaith. Er mwyn sicrhau ein bod yn cyflawni’r hawl, rhowch wybod i ni a ydych yn dymuno derbyn gohebiaeth a/neu alwadau ffôn oddi wrthym yn y Gymraeg. Bydd yr wybodaeth hon yn cael ei chofnodi gennym ni, a byddwn yn defnyddio'r iaith o’ch dewis ym mhob cyfathrebu yn y dyfodol. Diolch.\nUnder the Welsh Language Standards (No. 2) Regulations 2016, you have the right to communicate and correspond with the Royal Commission on the Ancient and Historical Monuments of Wales in your preferred language. To ensure we uphold this right, please let us know whether you wish to receive correspondence and/or telephone calls from us in Welsh. This information will be recorded by us, and we will use your preferred language in all future communication. Thank you.\n​\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]>\nSent: 20 May 2026 22:11\nTo: unclehowell <[email redacted]>\nSubject: Great House Farm, Williams/ Buckler Family, Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan\n\n\nSir / Madam,\n\nTo all those whom it concerns, BCC'd into this email.\n\nI write to request disclosure under the Freedom of Information Act 2000 and, where applicable, the Environmental Information Regulations 2004.\n\nThis request concerns Great House Farm / Ty Mawr (Great House), Llandough, associated estate lands, historic occupation and related heritage records. My family is Buckler and before that Williams, and we occupied Great House Farm from 1667 until our forceful dispossession of it in 1988.  Without our ownership ever having been estiguished and without BP ever having a ruling of ownership, only possession.\n\nIt's alledged our fee simple absolute and manorial title was stolen. And when BP registered our lands in their names in 1982, with our stolen title, my nan who challenged this all her life, during the 2 week land registry window to challenge BP's registration, which they achieved via purgergy and document/ identity fraud, she conveniently died. As my father did after his appeal of the 1987 mistake ruling was mistakely dismissed and his ECHR request blocked and home abruptly demolished, which he was prevented from stopping having been hospitalised and arrested without charges by armed force officers and possible SAS.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/0eXOoj7eUCo?si=hcgSA2pFgEE943HG\n\nI understand records may exist within the National Monuments Record of Wales, Historic Environment Record archives, archaeological consultations and planning files, including references already identified as:\n\n• Great House Farm HER site entry – GGAT03586s\n• Land at Great House Farm HER event entry – GGATE003872\n• The 1990 archaeological assessment commissioned by BP Properties Ltd.\n\nPlease provide copies of, or access to:\n\n  1.  All records concerning Great House Farm / Ty Mawr / Great House Estate, Llandough including:\n\n• surveys\n• reports\n• site notes\n• photographs\n• maps\n• aerial imagery\n• GIS layers\n• archive material\n• annotations\n• title references\n• occupancy references\n• landscape records\n• archaeological observations.\n\n  1.  All internal and external correspondence involving:\n\n• RCAHMW / NMRW\n• Cadw\n• GGAT / Heneb\n• Vale of Glamorgan Council\n• BP Properties Ltd\n• developers\n• planning authorities\n• landowners, estate representatives or consultants.\n\n  1.  Any documents discussing:\n\n• estate ownership\n• occupation history\n• family occupation or association\n• agricultural use\n• commemorative tree planting or memorial planting\n• estate boundaries\n• title history\n• acquisition history\n• disputes concerning ownership, heritage status or land use\n• estate restructuring, extinguishment, compulsory acquisition or title alteration.\n\n  1.  Copies of all material linked to GGAT03586s and GGATE003872, including the archaeological assessment and associated correspondence.\n  2.  Any records identifying historic occupants, proprietors, tenants or family associations, including references to the Williams family where present within archives.\n  3.  Metadata identifying file creators, accession history, provenance, dates and retention schedules.\n  4.  File indexes, archive references or schedules identifying further related material not already listed.\n\nPlease provide information electronically where possible.\n\nIf any material is withheld, please identify the exemption relied upon and provide the public interest assessment.\n\nRegards,\n\n\n\nRequester\n\n[address redacted]"},{"id":"gm-19e4b1bc46edc331","subject":"FW: Great House Farm, Llandough (aka Ty Mawr)","name":"FW: Great House Farm, Llandough (aka Ty Mawr)","date":"2026-05-21T15:16:03Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19e4b1bc46edc331.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19e4b1bc46edc331-fw-great-house-farm-llandough-aka-ty-mawr","scopeTopics":["cadw"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["cadw"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19e4b1bc46edc331","direction":"received","from":"(name not shown; gov.wales)","institutions":[{"id":"cadw","name":"Welsh Government / Cadw"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19e4b1bc46edc331.json","attachments":[{"name":"1990-08-xx - Great House Farmhouse, Llandough - GGAT Assessment.pdf","title":"1990-08-xx - Great House Farmhouse, Llandough - GGAT Assessment.pdf","url":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/pdf/evidence/1990-08-00_cadw_ggat-assessment-great-house-farmhouse-llandough.pdf","mime":"application/pdf","bytes":6504003,"sha256":"5ead134e4281cab9f7d4ed99cb85ceb8e247fd7ad3a4076c4267bd75252214e1"},{"name":"1988-07-29 - Barn at Great House Farmhouse, Llandough - survey photo.pdf","title":"1988-07-29 - Barn at Great House Farmhouse, Llandough - survey photo.pdf","url":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/pdf/evidence/1988-07-29_cadw_barn-survey-photo-high-resolution.pdf","mime":"application/pdf","bytes":960798,"sha256":"3efca3d5ec5bdd878a9846ff6089cf1cc478163a0ab724a281e8c29dd268037d"},{"name":"1988-07-29 - Great House Farmhouse, Llandough - survey photo.pdf","title":"1988-07-29 - Great House Farmhouse, Llandough - survey photo.pdf","url":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/pdf/evidence/1988-07-29_cadw_farmhouse-survey-photo-high-resolution.pdf","mime":"application/pdf","bytes":962955,"sha256":"451c6e3e28da630364405b75da6c0dd7aa8a3280ff635a0d635d11d3c2c6b54d"}],"textStatus":"available","text":"Good afternoon Requester,\n\nThank you for your email of 17 May regarding Great House Farm, Llandough, and also for your email of 20 May.\n\nI have searched our electronic records relating to Great House Farm and Barn and I can confirm they were assessed and rejected for listing in the 1988 community survey of Llandough. I have attached two photos, dated 29 July 1988. The \"YYY\" marking on the rear of the photo indicates that the buildings were considered marginally below the bar for listing. We hold no other electronic records.\n\nI note the SW Echo report suggesting that Cadw inspectors would visit again in December 1988, but I can find no record (as-yet) of this. The one paper file we have located, dated 2002, makes no mention of Great House Farm. I have asked a colleague to search for other related paper files, including the Welsh Office files, and I will write to you again when I have the result of the search. This may take a couple of weeks, and I must advise that is quite likely that at least some of these files will have been destroyed, but I will seek to disclose any records we recover to you.\n\nIn the meantime, would suggest contacting the following archives services, if you haven't already done so, as they may hold relevant records or be able to offer further assistance -\n\nGlamorgan Archives<https://glamarchives.gov.uk/>\nNational Library of Wales<https://www.library.wales/catalogues-searching/about-our-collections/archives>\nThe Royal Commission on the Ancient & Historical Monuments of Wales<https://coflein.gov.uk/en/contact>, including their Coflein<https://coflein.gov.uk/en/> catalogue of historic buildings.\n\nThese mapping services may also be of use to you:\nTithe Maps of Wales<https://places.library.wales/> (1840s)\nHistoric Ordnance Survey Maps<https://maps.nls.uk/geo/find/#zoom=5.0&lat=56.00000&lon=-4.00000&layers=102&b=1&z=0&point=0,0> (1820s-1960s)\n\nThe following research guide may too also be of interest - Land ownership records and deeds: an overview - The National Archives<https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/help-with-your-research/research-guides/land-ownership-records-and-deeds-an-overview/>.\n\nAnd finally for your records, you may wish to note this record about Great House Farm, held by the Royal Commission (Details | 20182 | Sites | Coflein<https://coflein.gov.uk/en/sites/20182>), this Historic Environment Record (GGAT02038s<https://archwilio.org.uk/her/chi3/report/page.php?watprn=GGAT02038s&dbname=ggat&tbname=core&sessid=CHI4idg6jee&queryid=Q528087001779371396>), and the attached Welsh Archaeological Trust report.\n\nYours Sincerely,\n\nMike Weatherhead\n\nArweinydd Tîm Gwaith Achosion / Casework Team Leader\nPolisi a Gwarchod / Policy and Protection\nArolygiaeth yr Amgylchedd Hanesyddol / Historic Environment Inspectorate\nCadw\n\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]>\nSent: 17 May 2026 05:11\nTo: Scheduled Monuments <[email redacted]>\nCc: [email redacted]; Cadw Planning <[email redacted]>; [email redacted]; Culture Division / Adran Diwylliant <[email redacted]>; [email redacted]\nSubject: BP vs Buckler, Great House Farm, Llandough\n\n\nTo whom it may concern,\n\nI am writing as the child of [member of the public] and William (\"Billy\") Buckler regarding the destruction of Great House Farm, Llandough, and the long-running dispute surrounding BP Properties Ltd v Buckler [1987].\n\nI have compiled a substantial historical archive, including newspaper reports, court references, family testimony, archaeological material, estate history, and public records relating to Great House Farm (\"Ty Mawr\"), a site associated with medieval, monastic, and Roman occupation.\n\nThe archive is currently being made into a website at:\n\nbpvsbuckler.datro.xyz<http://bpvsbuckler.datro.xyz/>\n\nThe material raises serious unresolved questions regarding:\n\n* The treatment of us Williams/Buckler family's longstanding occupation and asserted ownership claims. * The repeated use of possession proceedings without a full adjudication of superior title. * Missing or allegedly suppressed title documentation connected to Bute estate transfers and later transactions. * The demolition of an allegedly medieval structure during an active heritage controversy. * Whether the archaeological significance of the site was fully disclosed during planning and demolition processes. * The wider public interest and cultural significance of the site and its destruction.\n\nIt's looking to me like my family actually did obtain fee simple absolute and manorial title. Then it was stolen and attempts were made by the possessor of the stolen title, to make my family lessee's / tenants as we were when we were on the virtual freehold (999 year lease) aquired in 1667. Thereafter it appears to be a death of a thousand cuts of proceedural unfairness, audacity, fraud, court \"mistakes\" , purgery and more.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/4GXmR7mAas0?feature=shared\n\nContemporaneous newspaper coverage from the 1970s and 1980s demonstrates that these concerns were publicly known at the time, including heritage objections, petitions, and public claims of ancestral ownership.\n\nI am requesting:\n\n  1.  Review and preservation consideration of all surviving records connected to Great House Farm.\n  2.  Clarification as to whether any formal heritage review or investigation into the demolition process was ever completed.\n  3.  Guidance regarding access to archival and planning materials relevant to the site.\n  4.  Consideration of whether this matter warrants renewed historical or heritage examination.\n\nI appreciate that some claims remain disputed or unproven, and I am not asking recipients to accept every allegation as established fact. However, I believe the totality of the historical material warrants serious review rather than dismissal.\n\nI would be grateful for acknowledgement of receipt and guidance on the appropriate next steps.\n\nKind regards,\n\n\n\nRequester Esq. \n\nManor of Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan\n\n\n\nSganiwyd y neges hon am bob feirws hysbys wrth iddi adael Llywodraeth Cymru. Mae Llywodraeth Cymru yn cymryd o ddifrif yr angen i ddiogelu eich data. Os cysylltwch â Llywodraeth Cymru, mae ein hysbysiad preifatrwydd<\"https://gov.wales/about/welsh-government-privacy-notice/?lang=cy\"> yn esbonio sut rydym yn defnyddio eich gwybodaeth a sut rydym yn diogelu eich preifatrwydd. Rydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg. Byddwn yn anfon ateb yn Gymraeg i ohebiaeth a dderbynnir yn Gymraeg ac ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi. ----------------- On leaving the Welsh Government this email was scanned for all known viruses. The Welsh Government takes the protection of your data seriously. If you contact the Welsh Government then our Privacy Notice<https://gov.wales/about/welsh-government-privacy-notice/?lang=en> explains how we use your information and the ways in which we protect your privacy. We welcome receiving correspondence in Welsh. Any correspondence received in Welsh will be answered in Welsh and corresponding in Welsh will not lead to a delay in responding."},{"id":"gm-19e46daebd266c25","subject":"Re: Great House Farm, Llandough","name":"Re: Great House Farm, Llandough","date":"2026-05-20T19:26:50Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19e46daebd266c25.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19e46dae9e551406-great-house-farm-llandough","scopeTopics":["cadw"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["cadw"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19e46dae9e551406","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"cadw","name":"Welsh Government / Cadw"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19e46daebd266c25.json","textStatus":"available","text":"I have all my evidence. Consider CADW on notice.\n\nAll I'm asking is you cooperate to find out what really happened in this\ncase.\n\nI'm putting together the timeline and all evidence as I believe its in the\npublic interest and I have reason to suspect fraud.\n\nMore so I believe my family have the rightful superior claim and manorial\ntitle.\n\nAs the possible, rightful possesor of lordship of manor of Llandough, I\nwould be the executor of the estate.\n\nFraud unravels all. And the limitation clock only begins from discovery not\noccurance of fraud. Which is this year.\n\nSo please consider yourselves, CADW, on formal notice of this concern I'm\nraising of fraud. Not just for reperations and narrative justice for my\nfamily, but for archelogical and historical corrections, as the gaps in\nrecords and proceedural unfairness and court mistakes and things like the\nITV press coverage and 1978 news article which appear to have been\nsupressed, all imply fraud.\n\nIn summary it looks as if we weren't tenants but in fact held virtual\nfreehold since 1667 and eventual fee simple absolute manorial title. Which\nwas stolen. And thereafter my families standing was diminished and we were\neventually dispossessed by armed force. Which neither estinguished our land\nclaim nor means we surrendered, transfered, sold or gifted it. And\ntherefore still retain claim. A reason to pursue this matter.\n\nMy family were and still are terrorised into silence and alledgedly nothing\ncould be done about this because of the time that has past. That was until\nI discovered the fraud.\n\nIt would be ill advised for CADW or individuals, to participate in any past\nor ongoing efforts to conseal occurances of fraud.\n\nAlso consider this a freedom of information request. I require of CADW\nanswers. Records. All you hold concerning my family and estate. Without\nfurther delay.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester Esq. \nManor of Llandough, Vale of Glamorgan\nbpvsbuckler.datro.xyz\n\nOn Wed, 25 Feb 2026, 08:37 , <[email redacted]> wrote:\n\n> Dear Requester,\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you for your email regarding Great House Farm, Llandough. However, I\n> must inform you that much of the content in your correspondence falls\n> outside the scope of Cadw’s work.\n>\n>\n>\n> In relation to the claim of 'archaeological suppression' involving the\n> discovery of a Roman soldier in full armour in 1888, any such find would\n> typically have been reported in the antiquarian press at either the\n> national or local level. It is possible that records of this nature may be\n> held by local museums. It is worth noting that the first Ancient Monuments\n> Act was passed in 1882, focusing solely on bringing threatened ancient\n> monuments into guardianship. The legal recognition of nationally important\n> archaeology did not come about until the Ancient Monuments Consolidation\n> and Amendment Act of 1913.\n>\n>\n>\n> Upon reviewing the Cof Cymru database, I found that there are no\n> designated historic assets relevant to this topic. However, Archwilio\n> contains two records that document the involvement of the Glamorgan-Gwent\n> Archaeological Trust (GGAT):\n>\n>\n>\n> 1. Great House Farm HER site entry - GGAT03586s\n>\n> 2. Land at Great House Farm HER event entry - GGATE003872, which includes\n> GGAT's 1990 archaeological assessment report commissioned by BP Properties\n> Ltd.\n>\n>\n>\n> Any advice or correspondence that Cadw may have provided should be\n> preserved in the Royal Commission on the Ancient and Historical Monuments\n> of Wales (RCAHMW) National Monuments Record of Wales (NMRW). I recommend\n> you contact the NMRW to access any relevant documents. Additionally, you\n> may want to reach out to Heneb and the Vale of Glamorgan Local Planning\n> Authority (LPA) to explore what records they might hold concerning this\n> matter.\n>\n>\n>\n> Kind regards,\n>\n>\n>\n> Jenna Arnold\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> *Jenna Arnold*\n>\n> Arolygiaeth yr Amgylchedd Hanesyddol / Historic Environment Inspectorate\n>\n> Llywodraeth Cymru / Welsh Government\n>\n> Cadw, Rhydycar Business Park | Parc Business Rhydycar, Merthyr Tydfil |\n> Merthyr Tudful [postcode redacted]\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> [image: https://i.ibb.co/CVwfkw2/Cadw-er-lles-pawb.png]\n> <http://www.aelodaethcadw.gwasanaeth.llyw.cymru/>\n>\n> [image: https://i.ibb.co/NxJvcfW/For-us-all-to-keep.png]\n> <http://www.cadwmembership.service.gov.wales/>\n>\n>\n>\n> Ymunwch â Cadw <https://www.aelodaethcadw.gwasanaeth.llyw.cymru/>\n>\n> Join Cadw <https://www.cadwmembership.service.gov.wales/>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> *From:* Requester <[email redacted]>\n> *Sent:* 06 February 2026 05:21\n> *To:* Scheduled Monuments <[email redacted]>\n> *Cc:* [email redacted]; [email redacted];\n> [email redacted]\n> *Subject:* Formal Report of Suspected Indigenous Dispossession, Systemic\n> Fraud, and Archaeological Suppression – Great House Farm, Llandough\n>\n>\n>\n> *To whom it may concern,*\n>\n> I am writing to formally report a long-standing and systemic case of what\n> I suspect may be dispossession, fraudulent misrepresentation, and\n> archaeological suppression concerning my family’s ancestral land, Great\n> House Farm (historically Tŷ Mawr/Cedfin), Llandough.\n>\n> I am the descendant of the Williams/Buckler family, who held de facto\n> possession and indigenous tenure of this land from 1667 until our forced\n> displacement in 1988. I am currently the only member of my family who is\n> not too traumatized to speak of these events. My mother (Mary Williams\n> daughter in law), remains to this day too terrified to speak up about this\n> multi-generational \"lawfare\" campaign waged against us. Our family have not\n> seen justice or received reperations, nor have we sued or reopened this\n> case under limitations act clauses of fraud and consealment. Yet. But this\n> may not be just a grave injustice upon our family, but our nations\n> archeological history and indigenous people on our ancestral birthland. We\n> are Welsh Nationals, Welsh speaking, under Welsh flags. This is some\n> British Colonisation-grade trespass I'm seeing and it's tandamount to a\n> declaration of civil war on me, my family, community and people.\n>\n> *The core of this report involves the following suspicions I have of\n> concealment and fraud (see attached for my timeline and notes):*\n>\n>    1. *Archaeological Suppression:* In 1888, my family discovered a Roman\n>    soldier in full armor beneath the living room floorboards. This discovery\n>    provided \"actual notice\" of the site's national significance a century\n>    before its demolition. We allege this notice was systematically suppressed\n>    by corporate and state actors to allow for commercial development.\n>    2. *Fraudulent Misrepresentation in Planning:* During the 1987\n>    planning process, the 13th-century fortified manor was described by\n>    developers as a \"dilapidated utility building\" or \"ruined barn\" to secure a\n>    demolition permit that would have otherwise been denied under heritage\n>    protection laws.\n>    3. *Legal Identity Falsification:* In 1974, BP Pension Trust issued a\n>    \"unilateral license\" intended to bypass our possessory title. This\n>    correspondence was falsely addressed to \"Mrs. Buckler\" to bind my\n>    grandmother to her deceased husband’s name and legal status, despite her\n>    public rejection of their claim. She used her maiden name Williams and her\n>    husband Bucklers' intent to accept license died with him. My nan also\n>    alledgedly didn't expressly deny the offer and so accepted through her\n>    silence. But she was near enough voiceless from screaming for decades that\n>    she will not do deals with any of them. That's a different kind of silence,\n>    if any. And to have responded to license to Mrs Buckler would have been\n>    identify fraud as she was Mrs Williams. And she did in fact reject BPs\n>    license offer and advanced, countless times, but publically. I believe\n>    these objections were suppressed too. There's allegedly news coverage from\n>    October 30th 1974 but I'm yet to find the evidence. But see attached a 1978\n>    article I unearthed. My nan clearly maintains her position \"It's my home\".\n>    4. You'll also see in landmark legal case BP Vs Buckler 1987 the judge\n>    says that she might have responded to BP, but to BP Pension Trust, but the\n>    claimant was BP Properties Ltd which were two separate companies. But in my\n>    dads appeal when he challenges that BP Pension Trust Ltd should have been\n>    the claimant the same judge dismissed his appeal citing both BP companies\n>    are effectively the same. This is clearly a case of heads BP won, Tails we\n>    lost.\n>    5. *Missing Evidence:* Critical title deeds and Bute Estate records\n>    from our families deal with Daniel Thomas, for title after quarrying\n>    completed, in exchange for our permission to quarry, also disappeared from\n>    the loft of our home and the Cardiff Library according to my families\n>    accounts, precisely when they were needed to support our ownership claim in\n>    the Court of Appeal. Regardless we should have still remained on grounds\n>    we'd been there since 1667, and failing that on grounds of adverse\n>    possession.\n>    6. It was evident lawfare to remove us, to get to the burials known\n>    about since 1888, BP profited from the land sale, the state and Crown have\n>    the exhevated artifacts and my displaced family are left traumatised,\n>    cheated and dead. My dad's appeal was dismissed to when it shouldn't have\n>    been and his ECHR request blocked, before dying abruptly at age just 41.\n>\n> The confirmation in 1994 of a high-status Roman villa and over 1,000\n> burials on the exact site of our demolished home proves that the \"official\n> narrative\" used to evict us was predicated on the concealment of known\n> facts.\n>\n> Under *Section 32 of the Limitation Act 1980*, the limitation period for\n> action does not begin until such fraud or concealment is discovered. As the\n> heir to this possessory title, I am seeking a formal investigation into the\n> institutional failures that permitted this dispossession and the\n> destruction of a national monument.\n>\n> I have compiled my account and understanding of these events in timeline\n> order and I'm prepared to provide further testimony and evidence.\n>\n> Given the extreme trauma my family has suffered, I request that all\n> correspondence be handled with the gravity this human rights violation\n> deserves.\n>\n> Considering the lack of cooperation from my traumatised family. And\n> authorities who maybe complicit and implicated. And possible corruption of\n> records and fraud and consealment, I'm open to the fact my account and\n> understanding may not be perfect. But if even a single other person or\n> group looked into this too, even briefly, I'm sure you'll agree there's\n> merrit to the concerns I'm raising with all of this.\n>\n> I look forward to your response regarding the appropriate next steps for\n> investigation and restitution.\n>\n> I do ask for some small sense of urgency in that justice delayed is\n> justice denied. I understand this is ancient news for many, but my family\n> still feel a tremendous sense of disposition and disposition over all of\n> this. Bringing this all up means to re live it.\n>\n> I will have been 3 or 4 at the time, but I still remember my toys in our\n> house, being bulldozed. And my mum handing us rocks to throw at them,\n> bulldozing down our home. As my dad was held without charges to prevent him\n> defending us and our home. I'd have to visit him in a hospital bed after\n> his heart attack and see him in a coffin shortly afterwards.\n>\n> I'm 41 years old now. I'm a certified and experienced military commander\n> and leader. I left my military career with honours. I finished 22 years in\n> Oath in 2024, bond afide, my last 2 employed under the Welsh government.\n>\n> I kindly request but also require of you, that this matter be layed to\n> rest correctly.\n>\n> My family never surrendered. Never relinquished claim to that land. We\n> we're in honour in that we weren't there with force, stealth/ secrecy or\n> license. But BP and the British State did use force, stealth/ secrecy and\n> license.\n>\n> All things considered I believe I find myself in honour here, and the\n> British Establishment in dishonour. This matter must be escalated. I'm duty\n> bound.\n>\n>\n>\n> Regards,\n>\n>\n>\n> Requester\n> Sganiwyd y neges hon am bob feirws hysbys wrth iddi adael Llywodraeth\n> Cymru. Mae Llywodraeth Cymru yn cymryd o ddifrif yr angen i ddiogelu eich\n> data. Os cysylltwch â Llywodraeth Cymru, mae ein hysbysiad preifatrwydd\n> yn esbonio sut rydym yn defnyddio eich gwybodaeth a sut rydym yn diogelu\n> eich preifatrwydd. Rydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg. Byddwn yn anfon\n> ateb yn Gymraeg i ohebiaeth a dderbynnir yn Gymraeg ac ni fydd gohebu yn\n> Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi. ----------------- On leaving the Welsh\n> Government this email was scanned for all known viruses. The Welsh\n> Government takes the protection of your data seriously. If you contact the\n> Welsh Government then our Privacy Notice\n> <https://gov.wales/about/welsh-government-privacy-notice/?lang=en>\n> explains how we use your information and the ways in which we protect your\n> privacy. We welcome receiving correspondence in Welsh. Any correspondence\n> received in Welsh will be answered in Welsh and corresponding in Welsh will\n> not lead to a delay in responding.\n>"},{"id":"gm-19e46a26bb566e05","subject":"Re: Great House Farm","name":"Re: Great House Farm","date":"2026-05-20T18:25:09Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: 