sis that BP Pension Trust and BP Properties were separate entities;\n   - In subsequent proceedings, the same entities were treated as\n   effectively the same when dismissing an appeal.\n\nThis inconsistency materially affected the outcome and raises concerns as\nto fairness and legal coherence.\n7. Avoidance of substantive title determination\n\nThe case proceeded within a possession framework rather than determining\nunderlying ownership in an appropriate forum, resulting in the absence of a\ndefinitive adjudication on title.\n8. Possible misrepresentation / constructive fraud\n\nConcerns arise that:\n\n   - licence arrangements were asserted under a disputed identity;\n   - the authority of the issuing entity was unclear;\n   - planning and property descriptions may have mischaracterised the\n   nature of the building.\n\n9. New or previously unavailable evidence\n\nThis includes:\n\n   - missing or unavailable title documentation;\n   - evidence relating to arrangements (including that attributed to Daniel\n   Thomas);\n   - family testimony not previously considered;\n   - subsequent archaeological findings supporting the site’s significance.\n\n------------------------------\nC. Core Position\n\nTaken together, the 1987 outcome is arguable as unsafe due to flawed\nassumptions, missing evidence, and procedural unfairness. The resulting\ndispossession, demolition, and long-term harm give rise to a basis for\nreparative consideration.\n------------------------------\nD. Why the Matter Requires Proper Consideration\n\nThis matter should not be dismissed solely on the basis of judicial\nindependence because:\n\n   - The issues raised concern the *integrity of the process*, not simply\n   disagreement with a result;\n   - Allegations of *misrepresentation, non-disclosure, and procedural\n   irregularity* fall outside ordinary appellate disagreement;\n   - There is a *public interest dimension*, including loss of heritage and\n   potential systemic issues;\n   - The