onalarchives.gov.uk/details/c/F236775> The\n> manorial records for the Manor of Llandough are held at the National\n> Library of Wales, Glamorgan Archives and West Glamorgan Archives, and their\n> catalogues can be accessed on-line : NLW Catalogue\n> <https://discover.library.wales/discovery/search?vid=44WHELF_NLW:44WHELF_NLW_NUI>\n> , Glamorgan Archives Catalogue <https://canfod.glamarchives.gov.uk/en/>\n> and Archive Collections - Swansea\n> <https://www.swansea.gov.uk/archivecollections?lang=en>\n>\n>\n>\n> The Welsh entries in the online MDR were added in the 1990’s were based on\n> the survey returns to the Manorial Documents Committee received from\n> institutions and private owners during the 1920’s. These were subsequently\n> passed to the custody of the Historical Manuscripts Commission in 1959\n> which amalgamated with the Public Record Office to form The National\n> Archives in 2003. I have requested to see the original returns for Manor of\n> Llandough to see if they can provide any additional information on the\n> documentation relating to the manor (Ref. *HMC 9/311*:\n> https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/r/C5879336 )\n>\n>\n>\n> There are several other manors with a similar name in Glamorgan and a MDR\n> search using the search term “Llandough” gives the following result: Search\n> results: Llandough Glamorgan | The National Archives\n> <https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/results/c?_q=Llandough&_sf=text_man&_ocn=Glamorgan&_naet=M&_st=mdrc>\n> Sometimes the administration of different manors were brought together and\n> this appears to have occurred  with the Llandough and St May’s Church\n> Manors :\n> https://archives.library.wales/index.php/manors-of-llandough-and-st-mary-church\n>\n>\n>\n> I will contact you again once I have had an opportunity to look at the\n> original returns sent to Manorial Documents Committee. I hope this\n> preliminary information is of assistance.\n>\n>\n>\n> Yours sincerely\n>\n>\n>\n> [image: The National Archives logo]\n>\n> *Philip Gale *|* Head of the Collections and Cultural Property Team*\n> T: 020 3908 X 9193 | W: nationalarchives.gov.uk\n> <http://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/>\n> Twitter: @UkNatArchives\n> The National Archives, Kew, Richmond, Surrey [postcode redacted]\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> ------------------------------\n>\n> *From:* Requester <[email redacted]>\n> *Sent:* Wednesday, February 18, 2026 5:49:54 PM\n> *To:* Philip Gale <[email redacted]>\n> *Subject:* Re: Formal Notice for Record: Church View Close, Llandough /\n> BP vs Buckler 1987\n>\n>\n>\n> *EXTERNAL*: This email originates from outside of The National Archives.\n>\n> Dear Mr Gale,\n>\n> Thank you for your reply of 12 February. I would like to clarify and\n> narrow my request to ensure there is no misunderstanding regarding the\n> records I am trying to locate.\n>\n> My correspondence is *not* seeking an adjudication of ownership or title\n> — I fully accept the stated remit boundaries of the Manorial Documents\n> Register (MDR). Rather, my enquiry concerns the existence of *any\n> archival reference, index card, catalogue entry, or pointer to a document\n> historically known as the “Williams Title”*, which was, according to\n> contemporaneous family testimony, at one point:\n>\n>    1. Held in the *local public library* in the first half of the\n>    twentieth century; and\n>    2. Indexed in some manner (e.g., an index card or catalogue reference)\n>    that persisted even after the original document could not be located.\n>\n> My understanding of the MDR — as described on your own website — is that\n> it exists to provide brief descriptions of manorial records and details of\n> their locations:\n>\n> “An official index of English and Welsh manorial records providing brief\n> descriptions of documents and details of their locations… It does *not*\n> include title deeds or conveyances, and is *not* a register of ownership\n> of manorial lordships or rights.”\n> https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/manor-search\n> <https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/manor-search?utm_source=chatgpt.com>\n>\n> Based on that description, I would be grateful if you could confirm\n> whether:\n>\n>    1. *Any catalogue entries, index cards, reference pointers, or\n>    archival descriptions* relating to the *Manor of Llandough* or the *Williams\n>    family title* exist in the MDR or related archival catalogues.\n>    2. If so, the *reference identifiers* and information on how these can\n>    be consulted in person or via digitised records.\n>\n> To be precise, I am looking for historical references to the *index card\n> or listing* for the Williams Title — whether held in a library, local\n> record office, or other institutional archive — rather than a claim for\n> ownership judgment itself.\n>\n> If such a reference does not currently appear in the MDR, I would greatly\n> appreciate guidance on where any known catalogue cards or entry lists for\n> manorial documents from *Glamorgan / Llandough* prior to 1950 might now\n> reside (e.g., regional archives, National Archives holdings, or affiliated\n> local repository catalogues).\n>\n> Anyway I anticipate your forgiveness, if I have erred in writing to you\n> earlier.\n>\n> Thank you again and in advance for your assistance.\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n>\n> Requester\n>\n> On Wed, 18 Feb 2026 at 14:30, Philip Gale <\n> [email redacted]> wrote:\n>\n> Dear Requester,\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you for your e-mail of 10 February whose contents are noted. The\n> National Archives does not hold any records relating to your enquiry. The\n> Manorial Documents Register which this department administers is a register\n> for the location of manorial documents and not of the ownership and descent\n> of manorial titles:\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/archives-sector/finding-records-in-discovery-and-other-databases/manorial-documents-register/\n>\n>\n>\n> A similar position holds for The National Archives responsibilities for\n> public records and concerns about the past management of official records\n> is a matter for the relevant government department such as HM Land\n> Registry.\n>\n>\n>\n> Yours sincerely\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> [image: The National Archives logo]\n>\n> *Philip Gale *|* Head of the Collections and Cultural Property Team*\n> T: 020 3908 X 9193 | W: nationalarchives.gov.uk\n> <http://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/>\n> Twitter: @UkNatArchives\n> The National Archives, Kew, Richmond, Surrey [postcode redacted]\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> -----Original Message-----\n>\n> From: Requester <[email redacted]>\n>\n> Sent: 10 February 2026 22:15\n>\n> To: Archives Sector Leadership <[email redacted]>\n>\n> Cc: [email redacted]; [email redacted];\n> Paul Egan <[email redacted]>; [email redacted];\n> [email redacted]\n>\n> Subject: Formal Notice for Record: Church View Close, Llandough / BP vs\n> Buckler 1987\n>\n>\n>\n>         You don't often get email from [email redacted]. Learn\n> why this is important <https://aka.ms/LearnAboutSenderIdentification>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> EXTERNAL: This email originates from outside of The National Archives.\n>\n>\n>\n> FORMAL NOTICE OF RECORD CHALLENGE, ASSERTION OF RIGHT, AND DEMAND FOR\n> ENGAGEMENT\n>\n>\n>\n> Dear Sir/Madam,\n>\n>\n>\n> This Notice is issued for placement on official record and for reliance.\n> It is not informal correspondence. I urge you not to resort to silence,\n> deflection, or delay, as doing so following receipt of this notice\n> constitutes knowing reliance upon, and maintenance of, records alleged to\n> be materially defective through fraud, concealment, denial of due process,\n> and systemic erasure. Please report any suspicious activity and\n> discrepancies identified.\n>\n>\n>\n> This Notice is issued in pursuit of narrative justice at this stage;\n> formal judicial remedies are expressly reserved. It serves to document our\n> family's side of the story before it is completely erased and history\n> rewritten, ensuring awareness among custodians of land, heritage, heraldry,\n> archaeology, and public records.\n>\n>\n>\n> My elder brother, Rhys Buckler, and I are the sons of the late William\n> Buckler (d. 1991), defendant in the landmark legal case BP Properties Ltd v\n> Buckler (1987), and the grandchildren of the late Mary Williams, whose\n> dispossession, marginalisation, and exclusion from judicial determination\n> remain unresolved matters of record. Mary Williams was repeatedly and\n> materially falsely addressed/mislabeled in legal and administrative records\n> as “Mrs Buckler,” a misidentification that was never judicially tested and\n> which directly affected the handling, scope, and outcome of proceedings.\n> The Williams/Buckler family held claim to the land title, having acquired\n> and occupied Great House Farm continuously since 1667, based on family\n> records, historical evidence, and centuries of resistance to adverse claims.\n>\n>\n>\n> I place on record that I am the first member of my family able to speak\n> publicly and formally on these matters. Other close family members remain\n> too traumatised and intimidated by the historical conduct of these\n> proceedings—including violent eviction, demolition, and loss of\n> livelihood—to engage, despite the continuing consequences of dispossession.\n> That silence must not be misread as consent, acquiescence, or abandonment\n> of right. There is a material distinction between free silence and silence\n> produced by fear, trauma, and coercive experience.\n>\n>\n>\n> The story, as we have pieced it together, reveals a multi-generational\n> pattern of lawfare and siege: from Norman/English conquests in 1100\n> granting lordships over ancient Welsh Christian landscapes, through\n> monastic (1215–1539) and Crown seizures (1539 onward), to industrial\n> encroachments (e.g., 1876 Limeworks severance without consent) and modern\n> corporate possession actions. This culminated in the 1988 demolition of an\n> 800-year-old farmhouse amid police standoffs, leaving the family homeless\n> and the site scarred, now buried under Church View Close. Archaeological\n> vindication came too late—1994 excavations revealed over 1,000 Roman and\n> medieval burials, confirming family warnings (e.g., the 1870 Roman soldier\n> discovery under the living room floor, suppressed by authorities)—yet\n> heritage records celebrate the site while omitting our dispossession.\n>\n>\n>\n> The following assertions of right are now formally placed on record. They\n> have never been lawfully adjudicated or extinguished:\n>\n>\n>\n> 1.      Rhys Buckler may be the rightful Manorial Lord of the Manor of\n> Llandough, by right of primogeniture and continuous hereditary connection\n> arising from our family’s occupation of Great House Farm from 1667 until\n> our narrative, legal, and physical dispossession.\n>\n> 2.      I assert a claim to act as Squire of the Manor of Llandough, as\n> executive representative and guardian of the Buckler–Williams estate and\n> matriarchal lineage, with a mandate to pursue correction of record,\n> restitution, and restorative remedies.\n>\n>\n>\n> These are assertions of right, not settled declarations, placed on record\n> pending proper adjudication—which has been historically obstructed through\n> procedural narrowing, missing documents, and avoidance of full hearings.\n>\n>\n>\n> In 1974, Mary Williams was twice prevented from presenting her case and\n> obtaining a judicial determination of superior ownership, despite claims\n> grounded in long occupation, intent, and possession. The court confined\n> inquiry to animus possidendi (intention to possess) and excluded animus\n> domini (intention to own), reclassifying her from putative owner to mere\n> occupier and foreclosing title adjudication. BP sought possession only, not\n> ownership rulings—a choice unexplained and significant. Proceedings were\n> adjourned or quashed without resolution.\n>\n>\n>\n> This occurred despite evidence of ownership intent, including:\n>\n>\n>\n> *       Centuries-long occupation and resistance (e.g., failed 1938\n> auction due to local support; post-1945 rent disputes).\n>\n> *       Roman-era remains and historical significance (e.g., Guglielmo\n> Marconi's 1897 residence during radio experiments, with family aid erased\n> from records).\n>\n> *       Unformalized deals like the 1895–1905 Daniel Thomas quarrying\n> permission for promised title, undermined by vanished deeds.\n>\n>\n>\n> Judicial ownership examination would have engaged archaeological/heritage\n> protections, incompatible with redevelopment—consequences avoided, raising\n> credibility concerns.\n>\n>\n>\n> Serious unresolved anomalies persist, indicating something is still not\n> right with the record:\n>\n>\n>\n> *       Licence correspondence dated 31 October 1974 surfacing only on 4\n> November, potentially interrupting proceedings to prevent hearings.\n>\n> *       Claims of 1974 benevolent withdrawal under press scrutiny\n> unsupported by verifiable coverage.\n>\n> *       Land registration before Mary Williams’s 1983 death without title\n> determination; disputes possibly lodged/suppressed by Land Registry.\n>\n> *       Inconsistent treatment of BP entities across proceedings to family\n> detriment.\n>\n> *       Procedural barriers preventing effective defense (e.g.,\n> interrupted essential services post-licence; 1960–1987 court rulings\n> dismissing ancestral title as \"notion\" without evidence).\n>\n> *       Knowing bypass of archaeological constraints (e.g., 1978 trenches\n> ignored; no contemporaneous 1970s/1990s press on discoveries amid\n> development).\n>\n> *       Missing key documents (e.g., 1667 conveyance, 1811–1835 Lambert\n> Williams agreements proving status, 1982–84 library deeds vanishing).\n>\n> *       Risks of further erasure: Heritage databases celebrate \"Llandough\n> Roman villa cemetery\" while omitting our saga; modern residents unaware of\n> the \"battleground\" site.\n>\n>\n>\n> These anomalies suggest systemic theft, from colonial English subjugation\n> (e.g., 1543–1770 orbit shifts) to corporate siege, during Wales'\n> cultural/economic transitions. Authority derived through such means is\n> illegitimate under natural justice, customary Welsh principles, and\n> moral-legal frameworks. The record's distortion secures advantage, but\n> passage of time does not validate it.\n>\n>\n>\n> Preliminary assessment indicates cumulative benefits from redevelopment\n> (absent title extinguishment) exceed £100 million, forming a basis for\n> reparations—covering lost land, artifacts, livelihood, and trauma.\n>\n>\n>\n> I require relevant public servants, custodians, and office-holders\n> (including Land Registry, heraldry bodies, museums, archaeologists, MPs,\n> historians, and councillors) to cease delay, engage substantively,\n> review/correct/annotate records where defective, and prevent further\n> rewriting of history. Justice delayed is denied, especially with verifiable\n> evidence available.\n>\n>\n>\n> This Notice:\n>\n>\n>\n> *       Challenges the record's accuracy, completeness, and legitimacy.\n>\n> *       Place assertions of title/standing on record pending adjudication.\n>\n> *       Reserves all rights without prejudice.\n>\n> *       Puts officials/beneficiaries on notice that silence/reliance may\n> aggravate responsibility.\n>\n>\n>\n> For the avoidance of doubt, His Majesty the King was notified yesterday.\n>\n>\n>\n> This serves as an invitation to engage constructively before reliance\n> becomes untenable.\n>\n>\n>\n> Regards, Requester On behalf of the Buckler–Williams family [address redacted],\n> [location redacted], Vale of Glamorgan, Wales. https://bpvsbuckler.datro.xyz/ <\n> https://bpvsbuckler.datro.xyz/>\n>\n>\n>\n> Postscript — Disability and Succession (Material Notice): The\n> heir-apparent to the asserted Manorial Lordship is Rhys Buckler’s son, a\n> young man with Down syndrome. Any obstruction, marginalisation, or\n> differential treatment affecting his inheritance would engage the Equality\n> Act 2010 and UK international obligations on disability rights. Disability\n> cannot bar succession or recognition. This is recorded to prevent claims of\n> ignorance.\n>\n> Please don't print this e-mail unless you really need to.\n> -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n> National Archives Disclaimer This email and any files transmitted with it\n> are intended solely for the use of the individual(s) to whom they are\n> addressed. If you are not the intended recipient and have received this\n> email in error, please notify the sender and delete the email. Opinions,\n> conclusions and other information in this message and attachments that do\n> not relate to the official business of The National Archives are neither\n> given nor endorsed by it.\n> ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n>\n>\n> Please don't print this e-mail unless you really need to.\n> -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n> National Archives Disclaimer This email and any files transmitted with it\n> are intended solely for the use of the individual(s) to whom they are\n> addressed. If you are not the intended recipient and have received this\n> email in error, please notify the sender and delete the email. Opinions,\n> conclusions and other information in this message and attachments that do\n> not relate to the official business of The National Archives are neither\n> given nor endorsed by it.\n> ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n>\n>\n> Please don't print this e-mail unless you really need to.\n> -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n> National Archives Disclaimer This email and any files transmitted with it\n> are intended solely for the use of the individual(s) to whom they are\n> addressed. If you are not the intended recipient and have received this\n> email in error, please notify the sender and delete the email. Opinions,\n> conclusions and other information in this message and attachments that do\n> not relate to the official business of The National Archives are neither\n> given nor endorsed by it.\n> ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n>\n>"},{"id":"gm-19c9b0d3fb9acdf1","subject":"FW: Manor of Llandough - MDR Entries","name":"FW: Manor of Llandough - MDR Entries","date":"2026-02-26T17:44:15Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19c9b0d3fb9acdf1.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19c908fa7cddd153-fw-manor-of-llandough-mdr-entries","scopeTopics":["tna"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["tna"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19c908fa7cddd153","direction":"received","from":"Philip Gale","institutions":[{"id":"tna","name":"The National Archives"}],"refs":["F236775"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19c9b0d3fb9acdf1.json","attachments":[{"name":"HMC 9.311 Llandough Manor return.jpg","title":"Manorial Documents Register: HMC 9.311 Llandough manor return","url":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/pdf/foi/2026-02-26_tna_hmc-9-311_llandough-manor-return.jpg","mime":"image/jpeg","bytes":1821335,"sha256":"da2330be64850d79e7826f874b49133a7f1c6e0c541831317dcc6d872d14d284"}],"textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester,\n\nI have had an opportunity to view the file of original returns to the Manorial Documents Committee (Ref. HMC9/311) and the single return for the Manor of Llandough (see the attached image), and it provides less information than the existing entries on the on-line MDR. The information on the other manors in the return is equally sparse This may be because many of the surviving manorial records were not in custody of the National Library of Wales at the time of the original survey.\n\nThe best course of action is to search the archive service catalogues, and particularly the on-line catalogue of the National Library of Wales. Is it difficult to advise you further without more information, for example do you have rough date for the document called ‘Williams’s Title? A particularly  relevant archive collection appears to be the Penrice and Margam Estate Records, [c. 1147]-[19 cent.] / Penrice and Margam estate. - National Library of Wales<https://discover.library.wales/discovery/fulldisplay?docid=alma99193071102419&context=L&vid=44WHELF_NLW:44WHELF_NLW_NUI&lang=en&search_scope=In_The_Library&adaptor=Local%20Search%20Engine&tab=In_The_Library&query=any,contains,Talbots%20of%20margam%20archives&offset=0> as it includes the surviving manorial records for Llandough.\n\nThere is useful guidance on the different types of title deeds published by the University of Nottingham which might be helpful when searching catalogues and consulting original property records:\n\nIntroduction to deeds in depth - The University of Nottingham<https://www.nottingham.ac.uk/manuscriptsandspecialcollections/researchguidance/deedsindepth/introduction.aspx>\n\nI am sorry not to be able to provide any additional sources for your continuing research.\n\nKind regards\n\n\n[The National Archives logo]\nPhilip Gale | Head of the Collections and Cultural Property Team\nT: 020 3908 X 9193 | W: nationalarchives.gov.uk<http://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/>\nTwitter: @UkNatArchives\nThe National Archives, Kew, Richmond, Surrey [postcode redacted]\n\n\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]>\nSent: 24 February 2026 17:42\nTo: Philip Gale <[email redacted]>\nSubject: Re: Manor of Llandough - MDR Entries\n\nEXTERNAL: This email originates from outside of The National Archives.\nYou are a delight.\nMy family thanks you.\nThis means a great deal.\n\nRequester\n\nOn Tue, 24 Feb 2026 at 13:50, Philip Gale <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>> wrote:\nDear Requester,\n\nThank you for your e-mail and for clarifying the subject of your enquiry.\n\nAs you know the Manorial Documents Register (MDR) only gives general descriptions of the content, extent and location of the manorial records recorded in the register: LLANDOUGH MANOR | The National Archives<https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/c/F236775> The manorial records for the Manor of Llandough are held at the National Library of Wales, Glamorgan Archives and West Glamorgan Archives, and their catalogues can be accessed on-line : NLW Catalogue<https://discover.library.wales/discovery/search?vid=44WHELF_NLW:44WHELF_NLW_NUI> , Glamorgan Archives Catalogue<https://canfod.glamarchives.gov.uk/en/> and Archive Collections - Swansea<https://www.swansea.gov.uk/archivecollections?lang=en>\n\nThe Welsh entries in the online MDR were added in the 1990’s were based on the survey returns to the Manorial Documents Committee received from institutions and private owners during the 1920’s. These were subsequently passed to the custody of the Historical Manuscripts Commission in 1959 which amalgamated with the Public Record Office to form The National Archives in 2003. I have requested to see the original returns for Manor of Llandough to see if they can provide any additional information on the documentation relating to the manor (Ref. HMC 9/311: https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/r/C5879336 )\n\nThere are several other manors with a similar name in Glamorgan and a MDR search using the search term “Llandough” gives the following result: Search results: Llandough Glamorgan | The National Archives<https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/results/c?_q=Llandough&_sf=text_man&_ocn=Glamorgan&_naet=M&_st=mdrc>   Sometimes the administration of different manors were brought together and this appears to have occurred  with the Llandough and St May’s Church Manors : https://archives.library.wales/index.php/manors-of-llandough-and-st-mary-church\n\nI will contact you again once I have had an opportunity to look at the original returns sent to Manorial Documents Committee. I hope this preliminary information is of assistance.\n\nYours sincerely\n\n[The National Archives logo]\nPhilip Gale | Head of the Collections and Cultural Property Team\nT: 020 3908 X 9193 | W: nationalarchives.gov.uk<http://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/>\nTwitter: @UkNatArchives\nThe National Archives, Kew, Richmond, Surrey [postcode redacted]\n\n\n\n\n________________________________\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSent: Wednesday, February 18, 2026 5:49:54 PM\nTo: Philip Gale <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSubject: Re: Formal Notice for Record: Church View Close, Llandough / BP vs Buckler 1987\n\n\nEXTERNAL: This email originates from outside of The National Archives.\n\nDear Mr Gale,\n\nThank you for your reply of 12 February. I would like to clarify and narrow my request to ensure there is no misunderstanding regarding the records I am trying to locate.\n\nMy correspondence is not seeking an adjudication of ownership or title — I fully accept the stated remit boundaries of the Manorial Documents Register (MDR). Rather, my enquiry concerns the existence of any archival reference, index card, catalogue entry, or pointer to a document historically known as the “Williams Title”, which was, according to contemporaneous family testimony, at one point:\n\n  1.  Held in the local public library in the first half of the twentieth century; and\n  2.  Indexed in some manner (e.g., an index card or catalogue reference) that persisted even after the original document could not be located.\n\nMy understanding of the MDR — as described on your own website — is that it exists to provide brief descriptions of manorial records and details of their locations:\n\n“An official index of English and Welsh manorial records providing brief descriptions of documents and details of their locations… It does not include title deeds or conveyances, and is not a register of ownership of manorial lordships or rights.”\nhttps://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/manor-search<https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/manor-search?utm_source=chatgpt.com>\n\nBased on that description, I would be grateful if you could confirm whether:\n\n  1.  Any catalogue entries, index cards, reference pointers, or archival descriptions relating to the Manor of Llandough or the Williams family title exist in the MDR or related archival catalogues.\n  2.  If so, the reference identifiers and information on how these can be consulted in person or via digitised records.\n\nTo be precise, I am looking for historical references to the index card or listing for the Williams Title — whether held in a library, local record office, or other institutional archive — rather than a claim for ownership judgment itself.\n\nIf such a reference does not currently appear in the MDR, I would greatly appreciate guidance on where any known catalogue cards or entry lists for manorial documents from Glamorgan / Llandough prior to 1950 might now reside (e.g., regional archives, National Archives holdings, or affiliated local repository catalogues).\n\nAnyway I anticipate your forgiveness, if I have erred in writing to you earlier.\n\nThank you again and in advance for your assistance.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nRequester\nOn Wed, 18 Feb 2026 at 14:30, Philip Gale <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>> wrote:\n\nDear Requester,\n\n\n\nThank you for your e-mail of 10 February whose contents are noted. The National Archives does not hold any records relating to your enquiry. The Manorial Documents Register which this department administers is a register for the location of manorial documents and not of the ownership and descent of manorial titles:\n\n\n\nhttps://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/archives-sector/finding-records-in-discovery-and-other-databases/manorial-documents-register/\n\n\n\nA similar position holds for The National Archives responsibilities for public records and concerns about the past management of official records is a matter for the relevant government department such as HM Land Registry.\n\n\n\nYours sincerely\n\n\n\n\n\n[The National Archives logo]\n\nPhilip Gale | Head of the Collections and Cultural Property Team\nT: 020 3908 X 9193 | W: nationalarchives.gov.uk<http://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/>\nTwitter: @UkNatArchives\nThe National Archives, Kew, Richmond, Surrey [postcode redacted]\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n-----Original Message-----\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\n\nSent: 10 February 2026 22:15\n\nTo: Archives Sector Leadership <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\n\nCc: [email redacted]<[email redacted]>; [email redacted]<[email redacted]>; Paul Egan <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>; [email redacted]<[email redacted]>; [email redacted]<[email redacted]>\n\nSubject: Formal Notice for Record: Church View Close, Llandough / BP vs Buckler 1987\n\n\n\n        You don't often get email from [email redacted]<[email redacted]>. Learn why this is important <https://aka.ms/LearnAboutSenderIdentification>\n\n\n\n\n\nEXTERNAL: This email originates from outside of The National Archives.\n\n\n\nFORMAL NOTICE OF RECORD CHALLENGE, ASSERTION OF RIGHT, AND DEMAND FOR ENGAGEMENT\n\n\n\nDear Sir/Madam,\n\n\n\nThis Notice is issued for placement on official record and for reliance. It is not informal correspondence. I urge you not to resort to silence, deflection, or delay, as doing so following receipt of this notice constitutes knowing reliance upon, and maintenance of, records alleged to be materially defective through fraud, concealment, denial of due process, and systemic erasure. Please report any suspicious activity and discrepancies identified.\n\n\n\nThis Notice is issued in pursuit of narrative justice at this stage; formal judicial remedies are expressly reserved. It serves to document our family's side of the story before it is completely erased and history rewritten, ensuring awareness among custodians of land, heritage, heraldry, archaeology, and public records.\n\n\n\nMy elder brother, Rhys Buckler, and I are the sons of the late William Buckler (d. 1991), defendant in the landmark legal case BP Properties Ltd v Buckler (1987), and the grandchildren of the late Mary Williams, whose dispossession, marginalisation, and exclusion from judicial determination remain unresolved matters of record. Mary Williams was repeatedly and materially falsely addressed/mislabeled in legal and administrative records as “Mrs Buckler,” a misidentification that was never judicially tested and which directly affected the handling, scope, and outcome of proceedings. The Williams/Buckler family held claim to the land title, having acquired and occupied Great House Farm continuously since 1667, based on family records, historical evidence, and centuries of resistance to adverse claims.\n\n\n\nI place on record that I am the first member of my family able to speak publicly and formally on these matters. Other close family members remain too traumatised and intimidated by the historical conduct of these proceedings—including violent eviction, demolition, and loss of livelihood—to engage, despite the continuing consequences of dispossession. That silence must not be misread as consent, acquiescence, or abandonment of right. There is a material distinction between free silence and silence produced by fear, trauma, and coercive experience.\n\n\n\nThe story, as we have pieced it together, reveals a multi-generational pattern of lawfare and siege: from Norman/English conquests in 1100 granting lordships over ancient Welsh Christian landscapes, through monastic (1215–1539) and Crown seizures (1539 onward), to industrial encroachments (e.g., 1876 Limeworks severance without consent) and modern corporate possession actions. This culminated in the 1988 demolition of an 800-year-old farmhouse amid police standoffs, leaving the family homeless and the site scarred, now buried under Church View Close. Archaeological vindication came too late—1994 excavations revealed over 1,000 Roman and medieval burials, confirming family warnings (e.g., the 1870 Roman soldier discovery under the living room floor, suppressed by authorities)—yet heritage records celebrate the site while omitting our dispossession.\n\n\n\nThe following assertions of right are now formally placed on record. They have never been lawfully adjudicated or extinguished:\n\n\n\n1.      Rhys Buckler may be the rightful Manorial Lord of the Manor of Llandough, by right of primogeniture and continuous hereditary connection arising from our family’s occupation of Great House Farm from 1667 until our narrative, legal, and physical dispossession.\n\n2.      I assert a claim to act as Squire of the Manor of Llandough, as executive representative and guardian of the Buckler–Williams estate and matriarchal lineage, with a mandate to pursue correction of record, restitution, and restorative remedies.\n\n\n\nThese are assertions of right, not settled declarations, placed on record pending proper adjudication—which has been historically obstructed through procedural narrowing, missing documents, and avoidance of full hearings.\n\n\n\nIn 1974, Mary Williams was twice prevented from presenting her case and obtaining a judicial determination of superior ownership, despite claims grounded in long occupation, intent, and possession. The court confined inquiry to animus possidendi (intention to possess) and excluded animus domini (intention to own), reclassifying her from putative owner to mere occupier and foreclosing title adjudication. BP sought possession only, not ownership rulings—a choice unexplained and significant. Proceedings were adjourned or quashed without resolution.\n\n\n\nThis occurred despite evidence of ownership intent, including:\n\n\n\n*       Centuries-long occupation and resistance (e.g., failed 1938 auction due to local support; post-1945 rent disputes).\n\n*       Roman-era remains and historical significance (e.g., Guglielmo Marconi's 1897 residence during radio experiments, with family aid erased from records).\n\n*       Unformalized deals like the 1895–1905 Daniel Thomas quarrying permission for promised title, undermined by vanished deeds.\n\n\n\nJudicial ownership examination would have engaged archaeological/heritage protections, incompatible with redevelopment—consequences avoided, raising credibility concerns.\n\n\n\nSerious unresolved anomalies persist, indicating something is still not right with the record:\n\n\n\n*       Licence correspondence dated 31 October 1974 surfacing only on 4 November, potentially interrupting proceedings to prevent hearings.\n\n*       Claims of 1974 benevolent withdrawal under press scrutiny unsupported by verifiable coverage.\n\n*       Land registration before Mary Williams’s 1983 death without title determination; disputes possibly lodged/suppressed by Land Registry.\n\n*       Inconsistent treatment of BP entities across proceedings to family detriment.\n\n*       Procedural barriers preventing effective defense (e.g., interrupted essential services post-licence; 1960–1987 court rulings dismissing ancestral title as \"notion\" without evidence).\n\n*       Knowing bypass of archaeological constraints (e.g., 1978 trenches ignored; no contemporaneous 1970s/1990s press on discoveries amid development).\n\n*       Missing key documents (e.g., 1667 conveyance, 1811–1835 Lambert Williams agreements proving status, 1982–84 library deeds vanishing).\n\n*       Risks of further erasure: Heritage databases celebrate \"Llandough Roman villa cemetery\" while omitting our saga; modern residents unaware of the \"battleground\" site.\n\n\n\nThese anomalies suggest systemic theft, from colonial English subjugation (e.g., 1543–1770 orbit shifts) to corporate siege, during Wales' cultural/economic transitions. Authority derived through such means is illegitimate under natural justice, customary Welsh principles, and moral-legal frameworks. The record's distortion secures advantage, but passage of time does not validate it.\n\n\n\nPreliminary assessment indicates cumulative benefits from redevelopment (absent title extinguishment) exceed £100 million, forming a basis for reparations—covering lost land, artifacts, livelihood, and trauma.\n\n\n\nI require relevant public servants, custodians, and office-holders (including Land Registry, heraldry bodies, museums, archaeologists, MPs, historians, and councillors) to cease delay, engage substantively, review/correct/annotate records where defective, and prevent further rewriting of history. Justice delayed is denied, especially with verifiable evidence available.\n\n\n\nThis Notice:\n\n\n\n*       Challenges the record's accuracy, completeness, and legitimacy.\n\n*       Place assertions of title/standing on record pending adjudication.\n\n*       Reserves all rights without prejudice.\n\n*       Puts officials/beneficiaries on notice that silence/reliance may aggravate responsibility.\n\n\n\nFor the avoidance of doubt, His Majesty the King was notified yesterday.\n\n\n\nThis serves as an invitation to engage constructively before reliance becomes untenable.\n\n\n\nRegards, Requester On behalf of the Buckler–Williams family [address redacted], Vale of Glamorgan, Wales. https://bpvsbuckler.datro.xyz/ <https://bpvsbuckler.datro.xyz/>\n\n\n\nPostscript — Disability and Succession (Material Notice): The heir-apparent to the asserted Manorial Lordship is Rhys Buckler’s son, a young man with Down syndrome. Any obstruction, marginalisation, or differential treatment affecting his inheritance would engage the Equality Act 2010 and UK international obligations on disability rights. Disability cannot bar succession or recognition. This is recorded to prevent claims of ignorance.\nPlease don't print this e-mail unless you really need to. ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- National Archives Disclaimer This email and any files transmitted with it are intended solely for the use of the individual(s) to whom they are addressed. If you are not the intended recipient and have received this email in error, please notify the sender and delete the email. Opinions, conclusions and other information in this message and attachments that do not relate to the official business of The National Archives are neither given nor endorsed by it. ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPlease don't print this e-mail unless you really need to. ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- National Archives Disclaimer This email and any files transmitted with it are intended solely for the use of the individual(s) to whom they are addressed. If you are not the intended recipient and have received this email in error, please notify the sender and delete the email. Opinions, conclusions and other information in this message and attachments that do not relate to the official business of The National Archives are neither given nor endorsed by it. ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPlease don't print this e-mail unless you really need to. ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- National Archives Disclaimer This email and any files transmitted with it are intended solely for the use of the individual(s) to whom they are addressed. If you are not the intended recipient and have received this email in error, please notify the sender and delete the email. Opinions, conclusions and other information in this message and attachments that do not relate to the official business of The National Archives are neither given nor endorsed by it. ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\nAttachments (1)\n- HMC 9.311 Llandough Manor return.jpg (image/jpeg, 1779 KB): https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/pdf/foi/2026-02-26_tna_hmc-9-311_llandough-manor-return.jpg"},{"id":"gm-19c99538c709c80a","subject":"New response to your FOI request - Freedom of Information Request – Administrative Records for BP Properties Ltd v Buckler (1974)","name":"New response to your FOI request - Freedom of Information Request – Administrative Records for BP Properties Ltd v Buckler (1974)","date":"2026-02-26T09:41:54Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19c99538c709c80a.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19c99538c709c80a-new-response-to-your-foi-request-freedom-of-information-request-administrative-records-for-bp-properties-ltd-v-buckler-1","scopeTopics":["moj"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["moj"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19c99538c709c80a","direction":"received","from":"(name not shown; whatdotheyknow.com)","institutions":[{"id":"moj","name":"Ministry of Justice / HMCTS"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19c99538c709c80a.json","textStatus":"available","text":"You have a new response to the Freedom of Information request 'Freedom of Information Request – Administrative Records for BP Properties Ltd v Buckler (1974)' that you made to HM Courts and Tribunals Service.\n\nTo view the response, click on the link below.\n\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/request/freedom_of_information_request_a_2133?nocache=incoming-3323064#incoming-3323064\n\nWhen you get there, please update the status to say if the response contains any useful information.\n\nAlthough all responses are automatically published, we depend on you, the original requester, to evaluate them.\n\n-- the WhatDoTheyKnow team\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nWhatDoTheyKnow is a project of mySociety run by a small team of staff and\ndedicated volunteers.\n\nmySociety is a charity that believes that information should be accessible to everyone.\nIf this is important to you, please consider donating to help us do more:\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/donate\n\nFind out more about how we handle your data at:\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/help/privacy\n\nmySociety is a registered charity in England and Wales (1076346), and a limited\ncompany (03277032)"},{"id":"gm-19c9496dba492283","subject":"RE: Formal Report of Suspected Indigenous Dispossession, Systemic Fraud, and Archaeological Suppression – Great House Farm, Llandough","name":"RE: Formal Report of Suspected Indigenous Dispossession, Systemic Fraud, and Archaeological Suppression – Great House Farm, Llandough","date":"2026-02-25T11:37:16Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19c9496dba492283.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19c313b370d627d6-formal-report-of-suspected-indigenous-dispossession-systemic-fraud-and-archaeological-suppression-great-house-farm-lland","scopeTopics":["cadw"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["cadw"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19c313b370d627d6","direction":"received","from":"(name not shown; gov.wales)","institutions":[{"id":"cadw","name":"Welsh Government / Cadw"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19c9496dba492283.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester,\n\nThank you for your email regarding Great House Farm, Llandough. However, I must inform you that much of the content in your correspondence falls outside the scope of Cadw’s work.\n\nIn relation to the claim of 'archaeological suppression' involving the discovery of a Roman soldier in full armour in 1888, any such find would typically have been reported in the antiquarian press at either the national or local level. It is possible that records of this nature may be held by local museums. It is worth noting that the first Ancient Monuments Act was passed in 1882, focusing solely on bringing threatened ancient monuments into guardianship. The legal recognition of nationally important archaeology did not come about until the Ancient Monuments Consolidation and Amendment Act of 1913.\n\nUpon reviewing the Cof Cymru database, I found that there are no designated historic assets relevant to this topic. However, Archwilio contains two records that document the involvement of the Glamorgan-Gwent Archaeological Trust (GGAT):\n\n1. Great House Farm HER site entry - GGAT03586s\n2. Land at Great House Farm HER event entry - GGATE003872, which includes GGAT's 1990 archaeological assessment report commissioned by BP Properties Ltd.\n\nAny advice or correspondence that Cadw may have provided should be preserved in the Royal Commission on the Ancient and Historical Monuments of Wales (RCAHMW) National Monuments Record of Wales (NMRW). I recommend you contact the NMRW to access any relevant documents. Additionally, you may want to reach out to Heneb and the Vale of Glamorgan Local Planning Authority (LPA) to explore what records they might hold concerning this matter.\n\nKind regards,\n\nJenna Arnold\n\n[cid:image001.png@01DCA64B.15168E30]\n\n\nJenna Arnold\nArolygiaeth yr Amgylchedd Hanesyddol / Historic Environment Inspectorate\nLlywodraeth Cymru / Welsh Government\nCadw, Rhydycar Business Park | Parc Business Rhydycar, Merthyr Tydfil | Merthyr Tudful [postcode redacted]\n\n\n\n[https://i.ibb.co/CVwfkw2/Cadw-er-lles-pawb.png]<http://www.aelodaethcadw.gwasanaeth.llyw.cymru/>\n[https://i.ibb.co/NxJvcfW/For-us-all-to-keep.png]<http://www.cadwmembership.service.gov.wales/>\n\nYmunwch â Cadw<https://www.aelodaethcadw.gwasanaeth.llyw.cymru/>\nJoin Cadw<https://www.cadwmembership.service.gov.wales/>\n\n\n\n\n\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nSent: 06 February 2026 05:21\nTo: Scheduled Monuments <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>>\nCc: [email redacted]<[email redacted]>; [email redacted]<[email redacted]>; [email redacted]<[email redacted]>\nSubject: Formal Report of Suspected Indigenous Dispossession, Systemic Fraud, and Archaeological Suppression – Great House Farm, Llandough\n\n\nTo whom it may concern,\n\nI am writing to formally report a long-standing and systemic case of what I suspect may be dispossession, fraudulent misrepresentation, and archaeological suppression concerning my family’s ancestral land, Great House Farm (historically Tŷ Mawr/Cedfin), Llandough.\n\nI am the descendant of the Williams/Buckler family, who held de facto possession and indigenous tenure of this land from 1667 until our forced displacement in 1988. I am currently the only member of my family who is not too traumatized to speak of these events. My mother (Mary Williams daughter in law), remains to this day too terrified to speak up about this multi-generational \"lawfare\" campaign waged against us. Our family have not seen justice or received reperations, nor have we sued or reopened this case under limitations act clauses of fraud and consealment. Yet. But this may not be just a grave injustice upon our family, but our nations archeological history and indigenous people on our ancestral birthland. We are Welsh Nationals, Welsh speaking, under Welsh flags. This is some British Colonisation-grade trespass I'm seeing and it's tandamount to a declaration of civil war on me, my family, community and people.\n\nThe core of this report involves the following suspicions I have of concealment and fraud (see attached for my timeline and notes):\n\n  1.  Archaeological Suppression: In 1888, my family discovered a Roman soldier in full armor beneath the living room floorboards. This discovery provided \"actual notice\" of the site's national significance a century before its demolition. We allege this notice was systematically suppressed by corporate and state actors to allow for commercial development.\n  2.  Fraudulent Misrepresentation in Planning: During the 1987 planning process, the 13th-century fortified manor was described by developers as a \"dilapidated utility building\" or \"ruined barn\" to secure a demolition permit that would have otherwise been denied under heritage protection laws.\n  3.  Legal Identity Falsification: In 1974, BP Pension Trust issued a \"unilateral license\" intended to bypass our possessory title. This correspondence was falsely addressed to \"Mrs. Buckler\" to bind my grandmother to her deceased husband’s name and legal status, despite her public rejection of their claim. She used her maiden name Williams and her husband Bucklers' intent to accept license died with him. My nan also alledgedly didn't expressly deny the offer and so accepted through her silence. But she was near enough voiceless from screaming for decades that she will not do deals with any of them. That's a different kind of silence, if any. And to have responded to license to Mrs Buckler would have been identify fraud as she was Mrs Williams. And she did in fact reject BPs license offer and advanced, countless times, but publically. I believe these objections were suppressed too. There's allegedly news coverage from October 30th 1974 but I'm yet to find the evidence. But see attached a 1978 article I unearthed. My nan clearly maintains her position \"It's my home\". [cid:image004.jpg@01DCA64B.15168E30]\n  4.  You'll also see in landmark legal case BP Vs Buckler 1987 the judge says that she might have responded to BP, but to BP Pension Trust, but the claimant was BP Properties Ltd which were two separate companies. But in my dads appeal when he challenges that BP Pension Trust Ltd should have been the claimant the same judge dismissed his appeal citing both BP companies are effectively the same. This is clearly a case of heads BP won, Tails we lost.\n  5.  Missing Evidence: Critical title deeds and Bute Estate records from our families deal with Daniel Thomas, for title after quarrying completed, in exchange for our permission to quarry, also disappeared from the loft of our home and the Cardiff Library according to my families accounts, precisely when they were needed to support our ownership claim in the Court of Appeal. Regardless we should have still remained on grounds we'd been there since 1667, and failing that on grounds of adverse possession.\n  6.  It was evident lawfare to remove us, to get to the burials known about since 1888, BP profited from the land sale, the state and Crown have the exhevated artifacts and my displaced family are left traumatised, cheated and dead. My dad's appeal was dismissed to when it shouldn't have been and his ECHR request blocked, before dying abruptly at age just 41.\n\nThe confirmation in 1994 of a high-status Roman villa and over 1,000 burials on the exact site of our demolished home proves that the \"official narrative\" used to evict us was predicated on the concealment of known facts.\n\nUnder Section 32 of the Limitation Act 1980, the limitation period for action does not begin until such fraud or concealment is discovered. As the heir to this possessory title, I am seeking a formal investigation into the institutional failures that permitted this dispossession and the destruction of a national monument.\n\nI have compiled my account and understanding of these events in timeline order and I'm prepared to provide further testimony and evidence.\n\nGiven the extreme trauma my family has suffered, I request that all correspondence be handled with the gravity this human rights violation deserves.\n\nConsidering the lack of cooperation from my traumatised family. And authorities who maybe complicit and implicated. And possible corruption of records and fraud and consealment, I'm open to the fact my account and understanding may not be perfect. But if even a single other person or group looked into this too, even briefly, I'm sure you'll agree there's merrit to the concerns I'm raising with all of this.\n\nI look forward to your response regarding the appropriate next steps for investigation and restitution.\n\nI do ask for some small sense of urgency in that justice delayed is justice denied. I understand this is ancient news for many, but my family still feel a tremendous sense of disposition and disposition over all of this. Bringing this all up means to re live it.\n\nI will have been 3 or 4 at the time, but I still remember my toys in our house, being bulldozed. And my mum handing us rocks to throw at them, bulldozing down our home. As my dad was held without charges to prevent him defending us and our home. I'd have to visit him in a hospital bed after his heart attack and see him in a coffin shortly afterwards.\n\nI'm 41 years old now. I'm a certified and experienced military commander and leader. I left my military career with honours. I finished 22 years in Oath in 2024, bond afide, my last 2 employed under the Welsh government.\n\nI kindly request but also require of you, that this matter be layed to rest correctly.\n\nMy family never surrendered. Never relinquished claim to that land. We we're in honour in that we weren't there with force, stealth/ secrecy or license. But BP and the British State did use force, stealth/ secrecy and license.\n\nAll things considered I believe I find myself in honour here, and the British Establishment in dishonour. This matter must be escalated. I'm duty bound.\n\n\n\nRegards,\n\n\n\nRequester\n\nSganiwyd y neges hon am bob feirws hysbys wrth iddi adael Llywodraeth Cymru. Mae Llywodraeth Cymru yn cymryd o ddifrif yr angen i ddiogelu eich data. Os cysylltwch â Llywodraeth Cymru, mae ein hysbysiad preifatrwydd<”https://gov.wales/about/welsh-government-privacy-notice/?lang=cy”> yn esbonio sut rydym yn defnyddio eich gwybodaeth a sut rydym yn diogelu eich preifatrwydd. Rydym yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg. Byddwn yn anfon ateb yn Gymraeg i ohebiaeth a dderbynnir yn Gymraeg ac ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi. ----------------- On leaving the Welsh Government this email was scanned for all known viruses. The Welsh Government takes the protection of your data seriously. If you contact the Welsh Government then our Privacy Notice<https://gov.wales/about/welsh-government-privacy-notice/?lang=en> explains how we use your information and the ways in which we protect your privacy. We welcome receiving correspondence in Welsh. Any correspondence received in Welsh will be answered in Welsh and corresponding in Welsh will not lead to a delay in responding."},{"id":"gm-19c90bdf2c23a4ab","subject":"Re: Manor of Llandough - MDR Entries","name":"Re: Manor of Llandough - MDR Entries","date":"2026-02-24T17:41:31Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19c90bdf2c23a4ab.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19c908fa7cddd153-manor-of-llandough-mdr-entries","scopeTopics":["tna"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["tna"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19c908fa7cddd153","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"tna","name":"The National Archives"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19c90bdf2c23a4ab.json","textStatus":"available","text":"You are a delight.\nMy family thanks you.\nThis means a great deal.\n\nRequester\n\nOn Tue, 24 Feb 2026 at 13:50, Philip Gale <\n[email redacted]> wrote:\n\n> Dear Requester,\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you for your e-mail and for clarifying the subject of your enquiry.\n>\n>\n>\n> As you know the Manorial Documents Register (MDR) only gives general\n> descriptions of the content, extent and location of the manorial records\n> recorded in the register: LLANDOUGH MANOR | The National Archives\n>   The\n> manorial records for the Manor of Llandough are held at the National\n> Library of Wales, Glamorgan Archives and West Glamorgan Archives, and their\n> catalogues can be accessed on-line : NLW Catalogue\n>  \n> , Glamorgan Archives Catalogue  \n> and Archive Collections - Swansea\n>  \n>\n>\n>\n> The Welsh entries in the online MDR were added in the 1990’s were based on\n> the survey returns to the Manorial Documents Committee received from\n> institutions and private owners during the 1920’s. These were subsequently\n> passed to the custody of the Historical Manuscripts Commission in 1959\n> which amalgamated with the Public Record Office to form The National\n> Archives in 2003. I have requested to see the original returns for Manor of\n> Llandough to see if they can provide any additional information on the\n> documentation relating to the manor (Ref. *HMC 9/311*: *https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/r/C5879336\n>  * )\n>\n>\n>\n> There are several other manors with a similar name in Glamorgan and a MDR\n> search using the search term “Llandough” gives the following result: Search\n> results: Llandough Glamorgan | The National Archives\n>  \n> Sometimes the administration of different manors were brought together and\n> this appears to have occurred  with the Llandough and St May’s Church\n> Manors :\n> https://archives.library.wales/index.php/manors-of-llandough-and-st-mary-church\n>\n>\n>\n> I will contact you again once I have had an opportunity to look at the\n> original returns sent to Manorial Documents Committee. I hope this\n> preliminary information is of assistance.\n>\n>\n>\n> Yours sincerely\n>\n>\n>\n> [image: The National Archives logo]\n>\n> *Philip Gale *|* Head of the Collections and Cultural Property Team*\n> T: 020 3908 X 9193 | W: nationalarchives.gov.uk\n>  \n> Twitter: @UkNatArchives\n> The National Archives, Kew, Richmond, Surrey [postcode redacted]\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> ------------------------------\n>\n> *From:* Requester  \n> *Sent:* Wednesday, February 18, 2026 5:49:54 PM\n> *To:* Philip Gale  \n> *Subject:* Re: Formal Notice for Record: Church View Close, Llandough /\n> BP vs Buckler 1987\n>\n>\n>\n> *EXTERNAL*: This email originates from outside of The National Archives.\n>\n> Dear Mr Gale,\n>\n> Thank you for your reply of 12 February. I would like to clarify and\n> narrow my request to ensure there is no misunderstanding regarding the\n> records I am trying to locate.\n>\n> My correspondence is *not* seeking an adjudication of ownership or title\n> — I fully accept the stated remit boundaries of the Manorial Documents\n> Register (MDR). Rather, my enquiry concerns the existence of *any\n> archival reference, index card, catalogue entry, or pointer to a document\n> historically known as the “Williams Title”*, which was, according to\n> contemporaneous family testimony, at one point:\n>\n>    1. Held in the *local public library* in the first half of the\n>    twentieth century; and\n>    2. Indexed in some manner (e.g., an index card or catalogue reference)\n>    that persisted even after the original document could not be located.\n>\n> My understanding of the MDR — as described on your own website — is that\n> it exists to provide brief descriptions of manorial records and details of\n> their locations:\n>\n> “An official index of English and Welsh manorial records providing brief\n> descriptions of documents and details of their locations… It does *not*\n> include title deeds or conveyances, and is *not* a register of ownership\n> of manorial lordships or rights.”\n> https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/manor-search\n>  \n>\n> Based on that description, I would be grateful if you could confirm\n> whether:\n>\n>    1. *Any catalogue entries, index cards, reference pointers, or\n>    archival descriptions* relating to the *Manor of Llandough* or the *Williams\n>    family title* exist in the MDR or related archival catalogues.\n>    2. If so, the *reference identifiers* and information on how these can\n>    be consulted in person or via digitised records.\n>\n> To be precise, I am looking for historical references to the *index card\n> or listing* for the Williams Title — whether held in a library, local\n> record office, or other institutional archive — rather than a claim for\n> ownership judgment itself.\n>\n> If such a reference does not currently appear in the MDR, I would greatly\n> appreciate guidance on where any known catalogue cards or entry lists for\n> manorial documents from *Glamorgan / Llandough* prior to 1950 might now\n> reside (e.g., regional archives, National Archives holdings, or affiliated\n> local repository catalogues).\n>\n> Anyway I anticipate your forgiveness, if I have erred in writing to you\n> earlier.\n>\n> Thank you again and in advance for your assistance.\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n>\n> Requester\n>\n> On Wed, 18 Feb 2026 at 14:30, Philip Gale <\n> [email redacted]> wrote:\n>\n> Dear Requester,\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you for your e-mail of 10 February whose contents are noted. The\n> National Archives does not hold any records relating to your enquiry. The\n> Manorial Documents Register which this department administers is a register\n> for the location of manorial documents and not of the ownership and descent\n> of manorial titles:\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/archives-sector/finding-records-in-discovery-and-other-databases/manorial-documents-register/\n>\n>\n>\n> A similar position holds for The National Archives responsibilities for\n> public records and concerns about the past management of official records\n> is a matter for the relevant government department such as HM Land\n> Registry.\n>\n>\n>\n> Yours sincerely\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> [image: The National Archives logo]\n>\n> *Philip Gale *|* Head of the Collections and Cultural Property Team*\n> T: 020 3908 X 9193 | W: nationalarchives.gov.uk\n>  \n> Twitter: @UkNatArchives\n> The National Archives, Kew, Richmond, Surrey [postcode redacted]\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> -----Original Message-----\n>\n> From: Requester  \n>\n> Sent: 10 February 2026 22:15\n>\n> To: Archives Sector Leadership  \n>\n> Cc: [email redacted]; [email redacted];\n> Paul Egan  ; [email redacted];\n> [email redacted]\n>\n> Subject: Formal Notice for Record: Church View Close, Llandough / BP vs\n> Buckler 1987\n>\n>\n>\n>         You don't often get email from [email redacted]. Learn\n> why this is important  \n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> EXTERNAL: This email originates from outside of The National Archives.\n>\n>\n>\n> FORMAL NOTICE OF RECORD CHALLENGE, ASSERTION OF RIGHT, AND DEMAND FOR\n> ENGAGEMENT\n>\n>\n>\n> Dear Sir/Madam,\n>\n>\n>\n> This Notice is issued for placement on official record and for reliance.\n> It is not informal correspondence. I urge you not to resort to silence,\n> deflection, or delay, as doing so following receipt of this notice\n> constitutes knowing reliance upon, and maintenance of, records alleged to\n> be materially defective through fraud, concealment, denial of due process,\n> and systemic erasure. Please report any suspicious activity and\n> discrepancies identified.\n>\n>\n>\n> This Notice is issued in pursuit of narrative justice at this stage;\n> formal judicial remedies are expressly reserved. It serves to document our\n> family's side of the story before it is completely erased and history\n> rewritten, ensuring awareness among custodians of land, heritage, heraldry,\n> archaeology, and public records.\n>\n>\n>\n> My elder brother, Rhys Buckler, and I are the sons of the late William\n> Buckler (d. 1991), defendant in the landmark legal case BP Properties Ltd v\n> Buckler (1987), and the grandchildren of the late Mary Williams, whose\n> dispossession, marginalisation, and exclusion from judicial determination\n> remain unresolved matters of record. Mary Williams was repeatedly and\n> materially falsely addressed/mislabeled in legal and administrative records\n> as “Mrs Buckler,” a misidentification that was never judicially tested and\n> which directly affected the handling, scope, and outcome of proceedings.\n> The Williams/Buckler family held claim to the land title, having acquired\n> and occupied Great House Farm continuously since 1667, based on family\n> records, historical evidence, and centuries of resistance to adverse claims.\n>\n>\n>\n> I place on record that I am the first member of my family able to speak\n> publicly and formally on these matters. Other close family members remain\n> too traumatised and intimidated by the historical conduct of these\n> proceedings—including violent eviction, demolition, and loss of\n> livelihood—to engage, despite the continuing consequences of dispossession.\n> That silence must not be misread as consent, acquiescence, or abandonment\n> of right. There is a material distinction between free silence and silence\n> produced by fear, trauma, and coercive experience.\n>\n>\n>\n> The story, as we have pieced it together, reveals a multi-generational\n> pattern of lawfare and siege: from Norman/English conquests in 1100\n> granting lordships over ancient Welsh Christian landscapes, through\n> monastic (1215–1539) and Crown seizures (1539 onward), to industrial\n> encroachments (e.g., 1876 Limeworks severance without consent) and modern\n> corporate possession actions. This culminated in the 1988 demolition of an\n> 800-year-old farmhouse amid police standoffs, leaving the family homeless\n> and the site scarred, now buried under Church View Close. Archaeological\n> vindication came too late—1994 excavations revealed over 1,000 Roman and\n> medieval burials, confirming family warnings (e.g., the 1870 Roman soldier\n> discovery under the living room floor, suppressed by authorities)—yet\n> heritage records celebrate the site while omitting our dispossession.\n>\n>\n>\n> The following assertions of right are now formally placed on record. They\n> have never been lawfully adjudicated or extinguished:\n>\n>\n>\n> 1.      Rhys Buckler may be the rightful Manorial Lord of the Manor of\n> Llandough, by right of primogeniture and continuous hereditary connection\n> arising from our family’s occupation of Great House Farm from 1667 until\n> our narrative, legal, and physical dispossession.\n>\n> 2.      I assert a claim to act as Squire of the Manor of Llandough, as\n> executive representative and guardian of the Buckler–Williams estate and\n> matriarchal lineage, with a mandate to pursue correction of record,\n> restitution, and restorative remedies.\n>\n>\n>\n> These are assertions of right, not settled declarations, placed on record\n> pending proper adjudication—which has been historically obstructed through\n> procedural narrowing, missing documents, and avoidance of full hearings.\n>\n>\n>\n> In 1974, Mary Williams was twice prevented from presenting her case and\n> obtaining a judicial determination of superior ownership, despite claims\n> grounded in long occupation, intent, and possession. The court confined\n> inquiry to animus possidendi (intention to possess) and excluded animus\n> domini (intention to own), reclassifying her from putative owner to mere\n> occupier and foreclosing title adjudication. BP sought possession only, not\n> ownership rulings—a choice unexplained and significant. Proceedings were\n> adjourned or quashed without resolution.\n>\n>\n>\n> This occurred despite evidence of ownership intent, including:\n>\n>\n>\n> *       Centuries-long occupation and resistance (e.g., failed 1938\n> auction due to local support; post-1945 rent disputes).\n>\n> *       Roman-era remains and historical significance (e.g., Guglielmo\n> Marconi's 1897 residence during radio experiments, with family aid erased\n> from records).\n>\n> *       Unformalized deals like the 1895–1905 Daniel Thomas quarrying\n> permission for promised title, undermined by vanished deeds.\n>\n>\n>\n> Judicial ownership examination would have engaged archaeological/heritage\n> protections, incompatible with redevelopment—consequences avoided, raising\n> credibility concerns.\n>\n>\n>\n> Serious unresolved anomalies persist, indicating something is still not\n> right with the record:\n>\n>\n>\n> *       Licence correspondence dated 31 October 1974 surfacing only on 4\n> November, potentially interrupting proceedings to prevent hearings.\n>\n> *       Claims of 1974 benevolent withdrawal under press scrutiny\n> unsupported by verifiable coverage.\n>\n> *       Land registration before Mary Williams’s 1983 death without title\n> determination; disputes possibly lodged/suppressed by Land Registry.\n>\n> *       Inconsistent treatment of BP entities across proceedings to family\n> detriment.\n>\n> *       Procedural barriers preventing effective defense (e.g.,\n> interrupted essential services post-licence; 1960–1987 court rulings\n> dismissing ancestral title as \"notion\" without evidence).\n>\n> *       Knowing bypass of archaeological constraints (e.g., 1978 trenches\n> ignored; no contemporaneous 1970s/1990s press on discoveries amid\n> development).\n>\n> *       Missing key documents (e.g., 1667 conveyance, 1811–1835 Lambert\n> Williams agreements proving status, 1982–84 library deeds vanishing).\n>\n> *       Risks of further erasure: Heritage databases celebrate \"Llandough\n> Roman villa cemetery\" while omitting our saga; modern residents unaware of\n> the \"battleground\" site.\n>\n>\n>\n> These anomalies suggest systemic theft, from colonial English subjugation\n> (e.g., 1543–1770 orbit shifts) to corporate siege, during Wales'\n> cultural/economic transitions. Authority derived through such means is\n> illegitimate under natural justice, customary Welsh principles, and\n> moral-legal frameworks. The record's distortion secures advantage, but\n> passage of time does not validate it.\n>\n>\n>\n> Preliminary assessment indicates cumulative benefits from redevelopment\n> (absent title extinguishment) exceed £100 million, forming a basis for\n> reparations—covering lost land, artifacts, livelihood, and trauma.\n>\n>\n>\n> I require relevant public servants, custodians, and office-holders\n> (including Land Registry, heraldry bodies, museums, archaeologists, MPs,\n> historians, and councillors) to cease delay, engage substantively,\n> review/correct/annotate records where defective, and prevent further\n> rewriting of history. Justice delayed is denied, especially with verifiable\n> evidence available.\n>\n>\n>\n> This Notice:\n>\n>\n>\n> *       Challenges the record's accuracy, completeness, and legitimacy.\n>\n> *       Place assertions of title/standing on record pending adjudication.\n>\n> *       Reserves all rights without prejudice.\n>\n> *       Puts officials/beneficiaries on notice that silence/reliance may\n> aggravate responsibility.\n>\n>\n>\n> For the avoidance of doubt, His Majesty the King was notified yesterday.\n>\n>\n>\n> This serves as an invitation to engage constructively before reliance\n> becomes untenable.\n>\n>\n>\n> Regards, Requester On behalf of the Buckler–Williams family [address redacted],\n> [location redacted], Vale of Glamorgan, Wales. https://bpvsbuckler.datro.xyz/ <\n> https://bpvsbuckler.datro.xyz/>\n>\n>\n>\n> Postscript — Disability and Succession (Material Notice): The\n> heir-apparent to the asserted Manorial Lordship is Rhys Buckler’s son, a\n> young man with Down syndrome. Any obstruction, marginalisation, or\n> differential treatment affecting his inheritance would engage the Equality\n> Act 2010 and UK international obligations on disability rights. Disability\n> cannot bar succession or recognition. This is recorded to prevent claims of\n> ignorance.\n>\n> Please don't print this e-mail unless you really need to.\n> -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n> National Archives Disclaimer This email and any files transmitted with it\n> are intended solely for the use of the individual(s) to whom they are\n> addressed. If you are not the intended recipient and have received this\n> email in error, please notify the sender and delete the email. Opinions,\n> conclusions and other information in this message and attachments that do\n> not relate to the official business of The National Archives are neither\n> given nor endorsed by it.\n> ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n>\n>\n> Please don't print this e-mail unless you really need to.\n> -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n> National Archives Disclaimer This email and any files transmitted with it\n> are intended solely for the use of the individual(s) to whom they are\n> addressed. If you are not the intended recipient and have received this\n> email in error, please notify the sender and delete the email. Opinions,\n> conclusions and other information in this message and attachments that do\n> not relate to the official business of The National Archives are neither\n> given nor endorsed by it.\n> ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n>\n>"},{"id":"gm-19c908fa7cddd153","subject":"RE: Manor of Llandough - MDR Entries","name":"RE: Manor of Llandough - MDR Entries","date":"2026-02-24T16:50:55Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19c908fa7cddd153.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19c908fa7cddd153-manor-of-llandough-mdr-entries","scopeTopics":["tna"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["tna"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19c908fa7cddd153","direction":"received","from":"Philip Gale","institutions":[{"id":"tna","name":"The National Archives"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19c908fa7cddd153.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester,\n\nThank you for your e-mail and for clarifying the subject of your enquiry.\n\nAs you know the Manorial Documents Register (MDR) only gives general descriptions of the content, extent and location of the manorial records recorded in the register: LLANDOUGH MANOR | The National Archives  The manorial records for the Manor of Llandough are held at the National Library of Wales, Glamorgan Archives and West Glamorgan Archives, and their catalogues can be accessed on-line : NLW Catalogue  , Glamorgan Archives Catalogue  and Archive Collections - Swansea \n\nThe Welsh entries in the online MDR were added in the 1990’s were based on the survey returns to the Manorial Documents Committee received from institutions and private owners during the 1920’s. These were subsequently passed to the custody of the Historical Manuscripts Commission in 1959 which amalgamated with the Public Record Office to form The National Archives in 2003. I have requested to see the original returns for Manor of Llandough to see if they can provide any additional information on the documentation relating to the manor (Ref. HMC 9/311: https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/r/C5879336 )\n\nThere are several other manors with a similar name in Glamorgan and a MDR search using the search term “Llandough” gives the following result: Search results: Llandough Glamorgan | The National Archives    Sometimes the administration of different manors were brought together and this appears to have occurred  with the Llandough and St May’s Church Manors : https://archives.library.wales/index.php/manors-of-llandough-and-st-mary-church\n\nI will contact you again once I have had an opportunity to look at the original returns sent to Manorial Documents Committee. I hope this preliminary information is of assistance.\n\nYours sincerely\n\n[The National Archives logo]\nPhilip Gale | Head of the Collections and Cultural Property Team\nT: 020 3908 X 9193 | W: nationalarchives.gov.uk \nTwitter: @UkNatArchives\nThe National Archives, Kew, Richmond, Surrey [postcode redacted]\n\n\n\n\n________________________________\nFrom: Requester  >\nSent: Wednesday, February 18, 2026 5:49:54 PM\nTo: Philip Gale  >\nSubject: Re: Formal Notice for Record: Church View Close, Llandough / BP vs Buckler 1987\n\n\nEXTERNAL: This email originates from outside of The National Archives.\n\nDear Mr Gale,\n\nThank you for your reply of 12 February. I would like to clarify and narrow my request to ensure there is no misunderstanding regarding the records I am trying to locate.\n\nMy correspondence is not seeking an adjudication of ownership or title — I fully accept the stated remit boundaries of the Manorial Documents Register (MDR). Rather, my enquiry concerns the existence of any archival reference, index card, catalogue entry, or pointer to a document historically known as the “Williams Title”, which was, according to contemporaneous family testimony, at one point:\n\n  1.  Held in the local public library in the first half of the twentieth century; and\n  2.  Indexed in some manner (e.g., an index card or catalogue reference) that persisted even after the original document could not be located.\n\nMy understanding of the MDR — as described on your own website — is that it exists to provide brief descriptions of manorial records and details of their locations:\n\n“An official index of English and Welsh manorial records providing brief descriptions of documents and details of their locations… It does not include title deeds or conveyances, and is not a register of ownership of manorial lordships or rights.”\nhttps://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/manor-search \n\nBased on that description, I would be grateful if you could confirm whether:\n\n  1.  Any catalogue entries, index cards, reference pointers, or archival descriptions relating to the Manor of Llandough or the Williams family title exist in the MDR or related archival catalogues.\n  2.  If so, the reference identifiers and information on how these can be consulted in person or via digitised records.\n\nTo be precise, I am looking for historical references to the index card or listing for the Williams Title — whether held in a library, local record office, or other institutional archive — rather than a claim for ownership judgment itself.\n\nIf such a reference does not currently appear in the MDR, I would greatly appreciate guidance on where any known catalogue cards or entry lists for manorial documents from Glamorgan / Llandough prior to 1950 might now reside (e.g., regional archives, National Archives holdings, or affiliated local repository catalogues).\n\nAnyway I anticipate your forgiveness, if I have erred in writing to you earlier.\n\nThank you again and in advance for your assistance.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nRequester\nOn Wed, 18 Feb 2026 at 14:30, Philip Gale  > wrote:\n\nDear Requester,\n\n\n\nThank you for your e-mail of 10 February whose contents are noted. The National Archives does not hold any records relating to your enquiry. The Manorial Documents Register which this department administers is a register for the location of manorial documents and not of the ownership and descent of manorial titles:\n\n\n\nhttps://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/archives-sector/finding-records-in-discovery-and-other-databases/manorial-documents-register/\n\n\n\nA similar position holds for The National Archives responsibilities for public records and concerns about the past management of official records is a matter for the relevant government department such as HM Land Registry.\n\n\n\nYours sincerely\n\n\n\n\n\n[The National Archives logo]\n\nPhilip Gale | Head of the Collections and Cultural Property Team\nT: 020 3908 X 9193 | W: nationalarchives.gov.uk \nTwitter: @UkNatArchives\nThe National Archives, Kew, Richmond, Surrey [postcode redacted]\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n-----Original Message-----\n\nFrom: Requester  >\n\nSent: 10 February 2026 22:15\n\nTo: Archives Sector Leadership  >\n\nCc: [email redacted] ; [email redacted] ; Paul Egan  >; [email redacted] ; [email redacted] \n\nSubject: Formal Notice for Record: Church View Close, Llandough / BP vs Buckler 1987\n\n\n\n        You don't often get email from [email redacted] . Learn why this is important  \n\n\n\n\n\nEXTERNAL: This email originates from outside of The National Archives.\n\n\n\nFORMAL NOTICE OF RECORD CHALLENGE, ASSERTION OF RIGHT, AND DEMAND FOR ENGAGEMENT\n\n\n\nDear Sir/Madam,\n\n\n\nThis Notice is issued for placement on official record and for reliance. It is not informal correspondence. I urge you not to resort to silence, deflection, or delay, as doing so following receipt of this notice constitutes knowing reliance upon, and maintenance of, records alleged to be materially defective through fraud, concealment, denial of due process, and systemic erasure. Please report any suspicious activity and discrepancies identified.\n\n\n\nThis Notice is issued in pursuit of narrative justice at this stage; formal judicial remedies are expressly reserved. It serves to document our family's side of the story before it is completely erased and history rewritten, ensuring awareness among custodians of land, heritage, heraldry, archaeology, and public records.\n\n\n\nMy elder brother, Rhys Buckler, and I are the sons of the late William Buckler (d. 1991), defendant in the landmark legal case BP Properties Ltd v Buckler (1987), and the grandchildren of the late Mary Williams, whose dispossession, marginalisation, and exclusion from judicial determination remain unresolved matters of record. Mary Williams was repeatedly and materially falsely addressed/mislabeled in legal and administrative records as “Mrs Buckler,” a misidentification that was never judicially tested and which directly affected the handling, scope, and outcome of proceedings. The Williams/Buckler family held claim to the land title, having acquired and occupied Great House Farm continuously since 1667, based on family records, historical evidence, and centuries of resistance to adverse claims.\n\n\n\nI place on record that I am the first member of my family able to speak publicly and formally on these matters. Other close family members remain too traumatised and intimidated by the historical conduct of these proceedings—including violent eviction, demolition, and loss of livelihood—to engage, despite the continuing consequences of dispossession. That silence must not be misread as consent, acquiescence, or abandonment of right. There is a material distinction between free silence and silence produced by fear, trauma, and coercive experience.\n\n\n\nThe story, as we have pieced it together, reveals a multi-generational pattern of lawfare and siege: from Norman/English conquests in 1100 granting lordships over ancient Welsh Christian landscapes, through monastic (1215–1539) and Crown seizures (1539 onward), to industrial encroachments (e.g., 1876 Limeworks severance without consent) and modern corporate possession actions. This culminated in the 1988 demolition of an 800-year-old farmhouse amid police standoffs, leaving the family homeless and the site scarred, now buried under Church View Close. Archaeological vindication came too late—1994 excavations revealed over 1,000 Roman and medieval burials, confirming family warnings (e.g., the 1870 Roman soldier discovery under the living room floor, suppressed by authorities)—yet heritage records celebrate the site while omitting our dispossession.\n\n\n\nThe following assertions of right are now formally placed on record. They have never been lawfully adjudicated or extinguished:\n\n\n\n1.      Rhys Buckler may be the rightful Manorial Lord of the Manor of Llandough, by right of primogeniture and continuous hereditary connection arising from our family’s occupation of Great House Farm from 1667 until our narrative, legal, and physical dispossession.\n\n2.      I assert a claim to act as Squire of the Manor of Llandough, as executive representative and guardian of the Buckler–Williams estate and matriarchal lineage, with a mandate to pursue correction of record, restitution, and restorative remedies.\n\n\n\nThese are assertions of right, not settled declarations, placed on record pending proper adjudication—which has been historically obstructed through procedural narrowing, missing documents, and avoidance of full hearings.\n\n\n\nIn 1974, Mary Williams was twice prevented from presenting her case and obtaining a judicial determination of superior ownership, despite claims grounded in long occupation, intent, and possession. The court confined inquiry to animus possidendi (intention to possess) and excluded animus domini (intention to own), reclassifying her from putative owner to mere occupier and foreclosing title adjudication. BP sought possession only, not ownership rulings—a choice unexplained and significant. Proceedings were adjourned or quashed without resolution.\n\n\n\nThis occurred despite evidence of ownership intent, including:\n\n\n\n*       Centuries-long occupation and resistance (e.g., failed 1938 auction due to local support; post-1945 rent disputes).\n\n*       Roman-era remains and historical significance (e.g., Guglielmo Marconi's 1897 residence during radio experiments, with family aid erased from records).\n\n*       Unformalized deals like the 1895–1905 Daniel Thomas quarrying permission for promised title, undermined by vanished deeds.\n\n\n\nJudicial ownership examination would have engaged archaeological/heritage protections, incompatible with redevelopment—consequences avoided, raising credibility concerns.\n\n\n\nSerious unresolved anomalies persist, indicating something is still not right with the record:\n\n\n\n*       Licence correspondence dated 31 October 1974 surfacing only on 4 November, potentially interrupting proceedings to prevent hearings.\n\n*       Claims of 1974 benevolent withdrawal under press scrutiny unsupported by verifiable coverage.\n\n*       Land registration before Mary Williams’s 1983 death without title determination; disputes possibly lodged/suppressed by Land Registry.\n\n*       Inconsistent treatment of BP entities across proceedings to family detriment.\n\n*       Procedural barriers preventing effective defense (e.g., interrupted essential services post-licence; 1960–1987 court rulings dismissing ancestral title as \"notion\" without evidence).\n\n*       Knowing bypass of archaeological constraints (e.g., 1978 trenches ignored; no contemporaneous 1970s/1990s press on discoveries amid development).\n\n*       Missing key documents (e.g., 1667 conveyance, 1811–1835 Lambert Williams agreements proving status, 1982–84 library deeds vanishing).\n\n*       Risks of further erasure: Heritage databases celebrate \"Llandough Roman villa cemetery\" while omitting our saga; modern residents unaware of the \"battleground\" site.\n\n\n\nThese anomalies suggest systemic theft, from colonial English subjugation (e.g., 1543–1770 orbit shifts) to corporate siege, during Wales' cultural/economic transitions. Authority derived through such means is illegitimate under natural justice, customary Welsh principles, and moral-legal frameworks. The record's distortion secures advantage, but passage of time does not validate it.\n\n\n\nPreliminary assessment indicates cumulative benefits from redevelopment (absent title extinguishment) exceed £100 million, forming a basis for reparations—covering lost land, artifacts, livelihood, and trauma.\n\n\n\nI require relevant public servants, custodians, and office-holders (including Land Registry, heraldry bodies, museums, archaeologists, MPs, historians, and councillors) to cease delay, engage substantively, review/correct/annotate records where defective, and prevent further rewriting of history. Justice delayed is denied, especially with verifiable evidence available.\n\n\n\nThis Notice:\n\n\n\n*       Challenges the record's accuracy, completeness, and legitimacy.\n\n*       Place assertions of title/standing on record pending adjudication.\n\n*       Reserves all rights without prejudice.\n\n*       Puts officials/beneficiaries on notice that silence/reliance may aggravate responsibility.\n\n\n\nFor the avoidance of doubt, His Majesty the King was notified yesterday.\n\n\n\nThis serves as an invitation to engage constructively before reliance becomes untenable.\n\n\n\nRegards, Requester On behalf of the Buckler–Williams family [address redacted], Vale of Glamorgan, Wales. https://bpvsbuckler.datro.xyz/  \n\n\n\nPostscript — Disability and Succession (Material Notice): The heir-apparent to the asserted Manorial Lordship is Rhys Buckler’s son, a young man with Down syndrome. Any obstruction, marginalisation, or differential treatment affecting his inheritance would engage the Equality Act 2010 and UK international obligations on disability rights. Disability cannot bar succession or recognition. This is recorded to prevent claims of ignorance.\nPlease don't print this e-mail unless you really need to. ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- National Archives Disclaimer This email and any files transmitted with it are intended solely for the use of the individual(s) to whom they are addressed. If you are not the intended recipient and have received this email in error, please notify the sender and delete the email. Opinions, conclusions and other information in this message and attachments that do not relate to the official business of The National Archives are neither given nor endorsed by it. ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPlease don't print this e-mail unless you really need to. ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- National Archives Disclaimer This email and any files transmitted with it are intended solely for the use of the individual(s) to whom they are addressed. If you are not the intended recipient and have received this email in error, please notify the sender and delete the email. Opinions, conclusions and other information in this message and attachments that do not relate to the official business of The National Archives are neither given nor endorsed by it. ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------"},{"id":"gm-19c8a7763f11a7fc","subject":"RE: Epexio contact us form submission","name":"RE: Epexio contact us form submission","date":"2026-02-23T12:26:40Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19c8a7763f11a7fc.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19c3332c5fd8d6d4-epexio-contact-us-form-submission","scopeTopics":["glamorgan"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["glamorgan"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19c3332c5fd8d6d4","direction":"received","from":"Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg","institutions":[{"id":"glamorgan","name":"Glamorgan Archives"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19c8a7763f11a7fc.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester.\n\nThank you for your email.\n\nIf items are withdrawn from the collection, a record will be kept on file here at the Archives recording when the item was withdrawn and who withdrew it.\n\nI hope this is of help.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nRhian Diggins [cid:image001.jpg@01DCA4BF.A8715260]\nUwch Archifydd / Senior Archivist\n\nArchifau Morgannwg / Glamorgan Archives\n[phone redacted]\n[email redacted] \nhttps://glamarchives.gov.uk/\n\nFrom: Requester  \nSent: 20 February 2026 16:01\nTo: Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg  \nSubject: Re: Epexio contact us form submission\n\nEXTERNAL: This email originated from outside Cardiff Council, take care when clicking links.\nALLANOL: Daw'r e-bost hwn o'r tu allan i Gyngor Caerdydd, cymerwch ofal wrth glicio ar ddolenni.\n\n\nDear Ms Diggins,\n\nThank you for your clarification. You are being most helpful.\n\nI understand that if a document is permanently withdrawn from the collection, its catalogue description is removed.\n\nMay I ask a procedural question:\n\nWhen privately deposited records are withdrawn, does Glamorgan Archives retain any internal administrative record of:\n\n  *   the fact that the material was once held,\n  *   the identity of the depositor,\n  *   and the date of withdrawal?\n\nIn other words, while the public catalogue entry may be removed, is there an accession register or internal record that preserves evidence that the material once formed part of the collection?\n\nI am not asking you to adjudicate ownership. I am simply trying to establish whether archival systems ordinarily retain a trace of withdrawn deposits.\n\nThank you for your assistance.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nRequester\n\nOn Fri, 20 Feb 2026 at 06:00, Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg  > wrote:\n2026/0859\nDear Requester.\nThank you for your email.\nGlamorgan Archives had at one time a large number of paper catalogues, along with card indexes. The data from these was transferred into our catalogue when we moved from analogue to digital. The original catalogues and cards were not retained.\nOur catalogue only records descriptions of documents that are held within our collection. If a document is permanently withdrawn from the collection, we would remove the description from the catalogue. Many of our records are private records held on deposit on behalf of the owner. We have no control over what they do or don’t give us. The owner retains the right to withdraw these documents if they wish.\nYours sincerely,\nRhian Diggins [cid:image001.jpg@01DCA4BF.A8715260]\nUwch Archifydd / Senior Archivist\nArchifau Morgannwg / Glamorgan Archives\n[phone redacted]\n[email redacted] \nhttps://glamarchives.gov.uk/\nFrom: Requester  >\nSent: 19 February 2026 10:34\nTo: Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg  >\nSubject: Re: Epexio contact us form submission\nEXTERNAL: This email originated from outside Cardiff Council, take care when clicking links.\nALLANOL: Daw'r e-bost hwn o'r tu allan i Gyngor Caerdydd, cymerwch ofal wrth glicio ar ddolenni.\n\nDear Ms Diggins,\n\nI would be grateful for clarification on a specific point.\n\nIt has long been understood within my family that an index card existed referring to the title deeds of Great House Farm, Llandough. While the deeds themselves reportedly went missing, the index reference was said to remain at one time.\n\nMy question is simply this:\n\nIf an index card or catalogue entry once existed within Glamorgan Archives (or its predecessor systems), would a record of that entry survive following digitisation, even if the underlying document was withdrawn, transferred, or missing?\n\nIn other words, are superseded or removed catalogue references retained within archival metadata systems?\n\nI am not alleging wrongdoing. I am trying to establish whether any trace of such an index entry would ordinarily remain within your records management framework.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nRequester\nOn Thu, 19 Feb 2026 at 06:05, Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg  > wrote:\nDear Requester.\nThank you for your email.\nCan I suggest that you contact the National Archives ? They hold responsibility for public records in England and Wales and may be best placed to advise you.\nYours sincerely,\nRhian Diggins [cid:image001.jpg@01DCA4BF.A8715260]\nUwch Archifydd / Senior Archivist\nArchifau Morgannwg / Glamorgan Archives\n[phone redacted]\n[email redacted] \nhttps://glamarchives.gov.uk/\nFrom: Requester  >\nSent: 18 February 2026 16:53\nTo: Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg  >\nSubject: Re: Epexio contact us form submission\nEXTERNAL: This email originated from outside Cardiff Council, take care when clicking links.\nALLANOL: Daw'r e-bost hwn o'r tu allan i Gyngor Caerdydd, cymerwch ofal wrth glicio ar ddolenni.\n\nDear Ms Diggins,\n\nThank you for your reply and for clarifying Glamorgan Archives’ position regarding access and charges for copies of material. I appreciate the work you do in preserving the records entrusted to your care.\n\nI would like to reiterate that my correspondence is not a routine request for private research access. Rather, it is a formal expression of concern regarding the potential historical misdescription or suppression of facts relating to Great House Farm, Llandough, including heritage and archaeological records. My concern is about the accuracy and completeness of the archival record itself, rather than the provision of copies or personal research access.\n\nAs I am currently residing in Argentina, I am unable to attend in person, and my intention is not to request material for private use, but to highlight potential issues with public record integrity. I would therefore be grateful if you could advise which department, officer, or public body I should contact to formally raise concerns regarding historical misrepresentation within public records or archival descriptions, so that these matters may be properly examined.\n\nFor the avoidance of doubt, I am not alleging any impropriety on the part of current archive staff. My concern relates solely to historical processes and record-keeping.\n\nThank you very much for your time and consideration, and I would be grateful for any guidance you can provide.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nRequester\nOn Wed, 18 Feb 2026 at 11:51, Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg  > wrote:\n2026/0672\nDear Requester.\nThank you for your email, received on 9 February, and for outlining within it your concerns, which we have noted.\nGlamorgan Archives holds only the records that we have been given. We cannot make any assumptions as to whether the content of those records is accurate. And we have a duty to preserve the records which are presented to us as they are presented to us. In addition, Glamorgan Archives has an income target and, as such, we aren’t in a position to provide free copies of material in our care.\nYours sincerely,\nRhian Diggins [cid:image001.jpg@01DCA4BF.A8715260]\nUwch Archifydd / Senior Archivist\nArchifau Morgannwg / Glamorgan Archives\n[phone redacted]\n[email redacted] \nhttps://glamarchives.gov.uk/\nFrom: Requester  >\nSent: 06 February 2026 17:51\nTo: Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg  >\nSubject: Re: Epexio contact us form submission\nEXTERNAL: This email originated from outside Cardiff Council, take care when clicking links.\nALLANOL: Daw'r e-bost hwn o'r tu allan i Gyngor Caerdydd, cymerwch ofal wrth glicio ar ddolenni.\n\nMs Taylor,\n\nThanks for your reply and for outlining the options available to consult the estate plans referenced.\n\nI should clarify my position, as my correspondence is not a routine request for private research access, but part of a wider and serious concern regarding possible historical misdescription, suppression, and/or concealment of material facts relating to Great House Farm, Llandough.\n\nI am currently at the Welsh settlement in Argentina, having been displaced from my ancestral birthplace in Llandough. As such, it is not possible for me to attend the searchroom in person so easy as you're 7,500 miles away, nor to “pop in” to consult records on site. Requiring physical attendance or payment for access is therefore not a practical or equitable option in my circumstances.\n\nMore importantly, my concern is that certain archival descriptions, surveys, and planning-era records may themselves be inaccurate or incomplete as a result of earlier failures of disclosure. Specifically, a Roman burial reportedly discovered at Great House Farm in 1888 appears to have been treated as a “surprise” in later archaeological surveys (1978/1994), and an occupied, c.800-year-old domestic dwelling was repeatedly described in planning documentation as an “old barn” or “utility building.” These characterisations materially affected heritage protection, culminating in demolition in 1988.\n\nIn that context, I am not seeking to pay for copies of records whose accuracy is itself in question, nor to participate financially in processes that may inadvertently perpetuate errors arising from that period. My intention is to place relevant public bodies on notice of a potential issue of public record integrity and heritage governance, not to obtain documents for private interest. I have written to the Senedd calling for a public enquiry too. I've notified the land registry and serious fraud teams too.\n\nI am therefore asking that my correspondence be treated as a formal expression of concern regarding the reliability and completeness of the archival record as it relates to this site, and that this concern be noted accordingly. I would also be grateful if you could advise which department or officer is appropriate for addressing concerns of this nature, where the issue is not access but potential historical misrepresentation within public records.\n\nFor the avoidance of doubt, I am not alleging any impropriety on the part of current archive staff. My concern relates to historical processes and decisions, and to ensuring that those matters are properly examined rather than inadvertently reinforced.\n\nThank you for your time and consideration.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nRequester\n\n________________________________\nOn Fri, 6 Feb 2026, 10:44 Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg,  > wrote:\nRef: 2026/0633\nDear Requester,\nThank you for your email received on 5th February 2026.\n\nYou would be welcome to visit us to consult the estate plans DB/E/1-2 in our public searchroom and take your own photographs for free. Our opening hours are Tues-Thurs 9.30am-12.30pm & 1.30pm-4.30pm. We are currently taking bookings from Tuesday 17th February 2026. Please note that our public searchroom is closed for the week Tues 10th February – Thurs 12th February so that staff can carry out essential work on the collections. We will re-open to the public on Tuesday 17th February 2026.\nIf you have not visited us before, you are required to register. We ask that you bring with you two forms of ID, one showing your name e.g. Driving Licence and the other your current address e.g. current utility bill or official letter.\nAlternatively, if you are unable to visit us in person, and if you can confirm exactly which pages you require, staff can provide digital copies, for which we charge £15.60 for the first image and £4.80 each for subsequent images, payable in advance. Prices include VAT.\nYou can find a detailed list on the catalogue: Survey of marquis of Bute’s Glamorgan estates, by David Stewart.  If you would like a quote for digital copies, please let get in touch.\nWe hope this information is of assistance to you.\nYours sincerely,\nMelanie Taylor [cid:image002.jpg@01DCA4BF.A8715260]\nCynorthwyydd Cofnodion / Records Assistant\nArchifau Morgannwg / Glamorgan Archives\nClos Parc Morgannwg \nLecwydd / Leckwith \nCaerdydd / Cardiff \n[postcode redacted] \n[phone redacted]\n[email redacted] \nwww.archifaumorgannwg.gov.uk  / www.glamarchives.gov.uk \nwww.facebook.com/glamarchives \nwww.twitter.com/glamarchives \n\n**********************************************************************\n\nThe Council welcomes correspondence in Welsh, English or bilingually. We will ensure that we communicate with you in the language of your choice, as long as you let us know which you prefer. Corresponding in Welsh will not lead to delay.\n\nPrivileged/Confidential Information may be contained in this message. If you are not the addressee indicated in this message (or responsible for delivery of the message to such person), you may not copy or deliver this message to anyone. In such case, you should destroy this message and kindly notify the sender by reply email. Please advise immediately if you or your employer does not consent to Internet email for messages of this kind. Opinions, conclusions and other information in this message that do not relate to the official business of the Council of the City and County of Cardiff shall be understood as neither given nor endorsed by it. All e-mail sent to or from this address will be processed by Cardiff County Councils Corporate E-mail system and may be subject to scrutiny by someone other than the addressee.\n\n**********************************************************************\n\nMae’r Cyngor yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg, Saesneg neu’n ddwyieithog. Byddwn yn cyfathrebu â chi yn ôl eich dewis, dim ond i chi roi gwybod i ni pa un sydd well gennych. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi.\n\nMae'n bosibl bod gwybodaeth gyfrinachol yn y neges hon. Os na chyfeirir y neges atoch chi'n benodol (neu os nad ydych chi'n gyfrifol am drosglwyddo'r neges i'r person a enwir), yna ni chewch gopio na throsglwyddo'r neges. Mewn achos o'r fath, dylech ddinistrio'r neges a hysbysu'r anfonwr drwy e-bost ar unwaith. Rhowch wybod i'r anfonydd ar unwaith os nad ydych chi neu eich cyflogydd yn caniatau e-bost y Rhyngrwyd am negeseuon fel hon. Rhaid deall nad yw'r safbwyntiau, y casgliadau a'r wybodaeth arall yn y neges hon nad ydynt yn cyfeirio at fusnes swyddogol Cyngor Dinas a Sir Caerdydd yn cynrychioli barn y Cyngor Sir nad yn cael sel ei fendith. Caiff unrhyw negeseuon a anfonir at, neu o'r cyfeiriad e-bost hwn eu prosesu gan system E-bost Gorfforaethol Cyngor Sir Caerdydd a gallant gael eu harchwilio gan rywun heblaw'r person a enwir.\n\n**********************************************************************"},{"id":"gm-19c8a4a462ee229a","subject":"RE: Historic Adverse Possession Licence Interruption Cases (1950–2000)","name":"RE: Historic Adverse Possession Licence Interruption Cases (1950–2000)","date":"2026-02-23T11:37:23Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19c8a4a462ee229a.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19c8a4a462ee229a-historic-adverse-possession-licence-interruption-cases-1950-2000","scopeTopics":["legal-regulators"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["legal-regulators"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19c8a4a462ee229a","direction":"received","from":"Contactcentre","institutions":[{"id":"legal-regulators","name":"Legal regulators"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19c8a4a462ee229a.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Sensitivity: General\n\nDear Requester,\n\nThank you for your email of 14 February 2026.\n\nIf you have a specific question that we can help with, please Contact us<https://www.sra.org.uk/home/contact-us/> and we will be happy to assist you.\n\nWhen copied into an email with several other recipients we will not respond in any other way.\n\nDid my response help you today?\n\nPlease let me know if my response helped you today by clicking the feedback button below.\n\n[cid:image001.png@01DCA4B8.55728AE0]<https://form.sra.org.uk/s3/feedback-cc?csa=JADELANGFORD>\n\nIt should take you less than two minutes to complete, and we review all feedback that we receive.\n\nIf you have any further queries, please contact us<http://www.sra.org.uk/contact-us/>.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nJade Langford\nCustomer Services Adviser\nContact Centre\nSolicitors Regulation Authority/Awdurdod Rheoleiddio Cyfreithwyr\n[phone redacted]\n[email redacted]<[email redacted]>\nwww.sra.org.uk<http://www.sra.org.uk/>\n\n﻿From:    Requester <[email redacted]>\nSent:     14 February 2026 10:09\nTo:         [email redacted]\nSubject:              Historic Adverse Possession Licence Interruption Cases (1950–2000)\nAttachments:    Forensic_Evaluation_and_Restitutionary_Brief_Ty_Mawr_v0.6_clean.pdf\n\n**This message originated from outside the SRA- Please treat attachments or links with caution**\n________________________________\nSirs/Ma'am,\n\nAny response about my request for a public enquiry of BP Vs Buckler 1987 and possible fraud & consealment, collusion, conspiracy and deception?\n\nFraud unravels all. And begins when the fraud is discovered, not occured.  Yes?\n\nFurthermore,\n\nI am writing to invite interest in review of a potential coordinated action concerning historic adverse possession cases in England and Wales. As an indigenous Welshman. From Wales. Son of the late defendant, the possibly disspossed and victim of fraud; Mr. William Buckler.\n\nMyself and my lawyers and team are investigating circumstances in which long-term occupiers were issued licence letters (often described as “rent-free life licences” or similar instruments) during or after limitation periods, and whether in certain instances such licences may not have constituted legally effective interruption of adverse possession.\n\nThe investigation concerns cases primarily between 1950 and 2000 where:\nLong-term occupation had exceeded 12 years;\n\nPossession proceedings were either adjourned or unresolved;\n\nA licence letter was issued and relied upon to defeat a possessory claim;\n\nQuestions may arise regarding identity, acceptance, or material disclosure.\n\nAt this stage, we are seeking:\n\nA preliminary view as to whether a coordinated group litigation strategy (GLO or similar) could be viable;\n\nAdvice on limitation, concealment, and fraud thresholds;\n\nGuidance on structured claimant intake if sufficient common issues are identified.\n\nThis enquiry is investigative only and no public allegations are being made.\n\nWe are seeking professional assessment before any formal steps are taken.\n\nIf this is of potential interest, I would welcome a confidential discussion at your convenience.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester Esq. Sgt (Ret'd)\nManor of Llandough\nAnd Constituent at [address redacted],\nDinas Powys, Vale of Glamorgan\n[phone redacted]\n[cid:ii_19c5b8f9c7041f78afb1]\n\n\nFor information on how we handle your personal data, see our privacy notice<http://www.sra.org.uk/dpa>.\nThis email is intended for the addressee only. This includes any attachments. Its unauthorised use, further processing, storage or copying is not allowed. If you are not the intended recipient, please let the sender know and then destroy all copies.\nPlease note the sender is not authorised to conclude any contract on behalf of the Solicitors Regulation Authority by email.\nWe are the regulator of solicitors and law firms in England and Wales. The Solicitors Regulation Authority Limited is a company limited by guarantee registered in England and Wales. Our registered offices are: The Cube, 199 Wharfside Street, Birmingham, [postcode redacted]. Our company registration number is: 12608059."},{"id":"gm-19c89b943b5257b0","subject":"ATB/RE: Cais am Ddeiseb ac Ymchwiliad Cyhoeddus: Achos Buckler v BP (1987) a Dinistr Great House Farm, Llandough / Formal Request for Inquiry: The Great House Farm Dispossession (BP vs Buckler)","name":"ATB/RE: Cais am Ddeiseb ac Ymchwiliad Cyhoeddus: Achos Buckler v BP (1987) a Dinistr Great House Farm, Llandough / Formal Request for Inquiry: The Great House Farm Dispossession (BP vs Buckler)","date":"2026-02-23T08:58:57Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19c89b943b5257b0.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19c89b943b5257b0-atb-re-cais-am-ddeiseb-ac-ymchwiliad-cyhoeddus-achos-buckler-v-bp-1987-a-dinistr-great-house-farm-llandough-formal-reque","scopeTopics":["senedd"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["senedd"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19c89b943b5257b0","direction":"received","from":"Petitions Committee | Y Pwyllgor Deisebau","institutions":[{"id":"senedd","name":"Senedd"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19c89b943b5257b0.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Requester,\n\nDiolch am eich neges.\n\nFel dwedodd Lara, y ffordd gorau nawr bydd i greu deiseb trwy ein system arlein. https://deisebau.senedd.cymru/deisebau/gwirio\n\nMwy o manylion yma,\n\nGareth\n\n\nOddi wrth: Requester  \nAnfonwyd: 17 February 2026 13:32\nAt: Petitions Committee | Y Pwyllgor Deisebau  \nPwnc: Re: Cais am Ddeiseb ac Ymchwiliad Cyhoeddus: Achos Buckler v BP (1987) a Dinistr Great House Farm, Llandough / Formal Request for Inquiry: The Great House Farm Dispossession (BP vs Buckler)\n\n\nLara,\n\nDiolch am eich arweiniad.\n\nRwyf wedi mireinio fy mhethos arfaethedig ymhellach er mwyn ehangu ei gwmpas. Mae'n cynnwys nawr alwadau am ymchwiliad i achosion hanesyddol tebyg lluosog yng Nghymru, gan gynnwys archwilio problemau systemig posibl fel twyll, gorthrymu tystiolaeth, neu anwybyddu/gweithio o amgylch gwarchodaethau amaethyddol a threftadaeth mewn anghytundebau meddiannu anghyfreithlon—gan o bosibl atal y cloc cyfyngu ar feddiannu anghyfreithlon yn anghyfreithlon neu yn anghyfansoddiadol—tra'n defnyddio Fferm Great House (BP v Buckler 1987) fel prif enghraifft.\n\nMae'r ffocws yn parhau i fod yn gadarn ar faterion datganoledig: polisi amgylchedd hanesyddol Cymru, gwarchodaethau amaethyddol, rôl CADW, a diwygiadau i fynd i'r afael ag unrhyw fylchau a nodir, heb geisio ailymweld â dyfarniadau llys penodol.\n\nRwy'n cadarnhau bod gennyf gyfeiriad yng Nghymru: [address redacted]. Rwy'n etholwr yn Nyffryn Morgannwg ac rwyf wedi ysgrifennu at yr AS presennol a'r Cynghorydd Carroll yn Llandochau; maent yn ymwybodol o'r mater a chredaf eu bod yn edrych i mewn iddo.\n\nOs caiff ei fframio fel hyn—gan bwysleisio \"dosbarth\" posibl o ffermydd/teuluoedd Cymreig yr effeithir arnynt a methiannau polisi ehangach—a ydych chi'n credu y byddai'n bodloni'r safonau'n ddigonol i ennill sylw'r Pwyllgor a mynd ymlaen i gyflwyno/cyhoeddi?\n\nNid yw twyll byth yn rhy bell yn y gorffennol, gan nad yw'r cyfnod cyfyngu yn berthnasol mewn amgylchiadau o'r fath ac fe'i datodolir i gyd yn ôl y gyfraith. Mae hefyd yn gyfyngedig o'r dyddiad y cafodd ei ddarganfod—ac rwyf newydd ei ddarganfod—ac nid o ddyddiad y twyll honedig.\n\nEr gwaethaf hynny, rydym yn dal yn deulu Cymreig brodorol dadfeddiannol ar ein tir geni hynafiaid. Rydym yn dal yma.\n\nOs na, pa gamau eraill y byddech chi'n eu hargymell (er enghraifft, ymgysylltu ag Aelod o'r Senedd, adrannau Llywodraeth Cymru fel CADW, neu sianeli eraill ar gyfer codi materion systemig tir/amaethyddol)?\n\nByddai unrhyw gyngor ar gryfhau'r geiriad neu'r dystiolaeth ategol yn cael ei werthfawrogi'n fawr cyn i mi gyflwyno.\n\nDiolch—mae'r mater hwn o bwysigrwydd sylweddol i gyfanrwydd diwylliannol, amaethyddol a hanesyddol Cymru.\n\nCofion cynhes,\nRequester\n[address redacted]\n\n...................  [SAESNEG/ ENGLISH] .....................\n\nThanks for your guidance.\n\nI've further refined my proposed petition to broaden its scope, incorporating calls for an inquiry into multiple similar historical cases in Wales. This includes examining the possibility of systemic issues like fraud, suppression of evidence, or ignoring/working around agricultural and heritage protections in adverse possession disputes—potentially to unlawfully or unconstitutionally stop limitation clock on adverse possession—while using the Great House Farm (BP v Buckler 1987) as a key example. The focus remains on devolved matters: Welsh historic environment policy, agricultural safeguards, CADW's role, and reforms to address gaps without revisiting specific court judgments.\n\nAnd yes I have an address, it's [address redacted]. I am a constituent in the Vale of Glamorgan and I have written to the current MP and councillor Carroll in Llandough, they are aware and I trust they are looking into it.\n\nIf framed this way—emphasizing a possible \"class\" of affected Welsh farms/families and broader policy failures—do you believe it would meet the standards to gain the Committee's attention and proceed to submission/publication?\n\nFraud is never too far in the past as the statute of limitation doesn't apply in such circumstances and furthermore it unravels all according to law. It is also only limited from the date it was discovered and I recently discovered, not from the date of occurrence of the alleged fraud.\n\nNotwithstanding we are still an dispossessed indigenous Welsh family on our ancestral birthland. We are still here.\n\nFailing that, what alternative steps do you recommend (e.g., approaching a Member of the Senedd, Welsh Government departments like CADW, or other channels for raising systemic land/agricultural issues)?\n\nAny advice on strengthening wording or evidence would be appreciated before submission.\n\nThank you—this matter holds significant importance for Welsh cultural, agricultural, and historical integrity.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester [address redacted]"},{"id":"gm-19c8443dd5799f54","subject":"Please update the status of your request - Freedom of Information Request – Administrative Records for BP Properties Ltd v Buckler (1974)","name":"Please update the status of your request - Freedom of Information Request – Administrative Records for BP Properties Ltd v Buckler (1974)","date":"2026-02-22T07:32:44Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19c8443dd5799f54.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19c8443dd5799f54-please-update-the-status-of-your-request-freedom-of-information-request-administrative-records-for-bp-properties-ltd-v-b","scopeTopics":["moj"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["moj"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19c8443dd5799f54","direction":"received","from":"(name not shown; whatdotheyknow.com)","institutions":[{"id":"moj","name":"Ministry of Justice / HMCTS"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19c8443dd5799f54.json","textStatus":"available","text":"To let everyone know, follow this link and then select the appropriate box.\n\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/profile/sign_in?r=%2Frequest%2Ffreedom_of_information_request_a_2133%23describe_state_form_1\n\nYour request was called 'Freedom of Information Request – Administrative Records for BP Properties Ltd v Buckler (1974)'. Letting everyone know whether you got the information will help us keep tabs on\nHM Courts and Tribunals Service.\n\n-- the WhatDoTheyKnow team\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nWhatDoTheyKnow is a project of mySociety run by a small team of staff and\ndedicated volunteers.\n\nmySociety is a charity that believes that information should be accessible to everyone.\nIf this is important to you, please consider donating to help us do more:\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/donate\n\nFind out more about how we handle your data at:\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/help/privacy\n\nmySociety is a registered charity in England and Wales (1076346), and a limited\ncompany (03277032)"},{"id":"gm-19c7bc811dcb8436","subject":"Re: Epexio contact us form submission","name":"Re: Epexio contact us form submission","date":"2026-02-20T16:00:32Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19c7bc811dcb8436.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19c3332c5fd8d6d4-epexio-contact-us-form-submission","scopeTopics":["glamorgan"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["glamorgan"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19c3332c5fd8d6d4","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"glamorgan","name":"Glamorgan Archives"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19c7bc811dcb8436.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Ms Diggins,\n\nThank you for your clarification. You are being most helpful.\n\nI understand that if a document is permanently withdrawn from the\ncollection, its catalogue description is removed.\n\nMay I ask a procedural question:\n\nWhen privately deposited records are withdrawn, does Glamorgan Archives\nretain any internal administrative record of:\n\n   -\n\n   the fact that the material was once held,\n   -\n\n   the identity of the depositor,\n   -\n\n   and the date of withdrawal?\n\nIn other words, while the public catalogue entry may be removed, is there\nan accession register or internal record that preserves evidence that the\nmaterial once formed part of the collection?\n\nI am not asking you to adjudicate ownership. I am simply trying to\nestablish whether archival systems ordinarily retain a trace of withdrawn\ndeposits.\n\nThank you for your assistance.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\n\nRequester\n\nOn Fri, 20 Feb 2026 at 06:00, Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg <\n[email redacted]> wrote:\n\n> 2026/0859\n>\n>\n>\n> Dear Requester.\n>\n>\n>\n> Thank you for your email.\n>\n>\n>\n> Glamorgan Archives had at one time a large number of paper catalogues,\n> along with card indexes.  The data from these was transferred into our\n> catalogue when we moved from analogue to digital.  The original catalogues\n> and cards were not retained.\n>\n>\n>\n> Our catalogue only records descriptions of documents that are held within\n> our collection.  If a document is permanently withdrawn from the\n> collection, we would remove the description from the catalogue. Many of our\n> records are private records held on deposit on behalf of the owner.  We\n> have no control over what they do or don’t give us.  The owner retains the\n> right to withdraw these documents if they wish.\n>\n>\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n>\n>\n> Rhian Diggins\n>\n> Uwch Archifydd / Senior Archivist\n>\n>\n>\n> Archifau Morgannwg / Glamorgan Archives\n>\n> [phone redacted]\n>\n> [email redacted]\n>\n> https://glamarchives.gov.uk/\n>\n>\n>\n> *From:* Requester <[email redacted]>\n> *Sent:* 19 February 2026 10:34\n> *To:* Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg <[email redacted]>\n> *Subject:* Re: Epexio contact us form submission\n>\n>\n>\n> *EXTERNAL:* This email originated from outside Cardiff Council, take care\n> when clicking links.\n> *ALLANOL:* Daw'r e-bost hwn o'r tu allan i Gyngor Caerdydd, cymerwch ofal\n> wrth glicio ar ddolenni.\n>\n>\n>\n> Dear Ms Diggins,\n>\n> I would be grateful for clarification on a specific point.\n>\n> It has long been understood within my family that an index card existed\n> referring to the title deeds of Great House Farm, Llandough. While the\n> deeds themselves reportedly went missing, the index reference was said to\n> remain at one time.\n>\n> My question is simply this:\n>\n> If an index card or catalogue entry once existed within Glamorgan Archives\n> (or its predecessor systems), would a record of that entry survive\n> following digitisation, even if the underlying document was withdrawn,\n> transferred, or missing?\n>\n> In other words, are superseded or removed catalogue references retained\n> within archival metadata systems?\n>\n> I am not alleging wrongdoing. I am trying to establish whether any trace\n> of such an index entry would ordinarily remain within your records\n> management framework.\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n>\n> Requester\n>\n>\n>\n> On Thu, 19 Feb 2026 at 06:05, Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg <\n> [email redacted]> wrote:\n>\n> Dear Requester.\n>\n> Thank you for your email.\n>\n> Can I suggest that you contact the National Archives\n> <https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/>? They hold responsibility for\n> public records in England and Wales and may be best placed to advise you.\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n> Rhian Diggins\n>\n> Uwch Archifydd / Senior Archivist\n>\n> Archifau Morgannwg / Glamorgan Archives\n>\n> [phone redacted]\n>\n> [email redacted]\n>\n> https://glamarchives.gov.uk/\n>\n> *From:* Requester <[email redacted]>\n> *Sent:* 18 February 2026 16:53\n> *To:* Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg <[email redacted]>\n> *Subject:* Re: Epexio contact us form submission\n>\n> *EXTERNAL:* This email originated from outside Cardiff Council, take care\n> when clicking links.\n> *ALLANOL:* Daw'r e-bost hwn o'r tu allan i Gyngor Caerdydd, cymerwch ofal\n> wrth glicio ar ddolenni.\n>\n> Dear Ms Diggins,\n>\n> Thank you for your reply and for clarifying Glamorgan Archives’ position\n> regarding access and charges for copies of material. I appreciate the work\n> you do in preserving the records entrusted to your care.\n>\n> I would like to reiterate that my correspondence is not a routine request\n> for private research access. Rather, it is a formal expression of concern\n> regarding the potential historical misdescription or suppression of facts\n> relating to Great House Farm, Llandough, including heritage and\n> archaeological records. My concern is about the accuracy and completeness\n> of the archival record itself, rather than the provision of copies or\n> personal research access.\n>\n> As I am currently residing in Argentina, I am unable to attend in person,\n> and my intention is not to request material for private use, but to\n> highlight potential issues with public record integrity. I would therefore\n> be grateful if you could advise which department, officer, or public body I\n> should contact to formally raise concerns regarding historical\n> misrepresentation within public records or archival descriptions, so that\n> these matters may be properly examined.\n>\n> For the avoidance of doubt, I am not alleging any impropriety on the part\n> of current archive staff. My concern relates solely to historical processes\n> and record-keeping.\n>\n> Thank you very much for your time and consideration, and I would be\n> grateful for any guidance you can provide.\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n>\n> *Requester*\n>\n> On Wed, 18 Feb 2026 at 11:51, Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg <\n> [email redacted]> wrote:\n>\n> 2026/0672\n>\n> Dear Requester.\n>\n> Thank you for your email, received on 9 February, and for outlining within\n> it your concerns, which we have noted.\n>\n> Glamorgan Archives holds only the records that we have been given. We\n> cannot make any assumptions as to whether the content of those records is\n> accurate. And we have a duty to preserve the records which are presented to\n> us as they are presented to us. In addition, Glamorgan Archives has an\n> income target and, as such, we aren’t in a position to provide free copies\n> of material in our care.\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n> Rhian Diggins\n>\n> Uwch Archifydd / Senior Archivist\n>\n> Archifau Morgannwg / Glamorgan Archives\n>\n> [phone redacted]\n>\n> [email redacted]\n>\n> https://glamarchives.gov.uk/\n>\n> *From:* Requester <[email redacted]>\n> *Sent:* 06 February 2026 17:51\n> *To:* Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg <[email redacted]>\n> *Subject:* Re: Epexio contact us form submission\n>\n> *EXTERNAL:* This email originated from outside Cardiff Council, take care\n> when clicking links.\n> *ALLANOL:* Daw'r e-bost hwn o'r tu allan i Gyngor Caerdydd, cymerwch ofal\n> wrth glicio ar ddolenni.\n>\n> Ms Taylor,\n>\n> Thanks for your reply and for outlining the options available to consult\n> the estate plans referenced.\n>\n> I should clarify my position, as my correspondence is not a routine\n> request for private research access, but part of a wider and serious\n> concern regarding possible historical misdescription, suppression, and/or\n> concealment of material facts relating to Great House Farm, Llandough.\n>\n> I am currently at the Welsh settlement in Argentina, having been displaced\n> from my ancestral birthplace in Llandough. As such, it is not possible for\n> me to attend the searchroom in person so easy as you're 7,500 miles away,\n> nor to “pop in” to consult records on site. Requiring physical attendance\n> or payment for access is therefore not a practical or equitable option in\n> my circumstances.\n>\n> More importantly, my concern is that certain archival descriptions,\n> surveys, and planning-era records may themselves be inaccurate or\n> incomplete as a result of earlier failures of disclosure. Specifically, a\n> Roman burial reportedly discovered at Great House Farm in 1888 appears to\n> have been treated as a “surprise” in later archaeological surveys\n> (1978/1994), and an occupied, c.800-year-old domestic dwelling was\n> repeatedly described in planning documentation as an “old barn” or “utility\n> building.” These characterisations materially affected heritage protection,\n> culminating in demolition in 1988.\n>\n> In that context, I am not seeking to pay for copies of records whose\n> accuracy is itself in question, nor to participate financially in processes\n> that may inadvertently perpetuate errors arising from that period. My\n> intention is to place relevant public bodies on notice of a potential issue\n> of public record integrity and heritage governance, not to obtain documents\n> for private interest. I have written to the Senedd calling for a public\n> enquiry too. I've notified the land registry and serious fraud teams too.\n>\n> I am therefore asking that my correspondence be treated as a formal\n> expression of concern regarding the reliability and completeness of the\n> archival record as it relates to this site, and that this concern be noted\n> accordingly. I would also be grateful if you could advise which department\n> or officer is appropriate for addressing concerns of this nature, where the\n> issue is not access but potential historical misrepresentation within\n> public records.\n>\n> For the avoidance of doubt, I am not alleging any impropriety on the part\n> of current archive staff. My concern relates to historical processes and\n> decisions, and to ensuring that those matters are properly examined rather\n> than inadvertently reinforced.\n>\n> Thank you for your time and consideration.\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n> Requester\n> ------------------------------\n>\n> On Fri, 6 Feb 2026, 10:44 Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg, <\n> [email redacted]> wrote:\n>\n> *Ref: 2026/0633*\n>\n> Dear Requester,\n>\n> Thank you f