addition, Glamorgan Archives has an income target and, as such, we aren’t in a position to provide free copies of material in our care.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nRhian Diggins [cid:image002.jpg@01DCA0E6.05398450]\nUwch Archifydd / Senior Archivist\n\nArchifau Morgannwg / Glamorgan Archives\n[phone redacted]\n[email redacted]<[email redacted]>\nhttps://glamarchives.gov.uk/\n\nFrom: Requester <[email redacted]>\nSent: 06 February 2026 17:51\nTo: Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg <[email redacted]>\nSubject: Re: Epexio contact us form submission\n\nEXTERNAL: This email originated from outside Cardiff Council, take care when clicking links.\nALLANOL: Daw'r e-bost hwn o'r tu allan i Gyngor Caerdydd, cymerwch ofal wrth glicio ar ddolenni.\n\n\nMs Taylor,\n\nThanks for your reply and for outlining the options available to consult the estate plans referenced.\n\nI should clarify my position, as my correspondence is not a routine request for private research access, but part of a wider and serious concern regarding possible historical misdescription, suppression, and/or concealment of material facts relating to Great House Farm, Llandough.\n\nI am currently at the Welsh settlement in Argentina, having been displaced from my ancestral birthplace in Llandough. As such, it is not possible for me to attend the searchroom in person so easy as you're 7,500 miles away, nor to “pop in” to consult records on site. Requiring physical attendance or payment for access is therefore not a practical or equitable option in my circumstances.\n\nMore importantly, my concern is that certain archival descriptions, surveys, and planning-era records may themselves be inaccurate or incomplete as a result of earlier failures of disclosure. Specifically, a Roman burial reportedly discovered at Great House Farm in 1888 appears to have been treated as a “surprise” in later archaeological surveys (1978/1994), and an occupied, c.800-year-old domestic dwelling was repeatedly described in planning documentation as an “old barn” or “utility building.” These characterisations materially affected heritage protection, culminating in demolition in 1988.\n\nIn that context, I am not seeking to pay for copies of records whose accuracy is itself in question, nor to participate financially in processes that may inadvertently perpetuate errors arising from that period. My intention is to place relevant public bodies on notice of a potential issue of public record integrity and heritage governance, not to obtain documents for private interest. I have written to the Senedd calling for a public enquiry too. I've notified the land registry and serious fraud teams too.\n\nI am therefore asking that my correspondence be treated as a formal expression of concern regarding the reliability and completeness of the archival record as it relates to this site, and that this concern be noted accordingly. I would also be grateful if you could advise which department or officer is appropriate for addressing concerns of this nature, where the issue is not access but potential historical misrepresentation within public records.\n\nFor the avoidance of doubt, I am not alleging any impropriety on the part of current archive staff. My concern relates to historical processes and decisions, and to ensuring that those matters are properly examined rather than inadvertently reinforced.\n\nThank you for your time and consideration.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nRequester\n\n________________________________\n\nOn Fri, 6 Feb 2026, 10:44 Glamro / Swyddfa Cofnodion Morgannwg, <[email redacted]<[email redacted]>> wrote:\nRef: 2026/0633\nDear Requester,\nThank you for your email received on 5th February 2026.\n\nYou would be welcome to visit us to consult the estate plans DB/E/1-2 in our public searchroom and take your own photographs for free. Our opening hours are Tues-Thurs 9.30am-12.30pm & 1.30pm-4.30pm. We are currently taking bookings from Tuesday 17th February 2026. Please note that our public searchroom is closed for the week Tues 10th February – Thurs 12th February so that staff can carry out essential work on the collections. We will re-open to the public on Tuesday 17th February 2026.\nIf you have not visited us before, you are required to register. We ask that you bring with you two forms of ID, one showing your name e.g. Driving Licence and the other your current address e.g. current utility bill or official letter.\nAlternatively, if you are unable to visit us in person, and if you can confirm exactly which pages you require, staff can provide digital copies, for which we charge £15.60 for the first image and £4.80 each for subsequent images, payable in advance. Prices include VAT.\nYou can find a detailed list on the catalogue: Survey of marquis of Bute’s Glamorgan estates, by David Stewart.<https://glamorgan.epexio.com/en/records/DB/E/1-8/1-2> If you would like a quote for digital copies, please let get in touch.\nWe hope this information is of assistance to you.\nYours sincerely,\nMelanie Taylor [cid:image003.jpg@01DCA0E6.05398450]\nCynorthwyydd Cofnodion / Records Assistant\nArchifau Morgannwg / Glamorgan Archives\nClos Parc Morgannwg[map link redacted]\nLecwydd / Leckwith[map link redacted]\nCaerdydd / Cardiff[map link redacted]\n[postcode redacted][map link redacted]\n[phone redacted]\n[email redacted]<[email redacted]>\nwww.archifaumorgannwg.gov.uk<http://www.archifaumorgannwg.gov.uk/> / www.glamarchives.gov.uk<http://www.glamarchives.gov.uk/>\nwww.facebook.com/glamarchives<http://www.facebook.com/glamarchives>\nwww.twitter.com/glamarchives<http://www.twitter.com/glamarchives>\n\n**********************************************************************\n\nThe Council welcomes correspondence in Welsh, English or bilingually. We will ensure that we communicate with you in the language of your choice, as long as you let us know which you prefer. Corresponding in Welsh will not lead to delay.\n\nPrivileged/Confidential Information may be contained in this message. If you are not the addressee indicated in this message (or responsible for delivery of the message to such person), you may not copy or deliver this message to anyone. In such case, you should destroy this message and kindly notify the sender by reply email. Please advise immediately if you or your employer does not consent to Internet email for messages of this kind. Opinions, conclusions and other information in this message that do not relate to the official business of the Council of the City and County of Cardiff shall be understood as neither given nor endorsed by it. All e-mail sent to or from this address will be processed by Cardiff County Councils Corporate E-mail system and may be subject to scrutiny by someone other than the addressee.\n\n**********************************************************************\n\nMae’r Cyngor yn croesawu gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg, Saesneg neu’n ddwyieithog. Byddwn yn cyfathrebu â chi yn ôl eich dewis, dim ond i chi roi gwybod i ni pa un sydd well gennych. Ni fydd gohebu yn Gymraeg yn arwain at oedi.\n\nMae'n bosibl bod gwybodaeth gyfrinachol yn y neges hon. Os na chyfeirir y neges atoch chi'n benodol (neu os nad ydych chi'n gyfrifol am drosglwyddo'r neges i'r person a enwir), yna ni chewch gopio na throsglwyddo'r neges. Mewn achos o'r fath, dylech ddinistrio'r neges a hysbysu'r anfonwr drwy e-bost ar unwaith. Rhowch wybod i'r anfonydd ar unwaith os nad ydych chi neu eich cyflogydd yn caniatau e-bost y Rhyngrwyd am negeseuon fel hon. Rhaid deall nad yw'r safbwyntiau, y casgliadau a'r wybodaeth arall yn y neges hon nad ydynt yn cyfeirio at fusnes swyddogol Cyngor Dinas a Sir Caerdydd yn cynrychioli barn y Cyngor Sir nad yn cael sel ei fendith. Caiff unrhyw negeseuon a anfonir at, neu o'r cyfeiriad e-bost hwn eu prosesu gan system E-bost Gorfforaethol Cyngor Sir Caerdydd a gallant gael eu harchwilio gan rywun heblaw'r person a enwir.\n\n**********************************************************************"},{"id":"gm-19c6bcd68d9c4369","subject":"Re: Cais am Ddeiseb ac Ymchwiliad Cyhoeddus: Achos Buckler v BP (1987) a Dinistr Great House Farm, Llandough / Formal Request for Inquiry: The Great House Farm Dispossession (BP vs Buckler)","name":"Re: Cais am Ddeiseb ac Ymchwiliad Cyhoeddus: Achos Buckler v BP (1987) a Dinistr Great House Farm, Llandough / Formal Request for Inquiry: The Great House Farm Dispossession (BP vs Buckler)","date":"2026-02-17T13:32:28Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19c6bcd68d9c4369.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19c275c24363311c-cais-am-ddeiseb-ac-ymchwiliad-cyhoeddus-achos-buckler-v-bp-1987-a-dinistr-great-house-farm-llandough-formal-request-for-","scopeTopics":["senedd"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["senedd"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19c275c24363311c","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"senedd","name":"Senedd"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19c6bcd68d9c4369.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Lara,\n\nDiolch am eich arweiniad.\n\nRwyf wedi mireinio fy mhethos arfaethedig ymhellach er mwyn ehangu ei\ngwmpas. Mae'n cynnwys nawr alwadau am ymchwiliad i achosion hanesyddol\ntebyg lluosog yng Nghymru, gan gynnwys archwilio problemau systemig posibl\nfel twyll, gorthrymu tystiolaeth, neu anwybyddu/gweithio o amgylch\ngwarchodaethau amaethyddol a threftadaeth mewn anghytundebau meddiannu\nanghyfreithlon—gan o bosibl atal y cloc cyfyngu ar feddiannu anghyfreithlon\nyn anghyfreithlon neu yn anghyfansoddiadol—tra'n defnyddio Fferm Great\nHouse (BP v Buckler 1987) fel prif enghraifft.\n\nMae'r ffocws yn parhau i fod yn gadarn ar faterion datganoledig: polisi\namgylchedd hanesyddol Cymru, gwarchodaethau amaethyddol, rôl CADW, a\ndiwygiadau i fynd i'r afael ag unrhyw fylchau a nodir, heb geisio ailymweld\nâ dyfarniadau llys penodol.\n\nRwy'n cadarnhau bod gennyf gyfeiriad yng Nghymru: [address redacted].\nRwy'n etholwr yn Nyffryn Morgannwg ac rwyf wedi ysgrifennu at yr AS\npresennol a'r Cynghorydd Carroll yn Llandochau; maent yn ymwybodol o'r\nmater a chredaf eu bod yn edrych i mewn iddo.\n\nOs caiff ei fframio fel hyn—gan bwysleisio \"dosbarth\" posibl o\nffermydd/teuluoedd Cymreig yr effeithir arnynt a methiannau polisi\nehangach—a ydych chi'n credu y byddai'n bodloni'r safonau'n ddigonol i\nennill sylw'r Pwyllgor a mynd ymlaen i gyflwyno/cyhoeddi?\n\nNid yw twyll byth yn rhy bell yn y gorffennol, gan nad yw'r cyfnod cyfyngu\nyn berthnasol mewn amgylchiadau o'r fath ac fe'i datodolir i gyd yn ôl y\ngyfraith. Mae hefyd yn gyfyngedig o'r dyddiad y cafodd ei ddarganfod—ac\nrwyf newydd ei ddarganfod—ac nid o ddyddiad y twyll honedig.\n\nEr gwaethaf hynny, rydym yn dal yn deulu Cymreig brodorol dadfeddiannol ar\nein tir geni hynafiaid. Rydym yn dal yma.\n\nOs na, pa gamau eraill y byddech chi'n eu hargymell (er enghraifft,\nymgysylltu ag Aelod o'r Senedd, adrannau Llywodraeth Cymru fel CADW, neu\nsianeli eraill ar gyfer codi materion systemig tir/amaethyddol)?\n\nByddai unrhyw gyngor ar gryfhau'r geiriad neu'r dystiolaeth ategol yn cael\nei werthfawrogi'n fawr cyn i mi gyflwyno.\n\nDiolch—mae'r mater hwn o bwysigrwydd sylweddol i gyfanrwydd diwylliannol,\namaethyddol a hanesyddol Cymru.\n\nCofion cynhes,\nRequester\n[address redacted]\n\n...................  [SAESNEG/ ENGLISH] .....................\n\nThanks for your guidance.\n\nI've further refined my proposed petition to broaden its scope,\nincorporating calls for an inquiry into multiple similar historical cases\nin Wales. This includes examining the possibility of systemic issues like\nfraud, suppression of evidence, or ignoring/working around agricultural and\nheritage protections in adverse possession disputes—potentially to\nunlawfully or unconstitutionally stop limitation clock on adverse\npossession—while using the Great House Farm (BP v Buckler 1987) as a key\nexample. The focus remains on devolved matters: Welsh historic environment\npolicy, agricultural safeguards, CADW's role, and reforms to address gaps\nwithout revisiting specific court judgments.\n\nAnd yes I have an address, it's [address redacted]. I am a constituent\nin the Vale of Glamorgan and I have written to the current MP and\ncouncillor Carroll in Llandough, they are aware and I trust they are\nlooking into it.\n\nIf framed this way—emphasizing a possible \"class\" of affected Welsh\nfarms/families and broader policy failures—do you believe it would meet the\nstandards to gain the Committee's attention and proceed to\nsubmission/publication?\n\nFraud is never too far in the past as the statute of limitation doesn't\napply in such circumstances and furthermore it unravels all according to\nlaw. It is also only limited from the date it was discovered and I recently\ndiscovered, not from the date of occurrence of the alleged fraud.\n\nNotwithstanding we are still an dispossessed indigenous Welsh family on our\nancestral birthland. We are still here.\n\nFailing that, what alternative steps do you recommend (e.g., approaching a\nMember of the Senedd, Welsh Government departments like CADW, or other\nchannels for raising systemic land/agricultural issues)?\n\nAny advice on strengthening wording or evidence would be appreciated before\nsubmission.\n\nThank you—this matter holds significant importance for Welsh cultural,\nagricultural, and historical integrity.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester [address redacted]\n\n>"},{"id":"gm-19c6b8268892605e","subject":"RE: Cais am Ddeiseb ac Ymchwiliad Cyhoeddus: Achos Buckler v BP (1987) a Dinistr Great House Farm, Llandough / Formal Request for Inquiry: The Great House Farm Dispossession (BP vs Buckler)","name":"RE: Cais am Ddeiseb ac Ymchwiliad Cyhoeddus: Achos Buckler v BP (1987) a Dinistr Great House Farm, Llandough / Formal Request for Inquiry: The Great House Farm Dispossession (BP vs Buckler)","date":"2026-02-17T12:10:25Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19c6b8268892605e.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19c275c24363311c-cais-am-ddeiseb-ac-ymchwiliad-cyhoeddus-achos-buckler-v-bp-1987-a-dinistr-great-house-farm-llandough-formal-request-for-","scopeTopics":["senedd"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["senedd"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19c275c24363311c","direction":"received","from":"Petitions Committee | Y Pwyllgor Deisebau","institutions":[{"id":"senedd","name":"Senedd"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19c6b8268892605e.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester,\n\nThank you for your message. Senedd petitions are created and managed through an online system , and must meet the standards for petitions , and full rules on proper form . We would encourage you to check these first, as a Senedd petition may not be the most appropriate channel to pursue your issue.\n\nCofion/Regards,\n\nLara.\n\n[cid:image001.png@01DCA006.653C2280]\nLara Date\nAil Glerc, Pwyllgor Deisebau, Senedd Cymru\nSecond Clerk, Petitions Committee, Welsh Parliament\n________________________________\n[cid:image002.png@01DCA006.653C2280]\n[phone redacted]\n\n[cid:image003.png@01DCA006.653C2280]\nwww.senedd.cymru \nwww.senedd.wales \n[cid:image004.png@01DCA006.653C2280]\n@SeneddDeisebau\n@SeneddPetitions\n[cid:image005.png@01DCA006.653C2280]\nseneddcymru \nseneddwales \n________________________________\nSenedd Cymru yw’r corff sy’n cael ei ethol yn ddemocrataidd i gynrychioli buddiannau Cymru a’i phobl. Mae’r Senedd, fel y’i gelwir, yn deddfu ar gyfer Cymru, yn cytuno ar drethi yng Nghymru, ac yn dwyn Llywodraeth Cymru i gyfrif.\nThe Welsh Parliament is the democratically elected body that represents the interests of Wales and its people. Commonly known as the Senedd, it makes laws for Wales, agrees Welsh taxes and holds the Welsh Government to account.\nCroesewir gohebiaeth yn Gymraeg neu Saesneg | We welcome correspondence in Welsh or English\n\nDylid ystyried unrhyw ddatganiad neu sylw a geir yn y neges hon fel un personol ac nid o reidrwydd yn fynegiant o safbwynt Senedd Cymru, unrhyw ran ohoni neu unrhyw gorff cysylltiedig.\nAny of the statements or comments made above should be regarded as personal and not necessarily those of the Welsh Parliament, any constituent part or connected body.\n\n________________________________\n\n\nFrom: Requester  \nSent: 01 February 2026 11:02\nTo: Petitions Committee | Y Pwyllgor Deisebau  \nSubject: Cais am Ddeiseb ac Ymchwiliad Cyhoeddus: Achos Buckler v BP (1987) a Dinistr Great House Farm, Llandough / Formal Request for Inquiry: The Great House Farm Dispossession (BP vs Buckler)\n\n[https://senedd.wales/media/obuo0sep/senedd_logo_xs.png]\nE-mail protection couldn't recognize this email as this is the first time you received an email from this sender [email redacted]\n\n\nAelodau Anrhydeddus y Senedd,\n\nRwy’n ysgrifennu atoch i dynnu eich sylw at yr hyn yr wyf yn ei ystyried yn anghyfiawnder hanesyddol difrifol, yn ymwneud â’m teulu, y Bucklers, a dinistr Great House Farm yn Llandough — safle fferm a chartref o dros 800 mlynedd o hanes, gan gynnwys cysylltiadau mynachaidd a chysylltiad uniongyrchol â Guglielmo Marconi yn ystod datblygiad cynnar radio.\n\nMae’r mater hwn yn codi cwestiynau difrifol ynghylch hawliau tir, gweithdrefnau cyfreithiol, cofrestru tir, a hawliau dynol yng Nghymru.\n\nEr enghraifft, mewn datganiad i’r wasg yn 1974, dywedodd fy nain yn gyhoeddus: “It’s my home.” Mae’r datganiad hwn, sydd yn fater o gofnod cyhoeddus, yn gwrth-ddweud yn uniongyrchol ganfyddiad Llys Apêl 1987 yn BP v Buckler, lle dywedwyd ei bod wedi bod yn “ddistaw” ac wedi derbyn trwydded BP a gyhoeddwyd yn 1974.\n\nYn yr un achos, gwrthodwyd apêl fy nhad ar y sail bod BP Pension Trust Ltd a BP Properties Ltd yn “effeithiol yr un cwmni”. Fodd bynnag, yn y dyfarniad yr oedd yn ei herio, nododd y barnwr nad oedd ymatebion fy nain i BP Pension Trust Ltd yn cyfrif gan mai BP Properties Ltd oedd yr hawlydd, ac mai cwmnïau ar wahân oeddent. Yn ymarferol, roedd hyn yn arwain at sefyllfa “pen y cwmni’n ennill, cynffon y teulu’n colli”.\n\nYn sgil hyn, cafodd fy nhad ei dynnu o’i wely ysbyty, ei garcharu heb gyhuddiad, a gosodwyd gorchymyn atal arno i’w atal rhag meddiannu ac amddiffyn ein fferm 800 oed rhag cael ei dymchwel. Cafodd ei gais i’r Llys Hawliau Dynol Ewropeaidd ei rwystro cyn iddo farw yn 1991, yn ddim ond 41 oed.\n\nAr y sail hon, rwy’n gofyn yn ffurfiol am Ddeiseb i sefydlu Ymchwiliad Cyhoeddus i’r achos hwn, fel mater o ddwyn tir hanesyddol ac o dorri hawliau dynol yng Nghymru.\n\nRwy’n weithiwr proffesiynol ym maes seiber a rhyfela electronig milwrol. Mae technolegau AI pwerus bellach ar gael i bob un ohonom, ac nid oes unrhyw reswm technegol pam na ellid archwilio’r mater hwn yn llawn ac yn drylwyr. Mae’r hyn a ddigwyddodd i’m teulu yn ymddangos fel anghyfiawnder dwys, aml-genhedlaethol, ac yn debyg i ffurf ar “lawfare”, lle y trinwyd fy nheulu fel llafur di-dâl i gynnal ased tir hyd nes yr oedd yn gyfleus i’w ariannu. Mae’n arwyddocaol bod bron pob cam yn ein herbyn wedi digwydd ar yr eiliad olaf posibl.\n\nCeir honiadau bod sylw sylweddol yn y wasg yn 1974 o blaid fy nain a’n teulu, ynghyd â deiseb o tua 1,700 o bobl yn ceisio atal dymchwel y fferm. Ymhellach, mae’n ymddangos bod CADW wedi methu ag ymyrryd lle dylai’r safle fod wedi cael statws Adeilad Rhestredig Gradd II.\n\nNid wyf yn sicr a gofnodwyd erioed yn briodol yn y Gofrestrfa Tir fod y tir — a gofrestrwyd gan BP yn 1982 — dan anghydfod. Yn fy nealltwriaeth i, cafodd ei drin fel pe na bai mewn anghydfod o gwbl. Nid oes unrhyw lys wedi penderfynu’n derfynol mai BP oedd perchnogion cyfreithiol y tir; cafodd hynny ei honni yn hytrach na’i brofi.\n\nMae hefyd fater pwysig o hanes diwylliannol. Arhosodd Marconi gyda’n teulu yn y fferm wrth iddo ddatblygu radio. Mae cynghorwyr a’r plwyf yn Llandough yn gwrthod cydnabod hyn. Rwy’n deall bod Marconi yn cael ei ystyried yn wleidyddol eithafol ar y dde, ac, fel y gwelir yn achos y cerflun radio ym Mae Caerdydd, mae ei rôl hanesyddol yn cael ei hailysgrifennu. Mae’n rhesymol tybio bod ein teulu wedi cael ei ddal mewn ymgyrch wleidyddol ehangach o’r chwith yn erbyn y dde, ac mai am roi llety i Marconi y bu i ni wynebu canlyniadau canrif o hyd — problemau nad oedd yn bodoli yn y tair canrif flaenorol yr oeddem wedi byw ar y fferm.\n\nRoedd mam Marconi hefyd yn Jamerson o gwmni wisgi Gwyddelig Jameson. Rwy’n gofyn i chi gynnal eich ymchwil eich hun, gwneud eich ymholiadau eich hun, a dod i’ch casgliadau eich hun — ond yn anad dim, peidiwch â chaniatáu i hanes gael ei ailysgrifennu yma.\n\nMae fy nheulu bellach yn archwilio opsiynau ar gyfer siarad cyhoeddus, hawliau stori, a dogfennaeth. Yn nhermau cysylltiadau teuluol: fy nhaid oedd Ray Smith o Dempsey and Makepeace; mae fy modryb yn Shan Cothi; a dechreuodd fy ewythr y band Tigertailz.\n\nRwy’n disgrifio’r sefyllfa hon fel gwarthus. Mae fy nheulu’n mynnu cyfiawnder am gael ein dadleoli a’n dwyn o’n tir a’n heiddo gan y Sefydliad Prydeinig a BP, gyda phrin unrhyw amddiffyniad dan y gyfraith. Rydym yn deulu Cymreig brodorol, Cymraeg eu hiaith, wedi byw yng Nghaerdydd, Bro Morgannwg, a’r fferm yn Llandough ers cannoedd o flynyddoedd.\n\nO’r hyn y gallaf ei gasglu, ceir dwsinau o achosion posibl o dwyll a chuddio gwybodaeth, sy’n golygu nad yw statud cyfyngiadau yn berthnasol o reidrwydd. I’r rhai sy’n cefnogi dad-drefedigaethu Cymru ac annibyniaeth, prin y ceir enghraifft gliriach na BP v Buckler (1987) o’r hyn a ddigwyddodd i deulu ffermio Cymreig wrth herio’r Wladwriaeth Brydeinig a chorfforaeth fawr. Nid yw eraill wedi codi’r mater hwn, yn bennaf oherwydd trawma cenhedlaethol.\n\nRwyf wedi profi fy hun. Gadewais fy ngyrfa filwrol gydag anrhydedd, gan gwblhau fy llw 22 mlynedd. Cefais fy nghyflogi’n bona fide gan Gyngor Bro Morgannwg a Llywodraeth Cymru hyd at 2024. Gwasanaethais mewn rhyfeloedd yn y Dwyrain Canol. Fodd bynnag, nid yw’r anghyfiawnder fferm yn fater unigol.\n\nYn 2013, cafodd fy mab 36 diwrnod oed ei gipio, ac nid wyf erioed wedi ei weld ers hynny. Cafodd tŷ ei ddwyn oddi arnaf (40 Lombard Street, Y Barri). Yn 2023, cefais fy ngadael i gysgu mewn pabell tra roedd bron i 100 o Afghanistaniaid yn cael eu lletya’n dawel wrth ymyl ein pentref o 11 tŷ, tra roedd yn rhaid imi aros yno i ofalu am fy nain gan na allai gofalwyr ei chyrraedd.\n\nO ganlyniad, rwyf bellach yn byw yn yr hyn a elwir yn Setliad Cymreig yn yr Ariannin. Rwyf wedi fy nisodli ac wedi fy dadleoli i’r eithaf. Serch hynny, rwy’n ddiolchgar fy mod wedi goroesi gyda’m rhyddid, fy mywyd, a’m henw da yn gymharol gyfan. Serch hynny, hoffwn fod wedi aros yng Nghymru, fel Cymro, gyda safon a chynhaliaeth bywyd deg.\n\nMae fy ngwraig a minnau’n amharod i ganiatáu i’n merch hyd yn oed gamu ar Ynysoedd Prydain o ystyried yr hyn a glywn sy’n digwydd yno. Yn 2024, allforiais hyd yn oed fy nghi o Abertawe i’n heiddo yn y Caribî ar ôl i unigolyn Afghanistan fod yng ngardd fy nheulu yng Nghymru, gan geisio ei ddwyn.\n\nGofynnaf i’r Senedd gymryd y mater hwn o ddifrif, ac ystyried sefydlu Ymchwiliad Cyhoeddus llawn, tryloyw ac annibynnol. Nid er budd fy nheulu yn unig y mae hyn, ond er uniondeb hanes, cyfiawnder tir, a hawliau dynol yng Nghymru.\n\nYn gywir,\n\nRequester\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nSirs/Ma'am,\n\nI am writing to you regarding a significant historical injustice involving my family, the Bucklers, and the destruction of Great House Farm (an 800-year-old site of monastic and Marconi history).\n\nFor example, in a 1974 press statement my grandmother said publically 'It's my home'—that directly contradicts the 1987 Court of Appeal ruling that she was 'silent' and accepted of BP license issued in 1974. My dad's appeal for the BP vs Buckler 1987 case also got dismissed on grounds both BP Pension Trust Ltd and BP Properties Ltd were effectively the same company. But in the ruling he was appealing, the judge said that my nans replies to BP Pensions Trust Ltd didn't count as BP Properties Ltd were the claimant, and the two were seperate companies. So it was heads they win, tails we lose. My dad was ripped from his hospital bed, jailed without charge and slapped with a restraining order to keep him from occupying and defending our 800 year old farm from being bulldozed down. His ECHR request blocked before he died in 1991 aged just 41.\n\nI am requesting a Petition for a Public Inquiry into this case as a matter of historical land theft and human rights violations in Wales.\n\nI'm a military cyber and electronic warfare professional. We all have very powerful AI technology at our disposal which can make light work out of all of this. My family suffered an incredible injustice. It also appears to be multi-generational warfare/ lawfare. Whereby we were treated as free labour to upkeep the asset of the land and property until it was ripe to be cashed in. I say this as every action against us seemed to be last minute.\n\nThere was allegedly press covering the 74 case and supporting my nan and family and farm. There was a petition of 1700 people trying to defend us and the farm from demolishment. CADW looked the other way when it should have been a Grade II listed building. I could go on and on and on.\n\nI'm not even sure the land registry recorded that our land, being registered in 1982 by BP , was in dispute. I believe it was treated as if it wasn't dispited. No court actually definitively determined that BP were the landowners. It was just asserted.\n\nMarconi stayed with our family at our farm when inventing radio. The councillors and parish at Llandough won't even acknowledge this. I understand Marconi was far right and so, like the radio statute at Cardiff Bay has confirmed, he is being re-written out of history.  It stands to reason that our family was caught in this political campaign of left vs right. And for helping Marconi we found ourselves experiencing a century of problems afterwards. Which didn't exist for the 3 centuries we were at our farm before letting far right marconi stay with us. Marconi's mother was also Jamerson of the Irish Whiskey company.\n\nDo your own homework. Make your own enquiries. Draw your own conclusions.\n\n But please do not let history be rewritten here. My family are exploring options for public speaking, story rights, and a documentary etc.\n\nMy grandfather was Ray Smith, of Dempsey and Makepeace. My aunty Shan Cothi. My uncle started the band tigertailz.\n\nThis is an outrage and my family demands justice for being displaced and stolen from, land and property, by the British Establishment/ BP -  with little to no protections under law. And we're indigenous to the land, Welsh, Welsh speaking, Welsh family. Resident in Cardiff / Vale and that farm in Llandough for HUNDREDS OF YEARS.\n\nThere's tens of counts of fraud and concealment from what I can gather. Which means the statute of limitation doesn't apply in this instance. I know some are in favour of Wales decolonising and gaining independence. I think there's no better example of BP vs Buckler 1987 and what has happened to my family. Nobody else will bring this up because there's generational trauma. Notwithstanding we, a welsh farming family, are speaking up and against the British State and BP.\n\nBut I've proven myself. Leaving my military career with honours. Finishing my 22 years Oath, bona fide, employed by the Vale of Glamorgan Council, Welsh Government until 2024. I served in the wars in the middle east. I don't know what is going on, on that island. But the farm injustice isn't the only matter. My 36 day old son was abducted in 2013 and I've never seen him since. I had a house stolen (40 Lombard Street, Barry). And in 2023 I was left to sleep in a tent, while almost 100 afghans were secretly housed next to our 11 home village. Where I had to be to care for my grandmother, as no carers couldn't reach her. So I'm currently at the Welsh Settlement in Argentina. So I'm as displaced and replaced as I can be to cater for the insanity that's going on, on that island. And grateful I've managed to survive it all with my liberty and life and reputation somewhat still intact. But I would have actually liked to have remained in Wales as a Welshman. And have some quality and standard of life. My wife and I are reluctant for our daughter to even step foot on the British Isles with what we're hearing that's going on there lately. And I even exported my dog from Swansea to our place in the Caribbean in 2024 after an afghan was in my family's garden in Wales, trying to steal him.\n\nI look forward to your reply,\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester"},{"id":"gm-19c6782c59e0f10f","subject":"Automatic reply: Title Fraud and Procedural Concealment – Church View Close, Llandough (formerly Great House Farm/ \"Ty Mawr\")","name":"Automatic reply: Title Fraud and Procedural Concealment – Church View Close, Llandough (formerly Great House Farm/ \"Ty Mawr\")","date":"2026-02-16T17:32:24Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19c6782c59e0f10f.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19c6782c59e0f10f-automatic-reply-title-fraud-and-procedural-concealment-church-view-close-llandough-formerly-great-house-farm-ty-mawr","scopeTopics":["mp-vale"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["mp-vale"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19c6782c59e0f10f","direction":"received","from":"NARAYAN, Kanishka (MP)","institutions":[{"id":"mp-vale","name":"Member of Parliament (Vale of Glamorgan)"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19c6782c59e0f10f.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Hello,\n\nThank you for reaching out to me. This is just a quick note to confirm that we’ve received your email.\n\nAs your MP for the Vale of Glamorgan, my priority is supporting local constituents who need help with urgent issues. I receive a high volume of emails every day, so while I will do my best to get back to you as soon as possible, there may be a slight delay—especially if you’re contacting me about policy matters or upcoming legislation. Please know that your message has been logged, and I’ll respond as soon as I can.\n\nConstituency Casework\n\nIf you need my help with a personal case, please make sure your email includes:\n✔ Your full name\n✔ Your full address (including postcode)\n✔ A contact method (email or phone)\n✔ Details of your case (including any relevant reference numbers)\n\nA member of my casework team will be in touch as soon as possible.\n\nAm I Your MP?\n\nDue to parliamentary protocol, I can only take up casework for people living in the Vale of Glamorgan. If you’re unsure whether I’m your MP, you can check by entering your postcode here: <http://findyourmp.parliament.uk/> findyourmp.parliament.uk<http://findyourmp.parliament.uk/>.\n\nGetting in Touch\n\nThe best way to contact me is by email. My office is open Monday to Friday, 9 AM to 5 PM, and we’ll do our best to respond as soon as we can.\n\nYou can also keep up with my work here:\n📌 Facebook: [KanishkafortheVale]\n📌 Instagram: [@valekanishka]\n📌 X (Twitter): [@KanishkaNarayan]\n\nEmergency Contacts\n\nIf you need urgent assistance, here are some useful contacts:\n📞 NHS Wales (Non-Emergency Medical Advice): 111\n🚨 Emergency Services (Police, Fire, Ambulance): 999\n🏥 Mental Health Support (Samaritans Cymru): [phone redacted]\n🏠 Homelessness Support (Shelter Cymru): [phone redacted]\n👮 South Wales Police (Non-Emergency): 101\n\nThanks again for getting in touch—I really appreciate hearing from people across our community. My team and I look forward to assisting you.\n\nBest wishes,\nKanishka\n\n\n\nKanishka Narayan MP\n\nMember of Parliament for the Vale of Glamorgan\n\nMinister for AI and Online Safety\n\n\n\n\n\nUK Parliament Disclaimer: this e-mail is confidential to the intended recipient. If you have received it in error, please notify the sender and delete it from your system. Any unauthorised use, disclosure, or copying is not permitted. This e-mail has been checked for viruses, but no liability is accepted for any damage caused by any virus transmitted by this e-mail. This e-mail address is not secure, is not encrypted and should not be used for sensitive data."},{"id":"gm-19c67821e802f68d","subject":"Title Fraud and Procedural Concealment – Church View Close, Llandough (formerly Great House Farm/ \"Ty Mawr\")","name":"Title Fraud and Procedural Concealment – Church View Close, Llandough (formerly Great House Farm/ \"Ty Mawr\")","date":"2026-02-16T17:31:46Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19c67821e802f68d.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19c5b11eabf87d76-title-fraud-and-procedural-concealment-church-view-close-llandough-formerly-great-house-farm-ty-mawr","scopeTopics":["vale-foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["vale-foi"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19c5b11eabf87d76","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"vale-foi","name":"Vale of Glamorgan Council"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19c67821e802f68d.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Councillor Carroll & co,\n\nI write regarding potential gaps in the Council's planning and\nrecord-keeping processes relating to Great House Farm, Llandough, which was\ndemolished in 1988 following the 1987 Court of Appeal decision in BP\nProperties Ltd v Buckler.\n\nRecent evidence suggests the Council may wish to examine its own position\non this matter, as it raises questions about procedural compliance and\nrecord integrity that could expose the authority to wider scrutiny.\n\nCORE ISSUES\n\n**1. Identity and Procedural Integrity**\n\nThe 1987 judgment turned on a 1974 licence addressed to \"Mrs Buckler\". My\ngrandmother, Mary Williams, did not use or accept this designation.\nContemporaneous evidence (1978 newspaper article attached) records her\nasserting ownership under her correct name.\n\nIf the 1987 court was not presented with this evidence, the judgment may\nrest on an incomplete factual record. This raises procedural fairness\nconcerns relevant to any future review of the decision.\n\n**2. Heritage Information and Statutory Compliance**\n\nThe site's archaeological significance was documented by 1978 (Roman\nburials, monastic associations). Yet professional excavations in 1994\nrevealed 1,026 early medieval burials and monastic structures—findings that\nshould have prompted evaluation under the Ancient Monuments and\nArchaeological Areas Act 1979.\n\nCritical question: Did the Council conduct or require prior archaeological\nassessment before granting permissions to demolish? If not, this represents\na potential gap in statutory process that may affect the validity of the\npermissions granted.\n\n**3. Land Registry Disclosure**\n\nBP registered title in 1982 based on the disputed licence. The Council\nshould establish:\n- Whether any dispute notice or heritage caution was lodged;\n- Whether the Council disclosed relevant heritage information to the Land\nRegistry;\n- What planning records exist from 1978–1988 regarding the site's known\narchaeological potential.\n\n**4. Corporate Identity Inconsistency**\n\nDuring my father's appeal, the court treated BP Properties Ltd and BP\nPension Trust Ltd inconsistently—as separate entities when convenient, then\nas one when dismissing his claim. This raises questions about how\nthoroughly the litigation was scrutinised and whether the Council's records\nreflect this uncertainty.\n\nWHY THIS MATTERS TO THE COUNCIL\n\nThese issues go beyond a single historical dispute. They touch on:\n\n- **Planning record integrity**: Whether heritage information was\nadequately documented and acted upon;\n- **Procedural compliance**: Whether statutory protections (Ancient\nMonuments Act) were properly applied;\n- **Institutional liability**: Whether decisions were made on incomplete or\nmisleading information;\n- **Precedent risk**: Whether similar vulnerabilities may exist in other\nhistorical planning decisions.\n\nREQUESTS\n\nI ask the Council to:\n\n1. **Commission a review** of the planning permissions granted between\n1978–1988 for Great House Farm, specifically examining what heritage and\narchaeological information was available to decision-makers and how it was\nhandled.\n\n2. **Refer this matter to the Monitoring Officer** for examination of\nprocedural compliance and record integrity.\n\n3. **Establish** what correspondence, cautions, or dispute notices exist in\nrelation to this site's title or heritage status.\n\n4. **Clarify the Council's current protocol** for handling historical title\ndisputes, previously unknown heritage information, or allegations of\nmaterial non-disclosure in past planning processes—to prevent recurrence.\n\nI am not asking the Council to reverse the 1987 judgment or disturb current\nresidents. I am asking it to ensure its own records are accurate and its\nown processes are defensible.\n\nI attach the 1978 article and supporting documents, and am available to\nmeet or provide further information.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\nRequester, Sgt (Ret'd)\nExecutor for the Manor of Llandough\n[address redacted], [postcode redacted]"},{"id":"gm-19c63f949558570d","subject":"Subject: Freedom of Information Request - Ty Mawr (Great House), Llandough, near Penarth (1938-1987)","name":"Subject: Freedom of Information Request - Ty Mawr (Great House), Llandough, near Penarth (1938-1987)","date":"2026-02-16T01:03:26Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19c63f949558570d.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19c63e3b39172545-subject-freedom-of-information-request-ty-mawr-great-house-llandough-near-penarth-1938-1987","scopeTopics":["hmlr","vale-foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["hmlr","vale-foi"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19c63e3b39172545","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"hmlr","name":"HM Land Registry"},{"id":"vale-foi","name":"Vale of Glamorgan Council"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19c63f949558570d.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Sir/Madam,\n\nI understand you may already be busy, so please don't feel overwhelmed by\nthis FOI request—I'm conducting my own diligence and happy for you to take\nthe full statutory 20 working days to respond.\n\nUnder the Freedom of Information Act 2000, I request copies of all records,\ncorrespondence, memoranda, internal notes, minutes, file notes, land\nregistration applications, objections, or communications held by HM Land\nRegistry from 1 January 1938 to 31 December 1987 relating to:Ty Mawr (Great\nHouse), Llandough, near Penarth, South Glamorgan (also known as Great House\nFarm).\n\nLand registration applications, title transfers, or dealings involving\nMountjoy Ltd, Western Ground Rents Ltd, BP Pension Trust Ltd, and/or BP\nProperties Ltd (including the 1938 auction/reversion conveyance and 1969\nsale).\n\nInteractions, notices, proceedings, tenancy offers, licences (e.g., 1974\nunilateral lifetime licence letters), or disputes involving Mrs Mary\nWilliams (also referred to as Mrs Buckler), Mr Frederick Buckler, or\nWilliams/Buckler family occupiers—particularly the 1962 Cardiff County\nCourt possession proceedings (Dec 11 order by Judge Temple Morris QC).\n\nAny possession proceedings, adverse possession claims, or title objections\nconnected to the property (e.g., 1955 High Court, 1962 Cardiff County\nCourt, 1974 actions).\n\nPlease include archived, off-site, or scanned records where applicable.If\nexemptions apply (e.g., Section 40 personal data), specify which and why.\nIf costs exceed the appropriate limit, advise how to refine (e.g.,\nprioritize 1955-1987 or 1962/1974 events).\n\nI look forward to your response within 20 working days.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\nRequester Esq. Sgt (Ret'd)"},{"id":"gm-19c5b9fac64a3877","subject":"Historic Adverse Possession Licence Interruption Cases (1950–2000)","name":"Historic Adverse Possession Licence Interruption Cases (1950–2000)","date":"2026-02-14T10:08:35Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19c5b9fac64a3877.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19c5b8fa4ec2ed3d-historic-adverse-possession-licence-interruption-cases-1950-2000","scopeTopics":["senedd"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["senedd"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19c5b8fa4ec2ed3d","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"senedd","name":"Senedd"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19c5b9fac64a3877.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Sirs/Ma'am,\n\nAny response about my request for a public enquiry of BP Vs Buckler 1987\nand possible fraud & consealment, collusion, conspiracy and deception?\n\nFraud unravels all. And begins when the fraud is discovered, not occured.\nYes?\n\nFurthermore,\n\nI am writing to invite interest in review of a potential coordinated action\nconcerning historic adverse possession cases in England and Wales. As an\nindigenous Welshman. From Wales. Son of the late defendant, the possibly\ndisspossed and victim of fraud; Mr. William Buckler.\n\nMyself and my lawyers and team are investigating circumstances in which\nlong-term occupiers were issued licence letters (often described as\n“rent-free life licences” or similar instruments) during or after\nlimitation periods, and whether in certain instances such licences may not\nhave constituted legally effective interruption of adverse possession.\n\nThe investigation concerns cases primarily between 1950 and 2000 where:\nLong-term occupation had exceeded 12 years;\n\nPossession proceedings were either adjourned or unresolved;\n\nA licence letter was issued and relied upon to defeat a possessory claim;\n\nQuestions may arise regarding identity, acceptance, or material disclosure.\n\nAt this stage, we are seeking:\n\nA preliminary view as to whether a coordinated group litigation strategy\n(GLO or similar) could be viable;\n\nAdvice on limitation, concealment, and fraud thresholds;\n\nGuidance on structured claimant intake if sufficient common issues are\nidentified.\n\nThis enquiry is investigative only and no public allegations are being\nmade.\n\nWe are seeking professional assessment before any formal steps are taken.\n\nIf this is of potential interest, I would welcome a confidential discussion\nat your convenience.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester Esq. Sgt (Ret'd)\nManor of Llandough\nAnd Constituent at [address redacted],\nDinas Powys, Vale of Glamorgan\n[phone redacted]"},{"id":"gm-19c584c76930a2d7","subject":"CAS-315609 CRM:009700964","name":"CAS-315609 CRM:009700964","date":"2026-02-13T18:38:47Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19c584c76930a2d7.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19c584c76930a2d7-cas-315609-crm-009700964","scopeTopics":["tna"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["tna"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19c584c76930a2d7","direction":"received","from":"Your Views","institutions":[{"id":"tna","name":"The National Archives"}],"refs":["CAS-315609"],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19c584c76930a2d7.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Requester,\n\nThank you for your reply and correction email.\n\nYour correspondence has been passed through to my team to co-ordinate a response. As such I will be discussing contents of your messages with colleagues and will respond further in due course.\n\nKind regards,\n\nMartin  Willis\n\n\n\nMartin Willis | Quality Manager\nT: [phone redacted] | W: nationalarchives.gov.uk \nTwitter: @UkNatArchives |Pronouns: He/Him\nThe National Archives, Kew, Richmond, Surrey [postcode redacted]\n\n\n\nCorrection\nIn my previous correspondence, I referenced documents missing from Cardiff Library as \"reported missing in 1984.\" I wish to clarify that this date derives from my family's own chronology and recollection, not from an official report or confirmed archival record. I should not have stated this as an established fact, and I apologise for any confusion caused.\nNew Evidence\nSince my last email, I have obtained a Forensic Evaluation and Assessment of Restitutionary Claims (dated February 8, 2026) regarding the dispossession of the Williams family from Great House Farm, Llandough. This professional assessment provides additional context that may assist in understanding the archival gaps previously identified.\nThe forensic report specifically examines:\n\n  1.\nThe \"Mrs. Buckler\" nomenclature: The analysis characterises the consistent use of \"Mrs. Buckler\" in court records and correspondence as \"strategic misnaming\" rather than clerical error, given that Mary Williams never adopted her husband's surname. This raises questions about whether The National Archives holds any records indicating her formal objection to this identification or her consistent use of \"Mary Williams\" in legal proceedings.\n  2.\nThe 1974 license letters: The report notes that letters dated October/November 1974 were addressed to a \"non-existent legal persona,\" facilitating a judicial finding of \"silence as consent.\" I ask again whether The National Archives holds any corporate records from BP Properties Ltd or BP Pension Trust Ltd regarding this correspondence, or any indication that such records were considered for preservation.\n  3.\nThe 1974 adjournment: The forensic assessment questions why proceedings were adjourned part-heard on July 3, 1974, given your confirmation that principal judicial series contain no relevant material. I request clarification on whether any Cardiff County Court records from 1974-1975 were transferred to The National Archives under subsequent protocols, or whether they remain with the court.\n\nRevised Request\nGiven this additional professional analysis, I renew my request for guidance on:\n\n  *\nWhether The National Archives has received any complaints or inquiries regarding missing Bute Estate records or Great House Farm documents from other researchers;\n  *\nWhether corporate records from BP entities relating to this acquisition were ever selected for preservation; and\n  *\nWhether any mechanism exists for flagging concerns about record completeness where forensic analysis suggests potential irregularities in the historical record.\n\nI remain grateful for your time and professionalism, and I hope this clarification assists in addressing my inquiry.\nYours sincerely,\nRequester\n\n\n\n\nOn Sun, 8 Feb 2026 at 06:57, Requester  > wrote:\n\nMs Smith,\nThank you for your detailed response regarding selection policies and retention schedules. I appreciate the procedural clarity you have provided. However, I must respectfully indicate that your reply does not address the substantive concerns I have raised, and I am now compelled to escalate this matter formally.\n1. From \"Archival Silence\" to Potential Evidence Tampering\nYou correctly note that archival silences are well-documented in academic literature. However, the circumstances surrounding Great House Farm suggest something more concerning than routine selection decisions. I am now in possession of first-hand testimony (David Buckler, 2024) alleging that:\n\n  *\nFrederick Buckler (my grandfather) and his eldest son engaged a solicitor privately in the 1960s\n  *\nTitle documents were removed from the family home and allegedly sold\n  *\nThe family was deliberately misled with the phrase \"it's all been dealt with legally\"\n\nThis testimony, combined with the disappearance of the Bute Estate → Daniel Thomas deed index card from Cardiff Library (reported missing in 1984), suggests potential spoliation of evidence rather than routine archival selection. The question is no longer \"why weren't records preserved?\" but \"were records deliberately removed to prejudice a 320-year possessory claim?\"\n2. The Computerised Records Question\nYou direct me to search your catalogue. I have done so. However, I now ask specifically:\n\n  *\nDoes The National Archives hold any digitised or microfilmed copies of the Bute Estate records that might contain references to the Daniel Thomas quarrying agreement (circa 1895-1905) or the 1938 auction at the Bear Hotel?\n  *\nAre there any internal indices or finding aids (particularly from the 1970s-1980s) that might reference Great House Farm, Llandough, or the BP/Western Ground Rents acquisition records that are not reflected in the current online catalogue?\n\nThe 1994 archaeological excavation report (Cotswold Archaeological Trust) confirms the site's national significance. The absence of corresponding land transaction records from the same period is itself an anomaly requiring explanation.\n3. The BP Corporate Records Question\nYou note that parties are generally responsible for maintaining their own records. However, BP Properties Ltd and BP Pension Trust Ltd were corporate entities with statutory filing obligations. I ask:\n\n  *\nWere any BP corporate records relating to land acquisition in South Glamorgan (1969-1988) selected for preservation under the Companies Act or as part of the public record?\n  *\nHas The National Archives ever received or sought records from BP regarding the Great House Farm acquisition, demolition, or the 1987 litigation?\n\nThe Court of Appeal judgment [1987] EWCA Civ 2 notes that BP issued letters on 31 October 1974, not received until November 4th 1974 —letters that were allegedly \"accepted by silence.\" The original correspondence would constitute primary evidence of potentially fraudulent misrepresentation. If these were not preserved, I ask under what authority they were destroyed and whether this occurred before or after the litigation concluded.\n4. Request for Formal Investigation\nI am not seeking annotation of records. I am seeking:\n\n  1.\nA formal review of whether records relating to Great House Farm, Llandough (ST 168 733) were selected for preservation, destroyed, or remain non accessioned;\n  2.\nA search of microfilm, digitised, or legacy finding aid holdings for any reference to the 1938 auction, Western Ground Rents acquisition, or the Daniel Thomas quarrying agreement;\n  3.\nConfirmation of whether The National Archives has ever received complaints regarding missing Bute Estate records or BP land acquisition documents from other researchers;\n  4.\nGuidance on the appropriate authority to investigate allegations of document removal from Cardiff Library (1984) and potential fraud in the 1982 land registration.\n\n5. Public Interest and Parliamentary Scrutiny\nThis matter involves:\n\n  *\nThe forced removal of a family from land occupied since at least 1667\n  *\nDemolition of a medieval structure described as a \"dilapidated utility building\" to avoid heritage protection\n  *\nArchaeological discoveries (1888, 1994) that were known but officially \"forgotten\"\n  *\nA Court of Appeal decision ([1987] EWCA Civ 2) that may have relied on incomplete or fraudulent evidence\n\nI am copying my MP and the Parliamentary Ombudsman in this correspondence. I will also be submitting a Subject Access Request to HM Land Registry regarding the 1982 registration and a complaint to the Information Commissioner's Office regarding potential destruction of records relevant to ongoing property disputes.\nI request a substantive response addressing the specific questions above within 20 working days. Should The National Archives be unable to assist further, I request a formal referral to the appropriate body with jurisdiction over historical record fraud and spoliation.\nYours sincerely,\nRequester\n\n\n\n\nOn Sat, 7 Feb 2026 at 07:26, RESO Records Enquiries  > wrote:\n\nDear Requester,\n\nThe question about archival silences is one upon which there is a voluminous literature. Practically, however, the best way of making sense of what is and is not in the archives is to refer to the reports which shaped selection policies in the modern era - see especially the Report on Departmental Records (Cmnd 9136) and the Report on Legal Records (Cmnd 3084) - and the resulting retention and disposition schedules - see for example the one for the Court of Appeal (Civil Division) published here .\n\nWhat you will see from these is that the presumption for modern records is against permanent preservation. In general, apart from small samples to demonstrate the ordinary functioning of the courts/government departments, records are only preserved if they bear witness to moments of particular legal or policy significance or change. In the case of legal records, the presumption has been that parties would be responsible for maintaining their own records.\n\nIn terms of how to continue your search, my instinct is that you should consider exploring the holdings of the relevant local archive. You can also try some keyword searches in our catalogue - see, for example, these  search results which highlight these  series as potentially a relevant source of desired information. You might also wish to search the registered files of the land registry in records series LAR 1 . You should be advised, however, that departmental records tend to focus more on policy than upon the treatment of individual cases.\n\nI hope that this is helpful.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nCharlotte Smith﻿\n\nPrincipal Records Specialist, Modern Legal Records\nwww.nationalarchives.gov.uk \n\nEnquiry:\n\n\n\nI note and appreciate your confirmation that the principal judicial series (J 157, J 83, J 84) contain no relevant material. Given the scale of the consequences flowing from the dispute—forced removal, demolition of an occupied dwelling, and loss of heritage fabric—the absence of supporting court or administrative files is itself significant for researchers.\n\nAccordingly, I would be grateful for guidance on the following procedural points:\n\n  *   how researchers are expected to interpret gaps or silence in the archival record where substantial actions plainly occurred;\n  *   whether related non-judicial records (for example, departmental, planning, regulatory, or corporate records connected to land registration, planning consent, or archaeological works) are more likely to sit outside the obvious court series; and\n  *   whether there is an appropriate mechanism for flagging concerns about record completeness or contextual risk, without seeking alteration of the records themselves.\n\nFor the avoidance of doubt, I am not alleging impropriety on the part of The National Archives or its staff. My concern is to ensure that the historical record is approached with appropriate caution where absence may unintentionally convey certainty or closure that did not exist at the time.\n\nPlease don't print this e-mail unless you really need to. ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- National Archives Disclaimer This email and any files transmitted with it are intended solely for the use of the individual(s) to whom they are addressed. If you are not the intended recipient and have received this email in error, please notify the sender and delete the email. Opinions, conclusions and other information in this message and attachments that do not relate to the official business of The National Archives are neither given nor endorsed by it. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nPlease don't print this e-mail unless you really need to. -------------------------------------------------------------------------------