ibl o dwyll a\nchuddio gwybodaeth, sy’n golygu nad yw statud cyfyngiadau yn berthnasol o\nreidrwydd. I’r rhai sy’n cefnogi dad-drefedigaethu Cymru ac annibyniaeth,\nprin y ceir enghraifft gliriach na *BP v Buckler (1987)* o’r hyn a\nddigwyddodd i deulu ffermio Cymreig wrth herio’r Wladwriaeth Brydeinig a\nchorfforaeth fawr. Nid yw eraill wedi codi’r mater hwn, yn bennaf oherwydd\ntrawma cenhedlaethol.\n\nRwyf wedi profi fy hun. Gadewais fy ngyrfa filwrol gydag anrhydedd, gan\ngwblhau fy llw 22 mlynedd. Cefais fy nghyflogi’n bona fide gan Gyngor Bro\nMorgannwg a Llywodraeth Cymru hyd at 2024. Gwasanaethais mewn rhyfeloedd yn\ny Dwyrain Canol. Fodd bynnag, nid yw’r anghyfiawnder fferm yn fater unigol.\n\nYn 2013, cafodd fy mab 36 diwrnod oed ei gipio, ac nid wyf erioed wedi ei\nweld ers hynny. Cafodd tŷ ei ddwyn oddi arnaf (40 Lombard Street, Y Barri).\nYn 2023, cefais fy ngadael i gysgu mewn pabell tra roedd bron i 100 o\nAfghanistaniaid yn cael eu lletya’n dawel wrth ymyl ein pentref o 11 tŷ,\ntra roedd yn rhaid imi aros yno i ofalu am fy nain gan na allai gofalwyr ei\nchyrraedd.\n\nO ganlyniad, rwyf bellach yn byw yn yr hyn a elwir yn Setliad Cymreig yn yr\nAriannin. Rwyf wedi fy nisodli ac wedi fy dadleoli i’r eithaf. Serch hynny,\nrwy’n ddiolchgar fy mod wedi goroesi gyda’m rhyddid, fy mywyd, a’m henw da\nyn gymharol gyfan. Serch hynny, hoffwn fod wedi aros yng Nghymru, fel\nCymro, gyda safon a chynhaliaeth bywyd deg.\n\nMae fy ngwraig a minnau’n amharod i ganiatáu i’n merch hyd yn oed gamu ar\nYnysoedd Prydain o ystyried yr hyn a glywn sy’n digwydd yno. Yn 2024,\nallforiais hyd yn oed fy nghi o Abertawe i’n heiddo yn y Caribî ar ôl i\nunigolyn Afghanistan fod yng ngardd fy nheulu yng Nghymru, gan geisio ei\nddwyn.\n\nGofynnaf i’r Senedd gymryd y mater hwn o ddifrif, ac ystyried sefydlu\nYmchwiliad Cyhoeddus llawn, tryloyw ac annibynnol. Nid er budd fy nheulu yn\nunig y mae hyn, ond er uniondeb hanes, cyfiawnder tir, a hawliau dynol yng\nNghymru.\n\nYn gywir,\n\n*Requester*\n\n*----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------*\n\nSirs/Ma'am,\n\nI am writing to you regarding a significant historical injustice involving\nmy family, the Bucklers, and the destruction of Great House Farm (an\n800-year-old site of monastic and Marconi history).\n\nFor example, in a 1974 press statement my grandmother said publically 'It's\nmy home'—that directly contradicts the 1987 Court of Appeal ruling that she\nwas 'silent' and accepted of BP license issued in 1974. My dad's appeal for\nthe BP vs Buckler 1987 case also got dismissed on grounds both BP Pension\nTrust Ltd and BP Properties Ltd were effectively the same company. But in\nthe ruling he was appealing, the judge said that my nans replies to BP\nPensions Trust Ltd didn't count as BP Properties Ltd were the claimant, and\nthe two were seperate companies. So it was heads they win, tails we lose.\nMy dad was ripped from his hospital bed, jailed without charge and slapped\nwith a restraining order to keep him from occupying and defending our 800\nyear old farm from being bulldozed down. His ECHR request blocked before he\ndied in 1991 aged just 41.\n\nI am requesting a *Petition for a Public Inquiry* into this case as a\nmatter of historical land theft and human rights violations in Wales.\n\nI'm a military cyber and electronic warfare professional. We all have very\npowerful AI technology at our disposal which can make light work out of all\nof this. My family suffered an incredible injustice. It also appears to be\nmulti-generational warfare/ lawfare. Whereby we were treated as free labour\nto upkeep the asset of the land and property until it was ripe to be cashed\nin. I say this as every action against us seemed to be last minute.\n\nThere was allegedly press covering the 74 case and supporting my nan and\nfamily and farm. There was a petition of 1700 people trying to defend us\nand the farm from demolishment. CADW looked the other way when it should\nhave been a Grade II listed building. I could go on and on and on.\n\nI'm not even sure the land registry recorded that our land, being\nregistered in 1982 by BP , was in dispute. I believe it was treated as if\nit wasn't dispited. No court actually definitively determined that BP were\nthe landowners. It was just asserted.\n\nMarconi stayed with our family at our farm when inventing radio. The\ncouncillors and parish at Llandough won't even acknowledge this. I\nunderstand Marconi was far right and so, like the radio statute at Cardiff\nBay has confirmed, he is being re-written out of history.  It stands to\nreason that our family was caught in this political campaign of left vs\nright. And for helping Marconi we found ourselves experiencing a century of\nproblems afterwards. Which didn't exist for the 3 centuries we were at our\nfarm before letting far right marconi stay with us. Marconi's mother was\nalso Jamerson of the Irish Whiskey company.\n\nDo your own homework. Make your own enquiries. Draw your own conclusions.\n\n But please do not let history be rewritten here. My family are exploring\noptions for public speaking, story rights, and a documentary etc.\n\nMy grandfather was Ray Smith, of Dempsey and Makepeace. My aunty Shan\nCothi. My uncle started the band tigertailz.\n\nThis is an outrage and my family demands justice for being displaced and\nstolen from, land and property, by the British Establishment/ BP -  with\nlittle to no protections under law. And we're indigenous to the land,\nWelsh, Welsh speaking, Welsh family. Resident in Cardiff / Vale and that\nfarm in Llandough for HUNDREDS OF YEARS.\n\nThere's tens of counts of fraud and concealment from what I can gather.\nWhich means the statute of limitation doesn't apply in this instance. I\nknow some are in favour of Wales decolonising and gaining independence. I\nthink there's no better example of BP vs Buckler 1987 and what has happened\nto my family. Nobody else will bring this up because there's generational\ntrauma. Notwithstanding we, a welsh farming family, are speaking up and\nagainst the British State and BP.\n\nBut I've proven myself. Leaving my military career with honours. Finishing\nmy 22 years Oath, bona fide, employed by the Vale of Glamorgan Council,\nWelsh Government until 2024. I served in the wars in the middle east. I\ndon't know what is going on, on that island. But the farm injustice isn't\nthe only matter. My 36 day old son was abducted in 2013 and I've never seen\nhim since. I had a house stolen (40 Lombard Street, Barry). And in 2023 I\nwas left to sleep in a tent, while almost 100 afghans were secretly housed\nnext to our 11 home village. Where I had to be to care for my grandmother,\nas no carers couldn't reach her. So I'm currently at the Welsh Settlement\nin Argentina. So I'm as displaced and replaced as I can be to cater for the\ninsanity that's going on, on that island. And grateful I've managed to\nsurvive it all with my liberty and life and reputation somewhat still\nintact. But I would have actually liked to have remained in Wales as a\nWelshman. And have some quality and standard of life. My wife and I are\nreluctant for our daughter to even step foot on the British Isles with what\nwe're hearing that's going on there lately. And I even exported my dog from\nSwansea to our place in the Caribbean in 2024 after an afghan was in my\nfamily's garden in Wales, trying to steal him.\n\nI look forward to your reply,\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester"},{"id":"gm-19c181e18b3a6ad4","subject":"Please update the status of your request - Freedom of Information Request – Administrative Records for BP Properties Ltd v Buckler (1974)","name":"Please update the status of your request - Freedom of Information Request – Administrative Records for BP Properties Ltd v Buckler (1974)","date":"2026-02-01T07:32:31Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19c181e18b3a6ad4.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19c181e18b3a6ad4-please-update-the-status-of-your-request-freedom-of-information-request-administrative-records-for-bp-properties-ltd-v-b","scopeTopics":["moj"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["moj"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19c181e18b3a6ad4","direction":"received","from":"(name not shown; whatdotheyknow.com)","institutions":[{"id":"moj","name":"Ministry of Justice / HMCTS"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19c181e18b3a6ad4.json","textStatus":"available","text":"To let everyone know, follow this link and then select the appropriate box.\n\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/profile/sign_in?r=%2Frequest%2Ffreedom_of_information_request_a_2133%23describe_state_form_1\n\nYour request was called 'Freedom of Information Request – Administrative Records for BP Properties Ltd v Buckler (1974)'. Letting everyone know whether you got the information will help us keep tabs on\nHM Courts and Tribunals Service.\n\n-- the WhatDoTheyKnow team\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nWhatDoTheyKnow is a project of mySociety run by a small team of staff and\ndedicated volunteers.\n\nmySociety is a charity that believes that information should be accessible to everyone.\nIf this is important to you, please consider donating to help us do more:\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/donate\n\nFind out more about how we handle your data at:\nhttps://www.whatdotheyknow.com/help/privacy\n\nmySociety is a registered charity in England and Wales (1076346), and a limited\ncompany (03277032)"},{"id":"gm-19c12b0647dafb1f","subject":"Automatic reply: Systemic governance concern regarding historic land registration and administrative reliance - BP vs Buckler 1987 (inc. BP Land Registry 1982)","name":"Automatic reply: Systemic governance concern regarding historic land registration and administrative reliance - BP vs Buckler 1987 (inc. BP Land Registry 1982)","date":"2026-01-31T06:14:28Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19c12b0647dafb1f.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19c12b0647dafb1f-automatic-reply-systemic-governance-concern-regarding-historic-land-registration-and-administrative-reliance-bp-vs-buckl","scopeTopics":["pacac"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["pacac"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19c12b0647dafb1f","direction":"received","from":"HOC Public Administration and Constitutional Affairs Select Committee","institutions":[{"id":"pacac","name":"Public Administration and Constitutional Affairs Committee"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19c12b0647dafb1f.json","textStatus":"available","text":"﻿\n\nThis is an automated response.\n\nThank you for contacting the Public Administration and Constitutional Affairs Select Committee (PACAC) at the House of Commons.\n\nThis is to confirm that we have received your correspondence. Due to the high volume of inquiries, please note that we will respond, if necessary, as soon as possible.\n\nThe Public Administration and Constitutional Affairs Committee (PACAC) is appointed by the House of Commons to examine: constitutional issues; the quality and standards of administration provided by Civil Service departments; and the reports of the Parliamentary and Health Service Ombudsman (PHSO)<https://committees.parliament.uk/committee/327/public-administration-and-constitutional-affairs-committee/role/>. The Committee does not investigate individual cases and therefore we are unable to offer help to individuals with personal matters.\nYou can also follow the Committee on X (formerly Twitter) @CommonsPACAC<https://twitter.com/CommonsPACAC>\n\nKind regards\n\nThe PACAC Secretariat\n\nPublic Administration and Constitutional Affairs Committee (PACAC)\n\nHouse of Commons\n\n[postcode redacted]\n\nwww.parliament.uk/pacac<http://www.parliament.uk/pacac>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nUK Parliament Disclaimer: this e-mail is confidential to the intended recipient. If you have received it in error, please notify the sender and delete it from your system. Any unauthorised use, disclosure, or copying is not permitted. This e-mail has been checked for viruses, but no liability is accepted for any damage caused by any virus transmitted by this e-mail. This e-mail address is not secure, is not encrypted and should not be used for sensitive data."},{"id":"gm-19c12aff0d09009f","subject":"Systemic governance concern regarding historic land registration and administrative reliance - BP vs Buckler 1987 (inc. BP Land Registry 1982)","name":"Systemic governance concern regarding historic land registration and administrative reliance - BP vs Buckler 1987 (inc. BP Land Registry 1982)","date":"2026-01-31T06:14:04Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19c12aff0d09009f.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19c12a31e632de61-systemic-governance-concern-regarding-historic-land-registration-and-administrative-reliance-bp-vs-buckler-1987-inc-bp-l","scopeTopics":["pacac"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["pacac"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19c12a31e632de61","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"pacac","name":"Public Administration and Constitutional Affairs Committee"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19c12aff0d09009f.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear Committee Members,\n\nI am writing to draw your attention to a historical land matter which I\nbelieve may illustrate potential vulnerabilities in public administration,\nrecord-keeping, and the governance of land registration. My purpose is\nsolely to provide factual information for consideration, rather than to\nrequest remedial action.\n\nThe matter concerns land formerly known as *Great House Farm, Llandough,\nVale of Glamorgan*, later developed for residential use and historically\nassociated with *BP v Buckler (Court of Appeal, 1987)*.\n\n*Chronology of relevant events (as reflected in public records):*\n\n   -\n\n   *July 1974:* Court proceedings involving my grandmother were adjourned\n   without determination of ownership.\n   -\n\n   *31 October 1974:* A subsequent hearing did not proceed substantively,\n   following correspondence received from BP entities that same day. No\n   judgment on ownership was issued.\n   -\n\n   Throughout this period, my grandmother was not included in dealings\n   concerning the land, while the opposing party continued to assert ownership.\n   -\n\n   *1982:* The land was registered in the name of BP entities, despite\n   unresolved competing claims and no final judicial adjudication.\n   -\n\n   *1983:* My grandmother passed away after the registration, with\n   ownership never conclusively adjudicated.\n   -\n\n   *1987 Court of Appeal:* The record reflects an apparent inconsistency in\n   the treatment of correspondence and corporate entities. Specifically:\n   -\n\n      The court noted that my grandmother had replied to correspondence on\n      31 October, asserting ownership.\n      -\n\n      The reply was treated as directed to the “wrong” corporation (BP\n      Pensions Trust Ltd rather than BP Properties Ltd).\n      -\n\n      In a later appeal by my father, the same judge acknowledged that both\n      BP entities were effectively treated as the same for the purposes of the\n      ruling.\n      -\n\n      These circumstances resulted in the court upholding the opposing\n      party’s registration despite prior correspondence demonstrating an\n      assertion of ownership.\n      -\n\n   Following these proceedings, my father’s application to the *European\n   Court of Human Rights* was blocked.\n   -\n\n   The family home was demolished, preventing re-entry, even for the\n   retrieval of personal belongings.\n   -\n\n   Despite its historical significance and a petition to protect it, the\n   property was not preserved. The house had prior heritage interest,\n   including documented connections to Marconi.\n   -\n\n   *1991:* My father passed away at just 41.\n   -\n\n   *1994:* The land was subsequently excavated, with items of\n   archaeological interest removed.\n\nTaken together, these events demonstrate a situation in which *land\nregistration and subsequent reliance by public bodies proceeded without\ndefinitive adjudication of ownership*, raising questions regarding\nadministrative safeguards, procedural coherence, and record integrity.\n\nI am submitting this information purely for institutional awareness. I am *not\nmaking allegations of misconduct*, nor requesting investigation. I am able\nto provide a *concise factual timeline and references to public records*\nshould it assist the Committee’s understanding.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\n\nRequester"},{"id":"gm-19c12a1b5c48b53b","subject":"Concern regarding historical record following disputed land dispossession","name":"Concern regarding historical record following disputed land dispossession","date":"2026-01-31T05:58:32Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19c12a1b5c48b53b.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19c129f423e151ce-concern-regarding-historical-record-following-disputed-land-dispossession","scopeTopics":["tna"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["tna"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19c129f423e151ce","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"tna","name":"The National Archives"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19c12a1b5c48b53b.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Sirs/Ma'am,\n\nI'm writing to raise a concern regarding the integrity of the historical\nrecord following disputed dispossession of land i.e. BP vs Buckler 1987\n\nAfter forced removal and demolition of our home, archaeological excavation\nand removal of items took place on land whose ownership had never been\nconclusively adjudicated at the time of registration. BP asserted it, but\nit was never determined in a ruling beforehand as the July 1974 case\nbetween BP and my grandmother was adjourned and the October 31st 1974\nappeal and audience with a judge she made just 24 hours before, was\nsquashed by letters sent by BP that same morning.\n\nI am not making allegations here, simply stating facts about this landmark\nlegal case. And asking whether this type of circumstance is relevant to\narchival or historical record considerations.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\n\nRequester"},{"id":"gm-19c129e305c94717","subject":"Historical conveyancing and registration process concern - BP vs Buckler 1987","name":"Historical conveyancing and registration process concern - BP vs Buckler 1987","date":"2026-01-31T05:54:42Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19c129e305c94717.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19c129cd06379870-historical-conveyancing-and-registration-process-concern-bp-vs-buckler-1987","scopeTopics":["legal-regulators"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["legal-regulators"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19c129cd06379870","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"legal-regulators","name":"Legal regulators"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19c129e305c94717.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Sirs/ Ma'am,\n\nI'm writing to flag a historical concern relating to conveyancing and land\nregistration practice.\n\nA land title was registered in the BP vs Buckler case, by BP in 1982,\ndespite unresolved competing claims and absence of a final judicial\ndetermination, and that registration was later treated as conclusive by\ncourts and authorities.\n\nI am not seeking advice or action. I am writing only to ask whether this\ntype of scenario is of interest from a professional standards or historical\npractice perspective.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\n\nRequester"},{"id":"gm-19c129c2c9c4e8b0","subject":"BP vs Buckler 1987","name":"BP vs Buckler 1987","date":"2026-01-31T05:52:30Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19c129c2c9c4e8b0.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19c129b9c14b528d-bp-vs-buckler-1987","scopeTopics":["legal-regulators"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["legal-regulators"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19c129b9c14b528d","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"legal-regulators","name":"Legal regulators"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19c129c2c9c4e8b0.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Sirs/Ma'am,\n\nI am writing to ask whether a historical land dispute may be of interest\nfrom a professional or law-reform perspective.\n\nThe case involved reliance on registered title obtained prior to definitive\nadjudication, and subsequent judicial reasoning that appears internally\ninconsistent regarding party identity and correspondence.\n\nI am not seeking advocacy or review. I am simply asking whether such issues\nfall within areas the Bar Council monitors in relation to procedural\nfairness and the development of land law.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\n\nRequester"},{"id":"gm-19c1293a204d5aba","subject":"BP v Buckler 1987 and the 1982 land registration of Great House Farm, Llandough (now Church View Close)","name":"BP v Buckler 1987 and the 1982 land registration of Great House Farm, Llandough (now Church View Close)","date":"2026-01-31T05:43:10Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19c1293a204d5aba.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19c1280880fdd034-bp-v-buckler-1987-and-the-1982-land-registration-of-great-house-farm-llandough-now-church-view-close","scopeTopics":["vale-foi"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["vale-foi"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19c1280880fdd034","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"vale-foi","name":"Vale of Glamorgan Council"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19c1293a204d5aba.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Sirs/Ma'am,\n\nI'm writing in relation to land formerly known as *Great House Farm,\nLlandough (Vale of Glamorgan)*, subsequently developed for residential use\nand historically connected with the proceedings culminating in *BP v\nBuckler (Court of Appeal, 1987)*.\n\nI appreciate that the Local Planning Authority is not responsible for\ndetermining land ownership, nor am I asking it to do so. My purpose is to\nplace on record a *chronological summary of unresolved land-governance\nissues* which may be material to the integrity of the planning history\nrelied upon for that site.\n\nIn brief and as reflected in public records:\n\n   -\n\n   In *July 1974*, proceedings involving my grandmother and BP both\n   asserting ownership, were adjourned and did not result in a judicial final\n   determination.\n   -\n\n   On *31 October 1974*, a further listed hearing did not proceed\n   substantively, following correspondence sent that morning by BP entities.\n   No judgment on title was issued. My nan requested the audience with the\n   judge the day before, with her lawyer. But that got leaked, the press\n   descended on our farm (not that I can find evidence of news articles from\n   said press), and the following morning the two letters from BP arrived to\n   our solicitor, effectively squashing the court hearing and audience with\n   the judge my nan had sought, to make her case of ownership. Her reply to\n   both BP letters (one from BP Pension Trust Ltd and one from BP Properties\n   Ltd), that morning of the 31st of October, was also 'I own the land'.\n   -\n\n   Throughout this period, my grandmother was *excluded from dealings*\n   concerning land we asserted we owned, while the opposing party\ncontinued to *assert\n   ownership*, notwithstanding the absence of a concluded judicial finding.\n   -\n\n   In or around *1982*, the land was registered in BP’s name, despite the\n   earlier proceedings never having resolved the competing claims nor having\n   resulted in findings adverse to my grandmother.\n   -\n\n   My grandmother then died in *1983*, after the registration had taken\n   place, without compensation and without any final adjudication of her claim.\n   -\n\n   In the *1987 appeal*, the Court referred to correspondence as having\n   been replied to (albeit not to the correct corporate entity - which is\n   ultimately why we lost BP vs Buckler 1987), yet in my fathers appeal that\n   followed, whereby he argued that surely BP Pension Trust should have been\n   the claimant not BP Properties, the same judge from the case dismissed the\n   appeal citing that both companies were effectively the same. This is all\n   evidence on the face of the public record of these proceedings.\n   -\n\n   By that stage, and in the absence of a documentary title which had\n   effectively been overtaken by registration, my family was driven to advance\n   arguments in adverse possession as a *fallback position*, which itself\n   should have assured our continued occupancy.\n\nThe cumulative effect was that my family was *displaced*, the land was\ndeveloped, and no compensation was ever paid, despite ownership never\nhaving been conclusively decided in court. Whether intentionally or not,\nthe process operated in a manner where asserted ownership became treated as\nsettled fact.\n\nI am not alleging misconduct, nor am I seeking retrospective remedies from\nthe Authority. I am simply asking whether, given the above sequence, it\nwould be appropriate for the Authority to:\n\n   -\n\n   note that historic planning decisions were taken on the basis of *assumed\n   title*, and\n   -\n\n   record that the underlying ownership dispute was *never finally\n   adjudicated*, for the sake of transparency and future governance\n   integrity.\n\nThis correspondence is intended to be factual, measured, and\nnon-adversarial. I am happy to supply references to the relevant hearings,\nregistrations, or judgments if that assists your records. But what I'm\nhighlighting here is all on public record and in the public eye.\n\n\nYours faithfully,\n\n\nRequester"},{"id":"gm-19c1274015b09e39","subject":"BP vs Buckler 1987","name":"BP vs Buckler 1987","date":"2026-01-31T05:08:37Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19c1274015b09e39.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19c1272a0ff5003b-bp-vs-buckler-1987","scopeTopics":["phso"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["phso"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19c1272a0ff5003b","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"phso","name":"Parliamentary and Health Service Ombudsman"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19c1274015b09e39.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Sirs/Ma'am,\n\nI'm seeking guidance on whether a concern I have may fall within the remit\nof maladministration by public bodies.\n\nThe issue relates to a land registration made in 1982 which appears to have\nbeen relied upon by courts and enforcement authorities despite there being\nno definitive judicial determination of ownership at the time, and despite\nunresolved competing claims.\n\nI am not seeking compensation or a ruling on ownership at this stage. I am\nseeking an independent view on whether administrative process failures may\nhave occurred and whether it would be appropriate for the matter to be\nexamined further.\n\nI would be grateful for advice on whether this is something your office can\nconsider, or whether another oversight body would be more appropriate.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester"},{"id":"gm-19c1270aaa478aaf","subject":"Request for review of potential irregularities in 1982 land registration","name":"Request for review of potential irregularities in 1982 land registration","date":"2026-01-31T05:04:58Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19c1270aaa478aaf.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19c126c7628a9311-quest-for-review-of-potential-irregularities-in-1982-land-registration","scopeTopics":["hmlr"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["hmlr"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19c126c7628a9311","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"hmlr","name":"HM Land Registry"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19c1270aaa478aaf.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear HM Land Registry – Fraud Review Team,\n\nSirs/Ma'am,I am writing to request a review of the integrity of a land\nregistration made in or around *1982*, which later underpinned the\nproceedings commonly referred to as *BP v Buckler (1987)*.\n\nMy concern is to raise reasonable grounds of belief that the 1982\nregistration *may have been obtained or maintained through\nmisrepresentation, non-disclosure, or procedural irregularity*, resulting\nin a register entry that did not reflect a title definitively determined by\na court of law.\n\nIn summary:\n\n   1.\n\n   *No definitive judicial determination of ownership existed at the time\n   of registration*\n   Prior to the 1982 registration, the courts had not conclusively\n   determined ownership of the land.\n   -\n\n      Proceedings in *July 1974* were adjourned.\n      -\n\n      An appeal pursued by my grandmother on *31 October 1974* was\n      effectively neutralised following two letters sent by BP-associated\n      entities, rather than by a substantive judicial ruling on title.\n      -\n\n      Ownership was *asserted*, not adjudicated.\n      2.\n\n   *Competing assertions of ownership were unresolved*\n   BP asserted ownership, but so did my grandmother, who maintained\n   possession and occupation until her death in *1983*, after the registry\n   entry had already been filed.\n   At no point prior to her death was there a final court decision\n   extinguishing hers (or our families) claim or possession.\n   3.\n\n   *Inconsistent treatment of BP entities in later proceedings*\n   In the 1987 proceedings, the judge indicated that correspondence had not\n   been replied to because it was sent by a different BP entity (BP Properties\n   rather than BP Trust).\n   However, in my father’s related appeal, the same judge treated *BP Trust\n   and BP Properties as effectively the same entity*.\n   This inconsistency meant that:\n   -\n\n      We were said to have lost because of non-reply, while\n      -\n\n      The judgments simultaneously acknowledge that a reply was in fact\n      made and that the entities were treated as one.\n      This contradiction is material, as it goes directly to whether BP’s\n      asserted title was properly established.\n      4.\n\n   *Possession and long occupation were not displaced by lawful process*\n   My family had occupied the land openly and continuously for generations\n   without challenge. We consistently asserted ownership and, in any event,\n   possession sufficient to ground squatter’s or possessory rights.\n   Our removal was *not voluntary*; it was effected by force and exclusion,\n   not by a final determination of title.\n   5.\n\n   *Subsequent events reinforce the concern*\n   Following the appeal, my father’s application to the European Court of\n   Human Rights was blocked, the farm was demolished preventing re-entry, and\n   he also died thereafter in *1991*.\n   Shortly after his death in 1994, the land was excavated and items of\n   archaeological value were removed, despite prior knowledge of Roman remains\n   on the property (including a Roman soldier previously uncovered within the\n   living structure).\n\nTaken together, these matters raise serious questions as to whether the\n1982 registration accurately reflected the legal position at the time, or\nwhether it relied on assertions and correspondence that had not been\nvalidated by a court and that were later treated inconsistently.\n\nI respectfully request that HM Land Registry review whether:\n\n   -\n\n   The statutory requirements for registration were properly satisfied in\n   1982; and\n   -\n\n   The register may be inaccurate or was obtained by mistake,\n   misrepresentation, or material non-disclosure.\n\nI am able to supply supporting documents, title numbers, correspondence,\nand a concise timeline if required. But everything already on public record\nindicates and supports everything I've highlighted in this email.\n\nRegards,\n\nRequester"},{"id":"gm-19c0968af71d61f9","subject":"Application to Record or Rectify Interest Omitted at First Registration","name":"Application to Record or Rectify Interest Omitted at First Registration","date":"2026-01-29T10:59:39Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19c0968af71d61f9.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19c091a0a10b8e0d-application-to-record-or-rectify-interest-omitted-at-first-registration","scopeTopics":["hmlr"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["hmlr"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case correspondence); email addresses, phone numbers and private addresses redacted.","threadId":"gt-19c091a0a10b8e0d","direction":"sent","from":"Requester","institutions":[{"id":"hmlr","name":"HM Land Registry"}],"refs":[],"jsonUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/api/message/gm-19c0968af71d61f9.json","textStatus":"available","text":"Dear HM Land Registry Customer Support,\n\nI am writing to raise a formal concern that the registered title to land\nformerly occupied by my family, first registered in the name of BP\nProperties Ltd in or around November 1982, may not accurately reflect the\nlegal and factual position that existed at the time of first registration.\n\nI respectfully request guidance on the appropriate procedure to apply for\nthe entry of a notice or restriction, and/or for rectification or\nalteration of the register, on the basis that an occupied and disputed\ninterest was not recorded.\n\n*Summary of relevant facts (supported by court records and contemporaneous\nnewspaper reporting):*\n\n   1.\n\n   *Long-term occupation and asserted ownership*\n   My grandmother and family before her, occupied the farmhouse and\n   surrounding land openly and continuously for centuries, but certainly\n   decades prior to registration; without payment of rent and while asserting\n   ownership. The property was maintained as our family home and farm.\n   2.\n\n   *No conclusive determination of title in 1974*\n   Proceedings in July 1974 relating to possession and title were adjourned\n   and never substantively determined. Further proceedings in October 1974\n   were interrupted by correspondence from BP entities offering a purported\n   licence to occupy. As a result, there was no final judicial determination\n   in 1974 establishing BP’s title on the merits.\n   3.\n\n   *Express rejection of the purported licence*\n   My grandmother expressly rejected the licence, stating that the land was\n   hers. This rejection was acknowledged in later proceedings, but as having\n   been communicated to BP Pension Trust Limited, not the claimant in BP Vs\n   Buckler 1987 i.e. BP Properties Ltd.\n   4.\n\n   *Inconsistent treatment of BP corporate identity*\n   Subsequently the rejection was discounted on the basis that it was\n   addressed to BP Pension Trust Limited rather than BP Properties Ltd.\n   However, in dismissing my father’s appeal to that case almost immidiately\n   afterwards, the court and same judge, treated BP Pension Trust Limited and\n   BP Properties Ltd as \"effectively the same entity for substantive\n   purposes\". This inconsistency resulted in the rejection being acknowledged\n   but disregarded when it favoured BP, while corporate equivalence was relied\n   upon to defeat the appeal.\n   5.\n\n   *Forced removal and restraint (not abandonment)*\n   My family did not abandon the property or leave by our own accord, nor\n   did we fully understand what or why this was happening. In 1987, my father\n   and all of us, his dependants, were forcibly removed. Contemporary\n   newspaper reports state that he was also taken from a hospital bed,\n   detained without criminal charge, and made subject to a restraining order\n   preventing him from returning to the farm. He was not even permitted to\n   retrieve personal belongings.\n   6.\n\n   *Demolition following enforced exclusion*\n   While my father was later restrained from returning to occupy or defend\n   our home, the farmhouse was demolished. This enforced exclusion and\n   demolition rendered continued occupation impossible and removed the\n   physical evidence of long-term residence.\n   7.\n\n   *Registration despite occupation and unresolved dispute*\n\n   BP Properties Ltd was registered as proprietor in 1982, at a time when:\n\n\n   - the land was occupied by my family,\n   - ownership was actively disputed,\n   - no court had conclusively determined title, and\n   - cessation of occupation occurred only later through enforcement and\n   restraint, not voluntary departure.\n\nNo notice, restriction, or record of the disputed and occupied interest\nappears to have been entered on the register at first registration.\n\n*Purpose of this correspondence*\n\nI am not seeking to relitigate historic proceedings at this stage. I am\nseeking to ensure that the land register accurately reflects interests and\ndisputes that existed at the time of first registration, and that the\nabsence of abandonment and the circumstances of enforced removal are\nproperly understood.\n\nI respectfully request advice on:\n\n   - the appropriate procedure to apply for a notice or restriction\n   reflecting an omitted interest; and/or\n   - the process for seeking rectification or alteration of the register\n   under the Land Registration legislation applicable at the time.\n\nPlease advise on the relevant forms, evidential requirements, and next\nsteps.\n\nRegards,\n\n\nRequester"},{"id":"gm-19c03adce0cc91d5","subject":"New response to your FOI request - Freedom of Information Request – Administrative Records for BP Properties Ltd v Buckler (1974)","name":"New response to your FOI request - Freedom of Information Request – Administrative Records for BP Properties Ltd v Buckler (1974)","date":"2026-01-28T08:17:27Z","dateStatus":"email-date-from-original-message","category":"Case correspondence (original email, redacted)","evidenceVersion":"1.0.0","source":"Wayback case-mail API: redacted copy of the original email","sourceUrl":"https://wayback.bucklerfamily.estate/mail/gm-19c03adce0cc91d5.txt","threadSlug":"gt-19c035a275e5a494-new-response-to-your-foi-request-freedom-of-information-request-administrative-records-for-bp-properties-ltd-v-buckler-1","scopeTopics":["moj"],"recordType":"correspondence","tags":["moj"],"sourcePath":null,"classificationNote":"Ingested from the family's Gmail (verified case co